\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Keeps Low Profile As Dems Turn To \uc2 Obama\uc2 ; Dems Look To \uc2 Obama\uc2  As GOP Gains Midterm Momentum; Dems Ease Off Abortions Ads As Econ Woes Hit Voters; Four Senate Races Remain Toss-ups In Final Stretch; Key Senate Races Set To Come Down To The Wire; Dems Look To \uc2 Obama\uc2  As GOP Gains Midterm Momentum; Walker Responds To \uc2 Obama\uc2 : "I'm A Warrior For God"; GA Voters Weight In On Closely Watched Gov Race; SCOTUS Weighs Race As Factor In College Admissions; GOP Eyes House Majority To Launch Biden Probes\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Karl Rove, Robert Wolf, Kellyanne Conway, Robert  Wolf, Aishah Hasnie, Shannon Bream \par \uc2 8,804 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): I suspect you. You might call it bad news for them, good news for single folks around the country. Gerri, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 GERRI WILLIS, "THE WILLIS REPORT" HOST: That's right.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes. My wife was saying that Tom Brady will be the most eligible bachelor. So, what about Gisele? Should be pretty healthy.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: She isn't bad.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And obviously I was I was doing that from, from memory. Steve Harvey bought Tyler Perry's mansion --\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: -- overlooking the Chattahoochee for $15 million. So, you know.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Still some real money. You should play the Powerball, is the point.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Steve Harvey money is what I mean. Hey, thanks for joining us. See you again tomorrow. I'm John Roberts.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Happy Halloween. And THE STORY starts right now with Martha.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you very much, John and Gillian. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum, THE STORY is, what else folks? Elections are now just over one week from today. So, who better than two people with deep experience in campaigns and in the White House, veteran pollster Kellyanne Conway and Karl Rove both here with they're about a week away predictions about what we can expect next Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Also, Ohio Congressman Jim Jordan will be here ahead of our trip tomorrow to Ohio, we'll be in Columbus to have a town hall with \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and J.D. Vance. You don't want to miss that tomorrow night, and THE STORY will be live there at 3:00 as well with some of the voters on what they expect, what they want to ask come tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 But first, former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  is the one that Democrats are turning to, to ignite their voters right now as the president stayed home in Delaware, went to his granddaughter's a soccer game, I believe, over the weekend, did some early voting, had a generally quiet weekend himself as his former boss was hard at work in the swing states.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , 44TH PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Just about every Republican politician seems obsessed with just two things: own the lips and getting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's approval. That's their agenda. No, no, no, don't move. Vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the 44th president hopes that he can sweep in on his Halloween and be a superhero. In some of these races. He's flying to these three states in the coming days: Nevada, Arizona, Pennsylvania, are really important as we watch a toss-up Senate races here. The current president has zero campaign events scheduled today. This evening, he and the First Lady, they were at a memorial service, I believe today in New York. So, that's what they were occupied with today.\par \par \uc2 But then tonight, they're going to hand out trick or treat candy to young folks who are visiting the White House this evening. So, that is what they will be up to. New polling shows Republicans leading by four on the generic ballot in terms of, you know, would you rather see Republicans or Democrats in charge.\par \par \uc2 And brand-new Siena College polling, the New York Siena College, \uc2 New York Times\uc2  polls shows that voters in these key battlegrounds want Republicans to take control of the Senate eight days from now. So, with that, we bring in Karl Rove, Former Deputy Chief of Staff to President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and Fox News Contributor. Karl, great to have you with us, as we are now just one week and one day away from a very big election moment in the United States. What, where do you think students stand right now when you look at the big picture, Karl?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, look at the end of a campaign, a gravitational force has taken over. The, the kind of things that sort of undergird this election are, first of all, a deep concern about the future of the country, only about a quarter of the American people think we're going in the right direction, three quarters believe we're off on the wrong track. And then, the major issues that are dominating the agenda as we come down to the end inflation, the economy, crime, and in some places the border, all four of those tend to work for the Republicans. So, those kinds of things are coming into play as we get to the end, and as a result, not going to be a pleasant night for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you know, there's a lot of polls out there in this closing week. And one of the things that we're seeing is that Democrats are trying really hard, Karl, at this point, and President \uc2 Obama\uc2  has taken every opportunity he can get to say, look, it's all about economy, and it's all about jobs. And we also see that ads that mentioned abortion have ticked down by 10 percent. And there don't appear to be any new ads that talk about abortion coming out on the Democrat side. So, what is all this telling us?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, it's that they're finally waking up to the reality that this election is about the economy is stupid. It's about people deeply concerned about the future of the economy, believing that we face a recession, they're going to work, they're hearing about slowdowns. They see the orders, they're tapering off, they're worried about that. They know from personal experience, when they go to the grocery store, fill up the car with gas, go shopping for the kids, that everything is costing more. And for 18 months, we've had a decline in real wages every single month.\par \par \uc2 So, that paycheck is not stretching as far as he used to stretch. So, that's the reality. And finally, the Democrats after spending months talking about this election, being about abortion, and the threat to democracy, have awakened to the fact that ordinary voters are looking for answers on who's going to do something to look to chip put bring prices back into, into check and who's going to make certain that our economy is strengthened, and who cares about the violence that seemingly is overtaking too many of our communities.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, when you look at some of these toss up races on the House side, I mean that there's a very wide range obviously as to how this could go and in any one of these individual races, you may see places that are surprising. So, we really don't know what this is going to look like come election night, because so much of this is so tight. There's one take from Gallup that says that abortion is the second highest ranking issue among our voters right after the economy, and they have it at 42 percent. So, you know, give us a sense. I know you have a whiteboard there, Karl, of what, what you're seeing out there that you want people to understand right now?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Yes, well, first, first point about abortion. You're right by one standard if you say is this important? Abortion is up there. If you say, is it the most important issue? Gallup has it at four percent. It hit its high shortly after the Dobbs decision last summer at eight percent. It is dwarfed by the issues of inflation, economy, and crime and border, which are either in the case of the border, I think it's tied, but everybody else is, is, is more important in people's mind when you ask what is the most important question, the most important issue facing the country.\par \par \uc2 In fact, Gallup asked a really interesting question, what which party will do a better job handling the issue that you think is most important that, that allows the voter then to say, well, I'm thinking the most important issue is climate or inflation? They get to decide. That -- the answer that question is 48 Republican, 37 Democrat, that is the biggest margin, second biggest margin for that question for Republicans since they began asking that question the 1940s. So, that's one thing I'd say, let's step back for a minute from the hip, and the drib and drive in the back and forth to look at what's driving the voters decision.\par \par \uc2 The other thing I want to say is be very careful about the polls here at the end. You mentioned the New York Times Siena poll, let's take a look at this. These are the polls that have been conducted in the state of Pennsylvania. Beginning in mid-October. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  up two, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  up two, then the time, New York Times Siena poll up five, which, incidentally, was concluded before the debate, and then after the debate one poll since then, Oz up three. So, we got to be, this might be a little bit of an outlier because it came in now same in Georgia, again for the middle of the month. First, it was a tie than Walker by one, Walker by two.\par \par \uc2 Then along comes to New York Times Siena poll, Warnock by three and then one poll since that Walker by five. So, both of these races are like 1.5 for Fetterman here; 1.4 for Walker here, but there is a trend here and the trend is not to the good, not to the good for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I mean, obviously there was so much attention on that Fetterman poll, and he, except for the one that you showed there that shows up three. Fetterman has been ahead through this entire process. And as you point out, Karl, we don't have a clean post-debate poll. I think that Siena one included some post-debate reaction. But we don't have a full look at what happened after that. But it seems like Democrats feel like they can still win Pennsylvania. And we're going to dig in on this in a little bit. But I want to get your thoughts on that, Karl.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, look, I think, I think they can still win. But, but the poll that was conducted after the debate is the one that shows Oz up by three, and again, at the end, the gravitational forces are going to be working to the advantage of Oz not, not, not Fetterman, because the issues that Oz is running on, there are the issues that are driving voters: inflation, economy, crime, and in the case of Pennsylvania -- the issue of economic development through fracking.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Karl, thank you. Always great to see you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: You bet. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , as you may have heard, was caught on a hot mic fretting about Georgia's, you know, Georgia might be a little tricky. Georgia, we're not doing so well. He said more or less to President Biden on the tarmac the other day.\par \par \uc2 And a new poll that is just in suggests that that may be shifting, as Karl just talked about, as well. So, we're going to bring Georgia voters to you. There are folks that we spoke with several weeks ago. We're going to bring them back and ask how they are feeling, some of them undecided, with about a week to go about their choice. That choice is between \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and Reverend Warnock and also Kemp and Abrams very interesting races in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Kellyanne Conway live with her take coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, so the latest and final polling from the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and Siena College show these races are tight. This race is far from over. And it puts Democrats in ahead in all of the important Senate races in Arizona and Georgia and shows a dead heat in Nevada, between \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  and Laxalt