our vote, your voice and your choice don't matter, because you can't help the underdog, and you can't --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Are they skewed Democrat -- this -- you know, this is not -- I mean, why are they so off? Why are they so off?\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: They're off because of their sample part. And most of it is the science part. But let me just tell you about the Senate races quickly. They were way off from the Senate races as well.\par \par \uc2 They had \uc2 Thom Tillis\uc2  losing. He is -- every election, Republican senator they had. \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 ' losing. She won by nine points. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  won by 10 points.\par \par \uc2 Those are not squeakers. And I think their science is way off. It also, I just tweeted out if anybody wants to get Kellyanne polls. I don't like \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , but the new Elon Musk's \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , I like a lot. And so, I tweeted out, and that --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He certainly have a lot more followers.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: I tweeted out that I showed that Nate Cohn, who runs the upstart from \uc2 New York Times\uc2  has put out four or five articles last couple of months where he just can't be wrong this cycle.\par \par \uc2 First, he has the Democrats holding the House and he has Republicans winning everything. And so, that's not helpful to anybody in anything. But look, polls are a snapshot in time. They show you the fundamental shifts.\par \par \uc2 And right now, Robert, respectfully, the shifts are that voters are talking about rising crime and rising costs. Parents, moms are still upset about education.\par \par \uc2 And I think in Georgia, it goes to a runoff and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  pulls it out. They've gone a little too far in the personal attacks that bothers people when you're not attacking crime.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: You're not attacking inflation or attacking the opponent.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me --\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOLF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): I'm waited -- I'm waited for my --\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: And Fetterman.\par \par \uc2 I'm waited for my rebut, this is like -- this is like a --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I know. Here we go. Let's get Robert a chance to get in here.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: This is like a (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I know. Let's get in here. So, what is your preference for the outcome of this year's Senate elections? And you look at it, you've got 49 percent GOP control -- they want in Arizona. 49 percent in Georgia, they want GOP control. And Nevada, 48 percent want GOP control.\par \par \uc2 So, what's interesting here are when you look at these places, where you've got -- New York, California, Virginia, with the House races, right? You've got a lot of people who seem to be crossing over and Democrats who look like they're wanting to see a GOP outcome for these races. Who knows what's going to happen on election night? That's why we -- that's why we stick around for the actual results.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But you are not feeling that in your gut?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Listen, I think elections are much more local than we look at these national polls.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Maybe in the House, it has a bit of more of a wave. We saw that in 2018. And the blue side this year, you know, this talk on the red side.\par \par \uc2 I think with respect to Senate, this, we're talking about seven different candidates on each side. And it's a mano-a-mano type of environment. And I think if you look at each, I could give you a reason exactly why?\par \par \uc2 I mean, if you want to talk crime, that means you shouldn't be for Abbot or you shouldn't be for Kemp, because crime is up in Georgia and Texas. Well, if you want to talk --\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Come on. They can see defunding --\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Wait, time -- no, no, I didn't -- wait, let me just -- let me reply. I could cherry pick every candidate on each side.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Are you for defunding the police?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Not at all.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Great.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: I could check on -- I could on each candidate on every side. So, for example, let's just take Georgia. Unemployment is down, jobs up the highest they've ever been.\par \par \uc2 And they're getting $10 billion from the infrastructure plan. That's a reason for the on the economy, vote Warnock.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: No, that's the reason to vote for Kemp.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: OK. Well, that -- see the go --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, I mean, you've got a lot of --\par \par \uc2 WOLF: You can't have it both ways.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, yes. I mean, you've got a lot of --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WOLF: You cannot have it both ways.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Most -- some voters, do you have it both ways. They're going to -- they're going to just split their take.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: OK. Then --\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Like this chief executive. You know, when President \uc2 Obama\uc2  was there over eight years, they went from 28 democratic governors to 16. You lost 12 democratic governors.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WOLF: And then, went back up to 27. Wait, they went back up to 27.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Just people don't want him as chief executives.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, when -- I mean, obviously, you know, the president's approval numbers are really weak. Yeah. And he's been at home for the past two weeks, essentially. I mean, he's been made a couple of trips. Mike, he's going go to Pennsylvania, I guess.\par \par \uc2 You know, what does that tell you?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Well, listen, the incumbent president always gets smoked during the midterms. Always, when they're not at the top of the ticket. Look at \uc2 Obama\uc2  in 2010. Look at, you know, Trump in 2020.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But it tells you that people want --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: That they're invited on the trail.\par \par \uc2 Yes, well, they've been invited. That's not true. Biden was in --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: No.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Trump was out there a ton in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And Bush was even out there in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Biden was in Pittsburgh. Biden was in Pittsburgh. He's going to be in Philadelphia, he's going to be in Oregon.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But Biden has been just like he was during the last election, basically, at home.\par \par \uc2 And I, I don't see how that doesn't tell you something.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WOLF: I think most people don't want the incumbent with them. I take that.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Hey, Robert, I know a lot of Americans --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, they wanted \uc2 Obama\uc2  and Trump with them, big time.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: \uc2 Obama\uc2  was barely on the stump in 2014.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I can show you -- I can show you the places he went. Yes.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Not in 2014. Not 2014.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Well, 2010 he was the first midterm.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Right. And they learned from it.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Oh, well, no, excuse me.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: They got smoked.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Quick, quick final thought, Kellyanne.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Listen, a lot of -- Martha, a lot of Americans are working from home now. That should not include the president and the vice president of the United States. They should not be working from home. She needs more on her public schedule. He should be out there.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: And by the way, if you voted for the inflation --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WOLF: campaigning is not working. That's not going to work.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: But if you voted -- if you voted for the Inflation Reduction Act, why are all these democratic senators running away from it?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: They shouldn't be.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: It should be part of their pitch.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Totally agree. I knew it -- I knew (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, I -- I've -- no, I think it's true. I mean, I think that if you believe in what you're doing --\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What -- regardless of your approval numbers, you should be out there, and you should be making the point. Look, you got to hang in there with us, right?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: He'll be in Philly.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, I remember \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  saying at the convention, you know, nobody could have fixed this in one term, right?\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You have to stick with us. But that -- we're not seeing it. So, we'll see what happens. Maybe it work. Maybe it works in the end.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: And I look forward to watching you tomorrow with \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you. Thank you, very much. Well, looking forward to it too. Great to have both of you here. Kellyanne And Richard, good to see you both.\par \par \uc2 So, Georgia voters decided it all in the Senate run offs in 2020.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): You remember that night? Right? So, when two Republicans lost and the Senate went to the Democrats, that was the outcome. The last time there was a big race in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 That was two years ago. So, how do they feel about how things are going in Georgia now? What do they want for their future? We'll speak with them next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , 44TH PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: My point is not that being a football player disqualifies you from being a senator, if you had put in the work, seems to me he's a celebrity who wants to be a politician. And we've seen how that goes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  talking about \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , the Republican running against Democrat Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 As we mentioned, the toss up race could decide control, once again of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . And it looks like it could possibly be heading to a runoff. We'll see what happens on election night.\par \par \uc2 New reaction from Walker on the trail today that will show you. Our panel of Georgia voters is standing by. But first, to congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie live in Cartersville, Georgia, northwest of Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 Hi, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi there, Martha. Well, those \uc2 Obama\uc2  comments have really left a bitter taste in \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 's mouth.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice-over): Take a look behind me. He is actually wrapping up his second stop of the day, taking some selfies with some of his supporters. Even signing some autographs here and there.\par \par \uc2 Look, he is been starting up his campaign speeches all weekend. And today, hitting back at the former president's criticism of him, \uc2 Obama\uc2  really went after Walker's character on Friday, called him a celebrity that wants to be a politician.\par \par \uc2 Walker has since been telling supporters, I'm not a celebrity, I'm a "warrior for God", and that apparently, the former president doesn't belong in this state.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R-GA), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: Now, I pray for \uc2 Obama\uc2  too because he needs some prayer. Go all he doing is sitting in his nice house, coming down here trying to tell us how to live. You know, he don't even live here. Get out of here. You're not voting for me. You can't even vote. You can't even vote for \uc2 Obama\uc2 . You got to go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: And now, Martha, every single vote in this state matters. Take a look at this new University of Georgia survey. It shows a dead heat. 45 percent for both of the candidates. A margin of error just three points. So, we are still looking at a runoff for this Senate seat.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have been shocked at how little Walker's scandals have heard him.\par \par \uc2 You and after a second woman accused him of pressuring her to get an abortion. Walker, of course has denied this. Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  even caught on a hot mic last week, telling the president the state where we're going downhill is Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Also, from that same University of Georgia survey, Martha, 52 percent dislike how Walker has handled himself personally. But, I've been talking to voters here. Conservatives who say it doesn't matter what Walker has done in the past, they are just willing to forgive.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera):And they want to send a Republican to Washington somehow. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): Very interesting. Aishah, thank you very much. Joining me now, three Georgia voters who we spoke with a couple of weeks ago, right before the debate. It was the only debate between \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Donna Sharpe is a Republican. Marcus McCray, is a Democrat. And Bernadette Wright is an Independent. Great to have all of you with us.\par \par \uc2 So, Bernadette, let me start with you, you know, what's the impact of these stories that have come out about \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 ? And what was the impact of the debate for you? Where do you stand now?\par \par \uc2 BERNADETTE WRIGHT, INDEPENDENT VOTER, GEORGIA: So, my position. Thank you for having us again today. But my position remains unchanged.\par \par \uc2 In fact, after the debate, I have to be honest with you, I was a little bit disappointed in the performance of both of the candidates. But I left the debate still understanding that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  does not have a plan for us. All of the new stories that are coming out, he's spending a great deal of time attacking a pastor, and calling the pastor a sheep in wolf's clothing, while at the same time, calling himself a warrior for God, which is taking attention off of the issues.\par \par \uc2 Because it's almost unfathomable, that he could call himself those things based on all of the news about him, based on all of the complaints about him, based on all of the, just to be frank with you, lies that have been told during this campaign.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm a little bit just embarrassed, to be honest with you, about the turn that this has taken, and I'm definitely going to vote for Warnock.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Donna Sharp, let me bring you in. What -- what's your reaction to what you have learned? And does it matter to you when it comes to voting day next Tuesday?\par \par \uc2 DONNA SHARPE, REPUBLICAN VOTER, GEORGIA: Well, thank you for having me again. As far as the debate goes, I was pleasantly surprised. I thought that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  did a really good job of linking his opponent to President Biden. And as far as everything that's going on in the personal life of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , what matters to me is that he will represent my values. He is not going to end filibuster, he is not going to make Puerto Rico a D.C. states. He's not going to stack the Supreme Court. So, that's what's on my mind as a voter.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Marcus, what about you? What do you make of where this race is now, given the fact that we spoke last time before that only debate?\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MCCRAY, DEMOCRATIC VOTER, GEORGIA: You know, again, thanks for having -- thanks for having me as well. I don't think my votes changed at all. Honestly, I casted a couple of weeks ago and I'm not looking back.\par \par \uc2 I am weary of this race, to be perfectly honest, and I hope to God that we don't have a run-off because I really liked to be able to watch the Hallmark movies with my wife without \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  or \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  erupting us.\par \par \uc2 But I think the thing that is frustrating me the most is, you know, we're hearing \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  come out and say like, he's a warrior for God. And I get it a little uncomfortable when I hear people insert war metaphor into my -- into my religion.\par \par \uc2 I'm a Christian just like he is so with \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 . But I think what's important here is that we need to be honest about the fact that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is running for a job, like he's interviewing for a job and he has not shown us beyond the ability to talk about abortion, that he has any command of any of the other issues. And if I were sitting across interviewing him, I would really be ready to say to the panel that we just need to go ahead and send it back home.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. I want to -- let me just ask you, it's just very quickly, who do you think actually wins regardless of your feeling and what you just told us. What is your gut telling us right now? Marcus first.\par \par \uc2 MCCRAY: I think Warnock wins.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: OK. Donna?\par \par \uc2 DONNA SHARPE, GEORGIA REPUBLICAN VOTER: I think it's \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 ,\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Bernadette?\par \par \uc2 BERNADETTE WRIGHT, GEORGIA INDEPENDENT VOTER: I think that Warnock will win in a runoff.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: In a runoff, all right. So, Bernadette, let me stay with you and let's watch this from \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 . This is a soundbite from the final Georgia governor debate. Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D), GEORGIA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: I'm not a member of the good ol' boys' club. So no, I don't have 107 sheriffs who want to be able to take Black people off the streets, who want to be able to go without accountability. I don't believe every sheriff wants that but I do know that we need a governor who believes in both defending law enforcement, but also defending the people of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Bernadette, what do you think about that? And are you going to vote for Kemp or Abrams?\par \par \uc2 WRIGHT: I'll be honest with you, I'm still leaning towards Kemp but they both said some things that made me a little bit uncomfortable with each of the candidates during the debate. I don't agree with her stance 100% when it comes to crime. I don't agree with his stance 100% when it comes to crime.\par \par \uc2 I feel like he spends a lot of time talking about street gangs and things like that to kind of stir up the contingency, if you will, when a lot of the gun activity is not coming from street gangs. I feel like they could -- if you could just take the both of those candidates and put them together, we'd have a great plan moving Georgia forward but that's not going to happen. One of the problems that I do have with Kemp is the fact that when it comes to the money that we have -- we have a lot of money, Georgia is doing well. I think he did a great job responding to Trump. I think he's done a great job with Georgia. I really don't have any complaints.\par \par \uc2 But with all of the money that we have in surplus, I do think some of that money should go to programs. And some of it should come back to the people who poured into it where Stacey wants to take all that money and put it in programs. So I'm kind of like I'm on the fence but I'm still leaning towards Kemp because Georgia has it good.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. You got to week to go. Just a few seconds left here, Donna, what's your take? What do you plan to do when it comes to voting in the governor's race?\par \par \uc2 SHARPE: Oh, I'm definitely voting for a Kemp. I think that he did a really good job of showing that \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  does in fact want to defund the police.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Marcus?\par \par \uc2 SHARPE: So he's got my vote.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much, Donna. Marcus?\par \par \uc2 MCCRAY: So I'm voting for \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 . And, you know, honestly, I don't think that we're talking the right things about defunding the police. Like she's really saying that we need to give them something else to do and everybody agrees that they're doing too much. So giving them the appropriate resources to respond to things that they shouldn't be, it's exact right move tonight.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Marcus, Bernadette and Donna, thank you. Great to see you all again. Going to be a big week, big day next Tuesday, thank you. Good to see you all.\par \par \uc2 So coming up, Congressman Jim Jordan and Shannon Bream as the Supreme Court takes on affirmative action in college admissions. This case is a big deal, we're going to talk about it next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This is a big question. Should colleges seek to balance their incoming classes based on race or based on merit? The Supreme Court is about to answer that very big question. Asian students asking Harvard and UNC why they took fewer of them even though their scores were higher. It's a case that could change the game in college admissions. Fox News Digital spoke to two Hispanic American Harvard students about this debate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH HERNANDEZ, HARVARD STUDENT: People like to think, you know, this is America, the American dream, everyone can make it. But that is the dream, that is what you want to be the case. That is unfortunately not always the case.\par \par \uc2 ALMA CONWAY, HARVARD STUDENT: Efforts like these make it more focused on race. I think it should be focused on merit and what they're actually bringing into the college and the holistic view. I'll give Harvard credit for that. It looks at the whole person, not just one single aspect of them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Fox News Sunday anchor and chief legal correspondent Shannon Bream live at the Supreme Court for us today. Shannon, great to have you with us. Give us the outlines of this case.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: So, Martha, normally a case gets about 60 minutes to be argued here at the court. These two cases, and I'll explain two of them, went on for hours today. It was very heated at times. This is a difficult issue.\par \par \uc2 So you've got a Harvard, a private institution, and University of North Carolina system, which is a public institution. These Asian American students, the plaintiffs in this case, say they're the ones who end up getting discriminated against because they don't get the same -- they would argue preference as Black or Hispanic candidates, or applicants for the same positions.\par \par \uc2 So the argument today is about how can you use race currently under the Supreme Court's precedents and framework. You can use it in a narrowly tailored way. It's got to be part of a holistic or bigger package of each applicant that you consider. But these plaintiffs today are saying get rid of race altogether. They say it's working against them, and it shouldn't be working against anybody.\par \par \uc2 Justice Kagan had real concerns. She says, well, our schools shouldn't they mirror what we want to see in our business, in our law, in our military. So that's got to happen at the universities for us to get to those goals in our society. But Justice Gorsuch suggests, he said, listen, there are actually cottage industries out there that Asian American applicants are now using where coaches tell them to avoid using their name, their identity, their background if they can avoid it, because they don't want to get filtered up by the so called Asian quotas.\par \par \uc2 So these universities say we're not discriminating. This is the best that we can do to figure out how to have a diverse student body. But folks on the other side say you end up hurting some people in the process on the basis of race. This whole thing now goes to a vote privately with the justices on Friday. From there we won't know for months what they actually decide, because then they have to write the opinions and hammer that all out behind the scenes.\par \par \uc2 So I would say don't wait to expect this anytime soon, but when it does come, I would expect based on what we heard today could be a big change to the way higher education now operates here in the US, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Fascinating. Shannon, thank you very much. With that we bring in \uc2 Mike Davis\uc2 , a former law clerk for Justice \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2 , former \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  Counsel, and now he's the president of the Article III Project. Mike, great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 I want to start by playing a soundbite from Judge Ketanji Brown today in part of the questioning of Patrick Strawbridge, who is from the Students for Fair Admissions. He's the attorney. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KETANJI BROWN JACKSON\uc2 , SUPREME COURT JUSTICE: You haven't demonstrated or shown one situation in which all they look at is race and take from that stereotypes and other things. They're looking at the full person with all of these characteristics.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK STRAWBRIDGE, STUDENTS FOR FAIR ADMISSIONS ATTORNEY: But our point is that all those other characteristics are not barred by the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Give us your thoughts on what's happening here, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE DAVIS\uc2 , PRESIDENT, ARTICLE III PROJECT: So the equal protection clause of the 14th Amendment outlaws racial discrimination by states, including state universities, like the University of North Carolina. The Civil Rights Act of 1964 outlaws racial discrimination by private entities that take federal funding and including \uc2 Harvard University\uc2 . Affirmative action based upon race is racial discrimination, and it's a zero sum game.\par \par \uc2 If you are giving out admission slots to people based upon race, including, you know, wealthy Black families based upon race, that hurts Asian families and that's the whole point of this. The Supreme Court will finally over rule here, I hope, terrible precedent from 2003 where they held that these colleges can discriminate for 25 more years. This needs to be overruled and hopefully this current Supreme Court will end this five years earlier than it showed up to the current case law.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, it is -- it's such an interesting case because we have all -- I remember, you know, which box do you check on this application, right? So those boxes would go away. If this is overturned, Mike, they would not be able to know what your racial background or your heritage is.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Yes. I mean, if you look at these multifactor tests that they're talking about today, that's what these Ivy League schools like Harvard used to use decades ago to exclude Jewish applicants. They're using that now for Asian applicants. And what we see with Harvard, which is even more egregious, is the alumni interviews these applicants and they're getting good scores all around. And then the Harvard Admissions Office gives the Asian applicants bad personality scores to keep them out.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, that's sounds like a conspiracy, right? So, how does that differ from an admission's person saying, look, this person's scores are a little bit lower than someone else's but I feel like everything else that they've done makes them a better candidate for our university.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Well, that's fine. You can look at other factors, you can look at their like, if they grew up in poverty, you can look at their economic background. But why should \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 's kids get a better opportunity to get into Ivy League schools than low income Asian kids who had less opportunities given to them?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's going to be really interesting to see what happens here. And you think about all the ramifications in the corporate world as well, and the way that candidates are chosen for things all across the board. \uc2 Mike Davis\uc2 , thank you very much. Great to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So if Republicans do win the majority in the House next Tuesday, Congressman Jim Jordan, the top Republican on the House Judiciary Committee would than be in a position to launch investigations into a number of things including \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , and his father, the president, potentially. Congressman Jim Jordan joins me live from Ohio as THE STORY has to Columbus tomorrow. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The man accused of attacking Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's husband told police that he wanted to hold the speaker hostage and "break her kneecaps." That according to the core papers that have now come out. 82-year-old Paul Pelosi attacked at their home in San Francisco on Friday while Speaker Pelosi was in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Investigator say 42-year old David DePape broke into the home and smashed Paul Pelosi in the head with a hammer that he and Mr. Pelosi were struggling over when the police enter. They say Pelosi appeared to be unconscious. And they also found a rope, a roll of tape, another hammer and zip ties.\par \par \uc2 And breaking moments ago, a source with Immigrations and Customs Enforcement tells Fox, DePape is in the country illegally. He overstayed his visa. He is from Canada. He is now charged with assault and attempted kidnapping. Speaker Pelosi's office says that her husband is expected to make a full recovery.\par \par \uc2 So if Republicans do win the majority in the House, that would mean that Ohio Congressman Jim Jordan would be set to take over the Judiciary Committee that would give him power to launch investigations, and even a possible impeachment of President Biden. Congressman Jordan now, the ranking member on the Judiciary Committee, joins me now. Congressman Jordan, always good to have you with us. Thanks for being here today.\par \par \uc2 So, what would be your -- you know, obviously that is -- that's a big deal, and all of these investigations would take a lot of your time. So what was your priority be? What would you address first and what do you think is most important?\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): Yes. By far the most important is the Justice Department that's now political. You cannot have a political Justice Department and also have a free society, a free country. So that is the most important thing. And we're going to dig into this, Martha. We have had, and I've said this many times. We've had now over 14 \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents come to us asking for whistleblower status talking about how political that place is.\par \par \uc2 That has to change, because if you have a political Justice Department, if you don't have equal treatment under the law, every single liberty we enjoy as Americans is in jeopardy. You think about the five fundamental rights we have under the First Amendment, your right to practice your faith, your right to assemble, right to petition, freedom of the press, freedom of speech, they're all in jeopardy if the Justice Department doesn't treat people equally under the law. And right now, that is not happening as evidenced by the fact, 14 \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents have come to us seeking a whistleblower status and talking about how political things are there.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, it clearly they were upset about the way the Hunter Biden case was handled. Do you think about the fact that parents at school board meetings were labeled as potential terrorists who think about what happened to some of the rioters after the Black Lives Matter? Or even people who have been vandalizing prolife centers?\par \par \uc2 It is it is very curious why some of these are pursued, and some are not. You know, a lot of people out there would like to know, though, what your what your plans are with regard to \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . And what about this question of impeaching President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? Here's what the former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  had to say about that on the campaign trail. I'm going to let you get right after this. Let's play it.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: If they get control of Congress, they will spend the next two years investigating President Biden and their political opponents. That's all they want to do. They see it as payback. They've already got prepared, that they're going to impeach Biden. They're not sure what for. But apparently, that's beside the point.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Well, a question on that piece, that will be decided by the whole conference. You know, here's what I want to focus on two years ago, this month, 51 former intel officials signed a letter that said the \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  story had a "all the earmarks of Russian disinformation." That was BS. People knew it at the time it was BS but they put that together. And that became the pretext for suppressing that story so that we the people couldn't have access to important information just days before the most important election we have, election for President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 How did that get put together? How do those -- many of those people still have a security clearance were they talking to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 ? Because remember, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  went and talked to \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  and said, oh, \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , you might want to keep this up. This has all the earmarks of Russian disinformation that impacted the election. And I thought my colleague, \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2 , made a great point in committee a couple of weeks ago. He said, when is the \uc2 FBI\uc2  not going to influence an election?\par \par \uc2 2016, they spied on President Trump's campaign. 2018, it was the Muller investigation. 2020, they suppressed that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  story, call it Russian disinformation. And in 2022, they raid the home of a former president 91 days before the midterm election. So, why don't the \uc2 FBI\uc2  just stay out and quit being political, and let the country decide, let the people decide who's going to represent them.\par \par \uc2 So that is something we need to dig into for the for the sake of our country. We need to get an answer to how they put that together and suppress that key story. That's something I want to look into.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well, I think that that's very fair. I think a lot of people would look at that. And they do want to have confidence in the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . And they have seen sort of the picking and choosing that appears very partisan. And you have whistleblowers, as you say, from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  who say that they were told, basically, you know, put that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  case aside, we're too close to an election. We don't want to be involved in it. And as you point out, there's a good argument that they've been involved in pretty much every election in the past six or eight years or so.\par \par \uc2 With regard to \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , what was your intentions be?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: Well, that investigation will be run out of the Oversight Committee. I don't think it'll be headquartered in, you know, in the Judiciary Committee. I think my good friend and colleague, Mr. Comer, will run that investigation if in fact, the American people put us in the majority, which I think they're going to do. So it'll be there.\par \par \uc2 I know Jamie is very concerned about these 150 suspicious activity reports that the Treasury Department has, we'd like to know what those say. So that's something I think we'll focus on as well relative to the Biden family "business operation." All this is important, but the broad picture is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has this narrative where he's called half the country fascist and extremists, and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  seems to be trying to make sure that that is the narrative that they get.\par \par \uc2 This is what \uc2 FBI\uc2  -- what these whistleblowers have told us. There's even a purging some of these guys have said they're trying to get rid of folks in the \uc2 FBI\uc2  who have conservative beliefs. So that is part of is broad concern we have with the Justice Department, specifically the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . We're going to investigate the specific matters and that broad general concern.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's an appropriate role of Congress to make sure that the judiciary and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  are treating everyone in this country fairly. And we'll see what happens. Jim Jordan, good to see you. We're headed to your home state tomorrow and we are looking forward to it. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: I'm glad, you're coming. All right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So more of THE STORY right after this. Be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's THE STORY on this Halloween. Tomorrow we will be in Ohio. Then at 6:00, I will co-host a townhall with Bret Baier with candidates JD Vance and \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 . Exactly one week from tomorrow is Election Day folks, stay tuned. "YOUR WORLD" starts right now. We'll see you in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020221101eiav00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Border Crisis; Russia Continues Missile Attacks on Ukraine; President Biden Targets Taxes on Gas Companies; Elon Musk's \uc2 Twitter\uc2  Changes; Man Charged in Paul Pelosi Attack; New York Gubernatorial Race; Midterm Homestretch\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto, Nate Foy, Greg Palkot, Hillary Vaughn, Kelly O'Grady,  Madison Alworth, Christina Coleman, Eric Shawn, Josh Kraushaar, Aishah  Hasnie, Peter Doocy \par \uc2 10,336 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Eight days away, and, as Martha said, the tension is building right now, and so too the close races.We're on top of them all.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto. And this is your, well, pretty much all board to board political world. And it gets even more political and nastier and more in your face as each hour and day goes by. We're tracking all of that, beginning what role the president might be playing, oddly, well, a little bit passive and standing back.\par \par \uc2 Peter Doocy is at the White House -- Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And, Neil, there's been so much attention in the closing days of this cycle on just a handful of battlegrounds, Ohio, Georgia, Pennsylvania, but the Republicans who are trying to flip control of the Senate say they now see opportunities in some races that have not been getting quite as much attention.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-FL): I think we clearly pick up Georgia, we clearly pick up Nevada, and I think we have a really good chance here in New Hampshire. I think we have really good chance in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Tiffany Smiley is basically tied in Washington. Joe O'Dea is barely behind -- Leora Levy -- behind -- Leora Levy is down I think about five points. I -- it depends on the night. I think we're going to have a great night.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The closing argument for Democrats is all about Republicans, warning that, if Congress flips, then Social Security and Medicare benefits are in danger, as the Republican closing argument is more about undoing big-ticket Biden agenda items like the Inflation Reduction Act, even though they are going to need Biden's signature to do that.\par \par \uc2 There's a new ABC News/\uc2 Ipsos\uc2  poll that finds 38 percent of voters are trusting the GOP more with handling inflation, as just 21 percent say they trust Democrats. The president himself has had really limited exposure to the campaign trail lately. The only battleground on the schedule this week is Pennsylvania again. He's also going to drop into Florida and New Mexico.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm feeling good. I mean, I have been into, I guess now, 36 constituencies. So I'm going to be spending the rest of the time making the case that this is not a referendum. It's a choice, a fundamental choice, a choice between two very different visions for the country.\par \par \uc2 And that's what it's about.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: later on this hour, President Biden is going to go into the Roosevelt Room to deliver remarks, where we expect him to blame big oil companies for recent high gas prices.\par \par \uc2 There's also going to be this evening a trick or treating event. And based on everything that we have seen, the scariest costume for the president to see this year might be somebody dressed up as his approval ratings on inflation or the economy -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow, you really went there.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you very much. That was very clever.\par \par \uc2 I'm going to take that and run it as my line, Peter Doocy at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Aishah Hasnie following these developments right now, particularly in Georgia. You have got a gubernatorial race there, a Senate race there. You got the interest of the former president there. And you name it, the pile-on is on eight days out.\par \par \uc2 Aishah, how are things looking in Cartersville, Georgia?\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, interestingly enough, we have heard a lot of talk about former President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . His name has come up quite a bit from \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  himself. He did not like the former president's visit on Friday, because the former president went after him hard.\par \par \uc2 And so for the past couple of days here, including today, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has been hitting back hard on the former president. So take a look at the event that just wrapped up here in Cartersville. Quite a big crowd for Walker, about 100, 150 people very, very excited to see him.\par \par \uc2 And Walker really hit the former president and his criticism, because \uc2 Obama\uc2  called him a celebrity that wants to be a politician. Walker has since been telling his supporters he's not a celebrity, that he's -- quote -- "a warrior for God," and that \uc2 Obama\uc2  doesn't belong in this state. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), GEORGIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: I tried to tell \uc2 Obama\uc2 , I'm that warrior for God. He said, I'm a -- what did he say? I'm a -- what did he -- what did he call me? What did he call me? I'm like a celebrity.\par \par \uc2 I'm like, what in the world? He only hangs out with celebrity. He never hung out with me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: "Never hung out with me," he said. You know what? Every single vote here matters right now. If you were to look at some recent polling from the University of Georgia, you would see that this race is at a dead heat.\par \par \uc2 We are looking at a run-off if voters went to the polls today. No, Democrats still a little bit shocked at how little Walker's scandals have hurt him, even after the second woman came forward accusing him of pressuring her to get an abortion. Walker, of course, has denied that.\par \par \uc2 But it really hasn't affected him in the polls. In fact, Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  was caught on a hot mic last week telling the president that the state where we're going downhill is Georgia. What's interesting, especially about the abortion issue, Neil, is that I have been talking with voters, conservatives, asking them about this.\par \par \uc2 And they tell me, look, even if it were true, even if he had done that, they feel like he has basically changed his life, that they're willing to forgive him, because now he's pro-life. And they also just want to make sure they send a Republican to Washington to gain back control of the Senate. That is the goal for Republicans in this state -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it. Thank you very much for that, Aishah Hasnie in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the read on where we stand in eight days out. You always talk about who's getting the late surge, who's benefiting from all this early record voting, certainly early records for a midterm election year, to say nothing of the hundreds of thousands who vote in some of these key states via mail-in ballots.\par \par \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2  joins us right now, the Axios senior political correspondent, FOX News contributor. Also back with us is Samantha-Jo Roth.\par \par \uc2 Samantha, let me get your take first on what trends you might be picking up. I only say that because, in key races in the past -- and I know Josh and I have gotten into this -- we have seen late searches that defied polls at the time, in 1994, when Republicans were expected to gain, but nothing like the 54 seats they did, or 1980, that weekend before the presidential election, when, hard as it is to believe, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  and \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  were essentially tied, and then, of course, a few days later, a blowout.\par \par \uc2 Anything like that you're picking up or getting sort of vibes on?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA-JO ROTH, THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER: Well, Neil, as I was in Arizona over the last week, and it's very clear that this race, especially in the Senate, is very, very close.\par \par \uc2 And you can kind of see what the situation is looking like as to whether President Biden decides to campaign with these different candidates. And we're seeing that he is not in certain states, like Arizona and Nevada, states like that. And that is because the president is really a drag on these candidates.\par \par \uc2 These candidates are really trying to separate themselves from the president. We have seen that in their approach. And having the president there would be a problem. However, we are seeing President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , as we saw in the earlier report, in Georgia. We're going to see him going to Arizona midweek. We're also going to see him going to Nevada.\par \par \uc2 And it's interesting to see whether Democrats will be able to get their people out based on that excitement for \uc2 Obama\uc2 . But you have to remember, a lot of these states, it is -- they're really going to be relying heavily on these independent voters. They're going to have to get some of them, even some that are Republican, in the case of Arizona.\par \par \uc2 So the big question is, will bringing \uc2 Obama\uc2  or any of these other Democratic people out, will that hinder some of the efforts there?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I always wonder how it cuts, Josh, Samantha, what she was saying, when you have a big gun coming out on your behalf, in the case of \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , a gifted politician, no matter what you might personally think of him.\par \par \uc2 But he does well when he's on the ballot, not so well when he's pushing other people on the ballot. So I'm wondering if it will have the same effect here. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, Neil, let me know you have to do is ask some of the Democrats who were running when \uc2 Obama\uc2  was campaigning for them in 2010, 2014. They didn't have good nights.\par \par \uc2 Obama\uc2  may be the most popular Democratic surrogate right now, but I don't think he's going to move a whole lot of votes or energize folks that otherwise wouldn't be going to the polls. I mean, this race, as Samantha was saying, is all about President Biden and his pretty weak job approval numbers.\par \par \uc2 And I'm looking at that travel schedule, Neil, really, really closely. The president's going to be in Pennsylvania later in the week. But you know what his last campaign stop, what state he's going to be in? It's Maryland. Now, that's a sign of how blue you have got to go for the president to -- - - in a state where the president actually is popular, where he's wanted to campaign for a lot of these Democrats.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, like, he's just not welcome in a lot of these classic swing states, because, if you look at the polls, his job approval is hovering around 40 percent, which is well below where you got to be to be being welcomed by a whole lot of Democrats.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: In the end, also, Samantha, I have been reminded by \uc2 Mick Mulvaney\uc2  earlier today on FOX Business, which Samantha, I know you get at The Washington Examiner, but, if you don't have it, you should demand it. Many have, and they're doing very well, thank you.\par \par \uc2 But one of the things that came up, Samantha, when I was talking to him, was this idea of all the other stuff that's going to be happening before Election Day. Like, we will have the employment report out from the month before, that later on, on Friday. Then, of course, we get the next move on the part of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to raise interest rates likely another three-quarters point\par \par \uc2 We will have that to digest. And he also then spoke about inflation, period. I want to get your take on this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICK MULVANEY\uc2 , FORMER ACTING WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: So, as you sit here and look at inflation as too much money chasing too few goods, the Fed really can sort of help on the supply of money, but they're actually hurting the supply of goods. They're making it harder to get stuff to market.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration continues to make it harder to get stuff to market. So until we look at inflation from both sides, I don't think it's going away anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, if it doesn't go away anytime soon, he sees no improvement there, that's yet another overhang. What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 ROTH: Oh, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 And we're already seeing this from Democrats. There's a group of them that are calling on the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to really pump the brakes. They don't want to see that increased rate hike, another one. We are seeing Democrat John Hickenlooper, as well as Senator \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2 . They're writing to the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , asking them to take a break, to take a look at how exactly these rate hikes are actually impacting the economy.\par \par \uc2 As we know, this is going to be something that voters are going to feel. They're going to look at it. And as we were talking to voters out in Arizona last week, that was among one of the biggest issues we saw. But, as we know, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is an independent entity. \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  was put in place under President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 ROTH: And he is a Republican, but it is a big question of how the voters are going to see this. A lot of times, they forget that fact.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, they're just exasperated, period, with the rate hikes, I guess.\par \par \uc2 How does yet another rate hike sort out in this election? Because I don't think it'd be a shock that people. They are getting kind of numb and used to it. But for the voters who have not yet voted, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Yes, I mean, in the short term, it's not good for the White House, and it's going to slow the economy down. And there's a lot of worry, as Samantha was saying, about the progressives speaking out against the aggressive rate hiking of the Fed.\par \par \uc2 But I do think if Republicans -- in the future, in the medium term, if Republicans do take the House or maybe even the Senate, they're going to have some ownership in the economy. And they have been able to kind of stay on the sidelines, not really comment about what they think about the rate hikes. But I think Republicans are going to start to have to articulate how they view inflation, what they would do to tackle inflation if they do get control of Congress in November.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we will watch closely. Guys, I want to thank you both.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, something that might be too little, too late for a lot of voters depending on, well, how much they own it stock and how much it's been battered. October, normally one of the more notoriously awful months for the market, having one of its best, in the case of the Dow, up 14 percent on the month, the best single performance since back in January 1976.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq, S&P 500, all the major averages racing ahead, in a month known for big crashes like the one in 1929, the one in 1987, and numerous disruptions since in the years that followed, but not this year, not this time. We're on top of that.\par \par \uc2 Also on top of the issue of crime in New York. It has been \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 's signature issue in tackling the Democratic incumbent, \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 . So why is it that a policemen's benevolent association wrote a $25,000 check to her and not him? I asked him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R-NY), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: We have heard from a lot of \uc2 NYPD\uc2  officers who are pissed off learning that their money went to \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Early voting already under way in New York, and it is a contest that's getting some attention all over the country, because this bluest of blue states could have a contest on its hands to determine who the next governor will be.\par \par \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  has the advantages, of course, being in such a Democratic state, but that does not mean that \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  hasn't made an incredible contest out of it.\par \par \uc2 Eric Shawn in Thornwood, New York, with the very latest -- Eric.\par \par \uc2 ERIC SHAWN, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, that, he absolutely has. It's a precedent that \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  in hopes to replicate here in Westchester, just north of New York City, and that is to turn a largely blue suburban county red, just like Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin did in his state.\par \par \uc2 And, today, both Youngkin and Zeldin teamed up here on the campaign trail, holding a raucous rally here in Thornwood. When the governor bounded up onto the stage, he ripped into Zeldin's challenger, Democratic incumbent Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 . Youngkin told the crowd that Democrats' lax criminal justice policies have led to a spike in crime.\par \par \uc2 He said Hochul -- quote -- "stands up for criminals" and says that her law enforcement policies have left law enforcement -- quote -- "demeaned and demoralized."\par \par \uc2 Youngkin has been basically echoing Zeldin's harsh critique of Hochul and the Democratic policies that some say has caused the situation here, because crime has become a major flash point of this campaign.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: And the number one issue that I hear from so many New Yorkers about rising crime, soaring crime, DAs letting violent criminals run free.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  says that she doesn't understand why it's so important to all of us that we want to lock up criminals? She made an epic mistake that they will be telling stories about for a long time to come. It shows that she is out of touch with the reality on the ground.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: Hochul campaigned here just eight miles away from where we are now, with first lady Jill Biden over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 And she caused a controversy by claiming that the issue of crime is a -- quote -- "conspiracy" by Republicans and the media. She says that crime isn't as bad as it seems, that people are scared, noting that some gun crimes are down and that it's not as bad as people feel.\par \par \uc2 But she did hold a news conference with the gun safety group Moms Demand Action this afternoon, touting gun safety laws, as well as red flag laws, and trying to outlaw automatic weapons. And she slammed Zeldin's gun policies.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): People who have been involved in the effort to eradicate gun violence, get the guns off our streets and stop the slaughter are sick and tired of being used by candidates like \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  as part of a narrative which is false, which is that you can have -- be tough on crime, but refuse to talk about any commonsense gun safety legislation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHAWN: And then, Neil, there is this.\par \par \uc2 This is the campaign sign that they had here at the rally today, typical normal ones, Zeldin governor on one side, but take a look at what is on the other side, '94 Pataki for governor. That's when George Pataki famously defeated the Democratic icon Governor \uc2 Mario Cuomo\uc2 , who had served three terms.\par \par \uc2 They certainly hope that Mr. Zeldin can again repeat what Mr. Pataki did. We will know in nine days -- Neil, back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's right. So, 20 years ago, last time we had a Republican governor in New York. It bears watching.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Eric Shawn, following history, as well as politics in real time. Cooler than that.\par \par \uc2 All right, in the meantime here, this issue of crime has been the central one for \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 . But it was a bit of a surprise when out of the blue we heard that the policemen's benevolent association had written a $25,000 check to Governor Hochul.\par \par \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  was on with me on my weekend show and was startled as, well, Everyone was. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: We have heard from a lot of \uc2 NYPD\uc2  officers who are pissed off learning that their money went to \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , who is pandering to these pro-criminal allies in the state legislature.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, now, the PBA itself says it does not have a position in the race, that this was something that it did on its own separately, but many in the rank and file are saying, what the heck is going on?\par \par \uc2 Howard Safir is the former NYC police commission.\par \par \uc2 What did you think of that, Howard? As of this date, no $25,000 check for \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 . And there are some very angry members within the association wondering, how did that get done in the first place?\par \par \uc2 HOWARD SAFIR\uc2 , FORMER NEW YORK CITY POLICE COMMISSIONER: Well, I think you have to understand, number one, the PBA is not the police.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 SAFIR: The PBA is a union.\par \par \uc2 And it's a union that has a pretty clever president, Pat Lynch. And when you think about it, this is a bizarre way of hedging his bets. He knows where his Zeldin is coming from on crime. Zeldin, if he's elected, is not going to turn around on crime, whereas Hochul, who is not big on supporting the police and crime, they can get favor with her by donating to her campaign.\par \par \uc2 And you have to realize that a lot of the legislation that takes place in Albany that affects New York City officers are passed by legislators who have no stake whatsoever in New York City, but get money from the PBA. So this is, in a bizarre way, not a very dumb move.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, I see what you're saying. You're just sort of hedging your bets. You would think you would write a check to both in that event.\par \par \uc2 But if she is that prohibitive favorite, maybe not nearly as prohibitive as she used to be, you're just trying to assure keeping her in your good graces, right?\par \par \uc2 SAFIR: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's what that donation was all about.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, another thing Governor Hochul mentioned, as you know, Howard, it is this idea that \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  has only one plan, talks tough on crime, but won't do anything about the guns.\par \par \uc2 She conveniently leaves out she is not doing anything about bail reform either. But where do you think all of this goes?\par \par \uc2 SAFIR: Well, I think if Zeldin gets elected, you see that the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  is going to become much more assertive. They're going to go back to doing stop and frisk. They're going to do the kinds of things that made the city the safest in America during my tenure and Ray Kelly's tenure and \uc2 Bill Bratton\uc2 's tenure.\par \par \uc2 But if Hochul gets elected, I think you're going to see more of the same. You're going to see more of pandering to criminals. And, as I have said before, the only people who should be afraid of police are criminals. Are, right now, they're not. Right now, even parole violators, if they do something that before put them back in jail, they get right back out on the street.\par \par \uc2 So we have to stop this madness and start really enforcing the laws. She says crime is not so bad. But if you look at the 25 innocent people who've been shot, pushed in front of subways, assaulted on the street randomly, you know that she is not coming from the right place.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We're not making up these numbers either. She also said that these numbers are exaggerated. The numbers are what they are. So we report them. As a numbers nerd here, that's what I provide, folks.\par \par \uc2 But, anyway, Howard Safir, thank you very, very much. Good seeing you again.\par \par \uc2 I want to go to the other side of the country right now, where we're getting federal charges being formally filed against the man who brutally attacked Paul Pelosi.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the latest from Christina Coleman in San Francisco -- Christina.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA COLEMAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, according to this criminal complaint, the suspect admitted that he wanted to hold \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  hostage and possibly break her kneecaps. Now he's facing two federal charges, one count of assault of an immediate family member of a U.S. official and one count of attempted kidnapping of a U.S. official.\par \par \uc2 Police say 42-year-old David DePape broke into Pelosi's home early Friday morning, went upstairs, woke up Mr. Pelosi, and demanded to see his wife. When Mr. Pelosi told him that she wouldn't be home for a few days, DePape said he would wait.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi was able to slip into the bathroom and call 911. And when police showed up, he ran to open the door and grabbed onto the hammer that DePape was holding. Police they, when they arrived at the house, DePape gained control of the hammer and swung it right into Pelosi's head.\par \par \uc2 DePape was arrested immediately. And during a Mirandized and recorded interview, the complaint states -- quote -- "DePape stated that he was going to hold Nancy hostage and talk to her. If Nancy were to tell the truth, he would let her go. And, if she lied, he was going to break her kneecaps. He also said he viewed her as the leader of the pack of lies told by the Democratic Party, and that, by breaking her kneecaps, she would have to be wheeled into Congress to face the consequences of her actions."\par \par \uc2 Police also say they found a backpack, his backpack, at the scene, which contains a rope, a roll of tape, a hammer, and a rubber glove -- rubber gloves, actually. They also searched a garage that he was living in near Berkeley and found a sword, along with other things.\par \par \uc2 And, also, per an ICE source, DePape is in the U.S. illegally as a longtime visa overstay. Fortunately, after all of this, Paul Pelosi is expected to fully recover from his injuries -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Christina, thank you for that, I think, Christina.\par \par \uc2 A little bizarre.\par \par \uc2 All right, we have a lot more coming up, including the battle with the world's richest man with the new company he's taken over, and some interesting vibes he's given as to what he's going to do with it.\par \par \uc2 Let's get the latest from Kelly O'Grady in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Hey. Good to see you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, lots of changes coming from \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's new Chief Twit, as Elon Musk is styling himself. We will tell you what to and who might be upset about it coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're looking at a live Kari Lake event in Scottsdale, Arizona. She's running for governor, has a slight lead in the polls right there.\par \par \uc2 She's attracted huge crowds right now, as the countdown begins, a good deal of early and active voting in that state. We will keep you posted -- right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, the world's richest man has a rich problem on his hands. And it could be a Herculean one, $44 billion for \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and not a clear sign what he's going to do to justify the cost or to make it profitable.\par \par \uc2 Folks like \uc2 Mark Cuban\uc2  think he can easily do that, but it could take some time.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Kelly O'Grady. She's been following this every step of the way from, I guess, when Musk first proposed it. And Now he's got it.\par \par \uc2 Kelly, what's happening now?\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: Yes I know, going all the way back to April. I feel like I should, like, camp outside \uc2 Twitter\uc2  headquarters now.\par \par \uc2 But kind of the latest development has been that he's focusing on free speech. But a lot of advertisers are pushing back. GM actually specifically has said that they're pausing advertising on the platform.\par \par \uc2 I do want to read you a statement, because I think it's good context. They said, Neil: "We're engaging with \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to understand the direction of the platform under their new ownership, as is normal course of business, with significant change in a media platform. We have temporarily paused our paid advertising."\par \par \uc2 And we're hearing from a number of other advertisers that, if President Trump is let back on this platform, we're not going to continue advertising with \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But a lot of critics are kind of saying, well, it's kind of interesting, right, because you're going all in on TikTok, but, of course, they have their own issues with China and with how they use data. So it's kind of an interesting development to hear that from these advertisers, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I do wonder as well. I mean, he's got rid of the top three or four executives. Now, I think he had to pay up close to $200 million in severance for the for these guys. Has he hinted about who else and how many might go?\par \par \uc2 There had been talk 75 percent could lose their jobs. He dialed that back, but he hasn't given a number yet, has he?\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: No, he hasn't.\par \par \uc2 So I am talking to sources inside the company. And we have known, right, that layoffs are coming for a while. He's talked about this. At one point, there was a 75 percent number figure reported. Now it's looking like it might be more around 25 percent. And, of course, he's also going to go and hire folks as well, because he's going to shift his focus more to free speech.\par \par \uc2 He's even joking, maybe not joking, on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  right now that he might bring Vine back as well. That was the short form video platform that \uc2 Twitter\uc2  killed in 2016. So we don't have a number yet. But there's certainly a lot of trepidation from folks inside the company, because this is not the time that you really want to be getting laid off from a tech company, as we're seeing hiring freezes from \uc2 Meta\uc2 , \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , et cetera, right now, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, not the ideal environment it was.\par \par \uc2 All right, Kelly O'Grady in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 Thanks, Kelly, very, very much for that.\par \par \uc2 We are moments away from hearing the president of the United States. He's going to be addressing gas prices, and he's going to be pointing his finger at maybe a familiar target.\par \par \uc2 What do we know, Hillary Vaughn?\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, the president is expected to give oil and gas companies a choice, trick or treat. Either treat people to relief at the pump, or he has tricks up his sleeve, taxes.\par \par \uc2 We have more on what the president is expected to announce today coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, want to take you now to the White House. The president is targeting gas prices and the folks behind them. And that would be the big oil companies.\par \par \uc2 Let's listen in.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Since then, gas prices have actually come down going into the Strategic Petroleum Reserve here at home in America.\par \par \uc2 They're down more than $1.20 since their peak this summer. And they have been falling for the best -- the best part of the last three weeks.\par \par \uc2 In June, the average price, not the most common price, but the average price nationwide was over $5 a gallon. Today, the average price for a gallon of gas is $3.76. That's adding up to real savings for American families, the difference between those prices.\par \par \uc2 And this difference makes a difference in this difficult time. Americans across the country have stepped up and -- to do the right thing. But not everyone stepped up. The oil industry has not. It's not met its commitment to invest in America and support the American people.\par \par \uc2 One by one, major oil companies have reported record profits, not just a fair return on for hard work. Every company is entitled to that, a fair return for the work they do or innovation they generate. It means -- but I mean profits so high, it's hard to believe.\par \par \uc2 Now, the second quarter, the profits were really high. But the third quarter, last week, \uc2 Shell\uc2  announced that it made $9.5 billion in profits for the third quarter, $9.5 billion. That's almost twice as much as it made in the third quarter of last year.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's something. You think that's incredible? I thought, my -- that's as good as -- high as it's going to get. Then along came \uc2 Exxon\uc2 . \uc2 Exxon\uc2 's profits for the third quarter were $18.7 billion, one quarter, $18.7 billion, nearly triple what \uc2 Exxon\uc2  made last year, and the most in its 152-year history. It has never made that much profit.\par \par \uc2 In the last six months, six of the largest oil companies have made more than $100 billion, $100 billion. And we had a little discussion about this, the three of us and others, $100 billion in profits in less than 200 days. That's not bad.\par \par \uc2 And here's why this -- here's why this matters. I think it's outrageous, what they're -- the size of the profit. Here's why it matters. If these companies were making an average profits they have been making by refining oil over the last 20 years, instead of the outrageous profits they're making today, and if they passed the rest onto the consumers, the price of gas would come down around an additional 50 cents.\par \par \uc2 If they were investing their profits in historic -- historic rates in their U.S. operations, then America would be producing more oil today, and prices would be down even further. But rather than increasing our investments in America, or giving American consumers a break, their excess profits are going back to their shareholders and they're buying back their stock.\par \par \uc2 So, the executive pays are going to skyrocket. Give me a break. Enough is enough.\par \par \uc2 Look, I'm a capitalist. You have heard me say this before. I have no problem with corporations turning a fair profit and getting a return on their investment and innovation. But this isn't remotely what's happening. Oil companies' record profits today are not because they're doing something new or innovative. Their profits are a windfall of war, the windfall from the brutal conflict that's ravaging Ukraine and hurting tens of millions of people around the globe.\par \par \uc2 At a time of war, any company receiving historic windfall profits like this has a responsibility to act beyond their narrow self-interest of its executives and shareholders. I think they have a responsibility to act in the interest of their consumers, their community and their country to invest in America by increasing production and refining capacity, because they have had -- they don't want to do that.\par \par \uc2 They have the opportunity to do that, lowering prices for consumers at the pump. Now, if they don't, they're going to pay a higher tax on their excess profits and face other restrictions.\par \par \uc2 My team will work with Congress to look at these options that are available to us and others. It's time for these companies to stop war profiteering, meet their responsibilities to this country, and give the American people a break and still do very well.\par \par \uc2 The American people are going to judge who's standing with them and who is only looking out for their own bottom line. I know where I stand. And I want to let -- I'm going to -- you're going to hear more from me about this when the Congress comes back.\par \par \uc2 Thank you all very, very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: How was your call with Lula from Brazil today, Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: What?\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Your call with Lula from Brazil, the president-elect, how was it?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It went very well. We're putting the team together to get together and discuss what we can do together.\par \par \uc2 He wants to focus on the environment, democracy and dealing with the poor in his country and saving the \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Are you inviting him to the White House?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, we didn't get much out of the president there outside of commenting on the surprising election of the former president of Brazil back in that job, \uc2 Lula da Silva\uc2  winning that election over President Bolsonaro.\par \par \uc2 But just a couple of quick clarifications on what the president said about rocketing gas prices and the industry should do its part, given the profits they have made. Just a couple of points on that, attributing this all to the war\par \par \uc2 About 40 percent of the oil run-up we saw under this administration occurred long before boots were getting even near the ground near Ukraine back in early 19 -- '21. We should also say the big catalyst for, of course, the upward run-up in prices had to do with the fact that the president had almost immediately cut off the \uc2 Keystone\uc2  pipeline.\par \par \uc2 That was expected. And then, of course, the economy was coming out of its doldrums, and it's when we were all shut in, and that produced a boom in the economy, because everyone was getting back to work, and the commensurate boom in prices. But it was aggravated by the fact that production opportunities were limited for the industry, as if -- the president was making a note there about capacity, and we're not using what we have.\par \par \uc2 We have the oil companies now running at about 95 percent of refining capacity. I'm not an apologist for them. I just hear this line again and again about how they are deliberately gouging the American people. There's been no evidence of price gouging going on in the industry.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2 , the Justice Department, others over the last 40 years under Republican presidents and Democrats alike have tried to find evidence and investigated that, whether there was a cabal going on among the energy companies to deliberately gouge the American people. No proof was ever found over all of this time.\par \par \uc2 The industry has come back to say a number of times that, if they had more leases on more lands, where there was greater promise, and they weren't going to be investing billions of dollars at a time the administration has said it wants to phase out fossil fuels, it might be a very, very different environment.\par \par \uc2 But when you know you have to commit that type of dough, and you're hearing from an administration that really isn't keen on what you're doing, you think about, well, committing that kind of dough. I just want to put that out there, not to get dirty about it. I have no political axe to grind. I just want to make sure everyone's being accurate.\par \par \uc2 Hillary Vaughn, what is clear is, the administration has mentioned this again and again and again, if not for Ukraine, if not for the greedy oil companies, we wouldn't be in the pickle we're in. But it is a reminder, now a little more than a week between the midterms.\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Neil, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 In fact, what's interesting is, the president really left it open to Congress to decide what is excess profits and what these higher taxes should look like. He didn't come out and endorse his specific proposal. But there are proposals by progressive Democrats on the table right now that would put a windfall tax on big companies like oil and gas companies.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  has a proposal that he introduced in the spring that would put a 95 percent tax on windfall profits to try to punish oil and gas companies, who Democrats think are responsible for these rising prices at the pump.\par \par \uc2 It'll be interesting to see, after the results of the midterm election, whether or not that has any movement, if control of Congress were to change hands, in the House or the Senate. But this is an issue that the president had to come out and talk about today, because it is the top issue on voters minds.\par \par \uc2 Just over one week out from Election Day, a new poll from ABC found that inflation and the economy, over half of Americans that are registered to vote say those are the top issues that they're thinking about when they're deciding who to vote for next Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 And, additionally, the oil and gas industry reacting to reports of what the president was going to announce this afternoon, which was opening up the door for more taxes because the president, the administration does not think the industry is doing enough, doing all they can to lower profits.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Oil and Gas Association president telling me more taxes is just going to make things worse and gas prices even higher -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: One of the things this administration has done -- I noticed it during Hurricane Ian, when, before the storm hit, he had a finger-wagging moment where he told the industry, don't even think about gouging people, as if.\par \par \uc2 So he throws it out there, oftentimes without the proof. And what bothers me about that is, a lot of people will buy that hook, line and sinker, and take a look at profits from an industry and say, wait a minute, that doesn't add up. But as a percentage of revenues -- again, I don't want to get arcane and boring here.\par \par \uc2 I had plenty of that in droves myself. But I just think, Hillary, that we're not really looking at companies and where and how they make money and when. Now the industry is doing well, a couple of years ago, not nearly so well. So, everything kind of averages out.\par \par \uc2 But it would be a message, would it not, to other companies making a lot of money that, that if you make what is deemed to be too much money -- and we don't know what it is. That's a moving target, like companies and individuals paying their so-called fair share -- it's a slippery slope, is it not?\par \par \uc2 In other words, you can go after an \uc2 Apple\uc2  or someone else who is making tons of dough off a very cool device. Where do you draw the line?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 And that's something that Democrats have pushed for. This \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  windfall tax is not just targeted at oil companies, but all big companies that make over a certain number of profit -- or a certain dollar amount of profits.\par \par \uc2 The Chamber of Commerce, interestingly, looked at this and said, it's been proven that windfall taxes, when applied to energy, actually reduce supply, reduce production, and, by extension, make energy prices go up.\par \par \uc2 But when it's applied to other industries as well, it ultimately just hurts the consumer. It ultimately gets passed down. And so the Chamber of Commerce has come out to say that that's not a good idea. But it could be opening the door for more taxes like this in the future, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, we will have to watch very closely.\par \par \uc2 Hillary, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Susan Crabtree with us right now, RealClearPolitics.\par \par \uc2 Susan, one thing I left out on the oil industry -- and sorry to keep boring people, but it's my career -- when we talk about the permits and licenses and the 9,000 or so that the industry has available, it had 16,000 a little bit more than 18 months ago. So it's gone through a lot of them, and about a quarter of those that are still left are being protested by environmental groups. That could hang up in court forever.\par \par \uc2 Finally, there is the issue of committing yourself. When you have a permit or a license on land, you're not sure it's a gusher. You just went ahead and bid on it because you want that security. So they're not all gushes. Finally, you have the issue of the administration saying, longer term, we want to be all electric anyway, we don't like fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 So if you're an industry that's all fossil fuels, and you're looking about expanding production or building up your industry beyond what it is -- and it's substantial now -- you know that the party in the White House, the party that is dominating Capitol Hill for the time being doesn't like you, doesn't support what you want to do.\par \par \uc2 You could be wasting your money. That doesn't come out. I'm just curious about your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN CRABTREE, REALCLEARPOLITICS: Well, I was there, right, during the presidential debate, the primary debate, where he came out and said, I do want to end fossil fuels, U.S. dependence on fossil fuels\par \par \uc2 And it was in Motor City, in Detroit. And the stories that came out of that was that this is going to be a very pivotal moment for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , if he wins the presidency, and people were going to come back to that moment.\par \par \uc2 Now he wants to crack down on oil companies, but yet he wants to them to increase production. So it's a lot of mixed messages going along here. And when prices, gas prices go up, it's the oil producers' fault. And when they go down, he gets to take credit. Well, now they're going back up again in these key battleground states.\par \par \uc2 So he has a trust problem with the American people, because he's been all over the map on the issue. And now, if you look at that ABC poll that Hillary mentioned, it said that 30 -- also said that 36 percent of voters trust Republicans more when it comes to handling inflation and gas prices.\par \par \uc2 And that's 12 to 14 points more than they trust Democrats to handle the situation. And we have -- really, in battleground states right now, we have Nevada, one of the major battleground states for the Senate. It's fourth highest in gas prices, and Arizona is seventh highest.\par \par \uc2 So you have had a 50 and 104 percent increase in gas prices in these battleground states. And the voters are feeling the pain at the pump. And they are deciding that Republicans are the people that they trust more to vote for this next week on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, you have got to be consistent, right? In other words, I would love to blame my weight thing -- I'm maybe five pounds overweight, Susan.\par \par \uc2 But I'd love to blame that on a thyroid condition or something like that. No, no, I just like napoleons. And I have to take the bullet for that.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And I can't blame anyone for that.\par \par \uc2 So I don't think it would hurt the president to say, all right, this happened under my watch. I didn't gauge the reaction that we'd get from some of these policies, call it a day and move on. But when you spend an inordinate amount of time even questioning the average for gasoline sold in this country, and instead go to some bizarre sort of -- this is sort of like a mean average -- and I don't even know what he went to.\par \par \uc2 I just know you can't reinvent the statistics that they're -- that are out there.\par \par \uc2 CRABTREE: That's right.\par \par \uc2 He said that the gas prices were $5 when he came into office. No, they were $2.39, on average.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CRABTREE: And in these battleground states, we're really seeing that this is the top three issue. You have got the economy, inflation, including gas prices and crime. And you have abortion, which Republicans -- Democrats have really pushed and Biden has made it a big issue.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 CRABTREE: Fourteen percent.\par \par \uc2 So, maybe there's a miscalculation going on here, but we will find out next Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, you have got to -- if you're not going to take the blame or at least address oil prices when they're high and only when they're low, that alone should give you pause, right? I mean, but we will see how it falls out.\par \par \uc2 Susan, always great catching up with you.\par \par \uc2 Susan Crabtree.\par \par \uc2 CRABTREE: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Madison Alworth looking at another problem the president did not address, and that's what's been going on with diesel fuel prices, vital to this economy in far more ways than you think.\par \par \uc2 Madison's in Budd Lake, New Jersey, with more on that side of the story -- Madison.\par \par \uc2 MADISON ALWORTH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I liked the point that you made. The importance reaches farther than you might think, because, if we pull up to a gas station, like the one behind me, most likely, you're filling up with gas and not diesel. But, of course, there's certain cars and trucks that deliver all of the goods that we get that all rely on that diesel gas.\par \par \uc2 The issue today is that we are in a shortage of diesel. And to kind of understand that, our current supply sits at about 25 days. Normally, we have a 35- to 40-day supply of diesel. There is just no wiggle room.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDY MILTON, \uc2 MANSFIELD OIL COMPANY\uc2 : From the diesel standpoint, we're on just-in-time inventory, just like you would think about any distribution of any other goods. There's just not a lot of ability in the overall system.\par \par \uc2 When you -- when you beat the industry up, they're going to close facilities that aren't profitable. And, ultimately, we're, I think, unfortunately reaping some of that right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALWORTH: The ban on Russian oil has also put immense pressure on the market, and production here at home has been crushed over the past couple of years.\par \par \uc2 These are all refineries that have been closed in 2020 and 2021. So crude oil not only makes diesel, which I have just been talking about, but it also makes home heating oil, which is also seeing a huge run-up just in time for winter. This year alone, home heating oil is up 68 percent in price, and over the last couple of weeks, it's jumped 21 percent in price.\par \par \uc2 With the winter months coming ahead, people are starting to fill up their home heating oil tanks. They're starting to see that price tag jump. And then, like I said with diesel, which, of course, goes into those trucks that get us our everyday goods, our Christmas gifts, they're seeing that huge run-up.\par \par \uc2 The average price of diesel today, Neil, $5.30, but here on the East Coast in New Jersey, station behind me, they're charging $5.95, nearly $6 -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow, you don't realize how far those tentacles spread, even with the cost of travel.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very, very much for that, Madison.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, some more threatening moves on the part of Russia in Ukraine. If you think about it, by abandoning a key agricultural or wheat deal, they effectively sent food prices climbing. They have been strafing the country in attack after attack.\par \par \uc2 And now we're hearing that 80 percent of Ukrainians have a tough time getting water, thanks to his attacks.\par \par \uc2 Greg Palkot keeping track of all of this craziness in Kyiv -- Greg.\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Amazing stuff, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, this war continues. And it continues on many different fronts, as you noted, let's talk about the offensive activity first, over 50 cruise missiles, as well as drones targeting Kyiv, several other cities across the country. Many were knocked down, but many got through, the civilian infrastructure in the sights of Moscow, electrical grid, you're right, water supply, heating system.\par \par \uc2 And that's triggering shortages of water and power. We went to one neighborhood, Neil. Take a look at what we saw today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: A regular Monday thing here in Kyiv. We're told at least one Russian missile and a drone hit a nearby target, also knocked out the windows there. That happened around 8:00 a.m., certainly disturbing the morning routine.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE (through translator): I saw one rocket flying over my house and I heard another one.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE (through translator): I feel bad. It's very dangerous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Now, Moscow launched these attacks, remember, following an alleged Ukrainian drone strike on Russian naval ships.\par \par \uc2 Yes, it also pulled out of that U.N.-brokered grain deal over the weekend, that agreement aimed at getting much-needed food supplies out to the world. The State Department again today, Neil, saying that Russia is weaponizing what the world eats.\par \par \uc2 What might be reassuring, though, is, we're also getting reports that as many as 12 ships full of wheat and other important foodstuff have left the ports today. And, so far, Russia is holding fire. Basically, Ukraine is calling Russia's bluff.\par \par \uc2 Remember, all of these moves by Moscow were triggered by the fact that they weren't doing too well on the battlefield. It might turn out also that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's efforts to manipulate, well, the price of food, the supply of foods might be faltering as well, but, again, to get to our earlier point, the folks here in Kyiv and across Ukraine still very squarely in the line of fire -- back to you\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Incredible. Great report, Greg. Thank you very much, Greg in Kyiv on that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, well, they are illegal migrants after all, but one local range owner along the border has taken that to a whole new level, maybe complicated how we deal with this at the border.\par \par \uc2 Nate Foy in Eagle Pass, Texas, with more -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, a smaller group of migrants crossed the border illegally onto that same Normandy ranch today, but we saw a bigger group here in Eagle Pass. Take a look at this video. Between 150 and 180 migrants arrived here after crossing illegally, a lot of Cubans and Colombians, which we have been seeing, but also some Venezuelans, which, under the new DHS policy, will be deported to Mexico.\par \par \uc2 But take a look at this video that you just showed again, the infrared drone video from yesterday. Remarkable. It shows 400 migrants crossing the Rio Grande in a single file line and going on to that private ranch. Lot of Cubans and Colombians here as well, men, women and children, but single adult men were picked out here.\par \par \uc2 Take a look; 59 single adults were arrested for criminal trespassing. Look at this next video, Neil. The ranch owner has cleared the way for Texas DPS to arrest and charge migrants who come onto the property. You see DPS and National Guard escorting the migrants off the ranch here.\par \par \uc2 These migrants were not trying to hide, but some are. Take a look at this ride-along that we took with Texas DPS as they chased runners in the middle of the night, these migrants trying to evade Border Patrol checkpoints. In total, this night, with them, they arrested four migrants, three from Honduras and one of them from Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Back out here live, Neil, I can report that Border Patrol in the past 48 hours has encountered five sex offenders trying to enter our country illegally. We will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Nate, incredible. Thank you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Nate in Eagle Pass on that.\par \par \uc2 Back with us, Brandon Judd, the National Border Patrol Council president.\par \par \uc2 Brandon, I always learn a lot, but Nate just revealed me another layer I didn't realize here. But the fact of the matter is, illegal is illegal. Trying to get into this country the way they're doing it is not working. And it's, again, not legal.\par \par \uc2 So some ranch owners take it into their own hands to try to deal with it. But nothing ever seems to move the needle. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JUDD, PRESIDENT, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL: No, and it's not going to move the needle right now.\par \par \uc2 This administration is clearly set on allowing this to continue. A very disturbing report came out today that the president of Guatemala offered President Biden a chance to deport these individuals before they even got into Mexico, let alone the United States.\par \par \uc2 The president of Guatemala said, all you have to do is provide us with planes, and we will deport them back, taking all of the burden off of the United States altogether, if we would have done that. But this is something that the Biden administration has continued to refused.\par \par \uc2 All solutions, every single one of them that have been presented to this administration, they continue to refuse to implement, no new policies, no new programs.\par \par \uc2 And what's very devastating about what you're seeing with all these people right now, it's DPS. It's Texas DPS that are taking these people into custody. It's not the Border Patrol. It's not even us that are out there patrolling the border.\par \par \uc2 I just had Senator Tuberville on the border on Friday. And we drove along for 30 minutes and did not come across one Border Patrol agent. Not one of my fellow agents were out there patrolling the border, because all of us are assigned to doing administrative duties. And that makes this situation a lot worse and a lot more dangerous for everybody.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, there had been talk, Brandon, and that's maybe all it is at this point, so you can educate me, where the administration would have a third country option, where some of these migrants enter or could be processed, adjudicated, whatever you guys call it.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that note? No country, per se, has been named, but something obviously apart from Mexico, apart from the United States. What are you hearing on this? What do you think?\par \par \uc2 JUDD: That would be a solution.\par \par \uc2 But, again, this administration is not working towards solutions. They would have to negotiate this with a partner country. That's something that the Trump administration was already doing when it negotiated remain-in- Mexico. Then, of course, it was it was negotiating TPS. They were negotiating several different programs that would allow us to control the border, and this administration came in and scrapped everything.\par \par \uc2 And if they were working on something, that would be something that we could -- we could hold hope to and say, hey, at least they're working towards something. But there is nothing in the works. And that report, again, that just came out where the Guatemalan president just lambasted President Biden because he refused to act, that's just another example that this administration does not care about border security.\par \par \uc2 They're going to continue let the border be continually completely wide open. Border Patrol agents are -- we're going to have the handcuffs on us, rather than on the bad guys. And people are going to continue to get away. And we're not going to feel the effects until the future.\par \par \uc2 But we will ultimately feel the effects of everything that is currently going on the border.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's interesting too, because Guatemala did have its differences with Mexico, but its differences seem more pronounced right now with us. It's a no-win situation.\par \par \uc2 Brandon, wild stuff. Thank you, my friend very much.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Thank you, Neil. It's good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 Brandon deals with this himself, by the way, all the time. So it's in real time, not made-up numbers. The 2.4 million, the hundreds of thousands got- aways, all those are real numbers, on top of the 1 to 1.5 million allowed into this country legally. They become legal immigrants and eventually U.S. citizens.\par \par \uc2 That's the process. Right now, there is no process.\par \par \uc2 Here's "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020221101eiav00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Sen. Chris Coons (D-DE) is Interviewed about Elections; Both Parties Face Questions on Strategy; Fate of Abortion Rights Bills; Musk Faces Scrutiny; High Court to Hear Arguments on Affirmative Acton; John Yoo is Interviewed about College Admissions; Interview with \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Jonathan Turley, Lucas Tomlinson, Rich Edson,  Kellyanne Conway \par \uc2 9,875 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Toss-ups, tightening polls and turnout -- we're just over a week out from midterms and right now, both parties are bracing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: You deserve somebody who's independent, somebody like Reverend \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Democrats play defense, with the party's biggest stars dispatched to help candidates even in blue states. We have a live report this morning from battleground Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 And we will bring you the latest on the chilling attack at the home of House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , as fears of more political violence rise.\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The poll that matters is the poll that's taken on November 8th, at 8:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A deep dive on those attention-grabbing polls that dominate political chatter.\par \par \uc2 And we have pollsters from both parties here to explain what the numbers can and can't tell us ahead of November 8th, Republican Kellyanne Conway and Democrat Celinda Lake join us live.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: They can't take me down.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Then, Republicans aim to win the House and Senate, but they have yet to overcome razor-thin margins in key races. As Democrats are caught on a hot mic reassuring President Biden they can hold on.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): I think we're picking up steam in Nevada.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll ask \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  whether her party can close the deal. And we'll get reaction from Democratic Senator Chris Coons who has been on the trail helping a fellow senator in a tight race.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Elon Musk takes the helm of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Will he give former President Trump back his megaphone?\par \par \uc2 We'll ask our Sunday panel what happens now that the billionaire free speech advocate is in charge.\par \par \uc2 Also, it could be the biggest Supreme Court case this fall, arguments on affirmative action in college admissions. We'll discuss the cases, the history and the potential impact on kids applying for school.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Big-name Republicans and Democrats are swarming the campaign trail this weekend, selling their vision to voters. Democrats trying to shore up control of the House, trying to prevent it from flipping red. And the heat is on in the battle over 50/50 Senate, both parties on edge as races in Georgia, Pennsylvania and Arizona all appear to be a jump ball.\par \par \uc2 All of this against the backdrop of a frightening scene in San Francisco. House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's husband violently attacked in their home. Political leaders quickly condemn the attack and Americans find themselves reminded once again that lawmakers and U.S. leaders on both side of the aisle face increased threats of violence.\par \par \uc2 We've got a lot to discuss this morning. We will break it all down for you with our bipartisan pollster panel, Celinda Lake and Kellyanne Conway.\par \par \uc2 And in just a few minutes, we'll speak with \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 , and then Democratic Senator Chris Coons, a close confidant of President Biden.\par \par \uc2 But we begin in team coverage. Rich Edson is live in Pennsylvania's capital where a Senate race is coming down to the wire. But, first, to Lucas Tomlinson at the White House with the president's reaction to the attack inside the home of Speaker of the House -- Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, officials say if not for an alert 911 dispatcher and quick police response, this attack could have had a much different outcome.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is despicable.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): President Biden speaking in Philadelphia hours after the attack.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: There is too much violence, political violence, too much hatred, too much vitriol.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Police say the suspect's 42-year-old David Depape broke into the Pelosi home in San Francisco around 2:30 early Friday morning. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's husband, 82-year-old Paul Pelosi, called 911 from the bathroom moments after the break-in. The attacker calling out, "Where's Nancy?" When police arrived, they found Pelosi and the assailant struggling over a hammer.\par \par \uc2 Police say Depape was able to gain control of it and strike Paul Pelosi in the head before being tackled by the police. Paul Polisi had successful surgery for a fractured skull. He also suffered injuries to his hand and right arm. He's expected to make a full recovery.\par \par \uc2 The latest violence comes almost five months after a man attempted to assassinate Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 U.S. Capitol Police\uc2  say threats against lawmakers have more than doubled since 2017.\par \par \uc2 On the campaign trail, former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  condemned the attack.\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: If our rhetoric about each other gets that mean, when we don't just disagree, but we start demonizing them, making wild, crazy allegations about them, that creates a dangerous climate.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: San Francisco's police chief credited the dispatcher for her quick thinking.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM SCOTT, SAN FRANCISCO POLICE CHIEF: Her actions, in my opinion, resulted in both a higher priority dispatch and a faster police response. I think this was life-saving.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): Depape will make his first court appearance Tuesday. He faces multiple charges, including attempted murder -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Lucas Tomlinson from the White House, Lucas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We'll now go live to Rich Edson who is on the campaign trail in Harrisburg, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 One to watch, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It is, Shannon, and Pennsylvania is going to have be big enough this week for two former and one current president.\par \par \uc2 Democrats and Republicans in the general election campaign here and nationwide have been distancing themselves from their party's more unpopular presidents. Now, it's becoming unavoidable.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Fetterman is Pennsylvania. He had a stroke. He's recovering.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: At Tuesday's Pennsylvania Senate debate between Democratic nominee \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Mehmet Oz, Fetterman stroke recovery was on full display.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: The debate didn't hurt us too much in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: As Fetterman and Oz campaigned across Pennsylvania, the political heavyweights are now showing up. President Biden and Vice President Harris to a Friday night fundraiser in Philadelphia, former President Trump this week to Latrobe, and Biden again with former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  in Philly and Pittsburgh next weekend.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 Obama\uc2 , part of campaign to swing states nationwide.\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: It's got to be back in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: The 44th president has campaigned for Democrats in Georgia, Michigan and Wisconsin before Nevada and Pennsylvania this week.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are also deploying their party stars, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  campaigning for gubernatorial candidate \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): If you want to stop what the Democrats have done to this country over the last two years, we have to elect Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: While Republicans try to make this election about the president, Biden maintained it's not and said something both parties can agree with.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It's a choice. A choice between two vastly different visions of America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): First Lady Jill Biden is also getting involved here in Pennsylvania. Wednesday, she'll hit both ends of the state in Allentown and Pittsburgh for events -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Rich Edson in Pennsylvania, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, the president of Lake Research Partner, Celinda Lake, and former senior counselor to former President Trump, Kellyanne Conway.\par \par \uc2 I am so excited, welcome, to have you both here\par \par \uc2 CELINDA LAKE, LAKE RESEARCH PARTNER: Excited to be here and together.\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I mean, for those of us who love crunching these numbers and watching these races, I mean, we're getting down to the finish line. So, let me start with this, Suffolk University, "USA Today", they've got their poll, the generic congressional ballot, 49-45 in favor of Republicans, that's a flip from what they had over the summer in July, when they said 16 percent were still undecided. They say they break to the GOP, these undecided voters, at least this time around.\par \par \uc2 So, two things, we've got polls showing the exact opposite right now. So, Kellyanne Conway, what do we believe and are there that many undecideds left? Which way do they go?\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: There are some fence sitters and they wait until the very end, maybe debates are still happening, the candidates are making their closing arguments. You see some of the political heavyweights coming to campaign with them and for them.\par \par \uc2 But, essentially, the fundamentals have been favoring Republicans for a while and has an awful lot to do with two big things, one is the issue set. So, voters have been straightforward and uncomplicated. They've been telling pollsters for a year. I'm worried about rising cost and rising crime. It's inflation. It's education. For some voters, it's abortion, although even CNN and James Carville, and other Democrats have admitted that has faded as a top issue and intensity.\par \par \uc2 The second thing that's happening is Republicans have done fantastic recruiting diverse league of candidate. Seventy percent of Republican House candidates right now who are running in competitive Democratic House districts are voters of -- excuse me, candidate of color and/or female candidates, and we have a lot of veterans. That really has helped the seeing is believing surge in the Republican Party, where voters are getting really excited.\par \par \uc2 The last thing in that "USA Today" poll, they made clear. They say upwards of 40 percent of Hispanics and 21 percent of African Americans are right now favoring the Republican candidates for office. So, this is a real realignment of voters who were long-time constituencies of the Democratic party they can rely on.\par \par \uc2 I think fundamentals favor the Republicans in 2020, not single Republican house incumbent lost and we flipped 15 from blue to red, and that could happen again. Meaning, not a single house or Senate Republican may lose incumbent. There are one or two are a little in danger in the House. But think about that. I think that we're looking at 2022 in the House easily and taking over the Senate.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, that's the prediction from Kellyanne side of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 We're going to dig into some of the numbers about demographics, too, but people are a little bit suspicious, Celinda, about some of these polls. They don't know what to believe.\par \par \uc2 "The New Criterion" under the headline "Who Need Polls" says this, many of -- these two ladies need polls. We all need polls.\par \par \uc2 Many observers have a sneaking suspicion that some pollsters rig their surveys to favor one or the other political party in the hope of shaping the eventual election outcome.\par \par \uc2 They point to the race, Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 , for example, up in Maine. She was losing in the polls by five to 10. She won by nine. That's just one example.\par \par \uc2 People are suspicious.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: People should be suspicious and people should vote their conscience, not the polls anyway.\par \par \uc2 But I think if you look -- the best thing to do is look at the average of the polls, and the average of the polls says it's a dead -- it's a jump ball, as you said. It's dead even.\par \par \uc2 What's most important thing right now is the turnout and the biggest asset that we have is when President Biden, when President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , Dr. Biden go into these places, when our vice president goes into places, it really energizes Democrats. You can bet Democrats in Georgia and Democrats in Wisconsin feel a lot better today after having heard ex- President \uc2 Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, I think that's what's really important is who's going to turn out. We know the Republicans are energized, but if we got every voter who turned out and voted Democratic in 2018, we would win. So, it's too close to call and any pollsters who says they know what's going on is lying to you, vote your conscience.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. There are no reliable crystal balls.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Exactly right. We'd make a lot more money and we'd be in Vegas right now.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Exactly. Yeah, let us know if you guys.\par \par \uc2 OK. So, let's talk about these constituencies, these voting blocks. Specifically, "Politico" has this, Democrats are growing anxious again over Black turnout. They say in a poll by "Politico"/Morning Consult, last week, just 25 percent of Black registered voters describe themselves as extremely enthusiastic about voting in this election.\par \par \uc2 This is something I talked to Georgia gubernatorial candidate \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  when we had her on the show, that she is getting accusations from within the Black community that Democrats have taken them for granted as voters and really haven't delivered in many ways for them.\par \par \uc2 You cited the numbers, Kellyanne. Is there really a shift there, or the Democrats simply -- it's a turnout issue for Black voters?\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Well, the shift has started with President Trump. He did much better among Hispanics and African-Americans the second time than he did the first time improve his vote share. And I think it's continuing because it's now the party of the worker. It's a party of the job creator, job seeker and really the job holder, which is a vast majority of households in this country, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 But there's something else going on, I don't know why and I don't how. I'm happy for it, but the Democrats in this White House have basically ignored voter concern for better part of a year.\par \par \uc2 Voters have very plainly said to pollsters of every stripe, we're worried about rising cost and rising crime. Parents are still upset about lost learning and reduced test scores and the fact students and children face these emotional and mental health challenges within the schools. And so, that's not going to be -- we're parents. We're not a Democratic cohort to be studied. We're not in an interest group to be pursued. We're parents.\par \par \uc2 And so, I feel the Democrats have been trying to make the election about abortion, Trump, January sixth and climate change. A year ago in Virginia, \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  tried to make the election about Trump and abortion, and Glenn Youngkin said Virginia and education. So, they have been ignoring the will of voters.\par \par \uc2 And there's something else going on, "New York Times"/Siena College poll showed a 32-point shift among independent women month over month.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you think that's reliable? That's a huge shift.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: I think it's a little bit of outlier. It's an outlier. The fundamentals are the same.\par \par \uc2 But, look, if the generic ballot -- the Democrats have relied on the political gender gap and if women are starting to migrate towards the Republican ideals and candidates, I mean, we have -- we have \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  up by one point. She's under 50 percent. We had \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  up by six, but she's under 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 I mean, these are deep blue states with female Democratic legislator. I mean, let me just say one last thing about the polls, it's important.\par \par \uc2 Celinda, we're pollsters. If we weren't good at what we did, the free market would have kicked us out. I'm worried about media polls, because there's no accountability there for the lack of accuracy in the last few years, and I do worry that some people are using polls not to reflect and respect public opinion, but to create and manipulate it. And I worry about it.\par \par \uc2 But let's also be frank about what polls are. They're snapshot in time. They tell you about trends. They can't predict. If you take a poll in early October, it doesn't predict what's happening in November.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. Well, let's talk about the Hispanic vote as well because the financial time says this -- the Democrats fear losing Nevada because of what's happening with Hispanic voters there. They say Democrats nationwide are increasingly struggling to cling on to their once relatively secure Hispanic vote and Republicans are out there courting Latino voters with an economic message focused on jobs and inflation.\par \par \uc2 Celinda, what do you see in those numbers?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Well, first of all, I think the Democrats had a strong economic message for over a year, and the difference between the parties is we have an economic record. We passed the Inflation Reduction Act without votes from the Republicans. We passed getting prescription drug prices down without votes from Republicans. We passed crime bill and gun bill without votes from Republicans.\par \par \uc2 So, we have a record to stand on, and what's really important is to make sure in these last few days that record and that contrast gets revealed in these races and voters really focus on it.\par \par \uc2 I think President \uc2 Obama\uc2  is a huge help in getting the African-American turnout up and African-American voters solidified. I do think that the Democrats need to have and continue to have a stronger economic message, particularly around entrepreneur entrepreneurship and jobs and you see the president very strongly doing that with help from small businesses, with the CHIPS Act, and I think that will make a lot of difference with Latino voters.\par \par \uc2 The last thing I'll say about the Latino vote is watch out for the women. The Latinas are coming home really strong right now and turning out in record numbers.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And a lot of them are on the ballot, too.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: That's right. Record numbers in both parties.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. I could pick your brains for an hour.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both, Celinda and Kellyanne, for being with us.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Republicans are growing cautiously more confident about winning the Senate, but a few GOP candidates in critical races really still have to close the gap. We're going to sit down with \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  and Democratic Senator Chris Coons, they're next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: As both parties get ready to close out this last full week before the election, the closing arguments are key. The dominant issues remain the economy, high gas prices and which party can make American life more affordable.\par \par \uc2 Democrats say they're the ones actually passing laws that can be the solution. Republicans say Democrats had their chance for the last two years.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll speak with Delaware Senator Chris Coons who has been out on the campaign trail with Democrats in tough races like \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But, first, here in Washington, we are joined by \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 RONNA MCDANIEL\uc2 , CHAIRWOMAN, REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE: It's great to be here. Great to be in person. Thanks for having me. And congratulations on your show.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you very much. We appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 I want to start with the attack on Paul Pelosi. It's getting all the headlines this weekend. He is recovering from surgery, expected to do well, have a full recovery.\par \par \uc2 But "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " wasting no time, writing this: For many Democrats, the attack on \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's husband represents the all-but- inevitable conclusion of Republicans increasingly violent and threatening rhetoric toward their political opponents.\par \par \uc2 Still a lot we don't about this case. But what do you make much that accusation?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Well, I think that's unfair. I think this is a deranged individual. You can't say people saying, "let's fire Pelosi" or "let's take back the House" is saying go do violence. It's just unfair.\par \par \uc2 And I think we all need to recognize violence is up across the board. \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  was attacked. We had assassination attempt against \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And Democrats didn't repute -- you know, didn't repudiated that. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  didn't talk about the assassination attempt against \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But I think the only thing to remember is, if this weren't Paul Pelosi, this criminal would probably be out on the street tomorrow. We saw \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 's attacker was on the street right after he attacked him. This is what Democratic policies are bringing.\par \par \uc2 But, of course, we wish Paul Pelosi a recovery. We don't like this at all across the board. We don't want to see attacks on any politician from any political background.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: President Biden -- excuse me, President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , we played a little bit earlier, said everything needs to take down the language a little bit - -\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- the rhetoric and try to disagree on policy ideas, but not to demonize each other.\par \par \uc2 OK. Let's talk about where we are in the midterms. These were headlines at beginning of the year. Red tsunami warnings should worry Democrats. How big will the Republican wave be this fall?\par \par \uc2 Then by fall, these were the headlines: Red wave crashing? GOP momentum slips as fall sprint begins. GOP is on defense as chances of a red wave fade.\par \par \uc2 And this is in "The Atlantic" saying: Most Americans consistently say in polls that they believe that President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and congressional Democrats have mismanaged crime, the border and above all the economy and inflation.\par \par \uc2 So, why isn't this a clear GOP blowout at this point?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Oh, you know, we don't know, right? We don't know what it's going to be.\par \par \uc2 What I will say is in 2020, we saw a red wave in the House when we didn't lose a single incumbent and we picked up 15 House seats. And it put us five away from taking back the House.\par \par \uc2 And we're seeing that momentum, I feel it on the ground. People are very upset about crime, education, parental rights, about the economy, gas prices. It's death by a thousand cuts for so many families who can't afford rent, gas, groceries, Halloween candy. Everything is so expensive.\par \par \uc2 And the Democrats own it. They have the White House, the Senate and the House, and notice they're not running on their record.\par \par \uc2 And I think the American people are looking at this and saying, do really we want to give them this job again for another two years? Am I better off than I was two years ago? And it's a resounding no.\par \par \uc2 And that's why Republicans have put forward a plan and I'm very confident that we're going to have great wins on Tuesday, November 8th.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. You know the star power is out there. We played a little bit of former President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , more from him to come. He's out there echoing a line that we hear from the current president a lot, that Republicans are actually going after entitlements.\par \par \uc2 So, here is what President \uc2 Obama\uc2  said about people who've worked so hard for them and what your plan is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: They have long hours and sore backs and bad knees to get that Social Security. And if Ron Johnson does not understand that, if he understands giving tax breaks for private planes more than he understands making sure that seniors who worked all their lives are able to retire with dignity and respect, he's not the person who is thinking about you and knows you and sees you, and he should not be senator from Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And that is something that resonates with voters. I'm sure you hear and I hear it, too, that people considering their vote. They're worried about entitlements. It's something that the White House, Democrats, all the way down the ticket continue to talk about. And they say they're pointing to your proposals, to GOP documents to back up that claim.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to voters?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: I say that's scare tactics, and I say people in fixed incomes right now are dealing with a huge issue with inflation, Biden-flation, which has been caused by Democrats pumping so much money into this economy and abandoning our energy independence, which has caused so much -- caused prices to rise across the board.\par \par \uc2 And seniors who are on fixed incomes right now, they are hurting. They are hurting because of Democratic policies.\par \par \uc2 I ran to people all the time, they say I cannot afford my groceries, my drug -- my medicine that I need, to put gas in my car. And that is because of what Democrats have done and they own it.\par \par \uc2 They own it. They're running away from it. And the voters are so smart, they're going to hold them accountable for this economy, which is a failing economy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, if Republicans have great success, take the House, take the Senate, they can pass things. They can put things together. They're going to get vetoed on the president's desk.\par \par \uc2 So, how do then make the argument -- the Republicans make the argument going into 2024, past (ph) this scenario, that you guys are actually the party that can get something done?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Well, we do hold the power of the purse in the House, and the Democrats have shown the priorities with their voting. Instead of getting more Border Patrol agents, instead of getting more teachers for kids with deficits, instead of getting more police, they got 87,000 IRS agents. That's what they passed. So, the House can stop that.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has always talked about how he wants to be unified and work across the aisle -- well, this is his chance, because this is going to be repudiation of the Democratic Party. They have the reins for two years.\par \par \uc2 And if Republicans have big wins, they better recognize, they've got to come to the center and work with us on behalf of the American people. And that is exactly what we are laser-focused on -- how do we make lives better for voters who are in desperate need for leadership in Washington, instead of this partisan disaster we're seeing with rising crime, rising inflation, open border, drugs coming across and kids who are struggling from the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: One of the seats you have to pick to make this happened in the Senate is Pennsylvania. So, I want to give you a chance to respond to "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reporting on your comments in that race, saying: the head of Republican Party mocks the speaking abilities of Fetterman and Biden.\par \par \uc2 They're talking about a radio show you were on where you talked about them not being able to string together a full sentence.\par \par \uc2 Are you -- do you regret at all how that could be -- appear insensitive --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Well, I certainly want Joe Fetterman -- \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  to get better and I -- I would never make fun of somebody who has a stroke. But I will say, they should be getting in front of the press and they're not and they're hiding.\par \par \uc2 And Joe -- \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is following in the steps of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I know I'm conflating the names. But they're sitting in the basement. They're not getting in front of the voters and they're not being honest about their issues and -- about their issues.\par \par \uc2 He just lied about the fact that he wants to ban fracking. He's lied about the fact that he wants to put a third of the criminals back on the streets. These are things he said in his words. This isn't spin.\par \par \uc2 And so, they do need to get in front of the media and answer these tough questions, instead of hiding. So, I hope they do that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: If President Trump announces that he's going to run in 2024, will there be a competitive, full-throated GOP primary? Is it good or bad for the party?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Shannon, I am not talking about 2024. It is all about nine days from now and the midterms.\par \par \uc2 And what I will say about President Trump is he has done 30 rallies with our candidates. People actually want to be seen with President Trump, unlike Democrats who don't want to be seen with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . He's raised so much money. He's done 50 fundraisers for different candidates. He's raised hundreds of millions of dollars into the committees, the NRCC, NRSC and the \uc2 RNC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, he is out working on behalf of other candidates to make sure we win these majorities.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. We will see come -- well, I would say next Tuesday. But we know it's going to take some time for all the dust to settle. Chairwoman, thank you for making time to come in.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Joining us now, Senator Chris Coon -- Coons of Delaware.\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS (D-DE): Thanks, Shannon. Great to be on with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, I know you're out on the campaign trail, and part of that is in places that Democrats may not have expected they would be having some trouble.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about New Hampshire. Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 . She's got a GOP challenger there, General Bolduc. "Newsweek" says this: a Republican victory in New Hampshire would be a significant upset.\par \par \uc2 Out in the northwest, deep blue territory, "Washington Beacon" reports on the Oregon race, the gubernatorial race. It says voters there are fed up with Democrats. They quote one named Terry, quote: I don't like the Democratic approach to anything that's happening.\par \par \uc2 She's been a Democratic voter, she says, for 40 years, only recently changed her party affiliation. That governor's race there could end with a Republican for first time in 40 years.\par \par \uc2 Why are you guys struggling in such deep blue territory?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, Shannon, first, the folks who I've been campaigning with are running on record. Chair McDaniel just said we're running away from it. But we're running on it, because we've got real accomplishment to point to.\par \par \uc2 You didn't hear a detailed plan for how Republicans would fight inflation, would bring gas prices down, would make our country stronger.\par \par \uc2 But we've got a record both of President Biden passing significant bipartisan legislation on issues President Trump said he would deal with like investing in infrastructure, like bringing advanced manufacturing for chips back to the United States, where the president signed bills into law that were bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 We also have bills that only Democrats voted for, our Inflation Reduction Act, which is going to cap out of pocket cost, cap price of insulin and reduce prescription drug prices. That is one of the things that Americans say day in and day out and have said for years is one of the pressing costs that they are concerned about. Democrats have taken action and addressed it.\par \par \uc2 And last, the American Rescue Plan that only Democrats voted for provided funding to put teachers back in classrooms, to put cops back on the streets.\par \par \uc2 We've got a real record to run on. I wish we had a debate on the Senate floor between the Democrats' Inflation Reduction Act and the Republicans, but we didn't, because only the Democrats put forward a concrete inflation reduction plan. We passed it, and it's having a real impact.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 COONS: That's why I'm confident that \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  will be reelected.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, long time Clinton pollster and strategist \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2  has a piece on FoxNews.com, that actually says this, those things are working against you. He says this: The inflation electorate will hold Biden and Democrats accountable at midterm. He says: Most Americans blame \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats for the problems we're facing today and the electoral impact of this issue is singularly powerful.\par \par \uc2 He says polling shows a majority of Americans actually think the federal spending in the trillions is actually hurting the economy and making inflation worse. They're worried about gas prices, grocery prices. This is stuff they experience everyday.\par \par \uc2 It's happened on your watch, the Democrats' watch for two years. So, why should they give you another two years of running the country when they're struggling at 40-year-highs with inflation?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, first, inflation is worse in the United Kingdom, in the \uc2 European Union\uc2  because of the ongoing impacts of the COVID pandemic and Russia's war of aggression against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Our inflation rates are lower. But that doesn't make a difference if what you're seeing at the pump is high price. Gas prices have come down $1.25 since the summertime. We are seeing progress in reducing gas prices. We've taken strong action to address the prices at the grocery store. Both the Department of Agriculture and bills that we've introduced in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have concrete plans and we have taken action to address the prices that I hear from voters they care about. Healthcare cost. And we've got candidates on the other side of the aisle who say they would work to repeal what the president just signed into law that will help with prescription drug prices. I think that's a pretty sharp contrast.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, a lot of that doesn't kick in for years from now. And voters are going to the polls now. Many of them - millions have already voted as they wait to see how those pieces of legislation play out.\par \par \uc2 "The Wall Street Journal" editorial board says, though, it's not just about the economy. They say this. Democrats are in trouble because they nominated too many candidates with views on climate, immigration, climate and the economy that are all but impossible to defend in competitive races this year.\par \par \uc2 How worried are you about how far the progressive element of your party has pulled to the left, how that's resonating with voters, potentially, and what it may cost you in the midterm?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, two things. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has a strong record on crime as president. He said in his State of the Union, I don't support defunding the police, I support funding the police, and then acted on it. The American Rescue Plan specifically provided funding for state and local government to keep law enforcement officers on the beat. And his plan for this year provides more money to hire more cops, 100,000 more police officers.\par \par \uc2 And here in Delaware, we have three police agencies that just got a federal grant, as have hundreds of others across the country to hire more officers.\par \par \uc2 Let's look just over the line in Pennsylvania, where Mehmet Oz and \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  are - are campaigning in the closing days of an important race. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , as mayor of Braddock, a small town outside of Pittsburgh, took on violent crime. He has tattoos on his arms of the dates when young men were killed before he became mayor. He spent years working to end violent crime in that town successfully.\par \par \uc2 So, why is this an issue in the campaign? Because tens of millions of dollars of dark money ads from anonymous voters have created a mistaken impression. President Biden supports police and supports funding the police. And \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  has a strong record of fighting at the most local level to combat gun violence in the town where he was elected mayor.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But you understand, though, how some potential voters out there don't love his record on releasing potentially violent felons.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Look, I understand that there have been misleading ads in this campaign that have created the impression that he somehow is part of a defund the police cabal. That's not true.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But he's said things that have led people to be that that's his policy.\par \par \uc2 COONS: \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is someone who is a Pennsylvanian at heart and how Pennsylvania voters know will stand for them.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, quickly, I want to play this, because there's been a lot of talk about election deniers, steeling elections, all of those things.\par \par \uc2 Here's what the former Democratic presidential nominee \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  said just days ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: Right wing extremists already have a plan to literally steal the next presidential election. And they're not making a secret of it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so take that, along with this headline from "Newsweek," Democrats spent $43 million helping election deniers win their primaries. If it backfires, the Democrats will have to shoulder some of the blame for helping those far-right candidates into office.\par \par \uc2 So, aren't Democrats now contributing to kind of this conversation about election denying, steeling elections, that you guys have said you condemn?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, Shannon, look, those are both things I'm unfamiliar with. I do know that there are folks in my party really concerned about the mechanics of elections, about folks running for office in states like Arizona and Nevada who deny that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the 2020 presidential and who are running saying that they won't accept the result of an election with which they disagree.\par \par \uc2 I think all of us, in the wake of this attack on Paul Pelosi, need to say that we are going to stop demonizing folks because I think those kinds of - this sort of rhetoric that we've heard in too many ways, in too many places, can lead to violence by a small number of Americans who think that when we describe our political opponents as our enemies, we're calling for them to be attacked.\par \par \uc2 After the threat against Supreme Court Justice Kavanaugh, I worked with Senator Cornyn across the aisle to get signed into law a bill to expand police protection of Supreme Court justices and their families.\par \par \uc2 President Biden and Democrats have stood behind law enforcement for strengthening protections for those in public life. That's what I think we should be focusing on in this moment when leaders of both parties, but so far not former President Trump, have decried the attack on Paul Pelosi.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Senator Coons, we appreciate your time. Always great to talk with you.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, the Elon Musk era officially begins at \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Is he opening a door for former President Trump to reemerge on the platform? We're going to tell you what the former president is saying and we're bringing our Sunday group on how \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's regime change could impact 2024.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: And we've seen how that goes.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), GEORGIA REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE: He said I'm a celebrity. Yes. He got that one wrong, didn't he? I'm not a celebrity. I'm that warrior for God.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Former President \uc2 Barak Obama\uc2  and Republican Senate Candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  trading shots about each other's fame as Walker tries to win in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 It is time now for our Sunday panel.\par \par \uc2 "USA Today" White House correspondent Francesca Chambers, former chief of staff to \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , Josh Holmes, former Maryland Congressman \uc2 John Delaney\uc2  and \uc2 George Washington University\uc2  law professor Jonathan Turley.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to all of you.\par \par \uc2 OK, let's start there. A lot of back and forth there. And, remember, Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , just days ago, caught on the tarmac saying that Georgia was - you know, they were having a tough time there. He says it's hard to believe they will go for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . That kind of echoes something that Speaker Pelosi said in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " days go. She said, part of this is, I cannot believe anybody would vote for these people.\par \par \uc2 John, does that show a disconnect with where the American people are?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DELANEY\uc2  (D-MD), FORMER CONGRESSMAN AND FORMER DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: No, I mean, listen, that's politics. I mean if you think about where we are right now, most people would have assumed the Democrats would be in a really bad spot if you look at the historical kind of situation, right? Since World War II, the president's party has lost on average of four Senate seats, 26 House seats. And this is a toss-up. And the Democrats, I think, are in a very good position to make a strong closing argument around the economy. And that's what they actually have to do because, we all see it, that's the number one issue on voter's minds.\par \par \uc2 I mean, they're concerned. They see low unemployment and rising wages, on one hand, which is good. But, on the other hand, they see this inflation and high interest rates, which is bad. What the Democrats have to do is lean into the things that they've done, the tangible things that the party has done since it moved to the middle, passed a law to lower drug prices, that's going to kick in, as you just said. That's going to make a difference. Passed a huge bipartisan infrastructure bill that's going to rebuild this country. It's going to be enormously popular, including delivering broadband to rural communities. Passed a piece of legislation that's going to bring a semiconductor industry back to the United States and contribute to the huge wave of kind of reshoring jobs.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I don't know if -\par \par \uc2 DELANEY: And putting the country in a position to lead the energy transition. These are things to run on. And the Democrats have to lean into it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I don't - and they're very wonky, though, when people are going to the grocery store and filling up their gas than and they don't see it yet, Josh. And one of the races that's very tight where this conversation is ongoing is up in New Hampshire. We just talked to Senator Coons about it.\par \par \uc2 Not long ago, the Senate Leadership Fund, the Senate PAC aligned with your former boss, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , pulled $5 million in remaining ad buys there. Is that a missed opportunity? I mean other money has flooded in, but it's so close, is that a missed opportunity at a pick-up?\par \par \uc2 JOSH HOLMES, FORMER CHIEF OF STAFF TO \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , CAVALRY PRESIDENT AND FOUNDING PARTNER AND "RUTHLESS PODCAST" CO-HOST: Well, I think - first of all they spent over $20 million over the previous four weeks, right? So the idea that they've sort of abandoned New Hampshire I think is -- is a little crazy. But you also saw a bunch of other Republican groups get involved there.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is, is that they're spending a ton of money in North Carolina, Ohio, Pennsylvania, Georgia, Arizona, Nevada, I mean, you name it, Colorado and Washington state. A lot of opportunities there for Republicans. And I think it just goes to show where these are all being played out.\par \par \uc2 I think Nevada, Georgia and Pennsylvania are sort of the core of the Senate argument at this point. And it doesn't look like they're going to lose any seats that they've held. Like, Pennsylvania is basically that turning point, right? But if you look at Nevada and Georgia, for example, prime opportunities for pick-ups, I think Republicans, at this point, are in - in -- heavily favored to take the Senate.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, and, Francesca, you've got a brand-new piece out this morning on one of the hot issue in the middle of all of this is abortion and how the GOP kind of coalesces around a position.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 " WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Right. And Republicans are kind of in this mode where they might be the party that wins in this election, but then what do they do with that majority? Is abortion something that they will take on? And you have Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , who's really tried to rally consensus around a 15-week abortion ban, but that's gotten luke warm support so far in the GOP. And you hear anti-abortion advocates saying that they need to come up with a position that not only lawmakers but also their 2024 presidential candidates can rally around.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, on that point, President Biden has also said, this is the NPR headline, he will push to codify Roe if Democrats keep the House and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 So, Professor, he talks about codifying Roe, but what had been offered up in the Senate was not Roe.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, GWU LAW PROFESSOR AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: No. What's being offered - what he was referring to, I believe, in terms of that legislation, goes way beyond Roe. In fact, Roe was premised on the idea that states could craft their own laws. It created a floor.\par \par \uc2 The bill in Congress is basically a wish list of what people wanted Roe to be, but it wasn't Roe. And so that legislation, if - if it is enacted, would not only federalize abortion, but it would go lightyears beyond Roe.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. We'll wait to see which party is in the - the drivers seat about either one of those.\par \par \uc2 OK, I want to talk about \uc2 Twitter\uc2  because Elon Musk has officially taken over. The questions are, who gets to come back on? But here is a tweet that he put out. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  will be forming a content moderation council with widely diverse viewpoints. No major content decisions or account reinstatements will happen before that council convenes.\par \par \uc2 Listen, a lot of people disappointed. They want to see a free for all. But he says this is not going to turn into a hellscape.\par \par \uc2 What happens with \uc2 Twitter\uc2  now?\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Well, look, I think the most important developments, it's not basically who's on or who's off \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . The most important development with Elon Musk's acquisition here is to try to push back on the modern left's idea of content moderation, period, right? We've seen over the - over recent years a big effort on the left to try to silence any sort of opinion that doesn't fit a conventional wisdom narrative, right? Covid really, really exposed a lot of this where you couldn't even basically provide any sort of opinion that went against conventional wisdom and then people were banned from the playoff all together. Clearly one of the most dangerous things you can do in a democracy like ours. I think that's the biggest component to the Elon Musk piece.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, but - but there were bad actors who immediately flooded in with tweets that we could never air on this show.\par \par \uc2 DELANEY: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So he's got a tough job.\par \par \uc2 DELANEY: And this is concerning. I mean, listen, he's owned it since Friday, so we shouldn't jump to any conclusions about what these -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll give him a couple days.\par \par \uc2 DELANEY: We'll give him a couple days. And he is a once in a generation entrepreneur.\par \par \uc2 But these issues are really important. These issues of disinformation, hate speech, conspiracy theories. You know, if you think about the Paul Pelosi situation, the escalation in political violence, the fact that it's undermined democracy, some of the - kind of the conspiracy theories that go on. And, importantly, how it's eroding the notion of expertise, because that's what this stuff does, it basically says there aren't (ph) facts. And it's hard to move the country forward unless you have base set of facts that the experts agree on. And that's why these issues are so important.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And - and, Professor, this is coming at a time when there's a call to cancel Justice Barrett's book because people don't like her vote.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Right. I mean why burn books when you can ban them. And that's basically what these authors and editors have said is that we shouldn't be publishing a book by Barrett. Why? Because they disagree with her jurisprudence, her legal views. And that's where we are now, where writes are opposing free speech. It's like athletes opposing exercise. This is an existential right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, panel, we could go on, Thank you all for being here.\par \par \uc2 I can't wait for the commercial.\par \par \uc2 All right, we'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll bring in law professor John Yoo on a handful of high-stakes legal battles playing out at the Supreme Court right now, including the fight over whether this sitting U.S. senator should have to testify before a Georgia grand jury and the two separate legal challenges on affirmative action headed to the high court tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: In just 24 hours, the Supreme Court will hear arguments on one of the nation's most politically divisive arguments, affirmative action. It's a decades long debate that could take a major new turn now that the high court has a 6-3 conservative majority.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we will bring in \uc2 UC Berkley\uc2  Law Professor John Yoo, who's part of a group that filed an amicus brief in one of the cases.\par \par \uc2 But first, a look at the case that could rewrite affirmative action policy in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Face your own racism and dishonesty, Harvard.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): It is the legal fight parents, and their high schoolers, will be watching very closely. Tomorrow, lawyers for Students for Fair Admissions will ask the high court to strike down a 2003 precedent that allows colleges to consider race as one of many factor in admission. The group was founded by 66-year-old conservative \uc2 Edward Blum\uc2 . He says Asian American students end up being discriminated against by being held to selectively higher standards than other minority groups.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD BLUM\uc2 , STUDENTS FOR FAIR ADMISSIONS FOUNDER: People involved in this movement are liberal, they're conservative, they're moderate, they're libertarian.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The schools, \uc2 Harvard University\uc2 , which is private, and the University of North Carolina, which is public, both deny discriminating and defend their application process saying they want to promote a robust, intellectually diverse campus. Student opinion is all over the map.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We're not against that someone should not be providing their racial information, but how is that going to be used is a big question?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Race cannot be removed effectively from an application just because it is so central to so many people's identity.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The suit claims both schools violated Title VI of the Civil Rights Act by penalizing Asian American applicants, engaging in racial balancing, overemphasizing race and rejecting workable race neutral alternatives.\par \par \uc2 Harvard's president says, quote, considering race is one factor among many in admission decisions produces a more diverse student body, which strengthens the learning environment for all. And UNC says it wants the court to continue to allow universities to pursue the educational benefits of diversity in a narrowly tailored way.\par \par \uc2 Nine states currently ban the use of race in the public university admission process, including California. The University of California system says, when a state ban on affirmative action went into effect in 1998, the proportion of black and Latino freshmen at UCLA dropped by roughly half.\par \par \uc2 SETH WAXAM, LAWYER FOR HARVARD: It would be a very, very sad day in history if this is the way forward, not the way that SFFA is proposing to take us.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM F. LEE, LAWYER FOR HARVARD: SFFA wants to eliminate a thousand African Americans and Hispanics from the Harvard campus.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The case challenges a 2003 Supreme Court precedent which upheld narrowly tailored and holistic race conscious affirmative action programs as long as schools don't base admissions decisions solely on race or a quota system.\par \par \uc2 Then Justice Sandra Day O'Conner, the deciding vote in that case, wrote, the court expects that 25 years from now the use of racial preferences will no longer be necessary to further the interest approved today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And yet that is the very argument that lands at the Supreme Court tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, \uc2 UC Berkley\uc2  Law Professor John Yoo.\par \par \uc2 John, welcome to FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 JOHN YOO, UNIVERSITY OF CALIFORNIA AT BERKLEY LAW PROFESSOR: Hi, Shannon. Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so the schools say they have not discriminate said against Asian students or anyone else. This is from Harvard's brief, it continues to study whether consideration of race is necessary to assemble a class comprising students of different background. No alternative is presently workable. And, John, they say, until it changes, Harvard needs to be able to use race in putting together a diverse freshman class.\par \par \uc2 YOO: Harvard and other institutions all claim they need to look at race to ensure diversity. But what the people on the other side, the Asian students here, white students, students of all colors want is for universities to join the rest of the country and the Constitution and the Declaration of Independence, and \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2 , and \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , in believing that the government should be color blind when it treats us as individual citizens.\par \par \uc2 Actually, the Supreme Court only allows the use of race now only for college admissions. It doesn't allow its use in national security, in policing, in hiring, giving out money, giving out contracts. Why should schools be able to consider us by our skin color rather than, as \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  said, on the content of our characters.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, tomorrow they take up that case.\par \par \uc2 Right now something pending before them on an emergency basis is whether or not Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  should have to testify before a Georgia grand jury that is investigating allegations of post-election interference in 2020. His team says he has constitutional immunities to this. But Fulton County, who is trying to get him to testify says, should the senator's application be granted, meaning that subpoena be blocked against him, the grand jury's work will be indefinitely delayed, ensuring that information which could either clear the innocent of suspicion or increase scrutiny on the guilty will continue to lie beyond the grand jury's grasp.\par \par \uc2 What is the Supreme Court going to do here?\par \par \uc2 YOO: Shannon, I'm glad you read out the line that the lower courts are trying to draw here because I think the media has somewhat exaggerated what's happening here. Senator Graham does not have to testify about things he was doing as part of his legitimate legislative activities, like as chairman of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 , trying to find out what Georgia's laws were about absentee ballots and counting, for example.\par \par \uc2 But what the lower courts are allowing -- I think the Supreme Court's going to let the lower courts continue to run the process was to say, well, maybe the prosecutor down in Georgia is allowed to ask about things Senator Graham was doing politically, like talking with the Trump White House, or whether he was trying to get the Georgia officials to change their rules or to cheat essentially. That according to the lower courts here is outside Senator Graham's legislative duties. That's more like acting like a - just a Republican politician.\par \par \uc2 I think the Supreme Court may not want to get into the thicket of exactly what questions are going to be asked and so on and leave it to the lower courts to handle.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But, quickly, do you think they would grant a stay while this plays out on the merits? What are the odds?\par \par \uc2 YOO: I think that's a good point, Shannon. They might just say, let's have a stay because Georgia doesn't seem to be in a rush, just so it can hear fuller oral arguments about this case, even if we do, in the end, allow the lower courts to run things.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right a big day at the Supreme Court, a big week as we stand by.\par \par \uc2 John Yoo, thank you so much for joining us. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 YOO: Thanks, Shannon. It's great to be with you again.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, a couple of programming notes for you, including a big one about a town hall in one of the biggest battleground states. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Before we go, a couple of programming notes for you ahead of a major political week.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, you can join my college, Bill Hemmer, for an hour-long special ahead of Election Day, it's called "The Big Midterm Show." Bill brings together a panel of experts. They'll discuss all of the latest news and polls ahead of November 8th. That is tonight, 10:00 Eastern, right here on Fox News Channel.\par \par \uc2 And join Bret Baier and Martha MacCallum for a must-see town hall special on Tuesday night. They will be live in Ohio, one of the most heated races. Voters there will get to ask questions of both Democratic Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and Republican JD Vance. That will be on Fox News Channel live at 6:00 p.m. Eastern. You don't want to miss it. The Senate could hang in the balance there.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, keep it here with FOX NEWS SUNDAY next week. We're working on a massive midterm special from Fox News Election Headquarters in New York.\par \par \uc2 That is it for this Sunday morning. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a great week and we'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020221030eiau00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Sen. Chris Coons (D-DE) is Interviewed about Elections; Both Parties Face Questions on Strategy; Fate of Abortion Rights Bills; Musk Faces Scrutiny; High Court to Hear Arguments on Affirmative Acton; John Yoo is Interviewed about College Admissions; Interview with \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Jonathan Turley, Lucas Tomlinson, Rich Edson,  Kellyanne Conway \par \uc2 9,894 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Toss-ups, tightening polls and turnout -- we're just over a week out from midterms and right now, both parties are bracing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: You deserve somebody who's independent, somebody like Reverend \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Democrats play defense, with the party's biggest stars dispatched to help candidates even in blue states. We have a live report this morning from battleground Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 And we will bring you the latest on the chilling attack at the home of House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , as fears of more political violence rise.\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The poll that matters is the poll that's taken on November 8th, at 8:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: A deep dive on those attention-grabbing polls that dominate political chatter.\par \par \uc2 And we have pollsters from both parties here to explain what the numbers can and can't tell us ahead of November 8th, Republican Kellyanne Conway and Democrat Celinda Lake join us live.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: They can't take me down.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Then, Republicans aim to win the House and Senate, but they have yet to overcome razor-thin margins in key races. As Democrats are caught on a hot mic reassuring President Biden they can hold on.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): I think we're picking up steam in Nevada.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll ask \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  whether her party can close the deal. And we'll get reaction from Democratic Senator Chris Coons who has been on the trail helping a fellow senator in a tight race.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Elon Musk takes the helm of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Will he give former President Trump back his megaphone?\par \par \uc2 We'll ask our Sunday panel what happens now that the billionaire free speech advocate is in charge.\par \par \uc2 Also, it could be the biggest Supreme Court case this fall, arguments on affirmative action in college admissions. We'll discuss the cases, the history and the potential impact on kids applying for school.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Big-name Republicans and Democrats are swarming the campaign trail this weekend, selling their vision to voters. Democrats trying to shore up control of the House, trying to prevent it from flipping red. And the heat is on in the battle over 50/50 Senate, both parties on edge as races in Georgia, Pennsylvania and Arizona all appear to be a jump ball.\par \par \uc2 All of this against the backdrop of a frightening scene in San Francisco. House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's husband violently attacked in their home. Political leaders quickly condemn the attack and Americans find themselves reminded once again that lawmakers and U.S. leaders on both side of the aisle face increased threats of violence.\par \par \uc2 We've got a lot to discuss this morning. We will break it all down for you with our bipartisan pollster panel, Celinda Lake and Kellyanne Conway.\par \par \uc2 And in just a few minutes, we'll speak with \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 , and then Democratic Senator Chris Coons, a close confidant of President Biden.\par \par \uc2 But we begin in team coverage. Rich Edson is live in Pennsylvania's capital where a Senate race is coming down to the wire. But, first, to Lucas Tomlinson at the White House with the president's reaction to the attack inside the home of Speaker of the House -- Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, officials say if not for an alert 911 dispatcher and quick police response, this attack could have had a much different outcome.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is despicable.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): President Biden speaking in Philadelphia hours after the attack.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: There is too much violence, political violence, too much hatred, too much vitriol.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Police say the suspect's 42-year-old David Depape broke into the Pelosi home in San Francisco around 2:30 early Friday morning. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's husband, 82-year-old Paul Pelosi, called 911 from the bathroom moments after the break-in. The attacker calling out, "Where's Nancy?" When police arrived, they found Pelosi and the assailant struggling over a hammer.\par \par \uc2 Police say Depape was able to gain control of it and strike Paul Pelosi in the head before being tackled by the police. Paul Polisi had successful surgery for a fractured skull. He also suffered injuries to his hand and right arm. He's expected to make a full recovery.\par \par \uc2 The latest violence comes almost five months after a man attempted to assassinate Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 U.S. Capitol Police\uc2  say threats against lawmakers have more than doubled since 2017.\par \par \uc2 On the campaign trail, former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  condemned the attack.\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: If our rhetoric about each other gets that mean, when we don't just disagree, but we start demonizing them, making wild, crazy allegations about them, that creates a dangerous climate.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: San Francisco's police chief credited the dispatcher for her quick thinking.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM SCOTT, SAN FRANCISCO POLICE CHIEF: Her actions, in my opinion, resulted in both a higher priority dispatch and a faster police response. I think this was life-saving.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): Depape will make his first court appearance Tuesday. He faces multiple charges, including attempted murder -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Lucas Tomlinson from the White House, Lucas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We'll now go live to Rich Edson who is on the campaign trail in Harrisburg, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 One to watch, Rich.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It is, Shannon, and Pennsylvania is going to have be big enough this week for two former and one current president.\par \par \uc2 Democrats and Republicans in the general election campaign here and nationwide have been distancing themselves from their party's more unpopular presidents. Now, it's becoming unavoidable.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Fetterman is Pennsylvania. He had a stroke. He's recovering.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: At Tuesday's Pennsylvania Senate debate between Democratic nominee \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Mehmet Oz, Fetterman stroke recovery was on full display.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: The debate didn't hurt us too much in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: As Fetterman and Oz campaigned across Pennsylvania, the political heavyweights are now showing up. President Biden and Vice President Harris to a Friday night fundraiser in Philadelphia, former President Trump this week to Latrobe, and Biden again with former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  in Philly and Pittsburgh next weekend.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 Obama\uc2 , part of campaign to swing states nationwide.\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: It's got to be back in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: The 44th president has campaigned for Democrats in Georgia, Michigan and Wisconsin before Nevada and Pennsylvania this week.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are also deploying their party stars, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  campaigning for gubernatorial candidate \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): If you want to stop what the Democrats have done to this country over the last two years, we have to elect Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: While Republicans try to make this election about the president, Biden maintained it's not and said something both parties can agree with.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It's a choice. A choice between two vastly different visions of America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): First Lady Jill Biden is also getting involved here in Pennsylvania. Wednesday, she'll hit both ends of the state in Allentown and Pittsburgh for events -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Rich Edson in Pennsylvania, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, the president of Lake Research Partner, Celinda Lake, and former senior counselor to former President Trump, Kellyanne Conway.\par \par \uc2 I am so excited, welcome, to have you both here\par \par \uc2 CELINDA LAKE, LAKE RESEARCH PARTNER: Excited to be here and together.\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I mean, for those of us who love crunching these numbers and watching these races, I mean, we're getting down to the finish line. So, let me start with this, Suffolk University, "USA Today", they've got their poll, the generic congressional ballot, 49-45 in favor of Republicans, that's a flip from what they had over the summer in July, when they said 16 percent were still undecided. They say they break to the GOP, these undecided voters, at least this time around.\par \par \uc2 So, two things, we've got polls showing the exact opposite right now. So, Kellyanne Conway, what do we believe and are there that many undecideds left? Which way do they go?\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: There are some fence sitters and they wait until the very end, maybe debates are still happening, the candidates are making their closing arguments. You see some of the political heavyweights coming to campaign with them and for them.\par \par \uc2 But, essentially, the fundamentals have been favoring Republicans for a while and has an awful lot to do with two big things, one is the issue set. So, voters have been straightforward and uncomplicated. They've been telling pollsters for a year. I'm worried about rising cost and rising crime. It's inflation. It's education. For some voters, it's abortion, although even CNN and James Carville, and other Democrats have admitted that has faded as a top issue and intensity.\par \par \uc2 The second thing that's happening is Republicans have done fantastic recruiting diverse league of candidate. Seventy percent of Republican House candidates right now who are running in competitive Democratic House districts are voters of -- excuse me, candidate of color and/or female candidates, and we have a lot of veterans. That really has helped the seeing is believing surge in the Republican Party, where voters are getting really excited.\par \par \uc2 The last thing in that "USA Today" poll, they made clear. They say upwards of 40 percent of Hispanics and 21 percent of African Americans are right now favoring the Republican candidates for office. So, this is a real realignment of voters who were long-time constituencies of the Democratic party they can rely on.\par \par \uc2 I think fundamentals favor the Republicans in 2020, not single Republican house incumbent lost and we flipped 15 from blue to red, and that could happen again. Meaning, not a single house or Senate Republican may lose incumbent. There are one or two are a little in danger in the House. But think about that. I think that we're looking at 2022 in the House easily and taking over the Senate.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, that's the prediction from Kellyanne side of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 We're going to dig into some of the numbers about demographics, too, but people are a little bit suspicious, Celinda, about some of these polls. They don't know what to believe.\par \par \uc2 "The New Criterion" under the headline "Who Need Polls" says this, many of -- these two ladies need polls. We all need polls.\par \par \uc2 Many observers have a sneaking suspicion that some pollsters rig their surveys to favor one or the other political party in the hope of shaping the eventual election outcome.\par \par \uc2 They point to the race, Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 , for example, up in Maine. She was losing in the polls by five to 10. She won by nine. That's just one example.\par \par \uc2 People are suspicious.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: People should be suspicious and people should vote their conscience, not the polls anyway.\par \par \uc2 But I think if you look -- the best thing to do is look at the average of the polls, and the average of the polls says it's a dead -- it's a jump ball, as you said. It's dead even.\par \par \uc2 What's most important thing right now is the turnout and the biggest asset that we have is when President Biden, when President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , Dr. Biden go into these places, when our vice president goes into places, it really energizes Democrats. You can bet Democrats in Georgia and Democrats in Wisconsin feel a lot better today after having heard ex- President \uc2 Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, I think that's what's really important is who's going to turn out. We know the Republicans are energized, but if we got every voter who turned out and voted Democratic in 2018, we would win. So, it's too close to call and any pollsters who says they know what's going on is lying to you, vote your conscience.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. There are no reliable crystal balls.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Exactly right. We'd make a lot more money and we'd be in Vegas right now.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Exactly. Yeah, let us know if you guys.\par \par \uc2 OK. So, let's talk about these constituencies, these voting blocks. Specifically, "Politico" has this, Democrats are growing anxious again over Black turnout. They say in a poll by "Politico"/Morning Consult, last week, just 25 percent of Black registered voters describe themselves as extremely enthusiastic about voting in this election.\par \par \uc2 This is something I talked to Georgia gubernatorial candidate \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  when we had her on the show, that she is getting accusations from within the Black community that Democrats have taken them for granted as voters and really haven't delivered in many ways for them.\par \par \uc2 You cited the numbers, Kellyanne. Is there really a shift there, or the Democrats simply -- it's a turnout issue for Black voters?\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Well, the shift has started with President Trump. He did much better among Hispanics and African-Americans the second time than he did the first time improve his vote share. And I think it's continuing because it's now the party of the worker. It's a party of the job creator, job seeker and really the job holder, which is a vast majority of households in this country, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 But there's something else going on, I don't know why and I don't how. I'm happy for it, but the Democrats in this White House have basically ignored voter concern for better part of a year.\par \par \uc2 Voters have very plainly said to pollsters of every stripe, we're worried about rising cost and rising crime. Parents are still upset about lost learning and reduced test scores and the fact students and children face these emotional and mental health challenges within the schools. And so, that's not going to be -- we're parents. We're not a Democratic cohort to be studied. We're not in an interest group to be pursued. We're parents.\par \par \uc2 And so, I feel the Democrats have been trying to make the election about abortion, Trump, January sixth and climate change. A year ago in Virginia, \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  tried to make the election about Trump and abortion, and Glenn Youngkin said Virginia and education. So, they have been ignoring the will of voters.\par \par \uc2 And there's something else going on, "New York Times"/Siena College poll showed a 32-point shift among independent women month over month.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Do you think that's reliable? That's a huge shift.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: I think it's a little bit of outlier. It's an outlier. The fundamentals are the same.\par \par \uc2 But, look, if the generic ballot -- the Democrats have relied on the political gender gap and if women are starting to migrate towards the Republican ideals and candidates, I mean, we have -- we have \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  up by one point. She's under 50 percent. We had \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  up by six, but she's under 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 I mean, these are deep blue states with female Democratic legislator. I mean, let me just say one last thing about the polls, it's important.\par \par \uc2 Celinda, we're pollsters. If we weren't good at what we did, the free market would have kicked us out. I'm worried about media polls, because there's no accountability there for the lack of accuracy in the last few years, and I do worry that some people are using polls not to reflect and respect public opinion, but to create and manipulate it. And I worry about it.\par \par \uc2 But let's also be frank about what polls are. They're snapshot in time. They tell you about trends. They can't predict. If you take a poll in early October, it doesn't predict what's happening in November.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. Well, let's talk about the Hispanic vote as well because the financial time says this -- the Democrats fear losing Nevada because of what's happening with Hispanic voters there. They say Democrats nationwide are increasingly struggling to cling on to their once relatively secure Hispanic vote and Republicans are out there courting Latino voters with an economic message focused on jobs and inflation.\par \par \uc2 Celinda, what do you see in those numbers?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Well, first of all, I think the Democrats had a strong economic message for over a year, and the difference between the parties is we have an economic record. We passed the Inflation Reduction Act without votes from the Republicans. We passed getting prescription drug prices down without votes from Republicans. We passed crime bill and gun bill without votes from Republicans.\par \par \uc2 So, we have a record to stand on, and what's really important is to make sure in these last few days that record and that contrast gets revealed in these races and voters really focus on it.\par \par \uc2 I think President \uc2 Obama\uc2  is a huge help in getting the African-American turnout up and African-American voters solidified. I do think that the Democrats need to have and continue to have a stronger economic message, particularly around entrepreneur entrepreneurship and jobs and you see the president very strongly doing that with help from small businesses, with the CHIPS Act, and I think that will make a lot of difference with Latino voters.\par \par \uc2 The last thing I'll say about the Latino vote is watch out for the women. The Latinas are coming home really strong right now and turning out in record numbers.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And a lot of them are on the ballot, too.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: That's right. Record numbers in both parties.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. I could pick your brains for an hour.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both, Celinda and Kellyanne, for being with us.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Republicans are growing cautiously more confident about winning the Senate, but a few GOP candidates in critical races really still have to close the gap. We're going to sit down with \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  and Democratic Senator Chris Coons, they're next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: As both parties get ready to close out this last full week before the election, the closing arguments are key. The dominant issues remain the economy, high gas prices and which party can make American life more affordable.\par \par \uc2 Democrats say they're the ones actually passing laws that can be the solution. Republicans say Democrats had their chance for the last two years.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we'll speak with Delaware Senator Chris Coons who has been out on the campaign trail with Democrats in tough races like \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But, first, here in Washington, we are joined by \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 RONNA MCDANIEL\uc2 , CHAIRWOMAN, REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE: It's great to be here. Great to be in person. Thanks for having me. And congratulations on your show.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you very much. We appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 I want to start with the attack on Paul Pelosi. It's getting all the headlines this weekend. He is recovering from surgery, expected to do well, have a full recovery.\par \par \uc2 But "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " wasting no time, writing this: For many Democrats, the attack on \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's husband represents the all-but- inevitable conclusion of Republicans increasingly violent and threatening rhetoric toward their political opponents.\par \par \uc2 Still a lot we don't about this case. But what do you make much that accusation?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Well, I think that's unfair. I think this is a deranged individual. You can't say people saying, "let's fire Pelosi" or "let's take back the House" is saying go do violence. It's just unfair.\par \par \uc2 And I think we all need to recognize violence is up across the board. \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  was attacked. We had assassination attempt against \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And Democrats didn't repute -- you know, didn't repudiated that. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  didn't talk about the assassination attempt against \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But I think the only thing to remember is, if this weren't Paul Pelosi, this criminal would probably be out on the street tomorrow. We saw \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 's attacker was on the street right after he attacked him. This is what Democratic policies are bringing.\par \par \uc2 But, of course, we wish Paul Pelosi a recovery. We don't like this at all across the board. We don't want to see attacks on any politician from any political background.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: President Biden -- excuse me, President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , we played a little bit earlier, said everything needs to take down the language a little bit - -\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- the rhetoric and try to disagree on policy ideas, but not to demonize each other.\par \par \uc2 OK. Let's talk about where we are in the midterms. These were headlines at beginning of the year. Red tsunami warnings should worry Democrats. How big will the Republican wave be this fall?\par \par \uc2 Then by fall, these were the headlines: Red wave crashing? GOP momentum slips as fall sprint begins. GOP is on defense as chances of a red wave fade.\par \par \uc2 And this is in "The Atlantic" saying: Most Americans consistently say in polls that they believe that President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and congressional Democrats have mismanaged crime, the border and above all the economy and inflation.\par \par \uc2 So, why isn't this a clear GOP blowout at this point?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Oh, you know, we don't know, right? We don't know what it's going to be.\par \par \uc2 What I will say is in 2020, we saw a red wave in the House when we didn't lose a single incumbent and we picked up 15 House seats. And it put us five away from taking back the House.\par \par \uc2 And we're seeing that momentum, I feel it on the ground. People are very upset about crime, education, parental rights, about the economy, gas prices. It's death by a thousand cuts for so many families who can't afford rent, gas, groceries, Halloween candy. Everything is so expensive.\par \par \uc2 And the Democrats own it. They have the White House, the Senate and the House, and notice they're not running on their record.\par \par \uc2 And I think the American people are looking at this and saying, do really we want to give them this job again for another two years? Am I better off than I was two years ago? And it's a resounding no.\par \par \uc2 And that's why Republicans have put forward a plan and I'm very confident that we're going to have great wins on Tuesday, November 8th.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. You know the star power is out there. We played a little bit of former President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , more from him to come. He's out there echoing a line that we hear from the current president a lot, that Republicans are actually going after entitlements.\par \par \uc2 So, here is what President \uc2 Obama\uc2  said about people who've worked so hard for them and what your plan is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: They have long hours and sore backs and bad knees to get that Social Security. And if Ron Johnson does not understand that, if he understands giving tax breaks for private planes more than he understands making sure that seniors who worked all their lives are able to retire with dignity and respect, he's not the person who is thinking about you and knows you and sees you, and he should not be senator from Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And that is something that resonates with voters. I'm sure you hear and I hear it, too, that people considering their vote. They're worried about entitlements. It's something that the White House, Democrats, all the way down the ticket continue to talk about. And they say they're pointing to your proposals, to GOP documents to back up that claim.\par \par \uc2 What do you say to voters?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: I say that's scare tactics, and I say people in fixed incomes right now are dealing with a huge issue with inflation, Biden-flation, which has been caused by Democrats pumping so much money into this economy and abandoning our energy independence, which has caused so much -- caused prices to rise across the board.\par \par \uc2 And seniors who are on fixed incomes right now, they are hurting. They are hurting because of Democratic policies.\par \par \uc2 I ran to people all the time, they say I cannot afford my groceries, my drug -- my medicine that I need, to put gas in my car. And that is because of what Democrats have done and they own it.\par \par \uc2 They own it. They're running away from it. And the voters are so smart, they're going to hold them accountable for this economy, which is a failing economy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, if Republicans have great success, take the House, take the Senate, they can pass things. They can put things together. They're going to get vetoed on the president's desk.\par \par \uc2 So, how do then make the argument -- the Republicans make the argument going into 2024, past (ph) this scenario, that you guys are actually the party that can get something done?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Well, we do hold the power of the purse in the House, and the Democrats have shown the priorities with their voting. Instead of getting more Border Patrol agents, instead of getting more teachers for kids with deficits, instead of getting more police, they got 87,000 IRS agents. That's what they passed. So, the House can stop that.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has always talked about how he wants to be unified and work across the aisle -- well, this is his chance, because this is going to be repudiation of the Democratic Party. They have the reins for two years.\par \par \uc2 And if Republicans have big wins, they better recognize, they've got to come to the center and work with us on behalf of the American people. And that is exactly what we are laser-focused on -- how do we make lives better for voters who are in desperate need for leadership in Washington, instead of this partisan disaster we're seeing with rising crime, rising inflation, open border, drugs coming across and kids who are struggling from the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: One of the seats you have to pick to make this happened in the Senate is Pennsylvania. So, I want to give you a chance to respond to "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reporting on your comments in that race, saying: the head of Republican Party mocks the speaking abilities of Fetterman and Biden.\par \par \uc2 They're talking about a radio show you were on where you talked about them not being able to string together a full sentence.\par \par \uc2 Are you -- do you regret at all how that could be -- appear insensitive --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Well, I certainly want Joe Fetterman -- \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  to get better and I -- I would never make fun of somebody who has a stroke. But I will say, they should be getting in front of the press and they're not and they're hiding.\par \par \uc2 And Joe -- \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is following in the steps of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I know I'm conflating the names. But they're sitting in the basement. They're not getting in front of the voters and they're not being honest about their issues and -- about their issues.\par \par \uc2 He just lied about the fact that he wants to ban fracking. He's lied about the fact that he wants to put a third of the criminals back on the streets. These are things he said in his words. This isn't spin.\par \par \uc2 And so, they do need to get in front of the media and answer these tough questions, instead of hiding. So, I hope they do that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: If President Trump announces that he's going to run in 2024, will there be a competitive, full-throated GOP primary? Is it good or bad for the party?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Shannon, I am not talking about 2024. It is all about nine days from now and the midterms.\par \par \uc2 And what I will say about President Trump is he has done 30 rallies with our candidates. People actually want to be seen with President Trump, unlike Democrats who don't want to be seen with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . He's raised so much money. He's done 50 fundraisers for different candidates. He's raised hundreds of millions of dollars into the committees, the NRCC, NRSC and the \uc2 RNC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, he is out working on behalf of other candidates to make sure we win these majorities.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. We will see come -- well, I would say next Tuesday. But we know it's going to take some time for all the dust to settle. Chairwoman, thank you for making time to come in.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Joining us now, Senator Chris Coon -- Coons of Delaware.\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS (D-DE): Thanks, Shannon. Great to be on with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, I know you're out on the campaign trail, and part of that is in places that Democrats may not have expected they would be having some trouble.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about New Hampshire. Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 . She's got a GOP challenger there, General Bolduc. "Newsweek" says this: a Republican victory in New Hampshire would be a significant upset.\par \par \uc2 Out in the northwest, deep blue territory, "Washington Beacon" reports on the Oregon race, the gubernatorial race. It says voters there are fed up with Democrats. They quote one named Terry, quote: I don't like the Democratic approach to anything that's happening.\par \par \uc2 She's been a Democratic voter, she says, for 40 years, only recently changed her party affiliation. That governor's race there could end with a Republican for first time in 40 years.\par \par \uc2 Why are you guys struggling in such deep blue territory?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, Shannon, first, the folks who I've been campaigning with are running on record. Chair McDaniel just said we're running away from it. But we're running on it, because we've got real accomplishment to point to.\par \par \uc2 You didn't hear a detailed plan for how Republicans would fight inflation, would bring gas prices down, would make our country stronger.\par \par \uc2 But we've got a record both of President Biden passing significant bipartisan legislation on issues President Trump said he would deal with like investing in infrastructure, like bringing advanced manufacturing for chips back to the United States, where the president signed bills into law that were bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 We also have bills that only Democrats voted for, our Inflation Reduction Act, which is going to cap out of pocket cost, cap price of insulin and reduce prescription drug prices. That is one of the things that Americans say day in and day out and have said for years is one of the pressing costs that they are concerned about. Democrats have taken action and addressed it.\par \par \uc2 And last, the American Rescue Plan that only Democrats voted for provided funding to put teachers back in classrooms, to put cops back on the streets.\par \par \uc2 We've got a real record to run on. I wish we had a debate on the Senate floor between the Democrats' Inflation Reduction Act and the Republicans, but we didn't, because only the Democrats put forward a concrete inflation reduction plan. We passed it, and it's having a real impact.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 COONS: That's why I'm confident that \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  will be reelected.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, long time Clinton pollster and strategist \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2  has a piece on FoxNews.com, that actually says this, those things are working against you. He says this: The inflation electorate will hold Biden and Democrats accountable at midterm. He says: Most Americans blame \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats for the problems we're facing today and the electoral impact of this issue is singularly powerful.\par \par \uc2 He says polling shows a majority of Americans actually think the federal spending in the trillions is actually hurting the economy and making inflation worse. They're worried about gas prices, grocery prices. This is stuff they experience everyday.\par \par \uc2 It's happened on your watch, the Democrats' watch for two years. So, why should they give you another two years of running the country when they're struggling at 40-year-highs with inflation?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, first, inflation is worse in the United Kingdom, in the \uc2 European Union\uc2  because of the ongoing impacts of the COVID pandemic and Russia's war of aggression against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Our inflation rates are lower. But that doesn't make a difference if what you're seeing at the pump is high price. Gas prices have come down $1.25 since the summertime. We are seeing progress in reducing gas prices. We've taken strong action to address the prices at the grocery store. Both the Department of Agriculture and bills that we've introduced in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have concrete plans and we have taken action to address the prices that I hear from voters they care about. Healthcare cost. And we've got candidates on the other side of the aisle who say they would work to repeal what the president just signed into law that will help with prescription drug prices. I think that's a pretty sharp contrast.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, a lot of that doesn't kick in for years from now. And voters are going to the polls now. Many of them - millions have already voted as they wait to see how those pieces of legislation play out.\par \par \uc2 "The Wall Street Journal" editorial board says, though, it's not just about the economy. They say this. Democrats are in trouble because they nominated too many candidates with views on climate, immigration, climate and the economy that are all but impossible to defend in competitive races this year.\par \par \uc2 How worried are you about how far the progressive element of your party has pulled to the left, how that's resonating with voters, potentially, and what it may cost you in the midterm?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, two things. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has a strong record on crime as president. He said in his State of the Union, I don't support defunding the police, I support funding the police, and then acted on it. The American Rescue Plan specifically provided funding for state and local government to keep law enforcement officers on the beat. And his plan for this year provides more money to hire more cops, 100,000 more police officers.\par \par \uc2 And here in Delaware, we have three police agencies that just got a federal grant, as have hundreds of others across the country to hire more officers.\par \par \uc2 Let's look just over the line in Pennsylvania, where Mehmet Oz and \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  are - are campaigning in the closing days of an important race. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , as mayor of Braddock, a small town outside of Pittsburgh, took on violent crime. He has tattoos on his arms of the dates when young men were killed before he became mayor. He spent years working to end violent crime in that town successfully.\par \par \uc2 So, why is this an issue in the campaign? Because tens of millions of dollars of dark money ads from anonymous voters have created a mistaken impression. President Biden supports police and supports funding the police. And \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  has a strong record of fighting at the most local level to combat gun violence in the town where he was elected mayor.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But you understand, though, how some potential voters out there don't love his record on releasing potentially violent felons.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Look, I understand that there have been misleading ads in this campaign that have created the impression that he somehow is part of a defund the police cabal. That's not true.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But he's said things that have led people to be that that's his policy.\par \par \uc2 COONS: \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is someone who is a Pennsylvanian at heart and how Pennsylvania voters know will stand for them.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, quickly, I want to play this, because there's been a lot of talk about election deniers, steeling elections, all of those things.\par \par \uc2 Here's what the former Democratic presidential nominee \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  said just days ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: Right wing extremists already have a plan to literally steal the next presidential election. And they're not making a secret of it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so take that, along with this headline from "Newsweek," Democrats spent $43 million helping election deniers win their primaries. If it backfires, the Democrats will have to shoulder some of the blame for helping those far-right candidates into office.\par \par \uc2 So, aren't Democrats now contributing to kind of this conversation about election denying, steeling elections, that you guys have said you condemn?\par \par \uc2 COONS: Well, Shannon, look, those are both things I'm unfamiliar with. I do know that there are folks in my party really concerned about the mechanics of elections, about folks running for office in states like Arizona and Nevada who deny that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won the 2020 presidential and who are running saying that they won't accept the result of an election with which they disagree.\par \par \uc2 I think all of us, in the wake of this attack on Paul Pelosi, need to say that we are going to stop demonizing folks because I think those kinds of - this sort of rhetoric that we've heard in too many ways, in too many places, can lead to violence by a small number of Americans who think that when we describe our political opponents as our enemies, we're calling for them to be attacked.\par \par \uc2 After the threat against Supreme Court Justice Kavanaugh, I worked with Senator Cornyn across the aisle to get signed into law a bill to expand police protection of Supreme Court justices and their families.\par \par \uc2 President Biden and Democrats have stood behind law enforcement for strengthening protections for those in public life. That's what I think we should be focusing on in this moment when leaders of both parties, but so far not former President Trump, have decried the attack on Paul Pelosi.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Senator Coons, we appreciate your time. Always great to talk with you.\par \par \uc2 COONS: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, the Elon Musk era officially begins at \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Is he opening a door for former President Trump to reemerge on the platform? We're going to tell you what the former president is saying and we're bringing our Sunday group on how \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's regime change could impact 2024.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: And we've seen how that goes.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), GEORGIA REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE: He said I'm a celebrity. Yes. He got that one wrong, didn't he? I'm not a celebrity. I'm that warrior for God.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Former President \uc2 Barak Obama\uc2  and Republican Senate Candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  trading shots about each other's fame as Walker tries to win in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 It is time now for our Sunday panel.\par \par \uc2 "USA Today" White House correspondent Francesca Chambers, former chief of staff to \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , Josh Holmes, former Maryland Congressman \uc2 John Delaney\uc2  and \uc2 George Washington University\uc2  law professor Jonathan Turley.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to all of you.\par \par \uc2 OK, let's start there. A lot of back and forth there. And, remember, Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , just days ago, caught on the tarmac saying that Georgia was - you know, they were having a tough time there. He says it's hard to believe they will go for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . That kind of echoes something that Speaker Pelosi said in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " days go. She said, part of this is, I cannot believe anybody would vote for these people.\par \par \uc2 John, does that show a disconnect with where the American people are?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DELANEY\uc2  (D-MD), FORMER CONGRESSMAN AND FORMER DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: No, I mean, listen, that's politics. I mean if you think about where we are right now, most people would have assumed the Democrats would be in a really bad spot if you look at the historical kind of situation, right? Since World War II, the president's party has lost on average of four Senate seats, 26 House seats. And this is a toss-up. And the Democrats, I think, are in a very good position to make a strong closing argument around the economy. And that's what they actually have to do because, we all see it, that's the number one issue on voter's minds.\par \par \uc2 I mean, they're concerned. They see low unemployment and rising wages, on one hand, which is good. But, on the other hand, they see this inflation and high interest rates, which is bad. What the Democrats have to do is lean into the things that they've done, the tangible things that the party has done since it moved to the middle, passed a law to lower drug prices, that's going to kick in, as you just said. That's going to make a difference. Passed a huge bipartisan infrastructure bill that's going to rebuild this country. It's going to be enormously popular, including delivering broadband to rural communities. Passed a piece of legislation that's going to bring a semiconductor industry back to the United States and contribute to the huge wave of kind of reshoring jobs.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I don't know if -\par \par \uc2 DELANEY: And putting the country in a position to lead the energy transition. These are things to run on. And the Democrats have to lean into it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I don't - and they're very wonky, though, when people are going to the grocery store and filling up their gas than and they don't see it yet, Josh. And one of the races that's very tight where this conversation is ongoing is up in New Hampshire. We just talked to Senator Coons about it.\par \par \uc2 Not long ago, the Senate Leadership Fund, the Senate PAC aligned with your former boss, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , pulled $5 million in remaining ad buys there. Is that a missed opportunity? I mean other money has flooded in, but it's so close, is that a missed opportunity at a pick-up?\par \par \uc2 JOSH HOLMES, FORMER CHIEF OF STAFF TO \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , CAVALRY PRESIDENT AND FOUNDING PARTNER AND "RUTHLESS PODCAST" CO-HOST: Well, I think - first of all they spent over $20 million over the previous four weeks, right? So the idea that they've sort of abandoned New Hampshire I think is -- is a little crazy. But you also saw a bunch of other Republican groups get involved there.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is, is that they're spending a ton of money in North Carolina, Ohio, Pennsylvania, Georgia, Arizona, Nevada, I mean, you name it, Colorado and Washington state. A lot of opportunities there for Republicans. And I think it just goes to show where these are all being played out.\par \par \uc2 I think Nevada, Georgia and Pennsylvania are sort of the core of the Senate argument at this point. And it doesn't look like they're going to lose any seats that they've held. Like, Pennsylvania is basically that turning point, right? But if you look at Nevada and Georgia, for example, prime opportunities for pick-ups, I think Republicans, at this point, are in - in -- heavily favored to take the Senate.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, and, Francesca, you've got a brand-new piece out this morning on one of the hot issue in the middle of all of this is abortion and how the GOP kind of coalesces around a position.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 " WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Right. And Republicans are kind of in this mode where they might be the party that wins in this election, but then what do they do with that majority? Is abortion something that they will take on? And you have Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , who's really tried to rally consensus around a 15-week abortion ban, but that's gotten luke warm support so far in the GOP. And you hear anti-abortion advocates saying that they need to come up with a position that not only lawmakers but also their 2024 presidential candidates can rally around.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, on that point, President Biden has also said, this is the NPR headline, he will push to codify Roe if Democrats keep the House and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 So, Professor, he talks about codifying Roe, but what had been offered up in the Senate was not Roe.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, GWU LAW PROFESSOR AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: No. What's being offered - what he was referring to, I believe, in terms of that legislation, goes way beyond Roe. In fact, Roe was premised on the idea that states could craft their own laws. It created a floor.\par \par \uc2 The bill in Congress is basically a wish list of what people wanted Roe to be, but it wasn't Roe. And so that legislation, if - if it is enacted, would not only federalize abortion, but it would go lightyears beyond Roe.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. We'll wait to see which party is in the - the drivers seat about either one of those.\par \par \uc2 OK, I want to talk about \uc2 Twitter\uc2  because Elon Musk has officially taken over. The questions are, who gets to come back on? But here is a tweet that he put out. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  will be forming a content moderation council with widely diverse viewpoints. No major content decisions or account reinstatements will happen before that council convenes.\par \par \uc2 Listen, a lot of people disappointed. They want to see a free for all. But he says this is not going to turn into a hellscape.\par \par \uc2 What happens with \uc2 Twitter\uc2  now?\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Well, look, I think the most important developments, it's not basically who's on or who's off \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . The most important development with Elon Musk's acquisition here is to try to push back on the modern left's idea of content moderation, period, right? We've seen over the - over recent years a big effort on the left to try to silence any sort of opinion that doesn't fit a conventional wisdom narrative, right? Covid really, really exposed a lot of this where you couldn't even basically provide any sort of opinion that went against conventional wisdom and then people were banned from the playoff all together. Clearly one of the most dangerous things you can do in a democracy like ours. I think that's the biggest component to the Elon Musk piece.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, but - but there were bad actors who immediately flooded in with tweets that we could never air on this show.\par \par \uc2 DELANEY: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So he's got a tough job.\par \par \uc2 DELANEY: And this is concerning. I mean, listen, he's owned it since Friday, so we shouldn't jump to any conclusions about what these -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll give him a couple days.\par \par \uc2 DELANEY: We'll give him a couple days. And he is a once in a generation entrepreneur.\par \par \uc2 But these issues are really important. These issues of disinformation, hate speech, conspiracy theories. You know, if you think about the Paul Pelosi situation, the escalation in political violence, the fact that it's undermined democracy, some of the - kind of the conspiracy theories that go on. And, importantly, how it's eroding the notion of expertise, because that's what this stuff does, it basically says there aren't (ph) facts. And it's hard to move the country forward unless you have base set of facts that the experts agree on. And that's why these issues are so important.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And - and, Professor, this is coming at a time when there's a call to cancel Justice Barrett's book because people don't like her vote.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Right. I mean why burn books when you can ban them. And that's basically what these authors and editors have said is that we shouldn't be publishing a book by Barrett. Why? Because they disagree with her jurisprudence, her legal views. And that's where we are now, where writes are opposing free speech. It's like athletes opposing exercise. This is an existential right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, panel, we could go on, Thank you all for being here.\par \par \uc2 I can't wait for the commercial.\par \par \uc2 All right, we'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll bring in law professor John Yoo on a handful of high-stakes legal battles playing out at the Supreme Court right now, including the fight over whether this sitting U.S. senator should have to testify before a Georgia grand jury and the two separate legal challenges on affirmative action headed to the high court tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: In just 24 hours, the Supreme Court will hear arguments on one of the nation's most politically divisive arguments, affirmative action. It's a decades long debate that could take a major new turn now that the high court has a 6-3 conservative majority.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we will bring in \uc2 UC Berkley\uc2  Law Professor John Yoo, who's part of a group that filed an amicus brief in one of the cases.\par \par \uc2 But first, a look at the case that could rewrite affirmative action policy in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Face your own racism and dishonesty, Harvard.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): It is the legal fight parents, and their high schoolers, will be watching very closely. Tomorrow, lawyers for Students for Fair Admissions will ask the high court to strike down a 2003 precedent that allows colleges to consider race as one of many factor in admission. The group was founded by 66-year-old conservative \uc2 Edward Blum\uc2 . He says Asian American students end up being discriminated against by being held to selectively higher standards than other minority groups.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD BLUM\uc2 , STUDENTS FOR FAIR ADMISSIONS FOUNDER: People involved in this movement are liberal, they're conservative, they're moderate, they're libertarian.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The schools, \uc2 Harvard University\uc2 , which is private, and the University of North Carolina, which is public, both deny discriminating and defend their application process saying they want to promote a robust, intellectually diverse campus. Student opinion is all over the map.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We're not against that someone should not be providing their racial information, but how is that going to be used is a big question?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Race cannot be removed effectively from an application just because it is so central to so many people's identity.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The suit claims both schools violated Title VI of the Civil Rights Act by penalizing Asian American applicants, engaging in racial balancing, overemphasizing race and rejecting workable race neutral alternatives.\par \par \uc2 Harvard's president says, quote, considering race is one factor among many in admission decisions produces a more diverse student body, which strengthens the learning environment for all. And UNC says it wants the court to continue to allow universities to pursue the educational benefits of diversity in a narrowly tailored way.\par \par \uc2 Nine states currently ban the use of race in the public university admission process, including California. The University of California system says, when a state ban on affirmative action went into effect in 1998, the proportion of black and Latino freshmen at UCLA dropped by roughly half.\par \par \uc2 SETH WAXAM, LAWYER FOR HARVARD: It would be a very, very sad day in history if this is the way forward, not the way that SFFA is proposing to take us.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM F. LEE, LAWYER FOR HARVARD: SFFA wants to eliminate a thousand African Americans and Hispanics from the Harvard campus.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The case challenges a 2003 Supreme Court precedent which upheld narrowly tailored and holistic race conscious affirmative action programs as long as schools don't base admissions decisions solely on race or a quota system.\par \par \uc2 Then Justice Sandra Day O'Conner, the deciding vote in that case, wrote, the court expects that 25 years from now the use of racial preferences will no longer be necessary to further the interest approved today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And yet that is the very argument that lands at the Supreme Court tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, \uc2 UC Berkley\uc2  Law Professor John Yoo.\par \par \uc2 John, welcome to FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 JOHN YOO, UNIVERSITY OF CALIFORNIA AT BERKLEY LAW PROFESSOR: Hi, Shannon. Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so the schools say they have not discriminate said against Asian students or anyone else. This is from Harvard's brief, it continues to study whether consideration of race is necessary to assemble a class comprising students of different background. No alternative is presently workable. And, John, they say, until it changes, Harvard needs to be able to use race in putting together a diverse freshman class.\par \par \uc2 YOO: Harvard and other institutions all claim they need to look at race to ensure diversity. But what the people on the other side, the Asian students here, white students, students of all colors want is for universities to join the rest of the country and the Constitution and the Declaration of Independence, and \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2 , and \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , in believing that the government should be color blind when it treats us as individual citizens.\par \par \uc2 Actually, the Supreme Court only allows the use of race now only for college admissions. It doesn't allow its use in national security, in policing, in hiring, giving out money, giving out contracts. Why should schools be able to consider us by our skin color rather than, as \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  said, on the content of our characters.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, tomorrow they take up that case.\par \par \uc2 Right now something pending before them on an emergency basis is whether or not Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  should have to testify before a Georgia grand jury that is investigating allegations of post-election interference in 2020. His team says he has constitutional immunities to this. But Fulton County, who is trying to get him to testify says, should the senator's application be granted, meaning that subpoena be blocked against him, the grand jury's work will be indefinitely delayed, ensuring that information which could either clear the innocent of suspicion or increase scrutiny on the guilty will continue to lie beyond the grand jury's grasp.\par \par \uc2 What is the Supreme Court going to do here?\par \par \uc2 YOO: Shannon, I'm glad you read out the line that the lower courts are trying to draw here because I think the media has somewhat exaggerated what's happening here. Senator Graham does not have to testify about things he was doing as part of his legitimate legislative activities, like as chairman of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 , trying to find out what Georgia's laws were about absentee ballots and counting, for example.\par \par \uc2 But what the lower courts are allowing -- I think the Supreme Court's going to let the lower courts continue to run the process was to say, well, maybe the prosecutor down in Georgia is allowed to ask about things Senator Graham was doing politically, like talking with the Trump White House, or whether he was trying to get the Georgia officials to change their rules or to cheat essentially. That according to the lower courts here is outside Senator Graham's legislative duties. That's more like acting like a - just a Republican politician.\par \par \uc2 I think the Supreme Court may not want to get into the thicket of exactly what questions are going to be asked and so on and leave it to the lower courts to handle.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But, quickly, do you think they would grant a stay while this plays out on the merits? What are the odds?\par \par \uc2 YOO: I think that's a good point, Shannon. They might just say, let's have a stay because Georgia doesn't seem to be in a rush, just so it can hear fuller oral arguments about this case, even if we do, in the end, allow the lower courts to run things.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right a big day at the Supreme Court, a big week as we stand by.\par \par \uc2 John Yoo, thank you so much for joining us. Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 YOO: Thanks, Shannon. It's great to be with you again.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, a couple of programming notes for you, including a big one about a town hall in one of the biggest battleground states. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Before we go, a couple of programming notes for you ahead of a major political week.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, you can join my college, Bill Hemmer, for an hour-long special ahead of Election Day, it's called "The Big Midterm Show." Bill brings together a panel of experts. They'll discuss all of the latest news and polls ahead of November 8th. That is tonight, 10:00 Eastern, right here on Fox News Channel.\par \par \uc2 And join Bret Baier and Martha MacCallum for a must-see town hall special on Tuesday night. They will be live in Ohio, one of the most heated races. Voters there will get to ask questions of both Democratic Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and Republican JD Vance. That will be on Fox News Channel live at 6:00 p.m. Eastern. You don't want to miss it. The Senate could hang in the balance there.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, keep it here with FOX NEWS SUNDAY next week. We're working on a massive midterm special from Fox News Election Headquarters in New York.\par \par \uc2 That is it for this Sunday morning. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a great week and we'll see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020221030eiau00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Husband Of Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  Assaulted At California Home; \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  Performance Has Democrats On Edge; Former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  Campaigns For Georgia Democrats; Biden Defends Economy While Bashing Republicans; Federal Reserve Faces Calls To Pause Rate Hikes; Harris Focuses On Abortion Amid Economic Concerns; GOP Makes Last Minute Investment In Bolduc; Hassan Turns Attention To Abortion In New Hampshire; Justices To Decide Race Criteria In College Admissions; Supreme Court To Hear Case Concerning Affirmative Action In University Enrollment; Supreme Court May Rule On Petition By Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  To Block Subpoena For Testimony On Election Interference In Georgia; \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  's Husband \uc2 Paul Pelosi\uc2  Undergoes Surgery After Attack\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Steve Harrigan, Bryan Llenas, Jonathan Hunt, Jacqui  Heinrich, Mark Meredith, David Spunt, Shannon Bream, Kelly O'Grady \par \uc2 7,999 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GERALDO RIVERA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT-AT-LARGE: Yes, one more view, so you're critically OK? I mean, you probably agree.\par \par \uc2 TYRUS MURDOCH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Listen, I don't know. I haven't been, you know, on the service for two centuries covering it. I'm just the guy. Just, I'll take them when I get them.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Just a guy who can't say no.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right. Thanks, everybody for being with us. Have a great weekend. We'll see you back here on Monday.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening. Welcome to Washington, I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, the House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  's husband has just emerged from what doctors say was a successful surgery for a skull fracture and other serious injuries after a brutal beating at the couple's home in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 Authorities say the attack on \uc2 Paul Pelosi\uc2  came as his assailant shouted where's Nancy? Speaker was not home. She was here in Washington, as was her security detail. A suspect is in custody facing numerous charges.\par \par \uc2 Chief Correspondent Jonathan Hunt has all the late breaking details from our West Coast newsroom tonight. Good evening, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Speaker Pelosi's office just released that statement confirming what they call the successful surgery on 82-year-old \uc2 Paul Pelosi\uc2  and saying his doctors at \uc2 San Francisco General Hospital\uc2  expect to a full recovery following the violent attack.\par \par \uc2 It was 2:27 this morning when police were alerted to an incident at the Pelosi home in San Francisco. Officers apparently arriving so quickly that the assault on Mr. Pelosi was happening as they got there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM SCOTT, SAN FRANCISCO POLICE CHIEF: Our officers observed Miss Pelosi and the suspect both holding a hammer. The suspect pulled the hammer away from Miss Pelosi and violently assaulted him with it. Our officers immediately tackled the suspect, disarmed him, took him into custody.\par \par \uc2 HUNT (voice over): It's not clear from the police chief's comments who was initially holding the hammer. Whether the suspect brought it into the house or whether Mr. Pelosi had perhaps grabbed it for self-defense.\par \par \uc2 Police named the suspect as 42-year-old David Depape. He has been charged with attempted murder and assault with a deadly weapon.\par \par \uc2 Law enforcement sources tell Fox News Depape was shouting: Where's Nancy? Where's Nancy? They say they don't know his motivation or whether he was having some sort of mental health crisis.\par \par \uc2 Depape appears to have followed a range of conspiracy theories over many years, involving everything from 9/11 to the COVID pandemic.\par \par \uc2 And according to the reporting of the San Francisco Chronicle, in 2013, was then a hemp jewelry maker who lived with a group of well-known nude activists.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT (on camera): Whatever the motivation, the attack has been condemned from both sides of the political divide with many on Capitol Hill reiterating what perilous political times we are now facing as we approach the midterm elections, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jonathan, thank you. Statements from Republicans and Democrats all day today. We'll continue to follow it.\par \par \uc2 Currently, there is no protection offered to families of congressional members.\par \par \uc2 In June, the House passed a Senate bill to provide security for family members of Supreme Court justices and senior officers of the court. The vote was 396 to 27. The no votes came from Democrats who wanted the bill to be expanded to staff such as clerks. It did not cover members of Congress or their families.\par \par \uc2 This year, the House's sergeant of arms did give House members $10,000 to install security systems in their homes. But there have been many, many threats to members of Congress over the past couple of years.\par \par \uc2 Let's get some analysis on this from former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Special Agent Maureen O'Connell. She joins us from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Maureen, thanks for joining us. You know, you hear the details as we know them now, not a lot from authorities as of yet. What are your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 MAUREEN O'CONNELL, FORMER FBI SPECIAL AGENT: My thoughts are that \uc2 Paul Pelosi\uc2  called -- first of all, it's just an outrage. And I wish him and his wife well. I mean, this is just a horrible, horrible attack. And there's nothing worse really than being awakened in your own home with a violent offender. So many people in this country are experiencing that right now.\par \par \uc2 But my guess is that the breaking of the glass and that door is probably what prompted Mr. Pelosi to in fact call the police. And I have heard reports that the offender actually was in his underwear.\par \par \uc2 So, when police officers arrived, there probably was a moment of confusion, although we don't know what the offender looks like at all right now. But there might have been some confusion as to who the homeowner was, because in the middle of the night, you know, it wouldn't be unusual to find the homeowner in their -- in their underwear.\par \par \uc2 So, there may have been some confusion there. And I just think the law enforcement officers did a fabulous, unbelievable job in getting in there, answering the call, running toward danger and taking the violent offender down.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Are you surprised by the security scenario? Obviously, the speaker has her own security detail and that was with her here in Washington D.C. but he's the husband of the second in line to the presidency and how somebody gets in and how that all happens.\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: I am surprised because I own my own small security company and I do threat assessments on houses, residences, businesses all the time. And we find so many simple things that can be done to really increase the efficacy of the system that's in place at the time.\par \par \uc2 Oftentimes, there's just a million things that can go wrong. And it's really good to get a checkup on a regular basis for your system to check all the -- something as simple as the throw on your lock.\par \par \uc2 You know, some people have cheap locks where the throw only goes in, you know, a fraction of a -- of an inch as opposed to a nice big lock with a four inch throw that can really stop someone from getting in. That wouldn't be the case in this instance. But there are a lot of things that can be done to really fortify your residence and to do it in a very cost effective manner.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And last thing, we don't know a ton about this guy. You heard the report earlier from Jonathan Hunt, but the social media profile has some posts that he posted on January 6, then on the election, on COVID. What do you make of this just seeing what you see right now?\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: He just sounds like he's unhinged. I mean, women were afraid of him in his past. He's -- he just seems to have a lot of -- obviously, he's got a lot of issues and a lot of problems and the first responders and paramedics and law enforcement officers, they're the front lines and they see these issues, and they want to take these people into 72 hour lockups to be evaluated and stuff.\par \par \uc2 But all that has gone out the window, when you make everything a misdemeanor, there's no way that you don't give them any teeth to hold them -- to hold them in custody and get them properly evaluated. One of the big problems we're seeing right now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, well, we'll follow all of this and Maureen, we appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 O'CONNELL: Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In tonight's midterm spotlight, President Biden and Vice President Harris are in Philadelphia at this hour. They will make remarks shortly at a dinner for Pennsylvania Democrats, just a week and a half before the crucial midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Their party's hold on Congress appears in jeopardy tonight with Republicans projected to make pretty big gains in the house possibly with enough wins in the Senate to flip the chamber.\par \par \uc2 We have Fox team coverage. Steve Harrigan is in College Park, Georgia where former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  is speaking at a rally for Democratic senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  . Well, we start with Correspondent Bryan Llenas in Philadelphia tonight. Good evening, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening. Democrats could reportedly raise a million dollars at tonight's fundraiser at the Pennsylvania Convention Center behind me but as Democrats gather inside, outside the venue, a mobile billboard hired by Dr. Oz's campaign is replaying Tuesday night's debate, a debate some Democrats are trying to forget.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2  , FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Democrats are kind of worried about where this is going right now. And it's felt worse over the last week or so.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Tonight, Democrats are still assessing the damage caused by Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  's Senate debate performance on Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Yes, the debate performance is coming up a little bit in the Philly suburbs.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Fetterman and his Republican opponent Dr. Mehmet Oz now trying to win over the suburbs with moderate messages.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF PENNSYLVANIA: I believe immigration is what makes America America. And I believe it needs to be comprehensive, compassionate and bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF PENNSYLVANIA: Washington gets it wrong for a bunch of reasons but in particular because of some of the extreme positions that are taken.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: But with their backs against the wall, Democrats are now turning to the White House for help. Tonight, President Biden and Vice President Harris will headline a state Democratic dinner in Philadelphia, where Fetterman is expected to speak.\par \par \uc2 It's Biden's reported 21st visit to Pennsylvania spending more time here than any battleground state this cycle.\par \par \uc2 The president is hitting the trail despite a 36 percent approval rating in the Commonwealth according to the latest Franklin and Marshall College Poll.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are also hoping Fetterman can get a boost from Democratic Pennsylvania gubernatorial candidate \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  . The moderate is ahead 22 points in his race and he and Fetterman are expected to campaign together more.\par \par \uc2 But Republicans sense momentum as Dr. Oz tries to seize on the issue of Philadelphia surging crime. A new video from 2018 shows Fetterman praising controversial Philadelphia District Attorney \uc2 Larry Krasner\uc2  , who's facing impeachment over his progressive policies.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I really have been enjoying watching \uc2 Larry Krasner\uc2  , your new district attorney. Just take some common sense approaches like reducing or eliminating cash bail on a lot of different offenses.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): Tonight is game one of the World Series starring the \uc2 Philadelphia Phillies\uc2  . Republicans are spending some $700,000 on new ads targeted to Philly fans.\par \par \uc2 And now over to my colleague Steve Harrigan in Georgia where former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  is campaigning ahead of his reported trip to Pennsylvania next week, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hundreds of people have been outside for hours waiting for the appearance of former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  . Many people say it's clear why he's coming and not President Biden.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (voice over): Energy and turnout are what Georgia Democrats hoped for from former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  tonight, who is stumping for gubernatorial candidate \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  and Senate candidate \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 It's likely the former president will frame the election as a battle against Republicans who support what he says are extreme positions on gun laws and abortions.\par \par \uc2 Abrams echoes the emphasis on the abortion issue on the campaign trail, hitting her opponent in this rematch election, Republican Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D), GUBERNATORIAL NOMINEE FOR GEORGIA: He broke the promise that we hold in this country that we have the right to control our bodies and control our future. So, I'm here to tell you, it may have taken a man to break that promise, it's going to take a woman to put it right.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Kemp is focusing on the economy, trying to link Abrams with President Biden in campaign ads.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  backs \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  totally, unquestioningly. She sucked up to Biden. She backed his economic agenda.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Once a reliably red state Georgia is now divided. A RealClearPolitics poll shows Kemp leading Abrams while in the Senate race, former football star and first time Republican candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has a slight edge over Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Republican analysts say there's a good reason why it's \uc2 Obama\uc2  and not President Biden campaigning in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN ROBINSON, FORMER GOVERNOR NATHAN DEAL SPOKESMAN: They can't use the president because he's got terrible approval ratings here in Georgia. This is the best shot but as bad news for Democrats that this juncture, they're not fighting for the suburbs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN (on camera): Early turnout here is at a record pace. One in five Georgia voters have already cast their ballot, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Steve Harrigan in College Park, Georgia. Steve, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the tug of war over which party can get the U.S. economy back on track, we'll have a report from the White House.\par \par \uc2 And later, the ever changing forecast for the midterm elections. Why Republicans are pumping money into one northeast Senate race?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Higher than expected profits by \uc2 Apple\uc2  and other companies had Wall Street investors in a buying mood. The Dow surging 829. The S&P 500 gained 94. The NASDAQ jumped 310.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow was up 5-3/4 percentage points. The S&P gained almost four. The NASDAQ finished ahead 2-1/4.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Democrats are expressing some real concern tonight about the possibility and probably likelihood of another major interest rate hike before Election Day.\par \par \uc2 But as inflation continues to rock the U.S. economy, it's unclear whether the Fed will be able to achieve a soft landing, or if we, as a country, will tip into a recession. Either way, it could be a bumpy ride.\par \par \uc2 Here is White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Prices are still rising and consumers are still spending despite glimmers of progress in two economic reports released Friday, the president's biggest challenge, inflation remains.\par \par \uc2 Biden turning the focus to the GOP, writing, "The economic reports show we are making progress. Congressional Republicans agenda, on the other hand is very different. It would drive up inflation and add to the deficit."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GENE SPERLING, SENIOR WHITE HOUSE ADVISOR: Wed all like to see inflation coming down quicker.\par \par \uc2 VICTOR BLACKWELL, ANCHOR, CNN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SPERLING: But I think there are signs, you know, even this week, the numbers you're showing were either at expectations, or below expectations.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: A metric closely watched by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  showed inflation stayed hot in September, but mostly within expectations.\par \par \uc2 Personal consumption expenditures climbed 6.2 percent compared to last year. Stripping out volatile food, and energy, core PCE inflation increased 5.1 percent from the year prior.\par \par \uc2 Separately, the employment cost index showed wages and benefits climbed 1.2 percent from June to September.\par \par \uc2 A quicker pace than the Fed wants. No sign the labor market is loosening and rapid wage growth is moderating. All this, meaning, the Fed will likely stay on course for another interest rate hike next week.\par \par \uc2 STEVE MOORE, LAW ENFORCEMENT ANALYST, CNN: Wages up five percent? But inflation up 0.03 percent. Do the math there, people are getting poor.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: More congressional Democrats now urging the Fed to pause its aggressive campaign to tamp down prices, seeing warning signs ahead. \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  saying, "The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  was charged with the dual mandate of promoting maximum employment, stable prices, and moderate long- term interest rates in the U.S. economy. For working Americans who already feel the crush of inflation, job losses will make it much worse."\par \par \uc2 But the Fed is not supposed to take cues from politicians and the treasury secretary says the administration is doing what it can to supplement the Central Bank's actions.\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2  , UNITED STATES TREASURY SECRETARY: I don't see signs of a recession in this economy at this point.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): While the president has recently shifted his focus of his messaging away from abortion and onto inflation, the vice president is still all in on abortion.\par \par \uc2 Today, she hosted a reproductive rights roundtable, her 20th since May, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  , CDC, says we are experiencing the most severe flu season since 2009.\par \par \uc2 With at least 880,000 cases of lab confirmed influenza illness, 6,900 hospitalizations, and 360 flu related deaths so far nationally.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll go to the touchscreen, tell you where things stand just a week and a half before the big midterms.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. Ukrainian authorities announced further rolling blackouts in and around country's largest cities amid ongoing Russian strikes, targeting energy infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 The mayor of Kyiv says the city's power grid is operating in emergency mode, with electricity supplies down by as much as half compared to pre-war levels.\par \par \uc2 North Korea fires to short range ballistic missiles toward the sea. It's the first ballistic weapons launches in two weeks, comes as the U.S. military warns the north, the use of nuclear weapons, "will result in the end of that regime".\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Amsterdam, one of the big stories in the Netherlands tonight. The classic painting, Girl with a Pearl Earring goes back on display at the Mauritshuis museum in the Netherlands.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, climate activist targeted the 17th century masterpiece. One, gluing himself to the painting.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back. Tonight's 11 days from Election Day in the "MIDTERM WATCH". Take a look at the balance of power in the House. 220 to 211. A couple of vacancies. Republicans only have to pick up a net gain of five.\par \par \uc2 We're projecting a net gain of 16. But it could be bigger, depending on races that are really closing at the end. One of them is up in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 Karoline Leavitt, the Republican against Congressman \uc2 Chris Pappas\uc2  is closer than anybody thought it was going to be, and she could pull off an upset.\par \par \uc2 Now, let's take at a look at the balance of power in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  . 50-50. The vice president breaking the ties, so, control goes to the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 And if you add the toss ups in yellow and the lean Democrat in light blue, lean Republican in pink, there is a chance -- there's a good chance that the Republicans could run the table enough to pick up a control.\par \par \uc2 One of the reasons that's happening is a place we just were, New Hampshire. And this race with the incumbent \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  against General Don Bolduc, it is closer than anybody thought it was going to be at this point. And Democrats are pumping money to try to save this incumbent senator.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Mark Meredith has the story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DON BOLDUC (R-NH), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: Thank you very much. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Once considered a long shot, Republicans are now investing more time, money, and resources in New Hampshire, where candidate Don Buldoc is trying to unseat Democratic senator, \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 BOLDUC: Career politicians who focus on special interests and lobbyist. That must change.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: This week, the National Republican Senatorial Committee, which works to elect GOP senate candidates --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: She voted for inflation --\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Announced it will spend at least a million dollars on last minute ads to boost Bolduc.\par \par \uc2 The move comes only days after other GOP groups chose to scale back spending on the race. NRSC Chairman \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  , who visits New Hampshire Sunday writes, "Our polling, along with recent public polling, shows that this race is in the margin of error and winnable. The NRSC is proud to stand with General Buldoc."\par \par \uc2 Hassan, who won her Senate seat in 2016, by roughly 1,000 votes is urging New Hampshire voters to focus on abortion, when casting their ballot.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MAGGIE HASSAN\uc2  (D-NH), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: And I think it is very, very concerning that people don't trust women and their doctors to do to make these very complex and often tragic decisions.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Tomorrow, Hassan will campaign on the issue with First Lady Jill Biden in Manchester and Portsmouth.\par \par \uc2 And while Democrats lead in fundraising, political experts say they're not surprised the race is tightening.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: New Hampshire in midterms is historically volatile and swings a lot in midterms. I've seen it happen to Democrats. I've seen it happen to Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Bolduc may also see a boost on Election Day. Thanks to Republican Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  . He chose not to run for this Senate seat. And right now, he is seeking a fourth term in the governor's mansion. Polls also show him dominating in his race. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  will hear arguments Monday in two cases that could result in a major change to affirmative action policy.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt takes a look tonight from the Court.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): The nine justices will hear two separate cases that may overturn decades of precedent.\par \par \uc2 Schools began implementing affirmative action in the admissions process in the 1960s to promote campus diversity.\par \par \uc2 Now, a coalition of Asian American students is suing both \uc2 Harvard University\uc2  and the University of North Carolina, arguing the admission's criteria unfairly singles the mouth, compare to black and Hispanic students.\par \par \uc2 CALVIN YANG, HARVARD UNIVERSITY APPLICANT: I think the fact that I'm Asian American plays a pretty big factor into why I was rejected.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: The high court has traditionally upheld racial preferences but only in a narrowly tailored way that does not involve quotas. This issue could set up a clash between newest Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  and Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  . Jackson has signaled in other contexts that the Constitution supports race-based governmental policies. But Roberts said in a 2007 case that, quote, "The way to stop discrimination on the basis of race is to stop discriminating on the basis of race."\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WYDRA, CONSTITUTIONAL ACCOUNTABILITY CENTER: There is yet again, as we saw in the abortion case last term, a request for the Supreme Court to ignore precedent and to have a rather dramatic legal change that will affect the lives of young people and, frankly, all of society.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: These nine states currently ban affirmative action in the public college admissions process.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS DUPREE, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: I think the question is whether the court is poised to actually overrule its prior precedent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): Two people to watch during arguments on Monday, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  , the second black justice on the court, has opposed the use of Affirmative Action. \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  , the first Hispanic justice, is in favor of it. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge is refusing to bar a group from monitoring outdoor ballot boxes in Arizona's largest county where watchers have shown up armed and in ballistic vests at the so-called drop boxes. The judge says doing so could violate the monitors' constitutional rights. He says the case remains open though, and that the Arizona Alliance for Retired Americans could try again to make its argument against a group calling itself Clean Elections USA.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in FOX News chief legal correspondent, anchor of FOX NEWS SUNDAY, Shannon Bream. Good to see you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What's happening with the court? What are we waiting on that's coming out?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: At any moment we could actually get a ruling on this emergency request from Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  . He petitioned the court to block this lower court subpoena out of Georgia. The investigation there, allegations of postelection tampering in the 2020 presidential election. He has been fighting it all on numerous grounds, all the paperwork the two sides have filed now. So we literally wait to see. At any moment we could hear from the justices about whether they will get involved in his attempt not to have to testify.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow that. This horrible case about \uc2 Paul Pelosi\uc2 , we're told he's recovering from surgery from a skull fracture in this attack. It does hearken back to the threats that not only Capitol members get but Supreme Court justices and \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: His cases, there was still something ongoing this week with the accused who was accused of attempting to murder a Supreme Court justice, and a lot of people may not realize that the demonstrations still continue at the justices' homes. On every night of the week just about they go to a different home, and it's still on a weekly basis. So there is a lot of growing pressure and questions to the DOJ about why they are not enforcing this federal statute that seems to be very clear about people protesting or trying to pressure judges outside their homes. So far no arrests at the federal level.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congress passes a law that provides security for Supreme Court justices families, but you wonder whether this incident and other threats is going to expand their effort to protects Congressmembers and senators.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You've got over 500 of them, and leadership, as you know and our viewers will know, they have special protection. But average family members and average members of Congress absent a specific threat aren't going to get that level of extra protection.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This weekend, who do you have?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We've got \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  with us, the \uc2 RNC\uc2  chairwoman, to talk about why are some of these race as lot tighter than they expected it to be when it was a red tsunami prediction in the spring? We've got two pollsters from either side of the aisle to weed through what can we trust, what can we not trust? Are there really hidden voters out there? And Senator Chris Coons is going to join us as well to make the Democrats' final closing case.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The countdown is on.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll see you Sunday.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Up next, it did not take long for \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  to start shaking things up at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  . We'll have a live report.\par \par \uc2 But first, as we go to break, Jerry Lee Lewis has died. The rock and roll pioneer who called himself "The Killer" had hits such as "Great Balls of Fire," "A Whole Lot of Shaking Going On." Lewis had a tumultuous personal life. He was married seven times. Jerry Lee Lewis was 87.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One of America's most famous celebrity couples is breaking up officially. Tom Brady and Gisele Bundchen have announced they are ending their 13-year marriage. The football legend and fashion model superstar say they have amicably reached that decision. Both say their priority is with their children and are asking for privacy.\par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  's on-again, off-again takeover of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is on again and complete. Musk is making the social media giant a private company, at least for now, and very much in his own maverick image. FOX Business correspondent Kelly O'Grady has details tonight from Los Angeles. Good evening, Kelly.\par \par \uc2 KELLY O'GRADY, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening to you, Bret. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is officially the new owner of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  . The billionaire wasted no time in making changes to the platform. Musk has reportedly let go \uc2 Parag Agrawal\uc2  as well as the CFO and head of Legal Policy, Trust, and Safety, \uc2 Vijaya Gadde\uc2  . Gadde led the team responsible for removing President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  from the platform and censoring the "New York Post" Hunter Biden laptop story. Here is how she framed content moderation policies in 2019.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VIJAYA GADDE\uc2  , FORMER TWITTER EXECUTIVE: I personally, and this isn't my job to run the policy team, I believe everyone has a voice and should be able to use it, and I want them to be able to use it online. Now, where we draw a line is when people use their voice and use their platform to abuse and harass other people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O'GRADY: But what critics call a lack of transparency is behind Musk's quest to turn \uc2 Twitter\uc2  into a new digital town square. He clarified his first steps towards the future, tweeting, quote, "\uc2 Twitter\uc2  will be forming a content moderation council with wildly diverse viewpoints, no major content decisions or account reinstatements will happen before that council convenes."\par \par \uc2 But that has not stopped advertisers from questioning the takeover. \uc2 General Motors\uc2  confirming to FOX Business it has temporarily paused advertising until it better understand the testimonies decision. Many progressive critics are also concerned he will let former president Trump back on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  as the billionaire has previously shared he is against permanent bans. For his part, Trump is happy \uc2 Twitter\uc2  , quote, is in sane hands, as he put it, but tells FOX digital, quote, "I am staying on Truth. I like it better. I like the way it works. I like Elon, but I'm staying on Truth."\par \par \uc2 So the story is developing by the hour but it is quickly becoming a philosophical debate around what freedom of speech looks like and what role social media should play in determining it. Back to you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kelly, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the Friday Lightning Round, Candidate Casino, plus winners and losers with the panel.\par \par \uc2 First here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. WSVN in Miami as residents of a building on the same street where that condominium complex collapsed killing nearly 100 people are forced to evacuate after officials determined that structure is unsafe. Local TV stations report residents at the Port Royal say city officials informed them Wednesday that the building would need to be evacuated, but the notice to leave immediately was not delivered until Thursday.\par \par \uc2 FOX 32 in Tulsa as the deaths of eight people, including six children found in a burning home, are being investigated as a murder-suicide. Authorities say both adults are considered suspects. The causes of death are still under investigation, but the local fire chief says it does not appear anyone died because of that fire.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Minute Maid Park in Houston, Texas, from FOX 26. The big story there tonight, of course, game one of the World Series as the hometown Astros host the \uc2 Philadelphia Phillies\uc2  . You can see game one on FOX broadcast network. Pregame coverage starts at 7:00 eastern. So you can tape Jesse, watch that. You can watch whatever.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. PATRICK LEAHY, (D-VT): That's a terrible thing, and forever we tease about my mother's first generation Italian American, and so we talk about the -- call ourselves the Italians here.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  , (R-NY): Regardless of what branch of government you are, what your party is, what your ideology is, there is just no room for any of this type of violence or even threats of violence as part of the process.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Still learning information about this attack on \uc2 Paul Pelosi\uc2 , the husband of the Speaker of the House at their home in San Francisco. He, we're told, is out of surgery, a skull fracture. We're told the House Speaker is expected to be there at some point.\par \par \uc2 There is outpouring of support from both sides of the aisle today. Leader Kevin McCarthy's office put out a statement, saying "Leader McCarthy reached out to the Speaker to check in on Paul and said he is praying for a full recovery and is thankful they caught the assailant."\par \par \uc2 There's a lot we don't know about this thing yet, but let's bring in our panel, Philip Wegmann, White House correspondent for Real Clear Politics, \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2  , senior political correspondent for "Axios", and Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist."\par \par \uc2 Mollie, we know what the authorities have told us. We have heard some reporting and done our own about this guy's social media and digital footprint, but there's a lot that we don't know.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": I thought the former \uc2 FBI\uc2  special agent that you had on talking about it said it well when she said clearly this man does not seem to be doing well mentally. So we know that the alleged assailant does have this history and he does not seem to be the most balanced person out there.\par \par \uc2 There are a lot of questions like you would hope that the Speaker of the House would have much better security that wouldn't be able to be taken advantage of this way. And who knows what's all in play here. So I think a lot of people are eager to find out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Phil, that is a great question, and it's probably going to spur a lot more questions about family members not only, we talked about Supreme Court justices, but members of Congress.\par \par \uc2 PHILIP WEGMANN, REAL CLEAR POLITICS: Yes, absolutely. Not only is this conversation going to be happening in context of what currently happened, and the only response to political violence is to condemn it, and luckily that's what we have seen this far. But there's going to be a rewinding of the tape and a conversation about what happened with Supreme Court justices over the summer.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And still the protests are happening outside their houses, as Shannon mentioned. Josh?\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2  , "NATIONAL JOURNAL": There's just a growing worry about political violence across the extremes in our country. And look, I think we're going to learn more about who this guy is and what he was thinking, but we're hoping for a bipartisan condemnation of any political violence of any type. And I think what we're going to learn is San Francisco also has a lot of disorder in the city, and the police, were there enough police and law enforcement on the ground? Did they respond in time? San Francisco lives up to the stereotype and the reality that a lot of folks are facing in that city, and we will learn a lot more about what the police were active on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It sounds right now that the police were there very quickly, and we don't know the exact timeline, but it sounds like they acted really quickly, and so did the 911 operator.\par \par \uc2 OK, meantime the midterms just 11 days away. Here's the president and some reaction to what Democrats may be facing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The main driver of food prices is not the price of beef and eggs, et cetera, while they're up. It's packaged goods, packaged goods. You are going to see people not buying Kellogg's Raisin Bran. You're going to see them buying another raisin bran.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: When you can't afford to buy the stuff that you were able to buy a year or two or three years ago, it's tough, tough sledding.\par \par \uc2 I am really scared about a bloodbath 10 days from now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If you look at the RCP average of recent polls on the congressional generic ballot, now Republicans are up in that almost three points. Phil, how do you see it? How are you reading it from the people you are talking to?\par \par \uc2 WEGMANN: Currently the White House is reading the same polls as the rest of us. And that's why I think it's so interesting that the one state that the president is going to be going to now is Pennsylvania. And that clearly reflects the fact that they think it's worth the -- the risk is worth the reward there. The president is going to Pennsylvania rather than going to, say, Ohio, to stump for Representative \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  or Nevada for Senator \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  . I think what that reflects is that as they look at this polling, they are realizing that perhaps Pennsylvania might be one of the rare bright spots where the president can do some good for his party --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He has been there a lot, and he's traveling with the former president who is a big draw for some of these campaigns that are in need of that.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Really noteworthy how much President \uc2 Obama\uc2  is on the trail versus President Biden who had earlier promised that he would be doing quite a bit of campaigning, and yet has a very light schedule. We're just a few -- a week-and-a-half out ahead of the election. He has a very light schedule this weekend. This is not where Democrats hoped to be. And I think they are hoping that with Pennsylvania at least, that the situation is not as dire as you would think it would be after that disastrous debate, because they know that so many people voted before people got a chance to see Fetterman on stage, and they are hoping that that will be enough to carry them through.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Josh, we just got back from Georgia. If you look at that Senate race, the latest polls show \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  kind of surging. And that's -- there is at least one poll after the newest revelations that we talked about the other day on this show. Just got word during the show that the former Georgia coach Vince Dooley has passed away. He is -- and one of the last things we pulled this ad was an ad for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  . And take a listen to a piece of that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VINCE DOOLEY, FORMER FOOTBALL COACH: He's a real patriot of this country. He loves the United States of America. He also is realistic that he can be better. He is not a, quote, politician. There is a need in this country, there is a need in this state for somebody like Herschel.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He has, obviously, a lot of support from people who remember his days at the University of Georgia, and yet, facing all of these challenges, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  , is that changing the dynamic?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Well, look. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is a political legend, and a lot of the attacks have kind of blown off him like Teflon. He certainly has momentum in the Georgia Senate race. And a lot of it is because of the political environment. We are looking at a growing likelihood that this could be a pretty big wave coming ashore. And when you have wave election, Bret, it means that even candidates that may not have run the best campaigns can sometimes finish and get the majority. So Walker has had his bumps and bruises along the way, but polling has been encouraging for the Walker campaign. You heard \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  tell \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on that hot mic moment that Walker has actually got some momentum in the Georgia Senate race. And that is a race that could determine the majority. If Republicans win with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  Georgia, they have momentum in Pennsylvania with Dr. Oz. That's how you get to 51 Senate seats.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. I want to talk quickly about \uc2 Twitter\uc2  , \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  tweeting out, "\uc2 Twitter\uc2  will be forming a content moderation council with widely diverse viewpoints. No major content decisions or account reinstatements will happen before that council convenience." I guess, answering, Mollie, all the people who would like to see some people reinstated to \uc2 Twitter\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: This is a huge issue because \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has done so much to interfere in our elections by suppressing content, de-platforming people, elevating opinions that he had like. They may have a large, allegedly have a large army of bots that are deployed to manufacture opinions. So people are hoping for some relief from this, because a free election requires free debate, and we don't have that because of our social media companies. But at least it will happen at some point, hopefully.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, I want to take a live look, actually, at the San Francisco hospital where \uc2 Paul Pelosi\uc2  is. Again, we said that he is recovering from skull fracture surgery. We are told that the Speaker of the House has just pulled up there, and is -- has landed in San Francisco. Her motorcade is getting there to hospital as you look at that aerial and going to her husband's side. So, we heard word that the motorcade was coming there, and he is in recovery there at that hospital, as we continue to wait for word from authorities about more details of that attack. I wanted to bring you that live shot.\par \par \uc2 OK, we have this thing on Fridays where we go to this place called Candidate Casino. And who wins control of the Senate? OK, Phil, I know you haven't done this before. You've got $100 in chips, you have to place the chips. How do you place them?\par \par \uc2 WEGMANN: Real Clear Politics average has the Republicans winning the upper chamber, picking up Arizona and Nevada, and then Georgia after the runoff. So I'm putting 60 bucks on the GOP, 40 on Democrats.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, all right. I like the RCP name drop. OK, Mollie?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: I'm feeling bullish tonight for Republicans. I'm going to go ahead and put $80 on them controlling, with an understanding that things could break any way, so $20 for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Josh.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: I'm with Phil. I've got $60 on the Republicans taking back the Senate, a little more than I have had in the last few weeks. And the big reason is Arizona has moved to more of a toss-up race than where it was before, and that gives so many paths for Republicans to get that one seat.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In "Tuesday Tweets" I promised I would do another round of Candidate Casino on the World Series. OK, you got $100 in chips. Josh?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Give me $65 on the \uc2 Houston Astros\uc2  , and partly that's because I'm rooting for Dusty Baker. This would be his first, if he wins the World Series would be the first time he has won a World Series after winning over 2,000 games. So I know the Astros are cheaters, I know there are some problems there, but I want Dusty Baker to get it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Mollie?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: My grandfather told me to always root for the National League, so I'll go ahead and put it all on the \uc2 Phillies\uc2  . But I'm actually not watching baseball because I'm still upset about what they did meddling in Georgia politics last year.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We rarely see the black chip deployed, so that's good to see. All right?\par \par \uc2 WEGMANN: Astros-Phillies, I couldn't imagine a more boring World Series, so I'm taking all of my chips and going home and waiting for spring training.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That does not help the FOX broadcast network.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're going to talk about that after the show. Thanks, panel. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 It's Friday, and you know what that means. It's time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Happy birthday to a great president.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: High turnout and voter suppression can take place at the same time.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 BRIAN KEMP\uc2  , (R) GEORGIA: Nobody thinks that is even remotely makes sense.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gloria Allred came forward with this client. You are saying you do end know this person?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  , (R-GA) U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: What I'm saying is this is a lie.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  , (D) PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: I do support fracking, and I don't, I don't -- I support fracking, and I stand, and I do support fracking.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ, (R) PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: I never sold weigh loss products as described in those commercials. It's a television show like this is a television show.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 CHARLIE CRIST\uc2  , (D-FL) FLORIDA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Yes or no, Ron, will you serve a full four-year term if you are reelected governor of Florida.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: The only worn-out old donkey I'm looking to put out to pasture is \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Who doesn't love a yellow school bus? Can you raise your hand if you love a yellow school bus?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The most common price for gas in America is $3.39, down from over $5 when I took office.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: No.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They snuck that one in on me.\par \par \uc2 All right, Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, we will preview Tuesday's town hall in Ohio featuring the two candidates for Senate in that state, J.D. Vance and \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  . This weekend, please join Shannon Bream for FOX NEWS SUNDAY. That's the town hall. There you go. There's J.D. There's \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  . This weekend, FOX NEWS SUNDAY, her gusts include \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. It's been quite a week. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME", because I have been going over lately, I decided to end my show just a few seconds early and hand it to you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020221029eias00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Hannity Town Hall in Florida\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity \par \uc2 7,973 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: Hello, Florida! Hello! Hello.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Great to see you!\par \par \uc2 All right. That's it. I'm leaving New York!\par \par \uc2 (CHANTING)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. We got to do a show. All right.\par \par \uc2 We are broadcasting live from beautiful West Palm Beach in the free state of Florida and in just 12 -- and in just 12 days, you will decide the future of our great republic, and tonight, in just a moment, we'll be joined by Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , Florida's first lady, \uc2 Casey DeSantis\uc2  will be with us.\par \par \uc2 Any \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  fans in the house? \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  will be here.\par \par \uc2 Also, Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  will be here. \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  fans here, right?\par \par \uc2 Now, both DeSantis and Rubio, they are now poised for massive election day. Governor DeSantis is up by a whopping 14 points at a recent \uc2 University of North Florida\uc2  poll. That same poll shows Senator Rubio up by 11 points over Democrat, radical, socialist, leftist Val Demings.\par \par \uc2 You notice I add to the teleprompter. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 But tonight, momentum is absolutely for the Republicans. We start with a lot of good news tonight. It's not unique to Florida.\par \par \uc2 A new poll by Rasmussen out today -- yesterday, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  was up by 2.4 points by Trafalgar. As of today, Rasmussen has Herschel up by five.\par \par \uc2 In North Carolina, Republic \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  is up by four, according to Trafalgar.\par \par \uc2 The latest and this was just for this program Insider Advantage poll, Kari Lake is up by 11 over \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 We now have a dead even Senate race, which means that Mark Kelly is going to be packing and headed back to Arizona. By the way, to all my friends in Arizona, we were just out there, please understand, voting for Kari Lake, don't forget Blake Masters, that is an important Senate seat that we will win.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, so if you vote for Mark Kelly, you basically voted for \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and I know \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 . You're not going to like him. And that means every radical Democratic policy.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in the great live free or die state of New Hampshire, Republican \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , he will be easily reelected. And in the state Senate race, right now, General Don Bolduc has a real shot, it is a one-point race. He can defeat incumbent Democrat \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  who's done pretty much nothing for the state of New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 Now, this brings us to the commonwealth of Pennsylvania where after that very revealing debate, by the way, Democrats are mad at Fetterman that he actually went to the debate and people could see the real condition. They're not mad that he lost the debate, they're mad that he went to the debate. They wanted to keep in high fighting for a long time.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Dr. Oz is now up by three points according to a brand new Insider Advantage poll. That's great news for Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 And with just 12 days to go, Democrats coast to coast, they are all struggling to gain traction. None of them want to be seen with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  who earlier today along with \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , yeah, they had what's called a hot mic moment. They were consoling each other about their party's very bleak political outlook. Take a look at this..\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): That seat, we're in danger in that seat (INAUDIBLE) but it's close. We'll see. But we're, you know, it looks like, it looks like the debate, didn't hurt us too much in Pennsylvania as of today, so that's good. The state where we're seeing the polls, the state where we're going downhill is Georgia. It's hard to believe that they will vote for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Now, they know Georgia is slipping away from the Democrats thankfully and they know that Fetterman is not fit to serve. But, of course, they're going to try and push him through regardless. The left will do anything for a win, anything for power. If they got that power, that would mean, the end of the legislative filibuster. That would mean court- packing, that would mean D.C., Puerto Rico statehood.\par \par \uc2 Now even if it hurts the commonwealth of Pennsylvania, Democrats, they don't really care about good governance. They don't care about sound principles. They only care about political power.\par \par \uc2 Now, that's also true right here in Florida where professional politician \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  is running purely for vanity. Crist who now compares himself to Jesus, did you see that?\par \par \uc2 Charlie, you're not Jesus, okay?\par \par \uc2 Anyway, he has for almost -- run for almost every single office in the state of Florida as a Democrat, as a Republican, as an independent. He wakes up and changes any affiliation on any given day. It's kind of hard to pin down any Crist political beliefs because he has at one time or another, he's held all of them.\par \par \uc2 Now, currently, the main pillar of his campaign is climate change and Crist is now a card-carrying member of the climate alarmist cult. So, of course, he is in favor of limiting oil and gas production, the lifeblood of the world's economy. He's driving up the price of the pump that is hurting everyone here in this room tonight, and everyone watching on TV. He has vowed to veto any limits on abortion including up till the moment of birth.\par \par \uc2 And now -- pretty, pretty twisted. And now as one of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's biggest fan boys, Charlie's excited to be a rubber stamp for all things \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in the Senate. In the House, he voted with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  100 percent of the time. Can you believe that? One hundred percent. He's like a perfect Biden robot.\par \par \uc2 Now, recently, he praised Biden for his exceptional and phenomenal leadership, thanked him for his work on inflation and gas prices.\par \par \uc2 And needless to say, the Crist campaign has been a disaster. Recently, his campaign manager was arrested for a domestic violence. One of his consultants was accused of sexual assault. His running mate called Hispanic Americans racist and had very kind words for the communist murdering thug dictator \uc2 Fidel Castro\uc2  following his death.\par \par \uc2 Now, Crist has been accused of soliciting campaign donations from hurricane victims. Can you believe that?\par \par \uc2 And meanwhile, Governor DeSantis showed real leadership before, during and after Hurricane Ian and the recovery and the rebuild efforts have been amazing.\par \par \uc2 Now, the governor also showed real leadership during COVID. He kept the state of Florida free, open and safe. Kids in Florida were back in school in August of 2020. No other state was doing that.\par \par \uc2 And so many others were cowering in fear, he stood up to the media mob, he stood up to Fauci, he stood up to the Democrats, he stood up to bureaucrats and I have no doubt he will continue to stand out for you the great people of Florida.\par \par \uc2 Now -- are you ready to give your governor a warm Florida welcome? Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R), FLORIDA: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Are you happy to live in a free state? Are you all going to go out on November 8th so we can keep it a free state? And will you help me once and for all send a tired, worn out, old donkey back to pasture?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Very low energy crowd tonight.\par \par \uc2 Governor, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: But this energy shows you, and I think you and I agree on this, the red wave in 2022 starts in the state of Florida. We are going to launch it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: What are -- these polls coming out today. I mentioned Herschel now up by five, yesterday was two and a half. Now, he's up by five. Oz now up by three. Kari Lake went from up by five to now up by 11. You see now Blake Masters is about to overtake Mark Kelly in the state of Arizona. You see Bolduc is within a point of winning the Senate seat in the great state of New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 What's going on?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: I think we're going to win all those races and I think we'll win many more because we have a situation where voters are responding to a failed administration. There's just no way to sugarcoat it. Things have gotten worse since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was president.\par \par \uc2 The inflation's at a 40-year high. He's waged war on American energy. He's bungled the southern border like we've never seen before.\par \par \uc2 So on issue after issue, they're failing. The Democrats are still in lockstep with him. They will not break with their extreme policies and our candidates are offering a better vision. So I think we need to work hard over these next -- little less than two weeks. Everybody needs to be going out to vote. But if we do our part and we get all like-minded people, and not just Republicans, we get independents and some Democrats, if we all do that, I think this will be one of the best midterm elections of our lifetime.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I hope you're right. We know, "USA Today"/Suffolk poll came out, and the 16 percent of previously undecided people, they said almost all of them are breaking last-minute towards the GOP. You know, here, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  becomes president, and did you ever think things can get this bad this fast?\par \par \uc2 Forty-one-year high of inflation, record high gas prices, wide open borders, abandoning energy independence, all of these -- no law and order, no bail, defund, dismantle police departments, woke education versus traditional education. How did it go so bad in 20 months?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: So I don't know that I've ever seen it this bad in our country since I've been -- been around. But the thing that strikes me about this is all those problems you said are created and manufactured by Biden himself with his bad policy. You know, there's sometimes -- there's sometimes things happen with the economy. Sometimes energy prices go up because of global supply.\par \par \uc2 He's waging war on our own ability to produce. He's begging Maduro for energy. He's going to the Saudis and begging. You know, the Saudis -- they cut production and people said, oh, they're doing power politics against Biden.\par \par \uc2 Yeah, I think they probably are, but he put himself in this position by making our country vulnerable to this.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: What did you think when the Saudis revealed that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was not only rejected his request that they would increase production. He was told they would decrease it. They said -- he said, okay, can you wait to announce it for a month until after the midterm?\par \par \uc2 Now, if that was a guy with the last name Trump or DeSantis, wouldn't that be called collusion?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, yeah, I mean, literally, he would have been impeached if the Democrat standard would apply to him. I mean, they impeached \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  based on a phone call to Ukraine, about Ukraine. So, obviously, we know that --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: The phone call was, don't mismanage our money, that's what it was about.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Right, and there has been a lot of mismanagement. That was an absolutely valid concern that he had.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: How are -- I lived in southwest Florida, as you know, for a while. I'm on the east coast now. And not full time, but I'm coming, I'm moving.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 But I will not vote like the average New Yorker, I'll tell you that.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: But I told Sean before, because I've been bugging him about doing this for years. I'm like, why don't you come where your vote counts? But I do have to say, New York, we may elect, I think we will elect a Republican Governor \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  and you'll be helping that.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: It probably is the last chance he has.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: How many people are migrating do you know every day? Do you have an average? Because I read about 800.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Yeah, I mean, people -- I think between 800 and 1,100. We've led the country in net in migration since COVID by a country mile. We've had more adjusted gross income move into this state since COVID than has ever moved into a state in our country's history over a similar time period.\par \par \uc2 And I know a lot of people will ask, hey, if someone's coming from a deep blue state, are they going to vote the same way to bring those policies here? Well, here's the statistics, when I got elected governor, close to 300,000 more registered Democrats in Florida than Republicans. That was four years ago. Today, we have 306,000 more registered Republicans.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I was talking to the audience before you came out. To me, \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  conservatism works. America first conservatism works. MAGA conservatism works.\par \par \uc2 I don't like to call myself a conservative for no reason, but when you have lower taxes, when you believe in capitalism, when you control your borders, when you're energy independent, you're the master of your own destiny, why did we ever abandon what was working?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, I think also we have a situation where the left has gone so off their rocker that if you just show some common sense, you're going to get very far with a lot of voters. I mean, who releases criminals back on the street knowing they're going to commit more crimes again? What type of a prosecutor --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, yes. What type of a prosecutor would say, yeah, you can go ahead and steal $900 worth of merchandise, just don't steal a thousand, and we're not going to ever prosecute you? You see a lot of these cities have really gone into disrepute because of these really bad policies.\par \par \uc2 So we're just doing basic, common sense, tried and true American principles.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: One of the -- one of the most -- talked about decisions you made, you had 50 or 52 people that they signed a consent form. That they wanted to go to Martha's Vineyard. Martha's Vineyard is a sanctuary island, okay?\par \par \uc2 You're kind enough to transport them to their sanctuary island. How is it that sanctuary of sanctimonious leftists threw them off the island in less than 24 hours?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: I mean, and not only that they have a social services office there that says, you know, everybody is welcome, refugees welcome, all this other stuff. That's what they were advertising.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That's what they said.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: But I think what it showed is this, the elites in our country, they want to impose policies on all of us, but they do not want to have to face the consequences of their own policies, and that's what that shows.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Governor DeSantis who will be with us for the hour.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to one of the most powerful ads of the year. Take a look at this ad run by Governor DeSantis's campaign.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CASEY DESANTIS\uc2 , FLORIDA'S FIRST LADY: I get asked all the time, who is \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ? Well, he's the kid who grew up right here in Florida working his tail off, paying his own way through school, then volunteering to serve in the navy and deploying to Iraq. But if you want to know who \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  really is, when I was diagnosed with cancer and I was facing the battle for my life, he was the dad who took care of my children when I couldn't. He was there to pick me off of the ground when I literally could not stand.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Let's give a warm Florida welcome to the first lady of Florida, \uc2 Casey DeSantis\uc2 , who's here with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 One hundred percent cancer free?\par \par \uc2 C. DESANTIS: One hundred percent cancer free, thanks to God.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Thank God.\par \par \uc2 I know so many people -- my mom had stage four breast cancer. My mom was a prison guard. She worked double shifts her whole life, so we could go to private school. And she had stage four 19 lymph nodes, lost all her hair, double mastectomy, chemo, radiation, it's scary when you hear that word cancer, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 C. DESANTIS: It's terrifying. I'll tell you, Sean, that is the first time that I saw that ad. I have not seen that ad since I shot it, and when I did it, I asked everyone kindly to leave the room because I wanted to look into the camera and I wanted to tell people exactly who \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is, because when I went through one of the hardest things I've ever gone through in my life, I had a guy who was with me every step of the way, to literally pick me up off of the ground and he wasn't even just there for me, he was there for my kids. And imagine as a mom of three little babies, when you can't take care of your kids and put them to bed, but you got somebody like this helping you out, means the world.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: We're so glad you're well.\par \par \uc2 Politics is a rough and tumble business.\par \par \uc2 C. DESANTIS: You don't say.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I do -- I do. It's -- we call it a blood sport. You see the tough side of it and then you see when you're successful that you see the upside of it. It's a -- how hard is it on you when you hear him being attacked?\par \par \uc2 C. DESANTIS: You know what? I don't even paying any attention to it, because I don't I don't buy what the woke corporate media is throwing at them anymore. I think they know the truth. They saw a guy in \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  who stood up for the rights of the people of this state and stood up for freedom, and they are so proud at everything that he's done, and he was right through COVID.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of the media who were wrong, they never apologized for being wrong and so wrong.\par \par \uc2 So I think they're proud of Ron and I don't pay attention to anything they say, to be brutally honest with you.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah and being out -- you've also both been very active and involved. I lived in southwest Florida. How is the hurricane? Sanibel Island was destroyed. I saw the coast of Fort Myers, I was shocked.\par \par \uc2 R. DESANTIS: I mean, it was obviously a devastating storm, did a lot of damage. But I'll tell you, the response has been rapid. We had search and rescue, even when the tropical storm winds were still blowing in there, helping all the folks. They did over I think 3,000 rescue missions, the quickest power restoration that we've ever had in the state of Florida for millions of people out.\par \par \uc2 But you had those islands severed from the mainland and so people came to me, they weren't state bridges, but they said, governor, can you help? I'm like, let's help. So we did one bridge to Pine Island in three days. We did --\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 And then we did the Sanibel Causeway which was severed in three places. We did that that opened to the public three weeks from the day that the hurricane came through.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Temporary bridge.\par \par \uc2 I'm on a heartbreak, we got to take a break. First lady of Florida --\par \par \uc2 C. HANNITY: Pleasure.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Casey, good to see you. Thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 We're just getting started. When we come back, we'll continue with Governor DeSantis, Senator Marco is up next, as we continue from West Palm Beach, Florida.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Welcome back, as we continue live here on beautiful West Palm Beach.\par \par \uc2 Now to another critical race right here in the Sunshine State, Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  has opened up a commanding double-digit lead over Democrat Val Demings after this strong debate performance. Let's take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): The extremist on abortion in this campaign is Congresswoman Demings. She supports no restrictions no limitations of any kind. She voted against the four-month -- against the four-month ban, she voted against a five-month ban. She supports taxpayer-funded abortion on demand for any reason at any time up until the moment of birth.\par \par \uc2 Because you didn't support a 20-week ban, you didn't support --\par \par \uc2 REP. VAL DEMINGS (D-FL): Up to the time of viability.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: But when is that? That's the vague language they all give.\par \par \uc2 And then they talk about the doctor and the family, let me tell you who else is in that room, in that abortion room. The government is in that room because she brought them in there. She wants the taxpayer to pay for that abortion. That's government involvement, not just to pay for that abortion, but to pay for them all over the world.\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: Look, the senator can play national security expert all he wants. I know he needs that for his next presidential run.\par \par \uc2 DEBATE MODERATOR: Congresswoman, I need to stop you there and I want to give because you mentioned the senator, another 30 seconds to the senator.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Yeah, I mean, I don't know what she means by play national security. Yes, but I'm the vice chairman of the intelligence committee and was the previous chairman of it, so it's actually my job. But --\par \par \uc2 My opponent has been in Congress now for half a decade, and has never passed a single piece of legislation into law, not one. The only thing she does is vote 100 percent with Pelosi.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Now, Senator Rubio is right. Val Demings is nothing but a rubber stamp for \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . By the way, a 100 percent \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  voter, 100 percent of the time, now wants to be a rubber stamp for \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now she already supports the Biden agenda 100 percent of the time, like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . She doesn't have fond feelings about half the country. She even campaigned with a far left activist who actually compared Republican voters to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  and al Qaeda.\par \par \uc2 Now, Demings, also, he preys on the teachers' union boss who tirelessly worked to keep your kids out of school. She called her, quote, the amazing \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2 , you know, the teachers union head.\par \par \uc2 She also preys the left's very thoughtful plan to dismantle and defund the police. That's a genius move.\par \par \uc2 And like every other radical Democrat, she also supports open borders wants to limit the domestic production of oil and gas the lifeblood of our economy through the socialist new green deal. And during her five years in Congress, only two of her sponsored bills ever became law and both renamed post office buildings. Now, in contrast -- just the record.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, in contrast, \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  is one of the most productive, thoughtful, freedom-loving members of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Let's give a warm Florida welcome to Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Anybody that is preparing for a debate maybe any other candidates, I pray that they watch that debate with you and Val Demings. That's my job. That's what I do -- I thought that was a really good, powerful moment for you and the whole debate, I thought she did very poorly.\par \par \uc2 You're both up by double digits. You're both running against candidates that are a hundred percent in support of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's policies. I don't think there is a bigger choice election in our lifetime, Senator.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Well, first of all, we're not up by anything, because when -- the votes haven't been counted. So it's important everybody get out and we vote and we run through the tape because that's what they want us to do, but I - -\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: And I don't know what \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  is not, because he's the only one in Florida -- we were just talking about this a moment ago -- he will have lost to me in 2010, and then \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  in 2014, and now with \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . It's a rite of passage.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That's like the trifecta.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: And -- but he's also the only candidate I know that had those three people were introduced on the stage, \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 , \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and his fan. And \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  fan got his own introduction as well. So, the guy's not a serious candidate but I don't know how you can be with \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  100 percent of the time like Val Demings is, Pelosi isn't even with Pelosi 100 percent of the time. So it's a tough thing to do so, anyway.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And by the way, I don't think Joe knows today's Thursday. Ouch, that was mean, right? They're not responsible for what I say.\par \par \uc2 You know, Senator, I look at what's happened to the country and I was asking the governor about this the last 20 months. I -- my son is here with me tonight. He's 24 years old. He lives in Florida, and I worry about his future. I worry about my daughter's future. She's 21. I worry about everyone in this room, their family, their children and grandchildren's future..\par \par \uc2 I've never seen things get this bad this fast. To me, this is a very -- this is an inflection point for the country. Am I overstating it?\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: No, I think -- here's what we need to understand. The same party that has that -- a Democratic Party that, I don't say this with any pleasure, that's controlled by two groups. These laptop liberals and these Marxist misfits, that's the core of the Democratic Party today.\par \par \uc2 Not the voters, not Democratic voters, but the leadership of that party. That's what they take their orders from. And I'll be very blunt, if these people stay in power for another two years, they'll destroy the country. If they get their hands on this state or any -- they'll destroy the state.\par \par \uc2 And so, to me this choice and I'm glad the governor's here tonight, you know, I always say this to people -- people all want to come to America. All these guys like to talk about how terrible America is, but everybody wants to come here. And then the same people say, oh, and by the way Florida is a terrible place but they all want to move here.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: So --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: By the way, the greatest moment in COVID were all these Democratic governors and politicians that supported shutdowns in their states, they come visit you down here. And they all got caught.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: The cottage industry, I mean, if I had a dime for every one of the lockdowners who came left lockdowns to come to Florida, I'd be a very wealthy man. I mean, this happened over and over again.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: It did, and they got caught almost every time.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: That's the thing though. When your biggest critics, people can posture and they can emote politically and say this stuff, but how do they behave? And if even your worst critics are coming to your state, then you know you must have been doing something right.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I don't know what my favorite was, either was -- either \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  who came and then lied about coming, or AOC that came down and was partying without a mask on.\par \par \uc2 (BOOS)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Ooh, I see. I see we have a lot of AOC fans in the crowd.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: But my favorite moment is when you talk to one of these Democratic politicians and invariably, it comes down to, oh, so I've been down to Florida a bunch. Oh, yeah, why? He goes, my mom lives in Boca. So --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That's a great line.\par \par \uc2 You know, let me look at the other side of this. Everyone's talking about a wave election. Senator, you said something I hope everybody heard loud and clear. There are a lot of polls, but there's only one poll that matters, and that's on election day.\par \par \uc2 When we count -- now many of you can vote early in Florida. You've perfected the system down here thankfully. It's no longer 2000 or 2016. But it's so critical because if let's say the Democrats took over the Senate and they had a majority, I don't think you disagree with me they'd eliminate the legislative filibuster.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Right.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: They'd pack the courts. They'd make D.C. and Puerto Rico statehood done deal because they think that would guarantee them four Senate seats in perpetuity. They would do and they would continue down this path of new green deal radicalism. And I -- if that happened, I don't know if we'd ever dig ourselves out of it.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: No, they would also take over our election system. They pretty much said they want to do that. Val Deming says she also wants the federal government to potentially take over our property insurance, which is -- you can just imagine. Maybe that's what the 87,000 IRS agents will do is run the insurance company.\par \par \uc2 But -- I mean, you -- the list goes on of the parade of horribles of things they would do. Look, if they were in power, I'm convinced of this now, if they had a filibuster -- no filibuster, whatever, and they had been in charge during the COVID, they would have locked down the country. They would have done to the country what they did to New York, what they did to California, what they did to Illinois. That's what they would have done.\par \par \uc2 In fact, Val Demings has said as much, and I think both Demings and Crist are on record as late as I think last summer, asking for another shutdown in this state. They would have spent four something trillion dollars. They tried to and even in their infrastructure bill, they brag about -- only a quarter of that money went to infrastructure. The rest of it went to take on very serious problems like you know racist roads and discriminatory trees and whatever else they can think of.\par \par \uc2 And so, look, I'm sorry -- I hate to say this about my fellow Americans. These people are out of their minds, and they cannot be allowed to be near power.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, it raises -- the bottom line, Newt Gingrich defines it really well. The night he became speaker, that Republicans were out of power 40 years, I was the emcee of that event. I was a local radio host in Georgia at the time.\par \par \uc2 How do you reconcile capitalism versus socialism? How do you reconcile defund, no bail versus law and order? How do you reconcile energy -- domestic energy production and energy dependence? How do you reconcile capitalism, socialism? Put all these issues together, they're very different competing visions.\par \par \uc2 To me, the only answer is one side's going to win and it better be our side is -- I don't see any middle ground here.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Yeah, I mean, you know, just look at how these states have performed like since I've been governor. You have states like California and New York, people are fleeing those states. They're not safe. They tax, lockdown, all those other --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: They're not given \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  enough credit. He's the first governor in the history of California that had a net exodus of it -- out of his state, not a net increase.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: But you see though, that the different -- the Florida blueprint has worked. I mean, people just by bringing their wealth or their visitation moving here. But what they want to do is take those failed blue state models and they want to force that over the entire country. So they want to do things -- like Marco mentioned the elections, they want to outlaw voter ID in every election in our country. Can you imagine what happened? They want to mandate --\par \par \uc2 (BOOS)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: In Florida, since I've been governor, we ban ballot harvesting in Florida. But --\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: They -- they would federalize it and they would require ballot harvesting. And so, I agree with Marco in the sense of, it's not just -- maybe we disagree on taxes, maybe we disagree on welfare spending, these differences are fundamental. It's about really what it means to be an American and what are our foundational principles and the left wing of our country has departed from the principles that have made the country great.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: They're not --\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: There -- you can't reconcile these two versions. One side has to win and the future -- there was a time, you know, Democrats are a little left, Republicans are to the right, but we both operated like in the same universe.\par \par \uc2 Now, what's happened here is that these people, they live in these little bubbles, like the laptop liberals. They live in these little bubbles. The Marxist misfits, there's no hope for. But the laptop liberals, they live in these little bubbles where they have a lot of power, and everyone they know is just like them. And so, they have these ideas and they're immune from the consequences, okay?\par \par \uc2 Since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has been president, 4.4, almost 4.5 million people have entered this country illegally, okay? The governor flew 48 of them one day to make a point to Martha's Vineyard.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Forty-eight. They arrive -- they arrive, the state of Massachusetts immediately declares a state of emergency, 48 people. They mobilize a hundred and something National Guard troops, two to one ratio, then they do -- what all these laptop liberals do, they stage the sting at a church where they show look we're feeding them we're giving them toys and then they deport them to Cape Cod 24 hours later when no one was looking.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Less than 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: They live immune from there because it's not their schools, it's not their hospitals, it's not their cities that are that are being overcrowded.\par \par \uc2 And you know where they're moving to? They're moving to immigrant communities and Hispanic communities and states like Florida and Texas and Arizona who have to share all the burden because these laptop liberals, they live immune from the consequences of their craziness. And that's why they're that --\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I'll tell you what, one of the areas that you're uniquely qualified to talk about is the area of national security. I am very concerned. I don't think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  garners any respect for moral leaders, hostile regimes like \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  in Russia, President Xi in China, the mullahs in Iran.\par \par \uc2 The Saudis mocked him, made fun of him, denied his request to increase oil production. As a matter of fact reduced it and then exposed that he wanted to collude with them to delay the announcement of their reduction in the production of oil. By the way, if any -- if it was a Republican, they'd be put in jail, they'd be investigated, probably impeached.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Well, as you know --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I'm worried about the national security side of all this because we see what's happening in Ukraine and I believe President Xi when he says that he wants to, quote, reunify with Taiwan. It's not reunify. He wants to take over Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Well, to be fair to Biden, okay, as we all know. Saudi oil is cleaner for the environment than American oil is, right? So, you know, my - -\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Headline tomorrow, \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  says Saudi oil is cleaner.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: So, according to Biden. So, my solution is just to pump American oil put a sticker that says Saudi and confuse him and maybe he'll be for it.\par \par \uc2 So, but going back to the point that you're making, yeah, so let me give you one example, okay? We have -- and I love our military, but as civilian leadership, and so you look at, it's embarrassing. You look at their \uc2 Twitter\uc2  page and they've like video after video about the proper use of pronouns.\par \par \uc2 No, you need to be focused on how do we blow up Chinese aircraft carriers if they ever attack us. So, of course, they don't respect the guy. They don't respect him. And why would they?\par \par \uc2 And so I think they view him as incompetent. And then I think you see, okay, if you're bad to America and mean to Biden, he'll be trying to be nice to you. You're Iran, he'll want to do a deal. You're Venezuela, he'll want to do a deal.\par \par \uc2 But China, you know, \uc2 John Kerry\uc2 , who's still around, he's like the \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  to the federal level, he -- he's still around and he wants to do this climate deal.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: We're going to take this show on the road.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: And so, you know, China's sitting there saying, we're going to take these guys to the cleaner. You know, we're going to take these guys -- and that's what they're doing. So, you're absolutely right, it's dangerous, it's reckless and the China threat is the threat of the 21st century. Either they will be the world's most powerful country or we will, and I promise you, a world where China is the most powerful country in the world is not a good world. It is not the one I want to leave my kids.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I hope everybody's listening because that is a real clear present danger for the country.\par \par \uc2 All right. We're just getting started. We'll take a break. More with Governor DeSantis, more with Senator Rubio. When we come back, Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  will join us right here live on stage.\par \par \uc2 "Hannity" on the road live from Palm Beach.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. As we continue, we are live -- we're on the road in West Palm Beach, Florida.\par \par \uc2 Please welcome on stage, the other senator from the great state of Florida, Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  is with us. Just to give a warm Florida welcome to him.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: What is that?\par \par \uc2 What is this?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-FL): OK. So, in this role is when -- I'm the chairman of the National Republican Central Committee. I create an award for people that care about freedom.\par \par \uc2 Who do you think should get this? \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: -- you care about freedom, what you do on your show to fight for freedom around this country is unbelievable and unparalleled.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: So this is for you.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Wow.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well deserved.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: So when I was a kid, I played ice hockey. I wanted a Stanley Cup. Now, I got one.\par \par \uc2 Senator, you are working around the clock, I know because I've talked to you at 2:00, 3:00, 4:00 a.m. in the morning trying to get every Republican possible elective. Your role is to win the Senate.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Win the majority.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Win the majority. You know --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: It starts right here.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: It starts here.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Everybody's got to get out and vote and call all other friends and make sure Marco wins. Don't take a chance on these polls. Marco looks like he's doing well, don't take a chance. Go vote.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I really want to put emphasis on that because sometimes, oh, somebody's up by 10-point -- no, you have --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Don't take a chance.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Don't take a chance.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I want to win.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: So we see in a lot of progress. We know that Senator Rubio is up by 11 as of today. We're very happy about that. But then you see Herschel yesterday he was up two and a half now he's up five. Dr. Oz was down double digits, he's now up by three as of today, right?\par \par \uc2 Kari Lake went from a five-point lead for the governorship out in Arizona now she's up 11, and Blake Masters now is tied --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: He's going to win.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: He's going to win.\par \par \uc2 And I see that General Bolduc is now down only one point in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I'm going there tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Oh, you're going.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I've been traveling -- well, no, I'm going there Sunday. I'm going to Wisconsin in Ohio tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Okay, I'm not surprised, you can't remember where you are. That's a lot of traveling.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I'm in Florida. I know that.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah, part, you're in the free state of Florida.\par \par \uc2 Here's my question, is it possible we win Florida, we win Georgia, that we win North Carolina, South Carolina, New Hampshire, Pennsylvania, Wisconsin, Ohio, Missouri, Nevada, Arizona, maybe Washington state, maybe --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Tiffany Smiley's down two, double the fundraising of \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2 . So she has a shot. Joe O'Dea got a shot.\par \par \uc2 I think -- I think -- I mean, I think Don Bolduc's going to win. That guy is working his butt off every day.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Can you please tell \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , he took away six million dollars to give it back?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Not give it to \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I know. I mean, I mean, you know, listen, we got to go help.\par \par \uc2 By the way, if you want to help Don Bolduc, if you want to help Marco, you can give to nrsc.org. We'll take your money and we'll go spend it as fast as we can on these races.\par \par \uc2 We've got 12 days to go. We got -- we got to get -- we got to get the message out and we got to get people out to vote and we're going to work hard.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Look, we have people this is a town hall -- hi, your name is --\par \par \uc2 AUDIENCE MEMBER: Hi, I'm Angela.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Hi, Angela. A long time listener, first time caller.\par \par \uc2 AUDIENCE MEMBER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right, that's a radio thing. Thank you. What's your question?\par \par \uc2 AUDIENCE MEMBER: So, hello and thank you for the opportunity. Our family relocated down to Florida just last year.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: From?\par \par \uc2 AUDIENCE MEMBER: New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 AUDIENCE MEMBER: I just want to --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You're not bringing your politics with you, right?\par \par \uc2 AUDIENCE MEMBER: Absolutely not, absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And you're very welcome.\par \par \uc2 AUDIENCE MEMBER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Thank you