s Out; Rep. Michael Waltz (R-FL), Is Being Interviewed About How To Deter \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  On Its War to Taiwan; GOP New York House Candidate On Tight Race 12 Days Out; Thousands Gather At Grave Of Mahsa Amini; Putin Claims West Trying To Escalate Ukraine War; Fetterman Concedes PA Debate Wasn't Exactly Easy; Momentum Continues To Grow For GOP 12 Days Out; Jon Stewart Calls Out \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  For Corruption; \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  To Release A Memoir "Spare" In January\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Ari Fleischer, Marc Thiessen, Jessica Tarlov,  Bryan Llenas, Guy Benson, Brian Kilmeade \par \uc2 8,232 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hard to believe that 10 years has gone by already since that storm hit. It really is remarkable. Time flies.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Speaking of which, time flies, there goes our two hours. We'll be back here tomorrow. Thanks for joining us, everyone. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes, we will see you again tomorrow, which is Friday, and there's only going to be 11 days left until November the 8th, the midterm elections, the ballots are counted. I'm John Roberts, THE STORY with Martha in New York starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you guys. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum, and as John said, we are not 12 days to go and the President today trying to be upbeat at the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Great economic report today, the GDP report. Things are looking good.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But there are other reports that folks in the West Wing are getting worried about what they're seeing. And it's being shown in some of the headlines out there like these, Democrats scrambling into defensive posture grasping for a third rail, and new polling shows "Republican support rising with inflation driving voters." So, let's get a closer look at some brand-new polls. USA Today, Suffolk numbers. This is the generic ballot back in July, which was shortly after the Roe v. Wade decision.\par \par \uc2 Democrats were at their high point, 44 percent, when asked, do you want to vote for a Republican? Or do you want to vote for a Democrat? So, now look at the flip that we have seen. So, you've got, Democrat now at 45. So, they're hovering basically in the same area. But what you're seeing is that the undecideds are, are moving by the Democrat vote, and they are lining up with Republicans now at 49 percent. These undecided voters that we spoke with in Pennsylvania, amplify that move that we're seeing. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: How is inflation impacting your daily life? And maybe, Shane, you could talk about your business as an electrician, I know you run your own company.\par \par \uc2 SHANE ESLINGER, PENNSYLVANIA VOTER: Yes, driving the big, large truck around every day really sucks up the gas mileage and it, it certainly makes a difference on how far you want to travel. Take a job and -\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And are you making different personal decisions about travel or about buying a home, anything along those lines?\par \par \uc2 PJ DOLAN, PENNSYLVANIA VOTER: I took a different job because of the commute mainly, and the violence. I also teach school in the inner city. And I traveled nine miles, and it takes me an hour and 15 minutes to get home. That gas money coupling with that commute, it's just too much.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: With that, let's bring in our great panel today. \uc2 Ari Fleischer\uc2 , former White House Press Secretary; Marc Thiessen, American Enterprise Institute Scholar, both of Fox News Contributors; and Jessica Tarlov, Head of Research at Bustle Digital Group, and of course, a Co-Host on "THE FIVE." Great to see all of you today. Thank you all for coming in. So, Ari, the President comes out of the White House, you've got a GDP number up 2.6, but the internals are not so great, as we just heard from \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  just a few minutes ago, is he making a mistake to, you know, keep telling everybody things are great when these numbers appear to show that people don't feel that way?\par \par \uc2 ARI FLEISCHER\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Big mistake and it ties right into what you were saying about undecided. The President's message today, when he learned that GDP was 2.6 percent, after two negative quarters in a row with high inflation and when 70 percent of the American people say the country is on the wrong track? His message should have been: it's good, but not good enough. And this is why Congress needs to pass X, Y, Z.\par \par \uc2 He didn't do that. He bragged. And it's so out of touch. It's tone deaf. It's sending every wrong signal to the American people, right, prior to an election, except for the 30 percent who think that the country is on the right track. So, unless he wants to lose 70-30, it's a bad message. And this is why the undecided are breaking. This is a big part of the reason. They don't like what they're hearing. They don't like what they're seeing, and they don't approve of the President.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you can look at this election at this point, Marc, and you can look at where the money's going. And you can look at where the people and the movement is, right? President Biden really has been very close to home. It's kind of a repeat of the basement strategy that we saw during the presidential election. But today, he's going to Syracuse, New York. But when you match that with where \uc2 Obama\uc2 , and Trump, and even Bush were in these days during their own midterms, they were out there, they were all over the place. So, what is going on Marc?\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I mean, he's better off being in the basement if he comes out and says things like he just did. I mean, you know that -- if you just look at the two clips you showed, with the President going and saying things are great, and, and he says the economy is strong as hell and then the clips you showed of those voters, what did they think when they heard \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  say things are great, things are looking up? They think that he's completely out of touch. I mean, Ari and I served in the White House together.\par \par \uc2 One of the principles of presidential communication that we both learned is that when the President says things that are directly contradicted by the living reality of Americans, they not only tune him out, they want to push them out. And so, you know, when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  goes and says this when 78 percent of the Fox News poll, 78 percent of Americans think the economy is doing poorly, 71 percent say it's getting worse, and two-thirds of Americans blame \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , you don't go out and say things are great, things are terrific, because then people just think this guy's completely out of touch.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, uh, one of the headlines today, Jessica is from the Wall Street Journal op-ed: "Why Democrats are losing the midterms. And it goes on to say, with Trump out of the spotlight, voters are focusing on how far- left the Democratic Party has turned, and indeed, from the President, when asked, you know, what do you care about after the elections? He said, we're going to try to make sure that we can put Roe v. Wade back in into place through a national law, he talks about green energy. Is he off base here? And is that part of the problem, as the Wall Street Journal seems to be, seems to be suggesting there, Jessica?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Well, I don't think that talking about Roe v. Wade is something that's a far-left policy. Majorities of Americans agree that they want at least abortion to be legal up to some level. I know that people some people favor restrictions that 15 weeks, 20 weeks, 24 weeks or no restriction whatsoever. But that's an issue that touches the hearts and minds of people on both sides of the aisle. So, that's a good thing to talk about. \uc2 Green Energy\uc2  has obviously spilled over into a debate that's hurting \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , for instance, in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 I don't think we should be talking about that. And I tend to agree with Marc and Ari about the talking points when it comes to the economy, you should certainly be celebrating the fact that micron is putting in $100 billion in Syracuse or that Intel put in 20 billion in Ohio. But the real issue that I think is swinging the election right now is crime and public safety. And that's a big part of why \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is actually in New York campaigning with \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , who's in a tight race now with \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , because of the issue of crime.\par \par \uc2 It's affecting the race in Wisconsin with Mandela Barnes. It's a central piece of what's going on in Pennsylvania. And I think it's really important the contrast of the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  poll today between what's going on with Josh Shapiro, who's running for governor and \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . And Shapiro has really figured out how to talk about crime from a Democratic perspective. And the first step is acknowledging that it is a big problem, it is affecting people, and that it's not acceptable, and then laying out that X, Y, Z -- this is what we're going to do about it. And that's what I think is swaying us.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm going to ask you for a quick answer, Jessica, and then I'm going to go back down the line here. But while you brought up Fetterman, there's discussion today that he should be disqualified from running.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Why? I don't understand why? I watched the debate with everyone else. I understand that he was slow on some questions, and it seemed that there were issues communicating his point, but his doctors released a statement and people who have analyzed folks who have had strokes have said that he's on the road to recovery. We've accommodated people before in Congress in the Senate who have had far worse disabilities that he's recovering from, from a terrible incident. I don't see the problem here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Ari, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 FLEISCHER: Well, I'm reminded of all the conversations with COVID, when we should just the experts say, and it contradicts everything we see with our eyes. We all watched that debate. And we saw a man who is not capable of being orator, a senator, the principal job of a senator is to listen and then articulate. They're not doers. They're talkers. And then they vote. That's the principal job. And anybody who's going to hire somebody, if your job is to be orator, and they watched that debate, they know that he's not qualified anymore. So, I just think, who cares what his doctor put down in the letter, his doctor who was a campaign contributor to him, we all saw it with our own eyes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm reminded of Rick Perry, who couldn't remember three things. He could only remember two things, and it ended his presidential run. While he was up there. It is not for us to judge, really, it's for the American people to sit back and say, you know, does this make sense and the voters that we talked to yesterday and Pennsylvania, across the board, with one exception said that it changed, it changed their, their take about what is right for this individual and whether or not they want to vote for him to represent them at this moment in time.\par \par \uc2 Marc, you know, let's take one look at this inflation versus abortion, because it's 16-point spread. Think about the issues, inflation abortion, which matters to more. Inflation at six, at 56 percent, and abortion at 40. Are we going to be having conversations in the coming weeks about, about it being a mistake on the part of Democrats to put so many eggs in that basket, so to speak, and at the expense of talking about crime or bringing energy back home to the United States?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Look, if inflation was low, there was no crime wave this border was secure. Gas prices were low, then people might have the luxury of voting on abortion. But when you're sitting in a situation where you can't do it, you're literally not be able to choose between gas and food or between heating your home, and, and, and paying your rent. You can't have the luxury of voting on abortion. The only people who have the luxury of voting on abortion are liberal elites in major cities who've been insulated from the economic disaster that's fitted that's facing the rest of the country that's why it's not resonating because people have bigger problems right now than whether what kind of, what kind of what kind of abortion law the states could have?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Can I just add?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, quick. Yes, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Sorry, just very quickly on that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Sure.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: The Wall Street Journal as well has covered the abortion issue extensively. And they had a piece a few days ago about what's happened to women all over this country because of this woman having to wait to go into sepsis to get the medical, medical care that they use. And that happens to poor women, to rich women, to those liberal elites sitting in cities, and people living in rural counties. It's uniform, that women if they choose to, and maybe it happens by accident, can get pregnant and that they need health care treatment. And so, don't minimize this, that it's just one of these fanciful of you --\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: I'm not minimizing it, but --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: You are. You're saying it's a luxury.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Every governor.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: It's not a luxury.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, he's, he's saying that when you're, when you're trying to put -- go ahead.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: I'm saying, what I'm saying to you is it's not resonating it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We're just talking about the numbers, Jessica, when you look at what, what appears to be resonating with voters, as Marc is saying, this is -- it's falling low on the list.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I totally understand that. I know that there are more important issues.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: If you look at every governor, Jesse. Jesse, every, every governor -- so you got Brian Kemp was on a 15-week abortion law, six-week abortion law, he's winning by double digits. You got, you've got the in Ohio, DeWine has passed a six-week abortion ban. He's winning by 20 points. In every state where you've got these governors who are actually implementing abortion laws, they're not paying a price for it. So, this is not resonating with the American people.\par \par \uc2 And when Biden talks about this constantly, when Democrats talk about this constantly, what people are hearing is, you're not paying attention to what is important to me. You're not paying attention to what is affecting my life right now. I can't afford the basic costs of life. And you're talking about something that's completely extraneous to that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We got to leave it there. Thank you very much. Thanks, Jessica. Thank you, Marc. Thank you, Ari. We'll talk more about it later. Thanks, guys. Great to have you all. So, President Biden has really struck very close to home in the heat of these midterm elections. The absence has been notable that's been recognized by people across the board.\par \par \uc2 But right now, he is in upstate New York, we expect to hear from him. In just a few minutes. He's there to try to boost Democrat Francis Conole, in an important seat in the Syracuse area, which is now moved into the Lean-R category. OK, so we're watching this race very closely. The candidate, Brandon Williams, who was a Navy veteran, he joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: President Biden in Syracuse, New York this hour, where he is expected to double down on the argument that Republicans will make the economy worse.\par \par \uc2 That is what we've heard from him over the past several days. We're now 12 days to go until the big election on November the 8th.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): Syracuse is home to this very close nationally watched House race. This race is to replace \uc2 John Katko\uc2 , who is leaving, leaving the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 It flipped that seat -- he flipped the seat red in 2015. The district was redrawn this year, and our Fox Power Rankings has just shifted it from toss up into the Lean Republican column now, and that is where we find Brandon Williams. He is the Republican in that race. And we have also invited his opponent, Francis Conole to join us as well.\par \par \uc2 Good to have you here, sir. Thank you for being here, Mr. Williams. Tell me why you think this race is moving? What's happened over the last week or so that has changed this, this scenario for you?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON WILLIAMS (R-NY), CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: Sure. Well, nobody wants to give \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , you know, more power and more control over our economy.\par \par \uc2 Crime is rampant in New York State is no doubt you know, and they really need a fresh perspective. Particularly here in central New York, we're on the verge of a big turnaround here in New York State, with \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  surging, and Republicans surging. And people are just behind that. People that have been disenfranchised for a long time are just excited and standing up to vote.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Here is the Siena Poll, which came out recently. It's got you at 45 percent.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): Your opponent, Francis Conole at 40 percent. 15 percent are undecided at this point. So, this could really still go either way, according to this poll.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): Now, one of the reasons that the president is there today -- and your opponent is not going to be with him campaigning, is that correct? Today?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes, it would be pretty uncomfortable conversation, because my opponent all of a sudden decided that the Democrats needed new leadership.\par \par \uc2 I pressed him in a, you know, in a -- in our debate yesterday, and asked him about relationship with \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 . And because \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  has endorsed him, and he has embraced her, and all of a sudden he says, I really don't know her.\par \par \uc2 And so, you see this extraordinary flop and flip to try to escape \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , and voters aren't buying it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you know, anybody from this area knows that upstate New York has gone through very difficult times. That it used to be a manufacturing hub, it is not anymore. But now you've got this micron plant that is coming in to Clay, New York. Huge investment there.\par \par \uc2 You wrote an editorial and called it, Corporate Welfare. And your opponent says that now you're trying to change your tune on that. Explain.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Sure. Well, if you look at the bill, it was not $50 billion for the chip industry, it was $250 billion dollars. So, they added this $200 billion of slop for the green energy, corporate welfare folks. And that is why inflation is so high.\par \par \uc2 But the 50 billion for the actual relocation of manufacturing -- of strategically important, microchips back to the U.S. is something that I certainly would support.\par \par \uc2 I come from the tech industry. I've never been in politics before. And as a former nuclear submarine officer, you know, I feel strongly about National Defense, particularly, having deterred China in the Pacific.\par \par \uc2 And so, I really do understand the industry because of the software company that I founded in Silicon Valley that actually helps modernize manufacturing.\par \par \uc2 Industrial -- the reindustrialization of Central New York has always been keystone in my platform, and micron is just a -- it's just a great platform for that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Brandon Williams, thank you very much for being here. Thank you for your service. Your opponent is also in the Navy. I believe he's a reservist.\par \par \uc2 And it's great to watch this race and great to have you with us today. Thank you very much, sir.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Pleasure. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, also breaking today, Russia's \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  addressing the threat of nuclear war, and telling his country that the U.S. had no grounds for using a nuclear bomb in Japan.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Michael Waltz joins me live in New York after this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF RUSSIA (through translator): We are at a historical turning point. Ahead, there is probably the most dangerous, unpredictable and at the same time important decades since the end of World War II.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): Fighting in the streets of Iran as protesters marked 40 days since the death of 22-year-old Mahsa Amini. She died in custody of Iran's so-called morality police after she was judged as wearing her headscarf in not exactly the right way.\par \par \uc2 1,000s also heading to Amini's gravesite in --\par \par \uc2 Look at that -- look at the scene. These are the people that turned her out because she died 40 days ago. Look at the traffic, look at the outpouring of passion that we have seen since this event. As many -- just had to get out of their cars and make their way on foot to this.\par \par \uc2 People are enormously invigorated and upset in Tehran.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): So, with that, there are also tectonic shifts of world order. That is the message from \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  today. Now, accusing the west of laying blame on Moscow for nuclear threats.\par \par \uc2 Listen to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PUTIN: The West in recent years, and especially in recent months, has taken a number of steps to escalate. Well, as a matter of fact, they always play for escalation. There is nothing new here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Here now, Florida Republican Congressman Michael Waltz, a former Green Beret commander, who serves on the \uc2 House Armed Services Committee\uc2 , which is very important to him.\par \par \uc2 It's good to have you here, sir. Always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL WALTZ (R-FL): Yes. Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know what? I want to play another sound bite from \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . And then, I can get you to respond to both of these. This is also what he said earlier in Moscow today. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PUTIN: World domination is what the so-called West bet on in its game, but that game is, without doubt, a dangerous, bloody, and I would say, filthy ones.\par \par \uc2 It's a game that denies the sovereignty of countries and nations, their distinctiveness, and uniqueness, disregarding the interests of other states.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What's your take on where this stands right now? Where are we in all of this?\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Well, I think this is the most significant geopolitical shift since the end of the Cold War. We are seeing an alignment of autocracies, we are seeing China and Russia move closer together. We saw that in both Chairman Xi's speech to his party congress just last week, where he warned the West and warned his people as he's telling them to prepare for war. And he warned them of the coming storm.\par \par \uc2 You have that coupled with those comments from Putin, and both talking about the inevitable decline of the United States and their Western allies, and a new world order where their version of government is supreme.\par \par \uc2 And then, you know, it's as kind of important subtext to that, you have a Iran marching towards a nuclear weapon, and now at war in Ukraine, with their drones, providing missiles and their own advisers.\par \par \uc2 And we can't forget about North Korea that's on the verge of testing another nuclear weapon. This is an incredibly dangerous time. And my fear is that they all smell weakness in this White House.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, this was just going to say, you know, when they talk about the decline of the United States, and then I talked to voters in Pennsylvania, and they tell me, I asked them, you know, how optimistic are you about the future of the country? And they all just shook their heads -- I'm not.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: How patriotic do you feel these days? I don't. And so, this is exactly what China wants to hear.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: That's right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And a seizing upon. This is John Kirby talking about the coordination between Iran and Russia. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL: We know they may be considering some sort of support to Iran's ability to crack down on protesters.\par \par \uc2 And sadly, Russia has experience at doing that. So, we'll see. We'll watch where this goes.\par \par \uc2 But it's just yet another example of Russia and Iran now working together.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, these bad actors, are they're testing us?\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Oh, they absolutely are. And they have been, particularly, since the withdrawal from Afghanistan, and that debacle there, where, you know, the entire world got the message, the United States will not stand with its allies, and it will cut and run in the face of a ragtag terrorist army.\par \par \uc2 And I think we've seen that in our adversary's propaganda since then. And we've seen all of them on the march.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, one of the voters I spoke to yesterday was a former veteran -- was a veteran. And he said, I want a peaceful solution to this war. I don't want to be in another 20-year, extracted engagement. And have the U.S. get involved in this. I don't. What do you say to him?\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: No. He is right. And no one wants that. But what we have to realize is deterrence failed. You know, we remember the tough talk from Biden of the toughest sanctions the world has ever seen. But at the same time, Putin looked at Ukraine and thought he could get away with it. And still believes he can get away with it.\par \par \uc2 He believes this will fall off the headlines in the United States, that we will become weary of it, and that the Europeans will start backsliding, and I fear this as well, as the winner gets cold, and as their factories start shutting down.\par \par \uc2 We have to look at Taiwan and China in the same way.\par \par \uc2 And a war over the Taiwan Strait will make what's happening in Ukraine and the effect on the global economy look like child's play.\par \par \uc2 How do we deter Xi from making this move? I was just out there. We have to arm Taiwan. I think we have to make it much more-clear in terms of our strategic clarity. And we have to get our supply chains out of there.\par \par \uc2 The big difference is that we no longer make pharmaceuticals in the United States, China could choke them off tomorrow. We no longer make mine lithium that will -- that will power this this green economy that the -- that the left dreams off. And on -- and on -- and on that they could choke off tomorrow. So there are things that we have to do now and we are running out of time. Xi has made it clear he's going to do it on his watch.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, on the supply chain issues, I feel like I've been hearing members of Capitol Hill talking about that for years. And I think people are waiting for some actual change.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Well, you're going to see it after November 8th.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We will we see.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: And we are going to have a select committee on China. We are going to make those steps.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's good to have you here. It's a sobering message. It's really important one and I thank you for coming.\par \par \uc2 WALTZ: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much. So coming up, Democrat Pennsylvania Senate candidate \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  now being called the Midterms' October surprise after a very rocky debate performance. He is defending this way.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: I may not get every word the right way but I will always do the right thing in Washington DC.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Guy Benson and Brian Kilmeade when "The Story" continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: From day one, once he had a stroke, I felt he should have dropped that. I don't think he's healthy enough to hold the office.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It almost made him seem as if he was not even qualified to do the functions of the office.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: His 