winism." This is what happens, Tucker, when autonomy just goes nuts.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I mean, you hate to -- and I really try never to invoke the Nazis, because, you know, for many reasons, but they're famous for doing this exact thing, use doctors to murder the weakest in your society. And I thought that doctors kind of agreed after the Second World War not to do things like this.\par \par \uc2 CAMOSY: Yes, you would think and I'm the same way, I think we should be very, very hesitant to use those arguments. But at a certain point, Tucker, if the shoe fits, the candidate has to wear it.\par \par \uc2 Now the question is, can we stop this from coming to the United States? It hasn't got here yet. And I think we can, for instance, New York, Connecticut, even deep blue states like that have not legalized even assisted suicide yet.\par \par \uc2 And so what we need to do, Tucker, and all your listeners need to do this is support in whatever way you can healthcare that is about caring, not killing.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAMOSY: In fact, there's a coalition of people -- there is a coalition of people coming up with a brand new medical school, the Padre Pio School of Medicine in 2026 that is going to do this very clearly, very ably and if others would like to join a coalition of people who are doing this, I urge them to send me a message through my website, CharlesCamosy.com or through \uc2 Twitter\uc2  because we have to get off the couch and do something about this for those of us that see the writing on the wall here.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Shouldn't the \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2  weigh in and just say unequivocally, doctors of the United States don't get to kill children just because children are depressed. A doctor's job is to help people, make them better, not to kill them, give up on them when they're kids.\par \par \uc2 CAMOSY: Well, as I wrote at length in "Losing Our Dignity," Tucker, we have a very, very different healthcare system than we had even 20 or 30 years ago. It is hyper secularized. It is often based on cost-benefit analysis, even something called quality adjusted life years, which is basically ableism.\par \par \uc2 We've had a lot of talk about ableism over the last couple of days. This is ableism right here and it is no accident that the disability rights groups are our biggest allies in this. We should continue to employ them as again, we get up off the couch here and fight.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes. Amen. Get off the couch and fight. That's exactly right.\par \par \uc2 Professor Charles Jamison, thank you as always.\par \par \uc2 More news, straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, we're out of time.\par \par \uc2 As noted, don't forget check out our documentary on Blake Masters, who all of a sudden is winning Arizona. It is on FOX Nation right now.\par \par \uc2 And of course, we'll be back tomorrow night. The show that is the sworn enemy of lying, pomposity, smugness, and groupthink.\par \par \uc2 Have a great night with the ones you love.\par \par \uc2 Here is Sean.\par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020221027eiaq00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Pennsylvania Senate Nominees Participate In Contest; Gov. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  (R-GA) Is Being Interviewed About The Top Issues Motivating Georgia Voters; Biden Reveals Plan To Tackle "Junk Fees"; Concerns Over Low Oil Reserves; Georgia Republican Senate Candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  Interviewed On New Accusation He Paid For Woman's Abortion; Polling Indicates Possibility That Republican Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  Will Win Reelection While Republican Senate Candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  May Lose To Democratic Incumbent \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 ; Georgia Voters Discuss Reasons For Their Support Of Democratic Or Republican Candidates; Analysts State Pennsylvania Democratic Senate Candidate \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  Had Poor Performance In Debate With Opponent Dr. Mehmet Oz\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jonathan Serrie; Martha MacCallum, Jacqui Heinrich \par \uc2 8,117 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Whose car was that?\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That's a total dude fest.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Whose car was that?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I have no idea.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Oh, was that Geraldo's car?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, it said Geraldo.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: It wasn't that Geraldo, it was a different Geraldo.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: There's only one Geraldo.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, I agree.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right, thank God. That's it for us, SPECIAL REPORT is on the road from Georgia.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Welcome to Acworth, Georgia. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we'll be focusing on two crucial midterm races in the Peach State now a battleground state again, one for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , the other for governor on the road in Georgia, this is SPECIAL REPORT.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in the next hour, with less than two weeks to go before the midterms, we will talk with Republican Senate nominee \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and incumbent Republican Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 . We offered equal time to send \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , but they declined our offer.\par \par \uc2 We will visit with Georgia voters in this hour to learn what issues are most important to them. And we'll hear from a special panel of local journalists covering these races.\par \par \uc2 We are in Acworth, Georgia in the suburban Atlanta Bellwether county of Cobb, just northwest of downtown.\par \par \uc2 Before we get into politics, let's take a look at other headlines today. \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  says the unprecedented leak of the draft decision repealing Roe vs. Wade put him and his colleagues in physical danger.\par \par \uc2 Alito says justices were targets for assassination. So far, no results have been made public from the investigation into that leak.\par \par \uc2 A judge here in Georgia is ordering former White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  to testify before a special grand jury investigating whether then President Trump and his allies illegally tried to influence the state's 2020 election. Meadows's attorney says executive privilege and other rights shield his client from testifying.\par \par \uc2 Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is monitoring drills of the country's strategic nuclear forces in a show of force amid the heightened tensions with the West over the conflict in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Russia's defense minister says the exercise is intended to simulate a massive nuclear strike by Russia in retaliation for a nuclear attack on his country.\par \par \uc2 Now, the midterms, we are in Georgia which was once a solidly red state but things here have changed. Georgia went for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in 2020 and sent two Democrats to the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  after a runoff that ended up determining the balance of power in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Now, Republicans are trying to regain control.\par \par \uc2 The latest Monmouth poll gives us a breakdown, a demographic breakdown of this year Senate contest. The potential voter pool favors Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  by five points. Same for 2020 voters. His lead among extremely motivated voters is just one point and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is up four among voters describing themselves as more enthusiastic.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Jonathan Serrie is in the southern part of the metro area in Union City, Georgia tonight with the latest. Good evening, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. They're setting up the stage for Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , her bus tour expected to stop by this community center later this evening. In a state where elections are now won by single digit margins, the campaigns are red hot, including new allegations against \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE (voice over): Represented by activist attorney Gloria Allred, a new unnamed woman has come forward claiming \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  drove her to an abortion clinic in 1993 after she became pregnant as a result of their relationship.\par \par \uc2 GLORIA ALLRED, ATTORNEY: Mr. Walker drove her to the clinic and waited in the parking lot for hours until the abortion was completed.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Walker is running as an anti-abortion conservative against a self- described pro-choice pastor. Clips of sermons used against Democrat \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  during his successful first Senate campaign are resurfacing on social media, including a sermon on racism in which Warnock says America needs to repent for its worship of whiteness.\par \par \uc2 In her second bid for governor, Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  is doubling down on her attacks on Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , who defeated her by less than 55,000 votes in 2018.\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D), GUBERNATORIAL NOMINEE OF GEORGIA: He spent four years attacking our freedoms, attacking our freedom to vote in the state of Georgia through SB202.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Republicans say SB202, Georgia's revised voting law is not discriminatory and facilitated record turnout in this midterm, with more than one million early ballots already cast.\par \par \uc2 In challenging Georgia election laws, Politico reports a nonprofit group founded by Abrams paid more than $9 million in legal fees to the law firm of Abrams's campaign chair. The group Fair Fight Action insists there was no conflict of interest. Analysts say with a polarized electorate, controversy surrounding individual campaigns may have less impact.\par \par \uc2 ANDRA GILLESPIE, PROFESSOR, EMORY UNIVERSITY: Mobilization is more important than persuasion, and so most voters have already made up their mind.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE (on camera): In Georgia, the leading candidate must surpass 50 percent of the vote in order to avoid a runoff with polls showing tight margins, especially in the Senate race. A December rematch is a distinct possibility, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jonathan Serrie just down the road in Union City. Jonathan, thanks,\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: -- tonight that you support fracking, that you've always supported fracking, but there is that 2018 interview that you said "I don't support fracking at all". So, how do you square the two?\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): I do support fracking. And I don't -- I don't -- I support fracking and I stand and I do support fracking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The first and only debate in the Pennsylvania Senate race held last night, it featured Democratic nominee Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  at times as you saw there struggling with words or phrases against Republican Mehmet Oz. Fetterman is as we told you recovering from a stroke, he suffered during the primary.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in the anchor and executive editor of the story here on Fox News, Martha MacCallum. She's in Harrisburg, Pennsylvania tonight with reaction. Good evening, Martha. There's all kinds of people talking about this debate.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Yes, I mean, just watching that again, it is hard to watch. I think everybody wanted -- you know, from a human perspective, you want him to be able to get those words out. But he clearly had a lot of difficulty. And that's been the assessment really across the board.\par \par \uc2 I spoke to an interesting group of voters today. Three of them were undecided. When they went into the debate last night, we watched them, ask them all to watch it and come to us with their thoughts. And it was very interesting, because my first question to them was after watching last night, do you believe that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  the Lieutenant Governor here is able to function, is he capable of being a U.S. senator? And here's what happened.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you think that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  based on what you saw last night is able to serve as a U.S. senator? If you say yes, raise your hand.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it was pretty clear, Bret. The woman who said yes is a Democrat. She is a committed Fetterman voter. And she said she believes that he will improve over time. And she gave him credit for having the courage to get up there.\par \par \uc2 But the rest of them, the other three who went in undecided last night all said that after that debate, they felt much more comfortable with the idea that they would probably support Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, Democrats also jumped on the one point about abortion. Here's an exchange with Mehmet Oz on that issue. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF PENNSYLVANIA: There should not be involvement from the federal government in how states decide their abortion decisions.\par \par \uc2 I want women, doctors, local political leaders, letting the democracy that's always allowed our nation to thrive to put the best ideas forward so states can decide for themselves.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Just the reaction to that on the ground, is there a sense?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It got a lot of attention. I think that from the Oz side, they're saying that the whole point of the answer is that it should not be in the hands of the federal government, that it should be a local, more close to the people vote that should dictate the abortion rules in any given place.\par \par \uc2 But the Fetterman folks tell us that they raised $2 million off of fundraising that was largely based on that answer from Dr. Oz. So, we'll see if it has an impact. It's clearly the one thing from the debate that they are hoping will give them some leverage.\par \par \uc2 And then you've got places like The Philadelphia Inquirer, where the opinion page said that they all you know, took a vote, score the debate, and they felt that Fetterman came out ahead.\par \par \uc2 A lot of folks have already voted here, Bret, so we'll see what impact last night really has in the end.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's right. Trains going by right now. I look forward to seeing you next week in Ohio for our Town Hall with JD Vance and Tim Ryan, it should be fun. Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We'll see you there. Thanks, Bret. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Governor Kemp, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 GOV. BRIAN KEMP (R-GA): Great to be on with you, Bret. Great to have you in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's good to be here. Grew up here and I know you know that but what's your closing argument here?\par \par \uc2 KEMP: I am just telling people thankfully I said no to \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , wanting to keep our economy closed. I said no to her wanting to keep our kids stuck in their basement on a computer screen and we got our kids back in the classroom and I certainly said no to her idea of defunding the police, eliminating cash bail and it's unbelievable she's sitting on the boards of organizations that are pushing to defund the police movement still to this day.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I tell people who's been fighting for you? You know, whether you're a Republican or a Democrat, when you look back over the last 3-1/2 years, and certainly over the two during the pandemic, who was fighting to keep your business open, and give you the ability to go to work every day, to get your kid in the best environment in the classroom to help them succeed now and in the future? Who has been fighting to keep you safe and stand with our men and women in law enforcement? And that's me, and that's why I'm asking people for their vote. And I'm going to keep fighting for them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, I would know you obviously have to get across the finish line here and we're coming up to Election Day. You also have a big race here with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  against Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 . You watched as two Senate races went down to Democrats and the balance of power turned to Democrats after that run off here in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Are you -- are you convinced that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is going to win? And are you -- do you see voters out there that are Kemp, Warnock voters?\par \par \uc2 KEMP: Well, I can tell you, Bret, what I'm working on is getting the whole ticket across the finish line. I mean, listen, the Democrats have been beating us on the ground here for several cycles. I watched that happen in 2020. And we're not going to let that happen to 2022. We've raised a lot of money. We've got a bunch of boots on the ground that are out there knocking on doors every single day to turn our base out.\par \par \uc2 But also, to go to the average voter that's out there, the independent and center left Democrats and remind them of what our record has been helping them fight the 40-year high inflation that's been caused by this president with \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  support. This is the agenda that she supports.\par \par \uc2 She worked hard to get him elected, she auditioned to be his vice presidential candidate. And they are squeezing hard work in Georgian, 60 percent of Americans right now are living week to week.\par \par \uc2 The Georgia State Government working with the general assembly myself, we suspended the gas tax here since March, saving Georgians $0.30 a gallon, we returned a billion dollars of their tax dollars. We've cut taxes, we're going to give them another billion dollars back when we get back in January and do property tax relief grants to help them fight through the 40-year high inflation that Biden has caused us.\par \par \uc2 And that's who has been fighting for them and I'm fighting to get our team across the finish line. We got a bunch of great partners on the ticket with us. And we're trying to help every single one of them, top to bottom.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, there's a lot of people turning out governor and we've seen now up to a million, maybe more early voter turnout. I'm going to play a quick soundbite for you, this is President Biden and his White House press secretary, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is Jim Crow on steroids what they're doing in Georgia, and 40 other states.\par \par \uc2 Jim Crow 2.0 is about two insidious things. Voter suppression and election subversion.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: High turnout and voter suppression can take place at the same time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How do you respond to that? I mean, that's the president, that's the White House press secretary this week saying these numbers don't mean it's not being suppressed.\par \par \uc2 KEMP: I think that is -- I think that's why they're losing the support of the middle, Bret. I mean, nobody thinks that is even remotely makes sense.\par \par \uc2 They tried to cancel this two years ago, I stood up and pushed back because I knew this legislation that was going to make it easy to vote and hard to cheat.\par \par \uc2 You know, even \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , who was one of the biggest critics along with the president, and a lot of you know, woke corporate CEOs that were trying to pressure us in Georgia. She's still making this narrative that it is suppressive in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Everybody won't mention the fact we added additional days for people to vote early in the state and they're taking advantage of it. What this is, is early voting on steroids right now, and I hope \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's paying attention to it because the people of Georgia are going to send a message that they are tired of politicians like \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  lying to them.\par \par \uc2 They may not like everything that I've done, but at least they know where I stand. And they know that I have not lied to them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They are showing up, we've talked to a lot early voters. Governor Kemp, we appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 KEMP: Thanks for having me, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Talked to Governor Kemp a short time ago, we want to point out that we did give \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  the opportunity to have equal time on the show. The Abrams campaign turned us down. We will talk to \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  live later on in the show.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll go live to the White House where President Biden is playing the blame game ahead of the midterms.\par \par \uc2 And later, as we said, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  will join us live but first, a look at who Cobb County voters wanted in the White House in the past.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're here in Marietta square. It's the county seat for Cobb County. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won this county in 2020. Before that, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  won here, the first Democrat to do so since \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Georgia overall is becoming pretty purple, which is why this state is in the middle of yet another election battle.\par \par \uc2 We'll talk to some voters here, SPECIAL REPORT continues after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A jury in Wisconsin has convicted a man of killing six people, injuring dozens of others when he drove his SUV into a Christmas parade last year.\par \par \uc2 Darrell Brooks was found guilty of all 76 charges, including six counts of first degree intentional homicide. He drove into the parade in Waukesha last November 21st after fleeing a domestic disturbance with a former girlfriend.\par \par \uc2 New Jersey Democratic Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2  says he does not know the scope of a new federal investigation into his activities. An advisor to the Senate Foreign Relations Committee chairman released that statement this afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Media reports say the U.S. Attorney's Office for the Southern District of New York is conducting the probe. Seven years ago, Menendez was indicted on corruption charges. His trial ended with a hung jury and charges were dismissed.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. attorney investigating \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  is being asked to include more than 200 pages of bank records involving the president's son and his alleged connections to China.\par \par \uc2 Senators \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2  and Ron Johnson say they are giving Delaware U.S. Attorney David Weiss, copies of the records related to the Chinese government, military, and intelligence community.\par \par \uc2 Fox News Digital independently reviewed those records from \uc2 Cathay Bank\uc2 , which was founded by the Chinese Americans. \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  has not been charged with a crime, but has been under federal investigation since at least 2018.\par \par \uc2 Well, as the midterm elections approach and grow closer, the blame game is growing more intense at the White House. President Biden is trying to make voters believe his political problems are being caused by an assortment of opponents and circumstances.\par \par \uc2 The big issue remains the economy. White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has the story tonight.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): The White House has no plans to adjust its economic team after the midterms, but it can't say when Americans can expect relief from inflation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I'm not going to get into what -- when that's going to occur.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The president trying to make improvements around the margins, cracking down on companies that charge hidden fees and overdraft fees.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And each year, these junk fees in addition that companies charge, cost America's 10s of billions of dollars, weighing down family budgets --\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But it's not yet clear when Americans will feel that impact or how big it will be. Energy costs continuing to be a key driver of inflation still running near 40-year highs.\par \par \uc2 The president claims gas costs are approaching pre-pandemic levels. That's not quite true. Before COVID, a gallon of regular cost around $2.51, much less than the $3.76 consumers are paying on average right now. Biden blamed big oil.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And they're going to come down even further when gas companies -- when the oil companies agree to our demand. My demand to pass on the savings from the price of a barrel oil.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: A no comment from Biden on the Saudis warning not to sell strategic oil reserves.\par \par \uc2 ABDULAZIZ BIN SALMAN AL SAUD, SAUDI ENERGY MINISTER: It is my profound duty to make it clear to the world that losing emergency stock may become painful in the months to come.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: And it's not clear what that could mean as winter approaches.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Is there any indication that they plan to further cut production?\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL: I'd have to refer you to them, Jacqui. I don't have insight into what, you know, what their future decisions will or won't be.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The president said he would keep selling the country's emergency oil reserves to manage inflated gas prices. But it's unclear what he would do when it comes to oil. The U.S. is now down to a 25-day reserve supply. And high diesel costs factor into high cost of goods because it gets passed through because of shipping charges.\par \par \uc2 And yet, the Democrats reportedly only spent about $10 million on inflation ads ahead of the midterms, even though they dumped 10 times that amount into abortion ads. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich, live on the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks. Stocks were mixed today. The Dow was up two. The S&P 500 finished off 28-1/2. The NASDAQ lost 228.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we will talk live with the Republican nominee for Georgia for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . But first, look inside a local favorite.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Marietta Diner. It's a staple here in Cobb County since 1995. Been serving breakfast, lunch, and dinner 24 hours a day. There's 500 items on the menu. Politics is also on the menu. Candidates come here to try to sway voters. We've talked to a bunch of tables, a lot of them have already early voted.\par \par \uc2 Question is, in each race, will a candidate get to 50 percent or more? Because if not, in Georgia, they'll have to do it all again in a run-off in December.\par \par \uc2 Especially report continues after this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT on the road, Acworth, Georgia. One of the races that will decide which party controls the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  is right here in Georgia. Earlier we showed you the real clear politics average that has Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , the Democratic incumbent, up just barely in the average of polls. He is trying to hold on to his seat against \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , who is a legend in this state thanks to his performance as a running back with the national championship Georgia football team in 1980, also won the Heisman Trophy in 1982. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is joining us live tonight. Herschel, thanks for the time.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , (R-GA) U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously, we have to address the elephant in the room today, and that is another accuser has come forward. Anonymous, although she spoke to reporters today saying that she -- that you pressured her to have an abortion and that you paid for it. How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, that's a lie. And I hope people can see right now that \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and the left will do whatever they can to win this seat back. But I don't think they realize that they're messing with the wrong Georgian here, that I'm not going to stop. This seat is too important for me to stop, thinking that they can try to scare me or force me out of this seat, because they are not. I hope the people saw after that debate who is the guy ready to represent the people of Georgia, whereas \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is there to represent \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, well, this was Gloria Allred came forward with this client. You're saying you don't know this person?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: What I'm saying is this a lie. And I have said it once and I have moved on. My campaign has moved on because we're worried about what the Georgia people are talking about. They are talking about this inflation. They are talking about crime. They are talking about men and women sports. They are talking about this border. And right now, after the terrible showing that Senator Warnock did at the debate, they are desperate right now. And I have said this is a lie. I've moved on. And they want me to play these guessing games and all of this. But I'm not. I'm not into that. I'm in to winning this great seat, fight for the great people of Georgia, because that's what this is about.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I hear you. I hear your answer. I'm not going to belabor this, but I'm going to play this soundbite from the press conference today, and then I'm going to ask you a question on the other side.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He has publicly taken the position that he is, quote, "about life," end quote, and against abortion, under any circumstances when, in fact, he pressured me to have an abortion, and personally ensured that it occurred by driving me to the clinic and paying for it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That came forward with letters that they said were signed by you and a voicemail that they said was your voice calling from the Olympics in 1992.\par \par \uc2 So last thing on this. I talked to a number of voters today. Some of them, these accusations affected them, to be honest. They said -- they just weren't sure, and they didn't know if another shoe was going to drop. What do you say to those voters?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, what I say to those voters is if you are hesitant about voting for me, think about what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  have done in less than two years. They have given you this high inflation. They have given you open border. They have given you men in women sports. They've given you crime on the street. And right now they will do whatever they can and say whatever they can to win power back for this seat.\par \par \uc2 But not on my watch. They can try to lie, and I told everyone this is a lie, and it's not a lie. I'm here to fight for the Georgia people. And they know it. They are scared right now because of his terrible showing, because he was supposed to show me up at the debate, and he saw that he didn't do it. And now they are scared to death because right now they spent $60- something million dollars against me, and they're still virtually tied. I'm ahead, or he is ahead by one.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We should point out that we, like we did with \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , offered \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , the Senator, time, equal time with you. You have come on today, even on this day when these allegations have come out. And I'm not going to keep going down that road because I've heard your answer now a number of times.\par \par \uc2 That is your closing argument though, is that the bigger close that Republican control of the Senate is more important in Georgia than what likely you are going to have to deal with in these allegations in the final days?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Well, what I'm going to have to deal with and what the people are concerned about, and what the people are concerned about is crime on the streets. Right now, rape is up in Atlanta over 200 percent. Homicide is up over 47 percent. Right now we have open border that they seem to not care about. The vice president said the border is secure. Right now inflation is off the hook. And right now we've got a military, they are bringing pronouns in our military. He's called our men and women thugs and bullies, our police officers. And the morale is down, recruitment is down. I'm about solving problems, not coming up with excuses. It seems like that's what they are doing.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , we appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Hey, thank you now, and God bless.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): America has a preexisting condition. It's called racism.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , (R-GA) U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: Senator Warnock believes America is a bad country full of racist people.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: For you, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  wants to ban abortion. But for himself --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  paid for an abortion for his then girlfriend.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  made abortion a crime before many women even know they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The only way to stop this attack on the women of Georgia is to stop \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  backs \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  totally. \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  wants to double down on failure. Don't let \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  do to Georgia what they have done to America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You can't go two minutes on TV here without seeing one of those ads, ads from the four candidates on the top of the ticket.\par \par \uc2 Let's get some local perspective tonight from two Georgia journalists. Bill Barrow is a national politics reporter for the Associated Press based in Atlanta, and Shannon McCaffrey is a politics reporter for "The Atlanta- Journal Constitution" newspaper. Thank you both for being here. Shannon, let me start with you. Obviously, this accusation comes today. It's new. It's the second. You heard \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 's answer there. Your thoughts -- he didn't get specific on those specific allegations, but he denies them right now.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON MCCAFFREY, "ATLANTA JOURNAL-CONSTITUTION": Yes. He has been consistent throughout this campaign with taking from the Trump playbook and double down. You do not apologize. You do not explain. You double down. And I think it worked for him at least for the first allegation of an abortion that was about a month ago. So he is using it again.\par \par \uc2 And I think for his core supporters, it's all just noise to them. There are some independents in the middle who I think are going to hear this and it is going to give some pause when you see the cumulative effect of another allegation. But it's very consistent. As long as he can make this about control of the Senate, he's winning in his mind.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill, we talked earlier about it. And whether it moves the needle here -- I did talk to some voters that had pause, as I mentioned. But your thoughts on it?\par \par \uc2 BILL BARROW, "ASSOCIATED PRESS": This allegation, I think Shannon is right, talking about the cumulative effect. It plays into the way that \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  wants this race to be framed for voters, which is an individual choice here in Georgia between two men, Senator Warnock and Mr. Walker.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is there a split ticket voter out there that is a Kemp-Warnock voter?\par \par \uc2 BARROW: We have found those voters. The polls certainly suggest there is maybe a surprisingly large number of them. A lot of Republican and Democratic strategists you talk to say, look, when all the votes are counted, the gap between the two races really may not be that wide. But this is such a closely divided battleground, that Kemp-Warnock voter could make the difference in both races where you could conceivably see a split result.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Walker campaign put out a statement. It was pretty detailed, and it referenced \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  and those hearings. And for Republicans that sends off alarm bells. Gloria Allred was that as well, and the allegations didn't come to fruition. Perhaps that's some of the pushback, too.\par \par \uc2 MCCAFFREY: Yes, and you heard those allegations from \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  who was out with him on the campaign trail today, too. I do think that's a little bit of a dog whistle to speak. It just evokes that same attitude and those -- that same mood from that time period in which there was such polarization. And I do think it will be effective with some Republicans who will see it as a symbol that they need to get out there and back him.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last thing on the Kemp-Abrams race. It seems like there's a separation here, obviously in Georgia and both races you've got to get over 50 to avoid a runoff. Otherwise, you will be back in these seats in December, if you will come. Is he going to get there over 50? Does it look like it?\par \par \uc2 BARROW: He certainly could. I hesitate to make predictions. One thing that's notable about that race, though. Four years ago, that race, even though Governor Kemp won, that was a race fought on \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  terms. She was the clear national superstar, outraised him by a wide margin, had national media attention all the way through. And he fought through that and won.\par \par \uc2 This time he is the incumbent. She is still a national star, still raises a lot of money. But this race is being fought on his terms. She is the challenger trying to convince Georgians not to elect her to an open seat but to fire \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  and hand it over to her. And that's just harder to win.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and just 10 seconds, if Warnock and Walker go to a runoff, does it change the dynamics of the rails or is it still the same dynamic?\par \par \uc2 MCCAFFREY: Every runoff here, I believe, we have seen a lot of examples, has its own dynamics. But I think runoffs are about turnout. And I think if control of the Senate is at stake, that's when all bets are off. It's not going to be making new arguments. It's going to be getting their voters out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, thank you both for coming. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BARROW: Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, I'll talk with Georgia voters about the midterm campaign when we come back. But first, a trip to an old ballpark.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The World Series starts this Friday on big FOX between the \uc2 Philadelphia Phillies\uc2  and the \uc2 Houston Astros\uc2 . The current World Series champions, the \uc2 Atlanta Braves\uc2 , this is their home park. \uc2 Truist\uc2  park in Cobb County, Georgia. It's also the park where they were going to have the All- Star Game last year until it was moved to Colorado because of protests over Georgia's voting rights law. That law is in place, and now early voting is breaking all kinds of records. More than 1 million Georgians have already voted in these midterms.\par \par \uc2 SPECIAL REPORT continues after this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT on the road at Acworth, Georgia, suburban Atlanta. This is a crucial area for the two big contests, the U.S. and governor. Earlier today I spoke with a number of voters around here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: For a third straight year, Georgia is in the spotlight during an election season. In 2020, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  narrowly defeated \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by 0.23 percent. The close margin sparked a recount. Both 2020 Senate races resulted in runoffs and were held in 2021. Democrats were victorious, winning both seats with \uc2 Jon Ossoff\uc2  and \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  defeating both Republican incumbents.\par \par \uc2 DONNA ROWE, GEORGIA VOTER: Georgians got it when we didn't go out to vote in January. We got it then.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Republicans like former Army nurse Donna Rowe are confident that a strong turnout and the economic concerns will help flip Georgia's Senate seat back in their favor.\par \par \uc2 ROWE: I met a young girl that couldn't afford to buy ham hamburger because she wanted hamburger for dinner for her four children and her husband and her, and she was crying. And I said, what's the matter? And she said, I can't afford any of this. I can't afford to feed my children hamburger tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As you look at the state of Georgia right now and these races, how crucial do you think they are, and do you think people are getting it?\par \par \uc2 ROWE: This country is 60 percent almost over the cliff. And Georgia has a great sense of wanting to save this country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rowe spends much of her time working with other veterans in Cobb County. She worked as an Army nurse for five years and served in Vietnam for just more than a year.\par \par \uc2 ROWE: They fought for our freedom, Bret, that I see slipping away from us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Her background serving in the military has made her concerned for other veterans and believes the current environment in the Defense Department, the so-called woke culture, is discouraging enrollment.\par \par \uc2 ROWE: You don't need that in the military. We all bleed red. Whether we wear green or blue wearing a uniform, it doesn't matter what color we are.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Charlie Darrien owns a movie business located in Cobb County, also believes the economy is a top issue.\par \par \uc2 What's driving your vote?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE DARRIEN, GEORGIA VOTER: I would like to see somebody that is helping me to combat some of the things that I'm dealing with from a business standpoint, which I can't find employees right now. Nobody wants to work.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: She says the Republican agenda aligns with her business goals, but finds it difficult to back candidates who don't support abortion access. She is still unsure who she plans to support for Senate or governor.\par \par \uc2 DARRIEN: There are different points that are equally as important, but it's important. It's up there. So I'm actually trying to still kind of come to my decision with who is going to get my final vote.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Voters are also hoping to avoid a repeat of election chaos that followed the 2020 election which resulted in trials and challenges. Democratic gubernatorial candidate \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  has advocated for more access to polling locations. Titus Nichols plans to vote for her and says voter suppression is one of his top concerns.\par \par \uc2 TITUS NICHOLS, GEORGIA VOTER: You still have people stand in line for a ridiculously long amount of time, and you have fewer mail drop boxes than did you in the 2020 elections.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nichols serves in the Army National Guard and works as personal injury number. Despite high numbers for early voting in Georgia, he is still concerned recent efforts by Georgia Republicans to change election laws will hurt voter turnout.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: We saw after 2020 Georgia politicians were using it as an excuse to pass laws that are infringing and making it more difficult for someone to cast a ballot.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Nichols says he supports both Democratic candidates both hopes that Senator Warnock wins and advocates for a more liberal agenda.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLS: Having Warnock in office will allow Democrats to maintain control of the Senate, so you can pass more laws that are going to help increase, such as minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Financial analyst Jake Gajda has not fully supported either candidate in the Senate election but plans to back Governor Kemp for another term. He believes the incumbent has done a good job with inflation but wants to see more progress on lowering costs.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What drives you? What are you concerned about?\par \par \uc2 JAKE GAJDA, GEORGIA VOTER: I would say the economy is the biggest thing, inflation. I now drive 30 minutes to work and gas prices have gone up more than ever, especially in the city. It's a crazy price. So, yes, I'm looking for those to come down.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gajda says recent controversies surrounding \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  could lose him support among moderates. He also says divisive political rhetoric is making it difficult to pick a candidate.\par \par \uc2 GAJDA: The ads are very dirty on both sides. It's definitely huge and going to be a big deciding factor of how I vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Our thanks to those voters. I talked to a lot of others who did the early vote thing already.\par \par \uc2 Up next, our SPECIAL REPORT panel on a number of races -- Pennsylvania, New York. We'll take you around the country.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ, (R) PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: Are you unhappy with where America is headed? I am. And if you are as well, then I'm the candidate for change.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 , (D) PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: I'm running to serve Pennsylvania. He's running to use Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2 , (D) MICHIGAN: Mrs. Dixon says that I kept student out longer than any other state. That's just not true. Kids were out for three months.\par \par \uc2 TUDOR DIXON, (R) MICHIGAN GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: I'm pretty sure I just heard an audible gasp around town. We even had schools that were closed this year.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2 , (R-NY): You've got people who are afraid of being pushed in front of oncoming subway cars. They're being stabbed and beaten to death on the street with hammers.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2 , (D) NEW YORK: Anyone who commits a crime under our laws, especially with the change we made to bail, has consequences. I don't know why that is so important to you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bouncing around the country there. Meantime, the focus was on the Pennsylvania Senate debate last night. "Axios" writing "Fetterman's painful debate," quote, "Why the hell did Fetterman agree to this? One Democrat lawmakers and Fetterman backer told "Axios" this will obviously raise more questions than answers about John's health. Another Pennsylvania Democratic official worried. "Everyone is nervous. I traveled everywhere. Fetterman is a deep concern, and this debate will only increase it."\par \par \uc2 One perspective. Let's bring in our panel, FOX News senior political analyst Brit Hume, \uc2 Mara Liasson\uc2 , national political correspondent of National Public Radio, and syndicated radio host \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 . Brit, there is a lot to digest, but let's start in Pennsylvania. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I have been watching this and covering this stuff for more than a half a century, and I have never seen anything like that performance by Fetterman last night. I'm hearing some commentary in his defense that says, look, he is just going to be elected in the Senate, and the only thing that matters in the Senate is whether you can sit on the Senate floor and vote yea or nay.\par \par \uc2 But anybody who has ever covered the place knows there's a lot more to it than that. You have to debate on the Senate floor to advance be your points, to defend them. The same is true if you are called upon to offer amendments. Say you have an amendment to a bill that's very important to your constituents. You've got to be able to make the case for it in committee. You have got to be able to defend it. You've got to do the same thing on the Senate floor. You have to be able to engage your colleagues outside the Senate chambers and outside in the hallways and in their offices to round up support for what you are doing. All of those things are things that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , based on what we saw last night, is not now capable of doing. It's pretty striking.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here is another roundabout of reaction to the reviews. Mara, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS, (D-DE): I watched the debate last night. It was hard to watch frankly.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: For anyone who is coming into this totally undecided, it's hard to see them coming away terribly, terribly convinced by Fetterman.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Overwhelmingly people will watch that and see that he is still struggling from the fallout of his stroke. I think that much is obvious.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mara?\par \par \uc2 MARA LIASSON\uc2 , NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, NATIONAL PUBLIC RADIO: Yes, look, there is no one who said Fetterman did a good job last night. It was awful. However, the big question remains how much is it going to hurt him? Maybe we are in a new era of politics where nothing matters. We're so tribal we just want to make sure that the guy, as Brit said, can sit on the Senate floor and raise his hand for your team.\par \par \uc2 We haven't seen a lot of polling. We haven't seen hardly any public polling since the debate that tells us how much this has hurt Fetterman or how much it's helped Oz. Remember, Oz went into this debate with very, very low favorable ratings. He needed to make himself more appealing to Pennsylvania voters. We don't know if he did that. He certainly won the debate on points because Fetterman just lost it. But we don't know how it's affected the race yet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hugh, I have seen your twitter feed. I think you are a little more bullish on Republican chances.\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: I think last night was catastrophic, and not just for \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 's campaign. Down ticket in Pennsylvania, it was disastrous. But I think for candidates who are similarly positioned to Fetterman, like \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  who is hard left progressive in Georgia and Mandela Barnes who is a hard left progressive in Wisconsin, it destroyed their campaigns as well because Fetterman inelegantly attempted to repudiate their entire agenda, thus distancing himself from fracking and from crime and the border.\par \par \uc2 I think it also hurts Democrats who are kind of invisible, like Tim Bennett in Colorado and -- Michael Bennet in Colorado or \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  in Nevada, or even \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  in New Hampshire where Don Bolduc is. Fetterman's fiasco will be a defining event of this sequence of elections, I think. I think it has knock on effects everywhere, it was so bad for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And finally, Brit, here we are covering the \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  race. We had Walker on earlier in the show. His campaign put out this statement in response to the latest accusations, referencing the \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  days, and I talked about that earlier. But your thoughts about this and the final days of this campaign?\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, Bret, the landscape that we see with the issues and the unpopularity of the president and the fact of his first midterm all say Republican sweep and a big Republican sweep. Republicans have been coming up in the polls, but the polls don't say that. If you get a big Republican sweep, all kinds of these people are going to win. In Georgia, the candidate there will win. Out in Nevada, the candidate there will win. In Arizona, the candidate there will win for the Senate.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We will cover it all.\par \par \uc2 HUME: So there you go.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There you go. There's the prediction. Panel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to the 1885 Grill here in Acworth for hosting us. Thank you for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts right now. Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020221027eiaq00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Pennsylvania Senatorial Candidates \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  And Mehmet Oz Had Their First And Only Debate Two Weeks Before The Election, And Fetterman Did Not Perform Well, And Democrats Blame It To The Captioning System Used By Fetterman; Republican Gubernatorial Candidate \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  Attack Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  Over Her Poor Policy On Crime And For Allowing Criminals To Get Back On The Streets Without Bail; Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio- Cortez\uc2  Is Pointing The Fingers On Latino Voters For Not Doing Extra Effort To Advance Their Votes Against Republicans; Dems Panic Over Losing Support From Latino Voters; Sidewalk Service: Homeless Are Looking Up Washing Machines To Power Lines; Top Phrases That Annoying People Use\b0\par\par\uc2 Jesse Watters, Jeanine Pirro, Harold Ford, Jr., Dana Perino, Greg  Gutfeld \par \uc2 8,700 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Got it.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-VA): A bill that recognizes that a baby can feel pain, and I will sign that bill, and I think that's a place Virginia can land.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Governor and Mrs. Michael, thank you very, very much. We'll see you tomorrow. Here's THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: Hello, everybody. I'm Jesse Watters, along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, \uc2 Harold Ford\uc2 , Jr., Dana Perino and Greg Gutfeld. There he is. It's five o'clock in New York City, and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 Democrats in panic mode after a terrible debate performance could cost them control of the Senate. Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz clashing in their one and only debate before Pennsylvania voters cast their ballots. And it was a total train wreck for the left, Fetterman, who's recovering from a stroke, not only stumbling through his answers, but flip flopping on key issues. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), PENNSYLVANIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: Hi. Goodnight, everybody. And let's also talk about the elephant in the room. I had a stroke. He's never let me forget that. And my Dr. L believes that I'm fit to be serving, and that's what I believe is where I'm standing.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R), PENNSYLVANIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , during this crime wave, has been trying to get as many murderers convicted and sentenced to life in prison, out of jail as possible.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I believe that I run on my record on crime. I'm the only person on this stage right now that has can -- can, is successful about pushing back against gun violence.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: But there is that 2018 interview that you said, quote, "I don't support fracking at all." So how do you square the two?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I do support fracking and I don't, I don't. I support fracking and I stand and I do support fracking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Fetterman's camp blaming the captioning system provided for him as being delayed and filled with errors, but that quickly got shut down. Team Fetterman also dropping F bombs to spin the awful performance as a win.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE CARVELLO, FETTERMAN COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: I think the people of Pennsylvania saw a relatable guy who's been doing his recovery in public for quite some time, and for a guy who was just in the ho, you know, in the hospital months ago took it to Dr. Oz pretty (muted) tonight.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And Democrats and the liberal media having a hard time convincing themselves that the party just didn't totally blow it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: It was hard to watch. Frankly.\par \par \uc2 OZ: It's very obvious that he is impaired.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: And maybe the next cycle would have been the better cycle for him to run. Because we require representation.\par \par \uc2 SUNNY HOSTIN, CO-HOST, ABC: I think it takes real bravery to allow people to see your weakness. Right?\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: There was such intense scrutiny, often ableist, scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Republicans are out there tonight saying that Fetterman is unfit, he should withdraw, but do they not have a race in the state of Georgia, someone running for the very same position in \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  who's facing family scandals, lies about his past jobs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Greg, that was hard to watch. Did you feel bad for \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  up there?\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CO-HOST: No, I felt bad for Oz.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Did you?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. He was terrible. No, I -- no, I actually had -- I think Oz did a good, I mean, it was like -- it was like having bad Wi-Fi, you know, Fetterman just kept going in and going out. I felt kind of bad for him, but he could have diffused the bomb much better if he just said, you know, when he said elephant in the room. He should have said, look, my mind works fine, but my body's catching up. I feel like I'm a dial up modem right now in a 5G world.\par \par \uc2 Make a joke. But there wasn't a joke to diffuse the situation and this ableist scrutiny B.S. Look, pretending to be concerned or disgusted or, because people are talking about that. You put him out there. You put him out there, you're allowing everybody to watch this, so it's on you.\par \par \uc2 If you say people are being ableist, we don't mind Dan Crenshaw. Right? We don't mind Governor Abbott. So, we're not ableist. We are talking about is this guy fit for the job? And there's a reason why you're mad because you didn't want him to debate because it looks so bad. And now you have him out there. Now you're trying to pin it on everybody else.\par \par \uc2 But I did, I felt like I was watching Lia Thomas swimming with, you know, the girl swim team. It just wasn't fair. But I think that Oz did pretty good. He -- I felt like he was letting up on the gas when it got bad.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You did?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. I thought so.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You thought that was smart.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: OK.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Dana, we're actually -- we haven't shown you basically everything. I mean, you could look at every single answer and be like, my God, that was horrible.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CO-HOST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: What happens now?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: So, we were on a little text thread last night and whatever stream I was watching was about, I think two minutes behind where you guys were. And so, I was like, and I, so I was anticipating, I'm like, wow, that was bad. Like, I could -- I could see what was coming.\par \par \uc2 So, I think a couple of things. One, I think it's notable that Dr. Oz did not say anything about the stroke. He agreed to the closed captioning. He allowed that to happen. The second thing is, they started early voting in September, September 19th, I believe for this election. Over, way over a million votes have already been cast.\par \par \uc2 And there was a NewsNation that the -- the host of the debate, they had a focus group and there were some Democrats there who, and leaning, and independents that were leaning Democrat who were already thinking, boy, wait, what have we done?\par \par \uc2 And that doesn't necessarily mean that they would've voted for Dr. Oz. Maybe they would've, but they might have chosen \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2 , who was the other Democrat who was running in the primary.\par \par \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 's stroke was before the primary.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CO-HOST: Right.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Then he and his wife kept telling everybody, he's fine, he's fine, he's fine. Remember like for about eight weeks. And now they're saying, there are some Democrats saying, maybe he never should have debated anyway. I think that's wrong.\par \par \uc2 Like if you aren't really concerned about democracy and you want integrity in elections, I think debates are important. And as you move forward in the states and thinking about these rules, perhaps early voting should not be allowed to start until after the first debate has been held. I think that would be fair.\par \par \uc2 The next thing is I think this team, his team needs to get their story straight. The first thing they came out and said, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  did a great job, strong performance, and that guy uses the F word, right? Say how great it was.\par \par \uc2 Then the next thing they do is they try to say that the technology was to blame for the stumbles, and that forced the company Nexstar to come out and say, actually it worked perfectly fine. This is what they agreed to, and all of that.\par \par \uc2 If Republicans win in Pennsylvania, they're going to get the majority. So I think that's why you have a lot of Democrats sort of hand ringing this morning. But aside from the stroke, I do want to say I think that it was a bad policy debate for Fetterman on energy.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Horrible.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: On the economy and on crime, in particular, student debt, all of those it -- on the issues it wasn't strong either.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Really, really bad. And he was asked by the moderator twice, and I think the moderators did a pretty good job. Are you going to release your medical records? And he still, after this, will not release the records.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: He refuses to release the records. Then look, the guy had a stroke, I think this is five months ago. OK? Many people who have strokes are right now in rehab, in therapy, in cognitive therapy. It is clear that he did not have the ability to answer anything beyond the most -- the most, you know, well, how can I say this? The superficial.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Part of it. There was no in depth, no substantive discussion.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Those slogans.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: He could not go down to A, B, C, or D. And so, he promotes himself saying, See, I showed how good I am, how great I'm feeling. I don't know that anyone who watched that debate is saying to themselves, gee, that's the guy I want to represent me in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 This guy couldn't answer why he's against -- was against fracking four years ago, but now is in favor of it. And you could say it's a stroke and you know, God bless him. I hope he gets better. But we're talking about the Senate. We're talking about a country that's divided, a country where everything matters. Every fact. Every issue.\par \par \uc2 You just don't look at the subs, the superficial layer. You've got to go down. He's got a progressive socialist ideology where he wants to release one third of the criminals in prison. He wants to get rid of parole for lifers who are in jail.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is a guy who is not in the mainstream and there's no question. September 19th, they started voting and now he has a debate on October 25th, whether it's 650,000 or a million have already voted. They can't change that vote.\par \par \uc2 And there's one more thing I want to say. You know, it just reminded me of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Remember \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , he was in the basement. We've got COVID and his wife Jill is walking him around.\par \par \uc2 I feel like the same thing is happening here with Fetterman's wife saying, he is fine, he's fine. Maybe, maybe you should be taking better care of him. Maybe he should be in rehab. Maybe this isn't the time for him to be running. You know? That's just my opinion.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And of all candidates, it's Fetterman that \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  are going to go to Pennsylvania together and campaign with together. What does that tell?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD\uc2 , JR., FOX NEWS CO-HOST: Well, I think you -- I think you framed it right. This race here could very well determine and decide who is in the majority in the Senate. It was painful to watch. There is no doubt. This is a person, a human being who clearly is battling his recovery and battling with the recovery to get through it. And you have to hope and pray he does get through it.\par \par \uc2 But I'm not sure it affects the outcome in any -- in any meaningful way. I think that the issues here that to Dana raised, are issues that he's been campaigning on from the very beginning. And he won the primary. He's now sounds, seems like he's three or four points ahead here and as we get to the November here.\par \par \uc2 I do think an argument should be made about, can be made about this early vote piece. But voters who don't want to go vote early, don't have to go vote early. If Oz had been -- had had a poor performance, some may have said, well, maybe, maybe I should have held my vote back to see how he made it performed or how he may have fared during the debate.\par \par \uc2 Look, Obama, Biden, everyone is going to descend upon this state and frankly, for Democrats what they're really hoping is that Shapiro, the gubernatorial candidate, the Democratic gubernatorial candidate, who I think is the best democratic gubernatorial candidate in the country, can help pull Fetterman, over the -- over the finish line as well.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You want Fetterman in the Senate?\par \par \uc2 FORD: Well, I don't. What I do want or the -- some of his views and some of his ideas, I don't agree with all of it. I was more --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: He's got crazy ideas. You just heard how crazy they are.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Well, I was -- I was more of someone else in that race. I was support above in the primary, his politics, that politics of \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  was more like mine.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD: That being said --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: He is a --\par \par \uc2 FORD: I'm not sure this affected the out -- this is not going to influence the outcome of this race very much. I'd like this debate.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: He's a wounded animal that can't cut it in Washington.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And that's the sad part of it.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : That is the sad part. I'm not saying I'd agree with him. That is the reality of it.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right. Ahead, the other Democratic debate disaster. New York's governor attacks her opponent for wanting to put criminals behind bars.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: but we don't. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  was the only Democrat to embarrass themselves during a debate. New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  forced to confront surging crime during her matchup against Republican \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 . She was completely baffled by the concept of jailing criminals.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): You can either work on keeping people scared or you can focus on keeping them safe. I have worked hard to have real policies that are making a difference.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R-NY): \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  believes that the only crimes that are being committed are these crimes with guns. And you, you have people who are afraid of being pushed in front of oncoming subway cars. They're being stabbed, beaten to death on the street with hammers. With what we're halfway through the debate, she still hasn't talked about locking up anyone committing any crimes.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: OK. It is --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: Anyone who commits a crime under our laws, especially with the change we made to bail has consequences. I don't know why that's so important to you. All I know is that we could do more.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Can't blame that on a stroke. And the ladies of The View seem to be stumped on why people even care about crime.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOSTIN: And I think most people, having been a prosecutor, I know this, they care about violent crimes. They don't care about broken window crimes. They really don't care about that as much.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Well, they care --\par \par \uc2 HOSTIN: They care about violent crimes.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: In the last few months, \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  was nearly stabbed on stage while campaigning in New York and bullet came within 30 --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HOSTIN: Well, he's (Inaudible). That's true.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: That's not OK though. That's horrifying.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HOSTIN: No, I'm not saying it's OK. I mean, he is --\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: That means crime and political violence (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: God. If we didn't have The View, we'd have huge blank spaces in our show. It's incredible. You know, Dana, that's, I think -- I think Zeldin kind of helped expose the problem with this governor who wasn't elected, is that she's disconnected with what -- with real concerns.\par \par \uc2 I think, do you remember that when crime was happening, she was -- her big thing was pronouns. She was able to get the SUNY campuses to enforce their pronoun laws. Do you remember the real estate pronoun laws? That's what she -- those were what she called the victims were the pronouns.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And it was also very interesting. I felt -- I felt that he -- his energy matched the voter's concern.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Right? And that hers did not because hers seems like sitting back. She not see the polls where the number one issue in New York for -- to voters is crime. And then it went down a little bit long -- lower than that is the economy and inflation.\par \par \uc2 So that should have been something I think that she should have been able to match the concern of the voter with her energy. She did not do that. I thought his answer was very good as well when in talking about all the other types of crimes that you see, and people are very much worried about broken windows crimes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And we talk about it all the time.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's the philosophy that when you let that stuff go, everything else comes up.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Also, how could Hochul say that these criminals end up with consequences when with cash free bail. We've seen it over and over again. How many times do we tell a story like, well, this person now, this victim was killed. It turns out, guess what? He had a rap sheet a mile long and just got out last month after a burglary charge.\par \par \uc2 The other -- the other thing I would point out is when she says to Zeldin on crime, I don't know why that's so important to you. It reminded me of when, do you remember \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  in Virginia running against Glenn Youngkin said, I don't think parents should tell teachers what to teach their kids.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That line was a gift to the Zeldin campaign if they use it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes. It's a very what? Judge, it's like, it's a very, what difference does it make.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You know?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But it's part of who \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  is. I call her the accidental governor. I mean, no one voted her in. But it is this arrogant kind of condescending, you know, let the people eat cake. You know, why you --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You make a cake.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, I did make a cake today, but that's another issue. You know, but why, why are you so concerned about it? You know, with the changes, I don't know why that's so important to you.\par \par \uc2 This woman is clueless. She is clueless. If you listen to the whole debate, and I did, there was very little she said about what she was going to do. She didn't talk about the economy, crime, taxes, or energy. What she talked about was the fact that we're putting, we are paying $600 million for the Buffalo bill stadium, of which I believe her husband has a piece that doesn't own the actual concession.\par \par \uc2 And the other piece that she talked about is the COVID mask. When \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  brought it up where she paid 637 million or whatever that is, double the amount of masks everywhere else in the country, and she got a big contribution.\par \par \uc2 This is a woman who's part of the pay for play corruption in Albany. \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  was on his game. He talked every issue, whether it was crime, the economy, energy. He knows what the issues are and she says, what? What is crime to you?\par \par \uc2 My God, there was a shooting in front of his house while his daughters were home. Somebody came up to attack him on a stage. This woman is in La-la land.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And you know what else? I'll tell you what else. When she was with a Cuomo, I don't think she learned a thing because he kept her in the background.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: She was just there for, you know, when they needed a lieutenant governor. She was, however, somewhat polished, you know, she knew how to say no. I've never been engaged in any kind of corruption. But she never said what she was going to do for New Yorkers. \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  killed it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You know, Harold, in the green room I was really struck when you said, I like the cut of Lee Zeldin's jib. I'm going to try to vote for him twice. I said, Harold, that's illegal. And you said, that's the way I roll. And then you just hit me in the face and it was just totally ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 FORD: I don't remember it, quite like that, but I -- but I'll take your word. So, look, I think Zeldin had a pretty good night. I thought, Dana, to your point, and Judge, I think you were saying as well. I thought that Governor Hochul would've spelled out three or four things that she had done, two or three things differently she was going to do and one or two things even bigger. She was going to do if reelect or I should say elected because she's never been elected.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Right.\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : There might have been a missed opportunity there. But I think \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 's challenge is that he's pro-life, that he's connected to Mr. Trump, former President Trump. And I think what the governor thinks is if she's able to connect him to those two factors that that will prevent him from running up any significant vote here in New York City, which makes up 40 percent of the vote as we all know, the five boroughs though. We'll have to wait and see what happens. But I --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But he said on the abortion issue, he won't change the law in New York. He's not concerned about that. That the law in New York as it relates to abortion is fine. He's not --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 FORD: So, I think, so I think Democrat --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: He's not one of these zealots coming in saying.\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : Yes. Right. But so, I think -- I think Democrats would argue, well, there were people in the Supreme Court who said that and look what happened. So. I'm just -- we are analyzing the debate here. We're not necessarily.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORD: So, I think that's where his c