you can see, the woman's quick-thinking neighbor, who the senior herself, jumps into action.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Hit him.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR: The woman known as Miss Fay, who is a great-grandmother, runs out with her cane to stop the attack, as you can see. She even use her cane and hit the car several times which led to the suspect dropping her neighbor's purse. Miss Faye says her neighbors have been calling her hero and bringing her pies and other food to show their thanks.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I love her.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: We may need her in New York.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, we do.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We need -- we need a coroner.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, but are you guys worried about crime, said the governor.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Send a pie. I like that. Send me a pie if I save someone's life, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I think I have a car. He's stealing from little old ladies. It doesn't make sense to me. But you know what makes sense? My Show. Tonight, Rob Schneider, Peter Schweitzer, Kat Timpf, Tyrus. Oh, it's going to be great. Year two of my favorite segment, Greg Who's Got Crabs.\par \par \uc2 All right, shout out to Marc Siegel for helping me out with this segment. Red crab migration season --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh, wow.,\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: -- in Australia's Christmas Island. Check out the millions of large crabs emerging from the forest, heading to the water. And what are they going to do in the water, Harold? They're going to breed. They're going to breed.\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: I'm going to write that down. They do that the first rainfall of the wet season.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It's a red crab migration.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: And do you know what it's determined by, Judge?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: What?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It's determined by the phase with the moon. Right. They go there and what -- do you know what happens when they later joined by the females and the actions happens. And man, it gets disgusting.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, that one is squashed.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And then everyone gets crabs.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Everybody gets crabs.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK. Dana.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK, so there is a very hungry moose getting a head start on Halloween. He just shows up and he thinks oh, he's got an all you can eat Jack-o'-lantern buffet. The home's owners that he spent over $100 on pumpkin. They keep getting eaten by his greedy neighbors.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: But they've been conditioned. Now they know where to go where their fiber.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Fiber.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: He's out of his gourd.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Judge, you're up.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's very good. OK, so a golden retriever joins a baby in a crying contest. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 OK, so here's the deal. Every time the dog howls, the baby stops. So, I'm not sure if the dog is like sympathizing with the baby or tying the baby to shut up because when the dog howls, the baby stops.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Perfect.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: They're doing a whole hour on this on "SPECIAL REPORT."\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right, I don't have crab orgies in Australia, but I do have Jack-o'-lantern tailpipes. Look at that. Look at that. Dan Balz is going to do a deep dive in the Post tomorrow on that. That's what they do. They're drag racing all over the place. It's pretty, pretty cool looking.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, on "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME," we have Senator Ted Cruz, we have Jason Whitlock, and we have Dan Bongino. And that is it for us.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's a guy fest.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That is -- we have some women out there.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Who's car is that?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's a total dude fest.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Who's car was that?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I have no idea.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Oh, it was --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It says Geraldo.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Was that Geraldo's car?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, it said Geraldo.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It wasn't that Geraldo. It was a different Geraldo.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: There's only one Geraldo.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes, I agree.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right, thank God. That's it for us. "SPECIAL REPORT" is on the road from Georgia.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020221027eiaq00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Key Moments In Crucial Pennsylvania Senate Debate; Fetterman Pressed On Fracking Stance During Debate; Debate Renews Questions Around Fetterman's Health; Impact Pennsylvania Senate Debate Could Have On Crucial Face; Oz And Fetterman Spar Over Crime In Pennsylvania Senate Debate; Voters React To Senate Debate In Battleground Pennsylvania; Top Issues For Pennsylvania Voters Ahead Of Election Day; PA GOP House Candidate On Race To Unseat Dem Rep; GOP Candidate On Toss-Up PA House Race; Tight Gov, Senate Races Heat Up In Battleground Georgia; Georgia Sees Record Early Voting Despite Dem Doubts\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Bryan Llenas, Josh Kraushaar, Jeff Flock, Bret  Baier, Jonathan Serrie \par \uc2 8,571 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Today, the story is on the road for the midterm elections. We're live in Harrisburg, Pennsylvania, the capital of the Keystone State, where last night there was a highly controversial debate between Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Dr. Mehmet Oz. We'll talk to a panel of voters from across the state, some undecided, some Democrats, some Republicans, some independent, and we'll find out what they thought about what happened.\par \par \uc2 Live here inside the historic Civic Club of Harrisburg, where in moments, we will replay some of last night's debate for our live voter panel and we'll get their reaction as we sit here 13 days from election day. Also, the Republican senator in this state, \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2 , whose retirement leaves that seat open for either Oz or Fetterman, he joins us live today as well. But first, correspondent, Bryan Llenas takes a look at the debate that everyone is talking about today. Hi, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good afternoon. And one of those moments that everyone is talking about is when Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  was pressed on his stance on fracking. Last night, he said he has always supported fracking but in 2018, he said he did not and never has.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: I absolutely support fracking.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There is that 2018 interview that you said quote, "I don't support fracking at all." So, how do you square the two?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I do support fracking, and I don't, I don't -- I support fracking, and I stand, and I do support fracking.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voiceover): After last night's performance, there are now more questions, not less about Fetterman's health as he continues recovering from a stroke suffered in May. And amid the at times halting and muddling responses, he refused to release his medical records.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I didn't hear you say you would release your full medical records, why not? You have 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Now, again, my doctor believes that I'm fit to be serving. And that's what I believe is where I'm standing.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Fetterman's campaign says they've raised a million dollars fundraising on this exchange on abortion last night. Listen.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R-PA), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: I want women, doctors, local political leaders, letting the democracy that's always allowed our nation to thrive to put the best ideas forward so states can decide for themselves.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: If you believe that the choice of your reproductive freedom belongs with Dr. Oz, then you have a choice. But if you believe that the choice for abortion belongs between you and your doctor, that's what I fight for.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): Dr. Oz just wrapped a campaign event here in Harrisburg where he said the focus of the debate last night was his desire to bring balance to Washington, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Bryan, thank you very much. Bryan Llenas here in Harrisburg with us. And Axios, today, with this headline: "Fetterman's painful debate." It was a word that I think was on a lot of people's minds as they watched what happened last night. \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2  co-wrote that pience, he is Senior Political Correspondent at Axios and a Fox News Contributor, and he joins us here in Harrisburg. Josh, good to have you here today. Overall, what was your debate takeaway from last night?\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, look, the big challenge for Fetterman was to show he could pass the debate threshold, that he can show that he can have a conversation, can talk about the issues. There are a lot of voters concerned about that that CBS News poll, about 46 percent of voters have some concerns about his health, and the campaign tried to lower expectations. I don't think he hit that threshold.\par \par \uc2 There were a lot of answers that were very shaky. There were questions about whether he understood the questions. And you know, his health is the big issue looming large in this, in this very close Senate campaign. And Democrat -- it wasn't Republicans, it was Democrats I talked to, members of Congress, officials, that are supporting \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . That said he should not have debated last night. It hurt his political campaign.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Now, there was a lot of discussion about the people around him and what they've been doing and why they allowed this to happen last night.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: It's the elephant in the room. I mean, I think they thought he could do one debate, and then they could pivot to talk about the issues they want to talk about. The Fetterman campaign wants to talk about abortion and make that a big issue in the race. They thought if they could do the debate, get past the 60 minutes of time, they could actually focus on the arguments they wanted to make. But they would try to avoid debates in the first place. They, they realized they were losing the political argument. They had to show their face in front of Pennsylvania voters. And ultimately, you know, I think it did the campaign some damage last night.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, there's a lot of discussion today about ableism. A piece by Liz Plank in, on MSNBC: "The debate proves U.S. political races are far from accessible." It says, "If we want to see more lawmakers with disabilities, we need to rethink the way that we elect them. That means reimagining what a campaign looks like, and even how a candidate should sound." OK. And then, you've got this from Political Playbook: "The median voter in Pennsylvania is a middle-aged white person with a mid-five-figure salary who did not attend college, that demographic is perhaps the least likely to be following the Fetterman ableism debate on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and MSNBC." So, what's the impact of last night on this race, Josh?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Most voters, I think, in Pennsylvania are compassionate for \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  situation, he's just suffered a pretty bad stroke, he's recovering. That is something that they feel compassionate about. But they also care about whether he has the ability to do the job. Ultimately, you may like someone personally, but may not think that they're equipped to handle the job of a senator. And that is the question. The polling doesn't -- it's hard to capture these undecided voters, a lot of them are voted on the issues: the economy, crime, and inflation. But there's also a question of people who may just not think Fetterman can handle the job as a United States Senator.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we were talking before we went on air, you said everything is tightening. Everything is tightening. You said Arizona is now tied in the Senate race. There was also a debate last night in Michigan, there was one in New York. This is a moment from \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  and \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , excuse me, debating last night watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R-NY), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: We're halfway through the debate, she still hasn't talked about locking up anyone committing any crimes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: OK?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): Anyone who commits a crime, under our laws, especially with the changes we made to bail has consequences. I don't know why that's so important. All I know is that we could do more.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: She said, I don't know why that is so important to you -- we could do more.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Yes, the issue of crime is an issue in New York. It's an issue in Pennsylvania. Ultimately, health is the subtext of that debate. But crime was one of the major issues that voters are concerned about in Pennsylvania. And, you know, if you're a democratic official like Governor Hochul, and crime is going up on under your watch, if you're the incumbent, that's a really tough position to be in.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania is a much more competitive state. It's a swing state. And in the city of Philadelphia, there are a lot of questions about the District Attorney, \uc2 Larry Krasner\uc2  and his lack of prosecutions for some types of crime. That is a hot political issue, and it's one that a lot of voters are going to be making their decisions on when it comes to election day.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We're going to talk to some of them in just a few minutes. Josh, thank you very much. Great to have you with us here.\par \par \uc2 So, retiring Republican Senator \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2 , whose seat is the one that is so hotly contested right now, joins me. Senator, thank you very much. It's good to have you here today. What did you think about the debate last night?\par \par \uc2 SEN. PAT TOOMEY (R-PA): Thanks for having me, Martha. Well, uh, several things. And I like, I think most Pennsylvanians watching this mean, it was it was sad. It was sad to see \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  struggling and in such rough shape. I mean, honestly, this is a guy that really should be focused on trying to recover from what was obviously a horrific stroke. And it was a painful and difficult thing to watch.\par \par \uc2 That said, I mean, there is a record he has been on record for a year, you know, he ran, tried to run for this seat back in 2016, when he was defeated in a primary. So, there's a long history. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  comes from the radical left wing of the Democratic Party. He's not even from the middle of the Democratic Party, much less the middle of Pennsylvania. So, you know, \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  couldn't get elected in Pennsylvania. And \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is a \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  Democrat. I think Dr. Oz made those points last night and that was important.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Senator Casey, the other senator from the state of Pennsylvania, the Democrat Senator said this about last night and about Fetterman's candidacy. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BOB CASEY (D-PA): This idea that the, the, the speed of your response is somehow indicative of the way you would do the job is, is really faulty. I know this job well. John is prepared right now to be an effective senator. And I think with even more months of recovery, he's going to be back to where he was.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, he's sticking by him. What's your response to that, Senator?\par \par \uc2 TOOMEY: Well, where to begin? I mean, it first of all I know this job pretty well too. I've been doing this for 12 years. it's not about the speed of your response. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  simply wasn't able to respond. He wasn't really able to give a good opening statement or closing statement, which has nothing to do with response.\par \par \uc2 Look, a fundamental part of this job is engaging in dialogue. It's engaging on the Senate floor, in the cloakroom, at committee rooms, it's with constituents, it's with advisors, it's being able to pick up the phone when a regulator is treating one of your constituents unfairly, and making the case for why they've got to be fair to your constituents.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that's, that's the these are some of the things you do every day, day in and day out. The reality is, it requires that you be able to communicate. Two-way, orally -- you have to be able to do it. It's a fundamental part of the job. And you know, unfortunately for John, he's just, it's just not there.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, about 635,000 votes have already been mailed in in Pennsylvania of the I think 1.3 million who requested mail in ballots. So, you've got a lot of votes that is already in and the Fetterman team wanted this debate to happen as late as possible. What's the impact of that, Senator?\par \par \uc2 TOOMEY: Well, let's be honest. They didn't want to debate at all. They ended up reluctantly agreeing to one debate very late. And I think it's pretty obvious now why they made that decision. So, the people who voted early, voted without the benefit of having been able to see both candidates in a live, you know, spontaneous exchange, like we saw last night. I'm not a huge fan of all this early mail in voting, but, but that's our system. Those folks, unfortunately, didn't have the benefit of witnessing what happened last night.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, Senator Toomey, the retiring senator from Pennsylvania, whose seat voters will have an opportunity to fail in this election. Senator, thank you. Good to see you. Thanks for coming in today.\par \par \uc2 TOOMEY: Thanks for having me, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, so Pennsylvania voters from all sides of the political spectrum talked about the economy. They talked about rising crime in Pennsylvania. What issues are front for them? What matters the most? And what did they think about the performance on that stage last night?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You got to feel bad for him recovering from a stroke. I think you should be at home recovering and not running for United State Senate.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's Fetterman.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Why?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Why?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I preferred more social issues as opposed to kind of fiscal issues on this vote.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Unfortunately, for Fetterman, I don't think he's up to it. I don't believe in a lot of the positions he's taking. That was clearly, he's anti fracking. And last night, he said he's for fracking, which doesn't make any sense and also the, the crime.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): So, you remember this video, a crowd running wild and a Wawa convenience store in Philadelphia last month, stealing things off the shelves, ransacking everything.\par \par \uc2 Crime was one of the big issues, obviously, last night here in Pennsylvania. It's a big issue across the country. Our voters here with us at this historic civic club of Harrisburg.\par \par \uc2 In moments, we'll tell you what they thought about what they saw on T.V. last night. But first, fox business correspondent Jeff Flock joins us with his takes on Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Martha, hello to you. A lot of tough neighborhoods in Philadelphia where crime is a problem. But really, it has spread across the city, and in some ways, across the state. Take a look at the latest numbers on crime right here in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 FLOCK (voice-over): Thefts, armed robbery burglaries, all up. Some of them in the neighborhood of 50 percent. Murders this year in Philadelphia, the latest number, I just checked it, 438. That is roughly the same number as were killed last year in the city which was an all-time record.\par \par \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  says he got into politics to fight against violent crime. Dr. Oz last night telling the debate audience, if you did, sir, you didn't do a very good job.\par \par \uc2 Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: I ran on my record on crime. You know, I ran to the mayor back in 20 -- 2005 in order to fight gun violence, and that's exactly what I did. And working with the police, and working with our community.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R-PA), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: The Fraternal Order of Police from Braddock, the small town he represented endorsed me, they supported me because what he's saying is not true.\par \par \uc2 Violence skyrocketed in Braddock. I mean, the town wasn't in a good shape when John got there, it got worse when he was there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: In fairness, there were no murders during the time that Fetterman was mayor of Braddock, but violent crime was still very much a factor.\par \par \uc2 And Martha, I'll leave you with this much of the criticism but Fetterman comes from his role as head of the Board of Pardons in Pennsylvania, where he recommended commutations and pardons for some folks even convicted murderers. A lot of people think that is not the way to fight violent crime. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): Jeff, thank you very much. Jeff Flock in Philadelphia. Let's watch this other moment now from the debate. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Here is what I think we have to fight about inflation here right now. That's what we need to fight about inflation, you know, right now because it's a tax on working families, you know.\par \par \uc2 And Dr. Oz can't possibly understand what that is like. You know? He has 10 gigantic mansions.\par \par \uc2 OZ: I've been trying to talk about policy issues with the people of Pennsylvania. As a doctor, I listen to their ideas and I want to talk about them.\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  brings up houses, the irony is he didn't pay for his own house. He got it for a dollar from his sister and he hasn't been able to earn a living on his own. He's lived off his parents. So --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, let's see what some voters in Pennsylvania have to say about what they saw last night in this showdown. Noreen Johnson is a registered Republican. P.J. Dolan is an Independent front and center there. Matt Barrick, in the back is a registered Republican on the right-hand side.\par \par \uc2 Cynthia Watkins is a registered Democrat who works for the Pennsylvania House Democratic Caucus. And Shane Eslinger, behind Cindy is a registered libertarian. Great to have all of you with us.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much for taking time out of your day and joining us from all across the political spectrum.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You're a great cross section of citizens from this great state of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 So, one of the big questions last night that everyone is talking about today is how \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  did and whether or not he is able to serve after the stroke that he suffered, something everybody is sympathetic about.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm going to ask for a show of hands. Do you think that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  based on what you saw last night is able to serve as a U.S. senator?\par \par \uc2 If you say yes, raise your hand.\par \par \uc2 OK. So, nothing changed. Cindy, let me start with you then. Because I know you're committed to voting for \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . Were you sympathetic? Were you concerned about him last night up there?\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA WATKINS, DEMOCRATIC VOTER, PENNSYLVANIA: I wasn't sympathetic, I was somewhat concerned. But knowing what I know about strokes, I know that it takes a while to recover. And because somebody is not finding words or stumbling over words, does it mean that they are incapable of serving?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, P.J, you kind of put your hand halfway up? What was going through your mind as you watch him answering those questions last night?\par \par \uc2 P.J. DOLAN, INDEPENDENT VOTER, PENNSYLVANIA: A lot, Martha. Number one, as Cindy said, yes, he could be capable intellectually of the job. However, my take on it is if you're going for the job of \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , you need to be able to effectively communicate on the Senate floor. And if you can't do that, physically, that's a big issue.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And you're an undecided. You're a registered Independent, undecided voter. Did you make up your mind last night?\par \par \uc2 DOLAN: It definitely pushed me more towards Oz. I haven't made the decision yet, totally. But, I mean, what we saw last night was sad to say the least. I felt for him, I felt empathetic for him. And at the same time, concerned. I'm concerned that they even put him out there. To be honest with you.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we're seeing that twice now on this (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: One of the tough moments for him was his answer on fracking. Let's watch that answer with all of you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I do support fracking. I've never taken any money from their industry, but I support how critical it is that we produce our own energy.\par \par \uc2 LISA SYLVESTER, ANCHOR, WPXI, PITTSBURGH: But there is that 2018 interview that you said, "I don't support fracking at all." So, how do you square the two?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I do support fracking. And I don't -- I don't. I support fracking. And I stand, and I do support fracking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Shane, let me ask you. You're a registered libertarian.\par \par \uc2 SHANE ESLINGER, LIBERTARIAN VOTER, PENNSYLVANIA: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Undecided voter. I believe you're a veteran as well. Is that correct?\par \par \uc2 ESLINGER: Well, it's true.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Energy is obviously a big issue. So, his flip flop on fracking - - what's your -- what's your response?\par \par \uc2 ESLINGER: Well, as far as fracking is concerned, Pennsylvania needs it, especially in the energy, you know, problems we're having right now. So, it's very possible that before in 2018, maybe he was like that we got in this environment.\par \par \uc2 And now that we're in the economic climate, wherein he may have just changed his mind. So, that wasn't as hurtful of a exchange for Fetterman as some of the other ones in my opinion.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, did you -- you're undecided.\par \par \uc2 ESLINGER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Did you make up your mind last night?\par \par \uc2 ESLINGER: I -- yes. I will definitely not be voting for Fetterman, I guess is the best way to put it. So, yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But will you vote for Oz?\par \par \uc2 ESLINGER: Either, either hammer gear heart (PH), Libertarian, of course.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Did Oz persuade you at all last night. I know you --\par \par \uc2 ESLINGER: Yes. I thought his response on abortion was really well done with, you know, hey, send it to the states where it belongs. That's a very libertarian kind of thing to do.\par \par \uc2 And as electrician, he pushed the trades several times, he mentioned about you know, helping bring that along, which I thought was a very good thing for him as well. So --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Let's watch the response on abortion from Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OZ: There should not be involvement from the federal government in how states decide their abortion decisions. I want women, doctors, local political leaders, letting the democracy that always allowed our nation to thrive to put the best ideas forward so states can decide for themselves.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Noreen, you have said you support Dr. Oz. In that answer, he talked about it being decided by women and their doctors and local political officials, which he's getting some blowback for today. What did you think about the answer there?\par \par \uc2 NOREEN JOHNSON, REPUBLICAN VOTER, PENNSYLVANIA: Well, obviously most conservative Republican, anyone's are very, very pro-life. That being said, he's also a doctor. So, he very much speaks from a medical perspective. And I think that's what he was doing last night. He wasn't necessarily drawing a line between either side. He was more just to find the fact that he's a doctor, and he knows that sometimes there are medical reasons why it would be appropriate.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I want to ask you about the governor's race as well because we've been hearing that there will be some split vote. Mastriano -- maybe, you know, between Mastriano and Oz on the Republican side. How about you?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I mean, I would never split my vote because I am a party voter. I also know that our country right now is a disaster. So, there's only one party for me. And that's Republican.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A disaster, in what sense? What's on your mind?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Do you have about a week to talk about it? Everything from inflation to crime? You know, I just -- everywhere you look, it's really gone south.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: How many people -- raise your hand if you feel that the country is headed in the wrong direction?\par \par \uc2 Everybody? Two hands. So, last night, you're also an undecided voter, Matt. A registered Republican.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MATT BARRICK, REPUBLICAN VOTER, PENNSYLVANIA: Right, going in --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Did you decide last night?\par \par \uc2 BARRICK: Going into last night, I would have considered myself undecided and wanted to know more about both of these guys. Specifically, Oz. Fetterman has a bit of a record, where we've seen what he's done in government a little bit. Oz, not so much.\par \par \uc2 As P.J. mentioned earlier, going in -- I was a bit empathetic watching him. Because I feel like he needs to be able to make decisions. And I'm not certain looking at his performance last night that we could count on him as Pennsylvanians to make good decisions and represent us.\par \par \uc2 There was a neurologist, the one that I watched following the debate last night, who was saying, you know, she doesn't know any more than the rest of us. But typically, the therapy done in the first six to eight months, is about as good as it's going to get.\par \par \uc2 And so, I looked at that, and he's been five and a half months post stroke, to think this may be as good as he gets is very concerning.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Cindy, you raised your hand that you feel like the country is headed in the wrong direction? What -- in what way?\par \par \uc2 WATKINS: Well, speaking, first of all, as an African American, secondly, as a woman, and thirdly, as a senior, I see a lot of racism, sexism, and ageism that's affecting everybody.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And what about your feelings about the current administration? Are you supportive of President Biden? Do you think he should run again?\par \par \uc2 WATKINS: I'm not sure that he should run again, but I'm supportive of what he is attempting to do.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And how would you just -- how would you -- in what is that? When you look at inflation, when you look at crime, do you feel like he's on the right track with those two things? Let me put it that way?\par \par \uc2 WATKINS: I think so. And I'm also aware that one man can't fix everything, no matter what party.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, when I asked you about crime, how high on the list is crime? Do you feel safe in this country today? Or do you feel unsafe?\par \par \uc2 Who wants to --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Unsafe.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Unsafe.\par \par \uc2 BARRICK: So, to put it into perspective, this summer, my daughter -- my teen daughter wanted to go with a family that I trust and her friends to New York City.\par \par \uc2 Here again, we're talking Pennsylvania, not New York, but I had no issue with the family she was traveling with, had no, no worries about the decisions they were making. But seeing the news reports of what's happening in our inner cities, gave me complete pause and allowing her to go.\par \par \uc2 And that shouldn't be something you really need to think about. If you're okay with everything else about the situation. It's sad to think in America nowadays, you have to wonder what might happen just going to visit a place.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. You know, in terms of energy, how is inflation impacting your daily life? And maybe Shane, you could talk about your business as an electrician, I know you run your own company.\par \par \uc2 ESLINGER: Yes, driving a big, large truck around every day really sucks up the gas mileage. And it, it certainly makes a difference on how far you want to travel to take a job and do things like that, you have to like factor in a lot more considerations that you used to before.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And are you making different personal decisions about travel or about buying a home, anything along those lines?\par \par \uc2 DOLAN: I took a different job because of the commute mainly, and the violence. I also teach school in the inner city. And I traveled nine miles, and it takes me an hour and 15 minutes to get home. That gas money coupling with a commute, it's just too much and it all adds up.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DOLAN: It affects everything. Affects cost of goods, affects everything.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: How is it affecting you, Noreen?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, considering, you know, the Democratic Party runs on the -- being for the middle class, the middle class are hurting. So, clearly, everyone's affected by the middle class is hurting.\par \par \uc2 But directly to me, I would just say that, yes, I definitely travel less for lots of reasons. But, in general, if we don't watch our savings and our 401 (k)s, there is a good chance we'll be nothing left.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, in the beginning, one of the things that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  was kind of getting leverage on over Mehmet Oz is that he had spent most of his life in New Jersey and moved to Pennsylvania a couple of years ago. Is that a problem for any of you?\par \par \uc2 WATKINS: Yes, it's a problem for me. I call such people carpetbaggers.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's what -- that's what they have been called.\par \par \uc2 BARRICK: So, Martha, I would say entering last night that was a concern of mine.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARRICK: I got to say I didn't particularly like that he's spent most of his life, it seems, adult life anyway, in Northern New Jersey. That said, we find ourselves in a place where we only have two viable candidates right now, which is what I see as part of the problem. There is no realistic third choice that I could foresee winning. So really, when you break it down and look at that, do I see that as something that would keep me from voting for him, no. After last night, I feel pretty strongly now where I need to put my vote.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. So it seems like the undecided voters on the panel, after last night, are leaning towards Oz. Is that accurate statement? Undecided? Three undecideds nodding, nodding heads, all right. Very interesting. Great to have you with us and I look forward to talking with you more. We're going to do a continuation for the podcast that'll come out later this week as well. So great to have all of you with us, thank you very much for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Great group. So of the 33 House toss up races in our Fox Power Rankings, two of them are from Pennsylvania, which are now very much in focus. Candidates in each of those races are going to join me live when our democracy 2022 coverage continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Two of the toss up races in our Fox Power Rankings that could decide the balance of power in the House, are right here in the state of Pennsylvania. And we have two of those candidates to talk with us today. First up is Republican Jim Bognet it who is taking on Democrat Congressman \uc2 Matt Cartwright\uc2 . And we also invited Congressman Cartwright and we'll wait to hear back from him for hopefully another day.\par \par \uc2 This is the battle for the Eighth District. That district includes Scranton. It's where President Biden was born. President Trump won this district by almost three points in 2020, fairly close. Republican candidate Jim Bognet is here with me in Harrisburg. Great to have you with us, Jim. Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 JIM BOGNET (R), PA-08 CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This is a fairly tight race. I watched some of your debate the other night. Let's put the numbers up here in the most recent polling that we have. Cartwright at 51.7, you're at 48.2. What's your feel for what's going on right now?\par \par \uc2 BOGNET: We feel great. We have tremendous momentum. We had a debate last week. I mopped the floor with him. We're hearing from voters left and right all over the place that inflation, home heating, oil gas is the number one issue. And they know that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and my opponent, \uc2 Matt Cartwright\uc2 , have caused these problems because Mr. Cartwright votes with them 100% of the time.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So tell me a little bit about this district because viewers are going to be watching your district very closely on election night. It is a place that has gone back and forth. \uc2 Matt Cartwright\uc2  has tried to stay as close to the middle as he can, because obviously you're both aware that you have just kind of, you know, answer to more moderate middle of the road voters in that area.\par \par \uc2 BOGNET: Well, it's true that it is a swing district. President Trump won this district by three points. We're getting a ton of attention because of its \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's hometown effect. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's doing a fundraiser for my opponent, \uc2 Matt Cartwright\uc2 , today. They know that they're going to lose this seat.\par \par \uc2 There is so much energy for change. No one in Northeast Pennsylvania is happy where things are home heating oil, $6 a gallon, rent and gas is out of control. People want change. They know we're going on a bad path, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So let's take a look at \uc2 Matt Cartwright\uc2  talking about his support for the border policies and his take on immigration. We'll get your thoughts. Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MATT CARTWRIGHT\uc2  (D-PA): I'm proud to have supported two major border security bills that were signed into law by President Trump to increase border patrol funding, fight drug trafficking and secure our border, even though some in my own party did not support those things. I voted for over a billion dollars to build a wall on our southern border.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So that's your opponent talking about building a wall on the southern border.\par \par \uc2 BOGNET: Well he's been caught in a lie because when the big vote came up for the 25 billion for President Trump's border wall, he voted against it. In fact, President Trump said he's a disaster on illegal immigration. Here's the truth, \uc2 Matt Cartwright\uc2  has able enabled illegal immigration. He voted for a $1,200 stimulus check for illegal immigrants. He has coddled illegal immigration. He has been very bad on this issue.\par \par \uc2 People know that in my district. He's stood with Pelosi and Cartwright. I've been fighting against illegal immigration the whole campaign. We're glad that he's talking about this issue because people in Northeast Pennsylvania are sick and tired of illegal immigration and the drug trafficking, fentanyl, that it's brought to our part of the world.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. That's concerned all across the country to be sure. We've lost too many teenagers, too many people all across the country. Did you watch the debate last night? What was your take?\par \par \uc2 BOGNET: I did watch the debate. I think it was very clear that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is not fit to be a United States senator. Anyone who's not an extreme partisan knows that. And I really feel it's unfair to Democratic voters. We're asking that Democrats to vote for a man who while they may agree with some of his positions, is clearly unfit to serve in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Are you surprised that they didn't tap \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  or someone else to take that spot?\par \par \uc2 BOGNET: You know, I'm not surprised because they've -- the whole Democratic Party from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , \uc2 Matt Cartwright\uc2  to \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . They've really embraced this socialist progressive ideology. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  was the most liberal candidate, \uc2 Matt Cartwright\uc2  is the most liberal candidate, that's why they're supporting these people they've really left the center of the country behind. We're going to fix that when we win the election.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I know you have the former President Trump's support in this race that you're in right now. Do you want him to be the candidate, the Republican candidate in 2024?\par \par \uc2 BOGNET: I would never presume to speak for President Trump. We're very focused on this election. When President Trump makes his decision, everyone will know about it. But on this election, we're looking at November 8. We're going to win that and then we'll --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But if he decides to run, you would support him?\par \par \uc2 BOGNET: I'm a huge supporter of President Trump.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jim Bognet, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BOGNET: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks for being here. Also with us today is Republican Jeremy Schaffer in his race. He is running against Democrat Chris Deluzio. And we invited Mr. Deluzio as well. We hope that he will join us in the remaining 13 days of this race as we move through it. They are fighting for an open seat in the 17th district. So this is the suburban Pittsburgh Area on the western side of the state. So we're getting a good handle on the northeast corner and also in the Pittsburgh Area.\par \par \uc2 That is an area that Biden won by six points back in 2020. And the current congressman, Democrat \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2 , we were just talking about him. He is retiring in this race. He pulled off a major upset and a special election back in 2018, which at that point was said to be a harbinger of change to come that we did see in terms of Democrats taking over the House. So he won what was reliably a Republican seat then that was in a different district that has been redrawn, as we've seen in a lot of states across the country. This year he ran in the state in the state's Senate primary, but \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  lost to \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, as I said, that has raised a lot of questions about what we watched unfold last night. So let's bring him in. Jeremy Shaffer joins me now. Jeremy, good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 Give me a sense of where you feel this race is right now in the 17th District. You're in an area where we heard \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  say last night, that he's a Steelers fan through and through, and you're in Steelers' territory there.\par \par \uc2 JEREMY SHAFFER (R), PA-17 CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: Oh, we're definitely black and gold country here. But people are frustrated, people are upset. You know, we have inflation that's running rampant here. We have energy prices that are out of control, and we have crime. Just yesterday, we had a shooting in broad daylight in Downtown Pittsburgh.\par \par \uc2 And although my district doesn't include the city of Pittsburgh, most of the people who work in downtown Pittsburgh live here in the suburbs of Pittsburgh, which this district represents. And it doesn't have to be this way. And I'm running against a candidate who is a socialist, who's backed by \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and AOC. And this country does not need to move further to the left. We need to move towards the center and be able to get things done again in this country.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What are you, you know, when you talk about your own background, what do you want people to understand about what has brought you to this moment?\par \par \uc2 SHAFFER: Well, I'm a little unusual. I'm an engineer. I'm an electrical engineer, went to Carnegie Mellon, a local university here. But I'm concerned. I have five children. They range in age from six to 17. It's not normal for engineers to step up by being a small business owner. But I am so concerned about the direction of this country, that I believe it's time we had problem solvers step in.\par \par \uc2 We've got to turn this country around. We have rivers here in Pittsburgh, I like to use this analogy. Republicans, Democrats, Independents, we're all in the boat together. And there's a waterfall dead ahead right now if we continue on this course. And I'll make the hard votes in Congress to turn the ship around and to get us head in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This is a piece of an ad that you put out that got some attention for the language around health care. Let's watch it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Being married to a physician, Jeremy knows the importance of decisions between patients and their doctors. And as a father to four daughters, Jeremy will stand up for women's health care.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Oh, so your opponent says that that was sort of code language for supporting abortion. What's your take on that, sir?\par \par \uc2 SHAFFER: Yes. So first off, I am blessed to be married to an amazing wife, Stacy, who is a medical doctor. And we understand that women's health care is far, far more than abortion. We need to be able to have health care for women. That goes all the way from birth throughout all life stages. And I'm a prolife. I believe in exceptions for life and rape and incest. But I also believe like Dr. Oz said last night that this is an issue for the states. And that's something that I believe should be left to our Pennsylvania State Legislature.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Jeremy Schaffer, thank you very much. Good to have you with us today. We'll be watching your race.\par \par \uc2 SHAFFER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So coming up, Bret Baier with a "Special Report" spotlight. Bret is in Georgia today, home to another one of the four toss up Senate races that we're watching in our Fox News Power Rankings. Bret is up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Welcome back to THE STORY live from Harrisburg, Pennsylvania today where a crucial Senate race is now underway. Early voting is well underway here. One of four toss ups that could decide which party is in power in the Senate is in Georgia. Democrat Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is facing off against Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . "Special Report" anchor Bret Baier joins us from Georgia in a moment. He has Walker and Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  on the show tonight. First to correspondent Jonathan Serrie who's live in Union City, Georgia. Hey, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi there, Martha. Once a solidly red state, Georgia has become a competitive battleground. Democrats see that they have a fighting chance. You have them already controlling both of the state's \uc2 US Senate\uc2  seats. One of those senators \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is up for reelection and hopes to increase support among moderates by highlighting some of his bipartisan efforts in Washington.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Foreign countries tried to block us from selling our products, we needed someone to fight back. That's why I partnered with a Republican senator from Alabama to eliminate the regulations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  in her second bid for governor is rallying the progressive base with ambitious policy goals for health care, housing and education. But if elected, Abrams' agenda would face challenges from Georgia's Republican controlled legislature.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDRA GILLESPIE, EMORY UNIVERSITY PROFESSOR: they are likely to not have a veto proof majority. So they're not going to have a super majority in the state legislature more than likely. And so as a result of that, Abrams veto power actually does become really important.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Republicans point to record early voting numbers is evidence against Abrams continued claims that Georgia election laws are discriminatory and challenging those laws. Politico reports a nonprofit group founded by Abrams paid more than $9 million in legal fees to the law firm of Abrams campaign chair. The group Fair Fight Action insists that there was no conflict of interest. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Jonathan Serrie. And as I mentioned, both \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  will be on "Special Report" tonight with Bret Baier. And Bret joins us now from Acworth, Georgia. I hope I said that right, Bret?\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Yes, you got it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's pull up these latest polls because we've got some pretty good -- we've got some pretty fresh polls now from Monmouth that came out just a few hours ago. And here's where Warnock and Walker are, according to this poll. Warnock has a 5% lead in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Now that you're on the ground there, Bret, what's your take on what's going on there?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Martha, good afternoon. I think it could be closer than that. The average of recent polls has really tight, a couple points. I will say that there's a mix. It seems like a lot of Republican voters here are rallying back. They're kind of putting on their jerseys and saying this is about fighting for control of the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 . Obviously, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  faces the allegations that he's talked about. There's a new one out today. He's going to address that on our show later on. We'll be asking about that.\par \par \uc2 But he is fighting on the main issues that Republicans all over the country are fighting on. And that is inflation, the economy, crime, immigration, and all of those resonate here in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. One of the things that we will be watching closely, and we've been through this in Georgia before, is whether or not it's going to be headed to a runoff. And \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  addressed this in a new ad. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): That's right. I could be interrupting your Thanksgiving because if nobody gets 50% of the vote, there'll be a runoff, and nobody wants that to happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But it's highly likely. Right, Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, it is. Listen, you look at a race this close and you're looking at incumbent that is under 50% heading in. And you look at the tradition, Martha, that we've seen over the last three elections where Republicans may be under -- are undercounted in polls by anywhere between two and five points. Yes, it looks very likely that that race in particular, could go to a runoff. And then you're looking at the potential if Republicans don't pick off Pennsylvania or they don't pick off Nevada, that the balance of power in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  could come down to Georgia as it did last time.\par \par \uc2 And I think for Republicans here on the ground, they have these nightmares of the two races that they lost in a runoff that determined the balance of power in Washington. So, you know, as you are in Pennsylvania, I'm sure as I turn on the TV here, I can't -- you can't go two seconds without seeing another ad. So people are getting bombarded with messages.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. That Georgia race feels like it was just yesterday but it was a couple of years ago now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I know.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: With regard to the governor's race. This is \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  talking about the issue of voter suppression, which \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  has been hanging her hat on for years now. And here's \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  talking about it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: A high turnout and voter suppression can take place at the same time. They don't have to be -- one doesn't have to happen on its own. They could be happening at the same time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thoughts on that, Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, listen, quickly Governor Kemp has pushed back on that. We are now over 1 million early votes here. It seems like there are record breaking shattering numbers for midterms in the past. And they're saying, well, high voter turnout doesn't discount suppression. It's just a tough argument to make as you have more and more people heading into the polls. They're looking for the highest midterm turnout that they've seen in Georgia, period.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Bret. Look forward to the show tonight and your discussions with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and with Governor Kemp. And Bret and I will be in Ohio on Tuesday together and we're looking forward to that. It's a townhall with Senate candidates JD Vance and Tim Ryan. Our Democracy 2022 coverage continues, my thanks to Bret on the way out here. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So we want to say thank you to the Civic Club of Harrisburg who welcomed us into this beautiful historic home. And we appreciate that. And that's THE STORY for this Wednesday, October 26th. We're 13 days away from the election and THE STORY of that will go on throughout the next 13 days. We look forward to seeing you in Ohio on Tuesday when Bret and I are there.\par \par \uc2 And tomorrow, we will show you this in depth discussion that we're going to have now with the Pennsylvania voters that you heard from today as we do an extended version on the "Untold Story" podcast. So look forward to that, it will drop tomorrow. Your World starts right now. Thanks for being here, folks.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020221027eiaq00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview With Wisconsin Gubernatorial Candidate \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2 ; Interview With Gov. Glenn Youngkin (R-VA); Interview With Maryland Gubernatorial Candidate Dan Cox; Strong Early Voting Numbers; Midterm Races Near Final Stretch\b0\par\par\uc2 Bryan Llenas, Lauren Simonetti, Jonathan Serrie, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,984 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, well, the gloves are off in and the money race is on in Pennsylvania, where that Senate debate is sparking a whole new dash for cash.\par \par \uc2 Already, Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  claiming $2 million has come into his campaign since that brawl. It doesn't stop there, in Georgia, Nevada, Ohio, Arizona, candidates are crisscrossing their states looking for more money and more votes in the fight for control of Washington, D.C., and their own capitals.\par \par \uc2 And it is the fight for America's students that has the Republican governor crisscrossing the nation. Today, Glenn Youngkin is in Wisconsin, campaigning for the Republican candidate there, \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2  . Both men will be joining us here.\par \par \uc2 We have got a busy show for you. Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 Let's get right to it.\par \par \uc2 Lauren Simonetti following the money -- Lauren.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SIMONETTI, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Neil, 13 days and millions of dollars to go.\par \par \uc2 So, right before last night's one and only debate in Pennsylvania, the GOP Senate Leadership Fund super PAC poured $6 million into the Oz campaign, putting out warnings like this ad to attack Fetterman's record on crime.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You will see what happens when guys like Fetterman let the worst offenders out of jail early. Protect your family. Don't vote Fetterman.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SIMONETTI: Fetterman also brought in $2 million since the debate last night, his campaign calling those donations unprecedented and show grassroots enthusiasm.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, take a look here. The top super PAC supporting House Republicans, the Congressional Leadership Fund, pouring $11 million into these 16 close races, seven of which President Biden won by double digits in 2020, like District 47 in California. That is affluent Orange County. Biden took almost two-thirds of that vote two years ago.\par \par \uc2 But Republicans think that \uc2 Scott Baugh\uc2  can unseat progressive \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2 . They're spending another $1.9 million on that race alone. And, look, Republican money is being matched almost dollar for dollar by Democrats, \uc2 Michael Bloomberg\uc2  giving the House Majority PAC another $10 million.\par \par \uc2 It's not earmarked, so it could be spent where Democrats think they have a fighting chance, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK. There's certainly no shortage of dough.\par \par \uc2 Lauren, thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 Lauren Simonetti following those developments.\par \par \uc2 Bryan Llenas in Harrisburg, Pennsylvania. That debate that seemed to show a very, health-wise, well, at least compromised Democratic candidate, turns out that didn't hurt him when it came to raising some dough -- Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, no doubt about that, Neil.\par \par \uc2 You heard just now $2 million the Fetterman campaign says they raised since the end of that debate that everyone is talking about. In that debate, both candidates, Fetterman and Oz, tried to cast one another as the extremist choice.\par \par \uc2 At times in the debate, it seemed -- well, it mirrored just like their social media and political ad attacks that have been playing over the last few months. But they both touched on a lot of different topics, including crime, abortion and the economy.\par \par \uc2 Here's a colorful exchange on inflation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA): Dr. Oz can't possibly understand what that is like. He has 10 gigantic mansions. He -- we must push back against corporate greed.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R), PENNSYLVANIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: When \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  brings up houses, the irony is, he didn't pay for his own house. He got it for $1 from his sister, and he hasn't been able to earn a living on his own. He's lived off his parents.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Now, one of the biggest moments last night was when Fetterman was pressed on his evolving stance on fracking, not only for what he said, but how he said it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I absolutely support fracking.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: There is that 2018 interview that you said -- quote -- "I don't support fracking at all."\par \par \uc2 So how do you square the two?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: Well, I do support fracking. And I don't -- I don't -- I support fracking. And I stand -- and I do support fracking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Last night's performance from Fetterman has more people questioning his ability to serve following his stroke suffered in May.\par \par \uc2 Fetterman's campaign said the live closed captioning system used at Fetterman's request at the debate was -- quote -- "delayed and full of errors."\par \par \uc2 Nexstar TV shot back, though, writing -- quote -- "Both candidates were offered the opportunity for two full rehearsals. Mr. Fetterman chose to do only one. The closed captioning process functioned as expected during rehearsal and again during tonight's debate. Moderators' questions and Oz's responses were visible to both candidates."\par \par \uc2 The audience at home, though, and the press was not able to see what was being typed on the screens -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Bryan Llenas, thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 As Bryan pointed out, a lot of early voting has already gone on in Pennsylvania. In fact, if you look at the some 30 states that allow that and mail-in voting, you have had millions of people already vote, so, in the case of Pennsylvania, some of them before last night's debate that could have decided maybe a different vote.\par \par \uc2 We have Phil Wegmann real us, RealClearPolitics, Francesca Chambers, USA Today White House correspondent.\par \par \uc2 Francesca, we will not know exactly how that vote broke down, the early vote. But, in Pennsylvania, those early voters didn't get a chance to weigh or measure Mr. Fetterman's performance, which, by all accounts, was, well, controversial. I'm just wondering what the fallout will be here.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : We don't know how they voted. But we do know that Democrats tend to be the ones to vote earlier.\par \par \uc2 And they're the ones requesting more mail-in ballots than Republicans in the state of Pennsylvania, Neil, and you have the state of Pennsylvania warning already that because of how many mail-in ballots are being cast, that it may not be on Election Day when we know what the result of that race is, that it will take time to process all of those, because, in the state of Pennsylvania, they can't even begin to do that process until Election Day.\par \par \uc2 So Republicans and Democrats both bracing for what could be a long not just election night, but a little bit of a stretch there.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, in a lot of states, if memory serves me right, though, they count the early votes last and day-of votes first, and that was one of the anomalies that had President Trump saying at the time he had a big lead and couldn't understand what happened to that lead.\par \par \uc2 I'm not here to revisit history, but the way this could complicate election night, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 PHILIP WEGMANN, REALCLEARPOLITICS: Well, it does feel a little bit like we're tiptoeing around Armageddon, and hopefully things go much more smoothly than they did during the last presidential election.\par \par \uc2 But I think that what really underscores Republican frustrations in Pennsylvania is a poll that WPXI conducted immediately after that debate. It wasn't the most scientific thing, and it certainly wasn't rigorous enough to be added to the RealClearPolitics average.\par \par \uc2 But it does underscore the frustration. Oz, according to that poll, beat Fetterman 82 to 18 percent. And that's kind of a snapshot here of the performance, but also the frustration, because, in all likelihood, that means that many of those folks who cast their mail-in ballots did so at a moment when the lieutenant governor's health was still an open question.\par \par \uc2 It's less of an open question now. And that's got to frustrate the Oz campaign.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Francesca, if I can get back to the early voting and just the passion for voting here, and on both sides, as you pointed out, this is unusual in a midterm election year. You often see it in presidential election years.\par \par \uc2 But that's going to probably put this in the record books as far as voter participation. We just don't know who has the most jazzed voters. But judging from the economy as a number one issue and concerns about rising gas prices and everything else, you would assume, early on, it goes to Republicans, but what are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: And we have seen polling to that effect, Neil, about where the enthusiasm lies.\par \par \uc2 And Democrats aren't losing hope, even though abortion as a topic has went down in the polls compared to the issues, the economy, inflation, even immigration and crime, some of the things that Republicans have focused on, that they still -- they're still hopeful that their Democratic base will surge over the issue of abortion rights in favor of their candidates.\par \par \uc2 But, again, when you have a situation where the economy, inflation is in the double digits, you have also seen President Biden turn in the final days of this campaign to an economic message, where he's now referring to mega MAGA Republicans in his talking points about the economy and saying that, if Republicans take power, not only will they undo the legislation that he's done, but referencing how they could be responsible for the slight recession that he has said could be coming.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Phil, a little bit later on, we have Jim Messina, who was a top official in the \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration, coming the closest a prominent Democrat has to criticizing the president and his messaging and maybe sort of distinct disconnect on offering public statements on days that people were thirsting for more information, what are you going to do about inflation, what are you going to do about some of these other things, and that that is getting in the way of what he says Democrats should be focused on, the job market, which is strong, and some of these other things and legislation that they passed that could be strong.\par \par \uc2 His opinion, mind you. But what do you make of that, the growing concern now among prominent Democrats that the president's botching it?\par \par \uc2 WEGMANN: Well, I think Francesca is absolutely right. This administration has been focused on inflation and the economy.\par \par \uc2 But they have all also tried to do a two-step here and thread the needle. Last week, we saw the president speak also about the importance of codifying Roe. I believe that was a Tuesday. And then, on the Wednesday, he's talking about gas prices and inflation.\par \par \uc2 The risk there, though, is that if you sort of overemphasize the social issues, at the expense of the fiscal ones, that you could come across as a bit fringe. And I think that the administration and Democrats have corrected that, because, if you look at polling, for example, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 /Siena poll that came out just a few weeks ago, it shows that the number one issue among not just women voters, but also Democrats themselves, remains the economy.\par \par \uc2 Democrats were able to get an early advantage over the summer with the generic ballot because of the Dobbs decision. But I think that we have seen that fading. And if Republicans' optimism pans out, they're expecting independents and moderates to break their way come November.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We shall see. It's still early, 13 days to go. Guys, thank you both very, very much.\par \par \uc2 WEGMANN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, as we have been saying, a lot of people turning out early, and in record numbers at that, particularly in Georgia, one of the first states to allow this.\par \par \uc2 Now there are some 29 others.\par \par \uc2 That's where you will find our Jonathan Serrie in Union City, Georgia, with more -- Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Already more than a million Georgians have cast early ballots. I will have more coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, more than two dozen states, close to 30, actually, are already having early voting, but nothing like what we have been seeing in the Peach State, in Georgia, buoyed by very, very big races that are getting national attention for governor, of course, for that Senate seat.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Serrie is in Union City, Georgia, following all of those developments -- Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, there, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Very active here in the Peach State. Already, more than 1,017,000 Georgians have cast early ballots. Now, Georgia does not register voters by political party. So it's difficult to gauge who has the advantage at this point. In fact, early voting itself may be influenced by factors outside of politics.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDRA GILLESPIE, \uc2 EMORY UNIVERSITY\uc2 : Right now, early voting levels aren't tracking higher than 2018 levels. We don't know a couple of things yet.\par \par \uc2 One, that could be a change in behavior that has happened because of COVID.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Facing a challenge from Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , Democrat Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is trying to reach out to moderates by campaigning on some of the bipartisan legislation he worked on in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 In her second run for governor, Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  is rallying the progressive base with ambitious policy goals for health care, housing and education. And Abrams is doubling down on her claims that Georgia's election laws are discriminatory.\par \par \uc2 But supporters of Republican Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  say, just look at the heavy turnout. They say the heavy turnout disproves those claims -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Jonathan, thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 Well, to hear Republicans and Democrats tell it, it is the economy, stupid. Mostly, it's Republicans who are saying, well, it's a lousy economy, stupid, and mostly Democrats saying it's not as bad as you think.\par \par \uc2 Then there's Jim Messina, who was \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 's deputy chief of staff, orchestrated the former president's reelection in 2012, who takes a somewhat different view of things and is concerned about something called messaging. He spoke with me earlier on FOX Business.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JIM MESSINA, FORMER OBAMA WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF: I think history is important here, right?\par \par \uc2 The average -- the president's party over the last 40 years since World War II loses an average of three Senate seats and 28 House seats. Americans like divided government. Typically, the president's party gets their butt kicked or, as my former boss says, shellacked in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: And if you look at it, in the House, because of redistricting, because of other things, Democrats have to win 26 of the 31 most important races just to hold. That is a pretty tall order during this time.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: They have a real difficult time where the math does favor them in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Is there sort of like a jailbreak going on here? Do you worry about that?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: I don't think yet, no.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: I don't think -- we're 13 days out. And if you look at the early vote numbers -- so you and I have talked about this before. I hate polling. I think that polling is garbage and throwing every two years.\par \par \uc2 What I like to look at are the real numbers. Already, 12 million Americans have voted, and looking like both parties are coming out very, very strongly. I think we're going to have record turnout in November, which is good.\par \par \uc2 Now, the question is, where are these swing voters going to go? And right now, swing women are moving towards the Democrats, swing men are moving towards the Republicans. In some of these really close Senate races -- as you and I know, there's five to seven races that are going to determine everything.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania is probably the most important race in America.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think Fetterman lost it last night with his performance?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: No, no, no, I do not.\par \par \uc2 I think he lost a battle last night and may have won the war.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How so?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Because Oz's comment about putting local politicians in charge of abortion is -- they're going to put that on TV, Neil, between now and the election.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I get that, but a lot of people for the first time in Pennsylvania had a chance to see what's going on with this guy Fetterman and this stroke.\par \par \uc2 And I felt for him. I mean, it was awful.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Yes, look, my mom's had two strokes. I get it. There's lots of Americans who understand that. He's on the recovery.\par \par \uc2 I don't think one debate finishes you. As you remember, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  lost the first debate to \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  worse than any candidate had ever lost. And we ended up cleaning up his clock.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But that was a one-and-done debate, the New York gubernatorial, a one-and-done debate. How do you think that one went?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: I didn't see that one.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Yes. I'm mostly focused on the federal races.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, let me step back then, because you and I were talking about Democrats and this conundrum with the economy and inflation and all of that.\par \par \uc2 And I'm wondering how you think the president has responded. A lot of people say he's got a tin ear to it, for example, today, talking about a billion dollars in funding for electric buses for kids, when we know that kids' grades are failing, and maybe we should focus on the stuff they're getting at school than how they get to school. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Look, I think we need to focus on both. Like, you need to walk and chew gum at the same time, right? It takes years...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I wonder if the president can.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: I think he absolutely can.\par \par \uc2 Look at what he's done this year, passed a bipartisan infrastructure bill, passed the Inflation Reduction Act. He has real legislative accomplishments that Democrats should be running on. One of my criticisms about my party is, they're not running on that stuff. They're not...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, they're concerned that it actually made this inflation worse.\par \par \uc2 Do you think it has?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: I do not.\par \par \uc2 Look, I have 15 presidents and prime ministers around the world as my clients. And every one is struggling with these issues.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: The Ukraine war, the supply chain.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But the president seems to blame everything -- I do the same with, like, diets. I blame, like...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: You and me both.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But I just wonder why he always does that, takes credit when gas goes down, points fingers when it goes up, says the oil companies are gouging or throws it out there, when there's no proof of that.\par \par \uc2 I just wonder, is he on the same page as reality?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Look, I think absolutely he is.\par \par \uc2 And I think he's calling balls and strikes. Part of why people like...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Is he calling them or his staff calling them?\par \par \uc2 With \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , whether you liked him or not, your boss, I had a feeling that was his call, his take, he was driving it, with -- again, whether you agreed with his proposals or not. Same with \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , how he sort of pivoted after the 94 midterms. He changed. Would a \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  change?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: I think all presidents change, right?\par \par \uc2 Even \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  changed.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I really wonder about this one, though.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Well, people said that about Trump, and he clearly tried to tack.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Like, both -- all presidents get through the first election, their first shellacking.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So let's say he is swamped in the midterms, Jim.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I know what you're saying, that you hope that doesn't happen.\par \par \uc2 Clinton quickly pivoted. I just can't see a \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  saying, you know what, I am going to expand production, oil production in this country, or I am going to be open to maybe, I don't know, tax cuts or whatever. But I don't see it.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Boy, I do.\par \par \uc2 And let me tell you why. So, when I was negotiating Obamacare, it wasn't \uc2 Obama\uc2 ,who I took to the Hill to make the deal. It was \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , because, A, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  knew all those guys, and, B...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And he was the guy trying to win them over, right?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Right. And he was the guy who could cut the deal and could put away kind of the politics. And, actually...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He was a lot younger than.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Yes, but come on.\par \par \uc2 Like...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I agree. I'm not one to criticize old.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I want to keep cognizant of this as long as I can.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: But it's a high-pressure job.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I mean, I'm just wondering. Britain's moved to a young prime minister. That governor -- candidate is all of 44 years old. I mean, should the next president we have, Republican or Democrat, just be younger?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: I don't -- I think they should be competent. Like, after what we had for the previous four years, let's just have competence. I think that's what Biden is saying. And that's what he's doing.\par \par \uc2 We can argue about the substance.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: But -- or the way he presents it. He makes fun of himself presenting it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: But the fact is, we just need someone who knows what the hell they're doing in the middle of these tough economic times.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, you don't think he's showing his age? I see a discernible drop in cognitive skills here. Now, I could be wrong. Believe me, there are days I'm going to forget you were on my show.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Never, never, never.\par \par \uc2 But is that going on? Should we be worried?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: No.\par \par \uc2 Look, I was with him alone three weeks ago, a month ago...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: ... talking just like you and I are talking about politics and life. And he sounded just like you and me, sounded smarter than you and me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: I mean, I thought...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, that's easy to do in my case.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Well, definitely in my case.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're brilliant.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: No, no, no, no.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: By the way, on your -- you have been very much pursuing blockchain, the whole technology.\par \par \uc2 We have got crypto. Some people are loving it. Some people are hating it. They say its time has passed and all.\par \par \uc2 Where are you on this, obviously a big proponent here.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But where do you see it going?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Look, I think blockchain and crypto is one of the most exciting developments in our lifetime.\par \par \uc2 I think, when this is all over, we're going to be known, this generation, for COVID and for reimagining our financial system. I'm incredibly bullish on this. I think it's good to have a crypto winter, getting rid of some of the B.S. projects that are out there.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What about Bitcoin itself?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Look, I'm bullish. I'm buying Bitcoin right now. I think anything...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, in and around $20,000.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: I...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Can it ever go back to 60-some-odd?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Oh, I will bet you my \uc2 Porsche\uc2  it gets back to 60.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Really?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Life truly is good if you have that to bet.\par \par \uc2 Jim, very good seeing you. Thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 Predictions, by the way, on who the next Republican nominee will be?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Really? So, not Governor DeSantis?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: No, look, I was with him recently. Governor DeSantis is great on TV, but there's something missing on his ability to connect with voters.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And you think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can? And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  telling, in other words, it would be an act of disloyalty for anyone else to oppose me if I decide to run, you -- what do you think?\par \par \uc2 MESSINA: How many times has he cared about what other people think?\par \par \uc2 Like, here's the deal. Once you have had that job -- and this is why Democrats have said we know Biden is not going to run. They're both running.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Jim Messina on all of that stuff here, as we count down the days, 13 days now to Election Day, on the midterms here.\par \par \uc2 Maybe get his read on things and how his predictions pan out after the midterms and whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would be the Republican presidential nominee.\par \par \uc2 As he was wrapping up there, albeit it on tape, we're getting news out of \uc2 Facebook\uc2  parent \uc2 Meta\uc2 , sliding after just a lousy report, certainly lousier than the Street was figuring. This is beginning to be something we're seeing out of some, not all technology stocks. But \uc2 Facebook\uc2  shares are sliding about 10, close to 11 percent in after-hours trading.\par \par \uc2 This is very similar to the reaction we got with \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's numbers out yesterday. They too slid in after-hours trading, they too dropping in the next day's cash trading. That was the case today. So, not everyone convinced that Nasdaq or technology stocks are back, at least not yet.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We mean what we say about being fair and balanced.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, we talked to the Democratic candidate for governor in Maryland, today, the Republican. Dan Cox is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WES MOORE (D), MARYLAND GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: I will be the first African-American governor in history to sit in Maryland.\par \par \uc2 But I have been very clear with the people in Maryland I'm not in this race to make history. I'm in this race because we're going to make child poverty history. I'm in this race because we're going to make environmental inequities history. I'm in this race because we can make the wealth gap history.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, that was Wes Moore's pitch to be the next governor of Maryland, a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 Fair and balanced now, we go to Dan Cox, the Republican candidate for governor, kind enough to join us.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cox, thank you.\par \par \uc2 DAN COX (R), MARYLAND GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Thank you, Neil. Thanks for the opportunity. It's an honor.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, he had said, I'm sure you heard it -- and this is Wes Moore - - that he's better for Maryland residents, because he gets it, says that you don't, said repeatedly that you would be dangerous in office.\par \par \uc2 What do you think of that? How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 COX\uc2 : Well, it's all fake news.\par \par \uc2 The truth is out that everybody knows that he wants to defund the police. He said this on Salon.com. He actually believes in reinventing and reimagining policing. His Open Society friends came in my committee to literally seek to take pensions away from officers here in Maryland.\par \par \uc2 And when you look at the parents' rights movement right now, we have a plan on his -- his focus is to take parental rights away and to continue this gender ideology from pre-K on up. That's what's going on, on the ground. That's why those polls are not accurate, because parents are upset by this.\par \par \uc2 I actually put the parental rights bill in with 50,000 parents in support in Maryland to say, let's do something reasonable, like Governor DeSantis is doing in Florida. And when you look at the crime scourge too, I mean, look, it's out of control.\par \par \uc2 And so we're going to bring sanity back to Maryland and once again restore freedom for everyone.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, you're talking about bringing sanity back, and I'm sure you know the Republican governor, who is termed out, \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , is not supporting you.\par \par \uc2 He says that you're a QAnon whack job, that your associations and gathering and getting buses to go to Capitol Hill on January 6 at the time and support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's contention that the election was stolen, that you were, in fact, backed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you're just, in his words, unstable. How do you respond to that? You have heard this before.\par \par \uc2 COX\uc2 : Fake news. Fake news again.\par \par \uc2 And Governor Hogan is not on the ballot, but I have stood in the statehouse. And, remember, I'm the only elected official who actually has fought for Maryland in this race. I have been elected as a town council president and served very hard for the people of Maryland, bringing sanity to local government without raising taxes.\par \par \uc2 I'm the only option for a balanced voice of reason in Annapolis. If you have a one-party state back here in Annapolis, we're going to go back to the days of 42 tax increases, where, remember, they taxed the rain in Maryland. Governor Hogan stood against that. I stood with the governor on the House floor. And he knows this.\par \par \uc2 And the only problem is, we had a severe difference of opinion on the lockdowns. Right now, my approach to not locking down our schools has proven true, because, just this week, Maryland's national report card came out saying that we are at the bottom of the United States, Neil, the bottom for our children.\par \par \uc2 And, in my district, 34 percent suicidal ideation rate increase for children. And what does my opponent want to do? On the debate stage, you know what he said? He said that he's going to continue this approach that the CDC just announced to include the vaccine mandate for the child vaccine schedule, which, of course, could be implemented by the governor's order through the secretary of health.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, no, all of these issues are very germane. And I get that.\par \par \uc2 I think where the governor parted company with you and others in your party parted company with you is your associations with QAnon groups and Proud Boy groups.\par \par \uc2 COX\uc2 : That's not true.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Many that you have disavowed, I get that. But I think what...\par \par \uc2 COX\uc2 : I have no association with those groups.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK. All right.\par \par \uc2 But let me just ask you this. You had said not too long ago that "Mr. Trump is the only president that I recognize right now" soon after the election and the dispute over that was legitimate and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was the legitimate winner.\par \par \uc2 Do you still feel that way today?\par \par \uc2 COX\uc2 : That was after the election. We were actually -- I was on the lawyers for Trump team looking at constitutional due process.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I understand that, but do you still feel that way today, that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was not legitimately elected?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 COX\uc2 : I have always said that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 And I will tell you, Neil, what's interesting is that my opponent has \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  here in Maryland last night, who is an election denier. I'm an election affirmer. We have \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  in Maryland, who literally voted to decertify the 2016 presidential electors on the House floor. And yet that's not my approach.\par \par \uc2 My approach is a constitutional approach to support our election laws, to make sure that we have integrity, to make sure we have transparency.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But are you raising doubts about it? I know you wanted to hold off on officials counting mail-in ballots early. Maryland's highest court ruled that they can.\par \par \uc2 Are you laying out the possibility that, if you were to lose the election, you would protest that election?\par \par \uc2 COX\uc2 : No, absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 In fact, like I said, I'm an election affirmer. And what I did was stand for the constitutional process with Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , who, by the way, vetoed that bill.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 COX\uc2 : And, Neil, you need to tell the truth here, because when the governor vetoes legislation in Maryland, as in any state, only the legislature can override that. And yet that's not what was done here.\par \par \uc2 The governor said there's a concern with election integrity. Instead, they forum-shopped for a judge to overturn the governor's veto and allow early counting without signature verification on these ballots. That's a concern.\par \par \uc2 And you know what? We're going to go forward because we need to flood the polls. That's what's happening right now. The future of this country is at stake. And everyone needs to understand this. Maryland is being used as ground zero for the DNC to try to input their view on the world of vaccine passports.\par \par \uc2 My opponent wants to take control of our bodies, to take control of our children's education, centralize even zoning, he said, in Annapolis. I want to put power back to the people. That's why we're running. That's why we're going to win on election night.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you think that what happened on January 6 -- I know this has come up even in your state and how they go back and forth and debate this.\par \par \uc2 How do you describe the events of January 6?\par \par \uc2 COX\uc2 : Well, it's horrific to see that crimes were done and the Capitol broken into. I made that very clear. I stand against that.\par \par \uc2 And yet, to this day, what's happening right now at the kitchen table is not being talked about. People can't afford their bills. Instead, the Democrats have only one thing that they're running on, some kind of smear attempt on people who are good people.\par \par \uc2 Like, my family, I was there with my children with the president in the -- at the White House lawn. I was not at the Capitol. And, instead, we need to look at what's happening right now with children. The second killer, the highest killer of 15-year-olds, and under, Neil, is suicide for our children.\par \par \uc2 Why? Because of the egregious policies of the Democrats in the last two years. We're going to change that as your next governor of Maryland.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right. We will watch very closely.\par \par \uc2 Dan Cox, thank you for joining us, the Republican candidate for governor in the state of Maryland. Right now, the two-term governor there, \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , a Republican, is termed out, but has not thrown his support behind Mr. Cox.\par \par \uc2 But it's still very, very early. There's still another couple of weeks to go. We will keep a close eye on it.\par \par \uc2 Also keeping a close eye on Democrats now starting to abandon the president on what's going on at the border and also what's going on in general with the economy -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY (D-AZ): This is a crisis. Arizonans are sick of it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KELLY: This is a crisis. Arizonans are sick of it.\par \par \uc2 The federal government needs to do more. We need to have a secure border. And the administration has at times made some decisions that are not helpful to Arizona.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, that's how good Samantha-Jo Roth is of The Washington Examiner. She can make news with Democrats and Republicans alike, did so here getting Mark Kelly to concede that what's happening at the border is indeed a crisis.\par \par \uc2 She joins us right now.\par \par \uc2 Samantha, he has publicly kind of discussed this. You got him to really express this more elaboratively. But it is a concern right now. And I guess, in states like Arizona, it's a huge concern, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 SAMANTHA-JO ROTH, THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER: Oh, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 The voters that we have spoken with have told us that immigration is a major issue. And we actually went down to the border today in Yuma. And we were able to see some of the migrants actually crossing the border. There were about 800 or so today.\par \par \uc2 They're just crossing in, being processed by Border Patrol. And then they're eventually released. And then they figure that out. And a lot of communities are stuck with dealing with this influx of migrants.\par \par \uc2 So, in Arizona, this is something that is especially pertinent to this election. And we know that voters and recent polls have showed that voters care about this, right behind inflation and the economy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do we know, Samantha -- I mean, other Democrats are saying you got to pay more attention to the border -- whether the senator was talking about get to the border yourself.\par \par \uc2 When he talks about the administration making some decisions on this, what does he mean?\par \par \uc2 ROTH: So, Senator Mark Kelly has been really outspoken about the Biden administration's decision to end Title 42. And that is that decision. It was made out of public health, where they were allowed to refuse migrants and send them away, basically.\par \par \uc2 So the Biden administration has relaxed that rule. And now it is much more difficult to expel some of these migrants. And that is why we are seeing a lot of the buildup that we're seeing at the borders, the influx of migrants. And so Senator Mark Kelly has been very outspoken, saying that he disagrees with the Biden administration's decision.\par \par \uc2 We asked him, is the president listening to Democrats who are in border states like you? He was not able to answer that question for us. But it is clear that there is a difference in opinion between Mark Kelly and the president. And it may end up hurting some Democrats in this upcoming midterm election.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I take it he doesn't go so far as to reverse this president's policies on stopping building the wall that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was building, or maybe something's changed. Do you know?\par \par \uc2 ROTH: Well, we did see something really interesting at the border today I'm sure you have been following.\par \par \uc2 Arizona has put into place a lot of these shipping containers in the areas...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. I have seen that.\par \par \uc2 ROTH: ... where the border -- yes, where the border -- there's a gap.\par \par \uc2 So, what we actually were able to witness today, we did see migrants that actually were able to scale some of those containers and get to the other side. What we saw -- we did see Border Patrol able to easily apprehend them. And it's very interesting, because Senator Mark Kelly has said that he does agree with the state of Arizona that those shipping containers should be able to be there.\par \par \uc2 And those are in the gaps where President Biden's border wall -- or, rather, President Trump's border wall was not able to be built. And what's really interesting is, we were able to see about, I think, $2.5 million worth of steel just sitting there, it's not able to be used, because those were supposed to be used to build the wall. And, instead, it's just sitting there. And now we're seeing shipping containers.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, I was noticing that Senator Kelly is in the race of his life. They're separated by a point or two, tops, depending on the poll you look at, Samantha.\par \par \uc2 And I know crime has come up as an issue in so many other states. But it seems like the border is a big issue in this race. I'm wondering about how the voters feel he has responded to it.\par \par \uc2 ROTH: So, we are just starting to talk to voters. I think it's really interesting.\par \par \uc2 Senator Mark Kelly recently released an ad which showed an Arizona ranchers saying, I don't always agree with everything that Senator Mark Kelly says, but that he agrees with his border policy.\par \par \uc2 So we are seeing some people saying that they do agree with what he is doing. We have also seen a lot of voters who say inflation, the economy, those are just things that are really important to them.\par \par \uc2 And we're going to have to see if that really is their number one issue and whether they blame Democrats for those decisions, since they are at the top of the ticket and, in Washington, controlling Washington.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, very good job, Samantha, getting a lot out of these folks. And that stood out, certainly to me, today.\par \par \uc2 Samantha-Jo Roth of The Washington Examiner.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, you have all sorts of people trying to help all sorts of candidates in this race. On the Republican side, the Republican sensation of the Virginia is doing his best right now, Glenn Youngkin, to help \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2  get elected Wisconsin's next governor.\par \par \uc2 Now, that race is separated by only a point or two, the two men teaming up together to close the deal. They will tell you how. I will give you a hint: education -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, there are close races; then there are really tight races, like what is going on at the state of Wisconsin right now.\par \par \uc2 The incumbent Democratic Governor Evers is essentially tied with his Republican challenger, \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2 . The issues that have been dominant in this campaign are education, certainly what has been going on right now on the crime front, but it is, first and foremost, probably on education that the two have presented their most stark differences.\par \par \uc2 Joining Mr. Michels is that fellow on the right, who you know very, very well -- I should say, on the left -- I apologize -- Governor Glenn Youngkin, the Virginia governor, who championed big reforms and espousing the interest of parents, and that helped him get elected in a state he wasn't expected to get elected in.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, welcome to both of you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-VA): Great. Thank you, Neil. And I have never been on the left.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, there you are.\par \par \uc2 TIM MICHELS (R), WISCONSIN GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Thanks, Neil. Thanks for having us on.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, actually, and you're -- you're actually -- when you're facing your back, you are on the right.\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you, Governor, first to you on this. You are supporting his effort in Wisconsin. It's a close fight. Why are you there?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, first of all, Tim's a spectacular candidate.\par \par \uc2 And what we have seen over and over, as Democrat leadership in states has - - have so under -- underserved their constituents, Republican governors have done such a better job coming out of the pandemic, faster economic growth, safer communities.\par \par \uc2 And I think Wisconsin deserves a Republican governor, and they deserve \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2 . And \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  is going home.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, let me ask you about that, Mr. Michels, because, I mean, on education, for example, you support essentially a student/parents Bill of Rights, that, oftentimes, parents, students are ignored in all of this, and that Governor Evers hasn't done enough.\par \par \uc2 He has said there's more funding that's necessary for schools and maybe getting mental health professionals and all of that. You part company with that, that it doesn't go far enough. Could you explain?\par \par \uc2 MICHELS\uc2 : Yes, so education is one of our top priorities, certainly, along with crime, as you just mentioned.\par \par \uc2 What are we going to do in education? We're going to empower parents. I will sign the parental bill of rights. We're going to put parents back in charge of the education for their sons and daughters. Universal school choice takes those tuition dollars with the sons and daughters to the school they want to go to.\par \par \uc2 Right now, parents show up at a school board meeting, they say, I don't like what you're teaching or how you're teaching, they're given the stiff- arm. They're told, we're the educrats. We're smarter than you. We know what we're doing.\par \par \uc2 Parents are going to be back in charge. The educrats need to know that parents and taxpayers are their customers. We are going to do better in Wisconsin, and the rising tide is going to lift all ships.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, Mr. Michels, but Governor Evers says that the parents' interests are already being addressed. They already have their rights. The interest of their kids are still foremost.\par \par \uc2 Are you saying he's lying?\par \par \uc2 MICHELS: Yes, so that is what Governor Evers might be saying. I don't know.\par \par \uc2 I will tell you, he needs to come out on the campaign trail. I know he's going to start a 10-day tour. It's about time he got out of his basement and met with some voters out there. I have been all over this state, from Superior, to Kenosha, La Crosse, to Sheboygan.\par \par \uc2 Everywhere I go, parents are very frustrated with what's going on in education. They're very frustrated with what's being taught in their schools. They're very frustrated with the lack of teaching ABCs. We're going to get back to the ABCs and stop the CRT here in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, on crime, on crime, which you also point out is a very big issue -- and I know Governor Youngkin has been doing much the same in Virginia to make it a top concern -- you -- that you would appoint a parole commission head whose goal it is to cut some of these moves to release those from prison early.\par \par \uc2 It's something that you argue that Governor Evers has been doing and has been detrimental and made crime worse. Could you explain that?\par \par \uc2 MICHELS: Yes, Neil, let me give you a little background.\par \par \uc2 So, four years ago, when Governor Evers was campaigning to become governor, he made a campaign pledge to cut in half, cut in half the prison population here in Wisconsin. He has released over 1,000 convicted felons on early parole, 300 of them convicted murderers. Some of those are cop killers.\par \par \uc2 And, most shockingly, 44 child rapists are on the streets of Wisconsin today, because he has this goal to cut in half the prison population. There's 21,000 people incarcerated in Wisconsin, 1,000 out, quick math. He wants to least another 9,000 or 10,000 convicted felons early on the streets of Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 That is not what people in Wisconsin want. They want their person protected, their families protected, their property protected. I'm going to back the blue. And I'm going to get rid of the catch-and-release DAs.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Governor Youngkin, the one thing you both have in common as well is, you were supported by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . You didn't mention it very much, Governor Youngkin. And, in fact, you handled that very gingerly. And it was to your benefit.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Michels, you have been pretty much the same here.\par \par \uc2 I just wonder, Governor, if you advised him to accept the former president's support, but you don't have to keep pounding it?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, let me just back up.\par \par \uc2 In a state like Virginia and in Wisconsin, where you have got to bring lots of people together in order to win, and one of the things we demonstrated is that we could bring together forever-Trumpers and never-Trumpers and Tea Party members and libertarians, and, oh, by the way, lots of independents and lots of Democrats.\par \par \uc2 And this is not about Republicans vs. Democrats. This is about shared values to put parents back at the table, not just at the table, but at the head of the table in their kids' lives, and to fund police and back the blue and stop the craziness of catch and release and making sure that, when somebody commits a violent crime, they do time.\par \par \uc2 This is to recognize that it's taxpayers' money, not government's money. They have a $5 billion surplus here in Wisconsin. And what \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  wants to do is keep it. And what Tim wants to do is give it back to the people it belongs to.\par \par \uc2 This is a moment for commonsense solutions to kitchen table issues. And parties -- parties come together. And we have seen the Republican Party come together around candidates across the nation as this red wave that found its headwaters in Virginia last year is spreading across the whole country.\par \par \uc2 And I can't wait to see Tim grab his surfboard and surf right into the governor's mansion in Madison.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, by that, I assume, Mr. Michels, that the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  backing, he hasn't said anything bad about you. You don't mention or go bragging about it about the state. I guess it comes up in interviews like this one.\par \par \uc2 But do you and are you inspired by Governor Youngkin's example there do accept it, but you don't have to keep pounding it?\par \par \uc2 MICHELS: Yes, I will tell you what the biggest story is, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Everywhere I go, I run into guys that are in the union, the trades guys. And they tell me: Tim, I voted Democratic my whole life, but the Democratic Party has left me and my family. I'm voting for you, because what I see in you is a guy who wore boots his whole life, 12 years in Army boots on active duty as an Army ranger, 25 years wearing steel-toed boots leading the men and women at our construction company.\par \par \uc2 That's what people want. They want somebody who can identify with the hardships that they have been through. They want somebody is going to stand up for them. I'm going to put more money in people's pockets through massive tax reform. I'm going to make safer communities and neighborhoods, and we're going to have better educational opportunities for every son and daughter in the state of Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 That's the big...\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, Mr. Michels, that one other issue that is coming up in your race, again, tight as a tick, is this issue whether there should be a total ban on abortions. That seems to be the case that the state is making here.\par \par \uc2 You parted from that somewhat, but I didn't understand where you drew the line. Could you explain?\par \par \uc2 MICHELS: Well, what I have said is, if the legislature puts a bill on my desk with an exception on it for rape and incest, that we're in a representative democracy, and the legislature is closest to the people, and I would listen to that.\par \par \uc2 I'm not an authoritarian governor. I'm a commonsense guy that listens to the people. So I said I would sign it.\par \par \uc2 But let's talk about abortion in this race. Governor Evers, he's the radical abortionist. He's for abortion up until the moment of birth and even vetoed a bill that would -- that would allow a doctor to kill an unborn baby right after it's born. That is the radical issue in this campaign on abortion.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Governor, the issue is a big one, Governor Youngkin in Virginia as well. It is a tough one for some Republicans, because, if you are in a state that's more purple than it is red or blue, you balance something.\par \par \uc2 How do you balance this issue? Do you think that a total ban is required, or this state by state examining just how far that ban goes be waived?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, in Virginia, last November, Virginians elected a pro-life governor.\par \par \uc2 And yet, just 20 months ago on the floor of our General Assembly, the Democrat leadership was arguing to pass legislation that would enable abortion all the way up through and including birth, paid for with taxpayer money. That is extreme.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: And Virginians voted to have a pro-life governor.\par \par \uc2 And so we find ourselves in a moment where I have asked, recognizing we're in Virginia, for our leading legislators to come together and find a way to send me a 15-week bill...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: ... a bill that recognizes that a baby can feel pain. And I will sign that bill.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's a place Virginia can land.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Governor and Mr. Michels, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 We will see you tomorrow. Here's "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020221027eiaq00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview with Gov. Glenn Youngkin (R-VA) and \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2  (R), Wisconsin Gubernatorial Candidate\b0\par\par\uc2 Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 2,010 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may n