or`s mansion in Madison.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, by that, I assume, Mr. Michels, that the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  backing, he hasn`t said anything bad about you. You don`t mention or go bragging about it about the state. I guess it comes up in interviews like this one.\par \par \uc2 But do you and are you inspired by Governor Youngkin`s example there do accept it, but you don`t have to keep pounding it?\par \par \uc2 MICHELS: Yes, I will tell you what the biggest story is, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Everywhere I go, I run into guys that are in the union, the trades guys. And they tell me: Tim, I voted Democratic my whole life, but the Democratic Party has left me and my family. I`m voting for you, because what I see in you is a guy who wore boots his whole life, 12 years in Army boots on active duty as an Army ranger, 25 years wearing steel-toed boots leading the men and women at our construction company.\par \par \uc2 That`s what people want. They want somebody who can identify with the hardships that they have been through. They want somebody is going to stand up for them. I`m going to put more money in people`s pockets through massive tax reform. I`m going to make safer communities and neighborhoods, and we`re going to have better educational opportunities for every son and daughter in the state of Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 That`s the big...\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, Mr. Michels, that one other issue that is coming up in your race, again, tight as a tick, is this issue whether there should be a total ban on abortions. That seems to be the case that the state is making here.\par \par \uc2 You parted from that somewhat, but I didn`t understand where you drew the line. Could you explain?\par \par \uc2 MICHELS: Well, what I have said is, if the legislature puts a bill on my desk with an exception on it for rape and incest, that we`re in a representative democracy, and the legislature is closest to the people, and I would listen to that.\par \par \uc2 I`m not an authoritarian governor. I`m a commonsense guy that listens to the people. So I said I would sign it.\par \par \uc2 But let`s talk about abortion in this race. Governor Evers, he`s the radical abortionist. He`s for abortion up until the moment of birth and even vetoed a bill that would -- that would allow a doctor to kill an unborn baby right after it`s born. That is the radical issue in this campaign on abortion.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Governor, the issue is a big one, Governor Youngkin in Virginia as well. It is a tough one for some Republicans, because, if you are in a state that`s more purple than it is red or blue, you balance something.\par \par \uc2 How do you balance this issue? Do you think that a total ban is required, or this state-by-state examining just how far that ban goes be waived?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: Well, in Virginia, last November, Virginians elected a pro-life governor.\par \par \uc2 And yet, just 20 months ago on the floor of our General Assembly, the Democrat leadership was arguing to pass legislation that would enable abortion all the way up through and including birth, paid for with taxpayer money. That is extreme.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: And Virginians voted to have a pro-life governor.\par \par \uc2 And so, we find ourselves in a moment where I have asked, recognizing we`re in Virginia, for our leading legislators to come together and find a way to send me a 15-week bill...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: ... a bill that recognizes that a baby can feel pain. And I will sign that bill.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s a place Virginia can land.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Governor and Mr. Michels, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, Inc.  Copyright CQ-2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020221026eiaq00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview with J.D. Vance (R), Ohio Senatorial Candidate\b0\par\par\uc2 John Roberts, Sandra Smith \par \uc2 1,689 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Live Event\par \uc2 FXLV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Let's bring in J.D. Vance, Ohio Republican Senate Candidate.\par \par \uc2 J.D., good to see you. Thank you so much. We'll get to the extremist weirdo aspect in just a second.\par \par \uc2 But first of all, I wanted to put up the RCP, RealClearPolitics, polling average. It's got you 47.3 percent, your challenger \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  at 45.3. That's about where the race has been in recent days, though a Marist and Siena poll recently have got the race a tie (ph).\par \par \uc2 How do you think you could make the difference in the next 13 days and pull off a victory on November the 8th?\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R), OHIO SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: Yes. Well, John, I feel very good about where we are. You have to remember that Marist thought that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  would win the state of Ohio in 2020, and a lot of the best performing polls have us out by an even further margin than that RCP average. So I think we're in a good spot, but I also think we can't let our foot off the gas.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the fundamental issue in this race is that we've got to get back to commons sense economic policies, bring down the cost of energy, bring down the cost of food, and get back to border security.\par \par \uc2 And unfortunately, \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , though he pretends to be a moderate in his TV commercials, has voted with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  100 percent. He has put over $60 million into the state of Ohio trying to run away from his own record.\par \par \uc2 And that's why I always encourage people to go to JDVance.com and help us correct that record because, as \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  runs away from the absolute carnage of the last couple of years and as he attacks me in a very dishonest way, we're just trying to talk about the issues and we're trying to talk about the need for new leadership and a new direction in the state of Ohio.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Well, I thought --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Ohio --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes. Oh, there we go. Yes, Sandra's with us. Sorry. Your mic wasn't up, Sandra. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK. Can you help our viewers sort of dial into some of these areas that you've been focusing on in Ohio? I mentioned Mahoning County, white working-class, socially conservative, deeply religious. Donald Trump narrowly carried Mahoning Valley.\par \par \uc2 This was part of \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 's congressional district. Economy, we hear, is the top issue in that county. But as I just quoted you, you said you believe that you'll win a lot of Democrats, especially in that valley. Why do you believe that to be the case?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, in part just because of what we're seeing on the ground. There's a lot of enthusiasm, big crowd sizes all across the state of Ohio. Compared to what \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 's able to pull in, there's just no comparison.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, let's go back a little bit. Let's go 20 years back to when \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  was first elected in the Mahoning Valley. He won his congressional district by something north of 20 points.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, the Mahoning Valley, I think that he won his congressional district by just a few points, and frankly that's one of the reasons why he's running for Senate is because he knows he would have lost that district in 2022.\par \par \uc2 So as a lot of patriotic, white working-class voters migrate from the Democrat Party to the Republican Party, that's really good for our campaign. That's one of the big strengths for our campaign.\par \par \uc2 And actually -- I actually think that pollsters are undercounting those voters in this election. They don't trust pollsters. They don't want to be told by a political pollster what they're supposed to believe.\par \par \uc2 And so, they're the ones who very often are not even answering the phone, which is another reason why I think Ohio is going to be very red come November the 8th.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes, we saw in 2016, and to some degree in 2020 as well, that the polls in advance of the Election Day weren't exactly accurate. But let me - - let me stick with them for just a moment, because women voters are going to be an important aspect of this election.\par \par \uc2 And when you take a look at the new Siena and Marist polls it would seem as though \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  has got quite a big advantage in terms of women voters. Siena has got him up 54 to 38 percent in women, got him up 50 to 39 percent in women in the Marist poll. You have a big advantage among male voters, 55 to 37 in Siena, 54 to 39 in Marist.\par \par \uc2 A lot of people say that divide is being driven by abortion. And we do know that you are in support of Lindsey Graham's 15-week ban on abortion. But where do you come down on the issue of exceptions to that? If -- you know, in the case of rape, incest or other particular exceptions?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Yes, John, so I'm very pro-life and not ashamed of it. Of course, you have to have some reasonable exceptions here. But I just want to save as many lives as possible. I think that's the goal of a pro-life policy --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: So what's reasonable to you?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Make it easier for young moms to afford health care. Make it easier for people to afford -- you know, afford adoption services if they need to.\par \par \uc2 But I actually don't buy, John, that we're way behind with women voters. I think there's a --\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: OK.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: -- really, really big groundswell of women voters who are really angry about inflation, really angry about crime, especially in our big cities in the state of Ohio. And I think because of that we're going to do very, very well with women voters, with men voters, with everybody come Election Day.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes. I'm sorry, let me just drill down on that. You said reasonable exceptions. What exceptions are reasonable to you?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, look, I think it's always hard to say every single possible exception.\par \par \uc2 My goal, like I said, John, is to save as many lives as possible. To try to protect as many unborn babies as possible. I do think that different states are going to handle this situation a little bit differently.\par \par \uc2 That's why I kind of like Lindsey Graham's approach because it sets a minimum national standard while giving the states a big amount of room to negotiate and figure out their individual abortion policies.\par \par \uc2 I think that's the right approach, especially now when we're still trying to figure out what the world looks like in this post-Roe v. Wade world.\par \par \uc2 But look, I'm pro-life and I'm not ashamed of it. And I think there are a lot of voters, male and female, who just feel like I do, that they want us to be a pro-family, a pro-child, a pro-baby state. And I think that's where we're headed.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right, I'm going to move on, but one last chance for voters to hear what those exceptions might be.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Look, guys, I think that I -- like I said --\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: -- one obvious exception, of course, is you've got to make sure that, you know, you have life of the mother protected. There are other exceptions as well.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: My view is, we want to save as many lives as possible --\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK. All right, I want to move on to \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , this morning, and what he said about you, J.D. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH): So, we're focused on, how do we bring back manufacturing, how do we focus on the economy, how do we give a tax cut to workers to help them ride out the inflation, and really try to dismiss J.D. as being an extremist weirdo -- which of course he is, and everybody knows it --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: "And everybody knows it," he says. Are you an extremist weirdo?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, that's the kind of campaign you run when you know you're losing. And importantly, you know you have a record that's really out of step with the voters of Ohio.\par \par \uc2 It's so funny, in that clip he says that he supports a middle-class tax cut. This is a guy who's voted to raise taxes 113 times to the tune of $6.7 trillion.\par \par \uc2 Just a few weeks ago he voted for the so-called Inflation Reduction Act, which raises taxes on the middle class by $20 billion and then sics 87,000 IRS agents to go and collect those taxes.\par \par \uc2 So, I think the problem for Tim is that nobody buys this fake moderate image that he's presenting. It's why he's falling behind. It's why is own campaign is running out of steam and it's why we're going to win.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, J.D. Vance, we'll be watching this race very closely. It is of utmost importance. Thanks for joining us today. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thanks, J.D.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FXLV000020221026eiaq00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview With Sen. \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2  (R-WI) Running For Reelection In Wisconsin; New York Supreme Court Orders Backpay And Reinstates All Employees Fired For Being Unvaxxed; Unhinged Student Spits At Comedian During Protest; Trans Activist Who Shows Off His Bulge In Dresses And Bikinis On TikTok Interviews Biden At The White House; Interview With Rep. \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  (R-GA) Targeted By Another Swatting Police Call\b0\par\par\uc2 Tucker Carlson \par \uc2 7,519 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Do I do the shows for free? Sometimes it feels like that.\par \par \uc2 That's all for tonight. DVR the show, which apparently I'm doing for free.\par \par \uc2 Tucker is always next and remember, I'm Watters and this is my world.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening and welcome to TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT.\par \par \uc2 What's that sound you hear? Oh, it's the sound of America getting a little better. You can feel it happening.\par \par \uc2 Last night, we played you a clip from an unintentionally hilarious MSNBC Town Hall in which some pompous airhead reporter tries her best to explain to Trump voters why January 6 actually was an insurrection, an insurrection in which only the police had guns inside the Capitol, an insurrection which the police welcomed the insurrectionists inside the building to insurrect. Quite an insurrection.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the insurrection that went nowhere, where the leader was dressed in Viking horns, eating psilocybin mushrooms.\par \par \uc2 So, we thought it was pretty funny. What we didn't note though last night was the moment where that airhead reporter claims that a police officer was killed by the mob that day.\par \par \uc2 Now, if you watch television, you've heard that line so many times -- so many times that you may not even notice when people say it anymore. They've been saying it constantly for nearly two years.\par \par \uc2 "Police officers were killed, many of them." Why are they saying that? It's not true. It's provably a lie. It is in no sense accurate. No police officer was killed by the mob on January 6th. Anyone who claims otherwise is always welcome on this show live to explain who exactly was killed. What's that officer's name and how was he killed?\par \par \uc2 We don't expect any takers on that offer. So, the question is, why do they keep telling us that? Well, there's a reason. There's always a reason. They're telling us that likely, so we won't ask too many questions the next time we read about yet another January 6 protester going to jail. We'll just think "Oh, another cop killer getting what he deserves." That's what they're hoping you will think, and they're hoping that because if you knew the truth, if you knew why they're really going to jail, you'd be very concerned.\par \par \uc2 And here is the truth: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's Justice Department is imprisoning people for having the wrong thoughts, thoughts that we all imagine were constitutionally protected, as all thoughts are, but don't take our word for it. That's just a right-wing talking point on our cable channel.\par \par \uc2 You can read the Court filings in January 6 cases over the past year and a half and in virtually every instance, Federal prosecutors argue in public that the political views of the defendants, not anything they actually did, not crimes they committed, but instead their personal beliefs, what they think, those beliefs are reason enough to put them behind bars.\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's DOJ, for example, Army veteran Jessica Watkins needed to be detained indefinitely because she had, "extremist and violent views regarding how to address what she believed to be a fraudulent election." Oh, she was an extremist? It's a term they never explain, but that apparently is now a crime. So, we decide what extremist means and then we put you in jail for being one. That's how that works.\par \par \uc2 UCLA student Christian Secor, that was the guy who sat in \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 's chair on January 6th is now in prison, and he should be in prison, explained the Biden administration's lawyers, because he had "extremist beliefs" and a "history of adhering to extremist ideology." Really? Again, you decide what extremist is and then put me in prison for being one.\par \par \uc2 In the case of Robert Morss, that was the guy who was busted in possession of a Lego set of the Capitol, the DOJ put it this way: "It is difficult to fathom a more serious danger to the community" than someone who tried to "abort the certification of a lawful and fair election." It's the one kind of abortion they don't favor, the aborting of a fair and free election.\par \par \uc2 So, in every case, though, the defendant's real crime was not aborting an election. No. The real crime, and the DOJ says it right in the filings, is denying the outcome of the election. It was believing the wrong thing. It was not believing that a senile man who refused to leave his basement during the campaign somehow got more votes than any President in American history. If you refuse to believe that, you're now a felon.\par \par \uc2 Question the system and they haul you away. Those are the rules now as hundreds of imprisoned Republicans can personally testify if they could speak. The legal precedent has been established.\par \par \uc2 You cannot deny elections and yet, even in the face of the mounting evidence, this is now a very serious felony, there are some extremists who are not deterred. They're continuing to commit the crime of election denial and they're bold enough to do it on video. Here, ladies and gentlemen, is one of them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER US SECRETARY OF STATE: I know we're all focused on the 2022 Midterm Elections and they are incredibly important, but we also have to look ahead because you know what? Our opponents certainly are.\par \par \uc2 Right-wing extremists already have a plan to literally steal the next presidential election, and they're not making a secret of it. The right- wing controlled Supreme Court may be poised to rule on giving State Legislatures -- yes, you heard me that correctly -- State Legislatures the power to overturn presidential elections.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Did you hear that? Right-wing extremists will literally -- literally -- must teach that word at NPR -- literally steal the next election. That's pre-emptive election denial committed by Hillary Clinton herself.\par \par \uc2 But here's the thing, probably not likely that an FBI SWAT team is going to show up at Hillary Clinton's house when she's in the shower tomorrow morning and in fact, the next time a Courthouse is attacked in Portland, Oregon, or a church is torched in downtown Washington, DC, it's unlikely that a DOJ Court filing will cite what Hillary Clinton just said as an extremist ideology that led to violence.\par \par \uc2 Why is that? Well, because in the considered opinion of the Democratic Party and the DOJ, it's not election denial when they do it. No, it's just "election affirming care." And there's been a lot of election affirming care going around these past few weeks.\par \par \uc2 In Washington, in the hometown newspaper owned by \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 , "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," \uc2 Max Boot\uc2  just announced that, we're quoting, "If the current trends hold up, Republicans are likely to take over at least the House and quite possibly the Senate, too, along with many State offices. This is how democracies die, both at home and abroad."\par \par \uc2 In other words, if you vote for the wrong person, that's not democracy, which by the way, unlike dictatorship, does not always come to the conclusion that you favor. That's not democracy. That's the death of democracy. Democracy dies in both darkness and non-liberal election outcomes. Oh okay.\par \par \uc2 "New York" Magazine isn't waiting around for the next election. Here is their latest dispatch: "There are already signs of 2020 election deniers refusing to accept their own possible defeat this year," and that's half true, of course, but "New York" Magazine isn't talking about Republicans: "Most observers are primarily worried that Trump's 2020 shenanigans may have served as a dress rehearsal for 2024."\par \par \uc2 Wait, what? So that means you have Democrats two years before the presidential election telling us that that election is going to be rigged, but that's not undermining confidence in elections? That's not an attack on our sacred norms? Not when it happens in "New York" Magazine or at \uc2 PBS\uc2 , which, by the way, you continue to fund with your tax dollars, the channel nobody watches.\par \par \uc2 The only people who watch \uc2 PBS\uc2  are the ones who are stuck in traction in the attendance, going out for a cigarette and taking the remote. But they're still broadcasting and here's what they're saying, "Nearly one in three Republican candidates for statewide office support false election claims."\par \par \uc2 Why aren't they in prison and what is the endgame of these dangerous extremists led by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Well, "Vanity Fair" figured it out. They blew the plan wide open. "And then there's \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ," "Vanity Fair" reported. "He hasn't been implicated in any murders (that we know of) nor has he currently trying to kill anyone (again, that we know of). He is, however, said to be actively plotting to steal the Midterm Elections and the presidential one after that."\par \par \uc2 Did you hear that? \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to steal the Midterm Elections and then the Presidential Election. How is he going to do that? And isn't predicting he's going to do that, isn't that itself a crime? We can't say. We're not \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , but we do know how he's going to do it, because \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  has been giving that away for years. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to do that as he always has done it, with help from \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  himself. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CLINTON - 2019: We know the Russians hacked the DNC, hacked my campaign, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 Trump knows he's an illegitimate president who got illegitimate foreign help.\par \par \uc2 CLINTON - 2019: I do think that he knows that he's an illegitimate President.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY -2019: I believe he knows he's an illegitimate President.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY - 2019: You can run the best campaign. You can even become the nominee and you can have the election stolen from you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 Stolen from you by Putin? We better have a nuclear war with him. Oh, we're moving toward that. Too bad every Republican leader in Washington is for it. How dumb are these people?\par \par \uc2 If you take three steps back, you will note that nothing the January 6 defendants have ever said, even the guy with the Viking horns on acid, nothing they've said is half as crazy as what you just heard \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  say.\par \par \uc2 In Federal Courtrooms, January 6 defendants are being sentenced to prison because they're not exactly sure how it is that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  got 15 million more votes than Barack \uc2 Obama\uc2  did. \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 ? The rock star who was President and yet his Vice President was so stupid that even \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  could barely stand to be in the same room in? You're saying, he got 15 million more votes than he did. How did that work?\par \par \uc2 How did \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  get more votes than any President in American history? Is that not a fair question? No, it's not. It's a crime to ask and yet no one bats an eye when Hillary Clinton tells us that Putin is literally going to steal the election.\par \par \uc2 So, how is it that only one side is allowed to deny the results of elections? Okay, no one answers that.\par \par \uc2 Here is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  claiming that Democrats don't question election results.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This is a nation that believes in the rule of law. We do not repudiate it.\par \par \uc2 This is a nation that respects free and fair elections. We honor the will of the people. We do not deny it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: "We do not deny it." Unless, of course, two weeks out from a pivotal midterm election, we get the sense based on polling that we're about to be spanked like the bad little girls we are, at which point we start making excuses and planting the seeds for more election denial, just as we did after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won. That's exactly what's going on now.\par \par \uc2 Even as they try to destroy the careers and tear down the character of people who ask honest questions about the last election, and that would include \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2 , who is the senator from Wisconsin, the Republican, one of the very few Republicans in the entire \uc2 US Senate\uc2  who has actually improved every year that he served there, that's the opposite of the normal progression for Republicans in the Senate, in case you haven't noticed.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2  is running against someone who is legitimately far out. His name is Mandela Barnes. On \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , Barnes once rang in the new year by praising -- and few politicians do this -- by praising Iran's Ayatollah because he supports BLM and we're quoting, "The first tweet of 2015 from @khamenei_ir is #BlackLivesMatter. Let that sink in. May This be a most wonderful year for you and yours," Barnes wrote.\par \par \uc2 So, he has got the Ayatollah vote. Barnes also wants to abolish ICE, defund the police, and end cash bail. That would result in further chaos and the total destruction of American society. It might be worth following up on that. But no, the media in Wisconsin are still talking about \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2 's election denial.\par \par \uc2 Here's his hometown newspaper, which no one reads in fairness, attacking him the other day for it: "Election deceiver, science fabulist, billionaire benefactor. After 12 years, it's time to term-limit Senator \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 So, again, just to be clear, Democrats are sending people to prison for questioning the last election, trying to prevent people from getting elected in the Midterm Election for questioning the last election, while at the very same time telling you that the 2016 election was rigged, the Midterm will likely be rigged and we know for a fact the 2024 election is not on the level.\par \par \uc2 How does this work exactly?\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2  joins us tonight. Senator, thanks so much for coming on. Is it a little weird to be attacked by your hometown newspaper as in election denier on the very same day, the people who run the Democratic Party are preemptively denying the outcome of an election that hasn't even happened yet.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): Oh, hello, Tucker. Those are some of the better headlines I received from the mainstream media here in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Now, let's face it. What is happening here is a classic tactic of the left, falsely accuse your political opponents of doing exactly what you do. Let's face it, how many leading Democrats said that the 2016 election was legitimate? Now, they spent four years trying to overturn it, a corrupt \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation. It was the complicit and corrupt media that allowed all of this to go on.\par \par \uc2 They were fed all the false information. Why won't they expose the people that fed them false information? What about the 570 riots during the summer of 2020? Two thousand law enforcement officers injured. One to two billion dollars' worth of property damage, couple dozen people killed including two people here in Kenosha, why don't they talk about that?\par \par \uc2 No, all they do is focus on January 6, I think this has always been a massive diversionary operation against their -- from their own wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 So again operation of the left. It's a classic tactic. And unfortunately, they have the complicit and corrupt media just backing them up.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So you're saying things out loud you're not supposed to say out loud. We interviewed earlier today, Dr. Robert Epstein, who is a Professor of Psychology who studies \uc2 Google\uc2  and he told us that he has never seen \uc2 Google\uc2  put its sizable thumb on the scale more aggressively than in Wisconsin right now, that \uc2 Google\uc2  is trying to lose you the race and get Mandela Barnes elected because they're so threatened by the things that you are saying.\par \par \uc2 Are you aware of this and what's your response to it?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: No, I'm fully aware and I know it is his work. You know, he's talking about how \uc2 Google\uc2  uses their search to manipulate the opinion, how they only text, for example, "Get out the Vote" to Democrat supporters.\par \par \uc2 We saw \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  use his Zucker bucks, completely took over the Green Day election in 2020, and yet, this is the safest most secure election. Of course, it wasn't. There were all kinds of irregularities.\par \par \uc2 I held a hearing in December of 2020, because I was not going to scornfully dismiss the legitimate concerns of tens of millions of Americans, because observers weren't able to observe. Here in Wisconsin, we have the Legislative Audit Bureau, nonpartisan, bipartisan audit of our election, 31 recommendations, because things went wrong.\par \par \uc2 We had our Wisconsin Election Commission issue guidance that is contrary to State law; election clerks, procedures contrary to state law; and yet we're all supposed to say, "Oh, no, it was the most secure election." It wasn't.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: No, it wasn't. And they absolutely hate you for saying that out loud and that is why we're certainly rooting for you.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Ron Johnson\uc2  of Wisconsin. Good luck in that race.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Have a good night.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 So Blake Masters has never been in politics before. Now, he is running for \uc2 US Senate\uc2  in Arizona. It looks like he is winning. He is one of a handful of candidates who could determine the future of the Senate and of the Republican Party. He is completely different for most Republican senators you're familiar with.\par \par \uc2 So we spent we spent -- we sent our producers months embedded with 