't want them at work. They want the America that all American used to be and that's Texas and every day, Laura I meet, people from every state who are flocking here from blue States from blue States are literally fleeing as refugees. Someone told me tonight a NACA doshas where I was to come here to Texas.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Last night on MSNBC, \uc2 Beto O'Rourke\uc2  sees a different issue is the most important one Dan, watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BETO O'ROURKE, (D) CANDIDATE FOR GOVERNOR OF TEXAS: A majority of Texans, including a majority of Republicans disagree without its extreme abortion ban at the epicenter of a maternal mortality crisis. That is three times as deadly for black women. When you have foreclosed opportunities to seek reproductive healthcare, you have condemned women to suffering and to unnecessary death.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I guess Texas with all these people moving in, you're condemning women to suffering, Dan?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: No, we're not condemning women to suffering. And look at it's the Democrats who have the radical policy on life. They want to abort a child up until birth and beyond if they need to. And the Hispanic voters in the valley understand that that's why they're losing Hispanic voters in the valley and all over Texas and all over America. Texas will not turn blue now or ever. They used to say it would turn blue because Hispanic voters will become democratic.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, what happens with that argument?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: No, no, Hispanic voters will be Republicans.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Dan, we're watching this race. Thank you so much for coming in. And get back on the trail.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Lieutenant Governor, \uc2 Dan Patrick\uc2 . Thank you. And now to Minnesota where Democrat leaders led by Governor, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  have basically abandoned the state. And they do not deserve to be reelected. Walz gave up on the Police abandoning that third precinct. Remember, during the George Floyd riots I was just reporting from there a few months ago, and that led to increased crime. And of course, the Police department, they are just hemorrhaging officers.\par \par \uc2 He's abandoned them on the economy producing a state that ranks 45th in terms of business and tax climate. And finally, \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  has abandoned the voters on COVID. Remember, he tied Minnesota all those other States and locking everyone down and taking away their essential rights.\par \par \uc2 As a result, the race that one saw Walz's lead, I think it was swelled to like 18 points just weeks ago, is now tied.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is the Incumbent Walz challenger, Republican Candidate. Dr. Scott Jensen. Dr. Jensen, I haven't seen you for a few months. Last time, we were back in Minnesota broadcasting, which issue is fueling your comeback.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT JENSEN, CANDIDATE FOR GOVERNOR OF MINNESOTA: Without question it's crime. Minnesotans get it that there's a lawlessness that's pervaded our state, and arguably \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  is the godfather of the crime epidemic. He quit on the National Guard, he quit on the cops when it came time to protecting the third precinct. He told the state patrol to stand down when it came time for some of his demonstrators to want to tear down a statue because they were feeling pent-up. He has quit over and over again. I think Minnesotans have seen him quitting on kids, quitting on their education. He's quit on economy. He's attached us to California car mandates. He did an end run around legislature.\par \par \uc2 People are just saying, what's wrong with him? And he's not engaging. He's basically dodging his responsibility, ducking the debates, trying to distract the voters. He has sort of taken a page out of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's playbook. And people are saying we're not having it. On the other hand, Laura, our campaign, we're just trying to be straight up.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, I think Minnesotans are, it's an interesting state for people who haven't spent time there. It's common sense. I think they want commonsense solutions generally. But I have to explain to people watching this how bad it is in old Minnesota nice. Last year there, violent crime, like aggravated assault and murder, were up 22 percent in Minnesota compared to 2020. Public safety was the second most important issue for Minnesota voters. And as you said, it's so important, and after the economy and jobs.\par \par \uc2 So Dr. Jensen, look, you first came to the fore, I remember when you came on this show during COVID and you questioned the lockdowns and the approaches that the government was taking back then. Do Minnesotans still remember the tyranny of the COVID lockdowns and what Walz did to our kids given these scores on math and English tests?\par \par \uc2 JENSEN: I think they do. I think they see \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  as sort of the lockdown artist. He locked businesses down and he locked kids out of school even after it was clear that this was the wrong move to make. Minnesota has been decimated regarding our kids' education performance. He locked elderly frail people into nursing homes that were infested with COVID-19, causing them to die barbaric deaths.\par \par \uc2 I really believe that Minnesotans get it. And what we are he a telling people is listen. We're going to fight crime. We're going to get your kids a good education, and we're going to push back against inflation. We put 10-point plans on each one of those issues on our website. We tried to do everything we request to let Minnesota know.\par \par \uc2 There's a contrast between what \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2 's hunkering down in the basement strategy looks like compared to Scott Jensen and Matt Birk just being straight up and saying let's have a conversation.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: A conversation has to lead to action, and his actions have been absolutely disastrous for the state. The best thing said about that abandon hulk of a building, the third precinct, it's a monument to anarchy. And that is the legacy of \uc2 Tim Walz\uc2  after those Floyd riots. Dr. Jensen, we wish you the best on the trail. We'll be watching two weeks from tonight.\par \par \uc2 In moments, Houston area Congressional candidate Wesley Hunt joins me on set. Why he says black Republicans are no longer going to be sitting on the sidelines or hiding away.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we're just days away from the show's fifth anniversary. So we've got to see who the next surprise shoutout is from. I love these.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: Happy fifth anniversary Laura. Well done. Five years, still going strong.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA), HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: Laura, congratulations on five years, happy anniversary. But thank you for educating the American public on the issues that matter most.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This morning "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " wrote a piece titled "Black Republicans are no longer hiding as they run for Congress." Now, one of the most prominently featured was Houston area Republican candidate Wesley Hunt. A West Point grad and Army vet is leading his Democrat opponent in the polls, Hunt adding, "You're seeing black conservatives no longer hiding from what we do traditionally."\par \par \uc2 Houston area Republican congressional candidate Wesley Hunt joins us now. Wesley, what's happening here? I know this is a fairly conservative district.\par \par \uc2 WESLEY HUNT, (R) TEXAS CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: It is.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But just around the country, you see a change, like people have kind of had enough.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Well, I think what President Biden says, if you don't vote for me then you ain't black.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I love that.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: And I've been black for a long time. I think is very insulting to black voters. And I'm running here in the district, congressional district 38, it's the newest one right here in Texas. It's a predominately white district, a district that President Trump would have won by over 20 points, and a black man's going to be a congressman in that seat. And the reason why is because we don't care about race, religion, color, or creed. We just want good governance.\par \par \uc2 If for whatever reason the Democrat Party has pitched to black people that you have to vote for us no matter what, and that's just not true. And they've also convinced black people that white Republicans won't vote for black Republicans. Well, that's not true either.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Democrat pollster Cornell Belcher, has a theory, Wesley, about why blacks are moving toward the GOP. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CORNELL BELCHER, POLLSTER: Going in a 2016, I think we didn't know exactly the extent to which misinformation, and some of the Russian meddling had on the election immediately. We've seen this sort of trickery before, especially aimed at minority voters. Again, this is, once again another hurdle for minorities voting.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Wesley, he's already laying the groundwork to try to dismiss a Republican victory.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: I think what people have to realize is that this is the worst inflation I've seen in my lifetime. This is the worst border crisis that we've seen in my lifetime. Enough fentanyl has poured into our country to kill every American five times. This is the worst crime that we have seen in our cities. And by the way, this influences everyone, particularly black and brown people. And we are not stupid. We are living in this America.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And 2.2 million people have been apprehended, that doesn't include the got-aways.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: The got-aways.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Just in the last year --\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- 2.2 million. So if you do the math on it it's really like 10 million maybe over the last couple of years and all the got-aways and all the other problems.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is not just a Texas problem. This is an American problem.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: It most certainly is. And so when you couple that with the idea of defunding the police, and then you want to take away our Second Amendment rights, no wonder why we are all upset. And this is why black people are saying enough is enough. The Democrat Party has not done good by us. It's time to look at our options, and that's why I'm here.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Wesley, congratulations. I shouldn't say it, it's premature, but two weeks from tonight I'll call and say congratulations.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: It's an honor. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's great to see you. Thanks for coming to the studio.\par \par \uc2 And if Wesley were to win, he would join a Republican caucus absent \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 . I know you're so upset, Wesley. And in writing in "The Federalist," previous guest Mollie Hemingway points out how Cheney's plan to divide the Republican Party failed, writing "Separating Republican officials from their growing coalition of voters and the issues they care about has been the D.C. establishment's goal for years, but it's not working."\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Congressman Jim Banks, chair of the Republican Study Committee. Congressman, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  was on TV on Sunday and said, if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the nominee, then the party's going to split in two and the real conservatives are going to start a new party. What? How does that work?\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM BANKS, (R-IN) REPUBLICAN STUDY COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: It makes no sense, Laura. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is more Barack Obama than \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  who she was invoking on the Sunday shows on Sunday morning, because she was talking about all of these issues. She's trying to tear down the Republican Party on the way out the door as she exits after her loss in her most recent election.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, she's attacking Kevin McCarthy who understands what \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was talking about when he talked about peace through strength, and we have to rebuild America, restore America's strength from within before we can lead around the world. America can't lead around the world when we're so weak at home. And that's why Leader McCarthy and the Republican Conference, we are unified than ever before around an agenda that puts America first. And \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  doesn't fit in that Republican Party because she doesn't get it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I don't even want to give her any more time because she's already taking up too much of our headspace, so I want to move on to Hakeem Jefferies, of course, who, he's trying to sound very hopeful, the DNC chair, caucus chair, strangely optimistic about Democrats chances for the midterms. Check this out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES, (D-NY) HOUSE DEMOCRATIC CAUCUS CHAIR: Very hopeful. There are close races all across the country. We do have a track record of accomplishment that we can run on in terms of what we've already done. And, trust us, we have done the work. We have made a difference in the lives of everyday Americans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, trust us, we have a track record to run on? What part of this do we need to unpack right now? I don't know. A nose could have been growing at that point because it was like a Pinocchio situation.\par \par \uc2 BANKS: Well, Hakeem, like all these other Democrats, are auditioning to replace Speaker Pelosi, who's end is coming very soon on election night two weeks from tonight. It will be a red wave election. Republicans will win back the House majority and we will retire \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , whether that retirement leads her to the beaches of Italy or back to San Francisco, wherever that might be.\par \par \uc2 So Hakeem is sounding that message because he, like so many others, Adam Schiff, so many others want to lead the minority party, the Democrat Party, after this upcoming election. But nobody's buying it, Laura. The American people are hungry for change. They are going to fire these Democrats because they focus on all of the issues except for the ones that they care most about, which is inflation, the border, crime, the drug epidemic, the issues that matter most to Americans.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: They failed. They failed.\par \par \uc2 BANKS: They failed the American people, American families, and working people.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, two words, "they failed." That's the whole campaign. They failed. They had their chance, they failed. Move aside. Congressman Banks, we're looking forward to the leadership races, and I guess you're going to throw your hat into whip, so we'll be watching that as well. Thank you for joining us\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Sey\uc2  was a Levi executive riding high and set to become the jean company's CEO \uc2 Levi Strauss\uc2 . That was until she spoke out on the extended school closures due to COVID. She's here next and she's going to say why she refused to shut up and explains how the mobs are taking over corporate America, the woke mobs. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER SEY\uc2 , FORMER LEVI'S BRAND PRESIDENT: I lived in San Francisco for over 30 years. My job is there, it's still there. My home is there. But as this went on and on, and I have very little hope that schools will open in spring. My six-year-old, he's a kindergartner. His experience of school since September is two half-hours a day on an iPad. It's just not engaging, and I saw his personality really start to change. He was this happy, boisterous kid, and he became withdrawn and he didn't want to go outside.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, that was former \uc2 Levi Strauss\uc2  executive \uc2 Jennifer Sey\uc2  appearing on THE INGRAHAM ANGLE back in the spring of 2021. Now, at the time we didn't care about the position she held, only that she was so fed up with California's COVID policies she decided to move to Denver. Well, little did we know that that appearance would cause an outcry at the company. She was attacked and falsely labeled a COVID denier. And Levi's management gave her a choice -- shut up or leave. So she quit, choosing her principles over millions.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Sey\uc2  joins me now. She's the author of "Levi's Unbuttoned, The Woke Job Took My Job But Gave Me My Voice," available for pre-order now. What a great title. Jennifer, we couldn't have cared less that you were some big exec, but your story of leaving the state and moving to, as we were calling them, free states, was so inspiring and so shocking. So why was that so threatening to Levi?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER SEY\uc2 , FORMER LEVI'S BRAND PRESIDENT: Well, to be clear, I was definitely getting some pushback even before you invited me on your show, but that really was, I would say, a straw that broke the camel's back. Once I appeared -- although I was at the company for a full year after that, but it was a very difficult time.\par \par \uc2 I would say appearing on your show, it was -- employees were outraged. It was -- I spoke with someone, you in this case, that they perceived to be out of step with their progressive values, and that made me all kinds of things that they deemed unemployable. I was called a racist. I was called an ageist. I was anti everything. I was anti everything because I had spoken to you essentially about something that I think we very much agreed on, which is that prolonged school closures were harming children.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Jennifer you and I and a few others turned out to be right. Without a doubt, we were right. Look at what's happened to the children's test scores, their mental health, all the other things, anxiety, alcohol abuse, all of it. All of it was driven by the lockdowns and no one's paid the price, except the kids.\par \par \uc2 SEY: Yes, no one has paid the price, no one has taken any ownership. I will say everybody seems to want to distance themselves from the decision. The governor of New York just a few weeks ago said, oh, boy, that was a terrible decision, Dr. Fauci just a week ago I think got on ABC and said he had nothing to do with it.\par \par \uc2 So everybody seems to want to distance themselves from it and act like it just happened. It didn't just happen. It was a policy choice. And Democratic leaders, frankly, made it. My governor, my former governor in California, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , he's responsible. No one's willing to take any accountability for it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Jennifer, why are big corporations so liberal now, just one sentence? Do you have any idea? Why are they so woke?\par \par \uc2 SEY: Yes, my take on it is, CEOs today, they want to distance themselves from the tycoons and oil barons of the past. It's not enough to just be really, really rich anymore. They want to be celebrated as philanthropist and do-gooders. And so they adopt these social justice stances, they assert them through the company, and they banish anyone as proof of their goodness. Anyone who disagrees must be ousted.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, so it's virtue signaling extraordinary. Jennifer, thank you and good luck with the book. Everybody go out and buy it, "Levi's Unbuttoned."\par \par \uc2 Another surprise shoutout, our fifth anniversary, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS, (R) FLORIDA: Hi Laura. This is Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . I just want to congratulate you on your five-year anniversary of THE INGRAHAM ANGLE on FOX News. I remember trudging over to the studio in D.C. when I was a member of Congress, and obviously we've been able to do a lot of interviews since I've been governor, and even you've come down and done some fantastic townhalls that I know people in Florida enjoyed. God bless you, and keep up the good work.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Governor DeSantis, thank you so much. You're pretty busy these days.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020221026eiap00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Dems Facing Gloomier Forecast In Final 2-Week Push; GOP Gains Momentum In House Races With 2 Weeks To Go; Dems Scramble To Find Econ Message With Two Weeks Left; Inflation Worries Boost GOP Midterm Momentum; Dems Withdraw Letter To Biden Urging Ukraine Shift; Oz, Fetterman Face Off IN Pennsylvania Senate Debate Tonight; Top Teachers' Union Heads Reacts To Student Decline; DeSantis and Crist Face Off In Lone Florida Gubernatorial Debate\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Juan Williams, Jack Keane, Bryan Llenas, Bill  Hemmer, Grady Trimble, Pete Hegseth, Steve Harrigan \par \uc2 7,911 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Up in the next hour, we're going to be --\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Of course, because you can't, because you just can't work it up.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: There's so much going on, especially with the economy being the top issue. Democrats who are fearing the shellacking. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  says they're changing their economic message. So, I'll join her on that. John, great to be with you. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Is it too little or too late? I'm John Roberts. Sandra, see you again tomorrow. THE STORY starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): John, we're welcome to see you too. Everybody can stick around. We'll see you, Sandra, in a little bit. Thank you both. So, I'm Martha MacCallum Good afternoon, everybody. THE STORY is this: don't look away from any of this for a second, folks, because these races are moving. Our brand-new Fox Power Rankings are tracking them as Democrats start shifting their message now trying to scramble for votes in places that they thought were locked for them. Take a look at this.\par \par \uc2 This uprising New York governor's race. Were the once comfortable incumbent, \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , is in a bit of trouble here as Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  nudges this race now from what was a solid Dem. In that column, now, it is just likely Dem. So, these are moves that we watch very closely. Hochul's now trying to recalibrate, scrambling to say that everyone deserves a "subway ride" that is without fear after this horrific scene that we watched over the past several days and so many other beatings that have frightened New Yorkers.\par \par \uc2 These two races are also shifting. \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 , Head of the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee, he's a very influential, important Democrat, and he's from New York, is now in danger of potentially losing that seat to Mike Lawler of the GOP, who you may have seen on just moments ago with Sandra.\par \par \uc2 Then you've got \uc2 John Katko\uc2 's seat, he's a Republican, leaving office. It looks like it could now stay in GOP control in what is considered to be a part of New York State that could easily go to the Democrats. And these are places that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won by double digits. So, Republican Congresswoman \uc2 Nicole Malliotakis\uc2  is running against Democrat Max Rose, who lost his seat in 2020. Hello, Congressman Malliotakis, good to have you here today.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NICOLE MALLIOTAKIS\uc2  (R-NY): Good morning. Good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good afternoon. I know, it's a quick day, right? So, you're running against Max, Max Rose, he's the former congressman. This race, we have at 49 to 43 in some of the recent polling among, this is a Siena poll actually, among likely voters. Why, why do you think we're starting to see this shift as we get within two weeks away from election day, Congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 MALLIOTAKIS: Sure. My seat is a seat that we flipped two years ago, we need to hold on to it for us to take back the house. I think that you're seeing resulting these, these districts that are now trending more Republican because people in New York are fed up and tired of what is going on. They see that one party Democrat rule at the city, the state, and the federal level has not served us where you see skyrocketing crime.\par \par \uc2 We have high taxes that are making New Yorkers flee to red states. We have people like \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  in office, and Max Rose, who actually supported these bail laws. They had no problem up until this year when it became an election year to support these policies that have made us less safe. And \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , to this day, refuses to fix the radical bail law. So, New Yorkers want to change New York; they want to see public safety restored. They have to support \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Just the other day, somebody with 20 prior arrests, he was out on parole for attempted murder, and he shoved somebody in front of a train track. This is the kind of stuff we're going to see, we are seeing on a regular basis, and we will continue to see if we continue to have one party rule in our state.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it is, it's very disturbing. And we see it time and again people who have a history of breaking the law, and in many cases, their own families. In this man's case, believed that he was not safe to be on the streets. They feared him themselves. Why, you know, why are there so many people who are unstable on the streets of New York right now, Congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 MALLIOTAKIS: Well, first of all, they're career criminals right that have been released back onto our street to the dangerous laws put in place, yet people like \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  and Max Rose who supported even paroling murderers and rapists back onto the street. Thankfully, we've been able to stop that so far, but you also have Governor Cuomo who closed mental health beds in our state and so more people are on the streets and it's a revolving door.\par \par \uc2 They're coming out of jail and they're not getting the mental health treatment that they need to protect either themselves or other people. And so, that's really the core of the issue and they are not doing anything to address it. You saw Mayor de Blasio put a billion dollars in a thrive NYC where we're still wondering where that money is.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Justice should investigate it quite frankly. But again, every disaster that we're seeing right now, whether it's at the southern border, whether it's inflation, the economy, rising food costs, public safety, all created by Democrats, and the only way we're going to fix it is if we restore a balance two weeks from today. And people who want to help us do that, go to NicoleForNY.com.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Max Rose has also been invited to join us here, we hope he will do that. \uc2 Nicole Malliotakis\uc2 , thank you very much. Good to see you today.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Reince Priebus, Former White House Chief of Staff under President Trump and former Chair of the \uc2 RNC\uc2 ; and Juan Williams is Fox News Political Analyst, great to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 You know, Juan, let me start with you. Just a few short months ago, the Supreme Court came down with a decision that pushed decisions with regard to abortion back to the states to allow them to decide. And it was felt in many ways that that was going to be an issue that would make it really difficult for Republicans. Do you find it surprising that it appears that it is falling lower and lower on the list of priorities after we heard for decades that that kind of reversal would be so, so devastating? People do not seem to be as fired up about it, as many thought they would be?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL POLITICAL ANALYST: Oh, I don't think there's any decline in the passion around the abortion issue in the United States. I think it's pretty clear the polls show, you know, it's close to two thirds of Americans favor, a constitutionally protected right, for a woman to have an abortion. I think what we're seeing is the dominance of issues like the economy really diminished the impact of that still sustained passion around that issue, Martha, and even when it comes to something like crime that we've been discussing here today, again, I think, you know, people understand that, politically speaking, it can be a very powerful sort of Halloween fright moment. issue for Republican messaging.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I wish it was only Halloween that we have to worry about it. In my hometown, people are getting attacked in the coffee shop, so this is, this is --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The same thing that relatively speaking --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Relatively speaking, New York City, New York State are pretty safe places. And yet, it's playing out as a successful advertising messaging effort by Republicans.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Yes, I mean, the numbers are pretty clear. We have an increase in crime in every category in New York. So, I don't know that it's pretty safe. But so, here's the ad that \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  just put out, let me turn to Reince Priebus on this here. This came out on October 21, it's a very different tune than you'll see in her earlier ads. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A safe walk home at night. A subway ride free of fear. A safer New York for every child. That's what \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  is working for as governor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Reince, your thoughts on that, on that new ad from Hochul?\par \par \uc2 REINCE PRIEBUS, FORMER WHITE HOUSE CHIEF OF STAFF UNDER PRESIDENT TRUMP: Well, she knows what's in front and mind of New York voters apparently. But you know, that's why \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  is doing so well, but to your question before, I mean, the reality is the reason the Democrats aren't doing well and the Republicans aren't doing well is that you have to look at what is front and center in the minds of voters across America.\par \par \uc2 Number one, it's groceries. Every day, you're reminded how much the price of groceries are. Number two, what's the price of gas? Number three, crime, which is why, a governor that's not doing well on crime is trying to make it look like she is. And number four is parental rights -- parents that are ticked off at their schools, and what's happening, what their what their kids are being taught?\par \par \uc2 The Harvard, \uc2 Harvard University\uc2  put out a study two 