 What do you make of all of this, General?\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST (on camera): Well, first of all, the basic premise is wrong. I mean, wars start because of a failure of diplomacy. But they don't all end with diplomacy. Sometimes one side wins, and the other side surrenders, and that's the end of it.\par \par \uc2 The fact is, it makes no sense. I mean, listen, I'm not a political expert. So, I don't know what the political motivations are here. But I will -- I do track strategically what is going on in Ukraine every single day through the Institute of the Study of War. This much we know is happening.\par \par \uc2 Strategically, there has been a major sea change in Ukraine. The Russian military is being defeated by the Ukrainian military. Mobilization is taking place, and it's not meeting the expectations and likely not to be decisive.\par \par \uc2 And the fact is, Russia is making war on Ukrainian people to break their will, but they will not succeed at that. And President Zelenskyy and Ukraine military would never go to a ceasefire now. Russia would likely go to a ceasefire. Why? Because that would stop the Ukrainian offensive that's taking back their territory.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, territory is, it doesn't really tell a story. What we're really talking about is liberating the lives of Ukrainian people that are under the gun of Russian occupiers. And painfully we've seen evidence that they are committing genocide and wantonly killing and torturing innocent civilian people during that occupation.\par \par \uc2 So, this is what is happening. That's the reality on the ground, and is no pressure from this administer ration on President Zelenskyy to go into any negotiations because it's obvious, things are working to his favor, and he is liberating his own people. And that's -- that is what is taken place, why anybody would suggest that? And did we learn something from Afghanistan when the previous administration negotiated directly with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , and crafted a terrible deal with the Afghan government not participating in it. I cannot imagine us doing something like that with the Russians and without the Ukrainian government that represents the people.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's a great point. And we've seen that the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  already reneged on every promise they made about the freedoms that they would institute in Afghanistan. That all just went right out the window when we walked away. General, thank you very much. It's always good to talk to you, sir.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes. Great talking to you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So tonight's a big night in all of this. You've got the big Pennsylvania debate between \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Mehmet Oz. And so tomorrow, we are heading to Pennsylvania ourselves to be there to talk to people, what did you think about last night? You know, did it change your mind about who you're going to vote for? What did you think about how \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  did up there? How about Mehmet Oz? Bill Hemmer joins us with a preview coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Alrighty, folks. We are just hours away from a crucial showdown. Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  battling Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz for that open seat. It's \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2  seat. He's retiring. So it's a Republican seat that \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  hopes to flip to the Democrat side. And a new CNN poll gives Fetterman a six point lead in this race. That's according to the CNN numbers which came out. Those are pretty recent. We've seen tightening and other polls. This one is just outside the margin of error as these two gentlemen get ready for a very important night for both of them.\par \par \uc2 "America's Newsroom" co-anchor Bill Hemmer is here but first national correspondent Bryan Llenas who is on the ground in Harrisburg, Pennsylvania where we're going to be tomorrow talking to voters and getting their reaction to what they think about what happens tonight. Hi, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Well, this is the most anticipated debate in the nation. Remember Democrat Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  only agreed to one debate against Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz. It'll be an hour long. It'll be just the two of them in a TV studio. No live audience.\par \par \uc2 There will be live closed captioning at the request of \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  as he continues to recover from a stroke. Now Fetterman's campaign is trying to downplay the debate and lower expectations. The campaign sending a memo to the press and others admitting it's likely Fetterman will lose tonight because he's not great at it and Dr. Oz was a TV host. "We'll admit this isn't John's format as clearly comes into Tuesday night with a huge built in advantage."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BERWOOD YOST, FRANKLIN AND MARSHALL POLLSTER: I think the big question for Fetterman is his health, and how he's going to perform and whether there's any limitations that could keep him from representing the state.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: A new poll shows voters who are really focused on the issues in Pennsylvania are split between Dr. Oz and Fetterman. We expect both candidates to try to paint the other as the most extreme choice tonight. Oz will likely do this with crime and Fetterman with abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 But take a look at this. With likely voters in Pennsylvania who care most about a candidate's character and integrity, Fetterman blows Dr. Oz out of the water by 36 points. I bring this up, Martha, because Fetterman's whole campaign has been really based on attacking Dr. Oz as being an out of town or a wealthy guy who's not relatable. It'll be interesting to see tonight on the stage, whether or not he will relay that exact same message or if he'll be forced to talk about the issues, especially the economy, which Republicans like Dr. Mehmet Oz our favorite on. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Bryan, thank you very much. We'll see you there tomorrow. So "American's Newsroom" co-anchor Bill Hemmer watching all this, as he always does, very closely.\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Hi, Bill. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, it's interesting. So basically, they're going to have monitors over the moderators. And so each side will be able to read what the question is while it's being said. And then they get 60 seconds to respond, the first respond, right?\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Yes. And then a 30 second rebuttal and I think a 15 second other response if your name is invoked. These aren't just monitors, Martha. These are what are described as 70 inch monitors above each moderator, there are two moderators. And based on my understanding, there will be certain TV shots where the audience will be able to see them, voters at home be able to see that as well.\par \par \uc2 I think that CNN's poll is right that Oz has some work to do in order to get people to come over and support him. I also think if the condition of Fetterman is what we perceive it to be based on some of these interviews he's done, that he's got some work to do to prove that he can go ahead and close the deal. You mentioned the text. I'd be curious to know from Brian but my understanding is that all the texts will be in that monitored. The question and the answer from Oz and the question to Fetterman but not his answer, why is that important? That's important because in some of these interviews, he's shown that he has not been able to tie those words together in his words mush the words together, MUSH is the way he says it. And it would not give him or afford him the opportunity to go back and correct an answer. That could be critical tonight.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, it's unprecedented. We really never seen a situation like this. The other thinking though here is that if he surprises at all in terms of his ability to kind of string things together. That people will say, oh, you know, it wasn't that bad and maybe it turns out that that interview that he did kind of burst this realization for people in terms of what to expect. And you see his side dramatically trying to lower expectations here. Oz is a big TV star so it can be very interesting.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Yes. And I think, I mean, you mentioned earlier they're lowering expectations. This is what the campaign manager said just the other day, that Oz's team will try and paint him in a negative light, because of awkward pauses missing some words, or mushing other words together. So they're lowering the bar.\par \par \uc2 What I thought was interesting on behalf of Mehmet Oz was over the weekend. He was on Fox and he said, we need to talk about the policies, because if we get away from other issues such as Fetterman's health, and if the people of Pennsylvania focus on the Fetterman's policies, they will show a clear distinction between these two men.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: OK. Oz has got some ground to makeup, right? We know what Fetterman's record is when it comes to issues like crime. We'll see how he manages that tonight. But Oz has an opportunity here, all right? Keep in mind, he's my height, right? It's like 5'11". Fetterman is 6'9". The monitors you describe, the setting that we described, it's going to be absolutely unique to see how this thing goes. And I think it'll take a good three days for the people of Pennsylvania to decide how they think it went and how they feel about the two minutes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. This is the only debate they agreed to. Early voting has been going on for several weeks already in Pennsylvania. You got half a million votes that are already in, mostly Democrat votes because they're better at early voting.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: This is for the one based on support.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And I think it'd be interesting to watch Oz's reactions, right? Does he -- how does he react if Fetterman starts to stumble a bit? Is he gracious about it? Is he -- so I think everybody's going to be watching this very closely, the body language in all of this.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: By the way, there's also a debate in New York tonight and there's the debate in Michigan, all that Governor races in New York --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Which are both getting super interesting. So we're busy.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Yes. We have a lot to talk about tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks, Bill. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 So tomorrow, as I said, we will be live in Harrisburg, Pennsylvania talking to voters, what did they think about how all of this went down. There's about 9% to 12% undecided voters right now according to the most recent polling in Pennsylvania. So those folks we hope we're going to watch this very closely, and see if it moves the needle at all, we will be there and we look forward to having you all join us for that show tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 So here's another question for you, where is the outrage from the teachers unions on the devastating learning loss for our children all across this country? Where's their passion as a teaching organization? What is their plan? We're going to dig in with Pete Hegseth who reacts. He has written a book about the American educational system. And also we have a new statement that just came in from NEA President \uc2 Becky Pringle\uc2  at the big union right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We now know the damage that was done by shutting down our schools. We know it was devastating. So what did the teachers union say about digging in their heels on all of this as they watched our kids fall further and further behind. Fox and Friends Weekend co-host Pete Hegseth joins me in just a moment, but first to Fox Business Correspondent Grady Trimble reporting live from Chicago. Hi, Grady.\par \par \uc2 GRADY TRIMBLE, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. The teachers unions are certainly not connecting the dots between the school shutdowns that they pushed so hard for during the pandemic. And these dismal test scores. \uc2 Becky Pringle\uc2  with the \uc2 National Education Association\uc2  says the results confirm what educators have been sounding the alarm about for more than two years. The pandemic exacerbated existing gaps in opportunity and learning experiences between white students and students of color, as well as between well-funded schools and chronically underfunded schools.\par \par \uc2 No mention of how virtual learning could be even partly to blame for those results. \uc2 Randi Weingarten\uc2 , president of the \uc2 American Federation of Teachers\uc2  tweeted, kids need so much support to recover from the pandemic, the pandemic, but failed to mention she was one of the loudest voices in support of school shutdowns.\par \par \uc2 We also haven't heard back from \uc2 United Teachers Los Angeles\uc2 . Last year, its president downplayed the negative effects of shutdown saying, there's no such thing as learning loss. Parents groups say our leaders, including union heads need to put politics aside and take an all hands on deck approach to solving this problem.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KERI RODRIGUES, NATIONAL PARENTS UNION PRESIDENT: Definitely not surprised, but continually, completely horrified by the lack of urgency and, frankly, the lack of inspiration we're seeing from our policymakers, our administrators and our elected officials around really getting after this and addressing what is a national crisis that we're facing right now -- we're facing right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRIMBLE: So I asked Keri Rodrigues with the National Parents Union what is the solution, Martha. She says it's simple. Get kids in the classroom for longer and with high quality teachers.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. We need a school break programs, summer programs after school programs and a national effort to catch our kids up. And that should be led from the White House. First lady is a teacher, be great project for her to take on, in a really meaningful way to get our kids caught up. Grady, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So continue the points that I'm just raising here as we bring in "Fox and Friends Weekend" co-hosts Pete Hegseth, the author of "Battle for the American Mind: Uprooting a Century of Miseducation." Pete, good to have you here today.\par \par \uc2 I want to start with just a reminder of this \uc2 Chicago Teachers Union\uc2  dance that was released by video back during the pandemic. So let's take a look at this that was supposed to make people feel better. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So you remember that. And then you got this, flashback from the teachers union head in Los Angeles. No such thing as learning loss. Our kids didn't lose anything. It's OK that our babies may not have learned all their times tables. They learned resilience. They learned survival. They learned critical thinking skills. They know the difference between a riot and a protest. They know the words insurrection and coup. Pete, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, FOX NEWS HOST: Martha, that is staggering. I was going to read that quote. In her mind, the role of teachers unions in schools is to create little activists. And if you don't believe me, look at what unions push from Chicago to LA, to the NEA, to the \uc2 AFT\uc2 . They are not concerned about excellence for your children. These are educational cartels that exist to protect teachers. They're effectively Democrat super PACs to continue the funding and the gravy train, which are never tied to results in the classroom, which is why they were happy to keep kids on lockdown, on Zoom, because results aren't the metric they use.\par \par \uc2 The metric they use is how much can they pay their teachers, How much can they prevent them from doing additional work. You mentioned summer and aftercare, they don't want that because results are not what matters to them. So if you care about your kids, stop looking to the unions or your superintendent, and take your own kids education into your own hands. You are the union for your kids. These unions are cartels to protect themselves.\par \par \uc2 No wonder they have nothing to say. There's been no learning loss. Ask any principal or teacher whether kids have fallen behind. Of course they can. But we've fallen behind for 30, 40, 50 years to no consequence whatsoever for these unions.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. We need a national program. When I was a kid, there was the Presidential physical fitness awards, and it was a nationally run program to try to get kid in shape. I would love to see something along those lines.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: You can't do that anymore.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That give national pride.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: It's ableism. That's ableism.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And to make people say that we all have to get behind this effort.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: You can't.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, I know. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Well you can't because they always say that -- better as fit kids are not fit kids, it's sad. Anyway, thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Pete, thank you very much. Always good to see you. We're going to take a quick break here and we will be right back. Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and Democrat challenger \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  took each other on last night. And then they didn't hold back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RON DESANTIS (R), FLORIDA GOVERNOR: I just want to make things very, very clear. The only worn out old donkey I'm looking to put out to pasture is \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE CRIST\uc2  (D), CANDIDATE FOR GOVERNOR OF FLORIDA: Gas prices are up as well under your lack of leadership. You slipped in something that dropped the price of gas, but only in October right before the run up to the election. That's so political. It's disgusting.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Why are fuel and natural gas prices up because of the Biden Crist energy policies. They're waging war on American energy. They are not producing what we need to do. They're begging Maduro and \uc2 OPEC\uc2  for more oil.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, a slice of what happened last night. Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  faced off against Democrat \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  in their only debate before Election Day, two weeks from today. And that was not the only pretty spicy back and forth there. Senior correspondent Steve Harrigan joins us now live from Atlanta with a look at what happened last night in Florida. Hi there, Steve.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Martha, you could really get a sense from the background noise there the cheering in the shouting on each side. This was a raucous debate. These people came after a delay from the hurricane to see the only debate to become Florida's next governor. And these two men really went out each other. At times it did become personal. \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 , who was a one time Republican governor of Florida, now running as the Democratic candidate, he went on the attack. He said \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , the current Republican governor of Florida, was simply too busy trying to become president to run the state properly.\par \par \uc2 CRIST: I have a question for you. You're running for governor, why don't you look in the eyes of the people in the state of Florida and say to them, if you're reelected, you will serve a full four year term as governor. Yes or no. Yes or no, Ron. Will you serve a full four year term if you're re elected governor of Florida? This is not a tough question.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We did not agree on the candidates asking questions. Governor, it's your turn.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, listen, I know that Charlie's interested in talking about 2024 and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but I just want to make things very, very clear. The only worn out old donkey I'm looking to put out to pasture is \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Over and over again, Crist brought up abortion even with a question about teacher pay. As for DeSantis, he tried to link Crist and Biden over and over again, with inflation and failed economic policies. If you look at the last four races for governor in Florida, they've all been decided really by a razor's edge. DeSantis won four years ago with less than one half of 1% margin only after a recount.\par \par \uc2 So it's a really tough race, many people saying that DeSantis, though, has grown tremendously, both in national stature and his war chest. Most people see him leading at this point. Martha, back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Very interesting. And \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 , who you pointed out, Steve, used to be the Republican governor of Florida, now running as a Democrat. But it's a battleground state that we watch very closely and it does get very tight. You have a lot of people from the Northeast who've moved to Florida now. And it's a shifting electorate and one that the candidates have to keep kind of moving to keep up with they're in a lot of ways. Steve, thank you very much. Always great to have you with us, Steve Harrigan.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So thanks for being here today, everybody. That is THE STORY on this Tuesday and we wish you a very good afternoon. I hope you're going to join us tomorrow because we are looking forward to getting out on the road as we head to Pennsylvania. And a lot of folks will be watching this debate tonight between Mehmet Oz and \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . So we'll talk to some voters, some of whom are undecided when we see you there.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020221026eiap00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  Sworn in as British Prime Minister; Interview With Sen. \uc2 John Barrasso\uc2  (R-WY); Russia Raises Dirty Bomb Claim at United Nations; Interview With Maryland Gubernatorial Candidate Wes Moore; Midterm Countdown\b0\par\par\uc2 Gerry Baker, Dan Hoffman, Jennifer Griffin, Lauren Simonetti,  Alexandria Hoff, Bryan Llenas, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 9,057 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, you didn't hear this from me, but there is an election two weeks from today.\par \par \uc2 The midterm countdown is on. And so is the rush to get out the vote, and the early voting, at that, long lines in Georgia, long lines in Florida. In fact, we have been seeing this play out in long lines in some two dozen states, where early voting, mail-in balloting has been going on for a while right now. And this is a midterm election year.\par \par \uc2 Some crucial moments that could decide how that vote sorts out two weeks from now, particularly in four key states where we have key debates going on, in Pennsylvania for the Senate race, in Colorado also for the Senate race, and, in Michigan, the battle of who will become the next or stay in power as governor, and, in New York, a race that shouldn't even be a race in this overwhelmingly blue state, where the Empire State contestants right now will have their one and only debate tonight.\par \par \uc2 What a night, what a day and what a next two weeks.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto, and this is "Your World," a very political world, where front and center is the rush to close the deal. Typically, this favors the party that's out of power, but growing signs that it might really favor the party out of power. Way too early to say. And, yes, two weeks can produce a lot of different results.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Harrisburg, Pennsylvania, where all eyes are on what could decide that fate in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Bryan Llenas has more from Harrisburg -- Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 For one night only, a political debate in Pennsylvania, Fetterman and Oz debating in person for one hour, no TV audience. It'll be live in -- no audience, I should say, live audience. It'll be live in a TV studio.\par \par \uc2 Look, this race has been devoid of substantive issues. And, maybe tonight, they will start to talk about things like the economy. Take a look at this poll. It is a new poll that came out. And it says that, basically, the economy continues to be the most important issue for voters, especially here in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 And this poll finds that among voters who care most about the economy, they prefer Dr. Oz by a 2-1 margin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BERWOOD YOST, FRANKLIN & MARSHALL POLLSTER: Oz needs to have something to say about the economy, how he's going to do things and represent the state in the Senate. And I think he's also going to have to talk a little bit about abortion and abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Dr. Oz can expect Fetterman to attack him on things like gun control, the legitimacy of the 2020 election and to continue to attack him personally as being a wealthy, out-of-touch guy from New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 Fetterman can expect to have to explain his positions on things like crime, fracking, taxes and inflation. He's also going to have to focus on performance. A lot of attention will be paid to his health. Live closed captioning will be used tonight, at Fetterman's request, as he recovers from a stroke.\par \par \uc2 Can he assure voters that he's up for the job? Fetterman's campaign is actually downplaying expectations, sending a memo to the press and others, all but admitting he's going to lose the debate tonight to the TV doctor -- quote -- "The TV studio is Oz's comfort zone. We will admit this isn't John's format. But John's going to win this race even if he doesn't win the debate."\par \par \uc2 A Republican super PAC has just flushed this race with $6 million. The Democrats are sending in the big guns, President Biden and former President Obama. They will be here November 5, reportedly just three days before Election Day.\par \par \uc2 Already, Neil, about half-a-million people have already cast their ballots -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I like how you snuck in there, Bryan, the attack line that there I guess he's using, Fetterman, he's out of touch and he's from New Jersey, as if that closes the deal, right?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you, my friend. Great job, as always.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Alexandria Hoff, where they have a big debate going on between the gubernatorial candidates.\par \par \uc2 Now, what's -- odd, Alexandria, this is what is really striking here -- is, this is the state where the Democrat normally doesn't even have to worry about getting elected or reelected, because it's bluer than blue, but maybe not now. What's going on?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Neil, even among Governor Hochul's base, the thing is crime.\par \par \uc2 I mean, crime has ranked in the top two of the most concerning issues even among her base there. And that's according to a \uc2 Quinnipiac University\uc2  poll released last week. So we're expecting to hear a lot on that tonight, especially from Governor Hochul's Republican challenger, Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , who has made that issue really his thing and has made this race far more competitive than would have been expected out of a state like New York, as you mentioned.\par \par \uc2 Governor Hochul has adapted, releasing this new ad. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): You deserve to feel safe.\par \par \uc2 And, as your governor, I won't stop working until you do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF: So, just yesterday, Hochul joined New York City Mayor Eric Adams in announcing a new safety plan.\par \par \uc2 This follows outrage over a series of unprovoked attacks on the city subway system, including this one that happened Friday afternoon, where a 32-year- old was brutally shoved at random onto the tracks in Brooklyn. He survived with a broken collarbone.\par \par \uc2 Zeldin says Hochul speaking out now is just an attempt to play catchup. He shared this last night on "Special Report."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R-NY), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: So now she's decided, because she wants to save \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , that she's going to start talking about crime. We have been talking about crime because we have been talking about wanting to save the state.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Latest FOX News power rankings has moved this race from a solid D to a likely D. And it's having an effect on other statewide candidates.\par \par \uc2 This week, there have been four Northeastern shifts in favor of Republicans, three of them in districts in New York, one in New Jersey. Overall, the latest prediction is that Republicans will take a 16-seat majority in the House. That is a one-seat increase from last week, Neil, so we're expecting a whole lot out of tonight.\par \par \uc2 But nobody really expected this race to be where it is now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Nobody, indeed.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Alexandria Hoff, on all of that.\par \par \uc2 Well, a lot of eyes are certainly going to be on these debates and in these contests here. The fact of the matter is, though, debates might matter more on the presidential level. We can remember \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  might have secured his win back in 1980 thanks to his one and only debate with \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  at the time.\par \par \uc2 Of course, John F. Kennedy made his clash with the then-Vice President of the United States \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  famous for appearing presidential and a good person to turn to in that event.\par \par \uc2 Now, on the state level, even the local level, does it matter as much? Well, given the magnitude of some of these contests and the name-calling back and forth, probably.\par \par \uc2 Susan Crabtree, RealClearPolitics, is with us, as well as Eliza Collins, Wall Street Journal, as well.\par \par \uc2 Eliza, end it with you, begin with you on the importance at the Senate level or even the gubernatorial level on a debate, especially if it's a one-and-done-type event, as it will be in New York, as it will be in this Senate race in the Pennsylvania clash tonight. How important is it? Because we tend to give them the gravitas they may be deserved on the presidential level, but not here. Are we missing something?\par \par \uc2 ELIZA COLLINS, THE WALL STREET JOURNAL: Well, in these battleground states, these elections could be decided by 10,000 votes or fewer.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we saw that in 2020. And so that's not a lot of voters who need to sort of tune in and decide. It really depends how substantive these debates are and if voters that are watching are actually undecided. Voters who watch debates tend to be the type of voters who are already engaged in the political process and probably know who they're going to vote for.\par \par \uc2 But you don't need a lot of them to decide a race in some of these battlegrounds. And so it really could depend in at least a very close race.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Indeed, we have some record-setting activity, early voting, and that's going on in half the states, particularly in places like Georgia and Florida. So there is a lot to what she says, Susan.\par \par \uc2 And I am wondering, from your vantage point, there are still a small percentage in each of those states where the candidates in question or even the candidates up for debate tonight might yet be swayed. In the Pennsylvania race in particular, depending on the poll you look at, they're within the statistical range that could have the lead either way.\par \par \uc2 How do you see it going?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN CRABTREE, REALCLEARPOLITICS: I think these are two really pivotal debates that we're seeing tonight.\par \par \uc2 Usually, I would say no. Usually, debates or an exclamation point on the campaign wherever the candidates are going, but, right now, we're seeing a late surge with Republicans, especially with this dramatic surge by \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 . It's -- in the Quinnipiac poll that we saw, I thought the one particular statistic, that data point that was the most interesting was crime is ahead of inflation and the economy by 28 percent to 20 percent in that race.\par \par \uc2 And you had \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  just reacting yesterday to this crime surge, especially when people cannot plan to go out and enjoy their neighborhoods at night. They don't feel safe on the subway. You have seen so many attacks on the subway lately, and even \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  himself was attacked on the campaign trail. He was 20 points behind then. And now we see him either four -- we have six points' margin with the RealClearPolitics average of polls.\par \par \uc2 So -- and the similar with Mehmet Oz. You have the Senate Leadership Fund, McConnell's PAC, pouring $6 million into this race because they believe Mehmet Oz is within striking distance, even though Fetterman has led pretty significantly throughout the summer.\par \par \uc2 But it's really a jump shot with the status from his stroke, even though his doctor said he's recovering well.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 Eliza, if you think about it, it was last year at this time, right, \uc2 Phil Murphy\uc2  going into Election Day. The New Jersey governor had a substantial lead, I think about 15 points going in. He ultimately won, but by a couple of points, so that race ended up being much tighter than the poll allowed.\par \par \uc2 And I wonder if there is -- and you hear this from conservatives, Republicans, quite a bit, that the polls don't represent them accurately, and they're -- and they're always proven right after the fact, with much more competitive contests that turn into wins in some cases.\par \par \uc2 Has that been corrected here? In other words, is there a head fake going on here, or these numbers don't represent how people really feel?\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: You know, it remains to be seen, but I think the big question is really, do turnout match what the polls are?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 COLLINS: So, in some of these states, I have gone out and talked to voters, these issues like we're talking about crime in New York, I was just in Oregon for that competitive toss-up governor's race in blue Oregon. Voters are also talking about crime.\par \par \uc2 It really did feel like a toss-up. So, I think sort of the trends are captured in the polls, but we don't know who's actually going to show up on Election Day. Midterm voters tend to favor the party out of power historically. Republican voters tend to show up more. There's, of course, the question of those voters who showed up for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2016 and 2020. Do they show up when he's not on the ballot?\par \par \uc2 So all of those questions, I think, make it a big toss-up about a lot of these races. But I think polls are capturing trends and issues pretty well right now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, ladies, I want to thank you both for that.\par \par \uc2 As they were wrapping up, we're trying to get some more information this earthquake that's occurred out near San Jose, California. We're told that it's a 5.1-magnitude earthquake that again was just south of San Jose. Other reports put it directly in San Jose in the area of Seven Trees, which is very close by.\par \par \uc2 The information had it that it hit around 11:42 a.m. Pacific time, so a little bit more than three hours ago -- or -- I'm sorry -- I should say about an hour-and-a-half ago, three-hour different time here in the Eastern part of the United States.\par \par \uc2 Earthquakes that have been measured in the past in the area had them clocked at around 2.5 to 3.5 to 5 was about as high as we got a couple of years back. This was a 5.1.\par \par \uc2 The tweet coming from the California Geological Survey simply said -- quoting here -- additional shaking from aftershocks can be expected in the region.\par \par \uc2 We're continuing to monitor the region. But, as you can see just with some of these aerial shots we're providing here, it doesn't look to be any substantial damage. And life is going on, as is traffic, as is pretty much the case in California. It doesn't look too heavy there or too alarming there, but we will keep an eye on it for you.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, there does seem to be a focus right now in all of these races, particularly in Florida, on the state of the economy and inflation and what is happening even on real estate.\par \par \uc2 For a lot of people in that state, it's gotten out of reach, a sign of the strength and excitement around Florida, but also a problem for those now who are living with the expense of dealing with that. It came up in that debate lots and lots of times here. But, again, the debate did, as it is pretty much everywhere else in the country, come back to the economy and inflation and prices and who's better to deal with all of that.\par \par \uc2 Lauren Simonetti has more for us now -- Lauren.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN SIMONETTI, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Absolutely, Neil.\par \par \uc2 So, right out of the gate, this exchange set the tone.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FMR. GOV. \uc2 CHARLIE CRIST\uc2  (D-FL), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: We have a housing crisis in our state. Good luck buying a home. Good luck renting an apartment. And it's because Governor DeSantis has taken his eye off the ball.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  has voted with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  100 percent of the time to give us these inflationary policies.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SIMONETTI: Crist tried to get back on offense, blamed DeSantis for other high prices in Florida, like gas and utilities. He got this response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: We have the fifth lowest gas prices in the country right now because we did do a gas tax holiday. We're proud of that. What we're going to do for Floridians who commute, we're going to reduce tolls by 50 percent statewide for all commuters in the state of Florida.\par \par