ed at the hip in these races and inflation or the economy, period, number one, with very few exceptions. That's something that galvanizes voters in a presidential election year. We normally don't see it in a midterm one. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN BRENBERG, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, well, that's because we normally don't see inflation like this, Neil.\par \par \uc2 I mean, these are times we haven't seen for a long time. And you're right. It's galvanizing the electorate. It's interesting to watch these last 14, 15 days of the election, because you can see the desperation. You can see Democrats walking up to the midterms without a message on the economy, trying to change the narrative, trying to change people's minds.\par \par \uc2 And the fact is, the numbers are just coming in ways that are completely unfriendly to Democrats and helping folks like DeSantis down in Florida, potentially Oz up in Pennsylvania, across the country simplify their message. It's the economy. It's the prices you pay. If anyone's lost focus, it's this administration, the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 They have been talking about everything else besides the number one issue on voters' minds. We will see how that plays at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, the president has somewhat pivoted on the inflation issue by taking it on and saying Republicans will make it worse.\par \par \uc2 Now, I don't know what that strategy could mean or how it would play off, because a lot of people are looking around, I don't know. I have seen a double, a tripling in meat and other prices. I can't see it getting much worse. But it doesn't seem like a strategy that will benefit him in the moment or Democrats. But I could be missing something. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 BRENBERG: Yes, that one's a head-scratcher.\par \par \uc2 Exactly how are Republicans going to make inflation worse? I mean, the one thing Republicans have been talking about for months and months is bringing 