sn't necessarily support you. He doesn't say anything bad about you. But he won't support Cox, who he says, "in my opinion, is mentally not stable."\par \par \uc2 So there's a lot of friction between your governor, Governor Hogan, and, of course, this \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 -backed candidate. But you have heard what the governor says of Dan Cox. Do you agree with that?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, I mean, the governor has been clear, where he has said that not only is he not going to support the Republican nominee. He said he wouldn't even give him a tour of the governor's office. And he's called him unfit to lead.\par \par \uc2 But the thing that I know is that the reason that we are seeing the momentum around the state is not because people around the state are being asked to be afraid of something. It's because we're asking them to believe in something.\par \par \uc2 And we started this campaign over a year ago on the basic premise that this was about economics. This was about, how do you create platforms and pathways for work, wages and wealth for all of our families?\par \par \uc2 And I think the thing that's resonating amongst Marylanders is, we're tired of being at each other's throats. We're tired of caring more about where the idea came from than, is it a good idea?\par \par \uc2 And so we are truly prepared to go in and build a growing economy in the state of Maryland, to address the issue of public safety, and to also make sure we have world-class schools in our state. We can do that. And I think the state is rallying behind that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I have been following your race very closely, Mr. Moore, and I notice that you have avoided the trappings of -- I don't say trappings to make it sound awful, but to appear as a woke candidate or to get into the progressive wing in your party.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of back-and-forth that the party has not paid enough attention to the inflation problems, to what's going on at the border. How do you feel about that, that your campaign of seems to keep these issues up there, but not to become captive by them, and maybe not to become too woke talking about them?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes, I mean, I tell people where I have been a public servant for much of my life.\par \par \uc2 I led soldiers in combat in Afghanistan. I built a successful small business in the state of Maryland. I led one of the largest poverty- fighting organizations in this country. And while I have been a public servant for my whole life, I haven't been a politician.\par \par \uc2 So, I don't give in to the regular political talking points. And when we started our campaign and said, this is about economics. This is about creating economic growth for our state. And people asked me, they said, well, how do you plan on pivoting when you win a general election, or how do you plan on pivoting to appeal to certain groups?\par \par \uc2 My answer was simple. I don't. It's still about creating pathways for work, wages and wealth for all of our families. And that's exactly the way that I plan on governing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, when I asked you about the progressives in your party, sir -- and I know, as governor, this would not be your issue here. You wouldn't be in Washington.\par \par \uc2 But there was a move by at some 30 odd members to urge the president to get Russia to the negotiating table to find a peaceful settlement to Ukraine. They have since rescinded that. There's been a bit of divide in the party as to how to handle that.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that that's a good idea?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, I think, from what I'm continuing to see as we're going all around the state of Maryland, the issues that Marylanders want their governor to focus on is, what are we doing to get people back to work?\par \par \uc2 What are we doing to fix a broken childcare system that, in the state of Maryland, we have lost over 750 childcare centers since the start of COVID? What are we doing to focus on job re-skilling and job retraining, because, right now, there's two available jobs in Maryland for every single person filing for unemployment?\par \par \uc2 And so those are the issues that Marylanders are focused on. Those are the issues that they're asking their governor to focus on, to be the chief executive of the state. And I think that's the reason that we continue to get the momentum from both independents, Democrats and Republicans around the state who believe that the direction that we're leading in is one that they want their next governor to focus on.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, if you do become the next governor, you will be the first one after eight years of Republican rule. I know you would be the first African-American to govern Maryland, the third in the country.\par \par \uc2 But you have not presented yourself as the African-American candidate. Some people have likened your campaign to that of \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 's nationally. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes, well, I'm -- it's not lost on me the history-making nature of this race, that I will be the first African-American governor in history to sit in Maryland.\par \par \uc2 But I have been very clear with the people in Maryland I'm not in this race to make history. I'm in this race because we're going to make child poverty history. I'm in this race because we're going to make environmental inequities history. I'm in this race because we can make the wealth gap history. I'm in this race because the days of getting beaten by Virginia and all of our neighboring states economically, those days are going to be history.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's the thing that I have stressed to people, is that the history-making nature of this campaign, it's not individual. The history- making nature of that campaign is going to be collective for the state of Maryland.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We will watch very closely. Wes Moore, thank you very much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 The candidate for governor of the state of Maryland Wes Moore joining us right now.\par \par \uc2 I do want to explain that we did hear back from Dan Cox, his opponent, and he will be joining us tomorrow. There's been some friction between Mr. Cox and the present Republican governor, \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2 , I was just saying, who will not support him, says he's a bad idea.\par \par \uc2 But we want to make sure that Mr. Cox has an opportunity to respond to that tomorrow, and he will, fair and balanced, which we always aim for here.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, speaking up tomorrow and all these great races coming up, we will be having the candidate for governor in the state of Virginia, the Republican candidate. We will get his stand to give us maybe an idea of where they want to take this. We have \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2 , who will be getting some help from the Virginia Republican governor, Glenn Youngkin, as each now moves forward to try to get a sense of where we want to go.\par \par \uc2 The Republican push in Wisconsin by a guy who very few thought had a chance in Virginia tomorrow, here and only here.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Russia says not so.\par \par \uc2 There were comments it was making that Ukraine was planning to use a dirty bomb against Russia, some that the United Nations and among \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries saying that was going to be an excuse to launch just the same type of attack, using a dirty bomb on Ukraine. They were trying to settle all of this.\par \par \uc2 I don't know how far they got, but I know Jennifer Griffin does. She joins us from the Pentagon -- Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Neil, well, the potential for miscalculation right now is enormous.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Russia would be making an incredibly serious mistake it were to use a tactical nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 