ing the motherland, which certainly isn't true -- it's Putin who's launching an aggressive attack on Ukraine -- and the threat of nuclear war, the president, President Biden, responds with concern about Armageddon and a World War III, giving a throwaway to Putin's rhetorical brinkmanship.\par \par \uc2 And I think that's what Putin is doing here. He's a -- he's a KGB guy, and he's trying to mount this sort of propaganda information operations.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, it looks like he could be flailing then.\par \par \uc2 But, Dan, I wanted to sort of tap the former CIA station chief in Moscow brain here, which is a substantial brain, at that, I might point out, but these rumors that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is desperate. He's a cornered, wounded animal, and now these pictures that have emerged of him shaking hands with soldiers and what appears to be on his hand, like he had been hooked up to chemotherapy or something like that, that he's battling cancer.\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What did you make of that?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: So, I have asked a lot of my colleagues about that. And the conclusion is pretty much that there's no indication that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is suffering from cancer or any other debilitating disease, that he could have been hooked up to an I.V., but it doesn't mean that there's anything incredibly serious wrong with him physically.\par \par \uc2 But there is a lot wrong with his military campaign. And that's why he's tried illegally to annex Ukraine territory. It's why he's making these nuclear threats. It's why he's mobilized Russian forces for the first time since the Second World War.\par \par \uc2 But we know that, as you said, he's flailing and none of that is working.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, but it does raise questions, if he were a dying man or dealing with something, then all bets are off, right?\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: Yes, I mean, I think -- so, figuratively speaking, he is dying, in a sense, because his grip on the Kremlin, his regime's security is more tenuous today than it's ever been.\par \par \uc2 And that's what makes him so dangerous. And how we get out of this predicament is -- it's going to be a major challenge for us.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HOFFMAN: Because Putin doesn't want to give up control over his country. And he knows that he's to blame for this failed military operation.\par \par \uc2 And he's rained down hell on Ukrainian civilians. Ukraine's not going to stop fighting. They're going to carry this thing through.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Dan, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 Dan Hoffman, a former CIA station chief in Moscow on these latest developments.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the president might say he's planning retribution or some sort of response to the Saudis leading this \uc2 OPEC\uc2  and \uc2 OPEC\uc2  Plus country effort to cut energy supply or oil supplies and production by two million barrels a day, but that didn't stop a number of U.S. financial honchos from gathering in Saudi Arabia and participating at a conference they said was crucial to the world, finance and otherwise.\par \par \uc2 And it was at this conference that Saudi Arabia's energy minister seemed to lay out the problems it has with this administration. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ABDULAZIZ BIN SALMAN\uc2 , SAUDI ARABIAN ENERGY MINISTER: They are depleting their emergency stocks, had depleted it, used it as a mechanism to manipulate markets.\par \par \uc2 It is my profound duty to make it clear to the world that losing emergency stock may become painful in the months to come.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, so was that a threat, a recognition that maybe the Saudis did cut that supply in production in response to the administration tapping the Strategic Petroleum Reserve to the tune that he has, better than 200 million barrels of oil, and now another 15 million more being planned?\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Senator \uc2 John Barrasso\uc2  on this, the Wyoming Republican, the Senate GOP Conference chairman.\par \par \uc2 Senator, what did you think of what the energy minister was saying there? If that wasn't the reason for cutting production, he did seem to lay out the fallout that it would have, that it weakens the United States doing so.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN BARRASSO\uc2  (R-WY): Well, that's exactly what the energy minister of Saudi Arabia said, that the pain was going to get worse here in the United States, Neil.\par \par \uc2 For almost two years, American families have been feeling the pain, the biting pain of high prices at the gas station, at the grocery store. And when this administrator says it's going to get worse, this is a self- inflicted wound upon ourselves, Neil.\par \par \uc2 They understand that affordable energy is the lifeblood of a family and of a nation. And what we have done to ourselves is now held ourselves hostage by Saudi Arabia, because we have such radical energy policies, when the answer is right here in America. It's right under our noses.\par \par \uc2 It's right here in Wyoming, where I am. We have affordable, available, reliable energy. Yet the Democrats, every Democrat in the House, the White House and the Senate will not let us use it. We need to change the American energy policy to an American first energy policy, not be dependent on Saudi Arabia or anyone else for that energy.\par \par \uc2 We need to get back to energy independence. We have it here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I know where you're coming on that, Senator. And, obviously, the president and his message on stopping domestic production to the degree he has or making it more difficult did lead to this.\par \par \uc2 But the Saudis are interesting in and of themselves. They are clearly screwing us with this decision. They know that. Whatever your views of the president and all, they're going after America in a way that is not going to help the West, not going to help our country, very akin to the embargoes they led in the '70s. The last one almost drove them out of business.\par \par \uc2 With friends like that, who needs enemies, right?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Well, the Saudi energy minister is the brother of the crown prince.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Understood.\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: So he speaks with authority.\par \par \uc2 And I talked about 10 days ago...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, he seems very happy. He seems very happy and content with sticking it to us, right?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And I talked about 10 days ago to the ambassador from Saudi Arabia to the United States specifically about this decision to cut a million barrels a day production. And the ambassador confirmed to me that the position of the United States, both our national security adviser and our secretary of Treasury, \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , was to continue to beg and plead that they don't make the announcement that they were going to do that cut until after November.\par \par \uc2 A strong president would never allow this to happen. A strong president would say, we're not going to be held...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: We're going to produce it here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I know where you're coming from, Senator. The Saudis still did it. The Saudis still did it.\par \par \uc2 And I understand what you're saying about the president potentially botching it and begging for them not to do so. They did it anyway. But in zinging the president, they zinged all of us. Who needs friends like that, right?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: Well, we have had a relationship, a strategic relationship, with Saudi Arabia 15 presidents, seven kings, going back over 80 years. It's a security relationship related to Iran.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And they just cut oil two million barrels a day. They just cut oil to us two million barrels a day.\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: We don't need their oil. And we need to make it clear that we're energy-independent in this country.\par \par \uc2 We have the energy here, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: And the president's actions have been to draw down on the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, which is what that minister was talking about.\par \par \uc2 He's drawn it down to the level, the lowest level in 40 years. We're going to need to replace that at some point. But the president is so focused on the upcoming election and his terrible poll numbers that he does -- he seems to be willing to put our national security aside.\par \par \uc2 But the Strategic Petroleum Reserve is there for a national energy crisis, not for a...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, no, what is clear to me, Senator, is, he's putting his own interests ahead of our country's, right? He's pushing his -- wherever you say about the president, he is sticking it to us, and enjoying it and relishing it.\par \par \uc2 Whatever you think about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the Saudi kingdom is saying to the United States that has helped them time and again dealing with Houthi rebels from Iran and all that, he is saying to us, we don't care about that. We're going to stick you against the wall, whether you like it or not.\par \par \uc2 That's some how do you do, huh?\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: The strongest nation in the world should never find themselves in a position where you can get stuck against the world.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: This is a strategic mistake by the president and the Democrats, who put their environmental agenda above the security and the well-being of the American people.\par \par \uc2 And that's why -- that's why the Republicans are surging in the polls. And, Neil, I'm very optimistic about our opportunity to take back the House...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We shall see.\par \par \uc2 BARRASSO: ... take back the Senate, and then I'd be the chairman of the Energy Committee. And we would focus on affordable energy and American energy independence.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And not depending on guys like that, to your point.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you so much. Very good seeing you.\par \par \uc2 Senator Barrasso on all of that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Also, there's a new guy in charge in Britain now. He's a young guy, and he is in Britain going to change the way things have been going there. Can he succeed?\par \par \uc2 Why, if you're a Republican in this country, you might want to pay attention. I will explain after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, in his first full day in office, British Prime Minister \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  apparently did speak to the president of the United States a short while ago.\par \par \uc2 We're told that a number of issues came up, among them, the president asking if Sunak was going to ensure the people of Northern Ireland that they will continue to have security and prosperity through preserving the so-called Belfast Agreement.\par \par \uc2 I don't know, over the course of the conversation, where things went. But, of course, as you know, we have, in Mr. Sunak, a very young prime minister at that, all of 44 years old -- 42, I should say, the youngest in a couple of hundred years, but also the third prime minister in little more than, what, nine weeks, and the since 2016.\par \par \uc2 Gerry Baker, following all of this, "The Wall Street Journal at Large" on FOX Business, a hit show, gets some very great guests. He's been writing about a phenomena that we might just not want to ignore what's going on over there, particularly how the markets responded to his predecessor, \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 , who was he heaved out of there when there was a market, indeed, a Conservative revolt over her tax hikes that went nowhere faster.\par \par \uc2 Her tax cuts, I said, went nowhere fast because they weren't paid for. Anyway, Gerry gets into it better than I will.\par \par \uc2 But you say there's a message for Republicans if they take over at least two levers of government, or at the very least one lever of government here. What would it be, Gerry?\par \par \uc2 GERRY BAKER, HOST, "WALL STREET JOURNAL AT LARGE": Thanks, Neil. And thanks again for having me.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, I should say that I hope, when the president spoke to Prime Minister Sunak, that he didn't call him "Mr. Rashid Sanuk," which I think is what he initially called him, what the president initially called him yesterday.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I thought that, yes.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: But we can forgive that. I suppose it is -- he is a first.\par \par \uc2 Look, I mean, the point I -- the point I'm -- the overall I make is this. Britain obviously has been in a complete shambles, chaotic for the last couple of years, really, with prime minister after prime minister, economic weakness, political disarray.\par \par \uc2 I think the underlying point -- and this is where I think it has application potentially here in the United States or in fact here in the United States -- is that the underlying problem for the British Conservative Party is that, with Brexit -- Brexit, which happened in 2016, same year \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was elected, this was a revolt by so many working- class ordinary British people against the establishment, against the elite.\par \par \uc2 They'd had enough of being told that they should be part of a global system with open borders, free immigration, free trade, very much the modern creed of all of the -- progressive, modern creed of all those people who want kind of a globally integrated system. They voted against that. They voted against Brexit.\par \par \uc2 And then they voted for the Conservative Party, which delivered them Brexit, in the same way that Donald -- many of those same types of voters here in the United States who were concerned about immigration, who were concerned about open borders, who were concerned about global free trade voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The challenge in both countries -- and this is where the Conservatives have got themselves into such a mess -- is that those voters who voted for Brexit are not necessarily traditional. They're not at all Conservative voters, traditional Conservative voters, the sort of Conservative voters who want low taxes, deregulation, a smaller states.\par \par \uc2 They actually favor in Britain health spending and welfare spending, because they're working-class voters, former Labor voters. Their vision of a country, independent country of their vision of populist nationalism, is a country where there's a strong state.\par \par \uc2 So \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  tried to deliver that. They -- and he failed. Then \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  came in and kind of went back on all that, went to a more traditional Conservative approach, low taxes, low -- deregulation. That failed too.\par \par \uc2 So I think that is the challenge for these right-of-center parties, conservative parties like America's Republicans and Britain's Conservatives, which is, how do you -- how do you manage the expectations of these voters who come to you because they want -- they have a nationalist approach, they have a -- they have a strong belief in the nation-state, they want -- they have -- they're culturally conservative, but they also still want quite a strong economic program?\par \par \uc2 They don't necessarily want massive corporate tax cuts and massive deregulation. So that's the problem I think the Tories have got themselves into. Same here.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm sorry, Gerry. They're not like the Maggie Thatchers or the Maggie Thatcher Tories of that day.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes. No, no.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I guess what -- when the markets revolted and they revolted, it's that her plans weren't paid for.\par \par \uc2 We have less than a minute here. Do you think Republicans should be cognizant of that?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sometimes, we treat tax cuts differently, for example, than we do spending, but whatever they come up with better be paid for, or the markets themselves revolt?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 And that's the -- that was the problem that \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  got into, that you -- look, I strongly believe in lower taxes, in a smaller state. That's the way to generate more growth, and the country needs more growth. And that's the right way to go.\par \par \uc2 But you do have to do it in the framework of a sensible, prudent fiscal policy. You can't just do what \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  tried to do, big tax cuts and, by the way, big spending increases, without any idea of how to balance the budget. It is -- the market is going to be much more sensitive about seeing that budgets balance, that fiscal prudence dominates.\par \par \uc2 And that's going to be a challenge for Republicans, as well as it was for Tories.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, they could all suffer the same fate, Gerry Baker, "Wall Street Journal at Large."\par \par \uc2 Gerry Baker on FOX Business, great show, great writer, great thinker.\par \par \uc2 And a great day at the corner Wall and Broad, the Dow up, the S&P 500 up, Nasdaq up. Now, we had some numbers after the bell that might test that tomorrow, some disappointment out of \uc2 Google\uc2 , a little less with \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 . We shall see. It's a busy week.\par \par \uc2 Here's "The Five."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020221025eiap0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Hannity Town Hall in Arizona\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Sara Carter, Leo Terrell \par \uc2 8,500 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: Months ago, our producers embedded with Blake Masters running for Senate in Arizona. The documentary is amazing and it is out on FOX Nation in the morning. Hope you'll watch it.\par \par \uc2 We'll see you tomorrow. Here's Sean.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: All right. Welcome to HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 The midterms, they are only days away and tonight we are in beautiful Scottsdale, Arizona, where in just a moment, we will be joined by Republican Senate candidate Blake Masters. Any Blake fans here? Okay.\par \par \uc2 Gubernatorial candidate Kari Lake will join us who is now -- who is now opened up a three-point lead against Democrat, gutless, cowardly, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , in the latest Trafalgar poll. Hobbs who is currently collecting a paycheck as Arizona secretary of state has only shown up to work a grand total of 19 days in the last six months. I wish I had that job. She didn't even enter office for the entire month of April and August.\par \par \uc2 And meanwhile, Hobbs is not exactly out there working hard on the campaign trail either. She has participated -- and this is so unfair to you the hard-working citizens -- taxpayers of this state of Arizona, zero debates. She refuses to debate in both the primary and the general election. \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  would rather hide in her basement bunker just like her mentor, Joey Biden, is probably asleep during the show.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Kari Lake meanwhile has offered to debate any place, any time, anywhere. Hobbs can't seem to muster up the courage. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INTERVIEWER: Why have you refused your opponent? She wants to go head to head, toe to toe with you with the moderator?\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS (D), ARIZONA SECRETARY OF STATE: Look, it is honestly ridiculous that somebody who has based their whole campaign on these lies that are intended to dismantle our democracy that somehow the problem is that I won't give her a bigger stage to do that.\par \par \uc2 INTERVIEWER: Why not challenge her though in a public forum? I'm just curious.\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: As far as I'm concerned, the debate about debates is over. I'm running my campaign the way I'm running my campaign, and I'm going to continue to make the case directly to voters.\par \par \uc2 INTERVIEWER: At worst, it could come off as you're scared to step on that stage, and at best, you're not willing to confront. We're in a new era where sometimes some politicians are seen as bullies, and we've been taught since we were little kids, you got to stand up to a bully.\par \par \uc2 You won't -- you understand how that might come across? How do you make a case? We want to send you the fight for us, but you won't even step on stage.\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: I have stood up to this bully for the past two years and that's -- and Arizonans have seen that and I'm going to continue to do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Now, the real reason is she's gutless and she knows that she would be unable to defend her radical positions.\par \par \uc2 Let's go through them. For example, she supports no limitations on abortion whatsoever. That would mean you can have an abortion up until the very moment of birth. That would be called infanticide.\par \par \uc2 You might recall during the pandemic, she opposed the deportation of illegal immigrants and her only plan to address the border crisis is to provide amnesty for illegal immigrants. She does not support a border wall. She does not support federal funding for more border agents.\par \par \uc2 But they do like \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents, the Democrats. They like them a lot. In fact, she really doesn't seem to care about the border crisis at all, actually telling a local paper, quote, it's ridiculous that we're talking about this as a core issue in the governor's race. No, that would be a primary issue.\par \par \uc2 Now, meanwhile, during her time in the Arizona state legislature, Hobbs regularly opposed tax cuts. But she did support a hundred million dollar sales tax increase and also voted to hire more than 100 new state tax collectors. She's also faced a variety of scandals, listen to this, including allegations of racial discrimination from a state Senate staffer. It resulted in Arizona taxpayers shelling out three hundred thousand dollars on behalf of \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And that's not all, multiple former campaign staffers accused Hobbs of fostering what is an emotionally abusive work environment. And last spring, nearly 80 percent of her initial staffers, they quit. They didn't want to work for her.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, if you now support or have ever voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  hates you in fact on twitter she called Trump supporters, quote, neo Nazis.\par \par \uc2 Now, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  is a classic far left keyboard warrior behind a computer screen. She is an outspoken bully, but in real life she is a cowardly, incompetent, self-absorbed narcissist moron. That has never been more apparent and during this town hall.\par \par \uc2 Simple question, take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MODERATOR: What have you learned specifically learn from the Latino community?\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Oh, that's a great question. I don't necessarily think about it that way in those terms.\par \par \uc2 My sister-in-law she is a Latino and her family -- I love hanging out with them and processing my Espanol. Arizona wouldn't be Arizona without what the Latino community brings.\par \par \uc2 MODERATOR: So there's not one specific lesson you could share with me. All of that is primarily (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Uh.\par \par \uc2 MODERATOR: It's one third of a state.\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Yeah, yes, absolutely. I mean, I think there's many lessons.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Now, the people of Arizona, they deserve better. And thankfully, there is someone who is willing and able uh to work hard every day and stand up for the people of this great state and it is definitely not \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, let's give a warm Arizona welcome to your gubernatorial candidate, Republican Kari Lake.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Nice to see you. Thanks for coming.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R), ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Hello. How are you?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Very enthusiastically --\par \par \uc2 LAKE: You drew a crowd.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: No, I think they came for you. You're running for election. I did offer to buy them -- bribe them with \uc2 In-N-Out Burger\uc2  though.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Oh, that that'll bring a crowd.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That was my favorite place.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Welcome to Arizona.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Thank you for -- soon to be governor, right? I think --\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I'd never take anything for granted though. I mean, I've seen you up as high as five six points in the polls. But I tell that every candidate you have to run like you're behind.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Yep.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, it's two minutes left in the game. No timeouts. You're on your own 20. You got to march down the field 80 yards. You got to cross the plane, kick the field goal to win. Is that how you're running?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: You're speaking my language. My dad was a football coach, and we are at -- we are with seconds left on the clock right now. We have 15 days left in this election, and we are acting like we're 20 points back. But we've been doing that since day one, because we knew that we have to save this state.\par \par \uc2 And you know, we know from the crowds that we're getting that we are way ahead. We have -- we drew 4,000 people at a rally Saturday night. It was a great event, some of you might have been there.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Right, that's amazing.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: And Katie held her biggest event and I think there were 20 people there.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Right.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: So I mean, I'm not sure I believe all the polls. I know from what I'm feeling that there's something going on in Arizona and it's good.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I hope so. Now I want to go to this quote of hers, it's ridiculous to focus on the border.\par \par \uc2 This is a -- well, every state now is a border state. It's ridiculous to focus on the border. She actually said that and she supports amnesty.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Her plan is to let \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  handle it. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the reason we've gotten into this mess.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Joe does not know today is Monday.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: That's right. We have to wake him up in the middle of an interview. Did you see he fell asleep in the middle of an interview?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah. What does Dr Biden think? Uh, so, Mr. President --\par \par \uc2 LAKE: And then you look at her voting record when she was a state senator and she voted against the border wall. She voted against the virtual border wall, against the border strike force which would help protect Arizonans and she voted against -- this is the shocker -- she even voted against asking the federal government to reimburse us for the costs that we're incurring.\par \par \uc2 I don't know how she can look herself in the mirror. We have a fentanyl crisis, and I know that she's not talking to that many people on the campaign trail, but I'm talking to people every day, hundreds, sometimes thousands of people.\par \par \uc2 And when you see a mother come up to you and talk about losing her son or father, her 17-year-old son took one pill, a 19-year-old son took a half a pill and they're gone. We have to --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: It is the number one killer of our children now in the country.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: That's right.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And it's coming across that border that she doesn't understand why we're focused on it.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: And she's an open borders globalist. Let's face it. She's --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Right.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: She wants to take what Newsom has done for California which has destroyed that state, and do the same thing here in Arizona. And that's why I think we have a movement because people know if we elect someone like \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , we're going to turn into California 2.0. And nobody wants that. I'm pretty safe --\par \par \uc2 (BOOS)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: By the way, you have a lot of Californians moving in. I think there ought to be a rule. Maybe if you become governor, you can get the legislature to pass a rule, you're allowed to come but you can't ruin the state the way you ruin the last state you came from. That would be --\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I don't know how you figure that out though.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: No, I will say this though, the Californians are the ones who grabbed me at these events and go don't let Arizona go. We uprooted our children. We uprooted our business. We moved our home. We are here we don't want Arizona to turn into California.\par \par \uc2 So some of our California friends that moved here, they gave up a lot -- somebody's waving in the crowd. They don't want this to turn in -- they flee -- they fled California.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know what the most -- so here's an amazing thing, if you take a u-haul from anywhere in California to Texas or Arizona where we are. You know, in the case of Texas, it will cost you about from California to Texas.\par \par \uc2 If you take that same U-haul from Texas back to California, you'll pay three or four hundred dollars. You're doing U-haul a favor. If you negotiate, you could probably get it for free.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Probably. \uc2 U-Haul\uc2 , which is an Arizona company, they will pay you to get a U-Haul from Arizona to California because people are moving so fast.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Let's talk about, why will she not debate you?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Well, she's now missed her chance because we had the debate last night and it aired last night. It was a great conversation. I didn't yell. There was no antics.\par \par \uc2 You know, she's afraid. There's several reasons. One, she has terrible policies.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: This is a forum, though. There's a little difference.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Yeah, last night, we just had an interview because she wouldn't show up.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Right.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: But she's afraid to defend her policies because they're dead end, destructive policies. How do you defend wanting an open border when you've got so many fentanyl pills pouring across that they could kill every man, woman and child in this country? How do you defend that?\par \par \uc2 How do you defend your voting record when you voted nearly 90 percent of the time against small Arizona businesses, when you had a chance to help them? How do you defend tax increases that you are pushing for when you were in the legislature? And how do you defend -- I think this might have been the worst bill that sh