? Meanwhile, let's put Americans in jail if they disagree.\par \par \uc2 For some perspective on where we're going, we are honored tonight to introduce Lieutenant Colonel Daniel Davis, who is a senior fellow at Defense Priorities. He joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Daniel Davis, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 So the way that people are talking about the prospect of the use of nuclear weapons in Ukraine, I never thought I would live to see people who sort of throw it out parenthetically.\par \par \uc2 This is not the military you served, is it? People didn't just kind of say, "Oh, a nuclear war. No big deal."\par \par \uc2 LIEUTENANT COLONEL DANIEL DAVIS (RET), SENIOR FELLOW, DEFENSE PRIORITIES: No, in fact, when I was serving, it was at the highest of everyone's understanding that that's what we needed to avoid at all cost.\par \par \uc2 And you know, that's what the whole idea of Mutually Assured Destruction was supposed to ensure that didn't, so that governments whether it was, you know, the hating Soviets or, you know, the United States who are bitterly against each other, we all understood where you can't go and so that kept everybody safe.\par \par \uc2 But now that seems to be have lost -- we've lost that somehow. And the current administration as I wrote about in 1945, a couple of days ago, they're pursuing a policy that logically can't be accomplished without putting us at grave risk of a nuclear war.\par \par \uc2 Because they say on the one hand that we are going to help Ukraine no matter what it takes, as long as it takes, but then at the other hand, we understand that if Russia actually, if that policy succeeded and Russia was actually in danger of being pushed out and defeated on the battlefield, that the chances that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  out of desperation and fear resorts to nuclear weapons is extremely high.\par \par \uc2 Therefore, if Biden succeeded in that, our risk is higher. We simply can't go down that path. There is nothing in Ukraine that is worth losing one American city.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: What is so striking is that we're not having a national conversation about this because people like \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , who by the way has never even been in a fistfight in her entire sad life denounces anyone who ask questions about it as "pro Putin."\par \par \uc2 Have you ever seen, like this sort of zombie-like state the country is in where we're moving toward this inexorably, and no one is talking about it?\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Yes, and know what? I hear that all the time. Anybody who is talking about some kind of sane policy that keeps America safe, for some reason is talked about as being some sort of Putin apologist.\par \par \uc2 But look, here's the thing. Our government is supposed to exist to make sure that our country is safe and secure and prosperous. And if we're pursuing policies that put that at risk, we need new policies. And I don't understand why people want to be against that.\par \par \uc2 I do understand why they do -- they hate the Ukraine, people being caught this war, and I do, too.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, I agree.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: I care more about the Ukraine people than most, but we have to end the war and prevent it from being escalated. That's our primary objective.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Of course. I mean, our primary objective of the \uc2 US government\uc2  should be to help the United States, you would think, and I appreciate your reminding us of that.\par \par \uc2 Lieutenant Colonel Daniel Davis of Defense Priorities. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So we just said it is Monday we have a surfeit, an over-abundance of great tape for you this evening. For example, MSNBC has hosted a focus group with voters and tried to browbeat them over the insurrection of January 6th. It's so amusing.\par \par \uc2 Brian Kilmeade, our friend joins us after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, the problem with inviting civilians onto your TV show is they don't read the script necessarily, as MSNBC "reporter," Elise Jordan found out when she had a panel discussion with Trump voters from Pittsburgh.\par \par \uc2 Hilarity ensued. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELISE JORDAN, MSNBC REPORTER: \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  was at the insurrection and he was photographed breaching one of the restricted areas. Is that okay?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Which area? Because I saw a video where Capitol officers were taking away barriers.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And unlocking doors for people.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Opening doors.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I saw that, too.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I mean --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They opened the gates to let them in.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: So, it shouldn't be disqualifying for an elected official --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: If they participated in January 6?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No. Definitely not.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I didn't see him -- he didn't strike anybody. He didn't hurt anybody.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And the only one that died was the protester there, not a \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  officer.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: An unarmed female veteran who was shot by police.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That's the only one that died.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That's the only one who died.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: A police officer did die.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No. It was a stroke.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That's a heart attack. That's not --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That's not on site.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That's caused by that. He probably shouldn't have been a police officer. One woman was shot --\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: So what do you make though, overall of January 6th? I mean, it was -- watching that footage, it was pretty disturbing. I mean, there were people throwing excrement at the walls and it was our -- you know, it is the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: They looked a lot like Antifa's actions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That wasn't true.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Well, it is in the light of -- except on a much smaller scale, it looked the same as the Black Lives Matter riots.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That's what I saw, the similarities, to me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: What's funny is these voters, they're stupid, but actually the reporter is legit stupid, and she says "the insurrection." You can see them thinking, "What? Insurrection? Well, that's not an insurrection." They don't even know what she is talking about, because it's so stupid.\par \par \uc2 Brian Kilmeade is not stupid. He is among many other things, the cohost of "FOX and Friends," host of "One Nation" with Brian Kilmeade, best-selling author, author of the new book, "The President and the Freedom Fighter" now out in paperback, and he joins us tonight in the "Friend Zone" to assess.\par \par \uc2 First of all, before we get to that MSNBC atrocity, congrats on your book coming out in paperback. Your books have done really well, all of them.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST, "ONE NATION": Right, and thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And to say that "Brian Kilmeade, who is not stupid," is the most unique introduction I've ever had, Tucker, and I've had a lot of unique introductions.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It was heartfelt by the way.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: . on "FOX and Friends," and that had not had been in the prompter, otherwise, I was going to kill your writer, which is you, by the way, so I'm going to come back at you.\par \par \uc2 No, but what I'm trying to do and I think it is just perfect with your soundbites. I am surprised they aired that by the way.\par \par \uc2 I mean, when you have that, they are like, you know what, I'm going to come back. There's a problem with the tape. I had problems sending it back to the office. I don't think we should really air this.\par \par \uc2 But you know why? And when I tried to do it in the paperback, you always try to add stuff, and when I add stuff, it is the news. They are trying to take down the statue of Lincoln that Frederick Douglass dedicated because \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And they were trying to take that Frederick Douglass statue, they ripped off the pedestal, because I guess America is a bad place and what was so interesting, if you think about it on January 6th, as I read that text and then watched it is opposite day.\par \par \uc2 For years, we watched Trump supporters get beat up. We watched Antifa destroy the place in masks. We watched \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  wreck cities and attack cops.\par \par \uc2 We've never seen that really from Trump supporters. And to see January 6th, I'm never going to soft pedal would happen, you don't either. I never would in a million years, but that was a bad day, a horrific day.\par \par \uc2 Where is the condemnation of all those other days that led up to that that had everyone scratching their head going, "Am I watching this? Is this a Trump scene of them attacking the Capitol and law enforcement?" And there seems to be a lot of questions there that still need to be answered, especially with the focus group that we just witnessed.\par \par \uc2 But the first successful Democrat would say "I'm outraged about January 6. You know what I'm also outraged? About what happened in New York City streets in the middle of a pandemic. You know what I'm also outraged? I'm outraged that all these statues came down and the graffiti to Andrew Jackson in front of the White House and the nooses around its neck. I'm outraged that they're taking \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2 's name off a school. I'm outraged that they're tearing up our Founding Fathers because they didn't actually march in the same way we do in 2020, instead of judging people by the generations they lived in."\par \par \uc2 I am outraged that they're ripping apart the foundation of the country, and they're not happy as heck to be here while trying to make it better. I did not witness these riots trying to make the country better and that is where we're at. That's why I always will go back to history and say, "What did they do during troubling times?" They always seem to cherish the country. And even though they got violent in times in our past, and certainly a Civil War is an example of that, they always said, "Glad to be here. Let's make it better."\par \par \uc2 This is my vision, but we are the most successful multicultural country in the history of the planet, warts and all, and it is time to stop running from our past and I don't ever, ever say January 6 is okay.\par \par \uc2 For those people are trying to say, you never acknowledged the problems before. How dare you demand me condemn everything now?\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, and stop lying to us and berating us, you know, as if you occupy some position of moral authority, you don't.\par \par \uc2 Brian Kilmeade, so nicely put. Congratulations, by the way on your book coming out in paperback.\par \par \uc2 KILMEADE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well worth it. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 More news just ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Months ago, our producers embedded with Blake Masters running for Senate in Arizona. The documentary is amazing and it is out on FOX Nation in the morning. Hope you'll watch it.\par \par \uc2 We will see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Here is Sean.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United S