(voice-over): Oh, stop that. Oh, that's not nice. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (voice-over): Harold, didn't you want to brag?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO (on camera): Go ahead, Harold.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD J.R., FOX NEWS CHANNEL POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): 3-0.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: 3-0?\par \par \uc2 FORD: We won. We won.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, you coach soccer.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And you were the coach?\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Did your son make a goal?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And your son was in it?\par \par \uc2 FORD: I'm retired. My daughter, my daughter.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Take it that SPECIAL REPORT, just go.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Did your son score a goal -- daughter?\par \par \uc2 FORD: My daughter -- my daughter did not score this with --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But why did you have to admit that?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You know, that's it for us.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You could have changed the subject.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): I mean, I'll wait to hear the rest of the game story.\par \par \uc2 Good job, coach.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Soccer.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Thanks, Greg.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier. "BREAKING TONIGHT", President Biden trying to pump up his fellow Democrats with a little more than two weeks to go until the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 The president speaking to a party rally today, at the DNC here in Washington to staffers there. The speech coming, as most polls now suggests Democrats could lose control of the House and possibly the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . The dynamic has shifted in the past three weeks.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich starts us off tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you, Bret. We are witnessing a late shift in Democratic messaging as it were from all in on abortion to counter punching the GOP on the economy.\par \par \uc2 But there is some grey area with the president's claims.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): President Biden wants to undo the thinking that his policies will hurt Democrats in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Everybody wants to make it a referendum. But it's a choice between two vastly different visions for America.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Some on the left admit signs of trouble ahead.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 BERNIE SANDERS\uc2  (D-VT): I am worried about the level of voter turnout among young people and working people who will be voting democratic.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Others like the House Speaker at risk of losing her gavel, trying to manifest results by staying optimistic.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): The fact is, is that we fully intend to win. Was I not clear? We are going to win this election.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Vote, vote, vote. Vote.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Biden tried to steer the ship at a pep rally-style DNC staff meeting, by challenging the assumption, democratic losses are inevitable.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Which makes most pollsters they're not sure anymore, not about the outcome, but about polling.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Biden directing his party to point voters to the facts.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well guess what the big spending Democrat Biden did? A deficit fell by $1.4 trillion this year.\par \par \uc2 We created, with your help, 10 million new jobs so far.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But those facts aren't all they seem. Biden administrations own bureau of labor statistics says most of those jobs were added back after pandemic losses.\par \par \uc2 Only 514,000 were newly created, and the deficit dropped when pandemic spending programs expired.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, Biden used that deficit savings to pay for student debt cancelation.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): How are people supposed to take this kind of messaging on their most important issue seriously, when some of this feels like smoke and mirrors?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Let me tell you what the American people should take very, very seriously. Let me remind you of the Trump tax cut of 2017, $2 trillion that was not paid for. Not paid for.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: So, the president's claim about reducing the deficit might actually be more true if the courts successfully block his student loan cancelation program.\par \par \uc2 Friday, a federal judge issued a freeze amid some court challenges. The White House is fighting that in courts, still pushing ahead with their preparations.\par \par \uc2 They are also clearing up the president's false claim that he got that student loan cancelation program passed by Congress by a vote or two. A White House official tells me that he was referring to the Inflation Reduction Act, which freed up deficit savings in order to pay for that program.\par \par \uc2 The student loan cancellation of course never actually got a vote in Congress. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Had to say something.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich, live on the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks,\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Stocks shook off in early bout of unsettled trading and it ended higher ahead of a heavy week of earnings from big tech companies. The Dow gained 417. The S&P 500 was ahead 45. The NASDAQ was up 93.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): We are getting a look at the border crossing numbers tonight from September, and they are shocking. Migrant encounters and terror suspect arrests at the border were at record levels.\par \par \uc2 Our crews there down on the border continue to witness large groups of illegal immigrants coming into the country every day. Correspondent Bill Melugin is once again in Eagle Pass, Texas tonight.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): In what some critics describe as a blatant news dump, DHS released highly anticipated September border numbers late Friday night past 11:00 p.m. Eastern, and they are historic.\par \par \uc2 The numbers reveal there were more than 227,000 migrant encounters in September, the highest September in DHS history and the equivalent of two full capacity \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  football stadiums.\par \par \uc2 Fiscal year 2022 ended with nearly 2.4 million migrant encounters. That's equivalent to the population of Houston and it's the highest fiscal year ever recorded. Not including 600,000 known gotaways in the year.\par \par \uc2 There were also 98 Border Patrol arrests of suspects on the FBIs terror watch list in fiscal year 2022. That's nearly quadrupled the previous five fiscal years combined.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RODNEY SCOTT, FORMER CHIEF, UNITED STATES BORDER PATROL: Those are just the ones that we actually encountered. How many more were smart enough to wait until Border Patrol agents were overwhelmed and then come across.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Early this morning, Fox News witnessed another large group of migrants crossing illegally on the north side of Eagle Pass. CBP Sources tell Fox, in this Del Rio sector, in just the first three weeks of October alone, there have already been more than 29,000 illegal crossings here.\par \par \uc2 In the Rio Grande Valley, video provided by a law enforcement source shows a group of drug smugglers in broad daylight, loading up a waiting vehicle with bundles of drugs.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It looks like -- another (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Texas DPS later pursued and arrested the driver. An 18-year-old U.S. citizen. In his trunk, 10 bundles of marijuana, totaling 240 pounds.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret CBP Sources tell Fox News, a staggering 856 migrants died here at our southern border in fiscal year 2022. That is the highest number ever recorded. Yet, another record shattered.\par \par \uc2 We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin along the border. Bill, thanks.\par \par \uc2 South Carolina Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  has been given a temporary reprieve from testifying before that grand jury in Georgia about alleged attempts to illegally influence the 2020 election in that state.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  has blocked the order for now. The Fulton County District Attorney has until Thursday to tell the court why Graham should be compelled to testify.\par \par \uc2 Graham contends his status as a senator gives him immunity. There is disturbing news tonight about how American children are performing or not performing in school.\par \par \uc2 Fourth, and eighth graders are hitting decade's low marks in math and reading following school closures during the coronavirus pandemic. Correspondent Mark Meredith takes a look.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Two years after COVID-19 closed classrooms coast to coast, a government study finds the pandemic had a devastating impact on America's students.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL CARDONA\uc2 , UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF EDUCATION: If those data don't wake us up that we need to do better for our kids. We don't belong in this profession.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: The National Center for Education Statistics compared test results from this spring to 2019. It found, math comprehension among fourth graders dropped by five points in three years. There was an even steeper decline for eighth graders who posted an eight point drop in math scores since the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 DR. CAREY WRIGHT, CHIEF SCHOOLS OFFICER, NATIONAL ASSESMENT GOVERNING BOARD: This should be alarming to anybody that's looking at it. I don't think the nation has ever had results that looked this bad before.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Overall reading comprehension declined too, with both fourth and eighth grade students dropping three points compared to 2019. Education experts say the pandemic is certainly to blame for performance declines. But official stress remote learning is not the only reason scores dropped.\par \par \uc2 PEGGY CARR, ASSOCIATE COMMISSIONER FOR ASSESSMENT, NATIONAL: There is nothing in this data that allows us to draw a straight line from remote learning in and of itself to student performance is complicated.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: But Republicans insist it's not.\par \par \uc2 Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin, who campaigned heavily on promises to remove mask mandates and reopen schools full time since today's report is proof remote learning failed students.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-VA): Today's NAEP results are the proverbial final nail in the coffin. It's not that you're asleep, but you're clearly trying to cover up your own bad decisions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: The head of the \uc2 American Federation of Teachers\uc2  says today's report proves that there was learning loss nationwide, regardless whether schools were open or not.\par \par \uc2 For his part, education secretary \uc2 Miguel Cardona\uc2  says his department wants to see states use money from the American Rescue Plan to make up for lost time. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News Senior Political Analyst Brit Hume. Brit, good evening.\par \par \uc2 This Education report, these numbers are really tough. Parents have seen that slow down, but it's a big issue across the country.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST (on camera): It is, Bret. And it is one more issue that adds to voter discontent. And it's just there weren't enough of those already as the Democratic Party tries to climb out from under them -- in time for the midterms.\par \par \uc2 And what you can see about this issue as well as others is that there just doesn't seem to be enough time and the weeks remaining before the voting begins, at least for Election Day. Voting's already begun a lot of places.\par \par \uc2 For things to change in a way that would -- that would alter the landscape for this election, if anything. It only deepens the Democratic Party's problems and it emphasizes once more that closing down the schools, which not everybody was in favor of, although it happened it was pretty wide spread was a terrible mistake adding to the closing of businesses to make the shutdowns broadly speaking probably is better public policy decision as most of us have ever seen.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, Brit, you mentioned the issues that ABC Ipsos poll just are better suited to handle and it lists a bunch of different issues.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Economy, Republicans 36-24 inflation. Same, a little bit bigger, actually. Gas prices, 36-22. Crime, 35-22. Immigration 35-32, a little closer there. But overall, the issues that people are carrying about this election, Republicans continue in every poll we've seen to be up double digits.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Yes, and I think the polling changes we've seen, that poll reflects this, and others, as well suggest that momentum, which had seemed at least for a period of time during the summer to have shifted toward the Democrats after the Dobbs' decision on abortion.\par \par \uc2 That, that momentum such as it was has petered out, and that the landscape and the issues that seemed in favor to the Republicans for most of this year, are back in the driver's seat now being added to by findings about, you know, border crossings, and school performance and so on.\par \par \uc2 So, it's a pretty tough environment to be a Democrat. And two weeks to go, it's a little hard to see how that changes very much.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, the president is going to go back to Pennsylvania, we're told, with former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  campaigning there. But he's not going a lot of other places, at least so far.\par \par \uc2 Here he is, over the weekend, talking about the student loan forgiveness, and how he phrased it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I have just signed a law that's being challenged by my Republican colleagues.\par \par \uc2 Did you just get 10,000 written off? It's passed. I got it pass by a vote or two, and it's in effect.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously, that was an executive order. It was not a law passed, or that he signed into law from Congress. The White House saying that he was talking about the Recovery Act, and that freed up money for other things.\par \par \uc2 Bottom line, there are people questioning his ability to be a messenger for Democrats on the big issues.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, of course they are, Bret. I mean, the guy is obviously not fully on top of things. He is an old man, he is forgetful, as old men will be. I'm an old man, and I forget things. I know how about a problem that can sometimes be.\par \par \uc2 But in his case, of course, he is -- he is the chief executive of the \uc2 United States government\uc2 . And he needs to be on top of things. And he plainly is not. And the public can see that.\par \par \uc2 Now, how that figures into their voting in a congressional election? Obviously, it's complicated. But presidential approval, and certainly his cognitive abilities feed into that. It's always been a factor in these midterms, and I don't think it will be any different this time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Brit. As always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, Great Britain is about to have a new prime minister. We'll go live to London for a report.\par \par \uc2 Plus, an exclusive interview with Ukraine's defense minister about the prospects of a dirty bomb being unleashed in his country.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 Fox5 in Atlanta will to start a trial to determine whether Georgia can enforce its ban on abortions as early as six weeks into pregnancy. Critics of the law say women are harmed every day it's in place. State law bans abortions once a detectable human heartbeat is present.\par \par \uc2 Fox2 in St. Louis with two victims dead in a shooting at a high school. Police say an adult woman and a teenage girl were killed. Six other people wounded. The gunman was fatally shot by police.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Philadelphia from Fox 29. One of the big stories there tonight, the \uc2 Philadelphia Phillies\uc2  are going to the World Series. Fans' celebrating after yesterday's clinching victory.\par \par \uc2 Police say there were no injuries, just one arrest. The \uc2 Phillies\uc2  play the \uc2 Houston Astros\uc2  in Game One of the Fall Classic, Friday, on FOX.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look, "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: The teenager accused of killing four students at Michigan high school last year is pleading guilty to terrorism and first degree murder charges.\par \par \uc2 16-year-old Ethan Crumbley withdrew his intent to pursue an insanity defense. His parents are facing involuntary manslaughter charges for allegedly making a gun accessible and ignoring his need for mental health treatment.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  have announced charges in three separate cases stemming directly from the Chinese Communist government. Two Chinese intelligence officers are charged with trying to infiltrate a Justice Department investigation of telecommunications company \uc2 Huawei\uc2 , by stealing files and bribing someone who turned out to be an \uc2 FBI\uc2  double agent.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LISA MONACO, DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL: The defendants attempted to bribe someone they believed was an insider to steal the prosecutor's playbook, but the insider flipped the script on the defendants by working not for them, but for Team America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The other two cases deal directly with spying and threats to a Chinese American. This comes as President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  was recently elected to an unprecedented third term.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian authorities are claiming success and shooting down Iranian made drones being used by Russia. They're also trying to minimize concern over Russia's claim Ukraine plans to detonate a dirty bomb and blame Moscow.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, Trey Yingst spoke exclusively with Ukraine's Defense Minister about what's going on on the ground.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OLEKSII REZNIKOV, UKRAINIAN DEFENSE MINISTER: It's absurd and absolutely fake. It's a next stage of Russia information campaign against the civilized world. And also, they're trying to organize next kind of pretext for using by their side some kind of nuclear threats.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: The types of threats against Ukraine are changing. We have seen now Iranian made weapons on the battlefield. What do you need in order to stop these drones from targeting Ukrainian cities?\par \par \uc2 REZNIKOV: The priority number one for Ukraine, its air defense systems with a different types, short range, middle range and long range.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: There are reports that Iran has military personnel in Crimea.\par \par \uc2 REZNIKOV: Yes, it's true.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Minister Reznikov is also saying he's sure Iran has military personnel in Belarus as well.\par \par \uc2 Great Britain will have its first ethnic minority prime minister, former treasury chief, \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2 , has emerged as the winner in line to be the third prime minister in just a matter of weeks. He takes over for \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  who resigned last week after lasting just 44 days in office.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alex Hogan is in London tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Britain's Conservative Party tapping a new Prime Minister \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RISHI SUNAK, BRITISH PRIME MINISTER-DESIGNATE: I will make it my utmost priority to bring our party and our country together.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: During the pandemic's outbreak, the former finance minister received praise for supporting closed businesses and laid off workers.\par \par \uc2 Today, the 42-year-old who only became a member of parliament seven years ago addressed the difficult task of stabilizing the economy where inflation is at a 40-year high.\par \par \uc2 SUNAK: We face a profound economic challenge.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: The British Pound trading higher against the U.S. Dollar after his appointment was announced. Sunak will become the youngest Prime Minister to lead the country in more than 200 years. He will also be Britain's first minority P.M., as well as the first Hindu. The results quite symbolically falling on Diwali, the Hindu festival of lights.\par \par \uc2 Sunak is the son of Punjabi immigrants from East Africa. Indian Prime Minister \uc2 Narendra Modi\uc2  today called Sunak a living bridge for the Indians in the U.K.\par \par \uc2 But Sunak has also faced scrutiny over his personal wealth. Critics argue he's out of touch with the daily financial burdens of the average citizen, the former banker and his wife, the daughter of an Indian billionaire are worth more than $830 million.\par \par \uc2 Sunak also faced backlash this year for his wife's part time residency status, which had allowed her to avoid some British taxes at the time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (on camera): Sunak was a loud supporter of Brexit. This year, he's also vowed to reinforce aid for Ukraine. Today, he underlined the need for financial stability but he gave no specifics.\par \par \uc2 And tomorrow, Sunak will meet with \uc2 King Charles III\uc2  before officially assuming the new role, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex Hogan live in London. Alex, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we talk live with New York Republican Gubernatorial Nominee \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 . First, Beyond our Borders tonight, North and South Korea Exchange warning shots along the disputed western sea boundary.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's military says it broadcasts warnings and fired warning shots to repel a North Korean merchant ship that violated the sea boundary. The North's military says it fire 10 rounds of artillery warning shots towards its territorial waters. The sea boundary of the West Coast is a source of long running animosities.\par \par \uc2 And Indians as mentioned celebrate their national holiday Diwali. It's the Hindu festival that symbolizes the victory of light over darkness.\par \par \uc2 Many people light earth and oil lamps or candles and fireworks are set off. It also features gift exchanges.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Midterm watch now, countdown is on.\par \par \uc2 Joining us tonight, New York Congressman and Republican Gubernatorial nominee \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  from New York.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R), GUBERNATORIAL NOMINEE OF NEW YORK: It's great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, we didn't know we would be talking about your race this late, this close to the election. The latest RCP average has it about six points, single digits, but there are other polls in recent days that have it even closer. The Quinnipiac poll of issues most important for New Yorkers, crime is overwhelming at 28 percent, inflation at 20 percent. Is that what has turned this race around for you? How do you see it tonight?\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: Yes, absolutely. We have been going around the state and hearing from New Yorkers about the issues that matter most to them, and they keep talking about their breaking point in the state most often referencing rising crime and skyrocketing costs. And \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  has been ignoring these issues. This is what we have been talking about the most. And it's created an opportunity for us to be able to connect with not just Republicans but independents and Democrats as well.\par \par \uc2 We're seeing a surge of support right now. We feel it on the ground. There is a lot of momentum. The issues are on our side on many fronts, from the impacts of cashless bail to D.A.s who refuse to enforce the law, and people just struggling to make ends meet and deciding to leave for some other state where their money goes further. It makes a lot of sense to us why we are in this position to win this race, and 15 days out we are feeling fantastic. We are going to win this race, working as hard as we are and the support that we are getting. We have to save our state.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Governor Hochul is now out with a new ad, and she is addressing this issue of crime. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A safe walk home at night, a subway ride free of fear, a safer New York for every child, that's what \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  is working for as governor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And she was asked at a news conference, Congressman, whether she has found this issue late in this campaign. This is what she said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2 , (D) NEW YORK: I'm not letting the political theater out there affect what we have done. But when you have some very high profile, frightening cases all in a short time, despite the statistics that tell you it averages out, I understand fear. We're not saying we are done. We are saying we are laser-focused on this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So what's your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: Yes, it's absolutely driven by politics. She is 15 days out from an election. She was losing her lead in the polls. The most recent poll that came out on Friday actually show that we're starting to pull ahead of her, that Coefficient poll. The Quinnipiac poll you referenced had us within four. The Trafalgar had us within two. So now she's decided because she wants to save \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  that she's going to start talking about crime. We've been talking about crime because we have been talking about wanting to save the state. And what she is not talking about is overhauling cashless bail and giving judges discretion away dangerousness. What she is not talking about is how we need tom remove rogue D.A.s like Alvin Bragg who refuse to the law. She's not talking about the HALT Act which is resulting more attacks on corrections officers and other prison staff and inmates, or the people that she released, the thousands of people that she released early from prison because of another law called Less is More.\par \par \uc2 The pro-criminal law list goes on. She is not proposing rolling back any of it. Instead, she is trying to just get herself a two-week bridge to get over the November 8th election. But the problem for her is that the voters are smarter than she gives them credit for. They see right through it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congressman, political watchers in New York are convinced Governor Hochul in your debate tomorrow night is going to go after you for your support of former President Trump. Third on that list in Quinnipiac was protecting democracy, and they point to you on the House floor objecting to the certification of Arizona. How do you counter that? What do you say to the people who have concerns about that whole situation?\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: Well, listen, please do. As far as her obsessing over it, I said this a year-and-a-half when I got into this race. If we got to October and we are obsessing over the issues that matter most to New Yorkers, the attacks on wallets, on safety, on freedom, and the quality of our kid's education, and the Democrats are obsessing over the former president, we are going to be winning a very large majority of the undecided independent- minded voters. So if I was sitting in that debate prep room, if she is having one, I would tell her, yes, absolutely, you should be on saying over \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But to be clear you have always said that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is a legitimate president?\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: I have never called the 2020 election illegitimate. And I have been spending this entire campaign hearing from the voters of New York from all walks of life. And it doesn't get any more personal. Regardless of what you think of President Trump, for the person who is changing their routine of when to ride the subway, or if they're Jewish, taking off their yarmulke, or if they're a woman, maybe now they are talking about how they are hugging a poll or grabbing a guardrail because they are afraid of being pushed in front of an oncoming subway car.\par \par \uc2 The fears that we hear about, these are real tears, these are real concerns that people right now are looking forward, not backwards. And if we are talking about the future of our state and supporting our men and women in law enforcement again and ensuring that people can come visit New York and feel safe and go back to their routine where they can walk a Manhattan 15- block stretch without fear of being attacked with their head on a swivel or having to call an Uber to go two blocks, this is what she should be focused on. But she can't help herself. And she is also not willing to get behind the actual solutions.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speaking of that I only have a few seconds here. So you are governor. You get elected. What are you doing to change that dynamic?\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: The first thing I do day one, as soon as I am sworn into office, is telling the Manhattan District Attorney Alvin Bragg that he is being fired. I would love to be able to work with the legislature to fix these pro-criminal laws that have gone bad. But they have indicated that they are not going to do it. I'm going to declare a crime emergency here in the state of New York. The governor has that authority. And I'm going to suspend cashless bail on day one, and a bunch of these other pro-criminal laws to force the legislature to come to the table. We are going to start taking back our streets the moment that we are sworn into office.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , we will be watching this race closely. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In tonight's SPECIAL REPORT Spotlight, we take a look at two races Republicans are optimistic about in November. Starting in neighboring New Jersey, Democratic incumbent Congressman \uc2 Tom Malinowski\uc2  is once again defending his seat against a familiar opponent in the seventh district. He faces Republican \uc2 Tom Kean Jr\uc2 . who came within a percentage point of winning in 2020. This time the district has been redrawn to add more Republican voters, making it an uphill challenge for Malinowski. Our FOX power rankings today have that race leaning Republican.\par \par \uc2 In Arkansas, a familiar name and face has Republicans upbeat about retaining the governorship there. \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\uc2 , the current governor, \uc2 Sarah Huckabee Sanders\uc2 , who was former President Trump's press secretary, is the daughter of former Arkansas Governor \uc2 Mike Huckabee\uc2 , holds a 10-point lead over Democrat Chris Jones, and we have that in our power rankings as solid Republican.\par \par \uc2 A little announcement. We will be hosting a town hall featuring Ohio Senate nominees \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and J.D. Vance one week from tomorrow. Martha MacCallum and I will moderate that event live from Columbus, Ohio. We've have a live audience of Ohio voters for the one hour session. Tough but fair questions, we expect.\par \par \uc2 Wednesday, SPECIAL REPORT will be coming to you from Georgia. We'll hear from voters down there and candidates in the Senate and governors' races.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we talk with journalists from around the country about the coming midterm elections and some key races in their states, and later the panel on historic migrant numbers at the southern border.\par \par \uc2 But, as we go to break, sad news. Actor Leslie Jordan died today after a car accident in Hollywood. Jordan won an outstanding guest actor Emmy for his appearances on "Will and Grace" in 2005. He was also featured on "American Horror Story," and he was recently a guest on "Gutfeld!" Leslie Jordan was 67.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: In 15 days, 15 days, the choice couldn't be clearer, the stakes couldn't be higher. I know we can meet the moment.\par \par \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2 , (D-NY) DEMOCRATIC CONGRESSIONAL CAMPAIGN COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: The other side is working on their own power. So they have got a ploy to win back power. We have got a plan to fix your problems.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN, (R-OH) HOUSE OVERSIGHT COMMITTEE RANKING MEMBER: You can feel the momentum moving in our direction. The people get it. They know that Democrats are responsible for this mess, and they are going to vote for Republicans. And I think it could be a big night for us on November 8th.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The countdown is on. Let's bring in a special midterms panel, Manchester New Hampshire talk radio host Chris Ryan joins us, Michelle Price, national political reporter for the Associated Press in Las Vegas, and Luige Del Puerto is an editor with "Colorado Politics" and "The Denver Gazette." Thank you all for being here. We wanted to get different perspectives from places we don't hear about, and local perspective.\par \par \uc2 Chris, let me start with you. The big race in New Hampshire, obviously that Senate race, looks like Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  has a bit of an advantage, but it has closed in recent days. What's the feeling on the ground?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS RYAN, TALK RADIO HOST: The feeling is that \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  will probably win reelection, but there is some question as to whether Bolduc can close late, particularly as independent voters make their minds up late here. The question is whether Bolduc can get into within that striking distance, because independents and undecided voters at this point are largely going to vote upon the economy, the pocketbook issues and the things that are really affecting them as individuals at this point.\par \par \uc2 So, there is a feeling that there is about a two, three percent margin which could move in Bolduc's direction late, so he has to be in striking distance to do it. He was dealt a difficult blow in that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  pulled his advertising dollars away from Bolduc. But there is a feeling that Bolduc has momentum, he has been closing, the RCP average now at 3.6, the distance between the two.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you sense in New Hampshire that there is undercounting, or has been of Republicans?\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I do. I think that you have to be very careful which polls you look at. And I do feel that voters here are finnicky. They like to mess around with pollsters a lot. And I do feel that very often Republicans are under- sampled, and you have to take that into account when you are looking at our state. We have the largest number of our voting bloc is undeclared voters, and those folks are not angry but depressed about the current state of the economy and what their lives have looked like over the last couple years. That bloc, I think, is going to vote largely on pocketbook issues and may break for Bolduc.\par \par \uc2 The question is, will Bolduc be close enough when those folks make up their minds? They literally do it in the last couple days. Will he be close enough to reap the benefits of those folks being frustrated and depressed?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Luige, let me head out your way. Colorado, obviously a Senate race. The incumbent Michael Bennet up against Joe O'Dea. How do you see that race, again, again closing, still leaning Democrat, but closing?\par \par \uc2 LUIGE DEL PUERTO, "COLORADO POLITICS": Yes, the big picture in Colorado is I think the same as in many other states. We have -- Colorado is a Democratic state, and the Democrats have a numerical advantage of 110,000 more voters than Republicans. And the Republicans are running on a campaign based on the fact that they are accusing, really, the Democrats of creating the environment we are in right now. Higher inflation, soaring crime rate, those are the biggest issues we have in this state.\par \par \uc2 You are absolutely correct, Joe O'Dea and Michael Bennet, if you are just looking at the polls, for example, Joe O'Dea has consistently led in the polls. What's interesting, though, is in one of those polls, the Marist College poll, if you look at the independent vote, it's a bit closer than the registered voters, the spread is about three points. And I'm hearing from some people that that's probably a bit too close for comfort for the incumbent Michael Bennett.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Michael Bennet, yes. And I think that's what we are seeing. Michelle in Nevada, Republicans look to that Senate race with Laxalt against the incumbent \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 , and they see big possibilities in Nevada. And to those points about undecideds, sometimes they break late to the opponent. What's your sense on the ground there?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE PRICE, ASSOCIATED PRESS: That's right, Bret. The independents are huge swing factor in the race here. The Republicans are extremely upbeat. They are in a good mood. They are having rallies. The Democrats here, they talk about how they always want to run with the mentality like they are behind, and this is certainly the way they are kind of preventing it this time around. But right now, you are hearing a whole mix of people at the polls, but it's still early. Turnout is light.\par \par \uc2 So voters in Nevada tend to start paying attention late. They have got two weeks of early voting. We're going to really see it crystallize over the next two weeks, and then on Election Day is when it is really going to crank down here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Michelle, one more thing. The late Senator Harry Reid this big machine there, the culinary union, and it was really a Democratic fire wall. Is it there anymore for them in Las Vegas, in particular?\par \par \uc2 PRICE: It is. You have to remember, even in 2014 when Harry Reid was in the majority, the midterm races in Nevada, Democrats did not do well. So it's not a foolproof machine. That being said, in 2016, 2018, 2016 he retired, 2018 he was out of office. He was still around. In 2020 he was here. Those were good years for Democrats, but they were also close. This is just a swing state. The machine still here. It's still firing. But it might just not be its year.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, that's really interest. And thank you all for perspective on the ground. We'd love to bring you back as we get closer. We're going to do that with local reporters up until Election Day. Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 Up next, our SPECIAL REPORT panel on President Biden's message to fellow Democrats ahead of the midterms. Plus, those historic numbers along the southern border. And then, a veteran's incredible journey while raising money for charities.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We know, we know a lot of folks are struggling with inflation, driven by Putin's war in Ukraine and a global pandemic. It's little solace, but we have a lower inflation rate than most any other nation in the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, if you look at the stats around the world, the inflation rate is not the lowest. The U.S. is right there at 8.2 percent. Japan at three percent. But the president is making his pitch, and that was to Democrats in a DNC staff meeting here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Guy Benson, political editor at Townhall.com, host of "The Guy Benson Show" on FOX News Radio, Ben Domenech, editor at large of "The Spectator," host of "The Ben Domenech Podcast" on FOX News Radio, and \uc2 Mara Liasson\uc2 , national political correspondent of National Public Radio. You don't have a podcast on FOX, Mara? Everybody has got one.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARA LIASSON\uc2 , NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, NATIONAL PUBLIC RADIO: Hey, where is my podcast?\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Come over, the water is warm.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What do you think about that? He was pitching staffers, Democratic staffers. We're told he's going go back to Pennsylvania, but he's not doing a ton of campaigning.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: No, he's not, but it almost doesn't matter where he is. He's going to be on TV and you're going to hear the message.\par \par \uc2 But this is very hard. When you hear the party in power and inflation is as high as it is, who cares if we are above or below some other country. It's bad. Inflation is the most important economic indicator politically because it eats away at wages and wage gains, and every single time an ordinary person, anybody goes to the store, they feel it. So it's really, really hard. There's not a great message against inflation. There's just not.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Which is why maybe he is not getting asked to be in all these campaigns or appearances.\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, POLITICAL EDITOR, TOWNHALL.COM: Especially if that's the best he has got, saying that we are one of the best countries in the world when it comes to the inflation rate when it's not true. In the developing world, I just wrote down here, Japan, Switzerland, France, South Korea, Australia, Canada, Brazil, India, Singapore all have lower inflation than we have. And part of his argument seems to be, well, it's beyond our control. This is a global phenomenon. True to some extent, but that's not the full picture.\par \par \uc2 And there are whole slew of Democratic aligned economists from \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  to Steve Rattner who said the huge spending binge that the Democrats engaged in, especially earlier in the Biden administration really fueled what we're experiencing right now. And for them to try to kind argue, well, it's really not so bad and look at what we have achieved on the economy, there's a "Politico" story out just a few days that quoted a Democratic pollster saying that is our worst performing message. Like, you're welcome, America is their worst performing message. And it seems to be one that the president is clinging to.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Meantime we talked to \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , Ben, on the show, and he's within striking distance for governor of New York, which is shocking in New York. But crime does seem to be moving the needle in that race in particular.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Look, I think one of the things that we are underestimating about this current election is how many different subjects have long tails to them. We saw, for instance, the reporting coming out from the Education Department today regarding the terrible ramifications of school closures for school children across America in terms of how much they have been set back by the decisions that were made largely at the behest of both Democratic politicians and teachers' unions who even took the additional step of helping rewrite policy within the CDC in a way that I feel like is a complete scandal.\par \par \uc2 I think that when it comes to the crime issue, when it comes to these education issues, the Democratic Party has really been unable to reset the narrative in a way that would allow them to compete in this election, and it's one of the reasons why I think that we are headed now toward historic wave as opposed to one that, perhaps, even four weeks ago seemed to be more of just a modest normal midterm.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The other issue, especially along the southwest border, are these numbers, Mara. Fiscal year 2022, you've got known got-aways 599,000, 2.4 total encounters, and then some 90 plus that are on the terrorist watch list.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes, look, this is the Republicans' issue, immigration, crime, inflation, all of them are going in the wrong direction. And for people who really care about that, they don't even have to be in a border state, they can just be worried about illegal immigration in general, these are very good issues. These are issues where Republicans have big advantages. The issue where Democrats had an advantage, which was abortion, was very salient right after the Dobbs decision but has receded.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: New York is like Lucy and the football with Charlie Brown.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Republicans think they are going to get it and it's pull away at the last minute. Sometimes Nevada is like that, too, which I mentioned the culinary union and that firewall. Is this changing, though, the dynamic, you think?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: I think New York is still tough statewide for Republicans, maybe closer than anyone expected. Even if it ends up being a single digit race, that has implications down ballot in half a dozen important House races in the empire state. Nevada, so far, you talk to the people out there, you look at the numbers, the Republicans are pretty excited about where things are going in terms of early balloting and that sort of thing. But Nevada is definitely one of those states where it's like I will believe it when I see it for the Republicans. But I think we might see it this time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: To your point, just to make sure that everybody knows you are accurate, Biden's worst performing message, quote, "I'm stunned about how much of the Democratic commentary is winging it," he said in an interview. This is Stan Greenberg. "Republicans are hitting us on crime, border, and inflation. That has huge power. And we have this self-satisfied message of how much we have accomplished rather than being focused on what's happening to people."\par \par \uc2 Last word, Ben, is it -- is the messenger in \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  helping in any of these races?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: I don't think he is, and I think the conversation about how much he has been a drag and an anchor on their message is going to take over the conversation right after Election Day.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We shall see. Panel, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a special day. After seven months, a veteran completed a cross-country walk across America. Kenny Mintz tracked thousands of miles from Washington, D.C. to California in honor of fellow servicemembers and his late mother. It's the same route his mother drove with him at the age of four to start a new life. Mintz's walk supported the fight against pancreatic cancer and several military service charities. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, brand new FOX power rankings. The countdown is on to Election Day.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. Here's Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmi