peo\uc2  about that he joins me in just a short moment. And also Ted Cruz, Senator Ted Cruz here today. But first, let's get to the top order of business, 15 days to go, 15 days to go, right? I mean, it was six months ago, about five minutes ago, but now it is right around the corner, really surprising.\par \par \uc2 Democrat candidates in states like New York and California now under pressure, Michigan, we're going to take a look at best the governor's race, Tudor Dixon and \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  is also tightening up. So, we're going to take a look at those. But first, these are the headlines. OK, this is what was being talked about today. Politico, "Big Republican gains." The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , "Democrats fear that the midterm map is slipping away from them." And Axios, issuing a "red tsunami watch." President Biden just moments ago said this:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: So far, we're running against the tide and we're beating the tide. But we just have 15 days into one of the most important elections in our lifetime, and it's going to shape the way this country looks like for the coming decade.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He did zero campaigning over the weekend. He was in Delaware. Let's bring in Axios's Senior Political Correspondent, Fox News Contributor, \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2 . Josh, great to see you today. Let's get right to it. These are the Senate toss up races, as you see them, and as we see them. Nevada, Arizona, Georgia, Pennsylvania, but let's take a quick look here at Arizona, because this is a race that, at one point, the Senate leadership committee said you know, Blake Masters is pretty much on his own, right. And now, this thing is tightening up. What are you seeing here, Josh?\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: So, Martha, you know, there's a wave environment when you have a candidate in Blake Masters that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  didn't want to invest in was worried that he wasn't a strong enough of a candidate against Senator Mark Kelly, and yet the polls are tightening in Arizona. It's a very, very close race.\par \par \uc2 A lot of the undecided voters across the country, including in Arizona, breaking towards the Republicans. So, that's what you see, in a wave. It's not about the candidate quality all the time. It's about voters registering their discontent against the White House, against the party in power, and that happens across the country in races, no matter the caliber of the candidates that are running.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you think in Arizona, Josh, that, that the attention that's being paid to Kari Lake who's running for governor there, is that helping Blake Masters? Is that boosting him as well?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: So, I'm hearing that there are a lot of Lake-Blake signs in Arizona because Blake Masters is running a little bit behind Kari Lake. And Kari Lake in these final couple of weeks, certainly has more political momentum in that state. So, Republicans are hoping to use Kari Lake's popularity with the base to get Blake Masters over the finish line. And it may work, it may work if enough Republicans show up and vote straight, straight ticket in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I mean, it may -- the headline writers, well, we'll see if you guys are right, in 15 days about how big this is, you're saying a seismic shift that includes some of these House races. And I mentioned before, let's pull up the Fox News Power Rankings. And you'll see that 33 of those, it's the yellows, a little hard to see, it's in the middle of your screen there.\par \par \uc2 But, but I'll just tell you that about four of them are California races, four of them are New York races. Remember, Democrats thought that those New York races were going to be helped by redistricting. Now, it turns out that, that doesn't appear to be the case. Here's the \uc2 DCC\uc2  chair, Representative Patrick Maloney, and he's up against Assemblyman Mike Lawler in this race, how do you see this one, Josh?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: I always like to say follow the money when it comes to House races. There's not a lot of polling out there, but the money tells you what the picture is looking like. And yet, Democrats are spending a lot of money now at the last minute to try to protect their own leader, \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 , in a double-digit district that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  carried just, just in 2020.\par \par \uc2 And when you look at the big picture, Martha, Democrats or Republicans rather are spending over $25 million in house districts that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won by double digits, just two years ago. That is why we're seeing the growing possibility of a big red wave. You don't see these districts come into play very often, only when you see a big, big year shaping up for the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Follow the money is right, and it's a point that's worth pointing out. Again, $25 million going into races House races across the country that President Biden won by double digits that no party wants to be doing that but that's what they're doing right now. So, here's Biden's approval number on the economy, Josh. Among registered independent voters here, they've gone from 33 percent, approved in January, now down to 18 percent.\par \par \uc2 So, despite the fact that the message has been things are getting better, inflation is transitory. They're just joshing, you know, no pun intended, when they tell you that crime isn't happening. And it's also reverberating in these governor's races. Let's put up some of these governor's races that I don't think most of us expected. You know, in the beginning, you had the Michigan race, Tudor Dixon and \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , that one is tightening. And here's some of the other ones. What are your observations on these, Josh?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Yes, so the economy is certainly the number one issue across the country. But in these governor's races, especially some of those blue state governors' races, crime is number one. In Oregon, Republicans are optimistic that they could actually win the Oregon governor's race for the first time since 1982, because crime and homelessness are just a huge, huge problem in Portland and in other parts of the state.\par \par \uc2 New York, we're starting to hear a little bit more about the New York governor's race between Governor Hochul and \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  because of the crime issue. Crime is the number one issue in the state of New York, especially in New York City. And \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  is campaigning more than any other Republican I can remember in the city of New York because he thinks he can actually win some Democratic voters, some, some people who don't usually vote Republican. So, the governors.\par \par \uc2 Those races are less partisan, and they're more blue state voters that are more willing this year to consider a Republican candidate.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, we'll see. Independent women voters who are going to be very pivotal in this race, and they care about abortion, but they also care about crime and they care about price of meat, and the price of eggs and all of those things when they go to the store. So, it's really interesting what's going on. We'll see lots of you, I hope over the next couple of weeks --\par \par \uc2 Be with us when we had to Harrisburg Pennsylvania. That's also where we find Correspondent Bryan Llenas, who is following one of the nation's closest and most consequential races as Democrat candidate \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz prepare to square off in a big debate that will happen tomorrow night. So, we'll be there on the ground to find out what Pennsylvania voters thought about what they saw in maybe what will be the most closely watched debate of the season.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTER (D-PA), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: And another thing, I need a, elephant in the room, you know, we had a stroke back in May. And it really, and despite all that, I survived to be back. And that makes, I'm going to fight for you all because I'm back out on this race.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, our own Bryan Llenas is fresh off a visit to Braddock and that is where \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  made his name. He was mayor there for 13 years. Hi, Bryan, what are you learning there in Braddock?\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha. Well, yes, we spent the weekend in Braddock, Pennsylvania, now in Harrisburg. And really that is in Braddock is where he did make his name, his reputation. His political career started there. And his political ads even say that he "rebuilt Braddock." He still lives there.\par \par \uc2 He has tattoos of Braddock's zip code on one arm. He has the dates marking when someone violently died in the old steel town on the other. But the statistics tell a different story, under Fetterman, the population dropped 40 percent to just 1700 people. The poverty rate increased, and the number of violent crime incidents actually went up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: One resident told me Braddock rebuilt? Take a look around you. There's nothing here. Other locals have accused Fetterman of being a carpet back, an outsider, telling the press: "Braddock became all about him and less about the community."\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: When he ran for mayor, it was all hopes, you know, he'd bring it back. But I think he just used it as a stepping stone. He didn't really turn Braddock around. He bought property for himself, which he's profiting from, so.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So, you would think, as the mayor, you would go out, reach out to the local businesses and say I'm the mayor if anything I can do to help, let me know. That never happened. The renaissance of Braddock coming back, it never didn't happen. I mean, just -- I mean, how many vacant buildings we have here?\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (voiceover): Those were Braddock businessmen, conservatives who say look, they gave Fetterman a shot. Fetterman's campaign points out under Fetterman, there were no murders for five years. Local supporters say he brought affordable housing investment and a community center.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And he gave people the opportunity to come in and open up business, so you can't blame that all on him.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And everything don't happen overnight. Everything is over time. It's over time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, I do believe that there's a hope. There is hope. And he brought that, he brought that back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): Now as mayor, Fetterman was notoriously absent from city council meetings and as Lieutenant Governor of Pennsylvania, the A.P. recently reported that he was often absent from state business here in Harrisburg. A big topic of the debate could be his background tomorrow, as we watch that, that debate tomorrow. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We will be watching that together and there on the ground in Harrisburg, this coming Wednesday. Bryan, thank you very much. And we're going to get reaction from some of those folks when they watched it.\par \par \uc2 Independent voters, what do they think? Did the debate change their mind? How does \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  do on that stage? And how does Mehmet Oz handle those questions about where he's from? And all of the issues that have been major to this, this campaign processing Pennsylvania very interesting part of the big story here.\par \par \uc2 Also, \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , who fought to uncover COVID origin's when he was Secretary of State on what happened in the wake of COVID's historic learning loss for, for children across this country that resulted from those shutdowns that is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): Children in the United States failing to learn fundamental skills during the pandemic. It's no shock to this program. We talked about this a lot over the last year and a half.\par \par \uc2 The latest numbers from the National Assessment of Educational Progress show not a single state improved its fourth or eighth grade math or reading scores since 2019.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the vast majority of states dropped to their lowest scores in decades. In a moment, former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  who is an advocate for more choice in education.\par \par \uc2 First to correspondent Mark Meredith. Hi, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Martha, good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 Well, this report is comprehensive. It also confirms what many parents, teachers, and politicians long suspected, pandemic had a widespread and negative impact on education, especially for elementary and middle school students.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice-over): The National Center for Education Statistics compared math and reading scores of more than 446,000 students in fourth and eighth grade, the results quite telling. And in terms of math comprehension, fourth grade scores dropping five points compared to 2019.\par \par \uc2 There was an even steeper decline when it comes to eighth grade math scores with students scoring eight points lower.\par \par \uc2 One education expert warning the drop off may impact an entire generation of kids.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. CAREY WRIGHT, CHIEF SCHOOLS OFFICER, NATIONAL ASSESSMENT GOVERNING BOARD: This should be alarming to anybody that's looking at it. I don't think the nation has ever had results that looked this bad before. And it lets me know that there's a lot of children out there that are struggling and need very specific help.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: When it comes to reading, the government also found 