their kids to learn.\par \par \uc2 Martha, I ran -- I ran a machine shop before I ran for Congress, we needed students coming out of high school in college with the ability to basic math, reading -- be able to engage with their fellow -- or the colleagues to actually make things, the capacity for America to educate the next generation, there's no more important mission set.\par \par \uc2 And although I was working on things all across the world, these last four years, there is nothing that will undermine this republic, like the absence of education, we need a -- we need a effort all across 50 states to give kids a choice about where they can go to school and help their parents make that decision well.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And Cardona just said, you know, well, parents should have the resources, they need to be able to walk into the school and say, can you tell me what you're doing to get my kid caught up?\par \par \uc2 You know, we -- this needs to be a crisis level operation that is handled. If the Department of Justice -- of Education is worth anything, they ought to be able to handle this.\par \par \uc2 I want to switch gears for one moment. This video was shocking that we all saw over the weekend. This is the number two in leadership under President Xi in China.\par \par \uc2 Who was sitting right next to him. He was pulled out of this government meeting and escorted out against his will. Let's watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (voice-over): Really remarkable. He got yanked out of his seat. You couldn't see that at the beginning. He didn't want to leave it. He sort of turns to his old friend, President Xi, he's what's going on or something to that effect, I would imagine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): What do you see here? What are you seeing in this?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Yes. Yes. That's the former leader of China being escorted out by the most powerful man in China since Mao, and a man, \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  who not only now controls every apparatus of power inside the Chinese Communist Party, but is intent on changing the way we live.\par \par \uc2 These stories aren't disconnected, we need to make sure our kids are educated so that we can confront what is going to be an enormous onslaught by the Chinese Communist Party that, in fact, wants to change the way we live.\par \par \uc2 And the Chinese are educating their kids. We need to make sure that we do ours.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's such a good point.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 's effort there was demonstrably aimed at making clear, he's in total control, this is his show, and I think that tells America the mission that we have for the next -- probably the next decade, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And if our kids can't read, and they can't write, and they don't understand our history, and we're about, we will fail against China.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: We will get there.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's that urgent.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: That's right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: If we -- if we -- if we teach our kids that 1619 Project as a racist country, we're in an awful lot of trouble, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mike, thank you very much. \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , former Secretary of State, former head of the CIA.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Thank you, ma'am.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Good to see you, sir as always. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 So, coming up, we'll be joined by Republican Senator Ted Cruz. He is here in New York as the number of illegals flooding across the border now hit another new high under President Biden's watch.\par \par \uc2 Protesters cause chaos today also, at "The View".\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Last year, the federal government took in $4 trillion in tax revenues, most money in history we've ever taken in.\par \par \uc2 The problem is we spent nearly $7 trillion dollars and that's what ---\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We do cover climate here, guys. We do cover climate.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG, HOST, ABC: Excuse me. Excuse me. Ladies, ladies, excuse us, let us do our job. Let us do our job. We hear what you have to say, but you got to go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Take a look at the number of arrests at the southern border hit an all-time high last month. More than 227,000 last month. So, it brings the fiscal year total to 2.3 million.\par \par \uc2 I mean, sometimes these numbers just start to wash over you. But that is 203 -- 2,300,000 plus million people have crossed the border illegally just this year alone.\par \par \uc2 And then you've got the terror suspects who have also crossed the border at a record breaking rate. 98 people on that list, suspected terrorists walked right into the United States of America. That's almost four times as many as the past five years combined. During the Trump years, 11 got across.\par \par \uc2 Now we've got 98 this year alone. Wonder where they are. Where are those people? Texas Senator Ted Cruz is here with me in New York today.\par \par \uc2 First, to national correspondent Bill Melugin who watches all of this up close and personal in Eagle Pass, Texas. Hi, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Martha. Good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 The new fiscal year, fiscal year 2023 started on October 1st. And we're essentially picking up right where fiscal year 2022 left off. Case in point, take a look at this video we shot this morning right here in Eagle Pass on the northern outskirts of town.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice-over): A little town called Normandy. This is a large group of illegal immigrants coming across all at the same time. As you can see, most of them single adults turning themselves into Border Patrol.\par \par \uc2 But CBP sources tell us here in this Del Rio sector in just the first three weeks of October here, we've already had more than 29,000 illegal crossings in this sector. That represents a 50 percent increase over the same time last year.\par \par \uc2 Then, take a look at this wild video out of the Rio Grande Valley provided to us by a source what you're looking at are a group of drug smugglers who just swim across the river from Mexico. They're all carrying bundles of drugs. And in broad daylight, they start loading up a waiting vehicle with those drugs.\par \par \uc2 The men then go back to Mexico and the car drives off. And Texas DPS tells us they later got into a pursuit with that driver and arrested him. Take a look at these photos right here.\par \par \uc2 Once they pulled that car over, they found 10 bundles of drugs in that vehicle ended up being marijuana, 240 pounds of it. And it was a 18-year- old U.S. citizen who was behind the wheel. He smirked for his photo. He was arrested and charged with transporting those drugs.\par \par \uc2 And then lastly, take a look at this photo out of Arizona. A human smuggling stash house bust in Casa Grande, which is right in between Phoenix and Tucson. Border patrol in the Tucson sector finding 46 illegal immigrants crammed in a stash house that is pretty inland into Arizona's interior, showing these migrants got pretty into Arizona before they were caught by law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And those stash houses dangerous and deadly. And back out here live, case in point, fiscal year 2022 was the deadliest year on record for migrants here at our southern border. CBP sources tell Fox News, 856 migrants died here at the border in fiscal year 2022. That is the highest ever recorded. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, so that they don't have room for all of the dead among those migrants. So they're storing the bodies in refrigerated containers. It is a grim reminder of the untenable situation of that they have to deal with. Bill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Bill Melugin at the border. Let's bring in Texas Senator Ted Cruz. His new book, "Justice Corrupted: How The Left Weaponized Our Legal System" comes out tomorrow. So congratulations on your latest book. Senator, it's good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 And I guess one of the questions that comes into my mind when I listened to build solutions report is, if it's true that Republicans may retake the House and possibly the Senate as well, as we've been talking about today, just based on what we're seeing out there. Would they complete a wall across the southern border? I mean, what would be different in this situation?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Well, listen, let me say Bill Melugin's reporting is incredibly important. He's one of the very few journalists actually reporting on the chaos on the southern border. And the video, the coverage he puts out is really important.\par \par \uc2 This is a manmade crisis. It was caused by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats, three decisions they made caused this crisis. Number one, they halted construction of the border wall. Number two, they reinstated the disastrous policy of catch and release. And number three, Biden pulled out of the incredibly successful remain in Mexico policy. That's what caused this crisis.\par \par \uc2 We went from under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the lowest illegal immigration in 45 years to now under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the highest illegal immigration ever recorded. 4.4 million people have crossed illegally since Biden became president. And I'll tell you, a big part of my book, "Justice Corrupted," is it lays out the utter lawlessness of this administration.\par \par \uc2 The reason for that crisis, Biden and the DOJ refused to follow the law. And so the way to stop it is we got to actually deport people across illegally. If you don't do that, we'll never solve this problem.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. But I asked you what would change. You know, would we see a wall -- I mean, I think people are going to go, oh, I have to wait two more years, potentially, to see this problem go away, real quick.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: So we need majorities in both Houses. We need to have hearings, we need to have oversight. We need to impeach Alejandro Mayorkas, the Secretary of Homeland Security, and we need to fight to defund the 87,000 new \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents and put all that funding on securing the border. Biden and the Democrats are going to fight us. It's going to be a battle and I'll tell you, Martha, next year, it's going to be a battle where Republican leadership is going to be less than eager to take on the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 I hope when we have majorities, we actually use it and fight because this fight matters.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think it's incumbent upon, you know, if you win the majority, I think people want to see actual results.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So let's see this today, because you had an interesting time so far in New York, went to a baseball game and didn't get a warm welcome. And then you were on "The View" today and this happened. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG, "THE VIEW" HOST: Excuse me. Ladies, ladies? Excuse us, let us do our job. Let us do our job. We hear what you have to say but you got to go. You got to go. You got to let us do our job.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So what were they protesting?\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: You got to let us to do our job.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So what were they protesting?\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: They were climate radicals and they were they were screaming, but it shut "The View" down for a while. And I'll tell you, it was a bit of a circus but the reason I went on "The View" is I think it's important that serve it as reach much more broadly than just preaching to the choir, that we need to reach a lot of the viewers of the view, have only seen one partisan story the whole time.\par \par \uc2 You know, a few minutes later Whoopi Goldberg insists that she was obsessed with January 6. And she said it's only Republicans who engage in violence. And I couldn't help but laugh. I said did you miss an entire year of antifa riots all over the country, firebombing cities? And Whoopi insists I don't know what you're talking about. What antifa riots?\par \par \uc2 What the book "Justice Corrupted" does is it walks through the lawlessness of Democrats trying to abolish the police of \uc2 George Soros\uc2 . DEA is letting violent criminals go at walks through \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  and now \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  politicizing the Department of Justice, politicizing the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the \uc2 IRS\uc2  and using it to target their enemies. And I think it is an incredible threat to the rule of law and our liberty.\par \par \uc2 And this book tries to explain what the threat is. It's also the first inside account of what happened on January 6. I was standing there objecting on the Senate floor and I walked the readers through what was going on behind closed doors in the Senate and the legal challenges. And as you know, President Trump asked me to argue his case before the Supreme Court. The Supreme Court had taken it so I described that case and what the fight was all about.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Ted Cruz, senator from Texas, thank you very much. Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, you guys -- it's tough sometimes to be a visitor in New York. (Inaudible) congratulations to your Astros. The people were flipping the bird and doing all kinds of rude things.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: My only answer to that --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Because don't like you wear the big Astros jacket.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: My only answer to that is scoreboard.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Tough day for Yankee.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: And I know you're a Mets fan, so.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: (Inaudible) Mets fans, so we have a lot of tough days. Good to see you, senator.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you so much for being here today. So, President Biden telling his party today the Republicans will make inflation worse, as a new poll finds he is out of touch with voters on that issue. \uc2 Stephen Moore\uc2  and Austan Goolsbee will duke it out here in just a moment, the big election 15 days away. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: When I hear people talk about inflation as the -- we have to change that subject. Inflation is a global phenomenon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Terrible video and a terrible incident today, kids running for their lives after a shooting at a high school in St. Louis. Police say that the school was locked but that a gunman broke in after 9:00 am, killed a woman, killed a teenager and injured six other people. The police killed him in a shootout.\par \par \uc2 One girl says she came face to face with the gunman and his weapon jammed and she got away. Police say he was about 20 years old. No word yet on a possible motive. But another absolutely terrifying school shooting and two people lost their lives today in St. Louis.\par \par \uc2 So you can see this list of what people really care about as we get close to this election. Inflation is topping the list of the most important things 15 days away from the midterms. But look at this, a majority of Americans in that same Monmouth poll, 63% say that the President doesn't focus enough on the things that matter to them and their families. 63% that number really caught my attention over the course of the weekend.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in \uc2 Stephen Moore\uc2 , founder of the Committee to Unleash Prosperity and former senior economic adviser to President Trump and Austan Goolsbee, former chair of the Council of Economic Advisers under President \uc2 Obama\uc2  and professor at the \uc2 University of Chicago's Booth School of Business\uc2 . Gentlemen, great to have both of you with us today.\par \par \uc2 So, Austan, 63% say the President doesn't pay attention to the things they actually care about, which turn out to be inflation and crime, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE, PROFESSOR, UNIVERSITY OF CHICAGO-BOOTH SCHOOL OF BUSINESS: Well, I mean, when the inflation rate is high, it's going to be reflected in the polls. You see that in Europe too. I mean, they're having massive strikes and protests about the cost of living and inflation in France, in the UK, in Germany --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Is that supposed to make Americans feel better, though, Austan? I mean, I don't thing American --\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: It doesn't. That's my point, it doesn't. It doesn't make them feel better.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: It only informs is it because of some policy that's happening in the United States that there is inflation. But in the end, it doesn't make any difference? You can see in the polls, people are upset because inflation is high.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But I think they're saying that he's not addressing what matters to them. He's talking about other things. He keeps coming out, Stephen, saying, you know, wow, look at what we've done with the deficit. And people say even catch up as a dollars, you know, and it feels like he's just talking past people. And I think it's very reflected in that numbers to you.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MOORE\uc2 , FOUNDER, COMMITTEE TO UNLEASH PROSPERITY: I agree with that. And look, when Trump was president for four years, the average inflation rate was 2%, when he left office, the inflation rate was 1.5%, the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  said that we'd have inflation of maybe 3% or 4%, we're at 8.5%. So how did that happen, Martha?\par \par \uc2 My explanation is that we spent then borrowed and printed $4 trillion, through all of these massive spending bills. And Republicans, to their credit, for the most part, oppose these. Most of them were passed with only Democratic votes. And to tie this to these upcoming elections, I think it's one of the reasons Democrats are going to face a very high price in November because people it's not complicated, in my opinion, to connect the dots between these massive spending bills, multi-trillions, and the fact that we have inflation because you dump all that money in the economy, that's going to cause prices rise.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Steve, 5 trillion of the 7.5 trillion were passed under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So how do you reconcile that?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, look, there was -- and by the way, I was opposed to most of those spending bills. But you're right, Republicans not blameless here, Martha. I mean, Goolsbee is right here. The politicians of both parties have spent too much money, but what are we going to do something about it? The Democrats, I mean, as we speak, the President is trying to move forward with the $500 billion bailout of student loans. I mean, it just never ends.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Indeed, it doesn't. And it's always interesting to me. The American people are very smart and they understand what's happening, and they see that costs have gone up. And they do understand that a lot of it comes from the massive spending that the government has put in place.\par \par \uc2 Now, we both know that in a couple of weeks, the question is going to turn to who's running in 2024 for president. Because, you know, there may be a block against some of this, this policy if Republicans do take the house in the Senate. But the President was asked that question and this response is getting a lot of attention or it is a lack of response, Austan. And I just want to give you a chance to take a look at it and tell me what you think about the current president.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: I have not made that formal decision but it's my intention, my intention to run again. And we have time to make that decision.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 , FIRST LADY OF UNITED STATES: Dr. Biden is for it? As president?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Oh, Dr. Biden thinks that -- my wife thinks that I -- that we're doing something very important and that I shouldn't walk away from it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you think -- does that concern you any of these responses? The slowness of it, does it concern you, Austan?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Is that for me?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: No, it doesn't concern me. What do you mean? They're trying to put him on the spot about decisions in 2.5 years and he didn't want to answer.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you think he should have run again?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Look -- Me?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Do you want to see him president for four more years. Do you want to see that? Austan? Austan?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Oh to me?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Do you want -- no. Austan, do you want to see him be president for four more years?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: It's not up to me. I mean, I think he has a track record. He's going to -- we're going to see how the economy is doing when he runs, but he's had a lot of legislative accomplishments. And I think the first midterm always goes badly for the incumbent. And that doesn't really tell you anything about what it will be for President.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. It's a question that gets everybody's tongue. I got to go. I got to leave it there. Stephen knows --\par \par \uc2 MOORE: I want him to run. I want him to run.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. There you go. You're on the record. Thank you, guys. Austan, thank you. Steve, thank you. Good to see you both.\par \par \uc2 Also on top of mind, for American voters rising crime. I mean, the stories on the front pages of our papers are so upsetting. And it's an issue that came up in a hot debate in a tight Senate race in Washington State. Seattle radio host Jason Rantz when our Democracy 2022 coverage continues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY SMILEY (R), WASHINGTON SENATE CANDIDATE: We have a crime crisis in Washington State. Our cities are being destroyed by crime. Our police are not being supportive.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 PATTY MURRAY\uc2  (D-WA): In that slew of response you did not hear anything about gun violence. This is an issue we need to address.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A warning, this video is tough to watch but it is the reality of the New York City subway system right now. The man in the yellow hoodie, you'll see here sort of plants his feet and then he waits for the person to walk in front of him. And then he slammed this poor man down into the subway tracks that happened on Friday afternoon and Brooklyn. Police are still looking for the suspect. The 32-year-old victim was not hit by a train, thank God, but he broke his collarbone. His mother tells the post that he is so distraught.\par \par \uc2 He is suicidal. She had to take a knife away from him because he's so terrified of his own city. The city just announced they're going to add hundreds of police to overtime shifts and try to get more officers into the subway creating new site psychiatric units which help people with severe mental illness. Well, that's about time, seems like a good idea, right?\par \par \uc2 So crime is one of the issues that came up in the debate in the -- Washington Senate race, I should say. Republican Tiffany Smiley trying to topple Democrat \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  who has been in the Senate for 30 years. Correspondent Dan Springer with THE STORY from Seattle. Hi, Dan.\par \par \uc2 DAN SPRINGER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Hi, Martha. Got heated several times last night, Republican candidate Tiffany Smiley was on the attack all night long, blaming 30 year incumbent Democrat \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  and her party for inflation, high gas prices and rising crime.\par \par \uc2 Murray held the campaign rally over the weekend to boost enthusiasm. Fellow Senate Democrat Elizabeth Warren joined her in Seattle. But the crowd was very small. Murray has focused on just two issues, abortion and January 6.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: I will fight every day to make sure that we never allow those people who continue the big lie, including my opponent on her website before she changed it in July, to question that election because that sews mistrust in our country for our democracy.\par \par \uc2 SMILEY: I want to ask, you know, do you believe that me and my family are a threat to democracy, Senator Murray, because tell everyone here today. Either that or disavow your campaign's dangerous rhetoric that has spent millions of dollars to attack and paint me as someone I am not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPRINGER: Smiley, very emotional there. She's a veteran's advocate whose husband lost his eyesight in a suicide bombing while fighting for the country. Smiley blames Democrats pushing lacks drug laws and open southern border, and police defunding for the explosion of drug abuse on urban streets. In Seattle, the area -- the Seattle area this year, there have been 439 fentanyl overdose deaths. That's already well over last year's record total.\par \par \uc2 We're allowing a humanitarian crisis to unfold right before our eyes. We're allowing people to poison themselves to death and we do nothing. Just go along with DC and do nothing.\par \par \uc2 Murray's answer to rising crime was quote a ban on dangerous weapons. Polls show Murray with a lead in the single digits in the state \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won two years ago by 18 points. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. This race has tightened quite a bit in the past month. Thank you very much. Good to see you, Dan.\par \par \uc2 So with that we bring in Jason Rantz, KTTH Seattle radio talk show host. Jason, great to have you back on the program today. Thanks for joining me. So, what's your take on the ground there about where this thing is heading?\par \par \uc2 JASON RANTZ, KTTH SEATTLE RADIO TALK SHOW HOST: Things are much tighter than I think the polls will indicate. I think we've seen tremendous momentum particularly amongst independent voters shifting towards Tiffany Smiley. The reason why \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  doesn't want to have a second debate was because the more people that get in front of and -- actually see Tiffany Smiley here from Tiffany Smiley, they are likely to vote for her. And so what \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  is basically trying to do is lean on left wing media here to basically not cover this race, honestly, and Tiffany Smiley, from getting in front of voters in a major way.\par \par \uc2 And I think obviously when you do a really good debate performance it puts the pressure on someone like Murray to actually step it up. And she just doesn't have substance. And I think we saw that last night during the debate.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. They also talked about the issue of abortion. And I was thinking about this morning, Jason, because we all were sort of led to believe after that decision came down that it was going to change the dynamic. And I think there was good reason to think that that could be true, because we had been told for decades, and millions and millions of dollars had been spent to tell the people of America that if this gets overturned, is going to change life as we know it. It will be so revolutionary and catastrophic.\par \par \uc2 But if, if this change that we're seeing is true, it tells me that that issue, once it happened, it fizzled after 60 days. How is that possible?\par \par \uc2 RANTZ: So I think nationally it fizzled. I don't think that's the case here in Washington State. \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  is going on the offensive on this particular issue, trying to paint Tiffany smiley as a threat to abortion rights, which in Washington state, there is no threat whatsoever, it's easier to get an abortion here than it is to get a cup of coffee. That's how easy it is. And that's not going away.\par \par \uc2 But in Washington State, because it is so deeply blue, particularly in Seattle, if you are telling people that Republicans are evil that Republicans will go after your right to choose that does, in fact, motivate folks, even if they know that it's not truly a threat. They're looking on the left for that issue here in Washington State that's going to generate a little bit more interest from Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Because overall, when you're looking at the people who are interested in voting, it's Republicans because they're focusing on the issues that actually matter to them, like crime and inflation. But in a tight race and we're seeing this across the state, particularly in Washington's Third Congressional District and the Eighth, which one is a relatively safe Republican. The other one is kind of a purple district and we're looking to see if a Republican can pick it up.\par \par \uc2 But when it's that close, I do think it gives a little bit of an edge to the Democrats, which again, is why \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  doesn't want to get put in a position where she has to talk about anything other than what she is choosing to talk about, which are things that don't really matter to the vast majority of voters here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. And it's interesting to watch with the way the money moves around and these Senate races and this was a race that people were not putting a lot of money into because they didn't think there was a chance but now that has changed as we've gotten here.\par \par \uc2 Here's \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  on defund the police or re-imagined the police, as she calls it, I should say, earlier in 2020. And here she is last night, watch this contrast.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: I'm going to keep listening to the people who are at the forefront of these movements, including leaning on the work that's already been done to help re-imagine our nation's approach to public safety.\par \par \uc2 Under the American Rescue Plan, we put resources in there for our police officers so they could stay on the job and fight.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Subtle changes here in the language around any reimagining of police, Jason. Real quick.\par \par \uc2 RANTZ: Yes. I mean, she's a fraud on this particular issue. She's trying to pretend that crime, particularly in Seattle is not an issue. And it looked up the 911 calls around a campaign event that she held to make that point. And we had a ton of 911 calls, including a couple hours later shooting.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jason Rantz, it's always good to talk to you and we'll see what happens in Washington is going to be very interesting as we head into election night. Thanks, Jason.\par \par \uc2 RANTZ: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So big news in the UK where they are about to have their third Prime Minister this year alone. Former Treasury Secretary \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  winning enough support from conservative lawmakers to replace \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 . She resigned after just 45 days in office, the shortest prime ministership in history. Her plan to cut taxes and increased borrowing to get the economy moving and to create growth ended up sinking her.\par \par \uc2 Now, the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  had to take an emergency action to keep things under control and Sunak says that he knows he's facing a profound economic challenge. And he's promising to work for stability and unity. They ran against each other, Truss and Sunak, so now it's his turn to be the next prime minister of the United Kingdom.\par \par \uc2 And this today tributes are pouring in after the death of Emmy winning actor Leslie Jordan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LESLIE JORDAN, ACTOR: I'm the most sensitive person in the group. Are you stupid, Margaret?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He started in shows including the "Cool Kids," "Call Me Cat," "Murphy Brown, " "Will and Grace," which won him an Emmy in 2005. He reportedly died in a car crash today in Los Angeles, possibly after a medical emergency. Beloved Leslie Jordan leaves us at the age of 67 years old.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for joining us today, everybody, for THE STORY on this Monday, October the 24th, just 15 days to go until the midterm election which will chart the course of the next couple of years in Washington and our leadership. And THE STORY goes on. We look forward to seeing you back here tomorrow at 3:00. And then we go to Pennsylvania after the big Fetterman-Oz debate which is tomorrow night. We will sit down with viewers and voters. We'll talk to them about what they thought about how \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Mehmet Oz did the night before and whether it impacts their vote. Your world starts right now. Take care, everybody. See you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020221025eiao00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Border Crisis Continues; Rep. \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  (D-TX) is Interviewed about Immigration; CDC Considers Adding Covid to Regular Immunization Schedule; \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  is Interviewed about President Biden and his Award; Interview with Sen. \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  (R-UT); Poll Shows Independent Women Voters Leaning More Towards Republicans\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Bill Melugin, Rich Edson, Larry Kudlow, Karl Rove,  Juan Williams, Rich Edson \par \uc2 9,695 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Nearly two weeks to Election Day, seven million Americans have already cast their vote, as both parties debate the best path forward for the country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Republicans in Congress have a very different vision. They will crash the economy next year.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): The president comes out swinging, claiming Republicans will raise drug prices, cut Social Security and Medicare, and give the wealthy a tax cut if they win big in November.\par \par \uc2 But Republican candidates are pushing back hard, blaming Democrats and the White House for Americans' economic struggles.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R), OHIO SENATE CANDIDATE: We don't have to live like this, and if we get better leadership into Washington, D.C., we can actually solve some of these problems.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And Senate debates get heated, including an unusual race in Utah.\par \par \uc2 EVAN MCMULLIN (R), UTAH SENATE CANDIDATE: We need to send people to Washington who are willing to stand up both to Republicans and Democrats.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MIKE LEE\uc2  (R-UT): To suggest that I'm beholden to either party is contracted by the plain facts.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll ask Senator \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  about his reelection battle in a deep red state.\par \par \uc2 Then --\par \par \uc2 REP. HENRY CUELLER (D-TX): We need to do our job at the border.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The migrant crisis reaching new highs as asylum seekers flood New York. But one Texas city says it plans to stop sending buses north and wait until you hear why. We'll have a live report from the border and bring in Democrat Congressman \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , a moderate in a competitive race for his south Texas seat.\par \par \uc2 Then, a new poll shows a big change among independent women. We'll ask our Sunday panel which candidates could benefit.\par \par \uc2 Plus, \uc2 FOX Business Network\uc2 's \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  on fears of recession, and we'll show you the big honor Kudlow himself just received.\par \par \uc2 LARRY KUDLOW\uc2 , FOX BUSINESS NETWORK: It's humbling actually, humbling to me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right now on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 In this final countdown to the November midterms, we see dynamics shifting in races all across the country. Candidates are on the trail and making the case only they and their economy can right the economy and lead the divided country forward. They're facing off on debate stages with challengers hoping to break through against sitting lawmakers whose races had been considered safe.\par \par \uc2 It's actually playing out right now in a key Senate race in deeply conservative Utah. In just a minute, we're going to speak live with Republican Senator \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  of Utah who's in a tight match up for reelection to a third term.\par \par \uc2 But, first, a look at how his race went from a sure thing to maybe not so sure.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LEE: You, sir, owe me an apology.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Utah Republican \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2  on defense on the debate stage.\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: Senator Lee has been doing this with his pocket constitution for the last several years.\par \par \uc2 LEE: This is not a prop. And I don't carry it as a prop. This is a reference manual.\par \par \uc2 God bless you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: In a race that was once considered a lock for Lee, polls have tightened, and independent Evan McMullin is blasting Lee over his actions after the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: If you're committed to the Constitution, then stand up for our free and fair elections. Stand up for the peaceful transfer of power.\par \par \uc2 LEE: There is absolutely nothing to the idea that I would have ever supported, ever did support a fake electors plot.\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: McMullin, a former CIA officer, burst on to the scene in 2016, running for president as an independent hoping to peel away votes from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: I believe that Donald Trumps harms the strength and prosperity of this country.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: In Utah, I had this character that nobody ever saw before, Evan McMuffin. Do you believe this?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But McMullin is still behind in the polls and if he does win, he's vowed not to caucus with either party. That means in a Senate that could be held by the slimmest of margins, McMullin might end up being a swing vote.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Senator Lee now finds himself making an 11th hour plea for help from a fellow Utah Republican.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Mitt, if you like to protect the Republican majority, give us any chance of seizing the Republican majority. Please get on board, help me win reelection.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Joining me now, Utah Senator \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MIKE LEE\uc2  (R-UT): Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. We've got to get to a lot of big issues, the economy, abortion, others. But first, I want to talk about your race.\par \par \uc2 Your opponent -- by the way, had been invited to join us here on "FOX News Sunday" -- says that you betrayed your oath to the Constitution with regard to the 2020 election. Here's what he said right after that debate you guys had a few days ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EVAN MCMULLIN (I), UTAH SENATE CANDIDATE: Lee actively helped plan the effort to recruit fake electors and to overturn our election and American democracy. No one need take my word for it. It's a matter of public record, and he can dispute that as much as he wants, but the truth is the truth.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Here are some of texts that he cites between you and then White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 December 8th: If a very small handful of states were to have their legislatures appoint alternative slates of delegates, there could be a path.\par \par \uc2 January 4th: I've been spending 14 hours a day for the last week trying to unravel this for him.\par \par \uc2 That same day: I've been calling state legislators for hours and I'm going to spend hours doing the same today. I'm trying to figure out a path that I can persuasively defend.\par \par \uc2 Evan McMullin says the texts speak for themselves. How do you explain them?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Look, there's not a scintilla of truth to what he's suggesting there. The fact is, there were rumors circulating in the days and weeks leading up to January 6th, rumors suggesting that some states would be shifting out their slates of electors.\par \par \uc2 Now, as a U.S. senator, it was my job to open and count the electoral votes on January 6th. And we were trying to narrow down what was truth and what was fiction. I made phone calls to investigate the truthfulness of those rumors.\par \par \uc2 That's all. Not advocating, just investigating the truthfulness of them. There's only scenario in which Congress would have had a role.\par \par \uc2 I concluded after my investigation, that the rumors were false and on that basis, I voted to certify the results of the 2020 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, when you're saying, I'm looking for a path forward with the states electors or with state legislatures, that path forward would suggest that you were at some point onboard with the idea?\par \par \uc2 LEE: No. Look, the point here was that there was -- there was only one path and that path I was trying to get the White House and president's team to acknowledge, that was the only path. If a state concluded that it incorrectly certified results of its election, and on that basis shifted out its slates of electors, that would be only way of doing it.\par \par \uc2 Now, I didn't believe they were going to do it, and my own investigation played that out. My point there was to say we've got to figure out whether this is the case and I wanted the president's team to acknowledge that that would be the only scenario in which there would be any role for Congress. There wasn't, and on that basis, I voted to certify.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Let's go back to 2016, I remember being a reporter on the floor of the Republican National Convention when there was a vociferous response from you when it came -- and others to actually supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as the nominee. Here's that moment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Those opposed shall say no.\par \par \uc2 AUDIENCE: No!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I think I got that on my cell phone.\par \par \uc2 By the way, back in 2016, that year that you opposed \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you actually voted for Evan McMullin. I'm guessing you're not going to vote for him this time in this Senate race. But if President Trump runs again, will you be a supporter?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Look, if he runs again, I think there's a very high probability he gets nomination. I will, of course, support the nominee in 2024. And yeah, you're right about my vote in 2016.\par \par \uc2 I was wrong, I was really wrong. I didn't believe that President Trump would do the things he promised to do. And I was still sore over the way some of my colleagues had been treated during the 2016 election cycle.\par \par \uc2 But that vote I cast in 2016 was a huge mistake, just as it would be huge mistake for my fellow Utahans to vote for Evan McMullin today.\par \par \uc2 Evan McMullin is a Democrat. Look, he's been endorsed by the Democratic Party. He's raised 2.5 million last quarter alone on Act Blue, the Democrats' donor network. He spent $1.6 million feeding it right back into the Democratic industrial complex. He supported Democratic policies.\par \par \uc2 We have a saying -- if it quacks like a duck, if it walks like a duck and has webbed feet like a duck, in this case, it's a Democrat. And that's what Evan McMullin is.\par \par \uc2 Utahans don't want more of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's policies, policies that are costing them dearly. The average Utah family shelling out an additional $949 a month every month just for what they need to live on because of Biden's inflation.\par \par \uc2 Evan McMullin supports \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his policies. He would be a reliable Democratic vote as he is the Democratic candidate in this race.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We, as I said, have invited him. We'd love to hear from him, as well.\par \par \uc2 OK. You were on FOX News days ago. We played this clip, reaching out to your fellow senator, \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 , also a Republican, of course, there in Utah for his endorsement.\par \par \uc2 Here's a little bit of the reaction. From "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ", their headline is: Romney aides irked by Lee plea for support, highlighting GOP divide in Utah.\par \par \uc2 And here's what McMullin says.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCMULLIN: I think it's shameful what I saw this week from Senator Lee attacking our other senator. We need two senators who will work together.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. You didn't endorse the late Senator \uc2 Orrin Hatch\uc2  when he ran for reelection. You didn't endorse Senator Romney in his primary.\par \par \uc2 So, why should you get his endorsement now?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Well, first of all, I have always supported nominees for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  in my party in the general election, always. And in this circumstance, I'm pleased that I've got the endorsement of all 48 of my other Republican colleagues in the Senate. Even \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  and \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2 , we often disagree on a number of issues, and yet, \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  and \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2  and every other Republican senator have endorsed me. I'm grateful for their support.\par \par \uc2 Look, we're going to win this, but we need help. We need help explaining and we need help spreading the word that Evan McMullin is the Democrats' candidate in this race. And I encourage your voters and you viewers who want to vote in Utah or support my race, go to leeforsenate.com. We could use their help.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. If you win the Senate back and the House back as Republicans, the president has a warning, inflation sits at 40-year high. He says his policies need time to kick in. He is celebrating what they had passed and legislation that's gotten through the Hill.\par \par \uc2 He had this warning, though, Friday, for voters if they vote for the GOP.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: They will crash the economy next year by threatening full faith and credit for the United States, for the first time in our history putting the United States in default unless, unless we yield to their demand to cut Social Security and Medicare.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He says you all are just a "no" vote. You want to get rid of caps on prescriptions. You heard him talking about entitlements. He says you're about giving billionaires tax cuts.\par \par \uc2 So, the party of no, or does the GOP have a plan for tackling inflation?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Look, that as usual is an information-free, truth-free statement by President Biden.\par \par \uc2 The fact of the matter is his policies have made our economy worse. We brought in over $4 trillion into the federal government's coffers last year. We spent $7 trillion. That is literally what causes inflation.\par \par \uc2 Milton Friedman says there's only one cause for inflation in America, and that's overspending by Congress. Right now, we've got a Democratic Congress that rubberstamps everything \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  does and he wants more spending that results in inflation. Evan McMullin would support that. He would be part of the Democratic establishment and that is why Utahans are going to vote for me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, he also has run an ad that talks about Ukraine and Russia. He says you voted against Russian sanctions. You voted against aid for Ukraine. And that you're the only member of Utah's congressional delegation not blacklisted by \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  himself.\par \par \uc2 So, can you explain the vote and tell us what is your position on Putin and on his invasion of Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Look, I'm a strong believer in the fact that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is an evil man who needs to be stopped. I voted for the first Ukraine package. I voted against the second Ukraine package, along with package of sanctions a few years ago, not because of underlying issue or because in the affinity for him, but rather they were packaged with a number of progressive liberal policies that I object to.\par \par \uc2 For Evan McMullin to suggest otherwise is absolute farce.\par \par \uc2 As to why I'm not on black list for Russia, I don't know. But I look forward to being put on such a blacklist. They should put me on one, because I can't stand \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. We'll see if you end up there.\par \par \uc2 I want to talk about the issue of abortion. A couple of things here. First of all, would you sign to the 15-week nationwide ban that Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  is backing? After Republicans for years have said that this should be solely a state issue, most of them.\par \par \uc2 And also, the leak of that draft opinion from the Supreme Court, you clerked over there. Are you concerned about whether or not we're ever going to get an answer about that leak?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Yeah. Right. As for the leak, I'm troubled that we haven't gotten an answer. I believe they could have and should have gotten an answer right away. It baffles me to why they haven't figured this out.\par \par \uc2 I know some things are more difficult to investigate than others. I am surprised and it worries me, quite frankly, that we've gone this long and that suggests we may not get an answer.\par \par \uc2 As to Senator Graham's bill, I have immense respect for \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 . He's a dear friend. I can't sign to -- to it. Let me explain why.\par \par \uc2 We've been saying for nearly 50 years that this is not a federal issue. It should be primarily a state issue except in so far as we're dealing with federal funding and things that are distinctively part of Congress' role. So, now, that Roe has been overturned correctly, we can't suddenly make it a federal issue.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, the best way to save the most babies is to allow states, each state to protect babies in the way they deem most appropriate for their state.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. By the way, this is the toughest question. I know you're a big U2 fan. I know you've seen them on tour.\par \par \uc2 But Bono is kind of doing a solo thing right now. He's got a book tour, but he said it's music, too. Do you support Bono solo? Will you go see him?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Sure. I haven't listened to any of his new music that is solo. It's definitely worth investigating. If it's anywhere near as good as U2, yeah, sure, I would. I usually like the band as a whole.\par \par \uc2 Look, Van Halen wasn't as good when they got rid of David Lee Roth. And I'm not sure U2 will be as good when it's just Bono. But I'll check it out.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Listen, I'm with you, David Lee Roth versus Sammy Hagar. I'm sorry, folks, that's just my position.\par \par \uc2 We're going to leave there. Senator --\par \par \uc2 LEE: Sammy Hagar is fine by himself. But with Van Halen, it didn't work.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The magic was with David Lee Roth.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Senator, thank you for joining us this Sunday morning.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, we're going to bring in our Sunday group. We'll show the latest FOX News power rankings.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCAIL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 FOX News is out with its new power rankings, so I'm going to walk you through our latest midterm election projections.\par \par \uc2 Let's start in the House where Democrats right now hold 220 seats, a clear but slim majority. Republicans at 212.\par \par \uc2 But take a look, our power rankings forecast, Republicans winning around 232 seats, a 15-seat majority.\par \par \uc2 It is much more of a toss-up for control of the upper chamber. Right now, the Senate, 50/50, with Vice President Harris as that tiebreaking vote.\par \par \uc2 The rankings project that Republicans hold 49 of those, Democrats 47. That means those four races, those toss-ups, they make the debates all the more important.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): First thing we have to start doing is stop spending that kind of money. The Democrats were warned by \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 , if you do this, you're going to fire up inflation.\par \par \uc2 REP. VAL DEMINGS (D-FL): Of course, the senator who has never run anything at all but his mouth would know nothing about helping people and being there for people when they are in trouble\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It is time for our Sunday group. "Wall Street Journal" White House reporter Catherine Lucey, former Bush White House advisor Karl Rove and former -- well, current FOX News analyst Juan Williams, he's still here, and FOX News senior national correspondent Rich Edson.\par \par \uc2 Sorry about that, guys. I gotten a little off of the prompter. Good to see all of you today.\par \par \uc2 All right. Let's start here. This is interesting and it's got a lot of headline attention. Over in Slate they're writing about this. What to make of the poll that showed independent women swinging Republican. The poll found that women who identified as independent voters now back Republicans by 18 points when in September, they favored Democrats by 14 points.\par \par \uc2 Karl, you watched a lot of campaign and races. Is it possible there's really a 32-point swing?\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FORMER BUSH WHITE HOUSE ADVISER: No. Look, there is a swing, but remember, the sample size, we're going to get very technical here, the sample size of independent women is very small and the plus or minus on that is 20 points. So, there is probably a swing, but it's probably not 32 points.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I mean, that would be a big one.\par \par \uc2 All right. Also from that "New York Times"/Siena polling, "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " writes about this. They say in that polling, there's a variety of data that suggests the electorate's attention is shifting back to issues where Republicans are stronger, they're in stronger ground in public economy like the economy, inflation, crime and immigration. Juan, we've seen that in a number of polls. How concerned are you?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, you know, over the summer, Democrats message was helped by the news, Shannon. I mean, you think about it, gas prices down, the Supreme Court -- the Republican majority in the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, and then you had the January 6 committee in primetime making the case that President Trump was involved in an insurrection against the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But in September, gas prices went up and I think what we've seen is that stirred more concern about the economy and in this campaign, Democrat -- Republican candidates put Halloween fright wig on the economy and it's working. That's the dominant message, dominant concern among voters.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But do you not think the economy is frightening when you look at over eight points, too, in the last reading for inflation?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think it's a real concern, but I think in terms of the --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Even the White House concedes that?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yeah, I think it's a real concern, I just said that, but I think that what now, you have to think about is the fact that Democrats remain angry over abortion rights issues, Democrats are still outraged by Trump's lies and the insurrection, the threat to ban books, divide over race in society. But since civil war, I think there'd been 40 midterm election and only three has the party in power gained seat.\par \par \uc2 So, the reality is, we've got a jump ball election right now, you know? But it's no red wave, but it's not where Democrats were at the start of the summer.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, economy scary but not fright wig scary.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Just to be -- clarification.\par \par \uc2 OK. So, let's talk about the Senate. I was ahead of myself last week. They're going to have the Senate debate this week in Pennsylvania, Fetterman and Oz, and here's what "The Philadelphia Inquirer". They quote Republican strategist Matt Beynon saying, I'd argue this debate probably means more to this race than it has for any Senates race for the last 30 years.\par \par \uc2 Rich, I'm popping my popcorn. You've covered this race quite a bit. Do you think this debate will flip votes either way?\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, you've had \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  now for months. He's done TV interviews. He's done campaign events. This is going to be the first and only time that he's going to have a debate with his opponent.\par \par \uc2 And there are questions about his health. There are questions as to how this is going to play. And there are questions about how Dr. Oz is going to respond to all of that. There are concerns among some Republicans that perhaps he might be seen as too harsh about the health issue.\par \par \uc2 So, the stroke issue is a major. There's going to be this teleprompter. It's not going to look like a natural or a normal debate because of his health challenges. It is just another issue in a race that is one of the more unique races I've ever seen covering.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you've got the national issues that are playing in Pennsylvania. You have a Republican trying to hold the seat, a lieutenant governor against a novice politician, and then you throw a stroke on top of it. It's incredible.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right, and that split ticket issue where the Democrat seems to be dominating in the gubernatorial race, but this one has really tightened up, the Senate race.\par \par \uc2 OK. There are a lot of interesting gubernatorial races as well. So, let's talk about that. By the way, \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  is going to be on "Special Report" on Monday to talk about the New York race where things have tightened up.\par \par \uc2 Over in Arizona, they're not, Catherine, apparently having debates there. The headline is, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , "The Arizona Republic" running away from a debate with Kari Lake is the debacle that just won't die. They say, while Lake revs up an already energized base, Hobbs is often seemed invisible to the independents and moderate Republican she will need if she wants to win this election. She says the debate would be a waste of her time.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE LUCEY, THE WALL STREET JOURNAL: Yeah. I mean, this is a very tight race, as you know. This is a state that President Biden won by a really narrow margin and the debate -- the lack of debate has now become a narrative in the race, right? So, Hobbs is saying this would not be a good use of her time. She claims it would be a circus, that this wouldn't be helpful.\par \par \uc2 Btu she's now facing I think criticism and I think concerns from voters. And voters in all these races, and including in Pennsylvania, when one candidate had a stroke, expect to see their candidates, expect to seem facing questions -- expect to see them facing each other. And so, she is now spending time defending this decision as opposed to talking about policies or proposals in the race.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And, Carl, what do you make of this Arizona race? Because Kari Lake is surging in most of the polls, in that gubernatorial race, and where there is a very tight Senate race. Does she pull out? And does she pull Blake Masters along with her on the Senate side?\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Well, I wouldn't use the word surging. She's moving ahead, but the Democrats have nominated a particularly inept candidate in \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  who has run a dreadful campaign and this latest decision not to debate is a big mistake.\par \par \uc2 And yes, if Kari Lake wins by sufficient margin, she might just bring Blake Masters in on her coattails. Same thing in Georgia, if Kemp wins by a 5, or 6 or 7-point margin, he may just well bring \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in on his coattails. And in Nevada, it is clear that Sheriff Lombardo, sheriff of Clark County, Las Vegas, is helping \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  who's from the other end of the state, Waxhaw County, Reno, in his race.\par \par \uc2 So, and then we got this weird dynamic in Pennsylvania, where we literally have, you know, \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  and Mehmet Oz being seen as the more centrist candidates and the Republican nominee for governor and the Democratic nominee for the Senate being seen as extremist, outside of the mainstream.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Juan, what of these tight Senate races are you watching that you think Democrats will hold on to or multiple, if you do?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, I think that they will win in Arizona. I think, you know, just picking up on what Karl was talking about, I think you're going to see a large number of split tickets, split votes and I think people are writing about that.\par \par \uc2 I think that if you're looking for interesting races even beyond Washington, I looked at that race in Oklahoma for governor, \uc2 Kevin Stitt\uc2  and Joy Hofmeister and to me, this idea that a Democrat could win in a red state, like Oklahoma, I don't think they've had a statewide Democrats since '06, it's pretty amazing.\par \par \uc2 But, again, I think it reflects the idea that, you know what, we've got an election where turnout is key. You got to get your people to the polls and right now, what we've seen in Georgia for example with early voting is big turnout. Both sides are highly energized in that race by the way.\par \par \uc2 In the Oklahoma race, he was saying, hey, crime is out of control, this is another messaging. And she said, no, actually crime is higher here in Oklahoma than it is in New York or L.A. He said, no, it can't be, but it turns she was right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, in that, that is one of the issues that has definitely bubbled up in our polling and all across this campaign.\par \par \uc2 Catherine, quickly, do we think we'll see the president out more in some of these races?\par \par \uc2 LUCEY: Yeah, he's been out quite a bit already. He was -- he's been in Pennsylvania. He's going back to Pennsylvania. We know he's going back to Florida. He did a swing through the West Coast. He did a stump with Senator Bennet, who is running for re-election there.\par \par \uc2 He is going to be out more. He has not been doing big campaign rallies. He has been doing more, you know, issue events, policy events and a lot of fundraising on the trail. But we will expect to see him do more of that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: In very specific states.\par \par \uc2 OK. Panel, stick around, we're going to take a quick break here.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Texas Republican candidates in a historically blue region near the border are now raking in more money than Democrats ahead of Election Day. We're going to sit down with Democrat incumbent in this area, \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , who is fighting to keep his seat there and we'll go live to the border itself for a look at what's happening on the ground there. That's coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: There's growing frustration along the U.S.-Mexico border where officials are struggle to handle the number of arrivals. It is an issue that is spilling out into the campaign trail and also pitting border Democrats against the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 Let's turn to Bill Melugin, live in Eagle Pass, Texas, where he's been reporting on the border crisis since Biden's second month in office.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Well, migrants continue crossing here in massive numbers, really from all around the world. But we are starting to notice a major drop in the number of Venezuelans showing up. That's a result of a brand-new DHS policy which expels Venezuelans if they are caught crossing illegally in between ports of entry.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: It's before sunrise in Eagle Pass, Texas, where exclusive images from a Fox News thermal drone reveal a huge group of hundreds of migrants crossing illegally into the U.S., a large majority of them single, adult men. A scene that plays out here daily.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the week, an even bigger group crossed illegally in the same spot. More than 450 migrants all at once. In the last two weeks alone, Border Patrol says this Del Rio sector has seen nearly 18,000 illegal crossings and more than 6,000 known got-a-ways.\par \par \uc2 This week, "The New York Post" reported the White House asked El Paso's Democrat mayor to not declare a state of emergency over the migrant crisis there in an effort to avoid bad optics, something the mayor now denies, though he admitted to it in a city council meeting last month.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR OSCAR LEESER (D), EL PASO, TEXAS: And the White House has asked at this point for us not to do that.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: The white House says that never happened, telling Fox in part, quote, we did not make any such request, but we did make clear that the federal government stands ready to support the city of El Paso.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And, Shannon, speaking of the federal government, they conducted a very blatant Friday night news drop this past Friday. At 11 p.m. Friday night, they finally released the September border numbers. And those numbers are historic. They show that in September there were more than 227,000 migrant encounters. That is the highest number for a September in DHS history, and it's not even close. The numbers also show fiscal year 2022 ended with nearly 2.4 million migrant encounters. That is the highest fiscal year ever recorded and it does not count the known got-a-ways.\par \par \uc2 We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Bill Melugin, live from the U.S./Mexico border. Bill, thank you to you and your team down there.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now from San Antonio, Democratic Texas Congressman \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , whose district includes Laredo, along the border.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, welcome back to FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 HENRY CUELLAR\uc2  (D-TX): Thank you. And good morning to you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so you heard the numbers there. They came after 11 p.m. here in Washington on a Friday night. They are record breaking. I know you've talked with this administration repeatedly about the issue at the border, trying to get some help.\par \par \uc2 Here's what the vice president said last month.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have a secure border in that that is a priority for any nation, including ours, and our administration.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Congressman, based on your conversations with the administration, first, do you think the secure - the border is secure? And, secondly, do you think it is a priority for this White House?\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: You know, first of all, no, the border is not secure. When you have 1.7 million individuals last year, and now another 2.7, that's over 4.5 million individuals encounter at the border, plus the - if you add the getaways, that's going to be over 5 million individuals in just two years. No, the border is secure - it's not secured and we've got to make sure that we have repercussions there.\par \par \uc2 Look, I - I've talked to the administration a week before the December - this was in December of 2020 and I started telling the transition team in December of 2020, this is what's going to be happening. You all have to be ready. And if we don't have repercussion - we can be compassionate at the border, but you've got to enforce the law. And if you don't return people, then this is what you're going to have.\par \par \uc2 I saw the numbers from the Venezuelans that have dropped out. I - I actually want them to do that to every country. If we do that to every country, the numbers will go down. We will be able to control our borders. And that's important to us that live along the border.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, there's been a lot of attention play -- paid to El Paso. You heard Bill's reporting there. They have been bussing migrants to other places, like New York, which has since declared an emergency because of the influx they've gotten.\par \par \uc2 El Paso reportedly not going to continue the bussing because there's reporting out there that the feds are not going to send migrants to El Paso anymore, at least not in the numbers that they were.\par \par \uc2 Instead it appears they're using something that they had criticized from the Trump administration as cruel and it's actually a policy they're currently fighting in court, Title 42. AP says this, with the increasing number of Venezuelans arriving at the U.S./Mexico border as the November 8th election nears, Biden has turned to an unlikely source for a solution, his predecessor's playbook.\par \par \uc2 So, is it your impression that this administration is saying one thing publicly but doing things differently when it comes to actually handling the reality of what they're seeing at the border?\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: I think so. You know, with all due respect to the administration, you know, they said they want to get rid of Title 42, but Title 42 is used more than Title 8 to return people back. And if you look at what they're doing with the Venezuelans, doesn't that sound familiar, like the MPP, that is you wait outside before you come in, or you go through a port of entry. And notice that it's the same thing in many ways, it's the same concept. And I've always said that, look, we can spend all the defense on the one yard line called the U.S./Mexico border, but if we don't go to their 20- yard line, work with Guatemala, work with Mexico and - and get them engaged so they can stop those folks from coming in, then they're just going to be at our door steps and we've got to make sure that we understand the importance of securing our border.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Want to put up some polling that shows where voters think Republicans have the advantage in handling issues. They give a 21-point advantage to them on border security. So, your opponent, Cassy Garcia, says that you say the right thing when it comes to the border, but she says you can't point to anything concrete you've done.\par \par \uc2 Your response?\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: Well, you know, she's not from my district, so she probably doesn't understand what I've been doing in my district. I sit as the vice chair of Homeland Security. I'll be moving up. In the Appropriates, I - I have money there for more border patrol agents, money for their mental health, money - money for child care, a pay raise for Border Patrol. I work very well with her husband, Mr. Owens (ph), which is a Border Patrol chief in Del Rio. He's a friend of mine. He invited me to be the speaker when we had a graduation of the Border Patrol agents in New Mexico, in Articia (ph). So people know.\par \par \uc2 In fact, tomorrow, I'm announcing a $165 million Border Patrol checkpoint in Laredo. And so I can go on and on and on.\par \par \uc2 So, she's not from the district. So she probably doesn't know what I've been doing. But I can tell you, I've been supporting the men and women in green, and the men and women in blue, which are the ones at the ports of entry.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, Shannon, if you want to stop drugs, drugs are coming in -- over 90 percent of their drugs come in through ports of entry. So, we've got to support the men and women in green and the men and women in blue also.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, they've got very difficult job. That -- that poll, I want to put that up gain. It also, on the issue of the economy, it gives, on inflation, Republicans a 13-point advantage on how they're handling that.\par \par \uc2 Now, this week, Democratic Senator \uc2 Mark Warner\uc2  said that there was too much spending in the American Rescue Plan. One of the top Democrats over on the House side with you, James Clyburn, said this week that of the trillions in new spending, quote, all of us knew that it would drive up prices.\par \par \uc2 Does he speak for you in that, do you have any regrets on voting for those trillions in new spending?\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: Well, look, you know, I - I want to make sure that people understand, if you look at the deficit spending under the prior administration compared to the one now, there actually was more spending on the prior administration.\par \par \uc2 But I would say this, to help reduce the prices that we have right now, we got to focus on one of the biggest driver, and that's oil and gas. I support the oil and gas industry because, in - in - in my place, my district, there is 40,000 jobs. We lower the prices of - of oil and gas. I was the one that passed -- helped pass the ban on oil exports. So, we created more jobs here. We want to see domestic drilling here in the United States. You lower the price of petroleum, it lowers the price of fertilizer, it lowers the price of so many things that has an impact on our goods.\par \par \uc2 So, that's what I've been focused on, what I can control. And that is having domestic drilling here in the state of Texas or in the United States.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So many of the positions that you take, Republicans would say you should come join them and their party because so many of them are more aligned with them. You're often described as only pro-life Democrat in the House. David Wasserman, a top election forecaster over at Cook Political Report says this about you. He is now pretty much alone as a conservative Democrat in the House.\par \par \uc2 Do you worry that people who hold your positions are being squeezed out of the Democratic Party? Is there a place for you?\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: You know - you know, surely I'm a Democrat. I'm a conservative Democrat. I work in a very bipartisan way. I work with Senator \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  on so many issues, on the border issues. We work together. And I think every party has the extreme. We have the extreme left. We have the extreme right. But some of us are not sent up to Washington to go make political statements. We're sent to Washington to do the hard job, and that is govern. And you've got to work with Democrats and Republicans like my friend Tony Gonzales (ph). We sit on Appropriations and we work together. Michael McCall is another person that we work together.\par \par \uc2 So, there's ways of working together and I think there is definitely room for a conservative, moderate Democrat like myself.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Congressman, you are in a close race there. We're all watching these House districts along the border. Your opponent, Cassy Garcia, we mentioned, she's brought up the \uc2 FBI\uc2  raid at your home back in January. Is there anything you can say to clarify or explain that investigation or what's going on there?\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: Yes. You know, definitely there is -- I'm not the target of the investigation. That is what my attorney has told me. And we - we're going to go ahead and just move and focus on the election.\par \par \uc2 What I want to know is what she wants to do? You know, she's focusing on negative attacks. And I think people are sick and tired of the negative attacks. What they want to see is, this is a market of ideas. This is what I want to do. This is what she wants to do. Let the people decide.\par \par \uc2 And I will tell you one thing, if you look at the numbers in the past, I usually do about 10 percent better than most state and national Democrats that run in my district. I'll tell you why, because I get independent voters that come and support me and I get a lot of the moderate Republicans. And that's what I'm going to do. I'm going to get independent voters. I'm going to get moderate Republicans. A lot of cross over, along with the Democrats, and we're going to win this race.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Congressman, just a little more than two weeks to go. Yours is one to watch. We thank you for making time for u this morning. Great to speak with you.\par \par \uc2 CUELLAR: Thank you so much. And I invite you to come to Texas.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. I will come down shortly. It's a great place.\par \par \uc2 All right, up next, we're going to bring back the panel to discuss some of the biggest issues that parents are talking about ahead of the midterms.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's mega MAGA trickledown. Mega MAGA trickledown. The kind of policies that have failed the country before and will fail it again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That is President Biden this week introducing yet another attack on Republican economic policies as Democrats face headwind on issues like inflation and the border.\par \par \uc2 We're going to try to get to all of those.\par \par \uc2 Rich, I want to start with you, though, about something else that got headlines this week.\par \par \uc2 The CDC voting to recommend the Covid-19 vaccine be in the schedule of recommended vaccines for kids. A lot of parents - and, by the way, this comes on the same weekend that we get news that the CDC director, vaxed and boosted, now has Covid. So it's raising a lot of eyebrows. Townhall.com says this, a number of Republican governors and gubernatorial candidates are vowing they will not issue mandates requiring the shot. So, it's showing up on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: It's definitely showing up on the campaign trail. And this is an issue that Republicans want to talk about. They want to talk about the shutdowns. They want to talk about what's happened in schools over Covid.\par \par \uc2 But you have to remember, this is a recommendation from the CDC. And candidates on both sides are going to have to answer this because if they want to be leaders in their states and in their - in - in their communities, they're the ones who are going to have to decide whether this recommendation of medical experts from the CDC is something that they want for their schools.\par \par \uc2 So, it is an issue. It's something Republicans want to talk about. But t should be an issue because they're the ones who are going to have to decide on it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They are. OK, let's talk about the economy. We -- you heard there from the president about how well they've done. He says an historic drop in the deficit, $1.4 million - trillion, excuse me. Million would be fun. We're talking trillions out here in Washington.\par \par \uc2 "The Wall Street Journal" over there says it's president Rumpelstiltskin trying to spin economic straw into political gold. They say if Mr. Biden wants to play Rumpelstiltskin in the classic fairy tale, spinning straw into gold, he might recall that it had an unhappy ending for the spinner, Juan.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: I like that, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Well, I mean, he's -- no president drove up the deficit more than Trump, OK. So, in that sense, yes, he's right. But it's also the case that Republicans are succeeding on the campaign trail, as we said before, by talking about the economy, the number one concern of voters.\par \par \uc2 Now, the thing about this is, they tie inflation directly to Biden and Biden spending. But what Democrats are pointing out is that inflation is global. It's not just here in the United States. In fact, inflation here is better than it is in western Europe. The dollar remains strong while the euro and the pound are weak. So, the U.S. economy, with low unemployment, remains the best in the world. But it's not great and that's why it's a concern of voters.\par \par \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 , out this week as the British prime minister.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Look (ph) -\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: What was she doing? She was pursuing Trump-type policies that led to hyperinflation in Britain. So I think there are a lot of people who think the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is trying to tamp down inflation. If you go back to tax cuts for the rich under - like the Trump policies, you're going to send inflation out of the roof in the United States.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and, Catherine, he's getting criticism, though, on this announcement of more oil out of the Strategic Petroleum Reserve just in time for the midterms.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE LUCEY, "THE WALL STREET JOURNAL": Yes, the president announced the additional release this week. He's trying to work on this problem. He says, you know, gas prices are going back up. He's trying to counter the fact that there were cuts made by \uc2 OPEC\uc2  and that there's other international pressures. But certainly the timing is that we are coming barreling down to the midterms and this is the top inflation, high prices and gas are the top issues for voters and they have been for a long time.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, they stay at the top of the poll.\par \par \uc2 All right, the president, I want to play a bit of - when he was asked Friday, at least this aired Friday, about whether or not he's going to run for re-election.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have not made that formal decision, but it's my intention. My intention to run again. And we have time to make that decision.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Dr. Biden is for it? Mr. President?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Dr. Biden thinks that -- my wife thinks that -- that I -- that - that we're - that we're doing something very important. And that I shouldn't walk away from it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Karl, that's getting a lot of attention for reasons not that the White House would like it to.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Look, he's not going to be the nominee of the Democratic Party in 2024. The party - that party is not going to nominate an 82-year-old who was older his first day in office than they - any president on the last day of their office, and who is clearly struggling. And the country's going to demand a generational shift and it's going to start with no 82-year-old is going to be the Democratic nominee, and I suspect no 78 year old is going to be the Republican nominee.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You - oh, you heard it here. Karl is prognosticating.\par \par \uc2 All right, predictions, we'll see what comes true.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, panel. See you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we will bring in \uc2 Fox Business Network\uc2  host \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  on recession fears and also on the special honor he just received.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: My next guest has had the ear of several presidents and is a veteran host of a roster of financial news programs including "Kudlow" on the \uc2 Fox Business Network\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  was just awarded the National Review Institute's \uc2 William F. Buckley Jr\uc2 . Prize for leadership in political thought. During his acceptance speech at the Ronald Reagan Presidential Library, he talked about his life.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LARRY KUDLOW\uc2 , HOST OF "KUDLOW" ON FOX BUSINESS NETWORK": My life has had its twists and turns. Doors have opened. Doors have closed. I've had some fabulous experiences.\par \par \uc2 I always believed it was God's handywork, not mine. But when the opportunities came, I gave them my best shot.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Joining us now, \uc2 Fox Business Network\uc2  host \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome back. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 LARRY KUDLOW\uc2 , HOST OF "KUDLOW" ON FOX BUSINESS NETWORK: Thank you, Shannon. Appreciate it very much.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, we're going to talk more about your award, but first I want to do some news headlines with you.\par \par \uc2 The president talked about the economy and the outlook on Friday. He says his policies just need a little bit more time to work. He's doing the best with the challenges that the Trump administration left him. Here's some of what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: But look at what I've inherited and what I've done, and look at what they're offering (ph). They want to double down on the Trump tax cuts for the wealthy, make them permanent because they're going to expire in 2025. They want to send jobs overseas where big corporations can, in fact, pay lower wages and increase their profit.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, he says the previous administration, that includes you, left him a broken economy, Larry.\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Yes, you know, I've never understood these crazy assertions. Utter fraud. I mean just to look at the numbers, Shannon. When \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was inaugurated, the economy was growing at about 6.5 percent. The inflation rate was barely above 1 percent. And in the course of the next 12 to 15 months, 18 months, he took that economic boom and essentially turned it into a recession because of all the inflationary federal spending and regulating and the war against fossil fuels, which has caused, you know, 10 percent inflation.\par \par \uc2 The soft underbelly of the Biden economy, almost from day one, has been a continuous decline in the working folks wages. Real wages have fallen for 18 straight months year on year basis.\par \par \uc2 The inflation tax is killing the economy. We're on the front end of a recession. All these business titans, so to speak, bankers, \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 , \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2 , \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 , people like that, are now predicting recession next year. I mean it's a - it's just part of the fraudulent, almost like white is black, black is white.\par \par \uc2 He will not tell the truth about the economy. He inherited a boom. He turned it into a bust. We're in a lot of trouble now. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is raising interest rates. Stock markets are falling we are headed for a very deep recession. And these progressive policies have utterly failed. This radical, you know, war against fossil fuels, I mean, it just doesn't make any sense to watch him try to turn the truth around. And I think it's really hurt him. I mean I think it's hurt - there's no confidence in him. There's no confidence in his policies. And right now there's no confidence in the economy. And that's why the calvary is coming. That's why we're going to see a tremendous turnover in Congress in the next couple weeks in the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And we'll see what they do if Republicans do regain power in the House and/or Senate.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, I want to talk about your prize, the speech that you gave the other night. You talked about the importance of faith in public life. I know you and I have had conversations about how it led you though some of the storms in your life. But, you know, a lot of recent polling shows that Americans are increasingly walking away from the idea of faith as being integral to their life. What's your message on that front?\par \par \uc2 KUDLOW: Yes, you know, it's a disturbing thing. You and I have talked about this.\par \par \uc2 Look, Shannon, going back many years, almost 30 years ago, in my worst moments, I looked for God, I reached for God, I asked for his help, and I've been doing so for the last three decades.\par \par \uc2 I came to faith. And I want to say, by the way, with respect to \uc2 Bill Buckley\uc2 , who was a - you know, one of the great modern fathers of conservative, you know, Bill, in addition to being a free market capitalist, which of course I've always followed, Bill was a man of great faith. I mean Bill was a high church catholic and believed that religion had to play an important part in our lives, in our culture, in our families, in our communities. And, you know, there's only one \uc2 Bill Buckley\uc2 . I'm not \uc2 Bill Buckley\uc2 . But I sure have tried to follow that model.\par \par \uc2 And, again, Shannon, I know it's a Sunday show and we don't have time to go through a lot of stuff, but I think some of these people on the radical left, who want to take religion, I'm not making any specific religion, but just take religion, take God out of our schools, out of our work, out of our families, out of our lives, I think it's a huge mistake. I think it damages the culture. And I hope that people think twice about that in the future.\par \par \uc2 It helped me in my personal life and I think it can help everybody in their lives.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, it's helped me in mine, too. And I know that this country is a beautiful place of freedom where you can have a religion or none at all and we celebrate that.\par \par \uc2 Larry, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 That's it for Sunday morning. Thanks for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Have a great week. We will see you next Fox News Sunday.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020221023eian00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Time For Appeasement Is Over; Even If You Hate Trump The Left Still Hates...\b0\par\par\uc2 Laura Ingraham, Lauren Blanchard, Kevin Corke \par \uc2 8,141 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 The Time For Appeasement Is Over; Even If You Hate Trump The Left Still Hates You; Even Pence Can't Be Saved From Rabid Leftist Mobs; Campaigning For GOP Put Target On Youngkin Back; The Left Doesn't Have An Answer For Kari Lake; Opponent \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  Is Running Scared; Left Panics As Kari Lake's Support Surges; Kari Lake Fearless In Race For Arizona Governor; Tudor Dixon Closes Gap In Michigan Governor's Race; Whitmer's Solution To Declining Population: Amnesty For Illegals And Abortion For All!; Witless Whitmer Running Scared In Governor's Race; RCP: Wisconsin & Nevada Gov Races Shift To GOP; Dems Panic As Their Midterm Prospects Darken; Mover Over Dem "Stars", Here Comes The GOP; Disaster For Dems: A Red Wave Is Coming; Republicans Making Inroads In Blue States; Electric Bus In Connecticut Catches Fire; \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2  Interviewed About Passing Of Queen Elizabeth; Five Women At Daycare Charged With Child Abuse; Senate Minority leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  Criticized By Alaskan Republican \par \par \uc2 Party For His PAC Contributing Money To Incumbent \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2 's Campaign Against Primary Challenger \uc2 Kelly Tshibaka\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: You have to tune in and find out who they are. We hope you'll join us.\par \par \uc2 But, unfortunately, that's all the time we have left this evening. Thank you for making this show possible. In the meantime, let not your heart be troubled. Have a great weekend. Laura's next. We'll see you from Arizona on Monday. Have a good weekend.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: I'm Laura Ingraham. This is THE INGRAHAM ANGLE from Washington tonight. If you're a Republican, and you're a candidate for office, or you're already in elected office, you have two choices in the world. Number one, you can join the hard Left or number two, you can be attacked by the hard Left. That's it. Those are the only options.\par \par \uc2 Now, anyone who thinks that they're going to be protected because they get invited on CNN a lot or they're known as anti-Trump, you're deluding yourself. The radicals on the Left demand total obedience or sorry allyship. In if not, they'll try to drive you from public life. These tactics go along with the Left's overriding goal, of course, which is the destruction of the nuclear family and America's Judeo Christian tradition.\par \par \uc2 Take what the Left has just done to Never Trumper Senator \uc2 Ben Sasse\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Hey hey, hey ho, \uc2 Ben Sasse\uc2  has to go!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, despite the fact that Sasse voted to convict former President Trump in his second impeachment trial, the Left mobilized to oppose his becoming the new president of the \uc2 University of Florida\uc2 . He's still in contention.\par \par \uc2 Now he spoke at a student forum on campus were about 300 protesters showed up and they called the senator homophobic and racist in between yelling from the audience, of course. Remember, this is not about Trump, they think all Republicans are evil. When \uc2 Ben Sasse\uc2  is considered to be too conservative -- \uc2 Ben Sasse\uc2 , that tells you a lot about academia. The inmates are truly running the asylum.\par \par \uc2 Then there's \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , even after obvious efforts -- his obvious efforts to distance himself from President Trump's since election night, and of course, remember, his portraying himself as the conservative kind of without drama. Well, none of this has bought him any goodwill when he stepped into the belly of the beast this week at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday night he spoke at an event that was hosted by the school's Institute of Politics and Public Service and also the Young America's Foundation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Hate has no home here!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now despite speaking about the need for a return to civility in our politics, Pence's appearance drew disgusting protests and a boring, but predictable staged walkout.\par \par \uc2 Republican nice guys finish last. Just ask \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 . Remember what they did to him? And we'd be remiss in not mentioning Glen Youngkin. Now he won the Virginia governorship last year by focusing on the schools. It was a really well run campaign. And he didn't have a lot of, you know, Trump like controversy.\par \par \uc2 Looking pretty folksy in that fleece vest, yeah, sure. But when he rolled up his sleeves to campaign for GOP gubernatorial candidates across America, the media made him a target. And when he traveled to Arizona to campaign for Kari Lake, he became enemy number one. Now Youngkin embraces Kari Lake in Arizona despite election denialism. Glenn Younkin had moderate Republicans fooled, where the headlines and there are lot more.\par \par \uc2 Well, there's a lesson here. The time for appeasement is over. And it's something that Kari Lake turned into a huge plus during her primary battle. And right now, she's offering kind of a masterclass in how to put the media back on its heels.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R) ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Let's talk about election deniers. Here's 150 examples of Democrats denying election results. This is from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's press secretary, reminder, \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  stole the gubernatorial election from Georgians and \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , Trump is an illegitimate president. Is she an election denier? So it's OK for Democrats to question elections, but it's not OK for Republicans. It's a crock of BS. Every one of you knows it. We have our freedom of speech and we're not going to relinquish it to a bunch of fake news propagandists.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, that was awesome. It was such a delight to watch. And she's going to be giving the Left heartburn for years to come. And we look forward to every minute.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Kari Lake Republican nominee for Arizona governor. Kari, first of all, you know, everyone knows that I wasn't on your bandwagon during the primary, for sure. But I'm watching you take on the media and the only other person who's done it like you is DeSantis and Trump, that's it. I mean, of every one I've seen out there. What put the fight in your belly to do what you're doing today, given all these headlines?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Oh my gosh! Thank you for having me on. I'm so glad that you're -- that you're having me on. I appreciate it so much, Laura. I'll tell you I worked on the inside of the media for nearly 30 years, so I understand, as they say, how the sausage is made. And I've turned the tables on them.\par \par \uc2 Every event we go to, we draw massive media crowd. And we are always reporting and recording them. And we show the people what the media is up to, because usually they ask the nasty question that's full of an agenda and narrative. And then they cut that part out and never show the people how bad they appear. So we're just actually flipping the tables on them and revealing who they really are.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Kari, I want to play your opponent, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 ' latest rationale for dodging you. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS (D) ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: For me this debate about debates is over. Kari Lake is so desperate for a debate because she is scared to sit down and have an in depth conversation, because it will highlight her level of inexperience, the lack of qualification to be governor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Kari, it's your inexperience and lack of qualification that makes you want to debate. That kind of doesn't make sense. But your reaction.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: They said the same thing about \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 . They said he's just an actor. He just read scripts. He used to be a Democrat, what does he know? And he went on to be truly -- probably the greatest governor of this country ever saw when he was governor of California and turn that state around. And boy, do we need another \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  in California right now.\par \par \uc2 So I don't -- you know, nobody's listening to her. She's the one who's afraid to take the debate stage. And I consider Laura, a debate to be a job interview. The people of Arizona, the voters of Arizona are the hiring manager, and we're showing up to present our case for what we want to do for Arizona.\par \par \uc2 I'm happy to do it. I've cleared my schedule three times now to do it. And we're going to show up Sunday for the final chance for Katie to grow a spine and join me. I kind of doubt she's going to do it, though.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah, another empty chair there. Kari, it looks like Hobbs is just involved in some kind of, you know, registration fiasco over 6,000 ballots that were incorrect, or mailed out, and supposedly they're correcting that. But she's the Secretary of State and she's talking about your experience, given what she just oversaw?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Everything I've done in my life, whether it was my first job -- one of my first jobs as a janitor all the way up into a journalist, I did and I excelled at, and I did with great work ethic and hard work. She's been incompetent in every role she's ever played, including, and most importantly, as Secretary of State.\par \par \uc2 Yes, 6,000 ballots -- the wrong ballots sent out in our primary election. She gave guidance to how many ballots needed to go out. And in Pinal County, one of our biggest counties, one hour into election day, they ran out of Republican only ballots. She's completely incompetent. The Arizona Supreme Court has called her that in their recent ruling.\par \par \uc2 And she's only showed up for work 19 days over the last six months. She's had the Secretary of State's office closed due to COVID.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh my god!\par \par \uc2 LAKE: As I'm saying, get your butt back to work, Katie. You know, nobody gets to show up 19 days in six months.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, my. OK. First of all, you told me something I didn't know. I love that when a guest tells me something you don't know. That is that's -- that is absolutely a disqualifier right there. Of all the things I've heard about her, that's one of the worst.\par \par \uc2 Kari, now we heard today, of course, that President Trump was subpoenaed to appear before January 6 Committee after the election. You know, they're trying to hold on to relevance as long as possible. Your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: I'm just amazed at how they're obsessed with President Trump. I get asked about him every day on the campaign trail. I think he's fantastic. And I'll take any question that deals with President Trump. And I always start with, don't we miss him, and don't we miss his policies, and don't we miss the economy under President Trump?\par \par \uc2 But I'll tell you what, I've been on the campaign trail, Laura, about 500 and, I think, eight days now, nobody ever asks about what that commission is up to. Nobody ever asks about Ukraine. Everyone's asking about our wide open border, drugs pouring in, illegal immigration.\par \par \uc2 They're asking about the education system that's brainwashing our children. They're concerned about the crime that's rising on the streets, and that's why they're voting Republican. Because Republicans and the new Republican Party have the common sense ideas to the problems that we're facing. The Left is pushing nonsense. We're pushing common sense.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: One thing will you do first, when you get settled into office, if you should win, this?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Even before I get settled, after I take the oath of office, in the first hour we're going to issue a declaration of invasion on our border and we're going to take back control of our border from the cartels that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has ceded control to. And we are going to secure the border, stop the fentanyl from pouring across and the people from being trafficked across our border.\par \par \uc2 And then we're going to get to work on a whole bunch of other things, including restoring honesty and faith in our elections, and turning our education system around. We want to start offering after 10th grade in high school, we want to offer kids the opportunity to get trade skill training, vocational training, and career certification in high school. So our kids are ready for the work -- to be in the workforce out there. We've got lots of jobs, we just need the people to fill them.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Kari, I can't wait to see how this all plays out. If we don't talk to you before election night, and we very well may talk to you before election night, I think you're going to win this. So best of luck out there. Keep fighting.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And in Michigan, another gubernatorial race and another rising GOP star. Now once down as much as 18 points, Republican Tudor Dixon has closed the gap to just two points in the latest poll.\par \par \uc2 Tudor Dixon joins me now. Tudor, what's happening? Now we've had you on a bunch of times. And the polls have not been with you for all those appearances. But now a change, what's happening?\par \par \uc2 TUDOR DIXON (R) MICHIGAN GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Yes, thank you for having me tonight, Laura. Well, it's very interesting in the State of Michigan, parents are rising up and saying they want education back. They want their students to have a chance. They want to go back to the basics in education -- reading, writing and math. And they're fighting back against this woke agenda in our schools. And so far, that's really changing the race.\par \par \uc2 But also today we were just talking to the Detroit Economic Forum, and we're hearing from businesses that are saying we just need to have support from government instead of having government attack us. Job creators want to expand in the state of Michigan. They're hearing \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  say she has helped them. She hasn't helped them. We've lost more small businesses than any other state. We also lost 82,000 jobs. They hear what she's saying, they know she's gaslighting them. And they're ready to change -- turn the page on this horrible dark time in Michigan's history and see Michigan thrive again.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now Tudor, here's what Whitmer thinks will -- I guess, help stop state's declining population, I don't know what?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D-MI): We also need to lure people to make their lives here in Michigan. We need immigration reform on the federal level. We enshrine reproductive rights into law here in Michigan. I want to go into Indiana and Ohio and start stealing headquarters and cultivating talent and bring them to Michigan where you can have full rights to make your own decisions about your health and your body.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Tudor, is making Michigan an abortion sanctuary state at the top of the voters' concerns?\par \par \uc2 DIXON: Absolutely not. What kind of economic development plan is that when you have a bunch of businesses in the audience right there saying what are you going to do to help us? That's what she's offering is abortion for an economic development?\par \par \uc2 I know what the businesses need. They want to have reduced regulation. They want to state government that comes around them as a partner. They want to make sure we have a solid infrastructure, something that she promised in 2018. And today, when she said that exact comment, she laughed about the roads in Michigan, and said I know, I know everybody's concerned about the roads.\par \par \uc2 Yes, because that was your number one promise in 2018. And you haven't delivered on that. In fact, none of the promises that she made in 2018, she's delivered on. This is the kind of silly rhetoric we're hearing from her on economic development. We have a real chance to take this race back. We are two points apart. We need folks to go to tudordixon.com. And support because we are going to get Michigan back and we're going to thrive. We'll be the leaders in the Midwest again.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You should be.\par \par \uc2 DIXON: We'll great education.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yeah. The thing that I would, you know, say to people out there watching from Michigan is, is this really the best you can be? I mean, why are you losing all this population to States like Georgia and Florida and Tennessee? Why? I mean, is that acceptable to the people in Michigan? It can't be. We know what the crime rate is doing. None of this can be acceptable.\par \par \uc2 So I think it's an easy calculation. Tudor, I have a very good feeling about where this race is going. No more lockdowns in Michigan. Tudor Dixon we'll be watching.\par \par \uc2 DIXON: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And it isn't just Michigan, RealClearPolitics is now projecting that the governor's races in Wisconsin and Nevada as well will be GOP pickups. But President Biden today was offering a different take.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's been back and forth, with them ahead us ahead, them ahead. Back and forth. And the polls have been all over the place. I think that we're going to see one more shift back to our side the closing days.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Joining me now is Tom Bevan, President and Co-Founder of RealClearPolitics. Tom, have you been seeing any data suggesting a swing back to Biden in the last two and a half weeks or so?\par \par \uc2 TOM BEVAN, PRESIDENT, REAL CLEAR POLITICS: No, not yet. It's certainly possible, but right now we're seeing all the movement is toward the Republican Party. And we're seeing not just -- we're seeing it in the generic congressional ballot, which is really a leading indicator for a lot of these state races that are going on and that has shifted.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have gained almost four points in the generic ballot, and just in the past month, Democrats have lost about seven tenths of a percentage point. So Republicans now have the biggest lead they've had in the generic ballot in the RealClearPolitics average since about June -- in middle of June. So it looks like it's a late breaking wave for the GOP. And we'll have to see if there's any data that comes into the contrary in the next 18 days.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Is there a Senate race that we should be looking at more seriously than perhaps we have in the past month or so?\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: Well, I mean, New Hampshire is certainly on the radar screen, but it is one where we do not have a lot of polling. There was a round of polls that were taken right after the contentious GOP primary that ended to place on September 13.\par \par \uc2 We haven't had a poll in New Hampshire in over two weeks now. And if the movement we've seen in some of these other Senate races is occurring in New Hampshire as well, and there's no reason to believe that it's not, the swing toward the GOP, that race could be very, very close. Much closer than the polls are showing. Because again, the po