 they want to hear that people are going to listen to them and actually go and fight.\par \par \uc2 I've had a lot of money thrown at me by really both sides of the aisle, and it hasn't been able to take me down, because I'm actually going out there and connecting with voters.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Man, they really wanted to stop you, both sides did. But I wonder why? What you're saying does not sound radical at all to my ears. It sounds very moderate and sensible, just respond to the basic concerns of voters. Why is that such a threat to so many people.\par \par \uc2 KENT: There's just a narrative right now from our establishment, from this regime of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but then also the entrenched political establishment that really wants the status quo. They want to fight endless wars. We're seeing that with Ukraine. They're willing to risk a nuclear war.\par \par \uc2 They also want our jobs shipped overseas and they love these open borders. They love the fact that there's essentially anarcho-tyranny in this country right now, and they don't want people who are going to think for themselves, and who have an idea of how the system works from the inside.\par \par \uc2 I worked in the military. I worked in the Intelligence Community. I know the games that these people are running, and I'm going to go to DC and stop them.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: You do know the games they are running, maybe that's it. You understand how the system works, but shouldn't they just say that instead of trying to call you some kind of extremist? Again, if you're an extremist, you probably wouldn't have been allowed to carry a gun in the military for 20 years, no?\par \par \uc2 KENT: Yes, I was good enough to go and fight and die in their wars. I was good enough to hold a high security clearance and to go saddle up for them time and time again. But the second you want to say, "Hey, now I'm calling the shots, I've paid my dues. I want to help run this country," that's when they come after you.\par \par \uc2 And that is the game. That's the way these people work and we're just not going to tolerate any more and I think the American people have had enough of that and they don't even listen to this tired establishment anymore.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, you don't have to name names, I'm just interested. You've gotten a fair amount of publicity in this congressional race. Do you think there are a number of other Republican candidates running in this cycle who share your views?\par \par \uc2 KENT: I really do, Tucker. I think this is going to be a wave here, not just for Republicans, but for America First Republicans who are outsiders. We don't need this. This isn't the thing that we've always wanted to do with our lives, but we're stepping up away from private life, away from service in the military, because we legitimately care and we want to serve our country.\par \par \uc2 I'm seeing that up and down the ballot here in the district and I'm seeing that throughout the country. So, I'm actually -- as bad as things are in our nation right now, I'm very inspired and excited to be a part of this new political realignment.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Man, I hope you're right. Your race is certainly exciting.\par \par \uc2 Joe Kent, thanks for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 KENT: Thank you very much, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Hogan Gidley worked in the last White House as Deputy Press Secretary. He is now with the \uc2 America First Policy Institute\uc2 . He just had an investigation that found nearly a hundred counties across 14 swing States fail to preserve voter data from the 2020 election and that is a violation of Federal law.\par \par \uc2 What's worse is in the few counties studied that did manage to keep their election data, there were discrepancies between the number of people voting and the number of ballots cast. Ooh, that sounds like fraud. Don't say it. You're undermining our democracy by pointing out it is not real.\par \par \uc2 In Miami-Dade County, for example, there was nearly a two percent discrepancy between the number of people who voted and the number of ballots cast. That translates into a difference of more than 15,000 votes in one county.\par \par \uc2 To give you an idea of how bad Miami-Dade's record keeping is, one of our producers who has not lived in Miami for 15 years just received a voter card to vote in Miami-Dade County in the upcoming midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Hogan Gidley joins us now to explain what else he found. This is an amazing story that, I guess, we're not allowed to talk about, we are going to anyway.\par \par \uc2 Hogan, thanks for coming on. What did you find?\par \par \uc2 HOGAN GIDLEY\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: Thank you so much, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 Look, the law they're violating is the Civil Rights Act of 1960, which requires the States to preserve and maintain these lists and voter data for 22 months after a Federal election. And of course, we did go into these 14 States. We looked at these top most populated counties, a hundred of them. Of the 100, only six managed to keep any data whatsoever, and it was egregious when we took a look at those numbers.\par \par \uc2 But it gets worse, because we also took a look at the numbers of voters who voted and compared that to the number of ballots cast. They didn't match anywhere and when we asked the officials on the ground, they had no answers. It was absolutely ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned the 16,000 in Miami-Dade, that's one county in Florida. Does that matter in a State that big? Well, I don't know, ask \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 . He won his last election by about 10,000 votes. Cobb County, Georgia, one county we found 34,000 discrepancies. We know the last election there and 2020 was decided by 11,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 You wouldn't let this happen at your bank. If you went in there and gave them $100.00. The deposit slip should come back and say a hundred. What if it said 50 or 150? These numbers don't match anywhere. No one can tell us why. This whole thing is a scam.\par \par \uc2 And these laws are being violated across the country. These election officials are either stupid, lazy, ignorant, or malicious, and they're violating Federal law.\par \par \uc2 It's a serious problem out there and we have to fix it now. We're working with Secretaries of State on the ground to make sure we can get to the bottom of this and make sure they maintain and retain this data for this upcoming Federal election. It would be so much easier to check up on it.\par \par \uc2 Here is the big problem, even worse, the person and entity that has to actually enforce the 1960 Civil Rights Act is \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  and the DOJ. Do you think they're going to take their eye off of trying to call parents terrorists for looking into their education of their own children? No way.\par \par \uc2 We're looking for a path forward. We have to make it easy to vote and hard to cheat. By retaining this data, we can take a look at things in real time and potentially prevent illegalities, irregularities, and fraud across this country from happening again.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, I mean, I can't imagine an excuse for you know, 94 out of a hundred counties throwing away their voter data when they're not allowed to. I don't think there's a good a good explanation for that.\par \par \uc2 Hogan Gidley, I appreciate that explanation. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GIDLEY: Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So Canada, while the rest of us weren't paying attention has become a dictatorship and its dictator, \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  has just eliminated the most basic human rights of all, the right of self-defense. We have the details.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It was just a year ago that Canadians thought they were living in a free country. They imagined the government couldn't seize their bank accounts without a warrant, they felt they could defend themselves. It all turned out to be untrue.\par \par \uc2 As of today, ownership of handguns is illegal in Canada. Totalitarianism happens fast; under \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2 , it has.\par \par \uc2 Let that to be a lesson to us.\par \par \uc2 That is it for us tonight. Tune in every night at 8:00 PM.\par \par \uc2 Have the best weekend with the ones you love. We will see you Monday.\par \par \uc2 Here is Sean.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020221022eial00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Criticizes Republican Platform; Pennsylvania Senate Nominees Spotlight Issue; Groups Argue Double Standard Over Abortion Prosecutions; El Paso Stops Busing Migrants North; Democrats And Republicans Vie For Voter Enthusiasm; Ukrainian Police Shoot At Drones Attacking Kyiv; American Company Invents New Antimissile Technology; Republican Party Fielding Most Diverse Slate Of Candidates For Midterm Elections; Republican House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy May Replace Democrat \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  As House Speaker If Republicans Win House In Midterms; Historian Surveys Environmental Movement And Legislation From Kennedy To Nixon; Polling Shows Republican Advantage In Midterm Elections Due To Issues Of Crime And Economy Overshadowing Issue Of Abortion\b0\par\par\uc2 Rich Edson, Peter Doocy, Bryan Llenas, David Spunt, Bill Melugin,  Shannon Bream, Rich Edson, Jennifer Griffin, Bret Baier, Molly Line, Chad  Pergram \par \uc2 7,887 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JIMMY FAILLA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And finally come see your buddy Jimmy Failla at the Spokane Comedy Club next Friday, October the 28th, Saturday the 29th.\par \par \uc2 Come for the comedy, stay in Washington for the immediate contact high, you'll definitely get one.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: All right, that's it for us. Have a great weekend everybody. See you back here on Monday.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening and welcome to Washington. I'm Rich Edson in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, President Biden is sounding optimistic about his party's chances of hanging on to congressional control after the November midterms, the president spoke today at an event touting his plan to cancel hundreds of billions of dollars in student loan debt.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy starts us off tonight. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Rich. Only three weekends to go before the midterms and President Biden is not spending this one boosting Democrats on the campaign trail. Instead, he's trying to get in on the action a little closer to home.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I will not yield.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): President Biden field testing a new nickname for the Republican platform.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It's mega MAGA trickle down.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: President Biden is also celebrating that his plan to forgive or cancel some student loan debt is surviving court challenges.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Just yesterday, State Court and the Supreme Court said no, we're on Biden's side.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And he's chastising critics.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Ted Cruz, the great Senator from Texas. He said it's for slackers, "slackers who don't deserve relief". Who and the hell do they think they are?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Today's remarks in Delaware, where the president plans to stay all weekend. A Fox Brain Room count finds President Biden has spent 76 weekends away from the White House, outpacing President Trump's 54 weekends away at this point in his presidency.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We're at Del State because it holds such a special place in my heart.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Every day at home is a day not spent in a swing state. Yet, President Biden is expressing confidence about his side's chances in the November elections.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Back and forth, with them ahead, us ahead, them ahead, back and forth. And the polls have been all over the place. I think that we're going to see one more shift back to our side the closing days.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: At a private high dollar fundraiser last night, President Biden said of the Senate "We actually need 53 votes", but he insists he's not trying to call Democrats Joe Manchin and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  unreliable.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm just getting greedy.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: He's also giving himself high marks on a major midterm issue we haven't heard a ton about at the White House lately.\par \par \uc2 Ahead of this midterm, how big of an issue is crime?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think it's a real issue. And I think we have a great record on it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): Crime is the second top issue voters are most concerned about in a recent Fox News poll trailing only inflation.\par \par \uc2 So, even though President Biden is not one of these Democrats calling to defund the police, it's unclear if voters are satisfied with his approach so far, Rich.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Peter Doocy live from a beautiful evening on the North Lawn of the White House. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 It was a big day on Wall Street with investors optimistic about the possibility of smaller interest rate hikes over the next few months. The Dow surged 749, the S&P 500 was up 87, the NASDAQ jumped 245.\par \par \uc2 Markets enjoyed their best week since June, the Dow was up almost five percentage points. The S&P gained 4-3/4, the NASDAQ finished ahead 5-1/4.\par \par \uc2 Leading off tonight's midterm spotlight, Philadelphia's progressive district attorney talks about efforts to impeach him over the city's crime problem. That issue has been a key talking point in the hotly contested Pennsylvania Senate race. Correspondent Bryan Llenas takes a look tonight from Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  wants to release convicted murderers from prison.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D) SENATORIAL CANDIDATE OF PENNSYLVANIA: Public safety is why I ran for office.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The Pennsylvania Senate race could come down to one issue, crime and according to Philadelphia's District Attorney, \uc2 Larry Krasner\uc2 , the current bipartisan effort to impeach him in the State House has nothing to do with his progressive policies and everything to do with Republican efforts to elect Dr. Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 LARRY KRASNER\uc2 , DISTRICT ATTORNEY OF PHILADELPHIA: This committee only wants to look at Philadelphia. It doesn't want to look at the other counties. In Pennsylvania, we're just looking at Philly right now in a super-heated election cycle.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: But Republicans insist it has everything to do with Krasner. Since he was elected D.A. in 2017, the city's murder rate is up 78 percent while retail theft, armed robbery, commercial burglary, property and violent crime all continue to surge.\par \par \uc2 No wonder why Dr. Mehmet Oz's campaign is reminding voters today that his opponent Democrat Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  endorsed Krasner. Saying Fetterman said he was very excited about Krasner's criminal reforms and called Krasner's new Conviction Integrity Unit groundbreaking.\par \par \uc2 That Integrity Unit is now under fire after it helped to exonerate this man, 32-year-old Jahmir Harris. Harris was convicted of murdering a 45 groundbreaking man in 2012. But under Krasner's direction was released after new evidence found he was "likely innocent".\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Harris is suspected of shooting 50-year-old Charles Gossett in the back of the head last month.\par \par \uc2 LINDA CRUZ, CHARLES GOSSETT'S SISTER: They have no consideration for the victims. This system right here proves that this doesn't work. This does not work. They need a program for the victims of people like Jahmir Harris.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): Harris turned himself in today. As for his 2012 murder exoneration, the judge involved in that decision last year was very critical of \uc2 Larry Krasner\uc2 's office's handling of that case, and as well on top of that, they were very skeptical the investigation that ultimately led to Harris's release, Rich.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Bryan Llenas live in Philly. Bryan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Conservative groups are asking why federal prosecutors are going after anti-abortion protesters, while seemingly ignoring attacks on pro-life pregnancy and counseling centers.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt takes a look from the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): The Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, \uc2 FBI\uc2  officials began seeing an increase in attacks specifically on pro-life related businesses. This includes pregnancy resource centers, broken glass and spray painted warnings covered these centers across the country.\par \par \uc2 And while the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has seen an increase in attacks, prosecutions are non- existent under a specific law called the FACE Act passed in 1994. It punishes people who use or threatened to use force to injure, intimidate or interfere with anyone seeking either abortion services or pro-life pregnancy counseling services.\par \par \uc2 This year, DOJ officials indicted 26 people for FACE Act violations, but none have been indicted for attacks on pro-life centers.\par \par \uc2 Over the summer, \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  told Fox News the increase in attacks on pro-life businesses is notable and concerning.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , DIRECTOR, \uc2 FBI\uc2 : There's a right way under our First Amendment to express yourself. And violence and destruction of property is not it and we have seen a number of attacks against faith based organizations, pregnancy resource centers.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: But the lack of prosecution on behalf of the Justice Department has pro-life organizations concerned with a message for attorney general \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KATIE GLENN, STATE POLICY DIRECTOR, \uc2 SUSAN B. ANTHONY\uc2  PRO-LIFE AMERICA: I would say that he should do his job and apply the law fairly and equally and look into these many dozens of attacks and see that, you know, groups are saying that they're coordinated.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): Rich, no comment from the Department of Justice on the story. However, we are told the department or the \uc2 FBI\uc2  rather across the street from DOJ is still investigating, Rich.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: David, federal prosecutors did press charges on former Trump Adviser \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  for divvying a congressional subpoena, his sentencing was today. Could they use that same playbook with the former president?\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Well, the House Committee investigating January 6 actually lived up to its promise and formally subpoenaed the former president today.\par \par \uc2 If he ignores that subpoena, we'll have to see if the Department of Justice would actually file charges in court as it did with \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2 , the former presidential adviser.\par \par \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  was sentenced today to four months in federal prison for ignoring a DOJ subpoena and ignoring a congressional subpoena from last year.\par \par \uc2 However, he doesn't have to serve that now while his appeal plays out in the lower courts, Rich.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: David Spunt from the Justice Department tonight. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: There is a report tonight that the documents seized from former President Trump's Florida estate contained classified secrets about Iran and China, that's according to \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 . It writes: People familiar with the matter say the information could expose intelligence gathering methods and endangered sources.\par \par \uc2 South Carolina Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  is asking the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  to step in after lower courts have ordered him to testify to a Georgia grand jury about efforts to overturn the 2020 election. Graham contends his status as a senator, gives him immunity so far, that argument has been rejected.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's report on the border crisis, we have shocking pictures showing massive groups crossing over at night to a woman trying to smuggle migrants into the country with her small child in the vehicle.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bill has the story from Eagle Pass, Texas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Exclusive images from the Fox News thermal drone reveal a huge group of migrants crossing illegally into Eagle Pass, Texas during the pre-dawn hours this morning, hundreds of them surrendering themselves to the Texas National Guard, a scene that plays out here multiple times a day, every day.\par \par \uc2 The migrants are predominantly single adult men from all around the world, though, the number of Venezuelans crossing illegally has plummeted following a new DHS policy to expel them.\par \par \uc2 As a result of that policy, the Democrat led city of El Paso, Texas has announced it will no longer bus migrants to New York City or Chicago, citing a decrease in Venezuelans crossing into their city.\par \par \uc2 Over the past several months, the federal government released more than 40,000 migrants into El Paso, that cause the city to spend millions of its own dollars to bus almost 14,000 migrants to other Democrat led cities. Many of those migrants Venezuelans.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D), MAYOR OF NEW YORK: The mayor stated that he will no longer send buses here to New York due to that decompression strategy. I think the team responded to this crisis.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hi, Texas Highway Patrol, the baby is strapped in?\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: And in the Rio Grande Valley, an American mom with her baby in her car was caught smuggling several illegal immigrants in her vehicle.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Anybody else on the trunk or anything like that?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Not that I know of.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Troopers popped the trunk and find another man crammed into a compartment underneath a stroller. The mom leader told troopers she was trying to smuggle the illegal immigrants to San Antonio. She was arrested and charged.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And as you can see from our live Fox News drone right now, we have another small group of migrants crossing illegally here in Eagle Pass as I speak.\par \par \uc2 And it's also important to point out we are now 21 days into the month of October and the federal government still has not released the border numbers for the month of September. And those numbers are expected to show that we closed out fiscal year 2022 with more than 2.3 million migrant encounters, the highest number ever recorded. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Bill Melugin live from Eagle Pass Texas. Bill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News Chief Legal Correspondent and the anchor of "FOX NEWS SUNDAY", Shannon Bream. Hi, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF LEGAL CORRESPONDENT: It's good to see you, Rich.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: It's good to see you too. Thanks for joining us this evening.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about the midterms and let's just remind voters what we're looking at poll wise here. These are the polls that we have seen thus far the most important issues extremely or very concerned about, according to Fox News poll.\par \par \uc2 Inflation and higher prices, well outpacing everything out, 89 percent. Higher crime rates at 79 percent. Political divisions, Ukraine, schools, abortion policy, gun laws, border security, it seems as though that message for Republicans is really breaking through and these polls are tightening.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It does. And our polling also shows on those top issues about inflation, about the economy, about crime. Voters gave a double-digit advantage in favor of Republicans. So, they want to keep talking about those things.\par \par \uc2 But the president had to meet it head on today. We saw him talking about this and warning people, he used the words crash, Republicans will crash the economy if you give them a chance and vote for them in November.\par \par \uc2 He knows what the top issues are. They're not good issues for Democrats right now. And our polling has also showed us that voters assigned blame with the White House. They think the president and his policies are to blame for where we are.\par \par \uc2 So, his speech today trying to tackle that head on knowing it's at the top of voter's concerns.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Over the summer because of the court, Democrats had an awful lot of motivation and that seems to have waned from now. They have gotten criticism that they have focused too much on abortion and not these other issues, right?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That definitely is going to drive their base though, and they're trying to get to female voters, they think that's going to be a big help for them. It definitely spiked to them over the summer, we saw the congressional ballot, the generic flip in their direction.\par \par \uc2 Abortion, though, as you said, has slightly ticked down on the list. It's out of our top five for voters now. So, we'll see if any of that momentum lasts into the fall.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: These seem like good topics for Sunday. Who do you have?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I by the way have Richardson.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Come on.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I'm so excited you're going to join us on the panel. We'll have you to talk about all of these topics and more. But we've got Senator \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2 , who was in a very interesting, difficult more, you know, contentious race, I think than Republicans had hoped. They'd have to defend in that deep red state, but he's running against the independent Evan McMullen who's made some serious charges. He's also invited but we've got Senator Lee with us this weekend.\par \par \uc2 And Congressman \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  Democrat out of Texas who's tried to raise the alarm numerous times over what's happening at the border. Whether he's gotten any help from the White House or not, we'll ask him on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: It's going to be a great show.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I will see you then.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: I will see you then too. Shannon Bream, the host of "FOX NEWS SUNDAY". Shannon, thanks.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thanks, Rich.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Up next, a look at some of the high tech weapons on or coming to the battlefield in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our local affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 61 in Hartford as thousands gathered for the funeral of Bristol Connecticut officers killed in an apparent ambush. The service for Dustin DeMonte and Alex Hamzy was held at the \uc2 University of Connecticut\uc2 's 40,000 seat football stadium.\par \par \uc2 Fox 11 in Los Angeles as the widow of a former \uc2 University of Southern California\uc2  football player sues the \uc2 NCAA\uc2  for failing to protect her husband from repetitive head trauma.\par \par \uc2 His widow says Matthew Gee died in 2018 from permanent brain damage caused by countless blows to the head he took while playing linebacker for the 1990 Rose Bowl winning team.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NCAA\uc2  blames Gee's death on heavy drinking, drugs, and other problems.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Honolulu. One of the big stories there from KHON. Hawaii gets its first \uc2 Chick-fil-A\uc2  restaurant. Customers began lining up at 4:00 a.m. outside the store there.\par \par \uc2 Three other locations in the state are set to open in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look, "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): Perhaps a comeback for former British Prime Minister \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 . He is one of several lawmakers jockeying for support tonight, following yesterday's resignation by \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Johnson has not declared he's running. But bookmakers have made him one of the favorites. Johnson resigned in July after a scandal involving his appointment of a politician who had been accused of sexual misconduct.\par \par \uc2 One former conservative leader is urging against a return to what he calls the psychodrama of the Johnson-era.\par \par \uc2 Canada has new gun control measures in place tonight. They prohibit the sale, purchase, or transfer of handguns.\par \par \uc2 The freeze was announced in May as part of the country's strongest gun measures in 40 years. The law also prevents Canadians from bringing newly acquired handguns into the country.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we look at new technology that could stop the Kamikaze Iranian drones Russia is firing into Ukraine's capital. Both the U.S. and Israel have anti drone weapons. But so far, they have not sent them to the battlefield.\par \par \uc2 Chief national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin examines this new technology and how it could project -- protect not just Ukraine, but also Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice- over): Ukrainian police have had to resort to firing their rifles at the incoming Iranian made kamikaze drones, leading to frustration in Kyiv, and here at the Pentagon that there is no simple solution to stop the swarms of Iranian-made drones launched by Russia.\par \par \uc2 Anti-missile defense systems which already exist in Israel, and technologies here in the U.S. have yet to be sent to Kyiv. Israeli leaders have faced criticism for refusing to provide a ground-based laser system called Iron beam for fear of angering Russia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BENNY GANTZ\uc2 , ISRAELI DEFENSE MINISTER: I don't see us sending offensive military equipment.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: But a new American technology involving microwaves, called Leonidas already exists.\par \par \uc2 JOE LONSDALE, CO-FOUNDER, EPIRUS: You can blast microwave radiation and you turn off other electronics on, you know, a mile away.\par \par \uc2 In the future, the people who control the electromagnetic spectrum will control the battlefield.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The technology yet to be fielded by the \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  could not only protect Kyiv, but also protect U.S. troops at outposts in Syria and Iraq.\par \par \uc2 Electronic warfare weapons developed by American companies like Epirus can defend a target from one to two miles out. But soon they say that could grow to 30 miles.\par \par \uc2 LONSDALE: I think it would be critical for defending any territory including Taiwan against China. You could have a city state, you could have a small island, and now you could turn off any electronic weapons, any, you know, any missiles, any ships, any cars from miles away.\par \par \uc2 This is exciting technology for this great kind of freedom in the future. It makes it a lot harder for the bad guys to come bully people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: With the Pentagon's top naval officer now warning China could invade Taiwan by the end of this year, experts say testing such defensive systems on the battlefield in Ukraine could also help the U.S. defend Taiwan. Rich?\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Jennifer Griffin from the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, unprecedented diversity for Republicans on the ballot in the midterms. But first, BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight.\par \par \uc2 Chad government clamps down to the country's opposition, suspending seven political parties and searching party offices.\par \par \uc2 This comes a day after unprecedented protests around the country leave more than 60 people dead.\par \par \uc2 Pakistan's elections commission disqualifies former Prime Minister Imran Khan from holding public office for five years. It accuses him of unlawfully selling state gifts and concealing assets. Experts say Khan will also lose his National Assembly seat.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Cannes, France. One of the big stories there tonight. France inaugurates a memorial paying tribute to civilian and military hero dogs.\par \par \uc2 It features a sculpture depicting a World War I soldier and his dog huddled together. It is located in front of the Town Hall in Suippes, part of an area of northeast France that saw a major battles during World War I.\par \par \uc2 Just some of our other stories, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Republicans have the most diverse class of congressional nominees in their history on the ballot this November.\par \par \uc2 In Connecticut's fifth district, the GOP hopeful is the black son of Guatemalan immigrants. He's campaigning to be a check on the President Biden's agenda. Correspondent Molly Line reports from New Britain, Connecticut, tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MOLLY LINE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Criticized by opponents as the party of old white men, this election cycle, the GOP says it is putting forth the most diverse slate of candidates in party history.\par \par \uc2 REP. TOM EMMER (R-MN): There's a record number of black candidates, record number of Hispanic candidates, record number of women.\par \par \uc2 LINE: The list includes George Logan, who is fighting to flip Connecticut's fifth congressional district. The former state lawmakers is black, bilingual, and the son of Guatemalan immigrants.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE LOGAN (R-CT), CONGRESSIONAL NOMINEE: Democrat insiders are desperate. They'll say you'll only vote for me because I'm black.\par \par \uc2 LINE: His Democrat opponent, Congresswoman Jahana Hayes, is also black. But Logan is running as a prochoice, pro law enforcement moderate, promising to reign in the Biden administration's big spending. His win could signal a return to Congress for pragmatic New England Republicans.\par \par \uc2 LOGAN: More diversity in any organization makes the organization stronger.\par \par \uc2 LINE: In Rhode Island's second congressional district, Republican \uc2 Allan Fung\uc2 , the former mayor of Cranston, the first Asian American mayor elected in the state, is leading the polls.\par \par \uc2 ALLAN FUNG (R-RI), CONGRESSIONAL NOMINEE: I'm proud to be part of that group of new, fresh faces into the party.\par \par \uc2 LINE: More than 70 GOP candidates of color are running. Congressman \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2 , the Republicans point man on elections, believes a political realignment is underway.\par \par \uc2 EMMER: I would argue that every minority group in America that has traditionally voted Democrat is available to Republican candidates.\par \par \uc2 LINE: But a spokesman for the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee slammed Republicans for, quote, "finally engaging with communities of color in the year 2022 with flawed candidates." Despite the dig from opponents, observers say --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MCLEAN, \uc2 QUINNIPIAC UNIVERSITY\uc2 : We will definitely see more ethnic and racial diversity in the House Republican caucus.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 LINE (on camera): Which could ultimately lead to more support from minority voters. Rich?\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Molly Line. Molly, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, in our spotlight races, we take a look at a couple of the other big races involving diverse GOP candidates. Indiana's first district has sent a Democrat to Washington each election since 1930. This fall, Republican Air Force veteran Jennifer-Ruth Green is hoping to unseat incumbent Frank Mrvan. Our FOX News power rankings list the race is a toss- up. In Michigan, former judge Carl Marlinga and Republican John James face off in the newly created district 10. The \uc2 Federal Election Commission\uc2  shows James is heading into November with a big cash advantage. This race is listed as likely Republican.\par \par \uc2 And we may be in the final months of the \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  era as House speaker. If Republicans gain control of the chamber, she will lose the gavel and may decide to retire as well. Tonight, congressional correspondent Chad Pergram looks at some of the big changes that could be in store for the next Congress.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: It's an understatement to say that House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is coy about her political future.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you intend to be here in the new Congress as the speaker, as the minority leader?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): I'm strictly focused on winning the next election. Do you want to talk about that?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Democrats are in the dark.\par \par \uc2 REP. MARCY KAPTUR (D-OH): I think people are looking for a cue from the speaker. I think she probably wants to get through this election. They haven't gotten any signals from her.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Pelosi sticks to a singular mantra.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: First we win, then we decide.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Pelosi hinted this would be her last term when Democrats considered imposing term limits for leaders.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: What I said then is whether it passes or not, I will abide by those limits that are there.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But nothing is locked in.\par \par \uc2 CASEY BURGAT, GEORGE WASHINGTON UNIVERSITY: She is a master of using words that will mean certain things to specific people in the room and then go back and say hey, check the receipts, check the transcripts. I actually didn't say that.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy is looking to become speaker if Republicans win the House.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Will you be speaker in January?\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): I believe so. We'll win the majority and I'll be speaker, yes.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But McCarthy stumbled to become speaker seven years ago after suggesting the GOP only investigated Benghazi to undercut \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Everybody thought \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was unbeatable, right? But we put together a Benghazi special committee.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Some Republicans use that remark as justification to oppose McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 JASON CHAFFETZ, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: His margin isn't big enough. I think he's going to struggle. He's one landmine away from just blowing himself up.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: It's believed that McCarthy will succeed so long as former President Trump backs him. By contrast, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  isn't in Trump's good graces.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is a disgrace.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: McConnell could be on thin ice if the GOP doesn't claim the majority. But he insists he has the votes.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): I've been elected eight times without opposition. If anybody want to challenge me, have at it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): If McConnell is the GOP leader next year, he will surpass late Montana Senator Mike Mansfield as the longest serving Senate party leader in history. Rich?\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Chad Pergram on Capitol Hill. Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, how presidents nourished the environmental movement in the 1960s, and later, Candidate Casino.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Tonight we learned some fascinating details about the rise of environmental activism during the 1960s. Here's my colleague Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Thanks, Rich. Joining us tonight, author \uc2 Douglas Brinkley\uc2 . His new book, "Silent Spring Revolution, \uc2 John F. Kennedy\uc2 , Rachel Carson, Lyndon Johnson, \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2 , and The Great Environmental Awakening" comes out November 15. Douglas, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS BRINKLEY\uc2 , "SILENT SPRING REVOLUTION" AUTHOR: Thanks, Bret, for having me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I have a love of presidential history. I think this is really interesting when you look at the cross section of presidents who were dealing with the environment in different ways but came to kind of a similar solution.\par \par \uc2 BRINKLEY: Absolutely. I've written before about Theodore Roosevelt, the great conservation, national park president. But this time, I'm looking at three different presidents, \uc2 John F. Kennedy\uc2 , who really did more about seashore preservation than anybody. He saved Cape Cod in Massachusetts, Padre Island in Texas, Point Reyes, California. He really wanted to make sure we had public lands and public beaches along the ocean. And he didn't get enough credit for that.\par \par \uc2 And Lyndon Johnson was a frontiersman, rancher, kind of a wilderness maven. And Johnson did the wilderness act, which put aside millions of acres for hunting and fishing and hiking all over the west, and also did wild and scenic rivers. And \uc2 Richard Nixon\uc2  is like a bookend to T.R., because Theodore Roosevelt was a Republican. Nixon is the one who gave birth to the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2 , NEPA. He did the Clean Water Act, Clean Air Act. He ended up doing national recreation areas everywhere, endangered species. And so I explore why did Nixon do all that, in the book.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and obviously there's some conservatives who look at some of those agencies and say they overstep at times. But for Nixon, at this moment, you write, "With his White House besieged by Watergate woes and the trick Arab oil embargo, Nixon happily embraced the Endangered Species bill that fall as the glorious capstone to the conservation legacy. Contrary to what later critics would allege, it was among the least controversial bills he ever signed. The vast majority of Americans clearly supported a serious commitment to protecting vanishing wildlife. Even the \uc2 NRA\uc2  was for it."\par \par \uc2 It was a time in which the environment was getting to be a major issue based on what was happening.\par \par \uc2 BRINKLEY: That's right. Nixon came in. He was president only days, you had the Santa Barbara oil spill. He was a California guy living in San Clemente. A lot of Republicans were not in my backyard. I don't want my beach front homes polluted. Now, Endangered Species passed December 28th, 1973. Nixon signs it, but 92 to zero it passed in the Senate because at that moment, everybody wanted to make sure the bald eagle got saved from pesticides or chemicals. And the good news is, Bret, the eagles are back. They wanted to make sure the Florida panther came back. Now you have an NHL hockey team named about the species. He wanted to make sure that the grizzly bear was able to survive as a symbol of the Rocky Mountains' greatness and grandeur.\par \par \uc2 And so in a lot of ways, the original Endangered Species Act did its job. The rub becomes, is it overkill. And the fight goes on. Many people wanted charismatic animals saves, like the three I just named. But sometimes a small lizard could stop a construction project, and that's where real estate and commercial developers think the Endangered Species Act has been abused from its original intent.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. We've been doing this Common Ground segment on this show where Democrats and Republicans come together. It's interesting to see how they came together during this time, different presidents handling it differently, but for one goal. Douglas, it's a great read. The book "Silent Spring Revolution." \uc2 Douglas Brinkley\uc2 , we appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 BRINKLEY: Thank you so much, Bret. Take care.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rich, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the Friday Lightning Round on inflation's impact on the midterms, Winners and Losers, plus Candidate Casino. And later, the best of the best in Washington, this week's "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Using the Strategic Petroleum Reserve to respond to their failed energy policies is antithetical to American interests.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's not politically motivated at all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The election is not a referendum. It's a choice. But look at what I've inherited and what I've done. And look at what they're offering. They will crash the economy next year by threatening the full faith and credit of the United States. Everything they proposed, are proposing, will make inflation worse.\par \par \uc2 REP. JASON SMITH (R-MO): We've seen him add more than $10 trillion of new spending. The most spending ever in the history of any president in his first 20 months. We have to stop the bleed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Let's bring in our panel, Byron York, chief political correspondent for "The Washington Examiner," \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2 , "Axios" senior political correspondent, and Olivia Beavers, congressional reporter for "Politico." Byron, we had a surge for Democrats over the summer. That has really narrowed. What happened?\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, "THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER": Well, I hear a little desperation in the president's voice. What he's really saying, yes, we Democrats had a surge. Yes, that surge seems to have disappeared. But there's still time for another one, hoping that things might change just again.\par \par \uc2 And the problem was the abortion surge ended, the late summer abortion surge ended because economic issued reasserted themselves. It was a terrible inflation report on September 13th, and we've had more after that. People realized it was not going to go away. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  was going to keep pushing and probably lead us closer toward a recession. And there's simply no good news on the horizon in the next 18 days that can help Democrats. And that's why I think you saw the president coming out today say if you elect Republicans, they'll crash the economy.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Olivia, do the Democrats just have an impossible hand here? It's a midterm election. They control both house of Congress and the White House. They've got a tough economic picture on top of that. Are they focusing on the right things here, or are they just really playing an awful hand?\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, "POLITICO": Democrats saw this sort of shift of the issues that they were focusing on. They thought gun control and gun violence was going to be big. They thought abortion was going to be big. They thought that January 6th and protecting democracy was going to be big. And we started to see a shift away from that and to issues where voters tend to have more support for Republicans. I think there's a 10 percent advantage for Republicans who think that Republicans can handle the economy and inflation better than Democrats can, and that is a number one issue. So if there's a trust fall happen there, it's going to be in Republicans' favor.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Josh, you and I chatted about this the other day. We talked about how Republicans -- or Democrats were focusing so much on the abortion issue, especially after Roe versus Wade, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  coming out with his 15-week abortion ban, but perhaps at the expense about talking about other issues. How much has that hamstrung them?\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2 , "NATIONAL JOURNAL": It's been a problem because, first of all, they kind of were very reactive on the economy. When gas prices went down over the summer, they thought that they could take credit for that and that would hold them through November. Clearly, that's not been the case. And it's not just gas prices but the price of other goods has gone up and inflation continues to be a top issue for voters.\par \par \uc2 And the other challenge is that the Democratic candidates that are just talking about abortion, and a lot of Democrats have just thought that abortion was the only issue that they needed to talk about because that was the only issue where they had a big advantage on. But a lot of voters care about the economy. And the Democrats that aren't talking about the economy, that aren't talking about their own accomplishments, are finding themselves in a really tough challenge in the final few weeks.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Byron, I want to focus on the Pennsylvania race. It has been not only a race that has all the national issues, but you have this issue now with the Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  suffering a stroke. That race has tightened substantially over the last couple of weeks. Is that a product of what we're seeing nationwide, or is it specific to his stroke issue?\par \par \uc2 YORK: I think there's some specific issues. One, there is the question of his health. But also the race is also tilted more towards some actual issues. Dr. Oz has gotten a lot of good response from an ad hitting Fetterman on crime. Obviously, there's a debate next week, which will probably be, certainly after the Walker-Warnock debate, the most watched Senate debate this year. That is going to be a huge thing.\par \par \uc2 But you're right, Dr. Oz has been ticking upward, upward, upward. The latest poll show them tied. A lot of Republicans were a little worried that he could -- Oz would climb, climb, and climb, but maybe not quite get there. But now it does appear he's almost there.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Olivia, you cover the Hill. We were talking a little bit earlier about Republicans only need a handful of seats to take control of the House. And you saw Chad Pergram's piece tonight. It's not a given that this is going to be a seamless transition, that the Republicans have their top leadership in place if they win control of the House. What does that look like?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: You're right, Rich. Right now, we're under the general assumption, Republicans generally believe that Kevin McCarthy will be speaker. Steve Scalise will be the majority leader. And then we get to the whip, and that is really interesting because you have three candidates with great resumes who are all competing after each other. And let me tell you, it depends who you ask who has the lead. It's going to be very close. But then there's the conference chair race where you have \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  and Byron Donalds. Stefanik looks like she has a lead, but it is not fun having a challenger to her right.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Josh, back to the midterms nationally. Do Democrats have any good stories that they can point to over the past week or so?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Look, the abortion story was one that got them a sugar high over the summer. And now that sugar high is fading. They need to have a good story on crime, or at least a response to crime, because in many races, including the Pennsylvania Senate race, that is the biggest vulnerability. And it's because many candidates Democrats nominated are to the activist wing of the party when it comes to criminal justice reform. Fetterman in Pennsylvania, Mandela Barnes in Wisconsin. You've got an Oregon governor's race where the Democrat is also in that same progressive spot. So Democrats can play defense on that issue effectively, but they need to talk tougher on crime. And that's an issue they really haven't had a story other than just talking about funding the police.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: And Byron, they really do risk angering their base if they talk the wrong way about these issues.\par \par \uc2 YORK: Absolutely. We spent all 2020, much of 2021 hearing and alleged police abuses from Democrats. And even though many of them say I never supported defunding the police, many of them supported cutting funds for the police, maybe not zeroing it out, but cutting funds for police. And this is a record, and Republicans are hitting them over and over and over because they really did say that.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Olivia, the president was speaking about student loans today. How much do you think that moves the needle in the midterms?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: It's been interesting. The Democrats have done several things to try to push for their agenda. There was the Inflation Reduction Act, there was the child tax credits. It doesn't look like that's moving voters, so we'll see if this helps them with the momentum heading into the final weeks. But as of right now, some of their final pushes have not really gained momentum.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: All right, it's Friday night. Let's go to the casino. Place your bets. Place your bets. We're looking at the midterms here. Where are you allocating your $100 in very real money chips, Byron?\par \par \uc2 YORK: The question being will we know the results immediately or not immediately. I don't think it will be as bad as 2020, but I don't think we're going to know the next day. So I'm betting $75 on no and $25 on yes.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: All right, Olivia, when are we going to know who is controlling what here?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: I agree with Byron. I was a little more hawkish. I put 60 on no and 40 on yes.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: OK, Josh?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Apparently, I'm taking a riskier bet. I think we will know the results early enough in the night. It's going to be a wave. It's looking more and more like a Republican wave. And if it's a healthy Republican majority in the House, if they take the Senate, I think we'll know on the earlier side.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: OK, so we're betting on winners and losers. How about our Winners and Losers. Byron, who do you have?\par \par \uc2 YORK: Last week I made British Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  my loser because she had a really terrible week, seemed on the brink. Tonight, she gets a coveted rare double loser award for actually having quit after 45 days.\par \par \uc2 My winner is \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 , the former P.M., who may, may have the shortest exile in history if he can come back and win.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: OK, Olivia?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: I'm a T. Swift fan, so I'm saying for all of the Taylor Swift people who got to listen to her new album and maybe woke up midnight to hear it, that's the winner for today. The loser, there was an audio capturing a staffer, a city hall staffer that was basically making disparaging remarks about the New York City Mayor Eric Adams, so that is really sort of an embarrassing episode if you have it internally, and he got fired. So that would be loser.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Not great. Josh?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: My winner is \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , the Republican nominee for governor in New York. We talked about crime being a big issue. A new poll shows that a single digit race, largely, only really on Zeldin's campaign and only on the issue of crime these days, and it's cutting the numbers against Governor Hochul.\par \par \uc2 The loser, Dan Snyder, the owner of the Commanders, the \uc2 Washington Commanders\uc2 . He may be the least popular person in Washington right now. And that's saying a lot because there are a lot of unpopular politicians these days. The \uc2 NFL\uc2  owners may try to force him to sell the team, and it's been a bad 25 years.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: That's an incredible story, and competitive governors' races in Oregon and in New York state, who'd have thought\par \par \uc2 It is Friday and time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Ready, get set, go.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Should \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  run again?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have a particular fondness, I must tell you, for electric school buses. I love electric school buses.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Top domestic issue, inflation or abortion?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: All right, important. Unlike you, there's no one thing. We've got to be able to walk and chew gum at the same time.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I've got to say it carefully. Gas backwards.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: People are tired of being broke.\par \par \uc2 PENCE: Using the Strategic Petroleum Reserve to respond to their failed energy policies is antithetical to American interests.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It's not politically motivated at all.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I am a fighter. I'm not a quitter.\par \par \uc2 I am resigning.\par \par \uc2 TIM RYAN (D-OH), SENATE CANDIDATE: You kissed his ass, and then he endorsed. And you said he is the greatest presidents of all time.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R-OH), SENATE CANDIDATE: He says he challenged \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  for leadership, but then he goes on national TV and says that he loves her.\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D-GA), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: The most dangerous thing facing Georgia is four more years of \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GOV. BRIAN KEMP (R-GA): My record has been attacked because Ms. Abrams doesn't want to talk about her own record.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: As a parent, you're always waiting on the call.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: What is happening?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Stop China from sending the product.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There is no single answer to addressing the opioid epidemic.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: I said I would have punched him out, I would have gone to jail, and I would have been happy to do so.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: That's it for SPECIAL REPORT. I'm in Washington. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts now. Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020221022eial00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Inflation Top Concern 18 Days From Midterms; Biden In Delaware To Tout Student Loan Forgiveness Plan Critics Accuse DOJ Of Pro-Life Double Standard; Georgetown Students Walk Out Of Pence Speech\b0\par\par\uc2 Gillian Turner, Ed Lawrence, Connell McShane, Joe Concha, Bryan  Llenas, Molly Line, David Spunt, Mollie Hemingway, Jonathan Hunt \par \uc2 7,456 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS HOST: Good afternoon. I'm Gillian Turner in Washington for Martha MacCallum today. Well, President Biden's delivering this message to Americans now 18 days out before the midterms.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Election is not a referendum. It's a choice. It's a choice. And the Republican criticized my economic record. But look at what I've inherited, and what I've done. And look at what they're offering. It's mega-MAGA trickle down. Mega-MAGA trickle down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Inflation coming out on top, and yet another poll is the most important issue for Americans. Majority say, President Biden is not paying enough attention to the issues that matter to them. And to their families. We expect to see the President shortly at Delaware State University, he's touting the student loan forgiveness plan there. That's estimated to come with a hefty price tag more than $400 billion.\par \par \uc2 Joe Concha and Connell McShane are standing by to join us in a moment. First, we go to Fox Business White House Correspondent, Ed Lawrence, who joins us from 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue. Hi, Ed.\par \par \uc2 ED LAWRENCE, FOX BUSINESS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Gillian. Yes, you know, you showed the clip there. The President went after Republicans today in his speech this week. He's talked about student loan debt, relief, already. He's talking about the bipartisan infrastructure bill. And on Sunday, he's going to do a forum, from now, this presidential forum.\par \par \uc2 And in that, he's going to say that it is absolutely OK to use federal money, so a woman can then go get an abortion. Nothing specific this week about inflation. And when you ask the White House press secretary about inflation, she says the president empathizes with Americans, while he keeps asking for more time to make his transition.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Over the last few years, we face some of the most difficult challenges in our history, both at home and abroad. We're making real progress. We just have to keep going. I know we can. For everything we've been through, I've never been more optimistic about our America's future.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE (voice over): Again, the President did not mention the word inflation, but it by far remains the top priority for registered voters. You can see there and this latest Fox News polling, abortion on that list is actually sixth. We just couldn't fit in political division as number three, another reason, inflation sits atop that list. Overall, it's near 40-year highs, and it started to shoot up the month President Biden took office when he started undoing all the policies of the previous administration.\par \par \uc2 ART LAFFER, LAFFER ASSOCIATES FOUNDER: I do see the Republican stopping additional problems additional spending, and maybe even stopping some of the funding of the programs that were already passed. So, that's good news that we're going to stop the hemorrhaging, but we need to have a bigger change to, to create supplies at economic growth in this country once again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And after the speech about student debt relief, the President will go on to his vacation home in Delaware. Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Ed Lawrence at the White House for us this afternoon. Thank you. Let's go and bring in Joe Concha, Media and Politics Columnist for The Hill, also a Fox News Contributor and Fox Business Chief National Correspondent, Connell McShane. Gentlemen, let's dig into some of the key takeaways from these latest polls.\par \par \uc2 So, I'll just leave this out: 63 percent of Americans say that they wish the President would give more attention to their top issues, 46 percent of them polled by Fox News say that that is inflation, that is the issue and 89 of percent say they are very concerned about inflation, and higher prices. Connell, translate that into votes for us in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 CONNELL MCSHANE, FOX BUSINESS CHIEF NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, the president's, we're waiting on an event, Gillian, as you know, in a few minutes in Delaware to talk about student debt relief. Even if you're a proponent of that proposal, even if you think it's a good idea from the President, you have to acknowledge the timing is somewhat curious today, as they literally had an event at the White House before he left for Delaware, trying to tout the President's accomplishment says the White House sees it in reducing the deficit. And now, we have this event which he made the point earlier just even by the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  projections is going to cost at least $400 billion. Some economists think that this program on student debt relief might even cost more.\par \par \uc2 And to finish the point on inflation because that's what you were bringing up and you're right, it's the top concern of voters, Committee for a Responsible Federal Budget, which is a group of deficit hawks, as you know, they were supportive of the President's Inflation Reduction Act, but they say this plan on student debt relief would wipe out the "disinflationary benefits" of that, in other words go in the opposite direction. So, that's, that's an issue for this president for this White House when you look at the economics of it.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: So, speaking of that event just now at the White House, Joe, the President said don't worry because Christmas is going to come early. Take a listen. Early for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The polls have been all over the place. I think that we're, we're going to see one more shift back to our side in the closing days. And let me tell you why I think that, we're starting to see some of the good news on the economy, gas prices are down, sharply, in 46 of the 50 states because of what I've been doing. We're moving in the right direction, there's more to come.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: So, the votes are going to come back to Democrats over the next 18 days. That's what he says.\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Look at the premise, Gillian, right. Yes. The premise is that he says that gas prices are down sharply. When this President took office, gas prices were at an average of $2.39 per gallon. They are approaching $4.00 once again. So, when he says that he's talking about prices coming down from an all-time high, and they're still far too high for the public's liking.\par \par \uc2 So, that, that's gaslighting on gas, right. And the President says that Republicans are criticizing his economic records. No, Sir. It's not just Republicans. It's an overwhelming majority of independents, a solid chunk of Democrats; overall, we're seeing approval ratings on the president's handling of inflation in the 20s. And yet, he has the audacity to say that we're making progress.\par \par \uc2 Many Americans are sitting at home, and they're looking at their 401(Ks), and they're looking at what they're paying for food and for rent and for their mortgage and for their electric bills, which are going to go a lot higher this winter. And they're saying, no, Sir, things are not going in the right direction right now.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Gaslighting on gas, Joe, and people say that you're not funny. So, it's not true. Connell.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: A little funny.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Connell, Connell, what about these 11th hour moves by the president releasing more from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve this massive student loan cancellation? How is that going to factor into inflation?\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: Well, as I talked about a minute ago on the student loan cancellation. There's actually some debate about that, as the Committee for Responsible Federal Budget says you're going back in the opposite direction. There are some economists that have looked at it, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  did, \uc2 Mark Zandi\uc2  of \uc2 Moody's\uc2  did, and basically said it's not going to move the needle one way or the other very much.\par \par \uc2 So, I don't know that that will be a big factor in the election. I do think and I think the numbers back this up that there is a correlation in the short-term movement of gas prices and the electoral prospects of a given party. I mean, think about it. We saw it over the summer, right, when gas prices to Joe's earlier point did start to come down.\par \par \uc2 Democrats were polling better than they ever had during this particular cycle in the month of August, for example. But now, gas prices have started to actually creep up again over the last month, again, to Joe's earlier point to get closer to $4.00. So, although the President says they've come down over a number of months, correct, they've started to creep up again. And I think there's no accident, or no, it's not a coincidence that the Democrats have not been pulling as well there.\par \par \uc2 So, that is the one thing that people see every single day. That and food inflation that affects the you know, how their pocketbook looks and how probably their vote goes.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Gentlemen, we got to leave it there. Joe, you are funny, despite what the people say about you.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: You're a funny gal. How am I funny?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: You're super funny. Gentlemen, have a great, a great weekend. I really enjoyed talking to you as always.\par \par \uc2 MCSHANE: You too.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Coming up next, how a program run by Philadelphia's District Attorney exonerated a convicted murderer who is now wanted for taking part in the same alleged crime yet again. This is a story exclusive will sit down with a family of his latest alleged victim, coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LINDA CRUZ, BROTHER MURDERED IN PHILADELPHIA: To know that this man is guilty of murder, just a cold-blooded murder the same thing that he already did 10 years later, and just to get out within one year and murdered my brother is sickening.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Here's a look at some real heroes in action.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It sounded like a gun to me.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: A car driving right by that ambulance crew with somebody shooting out the window, that happened in Chicago last month. We are just getting the video into the newsroom now. The medical express crew ducking down in their seats as those bullets flew. They then safely stopped the ambulance in heavy traffic to treat a man on the ground. Well turns out he was not shot but fell off his bike trying to dodge the gunfire. No serious injuries, thankfully, and police are now investigating.\par \par \uc2 Well, an impeachment vote for Philadelphia's District Attorney, \uc2 Larry Krasner\uc2 , could come as early as next week. This follows criticism his policies have been fueling an uptick in crime. One of those policies resulted in the release and subsequent exoneration of Jameer Harris, he is now charged in the deadly shooting of a 50-year-old man Charles Gossett. Gossett was known in the Philadelphia music and art scene. He also worked on non-violent initiatives across the city.\par \par \uc2 In moments we're going to speak to Gossett' sister, she's standing by. First, we go to Fox News National Correspondent Bryan Llenas, he reports from Philly. Hi, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Gillian. Well, Philadelphia's progressive District Attorney \uc2 Larry Krasner\uc2  insists that the city's record murder rate and record number of carjackings has nothing to do with his progressive policies. And now, he is calling the Republican-led yet bipartisan committees investigation looking to perhaps impeach him politically motivated.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LARRY KRASNER\uc2  (D), PHILADELPHIA DISTRICT ATTORNEY: So, Philadelphia, like the rest of the state, and like the country has gone through a truly terrible spike in gun violence. But this committee only wants to look at Philadelphia. It doesn't want to look at the other counties, in Pennsylvania. We're just looking at Philly right now in a super-heated election cycle.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: This, as we're now learning that a convicted murderer who was released, thanks to Krasner, is now a suspect in a murder. Police are searching for 32-year-old Jahmir Harris after he allegedly drove two gunmen who shot and killed 50-year-old Charles Gossett in the back of the head last month.\par \par \uc2 In 2012, Harris was convicted of murder, but was exonerated, thanks to Krasner's new Conviction Integrity Unit, which found new evidence. But according to court transcripts, the judge in that case was