sideration for the victims.\par \par \uc2 This is to put prisoners that have been convicted back on the streets. This system right here proves that this doesn't work. This does not work. They need a program for the victims of people like Jahmir Harris.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: In your city, violent crime has been surging this year. It's up seven percent over last year. That's exceeds the average nationwide. Property crimes are up 30 percent, commercial burglaries are up by 50 percent in addition to homicides.\par \par \uc2 Is this -- I mean, you're kind of testament to the fact this is not anecdotal evidence, right? There are real people behind these statistics. Talk to us about other people in the community who have been affected by violent crime, like you and your brother.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: It's -- now that we this has hit home for the second time, this is the second murder in our family. So, this is really, like horrible. And we're been in contact with a lot of other families that have been victims of violent crimes. And we're fed up and we're not going to take it anymore.\par \par \uc2 The system and everything that's going on, it's broken, and we're here to fix it and \uc2 Larry Krasner\uc2  and his program that he thinks is working, it's not working. And we don't want it anymore. And the community doesn't want it anymore.\par \par \uc2 Get something that's going to help the victims of these violent crimes, not to help the people that are perpetrating these crimes. We're tired of this. It's the same old nonsense and it's not working.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Linda, I know it's really hard to talk about, especially on television. Thank you for doing it, taking time for us this afternoon to lay all of this out. We really send you our best, our condolences, and please stay in touch with us as this case unfold.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Thank you so much. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: You bet.\par \par \uc2 TURNER (voice-over): Well the issues that are turning a House race in deep blue Connecticut, increasingly competitive now, just 18 days out from the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Just coming up next is Democracy 2020 coverage enters its final phase. Stick with us.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEORGE LOGAN (R-CT), CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: We need to secure our southern borders. We need to control the inflow of fentanyl. My opponent hasn't said a word about it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Over 70 Republican candidates are running for a seat in the House this midterm cycle identify as Black, Latino, Asian, or Native American.\par \par \uc2 It's the most diverse group of candidates the party has ever fielded according to the NRCC.\par \par \uc2 TURNER (voice-over): Republican George Logan is challenging Democratic incumbent Jahana Hayes. He is posing a real challenge to the Democrat in a deep blue state.\par \par \uc2 We have Republican candidate George Logan standing by, who will join us in a moment. But first, we got to correspondent Molly Line. Hi, Molly.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY LINE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon. Well, you said, and what's really interesting here is that while the incumbent Democrat may have a bit of an edge here in the Fifth Congressional District in Connecticut, the Republican challenger is making this a very, very competitive race.\par \par \uc2 LINE (voice-over): First to the Democrat, Jahana Hayes became the first black woman to serve in the U.S. House from Connecticut when she won the seat in 2019.\par \par \uc2 The GOP nominee is George Logan, a black man, the son of Guatemalan immigrants, a former state senator.\par \par \uc2 Hayes, a former educator, named Teacher of the Year in 2016 is welcoming some big names like Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  who came here to discuss reproductive health care.\par \par \uc2 But Logan is running as a moderate. His victory could signal return to Congress for pragmatic New England Republicans.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LOGAN: More diversity in any organization makes the organization stronger. And the Republican Party here in Connecticut, and at the national level is doing that. We are welcomed --\par \par \uc2 LINE: The chair of the National Republican Congressional Committee, Congressman \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2  believes a political realignment is underway.\par \par \uc2 REP. TOM EMMER (R-MN): To record number of black candidates, record number of Hispanic candidates, record number of women, I would argue that every minority group in America that is traditionally voted Democrat is available to Republican candidates.\par \par \uc2 LINE: But a spokesman for the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee slammed Republicans for, "Finally engaging with communities of color in the year 2022 with flawed candidates." Writing, "Their politics of dividing Americans and promoting hate remains."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LINE: Now, even if many of these GOP minority candidates achieve victory, Democrats will still have greater numbers on that front. But Republican Party leaders are very proud of this class of candidates, which they feel reflects the diversity of America. Gillian?\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Molly Line in Connecticut for us this afternoon. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Let's go ahead and bring in George Logan, the Republican candidate who's running for election in Connecticut's Fifth Congressional District.\par \par \uc2 Sir, thanks for being with us. Let's pick up where Molly's reporting left off there.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LOGAN: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: The DCCC says that your party is stoking hate and filled fueling, excuse me division. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 LOGAN: It's absolutely ridiculous. Look, the folks here in the Fifth Congressional District, Connecticut, they're concerned about the same things that everyone else is concerned about.\par \par \uc2 Affordability is the issue of the day, community safety. You know, when we take a look at those issues that goes across all communities, whether it is the urban areas of suburban areas, my message is the same, whether I'm in the urban areas or suburban areas or rural areas.\par \par \uc2 The issue is, is that now we are doing more as a party to go into those communities because we share the same values. Family values, we understand free enterprise, truly equal opportunities. And we're bringing that to the folks in our district, and it's going well.\par \par \uc2 Now, look, you look down in Washington, right? The failed policies of the Biden-Harris administration, my Democrat opponent has supported them wholeheartedly.\par \par \uc2 Look at the anti-police bill that was passed in the House down in Washington, right? It tamper with qualified immunity. Here in Connecticut, we had a similar thing happen with the Democrats here.\par \par \uc2 And now, you know, we're weak on crime. More people are targeting our law enforcement. Just today, just this morning, I went to a funeral service where two falling -- a police officers was held.\par \par \uc2 And we had over 20,000 people there. People are tired. They're upset with the Democrat Party who's weak on crime. You know, these repeat offenders just keep coming out and doing more harm.\par \par \uc2 And we're giving the wrong signal to the criminals out there. We need to be tough on crime.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: George, your supporters often say that you are a moderate. It's something that's verging on extinction here in Washington on either side of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 But your political opponent says this is not the case. She says you have actually voted 100 percent of the time in lockstep with Republicans in Washington during your time in the State Senate. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 LOGAN: Sure. Look, here in Connecticut, there are only -- there are only36 state senators. 84 percent of all the bills when I was in the state senate, were bipartisan in nature. So, when it came to social issues, I'm certainly more moderate.\par \par \uc2 But where we differed, as far as Republicans in Connecticut and Democrats has to do on financial issues. There, we are certainly more conservative -- I am more conservative leaning when it comes to the financial issues.\par \par \uc2 And that is what the big difference is here in Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 My opponent, on the other hand, she has voted 100 percent in line with \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's policies. That is not in the best interest of the people, the Fifth Congressional District here in Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 We are building new supporters. Your folks are coming to my -- through our campaign to my web site, GeorgeLoganforcongress.com. We're surging in the polls.\par \par \uc2 We're -- you know, we're speaking for the people of the Fifth Congressional District, and we're being heard. Folks are tired of the status quo, and they want to change and we represent that alternative, and we represent that change.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: George, thanks for taking time for us this afternoon. We wish you all the best.\par \par \uc2 LOGAN: Thank you. Appreciate the opportunity. Take care now.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Of course, you got it.\par \par \uc2 TURNER (voice-over): Well, critics say the Justice Department has a double standard when it comes to prosecuting attacks on pro-life centers and abortion clinics in the wake of the Supreme Court overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Mollie Hemingway has a thought or two on this. She is going to join me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Welcome back. Anti-abortion groups are accusing the Justice Department of a double standard when it comes to prosecuting attacks on pro-life organizations. Dozens of them have been targeted since the leak of the Supreme Court decision overturning Roe v. Wade, according to the \uc2 Family Research Council\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Federalist senior editor Mollie Hemingway is here. But first, we go to justice correspondent David Spunt. He joins us live from the Justice Department. Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Gillian. The Department of Justice is using something called the Freedom of Access to Clinic Entrances Act or the FACE Act. It's a 1994 law that was signed into law by then President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and it's aimed at punishing people who are used or threatened to use force to injure intimidate or interfere with anyone seeking either abortion services or pro-life pregnancy counseling services.\par \par \uc2 Pro-life groups are calling foul because while the Department of Justice has plenty of cases pending in court against those who have attacked abortion clinics, we cannot find one instance where DOJ in the last few months, actually this entire year, Gillian, has charged someone for attacking a pro-life pregnancy center. We're talking about federal charges here.\par \par \uc2 We know that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is seeing an unusual uptick in attacks on pro-life centers following the Supreme Court overturning of Roe v. Wade. We know this is a fact because \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  told us so this summer in an interview, watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER WRAY\uc2 , FBI DIRECTOR: I think there's a right way under our First Amendment to express yourself. And violence and destruction of property is not it. And that's what the rule of law is all about. And we have seen a number of attacks against faith based organizations, pregnancy resource centers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Gillian, 26 people have been indicted under the FACE Act this year, all of them attack pro-choice related businesses or abortion clinics. There are zero indictments on the book this year for attacks on pro-life centers. We've asked the Department of Justice for more. They pointed to their website, which shows the number of arrests. However, I spoke to an official with the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . I am told the \uc2 FBI\uc2  continues to investigate on both sides of this. They don't look at audiology, and they are making these arrests and putting these investigations together. It's just up to DOJ to actually prosecute Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, David Spunt from the Justice Department for us, thank you. Let's bring in Mollie Hemingway. She's senior editor of the Federalist and a Fox News contributor. So Mollie, you heard David's reporting there. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  says this is all just a coincidence, right? Prosecuting 26 concurrent cases against pro-life clinics and zero against pro abortion clinics, so the number of attacks are pretty equal in terms of numbers.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I'm not even sure if there's -- if the comparison works there too, because the attacks on churches and pro-life centers and maternal care centers are frequently violent. There have been fire bombings, there have been, you know, there's been an assassination attempt against a sitting Supreme Court Justice attacks on these people's homes.\par \par \uc2 And then these prosecutions of pro-life protesters, under the FACE Act are of nonviolent protesters. So they are sitting in and blocking clinic access. A lot of people actually think that the FACE Act is unconstitutional. So the Department of Justice is going hard against these non-violent pro-life protesters. But there have been no prosecutions against the violence attacks against pro-life churches and centers.\par \par \uc2 And even though there have been groups that have been taking credit for it, there's this terrorist group called Jane's Revenge that has a tagline that they say, if abortion isn't safe, then neither are you. So there's an actual terrorist group out there claiming responsibility for some of these things. But the Department of Justice does not seem interested.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I think it's important to ask a fundamental question here, which is why there is an uptick in violence and threats against pro-life centers in the first place, meaning, the underlying kind of assumption there is that there's some sort of a connection between centers that choose not to offer abortion services, and the ruling by the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Yes, these maternal care centers are as they sound like groups and places where women who are needing help with their pregnancy or with their young children can get help they can get, you know, clothing and goods and just like support for getting jobs and other things. It is very interesting that Jane's Revenge and other terrorist groups like them, are attacking that choice that some women might make to give birth to their children and raise them.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: You heard there from the \uc2 FBI\uc2  Director of, well, that was from July, right, and he condemned violence against everybody, including pro-life practitioners and the like. I guess my question is, does that suffice, the comments three months ago? Have you seen any other action or verbiage out of the administration to dissuade this kind of violence?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: So when these people testify in Congress, or when they give interviews, they frequently say the right thing. When it comes to actual action, it is bizarre what's happening. And it's not just in the double standard between pro-life protesters and pro-choice protesters. It's also in things like violent riots that we saw in 2020, where federal courthouses, police precincts, the White House, were besieged and violently attacked with very little concern from the Department of Justice relative to the riot at the Capitol, which has been the largest prosecution and investigation in the Department of Justice's history.\par \par \uc2 And so I think most people want bad things prosecuted, they want laws followed. The problem for the Department of Justice is they are almost cartoonishly going after political enemies while protecting political allies.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Take a look at this. This is former Vice President Pence speaking at an event this week. He was booed by protesters at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 . They were essentially protesting his pro-life stance, his anti-abortion stance. As those students left in a fairly large group, the Vice President said, the former vice president said quote, it seems to me that having served many years in Washington leaders in this nation's capitol have never been more at a touch, more intent on imposing their agenda, or walking out on people that might have a different point of view. What do you make of that kerfuffle?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: I think a lot of Americans share this concern. One of the things that people like about America is that you're able to disagree, have debates, tolerate other people, we seem to have lost our -- we seem to have lost our culture of open and free debate. People want to shut down voices or walk out, not listen, that's not good for the future health of our Republic.\par \par \uc2 I do wonder if we're at a tipping point right now with social media companies having become so totalitarian and so censorious, you're starting to see a lot of pushback from people, not just conservatives who tend to be the ones targeted by that, just a lot of normal people saying, this is not what we should have in this country.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Kind of the way that people write about policymakers and politicians on social media, every leading headline is so and so slammed, so and so, you know, like so and so smacks down so and so. The rhetoric is all very agitating.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Right. You're allowed to disagree, and in fact, disagreement can be very, it can help people come to a better policy solution. But it seems that nothing in our media environment is working toward, allowing people to disagree and come to better solutions.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Yes. Well, Mollie, thanks so much for joining me. It's great to have you in person.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: All right, the comments about migrants at San Francisco's Mayor, a Democrat, had to apologize for and how they may be helping fuel Republican gains with Hispanic voters in the state. Congresswoman out of Texas, Mayra Flores joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, San Francisco's mayor now is apologizing for comments she made about immigrants from Honduras, and then dealing drugs. London Breed said it's not racial profiling, but quote, reality. Now she's out with a new statement. Republican Congressman Mayra Flores will join us to react in just a moment. But first, we go correspondent, chief correspondent Jonathan Hunt. He's joining us live from L.A. Hi, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CHIEF CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Gillian, let's get straight to exactly what San Francisco Mayor \uc2 London Breed\uc2  said and what she then apologized for. In an interview with local media, she said she wanted to talk about the reality of the drug situation in the city's tenderloin neighborhood and added quote, there are unfortunately a lot of people who come from a particular country who come from Honduras. And a lot of people who are dealing drugs happen to be of that ethnicity.\par \par \uc2 Now, some in the Latino community were offended by those remarks. And the mayor's denial of accusations that law enforcement officials have been racially profiling in policing the tenderloin neighborhood. So yesterday Breed said sorry issuing a statement that said in part quote, I want to apologize to the Latino community for what I said and how I said it. In trying to explain what is happening in the Tenderloin, I failed to accurately and comprehensively discuss what is an incredibly complex situation in our city and in Central America.\par \par \uc2 Parts of the Tenderloin district have become in essence an open-air drug market. And the mayor has previously admitted that the city has essentially let down residents there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR LONDON BREED (D), SAN FRANCISCO: Kids are walking by things that they shouldn't see on their ways to school. No one should have to see or experience that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: In her apology statement, Breed also said San Francisco officials, including presumably herself, had helped grow the drug problem by not effectively prosecuting dealers and promising more action she said she has a moral obligation to shut down those dealers, easier said than done, Gillian.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Jonathan, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Republican Texas Congressman Mayra Flores. She's currently up for reelection in the States 34th congressional district. She's also facing down Democratic opponent, Congresswoman \uc2 Vicente Gonzalez\uc2 . We've confirmed the date next week to have him on as well. Congresswoman, let's start with Mayor \uc2 London Breed\uc2 . What do you make of her apology? And do you accept it?\par \par \uc2 REP. MAYRA FLORES (R-TX): I don't accept it. She's not really sorry. She's apologizing because of the backlash that she's getting. But she really feels that way. So no, I don't accept her apology. And I find it very disrespectful, that she's making these type of comments. You know, immigrants like myself that come to this country legally, where people have strong faith, and God also has strong values and all about hard work. That is who we are.\par \par \uc2 Yes, we have a drug abuse problem in this country. And it's something that we need to work together both parties, because it's a very serious problem, but we can't be attacking certain ethnicity and put it all on them. I find that very racist and very disrespectful of her to make those type of comments. And that is why border security is so important, you know, our border patrol agents are --\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Yes. And well, speaking of that, speaking of that, let's take a look at this, looking forward to the midterms. This is a list of voters across Texas, their top tier priorities. You can see there inflation is number one as it is leading most national polls nationwide. But immigration is on there. It comes up at 13 percent. Do you feel like these priorities stack up or align with Hispanic voters particularly in your state?\par \par \uc2 FLORES: Absolutely. Border Security is a top issue in my district. We want border security. We want our border patrol agents to be able to enforce the laws and be able to do their job. Border patrol agents are exhausted, tired, handling the humanitarian crisis. They're processing women and children every single day and not able to focus on the drugs like fentanyl coming into our country and ending in the hands of our children, that now it's killing an average of 300 people a day. So this is something that is very serious and I encourage parents to please start talking to their children about this drug and how dangerous it is.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: To your point about CBP officers being overworked and exhausted to that end, we got exclusive reporting. Thanks to internal government documents I was given access to earlier in this week that revealed DHS is now asking for volunteers from every single government department and agency to head to the border to help support migrants. This includes employees of far-ranging agencies from the Department of Justice to the Department of State to Veterans Affairs, what do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 FLORES: This administration is truly an embarrassment. New Yorkers is an embarrassment. They know exactly what they need to do. They need to secure the borders. Put back in place the policies that weren't working before this administration and allow our border patrol agents to do their job.\par \par \uc2 But they continue to put in place policies that encourage people to come to this country illegally, knowing how dangerous it is, knowing that these immigrants are going through, go through abuse, rape and having to pay thousands of dollars to criminal organizations. This administration does not care about immigrants and doesn't care about their lives and they're playing politics with human lives here.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Congresswoman, we got to leave it there. But thanks so much for taking time to us -- for us today. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 FLORES: Thank you. Thank you. God bless you.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well, this breaking now. We have just learned about the teenager accused of killing four of his classmates in a Michigan High School. We're going to bring you the new details coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: This just in, breaking the teenager charged with killing four students at his high school in Michigan almost a year ago is expected to plead guilty next week, this according to one of the prosecutors in the case. Ethan Crumbley was 15 at the time of the shooting, he is now 16. He faces two dozen charges including murder and terrorism. Prosecutors say they expect him to plead guilty to all.\par \par \uc2 And the resignation of U.K.'s Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  is raising massive uncertainty about the future of the country's economy and whether the U.S. could soon see a similar economic meltdown. Here's what President Biden told reporters earlier.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you concerned about the spillover effects of the U.S. economy, given the political and economic?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: No, I don't think they're that consequential.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER: According to "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," the Wall Street's telling the White House and the Federal Reserve, it probably could, though a crash does not appear to be imminent, that's a quote. Let's bring in Nile Gardiner, former aide to \uc2 Margaret Thatcher\uc2  and current director of the Margaret Thatcher Center for Freedom at \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 . Nile, fascinating system of government you guys have over there. It entertains us here in the U.S. no end the degree to which you can have your foremost politician turning around in six weeks. What was behind, what was the fundamental factor behind Truss' undoing. Was it her tax policy? Or was it her sort of dithering and then backtracking?\par \par \uc2 NILE GARDINER\uc2 , FORMER AIDE TO \uc2 MARGARET THATCHER\uc2 : Yes, it's a great question Gillian. And actually, \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 ' tax policies put forward many budget were very good really tax cuts across the board, including corporate taxes, income taxes. And I think this was a very good high growth strategy.\par \par \uc2 The problem for \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  really was if she lost her nerve. She basically surrendered to the siren calls of opposition to her tax cuts coming from, I would say this traditional liberal establishment but also from the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , directly attacked \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  as well. And she basically gave in to all of her critics. Also inside the Conservative Party, there was a coup against her leadership from the left wing of the party. We call them the Tory wets, who didn't like the tax cut agenda.\par \par \uc2 And she then basically caved in. She sat her very good Thatcherite, Chancellor \uc2 Kwasi Kwarteng\uc2 . And that was the beginning of the end of \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  because she lost all credibility. So I don't think there was any problem with her overall agenda. It was the fact that she actually surrendered to those who were basically attacking her and attacking the pro-growth economic agenda of the other \uc2 British government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TURNER: If I understand correctly, her race to replace \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  was predicated on this tax program.\par \par \uc2 GARDINER: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Markets, politicians, everybody was so spooked when she came to office and said right, now I'm putting pen to paper, here are the policies --\par \par \uc2 GARDINER: Right, it was it was no surprise, actually, that she would unveil these tax proposals. And an overall, if you look at the British economy, it's not in a worse state than the U.S. economy under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . If you look at the massive levels of federal debt here in the United States, $31 trillion, the huge levels of spending here, the soaring inflation, I wouldn't say the U.K. is actually worse at all off than the United States under the Biden presidency.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Well careful. I'm going to end this segment right now. I do not like where this is going. But is it is that threat real here? I mean, economists, financial analysts are in a bit of a tailspin now trying to figure that out.\par \par \uc2 GARDINER: I think the Biden economy is absolutely disastrous. And many Conservative MPs that I've spoken to were very, very upset by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's attacks on \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 . And their view is that Biden is the last person on Earth to be lecturing the British about economic policy. And in fact, the United States really should pursue a low tax high growth agenda, rather than endlessly spending money, borrowing money, and fueling, of course, the inflation that we see here in the United States. So certainly, the Brits are very upset by the Biden intervention. And they thought he should really mind his own business.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Right now, we got to leave it there. But thanks so much for taking time.\par \par \uc2 GARDINER: It's my pleasure thanks very much.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: We appreciate it. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 GARDINER: You too.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Have a great weekend at home everybody. This is the story of Friday October 21st. As always the story goes on. Martha will be back Monday at 3:00 p.m. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020221022eial00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Twitter\uc2  Stock Drops; Russia Continues Drone Attacks on Ukraine; Hispanic Voters Moving Toward Republicans?; Recession Worries; Interview With William Shatner; Interview With Gov. Asa Hutchinson (R-AR)\b0\par\par\uc2 Trey Yingst, Charlie Gasparino, Peter Doocy, Daron Shaw, Alicia  Acuna, Jackie DeAngelis, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 7,492 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: So, what do you think these guys know that the president does not? A growing list of business titans sounding on the alarm of the economy and warning to batten down the hatches, because they say a recession is coming, and it could be a nasty one.\par \par \uc2 This as the president continues to insist the economy is strong as hell, even as many worry it could be going to hell.\par \par \uc2 We have got you covered with Jackie DeAngelis on those CEOs sounding the alarm, Alicia Acuna in the battleground state of Colorado on if voters are alarmed, Arkansas Governor Asa Hutchinson on that pricey student loan bailout that's got him alarmed, all that and William Shatner on some out- of-this-world developments he said should have everyone alarmed, back from space and back with us.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 So glad to have you on a very, very busy news day. Let's get right to it on those warnings with Jackie DeAngelis -- Jackie.\par \par \uc2 JACKIE DEANGELIS, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon to you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 That's right. Look, while there are many people out there still arguing whether or not we're in a technical recession, the CEOs of big banks and other prominent business titans are forecasting economic trouble to come, with the midterms only 18 days away. And, of course, this is top of mind for many voters out there.\par \par \uc2 This week, \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  CEO \uc2 David Solomon\uc2  warned that there is -- quote -- "a good chance" that the U.S. economy falls into recession. He also thinks there is more volatility on the horizon. He said it's time to be cautious. Solomon explained that inflation is more embedded and growth is slower, so asset appreciation will be tougher, also noting that policy decisions will have a forward impact.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 , the CEO of \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2 , had previously said that investors should brace for an economic hurricane. Last week, he said the U.S. is likely headed for a recession by mid-2023. Dimon cited steeper interest rates, runaway inflation and the war in Ukraine as reasons for his outlook.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 , \uc2 Amazon\uc2 's founder and chairman, also sounding the warning bells, writing up on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : "Yep, the probabilities in this economy tell you to batten down the hatches," while \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  warned \uc2 Tesla\uc2  executives this month that he had a super bad feeling about the economy.\par \par \uc2 A common thread here, Neil, is that whether you're a bank CEO or a leading consumer business, there's going to be a downturn. How steep, how long it lasts is debatable at this point. These captains of industry have their fingers on the pulse of American businesses and consumers, and they're also impacted directly by things like higher interest rates, worker shortages and higher commodity prices.\par \par \uc2 So they're feeling the effects of policy firsthand -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It makes sense.\par \par \uc2 Jackie DeAngelis, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 Well, Jackie told you how some of the high and mighty feel things are going on, but wouldn't you know average folks are concerned about the exact same thing, inflation, runaway inflation, at that, and prices that know no bounds, a big topic of concern, in fact, the topic with the midterm elections but 18 days away.\par \par \uc2 To Alicia Acuna in Denver -- Alicia.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ACUNA, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Yes, Colorado is no different than other parts of the country. Inflation is the number one issue for voters here, driven in large part by the housing market. That's because the housing market in Colorado has been cooling at a faster clip than most other places in the country.\par \par \uc2 Now, let's take a broader look across the nation right now. A \uc2 Monmouth University\uc2  poll found the top issue of adults sample to be led by inflation, no surprise there, followed by elections and voting and also crime.\par \par \uc2 They also asked folks their preference as to which party they want to control Congress. Republicans came in at 49 percent and Democrats 45. That's encouraging news for candidates like Colorado Republican Joe O'Dea, who is trying to unseat Democratic incumbent Senator Michael Bennet.\par \par \uc2 O'Dea is a political newcomer and a businessman who is hammering his opponent on the economy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE O'DEA (R), COLORADO SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: People are feeling it right now. They have lost 10 percent of their buying power over the course of the last year because we have got Democrats like Michael Bennet and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  that are just completely out of touch; $5 trillion in the market over a year-and-a-half, that's put a lot of pressure on people.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MICHAEL BENNET (D-CO): Here in Colorado, a gallon of milk costs $4.20. That's really tough on people. And I think one of the things I have been trying to explain to folks is that this is a global problem.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Bennet leads Joe O'Dea in polling and recently appeared with the president during \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's Western state swing.\par \par \uc2 But not all candidates want photo-ops with the man with low approval numbers.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Sir, why don't more candidates want to be seen in public with you like Mr. Fetterman?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: What are you talking about?\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  in Ohio said he doesn't want you there. Warnock said -- wouldn't say. Do you think they're making a mistake?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, there have been 16 I have already done. And we're getting a lot more asked, another 20 or so. I'm going to be going there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: So, Neil, you know, yesterday, we heard the president from the White House say that, as he expects the economy will improve, so will the chances for his party in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 That's a really big lift to happen on an 18-day timeline -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Did we ever find out who those 16 candidates were the president alluded to that would want his appearance?\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: No, that's a really good question, because there are a lot of candidates out there, and Michael Bennet was one of them for a while, who actually wouldn't answer the question as to whether or not they wanted to appear with the president. So getting a solid answer on that question is quite elusive, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Alicia, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Sure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, what is the fallout of this with that election so close, 18 days away, as we say?\par \par \uc2 Daron Shaw joins us right, now FOX News Decision Desk, Tiana Lowe with us from The Washington Examiner.\par \par \uc2 Tiana, it's all about -- and I get it -- the economy. But I'm always wondering if there is some seminal event or some sort of breaking event that breaks in a big way what otherwise seems like a tight battle, for example, for the Senate. We saw that in 1994, the midterm election in which we were expecting Republicans to do well, and the so-called Gingrich Revolution ended up being a lot better than well.\par \par \uc2 Similarly, in 1980 between \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2  and \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , when they were statistically almost tied the weekend before the election, then it broke all \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 's way days later. Any signs like that?\par \par \uc2 TIANA LOWE, THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER: No.\par \par \uc2 So, the fundamentals, obviously, have always favored Republicans, because Democrats are in control of both parties of Congress and the White House. Inflation is very high. We are now sort of in what I think most of us acknowledge is a bear market. And I think, I would personally say we are in a stagflationary period.\par \par \uc2 Democrats hoped that they could center abortion around this, but even at the peak of when abortion dominated the news in July, Gallup found that only 8 percent of voters said that it was what was motivating their vote. Now it's 4 percent. It really now is the economy, stupid.\par \par \uc2 Credit card balances are growing at their fastest clip in 20 years. Credit card balances are up 13 percent from this time last year, and you're already seeing, independent of the fears of a recession that the Fed will bring about, you are already seeing demand contraction purely because of this 8.2 percent inflation.\par \par \uc2 A lot of this gas price abatement that the White House has been touting has directly to do with the fact that consumer demand for gas fell to the lowest point since July of 2020. So, it already -- it's as though voters understand that they either have to save or they have to pay down debt, because we do know that credit card rates are going up, mortgages are going up.\par \par \uc2 And so, no, really, what we -- in terms of these polls tightening, it's really just been a lot of those undecided voters, those so-called double haters that Trump lost in 2020, they're really coming home to the GOP in 2022.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We will see.\par \par \uc2 Daron, is there any evidence of that? I mean, we're going to go through some of the tight Senate races here. As you know, this is the week RealClearPolitics there is a good shot that Republicans do, in fact, take the Senate. I think they see Georgia flipping and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  winning it in a run-off election.\par \par \uc2 I think they require at least 50 percent of the vote to get to that. But what are your thoughts? And what would galvanize something like that?\par \par \uc2 DARON SHAW, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, I think that the comment that was made earlier and at the top of the hour, correct, that the fundamentals are sort of asserting themselves.\par \par \uc2 And you mentioned some of the macroeconomic numbers. The micro numbers, that is, the opinions of individual level voters, are really coming through in the last couple of weeks. Just, for example, in our latest FOX poll, we asked people to rate the economy; 78 percent rated the economy as only fair or poor. Only 21 percent said the economy is getting better; 73 percent said it's getting worse.\par \par \uc2 And 71 percent of voters said they personally had to cut back in order to deal with the price crunch in inflation. One number, Neil, I will leave you with here as we move to the next topic, but amongst voters who disapprove of Biden on the economy, so voters who don't think he's doing a good job on the economy, right now, Republicans are winning 62 percent to 19 percent in the generic ballot.\par \par \uc2 Now, that's a huge number. But notice 18 percent of those voters who disapprove of Biden on the economy are currently undecided or toying with a third-party candidate. I think what might happen is that those voters could break, and they're likely to break for the GOP.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So let's play that out, then, Tiana.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that Democrats have been counting on and you touched on in your remarks earlier on this, the fallout from the anger from a lot of Democratic voters who heretofore might not have been inclined to vote in a midterm election, but would be because of anger over that Roe v. Wade reversal on the part of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Conversely, though, there are just as many Republicans angry at them, let's say, for doing that, and they might show up. I don't know if that is any of that stuff that's coming up in early voting that we're seeing out of Georgia, where it's record numbers, we're told, but what do you make of these other issues?\par \par \uc2 LOWE: So, clearly, it is the economy, stupid, especially when things are this bad. And crime and immigration wind up becoming economic issues, when you see, like in New York City, where Mayor Eric Adams is dedicating all of these public resources to tents and supplies to make sure these migrants who are still undocumented are not just homeless, not just taking up public spaces.\par \par \uc2 So when you let sort of this broken windows policy of both immigration and of crime take reign, it does become an economic issue, because it is more social spending going into things. But, really, it just comes down to, the fact is, from the start of Biden's presidency, there has been 13 percent cumulative CPI rate.\par \par \uc2 That means that for the average American who has not gotten cost of living pay increase, their paycheck covers 13 percent less than it did in January 2021. That's enough. You may dislike \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's probably not the one campaigning in your swing state, right?\par \par \uc2 You may disagree with what some Republicans have to say about more extreme abortion positions. But if you hear \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 's 15-week abortion ban, that's more liberal than what they have in France, right?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 LOWE: Then you can really pivot back to just focusing on the economic priority. And on that one, on that measure, Republicans have a very strong double-digit advantage.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We shall see. Still early in the go-round.\par \par \uc2 I apologize for breaking out of this a little bit early.\par \par \uc2 Wanted to alert our viewers to something you're not imagining at the corner of your screen there, a big surge in the Dow today, up better than 748 points. Now, it does seem to be a bit of an anomaly when we're talking about an economy that could be collapsing or at least staggering. But it's precisely because of that that the market seized on a possibility and a market rumor, some say, in fact, leaked by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  itself -- hard to prove this sort of thing -- that it is less inclined to keep up the pace of rate increases we have always seen.\par \par \uc2 The betting is that we will see a three-quarter-percent hike in interest rates when the Fed gathers next month. But this idea that we'd see an identical hike, which would be a fifth three-quarter-point hike in December, that seems to have eased a little bit.\par \par \uc2 And the betting is that the Fed is quasi-pivoting here, or hinting of that, they don't want to overdo it. Now, again, it's in the eye of the beholder, and we see these wild shifts day by day. But this shift today was noteworthy, because now they think that the Fed is fearing overdoing it. It's in the eye of the beholder.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, more goodies being planned by the president of the United States, as he outlines just a couple of them.\par \par \uc2 To Peter Doocy at the White House on that.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: And Biden is celebrating today that his plan to forgive or cancel some student loan debt keeps surviving in court.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: But, just yesterday, state court and the Supreme Court said, no, we're on Biden's side.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I will never apologize for helping working and middle-class Americans as they recover from the pandemic, especially not to the same Republican officials who voted for a $2 trillion tax cut that mainly benefited the wealthy Americans and the biggest corporations that wasn't paid for and racked up our deficit.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: As the midterms age closer, President Biden is also now field- testing a new souped-up way to describe Republican policies that he thinks won't work.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Mr. President, ahead of these midterms, how big of an issue is crime?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think it's a real issue. And I think we have a great record on it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And there you had the president talking about an issue rising in importance in polls, but that we haven't heard him talk a ton about, at least not lately.\par \par \uc2 We do also have a bite of the president describing Republican policies that we have not heard before.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It's mega MAGA trickle down, mega MAGA trickle down, the kind of policies that have failed the country before and will fail it again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: So, President Biden might not be spending as much time on the campaign trail as some of his predecessors, but he can get the campaign lines out from here -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Peter Doocy, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Mega MAGA policies that didn't work, and his are going to work better, that's the read from the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 Asa Hutchinson, the Arkansas governor, maybe he has a slight disagreement with that.\par \par \uc2 Governor, good to see you again.\par \par \uc2 He's essentially saying, sir, his policies are better than Republican policies, that you guys would make inflation worse if you were running the ship. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 GOV. ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): Well, he's certainly in campaign style. And I don't think it's a message that sells.\par \par \uc2 Everyone understands, fundamentally, this is a Biden inflation. It started with bad energy policy, jacking up the energy costs for every American. And then it goes into the supply chain and all of the food costs as well. So, he is the president of the United States. He's responsible for this. And he can't avoid it during this season.\par \par \uc2 And whenever you look at him passing out goodies, as you said, it is wrong substantively for the forgiveness of the loans. It's bad policy. It is not fair treatment. It penalizes those that have worked hard during college not to go into that debt. It transfers the debt to other Americans.\par \par \uc2 And I don't think it's good politics as well. We will see about that. But he's in full campaign mode. And I don't think he has a message that sells.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Do you have a message?\par \par \uc2 If Republicans do take over the Senate and the House, as some seem to think is a distinct possibility, they can grab both, that what happened to \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  this past week is a reminder that markets can be cruel, that they can look at tax cuts that have to be paid for just as much as spending, and, if they're not, they will punish who's ever in power?\par \par \uc2 What do you think?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Actually, I think it is a good lesson to be learned.\par \par \uc2 Whenever people are suffering from inflation, as they are in the U.K. at a 10 percent rate, and they don't see an economic policy that works, they hold public officials accountable for it. The unique thing is that, generally, they give them more time to work through those economic issues.\par \par \uc2 And, sometimes, tax cuts are short-term pain and long-term benefit to the economy. I think you saw that during the early days of the Reagan administration. His popularity went down, but he had a four-year term. And, of course, the economy came back because of those tax cuts.\par \par \uc2 And so there is something to look at. You also look at state by state, and states that have enacted tax cuts responsibly, where you can continue the services and it grows the economy, that's rewarded by the taxpayers. But they want to see responsible action.\par \par \uc2 And, right now, you have first got to get inflation under control. And then you look at spurring this economy and kicking it back in gear.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You talk about kicking things back in gear, but, as you know, sir, there's a global slowdown going on. And that, more than anything, might have been responsible for the markets racing ahead.\par \par \uc2 They actually see that kind of bad news as good news, in that it means the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  needn't hike interest rates so much. What do you think of that?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: Well, I saw the analysis that they're baking in the fact that perhaps the interest rates are not going up as high as what we anticipated the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  would do.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: But I also think that markets will simply respond to what they see as better results, but also a better plan.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that it's the interest rates that they're responding to now. From an economic standpoint, we're still pretty strong in Arkansas. We still see some signs of a slowdown.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: But we're hopeful, because we kept our businesses open, we will continue to be strong.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, Governor. Very good seeing you again.\par \par \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\uc2 , the Republican governor of Arkansas.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime here, another big issue is what's going on at the border. But many Democrats had hoped that an open border policy, at least some see it, would help them with a Latino vote. That appears not to be the case. In fact, maybe for the first time in history, it might go all the way to the other party, a majority of Latinos voting Republican -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Remember when William Shatner came back from space and he was a little emotional?\par \par \uc2 It had nothing to do with what he saw up there, but looking back at the planet he had just come from -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We're told that there's a lot of interest in the midterm elections this year.\par \par \uc2 Normally, they don't generate the kind of interest a presidential election does, but try telling that to Georgia voters, who, in record numbers, are taking advantage of early voting in the Peach State right now, and a good many of them Latino voters, who might tip this thing for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in the Senate race there.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Senator Phil Gramm, the former Texas senator, John Early, a mathematical economist who has looked at a lot of the stuff that's going on beneath the surface. And it's a fascinating examination of a vote that used to be almost solidly Democrat that might not be so much anymore.\par \par \uc2 Senator Phil Gramm, to you first. What are you finding out?\par \par \uc2 FMR. SEN. PHIL GRAMM (R-TX): Well, basically, in the last 50 years, the income of the Hispanic community has grown 20 percent faster than the country as a whole.\par \par \uc2 Hispanics are underrepresented in the bottom 20 percent of earners, where 90 percent of income comes from government transfer payments and welfare. They're 21 percent overrepresented in the American middle class, where 87 percent of work-age persons work. So Hispanics today are middle-class Americans.\par \par \uc2 They work hard for a living. And the idea that Democrats, by hiring Hispanic consultants and running outreach to tell people what Biden has done for them, they know what Biden's done for them. They go to the grocery store. They buy gasoline. What they want is opportunity.\par \par \uc2 And what we're seeing in Texas and, I believe, all over the country is a wholesale movement of middle-income Hispanics who are voting like older middle-income Americans.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: If that is the case, John Early, mathematically, it would go against anything we have seen in the past.\par \par \uc2 And especially given the border situation, when the knee-jerk reaction would be, well, of course, Latino voters would be offended by all the pounding on illegal migrants getting into this country, when, in fact, it appears just the opposite.\par \par \uc2 JOHN EARLY, MATHEMATICAL ECONOMIST: Well, I think one thing too important to remember is that this so-called Hispanic community is a very diverse community.\par \par \uc2 But at its core and at its center, it's a very middle-income community. It tends to have more of its adults working. They work longer hours. As Senator Gramm was explaining, they're also more likely to be of middle- class incomes than the average person. While they are less prevalent in the upper income levels, they are also less prevalent in the lowest income levels.\par \par \uc2 So why would we expect them to behave differently or react differently to the economic policies than middle-income hardworking Americans from other ethnic groups?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, if you think about just that, Phil Gramm, it might be a cool and cold reminder for Democrats who have been pushing an open border policy, or at least the perception that they're not too concerned about what's happening at the border -- many have since urged the president to do something about it -- but that they might have bet incorrectly on that and how the Latino vote would go.\par \par \uc2 GRAMM: Well, I think they have been guessing incorrectly for a long time.\par \par \uc2 But the bottom line is, this idea that you can get Hispanic vote by giving people more public benefits is an outmoded idea that may have had some sway 25 years ago. But Hispanics are middle-income Americans. They work hard. They want opportunity. They take welfare less than (AUDIO GAP) people do in America.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 GRAMM: And so the idea that you're going to buy their vote with more government benefits, the Democrats in South Texas are running on a platform, we're going to bring home the bacon at levels never seen in history.\par \par \uc2 Well, Hispanics have figured out that bacon is coming out of the smokehouse. They work for a living. And they want opportunity. And in South Texas, the Republican candidates are presenting a clear choice. They're presenting the American dream. The Democrats are presenting more government welfare.\par \par \uc2 And there's not a clearer choice in America than that. And it looks to me - - we won't know until the votes are counted -- that Hispanics are going as middle income Americans have increasingly been voting in recent elections. They're going to vote for America, for opportunity, for freedom.\par \par \uc2 And I think...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, Phil Gramm, while I still have you, I do really very quickly want to get your reaction.\par \par \uc2 President Trump has talked to our Brian Kilmeade a little while ago. While he didn't tip his hand as to whether he's going to run for president, he did raise the issue that it would be disloyal for anyone else to run if he chose to.\par \par \uc2 Quoting from the president: "Many of them have said that they would never run if I run. So we will see whether or not that turns out to be true. I think it would be very disloyal if they did, but that's OK, too. And the polls have me leading by 40 or 50 points."\par \par \uc2 But this issue of what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is saying, if he runs, it would be disloyal if others do, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GRAMM: Well, that's almost laughable. You're going to have a lot of people run for president.\par \par \uc2 I think America is ready for a change. I think it's ready for a change in both parties. I think you're going to have a competitive primary. And I think this election is going to set the predicate, because, based on everything I see, Neil, I believe Republicans are going to win control of the House and Senate.\par \par \uc2 And I think this idea that Hispanics had been promoting that, demographically, because of the explosive growth in the Hispanic community, that they were destined to be the majority party in America, has turned out to be a pipe dream.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But back to my Trump question, sir, you do not think it's disloyal for other Republicans to run if the former president decides to run himself?\par \par \uc2 GRAMM: Let me be clear. No, I don't think it's disloyal.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK. All right.\par \par \uc2 GRAMM: You run for president because you think you can provide service. And there are a lot of people who believe they can do a better job than Biden, a better job than Trump did. And they're going to run.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 GRAMM: And people are going to listen to what they have to say.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 All right guys, I want to thank you both. I apologize for extending it. I just wanted to get your thoughts on that.\par \par \uc2 We will have more right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, definitely not your kids' drones being used in Ukraine right now, very sophisticated, some say surprisingly sophisticated drones made in Iran that are being used by the Russians to attack, well, largely regular citizen centers across Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Trey Yingst in Kyiv with more -- Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Neil, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 The war has entered a new dangerous phase, as Iranian-made drones become the most significant threat for the Ukrainian people. We have seen the destruction these drones can do on a city like the capital of Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 This week, dozens were fired here. And while most were shot down, those that slammed into residential areas killed a number of people and injured dozens more. This is why Ukraine is calling for more air defense systems to be delivered, specifically that anti-drone technology.\par \par \uc2 It does come amid another concerning development about Iran that's being reported out of Crimea, where U.S. officials believe the Iranian military has personnel on the ground. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NED PRICE, STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESMAN: We assess that Iranian personnel, Iranian military personnel were on the ground in Crimea and assisted Russia in these operations.\par \par \uc2 Russia has received dozens of these UAVs so far and will likely continue to receive additional shipments in the future.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: The U.K. and the E.U. are trying to put economic pressure on Iran, sanctioning three individuals and a business that supply these drones to Russia.\par \par \uc2 But it may not be enough. Reports to indicate Russia is running out of precision-guided munition and that they may look to buy surface-to-surface missiles from the Iranians -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, Trey, for that.\par \par \uc2 Is there anything, by the way, that William Shatner cannot do? Oldest man in space, checkered and amazing career, and some reflections in a book that really makes you think, because he wants us to think.\par \par \uc2 He's coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  stock tumbling today. And a lot of people are trying to figure out whether it means that \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  -- or all of this is happening because \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  might have some plans that don't jibe with, well, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  shareholders.\par \par \uc2 Charlie Gasparino, what's going on?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE GASPARINO, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Not just \uc2 Twitter\uc2  shareholders, Neil. It's the federal government.\par \par \uc2 There's a big body of -- a big agency and the federal government that a lot of people don't know about. It's called \uc2 CFIUS\uc2 , the \uc2 Committee on Foreign Investment in the U.S\uc2 . And what they are threatening, or at least opening up an investigation, into whether Musk's use of foreign investors might violate some sort of a national security edict.\par \par \uc2 And, listen, this is the committee that Trump used -- essentially was attempting to use to get TikTok banned from the U.S. And they do have power.\par \par \uc2 Now, whether minority interest in a company, in a takeover, because, remember, he will -- he will be the majority owner -- Binance, which is a Chinese company, a couple -- I think Qatar's sovereign wealth fund and maybe -- and Prince Alwaleed of Saudi Arabia would be minority investors, but they wouldn't be owners, OK?\par \par \uc2 So whether that qualifies as something that \uc2 CFIUS\uc2  can stop is unclear. But, clearly, this is being investigated. \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  was first to report it. I understand it as well. And it could be serious. But, again, we don't know. I don't know how far \uc2 CFIUS\uc2  is -- but I don't think anybody knows really how far \uc2 CFIUS\uc2 ' power. Can it extend to minority owners in a bigger deal, where the majority owner is clearly someone who has national security clearance?\par \par \uc2 Starlink is -- forced \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  to get national security clearance. And he has it to do those spaceflights. So, why minority interest would prevent this from happening is beyond me, but they are looking at it, Neil -- back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He has, also reported, been looking at getting rid of a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Up to three-quarters of the work force. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: That is absolutely true.\par \par \uc2 Neil, look at it this way. He's paying $44 billion, way overpaying for this thing. It doesn't make money. It has lousy cash flow. I think he's paying $40 billion more than it's worth. And I'm like -- I'm not the only one who thinks that this is not a company that is very desirable on a lot of levels.\par \par \uc2 If that's the case, in order to make the numbers work, you have got to lay people off. The problem that he's going to have, Neil, is that, despite the fact that \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is a lousy business, it's extremely capital-intensive, because you got to monitor the bots. You got to monitor bad behavior.\par \par \uc2 There's a lot that goes into essentially putting a tweet out. And now he wants to put a bug in there that allows you to edit your tweets. That's very capital-intensive. So, how do you sort of justify laying off people and expanding \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and keeping it up to date is going to be difficult.\par \par \uc2 But that's where it is from a financial standpoint.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, my friend, very much, Charlie Gasparino.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, I always, always admired William Shatner as a kid, of course, from "Star Trek," but it's all the stuff he's done since then, and I mean a world of stuff, including going into space, including a message of keeping this planet clean and beautiful, that's impressed me all the more.\par \par \uc2 But now here he comes along with a book where he really gets deep and personal and lets you know what he's thinking. Not what you think from Captain Kirk -- after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: This one tweet: "Can't believe he's 82. Ask how he does it."\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM SHATNER, AUTHOR, "BOLDLY GO": I can't believe I'm 82 either.\par \par \uc2 It's genetic. And keep yourself open, I guess, and find yourself a $1,000 stock.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: You put your head down and you plod.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Is that your philosophy? Just keep going at it?\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: I don't know. There's no philosophy.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: I feel good. I want to create something, I think of something to do. Somebody says, hey, I will do that. And we're off.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, when you get a chance to be up there for the 10 minutes that you will, what's the first thing you're going to want to do?\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: I'm going to look out the window, Neil. I'm going to see the vastness of space and the fragility of the Earth.\par \par \uc2 And I'm going to continue that theme in my thinking and in my work.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, and, in my work, one of the greatest delights is talking to people I have always admired.\par \par \uc2 And as, a kid, certainly, William Shatner would be that. But it went way beyond just as a kid, because it went way, way after his famous role as Captain Kirk on "Star Trek."\par \par \uc2 It's how he has carried himself with that fame, and how he's shared it with the world, and his passion for keeping the world the beautiful place it is, and then boldly going into space, quite literally, about a year ago, as a matter of fact, on \uc2 Blue Origin\uc2 , \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2 ' rocket ship.\par \par \uc2 He was 90 years old at the time, 91 years young today. And he was kind enough to join us.\par \par \uc2 William Shatner, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: Good to see you. Pleasure to talk to you again.\par \par \uc2 You were playing that-year-old dialogue, and I'd forgotten, long since forgotten that I had said that. But it turned out to be true.\par \par \uc2 This book, "Boldly Go," which I'm talking to people about now because it's just out, I think it's about our entanglement with the universe. And I try to portray it in my own life, stories about how I think the universe has been taking care of me, especially in the musical pattern and -- but, more than that, this incredible conjoining that we have with nature.\par \par \uc2 And people take it for granted. And, sometimes, they don't think about it. Sometimes, they don't believe it. But we are intrinsically involved in everything in the universe. And not to keep that in mind and not to be aware of how fragile our Earth is, and, as you and I are speaking now, so many, maybe millions of entities are going extinct, I don't understand.\par \par \uc2 So that's what my book is about. And I think it's entertaining. It got some great news and reviews and all that. And I'm delighted with it. I'm delighted to talk to you about it.\par \par \uc2 But even more important than that, much more important than that, is the concept that we're losing our Earth. And we need -- I don't understand why there isn't a Manhattan Project, with all the incredible industrial might that we have in the world, as scientists have, why there isn't a Manhattan Project to deal with global warming.\par \par \uc2 I don't understand that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, and, by the way, that was one of the things you really -- you came back. You were quite emotional from that flight.\par \par \uc2 But I found it fascinating the reason why you were. You wrote about it that: "I had a different experience, because I discovered that the beauty isn't out there," referring to space. "It's down here with all of us. Leaving that behind made my connection to our tiny planet even more profound."\par \par \uc2 In other words, you looked back and said, it is fragile. It is hanging by a thread.\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: Indeed, and it does hang by a thread.\par \par \uc2 And it's a gossamer thread. It's not a rope or anything like that. And so here we are in this business segment of yours, and I just puzzle why there are industries -- and there are a few -- why there aren't so many more striving to do what they can to take the carbon dioxide, the methane, the pollution out of the water and the air, because, if we don't do that, we're going to be severely affected.\par \par \uc2 And it would seem to me to make good business, because we're going to need it. To be in this insanity about a war and people who can't come to some conclusion about our mutual fate, it's just -- it's just insane.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, no, but that's what caught me in this book. I was thinking, a lot of people might think, oh, this is a real uplifting book.\par \par \uc2 Actually -- and no offense to you -- but it's profound, in that it is not that kind of book. In fact, it's kind of sad. You talk about the fragility of your own life.\par \par \uc2 You start saying: "I'm aware that it's my final day, when it inevitably comes, and I will be filled with sadness and questions: Did I spend enough time with my family, with my friends, with my beloved horses, with my precious dogs?" as if you seem to be saying, like you have said something, that this is all ending.\par \par \uc2 And you were -- you're in great shape. You look fantastic. You're articulate as hell. Please tell me you're not down and depressed here.\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: No, no.\par \par \uc2 I am -- I feel as vital as ever. But I do know that 91, coming 92 in a few months, is a sentence.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: Look around me. People are falling away.\par \par \uc2 It's a -- it's a -- we're all going to die. And I'm going to die a lot sooner than you, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, I don't know about that. You have survived everyone. You have survived everyone.\par \par \uc2 But I'm just wondering. That whole experience of going into space and the way you looked at things, and I'm going back and remembering your career. You talk about this going full circle on your songwriting and your singing that was honored at the Kennedy Center, no less. And I can remember, some decades back, when it was hardly the case.\par \par \uc2 So, things that were dismissed years ago in your career are now high watermarks for you. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: I just think that -- that the -- we understand that the universe is vibrating, we're part of the universe vibrating, and this entanglement with everything.\par \par \uc2 I think there's ways of communicating to that vibration, whether it's prayer, whether it's -- whether it's thought, whether it's awareness, which I think it is, being aware all the time of how we're connected, and communicating with that, with nature and the universe.\par \par \uc2 It sounds crazy and amorphous. But I think it applies to our everyday life, and especially to the Earth and our position with the Earth.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Did you ever think, though, Bill, that you would be this iconic person? I mean, in the "Halloween" series, I learned that the Michael Myers character, that was a Captain Kirk mask he was wearing.\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I don't know whether you get residuals from any of those movies.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that's pretty amazing.\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: Well, it's -- it's amazing in its way. And, of course, I'm not sure I understand it. They sent somebody out to buy a mask, and whoever it was grabbed a mask.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: And -- but that may be the universe taking care of...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Who knew. Who knew.\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: So, who knows.\par \par \uc2 But the facts are that the -- we are intricately linked. And we have to recognize that. And coming from a business point of view, there are companies who are starting -- start-up companies that are trying to do things like take the carbon out of the air.\par \par \uc2 These are wonderful companies worth looking for and investing in and trying to make them work, because the ones that use all the resources of the Earth are limited, because our resources are limited.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 It's been your life mission. Gene Roddenberry, of course, his ashes were released in space. You have been in space. You're not interested in that, are you? Real quick.\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: Well, I -- the space. I was up there and saw the curvature of the Earth...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: ... saw the beauty of the Earth.\par \par \uc2 And so I write -- I have written about it. And...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And didn't need that.\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: And what I -- what?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You didn't need that.\par \par \uc2 I wish we had more time, Bill. Bottom line, you prefer that your ashes be buried here, a tree atop you, and life goes on.\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: That's right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It is...\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: I want to be buried with a tree on top of me.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's a worthy goal.\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: I want to be sure I'm dead first.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: A long time from now. A long time from now.\par \par \uc2 William Shatner, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SHATNER: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 