2 LAURA INGRAHAM, FOX NEWS HOST: How are you?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I'm good. You're cheery. How are you?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You should have seen what was just happening in here. OK. More like people swirling around. Well, I can't - I'm trying to hear you. I think I can hear you. But now we just had a swirl of activity. You know, it's like the Tin Man and The Wizard of Oz, you had to like brush him up before he, like, went out. We had to do that.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know what I'm beginning to get the impression of that you're like me. I get in the seat maybe 30 seconds before airtime.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I've seen you do that.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I'm beginning to think that you do the same thing.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, in radio I used to actually call into my own radio show from the elevator on the way. It was bad.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Did you really? Oh, man!\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, I have a problem with punctuality. It's a terrible flaw.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I don't know what it is. I have to - I think it's an adrenaline thing. I wait till the last second no matter what.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right. Well, we'll try to pick it up where you left off. And I can't wait for your town hall on Monday. Can't wait. All right, my friend.\par \par \uc2 I am Laura Ingraham. This is an INGRAHAM ANGLE from very busy Washington tonight. "Falling Stars" that's the focus of "Tonight's Angle."\par \par \uc2 Now how many times has the regime media told us that some emerging Democrat is going to be the next big thing, the next big star. Now on rare occasion, as in 2004, when Obama spoke at the DNC, the buildup paid off. But that happens maybe once in a generation.\par \par \uc2 Back in 2020, they desperately wanted Kamala to be the next Obama. She was a former prosecutor, tough, no nonsense, a woman of color.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: Your city council made that decision. It was a local decision.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: So that's where the federal government must step in.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Now, the federal government must--\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: That's why we have the Voting Rights Act and the Civil Rights Act. That's why we need to pass the Equality Act. That's why we need to pass the ERA because there are moments in history where states fail to preserve the civil rights of all people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, we all know where that all ended up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: What we need to do domestically, what we need to do to bring down the cost of gas? Well, right? Right?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I've seen a meme the other day that says, me Googling online how to make gas at home. OK.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Oh, don't do that. Don't do that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: That was hilarious. Now the border may be open, Ukraine might be a mess, but she still has that cackle. This is so sad for America. But this is what happens when firsts and diversity are given more weight than talent and merit. But the press doesn't care, right? They think they're smarter than Joe Q. Public and when they say someone's a star, that someone should win, period.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOUG HEYE, CNN POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  is a rock star. We already know that.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY BALL, NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, TIME: She is a rising star in the party, despite having lost that election. She's a very charismatic politician.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You have \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  , who I would argue, is not a rising star of the party, but already established as a leader of the party.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, really? Well, how's that all worked out? Governor Brian Kemp is going to beat her handily. He's up almost 6 percent in the RCP average in Georgia and has all the momentum.\par \par \uc2 And who can forget that former punk rocker turned political boy wonder from Texas?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Texas Democrats call it Betomania. And in the last days of this \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  campaign, \uc2 Beto O'Rourke\uc2  appearances are designed to generate rock star style pandemonium.\par \par \uc2 MARK THOMSON, SIRUSXM PROGRESS: Beto, though, has been a rock star ever since he ran in Texas. There's so many comparisons to Obama.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: He is a rock star among Democratic politics. He's a national figure. He can nationalize this race.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's such original commentary, isn't it? Apparently losing to Ted Cruz is not a sign he'll have better luck running for governor, because Governor Greg Abbott leads him by 8.5 percent in the RCP average.\par \par \uc2 Let's get over to Virginia, though, shall we, were \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2  was running in Virginia seventh congressional district back in 2018. And then the party sold her as a moderate Democrat. Lot of conservatives there, right, in that district. Well, of course, reporters, they went right along with the charade.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The district in suburban Richmond has not been held by a Democrat for almost 50 years. Spanberger will also be the first woman ever to represent the district. You said Democrats shouldn't appear to be too partisan.\par \par \uc2 REP. ABIGAIL SPANBERGER (D-VA): It doesn't always have to be an us versus them. We need to stand up for it as opposed to a left versus right or Republicans versus Democrats. It should be in American values that we all stand up for.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: She says nothing and means nothing. The truth is Spanberger is just another \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  liberal, voting 