ar \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Congressman, your opponent, Cheri Beasley was on MSNBC tonight. And while she claims that she's going to protect voters' wallets, and what she calls fundamental freedoms, she's also talking about something else.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHERI BEASLEY (D) NORTH CAROLINA SENATE CANDIDATE: I will fight when our freedoms are on the line. And I will fight to make sure that Roe vs. Wade becomes the law of the land. Whether it's protecting our wallet or protecting our fundamental freedoms, I'm the only candidate in this race who has solutions for both.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The baby has no fundamental freedom and that equation, obviously. But congressman, is that what North Carolinians are really clamoring for?\par \par \uc2 TED BUDD (R) NORTH CAROLINA SENATE CANDIDATE: Not at all. They're talking about three things, Laura. And again, thanks for having us. They're talking about inflation. And they're not talking about it in academic sense. They're talking about it in very real terms like this is a grocery cart they could afford two years ago, but this is all they can afford now. How can they afford back to school closed just a few weeks or month ago.\par \par \uc2 And then they're talking about crime. They don't feel safe here. They're worried about the border. The border down 2,000 miles away is affecting us right here in North Carolina. 72 hours from crossing the border. The human traffic is right here. The drug traffic is right here in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 And parents, moms, dads are coming up to me and say they want to save their kids' education. So education is the other thing that folks are talking about. The Democrats are way out of step. We know that Cheri Beasley is a complete rubber stamp for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  's policies. And I think North Carolina has figured that out and they don't want anything to do with her or \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  's policies.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Senator Scott staying on Beasley for a moment. She's cribbed the party line on the GOP.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BEASLEY\uc2  : Congressman Budd has been in service for six years and he was against the interests of folks here in this state. And he's far more entwined with corporate special interests and his own interests. He is aligned with the most extreme faction of his of his party.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, isn't it true that corporate America is still pouring millions and millions of dollars into the Democrat coffers, isn't that a fact, unfortunately?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Absolutely. But if you look at what here's what \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  's going to do. He's going to represent North Carolina. All of us, every one of our senators, if they just represent their states, we wouldn't have the inflation we have today. We wouldn't have the crime we have today. We would have parental involvement in schools. And so \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  's going to do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 Cheri Beasley she's about the worst, her and Fetterman of the worst on crime in the country. And then think about the crime that's going on all across this country right now as a result of the Democrats policies.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Congressman Budd, I think this is going to be exciting to watch. I think you're going to win this and you got a good a good man campaigning for you there in \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  . He has done a great job for the country out there. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Now, Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and the man who will likely be the next speaker, Kevin McCarthy, are headed for a collision over sending more money to Ukraine in the new Congress. It's a huge debate. McConnell has led Republican support for sending generous, seemingly unending military and financial aid to Ukraine. While earlier this week, McCarthy was more realistic about how long we can do this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): I think Ukraine is very important. I support making sure that we move forward to defeat Russia in that program. But there should be no blank check on anything. We are $31 trillion in debt.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Of course, McCarthy is right. It's pretty mild comment if you ask me. And the sooner the GOP wakes up to the fact and starts focusing on the needs of American families, given everything that's happening here, the better off Republicans in the country will be politically.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now House Republican Study Committee Chair Jim Banks, and Mollie Hemingway, Editor-in-Chief of the Federalist, and a Fox News Contributor. Congressman Banks, which argument do you favor that we shouldn't have a blank check going over there to Ukraine or apparently is no end to the spending?\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM BANKS (R-IN): Well, Leader McCarthy is exactly right. He's stating the obvious, no more blank checks around the world to solve problems overseas when America is on its knees at home. We can't be the leader around the world when we have such big issues to solve in America. We need to secure the border, address inflation, address crime, deal with gas prices first before we can help other countries.\par \par \uc2 And Leader McCarthy, he said it all of Washington, the mainstream Left Wing media, the liberals in Congress, they all went bananas when he said it. After we've already sent $65 billion to Ukraine--\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Do we know where that money has really gone?\par \par \uc2 BANKS: There's been very little accountability for it. Now Democrats are screaming and saying well, if McCarthy says that, we know he's going to be Speaker of the House, we're going to pass it another $50 billion in the lame duck. It's just absurd, its insanity.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Mollie this is really where the Republicans always screwed up, and they didn't understand this after 2006 when we lost the House and the Senate, the war in Iraq, then we lost the presidency in 2008, again in 2012. And apparently they just want to go back to that. The old establishment dinosaur is still clinging to power.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: You are seeing this divide in the Republican Party where there are some people who are still trying to go back to an approach to foreign policy that has been politically toxic. As you note, everyone supports Ukraine, everybody wants them to do well.\par \par \uc2 Everyone also understands that this can't be another situation like we've had in the past, where you have an endless war without a good strategy for victory, without even a defined strategy for victory, and so you're seeing this interesting difference. I think speaker - likely to be Speaker McCarthy understands where the party is and understands about all the other things that are very important for the Republicans to accomplish.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We had Jeb Bush over on CNN tonight, he popped up. We had - \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  was asked about this issue yesterday. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Kevin McCarthy, who wants to become the next speaker of the House has said, if Republicans take the gavel that America will no longer be a blank check for the war on Ukraine. Do you take issue with that?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: You know, the United States throughout our history has understood that we need to be the leader of the free world, and that includes being the arsenal of democracy. We need to continue to support the people of Ukraine as they fight for their defense.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Congressman, I feel like I'm in a time warp - the arsenal of democracy. First of all, our munitions are horribly depleted. We can't seem to recruit enough people for our army. We're in big trouble militarily - woke, et cetera. We're funding woke programs for DEI and lord knows what 