nda in the military, the Pentagon is going to provide travel funds and support for troops and their dependents who seek abortions, but are based in states where they are now illegal, according to a new Department policy released on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 When are Republicans going to turn the spigot off on this stuff, Congressman? I mean, I'm so tired of the Republicans coming on this show and saying, well, we can't do anything until we have the presidency. Bowl, you could shut this down tomorrow, if you wanted to.\par \par \uc2 BANKS: Yes, this is something that is entirely solvable by a Republican majority. I serve on the Armed Services Committee, we have to stop this garbage. And the focus of the Biden Administration on combating climate issues, and the new national defense strategy versus tackling China and Russia and our greater threats is, is what you get with them on their watch. It's the opposite of what you're going to get--\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Are you running for whip?\par \par \uc2 BANKS: I'm looking at, where I can fit in to be a part of the Republican majority--\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I see yes.\par \par \uc2 BANKS: --leadership and do my part to make sure that we keep the commitments that we're making on the campaign trial.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We're going to be following those races and committee chairmanships, right, Mollie? Because that's where all the dirty deeds get done. All right, Congressman and Mollie, great to see you both. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now get your kid jabbed with what's still an experimental vaccine or lose the right to an education. Harmeet Dillon is here next on how parents can fight back.\par \par \uc2 Plus, San Francisco has entered potentially irreversible, irreversible downward spiral. Victor Davis Hanson is here on the disturbing new details coming out of that city. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm going to ask first, if there's any clarifying questions about this motion to approve the recommended child and adolescent immunization schedule and the recommended adult immunization schedule for 2023? I don't see any hands raised at the moment. We'll proceed with a vote.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And the motion passed 15 unanimously in favor.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: These are sick people, I mean that literally. Here's what we know to be true. These vaccines do not prevent transmission. They provide no demonstrable benefit to children who are the lowest risk profile. And even the people who want to force this on kids found that the highest risk of myocarditis is in males aged 12 to 17.\par \par \uc2 Now millions of parents across the country are going to be forced into a decision between jabbing their kids with an experimental vaccine, or providing them with an education. Now think about how twisted that is. So the question becomes what's next? What can they do? What is the legal recourse here?\par \par \uc2 The government tries to say, oh, this is just a recommendation. It's not binding. Oh, really wait till we get to the blue States. We're lucky tonight to be joined by attorney Harmeet Dhillon to walk us through this. Harmeet, tell us what the posture of this is tonight and what this means?\par \par \uc2 HARMEET DHILLON, REPUBLICAN NATIONAL LAWYERS ASSOCIATION CHAIR: Well, thank you, Laura. So the \uc2 Advisory Committee for Immunization Practices\uc2  is a handpicked group of experts that include PhDs, doctors and public health professionals who make these recommendations on an annual basis.\par \par \uc2 And since they're handpicked, you aren't going to see a lot of sunlight between CDC leadership and these recommendations. And so naturally, I don't think anyone was too surprised to see them a rubber stamp, this vaccine, which has gotten so much of a boost from our federal government.\par \par \uc2 Now, what's interesting is, and the hue and cry about this anticipated decision, you've actually seen the CDC and the Health and Human Services, really try to walk back the impact of this recommendation. They're saying it's just a recommendation, it's no big deal. And this actually belongs in the hands of the States. We've also seen the CDC try to force vaccine mandates on employer, so they're kind of talking out of both sides of their mouths.\par \par \uc2 The problem is, Laura, that a recommendation at the CDC level becomes an automatic mandate in certain states that have adopted a lockstep approach towards whatever the CDC requires. However, states do have the discretion in every case. They can absolutely depart from that law. They can make exemptions like in Tennessee. The Tennessee Governor today announced that he's worked with the legislature to make sure that while they normally are in lockstep, they're not going to adopt this particular recommendation.\par \par \uc2 Even California state law provides that they're going to make their own assessment of what vaccines are required in consideration of the CDC, but it doesn't mean that it's an automatic mandate. So I think--\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Harmeet let me jump in here. Because what we found during COVID, right, is that this is there's always an incrementalism. So if the Democrats get full power, do you have any doubt in your mind that they will try to mandate this, at the very least? I mean, it applies to all schools in places like New York. But mandate this coast to coast, if they could, they would?\par \par \uc2 DHILLON: Well, if they could, they would have - you know, if they hadn't been slapped back by the Supreme Court in the case of the employer mandate, yes, we'd all be doing it at that level. But they did fail at that level. And so I think, yes, I think what they're going to be doing is pressuring Blue states to do this.\par \par \uc2 And so, like, there are successful examples of parents fighting back. So for example, the human papilloma virus, which was recommended by the CDC in 2006 on this very same schedule, states have not adopted that. California doesn't require it. And so that's because parents looked at the data and said, I don't want to get my teenage daughter this vaccine.\par \par \uc2 And so it is imperative on parents to do it throughout the country to say, no, we don't want this. And there should be a political price to pay for politicians who do not protect taxpayers and students against this vaccine, which, as you say, has dangerous for children, and is unproven.\par \par \uc2 There's also a potential litigation angle that could be taken. I have researched this and found that there aren't examples of prior legal challenges to the recommendations to put on this vaccine list. And there's also a gray area in the law as to whether this is something that's committed to the agency discretion or not.\par \par \uc2 So I don't think that's a really - I think the best way to go is politically. To fight this politically and we have a lot of power as taxpayers and parents to fight on this, Laura.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, let me tell everybody. Tonight everyone, everyone needs to hear this. If this is important to you, what you put in your children's bodies, at this point, you got to get involved in vote. OK. Vote for the candidates who are actually going to protect your child's and your family's right to make these decisions. Harmeet, Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now San Francisco appears to have descended into total chaos. That was the warning from the CEO of a popular clothing brand - I don't even know how to say that, Cotopaxi who just announced that company is leaving the city.\par \par \uc2 David Smith, no hardcore Republican, says, the closure is due to organize theft rings. Saying the location faces regular attacks by thieves who virtually have - with virtually no response from law enforcement. Well, of course he's right about that.\par \par \uc2 And new Fox News report notes that San Francisco residents are arming themselves with bats because the city's drug initiatives are making crime worse. And it isn't just crime, the failure of San Francisco everywhere, get this, a new toilet that's planned for the city's Noe Valley in the town square there is going to cost $1.7 million. And it won't be completed until 2025. Meanwhile, the causeway to Sanibel, Florida, destroyed by hurricane Ian earlier this month has already been fixed.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Victor Davis Hanson, senior fellow at the \uc2 Hoover Institution\uc2  , longtime California resident. Victor, the reality of two Americas here is becoming more obvious by the day. Are some of these cities lost for our lifetime at least?\par \par \uc2 VICTOR DAVIS HANSON, HOOVER INSTITUTE SENIOR FELLOW: At least for a generation. Here in California, we have a post-modern and a pre-modern society all at once. So we have people fornicating, defecating, urinating on the streets, carjackings, smash and grab, nobody can do anything about it. We have these biblical fires ever summer. We have brownouts. We're in our third year of drought. It's going to be really bad if we don't get snow very quickly.\par \par \uc2 And then when we look for answers, we get hyper regulation, hyper taxation, talk about late-term abortion, transgender, anything but the elemental problems. There's no solutions. High speed rail, it's a boondoggle. And the people in the middle are the middle class, and they're playing these exorbitant taxes and they're getting lousy infrastructure, no law enforcement, and they either have to carry bats or they have to leave the state for Florida or Texas or Nevada. And I think they're doing both. But, boy, you can be in one place in California and you can be in "Lord of the Flies," and the next moment you drive over to Menlo Park and you see Silicon Valley and they're in La La Land, and they're right next to each other. It's really strange.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But there's just a dissonance, right? There's no Venn diagram where they connect as far as understanding or recognition --\par \par \uc2 HANSON: No.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: -- that these policies are failing the people, Victor. I mean, all these big high-tech guys, they have no sense that the policies aren't working, really?\par \par \uc2 HANSON: I think that the only connection is that the elite are so wealthy given Silicon Valley that they're never subject to the consequences of their own ideology, so they have all these theories that they project on us, and then they're immune from it, and they're protected from all the chaos on the street or the chaos in the state govern answer or the infrastructure. And that's why the middle class has no protection and no help from the government or the elite, and they have to deal with the real problems on the street. That's why they carry a bat, or they're increasingly armed. And they can't -- they're schizophrenic and they don't know what to make of it. It's really scary. It's like the 19th and 21st century right next to each other.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, well, they have their protection, they always have. And Nancy can head up to Napa Valley anytime she wants. Victor, great to see you, as always.\par \par \uc2 Now, we're back at THE INGRAHAM ANGLE big board tonight with a host of races that the GOP never would have dreamed that they would have been able to compete in. Details on the growing red wave in moments.\par \par \uc2 Plus, I have a major announcement coming up, you do not want to miss it, so do not go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's time once again for THE INGRAHAM ANGLE'S top race to watch as we're just 19 days away from the midterm elections, if you can believe it. We're back at the big board with Chris Bedford, executive editor for the Common Sense Society. All right, Chris, let's kick things off in one of my favorite states. I spent a lot of time, everyone knows, in Minnesota. We have an attorney general's race where incumbent Democrat Keith Ellison, ug, is tied with Republican challenger Jim Schultz at 47. What is going on?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS BEDFORD, COMMON SENSE SOCIETY EXECUTIVE EDITOR: That's pretty wild. Minnesota is a liberal state, to say the least. Minnesota is the only state that voted against \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  in his reelection, for example, them and Washington, D.C. And Keith Ellison is a liberal icon, a former national figure, someone who ran, someone who also at the same time attacked the police over the violence after George Floyd. When I did reporting in Minnesota the last election, bartenders, store owners, regular people were upset with the level of crime. The capital city had become a ghost town, and he's not prosecuting.\par \par \uc2 He's aware of this, now, though. His first campaign ad was about how tough he is on crime, but the people who are suffering, they don't believe that one bit.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's a mess there.\par \par \uc2 Chris, let's take a look at what's happening in Minneapolis proper. Compared to 2019, homicides up 166 percent, carjackings up 519 percent, gunshot victims, 101 percent. This is the biggest issue. I saw it firsthand when I was there in the summer and the summer before that. It's big.\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: It's sad. I have seen it firsthand, too. Walk into a great dinner place, it felt like walking through a Marilyn Manson music video. It was creepy. The streets were dark. People were afraid. The waiters said their business was basically dried up. And people were responding by rioting all the time. The police couldn't get anything done. Even if someone was a murderer, someone needed to be taken in by police, there would be a riot, and they've lost control of the city.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Chris, let's turn to the House. Let's go to New York's 17th district where Republican Mike Lawler is trying to upset Congressman \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  who's the DCCC chair. Now, a McCarthy aligned super PAC is dumping $4 million of broadcast TV ads there to help Lawler, who is fantastic, by the way. What can you tell us about that race?\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: This is another race that in trouble. New York, you see, which is another liberal state, the Republican governor candidate is only a few points behind the Democrat. So that means that for Republican Democrats that are in districts that are much closer, that Democrat has got to run, they've got to be in trouble. So Republicans are gaining, they're ahead. They're probably going to take that, it looks like. They're probably going to take all of Long Island. It's going to be a very different map for New Yorkers. And it's, again, a response to the crime, the inflation, and all the issues that Democrats don't want to talk about.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And New York deserves better than Maloney. Now, I want to turn to Virginia's 10th district where Democrat \uc2 Jennifer Wexton\uc2  is neck and neck with Republican Hung Cao even in Cao's own internal polling. Chris, what can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: So this used to be a Republican district. This was \uc2 Barbara Comstock\uc2  's district, but it switched. And then Wexton won in 2018. She was one of those wave blue candidates. That doesn't mean that she's got a firm hold. Now, Hung Cao came on the scene. This guy is a special forces lifetime veteran. He's got a big family who he's extremely proud of. He's got a great sense of humor. He's answered all the questions correctly. He's a strong candidate. Even though northern Virginia keeps on getting more blue, in a wave year this is one of those wobbly districts that could go Republican.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Fairfax is having trouble in the schools with the woke agenda and also on crime, so that's big.\par \par \uc2 Chris, here's what \uc2 Jennifer Wexton\uc2  had to say about Governor Glenn Youngkin's school transgender plan, "One of the concerns I have about the plan is that it would not require that teachers use the proper pronouns. for kids. I don't think he's about the kids himself." Is Wexton trying to lose this race? This doesn't fly in Fairfax.\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: It's the kind of thing that has actually gotten parents of all political persuasions, Obama supporters, Biden supporters, Trump supporters out together. And people who have never been political, when they go out there to Fairfax and other areas, interview parents, people who have never been involved are really electric about this. It's what got Glenn Youngkin elected. When he ran on the grocery tax, no one cared. When he ran on the woke agenda in schools, then voters came out for him.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We have a crazy election cycle coming up. We have New England in play. We have pacific northwest in play. And we also have interesting things happening in states like Oklahoma where the Oklahoma governor's race, incumbent Governor \uc2 Kevin Stitt\uc2  is now down four points to Democrat challenger Joy Hofmeister. This was a state city Stitt won by 12 points in 2018, so this is a bit of an outlier compared to what we're seeing across the country for Republicans. What's happening?\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: It is. And Stitt won, and then he delivered on a conservative agenda. He's been tough on schools. He's been tough on hospitals, been tough on the wokesters. He's basically followed in the mold of maybe Abbott or DeSantis. His problem seems to be more local. He has been accused repeatedly of corruption, particularly around restaurant licenses. He had some resignations from his government.\par \par \uc2 And at the same time Hofmeister is someone who is well liked, who is a good campaigner, who has won a couple statewide races. This one, just like the Oregon race, may be something where people get into the ballot box and say, you know what, I'm going to go with my partisan feelings. I just simply can't vote against my party. But right now it's looking terrible for him and is bucking the national trends.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Wow. OK, there always has to be one. Chris, great to see you. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, an impending Republican majority in the House means that \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  will have to relinquish her speaker's gavel. She sleeps with that every night. But don't worry, Nancy. THE INGRAHAM ANGLE has been busy brainstorming some future employment opportunities for you, and we're going to share those next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The walls are closing in on the Democrats as we inch closer to November. Do you feel it? And \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  knows her days as speaker are numbered.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What do you say to your own caucus, to these young members that are, obviously, in tough fights, but they want to see the change?\par \par \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  , (D-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: I say just win, baby. Just win. If that's what you have to say to win, fine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: We at THE INGRAHAM ANGLE feel for old San Fran Grand Nan. So being the magnanimous team that we are, we've taken it upon ourselves to compile a list of post speaker careers for her. Charlie Hurt, FOX News contributor and opinion editor for "The Washington Times" who joins me on set with his own contributions. OK, Charlie, the first option is inspired by this recent documentary.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  , (D-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: If he comes, I'm going to punch him out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I would pay to see that.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: I've been waiting for this, for trespassing on the Capitol grounds. I'm going to punch him out, I'm going to go to jail, and I'm going to be happy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Given her gusto in that clip, we thought she might want to channel that aggression into the ring. There has got to be some senior boxing tour somewhere in California. You can see it, right? She's looking good there. She almost looks like Fetterman there.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES HURT, OPINION EDITOR, "THE WASHINGTON TIMES": Yes, I can see her like in an MMA ring.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Maybe she and \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  can go toe to toe, because Murray's going to be out, too.\par \par \uc2 HURT: Yes. I could see it. And she is a street fighter.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: She's smart.\par \par \uc2 HURT: Going all the way back to the streets of Baltimore. I wouldn't count her out.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I wouldn't mess --\par \par \uc2 HURT: She plays dirty, too.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: She's a fighter. She fights with what she believes in. She was ready to go with all that legislation. I don't agree with her on anything, but you have got to admire for her fight.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, you have a suggestion of your own for Nancy, and I hear it is inspired by this moment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: God bless you and God bless America. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I'm trying to rip my folder, but I can't.\par \par \uc2 HURT: She could get a job at Kinkos and become a paper shredder, and she could do it right there and work for the federal government. The federal government produces lots of documents, I'm sure they need to be shredded, and she could just spend her entire retirement.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: My shredder always jams.\par \par \uc2 HURT: If you leave the stapler in it or the paper clips.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Or you put lots of credit cards in because I always lose mine and then I find them, so I have lots of credit cards in --\par \par \uc2 HURT: But you know what I think she would be really good at is White House press secretary, because she's the only one who still seems to think that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has had a really great two years. She's really 