c2 It's a policy that is expected to provoke outrage among many pro-life Americans. But Pentagon officials insist it's needed to recruit, retain and maintain readiness.\par \par \uc2 Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin is at the Pentagon tonight with this. Good evening, Jennifer.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. Today, the Pentagon announced a major policy change in the wake of the Supreme Court's decision to repeal Roe vs. Wade.\par \par \uc2 The Pentagon will now pay service member's transportation expenses if they need to travel to obtain an abortion or receive reproductive care that is now outlawed in an increasing number of states where they may be stationed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PATRICK RYDER, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: Service members may be forced to travel greater distances, take more time off work and pay more out of pocket expenses to access reproductive health care, all of which have readiness recruiting and retention implications for America's Armed Forces. Taking care of our people continues to be the number one priority.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (voice over): The Hyde Amendment bars federal funds being used for abortions unless the life of the mother is in danger which means military medical facilities cannot perform abortions.\par \par \uc2 This new policy does not change that and TRICARE health insurance does not cover the cost of obtaining the procedure privately.\par \par \uc2 In the wake of the Supreme Court decision, 13 states have so far banned abortion which means that female service members and their family members assigned to bases in these states may not have access to reproductive health care if they need abortions.\par \par \uc2 Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  directed the department to come up with a plan to reduce the burden on the force. In a memo released today at the Pentagon, the Defense Secretary promise that privacy issues would be honored. Service members would not need to take time off, and that military health care providers be protected from legal and financial risks.\par \par \uc2 "Our greatest strength is our people. There is no higher priority than taking care of our people and ensuring their health and well-being."\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): The memo also directs the military to provide increased access to contraception. The department may face legal challenges from pro-life groups. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Up next, the CDC panel recommends adding the COVID vaccine to the list of immunizations for your school children. And later, the brutal beating of a woman in Washington, D.C. is the subject of tonight's report on America's crime crisis.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  has rejected an appeal from a Wisconsin taxpayers group, seeking to stop the Biden administration's student debt cancelation program.\par \par \uc2 Barrett did not comment and turning away that appeal from the Brown County Taxpayers Association, which has also lost rounds and lower federal courts.\par \par \uc2 The group wrote in its Supreme Court filing, it needed an emergency order to put the program on hold because the administration could begin canceling outstanding student debt as soon as Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Chances of a nationwide railroad strike may be increasing. Major freight railroads issues a statement last night rejecting a union request to add paid sick time on top of the 24 percent raises and $5,000 bonuses that were given in the five-year deal.\par \par \uc2 Six of the 12 railroad unions representing 115,000 workers have approved tentative agreement so far, but all of them must ratify the contract to avoid strike.\par \par \uc2 A panel from the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  is recommending the COVID-19 vaccine be added to the list of immunizations for public school students.\par \par \uc2 It's a decision that will be an unpopular one with some parents around the country.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Steve Harrigan has the latest tonight from Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 We'll have that story for you in just a moment. A little technical problem there.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a look at the increasing danger of fentanyl, disguised as candy as Halloween approaches. That will also be the subject of our "COMMON GROUND" segment.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Russian troops try to regain lost ground in areas of Ukraine annexed by President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . He declared martial law in the four regions yesterday, but his forces have suffered a string of battlefield setbacks and a troubled troop mobilization.\par \par \uc2 The wife of an American diplomat pleads guilty to causing the death of a British teenager, by carelessly driving. Anne Sacoolas initially fled the country after accident.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): She pleaded guilty today by video-link for -- from the U.S. to a less serious charge.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): And Philippine President \uc2 Ferdinand Marcos Jr\uc2 . backs a decision by his predecessor to cancel a deal to buy 16 Russian military heavy lift helicopters. He says his administration will purchase the equipment from the U.S. instead.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stores "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Well, that CDC report from Atlanta, we have it now. Steve Harrigan has it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And the motion passed, 15 unanimously in favor.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): The CDC's independent advisors voted today to recommend adding COVID shots to the immunization schedule for children, which could potentially be a first step on the way to school vaccination mandates.\par \par \uc2 It is up to each state to decide if a child has to be vaccinated to go to school. The pushback against adding COVID to the vaccination schedule began even before the vote was taken.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): As long as I am around as long as I'm kicking and screaming. There will be no COVID shot mandates for your kids. That is your decision.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Some vaccines on the CDC schedule are already mandatory for students such as measles, hepatitis, and polio.\par \par \uc2 Others like the flu shot are recommended but not mandated.\par \par \uc2 DR. JULIE MORITA, EXECUTIVE VICE PRESIDENT, \uc2 ROBERT WOOD JOHNSON FOUNDATION\uc2 : Including this COVID vaccine in the routine childhood schedule doesn't necessarily mean that states and local jurisdictions are going to run to require this vaccine for school entry.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Deep breath.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: The vote comes one day after another unanimous decision to include COVID shots in the vaccines for children program. Creating a safety net for the CDC to buy and provide vaccines for poor and uninsured children before the federal government ends COVID vaccine funding.\par \par \uc2 The votes come amid warnings of a possible winter surge in cases subvariants of omicron continue to emerge.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Just one-third of U.S. school children are vaccinated for COVID. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Steve Harrigan in Atlanta. Steve, thanks.\par \par \uc2 With Halloween just 11 days away, authorities are encouraging parents to double-check their children's treats as the big night approaches. Officials at the \uc2 Los Angeles International Airport\uc2 , LAX, found thousands of suspected fentanyl pills disguised as candy. Chief correspondent Jonathan Hunt has details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Packaged as Skittles, Whoppers, and various other candy brands, 12,000 pills believed to be laced with potentially deadly fentanyl discovered when a passenger tried to take them through a TSA checkpoint at \uc2 Los Angeles International Airport\uc2 . The L.A. sheriff's department says the seizure should serve as a warning to parents, quote, "With Halloween approaching, parents need to make sure they are checking their kids' candy and not allowing them to eat anything until it has been inspected by them."\par \par \uc2 And the drug enforcement agency points out that so-called rainbow fentanyl, pills deliberately colored to look like candy, has been found in 26 states this year.\par \par \uc2 JON HATAMI, L.A. DEPUTY DISTRICT ATTORNEY: What's happening is they are poisoning our kids. We have these dealers that are killing our children and are terrorizing our communities.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: The DEA points out about 42 percent of illicit pills contain more than the potentially lethal dose of two milligrams of fentanyl. So the pills seized at LAX could have killed around 5,000 people. More than 107,000 Americans died of drug overdoses and poisonings in 2021, with 66 percent of those deaths related to synthetic opioids like fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 HATAMI: We need to aggressively prosecute the dealers in these cases. We need to go out, and if we can prove the case, we need to charge them with second degree murder.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HUNT: The passenger carrying all those pills at LAX somehow got away. But the sheriff's department tells us they know that person's identity and the investigation is ongoing. Meantime, Bret, it's worth pointing out that the DEA now says, and this is a quote, fentanyl is the single deadliest drug threat our nation has ever encountered, staggering declaration, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So are they saying that this was specifically marketed to these kids?\par \par \uc2 HUNT: No. They think in this case this may have been simply a way to try to conceal those fentanyl pills. They weren't, importantly, those rainbow- colored ones we've seen pushed to kids so much. But this is a drug dealer. Who knows what the end goal was for these drugs?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jonathan, thanks. Great to see you in person.\par \par \uc2 HUNT: You too.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's talk more about the fentanyl crisis. Joining me in our Common Ground segment, Connecticut Democratic Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  and Louisiana Republican Senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2 . Gentlemen, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 Fentanyl, we talk about it a lot, but what is being done about it is really what people at home care about. Senator Cassidy, first to you. You're obviously on the same issue here, trying to stop it and change the dynamic. But what is happening?\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY, (R-LA): There is no single answer to addressing the opioid epidemic. It's responsible for the person -- the opioid. And sometimes they take it unknowingly. But it's also, hopefully, to stop its influx across the border or through the mail. There's other ways to address it as well. Treat the addiction so that there's fewer people once they have taken it who are likely to continue and to die. So there's different points along the way that we need to address it, and Chris and I have been working on multiple points -- multiple of those points.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, Senator Murphy, we look at the flow of fentanyl coming from China over to Canada, also Mexico, and then up through Mexico into the U.S. You recently were on a CODEL meeting with the Mexican foreign secretary and talking about this. You obviously work with your Republican colleagues on this issue. But what is being done?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS MURPHY\uc2 , (D-CT): Well, you are right that most of this product is coming in manufactured to the United States. These are, most of the time, tiny little pills. And you can make a whole lot of money off of a very small shipment that is easy to conceal. Over the course of both the Trump administration and the Biden administration, we made progress on stopping the Chinese from essentially mailing the finished product to the United States. It still happens, but not nearly as much as it used to.\par \par \uc2 What I was down in Mexico to take a look at is the current practice, which is the chemicals moving from China into Mexico, the manufacture happening in Mexico, and then these pills being shipped up into the United States. So, there's two things can you do about that. I agree with Bill that this has got to be multisystemic. He and I work a lot on the treatment options together.\par \par \uc2 But the two things you can do about the import is, one, stop China from sending the product, the chemicals, to Mexico. We can do a much better job of that. And second, stop a lot of this stuff when it's coming across our border, put a lot more personnel and a lot more technology at the ports of entry where most of the stuff moves. We're starting to catch more of it, but we could do a whole lot better.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, we talked about the deadly dose, Senator Cassidy. And if you just put up this picture, and we've used it before, of the dose that is needed for a fatality up against a penny, it's that little white powder right there. That's it. And then you have these rainbow pills, as Senator Murphy mentioned, that are circulating now, and we are seeing them, just had a story about in the candy box. So, this is real, and it's affecting communities not just along the border.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: Absolutely, 100,000 Americans died of overdoses last year, and about two-thirds or three-fourth of those related to fentanyl. Now, it starts with the individual. If a child was trick or treating, ideally the child was aware. It's a very small possibility that the child will be given something which is like this. But each of us need to have an awareness that there may be something we are exposed to that could expose us to fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 The teenager who is experimenting with momma's oxycontin, given a pill by another person, and it turns out it's not his momma's oxycontin. It's something somebody else. And that oxycontin actually is a manufactured pill which contains fentanyl. So each of us has to have an awareness that this can come into our environment.\par \par \uc2 Bret, everybody watching this program knows somebody or knows of somebody who has overdosed from fentanyl. It takes that vigilance, that personal vigilance to keep from -- and then Chris and I and others have been working on what we can do besides that personal vigilance. But I will say it starts with the individual.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Murphy, there is a lot of common ground here, and you guys are working together, but Republican critics would say that there is a -- there's not as much pressure on the border security, and that is an issue. And some of those, especially in those border states, it's an issue that has been an issue coming into the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Last year and actually the year before, we passed bipartisan budgets. And well, we had a disagreement on question of a border wall. We had agreement on dramatically increasing the amount of technology that goes into our ports because, as I said, most all of this fentanyl is moving on a truck in somebody's car, right across a manned port of entry. But we don't expect all those trucks and all those cars.\par \par \uc2 Well, we are about to, because we put in the money so that 90 percent of cargo trucks are going to be inspected, up to 50, 60 percent of vehicles are going to be inspected, up from like 10 percent just a year ago. So you're right. When it comes to the border, there is going to be disagreements between Republicans and Democrats. We'll fight those out. But there are places that we can agree, and one of those is that we should be inspecting every single vehicle right now that is coming across the border, because that's where a lot of this product is.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, Senators Cassidy and Murphy, we appreciate the time. We like doing these Common Ground segments to talk about what is actually happening between the two sides. And Senator Cassidy, I know you have a big football game this weekend, \uc2 LSU\uc2 , Ole Miss. So you will be busy there, too.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: I like that tie. It's a good \uc2 LSU\uc2  tie. Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, gentlemen, have a good one. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the brutal beating of a woman in Washington, D.C., another horrible example of America's crime crisis.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KYLA THURSTON, VICTIM: They were like literally chopping me down on the ground. Like if I would not have held on, they would have just threw me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In America's crime crisis tonight, a brutal example of the mayhem happening in many cities and towns across the country. Lucas Tomlinson is in southeast Washington where the crime occurred with the video which you may find disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (SHOUTING)\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: A 42-year-old woman assaulted on a D.C. bus in broad daylight for asking a group of teens to stop using vulgar language. Not one passenger stood up to help. The police were never called to respond. The bus driver accused of ignoring repeated calls to stop the bus.\par \par \uc2 KYLA THURSTON, VICTIM: They started physically assaulting me. And as I'm telling the bus driver, I'm like let me off, let me off, let me off. Open the door, open the door.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: This is just the latest in a worrying trend in the nation's capital. Aggravated assault has spiked 25 percent aboard D.C. mass transit in just the last month. Larceny has jumped over 50 percent in September. Robberies were also up seven percent. D.C.'s Democratic mayor who is running for a third term was asked about the attack.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR MURIEL BOWSER, (D) WASHINGTON D.C.: That is not the -- any type of crime that we want to see in our city at any time.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: And it's not just D.C. Similar scenes playing out across the country as a number of cities are seeing a rise in violent crime.\par \par \uc2 TED WILLIAMS, FORMER WASHINGTON D.C. DETECTIVE: The public and these major metropolitan citizens, they see what is going on, law-abiding citizens who just want to walk down the street and feel safe.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Thurston credits her Christian faith from saving her from the attack, walking away with just minor cuts and bruises. She did tell us the assault will make her think twice about her daily routine, saying she no longer takes the bus when kids are out of school.\par \par \uc2 THURSTON: The amount of kids that was on that bus, why didn't anybody else attempt to assist me? They probably thought the say, like they would have been assaulted as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Ms. Thurston has not reported her assault to the police and says she blames the attack on the breakdown of the American family. She says it's the parents who should be held responsible. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Lucas Tomlinson in southwest D.C. Lucas, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a slumping economy could cost President Biden his majority in Congress. It's already cost Great Britain's new prime minister her job. We'll discuss all of that with the panel, plus the latest on the midterms.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 10 in Phoenix as Arizona's governor refuses the Biden administration's demand to take down double stacked shipping containers filling gaps in the border wall. The state department -- state's department of emergency and military affairs says it will not remove the containers until plans for a permanent barrier are enacted.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Orlando from FOX 35. One of the big stories there from our affiliates, \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  successfully launches another batch of Starlink satellites to low earth orbit. A Falcon 9 rocket carrying 54 satellites lifted off from Cape Canaveral this morning. The rocket safely touched down a little less than nine minutes later on the \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  drone ship at sea.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Should \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  run again?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That was quick. Wow. Why shouldn't \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  run again in --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Because too many people will not vote for him.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I agree with the -- he's in class, but you don't know if he is really in class.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You can put anybody next to him and they are going to get it, and that's not good.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A lot of people think he is mentally unwell, and maybe they think it's his age, dementia, whatever.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's a focus group in Pennsylvania asked the question, as you heard, whether President Biden should run again. This factors in to he was campaigning today. He hasn't campaigned a ton of places across the country this -- ahead of the midterm elections. Monmouth has a new poll out about the generic ballot, who would you vote for if you have -- Republican and lean Republican, it's 49 percent. Democrat, lean Democrat 45 percent.\par \par \uc2 But