\par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: She thinks it's a little extreme and that's a little extreme for my mom. It's a little extreme.\par \par \uc2 Tucker is up next.\par \par \uc2 Always remember, I'm Watters and this is my world.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening and welcome to TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT.\par \par \uc2 In July of 1993, a radio station in Kigali, Rwanda began openly attacking one of the country's main ethnic groups, the Tutsis. The radio station was called RTLM, but many remember it as simply Hutu Radio because its audience was primarily Hutu.\par \par \uc2 According to Hutu Radio, Tutsi people were responsible for virtually every bad thing that ever happened in Rwanda. Tutsis had way too much money. They had way too much power.\par \par \uc2 Tutsis were way too privileged: They were greedy. They were bigoted. They were racists. They were dangerous. Everything about Tutsi-ness was repulsive.\par \par \uc2 For the most part, actual Tutsis in Rwanda ignored all of this. Hutu Radio was not aimed at them.\par \par \uc2 But then in July of 1994, just nine months after RTLM went on the air, a genocide began in Rwanda. More than half a million Tutsis were murdered, in many cases by Hutus whose rage had been stoked to violence by RTLM's broadcasts.\par \par \uc2 Entire Tutsi families were dragged from their homes and hacked to death with machetes. Hundreds of thousands of women were raped.\par \par \uc2 The world watched in horror as it happened, but did nothing to intervene. Instead, our leaders told us at the time, the genocide in Rwanda would live forever as a lesson to the rest of us about the capacity for evil that lurks inside every human heart, and the dangers of reducing our neighbors to the sum total of their ethnicity, they are individuals, not ethnic groups.\par \par \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  gave an eloquent speech actually on the subject in Kigali back in 1998. Look it up, and ask yourself as you read it, if any Democratic Party official could today say those same words? It's hard to imagine given what plays on a loop on that party's cable news arm MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 Have you watched MSNBC lately? Likely you haven't.\par \par \uc2 Like the Tutsis, you're not the target audience, but you should tune in sometime. It's remarkable.\par \par \uc2 Given that opposing racism is America's national religion, it may surprise you to learn that open race hate forms much of the substance of that channel's programming. And when we say race hate, we're not referring to the subtle coded variety. "You want border security. You're giving your kids piano lessons. You like Shakespeare. You believe in the SAT. You must be a racist." That's not what we're talking about.\par \par \uc2 We're talking about the kind of race hate you cannot mistake for anything else, the kinds of people just come out and announce, "I hate this race of people, and here is why I do."\par \par \uc2 It's hard to believe that anything like that is happening right now on American television, but it is, out in the open.\par \par \uc2 And the most amazing and the most creepy part of all is that no one is saying anything about it. It's all but ignored. And honestly, we had no idea it was going on either until we started getting texts from people: "Are you watching this? Can you believe this?" So we tuned in.\par \par \uc2 Apparently, on the left, what you're about to see is considered completely normal, even good, and that should worry you deeply.\par \par \uc2 You don't want to live in Rwanda, but at MSNBC, they're already there. Now you probably knew about \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 , the race lady, who has been fixated on race hate for years now. But MSNBC has a new host, someone called Tiffany Cross. She hosts a show called "The Cross Connection." Here's a selection.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CROSS, MSNBC HOST: Many of us have seen the dangers when powerful White people decide they want something, they annex it and they've never had a problem replacing the people who stand in their way.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL HARRIOT, SENIOR WRITER, THEROOT.COM: We see American White people are going crazy that they are resorting to violence.\par \par \uc2 ELIE MYSTAL, POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: This is literally what conservative White folks do when they don't get their way, they turn violent.\par \par \uc2 CROSS: The White people deputizing themselves in some position of authority to have jurisdiction over their life when they need to mind their blinking business.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I don't think it's our responsibility to be tasked with destroying and dismantling the racial oppression that's against us. That's just saying we're more at fault than the White people who constructed this system and the White people who continue to practice institutional racism.\par \par \uc2 MYSTAL: A majority of White people do not support policies that will unpack and unroll and reform this system of justice. This is what they want. Matt Gaetz is giving the White folks what they want.\par \par \uc2 CROSS: White replacement can strangle culture. So yes, we should all be concerned about White replacement. It is after all, a very threat to our survival here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Is there anything worse than White people? They're violent, they are heartless, they're cruel, they're deranged, they're secretive, they're dishonest. In fact as you just heard Tiffany Cross say, White people are a mortal danger to you and your loved ones, they threaten your life.\par \par \uc2 Are they poisoning the wells? Are they baking bread with the blood of your children? If not, according to Tiffany Cross and MSNBC, they're fully capable of doing those things. They've certainly done worse.\par \par \uc2 This is Hutu Radio.\par \par \uc2 But it's not an independent radio station in an African country, it is part of one of the biggest news organizations in the world, part of the biggest telecommunications company in the United States, \uc2 Comcast\uc2 , which owns it.\par \par \uc2 So you have to ask yourself, what does the \uc2 Comcast\uc2  Board think of this? Comcast Board is mostly White people, White people who according to the channel they own, decided they wanted something, then they annexed it, White people who steal because they're White. White people who could, "turn to violence when they don't get their way." White people are going crazy, endangering their communities.\par \par \uc2 So you have to ask yourself, why are they putting this on the air? Why are they allowing this?\par \par \uc2 This is not a policy debate. These are open attacks on people, on Americans on the basis purely of their race and that is just a selection. We could go on and on and on.\par \par \uc2 Are they aware that this is happening? Perhaps they're not? We weren't, to be fair.\par \par \uc2 But it is happening day after day after day after day. At a certain point, you have to ask yourself, if it continues and nobody stops it, do they agree with it? Maybe they do.\par \par \uc2 And just in case, you think that we are taking Tiffany Cross out of context, and pulling the worst quotes from out of context segment to make her seem crazy and racist. Here is more.\par \par \uc2 Here is Tiffany Cross at MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROSS: Look, a lot of folks in that Capitol insurrection, some of these folks were White women, and I know we're talking a lot about Oath Keepers and Proud Boys, but I do think that some of the White women who have adopted this ideology, is America ready to face the fact that some of those folks look like people they have elevated and put on a pedestal of being untouchable.\par \par \uc2 What do you say about this wing of White women who have been radicalized and are enablers to this very dangerous domestic terrorism movement that we've seen increased quite rapidly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, it's such as Whites, it's White women. Their women are bad, too. Women, of course, are the key to reproducing the White race, which is clearly a threat, as she says again and again to you and your family. They're dangerous. They want to hurt you.\par \par \uc2 Now, don't be fooled by the fact that Tiffany Cross can barely speak a coherent sentence. She was a Communications Major, apparently, according to the internet. That didn't work.\par \par \uc2 But the gist of it is very, very clear. White women are dangerous, because White people are dangerous. They are, by the nature of their DNA, potential domestic terrorists.\par \par \uc2 Now you can play the game, well, if any other group were spoken about this way, this should be shut down immediately. And of course, that's completely true. There is no other group in America, you could talk about the way that Tiffany Cross and \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2  and other anchors in MSNBC talks about White people.\par \par \uc2 But you didn't even have to play that game. It doesn't matter what the color is, it is always wrong to reduce people to the color of their skin, to their melanin content, to their DNA and it is even worse to attack them on the basis of that. And in fact, it's the basis of violence, actual violence -- actual violence.\par \par \uc2 But Tiffany Cross can't be criticized because she is oppressed. That's the key. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROSS: What we didn't see were enough voices willing to point out the deeper festering rot that has plagued this nation since it was born.\par \par \uc2 People who neither discovered nor built this land had been led to believe that America is theirs and theirs alone.\par \par \uc2 Will this democracy survive? Well, a Yahoo News poll says no.\par \par \uc2 But perhaps when you build a nation on stolen land, with stolen labor, it was never going to be a Republic we could keep.\par \par \uc2 And so here we are, celebrating the birth of a nation, independence for White men.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Okay, once again, calling out a specific race by name, you have to wonder about the other anchors on MSNBC, some of whom are that race. Do they notice this? Do they know what's happening on their channel? Are they okay with this? What do you think happens if we continue to talk this way?\par \par \uc2 You may not watch that channel, but some people do. What does this look like in a year or five years or 10 years? What kind of country do you live in? Well, a country at war with itself -- race war.\par \par \uc2 This woman, Tiffany Cross, whose clips you've been watching is so deranged by a racialist worldview that she believes all people of one color are oppressed by all people of another color and to prove it, she says, even \uc2 NFL\uc2  players, some of the richest people in our society, some of the most celebrated, the most famous, the most privileged, even they can't escape the all-pervasive hatred of diabolical Whites. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROSS: I've got to say, Mike, the optics just look bad to see all these Black men crashing into each other with a bunch of White owners, White coaches, and the complete disregard of Black bodies and Black life. I mean, it just represents a larger issue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So I mean, look, the average salary for an \uc2 NFL\uc2  player is more than $2 million a year. Sixty percent of \uc2 NFL\uc2  players are African-Americans. In some positions like quarterback, virtually all the first string players are Black.\par \par \uc2 So, okay, we don't have a problem with that. Most people don't have a problem with that, but if you look at that picture and say, "This is White supremacy," what are you really saying? You're saying that anybody involved in the sport who is White defiles the sport because whiteness is itself inherently corrupting.\par \par \uc2 White people are so evil that their mere presence in a sport that is overwhelmingly African-American and from which African-American individuals are benefiting is enough to destroy the whole operation? Because there are Whites involved.\par \par \uc2 What kind of talk is that? Well, it is genocidal talk actually. Not an overstatement, that's exactly what it is.\par \par \uc2 So again, you have to wonder what does \uc2 Comcast\uc2  Board think of this? It is not a rhetorical question, really, now that we're talking about sports, because \uc2 Comcast\uc2  also owns an NHL team, a hockey team, the \uc2 Philadelphia Flyers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And maybe not surprisingly, the NHL just published his first ever diversity report, and according to that report, we have a huge problem with hockey. And the problem is there are too many White people in hockey. Now, why is that a problem? No one actually explains. It doesn't need to be explained because it's just prima facie obvious, we've got a lot of White people, that's inherently bad because White people are inherently bad.\par \par \uc2 Again, do you want to live in a country where powerful people talk this way? What do you think it looks like in five years?\par \par \uc2 The head of the NHL's "Social Impact Division" said, "We have a lot of work to do." Hmm. Again, how does \uc2 Comcast\uc2  Board feel about this? Will the Board undergo some kind of forced diversification? What about the \uc2 NBA\uc2  in the \uc2 NFL\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 And really, we're not following all of this, but as \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2  and Tiffany Cross tell us, that's probably because we're White and too stupid to understand.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: I think for \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , she's had like, the triple problem of being a woman and so people not being willing to respect her the way that they would respect a male Vice President; of being Black, which we already know that what that carries with it is the anti-Blackness comes, you know, with the package, and then also being Vice President at a time that is really, really difficult.\par \par \uc2 CROSS: Joy and I talk about this all the time, Madam Vice President, and you know, this really, she is Black-Black, okay. She went to Howard, she is AK and when she talks to you --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CROSS: It is. I mean she sounds like me.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: She is so regular.\par \par \uc2 CROSS: She is so regular.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: She really is.\par \par \uc2 CROSS: And approachable, and I just -- it's unfortunate that more people don't see that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Don't see that.\par \par \uc2 CROSS: And there is also just the dumbing down of the American electorate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: We could go on and on, we could read excerpts from Tiffany Cross' book, which are brimming with racial hostility. We could play you a million clips from \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 , which are exactly the same, filled with open racial hostility.\par \par \uc2 But you get the point, and if you don't believe us, just go ahead and tune in to MSNBC, but it's not really about that channel or those hosts, it is about a society that thinks that's okay. Where it's not really about diversity, it is about hating other people on the basis of their race.\par \par \uc2 And it's a little bewildering to the rest of us that the whole point of America was that we're aspiring to a country where we are judged by the content of our character, not the color of our skin. And all of a sudden racial reductionists don't simply have the floor and the platform on cable news shows, but have apparently the unquestioning support of the biggest telecommunications company in the United States. What does that say about the country and its leadership?\par \par \uc2 Jason Whitlock is the host of "Fearless." He joins us to respond tonight.\par \par \uc2 Jason Whitlock, thanks so much for coming on. This seems -- and I'll just be honest, as noted -- I didn't really know this was happening. I'm not all that interested in hearing people talk about race and I'm definitely not interested in watching that channel, but once you do watch it, you think, how can this be happening and nobody says anything?\par \par \uc2 JASON WHITLOCK, HOST, "FEARLESS": Tucker, it's pervasive across corporate media. It is pervasive in the sports world. It's one of the reasons why I left \uc2 ESPN\uc2  and FOX Sports to be quite frank with you, and wanted to do my own independent thing because of the way that we talk about race in the corporate media, it is very unhealthy, it is very toxic. It's intended.\par \par \uc2 People have been bought and paid for to undermine this country. Tiffany Cross, \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2 , any of the other people doing this type of stuff, they know exactly what they're doing. They're doing exactly what they're told to do.\par \par \uc2 They are they're planting the seeds to make people believe that the American experiment is a failure and the Constitution needs to be rewritten.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: That's right. That's right.\par \par \uc2 WHITLOCK: And they're using race as their disguise. That's the entire agenda here. They're not hiding it.\par \par \uc2 America is a failure in their mind because the outcomes don't meet their demographic criteria or quotas. And so all of this is to bait all of us into a race war and distract us from an attack on America, its Constitution, and primarily, it's an attack on God.\par \par \uc2 People get upset that, "Oh, like Christian nationalism." This country was founded on biblical principles. Period. End of story. This is a biblical experiment, the United States of America, and it has been a success.\par \par \uc2 The atheists, the Marxists, the people hostile to God want to end this biblical experiment, and they're using race to tear it down. That's the nuclear weapon -- race, race, race, race, race. We haven't treated race right. We've got to write a new Constitution.\par \par \uc2 This whole thing has been a failure. It's a smokescreen. It's an attack on God. Those of us that are believers, regardless of our surface level differences of skin tone, if you're a believer, you've got to wake up and look at what they're doing.\par \par \uc2 They are attacking God. You think they're attacking you, they are not. They are attacking God and they are attacking a country that was founded on biblical principles.\par \par \uc2 Is this country perfect? No way. But it's better than anything else. It's the only biblical experiment that's ever been tried with a nation. The results are better than anybody else has ever gotten. The steps we've taken in this country to correct our racial problems are steps other countries haven't come close to.\par \par \uc2 This is satanic what they're doing, and they've been paid to do it.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I agree with that completely. And I also worry that once you unleash these forces, well, you can't control them. This could go in very ugly directions, and I think it's reckless, really reckless.\par \par \uc2 Jason Whitlock, I appreciate your coming on and for that very clear explanation of what is true.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring you now an update that we think is important on a story we told you about last night.\par \par \uc2 The CDC Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices was on the verge of adding the COVID vaccine to the childhood immunization schedule. That vote is scheduled for tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Now, as we said, if that vote passes, children will be forced to take the shot, a shot they do not need, no scientific basis for requiring it and a shot that could hurt them. They have to take that shot in order to be educated in the United States in a public school.\par \par \uc2 Well, in response to our segment, the CDC complained on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . They claimed that States and not the CDC established vaccine requirements for school children, but like so much else that we have heard from the CDC and it pains us to say this, but it's true, like so much else they have told us over the last two years, they're lying, and they know they're lying.\par \par \uc2 More than a dozen States follow the CDC's immunization schedule to set vaccination requirements, not suggestions, requirements for children to be educated.\par \par \uc2 For example, the \uc2 Virginia Department of Health\uc2  states that: "Vaccines must be administered in accordance with the CDC schedule." The State of Massachusetts says: "No student shall attend to preschool, elementary school, or secondary school program without a certificate of immunization documenting that a child has been successfully immunized in accordance with a schedule that was 'developed in accordance with the recommendations of the CDC advisory panel.'"\par \par \uc2 Tennessee says its immunization requirement "Follow the current schedule from the CDC." New Jersey, Vermont, Ohio say virtually the same thing, we could go on.\par \par \uc2 The point is the CDC sets the standard and then it becomes required across the country. And of course they know that. Of course, they know that. If they know anything, they know that.\par \par \uc2 So, we called the CDC and asked a simple question: Do you deny that once the COVID vaccine is added to the child's immunization schedule, many schools and States will require it based on your recommendation? And of course, they know that's true. We caught them lying. So they didn't even bother to respond to us because apparently we are American citizens and don't deserve a response.\par \par \uc2 It does not enhance your faith at all.\par \par \uc2 We know something weird while on \uc2 Amazon\uc2  the other day, you couldn't find certain books. Really? Are they banning books? Well, yes, they are, actually.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , the world's biggest bookseller has colluded with the Biden administration to censor books whose ideas they believe you shouldn't be exposed to.\par \par \uc2 Whoa. That shouldn't be allowed in a democracy, but they're doing it, and we have the details, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So you go on \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  and you remember this started as a bookstore, an online bookstore, it is the biggest bookstore in the world. They have everything. There's nothing you can't find on \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , including used books.\par \par \uc2 So, if you were to go into \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to read books by a man who is in the news and whose ideas are directly bearing on world events, you look for a guy called Aleksandr Dugin.\par \par \uc2 Dugin is one of Russia's most famous authors and political philosophers. He doesn't work for the government; he doesn't work for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . He's just a philosopher. So if you're interested in like, "What are they thinking over there?" You would search Dugin's author page on \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , but you would not find any results. Really? Kind of a big author to be left off \uc2 Amazon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So we reached out to \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to ask, "Why can't we find any books by this guy?" And then we realized because he's been banned from \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . So then we asked \uc2 Amazon\uc2  for a list of all books and authors who have been banned from their platform, and they wouldn't give it to us.\par \par \uc2 So we went back and forth, back and forth, and finally, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  provided a six-word response, and we're quoting: "\uc2 Amazon\uc2  complies with all applicable laws."\par \par \uc2 Hmm. Applicable laws? Well, in the United States, there are no laws against publishing books because we have the First Amendment. The government can never, under any circumstances, censor any book. Period. Or anything that you have to say. Period. Because that's the core of our Bill of Rights.\par \par \uc2 Then we learned that \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and the Justice Department were ignoring our Bill of Rights. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  apparently based its decision on a Treasury Department designation concerning "disinformation." And that designation applies not only to Dugin, but also to his family, though not to his daughter, who was murdered recently by the Ukrainian government, but we're not allowed to say that. What did she do wrong? Well, I guess she said the wrong thing, but that's cool because they are fighting for freedom.\par \par \uc2 But that's not the point. The point is in our country, which is very different from Ukraine, we're allowed to read whatever we want. But we can't now because the Biden administration is demanding that the biggest bookseller in the world censor books that they disagree with, and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  complies without asking any questions.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is as clear a violation of the First Amendment as you could concoct in a law school class.\par \par \uc2 So then we reach out to the Treasury Department. Did this really happen? Yes, it did. They essentially confirmed it: "We don't comment on possible enforcement matters, but the Treasury Department continues to vigorously enforce Russia-related sanctions."\par \par \uc2 Oh, really? There is no legal basis for ever censoring any book if you're the \uc2 US government\uc2 . That is not allowed. That's the main thing that's not allowed in this country. Period.\par \par \uc2 We don't care who wrote the book. You're allowed to read it. You can read any book you want. You're an American. And if you cease to be able to read any book you want, it doesn't matter if you're an American because you're just a serf.\par \par \uc2 Well, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has refused to provide us with a list of the other books they are banning, but they clearly are banning a lot of different books. And why can't we know what those books are?\par \par \uc2 We're going to continue to look into this, and if there are any legal challenges to the book banning, which is one click from book burning, we hope