 Unanue\uc2 , who is the CEO of \uc2 Goya Foods\uc2 , which is the biggest Latino food company in the United States.\par \par \uc2 So two years ago, he went to the White House to announce he was donating food to a food bank -- to a bunch of food banks -- millions of pounds of it, and during that announcement, he praised the then sitting President of the United States and that caused a boycott led by Sandy Cortez of his company, \uc2 Goya Foods\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But that boycott didn't exactly have the effect that Sandy Cortez believed it would have.\par \par \uc2 We spoke to \uc2 Bob Unanue\uc2  about what happened to his brand on a brand new episode "\uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  Today." Here's part of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BOB UNANUE, CEO, \uc2 GOYA FOODS\uc2 : The way I grew up with a work ethic and faith in God, I see that deteriorating and I can't stay silent about that. But it's not anything against anyone against our consumers. In fact, you know, when I first -- when I said we were blessed with President Trump, we're offering a couple of million pounds of food, that turned into four and a half million pounds of food. I was made a Commissioner in the White House Commission of Hispanic Prosperity and I was there to offer food to a country that had none. The food banks were empty.\par \par \uc2 And I sat there and I said, you know, President Trump was a builder. And I said that we were blessed and the Holy Spirit put those words on my lips and it gave me a platform. All of a sudden, for saying we're blessed, it put me on all these programs.\par \par \uc2 I pissed off a bunch of elites, woke people who are not really -- I mean, AOC, she is technically Latina. I don't think she speaks Spanish or anything, but and she's -- God bless her. She's a young kid. She doesn't know better.\par \par \uc2 But the reaction we got was from Julian Castro, Lin-Manuel Miranda, AOC was boycott, boycott. And of course, you're allowed to boycott a company, but you can't promote it like when President Trump was one of the resolute desks or Ivanka held a can of black beans. So you're allowed to boycott, you're not allowed to support.\par \par \uc2 But it was the greatest thing because we never lost our core business. You know, we sell to the masses, we sell rice and beans. Rice is protein, fiber, antioxidants, phytonutrients. You combine with rice, you get a complete protein. It's very healthy, and it's the main staple of our consumers.\par \par \uc2 And it's not the elites that are -- I mean, if we lost $3.00 from these elites, but we kept our core and Tucker, around -- I go anywhere, and people say hey, you know, I like what you said and I started buying your products.\par \par \uc2 People came up to me, I don't even know how to buy or what to do with your products, but I bought them anyway, so I said -- I gave it away, so don't give it away. Try it. You'll like it. We were doing so good that after AOC threatened to boycott us and we did so well, we made her Employee of the Month for selling -- for raising sales so dramatically.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: \uc2 Goya Foods\uc2 . Amazing. The country is changing.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow morning on FOX Nation, you can see that.\par \par \uc2 We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Holy Smokes, we're out of time. How did that happen?\par \par \uc2 We will be back tomorrow. Tomorrow is Thursday, so you will see us at 8:00 PM then.\par \par \uc2 Here is Sean.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020221020eiaj00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Announces Further Strategic Reserve Releases; Kyiv Struck As \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  Adds Martial Law In Annexed Areas; Vote May Influence State Vaccine Mandates For Schools; President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Denies Petroleum Release Timed To Election; Drug Overdose Epidemic At Crisis Levels; Rural America Flooded With Drugs; Overdose Deaths Spur Drug Sales; Rubio And Demings Clash In Only Debate Before Election; Oregon May Elect Republican As Governor For First Time In 40 Years; Website For Americans To Apply For Student Debt Relief Encountering Some Issues; President Biden's Statements On U.S. Oil Production Draw Criticism; Georgia Democratic Gubernatorial Candidate \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  Behind Republican Incumbent \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  In Polling; Centers For Disease Control And Prevention To Vote On Recommending COVID Vaccines Be Added To Childhood Immunization Schedule\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Jonathan Serrie, Trey Yingst, Brit Hume,  Mike Tobin, Phil Keating, Dan Springer, Gillian Turner \par \uc2 7,946 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Where is he? Come on, come back here.\par \par \uc2 This is why I asked in the break.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: He's so upset. Look at him. He's so upset.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: It's pointless -- it's pointless. I asked them -- I asked them and they don't edit it correctly.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: All right, well that's it for us, SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hi, Bret, are you there?\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Can we help in any way down here? Can we do some editing or something?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Thank you though.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks, Judge.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, President Biden is releasing another 15 million barrels of oil from the nation's strategic reserve in a bid to drive down fuel prices. That move of course comes less than three weeks from the midterm elections that could potentially strip his party of congressional control of one if not two chambers. The president's actions have left the crucial supply of oil intended for emergencies at its lowest level in almost four decades.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy starts us off tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. The president is saying he wants to increase American energy production without delaying the transition to green energy and clean energy. Officials are calling the move a bridge but now we know it's a bridge that leads the strategic reserve to its lowest level since 1984.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Strategic Petroleum Reserve is more than half full.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): Still, too low for critics concerned with national security.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's Strategic Petroleum Reserve, it's not the political petroleum reserve. That's really for a time of war that we need the energy.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That's disputed by the president.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It's not politically motivated at all. 400 million barrels of oil. That's more than enough for any emergency drawdown.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: President Biden is still blaming high gas prices on Russia.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Putin's invasion of Ukraine caused these price hikes.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And greedy oil companies.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And I'm calling on oil companies to pass the savings on to consumers.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The president claims big oil can ramp up production at ease.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: My administration has not stopped or slowed U.S. oil production.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But his administration is leasing federal land to energy companies at a much slower pace than the last four administrations through 19 months in office.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Gasoline prices are high at the pump today not because of the war in Ukraine but because of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's war on energy.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Officials say they plan to refill the reserves when oil falls to about $70.00 a barrel. Right now, it's roughly $85.00. Officials had been saying they were done selling the reserves off.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: So, we're not considering new releases beyond the 180 million.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Two weeks and one day later, they are considering new releases.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We're calling it a ready and release plan.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: High gas prices drive inflation, but the president's priority lately has been abortion. So, Fox asked what's the most important.\par \par \uc2 Just hoping to clarify for Michigan voters, top domestic issue, inflation or abortion?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: All important. Unlike you, there's no one thing, it crosses the board, domestic, ask me about foreign policy too, there's multiple, multiple, multiple issues, and they're all important. And we ought to be able to walk and chew gum at the same time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): And that's a new window into the president's thinking because he said issue number one was inflation back in May but now with this new promise to make a bill protecting abortion access, item number one on the agenda in a new Congress, he is signaling that inflation and abortion access are equally important to him, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy live in the North Lawn. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Attacks are escalating against Ukraine's capital city of Kyiv tonight. Also, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is taking another extreme step to try to salvage his invasion of that country. Putin has declared martial law in four of the areas he annexed last month.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Trey Yingst tells us what that means tonight from Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Ukrainian civilians of Kherson wait to be evacuated across Dnipro River by Russian installed authorities after orders by Moscow to leave.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE (through translator): I got a call from work saying that we are evacuating today. However, I knew yesterday evening already that there would be evacuations.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: As Ukrainian forces advanced on the occupied area, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  announced that Kherson, along with three other illegally annexed territories will enter a state of martial law Thursday.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , RUSSIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): We are working to solve very difficult large scale tasks to ensure Russia's security and safe future to protect our people.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: The people Putin refers to are Ukrainian and the protection he references could come in the form of nuclear weapons use, that according to Kremlin spokesman Dmitry Peskov who responded to a question about Moscow's nuclear umbrella, saying that recently annexed territories will be offered the same protection as the Russian Federation.\par \par \uc2 U.S. officials say Russia's allies like China and India should warn Putin against using nuclear weapons as the head of the \uc2 International Atomic Energy Agency\uc2  says the move would be unlikely.\par \par \uc2 RAFAEL GROSSI, \uc2 INTERNATIONAL ATOMIC ENERGY AGENCY\uc2 : Obviously, nothing can be ruled out, I'm not in the decision making mechanism of other country. But I believe that it would be an extreme measure.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: President Biden weighing in today on the increasing pressure his Russian counterpart faces.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: What it reflects to me is it seems his only tool available to him is to brutalize Ukrainian citizens to try to intimidate them into capitulating.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): We heard loud explosions today as Russian missiles and drones were fired at the capital of Kyiv. This city has become a more frequent target of attacks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey Yingst live in Kyiv. Trey, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Your child may soon need to get the COVID vaccine in order to be able to attend school.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, a CDC panel will vote on whether to recommend adding the shots to the agency's immunization protocol for children in schools. This could of course ignite a firestorm in many communities.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Jonathan Serrie has details tonight from Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN SERRIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Late this afternoon, the CDC's vaccine advisors voted unanimously to continue funding COVID shots for poor and uninsured children as the federal government transitions from providing free shots for everyone to a market based approach.\par \par \uc2 In a separate vote tomorrow, the panel will decide whether to recommend adding COVID vaccines to the CDC's childhood immunization schedule, which guides physicians and health departments. Although it remains each state's decision whether to require a vaccine for school attendance.\par \par \uc2 DR. WILLIAM SCHAFFNER, PROFESSOR, \uc2 VANDERBILT UNIVERSITY MEDICAL CENTER\uc2 : My personal expectation is that there will not be a rush to mandate this vaccine, if for no other reason that it continues to be at least in some quarters rather controversial.\par \par \uc2 SERRIE: Currently, only California and Washington D.C. have COVID vaccine mandates for certain age groups and enforcement is still pending.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, which bans mandates, the state Surgeon General tweeted: Regardless of what CDC votes tomorrow on whether COVID-19 vax are added to routine child immunizations, nothing changes in Florida.\par \par \uc2 For generations, states have mandated some vaccines on the CDC schedule such as measles and polio, while making others optional, such as seasonal flu shots.\par \par \uc2 America's demand for new Omicron targeting COVID boosters has been relatively low. Even as public health officials warned of a possible winter surge in infections driven by multiple Omicron sub variants.\par \par \uc2 DR. ASHISH JHA, WHITE HOUSE COVID COORDINATOR: These sub variants are concerning. But here's the bottom line of what Americans need to know. The new updated vaccines should provide a high degree of protection against these sub variants.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERRIE (on camera): And health officials are urging concerned parents to speak with their pediatrician although young people are less likely to suffer from severe COVID than older folks, the disease still ranks among the top 10 leading causes of death for children in the U.S., Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow the vote tomorrow and the fallout from it. Jonathan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News Senior Political Analyst Brit Hume. Brit, good evening, I want to take you back to our lead story. And that is the release of the oil from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve. Another question by Peter Doocy to the President today. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Is it politically motivated, sir, this move three weeks before the midterms?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, it's not. Look, it makes sense, I've been doing this for how long now? It's not politically motivated at all. It's motivated to make sure that I continue to push on what I've been pushing on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That wasn't Peter, but it was the question about politically motivated or not. Your thoughts on this move at this time?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, the president may say it's not politically motivated, but the circumstances suggest that's exactly what it is. Here we are just weeks away from a midterm election and just weeks away from they're saying in this very month, that they were not -- that the administration was not contemplating another release, gasoline prices once again are beginning to decline. And people are feeling it and the midterm is approaching.\par \par \uc2 If you go back and look Bret at the list of previous sometime -- previous releases from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, what you see is situations where you had hurricanes or other such supply shocks that gave rise to the release from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve. It is after all called strategic for a reason. And that is the worry is a national security one that will give rise to supply shock that could, you know, deeply harm the U.S. economy and affect our national security.\par \par \uc2 Well, you know, high fuel prices in the middle of an inflation are not a great surprise. And I think it's fair to say this is not exactly what the Strategic Petroleum Reserve was designed for.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And that's what the critics are saying as well. You know, this sound bite over the weekend, the president is out at this ice cream shop, got a lot of attention, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Our economy is strong 