D MALE: Police search warrant.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Police search warrant!\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are behind your back now.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: By the time the drugs get to rural America, they have been divided up into small portions for individual use.\par \par \uc2 The whole from this raid was a gun, cash, some meth, and of course, fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 As it proves, time and again, just a small amount of this fentanyl can be fatal.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN ALLEN, METRO DRUG ENFORCEMENT NETWORK TEAM: There's not a family in this area that hasn't been touched by this crisis. That's a guarantee.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): And a disturbing trend we're seeing with fentanyl, deaths lately. When there is a fatal overdose, the users will learn who sold that lethal dose, that dealer will then get a surge of new business because the addicts want to buy the strong stuff. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Amazing. \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  in Charles, West Virginia. Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, our Midterm Spotlight shines on Florida's Senate race, and an unusual gubernatorial contest in Oregon.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. British Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  says she is a fighter, not a quitter, as she faced his fury from her own Conservative Party over a botched economic plan.\par \par \uc2 Truss has apologized to Parliament and admitted making mistakes. Many opposition lawmakers want her to resign.\par \par \uc2 Iranian climber Elnaz Rekabi returns to Tehran after competing in South Korea without wearing a headscarf. It's an act widely seen as support for anti-government demonstrators amid weeks of protests over the Islamic Republic's mandatory hijab. Supporters say they are worried about Rekabi's safety now.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Doha, Qatar. One of the big stories there tonight, a pair of giant pandas sent as a gift from China, arrives in Qatar ahead of next month's World Cup, which will be on Fox.\par \par \uc2 They will take up residence and then out indoor enclosure in the desert nation. The facility is designed to duplicate conditions in the dense forests of China's mountainous Sichuan Province.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 1800 pounds of fresh bamboo will be flown in each week to feed him. That's a lot of bamboo.\par \par \uc2 Just some other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Tonight's Midterm Spotlight begins with the Senate race in Florida. Things turn nasty during last night's debate between incumbent Republican Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  and Democratic challenger Congresswoman Val Demings.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Phil Keating takes a look from Miami this evening.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. VAL DEMINGS (D-FL), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: Of course the senator who has never run anything at all, but his mouth wouldn't know nothing about helping people.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): The congresswoman likes to talk about helping people, she's never passed a bill.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEATING, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Sparks flew at the one and only Senate debate between Republican incumbent \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  and Democratic Congresswoman Val Demings.\par \par \uc2 Topics included inflation U.S. oil production, China, Russia, and abortion, which Rubio opposes in principle.\par \par \uc2 RUBIO: Number one, I'm 100 percent pro-life. That said, every bill I've ever sponsored on abortion, every bill I've ever voted for has exceptions.\par \par \uc2 KEATING: Demings is pro-choice up to the point of viability.\par \par \uc2 DEMINGS: No, Senator, I don't think it's OK. For a 10-year-old girl to be raped and have to carry the seed of her rapist.\par \par \uc2 KEATING: Both candidates have been flooding the Florida airwaves with campaign ads. Demings more so than Rubio