e closely. Dan Springer live in Portland, Dan, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Next we go to Rhode Island. Retiring Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jim Langevin\uc2  has represented that district, district two, for more than 20 years. He is not seeking reelection. Former Cranston mayor, Republican \uc2 Allan Fung\uc2 , is hoping to flip it red. Recent polling finds Fung leading Democrat Seth Magaziner by eight points. Our new FOX News power rankings have shifted that race from lean Democrat now to toss-up.\par \par \uc2 It's shaping up to be a difficult beginning for President Biden's plan to cancel billions of dollars in student loan debt. After just two days applicants are facing some serious problems. Correspondent Gillian Turner has the story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It landed and handled more than 8 million applications without a glitch or any difficulty.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: That was Monday. Now just 48 hours later. Americans are reporting major problems applying for up to $20,000 student debt forgiveness. So far, the website is blocking foreign I.P. addresses, meaning Americans abroad are locked out. The Education Department is now scrambling to produce paper applications. Married folks with joint federal loans also facing a glitch. They have been locked out of the program and had have to request a special waiver before applying for student debt cancelation.\par \par \uc2 For millions of other applicants, though, the process is easy, too easy according to experts who say the program is ripe for fraud and abuse.\par \par \uc2 BETSY MAYOTTE, INSTITUTE OF STUDENT LOAN ADVISORS: There will be people that will see that there is no verification and lie on the application, and say that they are eligible when, in fact, they are not from an income perspective.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Applicants are asked only for their full name, date of birth, Social Security number, and contact information. Applicants won't have to provide proof of income on which the loan forgiveness is supposed to be based.\par \par \uc2 MAYOTTE: I have seen people fake their own deaths to get a death discharge on their student loan.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Applicants are asked to self-attest under perjury to earning less than $125,000 per year, though they can face fines and jail time if they are found to have lied.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: Just a few things applicants over the weekend have said, it was the easiest application they have ever filled out. It took maybe 60 seconds. That's a quote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TURNER (on camera): And 12 million Americans so far have applied for debt cancellation. The government expects at least 40 million between now and December 31st. That's when the applications close. The department says they will verify a certain number of those 40 million borrowers to make sure they have told the truth about their income. This is the mechanism to protect against mass scale fraud, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They might have legal challenges as well. Gillian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of which, a Wisconsin taxpayer group is asking the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  to stop the president's plan. The plaintiffs from Brown County say the president's circumvention of Congress is an assault on the separation of powers.\par \par \uc2 The request goes to Justice \uc2 Amy Coney Barrett\uc2  based solely on geography. She can decide alone or refer the full court for action. She is likely but not obligated to ask the Biden administration to file a response within the next few days.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on President Biden's use of the Strategic Oil Reserve to try to drive down gasoline prices ahead of the midterms.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN, (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We need to responsibly increase American oil production without 