he is speaking to very liberal voters. Georgia is a very moderate state, a swing state. And when you are talking about abortion as an economic issue, that may appeal to the progressives that she talks to, but it comes across as coldhearted to your swing voters in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 And it's a problem that the Democratic Party at large has. When they talk about cultural issues, they talk about abortion, they have a hard time communicating to the middle of the road voters who may support abortion rights but when they -- when you hear her talk about abortion as an economic issue, it just turns off a lot of these same voters.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Morgan, thoughts?\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: It was one of the worst answers I have heard this entire election cycle. To equate and say if you are worried about inflation, just abort your baby is essentially what she said. And I think whatever suburban moms the Democrats are trying to appeal with, with the Supreme Court decision, it gets washed away with those nonsensical answers.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, let's turn to Oregon, Howie. This race, we just did a piece, Dan Springer. It's fascinating. This is a place that has not seen a Republican governor for a long, long time. If you look at the race right now, according to the polls, she is up. There is an independent that is getting support, did get support from former \uc2 Nike\uc2  founder \uc2 Phil Knight\uc2  and others, and basically on the issue of crime and homelessness in Portland.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Well, the fact that we are even talking about a potential Republican governor of Oregon tells you about how much the winds right now are in favor of the Republican Party after the Democrats with the abortion issue seem to have some momentum over the summer. No wonder \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  is trying to diffuse that issue.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, you look at one of the bluest states in the country, New York, where Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  who succeeded, she was lieutenant governor of \uc2 Andrew Cuomo\uc2 , is now a few points ahead according to the latest polls of her Republican challenger \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 . And so if Democrats are having to play defense in states like that, it tells you that in more competitive states where a lot of the Republicans had been written off by the press as being extremists, Trump-backed conservatives, you look at the numbers, and those races are within a few points.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So do you think speaking to the press that the media changed now and that it's starting to be conventional wisdom that inflation and money and crime and immigration are maybe more important?\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: I think even the most left leaning members of our profession, Bret, can't deny that the Republicans have the momentum now. And it's not just polls. It's the issues that favor Republicans, crime and inflation among them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Josh, anything else you're seeing three weeks out?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: That crime issue. The economy and inflation are the top issue across the country, but crime is a huge issue not just in New York and Oregon, but in Wisconsin, Pennsylvania, the two Senate races there. If Republicans take back the Senate, the crime issue and Democrats nominating extreme candidates on the left could make the difference.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: On issue that.\par \par \uc2 All right, panel, stand by if you would. Up next, new COVID-19 vaccine protocols may be coming for your schoolchild.\par \par \uc2 Later, more than 100 heroes come Washington. We have a special SPECIAL REPORT salute.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How can we protect the kids and get them back to school?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER: The way you do that, you get the people who interact with the children to be vaccinated and masked, you provide ventilation in the schools, you try to keep them in the schools safely.\par \par \uc2 DR. NICOLE SAPHIER, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: They have not proven with efficacy data that there is overwhelming benefit to all children receiving these vaccines, especially if some of the kids are getting side effects from the vaccines, that that in themselves is actually keeping them home from school, which is what we are trying to prevent.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Vaccines with kids and schools. Today the CDC advisers voted unanimously to continue funding COVID shots for poor and uninsured children. Tomorrow, a separate vote will decide whether to recommend adding COVID vaccines to the childhood immunization schedule, which guides physicians and health departments and could affect whether your kids have that shot across the board. Really up to the states.\par \par \uc2 We are back with the panel. Morgan, obviously there's going to be some communities that say we're not there.\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: Yes, and I think it was about six weeks ago I was on your show and you did a segment about low confidence in the CDC. And so parents have to look at this unanimous vote today by the CDC while also looking at the \uc2 European Union\uc2  who are not recommending these shots for children under 12. So I think ultimately it comes back to this same issue we have been arguing about since all of COVID, and that is should the government mandate this for your children or should the parents decide?\par \par \uc2 There's obviously things like measles and other things that we have been getting vaccinated for for decades. But I think you are going to see this action happen state by state, and you have already seen in Florida where the surgeon general has said it's not going to be mandated there, because it's illegal to do so in Florida.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, there's a lot of people, Howie, that just want more info, they want more data. They don't feel like they have all the information. "The Wall Street Journal"