 to get mandates for their kids, you are not going to get in almost any state in the entire country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And then you talk about students, Morgan, this is an A.P. headline, "ACT test scores dropped to lowest in 30 years in pandemic slide." And we are seeing other headlines like this across the country.\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: Yes, who do have predicted all of this, except so many people that did. What we should be focusing on is getting our children back to school. In fact, I can't even believe I'm saying that in the fall of 22 that we are still talking about getting children back to school. You could go through statistic after statistic, what you just talked about, from the A.P. test, that children are woefully behind in this country at a time, by the way, when they are not behind in the rest of the world. If we want to talk about our strategic competitors, China, Iran, Russia, and others, their kids are not falling behind the way ours are because of some really stupid decisions that have been made in liberal jurisdictions.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So do we think that this issue is going to play in some of these races this midterm, especially for governors who dealt with COVID throughout that time?\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Well, it certainly could be a good issue for Republicans who have been much more skeptical of vaccines and mandates, although it is maybe crowded out by --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Like DeSantis, for example, in Florida?\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Sure, absolutely. But I think we are just now beginning to grapple with the fact that schools were closed for so long, not just in terms of academic performances coming down but the social impact on our kids having to stay home and stare at a computer screen, including very young kids.\par \par \uc2 And so I think in the minds of a lot of parents, this is going to be seen as the heavy hand of government even though it's not an enforceable mandate, and it's going to be conflated with the idea that kids were kept out and that they had to wear masks long after adults didn't have to. And that made no sense as well.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But the teachers' unions still pushing for a lot of this stuff, masks in schools.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: There is a lot of scar tissue from what happened two years ago, and especially in these governor's races. Voters remember. And it's not just Republican voters. It's independents and even a lot of moderate Democrats that were upset at the teachers' unions for keeping these regulations in place as long as they did. And you're going to see those results, I believe, in the midterm election. It's an underrated issue in this election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And in some places, in the government, they are still using the emergency to do things.\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: Listen, we saw it in Virginia, in that governor's race. \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2  had a column about this just a few days ago. I think it's going to be a big, big motivator, continue to be a big motivator for parents. Kids need to be in school. Why are we even still discussing this?\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says the pandemic is over, but this goes against that message.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Definitely.\par \par \uc2 Panel, thanks so much. Good to see you in person.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a SPECIAL REPORT salute. More than 100 veterans from the Chicago area came to Washington, D.C., during an honor flight. Have you seen these things? They're really amazing. Veterans from all military branches serving during World War II, the Korean War, and Vietnam, they are treated as VIPs. They visit the war memorials, other attractions. Honor Flight Chicago was arranged for 9,000 veterans to experience this special adventure over the years, and we salute your service to the country and your sacrifice as well. It's really fun to be in Washington, D.C., and see these folks arrive.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we'll be coming to you live from Los Angeles, and we'll see what comes with that.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it with this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. There are a lot of times I end this show and it goes over, and it's, I take some of Jesse Watters' time. Tonight, I'm going to give back. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts seven seconds early.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020221020eiaj00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President Biden Releasing Another 15 Barrels Of Oil From The Strategic Petroleum Reserve To Quell Soaring Gas Prices And Inflation, But He Refuses To Admit That It's A Political Move Given Midterm Elections Is Right Around The Corner; President Biden Calls The Election In Georgia As Another Jim Crow 2.0 For Voter Suppression And Election Subversion By Republicans When Georgia's Early Voting Shows It Is Breaking Records; Democrat Gubernatorial Candidate Stacey Abrams Said That Having More Babies Can Worsen The Current Status Of The Economy And It's Going To Cost A Lot For Families That Are Already Struggling; Ousted New York Times Editor \uc2 James Bennet\uc2  Speaks Out Of The Paper's Woke Culture That's Been Running Inside; Huge Mistake: Former NYT Editor Blasts Paper For Caving To Woke Mob; Kamala Hires 3rd Chief Speechwriter; Reefer Madness: Weed Use Becoming A New Normal Among Young Adults\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeanine Pirro, Piers Morgan, Jesse Watters, Dana Perino, Greg  Gutfeld \par \uc2 8,264 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANIEL DAVIS, SENIOR FELLOW & MILITARY EXPERT, DEFENSE PRIORITIES: Emergency and not depleted before that happens.\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right. Got it, my friend. Very good seeing you. Danny, thank you as always, but more for your incredible service to this country. He's a very modest guy. I think the incredible still holds. Danny Davis on that. That'll do it here. Here comes THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS HOST: Hello, everyone. I'm Judge Jeanine Pirro, along with Piers Morgan, Jesse Watters, Dana Perino, and Greg Gutfield. It's five o'clock in New York City, and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 President Biden's desperation is showing again as he tries to pump up Democrats before the midterms. His party's chances are dwindling by the day, just as Americans are about to get pummeled by even higher gas prices. So, what is Joe's solution? A good old-fashioned bribe.\par \par \uc2 President Biden treating our nation's emergency oil stockpile as his own personal piggy bank. Biden is releasing another 15 million barrels, even though our reserve is at its lowest level in nearly four decades. And the best part, Biden isn't even being coy about his scheme.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: I have been doing everything in my power to reduce gas prices since Putin's invasion of Ukraine caused these price hikes.\par \par \uc2 I have been acting so aggressively. Without the steps we've taken over the past several months to ramp up production on lower prices and get relief to consumers.\par \par \uc2 Bring down the price you charge at the pump to reflect what you pay for the product. You still make a significant profit. Your shareholders will still do very well. And the American people will catch a break they deserve and get a fair price at the pump as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And seconds later, the same guy claimed that politics has nothing to do with it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: What is your response to Republicans who say you are only doing this SPR release because -- to help Democrats in the midterms?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Where have they been the last four months? That's my response.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Is it politically motivated, sir? This --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, it's not.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: -- three weeks before the midterms?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Look, it makes sense, but I've been doing this for how long now? It's not politically motivated at all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, so here we are. The truth is that gas went up 50 percent before the first shot in the Ukraine, and yet Biden is making a decision that he's got to tap the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, the lowest it's been in four decades. Why is he doing that?\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CO-HOST: He said because he's been doing it for a long time. That's his answer. I like to go grocery shopping and --\par \par \uc2 PIERS MORGAN, FOX NEWS CO-HOST: Really?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I'll go and I'll buy lamb chops and I'll stock up on dinners and I'll get home sometimes and I'll -- I'll text them. I'll say, so what are we having for dinner? And she'll say, Jesse, you know what? Sorry, I got home late. You're going to have to order delivery.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And I'm like, wait, I just went grocery shopping. You're saying now I have to order out. That's \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . That is what he's doing.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I get it.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: He is --\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CO-HOST: I did not follow this analogy.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: I followed it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You follow it.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CO-HOST: No, his -- his food delivery costs. I've been eating at him.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes, I know.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: For a few days.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Trust me. Yes. Especially the ice cream. So, if you're raining the rainy-day fund and it's not a rainy day. He's been saying, we're going to have nuclear Armageddon with Russia. He's been saying China is about to prey on Taiwan. He's been saying that climate change is making these hurricanes more ferocious.\par \par \uc2 Those are when you want to use the rainy-day fund, not when the Democrats here get to get rocked. This guy, no wonder \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  handles the finances and the money because he's reckless. He's absolutely reckless and he's leaving the cupboards bare.\par \par \uc2 Remember when Trump came in during COVID and he is like --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: -- the cupboards are bare. There's no PPE. That's because Obama and Biden left at bare. Look what happened when Trump came in. He said military didn't have any bullets. That's what President Trump said, but we had to restock. And now the same thing is happening with oil, and the same thing, judge, is going to happen with our armaments because we're giving it all to the Ukrainians. It's very, very irresponsible. And there's no environmental difference. Right?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Right.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: There's still going to be the same emissions, but American workers and companies aren't going to get paid.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Are suffering.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We paid for these stockpiles, the taxpayers. Now the taxpayers aren't supposed to be taking a bath here. What are we going to do? We're going to sell the oil that we've paid for to the Chinese because Hunter has a connection, and then we're going to take the revenues and then buy the \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents guns. That doesn't make any sense whatsoever. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  knows it, but that's all he has left.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: The truth is, Piers, that, you know, when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  talked about this, he said that, you know, that the big oil companies they -- they're making more money than ever, which isn't true. But that they should share the profits.\par \par \uc2 And you know when Jesse, when you talked about Hunter, don't you think Hunter should share some of the profits? Maybe 10 percent for the big guy, you know? You know, and the whole thing is anti-capitalist.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: Yes. I mean, I think the thing about President Biden, it's never his fault. Anything.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Nothing.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: It's the one thing that I've really noticed from his presidency. It's always somebody else's fault. Usually, he blames Putin or whoever he can think of. but actually here, if you chart the genesis of his relationship with the Saudis, for example, when he was running for president, he was very vocal. The Saudis had to become pariahs.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: I'm sure that didn't go down well with Saudi, who of course considered themselves to be an ally of the United States. Then of course he's been flirting again with the Iranians, that again, will not go down well with the Saudis. So, surprise, surprise, the Saudis are not overly keen to help him out in his moment of need about oil prices, and they've decided to cut production.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is throwing his toys out of a stroller about it. Well, you read what you sold to an extent. So, I think constantly blaming other people, which seems to be his default mechanism for anything which actually could be laid at his door. I think it's beginning to wear a bit thin with the electorate, and of course this is transparent -- transparently political. You know, this is done to try and correct what's going on at the gas prices where he saw a little blip up in the Democrat fortunes heading to the midterms, but now gas prices are edging back up again, not falling. He wants to try and stop it.\par \par \uc2 So, it's all very disingenuous, very political, and by blaming Putin, again, very Biden.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Hash tag.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Very Biden.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right. You know, Dana, the truth is that, you know, if he releases the 15 million barrels. I mean, it is, that's not going to have any significant impact on the price of oil.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's like a -- it's like --\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: Well, a say, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It's just about one day's worth. And it's a global market. We have, if you believe in markets, a global market for is what we have. And so that's what helps set the price. That's why you need to have more. That's why you should be producing more energy here in America.\par \par \uc2 I think a lot of people are still confused about why on day one his first order of business was to cancel the \uc2 Keystone\uc2  pipeline.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: Right?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And then you had them pass the Inflation Reduction Act, which was really the climate change bill. Look at all of the green groups on the left. When that bill passed, they were thrilled because they got everything that they were promised. And what happened is you had the president opposing domestic energy, trying to force a transition on the country that we're just not ready for, not because we don't want it, but we're just not quite there in the innovation.\par \par \uc2 The innovations will come and they're exciting and there's a lot of good stuff down the pipeline, but we're not going to need to get 