ions; New York GOP House Candidate On Race Against DCCC Chair; Poll: Oz Within 2 Points Of Fetterman; Victim: Told Teens To Stop Cursing, They Attacked; Fox Rides With Drug Trafficking Task Force In WV; Manslaughter Charge In Deadly NYC Subway Fight; New York Gubernatorial Race Tightens; RPT: First Lady Upset Over Biden's January News Conference\b0\par\par\uc2 Trace Gallagher, Bill Hemmer, Mike Tobin, Joe Concha \par \uc2 8,393 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: \uc2 Larry Kudlow\uc2  later on in the \uc2 Fox Business Network\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS HOST: Well, you are just a busy bee, aren't you?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It's a busy time, isn't it? 20 days until Election Day, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thanks so much for joining us, everybody. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'm John Roberts. We will see you back here again tomorrow, 1:00 Eastern Time for America reports "THE STORY" with Trace Gallagher in for Martha starts right now. Hi, Trace.\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS HOST: John, thank you. Sandra, bring beer. Thank you very much. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Trace Gallagher in for Martha MacCallum. In an effort to bring down prices at the pump, President Biden releasing millions more barrels of oil from our emergency supply and using the announcement to push Clean Energy. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm asking the Congress pass a permitting bill to speed up the approval of all kinds of energy production, from wind, to solar, to clean hydrogen, because we need to get this movie now. Quickly. Now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: He says the oil release is not politically motivated at all though, Republicans say just the opposite 20 days from Election Day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL (R-TX): He's playing politics with this national security asset that we have that's really developed and it really for a time of war that we need the energy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Let's bring in Katie Pavlich, Townhall.com News Editor, and \uc2 Mark Penn\uc2 , Former Clinton Adviser, now Chairman and CEO of \uc2 Stagwell\uc2  and Chairman of the Harris Poll, both our Fox News Contributors. Welcome to you both. Katie, I'm going to start with you. What really struck me about the President's gas speech, not the fact that it seemed totally unrehearsed, which was a little disconcerting for me. But he said that we've been having a rough four or five years, we went back and looked when, when he was inaugurated on Inauguration Day, gas was like $2.31, a gallon significantly higher now, not a rough four or five years, Katie, a very rough 21 months.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, TOWNHALL.COM NEWS EDITOR: Well, the continued depletion of the Strategic Petroleum Reserve by this President is outrageous, and it is a scandal. It is a supply that is supposed to be reserved for times of war for the United States, not for Ukraine, but for the United States, or in times of natural disasters. This administration seems to really love charts, they should take a look at the chart when it comes to the supply of the reserve. If you take a look, it's pretty flat until \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's administration, and it goes straight down because they continue to use it for political purposes.\par \par \uc2 And the White House continues to argue that the release of these barrels of oil had brought the price of the pump down. That's not true. We are in a recession, despite the fact that the White House doesn't want to admit it, the demand for oil and gas at the pump has gone down. And let's not forget that this administration sold millions of barrels of us reserve oil to China over the summer. So, there he's putting us in a crisis situation. And for them now to say that they want to refill the reserve at $70.00 a barrel, if we get there, while Democrats refuse to allow President Trump to do it at $24.00 a barrel, well, then telling people it saves the taxpayer money is just dishonest and doesn't add up with the math.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: It's a fair point, Mark, because you know, you look at this and you think, OK, they're going to release 15 million barrels, and they're going to release it over time. So, it's over a couple of weeks, and you got really a million, one million barrels a day going out. And you think, well, that's one-twentieth of the supply that we need. So, Katie's right, it doesn't have a significant effect on the price of gas. So, it's, it's, is it political is the answer, is the question?\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN\uc2 , CHAIRMAN AND CEO, \uc2 STAGWELL\uc2 : Well, look, first of all, as you know, even small amounts in a supply and demand market can have a significant impact on price. We'll see what the, what happens to the price that projection was that was going to go up now. Look, I think that, that Katie is right in the sense that these things were meant to be in conditions of war or things like Katrina, and I don't think we're going to run out of oil any day. But look, the voters are not stupid. This is just before the election. It's per day, every day before the election. They understand that this is part of a series of election, election time moves, that the administration has tried to put the best face on it. I mean, they're not -- everybody sees that.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, I think everybody does see it. And Katie, I want to play one more of the president soundbites. This is where he talks about blaming some other people not himself. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: With my announcement today, we're going to continue to stabilize markets and decrease the prices at a time when the actions of other countries have caused such volatility. And I've told my team behind me here to be prepared to look further, look for further releases in the months ahead if needed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Nobody really brings up, certainly not the President that this is self-inflicted, Katie, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Yes, again, outrageous that the president of the United States is depleting the strategic reserve because of his own failed war on domestic energy production. President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  campaigned on ending fossil fuels, some of his first things he did in his day one in office at the White House was to make sure he sold uncertainty into the oil markets. And now, he's turning around and demanding that they ramp up production when they have completely tied the hands of the energy market. They have done this to themselves. They've done it to the American people.\par \par \uc2 And now, the administration is putting the United States of America in a terrible position when it comes to national security by continuing to tap the strategic reserve. You're talking about politics, Mark, I want to put this up. This is WAPO analysis by Philip Bump yesterday, it's a key election metric for Democrats, is a sign in your neighborhood. Isn't that the truth? I mean, every time you drive to work, and you see these big 20- foot signs and the gases, in California, it's, you know, $6.00-$7.00 a gallon, but you have this visceral feeling, right? It's smacking you in the face every single day, Mark, and you get this feeling that something is just not right, when you're paying, when it cost you 140 bucks to fill your tank.\par \par \uc2 PENN: Well, look, inflation is the number one problem that the electorate says is important to them at this time. But when I worked with President Clinton, we had a $2.00 a gallon tripwire. If it got above $2.00, we would do everything within our power to bring it down. I don't know where the tripwire is in this administration, but it is an important factor in politics, it is an important factor in this particular election. And look saying that you should pass the permitting bill that's going to have an impact in three or five years, you know, is not a realistic option compared to what needs to be done now.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, Mark, goes back to the Clinton years when you know, this was a guy who felt our pain and now you have a president who didn't really seem like he feels a lot of pain in this, Katie. He's just blaming people, right, not accepting some responsibility and saying, here's how we're going to fix it just point, finger pointing all the way across. Your final thoughts.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Well, worse, insulting the American people by spending more time in Delaware, eating an ice cream cone and saying the economy is really strong. While Americans know that they're already in a recession. And they're paying exorbitant amounts at the gas station, while the administration tells them that nothing is wrong.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: By the way, the ice cream cone was up about, like 27 percent. So, just, just throwing it out there -- ice cream calling that's a special cone, you know that waffle cone? That brings the price up a little bit.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: That's very special.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Katie, Mark, great to have you on. Let's bring in Sandra Smith, Co-Anchor of "AMERICA REPORT." Sandra, it's always great to have you I know as soon as the President spoke right, today, you were very thrifty, and you brought out your American Petroleum response the institute. And I thought that's fascinating. It's a good place to start with you. Because it really is, you have the president kind of going after oil companies, right?\par \par \uc2 You have the president kind of giving out this blame: first, it's Putin, and then it's the oil companies, and then it's the refined, it's all this thing, and the \uc2 American Petroleum Institute\uc2  says the quote here, "The SPR was established to reduce the impact of severe supply disruptions, not as a long-term solution to the economic pain Americans are feeling at the pump. We urge the administration to recognize that short-term policymaking is no substitute for the long-term strategies needed to encourage American energy production." And this administration made it clear from the get-go, Sandra, they were not about to encourage the propagation of oil and fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: That's right, because tapping the Strategic Petroleum Reserve is a quick fix. That's not a long-term strategy. That's why in the face of the President's announcement to supposedly bring down energy prices or his effort to bring down energy prices, they're actually up -- they started spiking in the middle of this speech, and they've only gone up since. Oil prices are up four percent right now. They did not go down after he spoke, and these allegations of price gouging need to be addressed, because it's a very dangerous environment for a White House to be accusing oil companies.\par \par \uc2 We've even heard President Biden in the past say that there's growing evidence of price gouging by America's energy companies, when in fact, there had been multiple, many investigations launched at the federal and the state level that have never turned up actual evidence of price gouging. If there were, Trace, that would be illegal activity, and people would be held responsible and held accountable. So, that needs to be addressed. And then, this idea of blaming the oil companies for sitting on what they called record profits.\par \par \uc2 Well, since when is it the government, the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 's role to tell private business, what they should do with their profits? We live in a free-market society this is, this is Capitalism. You don't have the government dictating private businesses, how they should spend their, their profits or spend their money. So, that is also an interesting moment as well. Certainly, there's been moments in history where they try to work together to improve situations, but these are oil companies who were told that this is an administration whose goal is to end fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And I want to put this up if I can this is the Wall Street Journal editorial board piece titled: Get ready for the big chill, because you're talking about energy, we focus on gas prices but this is this: "Average U.S. household spending will increase for all heating fuels this winter, including natural gas, heating oil up 27 percent, electricity, propane. The blame higher oil and natural gas prices from demand outstripping supply. Coal plant shutdowns in particular have increased demand for natural gas." The bottom line here, and I've only got about 30 seconds left for you, Sandra, the bottom line here is, is that it's across the board. I mean, it is going to be painful this winter across the board to stay warm, and to get where you're going.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I just had the head of a heating oil company on my show last hour, who said he has never seen any time like this. He talks to his customers. They're fearful how they're going to make it through the winter, Trace. This is a very real moment where this, these high energy prices and this crisis is inflicting real pain on the American people. And we need a long- term strategy, not a, not these quick fixes that in some cases don't even serve to bring energy prices down at all. It needs to be addressed and it needs to be addressed as a whole matter. We were not in this situation just a couple years ago. It has dramatically changed, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, Sandra Smith, always great to see you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I forgot the beer, by the way.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: You forgot the beer. Roberts gave you the beer and you walked out without it.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: He's off having one already.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: We'll catch you; we'll catch you later.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Just joking.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Sandra, thank you. Well, meantime, a midterm deep dive into the key House races you need to watch as Republicans look to take a decisive majority and even target the seat of a top ally of Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA), SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES: We're holding our own. It's a matter of who turns out to vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, our latest Fox News Power Rankings making a small shift in favor of the GOP, with Republicans projected to take the House in a 15 seat majority. But with the midterm elections just 20 days away, there still remains clear pathways for Democrats to retain their majority.\par \par \uc2 In moments, two Republican candidates vying to flip blue seats red: Michael Lawler from New York, April Becker from Nevada. But first, "AMERICA'S NEWSROOM" co-host Bill Hemmer to break down some of the key House races. Bill, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Hey, Trace. Good afternoon to you. I've got three minutes, I want to tell you four stories. OK?\par \par \uc2 So, let's see how this goes. I want to start in Texas. Remember the gravitational pull that Republicans feel that they have now with the Hispanics? We're going to find out whether or not that's the case, whether or not that continues in about 20 days.\par \par \uc2 Everything you see here, Trace, is considered a key race, and three of those five are right along the border with Mexico.\par \par \uc2 We take you back to 2020 now, OK? So, this is Biden and Trump. You see all the blue down here for the Democrats? Well, there are specks of red that started popping up. And nobody really anticipated that. So, that does that continue now in 2022 in these midterms?\par \par \uc2 This is Mayra Flores, she won a special election a few months ago. She is facing off against \uc2 Vicente Gonzalez\uc2 , right along the Galveston Bay, Gulf of Mexico. She has an uphill climb, but if she does well, you're going to start to hear a lot more about the stories along this -- the border here between Mexico and Texas.\par \par \uc2 Monica De La Cruz, the Republican. That's a new seat here, 15. Recently withdrawn, she stands a very good chance of winning. And also, \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , who is the last pro-life Democrat in the Democratic Party in Congress today, a real strong challenge by Cassy Garcia, could be a very tight election there.\par \par \uc2 So, Trace, what I'm watching here on election night is what happens now down in these districts. And immigration, the border, going to be big topics down there.\par \par \uc2 That's Story number one. We're going to pop out here to the west and talk about story number two. That's the economy, its inflation, its gas prices.\par \par \uc2 Las Vegas is right down here, just above Nevada District 3. This is Susie Lee, the Democrat trying to win back her seat against April Becker, the Republican challenger.\par \par \uc2 Now this is one of two of the most expensive races in the entire country. They spent upwards of $25-$27 million alone, trying to capture this seat. It is razor tight right now.\par \par \uc2 Republicans feel good about it because gas prices are so high. Inflation is an issue, the economy is an issue. So, watch Nevada District 3 come election night.\par \par \uc2 The other two, one of them is in Michigan. This also is the second most expensive race in the entire country.\par \par \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2 , here in Lansing, Michigan, is facing off against Tom Barrett. Barrett was on our air about an hour ago, making his case to win.\par \par \uc2 They have spent $27 million between the two parties trying to keep this seat and Democratic column. Slotkin, she won back in 2018, won again in 2020. She is trying to win her third term.\par \par \uc2 This is a district, if you break it down based on parties, Republican and Democrat, it is razor tight, but Republicans have an edge. It's going to be a challenge for Slotkin, but we'll see how it goes come election night.\par \par \uc2 The last stop we have, Trace, is here in New York. Remember the beginning of this conversation is talking about all the key races. Look what you have in New York, right? There's one, two, three, four, five, six.\par \par \uc2 We got a second panel here. Hang on, Trace. We got one, two, three, four, five.\par \par \uc2 There was a poll that came out yesterday that showed \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , a Republican challenger for governor. Within four points of the Democratic Governor \uc2 Kathleen Hochul\uc2 . If that's true, Trace, if that's even close to being true on election night, you're going to see a lot of these districts swing.\par \par \uc2 And one of them could be right here in New York 17. \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  is the Democrat. He's running in a different district now because they redrew the lines in New York just like they did based on the census of so many other states.\par \par \uc2 He is the head of the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee. He is being challenged by Republican Mike Lawler. Now, Republicans have talked with confidence for weeks now that they can take Maloney down.\par \par \uc2 If that's even close to true, Trace, remember all those key races we showed you? New York could really be the center of the storm come election night.\par \par \uc2 And it hasn't happened in some time. But Republicans are confident they believe they are within sight of a lot of these races. And we'll see how it goes. And I know you've got more with Lawler. So, back to you, Trace, now.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, we've also got George Pataki coming on, Bill. He was the last Republican governor in New York, and the whole \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  thing is going to fascinate him, because it's getting very close. Bill, as always, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Republic --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Yes, (INAUDIBLE) watch.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, a Republican candidate for Congress in New York 17 District, Mike Lawler.\par \par \uc2 I mean, he is running against Congressman \uc2 Sean Maloney\uc2 , chair of the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee, as Bill was saying.\par \par \uc2 We invited him on, but did not hear back. So, we're going to speak with Mr. Lawler. And Mike, you know, listen, this really is. It's very tight. And Bill makes a great point.\par \par \uc2 You know, if this thing goes your way, this could be a bellwether for what happens overall throughout the country on election night.\par \par \uc2 MIKE LAWLER (R-NY), CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: Oh, absolutely. And you know, our latest polling has us up six points. And we are going to be the face of the red wave on November 8th. And it really comes down to a few things: inflation and crime.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWLER: And voters across this district are feeling it harder than anyone else in the country right now.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And it's interesting, Mike, because your opponent says when you talk about the economy -- and you're right, the economy and crime are the two big issues. Actually, in New York, crime is the number one issue.\par \par \uc2 When your opponent talks about the economy, he is not really boasting about anything except for lowering some health care costs.\par \par \uc2 So, there doesn't seem to be a lot for Democrats to talk about when it comes to the economy.\par \par \uc2 LAWLER: Well, no, you can't. You can't, you know, be proud of a 41-year record high on inflation. You can't be proud of skyrocketing gas prices and home heating costs. You certainly can't be proud of surging crime.\par \par \uc2 I mean, \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  said that his top priority was enacting cashless bail. Well, since cashless bail has been enacted, crime is up 36 percent in New York City, and over 40 percent of those released on non- monetary bail have been rearrested while those charges are pending.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWLER: He owns this. He has voted 100 percent lockstep with \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . And if folks want to send a message to \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  on election night, they should go to firemaloney.com and support our campaign to defeat him.\par \par \uc2 You say he owns it, and he doesn't back away from that. I mean, when you talk about cash bail, his response is that a poor guy should not be left in jail while a rich guy gets out.\par \par \uc2 You've called cash bail about the dumbest policy you've ever heard. Your final thoughts on this, Mike.\par \par \uc2 LAWLER: Absolutely. It is truly the single stupid as piece of legislation that has ever been enacted. And it has made New Yorkers less safe.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Sean Maloney\uc2  continues to double down on it and support it, as crime escalates. And people are being assaulted in the streets, pushed in front of subways, shot, and killed. And he refuses to do anything about it.\par \par \uc2 I will fight to end cashless bail. And that's why we need to ensure his defeat on Election Day.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAWLER: And people can go to firemaloney.com to help me do it.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, and there is a big wave concerning those subjects across the country. We'll see how it works out. Mike Lawler, thank you for coming on.\par \par \uc2 Here now, Republican candidate for Nevada's 3rd Congressional District, April Becker. April, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 As Bill Hemmer was saying, I mean, this race has been expensive in Nevada, and it's razor thin. You've got 20 days to go. What's the push out in Nevada right now?\par \par \uc2 APRIL BECKER (R-NV), CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: Hey, its economy. Economy, economy, economy. People are suffering here. We've got 16 percent inflation. We've got some of the highest gas prices in the country.\par \par \uc2 And on top of it, crime surging, we've got 22 percent increase in burglaries and break-ins. You know, people are suffering here and they're tired of it. It's -- we're all ready for a change.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, it's interesting. I want to put this USA Today Suffolk poll. This is in Nevada, on the top issues. Inflation/economy, you see 43 percent. Abortion, number two at 25 percent. Then, you get schools/education, climate change.\par \par \uc2 You don't see a big thing in crime in there. I'm wondering if that's unusual to you. And do you think that there is a sense that Democrats are saying to people that the crime and economy can wait because abortion is really the focus right now?\par \par \uc2 BECKER: Now, economy is the most important thing facing everyone here. Abortion is legal in Nevada. It's the laws have been in place for you know, since 1990. It's a non-issue in this state. With the ruling and Roe, no politicians can do anything about it.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BECKER: So, that's just gas lighting by the Democrats, and it's not working, you know --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: You know what --- you know what's interesting to me, April, is the fact that, you know, Latinos support. Really, you see it in Texas, and you see it in Nevada. You see it in California, in the mayor's race, in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Latino support is really starting to swing a little bit toward the GOP. Are you feeling that in Nevada? In Clark County?\par \par \uc2 BECKER: Absolutely. They're great people. They love this country and they're waking up to, you know, what the Democrat agenda is. And it doesn't fit with their values.\par \par \uc2 So, very excited. I have so many volunteers and they are so excited. It's just a great time. I cannot wait until what? We got 20 days left and I can't wait.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Wow, yes. It's going to be -- it's going to be a nail biter in Nevada. We wish you all the best of luck. Thank you for coming on. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BECKER: And vote Aprilbecker.com If you want to help this race.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: There you go. Meantime, the major shift in Pennsylvania's critical Senate race and what we have just learned about the health of Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . Next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: I had a stroke back in May.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You look great. You look great.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: And I'm so grateful to be here today because of health care saved my life.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Right!\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And breaking today, the campaign for Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , releasing a letter from his doctor, saying the lieutenant governor has no work restrictions and can work full duty in public office.\par \par \uc2 This is the first time since June 3rd that Fetterman's doctors have publicly commented on his recovery from a stroke he suffered back in May.\par \par \uc2 The letter also came within hours of a new poll showing Republican Mehmet Oz just two points behind Fetterman putting the critical Senate race within the margin of error. Let's bring in Tom Bevan, co-founder and president of Real Clear Politics. Tom, it's always great to see you. You're the man on these kinds of things, fascinated by the poll that now you have, you know, Mehmet Oz within two points of \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . Your thoughts on that before we move on?\par \par \uc2 TOM BEVAN, REAL CLEAR POLITICS CO-FOUNDER: Yes, I mean, this race has definitely closed down the stretch here. And it's become very, very tight. That's not the only poll that showing it a two point race. And look, there were two issues that were hurting Fetterman. One was the issue of crime was Oz has been focused on and been a lot of attack ads on. The other was his health, the issue of his health. Oz said 15 doctors sign a letter the other day, saying he needs to be transparent. This is something that the \uc2 Pittsburgh Post-Gazette\uc2  and other newspapers had editorialized about.\par \par \uc2 And so Fetterman I think it was hurting him to the point where he needed to do something and he did. You know, I don't know that that settles the matter. I think a lot of people are going to be tuning into the debate next week who might be undecided and to see for themselves whether they think that Fetterman is up to the job or not.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And you say whether it settles the matter. I want to play another, let me put up another quote from the doctor and his evaluation. The evaluation by the way was on June 14th, just so we're clear on this, and it was released on June 15th. But as we said, it's been since June 3rd since the Fetterman campaign has released any health information at all.\par \par \uc2 The doctor goes on to say here, his speech was normal and he continues to exhibit symptoms of an auditory processing disorder, which can come across as hearing difficulty. Occasional words he will miss which seems like he doesn't hear the word, but it is actually not processed properly. So normal, the doctor saying normal, Tom, but still struggling a bit when it comes to processing information.\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: Right. And that's something that, you know, Fetterman himself has said, and that's something we saw in that MSNBC interview that was done last week, where he use closed captioning, he'll be using closed captioning during the debate. But even the reporter said during their sort of small talk before the interview that he seemed to be missing, you know, pieces of the conversation, not completely, you know, getting everything.\par \par \uc2 And so, again, this is up to the voters to decide, and they will be able to tune in and see for themselves next week, whether they believe that he is, you know, they can evaluate his health status for themselves.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And yes, and wonder if Democrats want that information out there because the reporter who kind of did that interview with \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  was thrashed by the left media because of saying those things. I want to put up this, this is a head to head poll of Pennsylvania voters. This is 50 plus, ages 50 and over who say inflation rising prices matter more than the reversal of Roe v. Wade? Is that kind of your -- is that your take on this, Tom? Is that, you know, that we're really starting to go toward crime and inflation that abortion is really starting to pull back overall countrywide.\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: It's -- listen, Trace, this has been clear in the data for months. I mean, this is not some shocking revelation, the only section of the electorate where abortion became an issue of great urgency was among Democrats and it helped Democrats rally their base. There's no question about that. But it never really resonated with Republican voters. And it didn't resonate with independent voters. It has always been the economy and inflation by an order of magnitude over every other issue. So I think the fact that some people are just starting to, you know, recognize that now is, you know, sort of belies the case. I mean, it's been that way for months and will continue to be that way on Election Day.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. And then lastly, just kind of expand on something you talked about earlier, which is the upcoming debate between Mehmet Oz and \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . And we talk about debates and across the country. There is a debate on whether they are important to the race. This one really seems to be, Tom.\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: I agree. I agree. And for the reasons that we've talked about, I mean, I think a lot of voters are going to be tuning in to see for themselves unfiltered watch \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , you know, answer questions and, you know, his responses and see whether they and make the assessment for themselves instead of hearing it through, you know, the filter of campaigns in the media or whatever, they'll have a chance to see for themselves whether they think he is able to do the job representing the people of the state of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, it's fascinating insight. Tom Bevan, good to have you as always. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: Thanks, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Meantime, an exclusive ride along with the DEA Task Force as they execute a search warrant on suspected dealers of deadly fentanyl. And that's next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE TOBIN\uc2 , FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Here's a hall from this raid. He got a gun. He got some weed, about $1,000 where the cash. And this all appears to be fentanyl with different additives cut into it and a little bit of methamphetamine here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, we have to warn you this next video is very tough to watch. A woman attacked on a city bus in Washington, D.C. after telling some teenagers to stop cursing in front of little kids. She says the teens hit her, kicked her, threw things at her. And finally they tossed her off the bus. She told our local Fox station WTTG how it all began. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KYLA THURSTON, BUS ATTACK VICTIM: I got on the bus. And there was a young lady that got on with some smaller children. I got up to give her the seat. And after that, the kids were cursing. So I was just like, you know, can you go and stop the foul language? And at that point the kids became unruly. They started being disrespectful like saying things to me then next thing you know there was things, objects being thrown at me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, that's sad. She says her neck and arm were hurt and the back of her jacket is shredded. This happened on Monday. So far no arrests, Metro Transit Police say they are investigating.\par \par \uc2 Well, Fox News getting a firsthand look at the war on fentanyl in a state that's ground zero for America's overdose epidemic where the local attorney general says quote, the body bags are lining up. The senior correspondent \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  is live from Charleston, West Virginia on this. Mike, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Good afternoon, Trace. Drugs, particularly fentanyl having a deadly impact on rural America, West Virginia, Appalachia, are now considered high intensity drug trafficking areas. So you have agents and officers on task force is here trying to keep it from getting out of control.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Like you always do, give me some solid video.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice-over): Confidential informants like this one, our standard tools for drug cops.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: There's 140 --\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice-over): The informant is given cash, sent to meet a dealer and come back with drugs.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Some heroin slash fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice-over): That repeats, different informants, different dealers and small drug purchases.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: So he would cut it out of the stash and he came in and wait in front of you seeing that it was good.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice-over): Per capita, West Virginia leads the nation for drug overdose mortality. For every 100,000 people in the Mountain State, 84 die from overdose. Almost all of the deaths are linked to fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 CECE BROWN, SON DIED FROM OPIOID OVERDOSE: Oh, it was preventable.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice-over): Cece Brown knows the pain of that. Her son Ryan died of an overdose.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: You can never talk to them again. You'll never hear a knock on the door. It's forever when you lose a child.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (on camera): The work that was done yesterday with the informants was to establish probable cause. There's probably a stash of dope in the house where they were working. Then they go to the judge, get a warrant. And the guys in this task force are about to execute that warrant.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hands up.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Police search warrant.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Police search warrant.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Police search warrant.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Get behind your backs now.\par \par \uc2 TOBIN (voice-over): This raid netted more apparent fentanyl, meth, pot, about $5,000 and a gun. Big halls are rare here. This is the end of the trafficking room where drugs have been divided up for sale to individuals who just might die.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN ALLEN, METRO DRUG ENFORCEMENT NETWORK TEAM: There's not a family in this area that hasn't been touched by this crisis. That's a guarantee.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Now users don't necessarily know they're buying fentanyl when they want opioids or downers, they asked for slow. When they want speed or methamphetamine, they ask for fast or go. But they do want the strong stuff. And that often leads to fentanyl and fentanyl often leads to death. Trace?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Interesting, Mike, you say it's the end of the road that it's not the big loads that get there. You would think it would be easier to catch coming in. What's the major problem if you can?\par \par \uc2 TOBIN: Well, the major problem, of course, is intel. And the major problem frankly beyond that, the major problem is demand. As long as you have addicts, as long as you have buyers, someone who's going to meet that demand and make the money. Really most of the drugs that end up in this part of the country come from Detroit. It comes from Ohio, but as long as you have buyers that drugs are going to find their way here.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: \uc2 Mike Tobin\uc2  great stuff as always. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 Well, Joe Concha on what Americans view as the greatest threat to democracy. And Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , surging in the polls as crime rises to the top of voter's minds in New York, and that's next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R-BY), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: People are concerned about crime, they care about the economy. They feel like if they had to some other state that their money will go further, they'll feel safer, they'll live life freer and they're deciding to go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: While in New York City, subway passenger charged with manslaughter after a fight that turned deadly. Police say the victim, 48- year-old Heriberto Quintana, bumped into the suspect, 50-year-old Carlos Garcia, whose phone fell onto the tracks. Quintana refused to go down and get it. They got into a fight and he fell right in front of an oncoming train. Investigators tell "The New York Post" surveillance video does not show Garcia intentionally pushing Quintana. He is being held without bail.\par \par \uc2 While the New York governor's race heating up in a state that has not elected a Republican governor since 2002. Two polls released yesterday show Republican Candidate \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , gaining momentum in key parts of the state as he runs out a message of law and order, this one from Quinnipiac showing the race down to just a four point gap.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in 2016 presidential candidate and the last Republican governor to be elected in New York. That is George Pataki. George -- Governor, thank you so much for coming on, sir. We appreciate it. You of course as we said the last GOP governor, what do you think is narrowing this race? Is it crime? Is it the economy? Is it a little bit of both?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE PATAKI (R-NY), FORMER GOVERNOR: It's a little of both, but primarily crime, Trace. And we have a real horse race for governor in New York. And a month ago, I wouldn't have said that. But you just had one subway incident that you reported every day. There's crime after crime after crime committed in this city and across the state. And the public basically has had it. To win in New York, you have to get almost a million and a half Democrats to vote for the Republican candidate for governor. It's not easy.\par \par \uc2 But I'm out there listening to people. And it crosses party lines. Crime is an issue that is foremost in everybody's mind. And it's the reason Zeldin has a real chance.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And I want to put this up because this says the poll that that crime is in fact the most important issue in New York State and not by a little bit, by a lot, 28 percent. That's significant. That's 40 percent than the next top issue. And yet you watch the mayor of New York City, Eric Adams kind of downplay this saying, well, you know, the subway crimes are not that bad. It's only a few people getting beaten here and a few people mugged over here. People feel it, people have this visceral feeling toward crime in New York and everybody kind of recognizes it.\par \par \uc2 PATAKI: I cannot tell you, the number of my friends who have said they're afraid in New York City, and I have not heard that in decades. They're afraid to -- they certainly not going to ride the subway. They're afraid to walk on the street. They feel they have to look over their back all the time. And it's not just Mayor Adams who's downplayed it. It's Governor Hochul, who said we don't need to change the laws in Albany. We do. And \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  has said, flat out day one, he's going to declare a crime emergency. We have a crime emergency in New York State. And this is the reason why he has an excellent chance to win November 8th.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I wonder if the migrant issue plays into this? Or is New York too far removed? You have this tent city we've been talking about. Eric Adams, you know, saying this is a major problem. Is that something that you think plays into this at all, Governor?\par \par \uc2 PATAKI: Trace, I think the whole climate plays into it. It's not just crime. It's the hideous open borders. Then we're seeing the impact now with the tent city here in New York City. It's the energy prices because Biden has basically shut down the American energy industry. It's inflation because of trillions and borrowing and stimulus money from Washington. So all of that plays into the sense that we're headed in the wrong direction and that's helps \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. And I guess and my last question would be, you know, you go back to the economy, and you have Governor Cuomo's former assistant, \uc2 Melissa DeRosa\uc2 , saying there's a disconnect between Democrats, especially Democratic politicians, and New York City crime. Is that a fair assessment, lastly, Governor?\par \par \uc2 PATAKI: It's a totally fair assessment. Democrats are still talking about things that matter to people but much less than their personal safety or whether or not they're going to have a job or whether or not their kids are moving to Texas, because you can't afford to live in New York. So crime is the main issue. There are a number of other issues. \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  is soaring in the polls. We have a horse race and it's going to be exciting to see.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, and it's fascinating. Former New York City Governor or New York State Governor George Pataki, Governor, thank you for coming on.\par \par \uc2 PATAKI: Thank you, Trace. Great being on.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Meantime, Joe Concha on reports that first lady Jill Biden scolded staffers after a marathon presidential news conference. And he is next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: To me get some straight here, how long are you guys ready to go? You want to go for another hour or two?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: How many more hours am I doing this? I'm happy to stick around. Whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa, whoa, hang on, guys. We've only gotten an hour and 20 minutes. I'll keep going.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And thank you for holding this press conference. I hope there's more of them.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Anytime you have extra three hours, we can do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: First Lady Jill Biden reportedly ripped into White House staffers for not cutting off President Biden during that nearly two hour long January 19th press conference, quoting "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " here, she pointedly asked the group, which included the President why nobody stepped in to stop it, according to a person who was in the room. Where was the person, she demanded, who was supposed to end the news conference?\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Joe Concha media and politics columnist for The Hill and a Fox News contributor. Surprised at all, Joe, that Jill Biden not happy with the President going on for two hours and nobody is stepping in to say enough is enough.\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well Trace, I guess it all makes sense now, right? Well, when the President had repeatedly and awkwardly shared that he's going to get in trouble. If he answers questions from the press after speech, well, now we know who he was getting in trouble with. And it was with \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2 , the Chief of Staff, and not the White House Communications team, but Mrs. Biden. And a happy life I guess, is a happy wife after all, Trace, that's what I've been told anyway.\par \par \uc2 But this speaks volumes around what little confidence. The First Lady has her own husband to answer questions from, let's face it a mostly hospital press outside of Peter Doocy and Jacqui Heinrich, because like any spouse, she sees firsthand, Trace, up close behind the scenes the state of mind of this President.\par \par \uc2 And to a solid majority of Americans, they've seen enough for Mr. Biden, despite seeing very little of him. You see poll after poll, three quarters of Americans do not want this President to seek a second act, including eight and 10 Independents. Number one reason given, Trace, he's too old.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, here's some reaction by the way of Jill Biden upset over Joe's latest solo presser. You've got Congressman Austin Scott in Georgia. Apparently, the first lady knows a train wreck when she sees one. \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  in New York, the President of the United States should not need a babysitter when he talks to the media.\par \par \uc2 And really honestly, Joe, you know, you look at this morning's news conference, partially news conference, more of a statement on the gas prices and how they're going to bring down the gas prices, look a little unrehearsed and not exactly a confidence builder.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: It hasn't been. When the President keeps saying over and over again that gas prices are coming down fastest in history, he's using the bar from the all-time high number, right? So it's misleading. And the numbers, the numbers by the way, Trace, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's last formal press conference at the White House was the one you just showed in January.\par \par \uc2 He said three overall this year. For context, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had 35 during his last year in office or nearly 12 times as many and perhaps the reason Jill Biden doesn't want her husband to be held to account because he has no good answers in addressing inflation, gas prices, the border that is anything but secure. And that fentanyl crisis that's killing what, 300 Americans per day on average.\par \par \uc2 Without a teleprompter, Mr. Biden usually forces his team to clean up on aisle five, but not just all five but aisle 7, 13, and 20, Trace, and they just don't want him out there because it's a net negative always instead of a net positive.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And I'm really out of time here. I wanted to put this up. The majority say mainstream media is a threat to democracy, 59 percent. I have zero time to talk to you. But I'll give you five seconds on this interesting that mainstream media is a threat to democracy, Joe.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: There's really nothing to add to that. That's the perception now unfortunately. And I'm at five seconds, I guess, the media has to do a much better job than it has over the last seven eight years that's for sure, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Oh Joe Concha, great to have you on. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 That is the story of Wednesday, October 19th. But as always, the story goes on. Be sure to join me for "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT," 12:00 Eastern, 9:00 Pacific. "YOUR WORLD" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020221020eiaj00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Poll: Economy And Inflation Top Concerns For Voters; Republicans Blame Biden White House For High Prices; Candidates Trade Jabs In Contentious Debate; Danchenko Acquitted On All Counts; Democrats And Republicans Vie For Voter Enthusiasm; Sen. \uc2 Debbie Stabenow\uc2  (D-MI) And Sen. \uc2 Roy Blunt\uc2  (R-MO) On Bipartisan Push For Community Mental Health Clinics; Senators \uc2 Roy Blunt\uc2  (R-MO) And \uc2 Debbie Stabenow\uc2  (D-MI) Interviewed On Their Bipartisan Work In Congress; Report Indicates Biden Administration Asked Democratic Mayor Of El Paso, Texas, To Not Declare State Of Emergency Due To High Levels Of Immigration; Chinese Government Reportedly Purchasing American Technology Through Shell Companies; President Biden Campaigns For Democratic Candidates Across U.S.; Polls Show Advantage For Republicans In Midterm Elections\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Rich Edson, David Spunt, Kellyanne  Conway, Bill Melugin, Jennifer Griffin \par \uc2 7,893 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (voice-over): Look at this 70,000 pound humpback emerge from the ocean, and nearly shatter this fishing boat. The son and the father barely made it out alive.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (voice-over): Wow.\par \par \uc2 PIERS MORGAN, FOX NATION PRESENTER: Wow.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You were able to see the barnacles on the soft underbelly.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (voice-over): They were on the "NEWSROOM" today. The video --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Also, primetime tonight, crab season gets canceled by climate crazies.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Wow. Not crab season.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Wow. Crab season.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's it for us, SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Greg, we lose up with the dead Brazilian bird?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You know what? They do crazy things in Brazil.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I do, they really do.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You got it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening and welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier. "BREAKING TONIGHT". Three weeks from the 2022 Midterm Election. What appears to be a split decision from the White House, President Biden's team insisting he is doing everything he can every day to deal with inflation and the U.S. economy, by far, the top concern for midterm voters.\par \par \uc2 But the President himself is promising to make another issue, abortion. His primary legislative focus if Democrats are elected, even though polls show much less interest by the public now, across the board.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Peter Doocy has details tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Things are changing here at the Biden White House. The president once told us that inflation was his top domestic priority. Now, we are told, inflation is his top economic priority as he decides to stake his side's midterm chances on abortion access.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: As they say one of the times, you got (INAUDIBLE) and see nothing yet.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: A promise from the president, protect abortion access with a new law as long as Democrats pick up a few seats this November.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The first bill that I will send to the Congress will be to codify Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Abortion trails, the economy and inflation as voters most important issue in \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ' Siena poll.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): Abortion still is a top issue. But voters are making their decision in November, three weeks from today, about economic issues.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But President Biden's entire focus today was abortion.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: If you care about the right to choose, then you got to vote.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Critics accuse the White House of looking for a distraction.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON (R-AR): \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats don't have an answer on inflation because they're the cause of inflation.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: As White House officials argued, their policies are creating stability.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We are seeing an economy that is going through -- to -- into a transition, with more stable growth, more steady growth.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But growth has been negative for two consecutive quarters.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Could you afford to give up one month of your wages? 95 percent of Americans will say no. But that's what the Democrats have taken from you. Because one month of your wages is 8.3 percent of your overall year. Inflation is higher than that.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Biden's team claims they have evidence things won't be as gloomy as the latest projections.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): That he is been working on the economy every day for 19 months. Now, \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  economists are forecasting a 100 percent chance of a recession. So, how is it that we can be barreling towards a recession and the economy is, as the president says, strong as hell?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: So, here is the thing about the economy. The job market is strong, the labor force is strong. And that is not what we see usually before a recession.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: President Biden is not ruling out a recession. Saying last week, if there is one, he thinks, it will be slight. We are expecting to hear him talk about high gas prices in a speech tomorrow, where he will reportedly announced a plan to pull another 10 to 15 million barrels of petroleum out of the strategic reserves, the end of an ongoing effort to take a million barrels a day out for 180 days to try to lower prices.\par \par \uc2 But we are just out on the West Coast with the president in California. We know that in places like that, it hasn't been working. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In our SPECIAL REPORT "MIDTERM WATCH", we are exactly three weeks from Election Day, 21 days. Voters now can cast ballots in 40 states, a lot of them, the key states.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, as we look at the prognosis in our Fox News Power Rankings, it's a good environment for Republicans in the House, especially, too close races.\par \par \uc2 Moving to the right, our latest Fox News Power Rankings forecast has the GOP forecast to win a 15-seat majority. Now, if Republicans capitalize all over on election night, it -- their best case scenario would mean winning 248 seats, a 31 seat majority in the House.\par \par \uc2 However, there is still a path for Democrats to hold on to power. It's slimmer. Democrats' best case scenario comes out to 220 seats, two more than the majority of 218 to stay in power.\par \par \uc2 There is bad news out west, because you see these yellow, this is all the tossups, roughly 30 seats here. Lean D, Lean R, there are two races. Highly competitive House races that have gone from lean Democrat to tossup. One of those is in California. And that is where incumbent Democrat \uc2 Mike Levin\uc2  faces Republican challenger Brian Maryott.\par \par \uc2 Again, for the 49th district, Levin has won that district twice, but defeated Maryott by just six points in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Maryott has much less cash to spend in the final weeks than Levin, and GOP aligned groups are investing heavily to close that gap.\par \par \uc2 Now, a tossup onto the generic congressional ballot question. We have a slim lead for Republicans in likely voters, as you get closer to the election. One point in our latest, likely voter, Fox News poll.\par \par \uc2 In the Senate, there is still uncertainty. The name of the game there, our rankings remain the same from last time. Four very close tossup states: Arizona, Nevada, Georgia, and Ohio will decide the balance of power, we believe in the Senate chamber.\par \par \uc2 One of those pivotal races is in Ohio. And it could come down to Ohio. Last night, the two nominees held a debate, clash on a number of key issues.\par \par \uc2 Senior national correspondent Rich Edson shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R-OH), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: Shameful for you to accuse of that, giving that families --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: -- problem. The danger -- the danger -- my turn pal. My turn.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Oh, buddy, your turn.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: My turn.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): An hour long debate, so personal and combative, it was striking Republican J.D. Vance and Democratic Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  shook hands afterwards.\par \par \uc2 Ryan accused Vance of stoking racial violence and pushing concepts, claiming there's a conspiracy to replace white people in the United States.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: This is who he is running around with. Talking about replacement there.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: You're so desperate not to have a real job that you'll slander me and slander my family. It's disgraceful.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Thank you, Mr. Vance.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I think -- hold on, Derek.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Real quick. We got to get one more question in.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: I think I struck a nerve. I struck a nerve with this guy.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Vance has three children with his wife, who's Indian American. Vance also accused Ryan of failing to work to secure the border and says the U.S. should resume building the Trump administration's border wall.\par \par \uc2 Ryan says the government needs better border security and says the U.S. should punish the Mexican and Chinese governments for the fentanyl coming into the country.\par \par \uc2 The candidates also spent much of this debate trying to tie their opponent to National Party figures.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: You were calling Trump, America's Hitler. Then, you kissed his ass.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: He says he challenged \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  for leadership, but then he goes on national T.V. and says that he loves her.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: On abortion, they labeled each other extremist. Ryan says he supports a return to Roe vs. Wade, the standard the Supreme Court invalidated this summer. Vance appeared supportive of Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 's National 15-week abortion ban, though, refuse to get into details, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's one to watch.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: It is.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Rich, thanks.\par \par \uc2 "BREAKING TONIGHT", a big defeat for the special counsel investigating the Trump-Russia probe. The Russian think tank analyst accused of lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  about the infamous Steele dossier has been acquitted on all four counts.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt is in Alexandria of Virginia, outside the courthouse to explain that verdict. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Special Counsel \uc2 John Durham\uc2  and his team left with a loss from the courthouse here in Alexandria, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 This is his second loss. In about five months, he looked at the origins. He began looking at the origins of the Russia probe. About 3-1/2 years ago, this was his second trial. And many times, in this building behind me, it felt like the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was on trial, alongside the defendant, Igor Danchenko.\par \par \uc2 He was found not guilty on four counts of lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . He is the man that government says was the primary source for ex-British spy \uc2 Christopher Steele\uc2  who compiled the Steele dossier, a debunked that set of documents aimed at destroying then-candidate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 STUART SEARS, ATTORNEY FOR IGOR DANCHENKO: We have known all along that Mr. Danchenko was innocent. We're happy now that the American public knows that as well.\par \par \uc2 Nothing on camera from \uc2 John Durham\uc2 , the special counsel. He said in his statement, "While we are disappointed in the outcome, we respect the jury's decision and thank them for their service."\par \par \uc2 Durham alleged Danchenko lied to an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent four times when he claimed he received a call from an unidentified Russian male. He thought was a man named Sergei Millian, who had connections to Republican politics.\par \par \uc2 Danchenko repeated the story about the call to a man named Kevin Helson, a special agent four times. Danchenko's attorney said a call did happen.\par \par \uc2 Helson was a government witness, but actually defended Danchenko, Bret, telling jurors, Danchenko was a valuable informant.\par \par \uc2 Another big revelation during this trial, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , apparently offered \uc2 Christopher Steele\uc2  $1 million to corroborate his widely debunked dossier. This coming from an analyst on the stand last week.\par \par \uc2 Steele could not do it, but the \uc2 FBI\uc2  still went forward and use the dossier to surveil Trump campaign aide \uc2 Carter Page\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): Bret, we expect \uc2 John Durham\uc2  to write a narrative tying together this 3-1/2 year investigation. But clearly, Durham does have problems with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and we expect him to reveal some more of those problems when he tells his story to the public. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt outside the courthouse. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Strong earnings reports lead to another big day on Wall Street today. The Dow gaining 338. The S&P 500 was up 42. The NASDAQ jumped 97.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll get some insight about what the polls are telling us now could happen in next month's midterm elections from pollsters from both sides of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 Fox 28 in Columbus, Ohio, where two people are dead after a plane crash this morning into an auto dealership in Marietta. The Beechcraft King Air E90 went down about 7:15 a.m. No other injuries reported.\par \par \uc2 Fox 29 in Philadelphia as police officers are targeted by bikers at an old city gas station. Authorities say the attackers hurled bricks and bottles at the officers. No injuries or arrest reported there.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Fort Myers, Florida. One of the big stories there tonight from Fox4. A winning lottery ticket worth a share of a $494 million jackpot was purchased in the Southwest Florida City, hammered by Hurricane Ian late last month.\par \par \uc2 Lottery officials say one of the two winning Mega Millions tickets was purchased at a 7-Eleven in Fort Myers. The other winner was bought in California. A look for that ticket.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, MSNBC HOST: So, let's talk about rising inflation concerns along with crime, giving momentum -- new momentum to Republicans.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI (D-CA): Much of what you've said I don't agree with, that is to say the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  poll I think is an outlier poll, you'd cite one poll but all the other poll have a different --\par \par \uc2 MITCHELL: It's also the RealClearPolitics Average showing similar issues.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: No, but these -- but that was one that brought down the Average and it was an outlier. It wasn't even that big a sample. So, I dismiss that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  today commenting on new polls about issues and Democrat's chances just three weeks from the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Let's take a closer look at what the polls are telling us. Joining us tonight, pollsters Kellyanne Conway and Celinda Lake, the so-called pollstersists (PH) in their own words. Ladies, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 The Fox News poll out this weekend had these issues, extremely are very concerned about inflation, higher prices, and we've seen this but also higher crime rates, 89 percent, 79 percent.\par \par \uc2 You look at these issues, and then you look at something Celinda, like the Quinnipiac poll out today in New York, where \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  trails the incumbent \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  from -- by four points in that new poll. What are you seeing? Are you seeing trends here? How do you see it?\par \par \uc2 CELINDA LAKE, POLLSTER: I think there's a natural settling that's going on to a traditional midterm pattern. And I think it was to be expected, it's obviously makes for even more tight races, I (AUDIO GAP) think three challenges into the election.\par \par \uc2 One is to motivate our voters and make sure they turn out to vote (AUDIO GAP) clear contrast. We're doing that very successfully on abortion. We need to do that on the economy as well.\par \par \uc2 And three, go get more women voters and as Kellyanne and I wrote 10 years ago, it's up to the women voters, we need to win women by as much or more than we lose men by. And that's got to be our focus for the next three weeks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kellyanne.\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, well, unlike \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , I'm going to quote the New York Times Siena College Poll, because the reason that's an outlier over time is the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  has been pretty terrible to Trump and Republicans in their polling.\par \par \uc2 But this one shows this dramatic shift Bret and Celinda just from September among self-identified independent women. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  poll had self- identified independent women favoring Democrats by 14 points in September. They now favor Republicans by 18 points.\par \par \uc2 And that's because in part, the Democrats have talked too much about abortion. Of course, it's an important issue to people all across this country. But it was much more prominent in the \uc2 Google\uc2  searches in June, July and August than it is now. Crime outpaces it two to one according to the \uc2 Google\uc2  searches, you see no response from this White House whatsoever on the issue said that women and men say is most important to them. The economy, inflation, education, security, rising costs, rising crime.\par \par \uc2 And lastly, if this holds, if this generic ballot among women today in The New York Times Siena College poll showing 47 percent of women for the Democrats, 47 percent for the Republicans. That is a huge Republican year if it holds, because as Celinda knows, and we all admit, Democrats need a huge gender gap to be able to make up for the strong Republican male vote.\par \par \uc2 But look, I think women don't want to be narrowcast. They don't want to only hear about one issue because we women know we are not single-issue thinkers, single issue voters. And we're not -- we don't do one thing at a time ever.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Celinda, if you look at these incumbent Democrat senators who are up for reelection, and you see a poll where they're under 50 percent, let's say they're at 46, they still have a lead of four or five, but the undecideds are 10 or 12 points. Are you worried as a democratic pollster of that poll in this current environment?\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Well, I think both sides would be worried. But I think we have an advantage in that conversation. Because if you look at those undecided voters, the bulk of the undecided voters are non-college educated women over 50. We have a lot to say to them.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are proposing cuts in Social Security. We're proposing the highest COLA in history. Republicans are proposing taking away dental, vision, hearing for Medicare, we're proposing to add them. The Republicans all voted against getting prescription drug prices down. We got it through.\par \par \uc2 So, we have a lot to say to the biggest group of undecided voters out there. And our candidates have to just reiterate those messages and draw those contrasts as effectively as they have drawn the abortion contrast.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kellyanne, I have 30 seconds here, but if in this environment to late deciders wouldn't you think would go to the opposing party of the party in power?\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: They do generally. And I think that coupled with the enthusiasm the Republicans saw all through the primary seasons, Bret is important here. The Democrats aren't that excited to those. Celinda said they need to motivate them and that's true.\par \par \uc2 You also see Democratic candidates not being able to get out of their own way. They had -- they're facing an unwelcome and unpopular president and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  who they don't want on the campaign trail with them, yet, they voted for and they own and eat all of his policies, like the Inflation Reduction Act, Build Back Better, they've sent spent trillions of dollars, and Americans know that they've lost 25 percent of their retirement savings and nine trillion in wealth in the stock market.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it's going to be very difficult. I think if those Democratic incumbents don't think over 50, 51 percent of the vote, they're in big trouble. I think both the House and the Senate Republican.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Really fast, lightning. Celinda, one race you're looking to Democrats are picking it up.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Fetterman. I think we're definitely going to pick up the Pennsylvania gubernatorial. Keep the gubernatorial and pick up the Senate race.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kellyanne?\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: For Republicans, I'd say \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  against the woman in Nevada with three names and zero accomplishments is probably the best shot but I would not discount obviously Georgia, and certainly Arizona where I think Blake Masters did a really good job against Mark Kelly in that debate.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, you got three in there. Ladies, we'll see you next week. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LAKE: Thank you for having us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, our Common Ground segment up on Capitol Hill and later, Tuesday tweets.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT and Common Ground. We are thrilled to be in the Senate Agriculture Committee Room with our two guests from both sides of the aisle. Senator \uc2 Debbie Stabenow\uc2 , Democrat from Michigan. Senator \uc2 Roy Blunt\uc2 , Republican from Missouri. Thanks for having us up here.\par \par \uc2 You're the Senate Ag Committee chair, so we're happy to be in this room. But today, you're talking about something different and that is success on mental health. Explain that. And what happened today.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DEBBIE STABENOW (D-MI): Well, thanks so much for talking about this. This is so important and Senator Blunt and I have for years led efforts in community health centers, which are a model strongly supported on a bipartisan basis, where you set high quality standards for care, and then you fully fund the clinic.\par \par \uc2 But we've never done that on mental health or an addiction services. And so, we started looking at well, how can we do that. And we developed to what was called the Excellence in Mental Health and Addiction Treatment Act, which sets high quality standards. Fully funding mental health and addiction services as health care in the community.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You're announcing that it's expanding, because you're seeing success.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ROY BLUNT (R-MO): It's expanding. You know, people always feel like we've got to have a mental health system that works better. But we've really never been able to put a model in place that we can prove work.\par \par \uc2 And we've done that now, you know, as the last bill that President Kennedy signed into law 50 years ago, now 59 years ago, and it was the Community Mental Health Act. And the goal of that act was to close the big institutional facilities that weren't serving people very well and replace them with community based, high quality mental health facilities.\par \par \uc2 And the country spent most of the 70s and even in some into the 80s closing those facilities, but very few places have offered the alternative. And so, this is really back to what's really been a goal for the federal government for 59 years this month. And we had this news conference this morning talking about how the other 40 states could join the 10 pilot states and have exactly what the government said we were going to do 59 ago.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We are connecting the dots.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And it's even more important now after the pandemic, the CDC had these stats, August 2020 to February 21, percentage symptoms of adults with symptoms of an anxiety or depressive disorder increasing 36.4 percent to 41.5 percent. And percentage of those reporting unmet mental healthcare needs increased from nine to 11.7. And most of these are in younger people 18 to 29, which, so it seems like it's crucial at this point.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DEBBIE STABENOW, (D-MI): There's no question about it. What we are doing with the comprehensive clinics is that where they are, someone can walk in, just walk in, and begin to get the help that they need for themselves or their child or another member of their family. And they're required to give 24-hour crisis services.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So this stems from the bipartisan Safer Communities Act, and $8.5 billion to expand this program. A lot of people look at Capitol Hill and say this place is broken. It's so partisan. But here you are, Democrat and Republican, and you have worked together for a number of years.\par \par \uc2 BLUNT: Actually, the first bill, we were both on the Ag committee when we came to the Congress together --\par \par \uc2 STABENOW: In the House.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In the House.\par \par \uc2 BLUNT: -- 26 years ago.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What was the first bill?\par \par \uc2 BLUNT: First bill we passed, it was a wheat mold research bill.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Prescient.\par \par \uc2 STABENOW: Very, yes.\par \par \uc2 BLUNT: But it was for some of our communities, but we did it together. And we have done a number of things together since then. We helped found the federally qualified health centers caucus. So you find these things to work together. I think often they don't get the coverage, obviously, that you can get by being really angry about something as opposed to really getting something done. And this is going to make a big difference in people's lives.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When you hear people say Washington doesn't work, do you point to this relationship? You are losing him. He's leaving.\par \par \uc2 STABENOW: I'm locking the door. He is not leaving.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But here is a senator, and there's a few, who work hard to get to yes. It's a lot harder to get to yes than it is to get to no. Are you worried about the Senate composition?\par \par \uc2 STABENOW: You know, I do worry about that. And we have other colleagues as well that I have.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Portman.\par \par \uc2 STABENOW: Senator Portman on Great Lakes issues and so many other things. I think what worries me the most is somehow this sense of not trusting our country and the way we solve problems in this country. And we need people in government who believe that and trust that if you reach out in good faith with people, if you keep your word and you work hard, that you can actually solve problems. And that's what we do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Sure, you have differences on some big issues.\par \par \uc2 BLUNT: With people who have gotten to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  and have had positions that they've developed over years, it's probably not muse trying to talk them out of that. You find the common ground and move forward.\par \par \uc2 I do think the country is finally wrapping itself around the need to treat mental health like all other health, to get ourselves in a place in society where can talk about a mental health problem somebody in your family has just as comfortably as you talk a physical health problem.\par \par \uc2 STABENOW: I remember when you used to whisper somebody has cancer. He has cancer, you know. Now people talk about their cancer, their treatments. They go to treatment. They come back. They continue with their life. We want in the case of a mental illness and brain disease, or an addiction, to be something you talk about and say, yes, I got some help for that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you are seeing things work. I think the criticism always about big government is that it throws money at problems and doesn't see actual things work. You're actually seeing things in these states that are making a difference.\par \par \uc2 STABENOW: In fact, we were blown away. There was a 60 percent reduction in people going to jail with a mental illness in the communities that had these comprehensive clinics. There was a 63 percent reduction in people sitting in the emergency room, 41 percent reduction in people being homeless. And that was after only two years.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So as you come to the end of your term up here and your time here, will you look back at these big bipartisan moments as your biggest successes?\par \par \uc2 BLUNT: For sure. Whether it's the research dollars we've done at \uc2 NIH\uc2 , what we've done here, the efforts I've been able to make as a member of the Intel Committee, all would be among the things that we'd look at. The infrastructure bill that Republicans and Democrats voted for this year.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So are you worried about the Senate after you leave?\par \par \uc2 BLUNT: No. No, I think the Senate, I think we're going to move, get beyond this idea where anger is the coin of the realm, and I think this is one example of how much better it is to talk about accomplishment than it is to talk about how terrible the other side is.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You're coming up to an election, and both of you are going to fight for your teams.\par \par \uc2 STABENOW: There's no question, I want to be in the majority, and Roy would like to leave with the Republicans coming back into the majority. But there also ought to be a line, there ought to be lines somewhere.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Where that ends, and you start negotiating on something?\par \par \uc2 STABENOW: Right. And sort of also lines in how people talk about each and campaign and so on.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Last word. November 14th to the end of the year, are you looking for something to get done?\par \par \uc2 BLUNT: This is one of the things I'd hope I'd get done this year, to have this concept move nationally. We've got a handful of things that we're working on and I think are going to get done. I want to see our efforts at the \uc2 National Institutes of Health\uc2  for research continue to follow the pattern we've been on for eight years now, and I hope and believe most of those things can happen.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Blunt, Senator Stabenow, we appreciate your time. Thanks for coming on Common Ground.\par \par \uc2 We'll be back with SPECIAL REPORT in the studio after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In tonight's report on the border crisis, word the Biden administration is trying to manage the optics of that situation by leaning on local politicians there. In this case, the Democratic mayor of El Paso, Texas, is taking heat from both the president's teams and city leaders. Correspondent Bill Melugin has details tonight from Eagle Pass, Texas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: The city of El Paso, Texas, as quickly become an epicenter of the border crisis, averaging almost 2,000 illegal crossing per day recently. CBP facilities and NGOs there overwhelmed and overcapacity, leading Border Patrol to release migrants to city streets, where many of them are now living and sleeping.\par \par \uc2 Now "The New York Post" reports the Biden White House privately pressured El Paso's Democrat mayor, Oscar Leeser, to not declare a state of emergency there in an effort to avoid bad optics. El Paso City Council member Claudia Rodriguez in a private call Mayor Leeser admitted he was pressured, telling the "Post," quote, "The Mayor told me the White House asked him not to." The "Post" reports at least three El Paso City Council members have urged Mayor Leeser to declare and emergency, but the mayor tells the "Post," quote, "I don't bow to pressure from any side. I make decisions based on current circumstances and in the best interests of the citizens of El Paso."\par \par \uc2 For months now, in an effort to relieve pressure, El Paso has been bussing migrants to other Democrat led cities, including the big apple, and is receiving federal reimbursement for it. El Paso has now bussed more than 11,000 migrants to New York City and 3,100 to Chicago. Texas Governor Greg Abbott's bussing program has sent only 3,300 migrants to New York City and 1,000 to Chicago. Despite those smaller numbers, it has only been Governor Abbott who has been criticized for his bussing of migrants.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret, earlier this afternoon, FOX News did reach out to the White House multiple times to ask if they have any comment, any response whatsoever to this "New York Post" reporting that they pressured El Paso's mayor to not declare that local disaster. As of this newscast right now, we still do not have any response from the White House. I'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We have long known the Chinese government uses cyber intrusions to steal American technology, vacuuming up vast amounts of research, development, from defense contractors in particular. Now we're learning shocking new details about how Beijing has simply bought prized U.S. technology and software to advance its hypersonic missile program using shell companies to get around American export laws. Here's chief national senior correspondent Jennifer Griffin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT: Fifty U.S. firms sold technology products and software that may have helped China, especially when it comes to its missile program. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " followed the money, interviewing Chinese scientists, finding that Chinese military groups who are already on U.S. export control blacklists, purchased advanced U.S. software. These products were developed by American companies and funded by the Pentagon. The software may have advanced the Chinese hypersonic missile programs by simulating wind tunnel conditions.\par \par \uc2 DMITRI ALPEROVITCH, SILVERADO POLICY ACCELERATOR: There's no question that the last 30 years, a lot of American technology, a lot of American I.P. has ended up the PLA military. The reality is that our export controls have been like Swiss cheese. They've been full of holes, and it's very easy for the Chinese to play a shell game.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: \uc2 U.S. Commerce Department\uc2  officials who oversee export enforcement told FOX it's too early to tell whether these American companies knowingly violated the law. They are still studying the level of the aerodynamic simulation software sold to the Chinese front companies. One expert explains how the Chinese have used American peer reviewed academic publications to have American scientists do their homework.\par \par \uc2 IAIN BOYD, UNIVERSITY OF COLORADO, BOULDER: The Chinese have submitted lots and lots of papers, and really in the end have gotten lots and lots of free information and advice from the west because people review the papers and say, instead of doing this, you should have done that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The Commerce Department says it has sufficient authority, but American companies have a responsibility for better due diligence to understand how their technology, often funded with U.S. taxpayer dollars, could be helping the Chinese military advance. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jen, another topic. New evidence tonight suggesting the Russians may have been behind blowing up the Nord Stream pipeline?\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: That's right. Swedish investigators, Bret, released underwater photos and this video of the pipeline blast sites published by a Swedish newspaper today, showing that the blast came from inside the pipeline, indicating Russian culpability.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALPEROVITCH: The easiest way for them to do so would have been to load an explosive inside the pipeline through a device called a pig, a little device that goes inside the pipelines to clean them and expect them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The Russians control access to so-called pigs that clean the Nord Stream pipeline and could have attached explosives to that. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, we'll continue to follow that investigation. Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon, Jennifer, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Russian airstrikes have cut power and water supplies to hundreds of thousands of Ukrainians today. President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  says nearly one-third of Ukraine's power stations have been destroyed in the past week, causing massive blackouts across Ukraine. Zelenskyy calls it an expanding Russian campaign to drive the nation into cold and dark and to make peace talks impossible.\par \par \uc2 In France, industries across that country go on strike to push for pay hikes that keep up with rising inflation there. This comes after weeks of walkouts that hobbled oil refineries and sparked gasoline shortages around France. Workers in transportation, education, healthcare, are all pushing for salary increases now and protesting government intervention in the refinery strikes.\par \par \uc2 As President Biden deals with growing public dissatisfaction over the U.S. economy, we've reached the three-week mark before the midterms. The panel joins us next. Plus, "Tuesday Tweets" in a bit.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If you do your part and vote, Democratic leaders of Congress, I promise you, we'll do our part. I will do my part.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON, (R-AR): A vote for any Democrat this year is a vote for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's agenda. If you want affordable and reliable energy in this country, you need to vote Republican on November 8th.\par \par \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 , (D-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: We feel pretty good about it. I track these -- this -- these campaigns very carefully. I believe that we will have the mobilization to own the ground to turn out the vote.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY, (R-CA), HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: Remember in the last election, the polls all said the Democrats would win 15 seats and they lost 14. So you take nothing for granted.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The countdown is on, 21 days from tonight to election night. Meantime, the energy situation is a big situation, and the administration, it looks like, is going back to the well, literally, the Strategic Petroleum Reserve. "Reuters" says "U.S. could sell oil from emergency reserve this week. The Biden administration plans to sell oil from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve in a bid to dampen fuel prices before next month's congressional elections. The sale would market the remain 14 million remaining barrels from Biden's previously announced and largest ever release from the reserve 180 million barrels that started in May."\par \par \uc2 With that, let's bring in our panel, "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " columnist Marc Thiessen, Ben Domenech, editor at large of "The Spectator," host of "The Ben Domenech Podcast" on FOX News Radio, and \uc2 Mara Liasson\uc2 , national political correspondent of National Public Radio. Mark, this SPRO situation is serious.\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, COLUMNIST, "WASHINGTON POST": It's very serious, and he's using it as literally as a slush fund, no pun intended, to help his prospects in the election. He tried to get the Saudis to do it before the midterms and they wouldn't do it. Now he's going to the SPRO and doing this. They won't take any steps in the long-term that would help us restore energy independence. So this is all a political calculation, but the fact is Democrats like high gas prices because just like --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Even before a midterm.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: No, they want to lower them before the midterm. They don't want them now, but over time they want high gas prices, because just like the government raised the price of every pack of cigarettes because they wanted to get people to stop smoking. They think that the way to push Americans into the E.V. economy is to make gas too expensive for them to do it. They don't like the political blowback --.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: On November 9th, is you're saying, they'd like that on November 9th.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes, November 9th. They don't care about gas prices come November 9th. And so that's why they won't take any steps. The lowest number of acres leased of any president since World War II, lowest number of permits, nothing that would long-term help us regain our energy independence, only short-term to fix their political situation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mara, where do you see it? Today, the president was out with this event saying the first piece of legislation if you give me a Democratic Congress is to codify Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 MARA LIASSON\uc2 , NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, NATIONAL PUBLIC RADIO: Look, today's event was really interesting. I thought he directly talked about the problem for problems right now, and that is that they may have peaked too early. In other words, he said today, remember how you felt when Roe was overturned. Remember how angry and motivated you were, because that was a moment when Democrats seemed to get the advantage. There were more Democrats being registered. There was an outcry against the Dobbs decision by suburban women, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 Well, that's changed. And now things like immigration and inflation and gas prices and crime are coming back to the fore. So he was out there today saying let's focus on abortion. Remember how energized it made you feel. But these things come and go. And right now people are more concerned about the economy and inflation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right, and is it possible that there's not a lot to say, Ben. The Inflation Reduction Act is not.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I'm sorry. You just summed up the entire election with that. This is a situation that I think has been predictable for the last several weeks, which is that we are seeing return to fundamentals. The fundamentals of economic concerns, of concerns about inflation, of crime, et cetera, that people have on their kitchen table are overwhelming the kind of concerns that Democrats had hoped were going to give them a lifeline.\par \par \uc2 And the ultimate ramification of this, I think, is we are about to see a real blowout against the kind of Gen X Democratic leaders that you could potentially have had. You're going to have a number of stars either go down either from the congressional races or within their contests and statewide situations. \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  is someone who a lot of people could be speaker someday. \uc2 Beto O'Rourke\uc2  was somebody who a lot of people had hopes invested in. And what we see is, I think, a lot of these Democrats headed toward not just losses but embarrassing losses.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But Mara, is there something that turns around in three weeks? Is there something that Democrats could do that changes the dynamic, do you think?\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: I don't know if there is something that Democrats can do. There could be events that we don't anticipate, stories about more 10-year-olds who are raped who have to carry their babies to term, et cetera, et cetera. But I think -- or maybe there would be some kind of diminution in influence or gas prices, although I don't see that on the horizon. This is hard. Look, inflation is a global phenomenon. It's everywhere. It's not just in the United States. We are energy independent. The problem is that gas prices everywhere are high.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: But mara, no Democrat is out there running on the American Rescue Plan. They passed a $1.9 trillion bill that wrote checks to everybody, the most popular thing that a government can do, and nobody is campaigning on it. And the reason for that is because Americans know that's what unleashed the inflation. That is absolutely --\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Might have exacerbated it. To say that it unleashed it is not true.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Ask \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2 . It created the worst inflation in a generation. It put -- they put $.91 trillion into a $300 billion hole and caused this entire problem. And that's why Democrats 