y formula around the country. It's still not resolved. We are continuing to follow it.\par \par \uc2 Up next Bluesteel wants to know "Who would be your "Dream" person (living or not) to interview, and why?" OK, so living, right now I would like to interview \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  right now. That's what I would like to do. Going back, I think President Eisenhower would be a great interview. I wrote about him.\par \par \uc2 Lastly Cat Lover asks along that line, "When will we see your next book?" Well, I am working on it right now. Next year you will.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, an in-depth look at the Oregon's governor's race.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. We fit it all in. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" is the next show that comes up right now. Jesse?\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020221019eiai00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Candidates In Key Races Debate Three Weeks Out; Senate Control Coming Down To Four Toss-up States; GOP Expected To Win 15-Seat Majority; GOP Gains Momentum With 21 Days Until The Midterms; White House: Economy Is Going Through A Transition; Independent Female Voters Shift To GOP Over Econ; Dems Play Blame Game Over Rising Oil And Gas Prices; Report: White House May Release More Strategic Reserve Oil; 43-Year-Old Dad Stabbed To Death On NYC Subway; China Urging Former British Pilots To Help Military; Reports Claims US Military Losing Ground To China; Awaiting Verdict In Steele Dossier Source Trial\b0\par\par\uc2 Trace Gallagher, Juan Williams, Rich Edson, Edward Lawrence, David  Asman, Aishah Hasnie, Victor Davis Hanson, Geraldo Rivera, David Spunt,  Jack Keane, Alexis McAdams \par \uc2 8,195 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Underneath the camera today, and so I think I looked down and thought we were still in the first hour when I said that.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Yes, I love military time, but every time I switch our clocks, my wife gives me grief -- I got to keep them (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Just let me know it's happened and then I'll, then I'll, then I'll use it. OK, John, that was great. Well, we'll be back here tomorrow. Thanks so much for everybody for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: We'll see you again tomorrow. I'm John Roberts. THE STORY with Trace in for Martha starts right now.\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Time flies when you're having fun. John and Sandra, thank you. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Trace Gallagher in for Martha MacCallum. In the final three weeks stretch, some of the country's tightest and most consequential races, taking it to the debate stage.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. BRIAN KEMP (R-GA): No sheriffs are endorsing her statewide because of her stances on wanting to defund the police.\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D-GA), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: He has weakened our privacy rights and our, and women's rights.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Don Baltics record makes clear he would be a yes vote for a national abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would vote for the bill that she voted against, and that would be to stop spending to get inflation under control.\par \par \uc2 JD VANCE (R-OH), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: The Fentanyl crisis right now is so bad because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  have not done their job.\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: Our guy thought it would be a good idea to post on social media raise money for the insurrectionists.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, well, well, the latest Fox News Power Rankings are out forecasting a 15-seat majority for House Republicans, that's a wider margin than just one week ago. But the fate of the Senate still coming down to those four yellow tossup states on your screen. We're talking 21 days out. Let's bring in Mercedes Schlapp, Former Director of Strategic Communications Under President Trump and CPAC Foundation Senior Fellow and Juan Williams, a Fox News Political Analyst. Welcome to you both.\par \par \uc2 I guess we could put the numbers back on the screen, the power rankings, because as we said, you know, look gives a little more juice to the GOP this week from last week. We're talking about a 15-seat majority in the House, the Senate still a tossup Mercedes, your thoughts on what you're seeing?\par \par \uc2 MERCEDES SCHLAPP, FORMER DIRECTOR OF STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS UNDER PRESIDENT TRUMP: Trace, Trace, you're being too conservative. It's going to be more than a 15-seat win for the Republicans in the House of Representatives. I really believe it's going to be a strong red way for the Republicans. When you're looking at the generic ballot, especially in these competitive districts, they're trending Republicans. And guess what, the top issues they're looking at economy, inflation, and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's poor job performance.\par \par \uc2 Those seem to be the issues that are dominating in these swing districts, which I think will lead to a Republican victory. I think for the Democrats, they focus too much on the abortion issue. And quite frankly, they give mixed answers when it comes to the abortion issue, either, saying that they will accept abortion with no restrictions or regulations or in the case of you know, several other things, just simply not really answering the question.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And she kind of segue into the next, the next call for Juan, for you, because this is the most popular article on the Hill today that says, Democrats worry they peaked too soon ahead of the midterms, quoting here: "The political landscape has changed on Democrats in a short time putting them on the defensive in key areas. Focus on other domestic issues, including abortion, student loans, and gun violence may have also cooled down since the summer months with the economy staying in the forefront of the voters' minds." I mean, it's tough to cut through the madness, Juan, when you got the border, and you've got the economy, and you've got crime so high. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I love Mercedes' cheerleading, you can tell Republicans are feeling their oaths right now. Some recent polls really indicate that, that things have swung back towards Republicans after Democrats had a pretty good summer in terms of Biden passing his agenda of the Supreme Court's, you know, decision on the abortion case that absolutely led to a rise in voter registration, especially amongst suburban women.\par \par \uc2 And of course, you had some victories in special elections. But what I think I take from the power rankings, Trace, is that right now, what we can see for sure, despite the optimism from Mercedes is a Republican red trickle. It's not the red wave that we had thought was coming. Remember, everybody said, if you think about the economy, if you think about what Republicans say about crime, which is arguable, you know, who's it, who's to blame?\par \par \uc2 But everybody thought the environment is such that you blame the party in power in a big way. That's the, that's the historical pattern of midterms. Right now, it looks as if Republicans will take the house but not by any huge margin. And it looks as if, if you look at the voting odds at the moment that Democrats will hold on in the Senate. So, I think in terms of the power rankings, that's what I take away as: wow, this is not the way that I thought.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And I think, Juan, you know, when he talks about the crime, and they blame whoever the party in power is, I think they actually blame the party that wanted to defund the police, Mercedes. What I find telling in all of this is, you can talk about the hill, and you can talk about different, different op-eds. I find the op-ed in the Wall Street Journal fascinating because it's written by Doug Schoen and Andrew Stein these are both Democrats.\par \par \uc2 And it says, quoting here, "The Republican Party is on the cusp of a substantial midterm election victory that could rival their wins in 1994 and 2010." I mean, this wasn't written by, by some far-right guys. So, it is, it's one of those things where there are a lot of Democrats thinking, I don't like the look of the landscape with 21 days out, Mercedes.\par \par \uc2 SCHLAPP: Right. Well, look, I've been traveling across the country, with several of these candidates. And I've seen firsthand the enthusiasm coming from the Republicans. And the mere fact is that you're seeing, for example, Hispanic voters, parents that are standing up and saying, wait a second, I'm leaving the Democrat Party, I'm going to move towards the Republican Party, because when you are talking about these cultural issues, like gender confusion, critical race theory, I mean, the list goes on and on. The Democrats have been way too extreme on these issues.\par \par \uc2 And they haven't honed in on solving the problems that the American people are facing right now, which is rising prices, food prices, gas prices, electricity prices, they are being impact, impacted in their pocketbooks. The Democrats have not offered a solution other than they tried with the Inflation Reduction Act, which guess what, it's not reducing the inflation in any way, but more focused on climate change. And I think that the Republicans are offering the right answers for them by presenting agendas that will help secure the border and help get this inflation under control,\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Does it, does it bother you, Juan, that you're seeing, especially among women, independent women starting really to kind of break off toward the GOP, your last answer?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: What I see is that there are more women registered, especially behind the abortion issue. Some of them may be independents. And what you say is right, Trace, you see now more of a trend towards the GOP among them. But remember, the vast majority of those women who registered after the Dobbs decision from the Supreme Court, we're not Independents, they're Democratic voters. The key here is to get them out. And you know, the reason that I say that this whole thing is a little bit of a surprise.\par \par \uc2 I'm breaking with historical patterns is because of the abortion decision energizing so many Democrats. It's because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's approval rating, 46 percent in the latest Fox poll, has been going up for the last three months. It's because people have a sense that, yes, we're worried about the economy. But this U.S. economy is the best economy in the entire world. There's inflation everywhere. Not tied to any policies coming from the White House.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I'll tell you, though, that's a hard sale, Juan, because just are hurting. I mean, you can say \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , the holy man, it's you know, the economy is, is great. It isn't, and that's the problem and it's -- and a lot of people are hurting and people think that the President is dismissing --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No, I said it's the best, I said it's the best in the world, Trace. It's the best compared to any --\par \par \uc2 SCHLAPP: It doesn't feel like it's the best in the world right now.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: You, you ask the British, you ask the British about \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 . \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  is imitating Trump, and look what happens to the British economy?\par \par \uc2 SCHLAPP: We're not voting in Britain, OK. We're not voting in Britain, Juan. We're not voting in Britain.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And the economy here is in bad shape. Mercedes, Juan, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Oh, stop.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: More now from last night's Senate slugfest --\par \par \uc2 SCHLAPP: Thank you, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: -- out of Ohio between Democrat Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  and Republican JD Vance, as brand-new polling shows Vance now ahead by two points. He was trailing by one point a month ago, very tight results all within the poll's margin of error. The Senior National Correspondent Rich Edson following it all from Washington. Rich, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good afternoon, Trace, a close race, a combative debate between Republican JD Vance and Democratic Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 . It got personal as well when Ryan accused Vance of consorting with those pushing racist concepts.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RYAN: And this is who he's running around with, talking about replacement there. There's no big grand conspiracy. This is a country that's been enriched by immigrants from all quarters of the, the world. There's no, and the problem, the danger, the danger --\par \par \uc2 VANCE: It's shameful for you to accuse me that --\par \par \uc2 RYAN: My turn, pal. My turn.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Oh, buddy, your turn -- and my biracial children get attacked by scumbags online and in-person because you are so desperate for political power.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Vance has three children with his wife whose Indian-American. Vance says the government should resume border wall construction started during the previous administration. Ryan says, the government needs better border security and that the U.S. should punish the Mexican and Chinese governments for the fentanyl that's coming into the country.\par \par \uc2 Ryan defended the Inflation Reduction Act and says it will eventually curb inflation. Vance says, Democrats spending is driving inflation. They both called each other extremist on abortion. Vance spoke well of Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 's 15-week abortion ban without explicitly endorsing it. Ryan says, he wants to return to the Roe versus Wade standard.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RYAN: They want people to get a passport and have to go to Canada, largest governmental overreach in the history of our lifetime.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: I want to save as many lives as possible. That's the principle that guides me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Vance also says that Ryan is in lockstep with House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . Ryan says Vance is a kiss up to former President Trump though his language was a little more active than that. Trace, back to you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: It was a bit more colorful than that Rich, live for us in D.C. Rich, thank you. How President Biden is trying to turn the midterm conversation back to abortion. As voters worry about the high cost of living and economist, one there is a 100 percent chance of a recession. David Asman, Victor Davis Hanson, when THE STORY continues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: There's no guarantee that they're going to be, I don't think there will be a recession. If it is, there'll be a very slight recession. That is, we'll move down slightly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, there is a 100 percent chance of a recession in the next year. That's the warning from \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  economist, as the Fed continues to hike interest rates in an effort to cool down 40-year high inflation.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, President Biden, who says there is only a very slight chance of the U.S. falling into a recession, today, turned his attention to abortion rights, with just three weeks to go until the midterms.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The final say does not rest in the court now. It does not rest with extremist Republicans in Congress. And finally, say -- finally, say about your right to choose. That rests with you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: In moments, Fox Business anchor David Asman. But first, to Fox Business correspondent Edward Lawrence. Edward, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 EDWARD LAWRENCE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CORRESPONDENT: Hey, good afternoon, Trace.\par \par \uc2 Yes, the president wants you to focus -- our voters to focus on the abortion issue, not the economy, which is staring them in the face.\par \par \uc2 Some economists believe we're already in a recession. Inflation, according to the data is more entrenched now in this. So, I wanted to know exactly when all the spending and all the moves would actually make a difference in helping under this president.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: You've laid out eloquently what the president's been doing, is there a timeline for what Americans can start feeling some economic pain relief?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: So, in regards to the Inflation Reduction Act early next year, they will see some of the -- some of the pieces of that when you thought -- think about in energy costs, when you think about the Medicare kind of benefits from that. So, we'll see some movement on that early next year.\par \par \uc2 But, when we talk about inflation, gas prices, that's been something that's the American people have seen for the past several months.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: It's been more than just several months.\par \par \uc2 You could see here where President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  came into office and started reversing just about every policy from former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When now, the White House chief of staff is tweeting out that a gas announcement will come tomorrow. Reportedly, the president hopes to announce another 10 to 15 million barrels of oil release from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve.\par \par \uc2 Now, a source familiar with that decision tells me that the announcement will be in response to Putin's war. The reserve, though at its lowest level since 1984, and Republicans are concerned.\par \par \uc2 REP. JEFF VAN DREW (D-NJ): I think it's so crass. And I don't even have the art to articulate the words. To take our reserves, which are there to make sure that we're safe, and that we have those reserves in an emergency when we got a good deal on them, bought them at the right time. And we're really full, and use them just so you can try to win an election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: So far, no plans to replenish the Strategic Petroleum Reserve as the price of crude oil is now above $80 a barrel. Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Edward Lawrence live for us in the White House. Edward, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox Business anchor David Asman. David, always great to see you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID ASMAN, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK ANCHOR: Great to see you, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Let me to breaking news. I want to play this sound bite. This is a back and forth between Peter Doocy and \uc2 Karine Jean Pierre\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I thought you might be interested in.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: I am.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Let's play and get your response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: How is it that we can be barreling towards the recession, and the economy is, as the president says, strong as hell.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: We are seeing an economy that is resilient. We are seeing an economy that is going through to into a transition with more stable growth, more steady growth, and that is because of the work that this president has done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Going into a transition. David, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: A transition to a more stable economy. That sounds an awful lot like they want us to get used to a recession. Now, we're going to have one. It's a more stable economy.\par \par \uc2 Yes, it's negative. We had two negative quarters already this year. Two. That used to be a shorthand for a recession.\par \par \uc2 I mean, come on. They are -- they are playing word games here for a bad economy is what they're doing.\par \par \uc2 When you have two negative quarters, plus inflation that is accelerating, we saw it accelerating both at the consumer level, and at the producer level last week, you're in deep trouble.\par \par \uc2 Back in that, you know, we've been here before, Trace. That's what kills all of us who were -- are for productive finished to all this, to get out of this.\par \par \uc2 Back in the late 70s, they called it, limits to growth. Now, they call it a transition to a stable economy.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: It's the same thing. It's folks out there get used to it. You're not going to be affluent anymore. You're not going to -- your kids are not going to do better than you are, they're going to do poor than you are. That's not the way we have to go.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I wonder, since you're going back in time, I'm going to go back there with you. There, remember we had a president, a Democratic president, remember, he would -- he would feel your pain?\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: He would feel your pain. And now, we have a president who won't. I mean, he is saying that he's not acknowledging pain.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: It's as if to say, listen, I feel your pain is an admission of defeat, and he's now not going to do that.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And so, he says, oh, the economy is just strong as hell.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Right. Well, remember the one who said he felt their pain, \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  was actually one who cut taxes. He cut the capital gains tax rate that led to a boom in the economy.\par \par \uc2 He went with Republicans on working towards a balanced budget by lowering spending, of course, he came in and two years was drubbed in during the first midterms, and that led him to change his policies to go with Republicans.\par \par \uc2 He had workfare. Instead of paying people to stay home, he forced healthy, strong individuals who were on welfare to go out and get a job if they wanted government handouts.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the bottom line, the answers are so simple, because we've done it and tried it before. We just have to remove the roadblocks to growth.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: And starting with the biggest roadblock to growth of all of our production of energy. Starting right there, if we want to -- if we really want to hit inflation, where it's going to hurt inflation and not the American consumers, we have to eliminate all those barriers to growth, in energy in this country.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: And then, all barriers to other kinds of growth as well. That will lower the inflation and increase economic 