aiting to find out the president might also announce a fuel export ban. The White House press secretary today also would not confirm that. This comes as the average for unleaded now at $3.87 a gallon. That is down from two weeks ago, Trace but it's still higher than it was last month.\par \par \uc2 And finally, the president could also detail how he plans to refresh the petroleum stockpile. This is very important, Trace, because it is at its lowest point in nearly four decades. And this is something California Democrat \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  says that he was actually calling for, months ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2  (D-CA): We should be buying at the dip to refill the Strategic Petroleum Reserves. We haven't done that I wish we had. But I support more releases as long as we're buying and refilling it at low prices.\par \par \uc2 But the biggest thing is we need a tougher policy on Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK. So, speaking of Saudi Arabia, Trace, in the coming days, some Democrats like \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 , believe President Biden could also take action against the Kingdom for \uc2 OPEC\uc2 's decision to cut oil as they blame, practically, everyone and anyone else except for themselves on this worsening energy crisis.\par \par \uc2 But keep in mind, this is all happening. All of these potential moves come as new provisions from the left's Inflation Reduction Act increases taxes on natural gas and reinstates a previously lapse tax on crude oil and imported petroleum products at $16.4 per barrel.\par \par \uc2 So, not surprising that we're going to hear from the president tomorrow on gas prices as we're just now 21 days away from a huge election.\par \par \uc2 And according to a recent Fox News poll, Trace, a lot of Americans, about 53 percent of voters out there are not happy -- disapprove of the president's job on energy. Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I wonder why. Aishah Hasnie, live for us on Capitol Hill. Aishah, thank you. Let's bring in Victor Davis Hanson, author of The Dying Citizen, senior fellow at the \uc2 Hoover Institution\uc2 , and a National Review Institute fellow.\par \par \uc2 Victor Davis, great to see you as always. I mean, this kind of goes right into the piece you wrote in the Las Vegas Review-Journal, which says and I'm quoting here, the Saudis. We're talking about retaliation, right?\par \par \uc2 The Saudis are enjoying the Schadenfreude of seeing their former American critic now on his knees, demanding the purportedly dirty polluting oil produced by a supposed pariah state. The American public is repelled as they watch Biden's pathetic theatrics of global oil begging to help himself in the midterms. They are ashamed that their recently energy autonomous country is now imploring non-democratic regimes for every drop of their oil to the extent of threatening former allies and coaxing current emanate enemies. And that really is spot on to where we are today. Your thoughts on all this.\par \par \uc2 VICTOR DAVIS HANSON, INSTITUTE FELLOW, NATIONAL REVIEW: Yes, I mean, it's surreal, Trace. We've been begging some of the most illiberal regimes in the world, Venezuela, Iran. And then, our former ally, and sometimes ally, Saudi Arabia, to pump more of something that we think, I guess, is dirty, and polluting in which we have an abundance. And they don't want to do it.\par \par \uc2 And yet, this was the type of activity that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was impeached for, that is using his own political agenda to advance his career, in this case, the midterms at the expense of the national security of United States. It's not a good thing to alienate the Saudis gratuitously because they don't want to help \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  after he libeled them.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, what I don't understand as maybe you can help me is that, they wanted this. They wanted high prices. This was Steven Chu, Obama's Energy secretary's dream. So 