2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Trace, good afternoon. Deliberations began yesterday afternoon, still no movement from the jury as far as the verdict is concerned. This case is about lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , but special counsel John Durham representing the government is looking past Igor Danchenko in this case, saying the elephant in the room is the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Danchenko charged with four counts of lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . He is, according to Durham, the man who was the primary sub source for ex-British spy \uc2 Christopher Steele\uc2 , who compiled that dossier aimed at taking down candidate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Durham says he lied four times to an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent when he claims he received a call from an unidentified Russian male he thought was a man named Sergei Millian, who had connections to Republican politics. He was looking for dirt on Trump.\par \par \uc2 The government led by Durham says he never received such a call. Cellphone logs show that no calls came in. Danchenko's attorney say, wait a minute, what about apps, what about WhatsApp, what about signal, some of those encrypted apps. Danchenko repeated the story about the phone call to Special Agent Kevin Helson four times hence the four charges.\par \par \uc2 And, Trace, wrapping this up, Kevin Helson, who was a government witness, actually praised Igor Danchenko and said he helped with 25 investigations through the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and defended him as an important asset, important \uc2 FBI\uc2  informant. Still no verdict, we'll keep you posted when we get something, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I got to go, David. But I'm curious, four counts, do we expect this to be maybe guilty not guilty on some counts, or is this kind of an all or nothing platform?\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You know, Trace, it's hard to tell because it's the same story that was repeated four times to the same person, so it's hard to see that split.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. David Spunt l