able. I'd use a Fox.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, I like that.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: I need a very big trap for a fox. But anyway --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Good.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: You'd like it. The fox gets the fox. You get it?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I get it.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: You see where I'm going with that.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Road trip.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: Up next, no wonder there's no hope for the future. The spoiled Generation Z brats --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: Whatever they call themselves, said they actually love Cancel Culture. We'll be debating that next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Take that. All right, no surprise here. One of the youngest generations of Americans loves cancel culture. A panel of Gen Z-ers on Dr. Phil Show called penalizing people for offending others a positive thing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PHIL MCGRAW, HOST, DR. PHIL SHOW: Why are comedians not even willing to come on college campuses anymore because they're scared to death they're going to say something that offends.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Stereotypes used in comedic stand up is just simply just like not OK anymore. So, you can say whatever you want, but you need to be also be ready for like, the repercussions of what you say.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I think that while social repercussions are nothing new, what we penalize people for saying is changing and I think that that's a positive thing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: These people.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Just for that, I'll start with you. Is penalizing people for saying things a positive thing?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: Can't we just cancel the entire generation. Just take them -- take them to an island somewhere very far away where they can all sit there in their humorless little bubbles and whine and moan to each other and be offended by each other morning, noon, and nigh