 disagree with President Biden when he's talking about relaxing some of the regulations down on the border, completely disagree with that.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): I have not spent a minute thinking about what politicians should run for what in 2024.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MAGGIE HASSAN\uc2  (D-NH): I have stood up to members of my own party and to the president, because the president wants to lift this program called Title 42. I believe he wants to do it prematurely.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: All right, the debate over debates. Do they work? Do they move the needle?\par \par \uc2 Some Democratic candidates in some very high-profile races appear to be distancing themselves from the president with three weeks to the day to go to the midterm elections. And it is all happening on the heels of early voting. Are these two dates then changing things the way we see them developing now?\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto. Very glad to have you with us, my friends.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, we're going to be giving you the latest impact on the debates themselves.\par \par \uc2 Let's get a read from how the White House is handling so many Democratic candidates and maybe many at the White House itself on defense throughout all of this.\par \par \uc2 Peter Doocy is there.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Neil, like you said, lots of changes.\par \par \uc2 And in these state level debates, we haven't seen a ton of Democrats warmly embracing President Biden, more so his policies, but not really the president himself. But that could change, because we have been noticing a difference in the way the White House is talking about priorities.\par \par \uc2 A couple months back, President Biden told us his number one domestic priority was inflation. Now we're told that's his number one economic priority, as his focus starts to shift a little bit with this new promise to put forward a new law protecting abortion access, if Democrats can just add a few more seats this November.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If we do that, here's the promise I make to you and the American people. The first bill that I will send to the Congress will be to codify Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Those remarks were delivered here in Washington, D.C., not alongside vulnerable incumbents or Democratic candidates hoping to flip red seats, as economists at \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  up their chances of a recession, something White House officials say they don't see happening.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: He's been working on the economy every day for 19 months. Now \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  economists are forecasting a 100 percent chance of a recession. So how is it that we can be barreling towards a recession and the economy is, as the president says, strong as hell?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: So, here's the thing about the economy. Job market is strong. The labor force is strong.\par \par \uc2 And that is not what we see usually before -- before a recession.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Exactly two weeks ago, we were told not to expect any more releases from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve. Now there are various reports that 10 to 15 million more barrels may be coming out to try to lower gas prices ahead of the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  did not offer details, but she says the president is going to do that and talk about gas prices tomorrow -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Peter, thank you. We will be getting some updates on that momentarily.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, I want to go to Tom Bevan right now, what he makes of all this, the RealClearPolitics co-founder and president.\par \par \uc2 Tom, first off on this issue of so many Democratic candidates on defense when it comes not only the economy, but you could extend that to illegal migrants coming through the border and even on the general inflation news, to say nothing about how things are faring with the great Russian threat, the China threat, a lot of threats, what do you make of all of this?\par \par \uc2 TOM BEVAN, CO-FOUNDER AND EXECUTIVE EDITOR, REALCLEARPOLITICS.COM: Well, the data is pretty clear on this, Neil, and it has been for a long time, that the economy and inflation is the number one issue on the minds of the majority of American voters.\par \par \uc2 And that includes certainly Republicans, but also independents. The problem that Democrats find themselves in is, they have put all of their eggs in the abortion basket. I mean, they are running -- if you look across the country, the ads that are running, the mailers that are going out are all almost exclusively on abortion and protecting a woman's right to choose.\par \par \uc2 Now, that has had the effect of firing up the Democratic base, and that's certainly something that the Democrats need. They need a motivated base to turn out. But it has also put them in a position where they're not speaking to the number one issue for independent voters. And it makes them think that Democrats are out of touch on that issue.\par \par \uc2 We saw \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  address this yesterday. Michael Bennet on TV fielding a question the Inflation Reduction Act on Sunday didn't have a good answer. And so I think Democrats find themselves sort of struggling on the economy, on inflation, when it is clearly the number one issue on the minds of voters right now.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And in these debates, and the fact that they're so close to the election itself, three weeks away, and now you have early voting in effect here, you have someone like a \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  urging Democratic candidates do whatever they have to do to win, even if they're criticizing replacing the old guard, whatever it takes.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of that?\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: Well, that just shows, look, the Democrats are fighting an uphill battle.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you just step back and look at the fundamentals of this election, whether it's $4-a-gallon gas, inflation. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's job approval rating, particularly in some of these swing states is in the high 30s, low 40s. History tells us that the out-party suffers -- sorry -- the in-party suffers in their first midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 So there are all of these dynamics that are favoring Republicans this year. And Democrats are. I mean, you saw Tim Ryan in his debate with J.D. Vance last night running away from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . Mark Kelly did it in his debate, \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 . All of these Democrats are trying to give the signal to voters that they are independent, that they will stand up to their own party.\par \par \uc2 The problem is, as Republicans are pointing out, they voted with Biden a majority of the time, most of the time on a lot of these issues and have touted the legislation that they have passed. The problem is, that hasn't helped improve the economic circumstances of folks. Inflation is still raging. Gas prices are still up. And that's what people in their day-to-day lives are seeing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You hit the nail on the head there, Tom, because one of the things that has come up when we have explored this on FOX Business, which, Tom, if you don't get, you should demand, because one of the things that have been discussed rather appreciatively here is this idea all the promises in the world, and teasing the benefits of the Inflation Reduction Act and all that, won't do any good for Americans who are reminded every day they go to the store that prices are still stubbornly high.\par \par \uc2 And all these Fed hikes that have seen rates go -- quadrupled over this past year and by the end of the year with rates to come maybe quintupled and not anything budging -- so, when the president or others, maybe clinging to the jobs situation, says things are better, the foundation is good, I wonder if that is not actually angering more Americans than it's helping?\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: Yes, look, it's understandable the temptation to take the news and spin it to the best of your ability, right?\par \par \uc2 The problem is that, while you can tout low unemployment and the like, that is at odds with what folks are feeling in their everyday lives. And Democrats find themselves, quite frankly, woefully unprepared to address the issue. And they should have been ready for it.\par \par \uc2 I mean, again, it's been clear for months that this is what voters want, is most important to them. And Democrats are left sort of stuttering and saying, well, these benefits are going to -- you will see them early next year.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the White House press secretary said that the other day. That's not going to cut it. That is not good enough for voters who are, as you said, going to the polls already, and will be en masse in about three weeks.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're always reminded, it was a different race, different time, I grant you, Tom. But when George Bush Sr. was running against \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , and saying we were actually coming out of the recession that was gripping the country and that was hurting him in the polls and ultimately lost him that election, ironically, he was proven right.\par \par \uc2 We were coming out of it, in retrospect, but it was too little too late to help.\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: That's exactly right.\par \par \uc2 I mean, a lot of times, these -- the cake is baked, as they say, in the second quarter of an election year. And so when views harden among voters, particularly in the post-Labor Day period -- I mean, we have had a few weeks here.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: And what they have seen is, they have seen gas prices going up. And that last -- that last inflation reading was as bad as it's been. So, voters have pretty much made up their minds about what they think of the economy and who's responsible for it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We will watch closely.\par \par \uc2 Tom, always a pleasure. Thank you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Tom Bevan following that.\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: Thanks, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, if it is a case of Democrats potentially, potentially -- still have a ways to go -- peaking too soon, did they put too much emphasis on this Roe v. Wade reversal out of the Supreme Court?\par \par \uc2 That was supposed to be the wind at Democrats' back, particularly with female voters. Early indications are right now that it doesn't have quite the same oomph that it had. And among independent women swinging to Republicans, that issue is not the one that is galvanizing them, but more some of these meat-and-potato, bread-and-butter issues.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to Johanna Maska, the Democratic strategist. We have also got Noelle Nikpour, the Republican strategist.\par \par \uc2 End it you, Noelle, begin with you, then, on this notion that trying to sort of look at a group of voters, let's say monolithically women, for example, and assuming that the abortion issue would galvanize them and bring out the passions, it's not so far panning out that way. Three weeks from now, could be different, but your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 Noelle, can you hear me?\par \par \uc2 NOELLE NIKPOUR, REPUBLICAN FUND-RAISER: Oh, yes. Yes, yes, I can hear you. Sorry.\par \par \uc2 I think that they put their eggs in one basket. and I think that all you have to do -- the argument is made by the grocery stores, the gas stations, and by a whole litany of things tied to the economy, with people's 401(k)s, people watching the market, housing crisis with interest rates.\par \par \uc2 So even though abortion is a big looming subject, that issue, for the Democrats, when female voters go to the polls -- and this is why I think a lot of people are undecided as well -- that is one issue vs. a whole litany of other issues that are day to day they are dealing with.\par \par \uc2 They are dealing with inflation day to day, and I think that that is going to outweigh. And that's why you're going to see, as these Senate races and House races get closer and closer, it's going to probably resonate with the female voter even more so than that one single issue that the Democrats have put all their -- they're all in for that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Johanna, we tend to focus on women voters and all, but the ones we're honing in on here are independent women voters and where they -- where they stand right now.\par \par \uc2 And if there is even the slightest of shifts to Republicans, does that concern you, that maybe the bang for the buck you thought might have come from the Roe v. Wade reversal is not coming?\par \par \uc2 JOHANNA MASKA, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Look, Neil, I get really tired of this notion that abortion is a women's issue, because my understanding of having a child, of which I do, is that it takes two to tango, and that there are multiple people involved in the decision.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Hold on, if you let me write. Let me write that down, two to tango. Got it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: No, I did. But go ahead.\par \par \uc2 MASKA: Two to tango.\par \par \uc2 And it just -- it's cast as a women's issue. So, one, I do think that the Republicans are the losing side.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But that's what many in your party were saying, right? They were hoping for that and then -- right?\par \par \uc2 MASKA: Well, I'm tired of both parties.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 MASKA: I'm tired of both parties making it an issue, because Americans don't actually want the government in their bedroom. And they show that in Kansas.\par \par \uc2 However, when we're looking at midterm elections, and we're looking at poverty across America, where people can't afford to eat, they are looking at the candidates very closely. And they're asking, who's making the right decisions?\par \par \uc2 I think, absolutely, these debates are going to matter. If you look at a debate like last night with Tim Ryan and J.D. Vance, Tim Ryan was actually making the case for American -- the American economy and why we need to make these investments, which shouldn't have been called the Inflation Reduction Act. It should have been called America's energy security.\par \par \uc2 But whatever we called it, it actually does counterbalance the weight that China is putting into a clean technology future. And if we don't win that...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But it's not helping right now, right? People are ticked off -- but people are ticked off right now, right?\par \par \uc2 MASKA: Absolutely. Absolutely. Absolutely. And so -- absolutely, Democrats..\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, promising things -- that things will get better, and this is the means by which it will do it, it's not panning out.\par \par \uc2 MASKA: Yes, but the Republicans don't have a solution. They spent, spent, spent, including during COVID relief.\par \par \uc2 And Bret Baier was asking \uc2 Steve Mnuchin\uc2  about, what's going to happen? And he said, why isn't there inflation happening? And Wilbur Ross said, COVID was going to be good for the economy.\par \par \uc2 And I don't want to have any of the old debates. I actually think every candidate is running away from Biden or Trump.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, this is the debate right now with the president right now and the party in control right now.\par \par \uc2 You're quite right. But it is we are in the moment now.\par \par \uc2 MASKA: We are.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, Noelle, we could talk about Republicans and a lot of their plans that might or might not pan out. It's hurting the party in power for the time being.\par \par \uc2 And, again, looking abroad to what's going on in England, just because you promised tax cuts and all, but you don't find a way to pay for them, you could really lose a lot at the polls and support for your own government. So they might repeat those sins.\par \par \uc2 Having said that now, do you think Republicans should be much more specific?\par \par \uc2 NIKPOUR: Well, I think that you can look at our past record compared to what's going on with the Democrats right now, because we were energy- independent when we had Republican leadership.\par \par \uc2 We had better situation on our taxes. We had a lot more benefits than we are seeing what's happening now. So I think you can -- we have an immediate history that we can look back on. And so, when you're a voter, when you look at that, you can say, am I better off today than I was under Republican leadership, I think that that going to help make a decision a lot better.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But your party was no stranger to spending as well, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, even before COVID, you were running up massive debts.\par \par \uc2 NIKPOUR: We were. And that is a fact.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 NIKPOUR: And you're right on that. But it looks like both parties are spending.\par \par \uc2 But the thing of it is, what you're looking at from a voters' point of view is, what is better for me? What is better for me as a voter? All I'm concerned about is myself and which platform benefits me the best. And as we're looking and things are shaping up, it looks like the economy is front and center.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 NIKPOUR: And that's going to be the Republican platform.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It can cut both ways. We'll watch closely. Noelle, Johanna, thank you both very, very much.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, one of the issues that Johanna touched on is what Democrats might be cooking up right now to ease some of these inflation pains in real terms. And that is an idea that we supposedly see fleshed out tomorrow, where the president might tap the Strategic Petroleum Reserve yet again.\par \par \uc2 Aishah, what are we hearing on that front?\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, the only thing we're hearing right now on the record from the White House is that the president, yes, indeed, is going to talk about gas prices tomorrow, his solutions to bringing those prices back down for consumers, just 21 days before Election Day.\par \par \uc2 We're going to give you an idea of what options might be on the table coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oil prices have been really deteriorating of late, largely on fears of a global slowdown. Nevertheless, the president wants to give that yet another push, we're told, by increasingly more releases from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve.\par \par \uc2 Aishah Hasnie on what we might see as soon as tomorrow -- Aishah.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Well, we're going to see the president come out and talk about gas prices and his plan to reduce those prices.\par \par \uc2 But, today, reporters tried to press the White House press secretary on what exactly that's going to look like, the White House playing coy on the details. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Going to make an announcement tomorrow, and so I will let the president speak for himself and announce -- make his announcement on what he said last week. He would have more to share on what he is doing to bring gas prices -- or what he will continue to do to bring gas prices down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: OK, so didn't learn a whole lot from that.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  is reporting that the administration is inching closer to releasing more oil from the Strategic Oil Reserves, to the tune of about 10 to 15 million barrels. If it doesn't sound like a lot, it's not a lot.\par \par \uc2 Critics say it's a drop in the bucket when you really consider how much oil is being consumed globally every single day. On top of that, Neil, the president could also detail how he plans to refresh the petroleum stockpiles. This is very important, because it is at its lowest point in nearly four decades. Not good.\par \par \uc2 And, finally, a very big question: Will the president announced a fuel export ban? Domestic refineries are reportedly bracing for that news, although the White House again did not confirm that today. This all comes as the average price for unleaded is now sitting at $3.87 a gallon. That is down from two weeks ago, but still higher than it was last month.\par \par \uc2 Also, in the coming days, some Democrats believe the president could take action against Saudi Arabia for \uc2 OPEC\uc2 's decision to cut oil, as they blame the kingdom and practically everyone else on the worsening energy crisis. Keep in mind, though, all of these potential moves come as new provisions from the left's Inflation Reduction Act increase taxes on natural gas and reinstate a previously lapsed tax on crude and imported petroleum products at about 16 cents per barrel.\par \par \uc2 So, not surprising at all that we're going to hear from the president tomorrow, just 21 days ahead of this big election. And, according to a FOX News poll, about 53 percent of voters, Neil, are not happy with the way this president has handled the energy policy front -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Aishah, thank you very much for that.\par \par \uc2 At the corner Wall and Broad, though, the Dow was racing ahead today, 337 points, and largely on declining energy prices. Oil dropped again today, largely because many are increasingly seeing the whole world in a growing recession right now. And that will hit demand.\par \par \uc2 So, maybe you don't need to tap any reserve. You just need to hang on to your wallet and pray things get better, and soon. They're not for oil. Whether that translates to gas, anyone's guess.\par \par \uc2 Gary Kaltbaum, it's a weird kind of reaction from the markets. But you have reminded me over the many decades that that is the way the market works. It is concerned about a slowdown, but if it means that pressures on the inflation front ease, they will buy that, huh?\par \par \uc2 GARY KALTBAUM, FOX BUSINESS CONTRIBUTOR: And lower rates coming down. That relieves the pressure also on maybe our Central Bank.\par \par \uc2 Yes, we may be at one of those moments where bad news is good news. And we had some economic numbers in the last few days that were less than stellar.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: So, something to watch closely, but good to see oil prices dropping. Every dime at the pump is big stuff.\par \par \uc2 I just think, though, what they're trying to do out of the White House is just not going to do much, 10 to 15 million barrels out of the reserve no biggie, when we use 20 million barrels per day. It seems, for me, quite illogical.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, if you think about it, Gary, this Strategic Petroleum Reserve is meant for emergencies. I'm not trying to lighten the burden of high gas prices. But that's not what it was there for.\par \par \uc2 It was if we -- as we experienced during the \uc2 OPEC\uc2  oil embargo, all that oil was shut off from us. So the idea was, we would stockpile our own oil and save it for an emergency, for a rainy day. So, between the 180 million barrels that the administration committed to take from the reserve back in the spring, now the 10 to 15 million extra barrels, we're running out of room there.\par \par \uc2 KALTBAUM: We have gone from over 600 million to 400 million barrels. They're going to need to replenish it soon enough.\par \par \uc2 But, for me, the big story from day one has been and always will be economics 101, why they don't just come out one morning and say the emergency is this, and we are going to produce the living heck out of oil. It's going to take time. But we are ready to go at it. I can promise you, every trader, investor, speculator around the globe will sell the price of oil down and as well as short it, because we're dealing with markets here, Neil, not just the fundamental part of it, supply, demand.\par \par \uc2 And I can promise you, oil prices were in the 50s back in 2019, before COVID. I would love to get there again. We're back down towards the low 80s. Not good enough, I can promise you that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Gary, thank you very, very much.\par \par \uc2 Another development we're following as Gary was wrapping up there is this whole case involving Victor -- I'm sorry -- Igor Danchenko on charges that he lied to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . A verdict in that case has just been reached.\par \par \uc2 David Spunt has more for us -- David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Igor Danchenko, the Russian national accused of being the primary subsource for Christopher Steele, found not guilty, again, Igor Danchenko not guilty, in a court. A jury of his peers, 12 jurors decided they would not put him behind bars or not find him guilty on four counts of lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, Danchenko was in the courtroom at the time. We don't know exactly if he breathed a sigh of relief. We had to come out and get this news out as soon as possible. However, this is part of \uc2 John Durham\uc2 's investigation, the special counsel appointed by then-Attorney General Bill Barr back in may 2019, to look at the origins of the narrative that pinned \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to Russia back in 2016, early 2017.\par \par \uc2 We covered extensively efforts by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and others to investigate Trump. That was kind of the purpose of the entire Mueller report. So \uc2 John Durham\uc2  was tasked with looking at the origins of the Russia narrative to see where it came from. He charged a Hillary Clinton campaign attorney named \uc2 Michael Sussmann\uc2  last year.\par \par \uc2 Michael Sussmann\uc2  was on trial also for lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . He was acquitted in May. And this is \uc2 John Durham\uc2 , the special counsel's second loss in a court of law. We have been told that \uc2 John Durham\uc2  is still going to move forward with a narrative, something written, a report, a full report for the public to be able to see, kind of weaving this entire narrative and really putting a bow on this more than-three-year investigation.\par \par \uc2 But, right now, we can tell you Igor Danchenko found not guilty on all four counts. Neil, jurors began deliberating yesterday at 2:00 in the afternoon, went for three hours, went all day today. We were kind of wondering what was going on today. And then, about 40 minutes ago, they had a question about a conversation that took place with an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent.\par \par \uc2 Then, about 20 minutes later, they came back and they said not guilty on all four counts, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: One of the accounts, a fifth count, was dropped by the judge a little earlier.\par \par \uc2 So I'm just wondering what this does to special counsel \uc2 John Durham\uc2 's case going forward.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Yes, we will have to see what happens.\par \par \uc2 And that -- what was dropped is -- was a conversation that Danchenko allegedly had with a man named Chuck Dolan, who's a Democratic operative. The special counsel said that Danchenko spoke to Dolan and used some of that information in the Steele dossier, that document that was trying to take down \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 However, Danchenko's attorneys and the judge ultimately said Dolan and then Danchenko communicated via e-mail, so they technically did not speak. Therefore, Judge Anthony Trenga threw out that one charge on a technicality.\par \par \uc2 What happened today, Neil, was four different counts dealing with Danchenko talking to an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent named Kevin Helson and repeating a story about speaking to who he believed was a Russian that may have been giving some dirt on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Well, Durham said that never happened. He said that Danchenko lied to this \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent four times. Danchenko's attorney said, you can't prove that it didn't happen, because, while it may not be in phone records, there's WhatsApp, there's Signal, there's other encrypted services that people use to communicate.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, it appears the jury believed what the defense team had to say. And I will add, Neil, the defense team did not even put on a defense. They not only didn't call Danchenko. They didn't even put on a defense.\par \par \uc2 So, when Durham and the government was done last Friday, Danchenko's team effectively said, we're done too.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You just have to wonder whether this could be Durham's swan song here, that this was something that there was great expectation here. And it's just fizzled.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Well, we don't know if \uc2 John Durham\uc2  is going to charge anyone else. And legal experts have said, it's unlikely, because you look at the statute of limitations, we're talking about stuff that happened in 2016, 2017.\par \par \uc2 We're at six years now from 2016. In many cases, the statute of limitations has expired. So, there's -- there's not real -- a clear path to see if he's going to charge somebody else. But, if not -- and we have known this all along, that \uc2 John Durham\uc2  is someone that's very careful, that's very calculated, that pays attention to detail, and someone that takes his time.\par \par \uc2 And I think, from a 3.5-year investigation, we know that this, what happened today, may not be his final act. It may be his final act in a courtroom. However, we do expect some sort of narrative to come out, a final Durham report, just like we saw the Mueller report.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, even though \uc2 John Durham\uc2  is a special counsel, he still falls under the branch at the Department of Justice and Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 . And \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , the A.G., has been very hands-off, allowing Durham to do his thing.\par \par \uc2 He does not want to get in the way. He said: I have renewed Durham's budget. I have let him do his thing.\par \par \uc2 So while we don't 100 percent know if \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  will give the sign- off for \uc2 John Durham\uc2  to release his report, I suspect, having covered \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , that he will go ahead and give that sign-off to allow that Durham report to be released.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Is it -- go ahead.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: I'm just getting some information from Jake Gibson, our Justice producer. He said Igor Danchenko hugged his wife, who was in tears.\par \par \uc2 And, again, not guilty on all four counts, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm wondering, in the case of \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , whether, by not intruding in the case, he's just letting -- not the Durham's imploding. I don't want to imply that, but that this whole case might be imploding. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: It's hard to tell.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Honestly, it's hard to tell, because there was a lot, Neil, that \uc2 John Durham\uc2  wanted to bring into this trial that Judge Anthony Trenga would not let him bring into this trial for evidentiary reasons and really other reasons.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Got it.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: So, I think \uc2 John Durham\uc2 , if you would ask him -- and I wish we could ask him, and I wish he would do an interview with us, but he's probably not going to do an interview.\par \par \uc2 I wish we could ask and see if he felt like he didn't get a fair shake from the beginning, if he would do things differently. I would love to sit down and have an opportunity to talk to him. And we have the microphone set up right behind my shoulder here. And I suspect we will hear from Danchenko's team.\par \par \uc2 We will try to reach out to Durham and his team, but, likely, we will get some sort of statement from him, which is -- which is normal.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: I mean, the government typically does not come out and speak of if they lose a case. So that's not abnormal if he doesn't speak.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: David, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 No doubt we might be returning to you.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Just to put \uc2 John Durham\uc2  in some perspective here, he had handled other prosecution's final argument to the jury. So he was no passive player here, very active in this case.\par \par \uc2 He had argued all along that the investigation was a logical follow-up of the special counsel \uc2 Robert Mueller\uc2 's conclusion that there was no prosecutable evidence, and that was important, the term prosecutable evidence of collusion between Trump allies and Russia.\par \par \uc2 John Yoo with us right now, a very good read on this entire case, all legal matters, actually.\par \par \uc2 John, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 JOHN YOO, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: Neil, good to be with you. Sorry I can't be there on camera.\par \par \uc2 I think this is, of course, a big setback for the special counsel, because this is now two losses in a row. And I think we're going to have to wait to see the real story of what happened with the Steele dossier. The collusion between our law enforcement intelligence agencies and the Clinton campaign is going to have to come out in a written report, just like they would say, just like in a Mueller report, rather than seeing guilty verdicts.\par \par \uc2 It was never clear how much of this was criminal and how much of this was dirty political tricks, how much of it was bias on the part of our bureaucracy in the national security area. And I think that's what we're going to have to look for now is to see, what does Durham say in his report?\par \par \uc2 There's a lot of things I think that he's brought to light and these trials, but they didn't contribute to guilty verdicts and people spending time in jail.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And that came back to Danchenko himself, that the jury obviously did not feel that he had lied.\par \par \uc2 How significant is that and the way this posits the case going forward, if there is a case?\par \par \uc2 YOO: Well, the real story here has always been not Danchenko, not \uc2 Michael Sussmann\uc2 , in a way.\par \par \uc2 The real story here that I think, even though they're acquittals, the two cases brought to light was how gullible the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was, how much they cooperated with the Clinton campaign. That's the real story. And even though Durham lost these two cases, he was able to bring out a lot of that.\par \par \uc2 In a way, you think about these two cases, the jury could well have decided that Danchenko and Sussmann before him are not guilty because they didn't lie to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , because the \uc2 FBI\uc2  already knew they were lying. That's quite possible, though, that it wasn't important because the \uc2 FBI\uc2  already knew that they were spinning, that everybody knew the Steele dossier was unreliable.\par \par \uc2 They offered Steele a million dollars to try to prove any of it, and he couldn't. And so maybe the jury said, Neil, these guys are not guilty because what they said to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  didn't make a difference. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  already knew the Steele dossier was a bunch of hogwash.\par \par \uc2 That's why I think the real -- the real story is going to have to come in what be called on, just like the Mueller report, will become to be known as the Durham report on what really happened in 2016 between the Clinton campaign and the intelligence agencies.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But, John, to understand your point, the dossier itself has been rejected, has been discredited, it has been dismissed, but that was the means by which this attack line went that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his surrogates were working closely with the Russians to guarantee his election.\par \par \uc2 The whole premise was proven wrong, and that it was misrepresented and an exaggeration, and launched countless investigations all the way to the Mueller probe, if you think about it, and the very foundation of it was built on lies.\par \par \uc2 YOO: I couldn't agree more, Neil. You have got it exactly right\par \par \uc2 The dossier and this whole hoax that the Trump campaign was colluding with the Russians, think of all the damage that did to our country, not to mention the first two years of the Trump administration, all those investigation.\par \par \uc2 I think what people need to keep clear in their minds is, there's a difference between political dirty tricks, there's a difference between abuse of power, and violating the criminal laws. And that's what Durham was never really able to get, that anybody had committed a crime that he could prove in court to a jury beyond a reasonable doubt.\par \par \uc2 That doesn't mean, though, that people didn't abuse their power. That doesn't mean, though, that people did the wrong thing. And I think that's the story here that we have seen in these cases was how much the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the CIA, intelligence agencies, wanted to believe that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was in bed with the Russians, how much they used the vast powers at their disposal, which we gave them to investigate terrorism and espionage, and they turned it in on themselves.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well put.\par \par \uc2 No, that's brilliantly put down, John, in the end, a story that proved too good to be true, and apparently too good to be remotely accurate.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, \uc2 Netflix\uc2  shares are jumping after the bell.\par \par \uc2 It got a lot more subscribers than thought. We're going to explore that in just a second.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, we are following developments on the housing front, with interest rates right now for a 30-year fixed closing in on 7 percent.\par \par \uc2 What a great time to launch a real estate show. But if anyone can pull this off, step back, de-panic this situation, it's my friend for more than a decade Katrina Campins, hosting a brand-new show on FOX Business debuting tomorrow, "Mansion Global," 8:00 to 9:00 p.m. She will put it perspective for you and calm you down at the same time, because what was the housing meltdown in the last go-round is not what's going on right now.\par \par \uc2 Katrina, great to see you.\par \par \uc2 KATRINA CAMPINS, HOST, "MANSION GLOBAL": Likewise, Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Let's talk a little bit, before we get to the particulars of the show, where you see real estate now.\par \par \uc2 As you can see, we got rates going up to 7 percent or close to it. And people are nervous. There are a lot of people who feel they missed the boom, both on the buyer and the seller side. What do you tell them?\par \par \uc2 CAMPINS: And I think, as a real estate professional, and as a real estate contributor, I would actually advise sellers and buyers to stay put right now, which is normally not what you would hear, but...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: A buyer can't stay put if rates are going higher. They're getting priced out, right, or their mortgages are.\par \par \uc2 CAMPINS: Well, if they're renting...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, I see.\par \par \uc2 CAMPINS: ... or in their current place.\par \par \uc2 Like, I wouldn't go out and buy something right now, because I think rates are going to continue to increase. And I think builders have actually slowed down on housing starts. So that's also going to affect the situation.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That could a long wait, though.\par \par \uc2 CAMPINS: It -- it could be.\par \par \uc2 But I do believe, Neil, that now's not the right time to jump into the market. And a real estate professional would normally not tell you that. But I just think, as a seller, what incentive do you have to leave your current home to jump into another home and pay these high interest rates? It doesn't make much sense, right?\par \par \uc2 So there's not going to be a lot of existing homes coming on the market, even though, in the spring and the summer, we did see more homes come in.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAMPINS: And then builders, the stats just came out today. They have slowed down. And I think that the...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you draw a distinction -- I didn't want to jump on you there - - between what's going on now and what went with the housing meltdown.\par \par \uc2 You and I, of course, were talking during those days, when people were buying and selling properties sight unseen, right?\par \par \uc2 CAMPINS: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And, sometimes, if you had a pulse, you got a mortgage. Those days are gone.\par \par \uc2 So, are we as frothy as we were then?\par \par \uc2 CAMPINS: Those days are definitely gone. And I don't think those days were healthy, quite frankly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAMPINS: And that existed for the past three years.\par \par \uc2 In Texas alone, I mean, values -- look, over the past decade, Texas housing prices rose 99 percent.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 CAMPINS: That's not sustainable, you know?\par \par \uc2 And so I think that it's very healthy that the market has slowed down. Now, with that said, do I think that Fed -- the Fed's attempt to fight inflation with an increase in interest rates, do I think it's going to work? We will wait and see. But I don't think it's currently working, to be honest with you.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's only hurting the American people. Affordability is suffering as a result of that. And, ultimately, homeownership is suffering as a result of that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Where is it suffering the most?\par \par \uc2 We know the hot markets. You're a Florida expert and all that. And we talk about hot markets. Texas certainly was certainly one of them. What's happening now?\p