aming stuff and all.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Could this be the first sign that the beating of these issues that were sort of metaphors for the whole staying-at-home thing are back or could be coming back, or through the worst?\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I hate to make one earnings report to be the standard-bearer and then...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Understood, but if I can embarrass you and replay the tape, it's not a bad option, yes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: \uc2 Netflix\uc2  is home free.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: No, it's obviously not.\par \par \uc2 But it -- this is a good number. It does show you -- show that they can survive and maybe thrive in a non-lockdown environment. And I think it also shows that people make -- some people -- some movies are really good in the theater, but most people just want to stay home.\par \par \uc2 The experience -- the theater experience is just not so great. You can get your own popcorn and sit in front of your TV, and that's kind of what they offer. And it's...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But you're antisocial. I like the theater experience.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Well, that's true. That's true. Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're -- you're unusual.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: I hate the fact that, when I'm in the theater, people are talking.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I know.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: And you see them look on their phones. And that's usually me, but I hate it when other people do it.\par \par \uc2 And that's my problem going to the theater.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, that's one of many.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: Although there are some movies you cannot watch on your TV set. It's just unwatchable.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: I mean, I watched "Top Gun" on my TV set.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: It was horrible. I mean, I heard, in the movies, it was fast- moving.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: It was big. It was, like, wonderful. So...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You try watching The History Channel on the little -- anyway, I digress.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: I could see Cavuto in the movies.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You're the best. You're the best.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Look at the time. We have to go.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Charlie Gasparino, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GASPARINO: All right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: As Charlie pointed out, one stock, one performance does not a reversal of a trend make, but a lot of people have been worried about this.\par \par \uc2 The stock up in after-hours trading here. That doesn't mean it carries over or helps every other player, from an \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , to an \uc2 Apple\uc2 , to a \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , already announcing layoffs. It doesn't get us out of the woods. It just maybe provides, for some, some light.\par \par \uc2 We'll have more after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We didn't hear America singing. We heard America shouting. And now all of us, Republicans and Democrats alike, must say, we hear you. We will work together to earn the jobs you have given us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: After a midterm shellacking, less than two months later, President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  at the time addressing Congress to say that the era of big government was over, we were going to move on hand in hand with Republicans that make things better.\par \par \uc2 He did just that, working with Newt Gingrich, and he got himself reelected. Now, that means that, three weeks ahead of the midterm election, maybe we want to focus on how the president and indeed his party responds to those midterm results, especially if they pan out as some at least on the Democratic side fear they might, with the Democrats losing the House, possibly the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Enter Gerry Baker, who's here for "The Wall Street Journal at Large" on FOX Business, hit show there. And he steps back and says focus on the day after those results come in, assuming we get, Gerry, those -- all those results.\par \par \uc2 But this could be a key test for President Biden.\par \par \uc2 GERRY BAKER, HOST, "WALL STREET JOURNAL AT LARGE": Yes, historically, you very well picked out that clip of \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  in 19 -- at the end of 1994, beginning of 1995, actually, talking about the midterm elections and about the way -- the signal it sent.\par \par \uc2 Look, midterm elections are very important signaling devices. They do tell a country, particularly in the first term of a president, how -- they tell the president how the country feels about the direction he's been going and whether or not he should change course.\par \par \uc2 And, sometimes, presidents heed that, and not everybody liked \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , but he did heed that. He triangulated. He said the era of big government was over. He saw to welfare reform. He saw a budget, deficit reduction plan with Congress.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That was huge. That was enormous, yes.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: He basically sat down with Newt Gingrich, at least for a while, and did a deal, and changed course, and was able to win reelection in '96.\par \par \uc2 If, as I think...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But he read the tea leaves himself.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Exactly. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 I mean if, as I think seems increasingly likely, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democrats do get a shellacking next month, and I think you look at these polls, it looks increasingly likely in the House, certainly, and even the Senate now, it's going to be close, that it looks like they may well lose those key Senate seats.\par \par \uc2 What is the message the next day, as you say, or, whatever it is, two, three, four days afterwards?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Or if we go to another Georgia run-off, maybe it's January.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: That's right.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: But what will the message...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You need more than 50 percent to...\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 So, what will we all make of it? And, most importantly, what will the party that's received the shellacking make of it? And I think you're right. I don't think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  -- I think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is more like \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , who, when he received a shellacking in 2010, was kind of like, oh, shrugged his shoulders and just sort of more or less went on with his agenda.\par \par \uc2 And I think that did help to store up some of the bigger kind of pressures that eventually led to the election of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2016.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But it did help him get reelected.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: It did help him narrowly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: And against \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 , who was not -- arguably perhaps not the best candidate at the time. The economy was in better shape.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: So, I always...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: ... to that point, Gerry, whether were a lot of presidents look at a midterm election and look at the history of that and say, well, my odds are pretty good, whoever's the -- of a loss, of getting reelected.\par \par \uc2 Now, of course, that didn't work for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Didn't work for George Bush Sr. I get that. But that they might be thing, we don't have to change, no matter the results.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Because it will still be tight House. It will -- could be a tight to even Senate.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: I think a lot of them do think that.\par \par \uc2 I have got to say, though -- and, again, I wrote this in the column that you very kindly referenced today -- is, I think if the Democrats do get a hammering next month, I think the pressure on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to step down, say he's not going to run again in 2024 is going to be almost irresistible, that they're going to look for new leadership.\par \par \uc2 They will have -- they're going to look for a fresh start. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is just not capable of providing it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But isn't that tough for the party in power, when it's dealing with that, it switches leaders, whether forcibly or not, to win in a general?\par \par \uc2 Because whether it's a Lyndon Johnson, very unpopular, he decides not to run in the face of that, it's tough to pull it off, isn't it?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: It is. It is, but -- very, very, very tough. And I think you're right. I mean, in the last 60 years, Lyndon Johnson is the only example of an incumbent president who has -- quote unquote -- "voluntarily" stood down and decided not to run for reelection.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: But for Biden -- I think Biden's age is going to be a problem.\par \par \uc2 Then the other problem the Democrats going to have is, who do they then nominate? I mean, they have got a real challenge in that field. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  is the vice president.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, it will be open season, right?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: It will. It will.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And there will be primaries.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: And it'll be pretty ideological.\par \par \uc2 The other really interesting thing, though, Neil, is, do the Democrats get the message if they -- again, if they do lose next month, to turn away from some of the more aggressive progressive policies they pursued on -- whether it's on some -- on some of the green stuff and some of the environmental stuff, whether it's on crime, whether it's on immigration?\par \par \uc2 Are they going to get the message, whether it's on all this kind of a woke revolution that Democrats seem to be so enthusiastic about?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Are they going to get the message? Is the party going to turn around and actually go back to being more of a mainstream, centrist party?\par \par \uc2 And, again, if you look at those candidates, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  isn't going to do that. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  probably isn't going to do that. Elizabeth Warren might be out there again. Bernie Sanders might be out there again.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But can I flip that around for you, Gerry?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Sure. Sure.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I talked to Piers Morgan earlier today.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And -- on this notion of, it's not just Democrats who might want to take their cues from what's going on in the world right now, but conservatives, and this concerned what's happening in Britain as we speak.\par \par \uc2 This is from Piers Morgan.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Maybe a warning for conservatives who are already champing at the bit to take over the House, maybe the Senate, that they're going to cut taxes, that they're going to do a lot of things, but a reminder maybe in \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  you better have that all ironed out and how you're going to pay for it before you introduce it, right?\par \par \uc2 PIERS MORGAN, FOX NATION HOST: Well, the most startling and disturbing and ultimately unsuccessful thing that she said when she announced this mini- budget, very much -- she had only been in power several weeks.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN: Where she said she hadn't done the groundwork when it all fell in her face. It was like, how could you not do the groundwork, as a new prime minister in a financial crisis?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I think the message we were trying to get at, Gerry, is that Republicans too are under pressure.\par \par \uc2 Whatever they propose, whether it's taxes and everything, should everything be paid for?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Could the markets revolt on you, as -- I know Britain's a very special case and what's going on, I grant you.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But what do you think of that, what reminders it sends?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: I think there is -- I think there will be some of that.\par \par \uc2 Again, you have got both the message from the voters and, as you say, the messenger from the markets, the message from the bond market.\par \par \uc2 And I think we are -- look, I think purely aside from the electoral politics here and what happens in the next month -- and I have said this before, and you and I have talked about this before -- I think we have changed -- we have entered a new era in the last year, in the last few months, actually, as inflation has taken off, and it's clearly embedded in the system, as markets have come to digest that and to realize that we need to fundamentally change the way we think.\par \par \uc2 We're not going to be able to have zero interest rates, as we had for more or less the last 10 years. We're not going to be able to have a government that can print money and just not worry about it, because the government -- because there will be people out there to buy its debt.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAKER: We're not going to have -- those things created this inflation. They are now changing the climate for any government, which -- any government in the world.\par \par \uc2 You're right. The U.S. is in a stronger position than the \uc2 British government\uc2 , because it's a much bigger country.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: But it's every government around the world now is going to have to deal with this new environment in which free money or cheap money is just not available.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All bets are off. All bets are off.\par \par \uc2 BAKER: It's gone. It's gone. New era.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Gerry Baker, thank you very, very much, following all of that.\par \par \uc2 Now I want to take you to "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CAVU000020221019eiai00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Midterms Are A Referendum On The Party In Power; Democrats Out Of Touch With American Voters; Biden Blames Republicans For His Economic Failures; Democrats Celebrate Demographic Change In America; Voters Care About Inflation, Crime And The Border; Pro-Life Rock Climber Scales Skyscrapers To Protest Abortion; California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  Signs Bill That Punishes Doctors For Spreading COVID Misinformation; Republican George Logan Favored To Win Connecticut's 5th Congressional District\b0\par\par\uc2 Tucker Carlson, Marc Siegel \par \uc2 7,600 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Robert from Swoyersville, Pennsylvania. That can't be a real place. "The guy in Waukesha needs to calm down. Someone get that man an ice cream cone." And some help.\par \par \uc2 That's all for tonight. Tucker is up next. And I'm Watters, this is my world.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening, and welcome to TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT.\par \par \uc2 We are going to start the week by doing something we rarely do, which is bombarding you with political cliches. We usually leave that to MSNBC. But we're going to tell you what you already know, which is midterm elections are always and everywhere a referendum on the party in power. That's true. That's true this year, too.\par \par \uc2 But this year, they are not just that. They're more than that. In just 22 days, Americans will have the chance to render judgment on the unprecedented moral atrocities our leadership class has committed over the past two years.\par \par \uc2 The shameless lying, the arrogance, the stupidity, the total incompetence, the unfairness, the relentless and vicious racism toward the majority of the American population, all of which together amount to, yes, attacks on democracy itself. If you believed in democracy, you would not act like this. No one has ever run a developed country with this degree of recklessness.\par \par \uc2 The question is, is anyone ever going to be punished for it? That's what's really on the ballot this November 8th. So, for Republicans, the election should be easy, not that you should ever for a second underestimate their ability to screw it up anyway. They may. But the template for success is right in front of them. Just run against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There may be people out there who feel sorry for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but there's absolutely nobody who thinks or can argue with a straight face that he's doing a good job because he's not. So, just say that. You're not the Biden candidate. Run on the economy and crime, homelessness -- it's everywhere, have you noticed -- fentanyl and on what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has done to our Southern border. Stick to the obvious issues and you're probably going to win. How could you not?\par \par \uc2 Surveys show that voters care most about inflation, jobs, and immigration. As it turns out, those are exactly the issues, according to a recent Harvard-Harris poll, that voters also believe are the top priorities of the Republican Party. So, this is a moment Republicans rarely see. It's a moment where there's total alignment between their priorities and the priorities of the electorate. Again, that doesn't happen very often.\par \par \uc2 That same poll, if you dig a little deeper, shows that voters believe Democrats, by contrast, are fixated above all on January 6th. Oh, yes, January 6, the QAnon insurrection. January 6, where \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 's cops opened the doors and let protesters walk inside, all of whom are now nevertheless classified as dangerous felon revolutionaries, except for Ray Epps, who for some reason is still a good guy.\par \par \uc2 Okay, Democrats, that's a story they're running on. Lots of luck.\par \par \uc2 January 6 might make for a spicy CNN segment, but it is not a governing platform. Who's voting on that? Nobody.\par \par \uc2 At least one candidate seems to understand that. It would be \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , actually. Fetterman may have brain damage, but he's one of the very few Democratic office seekers who has bothered to explain what he might actually do if he's elected.\par \par \uc2 Here is \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  at a recent event getting specific about his governing platform.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN (D), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: Send me to Washington, DC to take on, to make sure I push back against work to work.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Do you hear that? If the voters of Pennsylvania send \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  to Washington, Fetterman promises, "to push back against work to work." No more "work to work." You can hear a man in the audience cheering, as Fetterman says that. That man gets it. He knows how badly we need to fight the hidden epidemic of work to work. This work to work pipeline is literally -- literally killing people and \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  will put an end to it. No more work to work.\par \par \uc2 So, there's that, the anti-work to work platform, whatever that might be. The other thing that Democrats are running on, the thing they believe we need a lot more of is of course, abortion, and you can see why.\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 Yahoo\uc2 ! News dutifully reported the other day, "America is facing a diaper crisis and the anti-abortion movement may not be helping." So really what you have here is a classic demand side approach to the ongoing diaper and formula shortages. Just don't have kids, dumbo, and then you won't need diapers. You also won't need a house or a car or a raise or extra food or money for college tuition. It's pretty simple.\par \par \uc2 When you get to this point and this point is a place where the economy is so completely distorted that only Democratic donors can still afford to have normal families, when you get there the solution is simple: Don't reproduce. Childlessness is the cure for the economy they created and that's why abortion is so very important especially now. We don't have enough diapers. How dare you.\par \par \uc2 It's so important the Democrats say they want to eliminate the filibuster to bring back more abortion. Once again, here is \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: My name is John "Fetterwoman." You know what I would do if I was that 51st vote? First, rid of the filibuster. Go. Right next, codify Roe.\par \par \uc2 Abortion is on the ballot now in November.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, now or in November or whenever the election is. Let's be completely honest, there is no chance that guy is going to win and of course, Pennsylvania is a purple state and of course, he's running against a guy who has been attacked a lot, Dr. Oz. Of course, he has the backing of every Democrat up and down the chain in Washington.\par \par \uc2 But let's be real. That guy in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 ? Refuse to believe it.\par \par \uc2 But it's not just Fetterman. Every Democrat is now talking like this, even ones who technically identify as women. They're not just John Fetterwomen. They're actual identified as women, women, whatever that means now.\par \par \uc2 At the White House today. \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  said that abortion restrictions, which apparently are not relevant to her, but she still cares a lot, are "not just disturbing, but also dangerous." To whom you wonder? She didn't get specific on that.\par \par \uc2 And then in Arizona, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , who is running for office there against Kari Lake, just went on CNN to announce that not only is she pro-choice, she will not support any restriction on abortion ever, anywhere, under any circumstances. Here she is:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DANA BASH, CNN ANCHOR AND POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Do you support any legal limits on abortion in Arizona?\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS\uc2  (D), ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Look, the fact is right now that we are under an extreme 15-week ban.\par \par \uc2 BASH: What do you support?\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Look, when you're talking about late-term abortion, that is incredibly, extremely rare.\par \par \uc2 BASH: But what do you support? What should the limits be?\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: The decision about abortion should be between a patient and their doctor.\par \par \uc2 BASH: So, there should be no limits in the law? It should only be decided in the medical office?\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Government making these kind of mandates interferes with the care that doctors need to provide to their patients. They don't belong in these decisions.\par \par \uc2 BASH: Okay. So just to be clear, if you become Governor, you will push for a law that has absolutely no limits in any point of the pregnancy on abortion?\par \par \uc2 HOBBS: Politicians don't belong in those decisions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: If \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  loses -- it looks like she's going to, praise God - - you hope for her sake that she'll spend a little bit of time asking herself, maybe in seclusion, "How did I get here? How did someone like me, with no obvious skills, unable to speak or think, how did I wind up running for the highest office in the State of Arizona? Maybe I should've done something else in my life." But in the meantime, you have to kind of marvel at how out-of-touch people like this are.\par \par \uc2 If you listen to unhappy middle-aged liberals like Katie Hobbes, you would think that hundreds of millions of Americans are lying awake at night, night after night, terrified they might not be able to get that federally subsidized late-term sex selective abortion they've been dreaming about. They want it, but they can't have it. Potentially, maybe in Mississippi, if they were to go there and get pregnant, which they won't, but whatever. It's just wrong, and that's clearly \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 ' main fear, but it's definitely not the concern of most people and we know this for a fact.\par \par \uc2 The latest "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " poll, probably not slanted to the right, finds that only five percent of Americans list abortion as their top priority. Keep in mind, that's both pro and con, both pro-choice and pro-life, just five percent. So whatever you think of Roe v. Wade being overturned politically, this is not a powerful issue, actually. It's a boutique issue.\par \par \uc2 If democracy were functioning as a democracy, it is supposed to function where the people, the broad bulk of people have a voice in government -- in fact, they're supposed to control government.\par \par \uc2 Most people aren't that interested and Democrats know that, they have pollsters, too, but because abortion is all their donors care about, they can't stop talking about it. It tells you a lot. No wonder \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  is refusing to debate her opponent Kari Lake.\par \par \uc2 Can you imagine getting up on stage and saying, "This is a decision -- this is a medical decision between a patient and her doctor." To which Kari Lake might say, "Hmm. Like a vaccine? Your body, your choice? How does that work? It's not actually your body or your choice. You only told us that, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 . It's the politician's choice what goes into your body or what comes out." But we're never going to see that because she refuses to debate.\par \par \uc2 So, this is the Democrats' platform, January 6 and abortions. That's what they're running on, sort of.\par \par \uc2 But actually, the real thing they're running on, their main platform is fantasy, denial. None of the problems you may have noticed -- and it's hard to ignore them or not see them -- but none of them in Biden's America are real. There is no recession. It's not a recession. That's racist. Stop saying that.\par \par \uc2 There's no fentanyl crisis, at least not in Ukraine, where it matters. How many Ukrainians are dying of fentanyl? Not many. Okay, no problem and to the extent there actually are problems in this country, the United States of America, Republicans did it.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 Robert Reich\uc2  at one point was a high government official. High may be a weird way to describe him, but he served in the Clinton administration. He was reputed to be a smart guy. \uc2 Robert Reich\uc2  just wrote a long piece explaining that rising crime is actually the fault of the Republican Party because guns.\par \par \uc2 "Rising crime rates are due to the proliferation of guns." Okay, why is it that the States with the lowest crime rates also have the highest gun ownership rates? Hmm. No one believes this. Not a single person believes this.\par \par \uc2 If you're trying to tell voters that QAnon somehow defunded the police, they're not going to buy it and in fact, people are tired of being lied to at this level. It's too obvious. They know crime is real. They're afraid of it, and they know exactly why it's spiked under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  because they defunded the police, they intimidated police who were still serving into not enforcing the law and in every possible case, they sided with the criminal -- the antisocial, anti-human savages who are murdering people, pushing them in front of subway trains. "They have rights." Really? What about the rights of the person pushed in front of the subway train? Oh, shut up, racist. Right? Got it.\par \par \uc2 People aren't buying this anymore. No one is buying it and that's why \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , Congressman from Suffolk County, New York, has apparently caught up to \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , the unelected Governor of New York. Why is \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  doing so well? It's not just his sparkling personality. It's because he's running ads like this one:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NARRATOR: There is no question that this fear of crime is real.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Then without warning, he turned to violence and continued --\par \par \uc2 NARRATOR: You are looking at actual violent crimes caught on camera in \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 's New York and it's getting much worse on \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 's watch.\par \par \uc2 On November 8th, vote like your life depends on it. It just might.\par \par \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  for Governor because it's time our families feel safe again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: What's the answer to that? What? That it's not real? Those are crisis actors? No. The answer is shut up and look away, racist. Don't notice it as your society degrades.\par \par \uc2 But people who do notice it and say so out loud are doing very well.\par \par \uc2 J.D. Vance in Ohio is one of them. He is the fortunate running against \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 , the Congressman from Youngstown, who in 2019 called for ending cash bail not just in Ohio, but nationwide. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEANNE HRUSKA, \uc2 ACLU\uc2 : We are seeing States end cash bail, State by State, would you support that nationally?\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH): Yes. I think the bail system is inherently unfair and what it does is it just -- it sets people down a spiral of not being able to go to work, not being able to take care of the kids, then you have adverse childhood experiences.\par \par \uc2 Those traumatizing experiences for kids lead to mental health issues, physical health issues, obviously behavioral issues, so I would be for eliminating it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Oh, just eliminate it.\par \par \uc2 Now, keep in mind, Ryan was supposed to be the new kind of Democrat, which is to say the old kind of Democrat, the workingman's Democrat who understood that you have to have a functioning society or else you can't continue. But if you actually listen to him, he's just another cringing NPR liberal like the rest of them, advocating for bail reform. Really? We know how that ends. It's been the single most deadly social experiment we have seen in decades.\par \par \uc2 In New York, lawmakers enacted bail reform in 2019 and as a result of that, judges can no longer consider the danger posed by suspects when deciding whether to keep them in jail. Really? You can't consider the danger they will pose to the public? Isn't that the whole point? Not anymore.\par \par \uc2 So, when the bail reform law took effect in New York in 2020, police in New York arrested thousands more violent criminals who already had active open cases against them, in other words, people who should have been locked up and would have been under the old system. But they weren't because of bail reform, they were out terrorizing and murdering new people and the numbers show