port, letthemlive.org and these women who are in crisis.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, of course, and it is hard to see why it's controversial to help pregnant women who want to have their babies, that's obviously a good thing. So, I mean, what is it like, I have to ask being 61 stories above San Francisco with no rope?\par \par \uc2 DES CHAMPS: You know, it differs person-to-person. For me, it's not that bad, but I wouldn't recommend anyone else go try it.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: How didn't you wind up climbing office buildings? I mean, it just looks like a guaranteed death from my perspective.\par \par \uc2 DES CHAMPS: Well, you know, I was inspired by a guy named Alain Robert and I had no money, no influence. I'm in college. And I thought to myself, what can I do to help this pro-life movement? And you know, all I had was the gifts God gave me.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Incredible. How do you learn to free climb a building? Do you just do it?\par \par \uc2 DES CHAMPS: Oh, well, you know, Tucker, if you want to come with me on the next one, I could show you how.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I'm 53, man, no. But I'm very impressed. But how do you -- I mean, how do you train for this? And how hard is that?\par \par \uc2 DES CHAMPS: Yes, you know, I train on rock outside, out here in Las Vegas at Red Rock Canyon in Yosemite National Park and it transfers over pretty easily. Surprisingly.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: That's incredible. And I have to ask because it's irresistible, which is scarier, climbing a 61-story building with no ropes or admitting that you're pro-life in a group full of NPR listeners?\par \par \uc2 DES CHAMPS: You know, I think it's scarier to go on "\uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 " than it is to climb a skyscraper, personally.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Oh come on. Least hostile environment you'll ever find.\par \par \uc2 And last question, is this illegal? I mean, like what happens when you get to the top?\par \par \uc2 DES CHAMPS: Yeah, you know, surprisingly, in Detroit