nt and the development, safety, and effectiveness of COVID-19 vaccines."\par \par \uc2 Now, what you're not allowed to criticize \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 ? It's now a crime in California. Oh, shareholders of \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , anyone? Probably.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Marc Siegel is an actual doctor joins us tonight to assess this.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Siegel, thanks so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 So when you graduated Medical School, wasn't the presumption that you get to determine what the best practices in Medicine are?\par \par \uc2 DR. MARC SIEGEL, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK MEDICAL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, it is not the Governor of California, Tucker, good evening.\par \par \uc2 This is very dangerous. Make no mistake about it. Using the word misinformation and deciding you know, from the Governor's pulpit or maybe the French Laundry restaurant, you know, what should a doctor should do is the latest, egregious move, and doctors need to revolt on this.\par \par \uc2 You know, first of all the word "misinformation," Tucker is against science. Science is not about consensus. It is about challenging. It's about saying existing dogma doesn't work.\par \par \uc2 Look back at Galileo in the 17th Century, who said the world was round and the world was flat. People said, "He is a heretic." Or Einstein in the early 20th Century, who said the theory of relativity space and time and gravity and they called him a heretic. And then someone you know, Elon Musk is busy saying that his electric car can beat a \uc2 Porsche\uc2  off the line, ended up being right called a heretic.\par \par \uc2 All of this jeopardizes science. Science is not about Tony Fauci or Biden or somebody saying "lockdown, lockdown, closures." We're seeing it now. We're seeing the great damage to our children because of the school closures and it's a disgrace.\par \par \uc2 Those are the misinformers. That's the misinformation and they're not being called out. And that's what \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , he should be called out for his misinformation for his mask mandates, for his vaccine mandates.\par \par \uc2 Here is what I want you to know, Tucker. In the doctor's office, the right doctor's office, none of this takes place. If I'm in the exam room, and by the way, a recent global survey says that 73 percent of people around the world still trust their doctors and nurses to provide medical information, I don't know if that's still true.\par \par \uc2 But I'll tell you what I do in the exam room. I listen. I say to the patient, "What's on your mind? What's bothering you?" And I don't tell them dogma. I don't say, take that vaccine, take that treatment, wear that mask. I don't do any of that. I listen.\par \par \uc2 And when I stop listening, Tucker, then I'm no longer a physician.\par \par \uc2 So my message to \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is shut up and listen -- Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It is such a smart point. He is not promoting science, but witchcraft, effectively. We know the spells. Don't deny them.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Marc Siegel, appreciate you coming on tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SIEGEL: Thank you, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So is it going to be a red wave in November? Well, we know that congressional seats that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won comfortably, are now becoming vulnerable, happening all over the country.\par \par \uc2 We're going to talk to a Republican running for one of those seats in Connecticut, of all places, and has a good shot of winning. That's straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 One of the things we try to do in our spare time is to identify smart young people who are doing good journalism, thinking clear thoughts and acting bravely and talking to them, assessing what they have to say and holding them up for public scrutiny. Try to give them a boost.\par \par \uc2 Nate Hochman is one of those. He is a young reporter who covers Washington. We spoke to him for a brand new episode of "\uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  Today" and we were really impressed. Here's part of it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I think there is a belief in every revolution that the people who support it can control it?\par \par \uc2 NATE HOCHMAN, REPORTER, "NATIONAL REVIEW": Yes. They always think that.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: They always think. Robespierre thought that. And then I think when they marched him out, you know, to the giant razor he was thinking No, I'm going to call it -- you know, but yes, actually you've lost control.\par \par \uc2 HOCHMAN: Yes. Yes. The uncorruptible Robespierre, right, like totally right.\par \par \uc2 You know, it's a cliche, but revolutions always eat their own. Right?\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes, they do.\par \par \uc2 HOCHMAN: And, you know, these -- the leaders of -- it is a top down revolution that's happening in America right now on the cultural sphere. But the top has a very sort of sanitized understanding of what's actually happening because it is more comfortable that way and they think that it's the Civil Rights Movement 2.0 and it's actually destroying the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: We thought that was a fair assessment. Nate Hochman, product of Portland, Oregon, worth listening to. That full conversation is on "\uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  Today" starting tomorrow at 7:00 AM on FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 So one of the most interesting congressional races now underway, and of course, there are a lot of them, 435 is taking place in Connecticut, of all places, a deep blue state. In the fifth district of Connecticut, George Logan is a State Senator there and he is challenging a famous member of Congress, Jahana Hayes. She won her seat by 12 points in 2020. But this time, he seems to be winning even though his Democratic opponent has raised a lot of money. Squad members like Sandy Cortez have brought it in for her.\par \par \uc2 But George Logan looks like he is going to win anyway. Kind of an amazing