ating their homes. That is something that is sure to be on voter's minds when they hit the polls just one month before that. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Town halls in New Hampshire looks familiar. All right, Hillary, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Early voting has begun in the key battleground state of Georgia. That state is home to another key Senate race, as we know. Pundits from both sides say Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is coming off a stronger than expected performance in his debate with Democratic incumbent \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the Georgia gubernatorial candidates will stage their own debate.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Charles Watson is in Atlanta tonight with that story.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES WATSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL, MULTIMEDIA REPORTER (voice-over): Georgia builders have begun casting their ballots in the Senate race that could decide the balance of power within the nation's capital.\par \par \uc2 For weeks, Republican challenger \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has been the center of attention, following reports that he paid for an ex-girlfriend's abortion despite his pro-life stance.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with NBC's "Today Show", Walker acknowledged, for the first time, that the $700 check that his ex-girlfriend says she used for the procedure is his, but he says he didn't know how she was going to use it.\par \par \uc2 He unequivocally denied explicitly paying for an abortion and accused his ex of lying.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R-GA), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: This is my check.\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, NBC NEWS: Why should voters believe you in this moment? Your work.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Voters should believe me because I've been very transparent about everything I've ever done.\par \par \uc2 WATSON: On the debate stage, Friday, Walker largely avoided the abortion issue and focused on tying incumbent \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  to President Biden's failing economic strategies.\par \par \uc2 Walker skipped out on the second debate, and Senator Warnock took the opportunity to hammer him unchallenged on everything from abortion rights to Walker's past with domestic abuse.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: He is threatened to kill his ex-wife, put a gun to her head. He's threatened other women. And when asked about that, he really hasn't given account for this kind of violence.\par \par \uc2 And I want to know from him why he thinks he is ready to represent the people of Georgia and --\par \par \uc2 WATSON: Statistically, the two candidates are in the dead heat. But when it comes to talking about issues that matter to voters, experts say Republicans may have the advantage.\par \par \uc2 TIA MITCHELL, WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT, ATLANTA JOURNAL-CONSTITUTION: The top issue is the money issues, the pocketbook issues that we all know and we're seeing. And that's where, in a lot of these races, Republicans, seemingly have the advantage.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 WATSON: The Georgia gubernatorial race is next to take center stage. Incumbent Republican Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  and Democratic challenger \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  will face off on the debate stage tonight in Atlanta, in a rematch that has been four years in the making. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Charles, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News Senior Political Analyst Brit Hume. Brit, good evening. We check in with you now just a little more than three weeks away from Election Day, and we've talked before about how these races, you know, are tight, but they don't really track the policy issues on different polls.\par \par \uc2 For example, New York Times Siena poll comes out. And the economy is 26 percent most important problem. Inflation is next, abortion is down to five percent. And on those top two issues as we've been talking about, Republicans polled 30-25 points above Democrats.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST (on camera): Yes. That's right, Bret. I mean, the political landscape because of which the poll you just cited there, what's that illustrates, as well as the fact that this is a president's first midterm when traditionally he, any president and first midterm loses seats. Plus the fact that this president's job approval rating is underwater.\par \par \uc2 When you put those several things together, and you have a -- have about as unfavorable situation for the Democrats, as you could imagine. And yet, as our reporting in the polling we've done -- we've seen and done indicates of in race after race. The races are tight.\par \par \uc2 I mean, to cite another Georgia race, \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  is -- he's ahead in every poll in Georgia. But there is one poll, Quinnipiac poll -- Quinnipiac is an organization been doing polls for a long time, has him up by a single point.\par \par \uc2 That's out of phase with other polls, but it gives you an example of how the races are tighter than you would think they would be if the polls are right, under the circumstances.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, and we talk a lot about polls here and different polls and how they're done and who's answering the polls. But Brit, to your original point, you've been covering politics for a long time, the environment in which this race is going to happen, you say is as negative for Democrats, as you've seen.\par \par \uc2 HUME: It is. It's, you know, on the issues, the ones that matter the most, Republicans are leading. Unpopular president, at least a president with a low approval rating, very low approval rating. And on top of that, of course, it's the first midterm.\par \par \uc2 If you put those three things together, you've got a very negative landscape. And you know, there is this issue that you just touched on, Bret, about the polling, and whether it is -- the pollsters have succeeded in overcoming the problem that they've had in recent cycles of under polling Republicans.\par \par \uc2 And I think there is a lot -- refuse rate is high, I think, among Republicans. A lot of people don't answer their phones. So, it's hard for them to get these posters to get a sample.\par \par \uc2 And so, we don't know whether the pollsters have fixed that or not, we're about to find out, I think. And let's hope that this pollsters' sake that they don't find it out the hard way.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's right. I'm going to put one more poll up here. It's Associated Press poll. Most voters are dissatisfied with the state of the country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): And this is overall, Democrats, Independents, and Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Independents are tracking with Republicans now, at dissatisfied at 76 percent. Independents, dissatisfied with the state of the country. Republicans are at 85 percent. I mean, those numbers are just not a great entry into this election for Democrat.\par \par \uc2 HUME: No. Now, they're -- those numbers are terrible. And, you know, normally in a situation where if you can win the Republicans by a wide margin, and you win the Independents, you're probably going to win the election.\par \par \uc2 Now, look, there are more Democrats typically than there are Republicans. So, you know, it's possible for Democrats under certain circumstances to lose the Republicans and the Independents, as long as the margins aren't too big and still come through well in the election. So, we'll see.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HUME: But, man, this is a tough environment.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, as always, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Now, a look at a pair of governor's races. In Maryland, former President Trump is holding a fundraiser for Republican candidate Dan Cox at Mar-a- Lago down in Florida.\par \par \uc2 He faces Democrat Wes Moore in November. Nonpartisan Cook Political Report has rated that race as solid Democrat in Maryland.\par \par \uc2 In Maine, incumbent Democrat \uc2 Janet Mills\uc2  as a slight edge for another term over Republican former governor Paul LePage. Mills has forged a bipartisan path through her first term in office, but has faced criticism from conservatives over school shutdowns during COVID and divisive lesson plans.\par \par \uc2 Federal Election Commission\uc2  records indicate top House Democrats \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2  and James Clyburn have continued to shell out 10s of 1000s of dollars in campaign funds to family members ahead of the November midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Fox News Digital, reports waters reelection campaign paid a total of $16,000 to her daughter in July and September of this year, and more than a million dollars over several years.\par \par \uc2 Clyburn paid $7,500 for office rent in August and nearly 80,000 since March of 2022 to a company operated by his daughter. Their campaigns have not responded to requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the Biden administration unveiling its national security strategy. We will tell you what countries are the main focus and why.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I think the lesson that China is learning from this war in Ukraine right now is to be patient.\par \par \uc2 So, when it talks about peaceful reabsorption of Taiwan, saying, well, we don't rule out military options.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: New documents released by the House Oversight Committee say former President Trump's company charged excessive nightly hotel rates for Secret Service personnel on dozens of trips.\par \par \uc2 The Secret Service says it will provide the committee with the requested information, there has been no response from the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the Justice Department says former Trump adviser \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  should serve six months in prison and pay a $200,000 fine for defying a congressional subpoena.\par \par \uc2 Bannon was subpoenaed by the House committee investigating last year's capital riot of January 6. The Department says Bannon pursued a bad faith strategy of defiance and contempt and publicly disparaged the committee. Bannon's lawyers deny he was acting in bad faith they are asking for probation.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, Russia and China are the focus of President Biden's national security strategy. The updated policy has been released after months of delay.\par \par \uc2 Chief National Security Correspondent Jennifer Griffin takes a look from the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice over): At the Communist Party Congress, President Xi delivered a defiant two-hour keynote speech and reiterated China's intent to use, "all necessary measures to take over Taiwan", where most of the world's high end semiconductor chips are made.\par \par \uc2 XI JINPING, CHINESE PRESIDENT (through translator): we insist on striving for the prospect of peaceful reunification with the greatest sincerity and with the greatest effort.\par \par \uc2 However, we are not committed to abandoning the use of force.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Xi offered no concessions to the west and said he would continue to modernize China's military. The Chinese leader is expected to be handed a third term potentially setting him up as president for life.\par \par \uc2 This as the Biden Administration released its 48-page national security strategy. "Russia poses an immediate threat to the free and open international system. The PRC, People's Republic of China, by contrast, is the only competitor with both the intent to reshape the international order and increasingly, the economic, diplomatic, military and technological power to advance that objective."\par \par \uc2 National Security Adviser Jake Sullivan said China is learning from Russia's war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN, NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: Putin is making reckless nuclear threats, willfully violating U.N. Charter, relentlessly targeting civilians, acting with a brutality that threatens to drag us all back into the dark days of Soviet expansionism.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Putin is so low on long range guided missiles. He's relying on these small Iranian drones that swarm civilian apartments in the capital Kyiv today.\par \par \uc2 Putin hopes to freeze Europe this winter, relying on allies like China and India who continue siding with Russia at the \uc2 U.N\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Iran's president facing a violent uprising by Iranian women accused the U.S. of involvement.\par \par \uc2 EBRAHIM RAISI, IRANIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): Now, following America's failure in militarism and sanctions, Washington and its allies have resorted to the failed policy of destabilization.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): The Biden administration recently fired a major shot across the bow at Beijing, imposing sweeping new export controls aimed at cutting off China from obtaining chips used in supercomputers, causing the complete collapse of the communist country semiconductor industry according to experts, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Uncle Sam wants you to help pick up after illegal immigrants along the southern border. We'll tell you about the latest push by the Biden administration. We'll take you there.\par \par \uc2 First, beyond our borders tonight, at least eight people are dead after a fire burns through part of a notorious Iranian prison. Online videos purport to show chaotic scenes at that facility. What exactly happened there remains unclear. That prison holds many political prisoners seen as a threat to the regime.\par \par \uc2 Britain's new treasury chief rips up the government's economic plan dramatically reversing most of the tax cuts and spending plans that new Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  announced less than a month ago.\par \par \uc2 The move raises new questions about how long the beleaguered British leader can stay in office.\par \par \uc2 And the Swedish parliament elects conservative \uc2 Ulf Kristersson\uc2  as Prime Minister. The vote was 176 to 173. His three party coalition does not have a majority but in Sweden, prime ministers can govern as long as there is no parliamentarian majority against them.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We have dramatic new video tonight from the southern border. A human smuggler crashes his vehicle, then bails out of that car, or that SUV, with a group of illegal immigrants as he is being pursued by Texas state troopers through the brush there. It happened in the Rio Grande Valley. Troopers later arrested the suspect, who is said to be -- and you see the chase there -- an illegal immigrant from Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, the Biden administration is trying to get federal employees to take a leave from their jobs and volunteer to do various work, janitorial services, for illegal immigrants at the southern border. Critics say this is the president's way of trying to hide just how desperate the situation has become down there. Correspondent Gillian Turner has details in an exclusive report.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: The \uc2 Homeland Security Department\uc2  is sounding the alarm, asking for volunteers from every \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  agency to deploy to the southern border to provide support services to migrants crossing into the U.S. legally and illegally. An internal Pentagon memo obtained exclusively by FOX News reveals the Defense Department is complying with the request, already recruiting from the military. No experience is required., just a willingness to serve, according to the documents.\par \par \uc2 DHS will cover costs for federal government employees to leave their jobs to travel to the border for 60 days at a time. A DHS memo explains "This activity will help ensure the individuals arriving to our borders receive compassionate and timely care while alleviating the strain on CBP law enforcement officers who continue to perform their frontline duties."\par \par \uc2 A DHS recruitment document provides a detailed look at what the volunteers will be asked to do in support of migrants, including basic housekeeping, picking up trash, sweeping, cleaning refrigerators, and shredding paper, prescription medicine runs, picking up medical prescriptions from local pharmacies, migrant personal property management, tagging, bagging, sealing, storing and retrieval of personal effects. DHS will cover the cost of transportation, lodging, and daily expenses the volunteers rack up while on assignment.\par \par \uc2 Critics say it's time the Biden administration faces reality.\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JUDD, ACTIVE BORDER PATROL AGENT: If there wasn't a crisis, why would they be advertising for services like this? This administration refuses to define the problem.\par \par \uc2 CHAD WOLF\uc2 , FORMER ACTING HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: That's where this administration is just falling asleep. They have no plan in place to actually mitigate this, to actually try to reduce the numbers to draw this crisis to a close.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TURNER (on camera): An internal memo sent to the Defense Department staff instructs them to disseminate widely and encourage this opportunity. So far a Pentagon spokesperson has referred to us DHS for comment on these documents. DHS has not responded to our request. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll keep asking. Gillian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There's said to be new pressure tonight on President Biden's chief of Customs and Border Protection. "Politico" reports five current administration officials who work with Chris Magnus are characterizing him as unengaged, absent from meetings, even falling asleep in some meetings, and attempting to shift blame to other agencies. Magnus acknowledges in a statement to "Politico" he has spent his 10 months in office getting up to speed on the agency's many complex areas.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is under fire tonight for a raid in Beverly Hills and the seizure of more than $80 million of private property. National correspondent William La Jeunesse explains from L.A.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Jeni Pearsons, like hundreds of others, kept her valuables here inside what was a private vault in Beverly Hills.\par \par \uc2 JENI PEARSONS, VAULT CUSTOMER: What we had were silver coins.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: But when she arrived one day last year to check her box, all she saw was this notice the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had taken everything.\par \par \uc2 PEARSONS: This is the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and it seems like they literally have a plan to steal like $80 something million dollars.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: It's $85 million to be exact, seized from 700 safety deposit boxes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT FROMMER, INSTITUTE FOR JUSTICE ATTORNEY: The purpose of this search wasn't to keep people's property safe but to try to steal it.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Lawyer Robert Frommer filed a class action suit against the government.\par \par \uc2 FROMMER: What happened at U.S. private vaults was the largest armed robbery in United States history.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Well, that's not what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says. It obtained a search warrant to seize the boxes inside, alleging it was used as a hiding place for criminals, quote, "The \uc2 FBI\uc2  stands by its ongoing investigation of U.S. private vaults, a business that criminally facilitated drug trafficking and money laundering." But 18 months later, the U.S. attorney's office has yet to file criminal charges against a single customer.\par \par \uc2 PEARSONS: I am certainly not a criminal, but, yes, this whole thing has been really intimidating.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: But the U.S. attorney's office points to the millions no one has claimed and the plea deal in which the vault company admitted it, quote, "recruited as its customers drug traffickers and other criminals."\par \par \uc2 FROMMER: The whole purpose of the Fourth Amendment is I can't take Paul's property because I think I'm suspicious of Peter.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Pearsons, like hundreds of others, got most of her valuables back. Some actually sued the government to do. on the other hand, some box holders never came forward, forfeiting their cash to Uncle Sam.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (on camera): The judge ruled the \uc2 FBI\uc2  did not violate any of the customers' rights against illegal search and seizure. An appeal is likely. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: William, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, is the U.S. economy, quote, "strong as hell" in the words of President Biden? The panel tackles that and the midterms next.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 11 in Los Angeles as police in Stockton arrest a 43- year-old man who may be a serial killer. Wesley Brownlee was taken into custody after authorities received multiple tips from the community there. They determined Brownlee was on a mission to kill Saturday morning. He is linked to the fatal shooting of six men.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Nissan Stadium in Nashville, Tennessee. One of the big stories there tonight from FOX 17, Mayor John Cooper and the \uc2 Tennessee Titans\uc2  have reached a deal for a new dome stadium on the east bank. The $2.1 billion facility enclosed would be built on land which currently serves as parking lots east of \uc2 Nissan\uc2  Stadium. It could be ready by the 2026 \uc2 NFL\uc2  season.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Who exactly thinks the president is doing a good job on inflation?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: We understand that there are challenges that are in front of us here in this country. That is why the president has taken action to lower costs.\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: In some ways, having achieved so much legislatively makes it hard to talk about it all at once because there are just so many accomplishments.\par \par \uc2 RONNA MCDANIEL\uc2 , REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE CHAIRWOMAN: None of them are talking about what they have done to make our lives better. What can they talk about? Inflation? Gas prices? Crime? Drugs? An open border? They have nothing to run on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Setting the table for the midterms here three weeks from tomorrow. As you look at our latest FOX News polls on the congressional ballot question, you see likely voters there, Republicans with the plus one, "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " had it a plus three in the generic ballot for likely voters. And on the issues GOP handles better -- crime, inflation, energy. There you see R plus 15, 13, and four.\par \par \uc2 With that, let's bring in our panel, Juan Williams is a FOX News analyst, David Drucker, senior congressional correspondent for "The Washington Examiner," and Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief of "The Federalist," David, welcome back.\par \par \uc2 DAVID DRUCKER, SENIOR CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT, "WASHINGTON EXAMINER": Good to see you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Set the table for these midterms. How do you see it? Seems like in the last couple of weeks there has been a shift.\par \par \uc2 DRUCKER: Well, I think we have to look at this from the fundamentals, right? So in the late summer Democrats definitely had a surge, brought themselves back into contention from a really horrible place at the beginning of the summer where we were looking at just a complete wipeout. And now what we've seen over the course of September and the first part of October is the fundamentals take hold again.\par \par \uc2 First of all, historically, the party that's not in power in the White House wins an average of 25 House seats and four Senate seats. What does that tell you? Republicans don't even need a wave to win control of Congress. They just need history to behave the way history has behaved.\par \par \uc2 Second thing is, the most important issues to voters were then and are now especially inflation, the economy generally, crime, energy, the border in certain places. All of those issues are playing in favor of Republicans. And there's just not anything Democrats can do about it right now except be Republicans, meaning, you can't go to the voters and not be who you are. And it's possible that President Biden's policies will eventually work, but they are not going to work in time for November 8th. And so they are fighting an uphill battle.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, in the short-term. Mollie, they are still having abortion issues, talking about it a lot. There is an event this week that the president is going to talk about. Abortion has ticked down in the past couple of weeks as far as importance for voters.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": Yes. It continues to be important for both pro-lifers and people who support abortion, but the economy is that issue that has really been strong and that continues to motivate voters. It does seem like Democrats have a really bad set of cards that they are dealing with. But they are the ones who dealt the cards. It is their policies that seem to be under scrutiny right now, and they are all going against their favor, whether it's the economy, the border, inflation. And this is just -- this is not the situation that they wanted to have at this point in time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yet all these policy issues are upside down, and we showed the plus for Republicans. A lot of these races, though, are tight, Juan, some of these Senate races.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: They are very tight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There is an "Axios" piece that says "Blue state headaches. House Republicans are increasingly confident they can make unexpected inroads in some solidly Democratic congressional districts, including some in California, Connecticut, New York, Oregon, Rhode Island. They are now pouring $25 million in some of the bluest political battlegrounds on the map." Sometimes that shows you, where the money is going, how the table is going to be set.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think that's right. And also, I think in that report you also saw that President Biden was out in Oregon this weekend, and he is defending \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2 . And you stop and you think, well, gee, why would a Democratic president have to spend time and effort doing such a thing if the mood of the country was in the favor of the Democrats at the moment?\par \par \uc2 We have been talking a lot about the economy. Clearly, it's the number one issue. You showed the statistics. It's a big plus for the Republicans. I think the Republicans have benefited greatly. But when you hear President Biden say the economy is pretty strong, it might invite some derision from voters who say, oh, I'm worried about the economy --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Strong as hell, and eating an ice cream cone.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Right, but you stop and think about it, the economy is hot. In fact, I think it's too hot. That's why we have inflation. You stop and think about the essentials, the fundamentals, things like unemployment, wages going up, you think about consumer spending remaining strong as we go into the holiday season, and you say, well, why are people so scared about the economy? Why is "The Wall Street Journal" surveying economists who say, oh, we may have a recession?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Because, technically it could be that, but at home, as you are doing the budget, one thing leads to another, and you can't pay the bills.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: This is -- I do not understand Democrat messaging here. Anyone who is trying to buy anything or build anything knows that prices are sky high. And you talk to business owners, and they say they feel like -- I was just talking to people who have a steel company last week and they said this reminds them of 2008, which was the great recession. I don't understand why Democrats aren't saying we feel your pain. We know this is bad. When they are saying, actually, the economy is great, it makes people feel like they can't trust them at all.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think that the country --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Go ahead, Juan. Finish.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I was going to say to you, Mollie, I think that in fact the Democrats have failed in terms of the messaging because it was the Democrats that kept this economy afloat by helping people during the time of the pandemic. And I think that right now you have a lot of naysayers, and it's working. It is scaring voters, but I don't think it's legitimate.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But here is the thing, David, is that some of these candidates are now being asked, the Inflation Reduction Act was passed. They tout it. But why isn't it reducing inflation? And Senator Bennet from Colorado said, well, it's going to happen in the long term, and Senator Warnock from Georgia said we are still in the midst of a pandemic. And people say wait, wait, wait, what's happening now?\par \par \uc2 DRUCKER: And this is the problem when you are the party in power and you are facing heat from the voters. You are not going to save yourself by saying you're right, you're in a really tough spot, because voters are going to say, but you put me there. And you are not going to save yourself by saying, trust me, our policies are going to work, because voters are going to say it's not working for me now, and there is an election tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Look, Bret, you and I have covered these midterm waves for several years now, 06 a Democratic wave, 10 a Republican wave, and so on. What do we see often? Very tight races until the bitter end. Some of them finish really tight, but independents swing a particular way. Right now, that is how it's looking.\par \par \uc2 The only thing I would say that might be different this time around is Democrats are legitimately more energized than they have right to be being the minority party -- or the party in power, excuse me. Normally what happens is the out party is energized to take back power. The in-party knows what's coming and they're just kind of resigned and nobody shows up. I think a lot of Democrats are going to show up. I also think a lot of Republicans are going to show up. So we could see a few surprises. But Republicans are in the driver's seat. You wouldn't politically want to be in any better position than they are in today.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, listen, if the Connecticut governor's race is tight on election night, that's a bad night for Democrats. On the flipside, if the Iowa Senate race with \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2  is tight, there is a new poll out that's tight, that's not a good night for Republicans. It seems like it's going the first part.\par \par \uc2 Stand by, panel. Up next, President Biden's national security strategy coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 XI JINPING, CHINESE PRESIDENT (through translator): As the wheels of history roll forward towards national reunification and the revival of the nation, the complete reunification of the motherland must be achieved.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD AUSTIN\uc2 , DEFENSE SECRETARY: The most pressing issue right now is Russia, but certainly China is a thing that we, the United States of America, will focus on.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 SETH MOULTON\uc2 , (D-MA): We don't have a plan to fight this war through the Taiwan people the way we are fighting Ukraine war through Ukraine. That means that Americans are going to die if Xi invades Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE WALTZ, (R-FL): I think we face a very real chance of Taiwan becoming the next Ukraine within the next five to 10 years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Reaction to a speech by Chinese President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 . "Wall Street Journal" op-ed, "The Thought of Chairman Xi, the west has been slow to recognize the seriousness of the threat, but perhaps the coronation of Mr. Xi will be the catalyst for a bipartisan awakening. Any response has to start with the recognition that U.S. military isn't currently prepared to meet China's threat."\par \par \uc2 We are back with the panel. Juan, this speech where, essentially, he says we got Hong Kong under control, Taiwan is next, it raised some high brows.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, we have been watching this. I think everybody understands the stakes. I think President Biden has spoken out. Some people say that he has spoken out of line, out of line with the acceptance by the United States of One China policy. But he has been more aggressive.\par \par \uc2 And I think what you see President Xi doing, I think he is watching Ukraine. Ukraine is the biggest foreign policy issue at the moment, and the question is whether or not the U.S. and its allies ever blink in terms of supporting Ukraine against Russia. If there's a blink, I think that's a green light for President Xi in terms of Taiwan, saying yes, there is a lot of talk from people like Biden about supporting Taiwan. But when push comes to shove, we think that it might be temporary, and we will win in the long run. If anybody is known for long-term thinking, it's the Chinese.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Chinese. And the question is, if he says it now what does that mean action-wise against Taiwan?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Well, he is speaking in a way that you would hope that the U.S. would be responding much more aggressively to these comments. The U.S. foreign policy consensus is not known for being particularly good for knowing what threats are down the road. And even this week we have this national security strategy, and you have the people saying well, Russia is the biggest threat than China. Not sure that's actually true. And that does show, though, that we really need to resolve this conflict in Ukraine. We need to figure out a way to end this in as favorable a way as possible for the Ukraine people so we can turn our attention to the actual threat that is China.\par \par \uc2 And people have been enabling them for far too long. President Biden, when he ran for office, said that they were not a threat in any way. It sounds like is he slightly changing his tune. But nobody would say that the world is a safer place right now than when he was elected. There's a lot that we need to be --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and there's a lot of adversaries, David, that at least seem like they are acting -- emboldened, Russia, Iran, China.\par \par \uc2 DRUCKER: Look, our adversaries are always going to test a new president. And at the beginning of Biden's presidency the Afghanistan pullout certainly emboldened a lot of our adversaries. I think that in Washington the discussion is often Russia or China. I was in Ohio last week covering a \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  race. I was talking to J.D. Vance, the Republican nominee, who I think is likely to advance to the Senate, and he said he was concerned about continuing to fund robust U.S. military aid to Ukraine because he wanted the country, he wanted the U.S. to pivot to China and focus on Beijing.\par \par \uc2 But I think that there's another way to look at this, as one is directly tied to the other. The Chinese are looking at how the Americans, how we are handling the Russian invasion of Ukraine. And any sign of weakness or acquiescence or a desire to sue for peace at all costs tells them that we do not have -- in fact, neither the U.S. nor the west will have the stomach to defend Taiwan, and, in fact, a lot of their expansionist goals. They want to supplant the U.S. as the world's global superpower. And I think by standing strong with Ukraine, we have an opportunity to show Beijing that it might be a little bit more difficult than they would like with their designs on Taiwan and the Pacific.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's complex, it definitely is. Panel, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In tonight's SPECIAL REPORT salute, helping disabled veterans and veterans overall learn the game of golf, form lasting friendship. The PGA's military program, PGA Hope, it falls under what's called PGA Reach, introduces golf to veterans to enhance their well-being. The program is being taught by PGA professionals free to all who have served. We spoke to some of the veterans about the event at Congressional Country Club and their service to our country.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Veterans from across the country attended PGA Hope National Golf and Wellness Week at Congressional Country Club. Many turning to golf for its physical and mental health benefits, including Navy veteran Michael Moore. He says the program also brought him closer to other veterans with shared experiences.\par \par \uc2 PO3 MICHAEL MOORE, \uc2 U.S. NAVY\uc2  (RET): It was a safe place. It was around more brothers and sisters from the armed forces, and it just -- like I could breathe. It just felt easy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Army veteran and Silver Star recipient Chris Cordova served through four deployments, but there is one he will always remember.\par \par \uc2 MAJ. CHRIS CORDOVA, \uc2 U.S. ARMY\uc2  (RET): It was, it was a tough day.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As a combat medic, he worked to save dozens of lives during the battle of Kamdesh in Afghanistan. In October of 2009, hundreds of \uc2 Taliban\uc2  fighters ambushed Combat Outpost Keating, 27 soldiers were wounded, eight were killed.\par \par \uc2 CORDOVA: My goal every day is to honor the sacrifices that those eight soldiers made. If it wasn't for their sacrifices, there's a strong chance that I wouldn't be here today.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Cordova served another 11 years in the Army. When he retired, he began playing golf alongside his family. He says since getting involved in PGA Hope, he has watched how other veterans have benefited.\par \par \uc2 CORDOVA: I have been through some struggles, but I have a very strong support system. I keep in touch with my veteran friends very, very close, and when I need them, I can reach out to them. But I think for a lot of folks that are estranged from their veteran community, this really helps bring them back in and find that sense of comradery again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): If you want to get involved, PGAreach.org. Full disclosure, I'm on the PGA Reach board, but I wanted to bring you that story.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for the SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. A few seconds late, but Jesse, I figured you'd give me a little something.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020221018eiah00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President Hitting Road To Reassure Americans; White House Officials Tout Economic Progress In Under Biden; Economic Woes Pose Threat To Dems As Midterms Near; Impact Of Inflation Reduction Act On Midterms; Pennsylvania Poll: Fetterman Has Edge Over Oz On Economy; Impact Of Economy And Inflation On Midterms; Jury Gets Case Of Steele Dossier Source Danchenko; Awaiting Verdict On Steele Dossier Source Trial; Suicide Drones Hit Kyiv, At Least 4 Killed; Xi Strikes Defiant Tone As He Seeks Third Term; Midterm Debate Schedule Heats Up In Key States; Econ Woes Pose Threat To Dems AS Midterms Near; Wild Brawl Outside Minneapolis Bar As Crime Surges\b0\par\par\uc2 Trace Gallagher, Chad Pergram, Robert Wolf, Bryan Llenas, Andrew  McCarthy, David Spunt, Joe Concha, Charles Watson, Will Cain \par \uc2 8,324 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Heck of a rally there as we hand off, John, and it is great to have you back today, Sir.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: It is great to be back and it's heartening to see the DOW going up for a change.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thought we'd mentioned it. All right. We'll be here tomorrow. Thanks for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'm John Roberts, THE STORY with Martha starts right now.\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: John and Sandra, thank you. And good afternoon, everybody. I'm Trace Gallagher in for Martha MacCallum. Andy McCarthy, K.T. McFarland, and Joe Concha, all ahead this hour on THE STORY. But first 22 days out from Election Day, yet another poll is finding by a longshot that the economy is the number one worry for likely voters as they head to the polls, and those likely voters favor Republicans overwhelmingly. Moments ago, the White House saying the President is hitting the road nonstop to reassure voters.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The President is going to get out there, as we have said multiple times to make sure that he's talking directly to the American people about how we are delivering, delivering on our promises, delivering on how we're really taking inflation seriously.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And this past weekend, he did in fact hit the road with this message. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Our economy is as strong as hell -- the internals of it. Inflation is worldwide. It's worse off everywhere else than it is in the United States.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Let's bring in \uc2 Stephen Moore\uc2 , Founder of the Committee to Unleash Prosperity and Former Senior Economic Adviser to President Trump; and Robert Wolf, Former Economic Adviser to President Obama, Founder and CEO of 32 Advisors, and a Fox News Contributor. We have five minutes, but we had to do the title, so now we've got like three and a half. Gentlemen, thank you so much for coming on.\par \par \uc2 Steve, to you first, you and E.J. Antoni wrote this op-ed in the New York Post quoting here: "Average American is losing 34K and everything else on Biden's watch." Quoting again, "The average 401(k) plan had over 135,000 at the start of the year today, those assets have shrunk on average, to about 101,000.\par \par \uc2 In other words, the average 401(k) plan down about 34,000 -- more than 25 percent in less than one year." And then Steve, you put in inflation and credit card debt is rising. And you have the President just moments ago, as we have heard over and over saying the economy is strong as hell people. People aren't just baffled at that comment. They're now getting kind of angry if you look at some of the comments.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MOORE\uc2 , FORMER SENIOR ECONOMIC ADVISER: Yes, Trace, I agree with that. I think when the President keeps talking about how well the economy is doing, and look, the, the jobs market is good, but it's what I call a cost-of-living recession, where people's paychecks are just not keeping pace with the higher prices, causing real high strain.\par \par \uc2 So, not only did the average family in the last year, lose about $4,000 of purchasing power, something you've reported on Fox, but what we show is that, you know, if you look at people's lifetime savings, somebody who's, you know, say 60-65 years old, spent their whole life saving up their nest egg. They've lost about 20 percent of it because of the, the terrible stock market and also the inflation.\par \par \uc2 And that's a big hit too for seniors, especially because it's almost like you're losing a down payment on a, on a retirement home.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, he makes a very good point, Robert, because you know, even if you're working and you're getting your five percent raise, you're still, you know, you're still three, 34 percent underwater. So, you're still 40 percent underwater in this whole thing. And so, when the President comes out and says, yes, the economy's strong as hell, it really is, it really seems a bit disingenuous or out of touch.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT WOLF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: So, Trace, let me unpack a few things there. First of all, we know that the stock market is at a point in time. If we were looking at the first nine months of the Biden presidency, he outperformed Trump pretty dramatically. If we looked at the way Steve would, would be talking about the economy just by the stock market, then \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  would be the two greatest presidents ever because they had the best returns in the stock market.\par \par \uc2 They blew away both Reagan and Trump. So, we know that the stock market's a point in time. And Steve's being a little disingenuous, because we know that it's because of raising interest rates. That, and by the way, we've been in agreement that we should be raising interest rates, because we thought the Fed was behind the eight curves. So, we know why the stock market, OK, is coming off. And so, I would tell you, it's not a presidential aspect to it, right.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: But they're raising interest rates because inflation is going up.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: They're trying to, they're trying to quell inflation here. So, there's a problem -- and inflation is eating people alive and gas prices, Robert, are eating people alive. And the President is not acknowledging that, and that is, and that is to a lot of people that I've talked to and a lot of people when you go online, it is making them angry that the President does not acknowledge they're struggling. Here's, here's some of the White House officials talking about how well they're doing on inflation. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG, TRANSPORTATION SECRETARY: When the President took office, we were facing an economy that was at risk of going into freefall. The American Rescue Plan stopped that.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA ROUSE, COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS CHAIR: We are starting to see signs that our red hot economy is starting to cool, we're better positioned than most other countries for the Feds to achieve its, their goals.\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, WHITE HOUSE COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVSIERS: Are we implementing the necessary policies to ease inflationary pressures? I would say the answer, the answer is unequivocally yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Instead of saying, Steve, how great we're doing over and over again, why not come up with solutions and say, listen, here's the way that we're going to handle this going forward. I don't, I just don't get it, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Yes, I don't get how the Biden Economic Advisors can say that we're doing everything we can to bring down gas prices, everything we can to bring down inflation, they're not cutting government spending, Trace, they're adding more. We all know that what caused the runaway inflation was the $4 trillion spat. And then, Biden, is just last week implementing another $500 billion program for student loan forgiveness.\par \par \uc2 So, and one other quick thing, it is not true what \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  said. We were in a strong recovery from COVID in the last six months of, of Trump's presidency and we have the vaccine coming out. We should be booming right now. We shouldn't be in this kind of stagnation, kind of melees economy.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. It does seem like and you look at gas prices that just outside the door, you walk in Robert, I mean, they're like seven bucks. They're pushing to seven bucks. And people feel that viscerally when they go and driving around. I'll give you the final thoughts on this. But somebody's got to say, hey, yes, I know. It's bad. We're working on it. We're helping we're trying. Your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Trace, no question. Inflation is hurting everyday Americans. But let's be clear, gas prices were coming down each and every day until Saudi cut supply by 10 percent, two million barrels. And we should be really going after what Saudi has been doing, as opposed to what America is trying to do. We know inflation is running rampant. I've been the first one to say that for months, you can ask Steve. And I do think we're doing a lot of things to try to make that come down.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: Well, we could produce a lot more energy here at home, Trace, that would bring the gas price down.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: We could. We could. And listen, I have asked the same thing a thousand times in California, you got the governor going after the refineries and you want to know what refinery in their right mind would try to produce more gasoline for a state that plans to get rid of gasoline and go to electric cars. It just doesn't make sense. Robert, Steve, great to see you both. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WOLF: Thanks, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Meantime, Senator Bernie Sanders pressed on inflation over the weekend. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: Senator, do you accept the criticism that the American Rescue Plan passed in early 2021 contributed to the current inflation issue we're dealing with now?\par \par \uc2 SEN. BERNIE SANDERS (D-VT): No, I don't. Inflation right now, as I'm sure you know, Chuck is an international problem.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  probing Democrat spending and its impact on the midterms, publishing this headline 22 days out, Democrats spent two trillion to save the economy. They don't want to talk about it. Let's get to our Senior Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram. He's live for us on Capitol Hill. Chad, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Trace, good afternoon. Over the summer, Democrats hailed their passage of the Inflation Reduction Act but inflation is still spiking. Democrats are struggling to campaign on the law. Despite its name, Democrat Michael Bennett finds himself in a tougher-than-expected re-election contest in Colorado. Bennett is resigned to the political realities of the Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MICHAEL BENNETT (D-CO): The elements of the Inflation Reduction Act aren't going to kick in for a while, Dana. Finally, we overcame pharma to have requirement that Medicare negotiate drug prices on behalf of the American people, capping insulin at $35.00 a month. I mean, these are things that are going to take a while to put in place.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Democrats know that inflation is a political albatross for their party.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE DINGELL (D-MI): I think inflation is real. I think it is one of the issues that is going to decide how people vote in November. I will also tell you that inflation has gone down the last, last few months to two percent increase.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Democrats may not see a political advantage campaigning on the Inflation Reduction Act. Republicans are sure to remind voters who they believe drove up spending and Democrats are avoiding the hallmark of their legislative agenda.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DARRELL WEST\uc2 , BROOKINGS INSTITUTION: It's tough to campaign on a bill called the Inflation Reduction Act when we have inflation running at more than eight percent. Voters can see through that. They can complain. Maybe the legislation didn't achieve its objectives and certainly is not reducing inflation right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Republicans and Democrats approved trillions in COVID aid during the pandemic but Democrats muscled through their own massive coronavirus bill last year along with the IRA over the summer. Those remain top of mind for voters. Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Indeed, they do. Chad Pergram, live for us on the hill. Chad, thank you. Republicans have an edge on Democrats when it comes to the economy, right? But that may not be the case in the closely watched Pennsylvania Senate race, what voters there are saying is next\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: We will win this race because of crime and because of inflation, which Fetterman has advocated for even more spending.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, we are seeing a twist in the polls right, in one of the contests that could decide control of the Senate. Our Fox News Power Rankings called the Pennsylvania Senate race a toss-up and the state is apparently going against the national trend when it comes to one of the top issues in this year's midterms. Axios Co-Founder and Executive Editor, Mike Allen, will join us in a moment but first to the National Correspondent Bryan Llenas, reporting live from New York. Bryan, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Trace, good afternoon. The Senate race in Pennsylvania has focused on crime, abortion, candidate personality and health despite the fact the economy and inflation are the top concerns for voters, according to the polls. Now, a more central focus on the economy could be coming though, as Democrat Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  attacks Republican, Dr. Mehmet Oz as being out of touch with working people, and Oz argues inflation would be worse with Democrats like Fetterman in office.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R-PA), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: Fetterman as advocated for even more spending, even more of the reckless spending of our children's money than \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  administration has accomplished.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: Does anybody else other than me with the irony of a man worth hundreds of millions of dollars with 10 gigantic mansions, thinks that it's OK for a person work for $7.25 an hour.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Now, according to \uc2 Monmouth University\uc2  polling, Fetterman is trusted more than Oz by Pennsylvania voters on every issue, including the economy.\par \par \uc2 This stands out, given that Republican candidates are favored on the economy and inflation nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Now, part of this could be likability. Polling shows the majority of voters have an unfavorable view of Dr. Oz. Expect both candidates to hone in on their economic message in the homestretch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BERWOOD YOST, FRANKLIN AND MARSHALL COLLEGE: What you've seen \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  do is try to say, I'm running against Washington. They created these problems, and my policies will help them. If your eyes and that same Senate race, I think you just blame the president and his party.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: As you mentioned, Trace, according to Fox News polling, Oz and Fetterman are in a tossup, their one and only debate is next Tuesday, October 25th. Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Bryan Llenas live for us in New York. Bryan, thank you. Let's bring in Mike Allen, Axios, co-founder and executive editor. Mike, always great to see you.\par \par \uc2 MIKE ALLEN, CO-FOUNDER, AXIOS: Thanks, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I want to put up these numbers from the AP-NORC poll, because they really are kind of surprising. You know, this is, which party do you trust to do a better job in handling the economy? And you see it right there. Republicans by 10 points.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's significant, because you would think that would kind of play into all these races. And when it comes to the Senate race, as Bryan Llenas was just telling us here, it appears that they're putting character higher, maybe in some cases than the economy, right?\par \par \uc2 Because, you know, Mike, you get this sense that he is getting better grades on the economy, when you know, really its character, they just liked him better. Is that a fair assessment?\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Yes, Trace, it was a very smart report. And the way that I would say it is identity, trust, a different way of saying character, which has become a breakout issue there.\par \par \uc2 So, just to zoom out for a second, Pennsylvania, one of the four true tossup races along with Nevada, where you want on your microphone, Trace, and of course, Wisconsin, and Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: And if you look at the topics in the race, if you look at what is being talked about, you would say Dr. Oz was pulling away, right? Because what's being talked about, inflation, Fetterman's health, crime, like this should all be helping Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 But, what Fetterman has done is very smartly honed-in on this identity issue. He's saying that Dr. Oz is from out of state.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: He is a weirdo. He is an outsider. Whereas, Fetterman painting himself as a legit son of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Worked hard to build a family there, and someone who will work for the people. And the drop-the-mic moment, the -- P.S., the resistance, when he accused Dr. Oz of being Cowboys fan.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: -- when they were playing the \uc2 Philadelphia Eagles\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, and listen, and I get the whole thing where, you know, you build your reputation on trust and so forth. But it's also fair to ask if voters care about \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  stroke.\par \par \uc2 The Atlantic wrote the following here, Mike. I'm going to read it to you and get your take. "Fetterman's neuropsychological or aphasia test results might suggest that he is more impaired than he seems. Or maybe those records show a complicated picture. One that would be easily misinterpreted by laypeople or intentionally misconstrued by political opponents.\par \par \uc2 Either way, keeping those records a secret isn't a great look for a candidate who has suffered a serious health setback on the campaign trail."\par \par \uc2 We haven't gotten any campaign health records from Fetterman since June 3rd. And it's important, and you talk about character, and you talk about trust, I think that plays into this. What do you think, Mike?\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Well, anytime that candidate is not releasing something, and it's going to give some voters pause early voters. But, Trace, people are going to see for themselves in that debate that you just teased. And people are going to believe their own lying eyes, are going to believe their eyes what they see.\par \par \uc2 And so, like more than in most races, that's going to could really change perceptions. Because it's, it's very rare that, that close the game day, you see them going out.\par \par \uc2 And of course, early voting, voting began. And in Georgia, we saw Senator Warnock vote today. So, the midterms have already started.\par \par \uc2 And Trace, a quick fun fact for your viewers, Pence, on the night of midterms, I would say, Pennsylvania, Pennsylvania, Pennsylvania. You tell me who's going to win Pennsylvania? I'll tell you who will control the Senate, there is an asterisk to that, and we very well may not know Georgia that night that could well be a runoff.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Right.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: And whether or not Republicans are ahead will be a massive -- will be a massive game changer in the momentum for that runoff, because you have to turn out all your people one more time.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And I think you're right, Mike. I mean, you're right. You're -- in Pennsylvania, everybody is going to be looking in Pennsylvania, and especially that debate, you know, it's kind of questionable whether debates are really working across the country, having an impact? This one stands to.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Boy, and I have to jump in, one of the reasons that they're not working is so few candidates are doing them. It's a real rarity in the cycle.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Right.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: But you're right, this is going to be an outlier. This is going to be must see T.V. for anyone who cares about politics.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. Mike Allen, good of you to come on, sir. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ALLEN: Thanks for having us, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, we are also on verdict watch in Virginia in the trial brought by Special Counsel John Durham against Igor Danchenko. The top source for \uc2 Christopher Steele\uc2 's anti-Trump dossier.\par \par \uc2 But Andy McCarthy says the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is the elephant in the room, and Andy joins me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Breaking right now, jury deliberations are underway in the trial of Igor Danchenko, the primary source for the now-discredited Steele dossier.\par \par \uc2 He is facing four charges of lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  after a judge dealt a blow to Special Counsel John Durham, throwing out a fifth charge last week.\par \par \uc2 In moments, Andy McCarthy will respond. But first, to our justice correspondent David Spunt. He is live outside the federal courthouse in Alexandria, Virginia. David, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Trace. Good afternoon. Jurors began deliberating about an hour and a half ago to give you an idea of where we are. Now, not clear if anything will be finished up today as far as the verdict is concerned.\par \par \uc2 I have to tell you, Igor Danchenko's legal team didn't even put on a case, showing you possibly how confident they are about how things will turn out.\par \par \uc2 We'll see if that proved to be an effective strategy once the verdict does come in, when it comes in. As you mentioned, they are deliberating right now over those four counts of lying to an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent. Judge Anthony Trenga removed that other count last Friday over a technicality. Danchenko was the man that government says was the primary sub source for ex-British spy \uc2 Christopher Steele\uc2 , who compiled that dossier aimed at destroying then- candidate Trump.\par \par \uc2 Durham allege that Danchenko lied to an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent four times when he claimed he received a call from an unidentified male, he thought was a Russian named Sergei Millian, who had connections to Republican politics.\par \par \uc2 The government says Danchenko never received such a call. Cell phone log show no calls came in on Danchenko's account. But his defense attorney said point blank, wait a minute, you can't prove the call didn't come from an app like Signal or WhatsApp, or another encrypted service.\par \par \uc2 Special Agent Kevin Helson, a government witness, and the man whom Danchenko allegedly lied to, backed the government's case, telling jurors Danchenko was a valuable informant.\par \par \uc2 Adding, American national security is more at risk without him. He claims Danchenko helped the bureau with some 25 investigations.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Listen, Trace. I've been saying this for about a week on paper. This case is about lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . But every day in this courtroom behind me, it became clear that Special Counsel John Durham was looking at something much bigger, meaning, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He also used the term. I know you said during the tease. Andy McCarthy said the elephant in the room is the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and he's your next guest. Well, John Durham, in his closing sentences today said the elephant in the room is the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: David Spunt, live for us outside the courthouse. Back to you as the news warrants. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Andy McCarthy, former federal prosecutor and National Review contributing editor. He is also a Fox News contributor. Andy, great to see you, David Spunt just set up the question for you.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW MCCARTHY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: The elephant in the room right? The \uc2 FBI\uc2  is the elephant in the room. Is that kind of the theme of this -- of this trial?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Yes, it's almost like the trial is secondary in the sense, Trace, of being a trial of Danchenko. What's really important and this is in terms of the public interest as much as anything else, was the performance of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . And what the trial has laid bare, is the fact that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  relied on preposterous information under oath, time after time, after time going back to the FISA court.\par \par \uc2 And their own regulations as well as federal law require that information is supposed to be verified before you go to the FISA court. They didn't even bother to interview Danchenko until they had gone twice to the FISA court under oath. And then, when Danchenko told them in January 2017, that the information that they hadn't been able to corroborate to that point was bogus.\par \par \uc2 Instead, of going back to the court and correcting what they had said, they went back two more times and relied on the same information.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, and it's amazing to me, Andy, because you make a very good point. But if Igor Danchenko, walks out a free man, no conviction, you know, it appears that the left is going to say, see, we told you, but there are no consequences.\par \par \uc2 What are the consequences for the \uc2 FBI\uc2 ? Because everybody who is watching this can tell, this is a black eye for the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . But what are the consequences down the road?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Well, I think what we'll have to see in the final chapter when this gets written, Trace, is Durham's final report. You know, up until now, he is just brought these false statements cases against a few people who came up in the investigation because they provided information to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , really, in terms of public interest.\par \par \uc2 The important things are, what did the \uc2 FBI\uc2  do with the information? And really, if you want to get down to brass tacks, was the \uc2 FBI\uc2  either tacitly or expressly in collusion with the Clinton campaign in order to smear Trump as a Russian asset?\par \par \uc2 That's really what, you know, the bottom line of what this all comes down to, and I don't think we'll get the full story on that until Durham does his final report.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I keep going back, Andy to the fact that the testimony of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  offered that \uc2 Christopher Steele\uc2 , and they offered him -- are going to offer him a million bucks to verify the dossier. And you think wouldn't outside sources verified the dossier not the guy who wrote the dossier? I got 15 seconds for you.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Yes. Yes. What I'd say about that, Trace, is that, you know, if it's not corroborated and the -- you're offering to pay to get it corroborated, that's a pretty good sign that it's not verified and you shouldn't swear to it in Court.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, several times. Andy McCarthy --\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Right.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Great to see you as always. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Thanks, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, the ominous warnings from Chinese leader \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  about Taiwan as he heads toward an unprecedented third term in power. Have you heard about this? And our democracy 2022 coverage continues with Joe Concha as former President Obama lines up events in three battleground states and takes this shot at cancel culture. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: Having fun, not being a buzzkill, right? You know as --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That wasn't for the Democratic --\par \par \uc2 OBAMA\uc2 : Yes. And sometimes Democrats are, sometimes people just want to not feel as if they are walking on eggshells.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Breaking right now, Reuters is reporting that Ukraine has been struck by a new round of Russian drone attacks. Ukraine's president didn't say where they took place, but did add that some of the drones had been shut down. That follows attacks earlier in the day by so called suicide drones exploding in Ukraine's capital. The Associated Press reports some look like Iranian drones, they set buildings on fire and killed at least four people.\par \par \uc2 Just last week, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  said there was no need for more massive strikes after he took revenge for the bombing of a crucial bridge between Russia and Crimea. But he also said they still had more targets to hit.\par \par \uc2 Meantime in China President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  striking a defiant tone refusing to rule out the use of force to keep Taiwan part of China, look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 XI JINPING, PRESIDENT OF CHINA (through translation): Resolving the Taiwan question is a matter for the Chinese, a matter that must be resolved by the Chinese. We will continue to strive for peaceful reunification with the greatest sincerity and the utmost effort, but we will never promise to renounce the use of force and we reserve the option of taking all measures necessary.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: The comments came during the opening of the Communist Party Congress which is expected to reelect Xi to an unprecedented third term. Let's bring in KT McFarland, former Deputy National Security Adviser under President Trump. KT, it's always great to see you.\par \par \uc2 Very scary times for the United States. I mean, President Xi has made it very clear that he wants to go after Taiwan. And my question to you is, and it goes back seven years to when Harry Harris, the then commander of the Pacific Fleet, said China has worry number 1. And these militarized islands in the South China Sea are a very big concern. And my question is, do we have the robust ability to do something if she goes after Taiwan?\par \par \uc2 KT MCFARLAND, FORMER TRUMP DEPUTY NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: If he goes after it militarily, no. I mean, at the Pentagon is war game to potential US-China conflict over Taiwan. We don't win that conflict. So number one, if it is number -- issue number 1, national security threat number 1, 2, 3 and 4, we really need to improve our deterrent capability in that part of the world.\par \par \uc2 But the interesting thing is what \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  said this weekend, we know that it's a core interest of theirs when I sat down with the Chinese leaders at the beginning of the Trump administration and during the transition, they said Taiwan, that's a core interests of China. In other words, no compromise, no discussion, we are going to take over Taiwan at some point. But it was always vague, you know, when would it happen, how would it happen, those are details they never got, they never got into. So it was a threat but it wasn't an implicit and immediate threat.\par \par \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  is now talking about it being more of an immediate threat, that they're going to take Taiwan and the clock is ticking. And it's going to be during his time in office, \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 's time in office. And that's that. Now, why are they doing that? They want it for a lot of reasons. But not only the sort of national pride reason, but it's also a strategic and military advantage.\par \par \uc2 The thing that you talked about with those island chains, if the Chinese control Taiwan, they control the Western Pacific. They control all trade and the world's most important maritime trade route.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And during that same speech, as you mentioned, KT, he said national security, President Xi said national security 18 times. It sounds like, you know, there's a push, a big push to modernize the Chinese military and that also is a very bad sign for peaceful US international relations.\par \par \uc2 MCFARLAND: Yes. And it couldn't come at a sort of scarier time. I mean, what's happening in Europe, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has just crossed over a line, right? He's now targeting civilian sites in Ukraine, clearly committing war crimes, but he's escalating that war. Meanwhile, in the Middle East, we've got Iran chanting death to America, and then providing Russia with weapons. And in the middle of all of this, there's Taiwan saying, well, you know, don't forget about us, while China say we're not forgetting about you. We want to actually bring you into the Chinese under control of Beijing. So there are a lot of uncertainties.\par \par \uc2 And then when you throw in the American political environment, and our upcoming elections, it becomes a very volatile time. Because any one thing could go wrong, I don't think there's a high probability of any one of these things happening escalating. But taking it all together, you know, it's not the odds I'm worried about, it's the stakes.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. We got about 30 seconds left and I'm wondering what you think about this unprecedented third term. I mean, we have these zero COVID policies and the people -- I mean, it is becoming more authoritarian by the day, KT?\par \par \uc2 MCFARLAND: Right, yes. \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  is now deciding. He's declaring that he's more important than \uc2 Mao Zedong\uc2 , founder of Communist China. So \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  has now under his control, the economy, the military, all foreign policy and national security and all social and domestic issues. He is the supreme leader of China.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. KT McFarland, always great to see you. Thank you for coming on.\par \par \uc2 MCFARLAND: Thanks, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And breaking right now in the Georgia Senate race, Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  out with new comments about allegations that he paid for an abortion. Joe Concha joins me on that next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: White the debate schedule is heating up with 22 days to go until the midterms, tonight we'll see two main events as Ohio Senate candidates JD Vance and Tim Ryan are set for their second debate of the campaign. And in Georgia Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  and Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  will get the chance to make a closing argument to voters on the first day of early voting.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Joe Concha and Jose Aristimuno, they're both standing by, but first to correspondent Charles Watson. He's lived for us in Atlanta. Charles, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES WATSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey good afternoon, Trace. Georgia Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and his Republican challenger \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  squared off in the debate of their own over the weekend. And while walker has been applauded by some on both sides for his performance in that debate, it hasn't been quite enough to scave off some of the controversies he's had in the past.\par \par \uc2 In fact, we heard from Walker this morning on NBC's Today Show where he pushed back on some of the allegations that have dogged him in the media for weeks. He denied claims that he paid for an ex-girlfriend's abortion. But for the first time, Walker admitted the check reportedly used to pay for the procedure was in fact signed by him. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS: Do you know what this $700 check is for?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: I have no idea what that can be for.\par \par \uc2 WELKER: Is that your signature?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Oh, it could be. It doesn't matter whether it's my signature or not.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: When pressed, Walker said definitively for the first time that check is his.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes, that's my chair.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATSON: Yet, this of course follows -- that exchange, of course, follows a debate performance from Walker on Friday, that has been roundly applauded by Republicans and some on the left. He declined to show up to its second debate on Sunday, where incumbent Warnock, where incumbent Warnock used Walker's absence to hit him on everything from abortion, to claims Walker lied about once being a sheriff's deputy.\par \par \uc2 Walker also pushed back on reports that his church moved to evict tenants from an apartment building during the height of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): This is one more example of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and his allies lie. First of all, there were no evictions and the Atlanta Journal Constitution reported that. And my church has no direct involvement in the day to day operations of that apartment building.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATSON: Yes. And, Trace, he mentioned it the other big race here in Georgia, the gubernatorial race with a Republican Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , going against Democratic challenger \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , those two are expected to debate tonight, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And we will talk about both of those. Charles Watson live for us in Atlanta. Charles, thank you. Let's bring in Joe Concha, media and politics columnist for the Hill and a Fox News contributor, and Jose Aristimuno, former Deputy Press Secretary for the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2 . Gentlemen, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Every poll, Joe Concha, to you first, has Republicans more trusted on the economy. I want to listen to this Georgia voter and then we'll go from there. Watch.\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: OK.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MCCRAY, GEORGIA DEMOCRATIC VOTER: I think people really care more about like the kitchen table issues, so they care more about the economy. There's been discussions in my circles about abortion, but I don't think it is the thing that is going to motivate most people. And it's not going to affect most people's lives. I think we need to be realistic about that. Like not everybody's having an abortion but everybody has a bill to pay at hand in mind.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Although when you put up this Quinnipiac poll, Joe Concha, you see here that \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  leading \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  by seven points. That's outside the margin of error. What's going on over there?\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Yes. Georgia is a struggle for the Republican and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  right now, some seen as a as a weaker candidate than in other states. And obviously Georgia is a very key race, Ohio is a very key race, Arizona is a very key race. It seems that Georgia and Arizona are the new battleground states when that used to be Florida and Ohio.\par \par \uc2 A new \uc2 CBS\uc2  battleground poll, Trace, just came out and I'm going through the cross tabs and on individual questions. And this is why Democrats aren't going to sleep well over the next 22 nights leading up to Election Day. I'll give you three quick examples. 63% of voters in key states, see and feel gas prices going up. Seven in 10 voters say the Biden administration is not doing enough to address inflation, 71% say Biden bears some or a great deal of responsibility for the current economy.\par \par \uc2 But this is the biggest takeaway. By double digits, Democrats are still losing Independents who report their personal financial situation is bad, and those for whom high prices have made their lives worse and more difficult. Elections come down to Independents. If Democrats can't win them in these close states, battleground states, Republicans are going to have very good night, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: He makes a very good point, Jose, because now these polls, we're just kind of getting him right. In the polls, the gas prices we've been talking about over the past couple of weeks. The polls are just starting to catch up when you see those big signs with gas prices at $5, $6 and $7 in some states, just now starting to catch up. So the polls that we're getting now are very important, and they don't show well for Democrats. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 JOSE ARISTIMUNO, FORMER DNC DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: Sure. Look, I think Joe has got a good point. First and foremost, let me just begin by saying that historically, we know that the party in power loses seats. So we're going to -- Democrats are going to lose seats in both the House and maybe in the Senate. But the question is by how much, right?\par \par \uc2 Republicans have to win two out of three key races, Nevada, Pennsylvania and Georgia. We're talking about Georgia. Now, it doesn't look too good for Mr. Walker that he's going to win that election. And if they don't win that, then Republicans got to win. Again, Nevada or Pennsylvania to be able to get a majority in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 So I think Democrats have a very good chance in keeping the Senate as it is, and then we're going to lose some Houses in the -- and some seats in the House. And to your question about polls, so polls come up and down. We know in 2016 every single poll in America said that \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  was going to be president of the United States, and she lost. So I think we got to take a grain of salt when it comes to looking at this polls as well.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. So want to get both of your takes on the whole \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 -\uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  race. I want to put this up on the screen. This is, I mean, it's tight. Look, it's 50/49 with the governor holding the lead there. You know, you got to go, the debate is tonight. You know, \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  still has not said this race. She lost (inaudible), right.\par \par \uc2 But when she, Joe, it's fascinating when the Democrats don't concede a race it is, you know, it is fighting for justice when the Republicans don't, it is an assault on democracy. What do you think about this race?\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Well, correct. And then \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , obviously, has a big challenge in Georgia because she lost her number one issue and that is voting rights. Because we saw in the primaries voting was higher than it ever had been before. And plus, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is polling in approval in something like the high 30s in Georgia, so that drags down her ticket. I think in the end, it comes down to the winning bumper sticker, Trace, the elevator pitch.\par \par \uc2 If you want lower inflation for smaller government and less spending, vote Republican. If you believe in us energy independence, vote Republican. If you want violent criminals to stay behind bars, vote Republican. If you believe the US southern border should be secure, vote Republican.\par \par \uc2 I'm not sure what the Democratic bumper sticker is. I'm sure Jose can probably fill in the blank there. But outside of abortion, I don't see what they run on in terms of a positive message particularly as it pertains to the economy.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Jose, give us a bumper sticker. I got 30 seconds left for you.\par \par \uc2 ARISTIMUNO: Results getting things done. This President has done more legislation, has passed more things than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did in four years, and this guy has done it in a year and a half. So I think we're going to be just fine coming November.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. Well, yes. But inflation is still high and the stock market is still low, and credit card debt is rising. Joe Concha, Jose Aristimuno, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Well, these were the scenes in 2020 as Minneapolis became the center of the Defund the Police Movement. But wait until you see the wild video of what happened there this weekend next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Mayhem on the streets of Minneapolis after a brawl broke out. This video taken by independent photo journalist Rebecca Brannon, it shows a violent scene outside of bar. A group of people attacking a man on the ground, punching him multiple times, even kicking him in the stomach. Crowds then harass police officers on horseback as they try to break up the chaos, look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Joining me now, Will Cain, co-host of "Fox and Friends Weekend" and host of the "Will Cain Podcast" Will, always great to have you on. We didn't show it but that video also, by the way included assaults inside the bar, assaults outside the bar, women twerking. We talked about the police being harassed on horseback. I mean, they're just zero respect for the police officers in and around that area. Your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 WILL CAIN, FOX NEWS HOST: And don't forget cursing out the horse. No one was saved from the target of assault and verbal abuse on this night in Minneapolis, which is sadly indicative of the course of Minneapolis over the last two years. If you talk to residents that's exactly what you will hear. It's been reflected in, for example, vehicle theft in terms of burglary. Overall crime rate in Minneapolis, up over 16%.\par \par \uc2 And I like, you know, there shouldn't be no neighborhood. Look, Trace, I don't I don't care where you live in America, there should be no neighborhood, there should be no community that has to live through chaos, and a culture of criminality. Everyone should be able to expect some level of safety in their life.\par \par \uc2 And there are those that look at it and say, well, it's 2:00 am. And it's after a bar closing. Let me tell you how this affects everyone's life, including the people in that street that are living through a volatile situation. In addition to crime being up, how about this, 911 response times in Minneapolis since the riots after the death of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  have gone from a 10 minute response time to a 17 minute response time.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: What can happen? Who can die? What kind of crimes can take place in 17, or seven extra minutes in Minneapolis?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And I want to put these numbers on the screen because as you say, nobody thought it could get worse than 2021. And look at 202, up 19.3%. That's not a bump well, 19.3% is a problem. It is a massive problem. Your final thoughts?\par \par \uc2 CAIN: It's the epicenter. And, look, the reason you pulled out Minneapolis is yes, it's in particularly bad it's in particularly a problem as you just put it, Trace, but it's also symbolic of the movement in this country over a two year timeframe. The epicenter of the defund the police movement, the place where they literally, literally chased police out of the police station and had them abandoned their posts. And then said about defunding them and discouraging anyone from going into law enforcement, here you see the fruits, the chaos, the stats, and the decreased quality of life for anybody that lives in Minneapolis.\par \par \uc2 And everyone watching right now that lives in other communities knows it's not just a problem in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I mean, who would go into law enforcement? Well, I mean, it's like going into oil refinery, who wants to be a refiner when you have states who want to get rid of gasoline. Who wants to be a cop when you have states that want to get rid of police? This could be the deadliest year in the history of this country for police officers. We still got 2.5 months to go and it could be the deadliest year. I've got about 20 seconds left for you, Will.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Well, the answer to your question directly, Trace, is that you're not going to want to find out who wants to go into law enforcement when you demoralize them, pay them less, put them in situations of danger, you will have less of a plea -- you'll have either a very, very brave and dedicated and principled commitment to this job, or you'll have people that you really don't want policing.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. That's exactly right. I mean, you know, you talk to some of these police agencies across the country and they say the biggest problem with policing is that they have lost respect. And when you lose respect you no longer have the power you need on the job. Will Cain, always great to see you, my friend. Thank you for coming on.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Thank you, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: All right. Well that is THE STORY of Monday, October 17th. But as always, THE STORY goes on. Be sure to join me for America's Late News "FOX NEWS @ NIGHT" 12:00 Eastern, 9:00 Pacific.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020221018eiah00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Biden Blames Others for Oil Prices; Democrats Shift Blame on Inflation; House Committee Votes to Subpoena Trump; Georgia Senate Race; Inside the CIA Museum; Interview with White House Economic Adviser Jared Bernstein; Interview with House Minority Whip Steve Scalise (R-LA)\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Mollie Hemingway, Richard Fowler, Jennifer Griffin \par \uc2 10,380 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Just 23 days from now, Americans will tell us who they want to run Washington.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We are better positioned than any other major economy in the world.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: President Biden on a West Coast swing to tout his work on the economy despite a series of negative reports this week, and has this message for voters.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: If Republican wins, inflation is going to get worse. It's that simple.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: While brand new FOX News polls show Americans are feeling the squeeze of inflation.\par \par \uc2 We'll ask White House economic adviser Jared Bernstein about the administration's plans to combat rising prices.\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ELISE STEFANIK\uc2  (R-NY): The American people are looking for a new direction and House Republicans have a plan.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Congressional Republicans say they are bullish on their chances to take the House in November.\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE (R-LA): We're going to fire \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But are they succeeding in making their case to voters?\par \par \uc2 We'll show you what our polls say.\par \par \uc2 And we'll speak live with the number two Republican in the House, Congressman Steve Scalise, about his party's plans should they take the majority.\par \par \uc2 Then --\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BENNIE THOMPSON\uc2  (D-MS): This investigation is not about politics.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The committee investigating the January 6th riot at the Capitol votes to subpoena former President Trump, just ahead of the midterms.\par \par \uc2 We'll ask our Sunday panel about the 11th hour move and whether Trump would comply.\par \par \uc2 Then --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: These artifacts have actually never been on display before here at CIA Museum.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: One of the most secretive buildings in the country opens up and our Jennifer Griffin takes you there to tour its treasures.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): And hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Voters are weeks away from speaking their minds in the 2022 midterms. Top lines from our brand new FOX poll show presidential approval will be critical for Democrats. And right now, it stands at 46 percent. That's actually up 6 points from June.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats still face serious headwinds. Check it out, 79 percent are extremely or very concerned about crime, while fewer, 71 percent say the same about abortion policy. But a whopping 89 percent say they're extremely or very concerned about inflation. In a moment, we will speak live with Jared Bernstein of the \uc2 White House Council of Economic Advisers\uc2  about the crushing new inflation numbers out just this week.\par \par \uc2 But, first, a look at how these issues are animating the country's biggest races, starting with abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TUDOR DIXON (R), MICHIGAN GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: The governor doesn't have the choice to go around a judge or a constitutional amendment.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D), MICHIGAN: A governor will absolutely impact these rights.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): The debate over a Michigan ballot measure that would enshrine the right to abortion is taking a center stage in this week's gubernatorial debates. Democrats pin their hopes on angry voters coming out in droves after the end of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 But our brand new FOX polls show only 50 percent of registered voters say Democrats handle the issues better. And there are other measures issues dogging candidates like crime.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R), PENNSYLVANIA SENTE CANDIDATE: It seems to a lot of voters inside Philadelphia that the criminals are being valued more than the innocent.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), PENNSYLVANIA LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR: I'm actually effective on crime and I believe in second chances. And I've run on that record.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: As the Pennsylvania Senate race tightens, voters who say crime is their top concern favor Republicans, 52-34 percent. And polls show the economy remains a major concern, putting Democratic Senate candidates like Ohio's \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  on defense over how President Biden is handling inflation.\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH): The problem we're having now with inflation is our supply chains went to China. And guys like him made a lot of money off that.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R), OHIO SENATE CANDIDATE: China's problems are exacerbated by our own leadership's failures. Those failures go back 20 years to when \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  started in Congress.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Joining us now, \uc2 White House Council of Economic Advisers\uc2  member, Jared Bernstein.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, WHITE HOUSE COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: Thanks for inviting me. Great to see you again.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You, too.\par \par \uc2 OK. Let's start here. The headline in "The Wall Street Journal" this morning says: economists are putting the probability of a recession in the next 12 months at 63 percent. That's up from 49 percent in July's survey. Soft landing will likely remain a mythical outcome.\par \par \uc2 Over at "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ", they say it's not going well. Underlying inflation trends are headed in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 Now, September numbers were worse than forecast. August and September helped a little by gas prices going down.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They seem to be moving in the wrong direction right now.\par \par \uc2 Is it time to admit the massive federal spending under this administration is not only not helping Americans, it may be hurting them?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Let's start right there, Shannon. I think it's a really important to get this number $1.4 trillion into the discussion right now. That's the amount of deficit reduction in fiscal year '22, okay? That comes on top of about $350 billion in deficit reduction in the year before that. So, this --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: As we talked about, that comes off of massive COVID spending.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Well, actually, part of it is unwinding of COVID spending, you're right about that.\par \par \uc2 But in percent terms, the bigger contributor to that decline in deficit spending is receipts, receipts coming into the Treasury, and that's because of how strong the economy has been.\par \par \uc2 And that also gets to the question you just raised and this issue of recession. We have an unemployment rate that's 3.5 percent, okay? There is no recession that would prevail with that kind of unemployment rate. We're obviously adding hundreds of thousands of jobs per month.\par \par \uc2 We have consumers who still have pretty strong balance sheets. We have job vacancies that are quite elevated. So, there's a lot of talent in the economy, a lot of strength.\par \par \uc2 And when President Biden just said on the tape you played that the U.S. is uniquely positioned to do two things, to ease the price pressures we face which are very real, you correctly referenced them right out of the gate. We called them like they are, squeezing family budgets, while maintaining these economic gains.\par \par \uc2 That's our mission. We believe we can accomplish that. We actually think the probability for a soft landing is good.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. In that "Wall Street Journal" piece, they also talk about predictions for Q1, Q2 next year to also be negative territory. Would you concede then? Because a lot of people do use the definition two bad quarters, negative action, equals recession. You're saying we're not there right now.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Correct.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But if we went through continued negative quarters through the first half of '23, would you then admit we're in a recession?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: First of all, it is not up to the White House to call recession. That's called by an economic group of --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But you, Jared Bernstein.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yeah, what I -- I would look at the following variables, happens to be the same ones that the group I was just talking about -- retail sales, payroll employment, consumer spending, industrial production, all of those have not been flashing red. All of those are not in recessionary territory.\par \par \uc2 Now, if you ask me to look around the corner and say where the economy is going to be, I got to tell you that everybody's crystal ball is a bit cracked right now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's -- yes.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: The uncertainty out there makes it tough.\par \par \uc2 But let's talk about energy which is so key here, okay? You just mentioned it yourself. In this consumer price report, where prices were, no question, elevated to a point that family budgets are squeezed, gas was down 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 And, in fact, the price of gas fell about a third in the third quarter of this year. Now, remember, it peaked out north of $5 a gallon, last seen, it was $3.89. I know because I checked this morning.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So did I.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: And there are 14 states with price of gas at $3.50 or below.\par \par \uc2 So, that's some progress and it relates to President Biden's release of oil from the Strategic Reserves.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. How long can we do that?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Good question.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: If we're doing millions of barrels at a time, there is a bottom to that.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So I knew you'd ask me that because I know you're very interested in the Strategic Reserve and its capacity.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: As all Americans should be.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yeah, as all -- on Sunday morning, what else are we thinking about than SPR capacity?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Basically.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: OK. There is -- there are still 400 million barrels of oil in the Strategic Reserve. It is more than half full.\par \par \uc2 I think people don't quite have the capacity number in their head. We've never done a draw that's of that magnitude. And the largest draw that we've ever done that President Biden presided over in March is 180 million barrels.\par \par \uc2 So, the fact is there is capacity to use the SPR to deal with some of the energy shocks we're seeing in the world. But I'm not saying we will. That's up to the president to decide, he hasn't made that decision yet.\par \par \uc2 But if you look at unconscionable action of Putin, weaponizing energy, amplified by the short-sighted and misguided decision by OPEC Plus recently, then sure, having the SPR there for potential release is an important --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: -- important tool.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And when those prices move in the right direction, that is a good thing, to ripple to the other issues that show up in the CPI.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But let's talk about this. When President Biden took office, you know, inflation was about 3 percent. Gas prices were about $2.39 I think a gallon. So, we can't possibly say that things have gotten better or anywhere near where they were two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about the realities for folks. Groceries up 13 percent last month -- year to year eggs more than 30 percent, milk more than 15 percent.\par \par \uc2 Our new polling shows that 71 percent of Americans say they are having to cut back just to afford necessities, not anything fun or luxurious.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And also, they say that compared to the situation two years ago, they are worse off, majority of them. So, what do you say to those numbers though? This has happened in your watch.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yeah. So, here's -- let me speak to them directly, which is all those facts are facts of which we are acutely aware.\par \par \uc2 And when President Biden came out to talk about this last inflation where he started by talking about exactly that, the squeeze on family budgets. And I think you have to ask yourself, is this administration doing what we need to do to help ease those pressures?\par \par \uc2 Now, remember, these are global price pressures. All of the statistics you just cited -- they're actually worse in Europe and the UK. Inflation is higher there in part because they're --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Not necessarily.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Well, they're more --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Not in France, for example.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: OK. But in the E.U., in general, I know inflation is double digits there, and the U.K. as well. And the reason is because they're more exposed to energy. And we just went through some of the energy numbers.\par \par \uc2 But I think what you have to ask yourself, Shannon, more importantly what people that you're talking to have to ask, do we have the policy set and is the Fed doing what it needs to do to ease these price pressures.\par \par \uc2 Now, you know the Inflation Reduction Act passed. This is a -- this is a measure that lowers prescription drug costs, that lowers the cost of insulin, that lowers the cost of medical care. There are 13 million families who'll be paying $800 less per year. If you are paying $10,000, if you're someone on Medicare, on your prescription drugs, you're going to save $8,000 under the IRA.\par \par \uc2 So, these measures are in effect and Republicans have said they want to take them away. So, if you are paying hundreds of dollars on insulin per month and now you get to pay $35 starting in January, they want to take that away. That to me is a recipe for more inflation, higher costs, not easing the pressures that you just took us through.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. I will ask Steve Scalise, the congressman is going to be with us in just minutes, we'll ask him about that.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, let's talk mortgages because we talked about this a little bit before we got started. Let's use the example of a median home price, about $390,000, 20 percent down, back when insurance -- or excuse me, inflation rate was about 3.05 percent, that would have been $1,000 -- $1,324 a month, okay?\par \par \uc2 So, let's fast forward that to now because mortgage rates have more than doubled this year alone. So, if you took that same house at 6.92 mortgage, $2,000 a month, it's $735 more each month. That's thousands and thousands of dollars for an average family if they want to get into a home price now.\par \par \uc2 The Fed is expected to hike rates November, December. We'll see how that far goes on. But the Fed chair has actually said there will be some pain, those are his words, with these hikes.\par \par \uc2 How painful is it going to get?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Well, I think you have to distinguish between people who are buying homes now and the vast majority of mortgage holders who locked in rates that are much lower than that.\par \par \uc2 You're definitely right that, you know, at that margin, the price of financing a mortgage, of course, goes up with the interest rate and with the mortgage rate. But again I think this gets to the question of are we implementing -- by we, I mean not just the federal government, but in this case the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  as well -- are we implementing the necessary policies to ease inflationary pressures?\par \par \uc2 I would say the answer is unequivocally yes. Now, we're not going to get into the Feds knitting, because we respect an independent Federal Reserve. But the president has very much approved of rate hiking campaign because they're the first and foremost inflation fighter.\par \par \uc2 Now, one of the first sectors that experiences the impact of rate hikes is housing and the housing sector is cooling. Now, that doesn't find its way into the price index for a while. It comes in with a lag.\par \par \uc2 But if you look at rents, rents have come down significantly. They're still highly elevated in terms of year over year change. But they come down 4, 5 percentage points. That's going to take a while to feed into the CPI, but it is moving the right direction.\par \par \uc2 And so, that's what I'm trying to get across here. That whether it's Inflation Reduction Act, whether it's our work in ports, whether it is our work with energy and the release of oil from reserves, we are doing all we can to ease inflationary pressures and we see results.\par \par \uc2 It's going to take time for those results to get into the price. We would like to see that happen a whole lot faster. We're going to try to work to make that happen.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah --\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: But we are engaged in the appropriate policies (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, let me put up these poll numbers then because the American people are confused about that. They don't think your policies are working.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to how the president is doing on the economy and the inflation, he is upside down by a wide margin in our latest polling.\par \par \uc2 So, why aren't they getting the message? I mean, clearly, people don't feel like this is working for them yet at home.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: I think because so much what's going on inflation gets just down to the trip to the supermarket --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: -- and the trip to the gas station.\par \par \uc2 I think if you ask people how they feel about paying over $5 of gas in mid June, and well under $4 now, I think it's about $3.89 nationally, $3.50 in 14 states, including Florida, North Carolina, and Texas. So, some significant population there, they tell you they feel pretty good about that.\par \par \uc2 And I think it's unequivocal that the president's fingerprints are on that decline in part because of the release of oil from the reserves. We're doing what we can in the food space.\par \par \uc2 I think that another reason -- another answer to your question, Shannon, is that some of what happens is getting back to normal, which consumers don't always recognize. So shelves are now stocked basically as they were pre- pandemic. People don't see containers going in and out of ports, but, in fact, the through put of containers getting through ports is back to where it was. In fact, it's ahead of where it was pre-pandemic.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But back to normal, like $2.39 for gas, are we going to get back to that normal?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: I'm sorry?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Two thirty-nine a gallon --\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yeah, I don't --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- when the president took office?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Certainly don't -- it's very hard to look around the corner and see where gas prices are going that far ahead. As I noted, they've come down significantly.\par \par \uc2 Most recently, they started ticking back down again, just a few cents in the past few days, and it has to do with refineries coming on line in the Midwest and in California.\par \par \uc2 I think the key story there is that is this administration doing as much as we can to help increase the energy supply? And I think the answer is unequivocally yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, I don't know, because a lot of folks would say if you're not -- you know, helping with permitting and drilling, and encouraging domestic production, you're not doing everything.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: So, let me read you a headline I just saw. This is from "\uc2 The Sierra Club\uc2 " the other day, just article that just came out.\par \par \uc2 For all of its good, the new climate legislation also mandates oil and gas drilling on public land.\par \par \uc2 So, that's "\uc2 The Sierra Club\uc2 " complaining about aspects of the Inflation Reduction Act that open up new lands for leasing. Now, the Inflation Reduction Act also invests $370 billion in hastening this very urgent transition to clean energy.\par \par \uc2 So this is a walk and chew gum moment. I don't think that anyone can, based on the facts, accuse this administration of not fulfilling both of those obligations right now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, a lot of refiners say they're having trouble managing the threat they took from the administration during the campaign that we're going to get rid of fossil fuel, toiling (ph) up for green and now trying to toil back for crude.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Wait a second, Shannon. We have American oil producers about to produce next year a record number of barrels per day. So we have a highly productive system in that regard. We're the largest oil producer in the land, in natural gas where -- I'm sorry, in the world. Natural gas, we're also way far ahead of competitors in that regard.\par \par \uc2 So I think whether it's permitting or whether it's American oil production, that's just a red herring. We're doing a lot there. We're producing a lot of energy.\par \par \uc2 I think the key thing that we need to learn from this lesson, and it's a geopolitical lesson, is that we need to do both. We need to make sure we're helping consumers at the pump today while we urgently transition to clean energy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Jared Bernstein, it is always great to have you in with us for discussion on Sunday. Thank you for coming in.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: My pleasure. Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Up next, we're going to get reaction from the second ranking Republican in the House and we'll get those brand new FOX News polls know about the GOP agenda or don't know. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: House Republicans making a big push in recent weeks to sell voters on their post-midterm plans. They call it "Commitment to America". But is it catching on?\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, House Republican Whip Steve Scalise of Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE (R-LA): Good morning, Shannon. Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Let's start here. Democrats are pointing to your own documents to tell Americans that you are preparing to cut entitlements.\par \par \uc2 Here's what the headline is: House GOP decides slashing is the new saving. This year's Republican Study Committee's fiscal year 2023 budget openly calls for slashing and privatizing Social Security, raising the retirement age to 70, and ending Medicare as we know it.\par \par \uc2 Now, I got a tweet from a viewer this week who says, he's a Republican, he's very worried about you cutting his Medicare. He says it's a deal breaker and betrayal by the GOP.\par \par \uc2 So, what's your answer?\par \par \uc2 SCALISE: Well, the answer is that's a typical red herring by Democrats and it's not something we proposed. In fact, we proposed strengthening, and shoring up Medicare and Social Security, which are both, by the way, headed for bankruptcy if we do nothing.\par \par \uc2 Democrats want to make that worse. Democrats actually recently passed a bill to raid money out of Social Security.\par \par \uc2 So, instead of making those programs less stable, what we want to do is shore them up.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, one of the ways you shore them up is get more people back to work paying into those programs right now. And one of the things Democrats did on day one when they came in taking over the House, Senate and the White House, is to start paying people not to work, to see more of the welfare programs where you used to have work requirements in place.\par \par \uc2 So you would have a real safety net which we believe in. But why should we be paying people to sit at home when there are companies everywhere looking for workers?\par \par \uc2 Everywhere you go, you go to a restaurant, you're waiting an hour for a table, and you're seeing a third of them empty because the government started paying people not to work. That drains programs like Social Security and Medicare.\par \par \uc2 So, let's strengthen those programs, and stop them from going bankrupt. And let's also, by the way, stop the government getting in this business of paying people not to work when everybody is looking for workers right now.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But fair to say that pointing to your own documents, there are some changes to these programs that would happen.\par \par \uc2 SCALISE: There's not anything that we propose in Commitment to America. In fact, the Commitment to America, we talk specifically about --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: In the fiscal year that the Democrats are referring to.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SCALISE: No, in the Commitment to America, we talk -- well, they didn't do a budget this year. You know, if you look at what they've done, they pass bills to increase taxes.\par \par \uc2 I know your previous guest, Mr. Bernstein, talked about a bill they passed to raise over $730 billion in new taxes, and, by the way, more than double the size of the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , sending 87,000 agents after hardworking families, and, in fact, even the Joint Committee on Taxation confirmed they're going after people making less than $200,000 a year, which is a violation of the White House's own promise.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, but --\par \par \uc2 SCALISE: President Biden said he wouldn't do that.\par \par \uc2 We're shoring up -- we want to shore up Social Security and Medicare.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Just to be clear, the Republican Study Committee's budget. So, that's something you signed on to, that's what Democrats are pointing to to say there will be changes to these programs, if -- if your budget passed.\par \par \uc2 SCALISE: Well, first of all, that -- yeah, that budget talks about shoring up and strengthening Social Security. That's not cutting Social Security. That's making sure for people that are on Social Security today, if nothing happens, there would be automatic cuts in law.\par \par \uc2 We don't want that to happen. And so, we've brought forward legislation to stave off cuts to Medicare. We want to stave off cuts to Social Security.\par \par \uc2 Democrats haven't supported any of that. They want the programs to go bankrupt. That's not a good thing.\par \par \uc2 We don't want Medicare and Social Security to go bust like the Democrats right now have us on a track to do.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Let's talk about some polling. These are some interesting new numbers just out this morning, where we asked people about who they prefer as congressional candidate. There is a three point advantage there to Democrats.\par \par \uc2 And when asked about how they felt about the House Republicans' Commitment to America, majority of them said they were totally unfamiliar with the program.\par \par \uc2 Do Republicans have a messaging problem in the midterms?\par \par \uc2 SCALISE: In fact, we just rolled this out. And I've been to a lot of districts in the last few weeks since we rolled it out in Pittsburgh, and I tell you what, people are responding very positive to it.\par \par \uc2 Number one, we've got phenomenal candidates, and we're going to win races in a lot of places you haven't seen. I was in the Northwest, Washington state, Oregon, where we're going to flip seats.\par \par \uc2 I was a few weeks ago in the Northeast, up in Maine, all the way down to Florida where on the Eastern Seaboard, we're going to be flipping a lot of seats from Democrats to Republican.\par \par \uc2 You know what they tell me, Shannon, they are furious with Biden and Pelosi's far-left socialist agenda that's led to increased spending, increased inflation, just the cost of everything you buy when you go to the grocery store, if you can afford to put gas in your car when you get there.\par \par \uc2 And look, the White House and Jared Bernstein just before me, they're talking, bragging as if gas prices are lower. Gas prices are about 60 percent higher today than when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took office, and he shut off American energy production. That's why it happened.\par \par \uc2 He's allowed \uc2 OPEC\uc2 , foreign countries. He's begged -- Biden's begged Russia, he's begged Iran, Venezuela, and other countries to produce oil when we should be producing it in America.\par \par \uc2 We do it cleaner than anywhere else. And by the way, if we produce more energy in America, we would be lowering energy costs like we had two years ago.\par \par \uc2 People are furious about all that. They're furious also about rising crime because Democrats embraced not only the defund the police movement, one of the craziest ideas I've ever heard. But now, they're going cashless bail, letting criminals on the streets after they committed violent crimes against families. And that's one of the reasons crime is out of control, and people are furious about that, too.\par \par \uc2 And so, they want a check and balance on this far left agenda.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, let's talk about the crime issue because that is one of the areas that polling shows us, the voters give Republicans a distinct advantage. They think that you are better equipped to handle that.\par \par \uc2 But critics say that you're scare-mongering about what's actually happening. They say some of these ads from Republicans on the issue of crime have racial undertones.\par \par \uc2 Here's something from "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ", an opinion piece. It says: violent crime is not soaring. In fact, it might be declining. Most violent crime is committed by White people and violent crime is generally worse in Republican-run states.\par \par \uc2 Your response?\par \par \uc2 SCALISE: Well, first of all, violent crime is out of control in most big cities. And that's what we have been focusing on. So, I guess they're admitting that their policies have failed.\par \par \uc2 But it's the Democrats who started this about two years ago when they embraced the defund the police movement.\par \par \uc2 And I think, by the way, voters on all spectrums, whether you're Republican, Democrat, Black, White, doesn't matter, you don't want to defund the police. Criminals want to defund the police, but that's who the Democrats sided with.\par \par \uc2 And when they found out the public really hated that idea, they started going to cashless bail, where you literally just have some D.A.s and prosecutors in communities letting criminals walk out on the streets right after committing violent crimes.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we saw it in New York. You know, \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , our candidate for governor, somebody tried to take his life on stage at a political rally, and the guy was let out the next day. That's what's going on that people are furious about. That's why it's a major issue in a lot of these races, and Democrats won't walk away from their defund the police and cashless bail approach that has failed so many cities across America.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You know the words they use to describe your agenda. They call it extreme MAGA agenda. This is what Speaker Pelosi says about the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 She says: 166 House Republicans, including GOP Whip Scalise, have co- sponsored a "Life Begins at Conception" bill that would criminalize all abortion after the moment of fertilization with absolutely no exceptions for rape, incest or the health of the woman.\par \par \uc2 That position does not poll well with voters. So, how do you defend it?\par \par \uc2 SCALISE: Well, I'm not a co-sponsor of that bill. The bill I'm a co- sponsor of is the Born Alive Act, which says that if a baby is born alive outside the womb, you can't murder that baby and call it abortion, which, by the way, that's legal in right now in states like New York. There are states where they still would allow you to kill the baby after it's born alive.\par \par \uc2 And I think most Americans, including people who identify as pro-choice think that's radically extreme, don't want that to be the case. Yet that's where Democrats are right now. And, by the way, they also want those abortions paid for by taxpayers, which is also an extreme position.\par \par \uc2 So, they're the ones extreme on this. They keep trying to talk about it. It's back firing on them.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, people are talking high cost of everything. They're not -- they're not looking at the Democrats' agenda, saying they want more of it. They're saying they're fed up with the far left shift that both Biden and Pelosi have taken us, not down a liberal road but down a socialist road.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 SCALISE: That's what people are rejecting.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to be --\par \par \uc2 SCALISE: That's what we're proposing an alternative to with the Commitment to America.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to be clear, though, did you sign onto as a co-sponsor of "Life Begins at Conception", that act? Did the speaker get (ph) that wrong?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SCALISE: No, I'm on the Born Alive -- I'm on the Born Alive Act.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I want to --\par \par \uc2 SCALISE: And go look up the Born Alive Act. Again, it says if a baby is born alive outside the womb, you can't murder that baby and call it abortion. Not a single Democrat in Washington supports that bill. That's how radical they are.\par \par \uc2 And so, that's the kind of thing that we're talking about.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Congressman, I want to be sure to ask. We are more than five years out from a shooting by a gunman -- list of lawmakers, clearly had a very political viewpoint for why he came after you and your colleagues at that congressional baseball practice. How are you doing five years later, and how worried are you about heated political rhetoric whether it's on the left or the right?\par \par \uc2 SCALISE: Yes, Shannon, I appreciate that. I - I continue to get better. I still do physical therapy once a week. And, look, I'm - I'm lucky to be alive. A hero saved my life. Police saved my life that were there that day. Brad Winstrup, my colleague, saved my life. So, there's a lot of heroes that were involved in - in me being alive.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, look, We always speak out against political violence. There's no place for it. This is the United States of America. And one of our strengths is that we can disagree and we don't -- the other side isn't the demon or the enemy if they're having a different viewpoint. You know, you try to debate. You try to go make your case and - and ultimately persuade people to your side. But political violence is never an answer to resolve our disputes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Congressman, we know there are a lot of weighty problems to tackle on The Hill and we hope and pray that the two sides can find some common ground.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for your time this morning.\par \par \uc2 SCALISE: God bless. Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You too.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we'll bring in our Sunday group on what happens next now that a House committee has voted unanimously to subpoena former President Trump. We'll tell you what he's says about whether he would actually show up and testify.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I was able to bring gasoline down well over $1.60 (ph), but it's - it's inching up because of what the Russians and - and the Saudis just did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: President Biden focusing blame outside the U.S. for the high gas prices we've been seeing in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 It is time now for our Sunday group. Senior politics reporter for "Axios," Josh Kraushaar, editor-in-chief of "The Federalist," Mollie Hemingway, Fox News contributor Richard Fouler, and president of \uc2 The Heritage Foundation\uc2 , Kevin Robert.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to all of you.\par \par \uc2 Let's start there on this gas price issue.\par \par \uc2 "The Wall Street Journal" opinion piece on Thursday says blaming Saudi Arabia or OPEC Plus or \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  for an energy crisis that results from a policy of switching from carbon fuels to clean energy is disingenuous. The oil prices U.S. consumers pay are due to choices their leaders made.\par \par \uc2 Kevin, you heard Jared Bernstein with me earlier in the show. He said they are ramping up production. We're doing great on the U.S. production side.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN ROBERT, PRESIDENT, THE HERITAGE FOUNDATION: Jared Bernstein's a nice guy but he's dead wrong. If you talk to Americans that know that gas prices are going up, at the same time they hear the president of the United States giving fist bump to leader of Saudi Arabia and also telling us that the solution is to buy energy efficient coffee makers. The way back to controlling inflation and American independence is going back to where we were just in 2019, Shannon, when America was a net oil exporter, that's beneficial for every American, even Americans who think that wind and solar production is a sweet thing.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And don't mess with the coffee makers. I think everybody's going to agree, Americans, you're going to have to settle them on that.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the president has also said this week, if you elect Republicans, if you give the GOP control, you just watch inflation, it's going to get terrible. This is the headline over at your place, "The Federalist." Molly, they say, get ready for Democrats' new strategy blaming future Biden inflation on a GOP Congress. We've seen that pretty uniformly this week.\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR-IN-CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST" AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, it's a fascinating strategy to try to blame Republicans when Democrats control the White House, both houses of Congress. And we have seen that American voters very much are aware of inflation, rising costs, problems with the economy. And that's actually one of the issues that they say is motivating them to vote.\par \par \uc2 And you look at, you know, all these polls that are out there that show that on the issues that American voters care about, whether it's inflation, the economy, crime, you know, these - these types of issues, they're tending to say that they think Republicans will do a better job. So, they can try that messaging but I don't know how much sway it's going to have with the voter.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to make sure we also get to the January 6th committee. We think this week was the last hearing, although they are saying it's not set in stone. There could be more things to come. But as part of this, at the end of the hearing, they voted unanimously to subpoena President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Professor Jonathan Turley, a law professor we have on quite a bit, said this about waiting to the last minute of the last that we know of hearing to do the subpoena. He says the delay undermines the credibility of the effort from the first day the J6 committee said Trump was central to its investigation.\par \par \uc2 So, Richard, did they risk this looking like political theater because they've waited till the last minute to drop this bomb?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FOWLER, FORBES CONTRIBUTING WRITER AND FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I think that's going to be in the minds of the viewers - mind of the voters that go to the polls. But I think it's worth pointing out here that from the beginning of this committee, and even if you talk to folks in Trump's camp, they - he wanted more Republicans on the committee, and it was - it was Kevin McCarthy who said no to that. And I think, as we sit here now and we watch some of the harrowing (ph) behind the scenes footage that we saw Speaker Pelosi and pictures of Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  scrambling to try to save the United States Capitol, who's missing from that picture is former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , which we find - which we saw last March. A federal judge said that he's most likely one of the causes for this.\par \par \uc2 So, I think this subpoena, while we - I like -- I doubt that we'll see \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on Capitol Hill, I think this subpoena speaks to where the January 6th riot started and how we got here.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: I just have to correct really quickly. It's not true that Kevin McCarthy is responsible for no Republicans being on the committee.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: No, no, no. Well -\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: He actually did appoint members to that committee. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  refused to seat them. Which has never happened in the history of congressional investigations or congressional committees. And so it's not that this lost credibility at this point, it actually never had credibility because there was not a single Republican appointed member on that committee, so it always looked partisan. And it also always looked --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But (INAUDIBLE) say he then - he then pulled everybody. McCarthy then pulled -\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Well, but - but because \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  removed his top two members. That's never happened in the history of Congress.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: But, Mollie, it's also worth pointing out, what you see throughout - what you're seeing through every hearing is you've seen high-level, high- ranking Trump officials testifying, showing that the president was not active on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Yes, so there was no due process on the committee and I think -\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: We also showed -- they also showed evidence that - that he - he didn't have - he didn't protect the Capitol on January 6th. We also show evidence --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I did -\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: What I think this shows is that - it shows that Democrats are very focused on this while the American voter is worried about how they're going to pay for their groceries, how they're going to pay for gas, and that this is their final closing message. I think it's going to go as poorly for Democrats this year as it went for Republicans in 1998.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, one of the key players in this has been Republican \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , who, obviously, lost her primary and she's not going to be returning to Congress, but this is what she said about this waiting to the last minute for the subpoena.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): I think we have felt it's very important that the investigation be conducted in a way that is rigorous and disciplined and responsible.\par \par \uc2 Collecting evidence from all those around the central figure in January 6th before we issued a subpoena for him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, Josh, she says this was just the natural progression of their investigation.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR, SENIOR POLITICS REPORTER, "AXIOS": Well, the bigger challenge for Cheney and the Democrats on the January 6th committee is that if Republicans take back the House, they could just withdraw the subpoena. So, this becomes - I mean, really, this is coming down to the - the political reality as much as the substantive revelations we learned from the hearings.\par \par \uc2 And as Mollie was saying, Republicans and Democrats alike are not focused on January 6th. Two percent - just 2 percent of the advertisements from Republicans and Democrats are focused on January 6th. Crime, the economy, immigration even rank much higher when you look at the messaging across all these big battleground states.\par \par \uc2 So, I think Cheney - I mean the hearings were very important and demonstrated what actually happened on January 6th. But most voters are looking forward and they're not paying attention to the hearings, they're paying attention to their pocketbooks.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, I think something that would make people pay attention is if the former president decided to show up. And he's like, yes, I'll do it. Let's bring it on. But he wants it to be live and he wants to make a lot of points that he wants to make.\par \par \uc2 Kevin, in any universe, does any of that happen?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT: I don't think so. I mean, ultimately, I think the president and his team know that the January 6th committee is a sham. The very first thing that Leader McCarthy needs to do when he's speaker is end it. And he ought to let Harriet Hagaman, who thankfully beat \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  in Wyoming, author the resolution. That would make Americans happy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Well, we'll see as it proceeds. The report is still forthcoming.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, stay with us because we've got a lot more to talk about.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we're going to take you to Georgia for this week's must watch Senate debate with a look at what became its biggest viral moment.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): One thing I have not done, I've never pretended to be a police officer and - and - and I've never -- I've never threatened a shootout with the police.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), SENATE CANDIDATE FOR GEORGIA: You know what's so funny, I am - work with many police officers. And at the same time, have - have --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. Walker. Mr. Walker.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: No, no, no, no, no, when he said a problem with the truth -\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. Walker - Mr. Walker, excuse me, Mr. Walker.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: When he said a problem with the truth, the truth is - is here.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Please, out of respect, I - I - I need to let you know, Mr. Walker -\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You are very well aware of the rules tonight.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And you have a prop.\par \par \uc2 WALKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That is not allowed, sir.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: No badges allowed. OK, that was Georgia Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and his opponent, Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , in one of the most shared exchanges from their heated Friday debate over the badge. Both candidates went on offense, trading shots on each other's records, as well as on health care costs and abortion.\par \par \uc2 All right, we are back with the panel now.\par \par \uc2 So, we need to talk about the issue of abortion. It obviously came up with personal respect to \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . He continues to say it is a lie, that he funded an abortion for a past girlfriend who has made that claim. But there was also this exchange from the two on the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WARNOCK: The patient's room is too narrow and small and cramped of space for a woman, her doctor, and the \uc2 United States government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WALKER: He's a neat (ph) talker, but did he not mention that there's a baby in that room as well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so, Josh, they talked more broadly about it, but they talked about the personal accusations and his denials on this issue too.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Yes, I mean, there's the personal aspect with Walker allegedly paying for an ex-girlfriend's abortion, and then there's the policy debate over whether, you know, Georgia voters are pro-choice or pro-life. Republicans hope this is a race that comes down to the environment, right? They're hoping that Georgia voters are going to want to check the Democratic power. And that was the -- Walker did a pretty good job. Like, if that -- if the goal was to say I'm not like Warnock, I'm going to vote with Republicans, and that's why you should support \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , then he was successful. But if it's about baggage, personal issues, about the candidates themselves, that's what Democrats are hoping prevails in Georgia. They think Warnock is a stronger candidate and voters are going to vote not just on the issues but on the candidates' personal strengths.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I mean Walker is one of the candidates that has given the GOP some adjeda (ph), a bit of a headache. This is a headline, "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " opinion this week, McConnell admits it, the GOP's Senate candidates are liabilities.\par \par \uc2 And now, Mollie, it's coming down to a handful of races where there have been some very difficult things that have popped up for GOP candidates and how they handle those issues.\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: It's also just true, though, that Americans are dissatisfied with how Washington, D.C., is doing things. So, Republican voters did nominate a bunch of outsider candidates. Whether you view that as a liability, if you're someone like McConnell, or a strength, because you're hoping to make some very significant changes to how - how things operate in this country is up for - is up for debate.\par \par \uc2 But I think, you know, one thing that was interesting last night's debate, we'd been told that Walker was a very bad candidate. In fact, he did a really interesting and impressive performance in his debate on that abortion question. He didn't just point out that the unborn child is left out of a lot of these discussions, he also pointed out that \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  wants everyone to pay for abortions, taxpayers to be forced to pay for abortions. So he said, he's talking about having the government outside of the bedroom, but here he's actually bringing us all back into that bedroom by forcing us to pay for something that, you know, a lot of Americans do not want to pay for.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, that's clearly one of the big issues on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about some more of our Fox News polls.\par \par \uc2 When asked who they prefer for, generically, a congressional seat, the Democrats are at a three point advantage. But that's all registered voters. When we dug a little deeper, those who feel certain to vote, they give a one point advantage to Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Kevin, why is this so tight if Republicans had had this, you know, representation there was going to be a red tsunami, well now it's a ripple, where - you know, what do Republicans do, how have they kind of bungled what was a lead for them at one point?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Actually those numbers, if you look at the history of polls last 60 or 70 years, indicate that the Republicans are doing well. I happen to think they could be doing even better if they had an even more robust policy agenda that focused on restoring self-governance to the American people. But, ultimately, just to get to the political science of it, Shannon, when Republicans on the generic ballot are within two or three points of the Democrats on - on generic preference, they win. And so I think the Republicans are going to outperform, probably pick up 30 to 40 seats.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, what about the Senate?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I'm also predicting a Republican senate, 51, 52, 53 Senate seats. And you know why? Because the election will turn on what voters care about, the economy and the rule of law, both the border security or the lack thereof, and the -- just tremendous increase in crime. The president and all of the people running on his party are messaging on the wrong things and ultimately it's too late for them to turn the corner.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, you talk about top issues. We have some of this in our Fox News polling as well. Now, out of the 12 concerns, we ask people to say how they were mot concerned. Two of the top ones, inflation and crime, 89 percent, 79 percent. Voters said that they trust Republicans more than Democrats to handle both by double digits, Richard.\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: Look, don't get me wrong, I think this is going to be a tight election. I'm not going to make any predictions. But I think where Kevin is right is where the Republicans struggle here is that they don't seem to have a clear, distinct message of what they will do if given the gavel and given control of the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 If you talk to folks in the \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  campaign, if you talk to folks in Wisconsin on the lieutenant - on Mandela Barnes' campaign, I talked to them this week, they're saying this, we see an expanded electorate, in Georgia specifically. Since 2018, there's been one million new voters added to the voting rolls. So they're going after these new voters.\par \par \uc2 And even in a world where \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  or Mehmet Oz outperforms their opponent in a debate, herein lies the problem, they're trying to convince a very small group of independents that they are the right close. Whereas in for Democrats, they're working to expand their electorate and they're also working on talking to the folks who aren't part of the Trump wing of the Republican Party, who have already made the decision that they're not going to vote for Republicans in this election.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and going into the home stretch, there are heavy hitters that are heading back out now. Former President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . There had been talk about whether he would show up. In some of the tight races that you mentioned, Josh, he is going to be there. Can it be a difference maker?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Well, look, Democrats don't have a lot of surrogates that they can use that are popular in swing states. So, President Biden's numbers in these swing states are very low. He was in Oregon. That's about as - that's about the battleground map for the president because you can't go to Wisconsin, Pennsylvania, some of these true battlegrounds. \uc2 Obama\uc2  is the one surrogate that might be able to make a difference, especially when it comes to turning out the base. Wisconsin, such a big race for the Senate map. He's going to Georgia also for Senator Warnock. Those are going to be tests and see whether \uc2 Obama\uc2  and whether the Democratic voter base can get excited about some of their candidates.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so this week the debate - I think we're all going to pop our popcorn and watch - is the Fetterman/Oz debate in Pennsylvania. What are you going to look for, Mollie?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: Well, one think that's interesting about that race is that it has been not focused on issues prior to this point because of personality issues, whether it's Fetterman's stroke or Mehmet Oz's, you know, some of the ways he's been focused on issues that aren't important to Pennsylvania. Now the issues really are coming into play. And so I think this debate, we'll actually see where they differ. You know, Fetterman is known for being someone who, as lieutenant governor, has helped some criminals out. That's become a major issue. Crime is becoming a major issues for Americans nationwide. And so I think we'll actually start to see where those - where they differ.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, maybe we'll have a watch party. We'll all get together. Snacks. OK, sign up sheet right afterwards.\par \par \uc2 All right, thank you We'll see you next Sunday, panel.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the CIA opens its doors for a rare glimpse inside. We'll take a look at items from some of the agency's most top secret missions.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The \uc2 Central Intelligence Agency\uc2  is marking 75 years by opening up, well, just a little bit. But, for one day only, reporters were allowed into its newly renovated museum to hear the stories behind the CIA's most prized possessions.\par \par \uc2 Fox News chief national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin, who has covered these stories from the outside, now takes us inside.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHIEF NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice over): At CIA headquarters, you don't need a clearance to see its new renovated museum.\par \par \uc2 JANELLE NEISES, CIA MUSEUM DEPUTY DIRECTOR: It's pretty much unlike anything you'll see anywhere else.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Filled with declassified gadgets, like this powder compact with coded messages embedded in its mirror. A dragonfly that served as the first UAV, or drone. Or this pipe.\par \par \uc2 NEISES: The idea here is that you would bite down on the pipe, and it would be able to send signals through your cavity right through here.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Janelle Neises is the deputy director of the new CIA museum.\par \par \uc2 NEISES: These artifacts have actually never been on display before here at CIA Museum. They're related to our ARGO mission.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I need you to help me make a fake movie.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: The covert extraction on which the 2012 Ben Affleck movie was based. Six Americans rescued in Tehran, one of the agency's most daring missions.\par \par \uc2 NEISES: So, we actually had a studio set up in L.A., Studio 6. We went so far as to have a script reading and invite the press. This artwork is one of my favorite artifacts in the whole museum, and it was done by Jack Kirby of the Marvel Universe.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Jack Kirby had no idea he was working for the CIA when he drew it.\par \par \uc2 NEISES: It's very possible that until ARGO got declassified that he had no idea.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: There are newly declassified items from Project Azorian, the famous Glomar mission which used millionaire Howard Hughes' drilling company as a cover to recover a sunken K-129 Russian sub.\par \par \uc2 NEISES: CIA engineers had to create a capture vessel to actually go to the bottom of the ocean, 11 and a half Empire State Buildings down, grab a multi ton sub and then bring it back up.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: There are examples of real life dead drops, like this crumbled milk carton.\par \par \uc2 NEISES: We want to make it so that it is something that blends in with its surrounding or is disgusting and no one would ever pick up.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Like this rat.\par \par \uc2 NEISES: We would treat the rat, hollow it out, you could put a camera in there, a micro dot reader, money, film, you sew it up and place it and then you could come pick it up. Problem during testing though was cats started stealing our dead drops.\par \par \uc2 And you would rub it with either tobacco sauce or wormwood oil, anything that was an animal deterrent.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: There is a section devoted to post 9/11 operations.\par \par \uc2 NEISES: This is the actual model that was used to brief President Biden on the Zawahiri mission.\par \par \uc2 This case honors our first teams into Afghanistan after 9/11. So, our first team was in country 15 days after the attacks.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: These stars, like the 139 currently on the memorial wall at Langley, hung at the CIA basin in Afghanistan to honor the seven officers hunting bin Laden, killed by a suicide bomber in Chost (ph).\par \par \uc2 NEISES: When we left Afghanistan, we made sure to bring this memorial back with us.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Nick Reynolds, author of "Need to Know," was the CIA Museum's historian until 2013.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS REYNOLDS, AUTHOR AND FORMER CIA HISTORIAN: I like the outlier. I like the people who stood up for themselves, didn't necessarily have the stamp of approval of the establishment.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: World War II spies, like Virginia Hall, shown here receiving the intelligence cross at a secret White House ceremony from the first head of the OSS. Her amputated leg cost her a job at the State Department, so she volunteered to drive an ambulance in France, eventually running 1,500 French resistance forces, becoming the first paramilitary officer the CIA hired in 1947.\par \par \uc2 NEISES: She lasted, in France, behind enemy lines, running assets and agents, she was getting downed pilots back out of the country and back into the U.K. She got people out of prison who were very important to the resistance without a single shot being fired.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: For Janelle Neises, her favorite piece in the exhibit are these boots, worn by an actual CIA officer, Dave, the teammate of Medal of Honor recipient, Mike Span, the first U.S. casualty on the ground in Afghanistan killed during a prison riot in Mazar-i-Sharif.\par \par \uc2 NEISES: While getting to Mike, he completely ran out of ammunition. He was firing to get to Mike to try to save him. He took Mike's weapon, used it to get to safety.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Dave's seven-year-old son started calling them daddy's lucky boots, realizing for the first time just how dangerous his dad's job was, not knowing where his father really worked.\par \par \uc2 NEISES: I really think they talk about the silent sacrifice this organization makes on a daily basis. We're very small. We're very agile. We're very good with very little.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: And we are proud to honor that sacrifice which often goes unrecognized.\par \par \uc2 That was Jennifer Griffin reporting from the CIA's Langley headquarters.\par \par \uc2 And now this programming note. You can join my colleague, Dana Perino, tonight for a deep dive into November's midterms. It's called "The Big Midterms Show." Dana will bring in a high-powered roundtable for a conversation about the issues and how the candidates are aiming to drive turnout. That is tonight, 10:00 p.m. Eastern, on Fox News Channel.\par \par \uc2 That is it for us this Sunday morning. We thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a great week. We will see you next "OX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, p