WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: We are, what did that do for us again?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He helped to launch us into space when he broke the speed of sound.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I knew that. I knew that. I'll see you guys here on Monday. Have a great weekend everybody.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I'm not so sure about that.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening, welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, we are nearing the end of the trial of the Russian national accused of lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  about the Steele dossier. The case will go to the jury next week with one of the five counts already decided.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt is in Alexandria, Virginia again tonight. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening to you. Two \uc2 FBI\uc2  employees on the stand today testified they wanted to further investigate some potential connections between one of the witnesses in this case, a man named Charles Dolan and his ties to the Kremlin in Russia, but they were told no, this comes as the federal judge in the case throughout an important charge against the man actually on trial.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice over): Russian national Igor Danchenko will now face only four counts of lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  after the federal judge in the case removed the first count over a technicality.\par \par \uc2 Danchenko was the man that government says was the primary sub source for ex-British spy \uc2 Christopher Steele\uc2 , who compiled a dossier aimed at destroying then candidate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Special Counsel \uc2 John Durham\uc2  alleged Danchenko lied to an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent when he said he hadn't "talked to a Democratic political operative named Charles Dolan about the Steele dossier".\par \par \uc2 Dolan gave Danchenko information that ended up in the dossier but the two communicated via e-mail. They didn't talk.\par \par \uc2 Dolan, a witness in the case has connections to Russia according to two \uc2 FBI\uc2  employees on the stand, specifically to Putin spokesman Dmitry Peskov.\par \par \uc2 The two \uc2 FBI\uc2  employees claimed the Mueller team quashed their efforts to investigate or even interview Dolan.\par \par \uc2 On paper, this case is about Igor Danchenko. But over the course of the week inside the courtroom, it became clear \uc2 John Durham\uc2 's focus was on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  itself.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the week, Durham turned on his own witness, aggressively questioning \uc2 FBI\uc2  analyst Brian Auten, who revealed the bureau offered Steele $1 million to corroborate his dossier. He never did and was never paid.\par \par \uc2 Special agent Kevin Helson, a government witness bucked Special Counsel \uc2 John Durham\uc2 's case, telling jurors Igor Danchenko was a valuable informant, adding American national security is more at risk without Danchenko still working for the Bureau. He claimed it Danchenko helped the bureau with around some 25 investigations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): And Danchenko's attorneys told the judge today Bret they're not even going to put on a case perhaps highlighting how confident they feel about where they stand. At least as of tonight, jurors will begin deliberations on Monday. Meaning, we could have a verdict the same day, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt we'll follow it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department has asked the federal appeals court to overturn a judge's appointment of an independent arbiter to review documents seized at former President Trump's Florida state.\par \par \uc2 Judge Aileen Cannon, a son of the Special Master last month to inspect thousands of records taken in the August 8th search of Mar-a-Lago and to weed out from the investigation, any that may be protected by claims of legal privilege.\par \par \uc2 A third straight day of bad economic news for President Biden, retail sales were again disappointing in September, as inflation sends prices soaring, limiting what consumers can spend on non-necessities.\par \par \uc2 It comes as the president's strategy seems to be if you think it's bad now, wait until Republicans are in charge. That's the message.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has the story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): It's been three consecutive days of tough economic readings but don't expect any policy changes at the White House.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The policies that we're doing to help mitigate and ease price pressures are having effect. Look at goods inflation, it was flat if you take out energy.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Friday, the Commerce Department reported retail sales were unexpectedly flat in September, households cutting back on buying cars, appliances and electronics amid stubbornly high inflation and rising interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Thursday, new data showed inflation jumped 8.2 percent from last year, largely driven by rising food, energy and housing costs.\par \par \uc2 And Wednesday, the Producer Price Index showed a strong indication inflation has not peaked. Biden's critics blame his spending.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Biden came in and spent and borrowed and printed $4.1 trillion. I mean, this isn't complicated folks.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The White House strategy tell people the grass isn't always greener. The president saying this more.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If Republican wins, inflation is going to get worse. It's that Simple.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: And the administration wants reporters to say it too. One official pointing the press to an interview coming next week with Republican House Leader Kevin McCarthy, saying he continues to showcase how eager MAGA congressional Republicans are to make inflation worse if it means a windfall for rich special interests at the expense of middle-class families.\par \par \uc2 But there's a less clear strategy on bringing down energy costs. The Treasury Secretary now saying the world needs Russian oil to stabilize global markets. The challenge is keeping it from benefiting \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF THE TREASURY: What we want to see is that worship boil continues to flow into the market and that countries are using the leverage provided by the existence of this cap to bargain lower prices.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): So tonight, the president will talk about lowering the cost of prescription drugs from just outside Los Angeles where the price of gas is about $2.00 higher than the national average, 6.20 a gallon there, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks took another big hit today over interest rate hike worries. The Dow loss 404, the S&P 500 fell 87, the NASDAQ dropped 328 hitting a new low for the year.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow was up a little over one percentage point, the S&P 500 was down 1-1/2. The NASDAQ was off three.\par \par \uc2 Tonight we examine whether the Biden administration will turn to Venezuela to help supply badly needed oil to the U.S. That country's leadership is still under indictment by the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Correspondent Aishah Hasnie takes a look tonight from the State Department.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): As the U.S. and Saudi Arabia trade barbs over oil cuts, millions of Americans brace for higher energy bills.\par \par \uc2 The State Department is considering easing sanctions on Venezuela so that companies like \uc2 Chevron\uc2  can pump oil there, despite the fact that the country's cartel leadership remains indicted by the Justice Department on narco-terrorism charges.\par \par \uc2 But according to a state spokesman, such a significant policy move would require constructive steps by Maduro to restore democracy in Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 The White House and Democrats in the meantime, continue making public threats to punish the longtime Gulf ally.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS (D-DE): It's unlikely that we will support any additional arm sales to the Saudis.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: If we can't get the oil from Saudi Arabia, if these actions and consequences don't change, Saudis and \uc2 OPEC\uc2 's decision on oil. Where do we get it from?\par \par \uc2 VEDANT PATEL, STATE DEPARTMENT PRINCIPAL DEPUTY SPOKESMAN: We need to slowly pivot away from this reliance and make investments in renewable energies that will allow us to keep up with our supply and demand.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: But Republicans and U.S. oil producers say the only immediate solution is unleashing domestic production.\par \par \uc2 Former Bush deputy national security adviser \uc2 Elliott Abrams\uc2  agrees.\par \par \uc2 ELLIOTT ABRAMS\uc2 , COUNCIL ON FOREIGN RELATIONS: The idea that we're going to -- we're going to punish the Saudis and that will somehow produce more oil supplies. That is not going to work. It's just going to bitter (PH) our relations a bit more.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And that could happen soon enough, Bret. California Democrat \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  tells Fox News he anticipates that the president will do something in response to \uc2 OPEC\uc2 's decision to cut oil in the next week, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Aishah Hasnie at the State Department. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News Chief Legal Correspondent, anchor of "FOX NEWS SUNDAY" Shannon Bream. Good evening, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF LEGAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good to see you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's start with the Durham trial. What do you take out of that? What he's kind of brought to the fore?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: What I think is interesting is that there's a lot we knew about the Mueller investigation. There were pages and pages written and released. But there's a lot we didn't know that Durham has dug out in this year's long investigation that we've all had to be patient and wait.\par \par \uc2 And these couple of trials that they've had have given us more information. This, you know, two \uc2 FBI\uc2  employees and analysts and an agent now stepping forward to say this democratic operative Charles Dolan had given some information to Danchenko that ended up in the dossier.\par \par \uc2 When they were trying to run it down, they were told a couple of times like we're not going to go down this path with Charles Dolan.\par \par \uc2 Separately, they both said that they had prepared information and given it to \uc2 FBI\uc2  higher ups, and especially one of them who her task was to look into the veracity of the dossier and yet she was waved off of looking into this democratic operative, who apparently fits in information that ended up there.\par \par \uc2 So, that's some out there including her colleague Andy McCarthy has said in some ways the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is on trial here because we're learning a lot more about how they operated with respect to the Mueller investigation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, stunning how little coverage it's getting. Even still, what is getting coverage is the economy and it's been a bumpy ride from the administration's point of view, from Americans point of view over the last week. You have a guest this weekend, you'll be asking about that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We do, we have White House economic adviser Jared Bernstein, exclusively with us on Sunday morning. And I got to give him credit. They've had tough numbers this week. And yet he shows up, he's willing to have the conversation about what's working, what's not working, he's going to make the case that people have to be patient.\par \par \uc2 But with core inflation at its highest reading in four decades and realities, like mortgages at 20 years highs, people being priced out. People pay more for everything, gas, groceries, that hits the reality of their everyday life. We've got some tough questions for him.\par \par \uc2 And brand new exclusive polls will roll out on Sunday morning that also show how the American people feel about who's responsible for what's going on with the economy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Steve Scalise will be on as well. All right, Shannon Bream, thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: See you then.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Police in Raleigh, North Carolina are trying to learn why a 15-year- old boy fatally shot five people yesterday. They say the suspect killed two on neighborhood streets and shot three others on a walking trail.\par \par \uc2 Among the dead is an off duty police officer. The suspect eluded authorities for hours before being cornered in a home and arrested. President Biden says his administration is working to assist local authorities there.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the latest on Russia's attempt to regain momentum in its invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And later, how the crucial Senate race in Pennsylvania could be determined by one specific demographic group.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: South Korea says North Korea has launched a ballistic missile and hundreds of artillery shells toward its eastern waters. It says the North fired 90 additional shells off its east coast and about 300 others from two separate western coastal areas.\par \par \uc2 South Korea says it scrambled fighter jets in response to the North's deployment of warplanes near their border.\par \par \uc2 Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  says he thinks a mobilization of army reservists he ordered last month to bolster his country's troops in Ukraine will be completed in two weeks.\par \par \uc2 This comes as he continues to threaten an escalation of the conflict. Correspondent Trey Yingst is again in Kyiv tonight.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: On a solemn morning in Kyiv, Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelensky\uc2  makes a rare public appearance at a local hospital.\par \par \uc2 He's marking Heroes Day to thank those who have died or been injured in war.\par \par \uc2 1,500 miles to the east, Zelenskyy's counterpart, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  spoke today in Kazakhstan. The Russian leader discussing the war and the possibility of a direct confrontation with \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF RUSSIA (through translator): Direct contact, direct clash of \uc2 NATO\uc2  troops with the Russian army is a very dangerous move, which could lead to a global catastrophe. I hope that those who are saying this are smart enough not to take such a step.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: New satellite images show that Putin has moved strategic bombers within 20 miles of neighboring Norway. This is the closest that planes capable of carrying nuclear weapons have been to a \uc2 NATO\uc2  country since the start of the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 With each passing day, Ukraine is taking more territory in the East. But the continued pressure has observers worried that Putin will escalate the conflict even further.\par \par \uc2 For Ukrainians, they're not only concerned about attacks, but also the communication equipment needed to defend themselves.\par \par \uc2 Right now, Ukraine is relying on \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 's Starlink equipment to have satellite comms (PH) on the battlefield. But the company has reportedly asked the Pentagon to pay for the service, with Musk tweeting it cost approximately $20 million dollars a month to support.\par \par \uc2 A senior U.S. defense official weighed-in on the development today, saying the allies are working together, looking for options to best support Ukraine's needs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): On the frontlines, we saw how critical the Starlink system is for Ukrainian troops. In many areas of eastern Ukraine, there is a total communication blackout. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey Yingst in Kyiv. Trey, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, going after the black vote in Pennsylvania's big Senate race. We'll take you there.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Palestinian reports say two Palestinians were killed as Israel's military carried out an arrest raid in a refugee camp in the occupied West Bank.\par \par \uc2 Militant groups claimed both slain men as members, though there were conflicting statements about the circumstances surrounding the death of one of them who was a hospital doctor.\par \par \uc2 Major flooding hit South Eastern Australia with rivers forecasts remain dangerously high for days there. Meteorologist blame a La Nina weather pattern for the above-average rainfall in Australia.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at London. One of the big stories there tonight. Climate protesters throw soup over Vincent van Gogh's sunflowers painting in London's National Gallery to protest fossil fuel extraction.\par \par \uc2 The oil painting is considered one of the Dutch artist's most iconic works. Two people were arrested.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to SPECIAL REPORT. In tonight's "MIDTERM WATCH", as the election nears, Pennsylvania is in focus. The Pennsylvania Senate race continues to be one of the hotly contested in the country. And now, both sides are making extra efforts to court one key demographic. Correspondent Bryan Llenas explains tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. MALCOLM KENYATTA (D-PA): I don't think Dr. Oz gives a damn about people in Philadelphia who have buried loved ones, who are living with gunshots ringing outside of their windows.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Harsh words from Democratic state representative and Fetterman ally Malcolm Kenyatta, suggesting, Republican Dr. Mehmet Oz's roundtable discussions with members of the Black community are not authentic.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R-PA), SENATE CANDIDATE: The desperate swipe at me, I've been to Kensington multiple times. Even before I was a candidate, it's the largest open air drug market in the country caused directly by weak left radical positions on drug use in the city of Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: A recent Fox News poll shows Fetterman has a 28 point lead over Oz with minority voters in Pennsylvania. Oz is trying to chip away at this margin, holding roundtable discussions in the black community aimed at highlighting surging crime and drug problems.\par \par \uc2 But The Intercept reports that a Philadelphia woman who shared her story at one of those events is a paid Oz campaign staffer. Fetterman's campaign manager says this is proof, the Oz events are merely enact. The Oz campaign called that a racist attack.\par \par \uc2 This war of words comes amid Republican attack ads, calling out Fetterman for a 2013 incident in which you confronted an unarmed black jogger with a shotgun. A line of attack, ironically first levelled by Kenyatta, when he ran against Fetterman in the Democratic primary.\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: So, you still refuse to say I'm sorry to this day. Fetterman said he heard gunshots and was defending his community.\par \par \uc2 The Democrat needs black voters to show up big to win.\par \par \uc2 Pollsters warn there are signs of waning enthusiasm.\par \par \uc2 BERWOOD YOST, DIRECTOR, FRANKLIN AND MARSHALL COLLEGE: Right now, his numbers are probably lower than they should be.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS (on camera): Another issue in this race is Fetterman's health. Tonight, Fetterman's wife Gisele is responding to the NBC reporter who said the candidate had a hard time understanding small talk before their interview.\par \par \uc2 She says those comments are appalling and says the reporter should be held accountable. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bryan, thank you. Let's take a look at how toss up races like Pennsylvania Senate contest could change the Senate majority.\par \par \uc2 This is our What If Senate map. These yellow states are the toss up states. The red and the blue are where they lean currently. And that's already set. The yellow are the tossups. If let's just say, the polls hold as they are now. And Georgia goes to the Democrats, as does Arizona.\par \par \uc2 But Republicans have a big night in Pennsylvania and Nevada. There is your break down, 52-48. Basically, Republicans need to pick up two of the seats no matter what the combination is. And there you see the breakdown where Republicans take control.\par \par \uc2 Now, let's say Republicans have a really big night and they pick up all four toss up races. That's what you're looking at for a red wave, perhaps spreading to traditionally blue states.\par \par \uc2 For tonight's spotlight races. We look at competitive Senate seats in Colorado and New Hampshire. Both lean Democrat tonight in our Fox News Power Rankings.\par \par \uc2 Let's first head west to Colorado. Incumbent Democrat Michael Bennet is facing challenge from moderate Republican Joe O'Dea. Bennet has the edge in polling. He is ahead by almost eight points in the RealClearPolitics Average of polls.\par \par \uc2 Bennet's campaign is trying to paint O'Dea as too far-right for Colorado. O'Dea, however, says he opposes an abortion ban has distanced himself from former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Now, in New Hampshire, the polls little closer. Incumbent Democrat Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  up almost six points over Republican Army veteran Don Bolduc.\par \par \uc2 Hassan has focused on abortion rights as a major campaign message. Bolduc is facing questions over his stance on the 2020 election outcome, initially said it was stolen by Democrats.\par \par \uc2 After winning his primary, he said the election was not stolen. But has just, last week said he does not have enough information on the topic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MAGGIE HASSAN\uc2  (D-NH): McConnell and the national Republicans are trying to buy the seat and put an election denier into the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . We can't let that happen.\par \par \uc2 DON BOLDUC (R-NH), SENATE CANDIDATE: They're not dealing with the three biggest problems that we have here in the Granite State: Inflation, inflation, inflation. People are choosing between heating and eating. They're not being listened to.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let's bring in pollsters Mark Penn and Steven Kent. Gentlemen, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 Mark, let me start to you -- with you. Where do you see the environment tonight as you look at these big Senate races?\par \par \uc2 MARK PENN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, the environment is, I think, one that's moving closer and closer to a wave election for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 I think you're seeing the top three issues be inflation, immigration, and crime. Republicans have significant leads on each of those. I think those issues are not going away. The inflation report was not helpful at all. And so the environment is certainly trending towards the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Stephen, where do you see specific races that you can point to you? I'm assuming you agree with Mark.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN KENT, POLLSTER: I do agree with Mark, and it's something that just a month ago we weren't quite so sure of. You did see a very strong likelihood of a Republican wave in picking up seats that were out of Republicans reach earlier in the year. But then when the Dobbs decision is handed down by the Supreme Court, you see abortion double in salience from a four percent most important issue to an eight percent most important issue. And that spooked, rightly, a lot of Republicans who saw that now they are going to have to defend these seats and chase these seats on social issues.\par \par \uc2 But what happened over the summer is that it has frozen in importance, and Democrats have not been able to raise it to the level of importance as the cost of living in the economy, which combined are the most important issue by the tune of about 36 percent with voters. This is a huge, huge deal and something they just haven't been able to reverse.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One of those places is Nevada. And the incumbent senator, \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 , is facing a real uphill battle, Mark, against the challenger \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , in part because of that inflation, in part because gas prices in Nevada are really high.\par \par \uc2 PENN: I think you are releasing these economic issues play themselves out. I think you are seeing some of the Democratic erosion among the Latino community also play itself out. Again, I think that was a race that people thought was going to be an uphill battle, and I think that is increasingly being put in the Republican column because of those bread-and-butter economic issues.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Stephen, in Georgia, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  had a bad week, obviously. He has denied these allegations about paying for an abortion, but it's lingering, and it has affected the polls post last week. It's still within shooting distance, though, in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 KENT: It is within shooting distance. I think the mood in the room for the entire country helps Republicans make up for areas in which they have made huge blunders with the quality of their candidates.\par \par \uc2 Now, if I can turn your attention to North Carolina where you have two candidates who are remarkably good, Cheri Beasley on the Democrat side and \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2 , Republican. They sort of embody to me the opposite of the environment in Georgia, because when you have two good candidates, nobody is talking about them on a national basis, and there has been very little attention paid to North Carolina for that reason. When personalities are not front and center, then the issues are.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  is going to be able to probably hold on to the Republican seat that is being vacated right now, probably by a win of about 1.6 points. And it's really sad for Democrats because this used to be a state where they could perform and compete, but it has slipped away from them on everything but governors' races for the past three or four cycles.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is another visual here, and this is the toss-ups in yellow, North Carolina you're mentioning is in pink here. It's a lean Republican in our power rankings. The light blue is the lean Democrat. Are there surprises, Mark, that you see on election night, just looking at the map right now?\par \par \uc2 PENN: I do think that the Senate map is a tough map for Republicans. I don't really think they're going to get above 51 if they get there. I think they've got problems here in Georgia and Pennsylvania. I think you've got a number of places here that are still too close to call. But I really do think that Georgia has become a problem. And Pennsylvania, Oz is close, but he hasn't proven that he can get across the finish line. That debate is going to be one important debate for the country.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You've got these races here. You've got Nevada, Georgia, and Arizona, Mark. That is a race where the Democrat, Mark Kelly, seems like he is in a good spot, but Blake Masters has been closing.\par \par \uc2 PENN: I think that is one where you have seen some closing, but it just doesn't look like Masters is going to get across the finish line. It would be really tough. I think you've seen the opposite kind of happen in Wisconsin where it looked like the Republican, Ron Johnson was toast, and he has come back from six down to six up. But in Arizona, I just don't think that's going to happen. I don't see the kind of momentum for him to really get across the finish line. Oz is much closer, if a race is going to tip over here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Stephen, last word here. On our what if scenario for the Senate, do you see, of the four toss-ups, a two pick up, or do you see the real bold pick up that it's a wave for Republicans?\par \par \uc2 KENT: I'm going to go with that it is going to surprise us just how much of a way that it is for Republicans. I think that there was a moment this year, particularly, again, with the Dobbs decision where we thought it was going to get away from them, but there are just so many factors, the gas prices, the continued inflation problem, it's pushing it right back in their direction. And Democrats have been pouring all of their money on a national basis toward promoting the issue of abortion, and it's just not going to fly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, that is where we are right now, 50-50. We'll see how it goes on election night here. Gentlemen, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 KENT: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, our "Whatever Happened To" segment looks at the dream and the reality of high-speed rail service in California.\par \par \uc2 As we go to break, Scottish actor Robbie Coltrane has died. Coltrane played the half giant Hagrid in the "Harry Potter" movies, a Russian crime boss and two "James Bond" films, and a crime solving psychologist on the T.V. series "Cracker." Coltrane's agent says he died today in a Scottish hospital. Robbie Coltrane was 72.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: An analysis by FOX News Digital finds nearly 270 public educators were arrested on child the sex related crimes in U.S. in the first nine months of this year. At least 199 of the arrests, or 74 percent, involved alleged crimes against students.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's "Whatever Happened To" segment, California's dream of having high-speed rail is now turning into a nightmare. National correspondent William La Jeunesse shows us tonight from Wasco, California.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's not that expensive. We cannot afford it.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: That wasn't true then or now.\par \par \uc2 LEE OHANIAN, \uc2 HOOVER INSTITUTION\uc2 : It has become a complete pipe dream. There simply is no path forward right now.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: California's high speed rail is the most expensive infrastructure plan ever undertaken in the U.S. It started with this, a slick ad campaign in 2008, bankrolled by lobbyists and big labor. The promise, a bullet train from L.A. to San Francisco in under three hours for $50 a ticket, with private investors and the federal government picking up two-thirds of the cost.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: We can't we have high speed rail? And so we are investing in that as well.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: But state and federal money dried up, investors never showed up, leaving California with a choice -- walk away, a major embarrassment, or continue construction. Finish the first leg, albeit nowhere near the riders to support it.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GAVIN NEWSOM\uc2 , (D) CALIFORNIA: I know some critics are going to say, well, that's a train to nowhere. But I think that's wrong, and I think that's offensive.\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So, what went wrong? Well, mismanagement, lawsuits, but most of all politicians altered the route to suit their own purposes, making it more expensive. Fourteen years later, the project is two years late and decades behind schedule. The cost, more than tripled from what taxpayers were promised, not $33 billion, but $113 billion.\par \par \uc2 OHANIAN: The project has become what you might call too big to fail. California has mismanaged billions of dollars in federal transportation funding that was coming from the other 49 states.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE (on camera): So current rail official say they say enough money to finish this 119-mile segment in the Central Valley, but without private investors to bail them out, this project does not pencil out economically to continue. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: William, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel with the Friday Lightning Round, Winners and Losers, and Candidate Casino.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 5 in New York as five police officers are injured stopping an alleged kidnapping that began on Long Island and ended in New York City. Police say a man forced a woman believed to be his girlfriend into a car at knife point. The suspect reportedly struck several \uc2 NYPD\uc2  cruisers, leaving three officers injured. Two others were reportedly hurt taking the suspect into custody.\par \par \uc2 FOX 4 in Dallas where a doctor charged with tampering with I.V. bags at of surgery center is arraigned on federal charges. Raynaldo Ortiz Jr. is accused of injecting heart stopping drugs into those bags while he was working as an anesthesiologist. Prosecutors say it caused 11 patients to have cardiac emergencies and one fatality.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Orlando from our affiliate, FOX 35. One of the big stories there tonight, a \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  capsule undocks from the International Space Station and returns to earth. The capsule splashed down a short time ago in the Atlantic. This was the fourth contracted astronaut mission that \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  has flown to the ISS for \uc2 NASA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN, (R-OH) HOUSE JUDICIARY COMMITTEE: The guy who testified to all that yesterday and today also happens to be the name that pops up from whistleblower who went to Senator Grassley who said two years ago this month there was a group at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  who suppressed the Hunter Biden story.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW MCCARTHY, FORMER ASSISTANT U.S. ATTORNEY: The only way to win this case, which is a very tough case for Durham, is to make it clear to the jury that he is not here to cover up any of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's malfeasance or missteps.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE PAPADOPOULOS, FORMER TRUMP ADVISER: They utilized both myself and the Steele dossier to continue to get warrants with absolutely no foundation, no basis, and they turned this country upside down.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There are not a lot of people paying attention to it, but this trial is ongoing. This is the \uc2 John Durham\uc2  investigation, and this trial, "The New York Post" writes it this way, "utter proof the \uc2 FBI\uc2  framed Trump, shielded \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 ." Yes, Igor Danchenko is on trial, but the spotlight ought to be on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and on what Durham's final report will have to say about the nation's premier federal law enforcement agency." "Wall Street Journal," "Durham's \uc2 FBI\uc2  indictment. Partisanship and incompetence aren't crimes, so the \uc2 FBI\uc2  isn't in the dock. But Mr. Durham is making the case for the public, and it's as ugly as they come."\par \par \uc2 With that, let's bring in our panel, Olivia Beavers, congressional reporter for "Politico," Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner," and "THE FIVE" co-host Jessica Tarlov.\par \par \uc2 Byron, this, again, is not getting a lot of attention, but it is significant.\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, "THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER": Well, it is. And it was not good news today that the judge throughout one of the charges against Danchenko. I am not going to predict. In the last case against \uc2 Michael Sussmann\uc2 , I thought Sussmann was totally guilty, and a jury completely disagreed and acquitted him.\par \par \uc2 The important thing about Durham has always been that there is going to be a report. When Bill Barr, the attorney general, appointed Durham back in 2020, he specifically charged Durham with writing a report in the form that could be publicly released. The value in the Durham investigation is that we are seeing what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  did to try to get \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2016 and 2017. A lot of investigators have tried to figure that out before in Congress, and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had stonewalled them. And this is probably the best we're ever going get.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jessica, for the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to try to pay Steele to corroborate the dossier, and he can't do it, $1 million, if you just look in the way-back machine of all of the coverage that the dossier got, it is significant.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, CO-HOST, "THE FIVE": It is significant, but I think that you are ultimately right in how you led with this, that they aren't a lot of people paying attention to it, and it's very hard, especially with midterms coming up, to get anything to change.\par \par \uc2 On top of that, the case that \uc2 John Durham\uc2  or his supporters, I should say, and certainly the former president, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , have made about the fireworks that are going to come out of these investigations have fallen absolutely flat, including the latest, as Byron cited, that a charge was thrown out today. So we will wait and see what the report actually has to say, but the idea that this is going to push anything forward besides probably some very partisan investigations if the Republicans take back the House in the midterms, I think that is just going to be about it, quite frankly.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And those investigations are probably going to happen if the Republicans take control?\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, "POLITICO": Yes. You showed a clip earlier of Jim Jordan. He is someone who has been involved, looking into the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . I remember covering, I think Byron as well, covering when they were looking at FISA abuse at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . It's a talking point. And this is another sort of point for them to say that, look, if you are going to go after Goliath, don't miss. And here, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , Durham was going after the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , saying you did not have your facts straight. This was not thorough enough. And so it feeds into some of the Republican points earlier on that they had, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  should have been more thorough.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, let's talk about the midterms, the economy. It has not been a great really, week or two for the Biden administration. Here is President Biden on what happens if Republicans win. It's the latest talking point.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: When it actually comes time to do something about inflation around the kitchen table, Republicans in Congress are saying no. The Republicans take control, the prices are going to go off, as will inflation. It is this simple. Sadly, they're going further. Already a vast majority of the House Republicans have signed on to the Republican budget that is going to cut -- hear me now, cut Medicare and Social Security. Cut it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, Byron, first of all, let's put up this graphic of inflation since President Biden took office. How does he sell that?\par \par \uc2 YORK: I'm sorry to laugh. That is real chutzpah there, saying if you elect Republicans, you're going to see bad inflation. I don't think that is simply going to sell.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing is, what are we, 25 days out now. I don't think there are any opportunities for Democrats to get any good news on the economy anymore between now and then. Obviously, inflation in the news we got this week was bad, consumer sales. Nothing is good news, and mortgage interest rates. And do not forget people's 401(k)s are plummeting. If you have your life savings in a 401(k) and it basically mirrors the S&P, you have lost a quarter of it this year. And this is a situation in which people do not vote for the party in charge.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We have seen, Jessica, where candidates go out on the stump and they say something completely different than what's the truth. What about this? Is he thinking that is going to stick?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I think that he is thinking -- we have seen a bit of a reversal in terms of how he is talking about inflation and what is going on in the economy, to return to a more traditional \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , I feel your pain, I understand there is more work to do. And I certainly think that taking a tact the same as the moderate Democrats who have been doing that all along is the we that we're going to be able to hopefully save the majority, certainly in the Senate. The House, I'm much more worried about there.\par \par \uc2 But the inflation numbers, they're don't lie. It's the number one issue. There are other ones that are creeping up, like crime and policing. Abortion is certainly something that is very animating on the left. But if you look back through history of this is going to be an economy election, and it's very hard for the party in power to maintain it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 That is a good intro to the place that we take you down to every Friday. It's the casino, Candidate Casino. Last time we played the Senate version. This is going to be the House version. Our FOX power rankings have it at 231 is the number Republicans are projected to pick up, the seats, 218 is a majority. So the bat is over/under that number, the power ranking number 231. Olivia, I know you have been studying your bets from last time.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: Last time was pretty bad.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, what've got? You've got $100 in chips.\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: I'm going to split 50-50.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: No, no, no.\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: I'm just kidding. I'm just kidding. I am putting $100 and saying that Republicans actually get a few more seats. Last time we are talking during the election, Republicans won more seats than we expected. I think that some of the August momentum that Democrats had over abortion might be starting to slow down, so I'm just going to say that they are over.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Black chip playing. All right, Byron?\par \par \uc2 YORK: Wow, that's pretty strong. I'm $75 on Republicans doing better than 231, $25 on them under. I do think that they are going to do better than most predictions.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jessica.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I can't believe that I have a higher bet than Byron York on this one. So have $90 on the GOP going over that 231, $10 on the Democrats there. I'm looking at races like in Rhode Island where \uc2 Allan Fung\uc2  is up eight points over the Democrat state treasurer, looking at Texas 15 where the DCCC and the House Majority PAC aren't even sending money, and worrying about how election night is going to look.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. I'm going to do a really lightning Winners and Losers.\par \par \uc2 YORK: Winner, Truth Social, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's social network. \uc2 Google\uc2  approved it for distribution for this week. They hadn't had a lot of good news lately. This is.\par \par \uc2 Loser is \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 , the U.K. prime minister, already had to dump one of her most important figures in the government. Speculation she is not going to last the year.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: My winner is \uc2 NASA\uc2 . They said that they were able to successfully change the trajectory of an asteroid with their DART spacecraft. My loser is Nury Martinez. So is the former L.A. city council president who got caught on audio saying racist remarks along with a few others and had to resign.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jessica, really quickly?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Winner is the Sandy Hook parents, the families. Money doesn't bring back your children, but big lawsuit, their win over Alex Jones. And then loser is the fishermen who cheated, who got indicted this week for stuffing their fish.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The cheating fisherman, there you go.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 It's Friday. It's time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Mistakes get made, and it could end in Armageddon.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I wouldn't necessarily have chosen the words the way he did at that particular event.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Off in the distance, artillery fire continues as \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is looking to put pressure on the Ukrainian army.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Putin is in a corner. And the war is not going the way that he expected.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You're voting to start a third nuclear war with Russia and China.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you regret signing onto the letter?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Oh, absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It had the classic earmarks, but it wasn't true.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It is not my fault if people don't look up definitions.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Gas is $6.50 in my district. First, I blame the Saudis.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It cost me about $28 at Taco Bell for lunch. That is a lot of chalupas.\par \par \uc2 TULSI GABBARD, (D) FORMER HAWAII REPRESENTATIVE: Today's Democratic Party is controlled by fanatical ideologues who hate freedom.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I love Venn diagrams. The circles, right? Three, usually.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The need for this committee to hear from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  goes beyond our fact-finding. He is required to answer for his actions.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Now it's the unselect committee of political hacks and thugs, and that's a scam.\par \par \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 , (D-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: I hope he comes. I'm going to punch him out.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It could be an election week again, just listening to that.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Let's hope not. I'm an old man. I can't take that anymore.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: One week.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. Make it a great weekend. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020221015eiae00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Crime Key Issue With 25 Days Until Midterms; Candidates Face Off On Crime During Debates; How Crime Could Make Or Break Key Midterm Races; Prosecution Could Rest Soon In Danchenko Fed Trial; Sen. \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  (R- LA), Is Being Interviewed About His Reaction To What President Biden Is Saying That GOP Will Make Inflation Worse; One Of Five Counts Dismissed Against Igor Danchenko; Steele Dossier Source Trial Puts Focus On \uc2 FBI\uc2 ; Rising Energy Prices Put Squeeze On Americans; Warnock And Walker Face Off In Georgia Senate Debate; Fetterman Interview Renews Health Questions; 82- Year-Old Wheelchair Bound Man Stabbed In LA; Wawa Closes Two Center City Philly Stores Over Crime\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Lawrence Jones, Nate Foy, David Spunt, Jonathan  Turley, Josh Kraushaar, Jeff Flock \par \uc2 8,296 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: And here we go. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. So, in Michigan, you will find four of the top 20 most violent smaller cities in the whole country. And last night, Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  and her opponent, Tudor Dixon, slugged it out in the debates that are happening across the country. Governor Whitmer has said in 2020, that she supported defunding the police in spirit, she said. But last night, here's what she said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GRETCHEN WHITMER\uc2  (D-MI): Investing in law enforcement is what we have done, what we need to continue to do to keep people safe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Her opponent went after that, and a lot more, Here's a peek.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TUDOR DIXON (R-MI) GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: The last four years have been disappointing, at best. Everything is more expensive. Our community communities are less safe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, switching over to last night's big debate in Wisconsin. There, we see incumbent Ron Johnson facing off against Mandela Barnes who has had to backpedal his own language around defunding police.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): One thing you have to use, you have to keep violent criminals in jail. And you have to support law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 MANDELA BARNES (D-WI) SENATE CANDIDATE: Do you not claim credit for that for the release of two to 3000 people while at the same time attacking me for it?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: But we were told is going to be very non-violent, people have completely rehabilitated themselves. That's (INAUDIBLE) here in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 BARNES: This is the hypocrisy. He is he is attacking me for the same thing that he is doing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right, that's what debates are for folks, very interesting to see what's happening as these candidates face off in these forums. And we got a big one coming up tonight. So, here's the Wisconsin race: 46 to 52. Ron Johnson was behind by a lot in Wisconsin. And now as you can see, according to these polls, he has pulled ahead, of course, the biggest polls on Election Day.\par \par \uc2 So, with that we bring in Lawrence Jones, he's just back from Wisconsin, he's been basically crisscrossing the country. That's why they should make, call your show cross country, right? And he's talking to voters, it's very, very interesting what you're learning from them and that show is tomorrow night at 10:00 Eastern. Lawrence, what was your feel in general, for this Wisconsin race when you were there?\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE JONES, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, it's clearly, inflation just like on a national level is the number one issue, but crime is definitely number two. And, you know, look at the way that the opponents attack each other on the issues. Before this race, the political pundits were told to leave the race in Wisconsin, that Mandela Barnes had won that; Ron Johnson was going to be defeated.\par \par \uc2 But he then started in these last few weeks to change his attacks to crime, defunding the police, re-offenders being out, and what Mandela Barnes want supported, apparently, apparently, he's backed off of that because of the debate. But as you know, he was once the golden child for the Democratic Party, they really forced all those candidates out and the Democratic primary.\par \par \uc2 He wasn't properly vetted. But now, you see Ron Johnson hitting them on the earlier this week, and I was there -- Ron Johnson was up by four points, and then new poll that you just showed, now he's up by six points. So, obviously, the attack when it comes to crime is working.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, it's so interesting when these candidates started to be looked for. And to put themselves forward, we were in the heat of the George Floyd moment in the country. And a lot of people were talking about to find the police. And now, it's coming back to bite them because crime has risen so much. Here's a little piece of your focus group, we have just, I think, one of the voters here. And of course, there'll be a lot more when we see it on Saturday. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ART SERNA, WISCONSIN VOTER: There's practical implications to policy. And when we get to, I'll use the term that's been going around too woke, in the sense it's not practically meaningful any longer. So, I do think that politicians and political parties need to think about the entirety of the human condition, right. There are reasons why people get driven to a point and we need to be able to address those things. But I think overall, the political national narrative around justice reform has kind of gone off the rails.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, it's interesting, because when we scan around the country, we look at these races. Tim Ryan spoke out. He's the Senate candidate, of course, running against J.D. Vance. He spoke out about getting rid of cash bail a while back, watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And we're seeing states and cash bail state by state, would you support that nationally?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 TIM RYAN\uc2  (D), OHIO SENATE CANDIDATE: Yes, I think the bail system is inherently unfair, I would be for eliminating it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, now, that is not what he is saying on the campaign trail, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Look, if I can make one prediction based on what I've seen across the country, those candidates and especially elected officials, that are political chameleons probably won't win this cycle. And what I mean by that is there are a lot of people that base their policy positions, based on the time that we're in what I'm looking at the consequences of their actions. The people that I had on that panel, they were not anti-criminal justice reform.\par \par \uc2 What they were anti was releasing violent offenders back on the street, and you've seen that across the country. I know Democrats want to make this emotional election. But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is not on the ballot right now, you really got to campaign on the issue and people care about the kitchen table issue. So, they're going to care about their pocketbooks, but they're also going to care about their lives and their family's safety.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We're about to talk to Georgia voters, but I just want to play this really quick. Um, this is former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  and former President Trump around the same time in the cycle, here's what they were doing on the road, watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , 44TH PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Philly, I'm back here two years later. Because our job is not yet done. I need you as fired up as you were in 2008.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , 45TH PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And just two days, the people of Tennessee are going to elect \uc2 Marsha Blackburn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, this week, President Biden was in Colorado, he is going to make an appearance in Pennsylvania, with Fetterman but he is really behind the scenes here, Lawrence. Quick thought.\par \par \uc2 JONES: It's because the candidates don't want him there. He's not a popular president. When you have over 70 percent of the country saying that they believe that we're heading down the wrong path, the wrong direction. Why would any candidate want him there on stage with them?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Its awkward.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Very awkward.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks, Lawrence. Thank you very much. Always good to see you and great to see your show.\par \par \uc2 JONES: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: "CROSS COUNTRY" coming up tomorrow night. So, this awful story just adds to so many of these. In the past two weeks, two police officers in Connecticut who thought that they were responding to a crime had been lured to that scene in an apparent ambush. Officer Alex Hamzy and Sergeant Dustin Demonte shot and killed, a third officer was injured, and investigators say that it was all apparently a cruel setup. Correspondent Nate Foy reporting live from New York. Hi, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha. It appears a 9-1-1 call about a domestic disturbance was a lie. And Connecticut State Police say a man used that lie to lure those officers to a home where he could kill two of them and injure a third. It's one of multiple attacks on police this week. This is not an isolated incident. Take a look at this map. Since Monday of this week, 12 police officers have been shot in this country four of them killed. You see the attacks here all over the country. Police say a big reason why is an anti-police culture.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PATRICK YOES, FRATERNAL ORDER OF POLICE PRESIDENT: You know, when there's a problem you call law enforcement officer. Well, guess what, we're calling you now. We need your help. We need people to step up right now and start supporting law enforcement. Speak out against those with radical changes in these, these failed experiments that are making our communities less safe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FOY: Well, Martha, another problem is keeping dangerous criminals locked up after they're arrested. On Wednesday, a man linked to murder and armed robbery shot three Philadelphia SWAT officers. Philadelphia's progressive D.A. Larry Crasner is often criticized for soft on crime policies. Former U.S. attorney \uc2 Bill McSwain\uc2  blames Crasner for promoting the disrespect of law enforcement. McSwain says it started with chants of "F the police" and no good cops in a racist system at his DEA victory party. And this problem is not confined to any one city.\par \par \uc2 Take a look, so far this year, 56 officers have been killed. Still two and a half months left, we are on pace to have the most ever on record in a single year. Last year, 64 officers were killed, which for now is the most in a single year. So, the trend, certainly not good. And there are real repercussions. According to the FOP as well, police retirements right now we're up 45 percent from last year, and resignations are up 20 percent. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. So, what's going to happen. I mean, you can just imagine as the job becomes more and more dangerous, it's going to be tougher and tougher to have good strong police officers in full force out there. Nate, thank you very much. Nate Foy in New York. So, as I mentioned our democracy 2022 coverage continues with Georgia voters today. We're going to speak with them live here in New York. They came up to talk to us as Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and Democrat Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  prepared to square off in what could be a make-or-break Senate debate. It happens tonight.\par \par \uc2 And a Russian national and main source of the Steele dossier is in court right now. This is a fascinating trial. As Special Counsel \uc2 John Durham\uc2 's last stand in the Russia probe reveals that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  went to great lengths to prove salacious dirt about then candidate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But unfortunately, they couldn't get the corroboration and went ahead with the whole thing anyway. Jonathan Turley after this,\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM JORDAN (R-OH): I'm frankly at the point where I don't know who you can believe at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , I think that's where the country is, and I think they're going to factor that into the election just 26 days from, from today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What is case this is? Special Counsel \uc2 John Durham\uc2  could rest his case as soon as today in the trial of a Russian who was a source for the so-called Steele dossier on former President Trump. His name is Igor Danchenko, and he is charged with five counts of lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  when asked about where he was getting information and what he was doing.\par \par \uc2 But Durham is also taking aim at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 's investigation into candidate Trump and Russia during the 2016 election. They are squarely in the focus here as well. We're going to talk to Jonathan Turley with his analysis of this from the legal perspective. But first to Justice Correspondent David Spunt to get us up to speed on what's happening today in that fascinating courtroom in Alexandria, Virginia. Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha, and we have some breaking news in the last 45 seconds. One of the counts against Igor Danchenko, the defendant, was just actually thrown out by the judge.\par \par \uc2 Producer Jake Gibson just walked out a couple of moments ago and told me not only to \uc2 John Durham\uc2 , the Special Prosecutor, completely finished his case; the defense, Igor Danchenko's attorneys, announced they won't even be putting on a case. So, this case will go to the jury on Monday. Judge Anthony Trenga will read your instructions on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The first count, Martha, deals with Igor Danchenko's communications with a democratic operative named Charles Dolan.\par \par \uc2 Dolan has about 30 years going back in democratic politics. And we know that Danchenko and Dolan spoke to each other regarding some allegations about some phrase inside the Trump campaign.\par \par \uc2 Igor Danchenko took some of that information, put it in the Steele dossier. Well, it turns out Dolan did not get that information from a Republican source as he told Danchenko. He admitted he lied to Danchenko.\par \par \uc2 The judge threw out that charge because there was some debate whether or not Danchenko actually spoke to Dolan on a telephone by voice or spoke to him, I shouldn't say, spoke to him or typed an e-mail to him.\par \par \uc2 The judge felt that, that charge needed to be thrown out. The other counts, the other four counts of lying to \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents remain on the table. The jury will hear that Monday. But Martha, the bottom line in this entire case, and it can be confusing.\par \par \uc2 There is a lot of names there's a lot of different countries and meetings involved. The bottom line here, Special Counsel \uc2 John Durham\uc2 , who began his investigation 3-1/2 years ago. This is his second trial looking at the origins of the Russia investigation.\par \par \uc2 And while on paper, this trial is about Igor Danchenko, in lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . Inside the courtroom, the special counsel has really uncovered a lot of problems within the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , dating back to 2016 and early 2017.\par \par \uc2 So, I'll end right there. But the big news, the judge just dismissed one count against Igor Danchenko. And the defense is resting. Meaning, they are not even putting on a case. Showing that they're fairly confident. Not fairly confident, very confident. Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. David Spunt, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in constitutional attorney Jonathan Turley. He is also a Fox News contributor.\par \par \uc2 I want to get to the fact that several analysts, including Kim Strassel at the Wall Street Journal and our colleague, Andy McCarthy, think that, in many ways, is the \uc2 FBI\uc2  that is under the microscope here in this trial.\par \par \uc2 But I do want to get your reaction to the fact that one of these charges has been dropped against Danchenko, and that his side is not going to put on a case.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, first of all, that was the weakest of the five counts. I was actually surprised it was included. I think the court is right.\par \par \uc2 In some ways, it strengthens the prosecution case not to have that count, because I felt that it was really something that was begging for a hung jury or an acquittal.\par \par \uc2 So, they'll go forward on those stronger, more direct counts. As for the defense, not presenting a case, that may not reflect confidence as much as strategy. You know, sometimes the defense really doesn't have much to put on while risking a possible impeachment or cross examination that could go wrong.\par \par \uc2 And David is certainly right, that you sort of look at the case in chief of the government and you decide whether you'll gain that much by presenting more of your own witnesses.\par \par \uc2 In this case, the defense did robust cross examination. So, I think they're going to play this conservatively and not present a case which can present targets for the prosecution. And so, you can lose ground as well as gain ground.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I want to read a quote from Kim's Strassel's piece on this this case. And \uc2 FBI\uc2 's -- Durham's \uc2 FBI\uc2  indictment is what it's called. She says, "Partisanship and incompetence aren't crimes, so the \uc2 FBI\uc2  isn't in the dark. But Mr. Durham is making the case for the public, and it is as ugly as they come."\par \par \uc2 And this, from Andy McCarthy. "Yes, Igor Danchenko was on trial, but the spotlight ought to be on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and on what Durham's final report will have to say about the nation's premier federal law enforcement agency."\par \par \uc2 Because really what we have learned here to vote layperson, I think, Jonathan, is that everybody was getting paid. Igor Danchenko had been on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  -- on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  pay list as well, to the tune of 100s of 1000s of dollars as an informant.\par \par \uc2 They told \uc2 Christopher Steele\uc2  in October of 2016, just weeks before the presidential election, they give him a million dollars to confirm what was in this dossier.\par \par \uc2 And what we learned is that the sources didn't even speak to the people that they said gave them this information and dirt on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . And then they turned it all in to get -- to get a FISA warrant, so that they could get a hook into the Trump team through \uc2 Carter Page\uc2 , who says even though he was also an informant, at one point, he was never paid a dime. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: No, it's very alarming. And one of the most important aspects of this special counsel is he will hopefully produce a report. You know, the statute of limitations is running out on a lot of crime. So, there is a limitation on how far he could likely go.\par \par \uc2 But the thing that most of us had hoped for with the appointment of a special counsel is to have report. Because it is really breathtaking how much either Special Counsel Mueller did not know or chose not to put into his report. We have learned a lot from this special counsel.\par \par \uc2 Including how the Hillary Clinton campaign funded the Steele dossier, denied they were funding it, they hid that funding through their attorney, Marc Elias. And they ultimately got false statements into the media. They put forward false claims into the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But then on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  side, it was really breathtaking. I mean, it had the feel of a canned hunt. I mean, they were saying, look, we'll give you a million dollars if you can bag Trump. And then they went ahead and hired the same person used -- I mean, they previously -- they kept as an informant, the person used in the Steele dossier.\par \par \uc2 And so, this cross pollenization (PH) raises additional questions. This is all very, very disturbing.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: And I think that this report from the special counsel is going to be one of the most important reports that we're going to get out of any of these investigations.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, let's hope something comes of it. I mean, it's good to have a report. My mind just keeps going back to \uc2 Jim Comey\uc2 , sitting there with president -- with candidate Trump and saying, gee, sir, we really want to show you this. We've got some really bad stuff on you. And it's about to come out in the press when they knew that they couldn't corroborate what was in there.\par \par \uc2 It is absolutely -- I mean, I hope that report -- I hope there is some meat, some meat on the bones in that report.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, thank you very much. Jonathan Turley.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, President Biden warning that a Republican controlled Congress could drive prices even higher. Republican Senator John Cassidy is here in New York with us to respond.\par \par \uc2 Also, ahead. Voters came up here from Georgia to talk to us about what's going on in their home state in the race between \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 . Very interesting discussion we're looking forward to with them.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): So, President Biden saying that the inflation that is at a 40-year high is not his fault, as voters grapple with higher prices. I don't have to tell you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Look, I mean, look at this. Food is up 30 percent. OK? Milks up 15 percent. You see how it all goes. There you can afford cappuccino because it's up 15 percent, at this point.\par \par \uc2 Also, gas and electricity, utilities, fuel, oil, all sky high as new polling finds 46 percent of Americans view their personal financial situation as poor.\par \par \uc2 Think about that. 46 percent of the country says my financial situation right now is in poor shape. That is not a good number. That's a nine-point leap since March of this year.\par \par \uc2 So, that is going to have -- that's going to leave a mark as we say, most likely when it comes to the election just a few weeks away. President Biden warning that their finances will only get worse, he says, though, if Republicans are elected. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We've got an election in the month. Voters have to the side. Democrats are working to bring down the cost of things that talk about around the kitchen table. Republican wins, inflation is going to get worse is that simple.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Republican Senator \uc2 Bill Cassidy\uc2  of Louisiana joins me now. He's a member of the Senate Finance Committee. Senator, good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY (R-LA): Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Nice to have you in New York. Thanks for stopping by. What's your reaction to what the president says there?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: It's hard to keep from laughing. Inflation was 1.9 percent when he took office under Republican policies. And now, it's been 13 percent since he took office, 8.5 percent year over year.\par \par \uc2 The president's policies have either been inept or incompetent or you name even malevolent, but they are hurting the American people. And this midterm will be a referendum on those policies.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, here is interesting, though, because when you poll people, this new poll from the AP-NORC, which is the same one that found that 46 percent thought their financial condition just felt like they were in a poor condition right now.\par \par \uc2 They say 35 percent say it's out of his control. It's not his fault. And we've heard for the administration over and over, that it's worse in other places that this is all because of Putin.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: So, the American Rescue Plan, as they called it, it could have been called the American recession plan was -- as $1.9 trillion of federal spending, every Republican voted against it, every Democrat for.\par \par \uc2 A communist left of center said it would ignite inflation. And it did. The Inflation Reduction Act, so-called they just passed is predicted to increase inflation over the next two years. And forgiving student loans, adds more out there to drive up inflation.\par \par \uc2 Now, these are their policies which have been uniformly supported by Democrats, uniformly opposed by Republicans. And that's where the responsibility at least, in large measure are lies for these higher prices.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, politically, I guess right now, with four weeks to go, he is going to continue to deflect. He doesn't want to say it's her fault, because he's got candidates that he's worried about across the country.\par \par \uc2 But when you step out of that world, you look at the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , they said, you know, we're probably heading into a global crisis sort of situation. \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 , all of these smart folks have been watching the economy for a long time, say, look, we're already in really bad shape.\par \par \uc2 And, in fact, in 2023, it's going to get worse. So, would it make more sense for him to be more honest with the American people about what's going on?\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: There's a cynicism that this administration is showing, reportedly asking the Saudis to not increase the price of fuel until after the midterm. Part of their debt loan forgiveness expires after the midterm.\par \par \uc2 So, they are using these inflationary policies, if you will, but they're trying to mitigate it until after the election. Now, that is wrong.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you think about that? I mean, you know, we haven't really heard that kind of negotiation or begging or whatever you want to call it with OPEC since \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: Yes, that's because -- that's because since \uc2 Jimmy Carter\uc2 , we've had a policy of developing North American energy resources. This administration's policies have deliberately attempted to tamp down a development of North American energy resources.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And it worked.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: It worked!\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It worked! That's the bad news.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: And now we're paying -- yes, 15 percent more for natural gas, you know, I think your statistic was 30 percent more for utilities. It's incredible. Think about it. If he had not stopped the Keystone XL Pipeline the first week he took presidency that would have been completed, and there would be 800,000 barrels a day coming from Canada to the Gulf Coast, my state in Texas, to be refined for our use. They killed that the first week.\par \par \uc2 They have not granted permits to drill off the coast of Louisiana. I'm told that the permits were applied for in January. Once they are granted within six months, our natural gas and oil becoming to be refined on our shores to create jobs and to lower prices. They've not given those permits this many months later.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Incredible. Thank you very much. Senator Cassidy, always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY: Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks for being here today. So coming up, as their Senate contenders, former \uc2 NFL\uc2  star \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  get ready for their big one and only debate tonight. We are joined live here in New York. We're so glad they came to visit us, just like Senator Cassidy did, a group of Georgia voters coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So it could be a crucial moment in a race that could leave control of the Senate potentially in limbo if they go to a runoff here. Georgia Democrat Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  face off in their one and only debate which happens tonight. Our Fox Power Rankings have this as a toss up, one of just four Senate races that are still out. There's a yellow ones, there's four states on that list.\par \par \uc2 So let's talk to Georgia voters and find out what they want to hear tonight. Donna Sharpe is a Republican, Marcus McCrary is a Democrat -- McCray, excuse me, and Bernadette Wright is an Independent. Great to have all of you here.\par \par \uc2 Bernadette, let me start with you. How important is this debate to you tonight.\par \par \uc2 BERNADETTE WRIGHT, GEORGIA INDEPENDENT VOTER: This debate is extremely important. When you look at how the candidates are talking about one another, no one is talking about the issues. And I feel like this debate is an opportunity to see whether or not \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  can really go to Washington and do what he says he can do. At this point, from everything I'm hearing, it just looks like he is somewhat of a puppet. And so, there's a puppet master behind it but I want to see what he does on his own without someone giving him a cue card, or without someone telling him exactly what to say. So I'm looking forward to tonight's debate so I can really see who both the candidates are.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So he has said, I'm this country boy, I'm not that smart. And he's a preacher. He's a smart man, wears his nice suit. So he's going to show up and embarrass me. He's kind of lowering expectations here, Donna, for his own performance. What are you going to be watching for tonight? How important is this to your vote?\par \par \uc2 DONNA SHARPE, GEORGIA REPUBLICAN VOTER: It's important. I think that I'm going to be looking at how he can answer the questions because I, you know, feel the same way. But I think he could be up to the task. I think he can - - I think it's good for him to be able to express himself because there's been so much out there slandering him.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, he has, you know, he had this whole situation with the girlfriend who said that she that he paid for the abortion. Here's a little exchange with one of our reporters at Fox Digital. Watch this. We'll get your reaction.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are there other women out there? Are there going to be other stories coming out there that you're going to have to deal with between now and Election Day?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: Well, you know, I think they really want to win the seat that bad, so they're coming up with anything they can come up with. But they don't realize that they walk in a grizzly bear. And I wanted everything I set my mind to and so now they got o fight on their hands. You know, before I'm just going to beat him by a little bit, but now I'm going to beat him by a lot.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: This is controversy over the last week, though, does it make you look like a hypocrite?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: No because I say this a lot.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Marcus, you know, you're a Democrat, what do you think about \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 ? And are you originally from Georgia? Did you grow up there? You know, what's your impression of him?\par \par \uc2 MARCUS MCCRAY, GEORGIA DEMOCRATIC VOTER: I grew up in Alabama. You know, I grew up knowing who \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  was and moved to Georgia, been here long enough to understand like the import that he is to the state. But I don't think that this is the test that he is up to. Like I think that we should actually be fielding candidates that actually qualified for the job. And I don't know what \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has done to prove to us that he has command of the issues, or any real reason to be in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, Bernadette, when you look at this, you know, abortion is an issue that a lot of Democrat voter dollars have gone towards, especially in the in the past few weeks, about $73 million, almost a quarter of their ads, third of their ads are focused on this. How important is that issue to you when you vote?\par \par \uc2 WRIGHT: To be honest with you, the Supreme Court has spoken, so the law of the land is the law of the land. I feel like that is an issue but it's not the primary issue. People are hurting economically. People are hurting -- trying to figure out how to put food on their tables. Jobs are an issue. There are more important things for people to talk about, not to say that it's not important but it's not a primary focus.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So you're an independent voter, which way are you leaning now?\par \par \uc2 WRIGHT: In the government -- in the governor's race?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: In the Senate race.\par \par \uc2 WRIGHT: In the Senate race, I'm actually leaning towards Ralph Warnock.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, because?\par \par \uc2 WRIGHT: Because \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has not demonstrated the ability to be able to lead from what I've been hearing so far. And anyone who stands up and says like you commented a few moments ago that they're not very smart, and that they're expecting the other candidate to embarrass them. I want someone smart to go there. I want someone who's able to be able to think creatively and out of the box, because if he can't think for himself and who's really going to Washington.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And what about in the governor's race?\par \par \uc2 WRIGHT: In the governor's race, I'm leaning towards camp and looking forward to the debate that's going to happen this week.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So you are truly an independent. You're looking at one Republican and one Democrat in the Georgia race. What are your friends, your women friends, saying about how important the abortion decision was to them and in Georgia?\par \par \uc2 SHARPE: Well, I think it's not just a Democrat issue, because I teach at a Catholic school. And I think a lot of Republicans are geared up to go fight for the unborn child. And so I have a lot of friends that are that way. But then I have other friends that are worried about the women's rights.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And is it for most people you talk to and, Marcus, let me ask you this one. Is that a driving issue for them? Are they more concerned about crime, more concerned about inflation? What are people talking about when you talk about this vote of what matters to them?\par \par \uc2 MCCRAY: So I think people really care more about like the kitchen table issue. So they care more about the economy. There's been discussions in my circles about abortion. And what has surprised me is that, there's been conservative women who've been concerned about the fact that we moved so quickly from what we thought was settled law based on what we heard from all of the Supreme Court candidates that we saw come forward over the last few years.\par \par \uc2 But I don't think it is the thing that is going to motivate most people, and it's not going to affect most people's lives. I think we need to be realistic about that. Like not everybody's having an abortion but everybody has a bill to pay at hand (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, I don't think he could really put it much better than that. What do you think about the job markets that the President has done so far, President Biden?\par \par \uc2 MCCRAY: I think he's actually played pretty well with a losing hand. He's actually been able to get a lot of his policy initiatives out. I don't know that we're going to know what those policy policies look like for a while, right? So we are -- we just passed the past Inflation Reduction Act, we're still fighting with whatever it is that the fed is going to do. Whatever that looks like, it's probably going to extend beyond his term.\par \par \uc2 We won't know the effects of what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  looks like for another 30 years, just like we don't know what all of the decisions that happened for \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , like we're just starting to get rid of the folks that were on the court.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 MCCRAY: It takes a while to realize that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So Marcus is giving the President a pass on inflation and on rising gas prices. Do you agree, Bernadette?\par \par \uc2 WRIGHT: No.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Why?\par \par \uc2 WRIGHT: I don't -- I feel like he could have did a better job with inflation, with all this spending that's coming out of Washington. We're printing money, and the value of the dollar is increasingly going down. I believe that a lot of the decisions that have been made in Washington have led us to the situation that we're in now. I believe it's a joke to say that we're not really in a recession when every time we go to the gas pump, every time we go to the grocery store.\par \par \uc2 We know we're in a recession. Every household in America knows it, why Washington doesn't know what, I'm confused about it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Quick final thought?\par \par \uc2 SHARPE: I mean, I agree. Well, actually, I disagree that he's doing a good job. And I don't think he was dealt a bad hand. I think he's made what he inherited works, in my opinion.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much, Donna and Marcus, and Bernadette. I really appreciate you coming up and stopping on your way back from Boston, Marcus. Good to see you all. I hope we'll talk again. It's going to very interesting to see what happens tonight. Thanks, guys.\par \par \uc2 MCCRAY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SHARPE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And in Pennsylvania, Democrats Senate candidate \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  steel dealing with a television sit down. That kind of backfired by raising new questions about his recovery from a stroke. His wife has just addressed this today. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GISELE BARRETO FETTERMAN, SECOND LADY OF PENNSYLVANIA: I don't like saying rage because I think that's a really unhealthy feeling and when you feel those things that only harms yourself. But I just, you know, what, a disservice that she did to not only my husband, but to anyone facing a disability and working through it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A story, a 15 year old boy charged with shooting seven people and killing five of them, including an off duty police officer. It happened yesterday in Raleigh, North Carolina. The injured victims include another police officer, a 16-year-old boy and a woman just days away from her 60th birthday. Here's the city's mayor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARY-ANN BALDWIN, RALEIGH, NORTH CAROLINA MAYOR: We have to end this mindless gun violence that is happening in our country. We have work to do but there are too many victims and we have to wake up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The 15-year-old suspect on the run for hours before police cornered him in a home and arrested him. No word yet on his possible motive.\par \par \uc2 So back to the midterms and a key toss up race Senate race in Pennsylvania, Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is recovering from a stroke but a sit down with NBC News is creating some new questions about his health after relying on closed captioning to read reporter's questions. The reporter said that when she talked to Fetterman without the captioning, it was "not clear." He could understand what she was saying. Now Fetterman's wife Gisele is calling all of it appalling.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARRETO-FETTERMAN: I don't know how there were not consequences, right? I mean, there are consequences for folks in these positions who are any of the isms. I mean, she was ableist and that's what she was in her interview. It was appalling to the entire disability community. And I think to journalism, so I was shocked. I'm still really upset about it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Here now Josh Kraushaar, senior political correspondent for Axios and Fox News contributor. Josh, great to have you with us today. I find this whole thing really very interesting on all sides because it seemed to me that the reporter was dispassionate and pretty clear about what was -- what the situation was like before and after the closed captioning, which in my mind is providing information about exactly what she reported, based on what she saw and heard.\par \par \uc2 Now, there's all kinds of, you know, backlash against this reporter all over \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . What do you think about this?\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes. Martha, it's pretty remarkable that you have a candidate's wife, essentially threatening a reporter for doing her job, for reporting what she saw, like you said. And I don't think it's helpful for the Fetterman campaign politically, was not the strongest interview for Fetterman both with the closed captioning or the small talk beforehand.\par \par \uc2 And when you're showing that you're so nervous about reporters reporting what they saw about his health, you're almost showing that you're worried about this race. It's not a sign of political confidence when you're threatening reporters are doing their job. When you're calling on, you know, other reporters to censor their observations about what they saw from that interview. It makes you wonder why Fetterman's campaign, why Fetterman's wife are so worried about what that interview showed the viewers who saw it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. I mean, he had to do a sit down interview because he keeps saying that he is able to serve in the Senate, right? And that's the argument that he was making. And clearly, they must have agreed, OK, we're going to shoot over your shoulder, we're going to show obviously, that there's a computer in front of you that has closed captioning. This is the same setup as we understand it, that's going to exist in the debate.\par \par \uc2 This is what he said. If you're offended or you would not consider voting for me because I'm having our interview over closed captioning, that's kind of surreal to me why anybody would want to make that an issue. You know, I'm curious what you think about the media response to this as well, Josh, because you talked about the political response and how they're handling it. But are you surprised that some of the media are lashing out at this reporter as well?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Well, look, voters are going to make up their own minds. The reporter did a very good job asking the right questions. They put the entire interview online. It was very transparent in terms of the questions and the whole context of everything that was asked. So there shouldn't be any criticism of how, you know, NBC handled and it was a very strong interview from an up and coming political reporter.\par \par \uc2 From a political standpoint, it does show that the Fetterman campaign is very online, you know, that a lot of voters in Pennsylvania aren't on social media, they're not on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  all the time. But it's sometimes feels like some of these campaigns, including the Fetterman camp, feel like when someone says something on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , there's some criticism on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that they want to engage and they want to blow it up.\par \par \uc2 And that's a -- it's a good reminder. We see in a lot of campaigns these days, that social media doesn't reflect a real life. And it feels like the campaign and some of the principles with the Fetterman campaign are very engaged in social media, but not listening to where the voters are and what the voters are paying attention to.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's interesting. I mean, he's had all of these glowing profiles done on him, that he was a sort of renegade, Mayor of Braddock, and all of these, you know, very fawning pieces over the years before we got to this stage of the campaign. And, you know, perhaps he's just not used to this kind of reporting, Josh.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: He's got a good story to tell. Like tell the story, don't attack the press who are reporting, in fact checking and offering what they see in their own interview. Tell your story and respond to that, to that -- to those questions. Whenever you're attacking the press, you know that there's something, you're losing politically when you're going after the media.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Maybe (inaudible) just to move on and keep going on the campaign trail, and get ready for that debate on October 25th. And we'll be watching. Josh, thank you very much, Josh Kraushaar, good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Thanks, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the crime crisis in Pennsylvania causing local chain Wawa to close up shop in some locations because of safety and security challenges. Number of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  did this as well. I mean, this is what's going on in the country. Stores are saying forget it. We can have things like this happen. This incident that happened last month when people just brazenly walked in and took whatever they wanted.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Police in Los Angeles are looking for the man seen in this video brazenly stabbing an 82-year-old man in a wheelchair as he ate inside of Taco Bell. What is going on in the world? This is so awful. We pause it before he plunges the knife. I mean, it's just too terrible to see. It happened last Monday in broad daylight.\par \par \uc2 Police say that the victim is recovering, thank God, and is in stable condition. But what a scare for this poor man and what is going on? I don't know.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, remember this story, nearly a hundred people ransacking a Wawa convenience store in Philadelphia last month. Now the company headquartered in that city suburbs say we don't know if we can stay in our own neighborhood anymore because of the crime. Fox Business correspondent Jeff Flock reports live from the aforementioned Philadelphia. Hi, Jeff.\par \par \uc2 JEFF FLOCK, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Martha, hello to you and hello from the Wawa store not long to be open. This is what it looks like. And, you know, usually when they close the store, it's because they don't have enough business. That's not the case here. Plenty of people but the problem is safety and security.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at the statement from Wawa. They say continued safety and security challenges and business factors have made it increasingly difficult to remain open in these two locations, this store and another one down the street. And the neighborhood we're in it's not a bad neighborhood. I mean City Hall is just two blocks down there. That's where the mayor holds up., \uc2 Jim Kenney\uc2 . He points out Wawa still has 38 stores in Philadelphia and they say it's their hometown, but not going anywhere. But crime has certainly caused this, has become a factor certainly in the Senate race.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Oz was on Fox earlier today talking about crime in Philadelphia in the state that he is running in to represent from the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: Listen, public safety is the cornerstone of a healthy economy. Just last night, a major chain said they're going to remove to their stores from downtown Philadelphia because of concerns about safety. Families are leaving Philadelphia, they don't feel safe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FLOCK: I leave you, Martha, with the picture of the district attorney here, \uc2 Larry Krasner\uc2 , a Democrat who, you know, tries to deemphasize the prosecution of shoplifting in stores like Wawa. Crime on the increase here, retail theft up 54% in the last year, armed robbery up 52%. It's tough out there and businesses don't want to do business where it's dangerous.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Jeff, thank you very much. We'll be in -- we're in Pennsylvania the week after next, after the big debate there. And that's THE STORY for this Friday. I will see in an hour though back from "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020221015eiae00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Interview with \uc2 Sarah Palin\b0\par\par\uc2 Harris Faulkner \par \uc2 1,062 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Live Event\par \uc2 FXLV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LARRY ELDER\uc2 , RADIO TALK SHOW HOST: They have got two issues. Their issue is abortion, and their issue is, by the way, these Republicans over there are systemically racist.\par \par \uc2 They have got that, and that's it. They can't talk about prices. They can't talk about gas prices. They can't talk about the border. They can't talk about the pullout of Afghanistan. All they can do is pound the table about abortion and about calling the people on the right systemically racist. That's all they have got.\par \par \uc2 And, come November, they are going to be toast in the House and hopefully in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS FAULKNER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: \uc2 Larry Elder\uc2  there saying Democrats don't have a whole lot to run on, and it looks like they are sticking to one issue that could be driving Democrats. Could be.\par \par \uc2 A new poll shows that June's Supreme Court decision overturning Roe vs. Wade did, in fact, light a fire under Democratic voters; 69 percent of them voted -- of those voters say it's motivated them to vote in the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 But, among Republicans, the primary motivator is still the economy, and those poll numbers reflected in the barrage of ads voters are now seeing. A report this week says Democrat candidates and aligned groups have spent some $18 million in an abortion and blitz -- ad blitz, more than 100 abortion-focused television ads airing in battleground states.\par \par \uc2 And while Democrats focus their messaging on that, Republicans are leaning into ads that remind voters about our grim economic conditions and the safety challenges because of the raging crime.\par \par \uc2 "In Focus" now, we welcome \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2 , former Alaska governor and GOP vice presidential nominee. She's currently a candidate for Alaska's lone United States House seat.\par \par \uc2 Welcome.\par \par \uc2 FMR. GOV. \uc2 SARAH PALIN\uc2  (R-AK): Thank you so much, Harris.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 PALIN: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Let's start right there, because I really want to get your take on reaching voters.\par \par \uc2 What do you see is going on behind the scenes there with voters?\par \par \uc2 PALIN: Well, Democrats are certainly focusing on issues that they want to wee-wee people up. They want to get them all stirred up, and in terms of crisis and drama.\par \par \uc2 But normal people, normal voters, they're talking about gas, groceries, grandkids. They're talking about long-term solutions to the challenges that we're facing. I think Democrats are wasting their money on those ads, by the way, the abortion ads and all, because that's not the issue.\par \par \uc2 The issue, the economy, they don't have good answers. It doesn't matter how much money they're throwing at an ad. They don't have good answers.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: I'm sure you saw the inflation numbers today.\par \par \uc2 PALIN: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: I mean, we are red hot right now.\par \par \uc2 PALIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: And the Fed wants to cool down the economy with higher interest rates.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we are getting farther and farther away from Americans being able to afford the American dream now.\par \par \uc2 PALIN: That's right.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: It's affecting everything.\par \par \uc2 PALIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 So, even more practical than waiting for the feds to do this or that is if Biden would allow access to our natural resources and allow energy to be produced in America. We have the U.S. domestic supplies all over the place. We have an overabundance of natural resources, instead of asking the Saudis, asking \uc2 OPEC\uc2  to either ramp up production or hold off on slowing down production anyway, in order for him to look good coming into the midterms, which is dirty politics.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PALIN: The rising cost of energy is the biggest driver in inflation, though, because energy costs touches everything. Everything is transported, petroleum products.\par \par \uc2 Everything we touch has some kind of petroleum product ingredient in it, so energy costs.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Even our electrical grids, right...\par \par \uc2 PALIN: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: ... which is something that California found out just recently. You can't charge that electric car. We're having to turn down the grid.\par \par \uc2 PALIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Well, then now you need fossil fuels for your cars. And, oh, by the way, 80 percent of your grid is still running on fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 PALIN: And it's not a difficult solution. It's a matter of political will. Just turn the spigot on. We have the pipelines in place, the safe, secure, efficient pipelines.\par \par \uc2 We have the drilling rigs ready to go. They're -- the primacy that's needed in each state that produces energy, that's very important too. But it's a matter of political will.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: I want to bring you back when we have more time, but I have to hit this before you leave.\par \par \uc2 The NRCC raised more than $42 million in the latest round of fund-raising. The DCCC raised $56 million, massive amounts of money this cycle.\par \par \uc2 PALIN: Yes. Well, the -- I -- hopefully, it doesn't just come down to who has the most money and you don't have to be independently wealthy to be willing to offer your name up in the name of service.\par \par \uc2 Hopefully our country and the electorate doesn't go that direction. Otherwise, commonsense constitutional conservatives aren't going to get elected. So, we have to have faith that the voters can see past the B.S. in the ads that cost so much money and all the fund-raising and all that, and just focus on what the issues are, what the people want, and what they deserve.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Yes, well, what we deserve right now is a break at the grocery store and at the gas pump. And...\par \par \uc2 PALIN: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 Gas, groceries, grandkids, yes.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Yes, there you go.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Governor Palin, thank you.\par \par \uc2 PALIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 FAULKNER: Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FXLV000020221013eiad0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden Admits "Slight Recession" Is Possible\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Joe Concha, Mike Huckabee \par \uc2 8,399 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: We're out of time. We'll be back tomorrow night at 8:00.\par \par \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  is next.\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: Do you have to cheat in a fishing contest? Put weights in the fish to seem heavier than it is? I mean, really? Is that -- is winning that important? You have to ask yourself.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It's like baiting deer. We're opposed. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You'll win honestly.\par \par \uc2 All right. Welcome to HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 And breaking tonight, moments ago, the trust fund brat that never worked a day in his life in a hoodie \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  was forced to answer why he is unwilling to come on this program and defend his radical positions. Here's a slight tease.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INTERVIEWER: \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  said that he is repeatedly issued an invitation to your campaign to be his guest solo for the three hours of his radio show and one full hour of his national TV show. He said he's got no response from you, which I assume means you are no. So my question is, sir, as the United States senator and I worked for one that would be the late John Hines, you have to do this -- you know, debating the opposition thing all the time and I was just curious as to what your reasoning is for not doing that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, we got an answer. We'll play Fetterman's full answer coming up tonight.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, we have just obtained exclusively for this program, two full hours of body cam footage from the 2020 incident involving Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  of Georgia and his ex-wife where she accused him of running her over with his \uc2 Tesla\uc2  following a domestic dispute, two hours. Now, those on the left who believe -- you know, say believe all women, are they feigning outrage over \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 ? Because he allegedly paid for an abortion many years ago are they only caring about uh believing women that accuse Republicans? Is this going to change the race in Georgia? We've got that tape coming up exclusively right here on the show.\par \par \uc2 And also, Arizona Democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  is running and hiding literally from questions and she still refuses to debate Kari Lake. We have exclusive footage from Project Veritas showing Hobbs frantically avoiding a reporter. We'll play that.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we begin with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  tonight who just sat down for one of the coziest -- they could have lit a fire and toasted marshmallows interview with fake news's CNN's fake Jake Tapper who coddled the president with a variety of softball questions virtually no follow-ups. That's fine, I expect that.\par \par \uc2 Make no mistake, fake Jake, he's not a journalist, he's a Democratic activist. He is a talk show host, I'm a talk show host. We're members of the press, I'm honest, he's not.\par \par \uc2 It's okay. All he needs to do is be honest with his viewers that he has a political side. He's a Trump-hating liberal Democrat like almost everyone else on fake news CNN and MSDNC.\par \par \uc2 Now, still despite Tapper's best effort to be gentle with the president, the ever so -- well, compromised president even politely picking up Joe's cheat sheet at one point. Biden still mangled you know at the end to make a total complete fool of himself, mangling one word after another. He said he was so proud of his son hunter the smartest guy he knows.\par \par \uc2 There may be true to that because he made all this money with no experience, he's got to give him give the devil is due. He said the vast majority of Americans approve of his decisions in office, I beg to differ, the polls do as well. And get this, he claimed that we are definitely not in a recession he said it.\par \par \uc2 Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER, CNN HOST: Should the American people prepare for a recession?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: No. Look, they've been saying this now how, every -- every six months, they say this. Every six months, they look down. The next six months and see what's going to happen.\par \par \uc2 It hadn't happened yet. It hadn't been there -- there has -- there is no -- there's no guarantee that they're going to -- I don't think there will be a recession. If it is, it'll be a very slight recession. That is we'll move down slightly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: See after two straight quarters of negative growth, consecutive quarters of negative growth, we are officially in a recession, not to mention the housing crash, the bear market, spiking interest rates. And to top it all off, inflation still at a 41-year high, gas prices are now more than double what they were under President Trump and they're going much higher very quickly.\par \par \uc2 And that brings us to Biden's utter humiliation at the hands of \uc2 OPEC\uc2  and Saudi Arabia. Remember, after you went begging and pleading with MBS, the guy he once called the murderer of Jamal Khashoggi and begged them to please produce more oil, the country that he said was a pariah nation, the kingdom of Saudi Arabia -- well, they in conjunction with \uc2 OPEC\uc2 , in full alignment with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and Russia, they decided to do the exact opposite of what Joe wanted and they produced less. He got on his hands and knees, kissed the ring in the ass of MBS and it didn't work. Even fist pumped them.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, two million barrels a day less, thanks to Joe's diplomacy. Last night, according to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , it was never about the oil. This is a lie. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: Do you think it's time for the U.S. to rethink its relationship with Saudi Arabia?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes. And by the way, let's get straight where I went. I didn't go to -- went about oil. I went about making sure that we made sure that we weren't going to walk away from the Middle East, and what was going on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Okay. He went there for the oil. He went there to beg Saudi Arabia for help in the midterms. We now have a new report, a bombshell report from "The Wall Street Journal" that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was pleading with \uc2 OPEC\uc2  and Saudi Arabia to delay the production decrease of oil for a few months, meaning it's likely that MBS and \uc2 OPEC\uc2  told \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  right to his face that he would not only produce -- not produce more oil, but he can cut production and he would cut production dramatically.\par \par \uc2 Quote: Saudi officials dismissed Biden's request which they viewed as a political gambit by the administration to avoid bad news ahead of the U.S. midterm elections. They were right. They laughed in his face. This is not just humiliating for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and it is humiliating, but he was groveling to the guy that he called a murderer.\par \par \uc2 The murderer literally spit in his face and spit in the face of the entire country the USA.\par \par \uc2 And despite Biden's efforts to keep this quiet, we now know the truth and Joe has no long-term solution for spiking gas prices, no plan to tap into our own domestic resources, no new pipeline deals on deck, just the political gamut and it failed and it failed miserably and we're all paying the price for it.\par \par \uc2 And now, he's accusing Saudi Arabia and \uc2 OPEC\uc2  of aligning with Putin which had already happened. How stupid can Joe be? They have been aligning for quite some time, all in an effort they have been coordinating to drive up energy costs by manipulating the world's supply, knowing that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is so stupid and so beholden to the new green deal socialist Democrats that he's going to artificially reduce the world supply, which means they get even richer.\par \par \uc2 They will continue to deny \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  begging and pleading again and again. Joe will keep begging and humiliating himself and humiliating our country, all because he's too stupid to produce it domestically and become energy independent like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  did.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately for the country, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  does not value logic or truth. He bows at the dark altar of the climate alarmist religious cult, never forget it. And with each passing day, reality becomes more and more blurred for the president.\par \par \uc2 In fact today, he falsely claimed that his son Beau lost his life in Iraq. This is pretty scary. It's like a -- you know, "where's Jackie?" moment. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Just imagine, I mean this sincerely. I say this as a father of a man who won the Bronze Star of the conspicuous service medal, and lost his life in Iraq.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That didn't happen. His son tragically died from cancer shortly after his second term as Delaware's attorney general. Tonight, it is not clear if Biden is confused, lying, or both. I suspect it is the significant cognitive decline that we chronicle regularly.\par \par \uc2 Either way, it's not a good look from the guy who has checked his watch remember over and over again during the dignified transfer of service men and women that were killed in Afghanistan because of Joe's awful and deadly withdrawal from Kabul. And frankly, Biden seems pretty classless, pretty self-absorbed.\par \par \uc2 Right on cue, here's Joe talking about himself yet again. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I grew up in Claymont, Delaware, and across, I went to a little school called Holy Rosary Grade School across from the Claymont fire hall. And all my buddies that came -- either became a firefighter, a cop or a priest. I wasn't qualified for any of them, so here I am.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, the last part's true. You don't qualify for those jobs and you certainly not qualified to be president. Now, given his first two years in office, that is painfully obvious for anyone with eyes to see.\par \par \uc2 Here with reaction, FOX News contributor Joe Concha, former Arkansas Governor \uc2 Mike Huckabee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You know, this report in "The Wall Street Journal", "Daily Mail" picked it up as well, that the Saudis rejected it. Now, we know that they probably rejected Joe right to his face and then Joe went to plan B, which is can you please not cut production until after the election. Now, wouldn't that be seeking outside influence in light and to -- in light of the upcoming midterm elections? In other words, if you're asking Saudi Arabia and \uc2 OPEC\uc2  to wait to reduce the world supply, are you trying to manipulate the country's vote?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MIKE HUCKABEE\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Okay, I'll jump on it.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Jump on it.\par \par \uc2 HUCKABEE: Yeah, I'll jump all over that one. What doesn't make sense is that we have not only an ample supply of energy for the next four or five hundred years, even if we had the kind of growth that we anticipate, so we could take care of our needs and even export energy. But the president shut that down. He can say that he didn't but he did.\par \par \uc2 And the fact is it not only has had a dramatic impact -- impact on prices, but it's also had an impact on our ability to be free. A country that has to beg people for its energy is a country that is enslaved to whoever it is supplying the energy. And right now, we are at the mercy of not the best actors in the world when it comes to our own needs of energy.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, it's so embarrassing, Joe Concha, and the media just totally ignores it, as they ignore the high price of gasoline. They were so anxious to report, oh, gas prices have come down, you know, 50 days in a row because of Joe, because Joe put a band-aid on the problem and that was releasing the strategic petroleum reserves, which by the way compromises our national security to artificially increase supplies which lower prices.\par \par \uc2 So we did that temporarily. Okay now that's had to stop because he's depleted half of them. So, now, prices are on the rise again in the media is silent.\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: It's amazing, and it's like me gaining 50 pounds, Sean, right? And my wife says, you know you look like you're in your second trimester at this point, I think you need to lose some weight.\par \par \uc2 So then I go out and I lose seven pounds in a month and then have the audacity to go back to her to say, you know, honey, I've never lost weight this quickly in my life and in this much weight so fast. That's the same thing with gas prices.\par \par \uc2 It went to an all-time high, they bring it down to the $3.50 range, which remember when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  left office, it was about two dollars and thirty cents a gallon. So still well above that, and they blame \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , Putin price hike and that whole hashtag before prices came down and now we see it going back towards it's almost at four dollars a gallon. It will only go higher.\par \par \uc2 And the fact that we have to depend on Venezuela and OPEC+ which is basically Russia and Saudi Arabia instead of energy independence that we had here at home, gas and oil leases off of the gulf coast, off of the Alaska coast, ANWR, obviously, XL pipeline.\par \par \uc2 And now we're dependent on all these other countries we backed ourselves into this corner and with the midterm coming, I don't see how this reverses at this point. But to talk about that interview last night, Sean, not many people watched. Twenty-ninth place overall among cable news shows. Your program more than tripled its audience, Jake Tapper's audience and you didn't have the leader of the free world on at last check so that's embarrassing.\par \par \uc2 And the interview was also pre-taped by the way. Why wasn't it live?\par \par \uc2 And when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has done the three TV interviews he's done this year, whether it was with "60 Minutes", NBC and now CNN for whatever reason, they're taped a couple days beforehand so they could go to something called post-production, edit what they need to, homogenize it and make the president perhaps look better than he would in a live setting. That's how these interviews should go. But the president will never agree to that in these news organizations are going to allow that.\par \par \uc2 Overall, I think Jonathan Turley asked a great question. Quote: If Hunter was a hopeless addict, incapable of criminal intent or sound decision making, why were foreign interests clamoring at his door to give him millions of dollars as a board member, lawyer or consultant? I would have liked to have heard Jake Tapper ask the president about that or the fentanyl crisis that's killing 300 Americans a day on average due to overdoses. But, of course, we didn't get those questions because we don't want to make the president uncomfortable or anything on national television, now, do we, Jake?\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah, we kind of do.\par \par \uc2 Governor, let me go back to you. By the way, it was great to have Sarah back on the program and she's doing great. Thank -- thank god she's cancer- free. She will be the next governor of Arkansas, I'm pretty confident.\par \par \uc2 As you look at the house and the Senate, the Senate in particular, we got Florida, we got Georgia, we've got North and South Carolina. We've got New Hampshire, Pennsylvania, Ohio. We got Wisconsin. We got Nevada. We have Arizona.\par \par \uc2 We seem to think that maybe \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  is in trouble in the state of Washington. Maybe even we're in play in Colorado. Where do you see this falling out? We're 27 days away from what I believe is an inflection point, a tipping point election, midterm election in this country. Where do you see the result going?\par \par \uc2 HUCKABEE: I honestly think Republicans are going to have a much better night than anybody predicts largely because the polling information is simply unreliable. And there's a reason that it's unreliable. Right now, if you're pro-life, if you're Christian, if you're conservative, if you are a MAGA Republican, which means you just think that America is a great country and we ought to preserve it and protect it and pass it on that way.\par \par \uc2 If you're one of those people, you're kind of thinking that any given day, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  may show up, bang your door down and haul you in. If not, they may put you on a list, they may call you a domestic terrorist, a threat to democracy.\par \par \uc2 So conservatives simply don't answer polls. So I think we're seeing something that really can't be factored in. And I was talking to Robert Cahaly of the Trafalgar Group, one of the few polling organizations that has some credibility. He's very blunt and saying no white men between 30 and 50 will answer a poll question.\par \par \uc2 And for the most part, most, all of the Trump supporters simply will not answer a poll. They don't put yard signs up anymore. They don't put a lot of bumper strips. But they will go vote because they know it's the last line of defense they have to protect their country and their families.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Governor, thank you for being with us. We're glad Sarah's healthy.\par \par \uc2 Joe's book "Come On, Man" is doing very well. Congratulations, Joe Concha.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 All right. There you have it, 27 days still the all-important midterm elections. We're looking at the gubernatorial race out of New Mexico. It is now uncomfortably closed for the Democratic incumbent. Take a look at this brand new ad exclusively provided to this program by the \uc2 Republican Governors Association\uc2 . Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP, POLITICAL AD)\par \par \uc2 AD ANNOUNCER: During COVID, \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2  locked down New Mexico but led hundreds of criminals out of prison early. Many went on to commit more crimes, including career criminal Chris Beltran. After \uc2 Lujan Grisham\uc2  released him early, Beltran violated probation, got sent back to jail.\par \par \uc2 Then, \uc2 Lujan Grisham\uc2 's administration released him early again. Days after his early release, he hunted his ex-girlfriend down, shot her in the face, murdered her.\par \par \uc2 This should never have happened.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Defund, dismantled, no bail. How's that working out?\par \par \uc2 Her Republican opponent Mark Ronchetti is now within a few points in the latest poll. It would be a long shot but this is a winnable race clearly for the GOP.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, out in the state of Washington, Republican Senate candidate Tiffany Smiley is now within two points of the state's longtime Senator \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  in what is a clear rebuke of the Biden agenda. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP, POLITICAL AD)\par \par \uc2 AD ANNOUNCER: With Biden and the D.C. politicians in charge, gas prices more than doubled. Senator Murray votes 96 percent with Biden. She just goes along. Groceries, housing, everything costs more with Biden and his D.C. cronies in charge. And Senator Murray just goes along, 96 percent with Biden.\par \par \uc2 Baby formula shortage, empty shelves, crime in our streets, and Senator Murray fades away and just goes along.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Here with more is Washington Republican Senate candidate Tiffany Smiley is with us.\par \par \uc2 All right. This race now is within the margin of error. A lot is going on out there. It's the same issues we see in the rest of the country and that is record high inflation, 41-year high, double the prices for a gallon of gasoline that we were paying under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , wide open borders, no bail laws, defund, dismantle the police and school systems that are a disaster and pushing a woke agenda.\par \par \uc2 That seems to even be impacting a state of Washington which maybe many people didn't predict would be in play.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY SMILEY (R), WASHINGTON SENATE CANDIDATE: Yes, Sean, you're exactly right. I mean, these aren't Democrat or Republican issues, they're American issues.\par \par \uc2 And when I stood up and fought for my husband and then and then took on V.A. reform, I was fighting for every Democrat, every Republican, and every independent, disabled service member and their family. So I've been at this for 18 months. I've listened to the voters of Washington state.\par \par \uc2 You know, a year ago, they were worried about the rising cost of living, gas prices, crime in our streets, overdoses from fentanyl. We are looking to super exceed 2021 in our over overdoses from fentanyl. I mean, this has to be stopped. These are life and death issues.\par \par \uc2 You know, I'm a nurse. Washington state needs healing, they need hope. And that's what we have been talking about from the very beginning. That's exactly why you are seeing these polls tighten. We've ran a great campaign, raised good money, we're still raising money because we know our message is a winning message.\par \par \uc2 We're seeing these polls tighten because we've gone up on air and shared our message. We double down and meet that with the voters on the ground everywhere. You know, not that long ago, I was at DeSoto homeless encampment, helping clean up a homeless encampment, cleaning up dirty needles. And I wanted to talk to the residents to understand their hardships and their barriers to getting help and having hope.\par \par \uc2 And as I was there cleaning up, a woman from across the camp who's helping cleanup as well. She said Tiffany Smiley, you know, I was kind of like, yes, that's me, I'm really a nice person, I don't know what ad you're watching but we're doing great work here in Washington. She said, I've voted for \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  every single time. This year, I'm honored to vote for you.\par \par \uc2 And it was a special moment where we're standing in the middle of just destruction where people are poisoning themselves to death and no one's helping them. Senator \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  is silent on the issues that are affecting Washington voters every single day. That's why we will win this race. Washington state wants hope and anyone can join us at smileyforWashington.com. We need money to continue to share our winning message all across Washington state.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: And \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  has been hand in hand, arm in arm, with all these failed policies that are hurting the American people and that includes the people in Washington state. Your state -- this is going to be an interesting race to watch.\par \par \uc2 Tiffany Smiley, thanks for being with us.\par \par \uc2 All right. Coming up, we have obtained the disturbing body cam footage, two hours of it, from the incident where \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  allegedly ran over his ex-wife with his car. We're going to show you all of what she had to say. Now the media is playing defense in Georgia and they're playing defense in Pennsylvania. We'll talk about both those races. That's all coming up, straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Now, a breaking news HANNITY exclusive surrounding the all-important Georgia Senate race, as we have obtained the full body cam footage of \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 's dispute with his ex-wife in March of 2020, where she accused him of trying to run her over with his car and he actually ran over her foot with his car, and where his -- well, crying ex- wife told police that Warnock is a great actor and they'd done all this and suggested that he has a violent past.\par \par \uc2 As always, we'll play with -- play the tape. We'll let you decide. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAPHAEL WARNOCK'S WIFE: And he just starts backing the car up and it wasn't going fast like I'm not -- I'm not bleeding, but I can't believe he would have run me over.\par \par \uc2 POLICE OFFICER: So you think it was intentional that he ran (INAUDIBLE) --\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAPHAEL WARNOCK'S WIFE: I just don't think he cares. I think, obviously, I was standing here the door is open and I'm leaning into the car. How can you drive the car when I'm leaning into it?\par \par \uc2 He knew it was a risk and he doesn't care. He's just curious about his own stuff. He's got a flight to catch and that's all that matters.\par \par \uc2 This man's running for \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  and all he cares about right now is his reputation. I've tried to keep the way that he acts under wraps for a long time and today he crossed the line.\par \par \uc2 I had to draw the line today, I've had enough of him thinking he can do whatever he wants because of who he is and treat me any kind of way and I just keep taking it and being quiet for his reputation's sake and I've had it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Now, she's making an accusation that he was using his car as a weapon to run her over and in fact ran over her foot in the process, as a result of this domestic dispute. It raises a question, why wasn't \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  arrested and charged in the incident? Which does in and of itself raise a lot of ethical questions, because isn't it pretty much standard operating procedure for police departments and all around the country when there's a domestic dispute and there is an allegation of violence that one person has taken and down to police headquarters, the one that is charged?\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Warnock's troubles don't stop there. He wants to end cash bail. He demonized police. You know, even referred to them as thugs and gangsters.\par \par \uc2 He also said you cannot serve God and the military. He compared America's legacy to, let's see, \uc2 Fidel Castro\uc2 . He praised, oh, \uc2 Louis Farrakhan\uc2  and the Reverend Jeremiah Wright. He wants CRT in our schools.\par \par \uc2 He supports abortion on demand through the ninth month, no restriction on abortion. He's been a rubber stamp for everything Biden and the new green deal climate cult alarmists. He's hell-bent on destroying pretty much our way of life in this country.\par \par \uc2 But Warnock is just one of many extreme socialists that are running for office. That includes the ever-struggling socialist to the left of Bernie Sanders, trust fund brat on a hoodie that's never worked a job in his life, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . An NBC News reporter even denoted the Fetterman struggled to speak didn't seem to understand the conversation prior to the interview. But, of course, that reporter faced pushback from left wing host Savannah Guthrie, the expert that she is. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC REPORTER: NBC News agreed to Fetterman's request to use a transcription program during this interview because he's still experiencing auditory processing issues from the stroke, meaning he can't always understand what he's hearing.\par \par \uc2 Walk me through why we need the closed captioning, how it works.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: Yeah, it's really just how things happen because I sometimes will hear things in a way that's not perfectly clear. So I use captioning, so I'm able to see what you're saying on the -- in captioning.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And, Savannah, we did find that in small talk before the interview without captioning, it seemed it was difficult for Fetterman to understand our conversation.\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC ANCHOR: And, Dasha, you had mentioned that last night on "Nightly". Since then, other journalists who've also dealt with Fetterman came forward and said they had a different experience.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Yeah, and, Savannah, that's completely fair that that was their experience. We can only report our own.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Maybe somebody can show Savannah Guthrie the tape of Fetterman saying kick the balls in authority and maybe calling Joe Biden Joe bin.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, get this, during an interview today with the Penn Live editorial board, well, board member Jeffrey Lord, a friend of this program, he asked Fetterman about my open invitation for him to join this program for the full hour and to do my radio show if he wants for the full three hours.\par \par \uc2 Here's the response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY LORDD, PENN LIVE: \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  said that he has repeatedly issued an invitation to your campaign to be his guest solo for the three hours of his radio show and one full hour of his national TV show. He said he's gotten no response from you which I assume means you are no.\par \par \uc2 So my question is, sir, as the United States senator and I work for one that would be to late John Hines, you have to do this, you know, debating the opposition thing all the time and I'm just curious as to what your reasoning is for not doing that?\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: I just -- I just don't believe that -- I mean, it's actually a fact. That FOX News and part of -- that \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  is a part of has spent the last five or six weeks, you know, unloading on us and saying outrageous things and going after us in a way more so than every other of the -- the other Democratic candidates combined. So this is not necessarily a fair and serious, you know, venue. And I just would not take that. It's an unserious invitation because he's all in for Dr. Oz and that's the truth.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Here with reaction, radio talk show host \uc2 Larry Elder\uc2 , along with Outkick founder Clay Travis.\par \par \uc2 Larry, let me start with you. Well, the thing that he doesn't really want to do is answer a question because he hasn't been asked a lot of hard questions. Here, in Pennsylvania, $81 billion industry, he wants a moratorium on fracking. He wants absolutely no restrictions on abortion. He wants uh the taxpayers to fund heroin injection sites that are safe.\par \par \uc2 This guy has advocated for more convicted murders to be released than any other candidate in the history of running for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . He wants to open up the prisons in Pennsylvania and let out a third at least of the population. This guy is more left than Bernie Sanders on every issue, Larry. No wonder he won't come on this program or answer anybody's questions.\par \par \uc2 LARRY ELDER\uc2 , RADIO HOST: Yeah, and it's interesting, Sean, he says he won't come on your program because you're, quote, all in for Dr. Oz. For crying out loud, I ran for governor as you know out here in California, the newspapers were all in on my opponent \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , "The Sacramento Bee", "The L.A. Times", I still did interviews.\par \par \uc2 If we Republicans don't do interviews because the media is all in on the other side, we wouldn't do any interviews.\par \par \uc2 And regarding \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 's videotape, that's very compelling stuff, Sean. But you and I both know there is a double standard. When a female accuses a Democrat versus when a female accuses a Republican, the media has a very different attitude.\par \par \uc2 Remember Tara Reade, she's the woman who claimed that that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  essentially assaulted her, and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  even said she believed Tara Reade. Now, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  works for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . So either she's working now for somebody she accused of a sexual assault or now she believes Tara Reade is a liar.\par \par \uc2 And you and I both interviewed Juanita Broaddrick who alleged that \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  raped her and that two weeks after, the alleged raid. \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  verbally intimidated her. To my knowledge, Sean, Hillary has never been asked about what Juanita Broaddrick said. So the double standards are just all in your face.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, Clay, let's go to \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  for a second. You saw this tape of his ex-wife alleging that he was using his car as a weapon to run her over and in fact ran over her foot. He's