nder the bus. He attacked them as the new Jim Crow.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: If you get raped, J.D. Vance and others are going to say you have to have that baby, that poor girl was raped by an illegal alien.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Somebody that should have never been in this state in the first place. I've always supported increased funding for Border Patrol. He spent 20 years in Washington, D.C. Heck, he doesn't care about the details of public policy.\par \par \uc2 RYAN: Ohio needs an ass kicker, not an ass kisser.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. Pretty interesting debate last night. Venture Capitalist and Author J.D. Vance joins me now, we invited Tim Ryan as well. We hope he'll join us on another day. J.D., welcome. Good to have you. What do you think of last night's debate?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: I think it was a lot of fun. I really enjoyed the opportunity to get to talk, of course, to the Ohio voters but also to push back on so many of the lies and the dishonesties that Tim Ryan has been running with over the last, the last couple of years. The thing that I really enjoyed about it, Martha, is just that it was very clear that he had a few particular canned lines. He had a few particular talking points, but he couldn't actually address the substance of almost any question. And I think it's pretty pathetic that after 20 years, the guy just doesn't care about the details of public policy, He clearly doesn't care about the details of getting things done. That might explain his record of failure over the last 20 years.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I want to get to the moment between the two of you, very sensitive story, obviously about this 10-year-old girl. But before you do that, I just want to clarify, clarify one part of what he said last night, he called you an ass-kisser, and he was referring to a comment by the former President Trump who made a comment to that effect at a rally about a month ago about you. What do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, I think the President was telling a joke about a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  story that got on his nerves. I actually talked to the President. Before he made that comment. I talked to the President after he made that comment. He was telling a joke. And like I said last night during the debate, I'm not too interested in taking a lecture from self-respect from a guy like Tim Ryan, who has literally publicly begged \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  for support, and begged \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  for a job promotion. I mean, if Tim Ryan can talk like that, he doesn't need to be giving lectures about dignity.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, you know, there's an interesting piece today about the lonesome run of Tim Ryan, because he has portrayed himself as a moderate in Ohio. He's not getting a lot of support from the Democratic National Party. What does that tell you about this race?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, I think that's a little bit of an untruth there, Martha, not from you, but from the Ryan campaign. I mean, they've gotten a ton of support from the National Democratic Party. They've gotten 30, 40, 50- million-dollar budget poured into this race on behalf Tim Ryan. They're whining because they're not getting even more support. But the guy's practically unlimited resources, unlimited T.V. commercials.\par \par \uc2 The problem for Tim Ryan is that his T.V. commercials are telling lies. He they're telling a story, that he's a moderate when in fact, in fact, he goes with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  , Biden, and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  100 percent of the time. So, I'm not surprised by that Tim Ryan is complaining that he's not getting even more money, but he's gotten a lot of support, and he's used that support to try to pull one over on us in Ohio level.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, you know, the Leadership Committee, the Senate Leadership Committee has poured about $30 million into your race. Ohio is not a place where they expected to have to do that. So, what do you say to Republicans about why this was has been such a very tight race in a place that has been reliably red in recent elections?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, I think there are a couple of things, Martha, I mean, first is that he's been a (INAUDIBLE) primary. The second thing is, I'm not sure it actually is that tight Martha. If you remember the polls in 2020 (INAUDIBLE) was going to, was going to lose by a point maybe win Ohio by a point of course, he won by eight points. The simple fact is a lot of the people who are most dedicated to me and to my campaign are not going to trust a pollster question in part because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  , the President of the United States, is calling them extremists for voting for the wrong people. And I will say, if people want to help us fight back and get our message out there, they can support us at JDVance.com, but we're going to win Martha, we're going to win this race.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me just play another moment from last night. Let's watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RYAN: We can't ignore the level of extremism that we're hearing from J.D. Vance on this position. No exceptions for rape and incest, right. If you get raped, J.D. Vance, and others are going to say you have to have that baby, state mandated pregnancies for a rape victim that is so far out of the mainstream, it's not even funny.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: I absolutely think the 10-year-old girl, the case that we've of course heard a lot about, that girl should be able to get an abortion if she and her family so choose to do so. The thing they never mentioned is that poor girl was raped by an illegal alien, somebody that should have never been in this state in the first place. You voted so many times against border wall funding, so many times for amnesty, Tim. If you had done your job, she would have never been raped in the first place.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, obviously, that was a powerful exchange. But it also raised some questions about things you've said previously about your stance on abortion. So, I want to give you an opportunity to clarify that. You had said in the past that two wrongs don't make a right, and that you didn't believe in exceptions for rape or incest. Have you changed your stance on that, given what you said last night?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Look, Martha, two wrongs don't make a right. But at the end of the day, I think we live in a democratic society. We live in a society where obviously people are going to disagree about where to draw the line. I've always been pretty clear, Martha, that legally, I think that we should draw the line. And then in that specific case, that particular girl, I think should be able to get an abortion. And the other point that I made, Martha, pretty consistently is the media is really preoccupied with so-called extremism for, for Republicans on the abortion issue.\par \par \uc2 I've always defended reasonable exceptions again, as a legal matter. The question is, why are Democrats supporting 40-week-old abortions? Why are they preventing doctors actually voting for legislation that would prevent doctors from providing care to a baby who survived the botched abortion. So, I just don't take the bait on this issue where the Democrats call us extremists, even as they're supporting radical pro-choice, pro-abortion policies that would be completely unacceptable anywhere in the civilized world.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I got to squeeze in one more quick question for you, and that is with regard to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You have at one point said you didn't really care what happened to Ukraine. Where do you stand now? The former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has said, time to get to the negotiating table. President Biden says that we have to give them everything they need, which camp are you in?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: I'm very clearly that let's get, excuse me, to the negotiating table, Martha. And one of the things that really was crazy that Tim Ryan said last night, that didn't get a whole lot of attention, as he said, we needed to respond to a nuclear attack by \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  in a very forceful way. What does it, what does that mean? Does that mean we're launching nuclear weapons ourselves?\par \par \uc2 And then we're ending human civilization. The only thing, the only responsible thing to do with this, Martha, is to try to bring this conflict to an end before it gets to the point of nuclear weapons. You can believe as I do, that the Ukrainians are brave people that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is a bad guy without pushing United States on the brink of nuclear war. What Biden, what Tim Ryan, what these guys are doing is incredibly irresponsible.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. J.D. Vance. Thank you very much. As I mentioned, we've invited Tim Ryan to bring us his side of this campaign, and we look forward to that as well. J.D. Vance, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, we are 28 days now from the midterms. The latest Fox News Power Rankings are now in, they came in just several hours ago. Correspondent Alexandria Hoff breaking down those numbers and showing us where the shifts are and are not. Hi there. Hi, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. You know the timing gets really interesting to this stage of the game, take Pennsylvania it's one of the four Senate toss ups where Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  has agreed now to debate Dr. Mehmet Oz. But mark the date two weeks from now, by then a lot of voters particularly on the Democratic side will have cast their mail in ballots early.\par \par \uc2 That's likely strategic but given states with early voting, there's not a whole lot of time left for candidates to fine tune their messaging, and Democrats might need to. The latest Fox News Power Rankings forecast has remained the same on the Senate side. It's anyone's game. 47 races going Democrat, 49 Republicans all coming down to those toss ups in Arizona, Georgia, Nevada and Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Now, on the House side, there have been some shifts benefiting both parties right now. There's 31 Toss up races, with the forecast predicting that Republicans will end up with 14 seats worth of breathing room there. Best case scenario for Republicans taking the house with as many as 246 seats. The best case for Democrats holding on with 220. But economic concern becoming more critical to voters is likely to hurt Democrat.\par \par \uc2 The latest poll out of \uc2 Monmouth University\uc2  shows that the number of Americans the largest number of Americans 82 percent rated it as their most important issue followed by crime at 72 percent. These are GOP-led issues along with jobs and immigration and all of those coming out on top of the issue of abortion for voters. Where this becomes really important is when you break out independents who often end up deciding these competitive races. In that same poll. 61 percent said they give preference to economic issues. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Alex, thank you very much. So, our midterm deep dive continues with Republican Senate candidate Dr. Mehmet Oz locked in a razor tight race. As Alex just said, this is a toss-up on our read in the power rankings, the Fox Power Rankings. Also, Europe's former top \uc2 NATO\uc2  Commander, General Richard Sheriff is here, he has long warned that nuclear war with Russia is possible as world leaders hold crisis talks today to consider Putin's revenge.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A look at some of the damage in Kyiv. We're getting a closer look today as the sun is up on this brutal situation, after more than 80 Russian missiles attack civilians across the country. Massive craters cemented next to playgrounds, as some in the Kremlin, reportedly demand more devastating strikes on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 President Zelenskyy is speaking with leaders of the \uc2 G7\uc2  in an emergency session, saying that "one person is blocking peace and this person is in Moscow."\par \par \uc2 Former \uc2 NATO\uc2  deputy Supreme Allied Commander General Sir Richard Sherriff standing by.\par \par \uc2 First, to our correspondent on the ground in Kyiv, Trey Yingst. Hi, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Martha, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 Today, Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  held emergency talks with \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders, following those missile strikes across the country yesterday. As the war grinds on, we got to close up in-person look at the northern border, this frontline less than a mile from the Russian border.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian soldiers are resupplying positions near the Russian border. Despite a successful counter offensive, they can't let their guard down. At the moment, there is no breakthrough of the defensive line. Deputy Commander Vitaly Bogdanovich (PH) says.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: As we were interviewing the commander, we could hear an artillery shell with overhead. So, we've got back in our cars, and we're headed to a safer location.\par \par \uc2 Kozacha Lopan sits along the Russian border. Outside the town beneath a field of sunflowers, a network of trenches is etched into the earth, dug by the Russians when they held this territory.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we're less than a mile from the Russian border. Just a few days ago, Ukrainian troops repelled another attack here.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian forces must now hold the land they successfully liberated over the past month.\par \par \uc2 In every region, we have military units and soldiers, Vitaly says. Our military is ready to resist occupiers that come to our territory.\par \par \uc2 As these troops brace for the possibility of a renewed Russian ground offensive, they also have a mission that is both important and personal. Hiking through forests and abandoned towns, Vitaly and his team search for a Ukrainian soldier killed in action. Eventually, finding his remains.\par \par \uc2 Despite the death and danger that plagues Ukraine's northern border, some still return. Those like 43-year-old Nataliya Yurrivna, who is desperate to pick up the pieces of her shattered home.\par \par \uc2 We don't know what to do or where to live, Nataliya says. Let all the world see what they did here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Let all the world see what they did hear. We hear that phrase so often. The Ukrainian people are desperate for the world to pay attention. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Trey Yingst, thank you very much for that in depth report from Kyiv. With that, we go to retire General Sir Richard Sherriff, former deputy supreme allied commander of \uc2 NATO\uc2  in Europe. General, thank you very much. It's good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 You know, I think a lot of people are looking at what's going on right now. They see it increasingly cornered \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  , who is using a lot of his military. We don't know how many of these missiles he's got left, and he's had to bring in recruits.\par \par \uc2 How desperate do you think his situation is? And what do you think happens next here?\par \par \uc2 GEN. SIR RICHARD SHIRREFF (RET.), FORMER \uc2 NATO\uc2  DEPUTY SUPREME ALLIED COMMANDER: Well, hello, Martha. And otherwise, as always, good to be -- good to be back with you.\par \par \uc2 I think his situation is bad. He knows he's on the back foot, which is why he's lashing out with this indiscriminate missile attacks across Ukraine, targeting civilian infrastructure and effectively using missiles as a terror weapon to undermine Ukrainian civilian morale, which it won't.\par \par \uc2 What is going to happen? Well, difficult precisely to say, but I think the Ukrainians are on a roll. All the indications are that they are determined to continue to prosecute various successful offensive operations against the Russians. They've pushed the Russians back in the north, and they are doing so in the south around Kherson.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: How --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SHIRREFF: And what about the Russians? All right, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: How concerned are you? Because I think there's an increasing concern in this country about where this all goes and whether or not the United States and the allies, the \uc2 G7\uc2  discussion today with Zelenskyy. Do we find ourselves in a war with Russia if we aren't already in some sort of proxy situation against Russia?\par \par \uc2 SHIRREFF: Well, we don't need to find that. We don't need to go that far. What we do need to do is exactly what the \uc2 G7\uc2  has said it will do, which is to continue to support Ukraine all the way.\par \par \uc2 And we've got to give Ukraine continue to give Ukraine the tools to do -- the tools they need to do the job. They've used an enormous amount of ammunition, missiles. They desperately need that defense capability, obviously, to protect their cities. But they also need to reinforce and build up that offensive maneuver capability that is taking the Russian -- taking the battle for the Russians and pushing them back.\par \par \uc2 I think it is entirely, entirely realistic for the Ukrainian -- the Ukrainian war and to at least achieve the -- a line of the 24th of February, which is where the line was before the Russians attack, if not further, and to continue to hit the Russians hard in depth.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This is \uc2 Sergey Lavrov\uc2  , talking about the possibility of negotiations -- of talks. Let's listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SERGEY LAVROV\uc2  , MINISTER OF FOREIGN AFFAIRS, RUSSIA (through translator): We are hearing all these U.S. official statements. They say that they are wholeheartedly open to negotiations and advocate for a political solution of the situation in Ukraine and around it.\par \par \uc2 But bad Russia is refusing all the proposals to make contact. I can tell you, this is a lie. We have not received any serious offers to make contact.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Should there be negotiation? Should there be a push to get to the table?\par \par \uc2 SHIRREFF: That is a matter for the Ukrainians and for President Zelenskyy. We, we cannot judge that. They have had their cities, their towns, their civilians, massacred, raped. Towns and cities destroyed by a neighbor.\par \par \uc2 And it is entirely up to us to support them, because any form of negotiation is going to offer a let-out to Russia. Of course, Lavrov is asking for negotiation now, while Russia is on the back foot.\par \par \uc2 But let's be clear, if Russia is given a chance of a breather, if there is a negotiation, Russia will use the opportunity to rebuild, retrain, regenerate its armed forces, and they will have another go at Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 There are plenty of hard ultra-nationalists in the Kremlin, and elsewhere. Not just Putin, but potential successors to Putin, who would like to see the Russian empire reestablished with Ukraine, wiped off the map and part of it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHIRREFF: And that cannot be allowed in the 21st century.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, the concern is one of them -- one of many that they may be running out of missiles and they might need to resort to nuclear weapons and very frightening outcomes and prospects, which clearly they're threatening, perhaps just kind of get some kind of negotiation. We'll see where it goes.\par \par \uc2 General Sherriff, thank you very much. Always good to speak with you, sir.\par \par \uc2 SHIRREFF: Thank you very much for having me.\par \par \uc2 Putin's escalation in Ukraine as the \uc2 IMF\uc2  warns for the global economy. The worst is yet to come. Famed economist \uc2 Mohamed El-Erian\uc2  live from New York after this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHAMED A. EL-ERIAN\uc2  , CHIEF ECONOMIC ADVISER, \uc2 ALLIANZ\uc2  : I fear that we risk a very high probability of a damaging recession that was totally avoidable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Breaking just a short time ago, a big blow, potentially for the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 Fox is now confirming that the country's third largest rail union will now not back that Labor deal that -- remember, it was brokered for 15-plus hours at the White House with Labor leaders last month.\par \par \uc2 And they came out and said they had reached this major agreement that avoided a strike. President Biden called that deal very consequential for the supply chains.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: If, in fact, they'd gone on strike, the supply change in this country would have come to a screeching halt, we would have seen a real economic crisis.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, what will we see now is the question. The White House official, one of them, saying, the union in question, and the railroad employers have agreed to a cooling off period that extends well into November. Giving them adequate time to continue their work and ensuring that our economy is under no immediate threat.\par \par \uc2 So, pull up to after the election, it sounds like, the Dow is a bit lower today, taking a breather from a huge slide that we have seen over the past several months.\par \par \uc2 There is a look at that. But the market is waiting for key inflation data that comes out on Thursday, as the Fed tries to move the levers of this economy to try to engineer a soft landing.\par \par \uc2 Very difficult to do at this point. And the nation and the world dive deeper into a recession that could last for years. It's very hard to get out of these, once you are clearly in them.\par \par \uc2 It is a high stakes job right now for the Fed. Wall Street not sounding so impressed at this point.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE DIMON\uc2  , CHAIRMAN, \uc2 JPMORGAN CHASE\uc2  : And talk about the economy, I'm talking about the stuff in the future. And this is serious stuff. Then, they're likely to put U.S. in some kind of recession, six, nine months from now.\par \par \uc2 EL-ERIAN: They didn't act in a meaningful way. And as a result, we risk mistake number three, which is by not easing the foot of the accelerator last year, they are slamming on the brakes this year, which will dip us into recession,\par \par \uc2 I fear that we risk a very high probability of a damaging recession that was totally avoidable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Totally avoidable. So, that is, of course, \uc2 Mohamed El-Erian\uc2  , chief economist -- chief economic adviser at \uc2 Allianz\uc2  and president of Queens' College at the \uc2 University of Cambridge\uc2  , and he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Mohamed, very good to have you with us. We have followed what you've been saying about all of this. So, it's good to have you here in person. And the \uc2 IMF\uc2  coming out today and saying, the worst is yet to come for the global economy. Are they right?\par \par \uc2 EL-ERIAN: They are right. And for the \uc2 IMF\uc2  to say this is a big deal. I worked there for 15 years, I can tell you we choose our words very carefully. And for them to say the worst is yet to come is very sobering for the rest of us.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, what are they telling us?\par \par \uc2 EL-ERIAN: They're telling us that growth is slowing down very rapidly, that many countries risk being dipped into recession. They tell us that inflation is sticky. It's not coming down as fast as we'd like it to be. And they're telling us something else. There's now financial stability risks, the notion that the markets may not function the way we want them to function.\par \par \uc2 That is a perfect storm. And if we're not careful, it could have significant economic damage to livelihoods.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, that's pretty scary. I think back to 2007, 2008, I use to cover the economy back then, and you're saying that there is a concern that the markets will not function. What do you mean?\par \par \uc2 EL-ERIAN: So, first, this is not 2007 and 2008, because the banks aren't involved. And the banks are like the oil in your car, Martha. If they break down, nothing else works. We're talking here about pockets have too much risk taking. We have the example of the United Kingdom. The United Kingdom is a \uc2 G7\uc2  economy and yet, its currency collapsed. Its interest rates went right up. It got reprimanded by the \uc2 IMF\uc2  , and the government had to do a small U-turn. You normally don't see this in the \uc2 G7\uc2  but what false did their market malfunction? So we have to maintain the integrity of our financial markets if we are to avoid a similar fate.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. So my question for you is this, you say the banks aren't involved. And yet, there are so much corporate debt out there. We know that Michael Berry, who wrote about that, 2000-2008 situation, said that there are I think 218 companies that he studied, that have a market capitalization of over a billion dollars that have no earnings, 218 companies. So aren't the banks involved if they hold the debt of all of these companies that are not profitable, that aren't making any money?\par \par \uc2 EL-ERIAN: So he's referring to what's called the zombie, the zombie companies, companies that survived on very low interest rates on the Fed injecting liquidity. No, that that is owned by pension companies, by insurance companies, by everyday investors, they're not held by the backs - -\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Not the oil that keeps thing going or no?\par \par \uc2 EL-ERIAN: No. They're not the oil, think of them more of other parts of the car. The car can still function but they're not the oil. But it is an issue so the last thing we want is for the average person to worry about the 401(k) in addition to worrying about inflation, in addition to worrying about the income security. So that's what we're trying to afford right now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the administration keeps saying, they told us it was transitory, which I know you have debunked. But they keep saying, well, this is a global problem. This isn't anything we did. It's not our fault. And yet, the Fed waited such a long time to start to ease, start to increase interest rates just a little bit. So is it the fault of the administration?\par \par \uc2 EL-ERIAN: It's the fault of the Fed. The Fed has made things worse. Even before the Ukraine war, even before Putin invaded Ukraine, we had a 7% plus inflation rate. The Ukraine war just made things worse. The Fed was basically asleep at the wheel. They mischaracterize inflation as transitory, meaning, don't worry, Martha, it's temporary reversible. It turns out to be sticky and high.\par \par \uc2 Then when they recognize at the end of November that it wasn't transitory, they can act boldly enough. So instead of, as I said, easing off the foot - - that foot off the accelerator, they're slamming on the brakes and the economy risk going through the windscreen.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No change, though, in leadership in either one of those areas when that started to happen, so we'll see where it goes. Thank you very much, \uc2 Mohamed El-Erian\uc2  , very good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 EL-ERIAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Hope we'll see you soon. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 So Dr. Oz has closed a massive gap in the Pennsylvania Senate race, really by focusing on crime in the Keystone State as Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  looks to prove that he can handle a debate which is now just days away. Mehmet Oz is here just four weeks to go until Election Day. He's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Crime is obviously top of mind for voters 28 days from the midterms. In Pennsylvania, it is front and center in that very tight Senate race which we have as a toss up. Democrat Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  accusing his opponent, Dr. Oz, of lying about his record according to The Philadelphia Inquirer, saying he's quote, most proud, Fetterman is, of stopping gun violence as mayor and fighting for the innocent and other individuals for a second chance." So Dr. Oz will join us to respond to that in moments but first of correspondent Alexis McAdams following a devastating fatal shooting of a 13-year-old boy in Philadelphia. What a heartbreaking story, Alexis.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Heart breaking for the family and it seems like every single day right in Philadelphia, there's another crime. So with this latest case, we're learning that this teenagers death of the 13-year-old marks 186 kids under 18 years old that have been shot so far this year in Philadelphia. I mean, that's staggering.\par \par \uc2 The latest case happened just last night, Martha. Philadelphia police looking for the gunman who shot and killed 13-year-old Jeremiah Wilcox. Police tell us he was shot in his head and in his face. No arrests yet and police have their hands full. Crime in the City of Brotherly Love is climbing total crime up 25% from last year.\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, investigators found more than 100 shell casings at a single crime scene. So many bullets they had to make their own evidence markers. So that's something new for them. This is investigators trying to track down a group link to at least two shootings that took place just minutes apart on Monday. In that first case, the group tried to rob a man at gunpoint while a toddler was sitting in the car with him. He was shot in the face but expected to be OK. The three-year-old in the backseat was not injured.\par \par \uc2 Just a few minutes later, police say it was the same group that tried to rob an 18-year-old man then shot him in the leg and took off. Police are combing that neighborhood for clues. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT SMALL, CHIEF INSPECTOR, PHILADELPHIA POLICE DISTRICT: These businesses have exterior surveillance cameras. So hopefully, these cameras recorded something that can help us with this double shooting investigation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: So far this year more than 3,000 shooting incidents and counting with more than 400 homicides. Police tell us the murders are down about 6% but, I mean, that's compared to a record high last year. According to a poll from earlier this year, crime is a top issue for Philadelphia voters which isn't surprising. \uc2 Pew Research\uc2  poll found that 70% of people who live in Philly say crime, drugs and public safety are the most important issues they face, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Alexis, thank you very much, Alexis McAdams. Joining me now Pennsylvania Republican Senate candidate Dr. Mehmet Oz. Dr. Oz, good to have you with us today. Obviously, these are heartbreaking stories. That's an amazing statistic, 186 people under the age of 18 have been shot in Pennsylvania this year. Your opponent basically says that it's ridiculous for you to portray him as soft on crime. He says, what a bunch of BS, does anybody really think the dude famous for selling phony diet pills knows anything about keeping our communities safe. What would you say to that, Dr. Oz?\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: There's a reason why the Fraternal Order of Police for the entire state of Pennsylvania endorsed me and they endorsed me unanimously. They know that these are addressable problems. And the reason they endorsed me is because \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  has been blocking their efforts to protect the people of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 When you mentioned these numbers, just to put it in perspective, almost half of all the murders are in young people under the age of 18. There's a lawlessness that permeates the very fabric of Philadelphia now. I lived in Philadelphia, in West Philly, when I was in graduate school. I could walk to school, it wasn't a problem. Parents don't let their kids leave the homes. And my opponent has also opposed cash bail, which prioritizes criminals over victims. Some of these people get out there. There'll be rearrested 100 times.\par \par \uc2 The prosecutors don't do their job. Cops aren't allowed to do their jobs, so no new cops join up. And violent individuals keep reoffending over and over again until it becomes one of these more exaggerated incidents, when you just show that 100 casings of guns being fired off. By the way, if you're offended by this, please go to doctoroz.com and support my campaign because \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  's campaign is supported by people from outside Pennsylvania who don't understand the impact of these policies.\par \par \uc2 But they have failed not just in crime, they've also failed in drug policies where he's advocated for legalization of all drugs. He wants all the cities in Pennsylvania to be sanctuary cities. You know, he takes his positions that are draconian. We have one of the highest fentanyl overdose death rates, and that actually accelerates homicides. When you have that law, this is with drugs.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, there's been a lot of back and forth on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  , and you were trailing kind of significantly for a while. And you sort of put your, you know, foot to the to the pedal and went all over the state. You've spent a lot of time in small group environments, talking to people across Pennsylvania about these issues. And it has been working, it has really narrowed this race considerably.\par \par \uc2 But now you're going to get an opportunity on October 25th to debate \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  . And you have talked about the fact that he suffered a stroke right around the time of the primary. So now we will see how he does the cover the New York -- of New York Magazine. Let's put this up on the screen just came out today released yesterday. The vulnerability of \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is the cover of the New York Post -- the New York Magazine, I should say. And here's a soundbite just from moments ago on MSNBC. Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), PENNSYLVANIA LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR: To be precise, I use captioning, so that's really the major -- that's the major challenge. And every now and then I'll miss a word every now and then, or sometimes I'll maybe mush two words together. But as soon as I have captioning, I'm able to understand exactly what's being asked. But even after the stroke, immediately after that, I was able to read everything and I haven't lost any memories or anything like that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. So, you know, what do you think about this as you head into that debate, Dr. Oz?\par \par \uc2 OZ: I have tremendous compassion for what \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  is going through. This is an area of specialty of mine. Heart failure, irregular heartbeat, strokes, these are things that I would deal with. But this question of his health is not about me, it's about the voters. He is not allowed any legitimate questions to be answered by his team about his medical records. He's not shared them. He's delayed this debate until so late in the cycle that the absentee ballots have all been mailed, and probably a third of the ballots could be actually sent in by the time I have been had the debate. What's he hiding?\par \par \uc2 Is he hiding his radical agenda or issues with his health, we don't know. Because the questions go to his campaign, it's like a black hole. Nothing comes back. But let me just point out why we call him No show Fetterman. He's missed a third of his obligations as lieutenant governor, rarely works more than a few hours if he does show up, he missed the majority of the city council meetings when he was mayor. He won't go out on the campaign trail and answer any questions from voters. He won't answer questions from press groupings like I do all the time. He's hiding behind something. And I'm calling him out as this opponent in this race.\par \par \uc2 And, Martha, this is critical. We cannot afford for democracy to die because candidates are not obliged to speak. Otherwise, it's like selling cereal boxes. He's got so much money. He's just running ads pretending he's something that we know he's not. So again, doctoroz.com, come chip in because otherwise democratic money will buy a 51st Senate seat. And I know I'll prevent as a toss up race right now. According to the major groups, we are very close. Whoever can fund their story and get it out better, and whoever can prevail in that debate is going to win this. And I will do well at the debate. We'll see how \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  does.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well, we will certainly be watching that. You know, I think for whoever the next senator is from Pennsylvania, and members of the Senate and the House on Capitol Hill, are going to end up dealing in one way shape or form with what's going on between Ukraine and Russia. And I want to ask you, because there is a big push to get more weapons to the frontlines of Ukraine. And Vladimir -- Volodymyr Zelenskyy, I should say, the president of Ukraine, is desperately asking for this. And then on the other side of the equation, you have former President Trump, who has supported you saying no, it's time for everyone to sit down at the table and end this thing with a negotiation. Which side are you on?\par \par \uc2 OZ: Oh, it'd be wonderful if he can negotiate a settlement. I think most people would desire that. My focus actually is on China as well, because China's watching what happens in the Ukraine. And then if they see that Russia can get away with what they had tried to do, then it will make it a lot easier for them to try to acquire Taiwan. So we've got a multiple country discussion going on here.\par \par \uc2 But I think for most rational people, you want to avoid additional escalation. And the longer these battles last, these wars go, the more difficult it is to make peace. So I think this is a good time to push hard for that if it's possible.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But do you support? Because you're saying you have to show them that they can't get away with this, you have to show China. So do you support sending more lethal weaponry into Ukraine? We've spent I think $28 billion on it so far, would you keep that money flowing? Would you keep that effort going?\par \par \uc2 OZ: I push for a peaceful resolution. We may have to make Ukraine even more of a porcupine to ensure that Russia comes to the table.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What do you mean by that?\par \par \uc2 OZ: And I think that will be a rational path forward. They armed them so they can defend themselves, and they have proven heroically that they can defend themselves if given the right armaments.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Indeed. And they have -- that is true. Thank you very much, Dr. Oz. Good to speak with you today. I hope you'll come back soon.\par \par \uc2 OZ: Take care.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So coming up, during \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  ' debut on late night TV as vice president, she boosted her boss, blamed the governor of Texas after a state visit to the state where she skipped the border and got lots of laughs and cheers on marijuana.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  , VICE PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: First of all, I love \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  , and I really do.\par \par \uc2 SETH MEYERS, NBC HOST: That is a governor of Texas, Greg Abbott, is participating in a piece of political theater.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And I just think it's an absolute dereliction of duty. If you see a problem, and if we agree that that we need to address it, then if you're a leader participate in a solution. I strongly believe -- and the majority of Americans agree. Nobody should have to go to jail for smoking weed, right? And so we start with that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Let's bring in Marc Thiessen, former chief speechwriter for President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  , now an \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2  scholar and Fox News contributor. Marc, always good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 MARC THIESSEN, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Good to be with you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So she said nobody should have to go to jail for smoking weed, right? And she got a big applause line and a lot of laughs on that. What did you think about that?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Oh, the hypocrisy. As district attorney in San Francisco, she put more than 1,900 people in jail for smoking weed, literally for pot possession. \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2  called her out on it on the debate stage in the primary, so this isn't like the first time this has been heard. And she goes on Seth Meyers and says nobody should be put in jail for smoking weed. Tell that to the 1,900 people you put in jail for smoking weed.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. You know, it's interesting. Here's that moment with \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2  , who by the way just left the Democrat Party. Here's that moment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TULSI GABBARD, FORMER DEMOCRATIC PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: Senator Harris says she's proud of her record as a prosecutor and that she'll be a prosecutor president, but I'm deeply concerned about this record. There are too many examples to cite but she put over 1,500 people in jail for marijuana violations and then laughed about it when she was asked if she ever smoke marijuana.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's also came up when she was on a radio show with Charlamagne tha God. And I want to replay this moment because this has been dogging her for quite a while. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLAMAGNE THA GOD, RADIO HOST: They say you oppose legalizing weed.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: That's not true.\par \par \uc2 CHARLAMAGNE THA GOD: I know.\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: And look, I joke about it, have joking, half of my family is from Jamaica, are you kidding me?\par \par \uc2 CHARLAMAGNE THA GOD: Have you ever smoked?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I have.\par \par \uc2 CHARLAMAGNE THA GOD: OK. And I inhale. I did inhale.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Marc, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes. I guess she didn't prosecute herself. I mean, you know, there's somebody got off scot free in San Francisco but it wasn't the 1,900 people she put in jail for that purpose. But look, the broader issue is this, you know that, it's -- \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  supported criminal justice reform for nonviolent offenses. He passed the First Step Act. But he was able to do that because on his watch, violent crime in the country had gone to an historical low. And so the country -- crime was under control and so you could have criminal justice reform.\par \par \uc2 Right now we are experiencing the worst crime wave in this country since the 1990s. This year, according to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  , there have been more murders this year in the United States than in any time since 1994. We have fentanyl over our streets killing the thousand -- over 100,000 people have died. It's the worst fight -- epidemic in our country's history. And their number one criminal justice issue is letting people out of jail for pot offenses. Why don't you get rid of the -- take care of the crime problem first?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Just a quick point before I let you go and I want to get your reaction to this. You know, the legalization of pot is one issue, the offenses is another issue. But since the legalization of pot, you have two issues. One is that basically that all of New York City smells like pot now, just for one thing, and I don't know that people love that situation. I don't know that they love living in that, having their kids in strollers all around that. The other issue is that pot has changed. It's a lot more potent than it used to be.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And a lot of people believe that, you know, offenders shooters, some of these people are in part driven by that drug use. So is this really a time to be chuckling and laughing about the impact of pot on the country?\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Not at all, and it can be laced with fentanyl too. So this is a very bad time to be doing this. And, you know, as you said in New York, everywhere you go, you smell pot. I can't smoke a cigar in the streets of New York but apparently I could smoke weed and be fine. And \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  says I shouldn't go to jail for it, so there you have it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Marc, always good to talk to you, Marc. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 THIESSEN: So it is the biggest trial yet of those who put together the Russia collusion story, which then as you know, fell apart. So, can \uc2 John Durham\uc2  win this very important trial? We'll tell you what you need to know about it next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: A very sad news breaking right now, a legendary actress has passed away. The family of Angela Lansbury saying that she died peacefully in her sleep at her Los Angeles home early this morning. The London born actress won five Tony Awards, Grammy Award for her work in "Beauty and the Beast" and also picked up one of three Oscar nominations for this performance in 1945, "The Picture of Dorian Gray."\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What a talent and what a legend of life's work she leaves behind. Lansbury was just five days shy of her 97th birthday.\par \par \uc2 And breaking today, a huge moment for the Russia collusion case is about to happen. The trial of Igor Danchenko, a Russian researcher and the main source of the anti-Trump dossier. He's accused of lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  about his role in providing that false information to ex-British spy \uc2 Christopher Steele\uc2  about the former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , and questions about who exactly was he reporting to, who commissioned him for this work?\par \par \uc2 The charges have been brought by Special Counsel \uc2 John Durham\uc2  who has been working for three years to get to the bottom of this case. Justice correspondent David Spunt live outside the federal courthouse where all of this will take place in Alexandria, Virginia. Hi, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Igor Danchenko charged with five counts of lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  . The judge wanted a jury seated as soon as possible in this case. He got his wish so jury was actually seated before noon. Special Counsel \uc2 John Durham\uc2  in the courthouse behind me today. He says that Danchenko, a Russian national living in Northern Virginia, was the primary source for British spy \uc2 Christopher Steele\uc2  , who can pile that widely talked about yet debunked Steele dossier full of salacious accusations about former President Trump. The indictment alleges Danchenko lied to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  about working for Steele as his primary source of information. The indictment also mentioned Danchenko's involvement with a man named Chuck Dolan, a PR executive who has ties to Hillary and \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  that date back to the 1990s.\par \par \uc2 Danchenko was the third defendant to be charged in \uc2 John Durham\uc2  's probe looking at the origins of the Russia investigation into Trump and his associates. The other two Kevin Clinesmith, a former \uc2 FBI\uc2  attorney pleaded guilty to falsifying a document used to obtain that FISA warrant against Trump campaign aide \uc2 Carter Page\uc2  . The other \uc2 Michael Sussmann\uc2  , a former Clinton campaign attorney who was acquitted in May for lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Martha, bottom line, Durham is clearly building a pattern, charging lower level officials with lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  as part of a narrative to show there was an effort to discredit then candidate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  about making allegations with ties to Russia. Now, Durham's grand jury in this overall investigation has expired, I should say, some believe Martha this may be his final stand before he actually retires as special counsel.\par \par \uc2 We don't know if that's going to be the case. But no matter how this turns out here, we do expect some sort of narrative report that \uc2 John Durham\uc2  will release at some point to the public through Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  , who has pledged not to stand in Durham's way. Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: David, Thank you. We'll be watching that closely with your help, David Spunt.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: sure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So today, Buckingham Palace has announced the date for the coronation of \uc2 King Charles III\uc2  . It will be Saturday, May 6th of 2023. He will be crowned at London's Westminster Abbey as have monarchs for the past 900 years. His Consort Queen Camilla will be crowned alongside him. The last king to be coronated was George VI back in 1937, so it's a long time since the King of England has been named. The announcement comes just about a month after the death of Charles mother, Queen Elizabeth II.\par \par \uc2 And that is THE STORY of October 11th, it's a Tuesday. THE STORY goes on. We'll see you back here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. Yo