val of President Biden on that issue, 30 percent.\par \par \uc2 Crime, very important. 72 percent. The approval, 32 percent. How much does that factor in to these races in the House in the Senate, the president's approval on those key issues?\par \par \uc2 FORD: Well, if the approval rating of the president was the decisive factor, Democrats would be losing all across the country and all the Senate races, and we'd be having a 60-40 - 65-35 conversation here.\par \par \uc2 I think voters had been able to separate a little bit. The president standing -- the president's position on some of the issues, and you're looking at -- they're looking at these races in a local way. I think some of the issues in Arizona are different than the issues in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 But I would agree with you. That map there shows a difficult road for Democrats to hold a majority in the House. It's going to take a magical evening for Democrats to maintain the majority there.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): And this is the worst-case best case on the House side. 246, best case. 215, worst case. This worst case for Democrats, 189. 220, best case.\par \par \uc2 Kimberley, where do you see this all kind of gelling? We're now into the time where people have been voting in a lot of these states. And a lot of this is already baked in the cake.\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: Well, if you look at the generic ballot, it would seem as though this surge or bounce that Democrats had over the end of the summer is fading somewhat. And it's beginning to look a little bit more like, Guy said like this could be a Republican year again.\par \par \uc2 At this point, and from my perspective in the House, it suggests it's not a question of whether Republicans win, but how big McCarthy's margin is, in the end. In the Senate, that's still going to be tougher, and that's going to come down to some of the problems that Republicans created for themselves with some very brutal primaries. And some candidates that are still struggling to, you know, make ground with some of their state's voters.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Another look at this, and this is just -- you can just see how many races we're following as far as likely lean, tossup. This is House races. And you go into each district, it's a different story.\par \par \uc2 Last thing, Guy, the Hispanic vote, the Latino vote is shifting. And some of these races along, for example, the border in Texas, they're toss ups now. This yellow column.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Yes. Keep an eye on Texas, keep an eye on Florida, keep an eye on Nevada when it comes to the Latino vote. I'm not sure the Republicans can win it outright this cycle, but just incremental progress on that front really shifts the electorate and scrambles the electoral picture quite a bit. It will be fascinating.\par \par \uc2 All right, panel. Thanks. We'll see you later on the show.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Up next. The trial begins for a Russian national accused in the investigation in the Trump-Russia probe, the \uc2 John Durham\uc2  investigation. And an update on whether the special master in the Mar-a- Lago FBI raid will get access to those classified documents. Keep here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  has rejected an appeal from the man convicted of killing nine black people at a South Carolina church in 2015.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Dylann Roof represented himself during the sentencing phase of his trial and tried to block evidence potentially portraying him as mentally ill.\par \par \uc2 Justices rejected his request to handle disputes over that evidence. Roof is on federal death row at a maximum security prison in Indiana.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): "BREAKING TONIGHT", the second trial coming out of the investigation, the \uc2 John Durham\uc2  investigation into the origins of the Trump- Russia probe is now underway just outside Washington.\par \par \uc2 It involves a