tle pond behind my house.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: President Biden gave virtual remarks on fire prevention but took no questions from press after an emergency huddle with \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders and Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  on Russian escalation in its war in Ukraine. FOX's Trey Yingst in country.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Right now, we're less than a mile from the Russian border. Just a few days ago Ukrainian troops repelled another attack here. These trenches were actually dug by the Russians when they controlled this area a few weeks ago. Off in the distance artillery fire continues as \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is looking to put pressure on the Ukrainian army as they defend the southern and eastern parts of their country.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The \uc2 Group of Seven\uc2  in a statement Speak condemned Putin's nuclear rhetoric and Moscow's latest missile attacks on Ukrainian citizens.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: President Biden has also discussed with \uc2 G7\uc2  partners what more we can do together to prioritize air defense capabilities for Ukraine, which has been and will continue to be a U.S. priority.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But no detail on what new steps the west is taking to deter Putin.\par \par \uc2 MIKE GALLAGHER\uc2 , (R-WI) HOUSE ARMED SERVICES COMMITTEE: He's also sitting on a stockpile of more than 2,000 tactical nuclear weapons. It's not only part of their doctrine, so called escalate to de-escalate doctrine that they don't rule out the use of these tactically, but they put them on every weapon system, including mines, including artillery pieces.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Russia's foreign minister suggested Putin would take a meeting with Biden next month at the G20 summit if it's offered.\par \par \uc2 SERGEY LAVROV\uc2 , RUSSIAN FOREIGN MINISTER (through translator): We've repeatedly said we never turn down meetings. If there is an invitation, then we will consider it.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Biden in an interview with CNN appearing to keep the door open, being careful not to insult the Russian president.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER, CNN ANCHOR: Do you think Putin is a rational actor?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think that he is a rational actor who has miscalculated significantly.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: So if he is not rational --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I didn't say he is not rational.\par \par \uc2 TAPPER: You said the speech is --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I think that the speech is -- his objectives are not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  called on allies to disburse funds to Ukraine immediately, as quickly as possible, after a bilateral meeting with Ukraine's finance minister, but she refused to answer a question from our colleagues at FOX Business about how the world would keep countries like China and India accountable to a pending Russian oil price cap, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live on the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in David Priess. He is the publisher and chief operating officer at the national security website Lawfare. He's also a former intelligence officer at the CIA. David, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 DAVID PRIESS, FORMER CIA OFFICER: You bet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How do you see this playing out right now with what \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is saying and doing?\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: It's really interesting to watch how this shift has happened with the escalation that Putin is now involved in. He thinks that he is going to even now somehow get the Ukrainians to submit. If I fire a few more missiles at a few more civilian centers, that will knock out their will to fight. And that's not what we have seen.\par \par \uc2 I imagine that my former colleagues at the CIA are presenting intelligence to the president and others in the government saying the Ukrainian will to fight a strong, and every time the Russians have escalated, when they've gone after civilian targets, it actually increases the Ukrainians will to fight, and it makes them want to see this thing through till the end even if it involves significant loss of life in the future.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Even if there are a lot of people in the U.S. who would like the Ukrainians to get to the table eventually to negotiate something that gets us away from this very tense moment.\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: Yes, really hard to go to the negotiating table if you are Zelenskyy right now, because the Russians have not only brutalized your people, but they are still holding on to significant amounts of Ukrainian territory. And that's not a good time to go to the negotiating table. Hence, we see the very strong counteroffensive over the last several weeks. Ukrainians are moving to a position where Putin is going to have some tough choices to make about what is he actually going to do to escalate, if at all, or is he going to pull out of some of these areas to enable some face- saving measure on his own part. Frankly, I'm not seeing it yet. Putin seems to be escalating the rhetoric just the same as generals are escalating the battlefield activity, but that is what we are looking for.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to turn to another aspect of Russia, and this deals with Russian interference. In October, days before the 2020 election, you signed onto this open letter that was published by "Politico." It said "We write to say that the arrival on the U.S. political scene of emails purportedly belonging to Vice President Biden's son Hunter, much of it related to his time serving on the board of the Ukrainian gas company \uc2 Burisma\uc2 , has all the classic earmarks of a Russian information operation." Why did you sign onto that?\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: Yes, because of what it says. It has all the classic earmarks of one of these operations. You will note elsewhere in the letter if you read it that it also says we don't know if this is a Russian operation at all. That has been dramatically changed in the retelling of the story. But the letter is merely pointing out so that this is the kind of thing that time after time after time people who study Russian disinformation, intelligence officers who look at Russian tactics over the long period of time, this is the kind of thing they like to amplify to sow discord within target countries. Sometimes that target country as the United States. Most often in the Cold War it was Europe, but the fact is the tactic is an old one, a tried-and-true one, and it's been successful --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But in this case it was not true. It was not true. In fact, "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " found that to that these are authenticated. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " writes "Thousands of emails purportedly from the laptop computer of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  are authentic communications that can be verified through cryptographic signatures from \uc2 Google\uc2  and other technology companies say two security experts who examined the data." "The New York Post" who did the story first says holy cow, here are these other verifications. And then in the debate, right before the election, now President Biden used it. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: Reckless, absolutely reckless. Putin heard this, our allies and friends heard this. This is truly --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We are talking about the issue.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: The laptop from hell.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: President Trump, we are talking about race right now, and I do want to stay on the issue of race. President Trump --\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: But I have to respond to that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Please, very quickly.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Because look, there are 50 former national intelligence folks who said that what he is accusing me of is a Russian plan.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : You mean the laptop is now another Russia-Russia-Russia hoax? Is this where you're going?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: This is exactly what, that's exactly what --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : This where he is --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So understanding how you characterized it, but he characterized it differently and used it in the debate just days before an election.\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: Yes, I will let President Biden speak for himself, he is capable of doing that. What I will do is say that it has all of the classic earmarks of a Russian campaign in the way that it was disseminated and propagated through media.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you regret signing onto the letter?\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: Absolutely not, because those words are still true. It has all of the classic earmarks --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think it changed the outcome of the election?\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: Oh, absolutely not. No.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Even though it wasn't true. It had the classic earmarks, but it wasn't true.\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: What is not true?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That it was Russian disinformation.\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: That's not what we said in the letter. Read the actual letter and we said we do not know if this is Russian disinformation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It has all the classic earmarks of a Russian information operation.\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: Exactly, the difference between an information campaign --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I understand. But the nuance that you were talking about --\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: -- and a disinformation campaign.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: -- the nuance did not get to candidate \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: -- and a misinformation campaign, it is not my fault if people don't look up definitions.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I know, but the purpose of the letter is to have an effect. And the nuance that you are talking about never made it to candidate Biden because he said it plainly on a debate stage. That obviously affected the dynamic, don't you think?\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: I would absolutely love for all news media to show nuance on all these issues instead of racing soundbites. And in this case some news media raced to soundbites. That's not helpful to for the American people.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You think your letter was helpful for the American people?\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: Instead of quoting one sentence from it, if people actually read maybe an entire paragraph, it shows that we don't know if it is Russian --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: In retrospect, you don't think it affected anything?\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: I don't know if it affected anything. We don't analyze American political environments.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But you were trying to.\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: What we were trying to do is point out that this has all the classic earmarks of a Russian information campaign, not to say that it is Russian disinformation, but to say that the propagation through American media and international media has all of the classic earmarks of a Russian information campaign, because we have seen it over and over and over again. They love to sow these kinds of divisions and exacerbate them. That is not American, that's Russian.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I got you, but it ended up being Biden information campaign because he used it in the campaign and in that debate.\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: I encourage you to ask him about that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And I will. Thank you very much for the time.\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: You bet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Coming up, the panel on what we look at with Russia, with fentanyl coming across the border. Keep it here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET), FOX NEWS SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: What you are seen before your eyes here is his attempt to defeat the Ukrainian people. He is attempting to crush their will and their support, and obviously when he fired 84 missiles on Monday morning and 24 drone strikes, that was a huge step in that direction.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The Biden administration needs to start setting the stages for real talks. Right now, it's just we're going to send more weapons. I think it's setting the stage for a larger conflict.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. KEITH KELLOGG (RET), FORMER PENCE NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: We are, in fact, a proxy to the war. For anybody is to deny that is absolutely foolish. So if we actually are a proxy, then we have to go all in in support, not troops, but with the equipment that Zelenskyy needs to win.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There's a lot of debate about what is happening with Russia and Ukraine and what the U.S. should or should not do. We're back with our panel, Harold Ford Jr., Kimberley Strassel, and Guy Benson. Guy, there is just this split about people saying, listen, we just don't want to go too far down this road and somehow be drawn into this. There is the other side that says Russia is weak now with its military and Ukraine is having success and we should do everything we possibly can.\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, POLITICAL EDITOR, TOWNHALL.COM: Right, and help the Ukrainians win, which it appears they are capable of doing, and they are willing to sacrifice themselves at a great cost. They are not asking for us to come in and fight the war for them. They want the tools to fight their own war against an aggressor right on their border who invaded their country, actually has done it multiple times.\par \par \uc2 So I've been with the Ukrainians since day one. It has been remarkable to watch them from go from sort of the little engine that could, putting up a better fight than expected, to actually on the offense and winning. I think that letting Putin off the hook at this point would be a huge mistake. Obviously, people who are way above my pay grade need to figure out how we do so without triggering some sort of volcanic response from him against us, but that also assumes that he is a rational actor, which are intelligent services, at least for now, seem to think that he is.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold, I have heard you say that all Democrats are for supporting Ukraine up on Capitol Hill. There is a part of the Democratic Party that is concerned about the U.S. getting drawn into something in a creeping situation, mission creep, if you will, not just the Republican Party with that split.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: There is no doubt. I think we all are concerned about where this may hit. I think Guy, I think he did a good job there of describing it. I think everyone underestimated the resolve of the Ukrainian people including \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . And \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  overestimated the will and desire of his army to achieve a personal vision of his.\par \par \uc2 I take -- I try my hardest to separate Putin from Russia. Russia is a country with a great history and a great future in front of them. But as Zelenskyy, while President Zelenskyy said today and has said over the past several weeks that if they are going to negotiate with anyone it can't be \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , because he is not the future of Russia. This is Putin's war and the Ukrainians are beating him. The Russian people should know that the American people want a future for them, but not for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . And they are going to have to make a decision at some point, as General Kellogg, General Keane, and many others on this network have said I think more eloquently and with more experience than I have.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kimberley?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLEY STRASSEL, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": If we want to avoid mission creep, the way we have to do that is get done with this. And the notion that we shouldn't be giving some things to Russia, some things to Ukraine because Russians might view that as escalation, we are all in on this proxy war. So where would we be now if we had ceded to Ukraine's request all the way back at the beginning to give them more airpower, to give them better patriot missiles, for instance, patriot missiles? We might be finished with us now. Instead, every day this goes where we pretend that we are not fully engaged is just dragging things along.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Stand by, if you would. By the way, it's good to see you in person.\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: It's great to be here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, more with the panel. And as we go to break, Angela Lansbury has died. The British actress was famous for Broadway roles like "Gypsy" and delighted television audiences for 12 season in "Murder She Wrote." Her children say she died in Los Angeles in her home just five days shy of her birthday. Angela Lansbury was 96.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID PRIESS, FORMER CIA OFFICER: Absolutely not, because those words are still true. It has all of the classic earmarks --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think it changed the outcome of the election?\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: Oh, absolutely not. No.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Even though it wasn't true. It had the classic earmarks, but it wasn't true.\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: What is not true?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That it was Russian disinformation.\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: That's not what we said in the letter. Read the actual letter and we said we do not know if this is Russian disinformation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It has all the classic earmarks of a Russian information operation.\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: Exactly, the difference between an information campaign --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I understand. But the nuance that you were talking about --\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: -- and a disinformation campaign.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: -- the nuance did not get to candidate \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 PRIESS: -- and a misinformation campaign, it is not my fault if people don't look up definitions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, all right, we just wanted to digest that for a second. We're back with our panel, Harold Ford Jr., Kimberley Strassel, Guy Benson. David Priess, a former CIA officer who signed on to that letter with 50 former intelligence officers that said that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  e-mails were all the classic earmarks of a Russian operation. Then \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  used that in the debate, as we played that soundbite. Kimberley, now every paper, every news organization has gone through this and said no, no, no, it's authentic.\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: It's remarkable. And now this kind of latter day parsing of all of this, Russian information versus disinformation and all the earmarks -- look, it was simply wrong, OK? And yet at a very crucial time, right before an election, these people marched in and gave their credibility to the notion that this truly was a Russian disinformation campaign, caused all the media to back them, and caused several social media places to not run "The New York Post," and basically stopped information from getting out to the public. And now they want to pretend that didn't happen.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It is amazing though. If you look back at that, why is somebody not saying, you know what, we got it wrong. Or the media going back and saying we got it wrong. Or -- this was days before an election.\par \par \uc2 BENSON: I think because it served its purpose, which was to help an election go a certain way. It's hard to get around that. The Biden campaign came out and said this is Russian disinformation. They asserted it. Then you had this backfilling from the experts, which was then cited in a presidential debate. The media did not cover it with very few exceptions. And social media, as Kimberley said, throttled it for a reason. They had their narrative, they had their story. It was completely wrong, and they did what they did because they wanted \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to win the election.\par \par \uc2 And now that it's plain as day what actually happened here and that they have egg on their face, I think some of these don't really want to grapple with that in a serious way. And you asked the right questions, I think.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, I would like to ask more. Harold?\par \par \uc2 FORD: I was not asked to sign the letter. I didn't sign the letter.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 FORD\uc2 : It sounds like there are some people that perhaps they should not have signed it. This is why the press should investigate every story with all the vigor and thoroughness they can.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If you're a former intel officer, though, you know, you know that signing this letter is going to have some impact.\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: Of course he did. That's why he did it. And can I also add, too, not only did they essentially do this, but we had an eyewitness, Bobulinski, at the time that they all ignored as well, too.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll keep following that. Panel, thank you very much. I wanted to get that in.\par \par \uc2 OK, it's time for Tuesday Tweets. First, William Galvin wants to know "What exactly is Mexico doing to curb cartels. I hear nothing anymore since President Trump left office about talks with Mexico on this joint problem." I talked to our guys on the border, they talked to the Border Patrol. They say not much. They can't see a lot that Mexico is doing. That's what they're saying.\par \par \uc2 Next up, Terry G says, "Keep up the good work. Have you ever invited \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  on your program? That would be interesting to watch." Well, good question. We did, in fact, do that. Here's a soundbite the last time she was on.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: How can you say the progressive position is still prevailing nationwide when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is winning so much.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ, (D-NY): I think one thing that isn't being talked about is the rampant voter suppression in this country. Right there in Ann Arbor where we had that rally, those kids were waiting three hours in line to vote in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And we'd love to have her back on.\par \par \uc2 Lastly, Bluesteel tweets "Haven't heard anything recently about Ben Hall. Can you provide an update, if you have one?" Yes, he's doing fantastic. He's back with his family. I just got an e-mail from him tonight. He said please tell your viewers I'm back with my family, improving daily. I'm so grateful for all of their support. It has meant a great deal to me. I look forward to working again when able. I feel blessed when I'm with my family. To be frank, he says, I feel blessed about just about everything now. We can't wait to have him back.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we speak with Wisconsin Senator Ron Johnson about his campaign.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. We got a lot in this show. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts right now. Jesse?\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020221012eiab0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Ohio Senate Battle; \uc2 PayPal\uc2  Under Pressure; Rail Strike Still Possible?; Date Set For Coronation of \uc2 King Charles III\uc2 ; Interview With Sen. \uc2 Jerry Moran\uc2  (R-KS); Migrant Wave\b0\par\par\uc2 Mark Meredith, Ashley Webster, Kelly O'Grady, Brett Velicovich,  Jacqui Heinrich, Griff Jenkins, Martha MacCallum, Laura Ingle, Neil Cavuto \par \uc2 8,569 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Your World w/ Neil Cavuto\par \uc2 CAVU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Here we go again, FOX on top of yet another massive wave of migrants arriving in Texas today, pressure mounting on the administration to do something about it or at least talk about it, as more buses are pouring into the Big \uc2 Apple\uc2  as a result of it.\par \par \uc2 And we, well, we're all over it with Griff Jenkins in Eagle Pass, Texas, on the crowds of migrants still streaming in, Laura Ingle on New York City's mayor now trying to slow those migrant buses down, and Border Patrol Council's Brandon Judd on how his officers are just trying to keep up.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, everybody. I'm Neil Cavuto.\par \par \uc2 So much to get to. Let's first get to the border in Eagle Pass. That's where you will find our Griff Jenkins.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey. Good afternoon, Neil.\par \par \uc2 And the Vice President Harris would just come for one day, every single day, we get these big groups here in the Eagle Pass area. It's been going on for months. Let me quickly take you to some drone footage we shot this morning. They arrived like clockwork.\par \par \uc2 This group, Neil, had 340 migrants in it from Peru, Venezuela, Colombia, Cuba, and even places as far as Sri Lanka. We were able to speak to a man from Sri Lanka. Here's a little bit of our exchange. Take a listen, Neil.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Why are you here?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We want to live here. We love -- we like U.S.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: You -- the U.S. And do you have any message for President Biden?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We thank him for accepting us for living here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And as you continue to look at the waves of migrants that have been coming every single day -- I have been here now for 11 days, Neil -- there's over 15,000 migrants have been encountered in just this area.\par \par \uc2 We spoke to one of the landowners. A woman named Magali Urbina owns a pecan orchard where migrants come every single day. She's very critical about why the vice president won't come here. Here's what she had to say. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAGALI URBINA, HEAVENLY FARMS: Well, she would see what I see. And I do not believe that the border is secure. But I would love for her to define what a secure border looks like, because maybe we have it wrong.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: And, Neil, not everyone is seeking asylum. Take a look at this photo of this gentleman. Wilmer Castro-Murillo, 44 years old from Honduras, was arrested two days ago up in Kinney County by Texas DPS.\par \par \uc2 Turns out, after a criminal check background, he has an Interpol warrant for murder out of Honduras, just all parts of this crisis down here. Finally, let me just tell you, those buses you mentioned continue to roll to New York City, particularly from El Paso. We're learning today that the El Paso government, who says they have had some 26,000 migrants released in their city, they'd like the feds to reimburse them for their efforts to the tune of $6.1 million.\par \par \uc2 Neil, we will send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Thank you, Griff Jenkins, at the border for that.\par \par \uc2 Well, New York City Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  is looking for another solution to the bus after busload of migrants that make their way to his city. How about slowing the buses down?\par \par \uc2 Laura Ingle has more on this in New York -- Laura.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGLE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Neil.\par \par \uc2 Well, another challenge is being folded into the influx of migrants making their way here into New York City, with bus drivers from Texas seen by The New York Post getting traffic tickets near the \uc2 Port Authority\uc2 , where they are dropping off migrant passengers.\par \par \uc2 Now, a team of NYPD Highway Patrol officers were spotted pulling over and searching several buses for about an hour last Friday, according to The New York Post, which reported the buses were being combed over for any possible infractions, some drivers getting tickets for violations, including tire tread fluid levels and windshield wiper placement.\par \par \uc2 One driver was seen holding up three criminal court appearance tickets after it was all over, many drivers saying they are just doing the job they were hired to do. And some question the motive behind the traffic stops. \uc2 NYPD\uc2 's chief of department told reporters they have an obligation to keep New Yorkers safe.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KENNETH COREY, \uc2 NYPD\uc2  CHIEF OF DEPARTMENT: The whole unit, our motor carrier safety unit, both within our highway district and within our citywide traffic task force, that focuses on commercial vehicle enforcement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGLE: So they're just doing their job.\par \par \uc2 Texas Governor Greg Abbott maintains his state is dealing with, of course, we have been talking about this, the unprecedented influx from what he calls President Biden's border policies and says he is forced to send thousands of migrants to Democratic sanctuary cities like here in New York, calling the traffic stops a -- quote -- "pathetic ploy" by Mayor Adams to stop Texas from busing migrants to his self-declared sanctuary city.\par \par \uc2 Now, after declaring a state of emergency last Friday, New York City Mayor Adams called the situation a humanitarian crisis that is what he calls unsustainable that will likely cost the city $1 billion by the end of the fiscal year -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, thank you very much for that, Laura Ingle.\par \par \uc2 That figure, just raise it by about eight times, and you have got a good idea how much states like Texas are spending to deal with the same problem.\par \par \uc2 Brandon Judd is the National Border Patrol Council president.\par \par \uc2 Brandon, good to see you again.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of these moves, at least in New York, where they're trying to slow the buses down by having these very, very long and involved inspections and citing them for a whole host of infractions? What do you think?\par \par \uc2 BRANDON JUDD, PRESIDENT, NATIONAL BORDER PATROL COUNCIL: Well, my answer is going to surprise you. I'm actually very happy that he's doing it, because what he's doing is, he's actually drawing attention to this.\par \par \uc2 He is unwittingly becoming the lead voice for border security. He's showing everybody the reason why we have to have border security. If he's not doing this, every single time he opens his mouth or every single time he takes an action like this, it's causing the mainstream media to cover it. And when they cover that, then we're tuning into the actual outlets that are going to give us the truth.\par \par \uc2 And as long as we have an honest conversation, if that honest conversation takes place, the American people are going to be outraged on what's going on, on the border, and they're going to show up to the polls. And that's what we have to have.\par \par \uc2 That's what has to happen. We have to solve this problem at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, you're quite right to say he is drawing attention this. And to be fair to \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2 , he had set faulted both some of the theatrics -- that's what he calls it -- out of Texas. But he's also cited the president for not recognizing the seriousness of the problem.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, the vice president was saying as much last night, that we need to come up with a bipartisan solution here. Good luck with that, though. I'm just wondering, is anything really changing here?\par \par \uc2 JUDD: No.\par \par \uc2 And that's what's really frustrating, because we have given, I have given this administration three different solutions. You like to talk about solutions. I appreciate that. We have given this administration three different solutions. And they have rejected every one of them.\par \par \uc2 Two of those solutions operate within his own parameters, and he rejects it. So he doesn't want solutions. He continues to want this border crisis to continue to go on, Neil. And if \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  would actually say the right things, if he -- if he wouldn't politicize this issue, if he would put pressure on this administration to secure the border, rather than giving him funds to house these individuals, if we would just secure the border, and if he would do that, put the pressure on the White House to do that, then he wouldn't have to deal with this.\par \par \uc2 And, instead, he continues to politicize the issue.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Let me ask you about the immediate things that have to -- I'm sorry, Brandon -- the immediate things that you would like to see done.\par \par \uc2 They talk about finding a permanent solution for this and working on the cause of the problem. Get that. Tried that. We go back and forth with that. But the more immediate need for you is just closing the porous border itself.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Would you be for resuming the wall building that began under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 But that helps with the got-aways. That helps with that individual from Honduras that has that criminal record. It doesn't help with asylum seekers. What we have to have is, we have to re-implement the remain-in- Mexico program, which we know he's not going to do. OK, that's fine.\par \par \uc2 Then surge immigration judges to the border. Hold these individuals in custody. Process their asylum claims expeditiously. And then they will stop coming, because they know that they're not going to be able to prove that they have an asylum claim. They will sent back to their countries. Then the cartels don't...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But the asylum claims themselves run up, as I understand it, far more than just sort of got-aways and what have you, that that's sort of the new way to get into the country.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Oh, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Claim that you need asylum. And you have to take them in, right?\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Yes, and that's the problem. We're not adjudicating the cases.\par \par \uc2 Instead, we're just releasing them into the United States. And they're not showing up to the court appearances later on down the road. So that's another solution that he knows about.\par \par \uc2 You can take the criminality out of the asylum process, surge CIS officers down to the ports of entry, create a lane up to where they're only going through the ports of entry. Then you don't have to have law enforcement. You don't have to have me dealing with asylum claims.\par \par \uc2 Right now, in Arizona, 275 miles of border, we have bare-bones agents on the border right now, simply because we're constantly dealing with asylum officers. These are all solutions that he can implement, and he can implement them tomorrow. He just doesn't do it.\par \par \uc2 Biden, it appears that he continues to want this crisis to go on. How much longer do we have to continue to see these images?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: You know, I just don't remember. You're far better at this, Brandon, than I will ever be, because you're right on the front lines there.\par \par \uc2 But this asylum push is sort of like a green card, no pun intended, to get into the country and have this adjudicated here. And, as you say, it can go on for years. And I don't see too many of those cases going back. So the asylum thing has gotten to be such a big thing now, it makes up eight out of 10 of those who find their way here.\par \par \uc2 JUDD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And the vast majority these claims, they don't have legitimacy, and that's the problem. These loopholes, these immigration loopholes, they're what is allowing the cartels to generate billions of dollars of profit on -- they're skirting and our laws and our loopholes. They're exploiting them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Amazing. Amazing.\par \par \uc2 But, Brandon, to your credit, you did warn us about this. Brandon Judd, the National Border Patrol Council president.\par \par \uc2 But this asylum thing is going to be the big issue here, because it has dwarfed all other issues when it comes to those making their way through the border and getting to stay here. We have to adjudicate those cases. We adjudicate them here. And they stay until they're resolved. And, sometimes, that can be many, many years.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, many, many problems on the energy front, and particularly the gas front, because those prices aren't declining. In fact, they're increasing, even with the promise of taking more oil out of the Strategic Petroleum Reserve. That wasn't supposed to happen. It is.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, gas prices still moving up, in the face of the president doing everything he says he can do to try to get them to go back down, as they were earlier in the year, when we had 99 straight days of declining gas prices. Now, about 19 of the last 21 days have seen them move up 17 straight days in the meantime.\par \par \uc2 The read from that was from Senator \uc2 Jerry Moran\uc2 , who joins us right now, the Kansas senator, the Republican, who recently wrote the following to the president, that: "The recent decision to release another $10 million from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve is not only reckless, but it will have a minimal effect on lowering prices at the pump."\par \par \uc2 The senator kind enough to join us.\par \par \uc2 Any reaction to that? Because it looks like he's still going to go through with the Strategic Petroleum Reserve tapping. It is getting low right now, but that this is his solution. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JERRY MORAN\uc2  (R-KS): Neil, we have been battling, I have been battling this administration their energy, I can't say policy, their energy decision, since the administration, since the president took office.\par \par \uc2 And virtually no response that I know of that ever suggests that the president is altering his course of elimination, reduction, certainly, in the use of fossil fuels. And while that's part of my correspondence with the president, the thing that set me off this week is the release of oil, petroleum from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, which the release is about a half of a day's consumption.\par \par \uc2 So it's not going to do anything, much of anything for the price. But importantly to me, and I think importantly, to the country, we live in a very dangerous world. Why in the world do we have a Strategic Petroleum Reserve? I don't think it was to satisfy the political needs of the moment.\par \par \uc2 It was to make sure the United States is prepared for some crisis. And we don't know when that crisis is going to occur. That's why we stored the petroleum away for purposes of national security. And we are now -- it is dangerous.\par \par \uc2 We are intruding upon our country's national security for purposes of a policy that doesn't exist in regard to energy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: We're told -- and we got first wind of it today, Senator -- that the president had made it very, very clear to the Saudis and, by extension, \uc2 OPEC\uc2  and \uc2 OPEC\uc2  Plus countries, that it would look very harshly at any cut in production. As it turns out, despite that, we got the two-million-barrel-a- day cut.\par \par \uc2 And the Saudis made it very clear that they were not going to be coerced into doing anything else. What did you make of that?\par \par \uc2 MORAN: Neil, how demeaning it is and how ineffective it is to beg the oil producers around the world to meet our needs, when we ought to be focused on -- if we had our energy plans in place, and we were producing more -- and, by that, I'm an all-of-the-above guy.\par \par \uc2 But you can't take fossil fuels, natural gas and petroleum off the table, if we're going to be energy-independent. And we can -- if we were that, if we were producing more, if we had -- meeting our own country's needs, we wouldn't have to be asking others for their help.\par \par \uc2 But if we did need help, they would have more reason to be helpful. That we have -- it's like we take our leverage away in dealing with other countries who are oil producers, and then we go around with our tin cup around the world, Venezuela and others, Saudi Arabia...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MORAN: ... many of those difficult places for the United States to deal.\par \par \uc2 This is not the way to protect the United States, its consumers, or to make certain that we can have an affordable pump fill-up on -- across the country.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, obviously, it's a very big issue in your state.\par \par \uc2 Your Democratic opponent, Mark Holland, who we did reach out to, is also pounding this issue, but also pounding the Roe v. Wade thing, and sensing right now that there might be more public support for his position on this than was considered the case, going back to Kansas voters, deciding not to amend the state Constitution to keep abortion legal.\par \par \uc2 Is this an issue that is stronger than we