ar. Westminster Abbey, be there or be square, May 6, the official coronation of now \uc2 King Charles\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 How will that go down? We're supposed to hear that it's slimmed down and cheaper. Really?\par \par \uc2 After this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, Charles is already the king of England. That, I know.\par \par \uc2 But it's all official -- I think I know -- I barely know this stuff -- next May, when he is going to go through a coronation, much like his mom had done better than seven decades ago. We're told it's going to be leaner, a little meaner affair. I don't -- well, the meaner part, I don't know about.\par \par \uc2 But I know Martha MacCallum knows this stuff inside and out, who has become since, she's hopped the pond so often, a British citizen herself.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: No, no. Still American, 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Not yet. Not yet.\par \par \uc2 So, this is on. This is on.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's on.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, he's talking about -- and you were reporting through all the -- Queen Elizabeth, the funeral and everything else, he wants a leaner, more streamlined royalty, but does that apply to his own coronation?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So I think you're going to see a little bit of a different presentation than what we saw in 1953, when Queen Elizabeth was coronated, which was enormous. There were 8,000 people. They added extra seats inside Westminster Abbey.\par \par \uc2 They televised it for the very first time. They wanted it to be very splashy. It was a big, important moment, this young beautiful queen on the world stage.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Charles has been waiting a long time for this. He's 73 years old. She waited 18 months for her coronation, because she was so young.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This is going to be fairly quick. And I think that's what everyone expected, because he's been around and waiting for this. It would be silly to drag it out for a long time.\par \par \uc2 They have -- the plans are in the works.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, but he's king already.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What will the coronation do?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, the coronation -- for one, they want an appropriate period of mourning after the death of the queen...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: ... and then time to prepare, although this is called Operation Golden Orb. It has been in preparation for a very long time.\par \par \uc2 And, as you saw with the queen's funeral, they do these things like clockwork.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: They do.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And I think it's ready to go. But you need a few months to get everything ready.\par \par \uc2 Plus, they want it to be in a good season, when the flowers are out and all of those things for the presentation of this whole thing.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Sure. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, I was -- originally, it was going to be -- I think June was the thinking, so May 6, right in that area.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Now, the backdrop for this and it might be better by next spring.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: But England's in a world of financial hurt.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And he obviously doesn't want to get people annoyed that this is overly ostentatious and all.\par \par \uc2 But you can't help it with a big event like this, right?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, they have to do enough to make it the grand experience that people have come to expect.\par \par \uc2 And it's one of the reasons that people tune in. And don't forget, people talk about how expensive the royal family is. However, they bring in such a tremendous amount of tourism to the United Kingdom.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Many would argue that they bring in more than they cost in terms of tourism. So there will be an enormous turnout for this coronation, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 And they do want to make sure that it's appropriate, given what's going on in the country, and that it's not over the top. So they have to kind of find that balance between enough pageantry to keep people interested and wanting to come to see it and not being over the top, given what's going on there with the economy.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Well, I think I'm a royal expert now because I have watched "The Crown," the whole series.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And my wife and I really got into it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's very well done.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's extremely well...\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And the reports were that the queen liked to watch it on Sunday nights, and that Philip wanted nothing to do with it.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I would imagine Charles did not.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Charles not a fan.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Would not.\par \par \uc2 The only reason why I mention it, Martha, is that the irony of time and how things have changed, where Camilla will, she will be at Buckingham Palace. She will -- what's her title?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Queen consort.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Queen consort.\par \par \uc2 Imagine if Diana were to be seeing this today. It's just -- everything is so weird to me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It is.\par \par \uc2 I mean, she will be queen consort. And Queen Elizabeth wanted it that way. She wanted to give that confirmation to her. The last queen consort was the queen mother. But she was...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What if she didn't get that title? I mean, it...\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Oh, she would have remained a princess, a princess consort, OK?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But just to -- just for those playing at home, she would never be the queen, right?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Understood.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: If Charles passes away, it goes directly to William. She would never reign.\par \par \uc2 She is simply the wife and the queen of the king, who is in the line of succession, with William and then George and Charlotte and Louis.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: How do you think Charles has handled things since assuming the throne? And, I mean, he's coming after -- his mother was a rock star and brought the country back together.\par \par \uc2 And her approval ratings were very low right after Diana's death, but she came soaring back. And how about him?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: She was enormously popular, and more popular than the monarchy itself. So he has his work cut out for him.\par \par \uc2 And I think his initial reviews were very strong.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: He carried himself very well throughout the course of the funeral and the ceremonies. But now he's sort of in the job.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And so the criticism, I think, will be stronger as he goes through this period and the coronation and all of that.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: What about Harry and Meghan? Are they going to this?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: They're done. I mean -- I mean, would they be there? I would imagine they will be there. But their role is very diminished, very sidelined.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Is their book still on or his book still on? All that is still on, isn't it, or no?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I -- apparently.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, I don't think that the efforts to sideline the book -- I think that's part of the deal. You can come back in if you -- if you're willing to end this book.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: He's not going to do that, right?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But there doesn't seem to be any sign that -- no, they're getting millions of dollars for it.\par \par \uc2 The other projects seem to be trying to move forward. And they just came out with a big portrait of themselves in California...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Saw that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: ... trying to sort of assert their position.\par \par \uc2 But my experience was, people in the United States care a lot more about Harry and Meghan than people in the U.K., who are just over them.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Is that right? Yes.\par \par \uc2 So the idea of getting and reestablishing relationship with his son, that's still -- with William, it's one thing. And William and Kate, they're future king. But, with Harry, it's over, it seems to me.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: For now, I would say. It doesn't seem as though any bridge was built during the course of -- I think there was just a respect for the queen and her desire to have her family at that very meaningful moment in the family's life.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But it doesn't appear that there's any bridge to changing that stature.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But, again, you have a very strong succession with Charles, then William, then George, then Charlotte, then Louis.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, there's no -- the heir and spare, the spare is not really necessary at this point. So...\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 By the way, how did you get into knowing all of this stuff?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know what? I'm just sort of a sponge for history.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And I have always enjoyed British history.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm actually going to Scotland in a few weeks to tour there.\par \par \uc2 I love -- I just am very interested in it. So it's a fun assignment.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I'm that way with municipal bonds. You just -- you can't...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You just get enough when you're studying municipal bonds, right?\par \par \uc2 I used to be like that, but I broke it.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, you got a life. You got a life.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: It's awesome stuff, so I know -- I know where you will be next spring in May. Enjoy Scotland in the meantime.\par \par \uc2 You taking the Royal Scotsman? You going to do that, or...\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: No, but I would love to do that sometime.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: There you go. There you go.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Have you done that?\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: I have not. I wanted to. We were supposed to do it actually before COVID hit.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And so we ended up in the Catskills. But that's a whole separate story.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We will put that on our wish list.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And if you need to know anything about royalty, I can try to help you out now that I have seen "The \uc2 Crown\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Neil. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Anything I can do.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm going to keep you on speed dial.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: Yes, there you go.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: She is so not going to do that.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: And understandably so.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, before we go, some sad news to share with you right now, and Martha did in the last hour. Angela Lansbury has died, of course, the star of "Murder, She Wrote." she was in the old acting style of the golden age of Hollywood. Just think about all the awards. She won five Tony Awards, at least one Grammy. She was nominated for an Oscar three different times, considered one of the most powerful actors of her time.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, on "Murder, She Wrote," she could always figure it out. She's gone on at age\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: All right, President Biden patching in with all the \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders today. And the big concern is the sudden rough turn that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has taken in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich with more on that from the White House -- Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Neil.\par \par \uc2 We just wrapped up the briefing there at the White House. Would have loved to have gotten some more questions, any questions, rather, to the press secretary. We didn't get called on today. Unfortunately, there were no questions about whether or not President Biden would meet with Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  after the Russian foreign minister suggested that the Russians would accept a meeting with Biden at the G20 summit coming up in November.\par \par \uc2 So we don't yet know if there will be a meeting of the minds on the Biden- Putin front. But in terms of the \uc2 G7\uc2  resolution, we did get a readout of the president's call. And the summary of that is basically more condemnation of what Russia, what Putin has been carrying out in Ukraine, also a vow to hold President Putin and those responsible to account for what they see as war crimes and innocent civilian populations being attacked.\par \par \uc2 But, beyond that, we did not get any answers from the president himself about how that call went today. And we also have not had any questions to the president since he made those comments about Armageddon at a fund- raiser last Thursday. He has not taken any questions from the press.\par \par \uc2 And we have heard from the National Security Council, basically tamping down any alarm over that. And in terms of saying there hasn't been any new intelligence that prompted those statements from the president, but in light of this new barrage from Russia on Ukraine, the urgent call for the \uc2 G7\uc2  meeting, and then the suggestion from the Russians that Putin might be seeking out a meeting with Biden, we would have liked to have gotten some more answers there.\par \par \uc2 The president did do an interview with Jake Tapper. And they have released a clip of that interview. The most salient point, I find, is that the president was asked to -- whether or not Putin was irrational, and he refused to say that he was irrational. So it sounds, Neil, like they're keeping things open for a potential interview.\par \par \uc2 I -- just reading the tea leaves there. He seemed not to want to insult Putin, while also saying that what he has done in this war is irrational -- Neil.\par \par \uc2 CAVUTO: OK. That's threading quite the needle there.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui, great job, as always.\par \par \uc2 Jacqui Heinrich at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Want to go to Brett Velicovich on this, the former Delta Force honcho, FOX News contributor. You do not mess with Brett. So that's why I always ask tough questions when we are on remote.\par \par \uc2 Brett, it's always good to see, my friend.\par \par \uc2 I find it interesting now that we have been waiting forever, it seems, for President Putin to respond to pressure from back home. We were thinking, though, that pressure would be from those who tired of his calling up reservists and the like, and that he might cool it. But it was quite a different kind of pressure from people who were even more warlike and conservative on this and pro-war than he is.\par \par \uc2 That worries me. What about you?\par \par \uc2 BRETT VELICOVICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, you're right.\par \par \uc2 And I think it only gets worse from here, Neil, especially as it leads up to winter. I mean, it's almost like the war is restarting again after the events of the last few days. But it also wasn't surprising to me. I think a lot of signs were pointing to these massive retaliatory strikes from Russia as a result of not only Ukrainians' tactical wins on the battlefield, but because Putin is getting humiliated on the international stage.\par \par \uc2 And he had to also address these domestic critics which were saying his invasion was failing. And so here's one of the largest armies in the world getting outmaneuvered and outgunned