ion as well, and they're more likely to get credit cards as just one example. And yet, despite all of that, it was okay to say Asian Lives Matter.\par \par \uc2 We planned a t-shirt to actually do something that was inclusive to say, actually, White Americans, you are allowed to be a part of this, too, because literally All Lives Matter. And in fact, no lives have been mattering.\par \par \uc2 Black Americans are suffering as a result, because of \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2 . We know that their neighborhoods have been rioted in inner city communities and the businesses are moving out of there. The results of \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  has been White supremacy, assuming that Black Americans were stupid and emotional, and would loot and riot their own communities to fill a false narrative about policing.\par \par \uc2 And so we got to say all of that by wearing one t-shirt.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It's just so interesting. I mean, if you're triggered, as they say, by a shirt like that, no one who was mad about it ever seem to ask like why? If it had said Malaysian Lives Matter, or you know, anybody -- you would -- a normal person will say, well, of course, all lives matter. But like, why does this make them so mad? It's weird, and dark.\par \par \uc2 OWENS: It makes them so mad, because there is actually in this country, a hatred toward White people. It is undeniable.\par \par \uc2 The worst thing that you can be in this country, far from what they're telling you, I would say two things. First and foremost, a Black child in the womb of its mother, that is the most unsafe place for a Black child to be. The second thing is a White, straight male, right? Because they would perceive that as being too normal, right? It's too normal for you to be a White straight male. You need to make yourself somehow problematized by saying that you're trans, that you're suffering, that you're gay, that you're Hispanic, that you have a biracial mother -- something.\par \par \uc2 People are dying to say that they're anything, but being White. And again, that is the commentary that we offer by wearing these shirts. You actually cannot right now today on TikTok, you cannot use the #WhiteLivesMatter because they say that it inspires hatred. And in fact, banning #WhiteLivesMatter is what inspires hatred.\par \par \uc2 The saddest part of this, Tucker, which I want to add is that after wearing the shirt and getting back here out in Tennessee, I went to a Farmers Market and I had White people coming up to me with tears in their eyes and I told Kanye this -- tears in their eyes saying, "Thank you so much" as if they are a forgotten people -- "For just allowing us to have our voices back."\par \par \uc2 They've been sitting in silence completely sidelined, told that they're not allowed to be American because there's a vicious narrative around that somehow Black Americans are suffering from policing and therefore White lives can't matter. They do. It's obvious. Kanye was correct.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes. I don't know why people put up with it. I mean, no group -- no group -- no matter what they look like or what they believe should put up with being singled out on the basis of immutable characteristics. There should be zero tolerance for that. And yet, there is enormous, as you said, enforced tolerance. I mean, it should stop now. I agree with that.\par \par \uc2 Candace Owens\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 OWENS: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, they're always telling you shark attacks are rare. Sharks aren't dangerous. Well, they are actually not that rare. Sharks are pretty dangerous, it turns out. Very few people have survived one to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 Our next guest was attacked by a Great White and lived to tell the tale. We are honored to speak to him, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Jared Trainor is 31 years old and he spent more than a decade surfing all across California, up and down the coast. Earlier this month, a coworker suggested that he head to Centreville Beach in the famous \uc2 Humboldt County\uc2 . So, he did. He paddled past the waves. He noticed that something was strange, a Great White Shark, the one thing all surfers fear showed up, knocked him right off the board, and amazingly Jared Trainor survived. He joins us tonight to explain what that was like.\par \par \uc2 Jared, thanks so much for coming on. Had you worried about this? So, I mean, California -- there are a lot of sharks off California. Were you ever worried about sharks?\par \par \uc2 JARED TRAINOR, SHARK ATTACK SURVIVOR: Yes. Sharks have been probably one of my biggest fears for a long time. Unless, I surf despite and I have surfed up and down the coast, but for the most part, I surf up here in \uc2 Humboldt County\uc2  and it was definitely has been on my mind.\par \par \uc2 I live in a town or a city called Fortuna and the surfing options that we have locally are pretty remote and not a lot of traffic or people in the water. So, surfing there quite a bit.\par \par \uc2 It's definitely on your minds. I'd say probably upwards of 70 percent of the time, I do surf there by myself. So, it was on my mind and being the month of October, I had some second thoughts about it.\par \par \uc2 And yes, that day, it was on my mind more than usual, probably just because it was the beginning of October.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It's kind of neat when your worst fears come true, because then, at least you get it out of the way. How did you -- this Great White Shark latches on to you. How do you get him off?\par \par \uc2 TRAINOR: You know, at the time, I had just paddled out I'd probably been in the water for 10 minutes. And you know, between sets of waves, you have some time to think about anything. And it was kind of even on my mind in that moment. And there were a lot of seals in the water.\par \par \uc2 So I actually, you know, it happened so fast that I actually had thought it was a seal. And so, I kind of came to while I was underwater, you know, and most of the pressure was around my knee and it was more localized. So, I thought that it might have been a seal. And yes, I pretty much just gave it one hard kick and it pretty quickly released and I got to my board and paddled in, kind of not knowing that I had, you know, many lacerations and I even checked my knee to see if it had broken my wetsuit and felt a little tear.\par \par \uc2 And almost initially, I felt somewhat inconvenienced or agitated that a seal might have given me an infection. And so when I made it to the beach, that's when I realized how serious it was.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Amazing. There's really nothing cooler than surviving a shark attack from a Great White. So congratulations on being alive and for what an incredible experience.\par \par \uc2 Jared Trainor, thank you.\par \par \uc2 TRAINOR: No problem. Thank you for telling me. I don't feel very lucky, but --\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: It's pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 So Raw Egg Nationalist is maybe someone you haven't heard of, quite well known online, very smart, writes anonymously, often at the "American Mind" and certainly on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . He is the leader of a movement that's looking outside the mainstream for ways to get healthy.\par \par \uc2 We spoke to him for a new "Tucker Carlson Originals" documentary, "The End of Men." Here is part of the conversation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: If the bro scientists have a spiritual leader, it would be a man who calls himself Raw Egg Nationalist. Here is his message.\par \par \uc2 RAW EGG NATIONALIST: The enemy today is what I like to call soy globalism. The globalist aim is to destroy nations and local communities and they do this by isolating communities and sickening through food and also through so-called medicine.\par \par \uc2 With all the dreadful chemicals we're exposed to on a daily basis, the globalist want you to be fat, sick, depressed, and isolated. It would be better to control you and to milk you for as much economic value as they can before they kill you. That's soy globalism in a nutshell.\par \par \uc2 Oh nothing live in the pod eat the soy.\par \par \uc2 The best response to this is a strong politics nationalism. The nation is only as strong as the individuals who make it up and that's where raw eggs come in. Eggs are a superfood packed with protein, fats, vitamins, minerals, and anabolic cholesterol. The absolute opposite of the disgusting rubbish the globalists want you to eat.\par \par \uc2 Eggs are cheap. You can't patent an egg.\par \par \uc2 By making the individual strong, you make the nation strong.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: We are honored to have Raw Egg Nationalist on the show now. He joins us anonymously. Raw Egg Nationalist, thanks so much for coming on. Why is it -- so your position as articulated in the tape we just played is, it's better to be strong and healthy than to be weak and unhealthy? Why is that so threatening to the people in charge?\par \par \uc2 RAW EGG NATIONALIST (via phone): Well, thanks for having me, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 I think it's threatening for a number of different reasons, and one of them at least is because illness is big business. Illness is huge business in the developed world, especially in the US.\par \par \uc2 So whether we're talking about food, about the awful processed food that most people eat that is laced with high fructose corn syrup and canola oil, whether we're talking about the medical regime, and the medical failure to address the root causes of people's physical and mental ailments, the prescriptive model of medicine, or whether we're talking about the harmful chemicals that are featured in the documentary, which is worth -- it is worth remembering are essential to modern manufacturing processes.\par \par \uc2 You can't make plastic without these horrible estrogenic chemicals that are wreaking havoc with men and women's endocrine systems. So, there's that. Illness is big business.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: But you would think --\par \par \uc2 RAW EGG NATIONALIST: And the second thing is --\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Oh, I'm sorry -- I'm sorry to interrupt you. And the second thing is what?\par \par \uc2 RAW EGG NATIONALIST: I was just going to say that progressives are afraid of what fit, healthy, motivated men can do. You know, men are a revolutionary force, plain and simple. History tells that time and again.\par \par \uc2 I mean, today is Columbus Day, right?\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RAW EGG NATIONALIST: So, yes, there's that too. Definitely.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: And I mean, that does seem to be what triggers them. The idea that if men aren't passive, if they're not medicated, if they're not just sitting inside all day, then maybe they will be a challenge to the hegemony of the people wrecking everything.\par \par \uc2 RAW EGG NATIONALIST: Exactly. I think the real power of this documentary, Tucker, is the fact that you're forcing them to face facts that they don't want to face. You're pointing to hard facts, to real trends that are substantiated by proper research by real scientists and they can't just sweep this away. They can't sweep it under the rug.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: No, they can't. Raw Egg Nationalist, you're definitely one of the stars of this document and we're grateful for that and for coming on tonight. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 RAW EGG NATIONALIST: Thank you for having me, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: "The End of Men" is the documentary. It's now on FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 So obviously, you can't celebrate Columbus Day. It's Indigenous Peoples Day. No one asked the indigenous people what they think of that, but it doesn't matter. They have only one representative in the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  right now, and that, of course, is Elizabeth Warren. She's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Happy Columbus Day. That's the day that we celebrate the arrival of civilization in the Americas, but not everyone is happy about that. Some people are mad about civilization.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Warren, for example, she just tweeted this: "On Indigenous Peoples Day, we celebrate the contributions, the extraordinary resilience, and the rich cultures of tribal nations and native communities, today and every day. The federal government must recommit itself to honoring its promises to native peoples."\par \par \uc2 But wait a second, you may ask yourself shouldn't native peoples be able to speak for themselves? Why should yet another shrewish liberal White lady get to speak for them? Seen too much of that already. Oh, but we would correct you.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Warren is not your average shrewish liberal White lady. She herself is an indigenous person. Elizabeth Warren is as indigenous as they come. Not just to Cambridge and in 2012, she gave us proof.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): My Aunt Bee has walked by that picture at least a thousand times remarked that her father, my Papaw, had high cheek bones like all of the Indians did.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes. They've all got high cheek bones, those Indians. Duh.\par \par \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  right now.\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST, "HANNITY": I can't top that. I just - - I know when I am beat. I cannot top that.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: I just want to send her a small pox blanket, it's unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You can't make it up. Tucker, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020221011eiaa00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Russian Missile Strikes Hit Kyiv And Several Other Cities; Putin Threatens More Missile Attacks; Biden Blasts Strikes Amid "Armageddon" Comment Fallout; Arizona Republican Candidate Vows To Secure Border; \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Attends Event But Skips Visiting Embattled Areas on The Border; Rep. \uc2 Adam Smith\uc2  (D-WA) And Rep. Doug Lamborn (R-C) Interviewed On Bipartisan Congressional Support For Ukraine In War Against Russian Invasion; Some Politicians And Parents Warn Of Halloween Candy Being Laced With Fentanyl; Democratic Strategist James Carville And Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  Warn Democratic Candidates Not to Focus Solely On Abortion In Midterms; Some Economists And Financial Leader Say U.S. Likely Headed Into Recession Over Next Two Years\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Trey Yingst, Jacqui Heinrich, Rich Edson, David Lee  Miller, Griff Jenkins, Brit Hume, Aishah Hasnie \par \uc2 7,763 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (voice-over): OK, 20 seconds. \uc2 NASA\uc2  astronaut and Marine Corps pilot Nicole Mann just became the first Native American woman to go to space.\par \par \uc2 She headed the Crew-5 mission aboard the SpaceX Dragon. And Mann is originally from California, member of Wailacki tibe, Round Valley Indian Tribes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO (on camera): Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 And that's it for us. SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hey, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): Hey, Judge. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier. "BREAKING TONIGHT", much of the world is condemning Russia, following a barrage of missile strikes against several Ukrainian cities including the capital of Kyiv. At least 11 people are dead.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  says Russia is trying to wipe his country off the face of the earth. Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  says the strikes were in retaliation to Ukraine's terrorist actions, in his words. We'll have reaction from the White House in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 But first, we go live to correspond to Trey Yingst in Kharkiv, Ukraine tonight. Good evening, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening. Ukraine is on edge tonight, fearing more Russian bombardment after a bloody day across this country.\par \par \uc2 I do need to warn our viewers, some of the following images or graphic.\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice-over): A fireball rises into the air as Russian missiles rain down on Kyiv. It's the worst attack on the Ukrainian capital since the conflict began.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 VITALI KLITSCHKO MAYOR OF KYIV, UKRAINE: It's the war against civilians. Putin need Ukraine without Ukrainians. And Putin attack Kyiv, a couple of weeks ago, months ago, a couple of months ago, and Kyiv was target and still target of Russians\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Kyiv's mayor calling the strikes what they appear to be, a deliberate attempt by Russian President Putin to hit civilian areas. At least 11 people were killed in the attacks that included Iranian drones launched from Belarus. And surface-to-surface missiles targeting 10 Ukrainian cities.\par \par \uc2 The Russians say they were only responding to a bridge explosion over the weekend that damage the only artery between Russia and Crimea. So far, the Ukrainians have not publicly claimed responsibility.\par \par \uc2 Putin is threatening to escalate if further Ukrainian operations are carried out against territory, he says belongs to Russia.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF RUSSIA (through translator): If attempts to carry out acts of terrorism on our territory continue, Russia's respond will be harsh and its scale will correspond to that of the threats made against the Russian Federation. No one shouldn't be in any doubt about that.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: \uc2 U.N\uc2 . Secretary General \uc2 Antonio Guterres\uc2  calling the strikes, another unacceptable escalation of the war, with \uc2 NATO\uc2  Secretary General \uc2 Jens Stoltenberg\uc2 , condemning the actions as horrific.\par \par \uc2 The Russian bombing also took out the power for much of Ukraine as critical energy infrastructure was hit.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): Here in Kharkiv, air raid sirens are sounding again tonight, and many people are sleeping in bomb shelters. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Stay safe, Trey Yingst in Kharkiv, Ukraine. Trey, thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: (voice-over): President Biden's team is joining the international chorus against Russia's latest actions. As you take a look at the stock market down on all of this news today.\par \par \uc2 The president is getting heavy criticism for his comment at a democratic fundraiser that the U.S. and Russia are closer to nuclear war than they've been in decades. In his words, Armageddon.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has that part of the story tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you, Bret. The White House condemned Russia's retaliatory attacks and vowed to support Ukraine for as long as it takes. But Russia's fresh threats have prompted the \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders to huddle tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 And right now, we have no idea how President Biden might explain his suggestion that the world may be headed for Armageddon.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The words of a president matters. They can send the nation to war or they can bring peace.\par \par \uc2 The words of a president matter. No matter how good or bad that president is.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Despite that philosophy, President Biden for four days has chosen not to explain his own, after telling a crowd at a fundraiser Thursday, the U.S. is as close to Armageddon as it's been since the Cuban missile crisis.\par \par \uc2 That \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is, "Not joking when he talks about the potential use of tactical and nuclear weapons or biological or chemical weapons." And Biden's team is still trying to figure out whether Putin has an off ramp.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARSHA BLACKBURN (R-TN): If he's going to make that type of assessment. Then, you deliver the news of that type of assessment from the Oval Office.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, FORMER UNITED STATES AMBASSADOR TO THE UNITED NATIONS: What he told Putin by saying Armageddon, you just strengthened Putin. You just gave him confidence to think that Biden scared.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Over the weekend, the National Security Council trying to soften alarm.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL: That his comments were not based on new or fresh intelligence or new indications that Mr. Putin has made a decision to use nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 What the president was reflecting was that the stakes are high right now.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The president's ally saying, that is appropriate.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS MURPHY\uc2  (D-CT): \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is right so just, you know, get this country ready for the fact that you are dealing with an incredibly dangerous human being in Russia.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But Biden's critic say it's a reflection of Biden's own missteps.\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): This was the, the central failure of Biden was that he couldn't deter \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . So, it's time. They've been dribbling out these weapons too slow, not enough range. We should be providing the Ukrainians every tool they need.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): And the president returned to the White House from his weekend in Wilmington and walked past the press, ignoring shouted questions on Russia, Ukraine, and the \uc2 G7\uc2  meeting tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 But he did make time to speak with Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelensky\uc2 . And according to a readout of that call, the president continued -- pledged to continue support for Ukraine, including with air defense systems. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich, live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There is another down day as we showed you on Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Thanks to a technology-led retreat and lingering concerns about another interest rate hike. The Dow losing 94. The S&P 500 fell 27. The NASDAQ dropped 110 to close at a two-year low.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): The third largest railroad union has rejected its deal with management renewing the possibility of a strike that could further hurt the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 About 56 percent of the track maintenance workers represented by the union oppose the five-year contract, even though it included 24 percent raises and $5,000 in bonuses.\par \par \uc2 The union president saying railroads did not do enough to address worker concerns about the lack of paid time off, particularly sick time and demanding working conditions change. Both sides indicate they will return to negotiations.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's midterm spotlight, Arizona Republican gubernatorial nominee, Kari Lake says if the federal government will not stop the flow of illegal immigrants along the southern border, then she will, as governor.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the incumbent Democratic senator from Arizona says the Biden administration's border policy is dumb. And former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  hoping to make immigration the issue in the campaign in Arizona. Senior national correspondent Rich Edson has our report.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Look at the crowd that you drew Kari.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was back in Arizona, holding a rally for Republican nominees for governor and Senate, with migrant apprehensions hitting a high along the southern border and significant increases in encounters in Arizona, immigration is one of the top issues in a governor's race poll show is a statistical tie.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R). GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE, ARIZONA: I will issue a declaration of invasion and we will work to stop these narco terrorists.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Republican Kari Lake says, if she wins, she'll use state power to administer federal immigration laws. Democratic Secretary of State \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , says Lake's plan to declare an invasion is empty rhetoric and dangerous.\par \par \uc2 And she blames both parties for the state of the southern border.\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS\uc2 , SECRETARY OF STATE, ARIZONA: Trump has centered his whole immigration policy around finishing the wall, and it's not done. And -- but Biden does need to step up immigration and border security.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: While her opponents campaigned with the former president, Hobbs told local reporters, Lake continues spreading conspiracy theories about the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 In the Fox News poll late last month, Arizona registered voters found the most important issues in the Senate race as border security, the preservation of American democracy, inflation, and abortion.\par \par \uc2 Incumbent Democratic Senator Mark Kelly is defending his seat against Republican \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On immigration, Kelly has criticized the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARK KELLY (D-AZ): When the president decided he was going to do something dumb on this and change the rules, you know, that would create a bigger crisis, you know, I've told him he was wrong.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): Kelly says the border is chaos and a mess, and he is pushing for more Border Patrol resources. Despite Kelly disavowing the administration, Masters has tried tying Senator Kelly to the president, calling him a rubber stamp for Biden's agenda, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And that was the only one debate that they've agreed to.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: That's been and it thus far, and we're seeing an awful lot of that, few or no debates in many of these contests.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Rich. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight spotlight races, feature a couple of other states dealing with an increase in migrants despite being located far from the southern border.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Our Fox News Power Rankings have both the Illinois and the New York governor's races listed as solid Democratic holds.\par \par \uc2 In Illinois, incumbent Democratic governor \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  faces Republican Illinois State Senator \uc2 Darren Bailey\uc2 . Bailey says Illinois cannot afford to spend additional money on migrants sent from Texas to Chicago.\par \par \uc2 Pritzker argues the state has an obligation to help but immigration reform is needed.\par \par \uc2 Meantime in New York, incumbent Democratic Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  is calling for a federal solution to address migrants sent to New York City. She will face Republican Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  in November. Zeldin has criticized Hochul for deflecting responsibility for the immigration crisis and for soft state policies that incentivize illegal entry.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Some polls put that race a little closer as Congressman Zeldin campaigns on issues such as immigration and crime, it really is America's crime crisis that's affecting him personally.\par \par \uc2 Two teenagers were shot yesterday outside his home. Correspondent David Lee Miller has details.\par \par \uc2 DAVID LEE MILLER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Republican Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  was back campaigning for New York governor this morning, marching in the Columbus Day Parade. He was joined by his twin 16- year-old daughters.\par \par \uc2 Sunday afternoon, a gunman opened fire outside the family's Long Island home.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R-NY), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Graced here and then the bullet was found right here.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: The congressman, his wife were awake campaigning. But his daughters were in the kitchen when they heard gunfire. They hid in the bathroom and called for help.\par \par \uc2 Two 17-year-olds outside were shot and took cover in the front yard. The shooter got away.\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: I am greatly concerned about crime. This is something that motivated me to get in this race to run for governor in the first place.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Police say it was a drive by shooting, not linked to this Zeldins, except for the random location, perilously close to where they lived.\par \par \uc2 ARIANA, ZELDIN, DAUGHTER OF REP. \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  When we hear the screaming, we definitely knew that it was gunshots.\par \par \uc2 MIKAYLA ZELDIN, DAUGHTER OF REP. \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2 : It's very scary and we didn't know if they were coming after us. And to have that in our lawn, it's like right in front of our house.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Zeldin's Democratic opponent, Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , who critics accused of being soft on crime also marched in the parade.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): I'm so pleased that no one was injured. It's a reminder we all have to work together to get guns off the streets.\par \par \uc2 This is the second violent attack involving Zeldin during the campaign. This summer, a man with a knife-like object tried to stab Zeldin as he delivered a speech.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Hold on. Definitely out of here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MILLER (on camera): In the aftermath of the shooting, police have increased security near Zeldin's home. The police commissioner in Suffolk County says that he is confident in his words there'll be an arrest sooner rather than later, and is asking the public to help. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Lee, thanks. Up next, the Republican congressman is calling out vice president Harris who is supposed to be the administration point person on the border for not going to the border.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. North Korea says its recent missile launches or a simulation of an actual war. State media reports the tests were part of a training to hit and wipe out potential South Korean and U.S. targets.\par \par \uc2 Kim Jong-un is threatening additional provocative tests, he says.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan's president says China's threats of military action are not an option and only push the two sides further from each other. \uc2 Tsai Ing\uc2 -wen made the remarks on National Day.\par \par \uc2 As part of the festivities, fighter jets and a Chinook helicopter, displaying Taiwan's flag flew over the island.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Vienna. And one of the big stories from Austria tonight, Austrian President \uc2 Alexander Van der Bellen\uc2  secures a second six- year term in office, vote projection sow the -- show the 78-year-old want to clear majority to avoid a runoff \uc2 Van der Bellen\uc2  has gained popularity across the country by projecting calm during times of national crisis.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories beyond our borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A small business advocacy group is suing to block the Biden administration 's efforts to forgive student loan debt for tens of millions of Americans. The Job Creators Network foundation argues the administration violated federal procedures by failing to seek public input on that program.\par \par \uc2 It's one of the handful of suits that have been filed by conservative business groups, attorneys and Republican lawmakers in recent weeks. The \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  estimates the program student loan forgiveness, if you call it that will cost taxpayers $400 billion.\par \par \uc2 A Republican congressman from South Texas says Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has abandoned the people along the border. Tony Gonzalez points out the administration's so called borders czar attended a fundraiser in Texas over the weekend, but did not come to his embattled district along the border.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Griff Jenkins is there in Eagle Pass, Texas tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Vice President Harris visiting Texas this past weekend.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATS: I'm just so thrilled to be with all of you and to be back here.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Without stopping at the border or even mentioning it in a speech about abortion rights. In fact, the vice president hasn't been to the border since June of last year when she visited a processing center in El Paso.\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES (R-TX): She could visit anywhere along the border. The fact is they've put their head in the sand, they've abandoned -- they've abandoned us along the border.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Republican Congressman Tony Gonzalez, his frustration is echoed by some border mayors who want to see something done.\par \par \uc2 JOEY VILLARREAL, MAYOR OF RIO GRANDE CITY, TEXAS: The border czar, Congress, the White House have squandered a golden opportunity to forge a distinguished legacy as champions for immigration and border security.\par \par \uc2 This as Fox News exclusively learns over the weekend, there were more than 13,000 migrant encounters across the entire southern border for more than 40 countries including Bangladesh, Iran, Somalia, Syria and Russia.\par \par \uc2 Here in the Del Rio sector, we witnessed large groups of hundreds of migrants daily. Some migrants even running from Mexican authorities before crossing the Rio Grande. An emotional reaction when they reach the U.S. side.\par \par \uc2 But the surge is taking a growing toll on local landowners who have their own messages for the vice president who insists the border is secure.\par \par \uc2 MAGALI URBINA, OWNER, HEAVENLY FARMS: I do not believe that the border is secure, but I would love for her to define what a secure border looks like, because maybe we have it wrong.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (on camera): Meanwhile, Bret, human smuggling is on the rise, the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  reports a 500 percent increase in smuggling arrests in the past six months. And in just the last 10 days, we've been here in the Del Rio sector where they've disrupted more than 10 smuggling attempts, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Griff Jenkins along the border. Griff, thank you. Let's bring in Fox News Senior Political Analyst Brit Hume.\par \par \uc2 Brit, good evening. You know, this is not a great look for an administration that's under fire about border security issues and the border overall for the fundraising to happen in Texas, even though it's miles from the border.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, it isn't of course. And this is an issue that seems that long last to gain some visibility. The longest time we had Fox covering this and showing video every day like that which you just saw of migrants just you know, walking across the shallows of the river or swimming across and pouring into the United States, most of them being a great percentage of them being allowed to stay, even though they were illegally here. And the administration wasn't doing much about it and still doesn't.\par \par \uc2 And for a long time, it wasn't really covered. The effect it seems to me of of those migrants being shipped north to cities and of course most famously to Martha's Vineyard seems to have awakened some of the media because of the reaction to it. And it's now getting some coverage. And it's outright lawlessness tolerated along the border and has been for years.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, there's some media Brit that often cite Fox News saying that we focus on this when it comes to an election. We've been down on the border for really years. But most recently, in the past year and a half, as the situation is exacerbated. It's a real story that we're covering. And it may really have an impact not only in border states, but a lot of states.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Well, in particularly Bret, when you see the overall issue of crime becoming more visible and people's concern about it rising. So, this feeds into that. It sort of backs up the idea that the administration is prepared to tolerate lawlessness in places where he thinks it'll be popular with a certain element of its base. And that's what I think we see happening.\par \par \uc2 And of course, this whole defund the police matter which many Democrats have disavowed now, but that was something that a lot of them were for, before they were against. And that's out there, too. So, you put these two things together, and you get crime up high and people's concerns are at least higher than it was.\par \par \uc2 BAIER Yes. And last thing, I mean, if anything tells the story about immigration, it's Democrats in places like Arizona talking in debates, talking in interviews, about how the administration is backwards on this issue.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Yes, it dropped the ball on this issue and the Democratic Party and President Biden seem willing to take chances with the political fortunes of members of their own party by refusing to do anything about it or anything of any consequence. So, I think that's another piece of this equation that ought to be kept in mind as we approach this midterm.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, one Democrat, one Republican, lawmakers seeking Common Ground. We'll bring them here, our weekly segment is next.\par \par \uc2 And later, what could be a nasty and dangerous surprise may await your trick or treaters this Halloween, at least that's the thought on Capitol Hill, we'll explain.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Our top story at the bottom of the hour, Russian missile attacks kill at least 11 civilians in Ukraine. President Biden today saying the assault demonstrates the utter brutality of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's illegal war. The president is promising to continue providing Ukraine with support.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the head of Ukraine's law enforcement says today's missile attacks by Russia damaged some 70 infrastructure sites, of which 29 were called critical.\par \par \uc2 Here in the U.S., a pro Russian hacking group is claiming responsibility for taking the websites of several U.S. airports offline this morning. A media reports citing U.S. officials saying Killnet launched a distributed denial of service attack. Among the airports affected were Los Angeles International, Chicago O'Hare, in Hartsfield-Jackson in Atlanta. The spokesman for Atlanta's airport says security officials are investigating.\par \par \uc2 Now over to Tehran. The sound of apparent gunshots and explosions echoed today through the streets of a western Iranian city, one of the hotspots of protests over the death of a 22-year-old woman in police custody. Activists in Iran see at least one man reportedly was killed by security forces in a village nearby. These incidents come as demonstrations rage on over the woman's death. Iran's government insists she was not mistreated, and the protests, they say, are getting foreign help.\par \par \uc2 It's time for tonight's "Common Ground" segment. We're going to discuss the war in Ukraine, foreign policy on a number of issues. Joining us are Washington Democratic Congressman \uc2 Adam Smith\uc2  and Colorado Republican Representative Doug Lamborn. Chairman Smith, I want to ask you about what you're hearing and seeing from Ukraine and how you interpret it.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ADAM SMITH\uc2 , (D-WA) HOUSE ARMED SERVICES COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: The big news here is Ukraine's counteroffensive is really having an effect. We've seen the progress they've made in Kharkiv, moving down into Luhansk and Donetsk. We're having progress towards taking Kherson. They've really punched back and started to retake territory. And this has caused a lot of chaos for Russia as we've seen as they've attempted their mass mobilization.\par \par \uc2 So I think that is the big headline is Ukraine's success. Russia obviously is having the brutal response that they're having, but the last key point on this is the coalition is holding together. The coalition to support Ukraine. And that's what Doug and I were able to see when we visited our \uc2 NATO\uc2  partners in Europe over the last week. It's tough, but they understand how important it is to preserve Ukraine's sovereignty. And the coalition is holding together. And that, I think, is good news.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congressman Lamborn, your common ground here with the chairman. What's your thoughts and what you saw from that trip?\par \par \uc2 REP. DOUG LAMBORN, (R-CO) HOUSE ARMED SERVICES COMMITTEE: Chairman Smith led this delegation of Armed Services members to four southern European countries -- Croatia, Greece, Spain, and Portugal. They are all members of \uc2 NATO\uc2 . They are all very united. They're looking for U.S. leadership. They're more or less doing their best to meet the pledged two percent of their GDP devoted to defense. And they know it's going to a hard winter for their populations, their citizens, because of energy costs going up. These are southern countries that are not as cold maybe as some in the north, but they're going to can have problems, maybe even politically, with people having really high heating bills and things like that. But they do want to do whatever it takes to defeat \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . What he is doing is an attack on western values, and it cannot stand. And they are very united, and that was heartening to see.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you're united, Republicans and Democrats, at least on the committee. I saw a tweet you had talking about the off-ramp chatter, Congressman Lamborn, when you said, "What do you get a dictator who wages an provoked war against his neighboring and commits unspeakable human atrocities for his birthday? Definitely not an offramp." Congressman, you know there are people in your party who look at what's happening now and say when is this going to stop, and how far does the U.S. go? How do you respond to them?\par \par \uc2 LAMBORN: Bret, there are extremists in both parties that are not with us 100 percent. There are some progressives who want to cut defense. There are some isolationists on the right wing. They are in the minority. And people like \uc2 Adam Smith\uc2  and I and other members of the Armed Services Committee, we hear about the threats every day. And we get it. We understand how serious this is. All of our colleagues don't necessarily hear all of the same things that we hear, but we are try and educate them so they will be with us on board. But I think that we have a strong unity, and it is seen in the fact that we have increased defense spending even over the president's proposed budget each of the last two years. And we are very united for the most part in Congress.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chairman Smith, the numbers are kind of staggering if you look at what we have put into Ukraine already. Just doing the math, it's about $27 billion roughly in U.S. aid to Ukraine. And obviously people look at that as you get closer to an election and say, what is the payoff for the U.S.? And then you add to that the concern over nuclear war and what the president said over the weekend at a Democratic fundraiser, that we're closer now to Armageddon than we have been since the Cuban Missile Crisis. What is your reaction to that statement and whether that was the right thing to say at this moment?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Two things. First of all, I think we have to continually make the case, because as Doug outlined, this isn't easy. The impact on energy certainly, but food, we've seen inflation go up. Ukraine and Russia provide important resources for the world that just aren't there now because of this conflict. We have to make the case for why this matters, and I think Doug did a pretty good job of it.\par \par \uc2 What Russia is attempting to do is bring us back to a world where military might and autocracy dominated neighbors. We want a rules-based international order or that respects sovereignty. So what is on the line certainly is Ukraine, no doubt about it, but it is a much broader principle. And trust me, China is watching as well as China makes claims against the sovereign territory of at least a half-dozen nations in Asia. This will send a strong message to whether or not they can just use their brute force to grab them. So this is important to us.\par \par \uc2 Now, as for the president's comments about Armageddon, I think it is absolutely right to be mindful of the risks here, but I think President Biden has struck a very good balance to date in terms of his policy. He has supported Ukraine. It's making the huge difference that we've seen. The HIMARS systems, the weapons that are getting there are beginning to give Ukraine that upper hand.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden deserves a ton of credit for pulling together the coalition that did that while also saying we are not going to directly fight Russia. We are not going to do that because we don't have that conflict. So --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But let me just interrupt you real quick. If you're going to go down that road and say that he is doing a great job, if we are as close as we are to Armageddon, shouldn't he have that conversation with the American people and not at a Democratic fundraiser?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: He has had that conversation with the American people. He has stated over and over again, and I won't restate it, that the policy that I just basically said, he has said over and over again. Presidents go to fundraisers, and they answer questions, and they have conversations. That is true of Democrats and Republicans alike. So I think the president has repeatedly said what he's doing here. I wouldn't necessarily have chosen words the way he did at that particular event, but none of us say everything exactly right every time. His policy, what he's actually been implementing, has been working, and it struck that balance in the way it needs to be.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And last word, Congressman Lamborn, your message is that there is unity up there on Capitol Hill on this issue specifically.\par \par \uc2 LAMBORN: There really is. We will support the funding that it takes. Every single person may not be with us on this, but we will support the funding. We have strong majorities. \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is doing exactly what Adolf Hitler did, and \uc2 Adam Smith\uc2  outlined that very well. He is invading smaller neighbors. He is provoking ethnic grievances. These are things totally against western values. And he's really close, dangerously close, to invading a \uc2 NATO\uc2  or at least harming \uc2 NATO\uc2  members where we have an all for one collective commitment to defend each other. So he is treading on dangerous ground, and he only understands force. We can't let up on the gas now. We have to keep working. And doing this, and I think under Chairman Smith and Ranking Member \uc2 Mike Rogers\uc2  of Alabama, we are doing a great job as a committee and will continue taking that message to all of our colleagues in Congress.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's a precarious time. People are worried about it, and we appreciate you coming on "Common Ground," both of you.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, it could be a scary Halloween for kids and parents. It has nothing to do with costumes. All this talk about that fentanyl and how the fentanyl crisis could threaten your children on that night. Is it a scare tactic? They don't think so on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Later, the panel on President Biden's impact on a key midterm race.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Officials in southwest Florida say the major search for victims of hurricane Ian has ended. Electricity has been restored to more than 2.5 million businesses and homes. Ian is responsible now for at least 119 deaths across Florida. Over the weekend many residents were allowed to what's left for Fort Myers Beach. It was that area's first direct hit from a major hurricane in the century.\par \par \uc2 We are three weeks away from Halloween. Tonight, Republican senators are warning parents about a very deadly trick that could be disguised as a treat. The message overall, be careful. Congressional correspondent Aishah Hasnie has details from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 DEBI NADLER, "MOMS AGAINST DRUGS" CO-FOUNDER: Smart as a whip, the greatest joy to be around.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Daniella Young was just 13 years old when she overdosed on a pill laced with fentanyl. The eighth grader from Nevada got it from a dealer she met on \uc2 Snapchat\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NADLER: It's going to be a year this week.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: As her cousin Debi Nadler and the rest of her family prepare to commemorate the one-year anniversary of her death, 12 Republican senators are warning parents to be on the lookout for candy colored fentanyl ahead of Halloween.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MARSHA BLACKBURN\uc2 , (R-TN): "The powerful drug cartels are coming after your kids."\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: But some of called the ad a scare tactic, pointing out that the midterms are just one week after Halloween.\par \par \uc2 NADLER: It's not a scare tactic, believe me.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: For Debi and law enforcement across the country, it's a crisis. Last month a bipartisan group of attorneys general sent a letter to President Biden calling on him to classify illegal fennel as a weapon of mass destruction, writing, "Enough sentinel has been seized in the last year to pull every man, woman, and child in the United States several times over." This Halloween, Debi isn't taking any chances when she takes her grandkids trick-or-treating.\par \par \uc2 NADLER: I was going to bring a huge bag of candy. When they got home I was going to swap out the candy. Once you've lived through this nightmare from start to finish, we are definitely on our toes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: But you don't go that far. As the co-founder of a drug prevention program, Debi says she's been telling parents to take their kids trick-or- treating to homes that they are familiar with, and also to check their candy at the end of the night. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good advice. Aishah Hasnie on Capitol Hill. Aishah, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on midterm elections and the Biden-Harris factor. Here is what some FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 11 in Los Angeles where the city council president resigns over comments she made about a coalition member's son. Councilman Mike Bonin is white and has a black child. In leaked audio from October of last year Council President Nury Martinez allegedly criticized the two-year-old's behavior at an event, saying Bonin was raising him, quote, "like a little white kid who needs a beatdown."\par \par \uc2 This is a live look at Chicago from FOX 32. One of the big stories there tonight, holiday celebrations throughout the city. The 70th Columbus Day parade was held this morning. Local leaders also met with Native American leaders to celebrate Indigenous People's Day.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BETSY JOHNSON, (I) OREGON GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: It's homelessness, its crime, it's open-air drug dealing. People are frightened and they are mad.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS\uc2 , (R) ARIZONA SENATE NOMINEE: Everything is on fire. Our southern border is a disaster. Crime keeps going up.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY SMILEY, (R) WASHINGTON SENATE NOMINEE: We have homeless encampments. We have people on drugs walking around our streets with no plan and no hope in sight.\par \par \uc2 JOSH SHAPIRO\uc2 , (D) PENNSYLVANIA ATTORNEY GENERAL: People have a right to both be safe and feel safe in their communities. And we need more police in our communities.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDIA TENNEY\uc2 , (R) NEW YORK REPRESENTATIVE: People are starting to recognize, even Democrats, that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his administration and the policies and his lack of competence is dangerous to our country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Sights and sounds about the conversation over the weekend as we get closer, 29 days till the midterm election. Meantime, a Democratic consultant everybody knows, James Carville, by the Associated Press, gave this quote, "A lot of these consultants think if all we do is run abortion spots, that will win for us. I don't think so. It is a good issue, but if you just sit there and they are pummeling you on crime and pummeling you on the cost of living, you have got to be more aggressive than just yelling "abortion" every other word."\par \par \uc2 So let's bring in our panel, Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman, co-host of THE FIVE, former White House press secretary \uc2 Ari Fleischer\uc2 , and Matthew Continetti, fellow at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 . Ari, what do you think about what Carville said there and how this race is shaping up?\par \par \uc2 ARI FLEISCHER\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: He is 100 percent right, particularly in the battleground districts. What he is saying may be true in an overwhelmingly Democratic district, but the places where the November election will come down to are obviously the battlegrounds where you have much more 50-50 contests, and pure Democrat rhetoric won't cut it.\par \par \uc2 So I think, Bret, when you look at this selection, the biggest trends still is a historical one. The party in power almost always loses a significant number of seats in its first midterm. That will happen again in just four weeks. The Democrats are on track to lose the House of Representatives. The Senate remains a jump ball. I think it's still 50-50. I would bet that it comes down to 51-49 after a long night watching election results.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll see. The progressives, Harold, are also piping up, \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  telling "The Guardian," "Democrats shouldn't focus on only abortion in the midterms. That's a mistake. I'm alarmed to hear the advice that many Democrats candidates are getting from establishment consultants and directors of well-funded super PACs that the closing argument of Democrats should focus only on abortion, cut the 30nd abortion ads and coast to victory. If Democrats do not fight back on economic issues and present a strong pro-worker agenda, they could well be in the minority in both the House and the Senate next year."\par \par \uc2 Is seems like \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  is laying the table out there.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: I think Bernie -- first off, thanks for having me. I think \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and James Carville have it right. The Republicans have laid out, I think, two agendas. I think \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  has laid out an agenda and Kevin McCarthy has laid out an agenda. And I think it's a positive when the parties let you know where they stand.\par \par \uc2 And the country is going to have an opportunity to choose. Do you want prescription drugs that are negotiated by Medicare which Democrats want, but it doesn't seem Republicans do? Do you want a fully funded police bill which Democrats all voted for, Republicans didn't. And do you want Zelenskyy to be supported, which many if not all Democrats want, and it feels like some Republicans don't.\par \par \uc2 This is not a statement about good or bad on these things, but these are things that I agree with. I think we should have Medicare negotiating prescription drug prices. I think we should fund mental health services. When Ari and I were on maybe a few months back, it was a terrible day, the Uvalde shooting was, and we both talked about the need for mental health services. Congress had an opportunity to pass a bill last week, and every Republican but one Congressman voted against it.\par \par \uc2 These are the things Democrats should lay out. Abortion alone is not going to lead us to victory. It's an important issue. It's an important freedom that women and families and doctors enjoy, but we have to make this about our future, and our economic future at that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Speaking of economics, Matthew, two voices that people listen to when it comes to that, \uc2 Larry Summers\uc2  and \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LARRY SUMMERS\uc2 , FORMER TREASURY SECRETARY: I think it's more likely than not that sometime in the next year or 18 months we will have a recession. I think that that is a consequence of the excesses that the economy has been through.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE DIMON\uc2 , \uc2 JPMORGAN CHASE\uc2  CEO: These are very, very serious things, which I think are likely to push the U.S. and the world, I mean, Europe is already in recession, and likely to put U.S. in some kind of recession six or nine months from now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Really, a lot of people focus on that more than anything else, Matthew.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: That's right, Bret. And if you ask voters today, most Americans will say that they are currently in a recession, they feel as though they are in a recession, and that's because inflation has cut their standard of living by a dramatic extent. Inflation is the issue for this midterm. The recession will be the issue for Democrats in 2024.\par \par \uc2 And I think that the reason that the Democrats are waking up to the fact that they are going to have a long night in four weeks is that on the issues that matter most to independent voters, the economy, crime, and the border, the Republicans have the advantage, and that will be decisive.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, as you look at our FOX News power rankings for the Senate, and it's very close, Republicans need to pick up two. The yellow is the toss- ups in the middle, and then you have lean D and lean R, there are 10 total races there.\par \par \uc2 Just ticking down the toss-ups, Georgia, you have Warnock up in the average of polls about four, obviously \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , we haven't seen the latest polls after that. Let's put up Georgia, here we go. Arizona is next. This is Mark Kelly just had a debate over Masters, still has a lead. Nevada, it's the other way around where the incumbent Democrat is trailing the opponent in Laxalt. And Pennsylvania is still a very tight race in Pennsylvania, and the latest polls. Ari, of those, what do you think is a most interesting those battles?\par \par \uc2 FLEISCHER: Well, I think Nevada is the most interesting. I think that's the one where Republicans have the greatest likelihood of gaining a seat. If you're the Democrats, Pennsylvania is the most likely place that they might gain a seat. That would be a hold for Republicans if Mehmet Oz can win that seat.\par \par \uc2 But Bret, I also think the polls, and we have seen this in the last several elections, understate, undercount Republican vote. And I think they do it by about two to four percentage points. And if this historical trend is what I think it is, if it's still a red wave, Republicans have a much bigger upside potential on election night than the Democrats do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold, if that's true, if what Ari just said is true about the polling, those lean D actually fall into the yellow toss-up categories and become possibilities for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Look, I think Ari is right. Historically, it's hard to dispute that.\par \par \uc2 I think that the wild card here is really, as much as abortion can't be the closing issue, that 20 points win in Kansas for those who believe in reproductive rights, and the race in New York, the special election just a few weeks ago where abortion was on the ballot with essentially a Democrat arguing that and a Republican arguing that Biden was not up to the job on economics, inflation, et cetera, and the Democrat won, that's a seat that has gone back and forth. It's going to come down to turn out, and swing voters in this issue are going to be, much like they were in Virginia, they were parents. They might be women in a lot of these places, and we'll have to see what happens.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And finally tonight, a special day. A generous community, dinosaur friends, support a child in need at a local charity golf tournament. Six-year-old Ezra King has bravely battled a brain tumor since he was 18 months old. The Woodstock Public Safety Foundation hosted this event, donated all the proceeds from the ticket sales, sponsors, and raffle to the King family to assist with their medical bills. Tickets sold out well in advance. Great stuff.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, a Russian analyst begins trial on charges lying to the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , that infamous Steele dossier. We'll cover it here.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts now. Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020221011eiaa00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Biden Warns of a Nuclear Armageddon; Interview with former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 ; Democratic Lawmakers Express Frustration with Saudi Arabia Following \uc2 OPEC\uc2 's Decision\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Lucas Tomlinson, Trey Yingst, Mike Pompeo, Juan  Williams, Charlie Hurt, Jim Gray \par \uc2 9,690 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 President Biden gives a stark new warning, just how close the world could be to a, quote, nuclear Armageddon.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): The president says Putin isn't joking about the possibility of major escalations as Russia's war on Ukraine falters.\par \par \uc2 And oil-rich nations slash production, leaving the White House looking for new help to bring down gas prices.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: There's a lot of alternatives. We haven't made up our mind yet.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We will bring in former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  on how the U.S. should respond to Russia and how to beef up our oil supply.\par \par \uc2 And we're weeks out from November's rematch Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  and Georgia Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 . We'll get Abrams response to our interview with Kemp just one week ago.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 BRIAN KEMP\uc2  (R), GEORGIA: Eighty-six percent of her money is coming from around the country and that's what we're fighting against.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And we will press her on abortion, the latest polls and her case to voters this time around.\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D), GEORGIA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: The fundamental difference between me and the current governor is that I care about you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Plus, the president pardons thousands of Americans busted for small amounts of marijuana.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It's time that we right these wrongs.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll ask our Sunday panel what it means for federal and state drug laws and about the timing of the announcement. Plus, new reports of legal trouble for the president's son.\par \par \uc2 And then a huge week in sports. Baseball fans debate whether a record- setting home run is officially the all-time best and football fans debate whether this guy should sue? Legendary sportscaster Jim Gray joins us live.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): And hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 The White House says it has no intelligence that any sort of nuclear attack is imminent, but the president's comments have created quite a stir. And now, there is a mystery over what caused a truck explosion Saturday in the middle of the bridge that connects Crime and Russia. It's severed a key supply line for Russia in its war on Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 This all happening as Russian President Putin says he plans to hold on to territory he says he's annexed in Ukraine. In a moment, we will ask former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  about how he would address the escalations.\par \par \uc2 But, firs, we begin with team coverage. Trey Yingst live in the ground in Kharkiv, Ukraine, with an exclusive report on what he discovered in a village Ukrainian troops reclaimed from Russia just days ago.\par \par \uc2 And Lucas Tomlinson who's traveling with the president in Wilmington, Delaware -- Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, Russian President Putin's state of mind is right now top of mind for President Biden.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): Speaking at a Democratic fundraiser this week, Biden said we haven't, quote, faced the prospect of Armageddon since Kennedy and the Cuban missile crisis".\par \par \uc2 The White House explaining it more a day later.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We have not seen any reason to adjust our own strategic nuclear posture nor we have indication that Russia is preparing to imminently use nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: The comments come as Putin has repeatedly indicated he could turn to nuclear weapons to protect Russia's gains in Ukraine. But the Russian army is still suffering humiliating defeats and as hundreds of thousands of Russians have fled the country to evade his mobilization order.\par \par \uc2 Also in the president's mind, the U.S. economy, which added 263 jobs last month, the fewest since April 2021, a sign of cracks in the labor market.\par \par \uc2 Still, Biden claiming it as a win.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: That's 10 million jobs since I've come into office. That's the fastest job growth at any point of any president in all of American history.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: It comes as gas prices remain 60 percent higher than when he took office and as many Democrats are furious at OPEC Plus decision led by Saudi Arabia to cut oil production by two million barrels a day.\par \par \uc2 Michigan Congresswoman \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  calling it a new low in saying all diplomatic options should be on the table, including the withholding of American military hardware and security assistance that Saudi Arabia in particular is so dependent on.\par \par \uc2 Saudi Arabia's top diplomat saying the U.S. has itself to blame.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: With due respect, you have high prices in the United States is because you have refining shortage that has been in existence for more than 20 years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): The president making headlines for pardoning thousands convicted of marijuana, and his son Hunter also in the news for an investigation to his taxes and a gun purchase -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll talk about that with the panel coming up.\par \par \uc2 Lucas Tomlinson reporting from Wilmington, Delaware -- Lucas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's turn now to Trey Yingst who is live with an exclusive for us on a newly liberated village in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, the village of Hlushkivka was liberated by Ukrainian troops just three days ago. Yesterday, we were the first journalist to enter this area amid ongoing battles.\par \par \uc2 I do need to warn our viewers, some of the following images are graphic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (voice-over): The sound of ongoing artillery pierces the air of Hlushkivka as 70-year-old Natalia Lashina (ph) hugs a Ukrainian serviceman. The village she calls home now sits along the eastern frontline. The ground she uses to grow vegetables is now soiled with blood of Russian soldiers.\par \par \uc2 The bodies of Russian soldiers still lay amid their destroyed positions. Ukrainian troops took over this land, recapturing their territory. But as you hear in the distance, fierce battles continue here in the eastern part of the Kharkiv region.\par \par \uc2 You stand here and hear shelling and you are shaking, Natalia (ph) says, how to express this, fear, only fear.\par \par \uc2 The nearby city of Kupiansk was occupied by the Russians for nearly seven months before being reclaimed four weeks ago. On the road into town, next to the graffiti of the Russian z symbol lay the remains of a civilian, a more accurate emblem of this gruesome war. Shattered windows and battered buildings line the streets. Behind broken doors, a look into life under Russian control.\par \par \uc2 Inside this government building in the town of Kupiansk, the remnants of the Russian occupation, a Russian newspaper and Russian flags inside and outside of the building.\par \par \uc2 Fruit sits atop a desk, an indication of how quickly Russian troops fled when the Ukrainian counteroffensive began. And in the halls where Russian President Putin was praised just last month, the man responsible for this unnecessary and unjust invasion is referred to only as a creature of destruction.\par \par \uc2 I don't think humans act like this, one Ukrainian soldier says. You have to be totally sick to do this to a country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): As we enter these liberated areas with Ukrainian soldiers, people alongside the road were waving and smiling after months of terror under Russian occupation, they are finally free -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Trey Yingst, reporting on for us in Ukraine, thank you very much for that powerful report, Trey.\par \par \uc2 And joining us now, former secretary of state, \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Secretary, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 MIKE POMPEO\uc2 , FOMRER SECRETARY OF STATE: Good morning, Shannon. It's great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's start with that bridge explosion. Extensive damage, this is a key artery and supply line between Russia and Crimea.\par \par \uc2 Who do you think is responsible? And what's the impact on the state of the war practically but also psychologically?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Well, Shannon, it's hard to know, my guess is that the Ukrainians have something to do with it, but we'll have to wait and see how that unfolds.\par \par \uc2 We do know for sure that this is humiliating for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . He built this bridge. And remember, the pictures of him driving across it was one of their great achievements.\par \par \uc2 I think it was in 2018 when that bridge was built. So, it's nearly brand new, and to watch in flame, to watch the risk to the supply lines out of Crimea into southern Ukraine be at risk is certainly something that is a game changer on the ground in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 As Trey reported this morning, Ukraine is doing phenomenal work at liberating town after town after town and the Russian military is failing desperately.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, I want to go back to the reporting also by Lucas from the White House about what the president said Thursday night. Here's a little bit more of his report from the White House transcript.\par \par \uc2 It says: The first time since the Cuban missile crisis, we have direct threat of the use of the nuclear weapon if, in fact, things continue down the path they've been going. We've not faced the prospect of Armageddon since Kennedy and the Cuban missile crisis.\par \par \uc2 The president went on also to say he doesn't think that President Putin is joking. He also says the administration is trying to figure out an off-ramp for Putin. Is there one? And would he take it?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Oh, my goodness. First off, those comments were reckless and I think they -- even more importantly, they demonstrate maybe one of the greatest foreign policy failures of the last decades, which was failure to deter \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  in the same way Trump administration did for four years.\par \par \uc2 When you hear a president talking about Armageddon at a random -- as a random thought just musing at a fundraiser, that is terrible risk to the American people, if he truly believes that he ought to be talking to us in a serious way. More importantly, he should be doing all the things that are necessary to deter \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Shannon, we've been here before, not only in October of '62. We've been here other times as well. And America has always pushed back against our adversaries by showing enormous resolve, executing quiet diplomacy in the same way that we did during our time in office, making very clear to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  that the cost -- the cost of him using nuclear weapon would bring the force of not only United States and Europe but of all the whole world against \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . We ought to be doing that. I hope that they're doing this quietly.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, there's a lot of talk this week that what \uc2 OPEC\uc2  did, going against lobbying of the Biden administration. They say it's going to cut 2 million barrels a day in production is actually a big gift to Russia. Energy is big leverage tool for them.\par \par \uc2 A piece in "Newsweek" says this: If oil hits $100 a barrel -- a real possibility now -- Russia will make a billion dollars a day.\par \par \uc2 So, how much of a boost for Russia, for Putin specifically, was that \uc2 OPEC\uc2  move?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: This is a failure of American policy. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is directly responsible for the place that the world finds itself in energy and, frankly, his party, the progressive left -- 25 years of thinking you're going to run the world on sunshine and wind mills, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 We have the capacity for self help here in the United States, to improve not only as he heard the Saudis speak about, \uc2 Adel Jubeir\uc2  talked about refineries, but we shut down a pipeline, we made it hard to permit. We got ESG (ph) rule that now denied the capacity to get American energy out of American ground for American consumers.\par \par \uc2 This is -- to point the finger at someone else, at \uc2 OPEC\uc2  or at the Saudis is an enormous mistake, when America has the capacity to produce energy independence for its own country, and frankly to provide energy for the world, as well.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We know there's a lot of frustration here in Washington with the Saudis over this, this week, from the White House on down.\par \par \uc2 One of the top Democrats in the Senate, Dick Durbin, tweeted this: From unanswered questions about 9/11 and the murder of Jamal Khashoggi, to conspiring with Putin to punish the U.S. with higher oil prices, the royal Saudi family has never been a trustworthy ally of our nation. It's time for our foreign policy to imagine a world without their alliance.\par \par \uc2 We all remember during the campaign, President Biden said they were a pariah. He said he wouldn't meet with the crown prince and in July, he was over there fist-bumping with the crown prince then. Sort of this gut-punch from \uc2 OPEC\uc2  this week.\par \par \uc2 Where does the relationship with the Saudis stand? Where should it stand?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Yeah. The senator has got it completely wrong. This is about putting the American people first, and to put the American people first means a deep, important security relationship with the kingdom of Saudi Arabia, as we've had for decades.\par \par \uc2 We did it during the Trump administration. We have friends in the Gulf, the Arab states. We have a deep relationship with the Israelis. We made America safer. We made it less likely that American kids would have to go fight and die in that place again.\par \par \uc2 They have been an important security part and they should continue to be so. And the Biden administration got the wrong end of the stick (ph), and so does Senator Durbin. They want to cozy up to the Iranians, instead of having good security partners in the region, giving them billions in this crazy deal they continue to pursue. It's a real risk to the American people. More money, more nukes from Iran is bad for us.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let me ask you about that because the "AP" has a headline talking about this administration trying to juggle the concerns over human rights issues and also that nuclear deal. It says: Biden juggles Iran nukes as Iranian repression grows. Can he credibly side with the protest movement while also trying to salvage the languishing 2015 Iran nuclear deal that would pump billions into Tehran's registry -- treasury?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Shannon, common sense. You can't -- you can't have it all ways. You can't give them billions of dollars in sanctions relief and hope that the regime will actually respect its own people. And more importantly, you can't give them billions of dollars and think they won't continue to attack Americans, attack people in Europe, continue their terror campaign against Israel.\par \par \uc2 This is a silly policy and, Shannon, as you think about it, these are all connected. Those same Iranians that the Biden administration is giving money to are now providing weapon systems to the Russians to kill Ukrainians and they're doing it all with tacit support from the Chinese communist party.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration I don't think comprehends the connectedness between these three problem sets, but the failure to do so makes America much less safe, much less secure and the risk to our prosperity is very real.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So many foreign policies to talk about, we could do an hour or two of this.\par \par \uc2 But I want to make sure that we get in about the \uc2 Taliban\uc2 , because word this weekend that a top CIA and State Department official both were in Doha to meet with \uc2 Taliban\uc2  officials first time face-to-face since a U.S. drone strike took Ayman Al-Zawahiri. Under the agreements, you know, you negotiated and enforced during Trump years, he should not have been here, but they say it was a strike on their sovereignty and violated international laws.\par \par \uc2 How do you think that conversation went this weekend?\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Goodness. I have no idea how that conversation went, other than I hope what they did was make clear to the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  that the behavior they engaged in violates the commitments that they made and that there will be real costs.\par \par \uc2 That's harder to do now, Shannon, now that President Biden made the failed decision to withdraw and the way that he did. It's more difficult to impose cost on them. But we should be clear, we are never going to let terror emanate from that place again and that America will use the full power that it possesses to prevent that from happening.\par \par \uc2 That's what we did from our time in office, when we negotiated that deal. We knew they were in violation. We know that this is a work in progress.\par \par \uc2 And when they violated the agreement, Shannon, we killed them. We struck them. We made sure they understood that American deterrence was powerful and real.\par \par \uc2 I hope this administration will continue to do the same.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Secretary Pompeo, thank you for your time. If you have anything to tell us about 2024 --\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: Thank you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- please come back. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 POMPEO: You'll be amongst the first to know, Shannon. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Amongst the first. All right. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we're going to bring our Sunday group on the pressure the president is facing from Democrats over how to handle foreign allies. They're next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: The trip was not essentially for oil. It was about the Middle East, about Israel and rationalization.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: President Biden was once again explaining the trip he made to Saudi Arabia just three months ago, as reporters pressed him this week on \uc2 OPEC\uc2  production cuts.\par \par \uc2 It's time now for Sunday group. "USA Today" Washington bureau chief, Susan Page, former State Department spokesperson, Morgan Ortagus, FOX News senior political analyst Juan Williams, and "Washington Times" opinion editor Charlie Hurt.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to all of you.\par \par \uc2 Morgan, I'll start with you. Your former boss was just on with us.\par \par \uc2 "The Hill" says this, that there are a lot of Democrats are very frustrated, and they're considering a NOPEC bill. It' prompting calls from congressional Democrats to rethink the Washington-Riyadh alliance particularly on the subject of weapons and defense technology sales.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of where we are?\par \par \uc2 MORGAN ORTAGUS, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESPERSON: It's incredulous that the Republicans are saying this because, first of all, the relationship that we have with \uc2 OPEC\uc2  and Saudi Arabia, just 24 countries in \uc2 OPEC\uc2 , they made the best decision for their countries, not for the American people, and that's what Biden and the administration should be doing.\par \par \uc2 We're at the lowest levels of strategic petroleum reserves since 1984. We've depleted a third of it. The President Trump lifted it up and bought more at $20 a barrel. Now to replenish it, President Biden will have to replenish like at $100 a barrel. This is all American taxpayer money, by the way.\par \par \uc2 So, what we should be doing is we have problems at home, we should fix them at home. We should pump more. We shouldn't look to enemies like Venezuela or Iran or even OPE to solve our problems at home.\par \par \uc2 And so, you should be making foreign policy decision based off the best interest of the American people and not because you want them to lower gas prices before the midterm. I mean, it's just -- it is totally backwards on their policy priorities.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, looking towards the midterms, there are challenges -- Ukraine, China, Iran, North Korea wouldn't event get to that with the former secretary. "The Washington Examiner" says this: Foreign crises complicate Biden midterm plans, say: troubled affairs abroad and the ripple effects domestically aren't likely to help Biden and the Democrats in the midterm election, Susan.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN PAGE, \uc2 USA TODAY\uc2 : Well, you know, I think the ripples probably matter more than the actually situation in foreign policy. We Americans do not traditionally with the exception of big wars with American involvement, base their votes on foreign policy. They generally base their votes on what's happening in their lives at home.\par \par \uc2 So, I would think issues of inflation which were tied to foreign policy, issues of crime, which is an emerging issue, issues of abortion access are the ones that we should really look to, to be shaping this very interesting, surprisingly close midterm election.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and so many things that you mention there tie into the economy. And we know that voters tend to give more credibility or credence or trust to Republicans when it comes to issues of crime, of economy, things that they want to talk about. Democrats want to talk about other things on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 But the reality of going to the pump and filling up, it's already been ticking up the last couple of weeks, and now this \uc2 OPEC\uc2  decision, "The Wall Street Journal" editorial board says the White House blames the industry for high gas prices while it does everything it can to make drilling more difficult and financially risky.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, I don't know if you agree with that or not, but are voters going to make Democrats pay?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE HURT, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Oh, I think without a doubt and I think that polls probably even fail to completely register how much I think that Democrats will end up paying for this.\par \par \uc2 But it is extraordinary. You know, if you don't want \uc2 OPEC\uc2  to mess with you before an election, don't let \uc2 OPEC\uc2  mess with you before an election. Have energy resources, don't attack U.S. energy independence, don't do -- don't drain the strategic petroleum reserve, don't do all of these things that allows \uc2 OPEC\uc2  to mess with you.\par \par \uc2 And this idea that you have Democrats -- first of all, you have Democrats complaining about the price of gasoline is kind of funny to me anyway because it is their -- it's not only their energy policy to and I think undermine and destroy American energy independence, but it's also their policy that we not use fossil fuels.\par \par \uc2 So, to listen to Dick Durbin and even Bernie Sanders and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  complain about the price of gasoline? And \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  wanted to bring up the NOPEC bill, complaining about price of gasoline -- no, this is -- this is your stated policy. You want people not to be able to afford gasoline, because you think that the internal combustion engine is going to destroy the planet. So, just own it and they're going to own in the election.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, Juan, to that point, we talk about how this is taking up. We now have this complication of \uc2 OPEC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But more broadly, "\uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 " was looking at what's happening with the economy, a good jobs report for White House to tout on Friday. But they say this -- there's a threat now that there is more pain. The markets are reacting to what's happening with those employment numbers. They say this: that increases the odds of the economy slipping into recession just as Democrats head into the next presidential election.\par \par \uc2 So, the news having a mixed, you know, result for them. So, we're not just now talking about the midterms and gas prices now, but more broadly, 2024, if we're heading into recession then, how does that bode for the White House?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: Well, that's never good. It's not good for everybody if you're the party in power to see the economy shaky or to see gas prices go up.\par \par \uc2 Now, the key here, especially with gas, I think I have a little different point of view, which is that we've seen gas prices go down, I think it was for almost 100 consecutive days. Now, we've seen it start to go up, specifically in the West.\par \par \uc2 It's not tied to oil production at the moment. It's tied to the fact that you have refineries that are going through seasonal changes and the like, and you can -- I don think you're going to see a rapid rise in gas prices in the months -- between now and Election Day.\par \par \uc2 I think the second thing to say is, this game is not over. You heard Morgan, Charlie, Susan talking about capacity to take steps against \uc2 OPEC\uc2  and the Saudis. The Saudis are not popular with the American people. There could be a really rally around the flag sentiment here if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  steps up and says, oh, yeah, you know, Saudis, you really want to anger us and you want to depend on the Russians and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and their invasions of Ukraine, go right ahead, see if they'll support you. See if they can keep peace in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 I think that there would be anger from the American people to say, you know what, we're under attack. It's not just \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . It's not just the Democrats. This is an offense against the American people.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, whether he would take the next step of changing some of his policies to create more domestic energy, we'll have to see.\par \par \uc2 OK. Panel, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, right now, polls show Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  trailing Republican incumbent \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  in the race for the Georgia governor seat, and voters have less than a month to make up their mind. We sit down with Ms. Abrams next on her pitch to the Peach State.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right now, the most high profile midterm races are coming down to the wires, and that includes the Georgia gubernatorial race that pits Democratic Stacey Adams against incumbent Republican \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 . In a moment, we will bring in Stacey Adams on the uphill -- Abrams on the uphill climb she faces, in her bid to become the nation's first Black woman governor.\par \par \uc2 But, first, a look at how this critical gubernatorial race became the one to watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): The long awaited rematch, \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  versus \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , the two thrust into the national spotlight four years ago in the 2018 governor's race, which Kemp won, but barely. The margin: less than 1.5 percent.\par \par \uc2 KEMP: The election is over and I'm honored to be Georgia's governor-elect.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: At the time, Abrams infamously didn't quite concede.\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: This is not a speech of concession. As a woman of conscience and faith, I cannot concede that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: In the four years since, Kemp and Abrams have both remained household names. Kemp making headlines signing new abortion restrictions and notching economic wins for his state. But he is arguably most known for his feud with former President Trump who railed against Kemp's decision to certify Georgia's 2020 Electoral College votes for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: I'll be here in about a year and a half against your governor, I guarantee you that.\par \par \uc2 Of course, having her I think might be better than having your existing governor, if you want to know the truth.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Abrams is now a rising star among Democrats who delivered the party's 2019 State of the Union rebuttal.\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: I'm honored to join the conversation.\par \par \uc2 We are Georgia.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Abrams is building credibility with Democrats and Hollywood types through her voter registration efforts and as even been floated as a potential vice presidential or presidential candidate.\par \par \uc2 KEMP: The fight for the soul of our state (INAUDIBLE) here that \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  is not going to be our governor or the next president.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Next month's Kemp-Abrams rematch also happening two years after Biden clinched Georgia and Democrats captured both of Georgia's Senate seats. May's primary saw massive turnout, so November's numbers could be historic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Joining me now, Georgia Democratic gubernatorial candidate \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Leader Abrams, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D), GEORGIA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. You're a successful author and attorney. You've said that words are very careful. You chose them carefully. They're important.\par \par \uc2 You've used the words "rigged" and "stolen" about the 2018 gubernatorial election. You said it was proper because you can back it up.\par \par \uc2 But a federal judge has now said the voter protocols that you've challenged in Georgia, by his estimation, didn't violate the Constitution or the Voting Rights Act. The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  adds this: "The judge, who was nominated by President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , added that Fair Fight Action did not provide direct evidence of a voter who was unable to vote, experienced longer wait times, was confused about voter registration status."\par \par \uc2 You take issue with Judge Jones's findings in this case?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: I take issue with the characterization that you've read. What the judge said in a 288-page decision was that repeatedly, under the Gingles standard, Georgia violated Section 2 of the Voting Rights Act. Under the Brnovich standard, which existed as of 2021, Gingles existed in 2020, and when we filed the lawsuit, Brnovich was the weakening of the Voting Rights Act that happened out of Arizona in 2021.\par \par \uc2 And under the new standard, he did not have the authority to find that we proved our case under violations of the Section 2. But he says that it was a racially discriminatory system. He spent copious amounts of time explaining how \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  oversaw a racist system that disproportionately affected brown voters. He said that while he was deeply concerned about what happened, he could not, under the Brnovich categorization, actually hold him accountable. That is very different than saying nothing happened.\par \par \uc2 And what we were able to prove and what took four months, what was the longest -- longest trial on a voting rights case in Georgia in decades, what the court said is that he was not able to find that we could meet the standard under the new weakened Voting Rights Act. And that's the reason that I'm pushing so hard for the Voting Rights Act to be restored and expanded.\par \par \uc2 But it's also why I'm running for governor. We need a governor who actually cannot be credibly accused of having overseen a racist system. And \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , according to Judge Jones, oversaw and helped architect a racist voter suppression system in the state of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, I've read the opinion as well and I would say the characterization is a bit different but he does ultimately, Judge Jones, say no violation of Voting Rights Act, and you mentioned there are different standards in case law that has evolved through the Supreme Court on that front.\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But the direct quote from his ruling says no evidence that a voter couldn't vote, experienced wait times, or was confused about voter registration status. And I know the issue here for you is voter suppression.\par \par \uc2 So, let's look at the numbers in Georgia. The last gubernatorial primary versus now, there's a net increase of 763,380 voters. That sounds like the opposite of voter suppression.\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: Voter suppression is not about turnout. It's about the barriers and obstacles to access. And that's one of the other conflations, I think, is very important that we distinguish.\par \par \uc2 Voter suppression exists when there is difficulty registering, staying on the rolls, being able to cast a ballot, and having that ballot counted. And in the state of Georgia, we adequately proved, and more and more voters have experienced difficulties with doing so.\par \par \uc2 In 2018, \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  by -- according to federal law, he violated the rights of 53,000 Georgians. There was a federal court that two years before it found him in violation with 30 -- more than 30,000 voters, holding those registrations hostage.\par \par \uc2 And what happened in the intervening years from 2019 to 2022, because of our lawsuit, we got 22,000 voters restored to the rolls. We got 2,000 naturalized citizens put out -- we got them restored and actually got them the ability to vote in the state of Georgia. We were able to challenge a number of issues and we saw legislative changes made in response to our lawsuits.\par \par \uc2 Voter suppression is not about how many people try to vote. It's about whether or not there are obstacles and barriers to voting that are created by the very state that should be guaranteeing their access.\par \par \uc2 And so, yes, I am always going to be concerned about access to the right to vote, especially when we have a governor who signed a bill that makes it even more difficult. We are hearing stories about disabled people who are having a difficult time navigating the new absentee ballot requirements; senior citizens who for 15 years received their absentee ballots automatically but are no longer able to receive them.\par \par \uc2 We know that these are barriers to voting. And in the United States of America and the state of Georgia, barriers to voting should never be considered a native good. We should always be trying to expand access for eligible citizens. And that's the fight that I will continue to fight.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. And that case has been going since 2018. So, let's talk about what you had to say in 2019 about that race and then we'll talk about now. Here's the statement.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: We had this little election back in 2018, and despite the final tally and the inauguration and the situation we find ourselves in, I do have one very affirmative statement to make, we won.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, that was then. This is now. Will you characterize a win by the governor, if he is reelected with more votes in his column, as legitimate this time around?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: In 2018, I acknowledged that he was the victor of the vote. And if you listen to the next 18 minutes of that speech, and I did this morning actually, I spent a great deal of time explaining that when I say we won, I'm speaking to the National Action Network, which works with marginalized and disadvantaged groups.\par \par \uc2 And my expressed purpose was saying to them that by having candidates actually pay attention to our needs, the way I'm going to the AAPI community and talking about access issues because of language barriers, the way I'm doing talking to Black and brown voters talking about access to capital, the way I'm going to Latino voters, talking about the fact that they have -- they're most likely to have not access to health care, that the responsibilities of any candidate and more importantly any governor is to serve their needs, and that our campaign proved that we won by getting more people involved in the process.\par \par \uc2 Repeatedly in that very same speech, if you fast forward any amount of time, I am clearly laying out the challenges that our voters face and the challenges our citizens face when we do not have government that sees them.\par \par \uc2 I acknowledged that \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  won. I acknowledged it actually repeatedly in that speech. I very clearly say, I know I'm not the governor.\par \par \uc2 But what I will not do is allow the lack of nuance in our conversations to dull and obfuscate the challenges faced by our citizens. Georgians are facing terrible hardships and while there are some who are doing well, there are so many more who need access to health care and are about to lose the sixth hospital under this governor, who are facing affordable housing challenges and he says he doesn't want to disturb investors. They are watching their freedoms being stripped away and he calls it a personal choice for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , but not a personal choice for the millions of Georgia women who need to access to abortion care.\par \par \uc2 Across the board, my fight will always be to stand up for Georgians, to stand up for Georgian voters. And I will always say that when we make progress, that is a victory we should claim because for too long, our progress was denied.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, let's talk about where you are in this campaign right now. Our latest poll has you down 7 points to the governor. And when you dig into the numbers and look at individual groups in all the latest polls, The Atlanta Journal-Constitution has this headline. They say: "\uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  has a surprising problem with Black voters."\par \par \uc2 Killer Mike weighed in this week. Here's his take.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KILLER MIKE, RAPPER: You need to go everywhere \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  just went because what \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  did was have an effective week with Black people. And I would love to see her do that, but if she doesn't, that ain't our fault.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Why do you think there is a perception of some disconnect between you and this very important voting group?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: I think it's a manufactured crisis designed to suppress turnout. And what I would say is if you look at my polling numbers and the polling numbers of my ticket mate, Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , we are polling similarly well with Black voters. We know, however, that Black voters, like every voting population, deserves the respect of having someone to come and speak to them, engage them.\par \par \uc2 And while \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  may have had two events this week, I've done more than 50 events in the Black community. And what I have done consistently for the last 15 years is reach out to and reach into communities. I'm excited about the turnout we're seeing. I'm excited about the engagement that we're seeing.\par \par \uc2 I know, however, that every election cycle there has to be some worry. And in this case, it is a worry that's being manufactured. But it is always an opportunity to engage. I do not take any voting bloc for granted. I may be African-American, but I'm not entitled to a single vote that I don't earn. And I have spent every day of this campaign reaching out to and reaching into communities to make certain that they know that when I become governor, I'm going to close that 100-year financial gap that \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  is currently overseeing between Black and brown businesses and majority-owned businesses.\par \par \uc2 I'm going to expand Medicaid to provide half a million people with health care, 40 percent of whom will be African-American. I'm going to tackle an affordable housing crisis that has grown under \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  to put Georgia as the number four state for evictions in the nation even though we only have the eighth-highest population. And we have the fourth-highest incarceration rate under \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  even though we are the eighth-largest state.\par \par \uc2 These are all very discernible and clear issues for Black men, for Black communities. And I'm the only candidate not only talking about these issues but providing solutions. And I encourage every voter to look at my website and understand my plans for Georgia.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to ask you, especially before you go, about abortion. You've made headlines on that topic lately. You're not the only one. Kanye West, or Ye, talking to our \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  this week had this to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KANYE WEST, RAPPER: I care about the fact that there's more Black babies being aborted than born in New York City.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The most recently available data from \uc2 New York City's Department of Health\uc2  seems to support what he's saying there. But more broadly, according to the CDC, about 38 percent of abortions were obtained in the U.S. by Black women. The \uc2 U.S. Census Bureau\uc2  though says that just 13.6 percent of our population is Black.\par \par \uc2 Do you share Ye's concern that Black Americans are disproportionately impacted, he says, by abortion?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: I share the concern of women across the state of Georgia that they are being denied access to medical care. That in the state of Georgia, Black women are most likely to die of maternal mortality issues, because they're denied access to health care under this governor before they get pregnant. They are denied access to medical care during their pregnancy because of the medical shortage that we have in the state of Georgia, fomented by this governor. That when we have this child, if they choose to carry a child to term, if they're even allowed the choice of what they want to do, that post-partum they are denied access to health care afterwards.\par \par \uc2 We know that in \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 's Georgia, a Black woman faces lethal choice, and that is to either have a crystal ball and knows she's pregnant before she can actually know, or face forced pregnancy with very little support. In the state of Georgia, \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  has said that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is entitled to his personal choices, but no woman is. And that is unconscionable.\par \par \uc2 And for Black women in particular but women writ large, the right to control our bodies, the right to our medical care should be sacrosanct. And unfortunately, unless I replace \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , there will be a desert of access for Black women across the South. Georgia will be the only state -- if I become governor, we will be the only state from Texas to South Carolina where women can have access to care. That's 56 percent of the Black population of the United States, centered in the South and denied access to lifesaving medical care.\par \par \uc2 And so, I'm going to fight as hard as I can to protect the right to abortion because that's how we protect the rights of women and that's how we protect the rights of Georgians.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Were you to become governor, where would you draw the line? Fifteen weeks? Viability? Thirty-six weeks? What's the limit?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: What I've always say is that abortion is a medical decision that should be made by a doctor and the woman. And that the point of viability as determined by a doctor should always take into consideration the life and health of a woman. That should be the standard.\par \par \uc2 But the arbitrary standards of timelines ignore the medical reality that it is a fallacy we know exactly when a pregnancy starts, that we know exactly where we are in the system -- I mean, in the terms, and what doctors will tell you is that they need to make decisions based on the woman they are treating. And what women will tell you is that they need the right to make medical decisions that can save their lives and save their ability to control their bodies and their futures.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Leader Abrams, we thank you for the conversation this morning. We hope you'll come on again soon.\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: I'd be honored to. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, big developments this week in the other big race out of Georgia, as Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 's personal life dominates national headlines. But will it make an impact locally? We're back with the panel, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R), WISCONSIN: I would suggest any state should really take a look seriously at the harm being done by legalization of marijuana and consider that very carefully.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. MANDELA BARNES (D-WI), SENATE CANDIDATE: Marijuana is harmless and something marijuana is something that states all over the country have embraced and they are seeing phenomenal benefits as a result of it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That was Senator Ron Johnson and his midterm opponent, Mandela Barnes, in their debate this week responding to questions about the president pardoning people for federal marijuana offenses.\par \par \uc2 We're back now with the panel. And, Charlie, you know I have to start with you.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: After our conversation during the commercial. OK. Not everybody is so excited. Senator Tom Cotton tweets this: "In the midst of crime wave and on the brink of a recession, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is giving blanket pardons to drug offenders."\par \par \uc2 HURT: The idea that marijuana is harmless is just a flat out lie, I don't really understand that. And -- you know, and it's sort of interesting because a lot of people on the more conservative side of things, you know, I don't really care what people do, but the idea that this is going to help -- you know, help with the crises that we have in this country right now in terms of work, in terms of people working, in terms of kids growing up and becoming, you know, mature, active, contributing adults, the idea that legalizing marijuana everywhere without studying the health benefits of it. Just walk downtown Denver, Colorado, and get back to us on how -- how wonderful it is to legalize marijuana everywhere.\par \par \uc2 And then in terms of President Biden's pardoning, it's meaningless. It's a -- you know, it's a ploy. It's sort of insulting. It's a desperate gambit in front of an election. Anything that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  does that doesn't address and fix high gas prices and inflation is not going to help Democrats.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, this has gotten some headlines and some from the campaign trail, but I think it's fair to say \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  got a lot more headlines this week, and continues to get them. Natalie Allison, who is covering the Georgia race for Politico, tweets this out: "The latest \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  abortion scandal is on CNN and yes, all the D.C. folks are aware. But 11 Alive in Atlanta tonight led with crime followed by more crime. So unless (ph) Democrats take out ads about it, don't assume voters in Georgia are aware or actively thinking about this."\par \par \uc2 Juan.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think she is wrong. I think that not only voters in Georgia, I think voters all over the country, it was the talk of the week, the story of the week. And the fact that the story just kept -- you know, the son comes out and says that his dad has not raised his children, multiple children. It's just -- it's kind of a horrific soap opera in that sense. I don't know if it's going to have impact in terms of voter -- how people vote in the state of Georgia, to answer your question about the local impact, because the polls so far are really not there. We had one outlier, but nothing strong.\par \par \uc2 But I -- you know, if you ask me, should it have impact? Gosh, abortion is a core issue for the right, for the Republican Party in this country. You cannot give me a better example of total hypocrisy by a Republican candidate on this issue. He -- he doesn't approve of abortion rights for people who have been raped or victims of incest, and yet here is a story that says this man is not -- is paying for abortions. Also family values, this is a guy that's admitted, you know, he put a gun to his wife's head. I mean, this is not a good story for anybody who cares about those core issues.\par \par \uc2 It seems to me Biden won the state of Georgia by I think less than 1 percentage point, Shannon, so and you look at the political consequence, it may be that voters don't turn out, somebody is just not excited anymore by the idea or that someone says, you know, I hate Democrats so much I'll vote for any Republican. But that's neither case is a good for Republicans. It's not good for Kemp, by the way, the governor...\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: ... if people don't show up.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And they don't want to vote in the other race for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . So what about that conundrum for pro-lifers, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , if this is true, has been a hypocrite on that issue, but on the other side of the ticket you have Senator Warnock basically who wants no limits on abortion?\par \par \uc2 ORTAGUS: Yes, and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has denied it unequivocally. And so if you are a pro-life voter, then you have two clear choices. One person is going to vote with your issues in Congress and one will not. When you look at poll after poll in Georgia, the last one that I saw, 28 percent of voters care about the economy and inflation. Abortion was about at 10 percent. And so if you look at your 401(k), if you're a Georgia voter this week, it's down year-to-date about 20 to 25 percent. So I think what's going on with the economy, the fact that if you are trying to pay for your child's tuition, if your 401(k) is down 25 percent, that's a year of your child's college tuition or more. That's what people care about in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. There's another headache out there for folks. This one goes to the White House, Washington Times saying it's a headache that won't go away. DoJ's Hunter Biden inquiry is back in the spotlight.\par \par \uc2 Susan, where does that play out, if at all, in the midterms with potential indictment coming?\par \par \uc2 PAGE: Well, an indictment before the midterms would be very serious. It'd get a lot of headlines and be -- cause personal pain for the president and political pain, as well. Although the tradition is that the DoJ would not bring an indictment this close to an Election Day.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Panel, thank you very much. We'll see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Tom Brady -- well, his fans, they were thrilled when he came out of retirement, maybe not everybody was. He is aiming for a 10th Super Bowl. It's an appearance he'd make at age 45. Legendary broadcaster Jim Gray joins us next on Brady and the week's biggest sports stories.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Home run history this week. New York Yankee outfielder Aaron Judge set the single-season record for the American League, hitting his 62nd home run. That earned him this fantastic New York Post cover and put him in the debate over whether this is the all-time league record.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now to discuss that, legendary broadcaster Jim Gray.\par \par \uc2 Jim, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 JIM GRAY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Shannon, great to be with you, thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. This is what The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , in an opinion piece, that said Judge has home run record, Bonds doesn't, reality is real. It says: "Our most pressing responsibility as a society now is (ph) to embrace reality among an endless proliferation of myths, Judge broke the record honestly. He is the rightful heir to Ruth and Maris."\par \par \uc2 What do you make of this debate?\par \par \uc2 GRAY: Well, he has authenticity record because he's not stained by steroid or steroid implications and performance-enhancing drugs. So Barry Bonds hit 73 home runs, we don't live in the Soviet Union, we don't erase records. That is the record. He did it while playing baseball. Maybe that record should not be recognized or it should be an asterisk because of the steroid era, but that's what he hit. But Judge hit 62, he's one of only six men to do it, three are tainted by steroids. Just him, Maris, and Ruth are over 60 and he has 62. So he has the authenticity record.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Let's turn to the \uc2 NFL\uc2 . A lot of news there. Obviously what happened with the Dolphins quarterback last week raised a lot of eyebrows and a lot of important conversation about the concussion protocol. I understand the \uc2 NFL\uc2  and \uc2 NFL Players Association\uc2  have reached a changed protocol.\par \par \uc2 GRAY: They are going to change it. And now, you know, if it's visual to the eyeball, if there's gross motor display that you cannot perform, you are coming off the field and they are not going to wait for all of the testing and for the independent testing that went on with the neurologist. So there is a change in protocol. Tua should not have been allowed back on the field. Everybody kind of missed that, even though he did go through, followed all of the protocols, and was cleared back to go out to play on the field. But you just can't have that happening, particularly with CTE and all of the issues with head injuries.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So those changes are coming now. Another big \uc2 NFL\uc2  headline has to do with Tom Brady. A lot of people consider him greatest of all time, but he's in headlines for different reasons, for personal reasons in the last week or so. Pewterplank.com, which is one of the Bucs fan sites that follows, says this: "Tom Brady is great due to the way he obsesses over the sport, can he really do that effectively when he goes through one of the most draining and emotional ordeals that a person can experience?"\par \par \uc2 Jim, what can you say about his performance on the field?\par \par \uc2 GRAY: His performance on the field has been somewhere between very good and excellent. He's completing 68.4 percent of passes. He ranks seventh out of 50 quarterbacks in the \uc2 NFL\uc2  with the highest completion percentage. He has thrown for six touchdowns, one interception, that one interception is the lowest amount of interception of any of those quarterbacks. So his performance, he would say, is fine, because the team is 2-2. And that's the most important statistic. They play the \uc2 Atlanta Falcons\uc2  later on today. That game is on FOX. And that game, we'll see. He's never lost to the \uc2 Atlanta Falcons\uc2 . He's a perfect 10- between the Patriots and the Bucs. So his performance on the field has been very good so far.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. We will tune in. But let's talk about another important headline this week. This is out of the world of women's soccer. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  says: "Soccer let women down whether they spoke up or not," a wide-ranging deep investigation here, they say, the report on dysfunction in women's soccer showed that abuse of players in the National Women's Soccer League was a, quote, "open secret, players had nowhere to turn."\par \par \uc2 What do you take from this report?\par \par \uc2 GRAY: It's despicable what has gone on with the National Women's League, the bullying, the retaliation, the name-calling, the coercing of sex. It's just -- it's just pitiful. It's horrible. It's awful. It's a 300-page report and all of these players really just had to tolerate some of this and didn't report it because they were fearful they wouldn't get scholarships and it would end their career. It's major breakdown of an institution and it's just -- it's just awful. And it has got to change. And there has got to be transparency.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, shining some sunlight.\par \par \uc2 All right. I want to get a real quick comment before we have to go about this protester who ran onto the field, an animal rights protester, who now potentially is talking about suing the \uc2 NFL\uc2 , has potentially filed a police report against the people that stopped him. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 GRAY: The field is for the players. And with all of these things that are going on, you don't know what type of threat he is. He has got smoke billowing out of his back. Monica Seles was once stabbed while she was on the field of play as a tennis player. You don't know what these folks have in mind with all of these things that do go on in stands, possible terrorism. Ariana Grande with the horrible thing that happened in Manchester after she performed. People walking out of the stadium. So what Bobby Wagner did was right and he should be supported. And nobody should run out on the field at any time other than the players.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And that is a case we will continue to watch. Jim Gray, thank you so much for joining us this morning. We hope to see you again soon.\par \par \uc2 GRAY: Thank you, Shannon. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Football coming up.\par \par \uc2 That is it for us today. I'm Shannon Bream here in Washington. Have a great week and we will see you next "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020221009eia900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden's Foreign Policy Failure Keep Mounting; Biden's Apocalyptic Rhetoric Endangers U.S.; Zelenskyy Demands NATO Escalate Russia Conflict; Regime Media Wants More Billions For Ukraine; Biden's New Economic Strategy: Blame Republicans; Biden Blames GOP For His Disastrous Economy; Dems Ignore Americans Suffering From Their Agenda; Latinos Ditching Dems In Droves; Hispanics Leading The Way For GOP To Win Congress; NY Gov Hochul Pretend NYC Is Safe; Poll: Zeldin Within Striking Distance; Zeldin: Hochul Is Divorced From Reality; Rep. \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  (R-NY) Interviewed About His Campaign For Governor Of New York; High Crime Rates In Cities Across U.S. May Have Negative Political Consequences For Democrats; Eleven Pro-Life Advocates Indicted For Incident At Tennessee Abortion Clinic In 2021; Bleached Eyebrows New Fashion Trend; Business Proposes Butter Board As New Charcuterie Board For Parties.\b0\par\par\uc2 Rachel Campos-Duffy, Raymond Arroyo, Lisa Boothe \par \uc2 8,386 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Ingraham Angle\par \uc2 INGANG\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: When we get back on Monday only 29 days till the midterms. We'll see you Monday. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL CAMPOS-DUFFY, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I'm Rachel Campos-Duffy in for Laura Ingraham, and this is a special edition of THE INGRAHAM ANGLE live from New York City tonight.\par \par \uc2 Remember when they told us about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDREA MITCHELL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is probably the most experienced foreign policy expert since George H. W. Bush.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Biden is doing everything that he can to reassure the world that American global leadership is coming back.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He's thinking about global leadership about how to keep everybody safe.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Not so much. There was the botched Afghanistan pull up, caving to China on every whim, and now of course, the dangerous, never ending war in Ukraine. Biden has turned our country into a doormat for China, and into Ukraine's bottomless piggy bank. And now it's all escalating.\par \par \uc2 Biden warning that under his watch, we're heading towards a nuclear Armageddon. Saying at a fundraiser, quote, "We have not faced the prospect of Armageddon since Kennedy and the Cuban Missile Crisis. And that's what we've got, a guy I know fairly well," he said to Putin. "He's not joking when he talks about potential use of tactical nuclear weapons, or biological or chemical weapons."\par \par \uc2 So what breaking new intel did the administration receive that would lead us to believe Armageddon was imminent?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Do you have new intel that caused the president to ratchet up the level of concern?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: No, the President was speaking about concerns about Putin's threats to use nuclear weapons just as he did at the \uc2 UN General Assembly\uc2 , and we have done over the past few weeks. You've heard us say this over and over again. You've heard it from the National Security Adviser, Jake Sullivan, how seriously we are taping this. And that's what the President was speaking to.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Joining me now is Wisconsin Congressman Tom Tiffany, who now sits in the seat my husband once held, and Ned Ryun CEO of American Majority. Ned, the blank checks we've been giving to Ukraine have apparently turned Zelenskyy into some sort of petulant child, because now he's demanding this of \uc2 NATO\uc2 . Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF Ukraine (via translator): What the \uc2 NATO\uc2  has to do to prevent and deter the use of nuclear weapons by Russia. Preventive strikes, preventive action so Russia would knew what would happen to them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Ned, his spokesperson was forced to clean this up saying that Zelenskyy actually wanted sanctions and not a strike. Hard for me to believe he got those two words confused. But again, he's calling for preemptive strike strikes on Russia. Is he insane? And where all the level headed statesman, you know, saying something, pushing back on this. I don't see them.\par \par \uc2 NED RYUN, AMERICAN MAJORITY CEO: No, they're not, which is a shame. I mean, that -- those words of Zelenskyy are the words of a madman.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RYUN: When he's saying a preemptive strike to prevent nuclear war, that would actually trigger a nuclear war in my estimation. But we've gotten to this point, because, even though there is no Intel supporting Biden's comments about Armageddon. His actions of continued, unlimited resources to Zelenskyy and talk of regime change in Russia, the further this goes, the more we do advance towards potential serious conflict in Europe.\par \par \uc2 At some point, you would think statesmen and adults would show up and say we have to talk about de-escalation and find an off ramp. Because I would remind people, Rachel, we're not talking about bombing goat herders in the Middle East, who by the way we couldn't beat. We're talking about escalating with one of the world's major nuclear powers, and involving ourselves in a very serious way between two very corrupt nations and a territorial dispute over a sub region of Ukraine. It's absolutely insanity and madness, and we have to stop.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Yeah. And not really in the U.S. interest.\par \par \uc2 RYUN: Not at all.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Congressman, I want to play this clip for you from Panetta -- \uc2 Leon Panetta\uc2 . Take listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you think Biden went too far in his comments or not?\par \par \uc2 LEON PANETTA\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: Well, you know, I don't think there's any question here that we are in a very dangerous war. It's also a pivotal war.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: What do you make of that? I mean, is there really any limit to what they're willing to do? I mean, they're admitting -- I mean, Leon's admitting it's a dangerous war. He knows that this is getting really bad, really fast. And yet, I can't understand what is the limit? How much further are they willing to give and risk to obtain the objective that they want, which is obviously deposing and getting rid of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. TOM TIFFANY (R-WI): Rachel, you recap some of the Biden Administration's actions very well. But I think it's very important to go back to the very first day \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was in office, he's very first action was to shut down Keystone Pipeline -- the Keystone XL. He announced to the world America is no longer going to be energy independent.\par \par \uc2 Then he said Nord Stream II, we're going to drop the sanctions that benefits \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . And then we had the disastrous withdrawal from Afghanistan. $85 billion of our war material left behind, 13 of our troops left behind, killed at the gates of the Kabul Airport. American weakness.\par \par \uc2 And let's look at just this week how bad our foreign policy is. The Saudis told us to pound sand. Kim Jong-un, firing missiles over South Korea and Japan. And now we have Zelenskyy basically dictating or attempting to dictate our foreign policy. This is what you have when we get American weakness. And this is what you have with a Biden Administration. And those so-called smart people you alluded to, I don't know what the heck they're doing.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Yeah, I seem to remember not long ago everyone telling us how dangerous the world was going to be under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ned, the regime media is demanding, of course, that we do sacrifice more and more for Ukraine. Time published a piece saying this. Quote, "Now is not the time for the West to soften its resolve in the fight against Putin, but to step it up. Putin will not stop until he is forced to stop. The recent $625 million security assistance package is an important step, but such support will need to continue until the job is done."\par \par \uc2 Ned, we've already committed more than 16.8 billion since February, how much more we expected to give? Or risk our children's lives? I mean, I seriously I can't believe we're talking about nuclear war right now.\par \par \uc2 RYUN: Yeah. No, this is absolute insanity that we're even having this conversation as we continue to send 10s of billions of our taxpayer dollars over to Ukraine with absolutely no end in sight. And if the goal is to have regime change in Russia, we are going to escalate towards something, I believe, very violent.\par \par \uc2 Again, I have never heard anybody make a compelling argument, Rachel, on any level, what on earth does this have to do with U.S. interests, especially the American taxpayer interests. It has nothing to do with it at all. In fact, this is really something that Europe has helped bring about with their weakness and their dependence on Russian oil.\par \par \uc2 So at some point, I hope statesmen show up and say we're going to have an offramp here and figure out how to solve this peacefully. Because right now it feels like we are -- they are trying to compel us to go to a place that we do not want to go as a country, but even I would argue, as the world.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Yeah. I mean, Congressman, the weaker, the poorer we get with these green policies, the less leverage that we obviously are going to have. You brought up Congress made a great point that on day one, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  got rid of the XL pipeline, he declared a war on American energy. And it's ironic, because they did all this supposedly for the environment, what could be worse for the environment than a nuclear war?\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY: There's no doubt it's American weakness. But what really concerns me, Rachel, is that are we taking the eye off the ball of what are really the greatest concerns? Communist China, they want Taiwan. Tehran, they're threatening death to Israel almost on a daily basis.\par \par \uc2 And you hear you have the Israelis and the Taiwanese, they're willing to pay for their armaments, and we're supposed to just give them to the Ukraine? I think things are upside down in regards to our foreign policy at this point.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: That is an understatement, Congressman. So great to have both of you, gentlemen, here with me tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to what's happening on U.S. soil. The Biden Administration was happy to take credit when gas prices went down a few pennies. But is back to using the war on Ukraine and fearmongering over Republicans in Congress as an excuse for prices spiking back up.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Republicans take control of the Congress, these historic victories we just won for the American people are going to be taken away. Every kitchen table cost is going to go up, not down. And I realize costs are going up on food. And I was able to bring gasoline down well over a $1.60, but it is inching up because of what the Russians and the Saudis just did. I'm not finished with that yet.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Joining me now is Raymond Arroyo, Fox News Contributor and author of "The wise Men Who Found Christmas". And also with me is Lisa Boothe, Fox News Contributor and host of "The Truth with Lisa Boothe."\par \par \uc2 Raymond, I'm kind of --\par \par \uc2 LISA BOOTHE, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Hey, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Good. Hey, nice to have both of you tonight. Raymond, I'm trying to follow what he just said. I think he was trying to blame Putin. But I don't think he even knows how to do that right anymore.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: No. Well, and you know, the Russians and the Saudis should be very worried, Rachel, because he said, I'm not finished with that yet. Maybe he's going to tank their economy too. So stay tuned for that.\par \par \uc2 Look, the reason that we are in this pickle, and we're seeing the gas prices go through the roof, it's not the Russians, it's not the Saudis, those prices were going up long before they did anything. This is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's policies.\par \par \uc2 I spoke to a oil executive here in Louisiana today. And he was telling me, the last time they had exploration leases and auction was under the Trump Administration. When Biden came in he voided all of those leases, even though these companies had paid for them.\par \par \uc2 And remember, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wanted to buy 77 million gallons -- or million barrels rather -- for our strategic reserve. It was the Democrats who stopped that. That was only at $15 a barrel. Today, we're in a real pickle, because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has decided to not once not twice, three times, but four times he is sapped that strategic reserve. We're down a third of what it was. And he wants to go back to the well, again. Americans should say no, and the Congress should as well.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: You know, Lisa, Raymond brings up such a great point. And that is that at the heart of all of this, are these green policies, they're cruel. They're making people poor on purpose. Is it time that we call out, as conservatives, the cruelty of these policies? Why are they doing this? Why do they want to sacrifice our children, our ability to pass on wealth to them or even just feed them for so many Americans on the altar of these green policies? What's it about? Is it about control? Is it about socialism? What is it?\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: Well, I think it's about control. And I think the pain is the point. That's the most important thing to realize about the Left's policies, and Biden's policies, the pain is the point. And the point of the Left's policies, what they're trying to do is, gut the middle class and create a larger portion of the population that are dependent on the federal government. That's what these policies ultimately do. That's what all these policies ultimately do, is gut the middle class.\par \par \uc2 And all these problems, all these issues that we face as a country are easily solvable, so much so that even \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can get it. You might have to, like, sit them down, give them some ice cream, speak slowly, but he can get it. And the answers, and the solutions are so simple. You don't want your cities overrun with illegal immigrants. Shut down the border. Border security. You don't want people getting gunned down in the street. Put criminals behind bars.\par \par \uc2 You don't want people paying so much for gas prices, unleash more American energy production. You go down the list, the solutions are so simple. But that takes us back to my point, is that the pain is the point. It's intentional. They don't want to solve these problems. They want these problems. They're creating them.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Yeah, I mean, Raymond, it was just two years ago, we were just energy independent. We were net exporters of energy. And now here we are begging the Saudis, getting dirtier oil again. More of the hypocrisy of them pretending like it's all about the environment. Getting dirty or oil from Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 I understand what Lisa is saying here about, you know, making us more dependent. I absolutely believe they want us more dependent on government, because that increases their power. But I think the control part is also about moving us to forms of energy that they can turn on and off on us. That they can control and that we will eventually be on some sort of Chinese style social credit system where unless we agree with what these big globalist want, then we don't get to drive our car that far. We don't get to get on a plane or train, that's happening in China right now.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Well, and look, this is about freedom. That's what you're really talking about here, our ability to move, our ability to create. I mean, we're talking about the manufacturing base in the United States. Biden today was talking about, you know, made in America -- he couldn't even say made in America.\par \par \uc2 And the reason for that is very, the only thing he's making in America is inflation, and national insecurity because of these policies. And when you're begging hat and hand, these regimes in Saudi Arabia and Venezuela to give you a cup of oil, because you can't and won't drill your own, that's a huge problem. And it locks your people down. Somehow we have to get past this cult of Green New Deal that Biden has decided to imbibe and embrace. It's destroying the country and making us very insecure.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Absolutely. I mean, Lisa, Raymond, you know, talked about us begging for oil. I mean, he went all the way \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to Saudi Arabia, he fist bump them, and then they gave him the finger.\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: Well, we could do it here and be completely independent. And your point about the control, I mean, look at California, Rachel, they're saying, hey, you can't go out and buy a gas powered car by 2035. But by the way, we're going to tell you when you can charge your electric vehicle, right. To the point about the control aspect.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is getting rid of fossil fuels is a death mission for the country. It's going to lead to death. It's going to lead to human suffering. It's not about a better way of life. It leads to a worse way of life. And they don't even care about any of this stuff. That's the kicker here, right?\par \par \uc2 And the fact that they've been able to get so many Americans to buy into their climate hoax. They don't care about this. Look at how many of them fly private, Rachel. It's a joke to them. It's about control to them, because the reality is they're all going to be flying private when you can't pay for your gas and when gas -- when they're driving up gas prices by getting rid of things like the Keystone and not allowing them, you know, American energy production to do its thing. And they're going to be flying private.\par \par \uc2 Or you can't use electricity because you can't afford it or it's no longer abundant. So that's the reality. They don't even believe the same hoax that they're pushing with their propaganda.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Lisa, makes a great point. That's a great point, Rachel, that Lisa is making.\par \par \uc2 BOOTHE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Between the Keystone Pipeline and our own exploration, we could make up for the oil that the Saudis and Russia are taking off the map very easily. And we're not doing it why? That's the question that every American and every congressperson should be demanding of this administration. It's within our reach to do this.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Raymond, Lisa, two of my favorite freedom fighters.\par \par \uc2 All right. Well, retaking America 31 days out. One of the fastest growing blocks in the country, Latinos, are leaving the Democrat party in droves. It's so bad that even MSNBC can't avoid talking about it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The Democratic Party has changed a lot and identify more with the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What things?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well, we're for god, country, family and hard work.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Jose used to vote blue too. Now he's also a Republican and most concerned with immigration and beefing up border security.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What are the concerns you have about immigration?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The fact of the matter is that we -- you know, we don't feel safe anymore --\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Joining me now is Republican Congresswoman Mayra Flores, who is running for reelection in Texas. And Yesli Vega, the GOP Congressional Candidate in Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, welcome. There is a new poll that shows that 40 percent -- of course, there's a new poll that shows that 40 percent of independent Latino voters are still undecided ahead of midterms. Is it enough to keep pounding the pavement on the economy and crime, the border? Or is there something else you could -- both of you could be doing to sway them? Are family and cultural values, the last component, that needs to be driven home?\par \par \uc2 Mayra, I was thinking about you because I follow you on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . I saw a tweet that you just recently put up. And you said, people take your children -- stop taking them to drag shows. Take them to church. And I thought, wow, that is a very authentically Hispanic thing to say. I think every one of us has either said that or have heard a mom say that. Is that the missing component? Is this the big disconnect that Democrats are missing -- the cultural religious values of Hispanics?\par \par \uc2 REP. MAYRA FLORES (R-TX): That's right. The Democrat Party walked away from the Hispanic community to cater to the Far Left that is anti-God, anti- family. And that is just not who we are here in South Texas. We're all about God, family and hard work. And they walked away from that.\par \par \uc2 And we care about border security, we care about the economy, we care about the inflation, the kitchen table issues that matter to everyday American. And they need to stop seeing as Justice Hispanics. We're also proud Americans, and we're important here in South Texas as well. And we just didn't have a voice before my election. But it's enough. And we finally do have a voice in Washington.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Yesli, we are not tacos. We're not breakfast tacos. I tell you that. The Dems are fretting over these polls. But when I hear them talk about wanting to you know, win back these Hispanics that they're losing in districts like your districts, I hear them talk about we need to show up or we need to stop saying Latinx. They're never talking about actually changing the policies. Aren't the policies what's -- what is actually repelling Hispanic voters from the Democrats at this point in time?\par \par \uc2 YESLI VEGA (R) VIRGINIA CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: Absolutely. They want to talk about --\par \par \uc2 FLORES: Absolutely, it's a policy --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 VEGA: -- anything other than their voting records. And I mean, do you blame them? I don't. I certainly wouldn't want to talk about my failures as an elected member of Congress with this current administration. It's been one catastrophic decision after another. And I think that we're winning and we're going to continue to win across the United States, come November 8, because we're talking about the issues that matter to the voters.\par \par \uc2 You know, the Democrat party for far too long has thought that we're single issue voters. We're will not, We care about the inflation. We care about the economy. We care about crime, we care about what's happening with the educating of our children, and we've had enough.\par \par \uc2 We've put our foot down. We're not going to sit on the sidelines. We're in the fight and we're ready to take our country back and get to work on November 8. We're going to win, we're going to win big and we're excited.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Myra, you know, our last guest talked a lot about you know, what's at the heart of this economy and the problems that we're facing, and so many of it is the Green New Deal, which they've injected into all these different bills, whether it was the transportation bill or the fake Inflation Reduction Bill. These policies -- these energy policies depriving us of being energy independent, it's killing Americans. In so many ways it's willingness economy.\par \par \uc2 So what is your response to that? Why do you think Democrats care more about the ocean being 1 percent cooler than they care about whether kids eat, whether parents put gas in the car to go to work or whether seniors heat their homes?\par \par \uc2 FLORES: They only care about themselves and their political party. But the people here in this country and in South Texas, especially the people of Brownsville, Texas, are struggling right now, to pay their utility.\par \par \uc2 I get countless calls every single day because their utility bill has doubled, tripled. People went from paying $300 a month for their light bill, to $900. Many people are paying, you know, $3,000 as well for their light bill. And this is all because of the Biden administration and their policies, and \uc2 Vicente Gonzalez\uc2  who supported those same policies.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Well, Yesli, Myra, both of you, impressive women who are moms and fighting for their communities and the real issues. Thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 As New York City crime rages, New York's Democrat Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  has the gall to pretend like it's not even happening. Her challenger Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  reacts to the unbelievable sound. Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: This is Alvin Charles. Last year he was charged in a stabbing attack on a New York City subway, and then freed without bail on supervised release and due in court later this month. He's behind bars now accused in yet another subway stabbing that happened a week ago tonight. Except this time, the victim, father of three Tommy Bailey, died.\par \par \uc2 New York City is becoming unbearable for the good people that live and work here. Over the course of eight hours yesterday, there were four separate stabbing attacks on the subway. And of course there are the unprovoked beatings. But New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  says not to worry.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): City is still the safest big city in America. Look at Chicago, look at San Francisco, look at Los Angeles, Atlanta and Washington. People leaving New York, I'm not sure where they're going to go, because we are the safest big city in America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Hey Kathy, cut the BS. Actually keep it going, because voters are seeing who you truly are. A brand new poll shows my next guest Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  is trailing Hochul by less than two points in the race for governor right here in Liberal New York. Congressman Zeldin joins me now. Congressman, so great to have you. First, your reaction to what Governor Hochul said.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R-NY): I mean, it's totally divorced 