ut and political persecution, in my opinion.\par \par \uc2 Jack, I want to get to you because this week there is a lot of news being made with Ye, former Kanye West, interview with \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 . He brought up the issue of life. He is very definitively and outspokenly proof life. He talked about the racist roots of Planned Parenthood. He talked about Margaret Sanger and the KKK's involvement in its founding. But what I actually found most interesting were all the things that he had to say about marriage and family. And I wanted to talk to you about it because I know those are issues that are really important to you. Take a look at this clip.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YE WEST, RAPPER: The media ridiculed me for getting the house next door to Kim to see my children. And they even said that I was stalking her and her new boyfriend because I bought the house next door to see my children.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: What do you think about his overall message about marriage and kids and being a father, being a black father?\par \par \uc2 JACK BREWER, FOUNDER, THE JACK BREWER FOUNDATION: It is so important these days. Kanye, he has a way to deliver that message that creates a stir. But then after the dust settles, you realize the spirit that he's coming from and the biblical context in which he's really standing on. And I think it's beautiful for a black man to be in a situation like that when we have so many fatherless kids throughout our community to actually stand up for his children, front and center, and be unbashful about that, to actually make the investment of time and money and resources to move next door to his children, especially given the fact that his mother hadn't necessarily done very godly things with their daughter, having her own social media and actually doing things that disrespect what Kanye's wishes were for that child.\par \par \uc2 And I think right now in the crisis we have in our country, the pro-life message, the Christian message, we are all being persecuted right now in the name of Jesus. And we all as Christians know that this day was coming. And so I think it's time for us to continue to lock arms and be even stronger and make sure that the people that we are voting for and the folks that we're putting into office are folks of God, men of women who are going to stand up for scriptures and biblical context. That is the only way that we're going to push forward in this nation right now and get this country back on track.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Yes. Governor, he also, Ye, formerly Kanye West, also talked about being a Christian in this moment, in this moment of persecution, but saying I was born for a time like this. You are a pastor, what is your message to people who may be less powerful than Kanye West, other Christians who are feeling that pressure in our culture right now?\par \par \uc2 HUCKABEE: The message is don't curl up in a fetal position, crawl up into your bed, and be afraid. God has not given us a spirit of fear, but of power and love and a sound mind. We need to stand up and lock arms, as Jack said so brilliantly, with other Christian believers. There are Catholic believers, there are evangelical believers, there are many kinds of people who believe that the Bible is truth, that we have a responsibility to God.\par \par \uc2 We need to quit seeing things horizontally. This is not a left-right, liberal-conservative, Democrat-Republican fight. This is a fight between good and evil. If you really believe it's OK to shred a baby in the mother's womb right up until the moment of birth, that's not a political position. That's a position that is shear evil. And we need to quit pretending that this is all about one person's politics over another. We are talking about the salvation of our culture and our civilization.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Courage begets courage, Ye showed that, but you two godly men do that every day as well. Thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 A terrifying new fashion trend, plus, Hunter Biden going to jail? Emily Compagno is up next. Stay right there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: All right, well, now it is our time to Culture Whip of the week. Joining me now is Emily Compagno, co-host of "OUTNUMBERED." Emily, we have so much to cover tonight, so let's jump right into it. There's a strange new trend that is happening for fashion week that even has celebrities getting in on the act, and it's bleached eyebrows. You can see here on Gigi and Bella Hadid. Kanye calls them Anna Wintour's dolls. And can we expect to see you rocking these eyebrows next week, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 EMILY COMPAGNO, \uc2 FOX\uc2  NEWS "OUTNUMBERED" CO-HOST: Not if I'm conscious and above ground, because that is the most hideous trend I've ever seen. So Rachel, obviously we lived through the 90s. We had our pencil thin brows.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: We did.\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: But no one should look like Skeletor, or dead. And that's what happens. And I call foul on "People" magazine, for example, who called it the coveted platinum eyebrows. No, it's not. It's bleached death. So a huge no thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: So our producer want the see what we would look like if we bleached our eyebrows?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: By the way, Madonna was the original. Let's show Madonna. I'm aging myself. I remember these days with Madonna. There she is. There's the bleached eyebrows.\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: But there was still a fine line.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: There was something going on.\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: That was more like the -- I can't get over what we looked like. We looked like a frightening Skeletor twin duo.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Yes, it is really scary.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: OK, so let's go. We're going to turn to something that's not so scary, that's actually uplifting. OK, we're going to turn to something called the hot girl walk that swept TikTok. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It is what I call the hot girl walk. You are only allowed to think about three things on the hot girl walk. One, things you are grateful for, two, your goals and how you are going to achieve them, and three, how hot you are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: And three, how hot you are. So you go on a walk and you play inspirational playlists or podcasts and you think about how hot you are and you think about being grateful. It's trendy. People are loving this.\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: This is why it caught on, because she called it the hot girl walk. Because the first two things are amazing and pretty basic, right. They're fundamental. Your expressions of gratitude and then your goals and how you are going to achieve them. And then throwing in the hotness, first of all, I love it because I am all for people feeling self-empowered and good about themselves, especially young women. But the trend part of it is calling it the hot girl walk and being like, do your hot girl walk. But yes, that's me in Central Park every day, just setting my goals, just expressing gratitude, not number three.\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: So I used to go on walks every day in the summer when I was back in Wisconsin. I think it does a lot for your soul. I think it works. I think that's why it's a trend. It's actually working.\par \par \uc2 Next one, everyone is crazy for charcuterie boards at parties, right. Well, you can really do anything with them, and so now there is a new trend. It's called butter boards. I don't think I can --\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Have you heard of a butter board? This is what one looks like, and I want to make them the next charcuterie board. If you're serving a group of people, spread a bunch of salt and butter on a play. I have tons of plates. You salt tons of lemon zest, any herbs or toppings you want. Serve with warm bread, and it just feels like really communal. I love it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: It looks delicious. Who doesn't love butter, salted butter?\par \par \uc2 COMPAGNO: Totally. You can keep loving it until you gain the freshman 15 and then the butter 30 and then the butter 50. So to me it looks like butter in moderation. Being Italian, I'm all about the charcuterie boards because that's the antipasto, right. So if it does not have meat and some olives and --\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: Well, it's got butter. I love butter, too.\par \par \uc2 My final thoughts are next. Thank you, Emily.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CAMPOS-DUFFY: That's it for us tonight. I am Rachel Campos-Duffy in for Laura Ingraham. Tune in tomorrow and Sunday morning for my regular gig, \uc2 FOX\uc2  & FRIENDS WEEKEND at 6:00 a.m. eastern to 10:00 a.m. And check out the podcast I have with my husband Sean "From the Kitchen Table." This week we talk about border policies, how they're allowing Mexico to turn into a cartel run narco-state, Ye West punished for telling the truth. We have a special guest on for that and you don't want to miss it. Thanks for watching. This is a special edition of THE INGRAHAM ANGLE. "GUTFELD!" is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020221008eia700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President Biden Makes Americans More Nervous By 