ed down. It was the Democrats who were yelling, defund the police. And \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  just a couple days ago wants to double down on it. And it's the Democrat cities where the mayor's control the police departments where we've got the biggest problems in this country.\par \par \uc2 And the fact that you've got New York, Los Angeles, Chicago, and Philadelphia won't even give their number of crimes to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  tells me that that increase in murders of 4.2 percent is probably double what they're saying it is. And so, it's a -- it's a shell game where they're just saying no, we never said that, you said it.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: And Geraldo, why is not a mystery. The polling is terrible for Democrats on crime.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: But isn't that interesting? I, Will, was just surprised when I saw that the abortion, inflation, immigration, all are -- you know, take a backseat to crime. The people really feel -- the main job of government is to keep people safe. And if -- and to make them feel safe, like you say you can't walk the tax department a couple of blocks away because you're afraid that something's going to happen. You know, it's -- people have to have a sense of well-being that society is going to take care of you, that the criminals will be suppressed and apprehended, and prosecuted. And when that starts falling apart, it's very, very concerning to me.\par \par \uc2 And I hate that what Democrats have dented cops. I just -- I have so many cop friends, and to see them how they feel, they feel beleaguered, they feel assailed by Democrats.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Jesse, the only other thing that I can ever see \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  talk about when it comes to crime is to tie it to guns. So, his answer to a rising crime rate is gun control.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes. Well, they're saying that Republicans don't want to vote for the assault weapons ban.\par \par \uc2 CAIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And that's what he's for. But yes, they don't want that. 