t's not a very popular position anymore.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Anyway we're talking about hundreds of rapists and murderers combined. All right, his radical views now they're extreme, but are they really out of step with Wisconsin, which is kind of hard to peg? This kind of tells the tale, does it not?\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: Absolutely. I mean, this is this is the difference between Ron Johnson been able to define his opponent and not. I mean, at this point, that senator has been releasing advertised advertisements said just take his Mandela's ads, and Ron John talks over them and says, this is what he stands for. You see him hitting a baseball and acting like a normal Wisconsinite. But in reality, his policies are for cutting the police. He says America's an awful founding and awful country says the green new deal should come to Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: A typical Democrat.\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: A typical Democrat, and a not typical Democrat state.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, let's turn to the house where incumbent Democrat \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  is battling this newcomer Cassie Garcia in Texas is 28th district. Why is this interesting? And why should we be focusing on this race?\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: So Cuellar is basically the last prolife Democrat left. He's someone who's been yelling at the President about the border. He's a nine term Congressman running against an openly conservative, Former Crew Staffer. And this is a place.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Ted crew staffer.\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: Yes, Ted crew staffer. And this is a place that used to be the future of the Democrats plans, the Rio Grande Valley, they wrote books about how the demographic change in America was going to usher in a democratic supermajority forever. And this is ground zero for that not working out. Democrats keep on attacking the border. These people are living this nightmare. And it's really hurting Democrats down there. He's had some support from Democratic leadership who've tried to bail him out a little bit. But in general, this is showing the continuing trend of Democratic voters, Hispanic voters turning on the democratic.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: So this primary that he had was so close, it went to a runoff, and then a recount. And then he may have lost if the Democratic leadership didn't step in, to help him even though again, he's at odds with the Democrat leadership on most of these big issues, like semi-automatic ban of weapons on whether the border is secure. So why do they want him to win if he's not going to just be a rubber stamp for their fall?\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: So he's over for I think he's about 40 percent against the President on the Democratic Party, but that 60 percent is helpful, and they know that the Rio Grande is changing. They know that is going more and more red, and the woman who ran against some young woman is a radical left winger, young one, the Democrats said hold on a second, there's no shot, she's going to win an actual general election and maybe we can get some concessions from Cuellar if we come in for to try and help them. But it's never good for an incumbent to almost barely scrape by in a primary before agenda.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Remember like a couple of years ago when they said well, \uc2 Beto O'Rourke\uc2  is going to be at the forefront.\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: It's Beto.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh sorry, Beto flipping Texas to Blue. I mean that was, I think we saw that headline so many times. Is that even --\par \par \uc2 BEDFORD: They are still doing think pieces. Now we have Mandela, the new Beto of Wisconsin. It's the same story.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Let's stay in south Texas, though, where GOP Congresswoman Mayra Flores is battling that Democrat Congressman \uc2 Vicente Gonzalez\uc2 . That's the 34th district. She is running for her first full term after she won the special election. We all reported on it. This really does seem like a Hispanic wave in some sections of the