d for the Democrats when even Sharpton is trying to reach for some way of justifying it all. But his solution? Well, to focus on this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REV. AL SHARPTON, CIVIL RIGHTS LEADER: The thing that is making this competitive is the Supreme Court decision on women's right to choose and the fact that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won't leave the stage.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Reverend Al, if that's your strategy, then keep it going, because you are going to kiss the Hispanic vote goodbye. New data from the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  shows that abortion ranked seventh in the list of top issues for Hispanic voters. The number one issue, like for everybody else, is of course something he even won't talk about, the economy.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Monica De La Cruz, who is a Texas congressional candidate, and Horace Cooper, National Center for Public Policy senior fellow, author of the new book, "Put Y'All Back in Chains, How \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's Policies Harm Blacks." Monica, what are voters telling you about these issues? Do issues like January 6th or climate change, are those the issues that are motivating them today?\par \par \uc2 MONICA DE LA CRUZ, (R) TEXAS CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: What's motivating Hispanics here in south Texas is the economy and the outrageous inflation that is driving up prices, driving up gas. People are suffering down here. They are having a difficult time putting food on the table, and they are stressed. And that's why we are seeing Hispanics in south Texas not just walking away from the Democratic Party but running away by the thousands.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, an interesting poll from NBC News, Telemundo, and "The Wall Street Journal" shows that congressional preference among Latinos who identify as conservative had Democrats up 10 points, nine points 10 years ago in 2012. Today, 73 percent of conservative Latinos prefer Republicans. Horace, that is a once in a generation shift, if people can follow that.\par \par \uc2 HORACE COOPER, PROJECT 21 CO-CHAIRMAN: Absolutely. What we are seeing is if you offer the voters your January 6th investigation or your LGBTQ priorities, or your climate change, it doesn't affect them. It doesn't touch them. And it doesn't address their needs. Basic conservatives, whether they're Latino, whether they're black, whether they're white, there are certain issues that they care about, and these are not among them. They repel them. But because they won't drop them, they are running them off.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, they go back to abortion and January 6th. That's pretty much it. They have Abortion, they like pot. Abortion, pot, porn, and January 6th. That's kind of it.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: You need to freeze when it gets cold.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Right, exactly, and climate change.\par \par \uc2 You know the Democrats are in trouble, Monica, and it looks like even CNN is starting to see trouble. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRY ENTEN, CNN SENIOR POLITICS WRITER AND ANALYST: Crime is an issue that Republicans love to talk about. You see it in a tone of their ads. Why? Because which party do you trust more on crime? Look at that, Republicans at plus 23 points.\par \par \uc2 The more that voters care about crime, the worse it is for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Monica, he was trying to flick it off with a vocal inflection there, but is it a surprise that voters should care about their own families' safety? That is somehow a shock to some of these commentators.\par \par \uc2 DE LA CRUZ: Well, that just goes to show how out of touch the Democratic Party is with voters in general, but specifically Hispanic voters. Hispanics believe in law and order. They want a strong border. They believe in strong laws and us enforcing that. We are seeing this here at the border where we have a large Border Patrol sector right here in Texas 15