ned as are Republicans with inflation, economy, jobs, gas prices, cost of living, all of those things.\par \par \uc2 Even crime and immigration are higher than abortion for most independent voters. And so I think Democrats are - while they're trying to, obviously, energize their base, which they need their base, they can't win without it. But they can't win, particularly in some of these battleground states without winning over independent voters. Right now their focus on abortion makes them look out of touch and all those other issues and those are issues that Independents are caring about.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: And they're already leapfrogging ahead, Congressman, to 2024. It's almost like people are through the midterms. We haven't had the midterms yet. But there are almost past it. And so a day after NBC broke that news that Biden was going to run again in 2024, another crack formed. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALYSSA FARAH, CO-HOST @THEVIEWABC: 2024 is Biden the best candidate to represent Democrats?\par \par \uc2 REP. CORI BUSH (D-MI): He's our President right now. He has experience, he has qualifications. I won't say if he's best, or if he's not.\par \par \uc2 FARAH: You commit to supporting him if he runs?\par \par \uc2 BUSH: If the President is the nominee is the nominee, then he will be the person that we support.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, that's a non-answer. She's obviously a Far Left Congresswoman. But they're going to be running fast from Biden, are they not, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 BANKS: No question about it. They know what's about to happen. On November 8th, the American people are going to reject \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and their policies, and give Republicans the majority because of it.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats see the writing on the wall. They all talked privately about it on Capitol Hill. They know what's coming. And they've got their sights on 2024, because they've already moved on from this election.\par \par \uc2 So when we get that majority back, we have to fight hard to do - again, do what we say we're going to do and make sure we keep the majority. And if we if we do what we say we're going to do, Laura, I really do believe the reward for that would be winning back the White House in 2024 to get our country back on track.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, that means we need real legislation, not just bullet point policies, though, right, Congressman? I mean, the commitment to America was nice, but there's not a lot of specifics. There was themes and themes are good, but you got to put meat on the bone. So that's what's going to have to happen.\par \par \uc2 But Tom, \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , is back on television. She's complaining, this time, about rigged polls.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D) FORMER GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Polls are snapshot and the question is who are you taking a picture of? And when you take a picture that is predominantly Republican, and has a disproportionately white population or that undercounts the participation of women, of course, it's going to lean in the direction of the incumbent. But when those polls actually look at and the cross tabs reflect the composition of Georgia, we are in a neck and neck race.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But Tom isn't RCP's polling average showing Kemp with a seven point lead right now, right?\par \par \uc2 BEVAN: Yes, yes. And, well, the other thing is. I mean, there can be - something when you have a race where it's close, you'll have some polls saying the Democrats are ahead, some polls that are saying the Republicans are ahead. That's not the case here. Every single poll across the board shows Kemp leading.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, some of those same polls show Raphael Warnock, ahead of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  by a point or two. So it's not the case that somehow 