s are unfair too. Grades are racist. Math is sexist. Sciences is anti-trans. And only standards that really makes sense and that are acceptable to the Gen C activists are no standards at all.\par \par \uc2 Case in point, a group of students just got an \uc2 NYU\uc2  Professor fired because he made his organic chemistry course too hard. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reports that 82 of Dr. Maitland Jones's 350 students signed a petition to get rid of him. Writing, "We're very concerned about our scores" and find that, "They are not an accurate reflection of the time and effort put into this class."\par \par \uc2 They accused him of reducing the number of midterms from three to two, not offering extra credit and removing Zoom access to his lectures. Oh, he also had a condescending and demanding tone. He also happens to be one of the most storied organic chemistry professors of all time, writing what many consider to be the seminal textbook on organic chemistry.\par \par \uc2 Now it should be noted that many who take organic chemistry, and I was one of them, because they want to become doctors, then kind of drop out because it's too hard. But these people are actually wanting to be doctors This is a frightening prospect.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Gordon Klein, former UCLA Professor. He too was the target of a student petition to be fired when he didn't cave into request to grade Black students more leniently after the death of George Floyd.\par \par \uc2 Also with me is Charlie Kirk, the author of the College Scam. Professor, great to see you tonight. Dr. Jones noted that students have been less engaged since COVID. Does that surprise you given your experience that we chronicled, what was that a couple of years ago?\par \par \uc2 GORDON KLEIN, FORMER UCLA PROFESSOR: It was. It doesn't surprise me at all. Laura, I think you summarized it magnificently. We have a coddled generation. We have a group of people who have learned to get by, whether it was due to COVID, whether it's due to civil unrest, whether they want to blame it at a solar eclipse, who knows what they're going to blame it on. They believe they have an entitlement to good grades and the lack of studying.\par \par \uc2 We can track that, because the extent you teach remotely, you get a printout, if you desire, of how many minutes some students spent with your class. One of my colleagues yesterday said he marked - he observed a student who was complaining about the grade that they had attended precisely nine minutes for the entire academic period.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, my. Unbelievable, Charlie, I know you're not surprised about all this. But in a statement to THE INGRAHAM ANGLE, \uc2 NYU\uc2  spokesperson, John Beckman - you can't say spokesman anymore, defended the termination, saying that "Dr. Jones was hired to teach and wasn't successful. \uc2 NYU\uc2  has a lot of hard courses and lots of tough graders among the faculty that don't end up with outcomes that raise questions about the quality and effectiveness of the teaching as this class did."\par \par \uc2 Charlie, you notice that he focused on outcomes. So the university wants their kids to get into med school whether the test scores and the work that goes into the subject isn't up to snuff, doesn't matter at all. Unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE KIRK, TURNING POINT USA FOUNDER: Yes, it's a high stakes game if you want to become a doctor. Let me just say as a millennial, I'm loving the fact that we can finally bash on another generation. Laura, for like five years, it was like millennials are the worse. Finally, there's a generation that's worse than us, finally.\par \par \uc2 And it's totally true, by the way. Those Gen Z-ers can't stop staring at their phones, TikTok. They're the worse. I mean that non-sarcastically. But honestly, do you think China is looking at us and making an excuse for the fact that 82 students, or whatever it was, complain about organic chemistry? Does that make us more likely to be able to defeat the Chinese Communist Party in the next, 30 or 40 or 50 years? Grade inflation, like all inflation in our society right now, is wrong. It lowers standards. It coddles kids.\par \par \uc2 And let's just remember, this is \uc2 NYU\uc2 . This is one of the top schools on the planet where if you sign a petition against your professor, you can have the leaders of that university capitulate to your demands. This sets an awful precedent. And look, organic chemistry, that's above my head. That's above a lot of people I know, very, very smart people. But guess what, you want to be in the best medical system in the world, you have to forsake equity and feelings and prioritize hierarchy and excellence over those things. And I guess Gen Z gets their way, which is complain all the time.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: My brother aced organic chemical history. I had to drop it, OK. Confession, professor, I just couldn't get it. That was my -- that was a warning to me. Med school is not for you. Now, here are the types of complaints about Dr. Jones. On the Ratemyprofessor.com, students said taking Jones' organic chemistry class put them in therapy. One student said the class gave them anxiety and nightmares. Another was self-diagnosed with orgo-PTSD. Professor, I thought college was supposed to build on a work ethic, character, mental toughness. But apparently the course has to change for the kid here.\par \par \uc2 GORDON KLEIN, FORMER UCLA PROFESSOR: You know, I never noticed that I created mental disorders of unique nature. Orgo-PTSD. No, we get criticized. I've been criticized as a white male who believes in the culture of rigor. I thought that UCLA is renowned nationwide and gets the accolade as the number one public university in America. We're supposed to get there and stay there due to a culture of rigor, which was built up over 100 years. And we are disintegrating before your very eyes.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Charlie, remember, \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  used to always refer to the soft bigotry of low expectations. That was really one of the most accurate ways of describing what we're doing to young people with the way they speak, the way they write, what they don't read and what they do read in schools. It's not making them better. It's making them less able to understand the world around them in a clear way. And yet this is what we're doing.\par \par \uc2 KIRK: It also makes them really depressed. It's the most depressed, suicidal, anxious, alcohol-addicted, drug-addicted, and psychiatric drug taking generation in history. Why? Because they're thinking about themselves all the time. Narcissism doesn't lead to a happy life. Worry about service, rigor, hard work, commitment, duty, honor, patriotism. That actually is a really good way to live your life. Do something hard and believed in delayed gratification, not just whether or not you have enough \uc2 Instagram\uc2  likes or TikTok likes in that single moment. I think a lot of the negative side effects we see in our society is an outgrowth of overfocusing on the feelings of generation and not trying to push them to higher standards or excellence.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Let's not forget adults are responsible for the way the kids are brought up, and it's really not their fault. This is the way they were raised by the culture and by their parents. Professor, Charlie, this is a subject we're going to address again. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Biden administration officials swore that thousands of Afghans who were bought here after the botched evacuation from the airport in Afghanistan, they were all fully vetted, right? Now we have a horrifying case of child abuse out of New Mexico revealing what we knew at the time. It wasn't true, there was no vetting. The shocking details, coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: A new DHS inspector general report is revealing tonight that after Biden's disastrous withdrawal from Afghanistan last year, the government's struggle to track thousands of Afghan refugees when they independently departed U.S. military bases. And then they couldn't even document when the refugees left those bases. So they're being marked present when in reality they're God knows where doing God knows what.\par \par \uc2 Now, when DHS would finally notice someone was missing, the report states that the agency didn't even bother trying to find them. And that's on top of the other lies the administration was peddling. Like this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I can absolutely assure you that no one coming into the United States of America who has not been through a thorough screening and background check process.\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS, HOMELAND SECURITY SECRETARY: We conducted comprehensive security and vetting screening of the individuals who arrived here under Operation Allies Welcome. We're extraordinarily proud of that program.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: But as we learned last month in yet another DHS watchdog report, refugees were not screened or vetted properly either. And that includes this next shocking story out of New Mexico. Afghan refugee Shah Mahmood Selab has just been accused of committing sex crimes against a 12-year-old boy. According to court documents, Selab sat next to the victim at a park, showed him porno videos, and started touching him. When the victim tried to get away, he says Selab punched him in the face twice. The victim ran and tried to hide the in a bathroom, but Selab followed him, locked the door, and started kissing him and forced the 12-year-old boy to touch him.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is New Mexico Congresswoman \uc2 Yvette Herrell\uc2 . Congresswoman, this came almost a year to the day that a female soldier at Fort Bliss in New Mexico was attacked by a group of Afghan men. How did that not set off alarm bells?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 YVETTE HERRELL\uc2 , (R-NM): Right. And thank you so much for having me. And you're exactly right. And we were on your show a year ago saying, look, we think there's a problem with the vetting process. We do not believe that the American people are safe. We feel like there was real problems because we knew the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  were the ones helping to evacuate the people out of Afghanistan. And you on your show, we told the American people there was a problem. We sent a letter to the president asking for confirmation of that. Of course, we did not get an answer that made sense. We asked for Mayorkas to be put in front of the Oversight Committee so we could ask the questions the American needed to know in regards to allies welcome home. We still got no answers. And now the report that came out in September confirmed everything we already knew. There were gaps in the process, the vetting was not adequate, and now we're seeing what's happening.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, we kept being told that we promise them, like 120,000 people were promised. I don't know who they were promised by. But the American people's safety and security is of paramount importance. And the fact is there is no real possibility of vetting 99.9 percent of the people who were handed up to the soldiers at that military -- military airport in Kabul. How do you ever vet them?\par \par \uc2 HERRELL: Well, you can't, because you can only vet against the paperwork you have available to you, and we did not have access to any of that. So we knew that they were coming into the country without properly being vetted. And then we saw, like you mention earlier, the attack on the service member on Fort Bliss after the Afghan were here and living on the base. And we knew right then, if they're willing to do that to a service member on a military installation, then what could happen to people around the nation? And now we're seeing just that. A child -- in fact, this isn't the first case of an attack on a minor. We didn't do our jobs. Now we've put the American people in peril. And what happens, Laura, if it's something more sinister? If it's a larger catastrophic issue that happens? Who is going to take the blame? I hope the president will, and Secretary Mayorkas, because this squarely lies on their hands.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, Republicans get pack power, Congresswoman, they've got to do something about this. Refugees are supposed to be either returned to their homeland if possible. If it's not, there has to be adequate safeguards if place. This cannot happen again. This just simply can't happen again. This is just preposterous. Congresswoman, thank you for staying on this. And when you guys get the majority back, we expect action on this.\par \par \uc2 According to internal communications, officials in Fairfax County, Virginia, public schools have taken steps to implement what's being called equitable grading -- this is kind of a follow on to our Gen Z segment -- in a bid to fight institutional bias. One father in that district says that these programs are going to drag his daughter backward. He's here next to respond.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 , TREASURY SECRETARY: We have put racial equity at the forefront of our agenda at Treasury and across the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The economy is collapsing, and that was Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  today advancing what we know to be the central goal of the Biden administration, the amorphous idea of equity above all else.\par \par \uc2 Now we heard it from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  with regard to hurricane aid distribution last week, and we've also heard it from Education Secretary \uc2 Miguel Cardona\uc2 , throughout his whole tenure. So it really shouldn't come as any surprise how prevalent this is now in our schools. According to "The Washington Examiner," Fairfax County, Virginia, has taken steps to implement equitable grading in order to fight institutional bias.\par \par \uc2 Now, some of the practices include grade penalty removal -- you know what that is -- unlimited ability to redo assignments, and eliminating zeros. So even if you don't submit assignments, you'll get a 50 percent regardless, because that's how the real world is, right?\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Harry Jackson, a Fairfax County parent. Harry, thank you for joining me tonight. I've talked to a few Fairfax County public school parents. They are enraged at this. I know your daughter goes to Langley High School. They claim that this is going to help everyone, including your daughter, as a student there. But what is it really going to mean for her and other students that do their work?\par \par \uc2 HARRY JACKSON, FAIRFAX COUNTY PUBLIC SCHOOLS PARENT: Well, thank you, Laura, for having me on. So let's talk about Langley and the solution that they're trying to implement and what problem they're trying to address. At Langley High School, every racial demographic group is doing quite well, even socioeconomic, low income families are doing well. They're well above state scores. So the solution that they're trying to impose is to a problem that doesn't exist.\par \par \uc2 In the case of my daughter, she's a straight A student. Yes, there's an achievement gap, but in the other direction. The black students at Langley are doing well. There is absolutely no problem. And what parents need to really worry about that Langley, even though that's in a bubble, it's a very wealthy school, is that this type of system with equity grading, there's to science to support it. In fact, it has disastrous results elsewhere throughout the country, cut as California and Maryland.\par \par \uc2 For example, in Maryland at Patterson High School, in the 2021-2022 academic year, out of 488 seniors, only 12 were at grade reading level. That's only two percent. Seventy-one students were at a kindergarten reading level, 88 first grade, 45, second grade. This is a type of system that is known to fail. When the student doesn't show up, they skip class, they just socially promoted. This will certainly increase graduation rates, but they students that will graduate will not be prepared for the outside world. They'll be ruining children's lives.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Harry, it's not the real world, right? In the real world, results do matter. Imagine if you're like the coach of a basketball team, and you said well, I know that that our team didn't score any points in the first quarter, but we'd like half of what the other team scored. They'd be laughed off the court, right. The coach would be fired immediately. But they want to treat students as if they're not smart enough or they're not capable enough of actually competing at just a regular level. But, again, it just he knows up hurting students who have to go out in the real world and compete.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Absolutely. And parents at Langley, they're furious, because they're reining in the system to reduce the quality of education for their children at a school that by all objective measures is doing quite well. What problems does exist? The only reason why they would ever reduce the quality of education at the school, which they first told parents that they weren't doing, we found later that they were deceiving us, is if they're trying to bring down the performance of students at Langley so it's more comparable to surrounding area high schools that might be doing --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Ding, ding, ding, ding, ding. Because you guys at Langley High School are privileged, Harry. So we have to even out the playing field. And so if you did really well, then something must be wrong with you. So we've got to level it all out. This is going to be backfire. I'm telling you, more people are going to pull their kids out of the public school. Harry, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now, how Monty Python predicted our current gender insanity? What is this? The Last Bite explains.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, Monty Python was not only brilliant, hysterical, but way ahead of the curve. Now, consider all the madness surrounding gender fluidity. They saw the breakdown over 40 years ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's every man's right to have babies if he wants them.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But you can't have babies.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Don't you on oppress me.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm not oppressing you. You haven't got a womb. Where's the fetus going to gestate? Are you going to keep it in a box?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: OK, hilarious, and, yes, a bit frightening.\par \par \uc2 All right, that's it for us tonight. We've already gotten a lot of reaction to our segment on Generation Z, the coddled generation, and their approach to school and work and everybody is a victim and call me by the right pronoun and getting professors -- storied professors fired from \uc2 NYU\uc2  because the class is too hard. My friend, something has to give. And that's where it's going to start with parents and adults who actually really care about this next generation. They all need to succeed as best they can, and we're going to have to try to help them.\par \par \uc2 All right, set your DVR so you always stay connected with us. Thanks for watching. Remember, it's America now and, you bet, forever. Greg Gutfeld, he'll take it all from here.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020221005eia400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Key Senate Races To Watch Five Weeks Out; Pennsylvania Race Tightening Five Weeks From Election Day; Battle For Congress Tightens With Five Weeks To Go; Pelosi Predicts Dems Will Keep Control Of House; \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  Denies He Paid For Abortion; Fox Rides Along With National Guard In Florida; Dozens Confirmed Dead In Florida As Cleanup Continues; North Korea Fires Missile With Enough Range To Hit Guam; Gen. Keane: North Korea Is Clamoring For Attention; Gas Prices On The Rise With Midterms Five Weeks Away; DEA Seizes Rainbow Fentanyl Inside Lego Box; Father Killed While Visiting College Student; A Look Back At The White House Response To A Biden 2024 Run; Loretta Lynn Dies At 90\b0\par\par\uc2 Sandra Smith, John Roberts, Martha MacCallum, Bryan Llenas, Bret  Baier, Nate Foy, Steve Harrigan, General Jack Keane, Laura Engel, Alexis  McAdams, Joe Concha \par \uc2 7,929 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Big two-day gain, and big news tomorrow is the \uc2 OPEC\uc2  decision on oil production and oil output. How much will they cut? Will they cut? So, we'll be watching that. Thanks so much for joining us, everyone. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And I'm John Roberts, could be digging a little deeper to fill up your car, again. THE STORY with Martha starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you very much, John and Sandra. Big news today in the Georgia Senate race. Bret Baier is on deck. Election day just five weeks away. An October surprise and a lot on the line.\par \par \uc2 Also, here today on THE STORY, Joe Concha, and General Jack Keane, also Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , who is the Republican nominee for governor in that race in New York as \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  speaks out on 2024. North Korea sends the Japanese into shelters, and a dad in a hotel for Parents Weekend is shot dead in the lobby. Unbelievable story.\par \par \uc2 But first, the race for control of the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 , the battle comes down to essentially netting five House seats and one Senate seat, that's what the GOP hopes to do in five weeks. The four yellow states on our Senate Fox Power Rankings map, these are the biggest ones to watch. They are Arizona, where Democrat Mark Kelly's lead over Blake Masters is now at six in Georgia. Five points separate, Raphael Warnock, and Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  who is facing controversy today. We're going to get to that in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 And in Nevada, Republican \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  lead over incumbent \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  well within the margin of error. He was with us here yesterday.\par \par \uc2 And in Pennsylvania, Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 's lead is now to six points over Dr. Mehmet Oz. That race is where we get started today with correspondent Bryan Llenas. Hi, Bryan.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN LLENAS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Martha. Well, the last poll you mentioned Martha is a USA Today, Suffolk University poll. Not only does it show a narrowing of \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 's lead from nine to six points over Dr. Mehmet Oz, but it also shows the number of voters who view Fetterman unfavorably, has skyrocketed almost 17 points since June.\par \par \uc2 Now, this jives with our Fox News polling which shows Fetterman's lead has dropped from 11 points in July, so four points now. It's not just the polling that is tightening. It seems, the fundraising gap is narrowing too. Dr. Oz raised $17.2 million in the third quarter from July to September. That includes $7 million of his own money, but still, that's significantly higher than the 5.5 million Oz raised in the second quarter.\par \par \uc2 The numbers suggest the relentless attack ads from Oz against Fetterman as being soft on crime may be working. The campaign released this new ad today attacking Fetterman for saying he will decriminalize all drugs. Fetterman's camp says he no longer supports that. Pennsylvania's police unions continue to back Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R), PENNSYLVANIA SENATORIAL CANDIDATE: They endorsed me because they don't feel supported right now by the lieutenant governor. And they're concerned that if they're going to run towards the danger, they're, they're at risk for being criticized for doing it, undermined in their efforts to do it, and the laws keep changing, so they don't feel protected when they do risked their lives.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LLENAS: Meanwhile, the Fetterman camp continues to hammer Dr. Oz on his authenticity. This ad compares Dr. Oz to quack Dr. Nick from The Simpsons, mocking Oz is a T.V. doctor scam artist who isn't from Pennsylvania. This has been a very negative and personal race. The first and only debate scheduled about the actual issues is exactly three weeks from today, on October 25.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's going to be a big night. Bryan, thank you very much. Great to have you with us today. With that, we bring in Brett Baier, anchor and executive editor of "SPECIAL REPORT" and Fox News Chief Political Anchor. Brett, good to see you. Today is Tuesday, which means we're exactly five weeks away from huge midterm coverage event. Let's start with Pennsylvania here because it's very interesting. Dr. Oz is not held back. He's out there a lot. Fetterman has been sort of criticized for not being out there as much, and that debate between the two of them, I think it's going to be a very consequential night. What do you make of this race right now?\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: Well, I think it's closing and I think every poll has, has shown that Oz has closed the gap. It's within the margin of error for the, for most of the polls. We're seeing two three, this latest one is six. But if you look closely, I think that there is a lot of movement towards Oz, in part, because he's been really hitting the crime issue. And that has had some effect. Fetterman has been out on the stump. His stump speech is about 12-13 minutes long and not filled with a ton of substance.\par \par \uc2 And as we said before, he hasn't done a ton of interviews, where he's pressed on some of the policy issues. So, that debate, which all has its own stipulations about how it's going to come forward with closed captioning, et cetera, is going to be fascinating to watch but late in the game since Pennsylvania has been voting for some time. By the way 28 states, Martha, have already started voting for the midterms.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, great point. With regard to holding the house, you see both sides sort of speaking as positively as they can at this point. They want to keep the fundraising dollars coming in. We heard \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  say that he thinks that Ohio and Pennsylvania and Wisconsin are in the, in his category on the GOP side. Here's \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  last night saying she believes that Democrats will hold the house. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): I believe that we will win the, hold the hot house, and we will hold the house by winning more seats out. We won the 40 seats then we lost someone. Trump was on the ballot. We lost someone in the Trump districts, but we held and held enough seats to hold the house with him on the ballot. He's not on the ballot now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, is that wishful thinking on her part given the redistricting or is there a chance that happens?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Most experts say it's wishful thinking. There are 30 toss ups in our Fox News power rankings of the House races. GOP would only have to win 20 percent of those, to win the house control. And in most of these districts, because of, as you mentioned, redistricting. There are at least four or five seats that seem to go the GOP way. That said, the abortion issue did factor in to some of these races. And Democrats picked up some points. So, it's, it's been a push and pull, but right now the GOP is still slated to pick up those seats and maybe she's just projecting that what Democrats may want to do.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Could be. It's, it always gets a little fierce and there's always an October surprise that drops around this time in these races. This one came from \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and a Daily Beast piece claiming that a woman said that he paid for her abortion back in 2009, and that she has a check and a get-well card with his signature on it. Here he is last night with \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2 . Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: They're claiming that five days later on September 17th, you sent a $700 check and that you sent it in a get-well card.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), GEORGIA SENATE NOMINEE: I haven't seen it. But you know, I can tell I sent out so many get-well, send out so much anything. But I can tell you right now, I never asked anyone to get an abortion. I never paid for an abortion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, obviously he has made being pro-life a big part of his campaign. His son has come out on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  lashing out against him. What's the impact of all of this, do you think, Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, to say, the Republican Party in Georgia, the National Republican Party says they're standing with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . As you mentioned, he had a firm pushback and denial on Sean's show last night. But clearly this is an issue that the, his opponent Raphael Warnock is going to probably jump on.\par \par \uc2 Both candidates have some past history with their families. Yet, the sun coming out talking on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is another added, you know, element to this. So, how does it play? Boy, this is a race that's really, really tight. Anything could sway it. And this next debate is October 14th in Savannah. That's going to be a big, big moment.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, these will be big nights in these very close races, and we'll be watching closely. Bret, thank you very much. Good to see you as always.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You bet. See you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, our Democracy 2022 coverage continues with Joe Concha as he weighs in later this hour. And Ian continues to lash out. This video shot yesterday in Daytona Beach. We're going to check in with Nate Foy standing by LIVE has just gone on a ride along with the National Guard, who are coming to the rescue in Florida. He's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARMINE MARCENO, LEE COUNTY SHERIFF: It's all hands-on deck, folks. I want to thank the residents of Lee County. The great residents, and I have to tell you, he's as we stand here, people are pulling up. We took such a catastrophic hit but everybody wants to help. It's very heartwarming during these tragic events because in minutes and hours, a lot of people had their lives washed away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Of course, so true. Look at these images -- just incredible, in Sanibel, Florida. That's the Sheriff of Lee County. We spoke with him yesterday on THE STORY. Hurricane Ian caused so much devastation. I mean, it is -- it looks like a bomb dropped in Sanibel, Florida. It's absolutely devastating. So, almost a week later now, you see some of these fires that are burning in some areas. There are dozens of deaths that have been confirmed across the state.\par \par \uc2 Crews have rescued almost 2,000 people and nearly half a million homes and businesses are still without power. Correspondent Nate Foy live in Englewood, Florida, with the National Guard as that stark contrast now of blue sky and total devastation that we often see after these tragedies. Nate, tell us about what's going on where you are.\par \par \uc2 NATE FOY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Martha, earlier today, we were at that press conference with Lee County Sheriff, Carmine Marcelino, where you just heard he talks about all the good that he's seen in the community after this hurricane. He also told us about illegal immigrants being arrested for looting. So, we're going to tell you about that in a second. First, I want to show you what we're doing right now. We are joined by the Florida National Guard. We're riding along in this community in Englewood. Not the hardest hit area of Southwest Florida.\par \par \uc2 That's Lee County, where Fort Myers Beaches. We're up in Charlotte County where you can see people here, their lives have been up ended. That looks like a bedroom where the roof has been completely blown off, the right side of that roof gone. You see a lot of debris is just strewn all around this neighborhood. People still out picking up the pieces. And that's why the National Guard is out today.\par \par \uc2 If we can have my photographer, Nick (PH) to show right now, we have tarps right here. We have food right here, and water for people who cannot reach the points of distribution. So, we're here with Sergeant Kenny Harris, thank you so much for taking the time to talk to us. I mentioned 25 points of distribution, where people impacted by the hurricane can pick up food and water.\par \par \uc2 How many people in your experience can't get out of their homes or can't get to those distribution sites?\par \par \uc2 KENNY HARRIS, UNITED STATES ARMY STAFF SERGEANT: Well, I have to say, roughly 100 people in the past three to four days.\par \par \uc2 FOY: 100 people in the last three to four days. What are some of, I guess, the most emotional, disturbing things that you've seen in your work trying to help these people get past this?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Well, I have to say, I've seen a home completely torn apart. It actually brought tears to my eyes to actually give back to the community.\par \par \uc2 FOY: And you're from Florida. I'm sure you've experienced a fair amount of hurricanes. Where does this one rank?\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: I will have to say this ranks number one. This is my first one that ever seen the damage this bad.\par \par \uc2 FOY: Well, thank you very much for your time and thank you for all you do for our country and for these people after this devastating event.\par \par \uc2 You know, this is the good here, Martha. But I mentioned the bad that Lee County Sheriff Carmine Marceno share today. Take a look at these two men. Lee County deputies say they are illegal immigrants who were caught stealing from a home that was badly damaged from Hurricane Ian in Bonita Springs.\par \par \uc2 The owner has a 20-foot sinkhole and deputies say that these two guys were stealing golf clubs, weights, and alcohol.\par \par \uc2 So, it's not the only case we're learning back out here live, Martha. Lee County deputies tell us they arrested four people for looting. Three of whom are illegal immigrants. I asked the sheriff if he has any more open investigations for looting, he told me he did not.\par \par \uc2 As for the National Guard here with Sergeant Harris. They're going to be out here until 5:00 p.m. And then, they'll be back out tomorrow because a lot of people need help. Thank you very m