r the Study of War, and Fox News senior strategic analyst.\par \par \uc2 General, good to have you with us this morning.\par \par \uc2 We have U.S. and South Korea drills that have been ongoing. What do you make of this move by North Korea? What are they trying to signal here?\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: Yes. Well, that's a great question in terms of what is he really up to.\par \par \uc2 This represents, actually the 20th missile firing this year. Five, actually in the last 10 days, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: This is the most missiles Kim Jong-un has ever fired in one year. So, it begs the question what is he -- What is he doing here? Well one, as always, he's clamoring for attention, because he wants to get sanction relief, and that has always been his foundational position.\par \par \uc2 The second thing that's happening here is the leadership in South Korea change. And I'm just going to be very frank and direct about this. The previous leadership on the President Moon, many of my friends in the region, who had prominent government positions in Japan and Taiwan, elsewhere, always believe that President Moon was in China's orbit, and actually was seeking reconciliation more than he should have been with North Korea, despite all the aggressiveness that they would display.\par \par \uc2 And in this one, all on through the Trump administration as well. President Yoon, who has taken over name sounds the same, very different person obviously. Is not in that orbit with China whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 They have aligned themselves completely with the United States back where we used to be a number of years ago. Not only that, but they have returned to a full up training regimen with United States.\par \par \uc2 And every time that has been happening throughout this year, Kim Jong-un has been firing missiles. As we speak right now, there has been exercise going on with Japan, United States