hs ago. And again, it's because of the historic steps that this President has taken.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. With that we bring in \uc2 Stephen Moore\uc2 , former senior economic adviser to President Trump, founder of the Committee to Unleash Prosperity and senior economic contributor for Freedom Work. Steve, good to have you with us. So, what's making the upward pressure on prices right now?\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN MOORE\uc2 , FORMER SENIOR ECONOMIC ADVISER TO PRESIDENT TRUMP: Well, first of all, I mean, it's almost laughable that the Biden administration says they're taking all the steps to lower gas prices because almost every step they've taken since Biden has come into office has been to reduce American supply of oil. You know, the first thing that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  did as president when he cancelled the Keystone XL pipeline.\par \par \uc2 So the gas prices are rising again, and I predict they're going to go back up above $4 a gallon, is because you just got a worldwide reduction in the supply. And you saw what the Saudis announced just the other day that they're going to cut back on production because they want to get that price back up to, you know, about $4 to $4.50 a gallon.\par \par \uc2 You know, we have a new study that just came out literally yesterday at the Committee to Unleash Prosperity where we estimate that if we had just stuck with the Trump, you know, pro-America production oil and gas policies, we would be producing about 3 million more barrels a day. And if you did that, by the way, we wouldn't have to deplete the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, which as you know, I mean, you've covered this, we are now at a lowest level in the strategic petroleum reserves since 1984.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: It's become a -- they're using it as a policy to increase supply. It's supposed to be an emergency vowel. Just in case, you know, in a desperate situation, the United States can't get our hands on oil for our own energy that we have the ability to tap into that. We have to pay it back, we have to put it back into the SPI. But it appears, and I think that's the policy that \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  is referring to when she says we brought prices down by tapping into that.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: So they just announced earlier today that they're going to probably stop taking oil out of the strategic reserve because it's so low. The problem there is when you asked me why the prices are going up, guess what. Once you reduce that supply of oil coming out of this strategic reserve, that's also going to push gas prices back up. So it's not a pretty picture when it comes to energy.\par \par \uc2 And it's self-inflicted wounds. We should just be producing all the oil and gas we have in North Dakota, and Oklahoma, and Texas, and West Virginia. We're just not doing it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. It's interesting because when you look at that policy change, I always wonder why when you come in to office you don't sort of say, OK, everybody show me what's working, show me what's not working, show me what aligns with what we said we were going to do if we came into office. But instead, you know, they shut it all down right out of the gate. And clearly now they've got an inflation problem that's bearing down on them five weeks from election.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: And don't forget, who are the biggest winners from this policy of Biden of shutting down American oil and gas, the Russians, the Saudis, and the Chinese, and the Iranians. And so, we're kind of playing into the hands of our enemy when we have a president who has to go prostrate to the Saudis and ask him to increase their oil output, there's something wrong. Why not go to Texas? Why not go to West Virginia?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And that plea to the Saudis -- yes, exactly. The plea to the Saudis clearly didn't work because they might have loosened it up a little bit, but now they're trying to back again.\par \par \uc2 MOORE: But