ing random rapes. Random shootings. Random murders, random stickups. And neighborhoods that weren't supposed to be that unsafe in the broad daylight. New Yorkers are scared, not scared, where like, you know, you're going to go hide and cry in your apartment, but when you're out, you're going to be anxious and you're going to be looking over your shoulder.\par \par \uc2 And we have enough to deal with, with the spouses and the bills and the children. It's enough. And then --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Move. It's time to move.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I, we are.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And the -- and the liberals are always talking about democracy. Democracy, democracy. Well, represent us. You're supposed to represent us. We're scared. You're supposed to do something about that. We're paying you so we don't have to worry about public safety.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We're not supposed to all take up Taekwondo. We're paying you so we don't have to take up Taekwondo. She says it's a one in a million event to get attacked on a subway where we're in the numbers. It's 450 in a million.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Right.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: So, her numbers are wrong. She needs to get back to work. She also said that if you are being stuck up, she said to spill a soda and distract the would-be robber. Like he cares if the rug gets stand like, pass me a paper towel.\par \par \uc2 That's crazy. You shouldn't have to say that to people. And she said she's a queer abolitionist on her web site. I don't know what that means. Do you know what a queer abolitionist means? I have no what that idea. She also said she wants to defund the police and empty the prisons. And she says, don't call 911.\par \par \uc2 So, if you have a public official who's saying, empty the prisons, don't call 911 and defund the police, she's the enemy.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Right.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: How is she not being investigated? How do we know she's not working with the Russians, the cartels, the crips, prove me wrong.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Well, she --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Because that's how you destroy this country.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: She wants people to bring sodas to a knife fight or a gun fight, apparently. Someone who does want to change this is Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  who's running for governor. Take a listen to what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R-NY), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: New Yorkers want to feel safe on our streets and in our subways. We saw the attack on the EMT in Queens. We need to repeal cashless bail in this state. We need to fire district attorneys like Alvin Bragg who refused to enforce the law. We need to unapologetically back our men and women in blue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Jessica?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: So, there a few weeks ago there was kind of a lot of hype, like is \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  closing in on \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 ? No, he isn't. He's still down 20 points. But this is an issue that's breaking through. And as Greg was saying, this is a universal complaint now in the city.\par \par \uc2 And something that's so upsetting to me about it personally, and I've lived here my whole life, is that I feel that we're on the precipice of a fundamental change in the character of the city. So something that's special about a New York, a Paris a London, is that everybody is on public transport, right? Like you can get into the subway and you'll see someone in black tie going to Lincoln Center, and then you can see someone a nurse who's commuting home, who's going to be staying on and heading further uptown, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 And that's what makes New York City fantastic and you're not seeing that anymore, even though things are reopening and there was all this speculation, you know, what will New York City look like after COVID? And people were really concerned about wou