ght law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Hurricane Ian slams the southeast U.S, decimating communities in Florida before accelerating north.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Basically a 500-year flood event.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Ian brings a record storm surge, flooding and untold damage to Florida, then hits the Georgia and Carolina coasts.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds rescued. Some have lost everything and rebuilding could take years. We will have live reports on the trail of destruction and plans for recovery.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Such destruction. It just is horrible here.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And we will ask FEMA administrator \uc2 Deanne Criswell\uc2  about what's being done to help Americans in the hardest-hit areas.\par \par \uc2 And we'll talk with Georgia Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  about the impact in his state and his offer to help his neighbors.\par \par \uc2 GOV. BRIAN KEMP (R-GA): We're ready to roll when they need us.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Plus, we will get the latest on Governor Kemp's highly anticipated midterm rematch against Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 . We've got brand new polls with just weeks to go.\par \par \uc2 Then, Russia ratchets up its rhetoric in its war on Ukraine. Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  saying he will annex occupied territory in Ukraine after referendums the West has called nothing but a sham. We will ask our Sunday panel how Putin's escalation could impact prospects for peace.\par \par \uc2 Plus, I'll sit down with FOX's Bill Melugin, who's been the only constant media presence on our southern border. We'll discuss what's seen first hand and why he came to Capitol Hill to ask questions of our nation's leaders.\par \par \uc2 And the people at the heart of the Supreme Court cases that will dominate the fall term as it kicks off Monday with a full courtroom for the first time since the COVID lockdowns began.\par \par \uc2 It's all right now on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has declared a disaster in Florida, South Carolina and North Carolina, in the after aftermath of Hurricane Ian. And we've learned the president will travel to hard-hit Florida on Wednesday, where dozens of lives have been lost.\par \par \uc2 Here's a look at the powerful storm surge that pummeled Myrtle Beach on Friday as Ian made landfall again. You can see how water surged over the beach and flooded the roadways.\par \par \uc2 But the most dramatic images we've seen are across the state of Florida. This is just a small glimpse of the damage along Fort Myers Beach where the recovery will take years. In a moment, we will speak with FEMA administrator \uc2 Deanne Criswell\uc2  about the tall task ahead for her agency.\par \par \uc2 And Georgia Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  standing by with the impact on his state.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we have live team coverage on the ground. Molly Line in Charleston, South Florida, but first we turn to Phil Keating in Florida -- Phil.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEATING, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hi, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 The massive search and rescue operation continue again today, day four, in the hunt for survivors, and, of course, grimly finding bodies, the death count that's supposed to go up day after day.\par \par \uc2 The other big job of the day: cleaning up all of this wreckage. The coastal counties in southwest Florida all look similar to this. Just wreckage and debris everywhere. Plywood, aluminum siding from ripped-open trailers, personal effects, all over the place. The storm surge also came here, so every single one of these trailers that got broken into by the water force, everything is ruined.\par \par \uc2 So, nothing is salvageable. Over here is a car. That's now ruined. The passenger side window completely blown out, plywood and other debris on it. Palm tree crumbling the house.\par \par \uc2 Over in Fort Myers Beach at the San Carlos RV park, all of the big, big RVs that were left behind, are now across the bay and wrecked and mangled into the trees. That was about a 12 to 15-foot storm surge there. People who stayed tell me they have a newfound respect for the power of a storm surge.\par \par \uc2 Rita and Gary Israel worked there with the little dogs. They returned to discover they have lost everything.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEATING: What did you see when you arrived?\par \par \uc2 GARY ISRAEL, RESIDENT: What you see -- devastation.\par \par \uc2 RITA ISRAEL, RESIDENT: Tears.\par \par \uc2 G. ISRAEL: Yeah, lives gone. And we're going to start over and we're going to rebuild. That's a plus.\par \par \uc2 KEATING: You're determined?\par \par \uc2 G. ISRAEL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEATING: Such nice people.\par \par \uc2 The other big issue is returning power to everybody who lost power. Initially 95 percent of all of Lee and Charlotte County households lost power, living in a blackout until sunrise. That number is down to 75 percent. So, progress is being made. The 12-mile section of I-75 that closed on Friday night into Saturday because the Myakka River breached the levee and flooded the highway, that's reopened in both directions, just one of 10 rivers in Florida that are currently at record levels of depth.\par \par \uc2 About 10,000 Floridians also remain in shelters right now. It's going to be another long, hot day for anybody returning to see what was left, see if they can take anything out. But it's a grim task.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah. So much work to be done. Phil Keating reporting from Florida -- Phil, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's turn to Molly Line live in Charleston, South Carolina with an update from there -- Molly.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY LINE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good morning, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 From Georgia, throughout the Carolinas, people braced for Ian's second U.S. landfall.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LINE (voice-over): The hurricane smashed into the coastline Friday afternoon, bringing high winds and flooding, leaving more than half a million people in the dark.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sir, I need you to get up in there.\par \par \uc2 LINE: In Litchfield, South Carolina, people and pets were rescued, loaded into high water vehicles. In Myrtle Beach, the raging sea claimed the Cherry Grove Pier.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: So amazing to think that something that powerful could do so much damage and it was just hurricane one.\par \par \uc2 LINE: A powerful storm surge flooded Pawleys Island, near Georgetown, where Ian made landfall. The island's beloved pier collapsed, and floated way. Piles of debris littering the beach and streets. Heavy equipment and repair crews descending over the weekend as homeowners returned to survey the damage.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The water came up really rapidly. We were surprised how fast it came up and it got all the way up to the very top of this mailbox post.\par \par \uc2 LINE: Historic flood-prone Charleston escaped fairly unscathed with minor flooding. Residents believed the impact wasn't like storms past.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You'd see waves coming across this whole park. They rolled in here. Fortunately, we didn't see that this time. We're lucky.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 LINE: People here are telling me that their hearts go out to the people in Florida -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Molly Line in South Carolina, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is FEMA Administrator \uc2 Deanne Criswell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Administrator Criswell, welcome to FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 DEANNE CRISWELL\uc2 , FEMA ADMINISTRATOR: Shannon, thanks so much for having me on today.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I know it was very important for you to get there on the ground and take a look around. You spent years as a firefighter, a first responder. How does that inform your assessments of what's going on, on the ground?\par \par \uc2 CRISWELL: I think the biggest thing, Shannon, for me is, I want to get down on the ground, see for myself what the impacts are and talk to people and hear what they're struggling with and what they think their needs are going to be.\par \par \uc2 You know, my time as a firefighter and a local emergency manager before, all disasters, they start and end at the local level. And so for me to be able to make sure we're providing the assistance they need, I need to hear from them and see for myself.\par \par \uc2 And I was able to do that over the last two days working with the governor, traveling with the governor to access the impacts and hear from people that really are going to have a long road to recovery.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: How does it work as far as pre-positioning assets? You know the storm is coming. You've got a number of states to try to get ready for. How did that work in this case and is it playing out in the aftermath the way you had hoped?\par \par \uc2 CRISWELL: Yes, it absolutely is, Shannon. I talked to the governor Friday before landfall to hear what his concerns were going to be. And then we started to preposition resources to make sure that we were going to have what we needed in place.\par \par \uc2 The biggest thing that we knew, and what the governor's concern was, being able to take care of the people that were going to be in the way of the storm's path. And so, we prepositioned the largest amount of search and rescue assets that I think we've ever put in place before, bringing together all of the federal family, our FEMA Urban Search and Rescue teams, the Coast Guard, the Department of Interior, the Department of Defense.\par \par \uc2 But this is just to complement the already really robust capability that Florida already had in place. Immediately following the storm's path, we were able to move these resources in, in support of the counties. They went in, in the wee hours of the morning to begin those search-and-rescue efforts and they are still out there today.\par \par \uc2 And that's just one of the resources. We also sent in commodities, prepositioned them, and we're moving them out into the impacted areas as we speak.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The front page of "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " today talks about an issue in Lee County, hardest hit by this storm. They say this. There was a delay, an apparent violation of the meticulous evacuation strategy the county had crafted for just such an emergency. It may have contributed to catastrophic consequences that are still coming into focus as the death toll continues to climb.\par \par \uc2 There are so many different entities involved here, local, state, federal. But when one section of the puzzle, it sounds like, may have gotten this wrong as far as warning residents when and how they needed to get out, how is that now playing out in Lee County, as we know there's still water rising there, there are still people very much in need of rescue?\par \par \uc2 CRISWELL: You know, I think this storm, it was really dynamic, and it was very unpredictable for a long period of time. Just 72 hours before the storm made landfall, Lee County wasn't even in the hurricane path, right. It wasn't even in the predicted zone of where it was going to make impact. And it continued to move south day by day.\par \par \uc2 As soon as the storm predictions were that it was going to impact Lee County, I know that local officials immediately put the right measures in place to make sure that they were warning citizens to get them out of harm's way.\par \par \uc2 So, right now, we have resources in place, regardless of where the storm made impact, and that's why they're in those communities right now doing search and rescue and providing assistance as needed.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And can you give people a sense, a lot of people think the storm goes through, of course, that's the immediate, most dangerous time. But there are now these days afterwards with worries about drinkable water, electricity being restored, water still rising and gushing through, bridges washed out. What are the days like after the storm and the FEMA response to that?\par \par \uc2 CRISWELL: It's a really important point, Shannon. I know we worry a lot about the direct impacts from the storm itself, if it's making landfall, but we see so many more injuries and sometimes more fatalities after the storm because there are so many dangers out there.\par \par \uc2 What I can say is, people need to stay vigilant right now. Standing water brings with it all kinds of hazards. It has debris. It could have power lines. It can have hazards in there that you just don't know about.\par \par \uc2 And so we want to make sure that people are being extra cautious, they're taking extra protective measures to make sure that they are taking care of themselves and their family as they're starting the repair.\par \par \uc2 You know, we also see heat come in and people are working hard to -- to start to fix their homes and clean out their homes, but they need to be taking care of themselves, taking breaks, because we also see an increase in the number of dehydration incidents or heat attacks. A lot of issues that come in after a storm. People really need to take extra care to make sure that they're taking care of themselves and their families.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to ask you as well, I heard from one survivor's account this morning, lives on a small neighborhood lake. And they say for the first time, regardless of all the storms they've passed before, this one really came in and was a flooding problem for them. They said they didn't buy flood insurance because they weren't technically in the plain (ph).\par \par \uc2 NBC has a piece on this citing back to a 2017 DHS inspector general report about FEMA flood maps, saying, hundreds of thousands of Florida homes lie in flood risk areas not recognized by FEMA's flood maps. That's according to their research group.\par \par \uc2 So, what do you say to folks? Are there change -- plans to change those maps? How important are they for people in making these decisions about buying flood insurance?\par \par \uc2 CRISWELL: I think the first thing that I would say to everybody out there is that where it rains, it can flood, right? And so, our flood maps are really focused on inundation at the coastal levels and riverine areas. But as we're seeing these storms produce more rainfall and wetter rains, it's coming down at rates that are far higher than we've seen in the past. People need to make sure that they're understanding what their risk is.\par \par \uc2 While we require flood insurance for certain areas, it is available for everybody to buy. And so insurance is one of those best tools that you can have. And, again, if you're in an area where it rains, it can flood. And flood insurance is your best bet to make sure you can protect your belongings.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Administrator Criswell, we know you're working around the clock. Thank you so much for that and for joining us today. We appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 CRISWELL: Shannon, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Joining us now Republican governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Governor, welcome to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 GOV. BRIAN KEMP (R-GA): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So how are folks doing? I mean, I know there's some coastal impact for you. And what's your ability like now to help your neighbors, Florida and South Carolina?\par \par \uc2 KEMP: Yeah, very minimal impact at all in Georgia. We really dodged the bullet and certainly blessed by that. And our thoughts and prayers go out to all those that have been affected in, and certainly, the loss of lives in other states, too.\par \par \uc2 We are really lucky. I instructed our Emergency Director Chris Stallings to start allocating our resources where they're needed in other states on Friday, really right after lunch. We had those discussions even starting Friday morning.\par \par \uc2 We haven't had a whole lot of request but we stand ready to help. We do have 14 heroes from the Georgia National Guard that are doing search and rescue in Florida and certainly have a lot of other resources that we can send to South Carolina or Florida if needed, and we stand by ready to that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Governor, on behalf of my Florida family and friends, we thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now, let's get to your race. It is a heated one.\par \par \uc2 We've got brand-new FOX News polls in your race that show you with a distinct advantage over your competitor, Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 . You're quoted this week as saying: I will tell people out there, don't believe any of these polls.\par \par \uc2 Now, ours has you up by seven. Do you believe our poll?\par \par \uc2 KEMP: Well, no disrespect to the Fox News poll, I'm not believing any polls. We're running like we're 10 points behind. The biggest poll and only one that matters, Shannon, is going to be on Election Day.\par \par \uc2 And so, I'm telling our folks, you know, don't get overconfident. We've got to keep working like we've never worked before. We're not only fighting for the soul of our state.\par \par \uc2 We're fighting a lot of outside interests from places like California and New York. \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , 86 percent of her money is coming from around the country. They want to bring their values to our state through her. And that's what we're fighting against.\par \par \uc2 And we need your listeners out there or your viewers to go to BrianKemp2022.com and help us fight that narrative from around the country, and keep Georgia moving in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. As you await the results of the November election, your team did have a win, a legal win on Friday night.\par \par \uc2 Here's a headline from "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 ": Federal judge rules against Fair Fight Action in Georgia voting lawsuit. That's a group aligned with \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In reaction to the loss, after four years of fighting, she said it uncovered a lot of problems in Georgia. And there are still issues of voter suppression and keeping people who want to vote from being able to vote in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 KEMP: Well, listen, \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  and her group lost on every single count in that ruling Friday. It's sad that since day one, she's used this whole process to line her pockets, sow distrust in democratic institutions and build her celebrity status, and using the legal system for her own political gain quite honestly.\par \par \uc2 Thankfully, Judge Jones made it clear to all Georgians and people around the country, that in our state, it's easy to vote and hard to cheat.\par \par \uc2 And, look, she's spinning that narrative, Shannon. I mean, we just had record turnout in our primary, not only in the Republican primary, but also in the Democratic primary. And this is the same crowd that caused us the all-star game, saying that the new law that we passed to protect the integrity of vote was suppressive and Jim Crow 2.0, and we've had record turnout.\par \par \uc2 And even though we lost the all-star game and Major League Baseball caved to her and that narrative, thankfully, the Braves won the World Series. Hopefully, we're going to beat the Mets tonight, win the division, and then repeat.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. By the way, since you know brought up the Braves, I want to ask you about that. They were just at the White House. There's been a lot of talk about them potentially changing their name.\par \par \uc2 An "AP" article that was written, it's sort of an opinion piece during the World Series last year, where you guys were successful, says: pull the plug on it. It says: Simply put, the Braves and their co-conspirator are on the wrong side of history, not unlike those who continue to defend the Confederate flag and statutes as nothing more than peaceful symbols of Southern heritage.\par \par \uc2 Is it time to have a more in-depth conversation about changing the name?\par \par \uc2 KEMP: Well, I thought that was absolutely ridiculous for the White House to do that when they were honoring the Braves' World Series.\par \par \uc2 Listen, the Braves respect and honor Native Americans. We've had meetings in the governor's office about that. I think, again, this is just the woke cancel culture, and really national values that are being tried to be pushed down to our state and other states around the country, which is why we've got to stand up and fight for our values are in the state of Georgia. And that's why this race is so important.\par \par \uc2 You know, do you want to have a state that's going the way of the country right now, and 40-year high inflation, bad domestic energy policy that's created a crisis at the gas pump, a crisis at the border, where every governor in the country is dealing with runaway fentanyl coming into their states? And that's what I've been fighting against and I'm going to continue to do that.\par \par \uc2 You know, we've been try to help Georgians fight through 40-year high inflation by doing tax rebates and cutting taxes, suspending the gas tax. And we want to do that in the future. We're going to do the same thing next year. Another tax rebate, but also do a one-time property tax relief grant to help Georgians fight through, you know, really, is a Biden-Abrams agenda in Washington, and we don't want that coming to our state.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, let's talk about some areas where critics think you're not doing so well. The issue of crime is one where voters do give Republicans an advantage. They trust you to handle this better.\par \par \uc2 But here is what your opponent said when she joined us here back in June about how you're handling crime in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D), GEORGIA GOVENOR CANDIDATE: Let's be clear, in the state of Georgia, violent crime went up 55 percent in 2019 to 2020 under \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Why should voters choose you if crime is up on your watch?\par \par \uc2 KEMP: Well, for one, unlike \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , I'm not going to defund the police. She said that. She believes it. She can spin that however she wants to now that she's in a political race again.\par \par \uc2 But those are the facts. We passed legislation to keep rogue local governments from doing that. She wants to end cash bail and have get out of jail free.\par \par \uc2 While she's saying all these things over a year ago when she thought it was the right to say because the political winds were blowing that way, I created a crime suppression unit. To date, we've arrested over 600 people that had outstanding warrants, 29 of them for murder.\par \par \uc2 She should be talking to local officials in charge of local police departments, especially in the past, and wondering, why are they not doing more?\par \par \uc2 Thankfully, now, we have the locals in the fight with us in Atlanta. We're doing a job that the state's not even supposed to do because our citizens deserve it.\par \par \uc2 And I think there's a clear contrast on crime. People do not believe what \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  is trying to sell them right now. And they certainly know what my record is.\par \par \uc2 If you want to know about that, ask your sheriff, ask your local prosecutor, ask members of the state patrol that you happen to see on the street or the \uc2 Georgia Bureau of Investigation\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We're going after street gangs. We're going after drugs, human trafficking, and other things in our state, and I'm going to continue to do that as long as it's needed.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Is it working?\par \par \uc2 KEMP: Absolutely, it's working. I mean, look, taking 29 people in a little a year over the streets that had outstanding murder warrants -- I mean, nobody else was chasing these people and arrest them in the city of Atlanta over the last two years.\par \par \uc2 But thankfully, we are. And now, we have the \uc2 Atlanta police department\uc2 . They've been great partners under the new administration, going after these -- these criminals, dangerous criminals.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Let's turn to another issue, where the two sides don't agree either. This is abortion.\par \par \uc2 You've got a ban on most abortions after six weeks. This is so-called heartbeat bill.\par \par \uc2 Here's what \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  says about that: There's no such thing as a heartbeat at six weeks. It's a manufactured sound designed to convince people that men have the right to take control of a woman's body away from her.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " said the sound that expectant mothers hear during a scan is created by the machine itself, which translates the waves of electrical activity into something audible.\par \par \uc2 Now, a majority of Georgia voters, it shows in recent poling, they are not for this ban.\par \par \uc2 Is it based on a mischaracterization of what's actually happening at that point in pregnancy?\par \par \uc2 KEMP: Well, I think what \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  says is absolutely ridiculous. As a father of three daughters, Marty and I both have seen heartbeats. You can take the volume off the machine, or whatever, but you can see the heartbeat. I know that. I've seen it myself, and I think the medical science is very clear on that.\par \par \uc2 The issue with \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  is you don't know where she is on this issue. You know, we passed the heartbeat legislation three years ago, Shannon. My position on this has been very clear.\par \par \uc2 But also, I value life in many other ways, a lot -- a lot of Georgians do agree on. We've done adoption reform, foster care reform. Marty Kemp and the general assembly in Georgia and our team is leading the country to end human trafficking, go after the perpetrators, but also to support the victims.\par \par \uc2 You never hear \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  talk about this -- those issues. You know, she was pro-life. Then she was for late-term abortion. And on your show, she wouldn't answer the question of when it would be inappropriate for someone to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well --\par \par \uc2 KEMP: And then after that, when she figured the polling was bad, she changed her position again.\par \par \uc2 So even if Georgians don't like my position on this issue, they at least know I've been truthful with them.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 KEMP: And they can't say the same for \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, we have invited her on the show and hopefully, we'll have a chance to ask her those questions and follow up as well.\par \par \uc2 I want to touch on something quickly. "The Atlanta Journal Constitution" reporting that former President Trump may hold a rally there in Georgia. And they count out what they see as potential risk and downside for you, if he does.\par \par \uc2 Will you appear with him at a rally in Georgia?\par \par \uc2 KEMP: Well, I'm not too worried about what the "Atlanta Journal Constitution" is speculating on. They've been no friend of mine.\par \par \uc2 I'm focused to speaking hard-working Georgians, making sure that they know I will continue to fight for them every single day, to fight through 40- year high inflation, a disaster at the gas pump, a disaster at the border, and keep our state moving in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 We got the lowest unemployment rate in the history of the state. We've had two record years in a row for economic development. No matter what your zip code is, you have the opportunity for economic prosperity.\par \par \uc2 That is what I'm focused on. I can't control a lot of things that any newspaper is saying.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, but, if the president, former president, shows up, would you campaign with him? Would you go to a rally? I know you guys have sort of a frenemy thing going on. But would it be a benefit to you or do you think a distraction to show up with him on stage?\par \par \uc2 KEMP: Well, look, that's not anything I can control. I'm focused on what I can control in this race.\par \par \uc2 I've asked all Republicans to unite and support our ticket. And that's certainly what I'm doing and I'm sure that he is as well because he knows, like all other Republicans and people in the middle, that the focus needs to be on this president, and what he has done that is hurting hard-working Georgians.\par \par \uc2 I mean, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said he would never raise taxes on working Americans and working Georgians. And he's now done that. At the same time, spending more money when we have 40-year high inflation.\par \par \uc2 That's what people need to be focused on. And don't get distracted by the arguments that \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 KEMP: -- and other Democrats around the country are making, because they don't want to talk about those issues.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right. Governor, we will watch and see if that rally happens, if you attend. We thank you for your time today and hope to have the same conversation with \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  very soon. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 KEMP: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, the deepening mystery over attacks on the Nord Stream pipelines. We'll bring in our Sunday group on the unfolding blame game and on Russia's frightening new rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): When people's lives and their property at risk like this, you know, we all need to work together regardless of party lines. And it's my sense that the administration, you know, wants to help.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I've spoken to Governor DeSantis on multiple occasions, as well as -- this morning, as well as mayors and county officials, both Republican and Democrat, from places most affected.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: President Biden and Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  on how they are working together to respond to Hurricane Ian, as political junkies read between the lines for hints of a potential 2024 match-up.\par \par \uc2 Time now for our Sunday group. "Politico" congressional reporter Olivia Beavers, former \uc2 RNC\uc2  communications director Doug Heye, former DNC communications director Mo Elleithee, and FOX News congressional correspondent Chad Pergram.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to all of you.\par \par \uc2 So, we keep having these remembrances this week of -- in the wake of Superstorm Sandy, Governor Christie walking with then, you know, President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , and a lot of political machinations over how that exactly went down.\par \par \uc2 So, now, people are talking about this latest matchup. And "The Wall Street Journal" opinion says: Ian will require deft handling of Florida's disaster capabilities and the media if DeSantis is going to emerge with his political viability intact. President Biden has to be careful not to appear to be playing politics with federal resources.\par \par \uc2 Doug?\par \par \uc2 DOUG HEYE, FORMER RNC COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR: Neither should play politics. You know, this is very difficult political work, but politically should be easy. Demonstrate you're a leader, show that you can walk and chew gum at the same time, and that you're doing the best for your voters, whether that's the country or that your particular state.\par \par \uc2 For \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , he should look at this as an opportunity -- be what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wasn't. Be Trump with substance. We know that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is a culture warrior, and he likes to get in those fights.\par \par \uc2 But what we're seeing right now is him demonstrating that he's a leader. He's a governor who is in charge. That's the right posture to be, not only politically, but doing what's right for your state.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And, Mo, this morning, we have the president tweeting, it's not a crisis in Florida, South Carolina, Puerto Rico, it's a U.S. crisis, signaling that unity.\par \par \uc2 MO ELLEITHEE, GEORGETOWN INSTITUTE OF POLITICS & PUBLIC SERVICE: Yeah. You've heard the president say this over the past few days as well, that, you know, making direct appeals to the people of Florida, that, you know, we are all with you. And I think that is exactly the right message for him to deliver. He'll be going down to Puerto Rico and Florida this week to survey the damage, to work with the local leaders. This actually is a great opportunity for our body politic if DeSantis and Biden appear together and are seen as working together.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and another area where there has been some bipartisan agreement has been the issue of Ukraine/Russia, what's happening there. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " talking about these latest sham votes, as most people are calling them, with the annexation of the Ukraine region. It says, the annexation move will effectively slam the door on any diplomatic resolution to the war in Ukraine. Military analysts are warning of a protracted, increasingly deadly conflict.\par \par \uc2 Olivia, where do we stand on how, you know, the two parties are responding to the issue of more aid, all the things that feed into this conversation?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: Well, the government just passed an interim government spending bill. And as you saw, there was $12 billion in aid. House Republicans largely voted against it and Senate Republicans were much more willing to pass it. But, largely, the parties are in support of Ukraine. And it's interesting, you know, even watching Congress sort of do this dance, right, you have Ukraine pushing to be admitted to \uc2 NATO\uc2 . And President Biden and Speaker Pelosi were both sort of saying, you know, we want to help them as much as possible, but they weren't really giving a yes as to whether they should be admitted because of Article 5, if you attack us, attack one person in \uc2 NATO\uc2 , you're attacking all of us.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and it seems like it's unlikely they would be fast-tracked given that it would immediately call \uc2 NATO\uc2  troops into the scene of this whole thing.\par \par \uc2 OK, so, talking about this on The Hill this week, which is Chad's specialty, the talk about the Nord Stream II explosions, the leaks there, Senator Menendez, Democrat, weighing in on who he thinks maybe be responsible.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BOB MENENDEZ (D-NJ): I think it's Putin's way of sending a message to the Europeans that I can affect you. I, you know, you're - you think you have the upper hand on me, but I can also affect you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The White House says it's still assessing, trying to figure out exactly who's responsible, Chad, but that issue playing out on The Hill as well.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Don't forget that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is always playing jujitsu or judo here. We have a midterm election coming up. In 1962, you had the Cuban missile crisis just before the midterm elections in 1962. Happened just before. Democrats only lost five seats in the House that year with John F. Kennedy. \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has been rattling the nuclear sabers here. Don't be surprised if that issue, the Nord Stream II, and the nuclear issue becomes a problem in October, just before the midterms, because that gets into the psyche of the American voter.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and we still have, you know, Russians fleeing the country. We have demonstrations there.\par \par \uc2 Mo, there are - there's continued saber-rattling by Putin about potentially using nuclear weapons, including, he says, to defend territory, which he would now define to include these new annexations.\par \par \uc2 ELLEITHEE: Well, and territory that just in the last day or two, after declaring it now part of Russia, we've seen Russian troops have to withdraw from because of the continued strength of the Ukrainian military.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think - I think Chad is right, that - that Putin is doing a lot of this in part because he feels like his back is against the wall, but in part because he does want to get into the psyche of the rest of the world. I don't think it's having the same impact that previous efforts to get into the psyche of the rest of the world have - have had. And you are seeing a more united front by the rest of the world, led by the United States. And - and - and that's adding to his anxiety, I think.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And one foreign policy issue that doesn't have a lot of unity, at least when it comes to specifically how it should be handled, is Iran. "The Atlantic" has a piece talking about the women taking to the streets, the thousands around the world who have been demonstrating against what's happened there and the death of this young woman, 22 years old. They call on the U.S. to do something in this piece, saying, the U.S., one step it could take, would be to suspend its participation in the talks to restore the nuclear deal. This is an historic opportunity for the two nations to forge a new bond if the U.S. chooses to choose Iranians in their hour of need.\par \par \uc2 Doug, this administration seems really bent on getting back to that deal and getting it done. A possibility that they use this as a bargaining chip?\par \par \uc2 HEYE: You know, it's such a prime directive for this administration to go back to the Obama years and cut that deal that it doesn't seem likely they'll do this. But I think everybody's reacted to this in the United States in the same way of what powerful images these are. And -- and, you know, as a man, I'd say, when you see change affected in the world, who leads it? It's usually women.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, the Iranian women have been very strong, literally risking their lives, and some of them losing them, in this case.\par \par \uc2 Well, panel, we're going to take a quick break here.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the issue that may be shifting momentum away from Democrats as we hit the final stretch before next month's midterms.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [09:38:28]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BETO O'ROURKE (D), TEXAS GOVERNOR CANDIDATE: Governor Abbott's grid failure is part of a pattern over these last eight years, warned about, for example, school violence and gun violence specifically against children, does nothing. Warned about problems within child protective services, our foster care program, does nothing.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): Beto continues to flip-flop on issue after issue after issue. He has flip-flopped on the border issue. He's flip-flopped on the energy issue, such as energy jobs and the green new deal. He's flip- flopped on defunding the police. Whether it's one issue or another, he keeps changing positions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Democrat and Texas Gubernatorial Candidate \uc2 Beto O'Rourke\uc2  and Republican Texas Governor Greg Abbott sparring over each other's records in a debate Friday night that was pretty civil. It is one of a huge roster of big races we are watching as we close in on the midterms.\par \par \uc2 And we are back now with the panel and we've got all kinds of polls.\par \par \uc2 Chad, I want to start with you for Arizona.\par \par \uc2 The current senator, Mark Kelly, is leading \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  by five points. There is a governor's race there that is too close to call at this point.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of that particular race?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The one thing that we're looking at going into the midterms here is, will we see ticket splitting? In other words, they vote one way for governor, one way for Senate. That might be the case in Arizona, certainly with Mark Kelly. He's a moderate Democrat. He's voted that way. He's been pretty consistent throughout that.\par \par \uc2 The other factor that people have been ignoring going into this, don't forget that this is the first election that we have had, first test drive, after the January riot last year. We're going to have a lot of candidates on the Republican side questioning the validity of the election. Do voters have confidence? How long does it take to sort this all out? Is it late November before we know control of the Senate, the House of Representatives? And don't forget that if you don't get to 50.1 percent in Georgia, Shannon -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: You determine it with a runoff in early January the next year. So, again --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: December, right?\par \par \uc2 ELLEITHEE: December.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: December.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: December. December, yes.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Last - last time it was January, right, right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right, because it was such a -\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But it takes a while.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But most -- most of those Senate races have always gone to that runoff.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh. Oh. Oh, boy. That's a headache that we'll plan for accordingly.\par \par \uc2 OK, so you talk about votes plenty, so let's talk about Pennsylvania because the Senate race there, Fetterman continues to lead Oz by four points. It's getting - it's an even broader split there for Josh Shapiro and \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  in the governor's race. He's pulling away too, 11 points in our latest polls. So, that's one where it seems like Democrats are actually unified.\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: Well, it's interesting watching how these different candidates are playing. I mean if you look at the Senate race, right, there's going to be a debate, I think, on October 25th. And that's when we're really going to see if all of these attacks that you've seen about, you know, Dr. Oz being a carpetbagger, whether Fetterman has the health and he can perform. We're going to really be seeing that up close.\par \par \uc2 In your polls that you showed, the attacks about Dr. Oz being a carpetbagger actually seem like they're losing steam. And some of the attacks that they're using on Fetterman are about crime. And whether those continue to go into November are going to be really interesting because the races are getting tighter.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They are, as they often do getting closer.\par \par \uc2 OK, so let's talk about Georgia, which will maybe be more like Arizona as far as splitting. The Georgia Senate race there, \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is leading \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in our latest by four points. But, \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , the governor that we had on a little bit earlier, he's leading by 7 points.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of that mixed vote in Georgia, Doug?\par \par \uc2 HEYE: A lot of questions about \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and whether or not he's a good enough candidate to win. And, you know, it's very interesting, typically when we talk about campaigns, we don't talk about Senate debates as playing a real roll in the race. Most voters don't watch them in their own states, much less nationally. So, in the Pennsylvania race, in the Georgia race, these are make or break moments for these candidates.\par \par \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is - is a great debater. You know, he's a minister. He's good on a pulpit. He's good on a stump. We don't know about \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  yet. He needs to have a strong night for him to be able to bring this home. And it ultimately, sorry to use the analogy, but take his campaign into the end zone.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, boy. Well, you know someone's going to go there, Doug, you had to do it.\par \par \uc2 OK, \uc2 Yahoo\uc2 ! News talks about this on Friday, that this shift that we're seeing, as the race and the polls tighten, they say inflation is shifting the midterm momentum back to the GOP. What do you make of that, Mo, because it is ticking up again.\par \par \uc2 ELLEITHEE: Yes, look, I -- there's so many factors in this election. That's what makes this particular midterm so unique, right? We -- the political landscape should not even keep Democrats in the game. But because of a number of factors, including candidate quality, some of the candidates that Chad referenced earlier, people like \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , people like Mastriano in Pennsylvania, or Oz in Pennsylvania, Republicans hit a little bit of a wall.\par \par \uc2 Add to that the Supreme Court ruling on abortion, and suddenly you see Democrats motivated much higher than they were before. And independents, who may have been freaking out about inflation and crime, now finding something else to be freaking out about on the other side of the ledger.\par \par \uc2 So, there is no clean precedent to look at to describe how this midterm is going to turn out. Whether or not the momentum is shifting back in the Republican direction late in the cycle, that's important. You want to have momentum toward the end. Will it be fast enough in some of these races is my question because, remember, in some states, the election has already started.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Oh, yes, they're already voting.\par \par \uc2 ELLEITHEE: Voting has already started.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELLEITHEE: And so Democrats, I think, are --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Pre-debates in a lot of these cases.\par \par \uc2 ELLEITHEE: Are - in - in a lot of these cases, and I think a lot of Democrat candidates are counting on that momentum they were catching late summer/early fall, having already paid off in some of these early votes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Chad, give us some context here because we always hear that the party in power, the president, regardless of party, that first midterm is never a good one for him and for his party.\par \par \uc2 There has been talk, earlier in the year, there would be this red wave and they would have this huge advantage, the GOP, in the House. That -- those predictions are tightening some.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The question is whether or not the Democrats peaked too early. Maybe they peaked in August, first of September. The other thing is, when you talk to political scientists, they don't know what this electorate looks like. Determining the universe of voters is very hard to gauge right now.\par \par \uc2 And the other factor, as you look at this, look at Florida. You know, Republicans are on mark to win about four seats here just in Florida alone. You've had a big weather event there. Do those people show up to vote? Do they get their mail-in ballot, whatever. You know, those are important factors. As I always say, you know, and \uc2 Harold Macmillan\uc2 , the British prime minister, said this, events are the most important factor in politics. In this case, it might be a weather event.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Quick final word, Olivia.\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: Well, you know, there's an interesting thing looking at natural disasters, that - that he was just talking about, which is the immediate and then the afterlife. And so whether they handle it well right now, and it comes back to bite them later on, that's what we're watching to see as well.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, panel, thank you very much. We will see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Up next, border encounters along the U.S. southern border will top 2 million this fiscal year, a record. And a major crisis for the administration. We're going to sit down with the Fox correspondent who has led our coverage along the border and has been pressing leaders here in D.C. when he showed up this week.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It is the other issue that dominates headlines in the south, immigration. Security along the U.S./Mexico border remains a major but often under-reported challenge for the White House. It is also undeniably a political vulnerability for Democrats with Republicans seizing on the issue in midterms.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now Fox correspondent Bill Melugin, who has been supporting and reporting nonstop from the border since nearly the start of the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's great to have you here actually in Washington.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: It's good to be here in person.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so you've been chasing some folks around trying to get some answers this week.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You had a question with the House speaker, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . Hear how -- here's how that went.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Kevin McCarthy told us he essentially feels that you are holding House Democrats hostage from speaking out about the border crisis because of the way you whip on the House floor. How would you respond to that? And do you believe the border is secure?\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI (D-CA): Well, I believe that we have to have a secure border. And I think that we are trying to address -- the president is - has a plan to address that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: She says we've got to have a secure border, and the president has a plan, but how have Democrats reacted as you've press them on this issue?\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: It depends who we're talking to, right? A few of them won't even make eye contact with me when we are chasing them through the hallways. Others were pretty gracious with their time and talked to us.\par \par \uc2 But, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , right there, as you just mentioned, she says, yes, we have to have a secure border, and the president has a plan, and he's working on it. What is the plan? I mean we're almost two years in now, right, and the numbers just keep getting worse. The administration keeps breaking their own records, right? Last year we set the record with 1.7 million illegal crossings, only to be broken this year, fiscal year 2022. We're at 2.1 million illegal crossings, and we still have another month left to go.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, there were several Democrats who we approached and they wanted nothing to do with me or the topic of the border or immigration. Wouldn't make eye contact with me. Had nothing to say when we pressed them on it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And, to be fair, the White House has had something to say about your coverage. There has been some pushback on how you're covering things. How does it feel to sort of get scooped into the story sometimes?\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: I'm going to let the noise go above my head. They apparently don't like the drone. They say our coverage is alarmist. Well, what's happening down there is alarming. There have been more than 1,300 migrants who have died down there since the beginning of fiscal year 2021. There have been almost 4 million people who have crossed the border illegally. There have been 266,000 unaccompanied migrant children who have arrived at that border. Some of them we've seen drown in the river.\par \par \uc2 And just to put that in a visual for you, that's three Rose Bowls worth of unaccompanied children who have arrived at the border since President Biden took office. It's chaos down there. And it is alarmist. That's why we put the drone in the air, so you can see the amount of people coming across every single day. What's happening down there is out of control. People are dying every single day. And right now Border Patrol agents feel like there's just not really any light on the horizon because there's no change in policy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, we're asking them to do an incredibly, almost impossible job.\par \par \uc2 Now, one of the Democrats who did talk to you is Congressman \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , because he is a Democrat down there in Texas. He is seen this firsthand. Here's a bit of your exchange.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 HENRY CUELLAR\uc2  (D-TX): This is our administration, I'm a Democrat. And, you know, they need to understand, they own it now. They own it now and they have to take the steps to correct this, otherwise, you know, when are we going to see an end to this?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And one of the things that you talked to him about is the DHS secretary's response in public versus maybe what he's saying in private or how he feels about this. What can you tell us about that conversation?\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Some really interesting comments that \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  made. Essentially, he said he has had conversation with Secretary Mayorkas, where the secretary has told him, look, I know what's happening down there is out of control. It's getting worse. It's not OK. But there's not a whole lot I can do about it because immigration activists have kind of been in the White House and they are the ones calling the shots right now.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  essentially says, Mayorkas feels like he's got to kind of zip it and just - just deal with it as the White House moves forward with their plan. And remember, last year we got leaked audio of Mayorkas. He was talking to border patrol agents in McAllen, in private, behind closed doors. What did he tell them when he thought nobody was listening? We're losing. This is unsustainable. If the border is our first line of defense, we are going to lose.\par \par \uc2 The numbers have gotten worse since those comments, but he goes out to the American public and says, the border is secure, and he testifies under oath that we have operational security of the border. Those are two separate things.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Can you tell us about your conversation with Kevin McCarthy too, because Republicans, when they've been in charge, haven't solved this either. A quick comment about your conversation with him.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Yes, that's absolutely true. And Republicans have had control of Congress. They haven't pushed any immigration legislation that make any significant progress. Neither have the Democrats now.\par \par \uc2 I sat down with Kevin McCarthy on Friday and he essentially said he believes if \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  were out of the way right now, that they could work on bipartisan immigration with Democrats. He feels that there are Democrats on the House floor who know what's happening at the border is unacceptable, who want to work with Republicans on it, but they feel they're kind of held hostage, or told to be quiet, by \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . Don't speak out about it. He feels if she weren't there anymore, maybe things would be a little bit different. She denies that though.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and -- well, and -- of course.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And if Republicans take over, they're going to have to put up some action for all the conversations they've had about this.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Bill, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And, by the way, you can see more of Bill's exclusive reporting throughout the day tomorrow all over Fox News Channel.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we're just one day away from the start of a whirlwind Supreme Court term. I'll have a look at the cases that are going to dominate headlines this fall.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The Supreme Court kicks off its new term tomorrow, the first Monday in October, after a tumultuous year resulting from the court's overturning Roe vs. Wade. The court is poised to tackle more political decisive issues amid questions over the court's so-called legitimacy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Good morning, Mr. Chief Justice.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice over): A new Supreme Court term with its newest member. Colleagues welcoming Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , the court's 116th member. She's expected to be a strong, progressive voice but the court's 6- 3 conservative majority for now holds sway.\par \par \uc2 The upcoming docket is as consequential as the last, appeals over voting rights and congressional election boundaries that could impact 2024 races and beyond.\par \par \uc2 CROWD (chanting): This is what democracy looks like.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Workplace speech versus LGBTQ Plus rights. And a case involving a Colorado web designer contesting a state anti-discrimination law she says would force her to promote messages that goes against her constitutional rights, including her religious opposition to same-sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 LORIE SMITH, COLORADO WEB DESIGNER: It's important that our First Amendment rights for free speech are protected.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And perhaps the most closely-watched case, affirmative action. The high court poised to severely limit when colleges and universities can use race as part of the competitive admissions process in order, they say, to promote a diverse campus. But a coalition of Asian American students says the policies discriminate, holding them to a selectively higher standard than black and Hispanic students.\par \par \uc2 CALVIN YANG, HARVARD UNIVERSITY APPLICANT: I think the fact that I'm Asian American plays a pretty big factor into why I was rejected.\par \par \uc2 CROWD (chanting): We won't go back!\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The new term comes as many Americans are looking back at the last term, when the court overturned the five-decade Roe vs. Wade precedent, returning the issue back to the states to decide access to abortion.\par \par \uc2 Our new Fox News poll found 63 percent of registered voters disapprove of the ruling. Just 32 percent approve. And when it comes to the Supreme Court's job performance, a majority disapprove, 52 percent, a complete reversal from just five years ago.\par \par \uc2 All this leading to concerns, especially from those on the political left, that the court is gripped in a crisis of confidence.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , CHIEF JUSTICE OF THE UNITED STATES: Simply because people disagree with an opinion is not a basis for questioning the legitimacy of the court.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Conservative Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , in a rare statement to "The Wall Street Journal," defending the court's rulings, however controversial. Quote, saying or implying that the court is becoming an illegitimate institution or questioning our integrity crosses an important line.\par \par \uc2 But some on the bench question whether it's hot button rulings are hurting its reputation.\par \par \uc2 ELENA KAGAN\uc2 , SUPREME COURT JUSTICE: When courts become extensions of the political process, when people see them as extensions of the political process, that's when there's a problem.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And we await word on the court's ongoing internal investigation into who leaked a draft of the abortion ruling to the media, an unprecedented breach of court protocol. Several justices have hinted the leaker remains unidentified, but that an update could be coming soon.\par \par \uc2 That is it for today. Thank you for joining us. I'm Shannon Bream. Have a great week. We will see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected