r audience by telling the truth about that.\par \par \uc2 Look, you're a friend of mine, I love talking politics, I love talking sports with you. I'm happy for all your success, congrats on the podcast.\par \par \uc2 Two of -- this is one gifted talented quarterback. So we saw what happened last night. We saw what happened a week before. Really scared me is the neurological response of his fingers.\par \par \uc2 Now, they're saying they followed all the protocols. He just got hit hard.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Listen, listen, they could say they followed all the protocols and nobody's in a position obviously to dispute that based on what transpired last night. He had to be carted off the field, and take it to a hospital. I think there's a huge debate as to whether or not he should have been released from the hospital, number one. And number two, put on a team, playing the fly back home.\par \par \uc2 You've had a bag of potato chips in your hand, Sean, when you're on a plane and what have you, and you just see how it's ready to blow up because of the pressure. That's what you're feeling a lot of times when you're up -- you know, you're up in the air, 30,000, 35,000 feet. You can have congestion or whatever the case may be. For the fact that he was allowed to fly the plane last night, I don't like that.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: No doctor would let him go if they felt if he was in any jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: OK, but I'm speaking to intent. I'm not questioning anybody's intent. I'm just wondering whether or not they made the right decision. But the real issue was what transpired Sunday against Buffalo, the fact that he got up after getting hit and then practically collapsed back onto the ground, and that at ultimately, he was allowed to come back out on the field in the second half.\par \par \uc2 That's what has everyone in the \uc2 NFL\uc2  community in an uproar right now, with the proverbial eyebrows raised because how is he allowed to come back and play. And then four days later, you're playing in another game. Usually, if you got to go into any kind of concussion protocol, it's take about six to seven days.\par \par \uc2 Now, they said it was his back. It wasn't a concussion. But I can assure you as a guy that works with football players, present and former, all day long, virtually none of them believed the game against Buffalo Sunday was not a concussion.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Look, I hope for a speedy recovery, a gifted talented quarterback.\par \par \uc2 All right. Now, I'm going to move into an area that we privately for years now have had going on. I'm going to bring it public. I make the contention, you take any sport, let's start with football.\par \par \uc2 You take -- you have people in the stands, all backgrounds all races socio- economic differences, all the -- all in the stadium and they all share one passion the sport that they're watching. They share a passion for a home team.\par \par \uc2 You know, I don't know about you, but I find myself high-fiving strangers, buying beers with people sitting next to me in front of me, behind me, whatever. You know, get along with everybody.\par \par \uc2 And then you throw in this mess called politics in sports. I'm against it. We stand for the national anthem. We start the game, end of the game, I love when the football players get into circles, both teams pray, sometimes the refs pray. Let's keep politics out of sports to the extent we can.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I get -- I know it's your show. Do I get to respond now? Because let me tell your audience what I told you when you brought that up, specifically when you brought up Colin Kaepernick. I told you to stop whining, okay? You're my buddy, I told you to stop whining, okay?\par \par \uc2 Maybe old Mark Levin, we know, the great one, I told him the same thing. OK?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Strangers high fiving.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Don't lie to