\par \uc2 Also \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has officially lost his only economic talking point ahead of the midterms. We're going to tell you what it is, so stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: As Hurricane Ian barrels up the coast causing more destruction, we take a look at what it's left in its path. Namely, devastation to Southwest Florida, as the Sunshine State enters a second full night of search and rescue efforts, we take you to Naples where we find our own Max Gorden.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAX GORDEN, FOX WEATHER CORRESPONDENT: Standing here on Fort Myers river front you can see the power of Hurricane Ian. Boats strewn about like they were toys. The wind rushed in. The storm surge tossed these boats all over the place. And for those who decided to hunker down and wait out the storm, a harrowing experience.\par \par \uc2 But now the helpers have arrived. The Cajun Navy is here and they say they've conducted more than 50 boat rescues all over the Fort Myers area. They've also delivered hundreds of meals to people. Here's what they say that experience has been like.\par \par \uc2 JAY CARTER, INCIDENT COMMANDER, CAJUN NAVY: We are currently in rescue mode. We're trying to rescue. There's still people out there. It has changed from so much of a life safety kind of rescue into just people can't get to supplies food, water, medical, things like that. So we have crews that are combined with rescuers and med units so they can kind of take care of a portion of that medical to get them safe.\par \par \uc2 GORDEN: Those of the Cajun Navy say, there's a big need for food and water donations here in the Fort Myers area. They'd love it if folks would drive down with those donations or you can also donate online at gocajunnavy.org. They're a great organization. They always help lots of people in natural disasters. All these folks come with boats of their own, and they are here with the goodness of their hearts, all here to help. In Fort Myers, Florida, Max Gorden Fox News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The Biden administration had one economic talking point going into the midterms, and it was that gas prices were falling from the record highs. OK, never mind the fact that the Biden Administration's policies contributed directly to that jump in prices.\par \par \uc2 But last week, reality struck and gas prices, of course shot up again, with some analysts now suggesting that $5 gas is right around the corner. But instead of being honest about all of this, Biden did what Biden does best, pass the buck.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This small, temporary storm impact on oil production provides no excuse - no excuse for price increases at the pump. None.\par \par \uc2 Say again to the oil and gas executives, do not, do not, do not use this storm as an excuse to raise gasoline prices or gouge the American public. The price of oil has dropped in recent weeks. The price of gas should be going down as rapidly it's not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh my god, he doesn't know anything about how oil is priced globally, or here in the United States. Zero. Or he's pretending not to. But here's the truth that Biden can't or won't admit.\par \par \uc2 Now back in June, his administration surrendered to the greeniacs over a key permit involving the Whiting Refinery in Indiana. Now, at one time, this was one of the biggest oil refineries in the nation, but now operations there are being slowed significantly, so the \uc2 EPA\uc2  can monitor certain refining equipment. OK.\par \par \uc2 Well, things get technical, but suffice it to say, there are many hidden ways that the enviro-freaks who are actually running Biden's \uc2 EPA\uc2 , they're making it harder for energy producers. Joining me now is Jennifer-Ruth Green, the Air Force vet running as a Republican for Congress in the district where this Whiting Refinery is located. Jennifer Ruth, thank you for being with us. How big a deal is this refinery among people of this district?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER-RUTH GREEN (R) AIR FORCE COMBAT VETERAN: Yes, ma'am. Thank you so much for having me. You know, the Whiting Refinery is a huge deal. So many jobs. It's incredibly large. And so many people work there. And obviously, energy production is important for us. Steel production is important for us. And these are areas where the current administration continues to fail our people day after day after day, with a lack of accountability and a lack of responsibility. And we're done with it.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Well, the fact that President Biden is still trying to like do this song and dance routine on that gas station owners or oil companies are determining the price of the oil, which is a total misapprehension of how oil is priced. It's a well-known phenomenon, but apparently, they're either pretending they don't understand it, or purposely misrepresenting it to pass the buck.\par \par \uc2 But this refinery is a prime example of how they get mucked up in the details of an operation, and then with all these regulatory issues, just grind the operations almost to a halt in some cases.\par \par \uc2 GREEN: No, you're absolutely right. So we see a couple of things consistently, passing the buck from the administration, consistently making it someone else's fault, President Biden, Congressman Mrvan, it's exactly what they continue to do over and over again. But also a lack of care and concern for the people that live here and work here.\par \par \uc2 You know, I'm no expert on fuel oil, gas energy prices, but what I can tell you is the fact that if we continue to break ourself for this Green New Deal with a clear disregard for the impact that is coming to our everyday people, we will break our people. And it is important for us as public servants to make sure our people are taking care of.\par \par \uc2 Congressman Mrvan and President Biden--\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: According to Cook Political, Indiana district 1, your district, is a tossup now. Biden won it by nine points. So we're up against Mrvan here. What does this tell you now about what the voters are seeing in this economy, crime and now energy prices, food prices through the roof?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Yes, ma'am. We are fired up, because people here in Northwest Indiana, Hoosiers, believe that they deserve better and that's what I want to bring to them. You know, as these gas prices go up, as these grocery prices go up, as Congressman Mrvan continues to spend our grandkids' money, he just focuses on the wrong things. And he's completely gone Washington.\par \par \uc2 What he consistently does beyond obfuscating, beyond passing the buck, beyond claiming that he's providing lower tax options for people is consistently focus on the rich and the elites.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: He's saying you're going to advocate to implement a federal abortion ban. I'm not sure that's what Indianans are caring about right now. But your response to that?\par \par \uc2 GREEN: Congressman Mrvan is being completely dishonest. I am pro-life. I'm going to protect life. But you know what? Congressman Mrvan's position on abortion that he voted for is that he would abort any baby for any time for any reason at taxpayer expense. That's extreme. I will protect babies. The majority of people want reasonable exceptions. People want to understand that life can be protected. And I want to save as many babies as possible and I'm committed to doing that. He is extreme. He's out of touch.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The Democrats are so off the deep end on the abortion issue, but it's their Hail Mary in this case. Jennifer-Ruth, we are following this race very closely. I have a good feeling about it. Everyone get out there and vote in Indiana district one. Jennifer-Ruth Green, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 The media are rushing to justify Biden's lapses, plus \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's goes adult. Raymond Arroyo explains it all. Friday Follies, that is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MOLLY LINE, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: You're watching FOX News Channel. This is a FOX weather alert. I'm Molly Line in Charleston, South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 The storm Ian has moved off into the inland areas now. The rain has stopped, but we are still getting some gusts of winds and the tens of thousands of people remain without power. But the sentiment was expressed to me by more than one person that Charleston missed a bullet in this storm.\par \par \uc2 There was some flooding in town. We had a chance to get in there and assess the damage, as did many of the people in this community out there waiting around in the floodwaters, which have largely receded. But to the north of this city, between here and Myrtle Beach much harder hit, particularly Pawleys Island, where there were some deep water rescues today with high water equipment. The fire department moving in to help, and even the pier was washed away.\par \par \uc2 You're watching FOX News Channel. I'm Molly Line. Now back to Laura Ingraham.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: It's Friday and that means it's time for Friday Follies. And for that we turn to FOX News contributor Raymond Arroyo. All right, Ray, there is a lot of controversy over the president searching for a congresswoman whom he knew was dead at an event this week.\par \par \uc2 RAYMOND ARROYO, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: God rest her soul, Congressman -- Congresswoman, rather, Jackie Walorski died in a car accident this summer, which Biden knew since he issued a statement on her passing at the time. But then the other day he did this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I want to thank all of you here, including bipartisan elected officials, Representative Jackie -- are you here? Where's Jackie. I thought she was going to be here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I was originally going to skip this story, Laura, because it isn't the first time Biden has embarrassed himself by trying to do something impossible at a public event. Remember back in 2008 when he called on paraplegic State Senator Chuck Graham?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Chuck Graham, state Senator is here. Chuck, stand-up, Chuck. Let them see you. Oh, God love you, what am I talking about? I tell you what, you're making everybody else stand up, though, pal.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Now "USA Today," Laura, is swooping to defend Biden's search for dead people. You saw what Jill Lorenz wrote in the "USA Today."\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Yes, she wrote that the mistake demonstrated a generosity and professionalism.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: You need only look back a couple of years to remember that some things are more important than age and a perfect memory. I'm sure China is happy to hear that.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: And Russia and everyone else. I'm sorry, but this speaks to the president's ability to carry out the duties of his office, Laura. If he can't remember basic things or write condolences about the Queen without a cheat sheet, it demands scrutiny. Look, I'm waiting for one of these people to say that his wandering after that Rose Garden event the other day was just Biden showing concern for plant life and his green agenda.\par \par \uc2 But Laura, I know what you are thinking, but stop it. "The View" ladies think it may have been a Biden brain fart.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We all have, when you think about, you grow up you use the term "brain fart." I don't know a human who hasn't sat in a room and gone to say something and was like -- oh, my gosh. I think that was very apparent what this gaffe was.\par \par \uc2 SUNNY HOSTIN, CO-HOST, "THE VIEW": I think what it really shows is that this country is so ageist. This country has a problem with age.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Is this unbelievable, Laura? Now it's ageist to question someone's cognitive ability and recall. You can't manage the affairs of state if you can't remember where you are, how to get off stage, or the name of someone who is deceased. I'm sorry, it's just rudimentary. I don't care what your party is.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Is Sonny Hostin going to go, if she has to get some important surgery, is someone in her family going to go to a 95-year-old surgeon or the 40-year-old surgeon who has just had triple board certified?\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Stop being ageist. You are being ageist, Ingraham.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, over at "Good Morning America," they are not worried about ageism, though, but they are worried about a cultural appropriation of Hailey Bieber's makeup routine?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Social media users calling out the super model claiming she is culturally appropriating the makeup routine, using dark lip liner and lip gloss.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It is a Latina and black woman lip combo.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Many pointing out that the lip style was accepted when a white woman did it, but not when a woman of color did.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA E. GARCIA: It is highly unlikely that a black or Latina woman would have seen success as her using their own trend. This is a systemic issue.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Now, Laura, wait a minute. So women of color who have worn lip liner for decades, no one accepted them? Stop the presses. Hailey Bieber apparently is now culturally appropriating brown lip liner. Why is GMA wasting time on this, Laura? And why did they miss Gwyneth Paltrow this week culturally appropriate in Fort Knox? You've heard of Goldfinger. This is goop-finger. And GMA didn't do any story on Cardi B who culturally appropriated Marilyn Monroe's hairstyle. Take a look. And what about Harry Styles culturally appropriating Scarlett O'Hara. Southern belles everywhere were very upset, and nobody cares. Where are these breaking news stories, Laura?\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I was trying to think of Lizzo with the flute at the archives, pied piper. I don't know what she was. She was just playing the flute. Let's just say everybody is guilty of everything, and so we can move on to the next topic.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: But Laura, you know what is so cruel about this, though? It's dividing people. Cultural exchange is all about races and nationalities sharing their customs and blending it. Jazz wouldn't have happened without the African beat and the Italian tarantella and the German umpapa. That collusion and combination of cultures created something unique. We need to celebrate that and stop this division.\par \par \uc2 And before we go, I've got to tell you about \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's launching a new line of adult happy meals, Laura. I guess people just don't want to grow up. \uc2 McDonald's\uc2  is teaming with Cactus Plant Flea Market, it's a fashion brand, for these adult happy meals. Each comes with a big boy meal and a prize, a collection of disfigured classic \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's characters. Remember Grimace and the Hamburglar? Only for some -- yes, but now they have four eyes, Laura. I don't know why. Maybe it's from eating the processed food. I don't quite know why they have four --\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Those are scary. Those go right up to the category of puppets for me, Raymond, figurines. Any type of figurines, puppets, clowns, especially mimes, I don't like mines, and you are sending me into the weekend with that image of the Hamburglar. No can do. No one wants to grow up.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Childhood does end at some point, and we've got to get off the slides.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: All right, Sam is telling us to wrap.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I know. Have a good weekend.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: She doesn't want us to play. All right, have a good weekend.\par \par \uc2 What happens when dating apps go wrong? Oh, my goodness. Former host of "Couples Therapy" Dr. Jenn Mann joins me next to explain. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: In October 2019, Pew conducted the survey to understand Americans attitudes toward dating. Most told \uc2 Pew\uc2  that their romantic lives weren't going well, and three quarters said that it was difficult to find people to date. In fact, modern dating has become such an issue it's now the plotline for many movies.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What do you really doing online?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: There are 8 million people in this city, 4 million men. And then you have some age limits, now it's a million. Half of them are married, 10 percent are gay. I want someone college-educated, not too ugly, and I don't want kids. So yes, I'm on 10 different dating websites to increase my odds.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The author of a recent "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " piece entertains that question, saying "Internet dating promises an abundance of choice, filters, and low barriers to reaching out, but as I found when I talk to people that don't often translate to high-quality interactions. Instead, daters find themselves caught in a cycle of dead-end interactions." The author's solution? It's time to go retro. "From the mid-1940s until 2013, heterosexual Americans were most likely to meet their romantic partners through friends. Families were also big in the matchmaking business. Think of what this more traditional model solves." Interesting.\par \par \uc2 Joining to me now, Dr. Jenn Mann, one of the most well-known psychotherapists in the country, best known as the host and therapist for VH1's longstanding hit show "Couples Therapy," and the author of "The Relationship Fix." Dr. Mann, great to see you tonight.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENN MANN, PSYCHOTHERAPIST: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This column was fascinating. And I hear this from a lot of our young producers. What is the biggest issue with these dating apps? There are so many today.\par \par \uc2 MANN: I think that these dating apps create a sense that people are disposable, that a lot of these apps really encourage people to make huge life decisions about who they want to partner up with based on very superficial things. And we all know we've either had the experience or know someone who is married, happily married, that met their partner, initially wasn't super attracted to them, got to know them, fell in love with them, chemistry developed. And really these apps just encourage us to objectify each other and dispose each other based on how people look.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, what is interesting is, I think here, it could be that the tide might be turning away from more impersonal apps and filters to finding a dating pool that's a little bit more traditional. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you like using the apps?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Not at all.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How come?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I just don't think they are conducive to good interactions with people.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Do you feel like they are mostly just for hookups?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would say so, yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I just don't really think I'd meet anyone on there that I would actually date.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That you feel like they are kind of for hooking up only?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes. Yes. A lot of weird people on there.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: A lot of weird people, Dr. Jenn. For these young people who actually want to get married and have children, what's the answer today?\par \par \uc2 MANN: Look, I think of dating as a pie. Think of it as an apple pie. Each slice of that pie should be a different approach to dating. One might be matchmaking. One might be your church or synagogue or someplace where you attend some sort of services. Another might be meeting them through friends. Another might be asking people around you to actually play matchmaker even if they are not an official matchmaker. So we really need to view these dating apps as a small opportunity. And yes, sure, you can use them. They can be helpful. We all know plenty of people who met their match there. But to completely and only exclusively rely on them, you're missing out on so many other opportunities.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, another issue, Dr. Mann, was revealed in this \uc2 YouGov\uc2  poll from a couple years ago, and it found that more and more Americans are rejecting monogamy, with 32 percent of adults saying their ideal relationship is non-monogamous. That number jumps to 43 percent among millennials. OK, is this because of the dating apps? Or is there something else going on here?\par \par \uc2 MANN: I think that there is an emotional disconnect, and what I see with a lot of millennials is kind of a fear of getting emotionally close, getting emotionally vulnerable, fears of rejection. I don't think these apps help any, because you spend a lot of time on these dating apps and getting rejected over and over and over again. And it kind of is part of that pathway to a relationship that I think people want to avoid.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: I can tell you from my conversations with twentysomethings, what young women say is that the men are total immature, they're immature and infantilized, a lot of it relating COVID and the pandemic and the lockdowns, and that affected these men more than women. Really quickly, do you see any of that?\par \par \uc2 MANN: Look, I wrote a whole column in "InStyle" magazine about the man- child. And I don't think it's exclusive to young men in this country. I think that there are a lot of men out there who expect to be catered to in ways that are not healthy and not conducive to a mature, loving relationship. So I think that everybody needs to be accountable to everybody, and that we have to mature and behave like adults in out relationships.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Grow up. As my mother, Dr. Mann, we've got to roll, but as my mother would say, everyone has just got to grow up, take responsibility. Dr. Mann, great to see you, thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 MANN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  just admitted something we have known all along. The Last Bite explains.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: If you wanted to really know how the open borders crowd views immigration, well, you should look no further than an honest admission from the speaker the House herself. This happened today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 , (D-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: We have a shortage of workers in our country, and you see even in Florida some of the farmers and the growers saying why are you shipping these immigrants up north. We need them to pick the crops down here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is what they think of the migrants, that they are just working widgets. So maybe they should just take them from the border and just plop them into their fields? Is that what she's saying? Unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 I want to thank you for watching this week. We continue to pray for the safety and recovery of all the people of Florida and up and down the east coast tonight. And remember, it is America now and forever.\par \par \uc2 "GUTFELD!" is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020221001ei9u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Florida Begins Unprecedented Recovery; Ian Leaves Central Florida with Record Flooding; Liberal Media Use Hurricane to Advance Democrat Agenda; A Closer Look at the Power of Hurricane Ian; Biden Cracking Down on Political Opponents; Food Shortages and the War on Working People; Parts of Fort Myers Look Like War Zone\b0\par\par\uc2 Tucker Carlson, Steve Harrigan, Bryan Norcross, Will Nunley,  Christina Coleman, Robert Ray, Max Gorden \par \uc2 7,968 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: Tucker Carlson Tonight\par \uc2 TCARL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST, "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME": All right, well, I'm glad they didn't have a lot of solar panels in Florida because they would all have been underwater, they never would have been able to restore power.\par \par \uc2 Tucker is up next. I am Watters. This is my world.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2  , FOX NEWS HOST: This is a FOX News Alert: Search and rescue operations are underway this hour throughout the State of Florida. Hurricane Ian, as you know struck the southwestern part of that state yesterday as a Category 4 storm and caused catastrophic damage. Roughly three million Floridians no longer have power. Many of them don't have communications.\par \par \uc2 Entire communities were flattened. There is no official count as of right now, but it is clear this could be the deadliest storm in Florida's history.\par \par \uc2 Let's go now to FOX's Steve Harrigan, who is in Placida, Florida in Charlotte County in the southwestern part of the state.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARRIGAN, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: So, Claudia, what are you doing now?\par \par \uc2 CLAUDIA, RESIDENT: I'm getting important stuff, my paperwork, my husband's personal things, because I lost him two months ago and now, I've lost my only home. So, I am packing it up and taking it to my car.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: You're packing it up in the garbage can?\par \par \uc2 CLAUDIA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Have you gotten any help yet?\par \par \uc2 CLAUDIA: No. But that's okay. I'm strong. I can get it. I can get the important things.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Thanks, Claudia. God bless you.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDIA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: All right.\par \par \uc2 CLAUDIA: I'll be alright.\par \par \uc2 HARRIGAN: Yes, you'll be all right. So Claudia says she's strong and that's what people are relying on, at least in these first early hours here, their own strength. Some people have been asking us for water, food and of course, communication is a tough thing as well with all the cell phones down.\par \par \uc2 Right now really, its neighbor helping each other here until help from more than 35 States does arrive.\par \par \uc2 Tucker, back to you.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Our Steve Harrigan in Placida, Florida.\par \par \uc2 We're going to assess the path of the storm and the damage that it's wrought with hurricane expert, Bryan Norcross, who joins us now for the third night in a row.\par \par \uc2 Bryan, good to see you. Tell us the status of the storm now, if you would.\par \par \uc2 BRYAN NORCROSS, FOX WEATHER HURRICANE SPECIALIST: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, the storm is gathering strength. Again, this is going to be a big rainstorm and a flood event for South Carolina, then on up into the Piedmont in the mountains of North Carolina, and Virginia. So, there's much more of this storm to come. Phases three and four, so to speak, as the floodwaters are just going down in North Florida. So that's phase two.\par \par \uc2 And then we get down to the original disaster and the biggest part of this disaster, which is in southwest Florida. This was the storm at landfall. And what happens with storms is it depends on both the strength of the storm and the direction of the wind on who gets the tremendous storm surge, which of course does all that damage to the coast.\par \par \uc2 So, when we look at this map, the white areas here, those are the winds of over a hundred miles an hour, and then the yellow area out here is winds from 60 to 100 miles per hour. So, what we see that's the wind speeds and then we see the wind direction. And if you notice the wind direction at landfall at three o'clock in the afternoon, that was right into the Naples area.\par \par \uc2 So, the flooding in Naples came first because they happen to have the direction of those strong winds that were pushing the Gulf water in for downtown. Now looking close up here, here is Fort Myers, it is actually well up the Caloosahatchee River, and so just beginning to push water up the Caloosahatchee, when the center of the storm over here went up that way, that changed the orientation of the winds and that push more water up the Caloosahatchee and the water up there couldn't get out and then more came and more came and more came, so it took longer to flood in Fort Myers.\par \par \uc2 Looking farther south down here, you can see that those hundred mile an hour winds were pushing water into Fort Myers Beach all the way over the Barrier Island there and that's why that place is just devastated. It's going to take a long time before that's actually a workable community again.\par \par \uc2 Here on Sanibel, you get water from a bunch of different directions and this is where the Sanibel causeway here was knocked out, so there is just a lot going on here, and then just to the north, Gasparilla, we've got water coming the other way. All the areas around that eye, Tucker, took this ultra-hard hit from this Category 4 Hurricane.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Bryan Norcross, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 There is a fire in the picture from Sanibel there of course, that's a feature always of storms like this, fires breakout and some of the damage through the pictures you have seen is from burning, not from flooding. Interesting.\par \par \uc2 We're going to have more reporting all night from the State of Florida, including on this show.\par \par \uc2 But first a step back, so when something this awful happens, something that affects the lives of millions of your fellow Americans, decent people step back for a moment. It's a moment that demands reverence and silence to consider what we've just seen and then the practical questions.\par \par \uc2 People are suffering. How are they suffering? They don't have power. They don't have communications. How many homes were destroyed?\par \par \uc2 You ask questions like that, and you try to assess what this means and the human cost of it. The one thing you don't do is immediately jump forward to score some sort of cheap and sleazy political point from it. That used to be obvious.\par \par \uc2 But for the past several years, it's been clear it's no longer obvious, at least to one political party. You saw this happen after the forest fires in California two years ago. It happened after tornadoes killed people in Midwest last year, and it's happening again now.\par \par \uc2 Immediately on cable television and on the floor of the legislative bodies across this country, self-described experts are demanding that you accept responsibility for whatever natural disaster that has just occurred. It's your fault, including for Hurricane Ian. You did this because you didn't support giving \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  trillions of dollars to fight climate change. So, you caused it. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOM SATER, CNN METEOROLOGIST: Decades ago, it used to be one or two a season, it would happen. Now, with climate change and these extreme warm waters, it's almost, you know, it's happening all the time.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC REPORTS: This storm, in a way, is kind of bad news for the people that are still trying to deny climate change as a factor.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC, "THE 11TH HOUR": Florida Republicans deny climate change as a monster storm barrels towards the coast.\par \par \uc2 BILL WEIR, CNN CHIEF CLIMATE CORRESPONDENT: This will be a first-time test for how you adapt to these new stronger storms on a warmer planet as a result of climate change.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, "ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES," MSNBC: The threat exacerbated, of course, by climate change.\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: Charleston, like Miami, gets water that comes up on a good sunny day. That's climate change because water levels are rising.\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, "THE REID OUT," MSNBC: Our Earth is getting warmer and there is just no doubt, I think, left that it is feeding these beasts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Imagine taking a science lecture from someone as stupid as Stephanie Ruhle or Ali Velshi. So, on that level, it's ridiculous and it's also, as we just suggested, unseemly and anti-human. Of course, people are dying, so wait just a moment before putting forward your demands for more political power.\par \par \uc2 But it's also factually untrue. So, you just heard one hare hat say, "It used to be one or two hurricanes a season it would happen. Now, it's happening all the time," and you hear this all the time, and it's a way of terrifying you into handing politicians more power over your life, but the fact is, it's a lie. It's not even remotely true. I mean, it's not a close call.\par \par \uc2 There has been, as a factual matter, no increase in hurricane frequency in the continental United States from 1900 to 2020. So, that's 120 years. It's been recorded. We have the data. They're on your screen right now.\par \par \uc2 In fact, as Michael Shellenberger has pointed out, someone who has actually looked at the numbers, the number of landfalling hurricanes has dropped slightly over the past century. Why? We're not sure, but we can probably guess it has nothing to do with climate or your SUV.\par \par \uc2 We do know that in that same period, deaths of human beings from natural disasters such as hurricanes have dropped a lot by about 90 percent, and the death toll is forecast to drop even more as people respond to the changing environment. That's what people do.\par \par \uc2 Government scientists at the \uc2 NOAA\uc2  , even the professional alarmists at the IPCC, predict that hurricanes will become 25 percent less frequent throughout the 21st Century. Oh, and that makes sense because the current Atlantic hurricane season is the slowest one in a quarter-century.\par \par \uc2 So, none of that is to downplay or minimize in any way the savagery of Hurricane Ian or the sadness of what it's done to your fellow Americans. It's horrible, but it does raise the question, how exactly are you responsible for it?\par \par \uc2 Well, of course, you're not in any sense. You're not liable for that. You didn't do that. You're a normal person. You're grieving for the people who were just killed. You're not trying to score political points on their deaths.\par \par \uc2 But Don Lemon steps into the breach, the breach between fact and rhetoric with a brand-new theory. Don Lemon claims that storms may not have become more frequent, but they definitely become more "intense." Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DON LEMON, CNN ANCHOR: The science shows what the science shows. It's undeniable what is happening.\par \par \uc2 But listen, let's