eproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020220928ei9r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Florida Braces For Powerful Storm; Hurricane Hits Cuba And Heads Into Gulf Of Mexico; Florida West Coast Residents Stock Up For Massive Storm; Biden Contacts Florida Mayors But Not DeSantis; Senate Voting To Break Filibuster On Stripped Down Bill; Nord Stream Pipeline Leaks Spark Sabotage Fears; Tallahassee Prepares For Hurricane Ian; America Online Cofounder \uc2 Steve Case\uc2  Interviewed On His New Book About American Entrepreneurship; Abortion Major Political Issue In Arizona Partially Due To Uncertainty About Procedure's Current Legality; Polling Indicates Crime As Political Issue Favoring Republicans For Midterms; Licensing Reform Stripped From Senate Legislation Tied To Government Funding\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Adam Klotz, Phil Keating, Jacqui Heinrich, Chad  Pergram, Trey Yingst, Mitti Hicks, Alicia Acuna \par \uc2 7,999 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 BRIAN KILMEADE, HOST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL (voice-over): Let's go Brandon. Tulsa, Oklahoma, (INAUDIBLE). And Newark, New Jersey, with the foxhole (PH) starts December 2nd.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (voice-over): Oh.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): How does it feel that Ainsley has a book out, Doocy has a book out, and you don't have a book out?\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): He always has a book out.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: (INAUDIBLE), Brian.\par \par \uc2 All right, that's it for us. SPECIAL REPORT is up next with the Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): You haven't seen Brian's cookbook?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Not yet, have that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Really, really good. Thanks, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier. "BREAKING TONIGHT". We begin in Florida, bracing for what could be a catastrophic Category 4 Hurricane Ian. The storm is due to make landfall tomorrow but rain from the outer bands of Ian is already coming down in many places.\par \par \uc2 2-1/2 million people are under evacuation orders. The storm has already pounded Cuba as a Category 3, with sustained winds of 125 miles per hour.\par \par \uc2 We have" FOX TEAM COVERAGE" tonight. Mitti Hicks is in Tallahassee where residents are hoping they miss the worst of Ian.\par \par \uc2 Phil Keating is in New Tampa where that could be ground-zero for the storm.\par \par \uc2 But we began with Fox News Meteorologist Adam Klotz at the weather center in New York with the latest track. Good evening, Adam.\par \par \uc2 ADAM KLOTZ, FOX NEWS CHANNEL METEOROLOGIST (on camera): Hey, good evening, Bret. The storm continuing to update in the models and maybe moving it just a little bit. But nonetheless, a powerful storm currently a Category 3, lifting up into the Gulf of Mexico, approaching Florida. And actually some of those outer bands, as you said.\par \par \uc2 Now, stretching across all of the Florida Keys getting up into the Sunshine State. That red polygon there, that is a tornado warned storm. You typically see that on the north eastern side of these storms.\par \par \uc2 And we've got a tornado watch that's currently in place is going to linger eventually lift its way up to the northeast, we could see some of these more isolated tornadoes pop up.\par \par \uc2 We've got plenty of advisories now stretching across the entire state. And just in the last hour, the hurricane warnings have been updated. Now, stretching all the way up into Orlando as this track has slightly shifted maybe just a little bit to the south, and a little bit to the east.\par \par \uc2 I'll show you that here. And we're really looking at a timeframe of tomorrow evening, tomorrow afternoon in the evening hours, maybe into Tuesday morning. But by Thursday, or excuse me, Thursday morning. By Thursday evening, this storm is going to be in the center of the state.\par \par \uc2 The winds very wide with this. So, this is a forecast model. This particular one bringing it just to the south of Tampa, between Fort Myers in Tampa, and then lifting up towards Orlando, by the time you get going into Thursday. That's a lot of time for wind, for rain, and for storm surge.\par \par \uc2 Now, in these systems, the storm surge is always the worst just on the southern edge of where the storm will hit or on the south eastern edge to be precise.\par \par \uc2 So, where does that leave us here? Well, if this storm just tracks a little bit farther to the south, suddenly everything we're looking at here in the red. So, getting close to Naples, including Fort Myers, stretching to the north areas that could see 10 feet of storm surge which would be absolutely devastating.\par \par \uc2 On top of that, we're going to see at least a foot of rain in a lot of these areas. Water is going to be a major concern across a large portion of the state, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Adam, quickly, once it comes to landfall, do we have a sense of where it's going after that?\par \par \uc2 Well, we're thinking it's going to lift up towards Orlando. We just saw an extension getting all the way into the center of the state up towards Orlando, where you're going to see probably gusts of hurricane force winds.\par \par \uc2 It loses some of that moisture, but it's still going to be a very strong storm in the center of the state by Thursday.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Adam Klotz, thank you.\par \par \uc2 KLOTZ: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Tampa St. Petersburg area appears to be the bullseye, as you just saw for Hurricane Ian at this time. Officials are urging people to get out of its way, citing what could be a potentially deadly storm surge.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Phil Keating is in that area tonight. Good evening, Phil.\par \par \uc2 PHIL KEATING, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, this is exactly what state and local leaders wants to see. A beach town nearly empty now a ghost town from here on out, showing that many, if not, most of the 3 million Floridians up and down the coast ordered to evacuate are following that and heading inland to higher ground, and potentially saving their lives.\par \par \uc2 KEATING (voice-over): Hurricane Ian made landfall early this morning in western Cuba as a Category 3. Pummeling the island with heavy rains and wind, causing damage.\par \par \uc2 In Pass-a-Grille, a tiny beach town southwest of Tampa, the last day over hanging protective shutters and plywood is today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE MILLER\uc2 , BERKELEY BEACH CLUB: We're just trying to do what we can do. Do you know what I'm saying? All we can do is just get stuff in here, protect our building, and then go from there. Only God is in control of what happened with the storm.\par \par \uc2 KEATING: Last minute grocery store runs for food and water? Good luck. Shelves have been depleted in many cases as Hurricane hardened Floridians fully expect to lose power for days.\par \par \uc2 Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  says activated 5,000 National Guard troops some on load from out of state and is pre-deploying search and rescue teams as a 10 to 15 foot storm surge and flooding is forecast.\par \par \uc2 He is dedicating special attention to the state's significant elderly population.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): We do have emergency refills of maintenance prescriptions. Those are authorized for another 30 days. We do report today 100 percent of operating long term care facilities do have a generator on site.\par \par \uc2 KEATING: This could be the worst storm to slam Tampa Bay in 100 years. FEMA Administrator \uc2 Deanne Criswell\uc2  magnified the seriousness of the threat today from the White House.\par \par \uc2 DEANNE CRISWELL\uc2 , ADMINISTRATOR, FEDERAL EMERGENCY MANAGEMENT AGENCY: Thank you -- Tampa.\par \par \uc2 By the time it reaches the shores of Florida, the storm is going to slow down to approximately five miles per hour. And this is significant because what this means is that Floridians are going to experience the impacts from the storm for a very long time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KEATING (on camera): And as of one hour ago, Tampa International Airport shut down for the rest of the storm. And in the morning, the same thing is happening over in Orlando. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hey, Phil, a lot of people getting out of the way this thing?\par \par \uc2 KEATING: Yes, absolutely. I mean, look at the ghost town. People are definitely making the preparations. So, that's certainly a positive.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Phil, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden, meantime, says he and his administration are doing everything they can to help the areas that will be affected by Hurricane Ian when it comes to safety of the rest of the of America.\par \par \uc2 The president and his team are not talking too much about the crime crisis. White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has a report tonight live from the North Lawn.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you, Bret. Yes, the last time President Biden address crime at all was almost a month ago and the House passed a police funding bill last week. But there is no vote scheduled in the Senate, and the president is not out here calling for it.\par \par \uc2 Instead, he spent today talking about last month's bill, lowering health care costs, plus a few words on the hurricane.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I told each one of them my conversation separately. Whatever they need. I mean it sincerely. Whatever they need, contact me directly. And they know how to do that.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): President Biden called two democrat mayors and a moderate republican mayor who are in Hurricane Ian's path, but not Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who is openly feuded with the administration on issues from COVID to immigration.\par \par \uc2 Instead, Biden had his FEMA administrator pick up the phone.\par \par \uc2 CRISWELL: We do not bring politics into our ability to respond to these disasters. We are going to support whatever Governor DeSantis asks of us. My regional administrator is with the governor right now, making sure that we're understanding what the needs are.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The storms impact may spread well beyond the Florida coast. The nation strategic oil reserves intended for use in emergencies like hurricanes are already at their lowest level since 1984.\par \par \uc2 Since Biden began selling them to bring down the cost of gas, officials now don't know if there's enough supply to cover the rest of hurricane season.\par \par \uc2 CRISWELL: It all depends on what the impacts from the storm might bring. And so, we need to do those assessments after the storm passes.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Energy vulnerabilities also playing out in Europe. The White House offering support after investigators get to the bottom of underwater leaks in two Nord Stream pipelines running from Russia to Germany.\par \par \uc2 European leaders and experts pointing to evidence of sabotage amid Russia's war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 White House officials use the moment to push a green agenda.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: This just drives home the importance of our efforts to work together to get alternative gas supplies to Europe and to support efforts to reduce gas consumption and accelerate true energy independence by moving to clean energy economy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: So, not on the White House agenda. Any new mention of crime, even as Republicans lean into new polling that shows voters trust them more to handle it by 14 points.\par \par \uc2 Last word from the White House on this, yesterday, blamed the Trump administration for the rising crime that Biden inherited. Bret.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Jacqui Heinrich live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A libertarian group in California is filing a legal challenge to President Biden's plan for student debt cancellation. The Pacific Legal Foundation calls the proposal an illegal overreach that would increase state tax burdens for some Americans who get their debt forgiven. Is believed to be the first lawsuit targeting the plan. It was filed in Indiana, one of several states planning to tax any student debt cancelled by the president.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Stocks were mixed today. The Dow lost 126. The S&P 500 fell eight. The NASDAQ gained 27.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): "BREAKING TONIGHT", a very fluid situation right now on Capitol Hill. Senate Democrats have just withdrawn a key energy provision and legislation designed to keep the government open past Friday that could cost them the support of a major player.\par \par \uc2 So, let's see where things stand right now. Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram is up on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  just requested \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  remove his streamline permitting provision from the government funding bill. It lacked the votes to clear a filibuster and threatened a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Senator Manchin, myself, and others will continue to have conversations about the best way to ensure responsible permitting reform is passed before the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice-over): \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  insisted for weeks that the interim spending bill would include Manchin's demand.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you surprised by a potential problem about the Manchin agreement here with progressives and liberals concerned about the environment?\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: The bottom line is very simple. And the permitting agreement is part of the IRA agreement. I'm going to add it to the C.R. and it will pass.\par \par \uc2 Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  urged a no-vote.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): This extraneous poison pill is not related to keeping the government open. What our Democratic colleagues have produced is a phony fig leaf that would actually set back the cause of real permitting reform.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Manchin has little goodwill among Republicans. GOP members have a beef with Manchin since he voted yes on the Democrats' tax health and climate bill, that's the so-called Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 Schumer wanted to reward Manchin for supporting the bill. Manchin faced opposition from the right and left.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOE MANCHIN\uc2  (D-WV): So we're going to let Putin or Bernie Sanders decide what the energy policies. And now, we have \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , and if he's whipping whatever he's done with the Republican colleagues, that's pretty strange bedfellows. And I'm hoping that's not the case.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: The Senate is now taking a procedural vote on the bill. The legislation includes $12 billion for Ukraine and help for the water crisis in Jackson, Mississippi. But no money for hurricanes in Puerto Rico and Florida.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): It funds the government through mid-December. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But, Chad, quickly, I mean, are we closer to a government shutdown or not?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: A little less now, this needs 60 votes, we'll probably get 60 votes on this procedural vote later tonight.\par \par \uc2 Move it through the Senate later this week and then the House probably Friday.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK. Chad Pergram, live on the Hill. Chad, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, why Ukraine is accusing Russia of a terrorist attack aimed at destabilizing European energy security?\par \par \uc2 And later, building the American Dream through entrepreneurship.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: All it takes is an idea, some hard work, a lot of sacrifice. But that's what it's all about. And when things end up, you know, being fruitful, that's, the American Dream.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): We will talk with author and entrepreneur \uc2 Steve Case\uc2  about his new book, The Rise of the Rest. But first, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight.\par \par \uc2 Fox 5 in New York, where a homeless career criminal faces charges in connection with an attack on a woman at a Queens subway station, September 20th. Police arrested the 41-year-old suspect last week. The victim suffered a severe eye injury.\par \par \uc2 Fox 5 in Las Vegas, as police respond to six overdose deaths in the last 36 hours. They say at least four of the overdoses were fentanyl related. Authorities believed the victims unknowingly took fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Indianapolis from Fox 59, our affiliate there. One of the big stories there tonight.\par \par \uc2 Indiana lottery officials say five winning Powerball tickets worth more than $3.1 million collectively were sold in the state. They say a $2 million ticket was purchased in New Albany and a $1 million dollar ticket was bought in Laurel. So, if you're in Indiana, check your numbers.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  nuclear engineer and his wife have entered a new guilty plea in a case involving an alleged plot to sell secrets about nuclear powered warships. The plea is by Jonathan and Diana Toebbe came a month after their previous agreements that had called for specific sentencing guidelines were rejected. The couple now face maximum sentence of life in prison and a $100,000 fine.\par \par \uc2 While Russian troops continue to fight and lose on the battlefields of Ukraine, the Russian government is dealing with a major conflict at home. Huge numbers of people are fleeing the country to avoid military service. This comes as a new problem develops for all sides deep inside a major body of water.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Trey Yingst has the latest tonight from Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Gas bubbles penetrate the surface of the Baltic Sea. Under the water, three gas leaks within 24 hours have been detected from the Nord Stream 1 and 2, pipelines that Russia and European partners spent billions to build.\par \par \uc2 ANDERS PUCK NIELSEN, MILITARY ANALYST, ROYAL DANISH DEFENSE COLLEGE: It's definitely something that begs the question is this actually sabotage or some kind of hybrid warfare that we are witnessing here?\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Well, neither line was operating when the leaks began. European officials are investigating what caused the pipes to rupture. Poland's Prime Minister blamed sabotage for the leaks and Ukrainian leadership is already blaming Russia, calling the event a terrorist attack aimed at destabilizing European energy security.\par \par \uc2 Russian officials did not take responsibility, saying the Kremlin is extremely concerned about the leaks. Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  faces growing pressure over his war in Ukraine after partially mobilizing 300,000 new soldiers.\par \par \uc2 This week, lines of cars want to exit Russia through the country's borders. Reports indicate more than a quarter million men have fled in recent days to avoid getting sent to fight.\par \par \uc2 DMITRY KURILIYUNOK, RUSSIAN NATIONAL (through translator): We like many others fear that against our will they could draft us to go and kill peaceful people in another country in Ukraine. We are completely against this war.\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Meantime, mobilization protests are continuing as recruitment officers round up middle aged men, many are receiving no training before being sent into Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine continues to take territory in the Donbas region, specifically focusing on the city of Limon since it's used as a main Russian supply hub.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 YINGST (on camera): Further east, voting on sham referendums wrapped up today, pro-Moscow officials say all four occupied areas of Ukraine have voted to join Russia.\par \par \uc2 Shortly after those results were announced, a senior defense official telling Fox News the U.S. is preparing a $1.1 billion arms package for Ukraine. Those weapons will be used to liberate this territory, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Trey Yingst live in Kyiv. Trey, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The company that owns \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  says a sprawling disinformation network originating in Russia sought to use hundreds of fake social media accounts and dozens of sham news websites to spread Kremlin talking points about the invasion in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Meta\uc2  says it identified and disabled the operation before it was able to gain a large audience. But the company says it was the largest and most complex Russian propaganda effort that it has found since the invasion began.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the risks and sacrifices business starters make.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I joke that the best thing you can do as an early entrepreneur is moved back in with your parents. One because that keeps expenses low and two, because highly motivating for you to get your business off the ground so you can move out.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll talk live with \uc2 Steve Case\uc2  about his drive to boost America's next generation of entrepreneurs and his new book.\par \par \uc2 Plus, tracking Hurricane Ian, we'll have another live report from Florida.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. More than a dozen people injured in a school shooting in Central Russia will receive treatment in Moscow. This comes one day after a gunman killed 17 people, wounded 24 others. It happened about 600 miles east of Moscow.\par \par \uc2 The 34-year-old gunman who was a graduate of that school killed himself.\par \par \uc2 Nepalese rescuers in a helicopter bring additional survivors of an avalanche to a hospital in Katmandu. Monday an avalanche at a lower elevation on Mount Manaslu swept several climbers killing a Nepalese guide and injuring others.\par \par \uc2 Famed U.S. Ski climber Hilaree Nelson fell off near the peak of the world's eighth highest mountain.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Tokyo, one of the big stories there tonight, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  emphasizes the U.S. commitment to regional security during a meeting with Asian leaders in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 She says she will visit the demilitarized zone dividing North and South Korea, the DMZ Thursday at the end of her trip to Asia.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As we told you at the top of the program, Hurricane Ian is expected to bring Category 4 winds and storm surge to the west coast of Florida tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 We just received word a few minutes ago that \uc2 Disney\uc2  is closing its Florida theme parks tomorrow and Thursday, Eastern Florida may escape the most intense fury from the hurricane. The region is under tropical storm watches and warnings.\par \par \uc2 Fox Weather Correspondent Mitti Hicks is in Tallahassee tonight with the latest from there. Good evening, Mitti.\par \par \uc2 MITTI HICKS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WEATHER CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Now, just because we are 200 miles north of where we expect to see the biggest impact, I can tell you that state and local leaders are telling folks in Tallahassee, now is not the time to let your guard down.\par \par \uc2 Now, we are here at the emergency operation center where state and local leaders are watching the conditions closely across the Sunshine State. Many of the folks who are evacuating from the south are coming here and Tallahassee is playing a critical role when it comes to those resources. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KERRI POST, DIRECTOR, LEON COUNTY, FLORIDA DIVISION OF TOURISM: We have a partial activation in our emergency operations center. Again, we're in close communication with all of our hotels and get our finger to the pulse on what they're seeing. The Red Cross is here, there's a lot of utility trucks, there's insurance adjustors. So we are and as they are kind of waiting to sees as they move further south into the peninsula.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 HICKS (on camera): Now, here in Tallahassee, we are expecting to see some damaging window gusts, and, of course, there are some concerns for flooding. Again, the message from state and local leaders is now is not the time to let your guard down. Live in Tallahassee, back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mitti, thank you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GARRY COOPER, RHEAPLY CEO: How do you manage a remote and growing workforce? How do you think about all the different social issues and prioritize those while also prioritizing growing your own company? I think it's a hard time to fundraise as a founder, and I think it's also a hard time to think about how to keep your employees happy and healthy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tonight, we are going that talk about entrepreneurship with a man who help found America Online, AOL, when the Internet was in its infancy. \uc2 Steve Case\uc2  is the author of the new book "The Rise of the Rest, How Entrepreneurs in Surprising Places are Building the New American Dream." Steve, thanks for joining us.\par \par \uc2 STEVE CASE\uc2 , ENTREPRENEUR: Great to be with you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Why did you do this book?\par \par \uc2 CASE: Because it's a story that had to be told. I spent most of the past decade traveling around the country by bus. We've traveled to dozens and dozens of cities, met hundreds and hundreds of entrepreneurs. And most people don't know what these entrepreneurs are building. Most people don't know that these cities are starting to develop really thriving startup communities.\par \par \uc2 And if you look at the data over the last decade, 75 percent of venture capital investment dollars are going to new companies just goes to three states -- California, New York, and Massachusetts. So 47 states have 25 percent. And so we are trying to get more capital to states like Florida and Ohio and Wisconsin and Colorado and Texas and all over the country. And that's what "Rise of the Rest," is all about, identifying these entrepreneurs with promising ideas and helping provide capital and mentoring so they can start companies and create jobs in those communities. New companies create most of the jobs.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's amazing story. And I have been reading through it. We have been playing some of the soundbites from some of the entrepreneurs that have you have talked to. You went to a bus tour in 43 cities. Your fund has invested in more than 175 companies across 80 cities during this time.\par \par \uc2 I want to read from one section of this about the undermining America. "People around the world are still largely envious and respectful of America. But that admiration is not a birth right. It must be earned continually. If we lose our way, it probably won't be because of rising power like China undermines us, although that is, of course, a risk. More likely it will be because we undermine ourselves. We can't rely solely on government to fix something that's wrong in the marrow of our communities."\par \par \uc2 You know, Steve, this is not a political book, but we are in a political time. How do you interpret that to what we are seeing now and what government can or can't do in this space?\par \par \uc2 CASE: Well, I think part of the divide we have in this country is an opportunity gap. There are some people in some places like Silicon Valley that are doing really well, and a lot of people a lot of places that are struggling, feel left out, feel left behind. The new jobs and the new companies and the new industries are happening someplace else. And that leads, understandably, to frustration and even resentment. So we have to be backing entrepreneurs everywhere. We need make sure they, anybody who has an idea can start a company.\par \par \uc2 I remember one company we backed in a suburb of Indianapolis, a mom with a concern about clean water after the Flint water crisis, she started a company there. It got scaled up in providing services all across the country. Another company in Richmond, Virginia, is providing sustainable packaging solutions. They just raised $140 million from \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . But they almost didn't get started because most of the venture capitalists are just focusing on places like California, New York, and Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 So we have to level the playing field. We have to create opportunity for everybody. We have to give everybody access to the American dream. And we have to recognize these new companies are the big job creators. So if we don't back them everywhere, we are not going to have strong communities. We're not going to have a strong nation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You've got three questions here about the potential for startups. You say, why me, why now, who cares?\par \par \uc2 CASE: Yes. I think that a lot of people see something in their lives and say that's a problem. I think somebody should solve it. The entrepreneurs go the extra step and they build a company to actually take action to make that happen. But sometimes people in some parts of the country feel like they can't really be entrepreneurs because all the action is in places like Silicon Valley. That's where all the money is. That's where a lot of people are.\par \par \uc2 Part of the message of the book is anybody can start a company anywhere. You see something that could be done better. It could be in the healthcare sector. It could be education. It could be many different things. Do something about it. Two of the people we backed in St. Louis started a company saying it's hard for women to find swimsuits that fit them and are stylish, and they started in company in St. Louis. Now they have $100 million in revenue. So this really is possible everywhere. That's really the message of the book. Hopefully, as we have dozens of stories of entrepreneurs, dozens of stories of cities that are really helping to drive the next wave of success for those entrepreneurs. It really is clear to me after traveling around the country, everywhere in the country you can be an entrepreneur. Every community can rise up, create new jobs. We just have to be more intentional about it. And that's why I wrote the book.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The book is really fantastic. It talks about the American dream. It talks about you can do it. But there is also a money element to this, right, Steve? You are in the money-making business, and this fund is making money.\par \par \uc2 CASE: Yes. No, absolutely. We have done a number of things through the \uc2 Case Foundation\uc2 , which my wife Jean leads that are trying to support a lot of philanthropic causes. But "The Rise of the Rest" is about backing entrepreneurs and generating returns. And we put together a great group of investors, entrepreneurships like \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  and Howard Schultz and families like the Koch family and the Walton family and private equity investors like \uc2 Henry Kravis\uc2  and David Rubenstein. Hedge fund people like Ray Dalio, other entrepreneurs like \uc2 Sara Blakely\uc2  and Tory Burch. People from all around the country that joined us in backing these companies.\par \par \uc2 And we said the best thing we could do for these companies, the best thing we could do for these cities, the best thing we could do for America is prove to investors who generally are sitting on the coast that money can be made backing entrepreneurs in the middle of the country. And that's what "Rise of the Rest" is all about. We've launched the fund. That's why I wrote the book. We need to tell this story. It's an optimistic story of America, and there are so many negative aspects that we focus on. Anybody reading this book will feel better about the future of America, better about the America's ability to continue to lead in what is now a global competition around innovation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It is about optimism. It's in the pages of "The Rise of the Rest." If you had one thing to say to somebody who is starting a company, what would you say?\par \par \uc2 CASE: Do it. Do it now and do it wherever you are. Don't put it off until tomorrow. Don't feel like you have to be somewhere else. If you see something that can be done better, as this book shows you, it's sort of a roadmap to take that idea and turn it into a reality. You too can be an entrepreneur. You too can create companies. You too can create jobs. You too can help renew your community. You too, collectively, can help make sure we have a strong America.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: America winning is a pretty good message. \uc2 Steve Case\uc2 , thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 CASE: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, our midterm spotlight focuses on an unusual Senate race in Utah, Plus the panel on how the November elections are shaping up right now.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It will be a couple of months before we know if \uc2 NASA\uc2 's experiment to try to nudge an asteroid off course was successful. Last night a \uc2 NASA\uc2  spacecraft slammed into the rock at 14,000 miles per hour. The hope is that such a move would affect an asteroid's path enough to avoid a potential collision with earth in the future.\par \par \uc2 We are now six weeks from Election Day, six weeks. Tonight, midterm spotlight shines on the Utah Senate race. Former 2016 independent presidential candidate Evan McMullin has the support of Democrats as he tries to oust two-term Republican Senator \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2 . The incumbent, once a staunch critic of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , voted for McMullin in the presidential contest when Trump won the presidency. He and lee made peace eventually. Senator Lee became a Trump defender and confidante on Supreme Court nominees. Now McMullin is turning \uc2 Lee\uc2 's close ties to former President Trump into a campaign focal point, promising to become a one-man caucus if elected.\par \par \uc2 Heading into their debate October 17th, Lee has almost a double the amount of cash on hand. That's according to the \uc2 Federal Election Commission\uc2 . The latest FOX News power ranking lists the Utah Senate seat as likely to stay in the Republicans' column.\par \par \uc2 Abortion is a major issue in neighboring Arizona where there is much uncertainty about what exactly what is legal and what is not now. Politically the situation is emboldening Democrats. Senior correspondent Alicia Acuna was the latest tonight from Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALICIA ACUNA, FOX NEWS SENIOR CORRESPONDENT: Confusion over two abortion laws on the books in Arizona has created an opportunity for Democrats running in this hotly contested state.\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS\uc2 , (D) ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL NOMINEE: They know this is going to hurt them at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: In the neck in neck race for governor, Democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  is promising to bring back pro-choice protections. This past weekend, two competing statutes went into effect as a result of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturning Roe v. Wade. One dates back to 1901 and bans the procedure except to save the life of the mother. The other allows abortion up to 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 JASON LAMM, CRIMINAL DEFENSE ATTORNEY: The safest bet for a provider is not to do anything and wait until there is further legal guidance from the court.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: My body, my choice!\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: The uncertainty has brought abortion back into the headlines ahead of the midterms.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: As a woman in the state of Arizona I am terrified for how this is going to play out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm hoping they keep it pro-life. It shows the dignity of people. Every life is precious.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: And thrown a wrench into the GOP strategy to keep the message on the economy, crime, and the border. GOP gubernatorial candidate Kari Lake was asked by \uc2 Maria Bartiromo\uc2  her thoughts on the state attorney general potentially enforcing a near total ban.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE, (R) ARIZONA GUBERNATORIAL NOMINEE: I'm pro-life. I have never backed away from that and never will I.\par \par \uc2 ACUNA: Even before the dust-up, an August FOX News poll found abortion is among the top issues for Arizona voters behind inflation and the border. In the Senate race, Democrat incumbent Mark Kelly, who is leading in the polls, tweeted he will defend abortion access. Republican candidate \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  did not respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 ACUNA (on camera): And in a little more than two weeks, early voting begins for both in person and mail-in ballot. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alicia, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Student activists held school walkouts across Virginia today. They were protesting Republican Governor Glenn Youngkin's proposed changes to the state's guidance on transgender student policies. Defenders of the governor's policies say they give greater respect to the role of parents in their children's lives.\par \par \uc2 Massachusetts based drugmaker \uc2 Biogen\uc2  is agreeing to pay $900 million to resolve allegations it violated federal law by paying kickbacks to doctors to persuade them to prescribe its multiple sclerosis drugs. The agreement settles a whistleblower lawsuit brought by a form are \uc2 Biogen\uc2  employee.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on what President Biden is not saying about the crime crisis, the effort to avoid a government shutdown, and the latest on the midterms. Plus, Tuesday Tweets, yours could be on tonight's show. You never know.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Does President Biden think America's big cities are safe?\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: It is not -- it is not a -- it is not a yes or no question. What matters is that is that we have the funding and we have done the work, put the policy forward. And that's what this president has done, again, without the help of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA ANDERSON, HERITAGE ACTION FOR AMERICA: Of course, crime is surging because there's no protections in place to keep people safe and secure. Who is going to keep our community safe? Who is going to keep our kids safe? And we are looking for alternative, and that's really what it is going to come down to on the ballot is who is going to actually talk about and commit to these issues.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, crime is one of those issues that's actually ticking up as we get closer to the election and people deal with what is happening in different cities, not just big cities but across the country. If you look at our latest FOX News poll about issues that Republicans are succeeding at, GOP handling better than Democrats, border security plus 22, crime plus 13. And there you see inflation and foreign policy.\par \par \uc2 With that, let's bring in our panel, Guy Benson, political editor at Townhall.com, host of "The Guy Benson Show" on FOX News Radio, Olivia Beavers, congressional reporter for "Politico," and Matthew Continetti, fellow at the \uc2 American Enterprise Institute\uc2 . Matthew, it seems like crime is having an impact, but we don't fully have a sense of it yet. But they administration is not talking about it a lot, but it has an impact.\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW CONTINETTI, AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: Absolutely. For 30 years crime gave the advantage to Republicans in a national political context. Of course, crime's impacts are felt will he local level, at the municipal level. But politically it has big national consequences. And so what we are seeing is crime surges, as violent crime surges, Republicans are reaping the benefits.\par \par \uc2 Now, I would also say, too, of course, it's liberal policies that are creating this crime surge, whether it's on incarceration, whether it's on policing, whether it's on addiction, on homelessness. All of this is creating a crisis in the cities. And even though there aren't many Republicans in the cities, Republican will benefit nationally.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Olivia, there was this effort for this piece of legislation on crime up on Capitol Hill. And it seemed like an effort for politically for Democrats to solve their problems. It seemed clunky, though, as it kind of moved forward.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, "POLITICO": Right. And while we are in the midterms, and right then you see that Democrats are trying to make a push, but right now they are actually just trying to not mess up anything as they head into the midterms after they were able to pass a series of legislations that they think is helpful.\par \par \uc2 But when Democrats are talking about crime, as you pointed out in the polling. They know it's a problem. They are trying to pivot and say Republicans don't care about crime because look how they handled January 6th. Or crime is a problem because of the way that there is enough not control about access to guns. And so they are pivoting. And when you talk to some of the swing state Democrats, they are saying it's a problem. Whether or not they are going to get the legislation done in time for the midterms, it doesn't appear so.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Guy?\par \par \uc2 GUY BENSON, POLITICAL EDITOR, TOWNHALL.COM: I found it very interesting, I believe it was yesterday, that \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  was confronted by our colleague Peter Doocy with the words of her predecessor \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , who now has a new role, a competitor of ours. And she was making the completely correct point that crime is a problem for Democrats and a big vulnerability. And Peter asked the easy question, which is why would she say that if it weren't true? And Jean-Pierre's only response was to at least try to pretend that Peter had taken Psaki out of context or mischaracterized her words when I believe the exact quote was crime is a major vulnerability for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 You can't really get around it, and so we got some talking at the edges. But when it comes down to it, they know it's making an impact nationally and in certain specific races as well. The Senate race in Pennsylvania comes to mind, specifically.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Matthew, do you think that things are baked in the cake now, or that there can be a lot of variabilities before November?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Structurally, Bret, this election favors Republicans when you look at the president's job approval, we you look at voter assessments of the economy, when you look at historical precedent. Usually, the first midterm does very poorly for the president's party. But we are still about six weeks out, which means that there's always room for one of those candidates to mess it up with a gaffe or some unplanned act, and you can always count on one or two of them to do exactly that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Exactly, shoot themself in the foot. OK, here is Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  talking about permitting reform and back and forth with Senator Manchin.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R-KY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: It is much too difficult to build things in America and unleash American energy. Liberal regulations and red tape are a huge, huge part of the problem.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOE MANCHIN\uc2 , (D-WV): I think there will be a time he looks back if he really evaluates this will that we never had this opportunity to take a major step forward that we are all in sync tonight.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And just tonight, Olivia, they pulled this permitting out of this bill for funding the government. Manchin said, "A failed vote on something as critical as comprehensive permitting reform only serves to embolden leaders like Putin who wish to see America fail. For that reason, and my firmly held belief that we should never come to the brink of a government shutdown over politics, I have asked Majority Leader Schumer to remove the permitting language from the continuing resolution we will vote on this evening." Boy, it just seems like he got rolled.\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: He was deal with two different factors playing against him. One he had Republicans like McConnell who, my colleagues reported, was whipping against. And when McConnell, Leader McConnell puts his mind to something in terms of whipping against it, he usually is pretty successful.\par \par \uc2 The other one is there were some progressive Democrats in the Senate who didn't like it for energy purposes. So you saw Bernie Sanders, Tim Kaine --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And that was always going to happen.\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: -- right, coming out against it. But he had a very hard time getting to 60, especially after that vote for the Inflation Reduction Act that put Republicans -- pitted them against him. So --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Manchin's point, though, Guy, is that if you don't do it now, and you have got this deal, push it through, even if it's just Republicans, why not do it for the good of America? Republicans saying we don't like the deal that you made. We're going to wait for Senator Capito's permitting bill when we control Congress, is that the deal?\par \par \uc2 BENSON: Yes, they have more leverage here. The Democrats don't have their own ducks in a row on this. So why help them on this? And if it's an opportunity to maybe force Manchin to twist in the window a little bit because of the deal he made with Schumer and the Democrats where they said, don't worry, this is going to happen, and now it might not, Manchin would say there are maybe a few more bites at the \uc2 Apple\uc2  down the line before the year is over, maybe in a lame duck session.\par \par \uc2 But I remember he was interviewed by you on "FOX NEWS SUNDAY" and he was confident that the Democrats were going to keep their word on this. He said if not, there would be consequences. Here is the first swing and a miss by the Democrats. We'll see if they swing and miss a few more times. What are those consequences?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We don't know, and I think that that's a great question for him to answer.\par \par \uc2 Lastly, Matthew, the energy question, as we see Europe dealing with what looks like an energy Armageddon coming this winter, how much do you think that factors in?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: It might break the alliance against Putin. I hope not. I think Europe has done a good job with the reserves.\par \par \uc2 To Guy's point, the consequences that Manchin promised are for us with more inflation thanks to that vote for the so-called Inflation Reduction Act. Bottom line, we need to open the spigots of American energy. Biden has taken us from energy independence to energy dependence in less than two years. Things need to change.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Panel, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 OK, it's Tuesday. It's time for Tuesday Tweets. First John Katz, "What's driving the recent European tilt towards conservative leadership?" Well, basically inflation, also the energy crisis, those are two big things. But we are seeing a lot of changes in politics in Europe right now, Italy and others.\par \par \uc2 Next we have GMR Chicago asks "What was your first concert, and who is your favorite band? I see you at the mic on "Rapper's Delight." Nice." Actually, my first concert was Billy Joel, but I was a big Rush guy. I was a Rush fan when I was growing up, and I had the big poster, the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 Up next Greg Mullens wants to know, "What's your over/under on the days after the election it will take to know which party controls 2023 \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 ?" I actually think we'll know that night. That's my guess. But I was wrong last time.\par \par \uc2 Lastly, Laura asks, "Who is your favorite panelist?" Wow, that's tough. I'm going to say \uc2 Charles Krauthammer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That's it for us, fair, balanced, and unafraid. Thanks for watching. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts right now. Who is your favorite panelist, Jesse?\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220928ei9r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Category 3 Ian Growing Stronger In Gulf Of Mexico; 3,200 Florida National Guard Deployed For Hurricane Ian; Florida Bracing For Impact Of Hurricane Ian; Florida Residents Scramble For Supplies Ahead Of Ian; Florida Restaurant Manager Prepping For Hurricane Ian; Washington GOP Senate Candidate On Key Issues In Race; GOP Candidate Looks To Unseat Senior Dem Senator In Washington; Georgia Senate Candidates Spar Over Abortion; Poll: Most Dems Don't Want Biden As 2024 Nominee; Democrat's Future Uncertain AS Biden Hedges On 2024 Run; Europe Investigates Leaks In Russian Gas Pipelines; Detained Woman's Death Sparks Outrage In Iran; DeSantis Gives Update On CAT 3 Hurricane Ian; Pro-Life Activist Pleads Not Guilty In Fed Court\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Max Gordon, Rich Edson, Dana Perino, Joe Concha,  Dana Perino, Charles Payne, Mitti Hicks \par \uc2 9,042 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: In Europe.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Oh, yes, I think so.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I remember traveling Europe with \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  back in the 1990s, and everybody was skewing to the left, not so much.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Yes, I think that pendulum is now swinging the other way. All right. That's it for us. I'm Aishah Hasnie. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Thanks for being here today. It's always a pleasure.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I'll see you again tomorrow. THE STORY with Martha starts right now.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you very much, guys. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum, Dana Perino and Joe Concha join us in just a few minutes. They're going to be here to react to some of the very new tough polling that we are seeing from President Biden's own party. We'll break that down with both of them. But first, obviously, the big story this afternoon. Fox News Weather Alert, Hurricane Ian is now picking up speed, you can see it churning away as it heads into the Gulf of Florida this hour.\par \par \uc2 Millions are bracing for what could be "catastrophic conditions" that they're asking people to be ready for there. We've never seen storm surge like the kind that is predicted in this area of Florida. They're expecting surges of up to 15 feet. The height in the past was four feet. So, they're trying to shut it all down right now. That's what you're looking at in the right-hand side of your screen. Airports closing, schools closing, waterways -- everybody battening down the hatches ahead of what is termed a 100-year storm.\par \par \uc2 So, most people by far have not witnessed anything like this who are living in Florida right now. This is the stunning space image that we've got for you from the space station. It gives you a sense of how mammoth this area that is affected is, that is ready to hit Florida. Fox Weather Correspondent Max Gordon is live in St. Petersburg on the ground in one of those areas that is expected to see some of this devastation. I can see just a little bit of wind picking up there now as you guys get ready for this. Hi, Max.\par \par \uc2 MAX GORDON, FOX NEWS WEATHER CORRESPONDENT: Hey, good afternoon. Well, mandatory evacuations are now in place here in Pinellas County. Folks living in Evacuation Zones A, B, C, and people living in mobile homes are being told to get out right now. We are in Evacuation Zone A -- this is one of the most vulnerable places for storm surge. So, we're expecting to see conditions worsen over the next day or so. Now, all of Florida is in a state of emergency, all 67 counties.\par \par \uc2 And so, 5,000 Florida National Guard troops will be ready to go with 2,000 guardsmen to hear from other parts of the country. 26 school districts have announced school closures across Florida. And in some cases, schools will be used as shelters. In the Tampa area, food water generators and pumps are all being staged before landfall. Power outages will be likely when the storm hits. And Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  says that restoring power is going to be a top priority.\par \par \uc2 Here in Pinellas County, \uc2 Duke Energy\uc2  says they're staging crews and they upgraded their power grid with new technology that reroutes power around problem areas to prevent mass outages. Though, trees falling into lines, high winds and floodwaters are all going to be big concerns. As this storm draws near, people have been scrambling for supplies. Some gas stations have run out of fuel, and drinking water has been snatched off of store shelves. Hardware stores are packed with people buying generators, and plywood to board up their homes. And many people told us that after they cover their windows, they are going to get out.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We're going to evacuate. We're going to go to Orlando to stay with some family. But we do want to board up the house as much as possible and protect whatever belongings we have just in case if it should help. We don't even know if it's going to help. We're still like in a panic mode. Like I can't even like dwell on anything right now, it's just go.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GORDON: Roads are starting to get congested here in the St. Petersburg, Tampa area as people evacuate. And \uc2 Tampa International Airport\uc2  will be closing in less than two hours, at 5:00 p.m. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Quite a scramble. Max, thank you very much. S\par \par \uc2 GORDON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, joining me now Greg Corvallis, Manager at Marina Jax Restaurant in Tampa and Sarasota where they are expected to be near the center of this thing. Greg, good to have you here. You got a smile on your face. I know you guys are working to get ready for this. Have you ever been through a hurricane in Florida before?\par \par \uc2 GREG CORVALLIS, MARINA JAX RESTAURANT MANAGER: No, this is actually my first one. I've been here for five years and just missed Irma by a few months which was back in September of 17. So, here we are living, living the dream here in sunny Florida.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, tell me what, you know, weather what area you're in and the restaurants that you manage are literally right on the water. I think we have some images of Marina Jax, which is a very large impressive building. It looks like it's very solid. You can see this whole windowed area but yet you're wrapped in Windows there. I would imagine those are hurricane proof or hope they would be.\par \par \uc2 CORVALLIS: Yes. And I am actually standing in the back of that restaurant as we speak here, which sits feet from the bay. So, it all depends on where this thing is going to hit. The models have changed. And we have everything packed up and 