. Read the polls, Jack. You guys are all the same.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. So, this just breaking moments ago, the January 6 committee has now postponed the hearing that was set for tomorrow because of Hurricane Ian that is bearing down quite far away from them in Florida.\par \par \uc2 So, that's curious. Our congressional correspondent Chad Pergram pointing out that the hearing probably wouldn't have gotten too much T.V. coverage because of the hurricane coverage that is likely.\par \par \uc2 So, he also reports that everyone on the panel says that they are praying for people in the path of the storm. And we're going to -- they will let us know, of course, when that hearing is rescheduled. So, stay tuned for more on that.\par \par \uc2 And take a look at this. More than half of Democrats and Independents who lean Democrat say that they do not want the current president to be their nominee in 2024. That according to a new Washington Post ABC News poll.\par \par \uc2 With that, we bring in Dana Perino, co-anchor of "AMERICA'S NEWSROOM" and co-host of "THE FIVE". And Joe Concha, media and politics columnist for The Hill, and a Fox News contributor and author of the new book, out today, Come On, Man!: The Truth About \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's Terrible, Horrible. No-Good, Very Bad Presidency". I think I had -- there was a children's book that had a very similar title.\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes. But ours (INAUDIBLE) to talk about --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But not Come On, Man, exactly.\par \par \uc2 Great to see both of you.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you for having us.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Great to see both of you. You know, one of the other numbers, Dana that's really interesting in here is people under the age of 40, a resounding 75 percent in that poll say that they do not -- they want someone other than the current president.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I thought it was amazing that ABC News would work -- do this poll. You know, commissioned a poll, work on the poll, and then not report on the poll.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That was pretty amazing to me that they would bury it that way. Because it is super bad news for the president, especially, with you are going into a midterm election where things are close, and many of the races, you don't want everyone thinking that they want you to be a lame duck president before you even get through that.\par \par \uc2 Whether he runs again, obviously, remains to be seen. He has hedged a little bit, but it's given every indication that he plans to run.\par \par \uc2 The big question for Democrats, I think, is not whether he runs, but if he doesn't run, then what? Because that's going to be their bigger question immediately following that decision.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. You know, Joe, you look at the issues that people care about economy, inflation, rising prices, crime, you know, a lot of these Republicans are working hard to tie back to President Biden with the enormous amount of money that was thrown at the COVID issue, trillions of dollars. And crime policies that have been very problematic for many of these candidates.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Inflation, economy going away the most important issue to voters going into this election. Crime rising not only in the streets, but in the polls in terms of people's perception, and what they're feeling. Right?\par \par \uc2 So, when you have those two issues as your top issues, and then the border, obviously is a very big issue as well. When we're talking about now, we're approaching if you include gotaways.\par \par \uc2 On top of the people that have been encountered and counted for, 5 million people entering this country in the first two years of this presidency, that is equivalent to the population of Ireland. All right? So, this is a big deal. And obviously, fentanyl killing, what? 300 people a day on average?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Because of opioid addiction.\par \par \uc2 These are things that the media can focus as much as they want on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the January 6 committee, in the Mar-a-Lago raid, but when people read, they're not reading the newspaper so much. They're reading their 401 (k)s, which I was just complaining about the Charles Payne a moment ago.\par \par \uc2 They're reading -- they're seeing their paychecks and their wages not going up anymore. They're seeing their bank accounts, they're not saving as much as they used to, and they don't feel as safe.\par \par \uc2 As for Dana's question before as far as the now what if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  doesn't run? I'll tell you what, we're going to have what we had in 2020. 28 Democrats vying for the money (PH). And that's what -- remember they had --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I remember that well.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I'm here for all of it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: But dates on two nights, they couldn't put everybody on stage.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: you know, I think that, you know, people feel it in there. They'd see cars getting stolen in their neighborhoods. I went into churches weekend, there was a sign please keep your bag close to you. We've had people stealing in church.\par \par \uc2 That I saw the same -- the same in the grocery store. Please keep your bag close, you have people stealing in the grocery store. It's really stunning how much life has changed, Dana, in so many ways.\par \par \uc2 But with regard to 2024, this is the recent poll of Trump and Biden. Pretty much tied. Biden at 48. Trump at 46. And the way the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  put it was, you know, basically that this is sort of the rematch that nobody seems to want.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But it -- but that looks like it may happen.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: It seems like that's, that 48, 46, 50, 48, like that's about where America is on almost -- on a lot of these issues. And so, I think, if and until there is another option -- if and until somebody says, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  says, I don't care that the president says he's going to run again, I'm going to primary him very unlikely, but it could happen.\par \par \uc2 Or if somebody on the Republican side says even if President Trump says he's going to run, I'm going to run as well, then you might see a little bit of change in those numbers. But until the American people are presented with another option, that's probably what you're going to get.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: And be very weary, by the way, of predicting who the nominees will be more than two years out before midterm.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Because in 2006 is when you're in the White House, the matchup was undoubtedly got to be \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  versus Hillary Clinton.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Because in 2006, is when you're in the White House, the matchup was undoubtedly going to be \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  versus Hillary.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: And in 2014, Jeb Bush, exclamation point, versus Hillary Clinton. So we don't know what's --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's true. Yes. No, I think it's absolutely true. And if you ask me today, do I think it will be Trump versus Biden, I would say probably no. Just looking into the crystal ball, and it does appear that people are going to run against both of these candidates --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Maybe. I mean, I think that the Democrats have a -- they have a situation where they've got to figure out a way to consolidate and make sure. Because what should President Biden do? If President Biden were to announce that he's not going to run again, is he just supposed to pass over his vice president? How delicate do you think that conversation is going to have to be to say I think the Democratic voters should decide. And then for him not to say I have so much faith and belief in my vice president that I think she --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: The kind of but you have to say, wouldn't he? And I don't know how many difference it would make if he did say it to voters.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I think it would make a big difference. Do you think people would support her because --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I think that if he did not -- if he does go ahead and support her that I think support for him will fall away even more. That's my prediction.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: And then he would be endorsing a candidate that didn't even get to 2020 as a 2020 presidential candidate. He dropped out in December 2019. Already unpopular, and then the unpopular guys endorsing her --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But think about the fact that president, the former President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , he didn't support his vice president. He didn't get behind \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said I wanted to run. He kept saying, I'm going to see who's out there and we'll take a look. I mean, how awful and humiliating was that for him not to be able to jump on board right away with his own vice president?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And then, we have to see what happens in the midterms. If the Democrats take a real bath on the midterm night, then I think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will have to say, OK, what am I going to do so that immediately he will -- people will consider him a lame duck president. Unless he comes out and maybe they say that Democrats do OK in the midterms. He might come out and say, I'm running again. And then, probably a lot of the conversation with the Democrats melts away, and they're going to have to grin and bear it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Interesting piece of the National Review Today, Midterm polls look better for Republicans than we've been led to believe, by Jim Geraghty today. And I thought this was very interesting because he's basically saying, you know, throw out -- there's a lot of likely voter polls, a lot of registered voter polls that are still out there. So when you look at the polls, folks, at home, look at the small print, see if the poll you're looking at is of likely voters or just registered voters. Because at this point in the game, if it's registered voters, it's probably not that accurate, right?\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Plus, state by state polling.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: The (inaudible) in midterms, right? Look at the other tab as well, what is the President's disapproval on the issues we just talked about. Inflation, it's near 70%, economy overall 60s, border 70% or higher, crime 60s. When you have numbers like that, how can you possibly win in November? I get abortions a big issue, but that's four versus one. And I just don't see that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And not a big issue for Republicans that they've done well on, so far this year is voter registration. So -- and the chances are -- if you sign somebody up to vote, the likelihood that that person will vote that year is very high, and Republicans are up on Democrats on that across the board.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, interesting. All right. You guys. Thank you. Great to have you.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Thank you. Buy the book.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Congratulations on the book. "Come On, Man!" How do you say your title?\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: "Come on, Man!" But that's horrible. It sounds like Randy Lawson that that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You work on that.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Yes, there it is. But in book stores now, it's doing quite awesome.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Congratulations. Thanks, Joe. Thank you, Dana.\par \par \uc2 So here's another question for you outside of this country is \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  escalating his war by attacking the Nord Stream pipeline that leads to Europe. That is what two prominent European leaders are contending today. And it is clearly rattling the US markets. KT McFarland and Charles Payne join us on that as this bear market continues to take a bite out of your retirement.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Big story today, take a look at these explosions in the Baltic Sea, and now leaks that are turning up from two Russian national gas pipelines to Europe. Now, these lines, the Nord Stream lines, one in two, are not in use at the moment, not pumping fuel through them. But Poland is calling this a clear case of sabotage in their opinion. And Denmark also is saying that based on their knowledge of what is going on, they also say that this was deliberate. The White House says it won't speculate but we'll support the Europeans as they investigate.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: This just drives home the importance of our efforts to work together to get alternative gas supplies to Europe and to support efforts to reduce gas consumption and accelerate true energy independence by moving to clean energy economy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Charles Payne here, host of "Making Money" on Fox Business. Charles, great to see you. Thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 CHARLES PAYNE, FOX BUSINESS HOST: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So she's sort of sidestep the whole issue of whether or not Putin is the one behind this potential sabotage, which I know was a concern in a lot of the markets today is this assignment he's escalating, all of this. And she went straight to green energy out of that question. But let's go to the heart of the issue here, what are you hearing about what's behind these explosions?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Like you said, Denmark Energy minister there said it's not an accident. There was a blast. The Danish prime minister said it was probably a deliberate act the same words echoed by Norway. So, you know, all of these countries are more or less saying, listen, this was certainly sabotage. This was manmade, this was a deliberate act. Then you have to ask yourself why.\par \par \uc2 You made the point, Nord Stream 1, \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2 , 2 never came online. You know, of course that was -- talking about doing business. We were begging them during the Trump administration, you know, set up these liquefied natural gas facilities. we've got cheap natural gas here, the cheapest in the world. We liquefy it. We put on a ship. We send it to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: They didn't do that.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, \uc2 Nord Stream 2\uc2  had already been weaponized, because it wasn't an operation. So \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  was already weaponizing natural gas anyway. You see how much gas has gone up over there in Europe, and you just look particularly in a place like Germany. They're going to have to subsidize this.\par \par \uc2 From what I read, Europe is going to have to spend close to half a trillion dollars to subsidize energy this winter. You know, so you talk about --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And I think the LNG price was up 25% today.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes, yes. Well, the LNG natural gas is what really has gone up the most, and coal, because coal is in abundance. Ironically in Germany, they say, well, we might extend the nuclear power plants a little longer.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Right.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. No, no, no -- here's the thing, though, it's -- when you hear the press secretary talk about this being a reason, you know, to expedite green, the green utopia, for lack of a better term. This is why Europe is in this position in the first place. There's a smart way to do this. The smart way to do this is not to make power, electricity, heating your home, prohibitively expensive for something that's not even plausible for at least 20 years.\par \par \uc2 So if everyone on both sides of the Atlantic had a policy where let's use the fossil fuels that we have, let's use the technology that we have, so we got a smooth, smart transition.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Transition.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: This is nuts. This is nuts.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Exactly. I mean, I was saying yesterday, you know, China has done both, right? China hasn't taken any of their traditional resources offline. And yet, they're making all this money off of lithium batteries and solar panels. So they're saying we see what you're all doing in Europe and the United States, but we're not changing a thing in terms of our supply lines on traditional energy.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: They dominate electric vehicles. All that money that we just passed, you know, for the climate bill or whatever, the Inflation Reduction Act, almost all of its going to end up in China and China's coffers, almost all of it is going to end up in China's coffers. They're building more nuclear power plants and all the countries in the world combined. They use more coal than anybody else on the planet, but they're powering their economy. They're keeping it going. It's like no one's worried about freezing this winter, and that's a legitimate concern here.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, there was a another piece in the National Review today. The economy is starting to buckle. And it talks about how the Biden administration told us in July that, you know, we know two plus two equals four, but to two consecutive quarters of negative growth does not equal a recession. And you look at all these signs. You look at what's happening in energy in Europe and all of the weights that are on the economy right now, do you think the economy is starting to buckle?\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: There's no doubt it's starting to buckle. And, you know, the whole specious argument of whether we're in a recession or not is so -- it's disingenuous and is misguided, particularly from the president of United States because you're talking to individuals, right? The average American knows if their lives is in a recession. We don't care about eight people in a left leaning think-tank time snap this\par \par \uc2 So one of the \uc2 NBER\uc2  says it started in July, it's starting to descend, whatever. We know that most Americans are saying, we're getting crushed by that ultimate one-two punch. Inflation, that's not coming down fast enough, and now recession. You talk about twin economic evils. And, you know, small businesses are paying the price already, regular folks are paying the price already. And here's the sad part, it's going to get worse. You see the stock market today, Martha?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: We were up out the gate pretty good. You know why --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Put the (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: Yes. Now we turn down. We had two pieces of economic data that came in a little better than expected. Any glimmer of hope has to be bashed like a whack-a-mole by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has to go after it. And just like that, oh, good news, crushed the stock market because the Feds going to crush the economy.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, (inaudible) $400 billion to pay off people's student loans. That's money in people's pockets that makes this number worse. It makes -- the punishment have to be greater.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: That part I tell you is so heartbreaking to me. You take a place like President Biden's always talking about his hometown where he grew up, right, Scranton, I looked up Scranton, 17% college grads. So in his hometown, 83% of the people there aren't getting a nickel. The elites in that town are going to -- the 83% have to pay extra taxes so that the elites can make it through this recession.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Charles, thank you. Charles Payne, good to see you as always. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 PAYNE: You too, thanks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So coming up, KT McFarland on the massive show of opposition, the protests in the streets in Iran, the likes of which we have not seen, and why she calls it a continuation of the Biden-\uc2 Obama\uc2  love affair with the Iranian regime. She will explain what she means by that next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: These scenes are incredible. Outrage spreading across Iran and the world after the death of a 22-year-old Iranian woman. Mahsa Amini died after the country's morality police detained her. Apparently she wasn't wearing her headscarf properly. And then she died while she was in their custody. And now you have brave Iranian women and men taking to the streets. Many of these women are burning their headscarves that can put you in prison, that act, easily.\par \par \uc2 Now Tehran accusing the United States of using the protests for its own inspired purposes to try to weaken Iran. Let's bring in KT McFarland, former deputy National Security Adviser under President Trump. KT, great to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 Every day that these protests continue, now in day 18 I believe. It's astonishing to see what's happening in the streets. Is this -- and really what the leadership in Iran is saying this is nothing. You know, this is a little ripple. It's nothing. Is it nothing?\par \par \uc2 KT MCFARLAND, FORMER DEPUTY NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER UNDER PRESIDENT TRUMP: No, it's a really big deal, why? Because, first of all, it started out as a feminist revolution, right? It's women wanting to take the headscarves off, women all over the country wanting to take the headscarves off. You know, that's not a really core issue in Iran right now, and the fact that this has started that way, and now is spread to all areas. It's now not just we want to take the headscarves off. It's -- we don't like the mullahs. We don't like the way they're governing. We don't like their repressive policies. We don't like their economy.\par \par \uc2 And so, it's potentially, you know, potentially the Iranian people could speak up and change their regime and why this administration refuses yet again, it looks the other way and is continuing to negotiate with Iran. Chilling, you know, chasing this chimera dream of an Iran nuclear deal, which isn't going to happen, and even if it does, it's flawed.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, they say that they are putting some sanctions against the morality police that they are standing up for these protesters, and that President Biden brought it up in his \uc2 UN\uc2  speech. There's a lot of comparisons to 2009, which we can talk about in a second, to explain it. But here's Jake Sullivan on why he says this time is different, KT.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN, NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: Part of the reason that there was a different kind of approach in 2009, was the belief that somehow if America spoke out, it would undermine the protesters not aid them. I think what we've learned in the aftermath of that is that you can over think these things, that the most important thing for the United States to do is to be firm and clear and principled in response to citizens of any country demanding their rights and dignity.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Very quick thought on that and then I got to jump to the hurricane news that is just happening. KT, quick thought.\par \par \uc2 MCFARLAND: Sounds great but they're not doing anything about it. They're still negotiating with Iran and they're going to lift the sanctions against Iran. They're going to empower the mullahs' regime. And once again, they're siding not with the pro-democracy people, not with the demonstrators. They're siding with the Iranian mullahs' regime.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: President \uc2 Obama\uc2  heavily criticized for not seizing that moment in 2009 after the death in the streets of Neda Agha-Soltan. KT, thank you very much. Great to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 MCFARLAND: Thank you. Thank you, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So Fox Weather live in Tallahassee, and we just been informed that Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is about to join us live and that is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Breaking news as we cover the hurricane that is headed for Florida. Let's go live now to Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  for the latest on Hurricane Ian. Fox Weathers' Mitti Hicks is on the ground there covering the story for us inside the Emergency Operations Center in Tallahassee and she's joined by the governor. Hi, Mitti.\par \par \uc2 MITTI HICKS, FOX WEATHER REPORTER: Hey, good afternoon. And that's exactly right. Thank you for joining us, governor. How are you?\par \par \uc2 RON DESANTIS, FLORIDA GOVERNOR: We're working hard.\par \par \uc2 HICKS: And, you know, you've been traveling across the state visiting these EOCs in cities across the state where we anticipate them being the hardest hit. What have you learned so far, governor?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, people are preparing, Floridians are taking it seriously. And as you know with these weather tracks, it's going to be North Florida one day, Tampa the next. Now, they're showing more Sarasota, Charlotte County Area, so we were down there. But I think that this is going to be a major event. You're going to have really significant storm surge. And one of the things that's going to be difficult is once it makes landfall, it's going to slow down and really kind of crawl. But that's going to dump an inordinate amount of rain. And so, you're looking yes a powerful hurricane, but a lot of surge and a lot of water, which is going to cause a lot of flooding.\par \par \uc2 HICKS: And when we think about these conditions, especially that flooding, you know, I'm thinking about the vulnerable communities. Is there a contingency plan in place for people in nursing homes or people who feel like they can't afford to evacuate?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, there have been medical evacuation starting this weekend, in places like Tampa where you have medically vulnerable people that are evacuated into more secure environments. Fortunately, though, you look at a place like Southwest Florida, a lot of those ALFs and nursing homes are on higher ground. They are built to withstand hurricanes. One of the things that we require in Florida is 100% of the facilities have to have backup power, because you have a lot of medically dependent seniors.\par \par \uc2 The structure is fine. They can keep them safe from the storm but they need to have power. And so, they are required to have generators and right now 100% of them are reporting that they have that.\par \par \uc2 HICKS: Governor, I know we've been talking about this but what is your message for people here today?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well take the precautions to protect yourself and your family. This Mother Nature is a very fearsome adversary. And it's not something that you want to test. You know, they talk about hiding from wind but running from water. And so, when you have massive storm surge that can hit, make 10, 12 feet, that is something that is life threatening, and you got to take it seriously.\par \par \uc2 And I think having talked to a lot of the folks and various counties along the Gulf Coast, you know, most people are taking it very seriously, which is a good thing.\par \par \uc2 HICKS: That is absolutely a great thing. So thank you for joining us, governor. Live in Tallahassee, guys, back to you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mitti, if you could ask the governor for one quick question for me, I'd really appreciate because I know he can't hear me. But I believe this is really the biggest hurricane event that he has had since he became governor. You know, how does he feel about what he's facing in terms of the responsibility of this?\par \par \uc2 HICKS: OK. This is a question from our anchor. So she says this is one of the biggest hurricanes that you've had in office, how do you feel about the situation that's going on?\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: Well, the thing is, since we have some of the most experienced folks that work in our emergency management, not just in the state of Florida but also at the local level. You know, we had a major category five, Dorian, that people thought was going to hit the East Coast of Florida in 2019. It turned and rid the coast, we had minimal impacts. We also had Hurricane Sally hit the panhandle in 2020. But this is just something that people understand how to do and they do.\par \par \uc2 So we love living in Florida, there's so many great things. This is one of the things you got to deal with. But I think the flip side of that is you've got a lot of people in the Emergency Management Committee at both the state and local level, who are true professionals.\par \par \uc2 HICKS: Great. Guys?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Mitti, just one more, if I may. And I know we have to let the governor go. But just ask him for me. You know, COVID obviously, there was a lot of lessons that were learned that in you talk about these elder care facilities, are there lessons from COVID that he is applying to his leadership in this effort?\par \par \uc2 HICKS: So we're one more question, I know you have to run. But the question is, you know, there have been a lot of lessons from COVID, especially in those nursing homes. Are you applying any of those lessons that you learned in COVID to those nursing homes in this situation?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: What was actually interesting, when we handled COVID, we used our emergency management structure to handle COVID. We didn't use as much the health department and some of the others that other states did, because we viewed it as an emergency in these folks. And yes, they're used to do in storms but in terms of the institutional structure, they had the ability to do it. And so, I would say a lot of what we did to help nursing homes and COVID was really born out of emergency management techniques writ large. And so, these are a lot of the techniques we apply to storms anyways.\par \par \uc2 HICKS: Great. Thank you, governor.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. Mitti Hicks, thank you very much. And please pass along our thanks to Governor DeSantis. It's great to have him with us and we hope that he'll join us again as we cover this over the next couple of days. And we wish the best to all of the people of Florida as they get ready for this. Thank you so much to both of you.\par \par \uc2 So pro-life activist Mark Houck pleading not guilty in federal court in Philadelphia just a short time ago. He potentially faces 11 years in prison for allegedly assaulting a 72-year-old Planned Parenthood volunteer shoving him to the ground twice. That's the accusation back in October. Now, Houck was taken into federal custody Friday. His wife claims that a SWAT team of about 25 agents swarmed their home, pointed guns at their family, his attorney back to those claims today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER BREEN, LAWYER FOR MARK HOUCK: Serious questions need to be asked to the attorney general what was he thinking? Why did they do this obscene show of force?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says that those claims are inaccurate and an overstatement, and how it was taken into custody without incident. That story continues to be a one that we're going to cover in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for joining us today, everybody. We'll keep an eye on hurricane in. That's THE STORY. We'll continue coverage as we move throughout the afternoon.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or re