0 states, a gallon of gas in your state of California, Gavin, it costs five dollars and eighty cents, by far the highest in the country.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds and hundreds of thousands of people, they have fled your state since you took office, after well over a hundred and, what, 70 years of population growth? Nobody wanted to leave until you got there. Well done, Gavin.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, FOX News has been the number one cable news channel, thanks to all of you watching, for over 80 straight weeks. So you're right about one thing. We are crushing the competition.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, here now with reaction is the author of the brand new book, "Come On, Man: The Truth About \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's Terrible, Horrible, No Good, Very Bad Presidency" on sale now in bookstores all across the country, amazon.com, hannity.com, Joe Concha is with us.\par \par \uc2 I forgot one other thing. If the state income tax in Florida -- he likes it -- he likes to talk about \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  a lot. State income tax in Florida is zero. The state income tax under \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is the highest in the country at 13-1/2 percent. I can't wait hopefully after the election. I'd love to see that debate between the two of them.\par \par \uc2 Gavin, by the way, we'll have you on this show if you want to debate, considering you're watching Fox all the time.\par \par \uc2 Joe, you watch the media as closely as anybody. This guy wants to be president so bad. I'm even going to make a prediction. He likely will be the 2024 Democratic candidate.\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: That's not that far out there. I don't think because \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's already indicated, "60 Minutes", just a week ago, that he's strongly considering not running. He wouldn't have put that out there if he didn't, and then \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  obviously would be seen as the younger better choice I suppose.\par \par \uc2 But instead of \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , who would never have the guts to sit down with any anchor or host on this network, instead of attacking the primetime hosts here, perhaps he should do a better job in serving the people of California better than he is now.\par \par \uc2 So here are some more facts and Mr. Newsom's welcome to challenge any of these. This isn't narrative. These are facts. As you said, highest income tax rate in the country, highest poverty level in the country.\par \par \uc2 Which governor, by the way, provides free health care to those who enter the country illegally, which may, I don't know, incentivize more people to come here without going through the legal process? Hint, his initials are G.N.\par \par \uc2 And, oh, by the way, California school system ranks 44th in the United States, 44th, Sean. On crime, they are the 36th safest city, I'm sorry, state in the country. But a few FOX News hosts, yeah, that's the real threat.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line is California is the most expensive state in the country to live in. It's profoundly unsafe. Its poverty rate is out of control. Its education system is in shambles, and that's your 2024 nominee. Come on, man.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: By the way, come on, man. Because in honor of your -- the release of your new -- I got my own copy. I'll hold it up for you. I think, there you go, all right, we got two copies. You know, we have a lot.\par \par \uc2 We have old montage. I'll just play my favorite come on man, because there's a lot of come on, man. This though is my favorite come on, man. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INTERVIEWER: But please clarify specifically, have you taken a cognitive?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , THEN-PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: No, I haven't taken a test. Why the hell would I take a test? Come on, man. That's like saying you -- before you got in this program, you take a test where you're taking cocaine or not? What do you think, huh?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Come on, man. Are you a junkie? Can you -- that was the next line. Come on, man. This guy is so off.\par \par \uc2 Now you are the best, in my opinion, media analyst in the country and you're unique because you're not part of the, you know, blue check mark elitist media mob \uc2 Twitter\uc2  cult, and you stand on your own two feet. You take a different narrative and you're far more objective and more knowledgeable. I think you should have your own media show right here on FOX, I've been advocating for it for a while as you know.\par \par \uc2 Her colleagues in the media won't report on him. They're not reporting on the radicalism of these candidates in this election. How dangerous is that to this great republic?\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Well, I know they won't write about him in terms of writing books about him because if you look at the October literary landscape, Sean, there's something like books coming out on a president. And it's not the president currently in office in \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , it's the guy who was in office about 21 months ago and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, yeah, the -- this president is not getting the scrutiny that that he should get in these situations and look, that I think the biggest takeaway for readers in this book, Sean, is that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has always been a left- wing ideologue. He always believed that big government is the answer and not the problem. And now, we see a president with Biden not a senator anymore now he has real power, showing that advocacy over and over again because you don't spend trillions of dollars during a recession and insist it's going to lower inflation unless you believe in big government.\par \par \uc2 But most importantly, Sean, I'll end it here, buy this book just for the chapter on corn pop alone. Yes, a full chapter on the late great pop and it's glorious. So that's why you want to buy this book, for the corn pop, believe me.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: The truth, "Come On, Man: The Truth About \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's Terrible, Horrible, No Good, Very Bad Presidency". By the way, it's even worse than that.\par \par \uc2 Joe, good luck with the book, amazon.com, hannity.com, bookstores everywhere.\par \par \uc2 All right. Now my interview with President Trump last week, it attracted a lot of attention especially over our discussion of the president's power to declassified materials from their time in office. As usual, the left wasted no time attacking the president for saying that he had the authority as president to declassify the documents, but they conveniently forgot about a 2012 ruling from an Obama appointed judge named Amy Berman Jackson the same judge who presided over, oh, \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 's case, oh, and \uc2 Paul Manafort\uc2 's case, in which he ruled in this ruling that the ability to segregate personal records from presidential records is that the sole discretion of the president during his term. And by the way, there's no set standard on how to deal with it.\par \par \uc2 Here with reaction, FOX News legal analyst Gregg Jarrett, and author of the new book, "The Price of Principle", \uc2 Alan Dershowitz\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Professor, let me start with you. Congratulations on the book. I know you're familiar with the case. Under the statutory scheme established by the Presidential Records Act, the decision to segregate personal materials from presidential materials is made by the president during the president's term, in his sole discretion.\par \par \uc2 And then to go on to say, talking about the National Archives, can't be reviewed by a court if the archives wants to challenge a decision. That it is a civil procedure, no criminal penalty, and on the classification issue, both President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , signed executive orders which remain in force to this day declaring the presidents have sweeping authority to declassify and not have to follow any mandatory declassification procedure.\par \par \uc2 Do you agree with that decision and that analysis, sir?\par \par \uc2 ALAN DERSHOWITZ\uc2 , HARVARD LAW PROFESSOR: Not only do I agree with it, but even if it was wrong, there could still not be any criminal charges, criminal investigation, criminal prosecution. This is one of the most complex and disputed areas of law. And in order to be convicted of a crime, you have to prove specific intent.\par \par \uc2 How do you proof expect a specific intent when even the Democrats can't agree what the statutes provide, they can't agree who has the penalty classified, they can't agree what the role of the archives are, when you have disagreement? The one thing that's clear in every liberal and civil libertarian should support me and you on this, you cannot have criminal charges without the kind of certainty that's lacking in this area.\par \par \uc2 Now can they bring a civil suit? Can they try to get them? All right. That's up for dispute.\par \par \uc2 Criminal charges, not up for dispute.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, this case was over tapes involving \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 's -- an interview that he had done, Gregg. \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 's White House, there's a report on "Newsweek" had lost 22 million emails. The Obama administration settled lawsuits over missing Bush White House emails and letters revealed that Obama and his foundation kept classified documents abandoned in a furniture warehouse and, of course, we know about Hillary Clinton's top secret classified emails that \uc2 James Comey\uc2  identified more than they found with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  after the raid and that does not include the deleted 33,0000 emails with Bleach Bit, the devices that were destroyed with hammers and the SIM cards that were removed. We don't know what was on all of that.\par \par \uc2 So, to me, this seems like a dual system of justice and weaponization of the DOJ, am I wrong?\par \par \uc2 GREGG JARRETT, FOX NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: Nobody raided Obama's unsecured open warehouse where he kept classified documents. And the case as you point out the very important case of \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  was litigated in court and the judge said, it's up to \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  the ex-president to decide what is presidential material and what is not including classified documents, he can keep them if he wants.\par \par \uc2 I'll read it here. The judge said: The National Archives does not have the authority to designate materials as presidential records. It lacks any right, duty or means to seize control of them.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Justice in that case argued that it's entirely up to an ex-president, he can keep any records he wants including declassified material and the president has unfettered authority to declassified materials. There are no rules or laws or regulations, it's completely up to him.\par \par \uc2 Look I've argued for seven long weeks since the raid that this was a lawless action by Merrick Garland's Department of Justice and \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2 's \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and as the court pointed out, it is a civil enforcement proceeding under the meaning of the presidential records act. It is not a criminal action.\par \par \uc2 So why did \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  and \uc2 Christopher Wray\uc2  raid Trump's Mar-a-Lago home? Because his last name is Trump. If it were Obama or Clinton, it would be perfectly fine.\par \par \uc2 So if \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  is foolish enough to bring a criminal indictment against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , I look forward to seeing him twist himself into a pretzel trying to explain his own hypocrisy.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Straight ahead, a new report, Alec Baldwin may face criminal charges for his role in the fatal shooting on the set of "Rust." Pam Bondi and Clay Travis, straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Breaking developments tonight in the Alec Baldwin saga. Prosecutors are indicating they may in fact face charges in his case for his role in the fatal shooting of cinematographer Halyna Hutchins on the set of the movie "Rust". Now, this all comes despite his past denial of any wrongdoing like he told ABC News he didn't pull the trigger.\par \par \uc2 Here with the reaction, Outkick founder Clay Travis and former Florida Attorney General Pam Bondi.\par \par \uc2 Pam, interestingly, the D.A. is requesting an additional six hundred and thirty-five thousand dollars, four separate trials of possible jury trials, and one of the -- didn't name the defendants but said one of the possible defendants would possibly be Alec Baldwin.\par \par \uc2 It sounds like he's getting indicted.\par \par \uc2 PAM BONDI, FORMER FLORIDA ATTORNEY GENERLA: It does. It does. It sounds pretty bad for him, Sean, and it's not unusual for a D.A to request more funds in a small county like that, especially if she said she may have to try four separate cases. What was telling and what she said was she wanted to hire experts in safety protocol on sets. Now, it's been reported that there have been numerous complaints about the safety procedures on the set of that movie which he produced he starred in. She also said she wanted to hire experts in firearms and as you said earlier, Alec Baldwin said that he didn't touch the gun trigger it just went off.\par \par \uc2 And it's so easy. I've done it many times with ballistics experts to prove whether or not someone actually pulled a trigger on a gun. So even if he was playing around, pointed it, fired it, knowing all of those things, he could potentially be charged with a second degree murder, reckless disregard for human life, whether or not he intentionally tried to hurt someone.\par \par \uc2 And you know, the bottom line even basic he could be charged with perjury because I think he's given multiple statements reports have said one to the law enforcement officers the day it happened. So I think he's in a world of trouble.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: An ominous posting on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , Clay. Lots of changes coming, but my family has kept me alive. They are my reason for living and his wife as well. Sounds to me like he is expecting to be indicted.\par \par \uc2 CLAY TRAVIS, OUTKICK FOUNDER: Yeah, it does. And, Sean, I remember coming on I believe with Leo on the night that he did that ill-advised, I think Pam would certainly agree and any lawyer out there probably would as well, public statement to tell his side of the story and try to get out ahead of this thing. And to your point ,now he has to make sure that all of his statements that he has made dovetail. And it sounds like there may be some inconsistencies in those statements that are now out there for him to be questioned about.\par \par \uc2 I'd also add on to this, Sean, how many people would this potentially be who are the most ardent \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  critics in the world telling us oh \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's going to face all these felony charges that keep going down with their own felony charges on far more serious related incidents than anything that has happened to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . It just seems like the more aggressive you have been in saying that Trump is Hitler, the worst human being on the planet, the more karma seems to swing around and end up with you in the in the actual legal system, facing far more jeopardy than President Trump does.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: This will to get a lot of national attention. That's for sure.\par \par \uc2 Pam, thank you. Clay Travis, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right. We have more "Hannity" coming up right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. Unfortunately, that is all the time we have left this evening. As always, thank you for being with us and thank you for missing this show possible. We hope that you will set your DVR so you never, ever miss an episode of "Hannity."\par \par \uc2 And in the meantime, you can let not your heart be troubled because Laura Ingraham and the "The Ingraham 