par \uc2 WATTERS: Boom, extends in for another TD. And then this is to Smith in the corner, high up, over the defender.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: We don't care.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Three and O. Redskins go down in flames. And tonight on "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME," \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  had a Fang Fang. It's similar to Swalwell. He had his own little Fang Fang and we'll explore that this evening.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I though he was your guests tonight.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Exactly. He always does that.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: To give you book a promotion.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, if you want to get things done, you have to think outside of the box. So, a 27-year-old woman Karly Pavlinac Blackburn had her mindset for a job at \uc2 Nike\uc2 . And instead of sending a resume through their online portal, she printed it on top of a cake and had it hand-delivered to Nikes headquarter in Oregon, OK. And she now has an upcoming meeting at \uc2 Nike\uc2  and we're wishing her well.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, I am going to be on \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  talking about a very interesting case where the \uc2 FBI\uc2  went in with guns blazing.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: If anybody wants to apply for a job at "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME, " that's how I want the resumes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: The cake?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EARHARDT: Just don't eat the phone number or you won't be able to --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That's good.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right, tonight, we got a great show. We got Sharon Osbourne, Guy Benson, Erin Perrine?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Pretty good. And Kat Timpf.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Is that Ozzy's wife.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You idiot. Let's do this.\par \par \uc2 EARHARDT: She's great. She works with --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Greg's Better Uses For A Face Mask News. This is -- you know, we're done with face masks. Do this, as this man did. He created a little hammock for his parrot in his car. Isn't that fantastic? Look how happy the parrot is right there just relaxing on his morning commute. He doesn't have to worry about a thing, incredibly happy.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: It looks like a Cyber Bohemia.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: A Cyber Bohemian. Extreme great use for a mask. All right, Jessica.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's amazing.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: So, an Indiana teen was the only student in the entire world to ace the AP Calculus AB Exam this year. Felix Zhang, a junior at Penn High School in Mishawaka got a perfect five rating on the College Board. The test administrator notified his principal, 108 out of 108. And 270,000 students took the test, the only perfect score.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Wow.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Yes, it changed your life that you knew that.\par \par \uc2 EARHARDT: That's awesome.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EARHARDT: All right, don't forget I'm going to be signing books. We have our live signing right after the show. Ainsleybook.com. thanks for your support. I love you and I'll see you all in the morning. Thanks for having me on.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's it for us.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, we love having you on.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: "SPECIAL REPORT" up next. Hello, Bret!\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020220927ei9q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Interview with Rep. \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  (R-NY); Interview with Sen. Joe Manchin (D-WV); Russians respond to Putin's Call to Beef up the Battlefield; Jack Keane is Interviewed about Russia, China and Iran; Impact of Trump Investigation; Media Outlets Leave Out Key In Teens Killing\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Lucas Tomlinson, Trey Yingst, Jack Keane, Katie  Pavlich \par \uc2 9,539 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Republicans make their case for the midterms, just weeks before votes that could shift the balance of power in Congress.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): I challenge the president to join with us. Let's go across the country and let's debate what his policies has done to America.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): The GOP's agenda and effort to win over the critical independent voters as the clock ticks down to Election Day.\par \par \uc2 Today, we'll look at the party's plans to fight inflation and keep Americans safe and hold the government accountable.\par \par \uc2 REP. ELISE STEFANIK (R-NY): Parents are the primary stakeholders in their kids' education, which is why we will pass a parents' bill of rights.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: While the president delivers his rebuttal in front of the largest teachers union in America.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: There's a heck of a lot at stake that's on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We will ask \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2 , the number three Republican in the House about her party's blueprint for victory in November, one critics are calling extreme.\par \par \uc2 Then, protests break out across Russia after \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  orders 300,000 Russian reservists to Ukraine. We'll ask retired Four-Star General Jack Keane what he makes of Putin's nuclear threats and the U.S. response.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Joe Manchin's vote was crucial to getting the Democrats' tax, climate and health bill passed last month. But now, the deal he made for that vote to speed up permits for natural gas pipelines and other energy projects is dividing Congress. And that means the government could run out of money this week.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): If there are people willing to vote to shutdown this government because of political reasons they don't like permitting, they have to answer that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll ask the senator about the growing opposition to his plan from the right and the left, only on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT: There is no better example of the left's chilling obsession with targeting political opponents.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  facing more legal peril from New York to Georgia to Florida. We'll ask our Sunday panel what it means for possible 2024 bid.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): And hello again from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 A little more than a month until the midterms, Republicans laying out their agenda should they retake control of the House come November. House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy released the four-point plan Friday in the battleground state of Pennsylvania. The focus: on a nation that's safe, free and prosperous, and a government that's accountable.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats were quick to react, calling the plan thin on details.\par \par \uc2 In the moment, we'll discuss the blueprint with House Republican Conference Chair \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's turn to Lucas Tomlinson traveling with the president in Wilmington for reaction from there.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Shannon. The Dow has dropped to its lowest point in nearly two years as inflation remains at 40-year highs. Many Americans are feeling the squeeze.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: They have no plan to fix all the problems they created.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy rolling out the GOP's commitment to America accusing Democrats of ignoring rising crime, rising prices and parents' rights when it comes to their children's education.\par \par \uc2 President Biden speaking to the nation's largest labor union, public schoolteachers.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy went to Pennsylvania and unveiled on what he calls a commitment to America. That's a -- that's a thin series of policy goals with little or no details.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Biden's address to the teachers offered little to address falling test scores in education. The president using the occasion to make political attacks.\par \par \uc2 Republican Congresswoman Jim banks fired back in a statement: The \uc2 National Education Association\uc2  is basically a partisan lobbying arm for the DNC that irreparably harms students during the COVID pandemic and continues to push anti-American ideologies in classrooms.\par \par \uc2 Some question the president bringing up abortion in front of educators.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: If Republicans win control of the Congress, abortion will be banned.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: the White House refused to clarify if there are any limits.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: I am answering -- I am answering, Peter. What Republicans are trying to do is take us backward. They're trying to take away the rights and freedoms of Americans. That's what we're calling out.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Late Friday, an Arizona judge ruled the state can enforce a total abortion ban. The White House calling the decision catastrophic, dangerous and unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): The White House has postponed President Biden's trip to Florida this week, he was supposed to address a DNC campaign rally in Orlando. That has now been scrubbed because of the approaching tropical storm Ian. This is as Congress is trying to pass a short term spending plan by Friday to avert a government shutdown, one month ahead of the midterms - - Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Lucas Tomlinson, live in Wilmington, Delaware -- Lucas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now from New York is the number three Republican in the House, Congresswoman \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to "FOX News Sunday."\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 ELISE STEFANIK\uc2  (R-NY): Good to be with you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, let's start with this description by "New York Magazine." It says: The document has lots of bells and whistles and factoids about the hellish reign of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his Democrat Party. What it doesn't have is a whole lot of specificity.\par \par \uc2 You heard that from the president, from other Democrats, too, who say, there are a lot of slogans. Yes, everybody wants a safe country. They want good education. They want crime to go down.\par \par \uc2 But it's the details of how you do that that matter. So, why not more details in this plan?\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: Shannon, there are ample details.\par \par \uc2 So, number one, an economy that's strong. The first focus on economic issues is we need to rein in inflation. We start by doing that by reining in the reckless trillions of dollars of spending that we have seen under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and unify Democrats far left government.\par \par \uc2 Number two, lower the price of energy, I hear that from my constituents in Upstate New York, whether it's home heating bills or the price of gas. We have multiple bills that have already been introduced that Democrats refuse to bring up when it comes to lowering those energy costs.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to supporting our law enforcement and having a nation that's safe, we have legislation that'll immediately provide funding through recruitment and retention bonuses to hire additional 200,000 police officers across this country.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to freedom -- a future that's built upon freedom -- I highlighted the Parents Bill of Rights. This is a bill that Republicans already introduced and we are going to have ready to pass in the first 100 days in the next Congress.\par \par \uc2 Day one, we intend to repeal the 87,000 IRS agents, the army of \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and Democrats have put into place to go after hardworking Americans, moms and pops, small business, and middle class Americans.\par \par \uc2 So, Democrats have no plans, they are attacking us because they are created crises across the country. We have the commitment to tackle the crisis that Democrats have created to make sure that we have a strong economy, a safe country, a future that's built upon freedom and a government that's accountable.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, one of the accusations about why there aren't more details, specific pieces of legislation are you're trying to hold the conference together. \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2 , the House majority leader, Democrat, says that this was about distracting from your narrative and your own deep divisions.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " also talks about this in the context to the Freedom Caucus, the more conservative members of the GOP, saying: They're expected to make starker demands of leadership in exchange for their votes.\par \par \uc2 I talked to some members of the Freedom Caucus who do feel like they are being taken for granted a bit in this process. What would you say to them and about accusations you guys are divided?\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: I would say the media is trying to divide us. The Democrats are trying to divide us.\par \par \uc2 We are united, the House Republicans, whether it's the Freedom Caucus, whether it's the Republican Governance Group or the Republican Study Committee. All members of Congress were able to provide input in this development of the Commitment to America.\par \par \uc2 But, most importantly, Shannon, the American people, our constituents provided input in this process. Those are where our best ideas come.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is, Democrats have no plans when it comes to inflation. They have no plan when it comes to the crime crisis or the border crisis. These crises have been created by Democrats, unifying control of government in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have a plan. We have legislation on each and every one of these issues, and we can't wait to get it done starting on day one as we earn back the House majority.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, that's a big promise to potential voters out there to cast their ballots for you.\par \par \uc2 There are those out there who will remember that the GOP promised to do things like repeal Obamacare. They got the Senate and the House back, they didn't do it.\par \par \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2 , again, one of the top Democrats on the other side, said: When Republicans did last control Congress. It was a, quote, do-nothing Congress. They were more about obstruction and not actually delivering.\par \par \uc2 So, how do you convince voters this time is different?\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: Well, first of all, \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2  is dead wrong. Look at the success. Look at the economic growth that we saw under Republican leadership, working with the House, the Senate, and the White House.\par \par \uc2 We have the most secure border in our nation's history. That's compared to the most unsecured border under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . It's been a crisis with over 3 million illegals who have crossed the border. And those are the ones that we count. That doesn't include the significant number of gotaways.\par \par \uc2 So, this is a crisis that Democrats have created themselves. The American people are smart. They know that while \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  started his inauguration day trying to unify the country, they have done nothing but dividing -- divided the country.\par \par \uc2 So, Republicans are going to achieve this, I think not only we are going to earn back the House, but we are going to earn back the Senate as well. And we will send every bit of legislation that we promised to the president's desk and it's up to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  whether he works with us or whether he will play politics and veto these important bills to rein in inflation, to support law enforcement to make sure we have a safe country, and to secure the border which is in free fall under Biden's amnesty policies.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, let's talk about the border, because a recent Reuters-Ipsos poll said that these -- what many Americans view as political stunts, busing and flying people into blue cities, or sanctuary places where they can then -- there are questions about what they have been told about going there.\par \par \uc2 Democrats say you don't have a better plan, and they don't think that this is a good look.\par \par \uc2 In that poll, a majority of Americans said that they don't think it's okay to be transporting people this way. These are real people with real lives.\par \par \uc2 Here's Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , Democrat, on these transports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): They should start behaving like governors and stop behaving like human traffickers. They are putting politics over people in the most egregious way possible.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, how do you respond to those who say the optics of this aren't good for Republicans, that these are real people?\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: Well, first of all, \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  should start acting like a leader rather than -- rather than a partisan hack. We have this border crisis because of the amnesty policies and the wide open border that's happened under Democrat government, under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . This is the most insecure border in our nation's history.\par \par \uc2 Go to South Texas, we have a president of the United States and a vice president who refused to go to the southern border. The vast majority of House Republicans have gone to the Southern border. We've seen with our own eyes Del Rio, Eagle Pass. We've seen that this is a humanitarian crisis, an economic crisis, a national security crisis.\par \par \uc2 All of these border issues, all of this immigration crisis is a result of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's failure. We have the most secure border in our nation's history, as I said, under President Trump.\par \par \uc2 We know the policies that work. It's build the wall. It's end catch and release. And it's to make sure that we have a rule of law in this country.\par \par \uc2 So, Republicans will absolutely work to secure the border.\par \par \uc2 Shame on \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  for being a partisan pawn. He has a record as being the most pro-amnesty, one of the most pro-amnesty Democrats in this current Congress.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is, Republicans are polling plus 39 when it comes to border security in the most recent NBC poll. We're plus 19 on the issue of immigration.\par \par \uc2 So, this is -- this is an issue of safety. It's an issue of security and it's an issue of who we are, the United States of America.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: Republicans will secure the border.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But when Republicans have controlled the White House, House and Senate, immigrations did not get resolved. The border was not fully secured.\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: But the border -- we made significant end roads. If you look at numbers of illegal crossings, that has skyrocketed, Shannon, under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . The portions of the border wall are significant. That was more constructed under President Biden and Republican leadership than ever before in our nation's history.\par \par \uc2 We were addressing the border security crisis, and that has unraveled starting on day one under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who has created this crisis. And the American people know that and they're going to hold Democrats accountable for it this November.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's talk about this. Senator Schumer, across the aisle and across the Hill had a critique on the plan that you rolled out this week. He said it's MAGA GOP extremist policies. He says a nationwide abortion ban, Medicare and Social Security on the chopping block, raising taxes on American families.\par \par \uc2 He's able to cite and specifically point to GOP policies for those accusations. Is that a party blunder?\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: Absolutely not, Shannon. And \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  has proposed the highest tax increase in our nation's history. This inflation expansion bill that Democrats just ramp through has tax increases on every working American. Republicans are on the party of lowering taxes. And that's in stark contrast to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , and \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  who have raised taxes.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to Social Security and Medicare, Republicans are committed to saving and ensuring those programs exist for future generations, and that means making sure that they are fixed, to exist for my generation and my son's generation.\par \par \uc2 And when it comes to the issue of life, Republicans are proudly pro-life. We support our most vulnerable. I am the newest mom in Congress and I know how important it is to stand up for life.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is attacking and it's because they have no plan.\par \par \uc2 Americans need a change of course. We need true leadership in both the House and Senate who will focus on the crises that hardworking families are facing across this country.\par \par \uc2 So, of course, Democrats are going to attack because, Shannon, they can't run on their record because it's a record of crisis and failure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Chairwoman, we thank you for joining us to make the case to voters for the midterms and we will see you again soon. Thanks for coming on.\par \par \uc2 STEFANIK: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, Senator Joe Manchin once again in the middle of key legislation on the Hill as his pipeline deal sparks objections on both sides. Can he get it done? He joins us live next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: When Joe Manchin struck a deal with \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  last month to pass Democrats' climate, health and tax bill, it came with the price. That price: to reform the tedious federal permitting process for new energy projects.\par \par \uc2 But now, that bill is facing stiff opposition from both progressives and staunch Republicans. And now, Schumer is trying to find a way to keep his promise to Manchin, while avoiding a potential government shutdown. This is all tied together.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now from West Virginia, Senator Joe Manchin, also chair of the Senate Energy and Natural Resources Committee.\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): Hey, good -- good to be with you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, let's start with "The Wall Street Journal's" assessment of what's actually in the deal. Of course, you would get significant benefits to the \uc2 Mountain Valley Pipeline\uc2 , very important for West Virginia, to the Southeast.\par \par \uc2 But they say this: It creates new regulatory risks for fossil fuels which is the opposite of what he promised. Mr. Manchin had political leverage but the bill shows he traded his votes on the cheap.\par \par \uc2 Did you, Senator?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 The article is not accurate and we have a reply. The reply will be going back in tomorrow, and it will go into tomorrow to "Wall Street Journal". So, you'll be able to see the reply, and basically answering every one of the things which they basically have evaluated wrongly.\par \par \uc2 But this is a chance in a lifetime for us to have energy independence, Shannon. For us to have security in this nation, which we need, and making sure that we can take care of the American people with low energy prices, producing more oil, producing more gas.\par \par \uc2 But we have to have permitting reform if you're going to deliver it. You have no way of infrastructure to deliver it. So, everyone knows that. My Republican friends know that.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They do. And your Republican colleague and friend there in West Virginia, your fellow senator, \uc2 Shelley Moore Capito\uc2 , has offered her own piece.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: She did that also as an amendment of the reconciliation bill. You didn't vote for that amendment.\par \par \uc2 If permitting was so important, why not voted for it then when she offered it?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, hers is a messaging bill, and we understand that.\par \par \uc2 In the reconciliation basically, we have a process that we're using, and I'm happy to say that Shelley, my friend, \uc2 Shelley Moore Capito\uc2 , and we work together, we put our country and our state before our political parties. She's totally supported of my approach basically as incorporated many of the things that she and 46 other Republican senators also had endorsed and agreed.\par \par \uc2 I just hope we can come together as Americans and forget about our party, the party politics that's going on and make this opportunity, a reality that gives us security for our nation, that gives us independence and be able to lower their gas prices, lower the energy prices in America and help our allies around the world.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, certainly, you understand where Republicans don't think they should be the ones come across the aisle to vote for a measure that primarily benefits you, which was done in exchange for your vote on a measure they didn't support -- not a single one of them vote for the Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 And "Politico" says this. They are quoting Senator \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2 , the top Republican.\par \par \uc2 Given what Senator Manchin did on the reconciliation bill, it's engendered a lot of bad blood. There's not a lot of sympathy on our side to provide Senator Manchin a reward.\par \par \uc2 So, how can you expect them to vote for this now?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Shannon, this is not about me. My Republican friends, I've been working for 12 years with them, and I know their number one item that they've had, the number one priority they've had is permitting reform.\par \par \uc2 We can't build anything in America. It takes five to 10 years. The developed world takes one to three years. And why should we so behind the developed world to bringing products to market, to be able to have the infrastructure to move energy around? And we're asking people around the world to do things for us, we won't do for ourself?\par \par \uc2 My Republican friends know exactly where we are. This is not about the previous legislation. This is something, a high priority that we have for our country. It's a -- it's a time in history.\par \par \uc2 If we don't, Shannon, take advantage of this and come together as Americans, we're going to look back five or 10 years from now and wonder why we're not able to meet demands, why are we allowing Putin to kind of control, dictate our energy policies, and what we are trying to respond to and not able to do it in a timely fashion because we can't move the energy in America -- whether it's going to be new transmission lines for renewables or basically for fossils and oil and gas that moves the products we need today. That's what this is about. And we need to come together as Americans.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, Senator Capito has said she would support your measure. She will vote for it.\par \par \uc2 Would you vote for hers if yours doesn't move and hers is offered as a separate measure?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, hers -- if it was a Republican and she knows that, if the Republicans were in the majority, I'm the only Democrat that's ever voted for really truly permitting reform. There was only one vote from my friend Dan Sullivan and his reform. And I -- if they were in control, I would do as I've been doing for the last 12 years, I would vote for permitting reform.\par \par \uc2 I'm asking this them to take the opportunity you finally have to make something happen in America, that we can deliver energy that we need, to lower gas prices at the pump, to lower energy prices in your home, and be able to take care and keep ourselves secure and independent, and be able to help our people and Europe who are hurting very badly and allowing Putin to put this absolutely, using energy as a weapon.\par \par \uc2 And it's just unbelievable that we're in this position right now with what Putin is doing and the world is changing, of what he's doing and the carnage he's putting on the people of Ukraine, innocent men and women.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: We have to respond.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And we're going to talk about that a little bit later in the show. It's reprehensible. And people in this country do want energy independence. There are those who want to try to balance that against, you know, the climate protections as well.\par \par \uc2 But you got to convince not only Republicans but Democrats within your own party as well.\par \par \uc2 So, let's talk about some of them. A group of eight senators sent a letter to the Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- said no way this measure should be tied to the funding bill. We ran out of money at the federal government next week. They said: We believe such important issues should be examine through detailed committee consideration in a robust floor debate separate from the urgent need to see the government stays open.\par \par \uc2 Their letter outlines their worry -- their worries. They're progressive. They're worried about the climate, and they say your measure would actually disproportionately hurt low income Americans and communities of color if it does get passed.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Shannon, let me just be very clear and very honest with you -- I never expected \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and the far extreme left to ever be for any permitting. They've never been for permitting reform. That's why we never had it.\par \par \uc2 This is one time when overwhelmingly, Democrats in both the House and the Senate are supporting. And from my Republican friends not to see that, which I know they do, and not be able to pass something they've been able to do for twelve years since I have been working with them, it would be basically a lost moment in history if we don't do this.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm hoping that they'll look at what we have in front of us -- the energy independence, security stopping Putin dead in his tracks, being able to do what we need to do to reduce the price of energy and helping people in their homes as far as energy cost there. We have a golden opportunity and we have a majority, overwhelming majority of Democrats supporting it.\par \par \uc2 And they're doing it because this is the right time and the right thing to do for our country. But, also, it's a time we basically can bring Democrats and Republicans together as we've done with the bipartisan infrastructure bill. We've done it with the CHIPS bill. And we continue to do things when we need to do something for our country.\par \par \uc2 This is the most important thing that we can do today. And it's the most critical -- critical problem that we have in front of us which is energy independence and security.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, as you look to try to get those votes together from both sides, here is something that Senator Kennedy, Republican out of Louisiana, had to say this week about this whole thing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN KENNEDY\uc2  (R-LA): I'm just a little surprised. Joe's been around here a long time, and he's a very -- he's my friend but he -- and he's a savvy politician.\par \par \uc2 You know, he made a deal about without -- without having all the ducks in a row. I'm even sure they know where the ducks are right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Did you miscalculate on the ducks, Senator?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: No. John is my dear friend. What a great person. I just enjoy him very much. And the thing, we have good conversations.\par \par \uc2 When they say they weren't involved, everything I've done for the last 12 years working with my Republican colleagues has been incorporated in this. We have talked about it and worked about it. So, they have tremendous amount of input in this.\par \par \uc2 But the process has come down to party line vote which is unfortunate, but it is what we're dealing with in a toxic political atmosphere. But I can assure you, everybody, we try to take everyone's input on this and my Republican friends' input is in this piece of legislation.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm just hoping that and I'm very optimistic that we have the opportunity, they realize this opportunity and they'll never pass again in our lifetime.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: When they had everything, when they -- in 2016 to 2020, Shannon, they had Republican president, Republican House, Republican Senate, we couldn't -- we couldn't move it because I was the only Democrat.\par \par \uc2 Now we're in a position where we have a majority Democrats and our Republican friends can take it across the line.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll see if they do.\par \par \uc2 This all traces back to your vote on the so-called Inflation Reduction Act. The Penn Wharton budget model and the CBO both found that it had statistically just negligible effect on inflation.\par \par \uc2 Right now, Americans are continuing to pay more for everything, including just groceries. We look at August to August, 13-1/2 percent jump last week. And then the markets are plunging and there's this headline. Billionaire investor \uc2 Carl Icahn\uc2  warns the worst is yet to come for investors and compares U.S. inflation to the fall of the Roman Empire.\par \par \uc2 So, what regrets do you have at this point in voting for the Inflation Reduction Act which hasn't cool things off, it's not projected to short term or long-term, in exchange for this deal now that may no actually go anywhere on energy?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Inflation is horrible and is extremely -- it's extremely damaging to our economy, and damaging to every West Virginia and every American. So, we have to fight inflation.\par \par \uc2 The Inflation Reduction Act, basically, first of all, does, puts more energy back into the markets. So, if you want to reduce prices, then put more products out there. If you want to get your gasoline prices down, produce more oil. If you want to get your home heating, produce more natural gas. Do it cleaner than anywhere in the world, replace the dirty energy around the world. And that basically helps the climate and gives us more product.\par \par \uc2 When you reduce basically the Medicare prices of drugs -- in West Virginia, 300,000 West Virginians rely on Medicare. We're reducing their pricing on drugs and so that has to reduce it.\par \par \uc2 When they're saying it's negligible, they're going to see it in the next 30 days? No, will they see it? Absolutely they will see it because this does nothing but gives us a chance to reduce inflation. And I -- you know, I have worked very hard to make sure we had an opportunity to be energy- independent and secure and fight Putin's war. I understood that -- I understood that \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and my left wing of the Democratic Party would never support anything that would let us be energy-independent with the energy we have in our own country.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, Senator, you have made the case. We'll see if your Republicans and Democrat colleagues are listening as you take those votes this week. Senator, thank you very much. We'll keep an eye on it.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Thank you, Shannon. I appreciate being with you. And I sure do put the country first and America. It's all about America, not about Democrats or Republicans.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's what Americans are hoping for. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 Up next, protests break out across Russia as discontent grows over Putin's order to draft more troops to prop up his struggling military campaign in Ukraine. We'll go live to Kyiv and discuss the fallout when "FOX News Sunday" returns.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Moscow's war on Ukraine taking central stage again as world leaders gather at the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  in New York. \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's call for 300,000 reservice to fight in Ukraine drawing swift condemnation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Let us speak plainly, a permanent member of the United Nation Security Council invaded its neighbor, attempted to erase the sovereign state from the map. Russia has shamelessly violated the core tenants of the United Nations charter.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  calling on world powers to stand up to Russia.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , SECRETARY OF STATE: Every council member should send a clear message that these reckless nuclear threats must stop immediately.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  also making another dramatic plea to the \uc2 U.N\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINE: Russia is afraid of real negotiations and doesn't want to fulfill any fear (ph) international obligations.\par \par \uc2 We must finally recognize Russia is a state sponsor of terrorism.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: These developments have many wondering where things stand now more than six months into the invasion. In a moment we'll discussed with retired Four-Star General Jack Keane.\par \par \uc2 But first, let's turn to Trey Yingst in Kyiv with a look at how Russians are responding to Putin's call to beef up the battlefield.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Trey.\par \par \uc2 TREY YINGST, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Shannon, more Russian troops are being forced into this war as Ukrainian soldiers make territorial gains, putting new pressure on Moscow.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Russian reservists hugged their loved ones good-bye before loading onto buses. These men are some of the 300,000 people called up to fight in Ukraine under partial mobilization orders given by President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I will go. Do I have an alternative, one man asks, or I will get criminally prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 The new soldiers lacked both experience and moral. Much like the Russian public, they're being dragged into a war they didn't sign up to fight. Anti-mobilization protests erupted across major Russian cities this week that saw more than 1,400 people arrested, further raising pressure on the Russian leader. A mass exodus at the country's borders, prompting neighboring Finland to restrict Russian tourists from entering.\par \par \uc2 Putin trying to do all he can to make up for significant battlefield losses in recent weeks. Today, annexation referendums are underway in Russian occupied areas of Ukraine. The voting to become part of Russia was described as a sham and illegitimate by Ukraine and its western allies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 YINGST: Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  was very clear in recent weeks, every part of this country will be liberated by his troops despite these referendums. As Zelenskyy describes it, it is Ukraine or death.\par \par \uc2 Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Trey Yingst, live in Kyiv. Trey, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Joining us here in Washington, retired Four-Star General Jack Keane, chairman of the Institute for the Study of War, and Fox News senior strategic analyst.\par \par \uc2 General, we are always grateful for your insights. Thanks for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET), FOX NEWS SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: Yes, wonderful to be here and congratulations.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 So, let's start on where Trey touched on, these referenda that are being held in Ukraine. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reports it this way, it's as Ukrainians were forced to vote while Russian officials or their proxy stood guard. The aim of the hastily called referendums, supported by pro-Russian residents and their proxies, as apparent to give President Putin of Russia a legally bogus pretext to gobble up their country.\par \par \uc2 What are we to make of these votes?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, he's seeking - he's just had a military defeat that was quite sizable. He's got to conclude that, at least night now, he's losing his war.\par \par \uc2 And this is an attempt to obtain a political victory, largely for his own domestic audience, that they have gobbled up this increased terrain in four different regions in Ukraine. I mean anybody looking at this knows it's a sham, as Trey pointed out and other commentators have. I mean this is a vote at gunpoint and it's not going to mean anything. And it certainly is not going to stop the Ukrainians from continuing their military operations inside these territories. After all, they have conducted some significant attacks inside of Crimea. Espionage attacks and also missile attacks, which have been annexed by Russia since 2014.\par \par \uc2 So, the Ukrainian operations will continue. The political sham, I think, is what it is. And the whole international community sees it as such.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And it seems like these referendums will also tie into some saber rattling over nuclear use by Putin. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " talks about this speech he had on Wednesday. They say he made two references or two threats. He reminded \uc2 NATO\uc2  that Russia possesses more modern weapons than the weapons \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries have and Putin pledged that Russia would use all weapons systems available to protect its territorial integrity, Russia's people, as well as Russia's independence and freedom.\par \par \uc2 How do you take those nuclear threats?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, well, first of all, the -- I think the probability of it is pretty low, but you have to take it seriously because of what these weapons, obviously, and the horrific applications and what it would mean to Ukraine and to the world rite large. But what's his motivation here? And I think he's brandished these weapons before, or at least he's had his surrogates do it. And I think he's had some success with it because it delayed the distribution of advanced weapons that Zelenskyy was begging for, for weeks and weeks and weeks. And it finally took months before we got those systems to him. He likely knows that on the table the United States is considering sending to Ukraine advanced fighters, like F-16s, after training the Ukrainians, tanks, infantry fighting vehicles and other equipment that will help the Ukrainians with a spring counteroffensive, which would be conventional, combined arms to drive them out of the Donbas region. So, he likely would like to curve that.\par \par \uc2 Now, let's assume he's serious and the use of a nuclear weapon is actually on the table, which I think is probably very low. But if you consider that, here's the factors he's got to consider. He would use the nuclear weapon and want to end the war. And that's not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainians, the people, as well as their military, are steadfast. They openly talk about the possibility of a nuclear weapon being targeted against them and they are going to fight and continue to fight.\par \par \uc2 The second thing is, his army on the battlefield, they're not trained to deal with a radioactive nuclear battlefield. The Soviet Union was. They were equipped to properly trained. These guys aren't trained to fight a conventional fight, much less deal with an environment like that.\par \par \uc2 And then the third thing is, this is about taking it serious. I believe the Biden administration has reported that they're talking to Putin and his surrogates about the consequences here. And I hope we're very direct with him about what their consequences would mean. I think that's likely an expansion of the war. It brings the United States and \uc2 NATO\uc2  into it. Not to use nuclear weapons, we wouldn't do that. I don't even think we would put a significant amount of ground troops. But we would conduct devastating air and missile attack, largely to destroy his military, inside of Ukraine. And he knows that and so does his military people know that. He wants to win the war. If he uses a nuclear weapon, it guarantees that he loses.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so let's turn to another region of the world also filled with tension at this moment. "60 Minutes," the president said last week, it - we would come, U.S., to Taiwan's defense if China launched an unprovoked attack. And then there was walking back some of that, clarification of that.\par \par \uc2 The Council on Foreign Relations\uc2  said, while pledging to defend Taiwan from China, Biden shifted on Taiwan independence and it matters because it says the White House has clarified, or they're trying to, that U.S. policy hasn't changed, but officials in Beijing will see this as further evidence that the U.S. is walking away from a one China policy.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Well, I think the American people must scratch their head here and say, what is going on. In 1979 there was a Taiwan Relations Act that China and the United States agreed to. And the essential ingredient in it that it would govern China's and the United States' behavior as it pertained to the Taiwan Straits. The central theme was peace and stability along those straits. And we said that we would help Taiwan defend itself, made no statements about whether we would physically help them to defend themselves in terms of our own troops. That is called the strategic ambiguity.\par \par \uc2 But, in the last 10 years, look what has happened. President Xi has accelerated the military intimidation and cohesion of Taiwan. Not only that, on repeated occasions he has said, if - if Taiwan does not capitulate and become a part of mainland China, then he will use military force to take control.\par \par \uc2 Now, all of this activity that he's done in 10 years to include threatening military force, I would suggest he has violated the 1979 Taiwan agreement to maintain peace and stability. And I think it's quite appropriate, and even more than that essential, that President Biden make a public declaration -- he said it four times already in response to a question -- but make it a public declaration that Mr. Xi, if you're going to attack Taiwan, you are forcing me to have to respond military to defend it. And I would encourage my allies to do the same.\par \par \uc2 I think that's where we are anyway. Certainly, that is where the president of the United States is. And he is the decisionmaker.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We're just about out of time, so I quickly want to ask you to comment on Israel. Their prime minister making comments at the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . this week. He says we'll do whatever it takes. Iran will not get a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, what we have to understand here, you know, you can have an academic discussion about whether nuclear weapons would be used in the Middle East by Iran. That's not relevant. The only thing that's relevant here is, Israel is convinced that if Iran has a nuclear weapon, it's an existential threat because they have repeatedly said for 40 years, they intend to destroy the state of Israel. And I believe they've had intelligence for a number of years that indicates that Iran would do just that. And the fact that they have 9 million people, densely located in a small country, and Iran had 86 million people in the mountainous large country, that Iran had seriously considered destroying the state of Israel and absorbing a nuclear attack themselves and believing that they could survive it.\par \par \uc2 Now, does that make any sense to anybody? No. But that's not important. The fact is, the Israelis believed it. And they believe that Iran clearly has this intent to destroy the state of Israel. So, this is a dangerous point we're at. It can lead to conflict, whether there is or not a nuclear deal, because Iran is in pursuit of a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: General, thank you. Sobering analysis, but very insightful. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Good talking to you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You too.\par \par \uc2 All right, up next, more legal setbacks for former President Trump as he hits the trail with more midterm candidates. We're going to bring in our Sunday group to discuss Trump's latest legal troubles and whether all the investigations could put an end to his `24 hopes.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LETITIA JAMES, NEW YORK ATTORNEY GENERAL: Claiming you have money that you do not have does not amount to the art of the deal, it's the art of the steal.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: Well, she campaigned on it four years ago. It was a vicious campaign. And she just talked about Trump, and we're going to indict him, we're going to get him. She knew nothing about me. I never heard of her.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  firing back at New York Attorney General Letitia James, who is suing Trump, his family business and three of his children for fraud and overvaluing their assets as the former president's legal problems grow.\par \par \uc2 It is time now for our Sunday group.\par \par \uc2 Reuter's White House correspondent Jeff Mason, editor of townhall.com Katie Pavlich, former Tennessee Congressman Harold Ford Jr., and Howard Kurtz, host of MediaBuzz.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to all of you.\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, EDITOR, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, HOST, "MEDIABUZZ": Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Good to see you, too.\par \par \uc2 All right, Howie, let's start with you because the president now has multiple states, federal issues, there's a lot going on, and he can have these interviews and press conferences but now it's in court and the judges are starting to crack down and not everything' going his way.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Well, I have conducted an intensive review of this and I've concluded that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is spending a lot of money on lawyers.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's true.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Look, this Letitia James civil suit in New York, it does have a partisan stench because she did run for office vowing to go after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and she's out there accusing him of crimes that she can't prosecute after the Manhattan DA declined to bring criminal charges. The Justice Department probe is back on track after an appeal. As well the special master is giving the Trump team a hard time. And it seems to me that even Republican senators are saying he can't just mentally declassify documents.\par \par \uc2 But if there's one silver lining here for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  it is this, the more that he is pursued and persecuted, his word, Republicans feel that he's under attacked by his political enemies, and he still has a virtual lock on the nomination.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, and Politico goes to that point. In this piece on Thursday they say his troubles are mounting but his support is consolidating. One Republican strategist who works with hardline congressional candidates around the country said, the more Democrats keep going after him, the better it is.\par \par \uc2 And, Jeff, it adds to his argument that the establishment, the deep state is after him.\par \par \uc2 JEFF MASON, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, REUTERS: It does. I mean if -- Donald trump is someone who has been able to avoid all rules of politics ever since he came on the political, national stage. He has said things. He has done things. There have been allegations against him that would take down nearly any other political candidates. They don't, and haven't, with president - former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 In this case, as - and Howie's right, he's got lawyers for a reason. It may not be the politics that brings him down. It could very possibly be the legal - the legal system that brings him down. But, politically, it has been up until now, and almost certainly will continue to be useful for him.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, there's that argument. And on the other side there are Republicans who are looking at the midterms. And they know Democrats want to be talking about the former president.\par \par \uc2 Dick Wadhams is a long-time GOP strategist. He's quoted in this article. He says, if we lose a lot of Senate races, or House races for that matter, that we should have won, and that can be traced back to the emergence of Trump once again as such a dominant figure in the midterm election campaign, I think that will probably hurt him long-term.\par \par \uc2 Katie.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Well, look, Democrats have dumped millions of dollars into primary races trying to get Trump candidates elected. That's something that they think is beneficial to them. This is also something they did, if you remember going back to 2015 and 2016, they all propped up \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  thinking they could beat him and then he beat \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  in that election.\par \par \uc2 So, when it comes to what they want to be talking about, of course \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is in the news with his legal woes. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is off at the top of the headlines. That's good for them when it comes to inflation, especially the market dipping this week. But also it helps with Republican voters in the sense that they do feel like the government has repeatedly brought these charges against the president. They have not been able to prove them. Here we go again with the Department of Justice and now the New York attorney general, who is running for election again on this idea of going after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That is something they like when it comes the narrative. But Republicans see it as another chance to hold government accountable and to roll back this idea that the legal system and federal law enforcement or state law enforcement should be used for political purposes rather than to go after actual criminality.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I mean she did, Letitia James, the attorney general there in New York, talked about that she would make this a big part of her job.\par \par \uc2 Trump Attorney Alina Habba says this, it's solely focused on advancing her political agenda. She says, we're confident the judicial system won't stand for this unchecked abuse of authority.\par \par \uc2 Harold.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR. (D), FORMER TENNESSEE CONGRESSMAN: Well, first off, happy Sunday.\par \par \uc2 I - it's amazing when politicians say they're going to investigate people, and then they do it. I think that one of the things that Republicans in the Congress are saying is that they're going to investigate the Bidens, Fauci, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 . And if they get the majority, perhaps they will do it.\par \par \uc2 I don't know all the details around and legality of these things. The thing that has struck me the most, and how we touched on it, is a special master who both the Trump team and the Justice department agreed to in this matter involving the search warrant executed on President Trump's home down in Florida. The president said for a long time he declassified things. He said that stuff was planted. And the special master has asked for some evidence around that.\par \par \uc2 So, for me, that -\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: So, just, put up or shut up.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. I mean you can just - you can't just say it and then walk into court and not say it.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Yes, I was trying to be kind because this is a president. But so - so, to me, that - whether it's the DOJ, whether it's Letitia James, whatever the case may be, it seems to me what's really got the president in a bind are the things he's saying. He'd be a tough client. You can't really control him and you don't know if he's telling you something that he's not telling - he's not telling you something he's telling other people.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, he is keeping attorneys busy.\par \par \uc2 I want to make sure that we talk about this case out of North Dakota.\par \par \uc2 This 18-year-old, Cayler Ellingson, who was killed in a hit and run by a 41-year-old man named Shannon Brandt. There's been little reporting more broadly across media. We have been following this case. The man said that this was over a political dispute.\par \par \uc2 Tom Bevan, who heads up RealClearPolitics, the founder of it, he talked about a \uc2 CBS\uc2  story. He tweeted this. He says, amazing, CBS News does a 400- word write-up of the North Dakota nut who ran down and killed a teenager over a political argument without mentioning he told police he thought the teen was a Republican political extremist.\par \par \uc2 Howie, this is a media story as much as it is a tragedy. What do you make of it?\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: I make of it that it sounds like it should be a much bigger story. I mean for someone to lose their life over some sort of political dispute. You would think maybe people don't - there are - the media agenda isn't furthered by pushing this. But it seems like it's been treated as a mere footnote, one that's an absolute tragedy. And I would like to know more about it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Harold, would this be a different story if this person was a MAGA, you know, individual who mowed down an 18-year-old Democrat, a young man?\par \par \uc2 FORD: First off, our prayers go to that family and to that community.\par \par \uc2 What disturbs me most about this story is that somehow -- we don't know all the facts yet. It appears that maybe he wasn't a - they didn't know one another. There wasn't a political argument. But what's amazing to me is that this guy who killed this 18-year-old kid thought he might get some sympathy.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 FORD: He thought he may get some kind of mitigating factor here if he said that the kid was a Republican or he had a political view that was different than his. We plunge into a very deep and terrible place if we can't all recognize, we may agree on some thing, disagree on other things. But to kill someone, and 18-year-old, whatever his age may be, because of a political agreement, is asinine and, frankly, doesn't speak - bode well for our political system.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I want to get a quick comment from both of you all about the political rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 Katie to you, and then Jeff.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Well, look, yes, we've seen the media erase Democrat from a lot of these stories. "The Las Vegas Tribune" had a journalist who was killed by a Democratic - or allegedly killed, he's been charged with murder, and Democratic politician. We saw a man who was upset about Justice Kavanaugh's pro-life views or his - the way he had done his opinions on the abortion issue show up and try to assassinate him outside of his home. The president has talked a lot about extremist rhetoric. We haven't heard him talk about this specific case. So maybe he should make a statement about this on both sides of the political aisle.\par \par \uc2 MASON: I would just say, political rhetoric can be dangerous and it's dangerous on both sides. And in this case it was tragic.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: On both sides.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Well said. Well said.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and we have seen evidence of that, unfortunately, here and across the country.\par \par \uc2 All right, panel, stick around. We will see you next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Before we go, though, a military couple together for nearly 20 years celebrating a big milestone on the very same day. We're going to share what they credit for their continued successes as they serve our country, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And now to some good news on this Sunday morning, because who doesn't need that.\par \par \uc2 The story of a military couple with a whole lot to celebrate. Meet Lt. Col. Courtney O'Brien (ph), and Lt. Col. Michael O'Brien (ph). These two Marine leaders have been married for 17 years. They've got two children. Earlier this month, Courtney took the helm commanding the Marine Ariel Refueler Transport Squadron. And, just down the flight line, on the same day, Michael took command of his own squadron. The O'Brien's credit each other for their successes. Michael says his wife, who is, by the way, the all- time leading scorer on the Naval Academy's women's basketball team, always reminds him of the rules and regulations. While Courtney says her husband encourages her to be more extroverted and assertive with communication.\par \par \uc2 Congrats on your new commands. We thank you both for your service.\par \par \uc2 That's going to be it for us on this Sunday. Have a great week. It's going to be a busy one here in Washington. And we will see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020220925ei9p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Stocks Drop After Federal Reserve Interest Rate Hike; Republicans Roll Out Commitment To America Agenda; President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Speaks To Union Teachers In Washington; Lawmakers Spar As Possible Government Shutdown Looms; Republicans Hope To Retake Kansas Governorship; Occupied Territories Hold Kremlin-Stage Vote; Ukrainian Soldiers Describe Alleged Torture; House Foreign Affairs Committee Chairman \uc2 Gregory Meeks\uc2  (D-NY) Interviewed on Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  's Call Up Of Reservists To Fight In Ukraine And Suspension Of Negotiations With Iran Over Nuclear Deal; Medical Examiner's Office In Texas Unable To Accommodate Bodies Of Immigrants; House Republicans Put Out Commitment To America Listing Policy Goals If House Of Representatives Is Won By Republican In Midterms\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Alexandria Hoff, Shannon Bream Connell  McShane, Garrett Tenney, Jennifer Griffin, Alexin McAdams \par \uc2 7,885 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TYRUS MURDOCH, IS A FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: He can't drop anything.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Right. Yes, if it's not that good, everyone will be disappointed.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And you have a lot money on that game, that must have hurt.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Lots of money, it did hurt.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right that's it for us. SPECIAL REPORT is not up next with Bret Baier. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening, welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight a massive sell off on Wall Street with investors running for the hills following another interest rate hike and deepening worries about inflation and a recession which may or may not have already begun.\par \par \uc2 The Dow finishing off 486 today after staging a slight comeback before closing. The S&P 500 lost 65. The NASDAQ fell 199.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow gave back four percentage points, the S&P 500 dropped 4-2/3. The NASDAQ was down five.\par \par \uc2 What about all of this? Connell McShane of Fox Business joins us now from New York. Good evening, Connell.\par \par \uc2 CONNELL MCSHANE, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CHIEF NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. Well, the Dow after four down days in a row now finds itself at its lowest level since November of 2020.\par \par \uc2 Now, investors have pretty much concluded at this point as interest rates rise, that the economy will have trouble growing not just here in the U.S. but all around the world. And it was one of those days on Wall Street where it was, you know, sell first and ask questions later in a Friday session.\par \par \uc2 All the worries seem to be magnified right now in the minds of investors from the war in Ukraine to the energy crisis in Europe. But really more than anything, it's the higher interest rates that we're seeing in the U.S., but also in places like Britain and Sweden, Switzerland and Norway.\par \par \uc2 And to that point about the global nature of all this, the day started with a report showing a sharp decline in European economic activity, which raises the risk of recession over there. And the new government in the U.K. led by Prime Minister \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  now unveiled a plan for tax cuts that really was not well received by investors who expressed concern about the impact on inflation and interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Here in New York, the Dow dropped into what would have been considered a bear market during the session. That's a 20 percent drop from a recent high but it did barely manage to avoid that distinction by bouncing back a bit before the close.\par \par \uc2 Energy related companies the likes of \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  , \uc2 Devon Energy\uc2  and \uc2 Halliburton\uc2  . Those are among the worst performers today. And that's really because oil prices continue to fall on concerns about demand, which when you think about it is really concerned about recession and more than anything else, that's what's hanging over this market right now, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Connell, thank you.\par \par \uc2 With just 46 days to go until the November midterm elections, House Republicans are now making their case to take control of their chamber of Congress. Their leader outlined the agenda today and it is a throwback to a successful campaign from almost 30 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alexandria Hoff is in Pennsylvania with what the GOP is calling its Commitment to America. Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. Yes. And the location for this rollout was intentional, it took place in a manufacturing facility just south of Pittsburgh in Washington County, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 And Republican Leader Kevin McCarthy, he said that that's to show that this plan benefits this kind of Washington and not elected leaders in Washington D.C.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Your next House Republican majority.\par \par \uc2 HOFF (voice over): 30 Republican House members joined Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy to formally make their pitch to the American people.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): The Commitment to America.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Promising if Republicans can gain control of the House come November, legislation will be waiting to remedy what McCarthy sees as Democrat led failures.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: They control the House, the Senate, the White House. They control the committees, they control the agencies. It's their plan, but they have no plan to fix all the problems they created.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: He says the Commitment to America Plan is the solution. It rests on four pillars, an economy that strong, a nation that's safe, a future built on freedom and a government that's accountable.\par \par \uc2 Today, President Biden offered a response to this morning's rollout.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We didn't hear him mentioned the right to choose. We didn't hear him mentioned Medicare. We didn't hear him mentioned Social Security.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Not far from where the Commitment to America was being pitched.\par \par \uc2 REP. STENY HOYER (D-MD): 20 or so miles, you may know better.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: House Majority Leader \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2  join fellow Democrats in calling the plan vague.\par \par \uc2 HOYER: Their new platform which isn't likely new, as long as slogans are short on details, which is usually the case.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: In terms of specifics, House Republicans offered these.\par \par \uc2 REP. ELISE STEFANIK (R-NY): House Republicans will immediately ensure that we hire 200,000 more police officers across this country.\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES (R-TX): When someone comes over and there is no law enforcement, all it does is encourage more people to come over. We have to end catch and release.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Because on our very first bill, we're going to repeal 87,000 IRS agents.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Republican House members also say the plan is unifying the conference ahead of the midterms.\par \par \uc2 REP. GUY RESCHENTHALER (R-TX): We've got people that have different approaches all the way from \uc2 Dave Joyce\uc2  to \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  , but we're all united behind Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): Republicans hope that this Commitment to America will have a similar effect as the 1994 Contract with America where Republicans won a decade's long battle to control the House, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex, thank you.\par \par \uc2 President Biden addressed one of his core support groups today as he tries to prevent Republicans from taking away the democratic majorities in Congress. The president telling union teachers, democracy cannot survive a GOP win in November. It took great exception to the just unveiled Republican Commitment to America agenda, but he remains vague on some of the key issues.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): President Biden has been involved in education policy for decades.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We go back a long way.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And today, he addressed America's largest labor union, the \uc2 National Education Association\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm going to talk about more than just the NEA today.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That union the NEA is accused of favoring the needs of teachers over students. One former NEA member said this summer, they're not just trying to change culture, they're trying to change what it means to be human, they have no ability to tolerate somebody who doesn't agree with them.\par \par \uc2 To that end, the NEA invested more than $140,000 in opposition research on parents groups that challenge the union. Research a group of Republican senators have likened to an enemy's list.\par \par \uc2 At a summer conference where that $140,000 was approved, the vice president praised the union.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The President and I are counting on you to help us move forward as a nation, just as NEA has done throughout its history.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: President Biden didn't get specific about education policy today, focusing more on abortion.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: 166 House Republicans have signed on to a bill that would ban abortion nationwide.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The Press Secretary then characterized the plan like this.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: That would put doctors in jail for providing women with essential health care.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But as White House officials rally against a GOP backed limit on abortions after 15 weeks, White House officials can't say what the president's position is.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: As you know, Kevin McCarthy put out the GOP agenda.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: I'm not asking about him, I'm asking about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and his position on abortion. How many weeks?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Peter, I'm answering your question. What Republicans are trying to do is take us backwards.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): Liberal activists have been saying they think their side is more energized ever since Roe v. Wade was struck down. But despite all that apparent energy, no specifics, no details about terminating a pregnancy and what this president supports is just about what he doesn't, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I've seen the back and forth there. Did you go back again? Was there ever any like acknowledgement of weeks?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: No, there was no acknowledgement of weeks. And she said she answered my question. But her answer was just about their strong opinions of Republican policy, nothing about what the president supports.\par \par \uc2 And what I will try again, the next time that I'm in there to drill down on is if Republicans support a limit on abortion after 15 weeks. Does President Biden support any limit? That was my first question today, I will try again, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We know you will. Peter Doocy live at the North Lawn. Peter, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in the anchor of "FOX NEWS SUNDAY". Also our Chief Legal Correspondent Shannon Bream. Good evening, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, we are coming up on yet another possible fiscal cliff here as Congress behind us debates the spending. You have Senator Joe Manchin, a Democrat from West Virginia on "FOX NEWS SUNDAY". He's right again in the middle of this.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. You talked to him not long ago about this. So, now we're a couple more weeks down the road. There's still a lot of infighting. He's taking it from the left and the right.\par \par \uc2 The right says this side deal that he came up with that got his vote that the Democrats needed for the so called Inflation Reduction Act. They're not going to vote for it. They say why should we give him a sweetheart political deal? It's political payback.\par \par \uc2 Then, you have these mounting voices on the left and both the House and the Senate, the progressives who say, we don't like what's in this deal. We don't like you know, speeding up permitting for oil and gas leases. We think it's bad for the environment.\par \par \uc2 So, he's got a lot of people to corral, left and right he wants to get enough votes to move forward.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We've seen these cliffs before where suddenly miraculously in a backdoor room, some deal gets done. Is that the going back do you think on Capitol Hill?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I don't know because I've talked with Senator Capito and Barrasso, their offices about what they're offering because they say we've got a very similar measure. It's separate and apart from what's -- it wouldn't be tied to the funding measure. So, let's get that done separately.\par \par \uc2 You know, would he be willing to do that? Would Democrats be willing like to go through that? There are differences. The staffers are going through the different plans to see what the difference is. There are some significant differences that we've seen so far.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meantime, there's money being debated for COVID Relief up there still, even House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  , our own Chad Pergram tweeted out, said today that the emergency provision of remote voting will continue because of the pandemic. This obviously just days after the president said:\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Right, pandemic is over. So, apparently, the pandemic is not over when it comes to getting more government funding and proxy voting.\par \par \uc2 One analysis I looked at said 72 percent of the proxy voting was done by Democrats. So, they're taking some heat here. It's a big election year for them, and many of them would prefer to be at home. They'd be prefer to be campaigning, but some had been caught on vacation with their proxy voting as well.\par \par \uc2 So, it's raising some eyebrows, especially on the heels of what the president had to say.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Definitely. OK, so we have Senator Joe Manchin, who else?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Congresswoman \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  is going to join us on the Republicans plan, critics on left and right of that as well, but she's going to tell us why they think it will lock to -- lock in the midterm vote for them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And Jack Keane?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, we're going to talk foreign policy, Russia, Iran, Israel, Ukraine, China. There's a lot to talk about with General.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: "FOX NEWS SUNDAY" this weekend.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: See you then.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Shannon, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, our midterm spotlight races featuring a tough contest for the top job in Kansas. We'll take you there.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 5 in New York, where Mayor Eric Adams announced his new tent cities to house the growing number of migrants. Two humanitarian emergency response and relief centers will be opened in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 New York has been scrambling to find ways to House migrants that have been bused from Texas.\par \par \uc2 Fox 11 in Los Angeles where school officials say they will make the anti- overdose medication Narcan available at all campuses by mid-October. It is the response to recent fentanyl overdoses by students including the death of a 15-year-old girl in a Hollywood school bathroom.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Dallas from our affiliate Fox 4. One of the big stories there tonight, everyone's favorite 55 foot tall Cowboy returns to his usual spot at Fair Park.\par \par \uc2 Big Tex will celebrate his 70th birthday this year. He's sporting a new shirt and jeans, thanks to the folks at Dickies. Every few years Big Tex gets a wardrobe change. It takes Dickies' crews about two weeks to make the shirts. There you go.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: News, it just gets you fired up. In tonight's "MIDTERM SPOTLIGHT", we look at three toss up states where education may be a key issue.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): In Nevada. Republicans are hoping to flip the governor's seat and a highly contested race. RealClearPolitics shows Republican challenger Joe Lombardo holding a1-1/2 point lead over incumbent Governor \uc2 Steve Sisolak\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Lombardo has made education the central component of his campaign, highlighting pandemic-related school closures under Sisolak's tenure.\par \par \uc2 In New Mexico, incumbent governor, \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2  has a 6-1/2 point lead over Republican nominee, Mark Ronchetti. A former T.V. meteorologist has been receiving campaign help from GOP heavy hitters hoping to flip the State. Florida Governor Ron DeSanti has campaigned there in recent weeks, discussing COVID mandates and education, as well as Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin, who focused his winning campaign, of course, on education and school issues. He will hit the trail for Ronchetti in coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Our third spotlight race tonight is in Kansas, as incumbent Democrat Governor \uc2 Laura Kelly\uc2  tries to hold off Republican state Attorney General Derek Schmidt.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Garrett Tenney has a preview there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 LAURA KELLY\uc2  , (D-KS): Derek, I will never apologize for protecting the lives of our children.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT TENNEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Democratic Kansas Governor \uc2 Laura Kelly\uc2  isn't backing down from her controversial decision to be the first governor in the nation to close schools for the rest of the year in 2020.\par \par \uc2 She is now locked in one of the tightest gubernatorial races in the country against Republican state Attorney General Derek Schmidt, in a race where education could be the deciding factor.\par \par \uc2 DEREK SCHMIDT (R), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: This governor has done more damage to more of our kids than any other leader in the history of the state of Kansas. And now we're dealing with the consequences on the back end.\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: Governor Kelly is touting her work with the GOP controlled legislature to get schools fully funded and turning the state's finances around from the $350 million shortfall, when former Republican Governor \uc2 Sam Brownback\uc2  left office in 2018.\par \par \uc2 KELLY: The kids are back in school, our economy is thriving. Our unemployment rate is the lowest it's ever been. Kansas is in good shape.\par \par \uc2 SCHMIDT: What good does it do to fully fund schools if you turn around and lock the children out of them?\par \par \uc2 TENNEY: Registered Republicans in Kansas outnumbered Democrats by more than two to one in most precincts. So, turnout will be key in this race that is drawing national attention with big name GOP governors like Florida's \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and Virginia's Glenn Youngkin, stumping for Schmidt this week.\par \par \uc2 DR. RUSSEL ARBEN FOX, POLITICAL SCIENTIST, FRIENDS UNIVERSITY: This is the Republican base. This is the people that Derek Schmidt needs to have fired up to make sure that they come out and vote, especially in what looks like to be a very close election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TENNEY (on camera): Two years ago, former President Trump won Kansas by 15 points. So, this race has been one of the top targets for Republicans ever since. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Garrett, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania Republican Senate nominee Dr. Mehmet Oz is getting a clean bill of health from his own doctor. Oz releasing his medical records as he continues to pressure Democratic nominee \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  to release his records as he recovers from a stroke.\par \par \uc2 The 62-year-old Oz's New York-based doctor pronounces him in excellent health.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Ukrainians in one section of that country are voting whether to become part of Russia. While many people there are calling it a sham election. We'll bring you there.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Hurricane Fiona pounded Bermuda with heavy rains and winds as it swept by the island. It's now forecast to approach Northeastern Canada later on tonight.\par \par \uc2 The Canadian Hurricane Centre has issued a hurricane watch over extensive coastal expanses of Nova Scotia, Prince Edward Island, and Newfoundland.\par \par \uc2 Pakistan is deploying 1000s of additional doctors and paramedics in the country's worst flood hit province to contain the spread of disease. Officials say there have been more than 300 fatalities among flood victims due to disease since July.\par \par \uc2 And Thailand's government says it will lift an emergency decree it imposed in March of 2020 to battle coronavirus. On October 1st, foreign visitors will no longer be required to show proof of being vaccinated and people found to be infected will no longer need to quarantine in Thailand.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Iranian T.V. says the death toll from protests across that country could be as high as 26. Demonstrations were sparked by the death of a young woman in police custody as we've been telling you over the past few days.\par \par \uc2 Iran has also disrupted Internet access to the outside world and tightened restrictions on popular platforms used to organize rallies such as \uc2 Instagram\uc2  , and WhatsApp.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian officials say 436 bodies have been exhumed from a mass burial site in the eastern city of Izium. They say 30 of them have visible signs of torture.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, voting is underway in Ukraine, in one part, on whether the occupied regions should become part of Russia.\par \par \uc2 The West is condemning the referendums as an illegitimate and rigged attempt by Moscow to annex areas in the east and south after nearly seven months of war.\par \par \uc2 Chief national security correspondent Jennifer Griffin reports from the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRIFFIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL NATIONAL SECURITY CORRESPONDENT (voice- over): Armed Russian troops arrived at resonance doors in the occupied regions of Luhansk, Donetsk, Kherson, and Zaporizhzhia, forcing fearful Ukrainians to cast their votes in a sham referendum, asking if they want to become part of Russia, taking the names of those who voted against Moscow's wishes.\par \par \uc2 This, as most of the 215 Ukrainian POWs arrived back home after being released as part of a prisoner swap, calling their families and describing their torture.\par \par \uc2 This Ukrainian soldier called his mom. Mykhailo Dianov, a Ukrainian musician who became famous in this photo while defending the Azov steel plant, showed signs of starvation after being released from Russian captivity.\par \par \uc2 Scenes of desperate conscripts fleeing Russia caused miles long traffic jams on roads leading to Georgia and Kazakhstan. And Finland announced it would close its border.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translator): I feel sorry for those who are forced to fight against their will.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translator): Like any sane person in Russia, we strongly oppose this.\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Inside this recruiting station, a Russian officer tells angry recruits to stop complaining. That's it. Playtime is over. You are soldiers now.\par \par \uc2 Young military conscripts were caught off guard. This father in the Far East Russian town of Yakutia says goodbye to his son Dmitry, a student handed his orders and told to board a bus before his mother had time to come and say goodbye.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN (on camera): His father hugged him and told him to be careful. Putin had promised that students would be exempt from conscription. Unnamed Russian officials say Putin plans to call up 1.2 million Russian conscripts. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jen, Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  met with his Chinese counterpart at the U.N . today, as a new report shows how China recruited scientists to work at America's premier nuclear lab. What do we know about that?\par \par \uc2 GRIFFIN: Well, that's right, Bret. A new intelligence report from Strider Technologies documents how China recruited more than 154 Chinese scientists who worked at Los Alamos laboratory, the top secret lab where nuclear weapons were first developed.\par \par \uc2 Some of these scientists were paid up to $1 million through Chinese talent programs to return to China. They ended up working on key Chinese military programs like hypersonic missiles and state of the art drones. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jennifer Griffin live at the Pentagon. Jennifer, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in now House Foreign Affairs Committee Chairman \uc2 Gregory Meeks\uc2  of New York. Mr. Chairman, thanks for the time.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 GREGORY MEEKS\uc2  (D-NY): Good to be with you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to take you to Russia and the fallout from the war in Ukraine. How do you see from your perch \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  at this time? Do you think that we'll start to see further crackdowns, uprisings, because of the war in Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: We certainly hope so, and I think we are starting to see that now as the Russia propaganda starts to disseminate right in front of them because people can see from their own eyes, whether it's about social media or et cetera what has taken place. You see protests building. He is trying to recruit these individuals, not from Moscow or St. Petersburg. He's trying to go to the far outskirts of those towns so that folks don't get to know the truth.\par \par \uc2 But if the truth needs to -- continues to be revealed, I think you will see and it's my hope that the Russian people stand up. And that's why you see men particularly, and women also, lining up at the airport right now trying to get out of there because they know that they could be sent to death with this Russian army, that troops are being killed on a regular basis because of the strong fight that the Ukrainians are putting up.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  " writes that the U.S. has sent private warnings to Russia against using a nuclear weapon. The Biden administration has been sending messages to Moscow about the grave consequences that would follow the use of a nuclear weapon in Ukraine. Have you been briefed about those communications?\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: Well, I can't tell you what I have been briefed on in that regards because that's all classified. But I can tell you that the president is resolved, this country is resolved. I can tell you that the Congress is united. My committee, of which we are closely aligned to make sure that there would be strong consequences if there is any type of nuclear weapon utilized by Russia. And so we are, I think that that kind of message is being communicated directly with the Russians to know that there will be severe consequences for the utilization of any type of nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me take to you Iran. There are these nationwide protests. We have been reporting on them. They seem to be growing. Critics are saying that the Biden administration could be speaking out more to support these protests on the ground, but that perhaps the administration may be holdings its collective tongue because it's still actively trying to get an Iran nuclear deal across the finish line. Is that how you see it?\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: No, not at all. I can tell you that right now there is no movement with the Iran nuclear deal because President Biden has refused to move for some items of that the Iranians want, like removing the Iranian revolutionary military guard from the agreement. What is happening, and the only reason that the president is looking at this agreement and removing, and he is not going to give in to Iranians, is to try to prevent the Iranians from having a nuclear weapon. That's the key. Here the negotiations that are not taking place -- or they're not taking place, they had been taking place because right now at a pause, but the whole idea here is to prevent Iran from having nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 We know that now we don't have any eyes on them. We know that if they did, and they have now more uranium enriched that could give them at least one bomb. So we've got to get our eyes back in there. We've got to get the uranium out of there so that we can make sure that that does not happen. The alternative will be going to war, and we hope had there can be an agreement to prevent war. But it's up to the Iranian people now or the Iranian -- I should say it's not the people -- the ayatollah to determine whether or not they will agree with the agreement that was put on -- to agree with the terms of the agreement that the United States allies and the United States have put up and put forth.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me play this for you. This is the Iranian president on "60 Minutes."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Are you open to a meeting with President Biden, a face-to-face?\par \par \uc2 EBRAHIM RAISI, IRANIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): I don't believe having a meeting or talk with him will be beneficial. We cannot trust the Americans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So why should we, the U.S., trust the Iranians when it comes to either an Iran nuclear deal or what we are witnessing with these crackdowns on these protests?\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: Well, we are not trusting the Iranians. That's the whole idea. We want to make sure that we have the eyes on where that program is. So when we were in the deal, we knew exactly what they were doing. We had cameras there that had been removed when we were out of the deal. We knew exactly how long it would take them, and at that time it was 14 to 16 months away from being able to have the kind of Ukraine --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Uranium.\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: -- uranium material so that they can build this nuclear weapon. Without eyes, we don't know. With eyes, with the inspections and 24 hour, 24/7 watching what they are doing, we have more control and prevent them from having a nuclear weapon. And that's the whole idea. It had nothing do with trying to appease them or have some kind of deal or want to talk to them in that regard. The only thing it has to do is prevent them from having a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me take you to the upcoming midterm election. A lot of polls obviously flying at us as we're getting closer and closer to November. Here's an NBC poll on issues, which party would do a better job on various issues. Republicans leading significantly on border security, crime, the economy. Democrats lead on health care and abortion. When you look at that, are you concerned considering the economy and crime and immigration, frankly, are big concerns in a lot of the country?\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: There's a lot of concerns throughout the country, but, no, I'm not, because when I look at what Democrats have done, and I have looked at what President Biden had promised, we see that those promises that met are promises that's kept. That's what he made with the American people. Those are the things that interested them. Despite the fact that we have come out of this pandemic when we were in the pandemic, he worked hard with the CARES program to make sure that individuals who were suffering from it, suffering from and dying from the pandemic would get the kind of aid that they needed to get them through it. We have done that.\par \par \uc2 But then when you look at the bipartisan infrastructure bill, when you look at what we just passed, the reduction -- the Inflation Reduction Act, when you look at the PACT Act for the military, when you look at how we are fighting and moving together, just yesterday Congress voted to make sure that we do help our local police, et cetera, with dollars so that they can firm themselves up. With those things, we are creating jobs, when you think about the CHIPS Act.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me ask you about the police thing, real quickly, Mr. Chairman. When some members of the Squad oppose that police bill, does that hurt Democrats? You had the \uc2 CBC\uc2  Chair \uc2 Joyce Beatty\uc2  say it came a long way. She endorsed it. but you had others speaking out against it. And obviously Republicans using the defund the policeman mantra that was big over the past months and years in the Squad on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: All I can say about that is you might have had four Democrats that voted against it. You had over 68 Republicans that voted against it. So, if you just look at the numbers. So overwhelmingly, 98 percent, 99 percent of Democrats voted for it. Substantially more Republicans voted against it. That speaks for itself.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And you think it will get through?\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: I do. I think that it will pass in the Senate and we'll get it on the president's desk, and the president will sign it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mr. Chairman, we appreciate your time as always.\par \par \uc2 MEEKS: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, we go inside a morgue at one of the busiest spots along the border to see the ramifications of illegal immigration crisis there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Multiple federal sources tell FOX News a man suspected in the hit and run death of a Florida deputy is believed to be in the country illegally. Michael Hartwick was killed in Largo, Florida, last night. Authorities say he was providing security at construction site when he was struck by a front loader. The driver fled the scene. The suspect was captured after a nine-hour manhunt. Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  's office tells FOX News it's investigating to see whether illegal immigrants are using fraudulent information to obtain employment with contractors there.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we have another FOX News exclusive from the southern border. We go inside one of the busiest morgues in the region where the staff is completely overwhelmed by the deaths of illegal immigrants. Correspondent Alexis McAdams has report from Eagle Pass, Texas. And a warning here, some of the images in this report may be graphic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS MCADAMS, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Another day at the Webb County medical examiner's office in Laredo, Texas. The small morgue now one of the busiest along the southern border as nearly every day the team wheels in another migrant body, adding to the more than 300 they have already processed so far this year.\par \par \uc2 DR. CORINNE STERN, WEBB COUNTY, TEXAS, MEDICAL EXAMINER: It is a crisis. I've labeled it. There you go. We're in the middle of a crisis.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: Dr. Corinne Stern has been a medical examiner in Texas for more than 20 years. Not all of the border counties have an M.E. so she serves nearly a dozen but had to stop accessible migrant cases because there is no more room.\par \par \uc2 STERN: It is full. I currently have not a single space in that cooler.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: It's a graphic and a grim reality at the southern border. A warning for viewers that these images may be difficult to see. This man from Mexico was found dead out on a ranch in Webb County. The medical examiner fingerprinted and documented the death. With nowhere to send these bodies, makeshift cemeteries are popping up near the border. These plots are marked by PVC pipe crosses.\par \par \uc2 STERN: Every single one of them was prevent.\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS: CPB sources tell FOX News that more than 3,000 migrants have died while trying to cross into the U.S. since the beginning of last fiscal year. While the crisis rages on, a heartfelt warning from the doctor who knows just how deadly this journey is.\par \par \uc2 STERN: Don't listen to what the coyotes are telling you. They could care less if you make that journey safely.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MCADAMS (on camera): And Bret, those crossings go on 24 hours a day, seven days a week here at the southern border. Just to tell you how busy it is, just yesterday within a 24-hour period I saw agents pull two bodies out of the Rio Grande River behind me, and migrant deaths are continuing to surge as the crossing continue. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So sad. Alexis McAdams, Eagle Pass, Texas. Alexis, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we take another look at my latest FOX Nation documentary series, "The Unauthorized History of Socialism, Volume II." One of the most interesting questions we asked is why, considering all of its failures around the world, socialism remains so attractive to many people here in America and around the world. Here is just some of what we learned.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There is no doubt that that the socialism of 21st century America is different from the socialism imagined by Marx and Engels almost 200 years ago. But at its root, its appeal is still the same, the idea that human beings could be remade, that the solutions to society's largest and most vexing problems lie in the realm of government.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Certainly, it's insinuated itself into the media and to universities and to government, even into corporate boardrooms. But I don't think in the long run they will win because it's essentially an anti-human, anti-individual, anti-American agenda.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You can learn much more by watching "The Unauthorized History of Socialism, Volume II." Again, it's streaming on FOX Nation as is the first original unauthorized history.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the GOP's Commitment to America plan. Plus, Winners and Losers.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NEWT GINGRICH\uc2  , FORMER SPEAKER OF THE HOUSE: If we win control, we need to know for ourselves what we're going to do. We don't need to spend six months hangings around this city waiting for the city to give us its instructions.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA), HOUSE MINORITY LEADER: Whether you can afford it, whether you feel safe, the challenge of your children getting left behind or a government that's run amok, who has a plan to change that course? We do. On that very first day that we are sworn in, you will see that it all changes.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: This November, you have to choose to be a nation of hope, unity, and optimism, or a nation of fear, division, and darkness.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Democrats push back to the Commitment to America, the GOP agenda rolled out today. House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  adding to President Biden, putting out a statement, saying, "today's rollout is the latest evidence of House Republicans wholehearted commitment to MAGA, going all in on an extremist agenda designed to greatly diminish America's health, freedom, and security."\par \par \uc2 With that, let's bring in our panel, Kimberley Strassel, a member of the editorial board at "The Wall Street Journal," Ben Domenech, editor at large of "The Spectator," host of "The Ben Domenech Podcast" on FOX News Radio, and Marie Harf, former State Department spokeswoman under President \uc2 Obama\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 OK, Ben, what about this rollout? Obviously, trying to come up with something tangible, much like Newt Gingrich did in 1994. Does this sell?\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: I think it does sell. I think it gives Republicans something to talk about that is trying to reset kind of their agenda in the run-up to November for the fall. They want to be talking about these issues of the economy, of taxation, of inflation, of crime, the border and other issues. And they want to be able to point to something that says what they would be able to do in a blocking maneuver at least.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the problem is for them that everything else going on around this around this is going to be used by the White House and by Democrats to try to distract them from this agenda. They want to reorient around this, and I think it gives them something to point to.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Marie, here is President Biden today specifically talking about the economy and inflation in one segment here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: In addition to inheriting a failed MAGA Republican economy, we also confronted a global pandemic and Putin's war in Ukraine. And that's driven the global inflation we see today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I just want to unpack that for a second. The failed Trump economy and then the pandemic and Ukraine war caused inflation. "New York Post," the average American has lost $4,200 in annual income since President Biden took office, entirely wiping out gains made under the Trump administration. So it's kind of tough to put that sentence together.\par \par \uc2 MARIE HARF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: President Biden is right that inflation is not an American phenomenon. It's happening all over the globe, and that is because of COVID, the continued supply chain interruptions and other things related to COVID, and it is because of the war of Ukraine. So President Biden is right when he says that.\par \par \uc2 On the campaign trail, I don't think you will hear Democrats leaning into that. They have talked about how gas prices because of President Biden have come down every day for months. They have talked about the fact that the Biden administration and Democrats in Congress negotiated for cheaper prescription drugs. So I think what he said was true, but the message on the campaign trail is going to be more tailored to American voters.\par \par \uc2 And on the other side, I think Democrats today feel like they heard a lot of platitudes and ideas but not a lot of specifics, and are going to be talking about things like abortion and women's health, things that Republicans didn't talk about today that was noticeably absent.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Sure. Obviously, we talked about earlier Peter Doocy tried to get some specifics on the abortion issues about weeks from the White House, that they were not going down that road. Kimberley, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLEY STRASSEL, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": First of all, keep this number in mind, Bret -- 1.6 percent. That was the inflation rate when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took office. So I don't think he can blame it on the Trump economy.\par \par \uc2 I think this is good, the Republican plan, also because of the way it was put together. In the end, they disseminated this out to a number of different committees, seven of them, panels that came up with all of these ideas. There are actually a lot of specifics underlying the proposal, something like 150 in the end that are behind this agenda. And I think especially if you are an independent voter out there -- the Republican base is pretty excited to come out so far, but there's a lot of people that are going to be on the fence. And they are looking at this party saying, is this still \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  's party? Is this a free market party? What is it? This is an attempt for the Republicans to define themselves.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Also seeing crime and immigration tick up, and even some other networks are starting to cover the border.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Yes, they actually are starting to pay attention. Isn't that interesting?\par \par \uc2 But one other aspect of this that I think we should appreciate. It was kind of a signal that Kevin McCarthy is going to lead in a very different way in terms of running the Congress than perhaps other Republican predecessors have. He seems to be someone who is much more interested in collaborating with the entire body and with all of the people within his conference, as opposed to just laying down the law from on high.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Winner and loser, Marie?\par \par \uc2 HARF: Yes. My winner of the week was the "Serial Podcast," a popular podcast, exposed some real injustices in our criminal justice system and succeeded in getting someone who had been behind bars for years out of jail this week.\par \par \uc2 And my loser was \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  . He is trying to force a national abortion ban debate on the Republican Party. Voters don't want that, and the Republican Party doesn't want that debate. So he's, I think, in the hot seat this week.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Kim, winner and loser.\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: My winner are Senate Republicans who blocked the Disclose Act, a bill that Democrats said would be about campaign finance but really would have teed up donors for intimidation. And my loser is New York Attorney General Letitia James, not for filing her lawsuit against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  , but for campaigning on a promise to do so before she even did any investigation, which really undermines any faith in that product now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Lightning winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: My loser is \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  for her ridiculous comments about baby heartbeats, and my winner are pumpkin spice lovers everywhere in America who will be surrounding us for the coming months.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's coming. Pumpkin spice is here.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Panel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right, hi. Hi there. It's Friday. Time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The pan