 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you. I appreciate that. So is it outdated? No, not for work it's not.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I agree. Class never goes out of style.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Never.\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 You either got or you haven't got style.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I wouldn't say anything, but I might think it was inappropriate.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's distracting. It's distracting.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: In your office, would this fly?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's tough to comment on that. You don't want to get yourself in trouble.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You'd have to consult H.R.?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would have to consult H.R.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You apparently are observing the dress code.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'm old school, Raymond, just like you.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The one on the right is edgy.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Not too short.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You like that hemline?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I can't complain. I can't complain.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What the hell is that?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A dress.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Says who?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Calvin Klein.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It looks like underwear.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I would not choose that to wear at the office.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Mystery is always a good thing, a little bit of mystery.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Queen Elizabeth would agree with that.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: I agree. That's how she got Prince Philip.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Not my style.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's actually not my style either.\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: You wouldn't look so good in these stockings. I agree, I wouldn't want to see that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ARROYO: Laura, I was stunned by the number of men who were reluctant to comment on this. Everybody is worried about getting in trouble. So it really is up to the ladies in the office to teach their younger peers that there are standards in the office space, and that's for the betterment of all.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Raymond, soon it will be men in miniskirts, OK, because that's where we are headed next. Raymond, great to see you. Happy Friday.\par \par \uc2 Immigrant fueled crime produced a populist uprising in Sweden's elections. What can this tell us about our own midterms and the future of the movement? A Swedish member of parliament joins us in moments to tell us. Stay there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Oh, yes, there's been a political earthquake in Sweden and the reverberations could be felt stateside as our own elections are right around the corner. The populist party rose to power there on a message of curbing this runaway immigration and confronting the out-of-control crime that followed.\par \par \uc2 This didn't happen in a vacuum. Take what happened back in May. Over the course of just eight days in the small neighborhood of the town of Orebro, three young men were shot dead. The neighborhood in which they were killed is made up almost exclusively of Syrian migrants. In fact, it's just one of 61 areas in Sweden listed by police as risks of gang violence. All of them happen to have high proportions of immigrants.\par \par \uc2 Right now, Sweden has the highest per capita number of deadly shootings of all 22 European countries. And among shooting suspects, 85 percent are first or second generation immigrants over into a Swedish newspaper. And it's why my next guest has promised his conservative Swedish democrats will be watchdogs for change when they assume power. Joining me now is Charlie Weimers, member of the European parliament from Sweden. It's great to see you tonight. Thank you for joining us. You have a lot of fans in Sweden. I have a lot of Swedish friends, and they recommended that you come on tonight, so thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE WEIMERS, \uc2 EUROPEAN PARLIAMENT\uc2  MEMBER: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: What do Americans need to understand about what's happened in Sweden over a relatively short period of time?\par \par \uc2 WEIMERS: Well, you need only to go to events tonight to see what's happened to Sweden. Tonight, Sweden broke the record of deadly shootings, 48 this year. We had a big explosion outside Stockholm tonight which was heard even in downtown Stockholm. Five-hundred bombings the last four years under social democratic rule, one shooting a day.\par \par \uc2 Sweden is not Pippi Longstocking land anymore. And the Swedes are protesting against that. They want change. They want Sweden to be a safe country again, and that's why they voted for us in so big numbers.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, a "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " columnist threw a tantrum after the election in Sweden, writing a piece entitled "Sweden is becoming unbearable. The Swedish far right has profited from the country's growing inequalities, fostering an obsession with crime and an antipathy to migrants. It's advance marks the end of Swedish exceptionalism."\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, the end of Swedish exceptionalism? You used to be a country that was fairly homogeneous but welcoming of all people, and pretty united. Now where is that?\par \par \uc2 WEIMERS: Well, 40 years of multiculturalism has ended Swedish exceptionalism, I can tell you that. And the journalist at "the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " might wonder why it happens that the Sweden democrats are the biggest among Swedish Iranians for naming one example. Of course, the reason for that is that immigrants who come to Sweden work hard, and who don't want Sweden to become more like the Middle East, they tend to vote for us. And you can see that in the discussion on Islamism. We were the only ones raising that issue for many years. Now everyone else is starting to adopt our policies.\par \par \uc2 But we still have a way to go, because the state is still funding these Islamist Muslim Brotherhood linked organizations. Sweden is still a safe country for Americans to visit, but there are parts in Sweden where I don't recommend anyone to go who are -- no-go zones. That's the fact of the matter. And we are going to change that.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The type of crime that's being reported in Sweden from friends of mine in very small villages outside of Karlstad or the area. Their crimes being described as crimes of humiliation, of children. Police say unlike anything they've ever seen. And a lot of this linked to the Salafist Islamists who are joining these gangs and ruining formerly peaceful neighborhoods and communities. But the Salafist Islamist problem is just horrific there.\par \par \uc2 WEIMERS: Yes, Sweden was number two jihadist exporter to Iraq and Syria during the time of ISIS. And these humiliation crimes, they have increased. The last years, we also call it domination violence where a young person can be urinated at at gunpoint, tortured for hours, just because he is a Swede. And there is such resentment among some people. Not everyone, but enough people for it to be an increasing problem.\par \par \uc2 And we are the only ones addressing this. And the center left, they keep on fighting for more migration to Sweden even though 20 percent of the population today in Sweden is born outside the country. That's more than America had during the height of its immigration. And we just cannot cope. We need to restrict immigration so that we can solve these problems.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Charlie, you have to put a halt on that migration coming into Sweden, otherwise you're going to lose Sweden forever. Swedes have stood up and they said can't do it anymore. Charlie, we are going to be watching this. Thank you so much for joining us. We are going to keep our eyes on this movement and how you address these problems. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WEIMERS: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: The overwhelming narcissism of the enviro freaks is on display. The Last Bite will explain.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: Now, one of them best tennis players, most talented of all time, Roger Federer, he was about to play his last match when a climate nut bag ran onto the court and did this. Don't worry. He's fine. But he didn't stay in that court very long.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (MUSIC)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGRAHAM: This is, this is unhinged behavior. And the greeniacs reward the emotion, the insight of all that. No, there is no insight. They just want attention and they want power. And they're trying to turn all of our kids into these kinds of little enviro green nut bags as well. These are stunts. Whether it's gluing yourself, your hand to a Botticelli painting or lighting yourself on fire, these kids aren't well. And the green movement is not good for them.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us tonight. Thank you for watching. Remember, fly your flag, be with your family and your friends this weekend. Have some fun even in tumultuous times. It's America now and forever.\par \par \uc2 Greg Gutfeld is next.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document INGANG0020220924ei9n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 GOP, Dems Sharpen Messaging 47 Days From 