again tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: See you tomorrow. Thanks, John.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  's here, he says, he would come sir, as well. Good advice. All right. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum. Now, this is the story in our nation's capital today. As I said, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  is joining us here on set. And as is General Jack Keane, and then \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2  a bit later with her take on the lawsuit that was launched against former President Trump, her husband Eric, and Don Jr., and Ivanka, as the former president says that he can declassify anything he wants. We'll get to all of that very busy day here. Lots of talk about with 47 days to go until the midterms. Things are tightening up out there. Here's a look at the focus sharpening to abortion and the economy as both sides try to turn voters their way.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: 40-year high inflation, ripping away your savings.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Dr. Oz would vote to eliminate abortion rights here in America.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The economy, inflation and gas prices are the top three issues.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Anti-choice MAGA Republicans.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  , 45TH PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Biden and the Democrat Congress keep spending us further and further into oblivion.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Extremist so-called leaders are passing laws to criminalize health care providers and punish women.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You get the idea of Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  on the hot seat over the timing of a bill that he launched to ban most abortions in the United States after 15 weeks. We're going to talk to him about his commitment to the bill as the Senate hangs in the balance but first to Fox Business Anchor David Asman, and the strapped United States economy. David, good afternoon to you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID ASMAN, FOX BUSINESS ANCHOR: Good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me start actually with this soundbite from \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  yesterday, talking about his pattern of increasing interest rates in order to cool things down a bit. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2  , CHAIR OF THE FEDERAL RESERVE OF THE UNITED STATES: We don't know. No one knows whether this process will lead to a recession or if so, how significant that recession would be.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, another big fat rate hike and they're trying to get a soft landing here. How is that looking, David?\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Well, from his perspective, it's not looking good. He essentially admitted that if, if we have to have a recession to cut inflation, we're going to have a recession. I mean, that's why the market reacted as it did. It was down over 500 points at the end of the day. What, what frightened me the most about what he said he downgraded economic growth for 2022 down to zero point -- excuse me -- 0.02 percent, point, that's basically as close to flat as you can get.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: And when you have eight percent inflation that means you're going downhill fast. I mean, it's just no, no denying, it's -- this is, this inflation is taking a month out of everybody's yearly salary. It is causing all kinds of problems for Americans. 60 percent of Americans are living paycheck to paycheck. It means essentially that we are we are in for very tough times. Once again, remember, Biden came into office with six percent economic growth. It's now slated at 0.02 percent. And we had basically zero inflation or 1.4 percent inflation. So, things have gotten gone south very fast. It's bad news.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: That's pretty stark. So, he came in with six percent growth in the country. Now, we're zero, at 0.02. Inflation was at one and change, now it's said eight and change.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let's take a look at these quick polls here. New York Times Sienna, econ issues are more important than societal ones. 49 percent of voters say yes, although they care about all of these other issues. Almost half of the voters say the economy is number one for them. And then, this one, voters agree more with the GOP than Democrats on the economy. That's at 52 percent over 38 percent, David. You know, these are sort of the, these are the lines that we're all watching as we get 48 minutes, 48 days, I should say, away from these midterms, quick thought on that.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Well, the public, the public understands things a lot better than the experts do. The experts live in a bubble. Let's face it, whether they're the Fed chair or the president, they don't go out shopping in the grocery smart, grocery store, they don't see their salaries cut as much, they still get their perks. The American public knows that what really hurts me the most and what is hurting the economy the most, there are no incentives from this government, for the private sector to grow.\par \par \uc2 All the growth that this government is interested in is growing the government, making the government bigger, they want to change, transform society. The President uses that word. It's not just about green energy. They want to transform industrial production from the private sector to the government sector. They want the government to control industrial policy, not necessarily all of the means of production, but certainly the direction in which industrial policy goes in this country and that never works. It doesn't work. When you get far to the left, in communist countries it doesn't work in mild socialist countries. It's not working here. And the proof was just laid out in black and white by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  yesterday.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Very interesting. David, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ASMAN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: David Asman in New York. Joining me now Republican South Carolina Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  . Senator, always good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, it's interesting, as David was just laying out, basically, where Republicans stand on the role of government, that it should be smaller and more out of your life. And then, Democrats believe that the government can give you a lot of solutions to your problems, very clear definition.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You have put yourself your neck on the line with this abortion bill, a 15-week ban, and a lot of your colleagues want you to be quiet about this whole thing until after the election. But you're saying no, you want people to know where you and you believe your party stands, right?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes, I want people you know, nothing not can say about abortion is going to make people believe the economy is good when it's not. I trust the voters, people in America can absorb a lot of information at the same time. What most people don't know about the Democratic Party, when it comes to abortion, is that about four months ago, five months ago, they had a vote in the capitol right behind you, that would change every law on this nation, allow abortion on demand up to the moment of birth as a national standard, wiping out all state laws to make you like China, which allows abortion on demand up to the moment of birth in North Korea. So, 16 percent of the people want to you know that abortion is on our mind, here's what I would tell you about the Democratic Party. They're wrong on the border, they're wrong on crime.\par \par \uc2 They're wrong on inflation, but they're trying to make your country be like China when it comes to unborn babies. You know, abortion on demand up to the moment of birth is cruel, is that aligned with the world? In France, for God's sakes, you limit abortion at 14 weeks. My bill says at 15 weeks, you can do more at the state level. But at 15 weeks, I want to draw a line in the sand and say we're going to stop abortion, life of the mother exceptions rape and incest, to put us in line with 47 of 50 European nations you're almost at the fourth, the fourth month.\par \par \uc2 And what am I trying to say? I'm trying to say the unborn child is a, has a human right at a certain point in time not to be dismembered. So, it's not a state issue. For me, it's a human rights issue. At 15 weeks, you can feel pain, you provide anesthesia to save the baby's life. I can only imagine what kind of death it must be to be dismembered after that, and the public's with me -- 70 percent of the people would like to have limits on late term abortion.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean, even when, when you look at the sort of battle lines over this, 15 weeks is very moderate. I mean, there are a lot of pro-life people who would like it to be far south of that, you're looking for a 15- week minimum in the country and then states can make decisions based on that after that. Here's \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  arguing, running for governor in Georgia, against \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 She's arguing that people who have had, you know, sonogram, where they can hear the heartbeat, I've had three of these at six weeks that they're being lied to that there's some sort of conspiracy that medical imaging companies and every doctor and every nurse who does these scans, here's what she's telling everybody. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D), GEORGIA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: There is no such thing as a heartbeat at six weeks. It is a manufactured sound designed to convince people that the men have a right to take control of a woman's body.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I mean --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Oh, OK. Let me correct myself.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Who's in on this conspiracy?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: OK. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Because sometimes they can sort of find the heartbeat then they go a little over here and then they definitely find it so, so apparently, we've all been bamboozled into thinking that we're hearing heartbeats at six weeks.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: So, what she's doing is marginalizing the unborn child to justify a boarding up to the moment of birth in her world, in the Democratic Party's view of abortion. There are no restrictions abortion up to the moment of birth is what they believe. Now, when you understand the heart beats at six weeks, by 15 weeks it's beat 15 million times. The baby can feel pain.\par \par \uc2 The only way they can sell abortion on demand up to the moment of birth is to marginalize the unborn child to be something some glob. It is a developing person, the French limit abortion at 14 weeks. 47 in 50 European nations do it below 14 weeks. So, what I'm trying to say is if you're pro- life, you need to be pro-life even in an election year. Some of my colleagues who are great pro-life leaders need to speak up. \uc2 John Thune\uc2  came on for my bill today. We're with the American people we're on the right side of this issue, it is --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, you think those in your party who want you to be quiet, wait until after the election, they're not happy with you that you brought this up at this point, what would you say to them?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Well, they're naive, you're naive. We're a national party. Here's my position on the border, here's my position on crime. Here's my position on inflation, on taxes, on a strong national defense. We owe it to the American people to tell, tell them who we are. And here's who we are, as a National Party. At 15 weeks, we've said drawn a line to put us in line with Europe, in the civilized world, we reject, reject Chinese abortion policy. And \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  is trying to change science. She's trying to deny that the baby has a heartbeat when it does, because if you understand how the baby develops, you're going to reject abortion on demand up to the moment of birth.\par \par \uc2 They're trying to marginalize me. Now, I'm a pro-life guy, I always have been, always will be. But I'm not a single-issue voter. I'll vote for a pro-choice person. Like Joe O'Dea, if I were in Colorado, he's pro-choice, but God knows he would change what's going on in the country. I guess here's what I'm saying to the pro-life movement, stand up for the baby in a reasonable way. They need you now. It is good to be pro-life. There is no bad day to be pro-life. And what the Democratic Party is trying to do is marginalize the unborn child, deny science to justify abortion on demand up to the moment of birth. And when America understands the birthing process, they will be with us.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Let me ask you one unrelated question before I let you go because we're seeing massive protests in Russia against this conscription effort by \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  . You've got people being dragged off the streets by police. Quick comment on where you think that's headed.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: I think this is the beginning of the end for Putin. He's asking people to die in a war they don't want to fight. He's destroying the Russian economy, destroying the Russian military, there's going to be a push back. And it's just a matter of time that they take him out. Because there's no way for Russia to be a part of the family nations under his leadership. So, they're running out of soldiers. 300,000 people are going to be drafted against their will of the Ukrainians are doing a hell of a job. Putin's days are numbered here if we just stick with Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you, Senator. Always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Thank you. Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thanks for coming in tonight. Good to have you on set. So, coming up to big interviews with \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2  and Iranian Activist \uc2 Masih Alinejad\uc2  , as women rise up in protest in the streets of Tehran. What we are seeing there is just incredible. It's a huge development in Iran, challenging the mullahs, and yet the United States allows their leader at the U.N .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And one U.S. activist \uc2 Masih Alinejad\uc2  , who we have spoken to a lot, she is saying to President Biden: "Where are you @JoeBiden? You gave a visa to Masha Amini's killer and the murder of millions of Iranians but totally silent about the brutality of Raisi's regime against ongoing protests in Iran." And she goes on, on that. So, what do you what do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Amazing scenes that we're seeing play out on the streets of Tehran and all over Iran. Protesters everywhere.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is not the kind of scene that you typically see in the streets of Iran. At least nine people have been killed as these brave protesters rage across the state -- across the country in response to the death of a 22-year-old woman who was jailed because she wasn't wearing a scarf over her head.\par \par \uc2 The streets of Tehran hitting a boiling point as women brazenly burn their hijabs in the streets and chant death to the dictator.\par \par \uc2 Protests also breaking out in New York City as the Iranian president, in New York, speaking at the United Nations.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHAMED SHIRAZI, PROTESTER: We are here to protest against the criminal, mass murderer, Raisi, the so-called president of Iran, which appointed by Supreme Leader --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Here is what the Biden administration had to say about Raisi, being on U.S. soil.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM (on camera): The question is why is Raisi allowed into the United States, and further that she brings up? Why are you all at the negotiating table with this leadership that is carrying out these heinous killings?\par \par \uc2 NED PRICE, SPOKESPERSON, UNITED STATES DEPARTMENT OF STATE: So, two points on that Martha, as the host country of the U.N ., we are generally obligated to allow in foreign leaders who are attending the U.N .\par \par \uc2 There are certain restrictions that are on the Iranian delegation. They're not allowed to go certain places, they're allowed to attend the U.N . And we're generally obligated to do that as the host of the U.N .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So in a moment, we'll be joined by General Jack Keane, who is with me here in D.C.\par \par \uc2 But first, to \uc2 Masih Alinejad\uc2  , an Iranian journalist and activist author of The Wind in My Hair: My Fight for Freedom in Modern Iran. Masih has led a movement of women to remove their hijabs to let their hair be free. To ride bicycles, to do all sorts of things that women aren't allowed to do.\par \par \uc2 Masih, what is going through your mind as you watch these brave women in the streets of your country?\par \par \uc2 MASIH ALINEJAD\uc2  , IRANIAN JOURNALIST AND ACTIVIST: For years and years, you know that in your show, I have been dreaming a day that Iranian woman will burnt hijab in public, and this is happening.\par \par \uc2 The brutal death of Mahsa Amini, who was killed by hijab police is becoming a turning point for Iranian women. But at the same time, it is going to be a tipping point for the Islamic Republic.\par \par \uc2 You know, Iranian women are furious across Iran, different cities, shoulder to shoulder with men. They are protesting not only against forced hijab. They are protesting against Islamic Republic. They calling Islamic Republic ISIS.\par \par \uc2 Because Mahsa was an innocent girl. She was not even participating in any protests. She was not even uncovered. She was wearing inappropriate hijab and that is why the blue police brutally killed her.\par \par \uc2 The mother, and father, family members of Mahsa have been saying that their daughter was beaten up by police, but Iranian television deny that. Right now that I'm talking to you, they shooting people, they killing people, they beating people in the street to convince them that we didn't kill Mahsa.\par \par \uc2 Martha, I'm telling you, Iranian woman one day is going to get rid of this regime. The whole people are with Iranian women. Will we'll get rid of our ISIS? But the history will judge President Biden who gave visa to our killer, to our murderer.\par \par \uc2 This is the time that U.S. administration must see and meet the opposition leaders. They have to meet the people of Iran, they have to recognize the people of Iran. They should not give visa and legitimize our regime.\par \par \uc2 Right now that I'm talking to you, Revolutionary Guards is in the sanction list, is in the terrorists list.\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  gave visa to a member of Revolutionary Guard. Can you believe that? (INAUDIBLE) is here in New York with Ebrahim Raici's delegation. Why?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ALINEJAD: You have to actually shut down Iran's intersection, because they're killing people. But you negotiating with our murderers? Sorry is my anger.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Masih, you have --\par \par \uc2 ALINEJAD: My anger is the anger of my source, family.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You had someone who showed up with a gun at your house, you have also been on a list, and your family has been in danger, and in prison. Here is Raisi at the U.N ., saying that there's a double standard facing Iran when it comes to Human Rights. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EBRAHIM RAISI, PRESIDENT-ELECT OF IRAN (through translator): Islamic Republic of Iran reject some of the double standards of some governments vis-a-vis human rights, and sees that as the most important factor which is rendered banal the topic of human rights in the eyes of many.\par \par \uc2 Until we have these double standards where attention is solely focused on one side and not all equally, we will not have true justice and fairness. Human rights belongs to all, but, unfortunately, is trampled upon by many governments.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Your response to that, Masih, before I let you go?\par \par \uc2 ALINEJAD: I'm not even listening.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Can you -- could you hear it?\par \par \uc2 ALINEJAD: I cannot even listen to him. This is a torture to me. I cannot believe that the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  welcomed a terrorist to educate them about human rights.\par \par \uc2 Look, he actually hold the picture of \uc2 Qasem Soleimani\uc2  , who was killed by the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  . You know what? Iranian people inside bring down the picture of \uc2 Qasem Soleimani\uc2  , and they wish they see that Ebrahim Raici get arrested by the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 They wish Ebrahim Raici will see the same faith of \uc2 Qasem Soleimani\uc2  . This is the true anger of Iranian people. He was the terrorist. And he came here with the member of Revolutionary Guards.\par \par \uc2 I don't care about my life. Yes, I was the target of kidnapping plot, assassination plot, I would rather die, but not see that the Western countries legitimizing our murderer right now.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Masih, thank you very much. It's always good to talk to you, and to get your perspective on all of this. These are very tumultuous times in your country. And we will speak with you soon, Masih. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ALINEJAD: Martha, you've been always supportive. You've been always supportive.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ALINEJAD: From the beginning when we launched campaign against forced hijab. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Thank you. Thank you, Masih. We'll speak with you soon.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is retired Four Star General Jack Keane, former vice chief of staff of the Army, chairman of the Institute for the Study of War, and Fox News, senior strategic analyst.\par \par \uc2 General, great to have you with us. What goes through your mind as you watch what is playing out right now in Iran?\par \par \uc2 GEN. JACK KEANE (RET.), FOX NEWS SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: Yes, I want to just remind you as how repressive this regime really is.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we had bloody November in 2019. And unfortunately, we're going to have the repeat of this pattern in terms of what they will do.\par \par \uc2 And they killed 1,500 protesters then, just shot them dead. And in prison, hundreds of others that are subjected to torture. And the prelude for that is they shut down the Internet and social media.\par \par \uc2 And what were they're about to do now in the beginning stages of is just that. And this thing will get very violent on the part of the regime. They are absolute masters that clear in the streets. We had a huge demonstration in 2009. The \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration back then, they were carrying signs if you recall, \uc2 Obama\uc2  hear us. He said nothing.\par \par \uc2 We had one an opportunity we had. President Biden is standing at the same podium. And I agree with her that Raisi, the terrorist, the president of Iran is about to take that podium as somewhat delayed from him, and we don't call him out. We don't call out his regime or China or North Korea.\par \par \uc2 We did Russia and what he said about Russia was very appropriate and he did it with conviction.\par \par \uc2 But these other repression regimes are destabilizing the world, not just adversaries in United States. And we had a huge opportunity to use this incident in front of the entire world body of leaders, and dramatically demonstrate to them what this regime is all about.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: If he talked about democracy and the importance of democracy, right, around the world, why not take that moment to say we see these people in the streets of Iran.\par \par \uc2 Because the same thing happened. There was another young woman who was killed in the street back in 2015, in the \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration, as you say, that the protesters came out, and it was largely talked about at that point, this is -- this is a potential tipping point.\par \par \uc2 If places like the United States support this movement and get behind them, perhaps, these people will have the power to overthrow to topple this government. But we didn't.\par \par \uc2 And now, we're doing -- we're doing the same thing over again.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: I met Lech Walesa.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Well after he became president, and he retired, and with a small group of people and with tears in his eyes, he talked about hearing the voice of Reagan, when he was in the streets leading the movement. He was emotional, he has never forgotten, and he knew America had his back.\par \par \uc2 And where are we? Where is -- where is our voice here? And it's really quite extraordinary. These adversary countries of ours, all repressive regimes, the thing that they fear, more than anything else, it's not the United States military, it's not another -- it's not a border country, it's their own people.\par \par \uc2 They know that if they're going to lose power, it is only going to be as a result of the overthrow of the regime by their own people. And that's why they're so repressive.\par \par \uc2 And even though this incident, for Americans, it seems small, wearing a scarf or exposing some of your hair, but that pulled a scab, and the wound is exposed because the people writ-large, or denied personal individual freedoms.\par \par \uc2 They're being repressed on a daily basis, and they use that incident as a catalyst to express their emotions and passions about this.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Where is our voice, again?\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Where is -- where is our voice, right?\par \par \uc2 Masih's brother is in prison, because she is here speaking out and empowering women to take off their hijabs, put themselves on social media. Her brother is not with his children and his family anymore in Iran.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the ramifications of these small acts of freedom and expression are, are life threatening for these people.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And it would have been great if we had heard that from the president yesterday when he talked about how we support democracies all around -- all around the country.\par \par \uc2 So, which -- can I just get a quick thought from you on these protests in Russia and whether or not you think there is a tipping point there as well.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: It remains to be seen, but certainly, what's happening here, calling up the 300,000 reservists, and the fact that some of these people unexpectedly are going to go into combat, many of them are physically unfit, medically unfit, and I think emotionally and psychologically want no part of this thing. And so --\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Which is not make a good soldier.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes. So, they're protesting and we'll see. I mean, Putin, you'll see what he does. I mean, very quickly, he's got over 150,000 people organized just to stop civilian protests in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: And that's why they're on him very quickly. Again, Putin realizes, his threat to his regime is not the United States. It's his own people.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Well, the power of people is very -- is very strong, and that Lech Walesa quote is ringing in my ears. Just a little bit of support and encouragement from the right place can really help people in these situations.\par \par \uc2 General Keane, thank you.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, great talking with you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Always good to have you with us. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 So, \uc2 Eric Trump\uc2  now lashing out at New York Attorney General Letitia James, as "the most corrupt attorney general in United States' history," after she hit some members of the family with a massive lawsuit, alleging an astounding pattern of fraud by the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 Eric's wife, Lara, who is not a target of this lawsuit, joins me next with her reaction.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Former President Trump firing back at New York Attorney General Letitia James for suing him, three of his children and the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  for financial fraud, calling it a continuation of a witch hunt. Former Trump attorney turned Nemesis \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  was the key player under this investigation in terms of the information that was disclosed and he now predicts this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL COHEN\uc2  , FORMER TRUMP LAWYER: It's that documentation which nobody has ever had before, and he never would have given it that ultimately sparked this and sent this into the investigation that's going to ultimately terminate the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  , Donald, Don Jr., Ivanka, Eric, Weisselberg, McCarney and the rest of them. That's going to put an end to the entire company.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: Do you think that?\par \par \uc2 COHEN: I believe so.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. So we'll see what \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2  has to say about that in moments, but first a correspondent David Lee Miller following this story with the very latest this afternoon from New York. Hi, David Lee.\par \par \uc2 DAVID LEE MILLER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Martha, the former president says the civil lawsuit accusing him of inflating property value to get low interest loans and other benefits has no merit and is a political vendetta by New York's Democratic Attorney General Letitia James. In an interview with \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  , Trump says he was targeted by James during her first run for attorney general.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  , FORMER PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: Well, she campaigned on it four years ago. It was a vicious campaign and she just talked about Trump and we're going to indict him, we're going to get him. She knew nothing about me.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: In his interview, Trump said he was under the impression the AG wanted to sell but Trump feared doing so would damage his reputation. He said agreeing to pay even a small fine might make him look guilty. While the lawsuit claims Trump's financial statements listed a staggering number of inflated assets. Trump said banks had an obligation to do their own homework when reviewing financial documents.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2  : We have a disclaimer right on the front. And it basically says, you know, get your own people, you're at your own risk, this was done by management. It wasn't done by -- it was done by management, so don't rely on the statement that you're getting. And it's -- and by the way, it goes on for like a page and a half. It's a very big disclaimers.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Trump says none of the banks lost money on their loans. Former Attorney General Bill Barr called the lawsuit a political hit job. He says real estate valuations are not an exact science.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Attorney General James, meanwhile, saying it is possible that Trump broke federal law and is now referring the matter to the \uc2 IRS\uc2  as well as the US attorney for the Southern District of New York. The lawsuit seeks to bar Trump and three of his children from serving his officers in any businesses licensed in New York State, and is calling for $250 million in restitution for benefits that she says were unfairly obtained, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. David Lee, thank you very much. With that we bring in \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2  , the former president's daughter in law, former 2020 senior campaign adviser and a Fox News contributor. Lara, good to have you with us today.\par \par \uc2 So, you heard at the beginning of the introduction there, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  , who used to work very closely with the family and used to be one of the most sort of strongest fiercest advocates for the former president. Now he says that he believes that the information that he turned over will take down the entire Trump real estate organization. What would you say to him about that?\par \par \uc2 LARA TRUMP, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  has a history of lying and he is obviously a very unhappy personal individual. And he's had his own personal finance problems, which I think led him to this point. And the best I can say for him is that we'll pray for him, because nothing that has come out of his mouth, for years now, has been factual or accurate in any sort of way.\par \par \uc2 But what you have here, Martha, with this lawsuit, alleged by the attorney general of New York. If you're a New Yorker, you should be absolutely disgusted because instead of actually working to make your state better, to make it a better place for you to live, this is where the attorney general had has been putting her time, her energy and your taxpayer money into this investigation of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 This was an investigation, by the way, in search of a crime. There was no merit to any of this and then she got to the end of it three years later, and millions of documents later, and she found, gosh, there was no crime that was committed, so she has had to default to a civil lawsuit here. And when you think about it, there were no damages. There was no loss. There was no defaulted loan payment. Actually, these banks made hundreds of millions of dollars off of this and everyone knows what this is about.\par \par \uc2 It is another political attack against anyone with the last name Trump in this case, of course, my father in law, my husband and his siblings. It's disgusting.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Here's what she said, James was talking about it. And, you know, we know that there was a long investigation. This story has been around for years about the valuations of these properties. And there was an intense investigation that they tried to do a criminal -- go the criminal route, as you say, which fell apart. They decided that they didn't have a case. And so, here's what she said yesterday at the press conference. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LETITIA JAMES, NEW YORK ATTORNEY GENERAL: It is a tale of two justice systems, one for everyday working people and one for the elite, the rich and the powerful. Mr. Trump and his allies may say that these penalties are too harsh, or that this is part of a witch hunt. I will remind everyone that this investigation only started after \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  , the former lawyer, his former lawyer testified.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: And here's Andy McCarthy after he heard that. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDY MCCARTHY, ASSISTANT UNITED STATES ATTORNEY: Prosecutors crawled through Trump's financial records for three years. I had a laugh at the end of her -- or during her press conference when she talked about how she was referring the case -- referring her complaint to the Justice Department. The Justice Department had this case in the first place. And they looked at it very hard. They decided that \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  was not a promising foundation to build a fraud case on. I don't think this case is ever going to trial. This is going to take years.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, it's just one of several cases, Lara, how is your father in law, you know, what's his reaction? We saw him on with \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  last night, but, well, what's the family response to this at this point?\par \par \uc2 TRUMP: Well, it's just really disappointing Martha because, you know, sadly, this has become commonplace for all of us in the Trump family, this sort of treatment and investigation, and these allegations that always end up being false. And, you know, what we're holding up fine because we know that there was no wrongdoing, that, you know, nothing happened here. But sadly, this just goes to speak to, I think, where we are in America that you can target your political adversaries in this sort of way.\par \par \uc2 And let's be really clear about the attorney general. 47 days from now, she is up for reelection, and she is behind in the polls, behind her Republican opponent. She promised and campaigned on the fact that she was going to take down \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  . So now she's got to bring something forward here as she's closing in the gap towards the midterm elections. But it's just disgusting, it's sad to see, but we always believe in our family that, you know, the truth will ultimately show itself and shine through. And in this case, we believe that as with any other case out there you could talk about.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. Yes, thank you very much, Lara. It's good to have you here this afternoon. And, you know, what always amazes me, this is the lawsuit that was just announced, even though it's been kicking around for three years. But when you watch across the rest of the networks, there's this glee that, you know, this is it. This is the final thing that's going to take down the Trump administration. They're all excited even though that the beginning of the lawsuit was just announced. So yes, I don't know.\par \par \uc2 Lara, thanks. Always good to see you.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2  : You got it.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So from full stop on running again to leaving it in the hands of fate, we're going to take a look at President Biden's evolving answer to the question will he run again in 2024. And there are some interesting things to pick up on here, the way that we took a look at the way he's talked about this over recent months. We'll show it to you when we come back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: I'm a great respecter of fate. And so, what I'm doing is I'm doing my job, we're going to do that job and within the timeframe that makes sense after this next election cycle here, going into next year and make a judgment on what to do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, take a look at this. A fiery deadly crash, good Lord. That happened just north of Dallas. It was dashcam video that captured that semi truck falling over the side of the highway before bursting into flames. On Tuesday, Police in Allen, Texas say that the truck collided with a car before swerving and flying over that concrete barrier. The truck driver sadly died on the scene of that crash. No one else was injured.\par \par \uc2 And now we're learning that President Biden may wait until the middle of next year before he makes a decision on a 2024 run. That came from an interview with Biden's close allies, Senator Chris Coons, that was done by Axios. So with that, we want to take a look back at the evolving stance from the President and the White House in their answers to the 2024 question. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Yes, my plan is to run for re election. That's my expectation.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2  , FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: The President has every intention of running for reelection.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY; His answer has been pretty simple which is, yes, he's running for reelection.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  , VICE PRESIDENT OF UNITED STATES: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is running for reelection and I will be his ticket mate. Intends to run. And if he does, I intend to run with him.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: My intention as I said to begin with is that, I would run again, but it's just an intention. But it is a firm decision that I've run again, that remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK. So here we go. Let's bring in former White House press secretary and Fox News contributor \uc2 Ari Fleischer\uc2  , and Fox News political analyst Juan Williams. Gentlemen, welcome. Good to have both of you here. Let's also take a look at this from Chris Coons in that Axios interview. He's obviously one of the closest allies to President Biden. He predicted on Tuesday, that Biden will wait until the middle of next year before deciding whether or not he will run. Coons says that the post midterm election landscape will be a key indicator as to what direction the Democratic Party will take ahead of 2024 era.\par \par \uc2 Ari, let's start with you. Your thoughts on what the President has been saying, what the Vice President has been saying.\par \par \uc2 ARI FLEISCHER\uc2  , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, he's taken the Sherman out of being Shermanesque. You know, he used to be clear about it. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  went so far as to leave out the word intention. She declared he's run, she declares she'll be the ticket mate. And I think Chris Coons has got it exactly right. The Democratic Party is going to take a second look at \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  , why on earth after they lose the House, may lose the Senate, would they want an unpopular soon to be 82-year-old man to lead the ticket for a second term. This is anathema to Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Now as a Republican, we would love \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to run. There is nothing Republicans want more than the Biden-\uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  ticket, an 82-year-old man if he is reelected in 2024. Dream come true for Republicans that I think the Democrats are going to take a second look.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes. So, Juan, you know, one of the issues, I think a lot of people think that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not going to run again for reelection. You can tell me if you think that's the case or not. But one of the problems is that he can't say he's not running unless it looks like there's some pretty strong contenders that people can get excited about behind him.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, sure, I mean, to me, this is a little bit of a preemptive strike here, you know, by Ari. I like Ari so much but, you know, I don't think Republicans really want the one guy who has proven that he can beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to run, because Trump is, at the moment, the leading candidate for the Republican nomination.\par \par \uc2 In addition, you got to keep in mind, we'd be having a different conversation, Martha. We had this conversation just three months ago. President Biden's poll numbers have gone up, so he's at about 45% which is about average for a president at this point in his first term. The Democrats are doing better in terms of generic polling as to who would hold or not hold the Congress right now. And we've seen a bunch of issues break the Democrats way.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, it's still likely that Republicans will win the House, really up in the air as to what goes on in the Senate. That will set the tone for any decision that you get from Biden and Biden's team about what he's going to do. One last point quickly, you think about President Trump. He didn't announce officially a reelection bid until June of '19 and the election was in '20. Of course, he had announced right from the start to the \uc2 FEC\uc2  plan to do something after he initially won. And if you go back to \uc2 Obama\uc2  , it wasn't until April of '11 before the '12 election, that he said he was going to run. So to say that till the middle of next year, that's not unusual.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Ari, what do you say to that?\par \par \uc2 FLEISCHER: The candidates that want to say they were young, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  's not. And that's the essence of the issue. The Democratic Party after this November election is going to look at a 