 .\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, they have a heartbeat.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: You didn't have to do that.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: All right, that's it for us, SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Scott Baio, I want to tune into that. Judge, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, pre-election politics and playing hardball on Capitol Hill. Negotiations on a new crime bill entering a critical stage ahead of the midterms. And House Republicans are leveraging the possibility of a government shutdown to force action on the border crisis.\par \par \uc2 We have Fox team coverage from the Hill, Chad Pergram up there with the latest on the Democratic makeover on soft on crime perceptions.\par \par \uc2 But we begin with Congressional Correspondent Aishah Hasnie in the GOP's gamble on the immigration issue. Good evening, Aishah.\par \par \uc2 AISHAH HASNIE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening to you. With the crisis worsening at the southern border and no immigration reform inside here in the Senate, House Republicans are now raising the stakes.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. STEVE SCALISE (R-LA): We're going to have a plan to secure America's border.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (voice over): House Republicans are rolling out their solution to the border crisis.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Republicans have a plan for a new direction.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Today, GOP conference members got a private firsthand look at the plan inside minority leader Kevin McCarthy's Commitment to America agenda, a legislative roadmap similar to former Speaker \uc2 Newt Gingrich\uc2 's 1994 Contract with America.\par \par \uc2 On the border, the GOP plans to end catch and release, reinstate the Remain in Mexico policy and finish the border wall. Democrats are already hitting back.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): Well, I'm not concerned with anything that they're going to present over the next few days because the American people will see through it clearly.\par \par \uc2 HASNIE: Democrats are under pressure to do something about migrants being bused to sanctuary cities. But talks on immigration reform are stalled on Capitol Hill and Congress is just eight days away from a government shutdown.\par \par \uc2 McCarthy adding pressure to the political posturing, tweeting: If Biden and Democrats don't use this government funding bill to address the border crisis immediately, I'm voting no on this bill. And I urge my colleagues to do the same.\par \par \uc2 Republicans see southern border security as a top issue that will bring out voters, some putting it ahead of pocketbook issues.\par \par \uc2 As Fox's most recent power ranking show, the GOP is expected to win the House but by just 13 seats. Former House Speaker and Fox News Contributor Newt Gingrich who was at this morning's private meeting thinks the margin will be larger now that members have McCarthy's messaging.\par \par \uc2 NEWT GINGRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL POLITICAL ANALYST: They're somewhere between plus 15 and plus 70. And none of us will know until election night.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HASNIE (on camera): And Leader McCarthy will announce his Commitment to America game plan in Pittsburgh tomorrow and it also includes subjects like crime, the economy and education. The other three midterm issues that speaker -- former Speaker Gingrich really believes will drive voters to the polls for Republicans, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Plus 70 would be something. Aisha, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Democrats are trying to get away from the perception that they're anti-law enforcement ahead of the midterms or defunding police. Even though many progressives still do support defunding the police. Party leadership is hoping to get on the record with new funds as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram tells us where things stand tonight. Good evening, Chad.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. Crime is a liability for Democrats this fall. That's why Democrats aim to pass a batch of bills to bolster police, but liberals balked. Republicans relish the infighting, but Democrats downplayed differences over defunding the police.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. PETE AGUILAR (D-CA): We care about results and ultimately passing these bills off of the floor and ensuring that they become law is what we want. And so, yes, legislating is hard, legislating is difficult, but we prove that we can get it done.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (voice over): Members of the Squad like \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  and \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  oppose the bills, they argued the bills failed to hold police accountable.\par \par \uc2 In fact, on a procedural vote, Democrats persuaded Squad member Ayanna Presley to vote present rather than no to keep the left from killing the Democrats bills.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHELLE FISCHBACH (R-MI): Presumably the changes were made at the behest of the left wing defund the police activists that unfortunately have become the loudest voice in their party.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Not all liberals resisted the police bills as much as the Squad.\par \par \uc2 REP. JOYCE BEATTY (D-OH): I look at the glass half full is that we were able to take our big tent and put together the best deal that we can. It will never be perfect, and everyone will never like everything.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Democrats prep the bills to neutralize GOP attacks about defunding the police. But Democrats reminded voters that the GOP has its own issues with law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM MCGOVERN (D-MA): I'll remind my Republican colleagues that just last month, a number of them openly and unabashedly messaged around defunding and dismantling the \uc2 FBI\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Democrats just passed the police bills last hour, but they might be political window dressing. It's doubtful the Senate can sink up before the election, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow it all. Chad, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The death toll is up to nine tonight in Iran from street protests there over the death of a young woman in police custody. Iran's largest telecom provider has now shut down internet service to the country to try to turn down the temperature there.\par \par \uc2 The Biden White House is not saying much about the situation. But at the same time, it's still hoping for a nuclear deal with Iran as the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  continues to meet.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Peter Doocy is live again in New York tonight with the latest. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. We just watched as the Beast carrying President Biden rolled down First Avenue towards the Wall Street landing zone where Marine One is waiting to begin his journey back to the White House.\par \par \uc2 And as the president leaves town, the big takeaway from this trip is that he prefers diplomacy even in dealing with other countries that accused the U.S. of all kinds of bad behavior.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I've spent a lot of time with not only the president of China but others about the international waters.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice over): China's Xi not in New York. Neither is Russia's Putin but Iran's President \uc2 Ebrahim Raisi\uc2  is.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 YOUNG KIM\uc2  (R-CA): The Iranian regime and President Raisi is a thug.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Iran's president has doubts about the Holocaust.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Do you believe the Holocaust happened, that six million Jews were slaughtered?\par \par \uc2 EBRAHIM RAISI, IRANIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): Look, historical events should be investigated by researchers and historians.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But the Biden White House is keeping alive the prospect of a nuclear deal with Iran.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PRESS SECRETARY, PENTAGON: Secretary Blinken says it's unlikely we're going to get a deal anytime soon. That doesn't mean that the diplomats are giving up.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: As diplomats work, the president has been relatively muted in reacting to protests in Iran following the death of a young woman who died after being arrested for improperly wearing a hijab.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We are not bashful about calling things out when we see it. Nobody should be hurt or killed or punished for peacefully protesting.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Another diplomatic hiccup, the president of South Korea insulted U.S. lawmakers about global health funding. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  translates his remark as "It would be so humiliating for Biden if these idiots don't pass it in Congress."\par \par \uc2 No response from the White House as the president went about his day meeting with FEMA workers responding to Hurricane Fiona in Puerto Rico.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: And, I know, bad joke, I'm from the federal government, and I'm here to help. But we really are. And I think we -- I hope you're satisfied with the response so far.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): President Biden just spent a little less than two hours at a mid-town fundraiser where only 35 people managed to scrape together $1.8 million for the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 So, President Biden is helping seven figures at a time even though some of these Democratic candidates have been keeping him at arm's length, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy outside the United Nations. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 The head of the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 , the CDC is apparently not on board with President Biden over his belief the coronavirus pandemic is over.\par \par \uc2 Dr. \uc2 Rochelle Walensky\uc2  spoke to ABC News about the current climate today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What was your reaction when you heard President Biden say the pandemic is over?\par \par \uc2 ROCHELLE WALENSKY\uc2 , DIRECTOR, CDC: We're in a different place.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: But the pandemic is not over from your perspective?\par \par \uc2 WALENSKY: Well, we continue to encourage people to do all the things that they can do to protect themselves.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I'll take that as a -- as a no.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Walensky receive the new COVID booster shot today. She says millions of Americans have received that updated vaccine.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, major news about almost $50 billion of your tax money stolen from a program designed to help people during the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Lucas Tomlinson joins us with the staggering details. Good evening, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. It's a lot of money. The Labor Department's inspector general says $45.6 billion was stolen from the country's unemployment insurance program using social security numbers obtained from dead people and prisoners. The extent of the damage is far greater than first estimated over a year ago, which back then put the number at $16 billion stolen. Now, the extent of the fraud is nearly three times greater and the amount of stolen cash could be even more.\par \par \uc2 The I.G. report released this afternoon says "Despite the Office of Inspector General's continued efforts to identify potentially fraudulent payments to ineligible claimants, we continue to experience delays in obtaining the needed unemployment insurance data."\par \par \uc2 According to the I.G. report, scammers filed bogus unemployment claims in multiple states using more than 200,000 stolen social security numbers from dead people and prisoners from federal jails who were ineligible to get these funds paid for by the roughly $5 trillion in taxpayer funded COVID Relief over the past two years. This is the third major scam involving COVID relief funds in just the past two days, Bret.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, the Feds uncovered what they called at the time, the largest pandemic relief fraud, yet, charging 47 people who are stealing $250 million that was supposed to go to feed hungry children in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 And now outside Chicago, more than two dozen people have been charged in another scam using pandemic relief cash. Authorities say some of the suspects used the funds to spring themselves from jail by using the stolen funds to post bond.\par \par \uc2 And Republican Senator Roger Marshall from Kansas has introduced a new resolution to end the COVID emergency declaration in response to President Biden's recent comments on "60 Minutes" saying the pandemic is over. I'm sure these fraud cases will help as well, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That seems like a drip drip drip. We're getting something new every day.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: We almost need a grapevine -- a COVID grapevine to keep track of all this, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Lucas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A North Dakota man who says he intentionally ran down a teenager with his SUV over the teenager's Republican politics is out of jail tonight. That teenager is dead. Correspondent Matt Finn has details.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MATT FINN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): \uc2 North Dakota Highway Patrol\uc2  tells Fox News, 41-year-old Shannon Brandt called 911 on himself last Sunday admitting to hitting 18-year-old Cayler Ellingson with his car, killing him.\par \par \uc2 Police say the two had some type of altercation at a street concert in the small town of McHenry. In the early morning hours, the teen called his mother saying Brandt was chasing him. Brandt told police the teen was threatening him.\par \par \uc2 Court documents revealing "Brandt admitted to striking the pedestrian with his car because he had a political argument with a pedestrian and believe the pedestrian was calling people to come get him."\par \par \uc2 Brandt admitted to state radio that he hit the pedestrian and that the pedestrian was part of a Republican extremist group. Police tell Fox they are not ruling out that a political argument started the deadly incident but they haven't confirmed Brandt's account.\par \par \uc2 CAPT. BRYAN NIEWIND, NORTH DAKOTA HIGHWAY PATROL: When Brandt called 911, he identified that there was maybe a political motivation to what was occurring. That's the only individual that we've heard that from so far. We haven't been able to corroborate that at all.\par \par \uc2 FINN: North Dakota highway patrol says they also have not confirmed that the teen belong to any right wing extremist group. They do not think Brandt and Ellingson knew each other.\par \par \uc2 Brandt is charged with vehicular homicide with DUI and leaving the scene of a fatal crash. The investigation is ongoing as the rural community mourns a seemingly senseless and tragic loss.\par \par \uc2 NIEWIND: This is a very very small community. So, this is really hitting home for those people out there. Especially for the family of Cayler Ellingson and all his family and friends.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FINN (on camera): And Brandt is free on $50,000 bond. Police tell us he has lawyered up and has stopped talking to teen's mother, declined to speak with us, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: More on this with the panel. Matt, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Worries over inflation interest rates and a possible recession sent stocks down again today. Some say we're in a recession. The Dow lost 107, the S&P 500 fell 32, the NASDAQ dropped 153.\par \par \uc2 Up next, one of the most dangerous jobs in the world. We'll take you there. We'll show you what it's like from Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 61 in Hartford, Connecticut as internet host Alex Jones takes the stand in the trial to determine how much he has to pay to families of Sandy Hook shooting victims.\par \par \uc2 Jones was found liable for calling the mass shooting at the school a hoax.\par \par \uc2 Fox 35 in Orlando is the first magic mushroom dispensary in the country opens in Tampa's Ybor City neighborhood. The mushrooms are illegal, but the version sold at this store is a weaker and legal dose that does not contain a mind altering compound.\par \par \uc2 The store owner says the mushrooms are beneficial for trauma, PTSD and anxiety.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Phoenix from our affiliate Fox 10. One of the big stories there tonight. After Rob Schneider surprises customers at a drive thru restaurant, Snyder was serving up chicken fingers and movie tickets to promote his new film "Daddy Daughter Trip" which opens next week, that was a surprise for those restaurant tours. The movie was shot in Arizona where Schneider lives.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT, we'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: People in Bermuda and far eastern Canada are preparing for a blast from Hurricane Fiona. That storm is headed that way after leaving hundreds of people stranded across Puerto Rico, as we've been showing you for the past couple of days.\par \par \uc2 Government officials have been working with religious groups, nonprofits and others raving landslides, thick mud, and broken asphalt by foot to try to provide food water and medicine for people in need.\par \par \uc2 About 850,000 people in Puerto Rico are still have no electricity. More than 400,000 customers are still without water tonight.\par \par \uc2 A Malaysian defense contractor who orchestrated one of the largest bribery scandals in U.S. military history, has been arrested in Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. Marshal Service says Leonard Glenn Francis, also known as Fat Leonard was arrested Tuesday, as he was about to board a plane in Caracas.\par \par \uc2 Francis was under a home arrest in San Diego when he cut off his GPS, ankle bracelet, and escaped in early September, prompting an international manhunt.\par \par \uc2 Russian and Ukrainian forces exchanged missile and artillery fire today as both sides refuse to concede territory. The battles come as Russia struggles to regain momentum after suffering losses during a blistering counter offensive over recent days by Ukrainian troops.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, our team there shows you one of the most dangerous jobs in the world. Correspondent Jeff Paul and his team watch technicians work to disarm some of the conflicts deadliest weapons.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): It's a job where every little move can made the difference between life and death.\par \par \uc2 Somehow, these women and men strap on their ballistic gear and push past the fear as they hunt for explosives.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KOZACHOK ANDRII, HALO TRUST TECHNICIAN: Well, in our work, there is no need to worry. After all, you can twitch and make a wrong move.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Kozachok Andrii and his family fled the frontlines of their home in the Donetsk region. Now, he and many other Ukrainians in the Kyiv region working for the nonprofit \uc2 HALO Trust\uc2 , are scanning and digging for hidden explosives left behind by Russian forces.\par \par \uc2 PAUL (on camera): Everything on this side of these red tip wooden sticks has been cleared. But beyond that into this forest, there's an untold number of dangers that have yet to be discovered, including grenades attached to tripwire, and anti-tank mines.\par \par \uc2 In fact, just a couple of months ago, a farmer hit one right here on the side of the road. Fortunately, he survived, but others aren't so lucky.\par \par \uc2 PAUL (voice-over): As fighting continues in the East, Ukraine took center stage at the \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  says no nation should be able to redraw borders by force and criticized Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  for his recent threat to use all weapons systems available.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF STATE: One man chose this war, one man can end it. Because if Russia stops fighting, the war ends. If Ukraine stops fighting, Ukraine ends.\par \par \uc2 But when Russian Foreign Minister Sergei Lavrov spoke, he painted a much different picture. Saying, Russia is the victim of Western aggression.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PAUL (on camera): Now, much like the tactic used in 2014 with the annexation of Crimea, four occupied regions in Ukraine, starting tomorrow, we'll begin voting in a Kremlin engineered referendum on whether or not to join Russia.\par \par \uc2 However, U.S. and Ukrainian officials are calling it a sham, and don't believe the results should be recognized. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jeff Paul in Kyiv wrapping up his tour there. Great job on the ground, Jeff. Thanks.\par \par \uc2 Are now -- up next, our midterm spotlight race of the day and a look at what both parties are trying to do to get the female vote this year.\par \par \uc2 First, beyond our borders tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): A stampede for tickets to a cricket match in India leaves 20 people injured. It happened as nearly 30,000 people waited in long lines there for only 3,000 tickets. Seven of the 20 people hurt were hospitalized in serious condition.\par \par \uc2 At least two people were killed during a powerful earthquake in Mexico. The magnitude 6.8 quake damaged buildings, set off landslides in the western part of the country. Mexico's president says it was an aftershock from a more powerful earthquake Monday that also resulted in two deaths.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Sydney, Australia. One of the big stories there tonight. Australian wildlife experts rescue 32 of 230 whales stranded on the west coast of Tasmania yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Only 35 whales survived the pounding surf overnight. The whales beached two years to the day after the largest mass stranding in Australian history was discovered in the same harbor.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Tonight's "MIDTERM WATCH", race report looks at the battleground state of North Carolina. President Trump won that state in 2020 by just 1- 1/3 percentage points over \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RealClearPolitics polling shows a tight Senate race to replace retiring Republican \uc2 Richard Burr\uc2 . GOP representative, \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2 , holds a narrow two point lead over Democratic challenger and former North Carolina Supreme Court Chief Justice Cheri Beasley.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile the House seat vacated by Budd has become one of the most competitive races in the country.\par \par \uc2 Our Fox News Power Rankings designates the redrawn 13th Congressional district as one of 30 toss-ups right there in the yellow for control of the House in November. Two term Democratic State Senator Wiley Nickel hopes to flip the seat against Republican challenger Bo Hines.\par \par \uc2 Both sides are putting a premium on the female vote as the election nears. Tonight, senior national correspondent Rich Edson looks at what issues the respective parties are using.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA COOLEY, REPUBLICAN VOTER: We have a family of eight, and spending $500 three years ago, two years ago even would have gotten us at least 