R: The investigation into materials taken from former President Trump's Florida home is on again tonight after a court ordered pause. An appeals court ruled late yesterday that the examination of the documents by federal authorities can now resume. Correspondent David Spunt is at the Justice Department tonight with the latest.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Former president \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on the defensive six weeks after the \uc2 FBI\uc2  searched his south Florida estate.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT: It's a very unfair situation. I don't see how they could prosecute me.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Among nearly 11,000 seized documents, roughly 100 were marked classified. But, once again, Trump claims he declassified them before leaving office.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : It doesn't have to be the process as I understand it. It doesn't have to be. If you are the president of the United States you can declassify just by saying it's declassified, even by thinking about it.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: But multiple national security experts tell FOX News there is a binding process. In its ruling about the documents, the appeals court wrote "Plaintiff suggests that he may have declassified these documents when he was president. But the record contains no evidence that any of these records were declassified." The court ruling means federal investigators can once again begin reviewing the classified material immediately.\par \par \uc2 The criminal investigation was put on hold after District Court Judge Aileen Cannon appointed a special master to decide if any material was attorney-client or executive privilege.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS DUPREE, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: The fact that this is a court of appeals, two out of the three judges were appointed by President Trump himself, I think would make this decision harder to attack as kind of a partisan approach by judges.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: The power of the special master is now limited. Judge Raymond Dearie no longer has access to the classified material and neither does the Trump team.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): Bret, former President Trump's attorneys have until the end of the month to certify everything on the list of items seized is accurate, those items seized from Mar-a-Lago. That may contradict some of the comments made by the former president, including the allegation that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  planted evidence. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  vehemently denies that charge. It's also possible the former president's legal team could take this case directly to the Supreme Court. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt at the Justice Department. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Critical Race Theory, or CRT, which links racial discrimination to America's foundations and legal system, has become a hotly debated subject on the campaign trail and in school board meetings across the country. Parents and politicians argue whether CRT should be taught in public schools.\par \par \uc2 But as you will see in my new FOX Nation series "The Unauthorized History of Socialism, Volume II," CRT is actual rooted in ideas that go back decades.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Providing the framework for "The 1619 Project" and \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  is an approach to history called Critical Race Theory or CRT, an adaptation of critical theory, which was developed more than a half century ago by the Marxist scholars of the Frankfurt school.\par \par \uc2 IAIN MURRAY, AUTHOR, "THE SOCIALIST TEMPTATION": Critical Race Theory just applies the ideas of critical theory to the problem of all racism in America. So it says the problem of racism isn't just one individual's reaction to another individual. It's not just individual bigotry. It's because the system that was set up. America itself is founded on things that either facilitate or entail racism.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED 