ll despite divisions. This is according to the A.P., "The House ended up approving the money including for departments with fewer than 125 officers and aid for de-escalation training and mental health services. To get more liberals on board, language was included that would allow the Justice Department to have discretion over which police departments are permitted to receive the grants. It would also allocate $50 million for data collection on police practices and community safety."\par \par \uc2 That was not sitting well with some, including Congresswoman \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2 . She put out a statement saying, "Even the most barebones accountability like those in the House passed Justice in Policing Act were not incorporated into Representative Gottheimer billed which would add nearly a quarter billion dollars in police funding over the next five years without addressing the crisis of police brutality."\par \par \uc2 With that, let's bring in our panel, Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner," \uc2 Mara Liasson\uc2 , national political correspondent of National Public Radio, and syndicated radio host \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Hugh, it's a real question about whether the Senate moves forward with this bill or not. It's obvious precarious to start have having these discussions with progressive Democrats and talking about defunding or the like just weeks before the election.\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: I think it was a bad move by the Democrats. It's the same \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2  and the likeminded Democrats here. Report let in from Minnesota. Voting begins in Minnesota tomorrow in the third congressional district, so-called moderate \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2  is being challenged by, for example, by Tom Weiler, a submariner turned first time congressional candidate. Wherever he goes Weiler talks about crime and fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 Dean Phillips\uc2  has spent all month negotiating with the squad, these radical members, to try and pretend that they are tough on crime. It doesn't do anything about fentanyl. It doesn't do anything about the border. It doesn't do anything about the burned out areas of Minneapolis. I think it is shouting into the wind, and the Democrats would have been better off to spend their time on the ground talking to voters about fentanyl and the border and promising to do something next time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mara, "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " writes it this, "Law enforcement funding package splits Democrats ahead of the midterm election with the legislation languishing. Vulnerable Democrats are losing out a potential political boost from passing a pro-police bill. There is little time remaining before November to campaign on such a vote or produce an advertisement attempting to claim credit." As we mentioned, the House moved it forward, but there's real questions in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 MARA LIASSON\uc2 , NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, NATIONAL PUBLIC RADIO: Yes, the House passed a police funding bill. Yes, there are liberal leftwing members of the House caucus that don't want to pass that. But they didn't prevail. I don't think it's going to go anywhere in the Senate. I think most Democratic operatives that you talk to think crime is just a bad issue for Democrats this year. They would be better off talking about other things, about abortion, about political violence, about gun safety. But crime is something that works against Democrats because a lot of Democrats are mayors of these big cities that have seen their crime rates spike. And I agree, it's kind of late for Democrats to turn this around.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Byron?\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, "THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER": Yes, these were for messaging bills. That was the whole purpose of them, to fight the idea that Democrats are soft on crime when, in fact, many of them actually are soft on crime. Not all the measures were that way. One of them got 150 Republican votes, the one to give more money to smaller police departments. But others had kind of standard Democratic ideas like sending mental health professionals to crime scenes as opposed to police officers.\par \par \uc2 And the idea is Democrats can go on the campaign trail and say I voted to fund the police, not defund the police. Republicans are not at all worried about this because they think the whole perception, public's perception of the Democrats and crime is set in stone.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, and some big races, Democrats are already using that fund the police line in those ads.\par \par \uc2 Now, we remember President Biden in front of Independence Hall with that speech about extremist MAGA. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: MAGA Republicans have made their choice. They embrace anger. They thrive on chaos.\par \par \uc2 We are all called by duty and conscience to confront extremists who put their own pursuit of power above all else.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He did speak out against violence, we should point out. But you have this case now in North Dakota. A.P. writes this way, "The driver argues political argument preceded teen's death. Shannon Brandt told investigators he left the scene after striking," his name is Cayler Ellingson, "returned briefly, called 911, then left again. The court documents said Brandt told investigators he had been drinking alcohol before striking Ellingson and thought the teen was calling people to do him harm after they argued and that he felt threatened." He said the teen, Hugh, was a Republican extremist, and --\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: It doesn't add up, Bret. It's a DUI. It's vehicular homicide, it's a hit and run. The fellow who has now been charged and released on an astonishing low $50,000 bail came up with a story. I think it sounds like some kind of drunk excuse for running into a kid, whether or not he was having road rage, whatever. But this 18-year-old Mr. Ellingson is dead, and this defendant is going to be back behind bars very soon. I don't think it's a political case yet. I would be very reluctant to go there. It sounds as though the only person to raise the issue of political violence is the suspect who is already not credible.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Exactly. So the point being, Mara, all of these cases, you can go book to the shooting on the GOP baseball field. They do get handled differently. This one, to Hugh's point, is probably the driver making excuses. But they do get covered and dealt with differently.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes. January 6th is in there, too. There is too much political -- I don't think there is a case of political violence, the one in North Dakota. But I do think there is too much political violence. Every party should call it out whether it happens from someone sympathetic to their side or not, period.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And last word, Byron.\par \par \uc2 YORK: Let's wait a little while to find more about what happened. But you did play President Biden's speech, and we have heard many, many warnings about the possibility for political violence spreading in this country, and those warnings usually are of MAGA Republicans attacking progressive Democrats. This case does not fit that mold at all, and we should wait a little longer to see if there is anything to support what the suspect himself said about the victim being a, quote, extremist Republican.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, stand by, if you would. Up next, trouble in Iran and the administration's reaction to it. And the GOP pressuring Democrats over the border crisis, where that's going.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Would it work against our interests to be speaking loudly in support frequently of these kinds of protests.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: We are not bashful calling things out when we see it. And we have talked about the protests in Iran. Nobody should be hurt or killed or punished for peacefully protesting anywhere in the world.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BERMAN: John Kirby talking of the National Security Council talking about the protests, the street protests in Iran over the death of this woman in police custody. They have been going, they have been stepping up as people are taking off their hijabs taking to the streets amid pushback of the Tehran government. \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 , Treasury Secretary, issuing a statement about sanctions against Iran's morality police and senior security officials in the wake of this death, but there hasn't been a lot of public statements from the administration. We'll start there, back with the panel. Mara, what about this?\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes, look, what happened in Iran was pretty horrific. A woman seems to have been beaten to death in police custody, and she was taken into custody because she exposed too much of her hair. You saw in those pictures of the protestors, there were a lot of women without hijabs in that crowd.\par \par \uc2 So the Iranian government is facing a lot of unhappy citizens. It's not just about how they treat women. It's also the economy, which is in bad, bad shape there. And the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  is on record supporting the protesters. And obviously they could do more and probably should. But the point is that the Iranian government is feeling pressure from its own people.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. President Biden was Vice President Biden, Hugh, when the \uc2 Obama\uc2  administration took criticism for not speaking up louder for the Green Revolution in Iran that some people believed could have risen up there.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: This is a bad replay of that. When President \uc2 Obama\uc2  was silent at the beginning of his term, the Green Revolution, which might have gotten traction and fell away and was repressed. Thirty-six people are reported to have been killed in the last six days after the death of Mahsa Amini in Iranian custody. The numbers that we have can be lower than that, but that's according to the Iran \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2 . It is inconceivable that we are dealing with Iran right now to relieve sanctions, and Admiral Kirby was very general in his condemnation, not specific to the Iran and Mullahs. The Iranian president was at the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . denouncing the U.S. this week. The Iranian president is one of the hardliners.\par \par \uc2 It is inconceivable that we would send them million and billions of dollars, which will go right to Russia because that's their strategic partner. It's no figuring out the Biden administration's Iran policy. There isn't one.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That's the point, Byron. And that question by Jackie (ph) as we are negotiating this Iran deal, nuclear deal, they say they are speaking out about protests. But they still want the nuclear deal.\par \par \uc2 YORK: Absolutely. And these are really fundamental protests in Iran. These are we do not to live in Islamic theocracy. The protests take an extraordinary amount of courage for people to be engaged in. And, yes, the president spoke about it at the United Nations, but it really was pretty tepid. And I think the Republicans, a lot of Republicans are talking about the Green Revolution, what \uc2 Obama\uc2  did when Biden was vice president.\par \par \uc2 So, the fact is you are going to hear a lot from the political opposition saying that President Biden is just kind of laying low, soft pedaling what is going on in Iran because he so wants this deal. And that's not going to go away.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, up on Capitol Hill, Republicans are putting forward an agenda officially tomorrow. But met with all the Republicans today. "Politico" says "Kevin McCarthy reaches for the GOP's brass ring, a unifying agenda. Nearly two years after they first shared their plans for a House GOP blueprint, September of 2020, after Friday it will be full steam ahead as Republicans plan town halls, media interviews to drum up voter interest in their governing document. This timeframe also tracks with Gingrich's," Newt Gingrich's, "nearly three decades ago." This is a commitment, that was a contract. Mara, it's the same blueprint.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes, it's a blueprint. I don't think it will have much effect on the election rules, but it's really helpful to have one if you are going to be new majority. You've got to have a list of things that you are going to do positively, not just attack the administration. So I think it's a good thing to do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hugh?\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: Inflation, crime, and the border, they're all in the commitment, and they can't say it enough between now and the election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And for candidates, it gives them a blueprint, Byron, to say I'm signed on, and here is what the GOP will do?\par \par \uc2 Absolutely. It's really actually quite important in the election, because you can say as a candidate, things are terrible, things are terrible. Democrats are to blame. But voters do want to know what you are going to do about it. And this whole thing, Kevin McCarthy has worked very hard to get every single Republican in the House on board with this, will talk about the economy, crime, and the border, as Hugh mentioned.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And finally tonight, a SPECIAL REPORT salute.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It went back inside, so I rushed inside to get them out. And I was going to go out of the back of the house.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It might be a SPECIAL REPORT delivery. An \uc2 Amazon\uc2  delivery driver being hailed as a hero after saving a family from their burning home. Kevin Rivera noticed the blaze as he was finishing his route in New York. He rushed inside to help several people safely escape, including a woman and a baby. Firefighters arrived at the home and found the family outside. Nice job.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, we will talk with House foreign affairs committee chairman Democrat \uc2 Gregory Meeks\uc2  about various international challenges for the U.S. and what is happening on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts right now with Scott Baio.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220923ei9m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Slams \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  Invasion Of Ukraine; \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  And Family Accused Of Inflating Wealth; \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Says Pandemic Over Amid Vaccine Job Losses; \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  Threatens Nuclear War Amid Recent Setbacks; New Federal Reserve Rate Hike Amid Inflation Worries; Fears Latest Rate Hike Could Add To Federal Deficit; \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  Warns GOP Will Find Ways To Finish Strong; Polling For Midterm Senate Races Across U.S. Within Margin Of Error Between Democratic And Republican Candidates; Russia President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  Calls Up 300,000 Reservists To Fight In Ukraine And Hints At Possible Use Of Nuclear Weapons; President Biden Gives Speech To \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  Criticizing Ongoing Russian Invasion Of Ukraine; New York Attorney General Files Civil Suit Against \uc2 Trump Organization\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Brit Hume, Griff Jenkins, Laura Ingle,  Jackie DeAngelis, Greg Palkot \par \uc2 7,905 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: And he's got the leading batting average in RB -- leading RBIs in the league as well. He's in really (INAUDIBLE) triple crown and maybe be the MVP. Congrats to him and hope he gets it soon.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: How far behind Barry Bonds?\par \par \uc2 FORD JR.: Mostly those guys, they think about them a little differently.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Asterisk. There is an Asterisk.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's it for us. Watch your mouth, Dana. SPECIAL REPORT up next. Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Hello, Greg. Good evening welcome Washington. I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, we're following several major stories. The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is raising its key interest rate again to try to combat runaway inflation.\par \par \uc2 Russian leader \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  calls for massive troop mobilization and warns he could use nuclear weapons in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 We began with President Biden saying Russia is trying to extinguish Ukraine's right to exist. It was the president's first address to the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  since