that. After countless attacks on Americans and multiple confirmed plots against U.S. officials, the Biden administration continues to cozy up to Iran in hopes of a mythical, so-called deal\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But the president says he prefers diplomacy.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: While the United States is prepared for a mutual return to the Joint Comprehensive Plan of Action if Iran steps up to its obligations. The United States is clear. We will not allow Iran to acquire a nuclear weapon.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The president didn't spend much time talking about China.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We do not seek conflict. We do not seek a Cold War. We do not ask any nation to choose between the United States or any other partner.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And even though he was today's headliner, the president left diplomats with this.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Thank you for your tolerance listening to me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (on camera): He spoke for 29 minutes and the only border he brought up was the one between Russia and Ukraine. Never mentioned the crush of migrants at the U.S. Mexico border. Immigration, migrants, asylum seekers all words this president did not use today, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter, meantime, overseas these protests in Iran are growing. This is over the death of this young woman after she was arrested in Tehran by the Iranian notorious morality police. So, how closely is the president monitoring these street protests? Is he speaking out about it?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: He's speaking out about it in broad terms, Bret. I spoke to a White House official, a senior official who's traveling with the president for these meetings who told me the president is being kept abreast of developments in Iran. It is something that's on his radar.\par \par \uc2 Like you said, these protests started when a young woman died in police custody after an arrest for wearing a hijab improperly.\par \par \uc2 Now, these protesters are taking to the streets and taking off their headscarves in defiance. President Biden says he stands with the brave citizens and brave women of Iran demonstrating to secure basic rights. But there are no plans for President Biden and Iran's president to hash any of this out in person even though they are both here, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy outside the United Nations. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 We'll have a full report on \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 's latest moves and the threats from Russia later in the show.\par \par \uc2 Now, to Wall Street, it took a hit on the heels of today's three quarters of a point Fed hike -- the Fed rate hike. The Dow plunging 522, the S&P 500 lost 66, the NASDAQ fell 205. We'll have stored on that in a bit as well.\par \par \uc2 There is new legal trouble tonight for former President Trump and his family. It has to do with allegations he vastly inflated his actual wealth.\par \par \uc2 Senior Correspondent Laura Ingle has the details tonight from New York. Good evening, Laura.\par \par \uc2 LAURA INGLE, FOX NEWS CHANNEL SENIOR CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, Bret. Well, New York Attorney General Letitia James announcing her office has filed a civil lawsuit against former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , three of his adult children and executives of his company accusing them of a pattern of financial fraud.\par \par \uc2 The 220-page lawsuit accuses Mr. Trump and his family of making more than 200 false and misleading asset valuations in the company's financial statements since 2011, including the former president's triplex apartment in Trump Tower, which he claimed is 30,000 square feet. James says it's less than 11,000.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LETITIA JAMES, NEW YORK ATTORNEY GENERAL: This investigation revealed that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  engaged in years of illegal conduct to inflate his net worth to achieve, to deceive banks and the people of the great state of New York.\par \par \uc2 Claiming you have money that you do not have does not amount to the art of the deal. It's the art of the steal.\par \par \uc2 INGLE (voice over): And this lawsuit calls for the former president to pay a quarter of a billion dollars in restitution.\par \par \uc2 Trump firing back on Truth Social saying in part, another witch hunt by a racist Attorney General Letitia James, who failed in her run for governor getting almost zero support from the public and now is doing poorly.\par \par \uc2 A former Attorney General Bill Barr calls James's lawsuit a political hit job.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BARR\uc2 , FORMER ATTORNEY GENERAL: What ultimately persuades me that this is a political hit job is she grossly overreaches when she tries to drag the children into this.\par \par \uc2 INGLE: The former president is facing a long list of investigations in several states, including two separate investigations into his part in the January 6, Capitol Hill incident, the classified document investigation by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , as well as an investigation into his part in the Georgia election results, and the Manhattan D.A.'s criminal suit.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 INGLE (on camera): Now, if James is successful, it could prevent the former president and three of his children from continuing to run their real estate empire here in New York State, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We need a whiteboard for all of this. Laura, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The House has passed legislation just a couple of hours ago to overhaul the rules for certifying the results of a presidential election. This bill is similar to bipartisan legislation moving through the Senate now, it would overhaul an 1800 era statute, known as the Electoral Count Act that governs along with the U.S. Constitution, how states and Congress certify electors and declare presidential election winners.\par \par \uc2 Its reaction obviously to the events following the 2020 voting and former President Trump's contention he actually won.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, a look at what's happening to the workers who lost their jobs over their refusal to be vaccinated against the coronavirus.\par \par \uc2 President Biden insists the pandemic is now over, he told "60 minutes" that and today, a federal judge ordered a big change in one of the president's COVID policies.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Griff Jenkins joins us tonight with this. Good evening, Griff.\par \par \uc2 GRIFF JENKINS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Another ruling in a sea of lawsuits. This one at Louisiana federal judge permanently blocking President Biden's vaccine mandates in 24 states. You can see here on this map it is largely red states not including bluer areas like New York and California where many are fighting to get their jobs back after refusing to comply with vaccine mandates.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KNOX, FIREFIGHTER, LOS ANGELES: It's destroyed careers, it's destroyed families.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (voice over): 22-year veteran firefighter John Knox has gone nearly a year without a paycheck after being put on leave for rejecting L.A.'s vaccine mandate, despite answering the call during the hardest times.\par \par \uc2 KNOX: We still had to do our job. We couldn't -- you know, we can't perform 911 duties via Zoom. It just doesn't work, you know, it's hard to put out a fire that way.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: This as the White House attempts to walk back President Biden's declaration that the pandemic is over.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: So, we are in a different time. He's been very consistent about that.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: That isn't sitting well with New York City sanitation worker Curtis Cutler, denied a religious exemption and fired despite being a deacon in his church.\par \par \uc2 CURTIS CUTLER, FIRED SANITATION WORKER, NEW YORK CITY: Mr. President, if you would, please listen to my story. And please think about all of my fellow brothers and sisters who are in New York City or all the areas who have been affected by those who won't let this mandate go. Please put them back to work.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: Even before the president's comments, this New York City firefighter's testimony began drawing attention.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN FOGARTY, FORMER FDNY CAPTAIN: I was allowed to work through the pandemic, but I wasn't allowed to eat in a restaurant. I can wear the uniform, go to a burning building but not eat here. What's wrong with you people?\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: At the federal level, State Department diplomat Marcus Thornton is leading the fight against President Biden's executive mandate. Representing a group of more than a thousand federal employees in a lawsuit in a federal appeals court, opposing the vaccine for personal religious and medical reasons.\par \par \uc2 MARCUS THORNTON, PRESIDENT, FEDS FOR MEDICAL FREEDOM: over the past year, the government has threatened me with termination, excluded me from the workforce, discriminated against me and subjected me to harassment for standing up for those very principles.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JENKINS (on camera): And the ruling in the federal case is expected in the coming weeks. Meanwhile, New York City dropped their private employer vaccine mandate yesterday, however, Bret public servants are still forced to comply.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There are a lot of these workers that are affected like this.\par \par \uc2 JENKINS: There are thousands and we don't actually know that number. The federal government doesn't publicize that. And there's New York, California, all states have a lot of lawsuits. And not only that, we can't account for those that simply left their job because they didn't want to get tangled up in it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Griff, thank you. We'll follow it.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in Fox News Senior Political Analyst Brit Hume. Brit, good evening, I wanted to take you back to our first story. And that's President Biden at the United Nations, his words about Russia, the threats we're seeing from \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . Your thoughts on that speech and what he's saying?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST (on camera): Well, I think Russia was the centerpiece of it really, given all this been happening. And I think, you know, I got to say this for the president. He's been pretty steady on Russia.\par \par \uc2 I mean, he is -- he is -- he said from the start that our objective was to help Ukraine in whatever way we could short of committing American forces. And that is basically what has happened.\par \par \uc2 Now, there have been complaints along the way that we weren't transferring weapons soon enough, or engraved enough numbers and so on. But if you look at what's happening on the battlefield, for the moment, at least, it appears the tide has turned in Ukraine's favor with no -- you know, thanks in considerable part, to weapons we have supplied and the president has kept us out of it other than that.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think, you know, on that point, he's been steady, and one imagines that he have some credibility inside of the \uc2 U.N\uc2 ., that remains to be seen of course, because countries, you know, don't want to commit.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, it's interesting. I mean, to that point, he's been firm about sending weapons and not sending U.S. troops in Ukraine. Yet, when asked about Taiwan on "60 minutes", he was really pretty firm that if China attacks, U.S. forces would defend Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 HUME: He was indeed, Bret. And he wasn't asked about it in any way that left room for ambiguity. He was very firm and very direct that yes, American forces would be committed to Taiwan to defend it.\par \par \uc2 Now, that goes well beyond the obligations the U.S. has under the Taiwan Relations Act. And it also goes well beyond what the administration since has been saying through anonymous quotes and comments about what our policy is.\par \par \uc2 But the truth of the matter is, Bret, the person who set foreign policy in the executive branch, the \uc2 United States government\uc2  is the president. And if he says it's the policy, it should be the policy. And if it's not, that should require an investigation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. So, I mean, the difference here, between the Russia firmness and kind of staying on message and Taiwan, and now we look at the COVID story that Griff just did with this federal judge kind of overturning the vaccine mandate for head start and there's a number of federal cases dealing with masks and mandates of vaccines.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Once more, you know, in part about what the president has said. He said the pandemic is over. He said, you know, we're still struggling to deal with the disease in some ways, but not like it was, the pandemic is over.\par \par \uc2 Well, there's all kinds of stuff that depends on the funding and money available and policies and practices that are being put into effect or have been put into effect and still are, that depend on the idea that we're in a pandemic.\par \par \uc2 If he says it's over, the consequences should be serious. This is going to be fun to watch when they -- how they deal with this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's been a walk back for a couple of days. We'll see what happens next.\par \par \uc2 Brit Hume, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  decides on another interest rate hike as we just told you, we'll look at that -- what that means potentially for you and your family.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 10 in Phoenix as the owner of the NBA Suns and the WNBA Mercury says he will sell those teams after an investigation into racism and sexual harassment claims. Robert Sarver was suspended and fined by the \uc2 NBA\uc2  last week.\par \par \uc2 Fox 35 in Orlando as \uc2 NASA\uc2 's new moon rocket suffers another fuel leak, engineers tested the plumbing ahead of a launch attempt as early as next week.\par \par \uc2 Hazardous hydrogen fuel began escaping at the same place at the same time as before, despite new seals and other repairs.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Chicago from our affiliate Fox 32. One of the big stores there tonight, two people come forward to claim the 1.34 billion with a B dollar Mega Millions jackpot.\par \par \uc2 You know how many tickets we bought in this office for that? This occurs almost eight weeks after a single winning ticket to the late July drawing was sold in the Chicago suburb.\par \par \uc2 Lottery officials say the winners agreed to split the prize if they won. They have elected to remain anonymous. Good idea, I think.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the Beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A newly released report from the \uc2 Homeland Security Department\uc2  Inspector General concludes the Border Patrol did not always assign migrants and alien registration number before they were released into the U.S. The report says it was an effort to speed up processing.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Move migrants out of overcrowded facilities as fast as possible. The number allows immigration officials and law enforcement to track and locate a migrant's immigration file for a complete history of immigration encounters.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): It contains critical documents such as immigration forms, agent narratives of apprehension, and records checks. It would be very difficult to find a migrant after release who does not want to be found if there is no such number.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , as we mentioned, is raising its key interest rate by three quarters of a percentage point for the third straight time.\par \par \uc2 Fox Business correspondent Jackie DeAngelis is in New York tonight with a look at what that may mean for you. Good evening, Jackie.\par \par \uc2 JACKIE DEANGELIS, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK FINANCIAL CORRESPONDENT: Good evening to you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 That's right. \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  raising rates, three quarters of a percentage point. It was widely expected. Interest rates in this country are now at their highest since 2008.\par \par \uc2 Now, there have already been a total of five rate hikes this year. The last three have been 75 basis points and more are expected as the Fed tries to tame 8.3 percent inflation.\par \par \uc2 Powell had this to say today, when asked how long people will feel the pain of inflation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEROME POWELL\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, FEDERAL RESERVE: How long? I mean, it really depends on how long it takes for wages and more than that, prices to come down, for inflation to come down.\par \par \uc2 And you saw, you -- what you see in our -- in our projections today is that inflation moves down, you know, significantly over the course of next year, and then more than next year after that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS (voice-over): Right now, higher rates mean consumers will feel that pain.\par \par \uc2 For example, the rate on a 30-year fixed mortgage is just over six percent. Last year at this time, the rate was just under three percent.\par \par \uc2 If you're buying a $500,000 house then, versus now, and put 20 percent down, you'd be paying $747 more per month now. That's a payment increase of 45 percent.\par \par \uc2 And it doesn't end there. Today's move could push car financing over six percent as well. And credit card rates while they are generally on the very high side, they could creep up even more too.\par \par \uc2 And the data suggests that consumers are relying on credit cards more. As inflation forces them to eat into their savings, credit card debt is up 13 percent year over year.\par \par \uc2 DEANGELIS (on camera): The government isn't exempt either. The nonpartisan Committee for a Responsible Federal Budget warns this rate hike could add $2.1 trillion to the federal deficit over the next 10 years. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Jackie. Thank you. Up next, our spotlight midterm race and fair and balanced analysis of the latest polls from political pollsters, Kellyanne Conway and Douglas Schoen.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. Three people are dead after an explosion in Afghanistan's capital.\par \par \uc2 Taliban\uc2  officials says the blast occurred in a restaurant. Police have not said if it was an accident or the result of an attack there.\par \par \uc2 One American and two Russians arrive at the International Space Station. They launched from the Kazakhstan space facility today aboard a Russian spacecraft. It's the first flight for American Frank Rubio since he became an astronaut in 2017.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll be hearing that music a lot. Tonight's midterm "SPOTLIGHT" race report is on the Wisconsin Senate seat, where just one point separates Republican incumbent Ron Johnson, Democrat nominee Lieutenant Governor Mandela Barnes.\par \par \uc2 However, who is leading depends on who you ask. This week's Siena College poll has Mandela had won. Last week's \uc2 Marquette University\uc2  survey has the exact opposite margin in favor of Johnson.\par \par \uc2 This fast moving climate may be why some pollsters are steering clear Wisconsin. The evidence is building that a wave of women voters might be the difference maker. Wisconsin is one of several states where voter registrations among women have surged since the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade in June.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL CLINTON\uc2 , 42ND PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We could hold both of these houses, but we have to say the right things. And we have to note, the Republicans always closed well. Why? Because they find some new way to scare the living daylights out of swing voters about something. That's what they did in 2021 when they made critical race theory sound worse than smallpox.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . Let's get some analysis on that and where things stand tonight. Joining me are two pollsters. Kellyanne Conway, former senior counsellor to President Trump. And former adviser to President Clinton that man, Douglas Schoen. Thanks both for being here.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS SCHOEN, FORMER ADVISER TO PRESIDENT BILL CLINTON: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kellyanne, let me start with you. You heard from President Clinton there. What do you think of this environment, what he just said?\par \par \uc2 KELLYANNE CONWAY, FORMER SENIOR COUNSELOR TO PRESIDENT TRUMP: Well, Bret, the voters had been very clear for a long time what's motivating them this election cycle. Rising costs, rising crime would be at the top of the list.\par \par \uc2 You throw in there some abortion, some climate, but you also throw in there some fanatics. Every day, affordability has been imperiled. The feeling of safety and security in your neighborhoods, at the grocery store, at the gas pump, but also in your ability to pay the rent, in your ability to feel that your kids are being taught a curriculum that comports with what you think they should be taught in their schools.\par \par \uc2 So there is this great deal insecurity, instability, and a lack of public safety. I think that's been big for a while. And look, Trump and abortion are the two things that the Democrats want to run on this time. \uc2 Terry McAuliffe\uc2  tried that a year ago. He was running for his old job in Virginia. Instead of standing up and saying I'm going to do a job like did I before, here's what I did before, he ran on Trump and abortion. And Youngkin replied with Virginia and education.\par \par \uc2 So on Friday the Republicans will unveil their commitment to America. Leader McCarthy and others will do that. And it is an affirmative, forward looking, concise but meaty and doable plan. People in focus groups don't care if your plan is perfect. They care that you have one.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Have a plan. Doug, react to that, if you would, but I also want to put up an NBC poll recently on issues and which party would do a better job at various issues. Border security, Republicans, 56-20, crime Republicans 45-22, economy, Republicans 47-28, they're upside down on healthcare and abortion. But when you look at those numbers from the Democratic side, what do you see?\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: I get worried. What president Clinton said may well have been true about 2021, I will leave that to others. But for 2022, the Republicans have issues, huge issues. Kellyanne was right, inflation, prices, crime are huge, huge issues that are working for the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 What's, frankly, extraordinary to me, and I think a good economy in terms of consumer spending explains part of it, is the fact that in the Senate races, as you were suggesting in Wisconsin, Bret, they're largely within the margin of error. We could go, as you might want to, race by race. But the Democrats are doing relatively well. There has been a slight but not that dramatic improvement in Democratic performance in the House races, but I'd say the Republicans are on target to win the House, the Senate is 50/50 as we sit here today.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So let's just go into a couple of them. Pennsylvania is one everybody is really paying attention to. If we put up the Real Clear Politics average, and that is average of recent polls. It's still Fetterman with a slight lead. Kellyanne you've kind of been studying Pennsylvania and some of the polls there.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: I have. That was a reach state in 2016. Let me say this. I think in all of these Senate races, very similar to the presidential races in the 12 or 13 swing states, Bret. You're going to see two things, throughout -- both candidates under 50 percent and both candidates within a few points of each other, which is actually shy the margin of error exists. So the lead will go back and forth.\par \par \uc2 But what's different now than it was when Doug and I started this decades ago is that you can't just wait for those late in the game breaking deciders and just message to those independents and swing voters who really are aligned with the Republicans on most issues right now. You have to go early also with your ad campaign, because early voting has started in earnest in so many of these states. You can't wait until the last couple weeks for your October surprise.\par \par \uc2 So that's fundamentally, and for all the talk about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's approval rating going up, Reuters has him at 39 percent. And that's just Democrats coming back home because they are happy with all his out-of-control spending. They're happy with him making joyful noises on abortion and climate.\par \par \uc2 But I have this to say about these Senate races. I think it's a jump ball right now. I think the Republicans are going to eke it out. And they have great pickup opportunities in Arizona, Nevada, Georgia, New Hampshire would be their best opportunities. And then, of course, they have got to protect Wisconsin and Pennsylvania --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Even North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: And North Carolina. But I say this to you because the polls were so bad in 2016 and 2020 in host of those states, and they heavily favored Democrats. Just quickly, in Wisconsin an ABC News/"Washington Post" poll showed \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ahead by 17 points. Bret, Doug, if Biden or Trump were ahead by 17 points, it wouldn't be Wisconsin. They shouldn't even publish a poll like that. So I think these are really underrepresenting a Republican bounce there year.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There is frustration, Doug, in polls in general, Democrats and Republicans looking at them. You say Florida for Democrats is someplace that they could look to surprise. If you look at the Rubio-Demings race, it's really tight in the average of polls. If you look at the governor's race even, DeSantis and Crist, and people look at that and says DeSantis has this big war chest and he is really popular. It's not that big of a poll difference.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: That is correct. And couple of things going on, some of which Kellyanne emphasized, others I think deserve highlighting. The candidates that the Republicans are running that Kellyanne was underscoring outside of Florida have very high negative ratings. Dr. Oz, in particular. Arizona, similarly high negatives. Rubio doesn't have high negatives in Florida, but Val Demings is running on crime. She is running as a conservative on law enforcement, which is what Democrats have to do. They have to run away from liberal big spending that Kellyanne speaks about and be like Bill Clinton Democrats, and as he said, say the right things, which is separate yourself from the national party.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But that's tough, isn't it, Doug, to separate if your national party is out there with a message?\par \par \uc2 CONWAY: Saying defund the police.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: It's very tough, but not impossible. And that's why the Trump issue does come into play with people like Bolduc in New Hampshire, Dr. Oz, Arizona --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: -- because the negatives the Republican have are well over 50 percent. The generic vote, the partisan divide favors the Republicans. Kellyanne is right about that. Whether that will manifest itself on Election Day is the big question.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, that is what we are going to talk about a lot with people like you all. Thank you so much for coming.\par \par \uc2 SCHOEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  implies he might use nuclear weapons in Ukraine. We are going to look at that. Later, the panel on what the president said to the United Nations about Russia, Ukraine, and China.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hurricane Fiona strengthened into a category four storm today after devastating Puerto Rico, as we mentioned, then lashing the Dominican Republic and the Turks and Caicos Islands. It's forecast to squeeze past Bermuda later this week. The storm is blamed for directly causing at least four deaths in its march through the Caribbean. A majority of people in Puerto Rico were left without power and running water. Some still are.\par \par \uc2 Russian leader \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  says he is not bluffing about the possibility of using all means at his disposal to protect Russian territory. That, of course, as we mentioned before is seen as a reference to nuclear weapons. He is also ordering a mobilization of reservists in his country, a lot of them. Senior foreign affairs correspondent Greg Palkot has the latest tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is getting desperate. With the war in Ukraine dragging into its eighth month and a counteroffensive by Ukraine gaining background, in a rare televised address he called for a mobilization of reserve soldiers said to number some 300,000, the first such call up since World War II.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , RUSSIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): To protect our motherland, its sovereignty, and territorial integrity.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Putin also backed sham votes in areas of Ukraine now occupied by Moscow. They're expected to rubberstamp joining Russia.\par \par \uc2 PUTIN: We will support the decision about the future which will be taken.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Maybe most disturbing, a not so veiled threat about the possible use of nuclear weapons. Putin claims \uc2 NATO\uc2  states are engaged in nuclear blackmail against Russia, to which he'd respond.\par \par \uc2 PUTIN: If the territorial integrity of our country is threatened, we will, without doubt, use all available means to protect Russia and our people. This is not a bluff.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: It's irresponsible for a nuclear power to speak that way about the possible use of nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: In a virtual address from Kyiv to the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . general assembly today, Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  had this response.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT: A crime has been committed against Ukraine, and we demand just punishment.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Chanting "no more war" on the streets of Moscow and other cities tonight, yet another reaction -- crowds of Russians protesting the call up orders, clashing with police, hundreds arrested, facing jail sentences of 15 years. This after a report had run on airline tickets for reservists wanting to flee.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE (through translator): I have an 18-year-old son. I don't want him to die.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Amid word tonight of 10 prisoners including two Americans released in a Russia-Ukraine prisoner swap mediated by Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): The aunt of one of those Americans released who was fighting alongside Ukrainians, a former Army staff sergeant, had this to say -- we are just so deeply grateful. A lot of concern all around. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow it. Greg, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Here in the U.S., cancel culture is now a dominant feature of modern life on campus, in the workplace, and in the media. In a new series on FOX Nation, I trace the roots of today's politically correct censorship back to the teaching of Herbert Marcuse, a socialist academic from the \uc2 University of California San Diego\uc2 . He is just one of the thinkers who helped shape today's unique brand of socialism.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: U.S. involvement in Vietnam escalated, Paris erupted in student riots in 1968. And Marcuse was there. Suddenly he was a celebrity, courted by the press, who crowned him the godfather of the new left. Paradoxically, he did not embrace the freedom of expression that allowed him an international platform for his views. Instead, he developed a theory of what he called repressive tolerance, providing the basic argument for what today is called cancel culture.\par \par \uc2 IAIN MURRAY, AUTHOR, "THE SOCIALIST TEMPTATION": Marcuse believed free speech was a tool of oppression. Instead of believing in free speech, you should actually try to excise harmful speech from the dialogue.\par \par \uc2 JOSH MURAVCHIK, AUTHOR, "HEAVEN ON EARTH, THE RISE, FALL, AND AFTERLIFE OF SOCIALISM": Ironically, the way to true freedom and democracy is to institute oppression against the people who are the purveyors of the ideas of the ruling class. We have to stomp on the voices of intolerance, and to have tolerance flourish we have to suppress all the intolerant ideas in people.\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: This is what leads to the de-platforming of inconvenient speakers. It is what leads to trigger warnings on books, the idea that you bore to encounter an idea which may harm your development as a critical theorist. And, in the end, it leads to hate speech laws.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You'll want to see this. You can watch all five episodes of "The Unauthorized History of Socialism, Volume II" on FOX Nation. And you can find my original series on socialism there as well.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, the panel on President Biden's United Nations speech and new legal trouble for former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: No one threatened Russia and no one other than Russia sought conflict. No matter what else is happening in the world, the United States is ready to pursue critical arms control measures.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO \uc2 U.N\uc2 .: What he didn't do, which I wish he would have gone further, is call out the countries that are helping Russia.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. KEITH KELLOGG (RET), FORMER PENCE NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: We need to take his threat very, very seriously, and the president needs to racket to that. He is moving into the area where he's going to be a wartime president, and he needs to understand that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Reaction to President Biden at the United Nations talking about Russia. Meantime, that nuclear threat that we have been referencing from \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , other senators reacting to it, Senator \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  with ah statement, "If Russia uses nuclear weapons, peaceful and responsible nations would be forced to turn back the clock for those aligned with a nuclear terror state."\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Russia is recruiting people, trying to get them, and it's not really recruiting. Breaking Russian airlines order to stop selling tickets to Russian men 18 to 65. Flights from Moscow to the capital of Georgia, Turkey, Armenia which do not require visas for Russians, for September 21st were unavailable within minutes of Putin's announcement, according to Russia's top planning websites, essentially getting men those ages into the military to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Juan Williams is a FOX News analyst, FOX News media analyst and host of FOX's MEDIA BUZZ, Howard Kurtz, and Mollie Hemingway, editor in chief at "The Federalist." Mollie, we have been hearing about how Ukraine is making -- having success, all the weapons and money going in from the U.S. You hear General Kellogg. That is not where the American people are as far as U.S. getting involved in the war. Where do you see it tonight with these new threats and where Putin is?\par \par \uc2 MOLLIE HEMINGWAY, EDITOR IN CHIEF, "THE FEDERALIST": We are in a very serious situation. The speech that Biden gave at the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . today reminds me that at the beginning of this conflict Biden said he was going to unite the world against Russia. He has completely failed to do that outside of western Europe. Obviously, China and Russia are fairly well allied. You've got countries in Africa and the Middle East. And then even the countries in Europe are not really doing that much. And polling does show that Americans do not want us to be doing so much more than these wealthy European countries.\par \par \uc2 But what Putin said is that he is mobilizing forces. That's an escalation. And he reminded people of his ace in the hole, that he has 6,500 nuclear warheads. So we need to be taking this very soberly, very seriously, and thinking about ways to do escalate the situation, unless we want that to happen.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here is national security council coordinator John Kirby.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS: He doesn't have a whole lot of sympathetic ears in Russia or elsewhere around the world, that's clear. And I think it's also increasingly clear to Mr. Putin that he's not doing well in Ukraine. Why else would you call up a mobilization now of 300,000 and some odd troops?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Howie?\par \par \uc2 HOWARD KURTZ, FOX NEWS HOST: President Biden gave a strongly delivered speech, I'll have to say that. But increasingly, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  looks desperate. His military has failed, is retreating. Biden had to respond to the nuclear threats, to the latest evidence of atrocities, and also to this annexation plan.\par \par \uc2 But what really has changed as a result of this speech? Russia is a member of the Security Council, can veto any enforcement action. And the crippling sanctions haven't crippled Moscow. And not to jump ahead, but in a media footnote, reaction to the president's \uc2 U.N\uc2 . speech was completely obliterated on MSNBC, to a lesser extent on CNN, when seconds later New York's attorney general announced a civil suit against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his company.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, we'll talk about that in a second. Juan, your thoughts. I want to get your thoughts about the speech, but also the president didn't talk about some things, didn't really talk about Taiwan and China, didn't talk about Iran and the protests that are ongoing. He's put out some statements and that sort of thing. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: I think he gave a strong, steadyhanded speech with regard to the success of U.S. policy and the success in holding the western allies, to Mollie's point about there are other countries that really are more towards the Russian side of this. But the important thing for the moment with winter approaching and reliance on Russian gas and oil being such a critical issue for the western allies is that they remain solid with U.S. policy, and Putin is very clearly losing.\par \par \uc2 The reason strong and steady is so important here is because of the nuclear threat. I don't think we saw any half-stepping, backstepping, skittishness from the president of the United States. To the contrary, I think he was making it very clear the United States remains committed to supporting Ukraine as it's being invaded by its neighbor. And when you think about the fact that Putin is talking about 300,000, not a general call -- this is really the armed force reserve now being called up -- I think it's a signal of Putin saying I'm losing, I don't know how I'm going to lose. I don't want to lose. But I've got do something right now.\par \par \uc2 The estimates are that more than 15,000 Russian soldiers have been killed, much more have been wounded. I don't think there is any question he would not be taking these steps that we are seeing if he was not aware that he is losing.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mollie?\par \par \uc2 HEMINGWAY: It's true that there have been these setbacks. And people keep saying this is evidence that he is losing, or this is proof that he is losing. But these actions that he is taking, mobilizing troops, instituting a draft, reminding people of nuclear weapons is also evidence that he is unwilling to lose. And so we need to think very seriously. Again, is this where we want to be putting our resources? We have an economic crisis right now. We have energy situations that are going to be very bad and they're going to get very bad in Europe. We do not have unlimited money, and the American people have already been saying they are not entirely sure that we should be the only ones really investing so heavily here.\par \par \uc2 We have other issues in China. We have other -- there are many other important parts of the world. And so we don't need this kind of schoolyard posturing that we see from people, like senators or senators who become presidents. We need people who are very serious and understand that we do not want a nuclear conflict here. We need to end this as quickly as possible and as favorably as possible to the Ukrainian people.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, let's turn to that story you just mentioned. Here is the New York attorney general and the former U.S. attorney general reacting to it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LETITIA JAMES, (D) NEW YORK ATTORNEY GENERAL: Statements of financial condition were greatly exaggerated, grossly inflated, objectively false, therefore, fraudulent. And as a result of that, we are seeking relief, and Mr. Trump, the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , his family, they should all be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BARR\uc2 , FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: It's hard for me not to conclude it's a political hit job. This is a woman who campaigned for office saying -- promising she was going to go after Trump. What persuades me that this is overreach is that she went after the children. She just says they conspired. She has no evidence that they played a role in this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This is a civil case. It's about 300 pages long, Howie. It goes deep into the Trump business. Thoughts?\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: The reason that Letitia James is bringing a civil suit is that the Manhattan D.A. looked at much of the same evidence and decided not to bring criminal charges. So she gets up there as a Democratic attorney general who did, in fact, campaign that she would go after Trump, and she is accusing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his adult children and the company of all kinds of crimes that she can't prosecute.\par \par \uc2 Now, maybe she has the goods. But as she went through the details of the evaluations and appraisals, it's incredibly complicated stuff. Every real estate company tries within legal bounds to make its balance sheet look better. Trump side is going to come back with own experts and they're going to say, no, this is all perfectly reasonable. This will be fought out in the courts for a long time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And quickly, Juan, the people who are hurt are the banks that he took --\par \par \uc2 KURTZ: Big banks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The big banks?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No. In fact, that's his defense, that the banks made money.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right. But I'm saying there are criminal referrals to the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . There are criminal referrals that she will make, but is it there?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: You know what? She is on the line. It's not a one-way street. If she loses, she looks like she has got egg on her face.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We are going to follow it. Panel, thanks, as always.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a special day. Ten months after running away, Conway the pup was reunited with his family. Ten months, that's a long time. Florida deputies found this dog about 30 miles from his home. The family says they were overjoyed, overwhelmed with love to have him back. Oh, that's awesome.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, the inspector general report on the military's coronavirus vaccine policy.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for the SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. Here's Jesse. Hi, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220922ei9l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Migration Source Evolves During Biden Presidency; DeSantis Continues War Of Words With Biden White House; New Poll Gives Republicans Confidence Boost; Criminal Probe Halted Until Special Master Finishes; Parts Of Puerto Rico Without Water Or Electricity; Some Democrats Dispute \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  's Remarks On Pandemic Amid Funding Battle; Representatives \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  (D-CA) And Nancy Mace (R-SC) Interviewed On Their Bipartisan Efforts On Cybersecurity; Protests Sparked In Iran By Death Of 22-Year-Old Woman While In Custody Of Iran's Morality Police; Marxist Background Of Black Lives Matter Organizers Examined; New Polling Shows Close Senate Races In Upcoming Midterms; Georgia Democratic Gubernatorial Candidate \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  Attempts To Answer Criticism That She Denied Losing Previously Gubernatorial Election\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Peter Doocy, Mark Meredith, David Spunt, Chad Pergram,  Nicole Valdes, Greg Palkot \par \uc2 8,010 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: And he has a brother named Max.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Who's going to be big, Dana. Who gives a Shih Tzu?\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Oh.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Oh, Greg.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: No, really, who gives one? Was he a gift? I don't know.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, I know, I mean.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: They're wonderful dogs. All right.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Did she get it as a gift?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes, she did get it as a gift.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right, we've had enough of your --\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: She got it as a gift. So, there you go, people with dirty minds.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: That is all for us.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: SPECIAL REPORT is up next with Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): We could just listen to more Greg and Shih Tzus.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Please.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Thanks, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier. "BREAKING TONIGHT", it could be, or could it a preview of the 2024 presidential election? Well, some people think it is.\par \par \uc2 Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  -President Biden squaring off over immigration. Democrats are incensed that Republican governors including DeSantis for sending illegal immigrants to northern cities, now a class action lawsuit on behalf of the migrants flown to Martha's Vineyard has been filed.\par \par \uc2 Governor DeSantis and his GOP brethren blame the president for taking immigration situation somewhat under control when he arrived and blowing it up in their words.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Peter Doocy has details tonight from the North Lawn. Good evening, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret. The White House says they are aware that one of those planes carrying migrants may soon touchdown at an airport near President Biden's beach house in Delaware.\par \par \uc2 And in the meantime, President Biden is telling us that the reason for this influx of migrants at the border is changing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: With fewer immigrants coming from Central America, and from Mexico. This is a totally different circumstance.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY (voice-over): Totally different because Central America and Mexico are not in the so-called Northern Triangle, the vice president has been focusing on.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The president asked me to take on a role of leadership in addressing the root causes of migration. And to focus on the Northern Triangle countries.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That would be El Salvador, Honduras, and Guatemala. Now it's --\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: What's on my watch now is Venezuela, Cuba, and Nicaragua.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: 12 people arrested at the border in August are on the \uc2 FBI\uc2  's terrorist screening database. Among 78, so far, this fiscal year, tripled the last five fiscal years combined, which demonstrates that warnings have been ignored.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I can say quite clearly, don't come --\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Do not come. Do not come.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: They have been coming in record numbers. And Republican governors, including Florida's \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  , believe, flying migrants to Blue districts inland is making a symbolic impact.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): It's opening people's eyes to the solution, which is let's have a secure border. Let's have Remain in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The White House claims, they are willing to talk to DeSantis and others.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We are always happy to have conversations about ways to further improve border processing.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: So, they're happy to have conversations, but they haven't picked up the phone yet. And it doesn't sound like that is coming soon.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: I mean, there is no understanding to be reached.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: DeSantis argues the larger problem here is Biden's immigration policy.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: They'll say like, oh, you know, sending a bus from Texas is a stunt.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Using them as a political stunt.\par \par \uc2 DESANTIS: The biggest stunt was Biden coming in as president and reversing Trump's policies just so he could virtue signal that he was against Trump.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Mr. President --\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The president himself laughing off questions about migrants landing in Delaware and offering DeSantis an invitation.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: He should come visit. We have a beautiful shoreline.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: President Biden is now in New York City ahead of the \uc2 United Nations General Assembly\uc2  . And according to a briefing that we got from the National Security Adviser Jake Sullivan, when talk turns to foreign policy, the president plans to focus more on stuff happening overseas as opposed to at the southern border. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Peter Doocy, live on the North Lawn. Peter, thanks.\par \par \uc2 In our SPECIAL REPORT "MIDTERM WATCH", we are 49 days. Seven weeks from Election Day. In our just released Fox News Power Rankings, Democrats are building some momentum heading into November.\par \par \uc2 For the first time this cycle, these rankings give Democrats at least pathways to retain control of Congress, though, Republicans still have an advantage. And this is why. Inflation remains the number one issue in every major poll.\par \par \uc2 The latest Fox News national survey shows 59 percent of voters extremely concerned about inflation. If you dig deeper, it also shows that issue favors Republicans by a wide margin.\par \par \uc2 The Republican Party leaves by 12 points with people voters who are extremely concerned about inflation. Voters prioritizing abortion prefer the Democratic Party by 29 points.\par \par \uc2 Now, inflation may be moving a race in Georgia. This is the Senate race down there.\par \par \uc2 Recent Atlanta Journal-Constitution poll of likely voters shows incumbent senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  at 44 percent. Republican challenger, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  stands at roughly 46 percent.\par \par \uc2 This has changed in recent days. There's been a surge for the Walker campaign. Republicans looking for more good news. We'll find it in the governor's races with gains in Oregon and Georgia.\par \par \uc2 This is the Fox News Power Rankings. Oregon now has gone to a tossup. Georgia now lean Republican. You can see these are likely solid in D and R.\par \par \uc2 Georgia incumbent \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  has eked out a lead over \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  , the latest RealClearPolitics Average of polls there, has Kemp leading Abrams by more than six points.\par \par \uc2 He's just north of the majority vote, he would need to win a second term without a runoff in Georgia, as we know that's the rule there.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Mark Meredith takes a deeper look at our newly released Fox News Power Rankings in what could be an incredibly close battle 49 days from now for control of the House and Senate.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening to you. Well, as you mentioned, with less than two months until Election Day, the data shows Democrats have a reason to be more optimistic than they did at the beginning of the summer, but several races are still considered true toss ups, and has got both party scrambling to reach undecided voters between now and November.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RONNA MCDANIEL\uc2  , CHAIRWOMAN, REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE: We know this is crime in the economy.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (voice-over): \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  , says by focusing on those two issues, Republicans can claim victory in November.\par \par \uc2 New Fox News Power Rankings show the strategy may work. Fox predicts Republicans will take control of the House for the first time since 2019. But their predicted margin for victory is slipping, down three seats from last month.\par \par \uc2 Estimates show the GOP will win 231 House seats, 13 more than needed to gain control. But Democrats do have a path for victory. With at least 30 House races spread across the country labeled toss ups. Today Democrats double down on their midterm message.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): Democracy is on the ballot. Reproductive freedom is on the ballot. Gun Safety is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Democrats are spending heavily to keep abortion front and center with roughly one in three ads focused on reproductive rights, according to the Associated Press.\par \par \uc2 The battle over abortion also impacting the Senate, where Fox predicts control remains a tossup.\par \par \uc2 Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  , who is not up for reelection this year, says the GOP should embrace the issue.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): This is not a states' rights issue, this is a human right issue.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Fox labels four Senate contests as tossups: Arizona, Georgia, Nevada, and Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 In the Keystone State, Dr. Mehmet Oz is campaigning in traditionally left- leaning Philadelphia, as polls show a tight race between him and Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE, PENNSYLVANIA: We do have the opportunity today to address what's going on a Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: While the Senate race is a tossup, Fox reports the Pennsylvania governor's race now leans in Democrats' favor. Overall, there are 36 gubernatorial elections this cycle, and seven races are considered toss- ups, including Arizona, Kansas, Maine, New Mexico, Nevada, Wisconsin, and Oregon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): Now, political pundits believe Oregon is worth watching because voters there have three candidates on the gubernatorial ballot to choose from. So, some people believe the liberal voters may split between the democratic and independent candidates that may create a pathway for the GOP candidate.\par \par \uc2 Republican has not been in the Oregon governor's mansion, Bret, since 1987. More than 30 years ago. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, Mark. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We touched on Georgia races tonight. One down as part of our midterm watch. We will update you on at least one spotlight race in each state over the next seven weeks, before the midterm election.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Stocks fell to two-month lows today in a cyclical lead sell-off. The Dow was off 313. S&P 500 lost 44. The NASDAQ fell, 110.\par \par \uc2 A stunning signal from former President Trump's legal team tonight on the possible Jeopardy he faces. It came as the newly appointed special master in the Mar-a-Lago raid case met with both sides today in Brooklyn.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent David Spunt is in Brooklyn tonight with the latest. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening to you. The former president's legal team indicated the possibility that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could be indicted.\par \par \uc2 The hearing today was quick, about 45 minutes, but contentious at times. This happened in Brooklyn, New York, behind me.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (voice-over): Judge Raymond Dearie, the special master asked Trump's attorneys for proof that Trump did indeed declassify documents seized by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  . Trump's legal team took issue with the request, arguing they didn't want to give too many details over fears of exposing Trump criminally.\par \par \uc2 Dearie said, "You can't have your cake and eat it too."\par \par \uc2 Dearie will serve as a neutral third-party arbiter, who along with a team will sift through some 11,000 documents and separate any of that may be covered under attorney-client, and or executive privilege.\par \par \uc2 Federal Prosecutor -- federal prosecutors -- excuse me, say around 100 of those documents are classified. Trump's attorneys wrote to Dearie in a filing last night, and use the words, "subsequent indictment". Meaning, they are prepared for a possible indictment.\par \par \uc2 As the Dearie examines the records, Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  and the Department of Justice are appealing to the 11th Circuit Court of Appeals in Atlanta over the need for a special master in general.\par \par \uc2 This could short Circuit the whole special master review. The reason, Bret, Judge Cannon -- Aileen Cannon, pictured here, told federal prosecutors late last week, DOJ must stop looking through the classified documents until Dearie is finished with his special master work.\par \par \uc2 DOJ argues the time is ticking over potentially ongoing national security concerns.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): The bench comments from Judge Dearie today may give some comfort legally to the Department of Justice. Remember, it was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  's legal team who suggested Judge Raymond Dearie, in the first place, to serve as special master.\par \par \uc2 Dearie has until November 30th to complete his review, although, that could be extended if warranted. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt in Brooklyn. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A Georgia court filing alleges of fake 2020 elector misrepresented her role in an alleged voting equipment breach and allowed a forensic team on authorized access to machines.\par \par \uc2 The Secretary of State's office says former Republican Party official Cathy Latham allowed the group to copy virtually every component of the voting system. The Coffee County elections office released security footage of Latham meeting with the investigators.\par \par \uc2 Her lawyers say she has not knowingly been involved in impropriety in any election.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the devastation in Puerto Rico, left behind by Hurricane Fiona. We'll take you there live.\par \par \uc2 And later, our "COMMON GROUND" segment, featuring representatives Nancy Mace and \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  tonight.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our Fox affiliates around the country are covering tonight. Fox 32 in Chicago, where, at least, eight people are injured after an explosion at an apartment.\par \par \uc2 Firefighters are still searching for anyone who may be trapped inside that structure. Officials are working to determine the cause of that blasts.\par \par \uc2 Fox 61 in Hartford, Connecticut, as Internet host Alex Jones appears at the courthouse for his trial to determine how much he must pay the families of Sandy Hook victims.\par \par \uc2 Jones was found guilty of libel for calling the massacre a hoax. He could be called to testify about the money at some point this week.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at San Antonio, Texas. One of the big stories there tonight from Fox 29.\par \par \uc2 A $19 million lottery ticket is sold in Montgomery County. Lottery officials say the cash value option was selected.\par \par \uc2 The winner will receive almost $12 million before taxes. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 That's a nice live look "OUTSIDE THE BELTWAY" from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hurricane Fiona turned its fury to the Turks and Caicos Islands today, hitting as a Category 3 storm.\par \par \uc2 Fiona has already devastated Puerto Rico, where most people remain without electricity or running water tonight.\par \par \uc2 Fox weather correspondent Nicole Valdes is in Puerto Rico live tonight with the latest. Good evening, Nicole.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE VALDES, FOX WEATHER CORRESPONDENT: Good evening Bret. You will notice the sole power truck parked behind me as rain continues to fall across portions of Puerto Rico, 80 percent of the island is still in the dark nearly 800,000 remain without running water and the heartbreak is evident. Truly as Puerto Ricans deal with the devastation of Fiona on the anniversary of one of the most catastrophic storms to ever hit the island.\par \par \uc2 Hurricane Maria made landfall here five years ago to the day, it is blamed for hundreds of deaths and the island wide blackout that lasted for months here now have many questioning how long the current blackouts will last.\par \par \uc2 And unfortunately, how many people may suffer in its wake.\par \par \uc2 Today, we traveled to some of the more rural communities including the small town Cayey, it's home to about 43,000 people. The mayor there has been outspoken on how dire the need is for supplies and resources for his community that remains in tragic disarray today.\par \par \uc2 This is a video of their makeshift command center where they're looking to try to get resources to homes. They've set up actually inside a local baseball stadium. He took us up into the mountains to demonstrate the challenges they continue to face, including dealing with some roads that remain impassable.\par \par \uc2 We came across this one with feet of mud and an entire tree preventing volunteers and emergency responders from getting to more than 15 people, seven or eight families unable to leave their homes including one pregnant woman who is struggling to ration the little water and food they have left. Of course, their neighbors struggling to wrap their heads around the impact, a Category 1 storm was capable of.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I still can't believe we're talking about generators not working in hospitals. For me, it's unbelievable. I have no words. It's unacceptable. And I think we should -- we need to do something as soon as possible. We need to get together and we need to come up with a plan. So things like this, you know, don't continue happening.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VALDES: And Bret, as our team drove town to town today, we noticed families pulling over on the side of the freeway to tap into a water pipe for drinking water.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, it's just one of the many tragic examples of the impact Fiona has left on so many on the island of Puerto Rico, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Unreal. Nicole, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Democrats are taking issue with President Biden of whether the coronavirus pandemic is really over, that's coming up.\par \par \uc2 Plus, our Common Ground segment featuring Representatives Nancy Mace and \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 First, beyond our borders tonight. Russia blames Ukraine for shelling a thermal power plant near a nuclear facility. Moscow's defense ministry says 24 shells were fired in a 24 hour period.\par \par \uc2 It says Russian forces returned fire and that radiation levels at the site remain normal.\par \par \uc2 Australian wildlife authorities investigate the deaths of 14 young sperm whales. The animals were found beached on an island off the southeastern coast of Australia. Swimmers and surfers are being warned to avoid that area in case the corpses attract sharks.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Jerusalem. One of the big stories there tonight, workers remove handwritten notes from the city's western wall ahead of the Jewish New Year.\par \par \uc2 Visitors often place the prayer letters in the cracks of the holy site. The removal takes place ahead of Rosh HaShanah, which will be celebrated Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories beyond our borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Federal authorities have charged 47 people in Minnesota in connection with a scheme to take advantage of COVID-19 rule changes and steal $250 million from a federal program. Prosecutors say the defendants created companies that claim to offer food to low income children.\par \par \uc2 During the pandemic, the \uc2 USDA\uc2  has allowed four profit restaurants to participate in reimbursement programs. The lawsuit alleged few meals were actually served and the defendants used the money to buy luxury cars, property and jewelry.\par \par \uc2 New York City is ending its vaccine mandate for private sector employers started November 1st, city employees are still required to be vaccinated there.\par \par \uc2 Mayor Eric Adams faced criticism over that mandate, some workers who refused shots were fired while professional athletes and entertainers had special exemptions from those rules.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Democrats are pushing back on President Biden's contention that the coronavirus pandemic is over. It is a bid to add billions in special COVID related funding to the massive spending many people believe, at least on the right, is fueling runaway inflation.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Correspondent Chad Pergram reports tonight from Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Yogi Berra famously said it's not over, 'til it's over. Congressional Democrats say the pandemic isn't over regardless of what the president said.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAKE AUCHINCLOSS (D-MA): He did not say COVID is extinguished. COVID- 19 is going to be endemic for years to come.\par \par \uc2 REP. STENY HOYER (D-MD): The good news is mortality rates are very low. And I think that's really what the president meant.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Republicans won't characterize the pandemic status.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Republicans think that there is a pandemic?\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): Well, I think the American people have decided to get back to normal. That does not necessarily mean that COVID has disappeared.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Democrats aim to pack $21 billion for COVID into a bill to avoid a government shutdown next week. But the president's statement could complicate that.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY (R-LA): This is kind of mind boggling. He wants tens of billions for COVID. And he says the pandemic is over. Democrats weren't banking on Republicans to help with COVID anyway.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): The Republicans opposed it even before the president made his comments.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Schumer won't budge from a pact he made with Joe Manchin to expedite energy permitting and the upcoming Band-Aid spending bill called a C.R.\par \par \uc2 Is there any circumstances whatsoever that that could be taken out of the C.R.\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: I told you.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: But you said that for two weeks. I'm just getting --\par \par \uc2 SCHUMER: I'm saying I will say it for two weeks in one day. Next.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Republicans could vote no because the deal secured Manchin's support for the climate tax and health package. Progressives oppose it because it cuts against their position on climate change.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): I've never seen a stranger bedfellows than \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and the extreme liberal left siding up with Republican leadership just like the revenge politics.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): Manchin releases details of his deal tomorrow. Government funding expires October 1st. Democrats need GOP help to overcome a filibuster on any bill to avert a shutdown, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad Pergram live on the Hill. Chad, thanks.\par \par \uc2 This week on our Common Ground segment, South Carolina Republican Congresswoman Nancy Mace and California Democratic Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  . Thank you both for being here.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We are coming up to an election, and obviously a lot of times that deals with different sides pushing back and forth. You all have worked together a couple of times on bills dealing with cyber, and you all have both talked about the ability for bipartisanship to work up there. First to you.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2  , (D-CA): Well, we have to work together to get things done. Representative Mace and I worked together on cybersecurity. We realized that we are vulnerable to threats from China, to threats from Iran, especially with quantum computing and on cybersecurity. And so we've passed two bills together. And there are other things we can do together to bring manufacturing back, to help our economy grow.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 MACE: It's amazing what you can do when you get off social media and start talking and communicating with one another, because there is common ground. There are places where we can come together. As Ro was saying earlier, cybersecurity being one of those places. And I am a freshman. I am a conservative. But I work with Democrats, a Reagan Republican, so to speak, because it's important to deliver results for my state and my country. That's why we're here. That's our purpose.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: As you look at our FOX polls, extremely concerned about -- as you are heading to the election -- inflation, higher prices. You both have talked about that issue. You come from different possible solutions.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Right. And I actually -- I blame both sides for inflation. We have had deficit spending for years by Republicans and Democrats alike, ratcheted up, obviously, from COVID-19. At the end of the day, I think our goals are very the same, but how we get there is sometimes, the path to getting there is different. I have never voted to raise taxes. I don't want to support huge spending bills with massive deficit spending. I think that's very problematic and contributes to the inflation issues that we have right now for the long term. That's problematic.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you agree that people feel that inflation and might be the top issue?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Yes, absolutely. Here is one of the causes of it, though. We offshore too much of our production. We don't make enough things in America. We don't make masks. We don't make enough baby formula. We don't make semiconductors. We're shipping all these things to China and then paying shipping costs.\par \par \uc2 One thing we come together is what I say is a new economic patriotism, make things in this country, in the long term bring down inflation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You both have also talked about immigration and a broken border system. Congressman, you've talked about real concern about the leadership of that. Expand on that.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: It's obviously not working. No one can look at the system and say it's working.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You can say the border is not secure.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I can say it's not as secure as it should be, absolutely. We need to make sure not enough drugs are getting that not enough -- criminals are getting in. But also, the system isn't working partly because we don't have comprehensive immigration reform. President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  proposed a program. He said if you want to come here, work, you get a permit, and then you can go back. That would help. It would help the ranchers and farmers who want to hire folks on the border and it would make sure people aren't subverting the process. And we passed that in the House. I would hope something like that could get done.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So I covered the White House under \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  as he tried to get that done. And that word, "comprehensive," seems to be a real problem up on Capitol Hill for both sides to get that massive piece of legislation through. Is there hope that even around the edges there could be some immigration, something that happens on Capitol Hill?\par \par \uc2 MACE: I think so. And I actually think it's a simpler issue than that. If you look at the demand that we have for workers visas here, particularly during tourist season. I know in my state of South Carolina, we have a huge demand because we don't have enough workers for those. So not everyone that comes here wants to be a citizen. Some just want to work for a temporary amount of time and go back home, work and pay taxes, send that money back to their country. But going back to manufacturing here in the U.S., looking at getting manufacturing out of China, incentivizing companies to come back to the United States, our regulatory policies here make those things happen, make us go to China more than here or to the south of here.\par \par \uc2 If we took some of those supply chain issues from China, went down to Mexico or Central America, South America, that also stems the tide of illegal immigration. And when there is prosperity in other economies there is peace and there's less illegal immigration.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When we talk to Democrats and Republicans or people who come from different sides of issues, we often find that there's a lot of things that they could agree on but that because of the partisan bickering both sides go to their corners. Is that a fair statement?\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I think it's fair. Bret, look, Nancy and I don't agree on a lot of issues. But we don't question each other's patriotism. We don't question each other's character. We agree to disagree civilly. And we look for the Venn diagram, where there is intersection and where we can make progress. And I think if you just took that approach, we'd solve more issues for the American people.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What did you make, Congressman, of the president about the pandemic is over. You heard Chad's piece. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  " writes "Biden is right, the pandemic is over. That doesn't mean the coronavirus is no longer causing harm. It simply signals the end of emergency state as COVID has involved into endemic disease."\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: I think "The Post" is right. Look, the worst of it is over. We are returning more to normal life, but we have to realize that we still have a virus, and we need booster shots and still need vaccines. Here is what I have said, Bret. I give the previous administration credit partly for developing the vaccine. I give this president credit for distributing it. Why can't we just say that? Why don't we look for ways that we celebrate what this country is capable of doing?\par \par \uc2 We invented the vaccine. We have distributed it. We have done better than any other nation in the world. Why don't we just celebrate that as Americans?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: This might not be common ground because the president and his administration obviously used the COVID emergency to wipe out student loans in some corners just recently, a couple weeks ago, and now is asking for a lot of money up on Capitol Hill. Is that all going to come together?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Right. COVID-19, I believe, has been abused to expand government powers at the federal, state, and local level, and to just explain away the deficit spending that we have right now. You have blue collar workers that are paying for the college education, the Harvard Law degree and white collar education, and that's just wrong. And we can't afford it right now.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the amount of taxes that are going to have to go up to pay for some of this legislation, the deficit spending, again contributing to inflation, the cost of food just go to the grocery store is far beyond and exceeded beyond the inflation figures. Every time I go to fill up at the gas station, I'm paying for a very expensive pair of basketball shoes for one of my kids. And that's how expensive it is right now. And it's something we have to address, especially looking forward after the midterms, Republicans have to have a solution.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Quickly, what's the next thing you are going to work on?\par \par \uc2 MACE: Hopefully, it's more cybersecurity. We have so much, as Ro was saying earlier. We had 11 federal agencies that were hacked by agents of China and Russia just two years ago. So we are behind on securing both the federal government and private sector as well. We have got to get started on that.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: We need a 21st century national security strategy that recognizes A.I., quantum computing, and the threats we face, make sure America leads. And I look forward to working with Nancy on that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Listen, pass the word up there about this segment. We will have more people come down.\par \par \uc2 MACE: Thank you, Bret. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KHANNA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, Iranian security forces clash with protesters after a Kurdish woman dies in police custody.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Senate Intelligence Committee is calling for reforms to confront foreign intelligence threats. Lawmakers released a bipartisan report detailing efforts from adversaries to steal information from U.S. entities. We just heard about cybersecurity. Some targets included the financial sector, academics, and various government departments. The report says U.S. counterintelligence efforts are not ready to effectively defend against these threats.\par \par \uc2 Overseas now, a top United Nations official is demanding an independent investigation into the death of an Iranian woman held by the country's morality police. The incident has sparked violent demonstrations across the country. Senior foreign affairs correspondent Greg Palkot shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: Iran in turmoil, protests in Tehran and across the country. Several people killed, injured, arrested. Police opened fire on angry mobs. All triggered by the death of 22-year-old Mahsa Amini who died after being taken into custody by Iran's so-called morality police for not wearing a hijab, or head covering, in the proper way. Authorities claim she died of natural causes. Her family and others say she died from injuries after being beaten.\par \par \uc2 RAVINA SHAMDASANI, U.N. HUMAN RIGHTS OFFICE: Ms. Amini's tragic death and allegations of torture and ill-treatment must be promptly, impartially, and effectively investigated by an independent, competent authority.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: The U.S. weighed in as well.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN, NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISOR: It is a reflection of the complete repression and brutality of a regime that a woman simply seeking to exercise her basic rights could die in this way.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Iranian authorities say butt out.\par \par \uc2 NASSER KANAANI, IRANIAN FOREIGN MINISTRY (through translator): We strongly reject any intervention by U.S. officials in the Islamic Republic of Iran's domestic affairs.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Still, Iranian President \uc2 Ebrahim Raisi\uc2  , in New York to speak at the U.N ., is under fire. Authorities claim they will look further into the incident, but protests run deeper.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE (through translator): This behavior that causes the death of someone is not right.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: And this comes amid uncertainty about the health of 83-year-old Supreme Leader \uc2 Ayatollah Khomeini\uc2  as the U.S. considers renewing a pact with Tehran regarding its nuclear program.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL RUBIN\uc2  , AMERICAN ENTERPRISE INSTITUTE: Iran is a tinderbox, and police abuses like this, this spark of outrage, cause a wildfire.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): Expert Rubin says this is not the time to be giving Iran and its repressive Revolutionary Guard new funds from the easing of sanctions. Instead, Washington should be looking to ways to channel this new anger against the regime. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Greg, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Back here in the U.S., over the past decade \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  has emerged as both a popular slogan and an influential organization. But as you will see in my new series on FOX Nation, "The Unauthorized History of Socialism, Volume II," the BLM agenda goes far beyond traditional notions of civil rights and racial equality.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Three women came together in 2013 to form the \uc2 Black Lives Matter Global Network Foundation\uc2  , a nonprofit organization that by 2020 was raising more than $90 million a year. \uc2 Opal Tometi\uc2  , \uc2 Alicia Garza\uc2  , and Patrisse Cullors made no secret of their background.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The first thing I think is that we actually do have an ideological frame. We are trained Marxists.\par \par \uc2 MIKE GONZALEZ, AUTHOR, "BLM, THE MAKING OF A NEW MARXIST REVOLUTION": The people who founded the BLMGNF are Marxists who say they are Marxists.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Please join me in giving a very warm welcome to Melissa Harris-Perry, Patrisse Cullors, and Professor Angela Davis.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: First of all, Angela Davis is the most important intellectual mentor of the \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  founders. Angela Davis, for those who many people don't know, is a communist. She goes to universities, fills up auditoriums, gets up and says I'm a communist, I have always been a communist. And the students rise up and give her a standing ovation because they have no idea what communism means.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You can watch all five episodes of "The Unauthorized History of Socialism, Volume II" on FOX nation. You can also find the original series on socialism there as well.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the new FOX power rankings, the midterms, and President Biden's political future in 2024.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The Democrats get wiped out in the midterms, and only less than 40 percent of Democrats now select him as the potential nominee, then as a practical matter he won't be able to run.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES, (D-NY) HOUSE DEMOCRATIC CAUCUS CHAIR: If President Biden chooses to run for reelection, I look forward to supporting him. But our focus right now is on continuing to communicate with the American people what the stakes are in November.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I do he runs again. I'm going to start a campaign to make sure he runs again because I know we will win the presidency in 24.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Talking about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  , his comments, the president on "60 minutes" about whether he will run or not. Meantime the Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  expressing some confidence, closed door confidence. "Axios" says "McConnell has publicly downplayed expectations for winning the majority with a prediction in mid-August that the House is more like than the Senate to change hands. His private confidence reflects some newly found hope among Republicans that their political prospects have improved."\par \par \uc2 And that brings us to the FOX News power rankings in the House. First of all, you have a prediction that the Republicans gain the House. There you see 30 toss-up seats, need 218. Right now, it looks like there is going to be a pick-up. We don't know how many, but this is based on current polls and projections as we see them at FOX.\par \par \uc2 And then in the Senate, right now the forecast is a toss-up in the Senate but potentially that Republicans could pick up the seats needed to turn control.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, \uc2 Josh Kraushaar\uc2 , "Axios" senior political correspondent, Vince Coglianese, editor, director for "The Daily Caller," radio host as well, \uc2 Mara Liasson\uc2  , national political correspondent of National Public Radio.\par \par \uc2 OK, Mara, do you buy that kind of layout?\par \par \uc2 MARA LIASSON\uc2  , NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, NATIONAL PUBLIC RADIO: Yes. Look, I think the Senate is definitely a jump ball. I can see it being 50-50, I can see it going like your poll, 52-49, or the other way. I think there's just a handful of battleground races. The thing about the Senate is candidate quality, as \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has said, really matters. And then you've got all the fundamentals which are favoring Republicans this year, inflation being a top concern. The party in power usually loses seats.\par \par \uc2 But it's very, very close. Yes, I don't think that the House has changed in terms of Republicans being favored to take control, but I think the margin by which they may do so has struck.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Vince, I'm going to put up the power rankings in the Senate. And this is the breakdown of the toss-ups. We won't go into it right here, but you can get it online on the FOX News politics site. And basically you have the toss-ups in the middle in yellow and then the leans blue and red. And it will change, obviously, in the weeks leading up, the seven weeks leading up to Election Day. How do you see it today?\par \par \uc2 VINCE COGLIANESE, "THE DAILY CALLER": I think right now you are right, candidate matter, candidate quality matters. But I think what matters more here in the midterms, presidential quality. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  continues to poll at very low ratings. And people are going to see their votes across these states as proxy vote for how they feel about how the country is being handled right now.\par \par \uc2 And that is always the case, as Mara indicated. When you have very unpopular president, you are going into the midterms the party in power, it's a real struggle for Democrats. And when it comes to these candidates, look at a guy like \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  , not a great candidate. He's a guy who said this week in Georgia that he thinks he is going to lose his next debate. But still he polls ahead of \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  at this moment. That's a sign that people are very upset with the current state of affairs, and Republicans have a very good chance of winning this --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We mentioned Georgia earlier. How do you see that. Is that inflation driving that race?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: It's inflation. It's just economic malaise. And that's sort of the story of the midterms in a lot of these big Senate races, Bret, that the bases are energized. Democrats are energized on abortion. Republicans are energized on immigration, the economy. But it's the independents that are going to swing this midterm election, and they are much more economic focused than the bases of the individual parties.\par \par \uc2 Look, inflation isn't going away. People have a very negative mood about the future of the country economically speaking. Those are the voters that are going to make or break these races, and they're tilting to the Republicans in the home stretch.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Staying in Georgia, the governor's race there, \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  in the recent poll trails pretty significantly to incumbent governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  . She has been talking and questioned numerous times -- and we'll put up the poll -- she's been questioned numerous times about denying that she lost the election to Kemp, and she has been trying to walk that back in recent days. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS, (D) GEORGIA GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: This is not a speech of concession, because concession means to acknowledge an action is right, true, or proper. As a woman of conscience and faith I cannot concede that.\par \par \uc2 There was a deliberate and intentional disinvestment and, I think, destruction of the administration of elections in the state of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 It was not a free and fair election. We had thousands of Georgians who were purged from the polls wrongly.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You know there are some Republicans who are arguing that President Trump's efforts to undermine the election are no different than yours in 2018 where you did not concede the gubernatorial race.\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: Well, it's not simply different circumstances. It's apples and bowling balls.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I don't know, apples and bowling balls, as you look at the latest polls.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: The thing that's so interesting about Georgia is that for Warnock to hang on and Kemp to win, you have to have a lot of people to split their tickets, which is vanishing phenomenon.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There's some concern about the African American vote for Abrams.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes, yes. But, really, really interesting that in some polls Warnock is doing so much better than Abrams. She is helping him because of all of the efforts she has made over the years to register voters. But the governor there is popular, and generally people think governors have done a good job, and Kemp is one of them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Vince, what about those answers though? It reminds me, a little bit sometimes, of the White House press secretary. It's like those are not the drones you're looking for. Like, it just is something opposite of what we know.\par \par \uc2 COGLIANESE: She comes across at one of the leading conspiracy theorists active in American politics right now. In fact, what I found interesting about the FOX News power rankings in terms of Democrats' strengths is two issues -- abortion and voting rights. Abortion, obviously contentious and a real issue. Voting rights, this is contrivance that the left has come up with. It is a conspiracy theory that has dominated their conversation on the left. And the reason that's the case, and can I say that with certainty, is look at places like Georgia and Texas which established these voter integrity laws. Their turnouts have never been higher. No one has had a lack of access to the ballot. And in fact, if they did, Democrats would be trotting them out as stories of people who were denied access in places like Georgia. And, again, just not the case. And it's interesting that that is energizing Democratic voters because of people like \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Josh?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  has run a very poor campaign that has been more focused on the base. Her whole theory of the case is get out the base and that's enough to win an election in Georgia. But in these swing states and these states where independents are going to make up the difference, that is a message that's falling on deaf ears. And look, it's not just Georgia. It's Nevada, Pennsylvania, Wisconsin. Republicans have momentum in these swing states as independents start to make up their minds.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mara, how does that answer from President Biden about whether he is running or not to "60 Minutes" affect anything? \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  , "The Wrap" says \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is unequivocally running for president in 2024, the governor of California, if Biden doesn't.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: If Biden doesn't.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, it's a big if.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: That's a big if. Look, right now Biden has to say, he says it's his intent to run. It's too soon to officially announce it. He becomes a lame duck the minute he says he is not. There are a lot of Democrats who are getting ready to run only if he doesn't. Right now, I don't see the kind of primary challenge to a sitting president that generally kneecaps them and makes them unable to win a general election even if they get the nomination. I don't see that right now.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But do you think that's true?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Yes. I mean, look, I think Newsom is making a play post Biden.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes, post Biden.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: And look, if Biden doesn't run, there is another Californian that is front and center in Vice President Harris, and it's going to be tough to compete on the California --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But if he is going to run, this is President Biden, why not say it?\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: You don't want to be a lame duck. You don't want to open up the political lane so early in this cycle. I think age is also the elephant in the room. No matter what he wants to do, being almost 80 years old and running for reelection, this is a big political challenge.\par \par \uc2 COGLIANESE: He is not running. He is not running.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Vince is putting it down right now.\par \par \uc2 COGLIANESE: That -- we love psychoanalyzing the president. You saw that answer. That's a guy who is not running.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, guys, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a special day overseas. An amazing moment in Jordan, a four-month-old girl was found alive under the rubble of a collapsed building. This is after 26 hours. At least 14 other people were killed there. The child was discovered sleeping peacefully, again, after more than a day. Once lifted out she began crying, and obviously tears of relief from her rescuers. That is fantastic news and a special day.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, President Biden addresses the \uc2 U.N. General Assembly\uc2  . We'll bring you there.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts now. Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220921ei9k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 White House Reacts To Reports DeSantis Flying Migrants To Delaware; Biden Home State Preps For Possible Migrant Flight; Sen. \uc2 Mike Braun\uc2  (R-IN), Is Being Interviewed About Inflation Outpacing Wages In America; Power Rankings: GOP Keeps Advantage As Dems Gain; \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chair On State Of Key Races Seven Weeks Out; White House: Government Spending Not To Blame For Inflation; Americans Face Soaring Prices As Midterms Near; Fentanyl Seizures Surging At Southern Border; Border Crisis Fueling Flow Of Deadly Fentanyl; Ukraine Accuses Russia Of Nuclear Terrorism; Special Master Grills Trump Team On Classification\b0\par\par\uc2 Sandra Smith, John Roberts, Trace Gallagher, Peter Doocy, Joe  Concha, Juan Williams, Bill Hemmer, William La Jeunesse, Pete Hegseth, Jeff  Paul, Jack Keane, David Spunt \par \uc2 7,974 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS HOST: Seven weeks to go, John, and what do you know? We can tie it back to what's happening in the bottom right corner of your screen. It's still about the inflation. It's still about the economy for American voters.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS HOST: Yes. I mean, for a lot of people, that is the number one issue, no question about that.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: All right. We'll keep covering it. Thanks for joining us. I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'm John Roberts. THE STORY starts right now with Trace Gallagher in from Martha. Trace.\par \par \uc2 TRACE GALLAGHER, FOX NEWS HOST: John and Sandra, thank you. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Trace Gallagher in from Martha MacCallum. Breaking right now, the White House confirmed it is coordinating closely with Delaware State officials amid reports that the President's home state will become the latest to receive migrants from Texas on flights facilitated by GOP governors.\par \par \uc2 Now, this would follow a string of planes and buses transporting migrants out of Texas. New York says, an influx has brought the city to a "near breaking point." In Washington, D.C., the mayor declared a public emergency. In Massachusetts, a military base now converted to migrant housing. Mercedes Schlapp, Joe Concha, and Juan Williams are standing by, but first to White House Correspondent Peter Doocy. Peter, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Good afternoon, Trace, in the press room, they talk about these flights in very serious terms. We heard from the President himself, he was kind of trying to laugh off the idea of saying that if DeSantis is going to send a flight to his home state of Delaware, then DeSantis should tag along.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: The migrants to Delaware, do you have any comment or respond to that, Sir?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: He should come visit, we have beautiful shoreline.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Just before that in the briefing room, the White House claimed they were willing to engage leaders in Texas and Florida, who had been busing migrants, points north to make a point about the burdens an influx of migrants can have on small towns.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: It is a cool pre-meditated political stunt. That is not what they're, that is what they are doing. And so, we've always, we're always happy to have conversations about ways to further improve border processing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: But even though she says they're happy to have conversation, conversations, she admitted, nobody at the White House has picked up the phone.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIERRE: I mean, there's no understanding to be reached.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: That's that. As for Governor DeSantis, he won't confirm that a flight is in the air heading for Biden's home state, but he is confirming that he thinks his plan to put migrants on these planes is working.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): It's not just flights, you know, we have ground, we have other things that we can do. And I'll tell you this, it's already made more of an impact than anyone thought it could possibly make. But we're going to continue to make more of an impact.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And President Biden also said today that the reason this is happening is because of an influx in migrants for the first time, he says, from Venezuela, Cuba, and Nicaragua. And that is very important because none of those three countries are part of the so-called Northern Triangle. The Northern Triangle is what the President told the Vice President she needed to address most urgently in addressing the root causes of migration. So, that's a big change. Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: It was all about root causes. Peter Doocy live for us in the White House. Peter, thank you. Let's bring in Mercedes Schlapp, Former Director of Strategic Communications under President Trump and CPAC Foundation Senior Fellow; Joe Concha, Media and Politics Columnist for The Hill and a Fox News Contributor; and Juan Williams, a Fox News Political Analyst. Thank you to you all.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it's interesting, because now you see \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , talking about there, Mercedes, he's making jokes saying, oh, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  should really come and see the shoreline in Delaware. But it's fascinating, because the President has said that called these drop offs "simply wrong," right? And you just heard a \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  say that this is a political stunt. The President, they have been doing this, they had been flying migrants around this country and dropping them off for more than a year, Mercedes.\par \par \uc2 MERCEDES SCHLAPP, FORMER DIRECTOR OF STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS UNDER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Yes, I mean, this is no joking matter in any way. And look, the Biden administration has created a humanitarian crisis, it has created a national security crisis at the border. And what you do when you lead in the White House, and I can tell you, when I worked in the White House, is you bring in the different parties, you bring in the different leaders, the governors, the local officials, to tackle these very serious issues, like the continual flow of illegal migrants coming through the border.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we saw this video of one boy that literally almost drowned in the river, crossing the border. This has to stop and we need to stop becoming a magnet in this country. And so, for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and Greg Abbott to stand strong and send a message to these sanctuary cities are, is absolutely critical because the burden has fallen on these border states. And, and in essence, there needs to be responsibility and some burden taken on by these liberal sanctuary cities that are run by Democrats\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, just to kind of add to Mercedes was saying there, Juan, because you have the Texas now in Bear County, in Bear County, Texas, the sheriff in Bear County, Texas now, you know, starting this investigation into \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Bear County, by the way includes San Antonio, you know, they forget that it wasn't too long ago back in June where 53 migrants were found dead in the back of a big rig in Bear County, Juan.\par \par \uc2 And now, he's going after DeSantis saying, oh, this is this is inhumane? No, what's inhumane is letting these people make that trek from the southern border of Mexico, all the way up. That is inhumane. And John, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  just saying there's been no conversation with Republicans. They say, it's a problem, no conversation, your thoughts, Juan?\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I mean, clearly, we need to have conversation. And you know, this is an issue where Republicans and Democrats have come close to some kind of congressional solution in 06, and 13, and 18 -- every time the Republicans pull up because they prefer really weaponizing immigration as an issue. And in this case, DeSantis is really using human beings, as a, you know, political theater, and it's really sad. He's misled them as to where they're going. These are people who are not even from Florida.\par \par \uc2 That's why you have the Bear County, Texas Sheriff looking into DeSantis's actions, and he clearly wants this to be a midterm issue. He sees this as benefiting himself as a future, possibly future presidential candidate but also benefiting Republicans in the midterms. The reality is that people who are using this way, it's a huge insult to all of us as Americans and to these people who are fleeing violence, oppression, sometimes incredible poverty and have every right to apply for asylum under American Law. That's the reality and yet he's making a joke.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: These bus trips, Juan -- these bus trips are voluntary. We have people down there, the people who get on these things are asked they are not told to get on these planes and buses, they are asked --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: They are misled, Trace. They have been misled as to where they're going.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: They have not been misled, Juan, they're being told where they're going. And they're saying they were told when they got there --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: That's not true.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: That there were going to be, there were going to be jobs available to them. Now, that's misleading, that's, that's a fair point, but what I'm saying is the administration --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, he's from Florida and he's sending people from Texas, this is not even his state.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And the President has been sending people all over the country for more than a year in these planes and dropping them off in the dark of night in Westchester County. And these people, it's the same thing. It's OK for the President to drop around.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: It's not the same thing.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: It is the same thing, Juan. It is the very same thing --\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Not if you are sending people -- first of all, you got to realize, Trace, that no one in Massachusetts was told for example, in the latest drop off, so there was no reception.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Nobody was told in Westchester County that people --\par \par \uc2 SCHLAPP: No one in Texas.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Or Texas.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: To help these people. And what you get when the President and the administration do this is they are trying to set up a situation where these people can be welcomed, they can be taken care of, they can be offered work potentially, that, and processed for asylum.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: But they're not. They have been dropped off in Westchester County many nights. We have reported on this. And it's unclear where they have gone, but they have gone to temporary places, and then they have left. And most of them will never be heard from again.\par \par \uc2 Joe Concha, your thoughts on this, this whole concept. And I just want to point this out is that you know, we -- the hypocrisy goes so deep. You have \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  saying this is morally reprehensible, the same \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  who put homeless people on buses a few years ago and gave them one-way tickets -- and he called the program Homeward Bound for people who didn't have a home. Joe, sorry.\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: And now, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is outraged over these flights to Martha's Vineyard. And look, the outrage over the safety and well-being of those illegally entering this country is profoundly misplaced. To Mercedes' point before, here are the numbers from DHS themselves: 750 people have died attempting to cross the U.S. southern border in the past year.\par \par \uc2 That's more than two per day according to DHS. And that's because of a border that's anything but secure, and because of a candidate and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , who invited them here and drop the remain in Mexico policy, and stop border wall, border wall construction, and because of a Governor Newsom who was giving them free health care.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, I want to know in terms of fentanyl, when the President is really going to start addressing this because fentanyl is manufactured in China. It's shipped over to Mexico, and it's bought across this border, and it kills more than 300 Americans on average per day. That's where the outrage should be. It's not a leading cause of death for those between the ages of 18 and 49 in this country. And when Scott Pelley by the way of "60 Minutes" had a chance to ask \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  why he has not been to the border as president and barely talks about border security, he failed to do so, that was the ultimate bias of omission. Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I started my career 35 years ago along the border I walked hundreds of miles with these migrants because the problem was very saying then, Juan, is this is an inhumane trick that they are incentivizing people to make and it's the same problem for 35 years. And Joe's right, they're dying in backs of trucks in the hot desert and the, the drug crisis is coming across and it just seems to be this kind of --\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: And drowning.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And drowning, exactly right. Mercedes, Joe, Juan, thank you all. Great conversation. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Thanks, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: You will have more on the record-setting border crisis under President Biden's watch with Pete Hegseth later this hour, and a mixed bag of good and bad news for the GOP in our latest Fox News Power Rankings. Bill Hemmer and \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chair \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 , kick off our democracy 2022 coverage, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: The Fox News Power Rankings out today exactly seven weeks from an election night that could change the country's political landscape. The big takeaway? The GOP has the advantage, but Democrats are building momentum. \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2  is standing. First, to "AMERICA'S NEWSROOM" co-host Bill Hemmer.\par \par \uc2 Looks like Democrats are gaining so little momentum, Bill.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BILL HEMMER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): Interesting --\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: But really would have to kind of run the table on the House to retain control.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Yes, you're exactly right. Good afternoon to you. Nice to see you, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Here is where we believe we are seven weeks out. Now, this is not science, but it's the best we think we can project right now before November 8, early next month in November.\par \par \uc2 We believe right now, Republicans are firming about 216 seats. But you mentioned the tossups, Trace, these are going to be critical. Critical for both sides in the House, and in the Senate. I'm going to show you why.\par \par \uc2 You got 30 tossups. Remember the simple majority to take control is 218. And look at this scenario right here. I'll just advance it one time for you.\par \par \uc2 Just pay attention to the left side of your screen.\par \par \uc2 We believe the worst case scenario for Democrats on November 8th, would be 189 seats in the House. The best case scenario, however, is look at this. 219.\par \par \uc2 Under that scenario, if they run the table, the 30 tossups, they would maintain control of the House, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  would still be Speaker. They'd have a two seat majority at 219.\par \par \uc2 For Republicans, we believe the worst case scenario as of today is 216. So, you're two seats shy of the majority.\par \par \uc2 If you run the table on the 30, the math is pretty simple, Trace, you're up at 246, and that would easily give potentially Kevin McCarthy, the potentially future Speaker of the House. A lot of levers and a lot of room to run on the House side.\par \par \uc2 Now, let's go over to the Senate to tell you where we are right now.\par \par \uc2 We believe right now, Republicans are solid at 49. We believe Democrats are solid at 47. Need a majority of 50 in the middle. But you've got these four tossup seats, where, Trace, I think you can make the argument seven weeks out that we may not know who controls the balance of power in the Senate on election night.\par \par \uc2 And I'll show you why here, OK? Let me come back to this in a moment here. But here is the best case worst case scenario. We think, as of now, Democrats could be down to 47, or Republicans could drop at 49.\par \par \uc2 Democrats could go up and only slightly we think the 51. Republicans could top off at 53. That would be a major -- that would be -- if you take the House and you take the Senate, you use words like shellacking that President \uc2 Obama\uc2  used 12 years ago in 2010.\par \par \uc2 But come back to this tossup barrier right here, OK? These are all this seats in the Democratic panel where you think a solid Democrat likely in lean. These the Republican columns solid likely in lean.\par \par \uc2 It's these four seats in the middle here, Trace. It's the Arizona. It's Pennsylvania. It's Nevada. It's Georgia, all these races right now we're too close to the call.\par \par \uc2 Why did we say we may not have an answer on election night? Is because these races in some say -- even Wisconsin, they are razor thin on the margins right now when it comes to the polling.\par \par \uc2 And in Georgia, you need 50 percent to clear the threshold to avoid a runoff. Remember that?\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: That's where we are seven weeks out, we'll see what we get real soon. Trace, back to you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Interesting that \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  in New Hampshire is now in that lean category for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Yes, you see that.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Very good.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: Right.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Great analysis, Bill. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 HEMMER: OK, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Appreciate it. Let's bring in \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 , chair of the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Ronna, it's always great to see you. I want to put up these numbers. Because I mean, we're kind of inundated with numbers here. But this is NBC polling.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: What is your preference for the outcome of November's congressional elections? You see, it's deadlock there.\par \par \uc2 Republican-Democrat control 46-46. And then, they asked which one of the following is more important in deciding your vote? A candidate's position on dealing with the cost of living, so inflation way up there at 59, and then abortion down to 37.\par \par \uc2 Those appear to be two of the major issues here. But yet the economy, the GOP, really, you know, really has a chance to gain some ground here because the economy is not doing well.\par \par \uc2 RONNA MCDANIEL\uc2 , CHAIRWOMAN, REPUBLICAN NATIONAL COMMITTEE: Absolutely. And that's something everybody wakes up every day thinking about. You think how am I going to pay for groceries? How am I going to get gas?\par \par \uc2 Every time you're going to the grocery store, you feel the price of inflation. Not everybody wakes up every day thinking about abortion. Voters are also worried about crime, they're worried about education, they're worried about the border.\par \par \uc2 So, these are the issues we know voters care about. Most Democrats want to make it about abortion, they're going to spend all their money to distract from the fact that they have the House, the Senate, and the White House.\par \par \uc2 They have done nothing to improve the lives of the American people. But there is one simple question, are you better off than you were two years ago when \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took office? And for most every single American, the answer is no.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: It's interesting though, Ronna, because you're right, in the House. It doesn't appear that abortion is going to be one of the major topics that will sway this thing. But in the Senate, it's different because you're talking about state by state. And in New Hampshire, we were talking with Bill earlier about \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  and Don Bolduc.\par \par \uc2 Well, that's where abortion could play a pretty pivotal role in the outcome of that election. Does that concern you at all?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: It's going to be different by state, like you're saying, Trace. Some states are going to have abortion ballot initiatives. So, that's going to determine the outcome.\par \par \uc2 But I'll tell you what, I think John Bolduc can win against \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 . We've got \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  at the top of the ticket. I was just there. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is pulling ahead in Georgia right now. Ron Johnson's ahead in Wisconsin, \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 's ahead in Nevada.\par \par \uc2 I'd rather be us than them. And I'm very optimistic about where the Republican Party is heading into these midterms.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes, it's interesting, because we talked about the NBC poll. There the man who did the poll is Bill McInturff of GOP pollster, he says the following. "This is a campaign about the economy, cost of living, crime, and border security, and Republicans are winning this campaign. But there is a second campaign on abortion, democracy, and climate change, and Democrats are winning that campaign."\par \par \uc2 And it's interesting because I pulled out of there. This is a campaign on abortion, democracy, and climate change. How is the Republican Party allowing the Democratic Party to paint them as being a threat to democracy over and over, and it appears to be working?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: I don't think it is working. I think Biden tried that with his death star speech. His doom and gloom trying to attack half of the country. And I think it backfired on him, actually. After he gave that speech, we started to see Independents and polls start shifting our way.\par \par \uc2 So, I think voters are absolutely focused on crime and the economy. Democrats are focused on distracting. They don't want the American people reminded that they hold the balance of power. That they are responsible for these policies that are making lives harder and more expensive.\par \par \uc2 And Republicans are winning on these issues, and we're going to keep hammering it home until Election Day.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: I got to go, Ronna. But I want to -- I want your take on -- is there, in your estimation -- in your party's estimation, is there a bellwether race that you're really looking at, that you think will kind of give us some kind of a -- of a -- of a -- the marker about how this is going to go?\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Not between now and Election Day. I mean, I think Pennsylvania is key. Fetterman is a pro-crime socialist. He embodies the progressive wing of the Democrat Party.\par \par \uc2 And obviously, Oz is running on kitchen table issues, crime, the economy, and that's going to be a battleground Senate seat, and it will be very critical for the presidential race of 2024 as well.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. \uc2 Ronna McDaniel\uc2 , always great to see you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MCDANIEL: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: While Republican Senator \uc2 Mike Braun\uc2  is back with us after slamming President Biden's inflation neck celebration. Remember that in the White House lawn? Insulting, he called it.\par \par \uc2 What the senator says about these new comments from the president, when THE STORY continues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY, CORRESPONDENT, \uc2 CBS\uc2  NEWS: You're not arguing that 8.3 is good news.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: No, I'm not saying it's good news. But it was 8.2 or -- 8.2 before. I mean, it's not your act -- we act -- and make it sound like all of a sudden, my God, it went to 8.2 percent. It's been --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PELLEY: It's the highest inflation rate, Mr. President, in 40 years.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I got that. But guess what we are. We're in a position where, for the last several months, it hasn't spiked. It has just barely -- it's been basically even.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [15:26:45]\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: We'll take a live look now. At the corner of Wall and Broad. The Dow down 326. Stocks kind of hitting the skids right out of the gate ahead of another possible interest rate hike from the Fed. It is trying to slow down inflation, which is now at a four-decade high.\par \par \uc2 A top White House economic adviser told Martha here on THE STORY that government spending is not to blame. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, WHITE HOUSE ECONOMIC ADVISER: So, one issue is, is the government stepping on the fiscal accelerator? That is, is government fiscal policy contributing to inflation say this year? And the answer based on over a trillion dollars of deficit reduction is no. That doesn't negate the point that inflation is too high, which it is, and we're doing everything we can to help.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Let's bring in Republican Senator \uc2 Mike Braun\uc2  of Indiana. He is a member of the Budget Committee. Senator, it's always great to have you on. I want to put this up, because it's worth putting up.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MIKE BRAUN\uc2  (R-IN): Good to be on.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: This is inflation outpacing wages. It is 5.2 percent wages, 8.3 percent inflation. Clearly, you know, even if your pay has gone up, you're still losing money in the long run.\par \par \uc2 And it's fascinating to me, again, we played it earlier in the show about President Biden saying. Well, it's not like inflation just spiked. It's just -- it's not comforting for the American people. Your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: Trace, I call that Bizarro World, because I've been here a little under four years, and the other side of the aisle thinks that can take any current piece of information. And just by saying it is not bad or something making you believe it.\par \par \uc2 We need to as Republicans make it easier to understand, first of all, pre- COVID with the Trump economy, no inflation. Best economy we had and I was part of it for decades. It was working.\par \par \uc2 Then, we got to look at what were the consequences the last time this happened. It took Paul Volcker, and in the early 80s, to tamp it down five years.\par \par \uc2 So, we can't let them get by with making it sound like well, the things they have done are actually helping. And in that Inflation Reduction Act, most of the deficit reduction on anything is phony. It occurs later on, not immediately.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: The spending is out of the gate. They are not going to get by with it. We, as Republicans have to articulate it a little better, make it easier to understand, so that they don't.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. I remember, you know, I used to -- I used to go out in Palm Springs and Sonny Bono was the mayor out there. You know, and say, you know, what, when things are up, when things cost more, and the Dow is down, people are angry. And they're always angry at the people who are in charge, the leaders. And that's how you tell them.\par \par \uc2 What I was looking at all these numbers today. What struck me the most, Senator, was the fact that \uc2 CBS\uc2  is reporting that 46 percent of Americans who make between $80,000 and $100,000 a year are carrying a monthly credit card debt, paying those huge interest rates. That makes people stressed.\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: And it's going to be more stressful because the Fed, back in the 80s had to take interest rates up to 20 percent. And here, the most indebted part of our economy, along with consumers, again, would be our own federal government.\par \par \uc2 So, this nonsense of saying that they not creating inflation by the extra normal spending, not to mention, the half a trillion dollars-worth of loan forgiveness, if not more and its full effect for student loans, it's all inflationary. We need to keep it simple, point it out, don't relent right up until the midterms or they'll do like they often do with in on Madison Avenue.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. And I got to go but, you know, yesterday, Jared Bernstein, we played some of that conversation he had with Martha where he kept talking about the gas prices are down, $1.30, gas prices are down, doesn't want to talk about the inflation number being up. That's kind of the thing is if you say it enough, it's like saying the border is secure. If you say it enough, somebody's going to believe you. Your final thought senator.\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: Not going to work this time. And by the way, they did everything to trip up the oil and gas industry, took it up to $5, and then brag about coming down. Got to call him out on it.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. Senator \uc2 Mike Braun\uc2 , great to see you as always. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 BRAUN: You bet.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, Fox is live in an Arizona port of entry where agents are attempting to stop massive amounts of fentanyl from flooding the border, as migrant arrests reach an unprecedented new record. Pete Hegseth is standing by with more on that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): -- Protection personnel have already encountered more than 2 million illegal immigrants and counting justice fiscal year. This is a larger group of people than the entire populations of 14 states. And this record shattering 2 million apprehensions only counts the subset of people that were actually caught.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RON DESANTIS (R), FLORIDA GOVERNOR: When Biden is flying these people all over the fruited plain in the middle of the night, I didn't hear a peep out of those people. The only thing I hear them getting upset about is you have 50 that end up in Martha's Vineyard then they get really upset. But what it shows is if 50 was a burden on one of the richest places in our country, what about all these other communities that have been overrun with hundreds or thousands.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: There you have Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  defending his flights taking migrants from Texas to Massachusetts to keep them from ending up in his state, but he would not confirm another flight reportedly President Biden's Delaware home. As we mentioned the White House says it's heard the reports and is working with local officials to welcome the migrants. We, of course, are keeping an eye on the airport in Delaware and we'll keep you posted.\par \par \uc2 "Fox and Friends Weekend" co-host Pete Hegseth is standing, first to the national correspondent, the senior national correspondent William La Jeunesse. He's in Nogales, Arizona with a look at how the border crisis is giving way to a flood of fentanyl. William?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM LA JEUNESSE, FOX NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Trace, the port of entry here in Nogales is the busiest checkpoint on the Arizona- Mexico border, that's 370 miles. Now this is very narrow canyon but if you look at the drone shot, you'll going to be able to see that 24/7 line of cars, trucks and pedestrians waiting to get in, most are coming to work to shop or deliver cargo, but an unknown number are carrying drugs.\par \par \uc2 Since August, officers here have seized more than 4 million fentanyl pills. Double last year's total, including this bus we saw yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: Moments ago, officers at the port notice something irregular about the truck. It was pulled into secondary inspection. When they dropped the gas tank, look what they found. They had cut a hole open and drop these drugs floating in the gasoline.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So the officer had noticed a small gap between the bed and the truck. So, he sent it through these side scan radars, x-rays if you will. And like you can see right here, they see through the skin of vehicles for abnormalities. Well after taking the truck apart, they found a trapdoor hiding the fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUADALUPE RAMIREZ, CBP DIRECTOR OF FIELD OPS, TUCSON: A huge misconception that the narcotics are coming through the desert. The reality is that in Arizona, so far this year, we've intercepted over 21,000 pounds of narcotics along the border. 18,000 pounds of that is caught at the port of entry.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LA JEUNESSE: So, nationwide overall drug seizures are down slightly but not fentanyl. See that green spike? Actually 85% of all drugs entering US illegally are seized at ports of entry from Brownsville to San Diego. And they're not carried by migrants crossing the border. Now to handle that volume, cars, trucks and people, what they're doing -- the government's secret weapon really are dogs.\par \par \uc2 So this German shorthair we were with yesterday joins hundreds of other dogs employed by DHS. This one scanned hundreds of boxes from China, Mexico mangoes in a matter of minutes. The reward is several minutes with a toy. But the bottom line, Trace, is we are a world away here from places like New York or Chicago where they have that fentanyl epidemic. But a place like this is actually the front line of America's defense against drugs. Back to you.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Indeed. William La Jeunesse live for us in Nogales. William, thank you. Let's bring in "Fox and Friends Weekend" co-host Pete Hegseth, an Iraq and Afghanistan war veteran and former prison guard at Guantanamo Bay. Pete, great to see you as always.\par \par \uc2 I mean just William's report there, is just -- it's fascinating in the fact, Pete, that he says 4,000 fentanyl pills came across that border in Nogales just in August. The population by the way of Nogales is 20,000. And you're talking about 4,000 pills. It's unbelievable the amounts of drugs that are coming north.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, FOX NEWS HOST: Unbelievable. The only -- the other word for a fentanyl pill is a poison pill. This is intended to poison the American people who are not, in many cases, not attempting to overdose or trying to take what they believe are illegal drugs but it's being replaced with something that ends their life.\par \par \uc2 Great report from William La Jeunesse. We're the only network reporting on these things. And guess who's counting on that, China and Mexico ultimately, that no one sees that picture right there. We can't even stop the drugs coming across our ports of entry because they are so heavily traffic, let alone deal with what might be coming on the human side, through our southern border in Texas and Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Here's the number to wrap your brain around. Five million -- if you count encounters, known got-aways and likely got-aways, 5 million illegals have crossed our border into our country since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took the Oath of Office in January of 2021. Nearly 5 million, nearly the population of Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: What does that do to our schools, to our basic services, to gang violence, then to drugs, it only empowers and enriches the cartels, and yet nothing from our White House, nothing from the podium. They tell us, you know, these are not the droids you're looking for. There's no problem down there. It is an invasion. There's no other word for it. The states of Texas and Arizona should declare it as such.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And the media is not covering this but I found it fascinating that the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  came out and said, oh, by the way, the administration says there have been 1.3 people sent back to Mexico, and the times never says anything about the 2 million people who are now in the United States since President Biden took office, Pete.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: And they also tell us that don't worry, they're all legal migrants with a status and they will, surely -- surely the reason they want to leave Martha's Vineyard, Trace, is so that they can go back down to San Antonio and appear for their hearing. And after illegally breaking our laws become part of an asylum process. So, the Democrats through leadership at DHS have forced the border patrol to be facilitators, and give a legal pathway. And now we're going to deal with this for the next 5, 10, 15, 20. We had a DREAMer conversation, wait till DREAMer 4.0, DREAMer 5.0 DREAMer 6.0. This is not something that will go away and that's what the ideologues on the left side of the spectrum who believe in open borders are counting on.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And I got to get this in because Pete Hegseth is hosting the Patriot Awards come November. Give me 10 seconds on that, Pete.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: November 17th, the award show America deserves, tickets going fast. Don't miss it. Fox nation.com/patriotawards, tons of other parties and special events as well.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Pete Hegseth, great to see you my friend. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Thank you, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: General Jack Keane as Moscow preps "sham annexation votes" and Ukraine alleges terrorism at the hands of Putin next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, Ukraine calls this nuclear terrorism, a Russian missile strike near the country's second biggest nuclear power plant, missing the reactors by about 300 yards. Retired four star general Jack Keane is standing by but first to Jeff Paul. He's reporting live from Kyiv. Jeff?\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Trace, first it was the \uc2 Zaporizhzhia Nuclear Power Plant\uc2 , now it's another one, this time in the Mykolaiv Region. And Ukrainian officials say this was a close call. They are accusing Russian forces of firing a missile at another nuclear power plant, this time in the southern region of Ukraine. It not only left a crater about six feet deep and 13 feet wide, but it damaged industrial equipment.\par \par \uc2 Officials say thankfully, the missile didn't hit any of the three reactors but it was about 330 yards or roughly three football fields away from hitting them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 IHOR POPOVYCH, PIVDENNOUKRAINSK NUCLEAR POWER PLANT (through translation): We assess its strike as yet another act of terrorism. In this case, an act of nuclear terrorism, as the explosion happened not far from the nuclear facilities.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Now, as you crank continues to gain ground and move towards the east, Russian forces clearly intimidated by those actions. That's why Ukrainian officials believe today that separatists in Ukraine are announcing this referendum that's going to start on Friday, to allow people to vote whether or not to separate from Ukraine and join Russia. Of course, Ukrainian officials call this a sham as well as US officials and they don't believe that anyone in the world should look at these results and recognize them. Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Jeff Paul live for us in Kyiv, the capital city. Jeff, thank you. Let's bring in retired four star general Jack Keane, a Fox News Senior strategic analyst, former vice chief of staff in the army and Chairman of the Institute for the Study of War. General, it's always great to see you.\par \par \uc2 When you listen to Jeff Paul's reporting there and you hear these stories about another nuclear power plant being targeted by Russia. And then you have this, what the Ukrainians are calling sham votes to annex territories, like Luhansk and Donetsk. What do you make of that? Are these signs of desperation by Russia?\par \par \uc2 JACK KEANE, FOX NEWS SENIOR STRATEGIC ANALYST: Well certainly it has been a major setback as a result of the successful Kharkiv counter offensive. What Putin is attempting to do here, one of his major strategic objective strays has always been to break the will of the Ukrainian people and get them tired of this war because of the casualties, you know, to their loved one, certainly who are fighting the war but also casualties to themselves and the degree of suffering.\par \par \uc2 So what's about the nuclear plants, they don't want to hit a reactor certainly because that will cause as much trouble to their own forces as it is going to the Ukrainian people and even possible the people in Russia itself. But they're playing a very dangerous game, as Jeff has pointed out here, and using missiles and also artillery very close to those reactors.\par \par \uc2 What they want is to shut off the reactor's ability to connect to the grid, to the electric grid. That is the tactical objective. So that winter is coming and the power is shut down for the Ukrainian people as part of our strategic objective.\par \par \uc2 The second thing you mentioned is a referendum that's going on in four regions, Luan, Kherson and also Zaporizhzhia. The Russians don't even control all of that but they want these referendums as a political victory after having just suffered a very tactical defeat. By annex and in part of Russia, and then they can say they can use all military means available to them, Trace, to be able to protect this part of Russia, which everybody knows is really Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And I don't think it's something that we or the Ukrainians need to be overly concerned about, even though it's a political move, because the Ukrainians have attacked Crimea multiple times. And it's a part of Russia in Russia's mind, and Ukrainians mind it's not. And they have not escalated as a result of that.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: And I'm wondering, general, because it sounds like this Ukrainian counter offensive has a lot of high ranking people in Russia, in the Kremlin, as well as Russia's proxies, very concerned about how protracted this war has become, and who gets the upper hand eventually.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Well, Putin is so mad by now has made up his mind that this was not going to end in weeks and months. That is a protracted war. And he believes time is on his side because eventually, over time, he has more people. He's got more ammunition and he's got more equipment than Ukrainians have. And he believes a couple of things will happen over time.\par \par \uc2 One break the will of the Ukrainian people, two break the will of the international community that's providing arms, munitions and economic financing to the Ukrainians. He thinks in time that will happen, that's why they it's absolutely critical the international community stay engaged here and the United States leads and continues to lead aggressively in that manner.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Yes. And, general, I don't have really time to play the soundbite here but I want to get your take on the President saying on Sunday night, that if Taiwan was attacked by China that, yes, in fact, the US would defend that island. What are your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Well, first of all, I mean, the policy stated is supposed to be strategic ambiguity. And for our audience to understand, this goes back to 1979 Taiwan Relations Act, China and the United States agreed to maintain peace and stability across the Taiwan Straits. But President Xi has violated that year over a year for 10 years. And it's high time and I think it's very appropriate for the President to say, look, if China is going to continue their actions, they're going to use force to take back Taiwan. Of course, the United States is going to respond to that.\par \par \uc2 We're not just going to provide them arms and munitions, we will respond militarily to that act. He said it four times I think he means it, and that should be our policy.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Except the White House keeps walking it back. General Keane, it's always great to see you. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 KEANE: Yes, great talking to you, Trace.\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Well, the special master overseeing Mar-a-Lago raid documents meeting with former President Trump's legal team and DOJ prosecutors just a short time ago. The key takeaways from today's legal showdown are next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: Breaking right now, the special master grilling former President Trump's lawyers about his claim that he declassified documents the \uc2 FBI\uc2  seize for Mar-a-Lago, the judge meeting with the feds and the Trump legal team today. Justice correspondent David Spunt live at the courthouse in Brooklyn, New York. David, good afternoon.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Trace, good afternoon with a raid on Mar-a-Lago on August 8th happened in South Florida. The reason I'm here right now in Brooklyn, at the Eastern District of New York Federal Courthouse is this is where the special master, Judge Raymond Dearie, is located.\par \par \uc2 Now to bring everybody up to speed, this can be a complicated case about 11,000 documents were taken by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  from Mar-a-Lago on August 8th. We remember seeing those images. The Trump team wanted a special master, a neutral third party person to review some of those documents, specifically about a hundred documents the government said were classified.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Justice said we didn't need a special master. The judge in Florida said, well, we do need a special master and appointed the man who works in the courthouse behind me, Judge Raymond Dearie.\par \par \uc2 Well, Judge Raymond Dearie today was suggested by the Trump team and actually chided Trump attorneys because Judge Dearie asked the Trump attorneys, specifically Trump attorney Jim Trusty specifically to provide more information about the documents that the former president claims he so called declassified.\par \par \uc2 Jim Trusty said the legal team was not in a position to do so at this point, needed more time to do so. The judge seemed irritated at that point and said, "You can't have your cake and eat it too." So, Trace and everybody watching, where do we go from here? Well, Judge Dearie has specific orders to finish up his investigation, his review of the nearly 11,000 documents by November 30th. And we're talking just about a hundred documents that the government claims are classified.\par \par \uc2 Trump's team says they are not classified and the government says they are classified. Dearie says he is inclined to believe they are classified. That's where we are today a day, a victory somewhat for the Department of Justice in court. But, of course, this case is likely to go before the Appeals Court, could end up before the Supreme Court. So we are far out from this thing being over, Trace\par \par \uc2 GALLAGHER: David Spunt live for us in Brooklyn. David, thank you. That is THE STORY of Tuesday, September 20th. I'm Trace Gallagher.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document HUNDD00020220921ei9k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Firestorm Over Migrant Drop-Offs; DOJ to Appeal Ruling; Kagan's Comment about Court; Fetterman and Oz Debate; Biden's Job Approval; Interview with Sen. \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  (R-SC); Interview with White House Economic Adviser Jared Bernstein\b0\par\par\uc2 Shannon Bream, Jonathan Turley, Andrew McCarthy, Gillian Turner,  Juan Williams, Lucas Tomlinson \par \uc2 9,794 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Fox News Sunday\par \uc2 FOXN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: I'm Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 Policy fights ignite in the final stretch of the midterms -- as governors fly migrants, the president boosts unions and a Republican senator puts his party on the spot over abortion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): We're not afraid to talk about it. We're proud to be pro-life.\par \par \uc2 BREAM (voice-over): Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  's proposal putting the issue front and center just two months until midterms.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: Abortion rights are on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): The real question, which is still not being asked, is what restrictions will the Democrats support?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll speak live exclusively with Senator Graham about the blowback from this big gamble. Senator Graham, only on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 Plus --\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The American people won and special interests lost.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The White House takes an ill-timed victory lap on inflation, as markets tank. And our new FOX poll shows Americans feel the sting of high prices.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-FL): You know, they're living in fantasy land.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And the president helps close a deal to keep trains on the tracks, but faces questions about what each side is actually getting.\par \par \uc2 We'll ask White House economic adviser Jared Bernstein about inflation, the union deal and complaints the White House is tone deaf.\par \par \uc2 Then -- Democratic governors blast Southern state Republicans for sending migrants north to blue state cities, as both sides call each other hypocrites.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GAVIN NEWSOM (D), CALIFORNIA: What \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is doing is a disgrace.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R), FLORIDA: It just shows you, you know, their virtue signaling is a fraud.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We'll bring in our legal panel on the justification and what one governor's charge that this could be kidnapping.\par \par \uc2 And debates locked in some high stakes Senate races in Georgia and possibly Pennsylvania. Our Sunday panel will discuss the negotiations and the stakes for both sides.\par \par \uc2 All, right now, on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BREAM (on camera): Hello again from FOX News in Washington.\par \par \uc2 We are now just about 50 days out from midterms and headlines on huge issues like immigration, inflation and abortion are changing the dynamics. Just this week, the president again made his case that inflation, he says, will soon ease.\par \par \uc2 And spats between governors over migrant arrivals forced vulnerable candidates to respond.\par \par \uc2 In the middle of all of this, Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  caused outcry in his own party by proposing a bombshell bill banning abortion after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Senator Graham defended himself on FOX News digital in an op-ed writing, quote: Voters in key battleground states prefer a candidate who supports protections at 15 weeks to a candidate who believes in abortion on demand at any stage of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 In a moment, we will speak live exclusively with Senator Graham and ask him whether his colleagues agree with him.\par \par \uc2 But, first, let's turn to Lucas Tomlinson live at the White House where the administration faces a tough balancing act on the economy.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Shannon, President Biden's poll numbers have increased 3 percent over the past two months, but overall, his job approval rating remains under water.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (voice-over): A new FOX News poll says 56 percent of voters now approve of the president's job performance. Only 3 percent say inflation is under control, 55 percent say not at all.\par \par \uc2 James Taylor crooning, President Biden celebrated the passage of one of his signature pieces of legislation.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I signed the Inflation Reduction Act into law, the single most important legislation passed in Congress to combat inflation.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: While Biden was spiking the football on the South Lawn, stocks fell, the biggest drop in more than two years as recession fears continue.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday, another chartered busload of migrants arriving at the vice president's house for the second time in two days.\par \par \uc2 More buses also arriving in New York from Texas. Governor Greg Abbott says he plans to send even more.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R), TEXAS: If they will not go to the border, we're taking the border to them.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Florida Governor DeSantis also sending migrants up north, 50 arriving by plane on the wealthy island of Martha's Vineyard, over the objections of some residents who say they can't handle that many and called in the National Guard.\par \par \uc2 The migrants were quickly put on a ferry bound for Cape Cod.\par \par \uc2 The vice president still claiming the southern border is secure. Not everyone in her party agrees.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOE MANCHIN\uc2  (D-WV): It's wrong. She's dead wrong on that.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Experts say inflation, the border and abortion are on the ballot this fall, after Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  introduced new legislation banning abortion after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 The Senate's top Democrat says this is not a path to victory.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): They're not going to be able to run and duck and bob and weave and tie themselves in pretzel knots.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): President Biden is in London where he will attend Queen Elizabeth's funeral Monday morning -- Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Lucas Tomlinson for us at the White House this morning -- thank you, Lucas.\par \par \uc2 And joining us now here in Washington, Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome back to "FOX News Sunday". Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): Thank you. Congratulations, by the way.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you so much. Great to have you here this week.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. This is how "Politico" described a top Democrat reacting to your introduction of this bill: Graham's stunt is a godsend and helps us remind voters Republicans want to ban abortion everywhere.\par \par \uc2 And from the conservative side, you got Deroy Murdoch over at "American Spectator", he said, basically, you've handed the left a box of grenades.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: OK. Let me just say what I believe, number one. I'm pro-life, even in an election year. And to those who suggest that being pro-life is losing politics, I reject that. So, listen to what I'm saying and you decide today who you're with.\par \par \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  introduced legislation several months ago that would allow abortion on demand up to the moment of birth, like China and North Korea, for the entire nation. What did I do in response? I said, at 15 weeks, when the baby can feel pain and suck his thumb, that we're going to ban abortion except in cases of rape, incest, life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 We're talking almost four months into the pregnancy. It puts us in line with France, who's 12 weeks, Germany, England who's at 14 weeks. Only in Washington is it extreme to protect a baby at 15 weeks from an excruciating death.\par \par \uc2 Only thing I got going for me is every pro-life group in America and about 70 percent of the American people. So I don't apologize for being pro-life. I think the pro-life movement has found a position that most Americans will agree upon. There will be plenty of pro-choice people who will say at 15 weeks, we should ban the late term abortions, exceptions for rape, incest, life of the mother.\par \par \uc2 The people are with me. And the Democratic Party is extreme on the border. They're extreme on crime. They're extreme on inflation.\par \par \uc2 And to my Republican colleagues: now is the time to stand up and be counted. The pro-life movement is counting on you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: When you ask about abortion, the answer can't be, I'd like to lower inflation. Give a logical answer.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, let -- but let me get you to this. This is what you said back in June. This is around the time the Supreme Court was getting ready to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Right.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That you guys have argued for to send it back to the states. Here's what you said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: There's nothing in the Constitution that creates a right to abortion as a constitutional principle. This was a judge made, out of cloth law. Now, what this court has done is taken us back to pre-1973 where each state can decide, through their elected officials, when life begins and how to treat -- treat life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, back to the states.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Here's what President Biden said: When a lawmaker goes from touting states rights to touting a nationwide ban, it becomes clear they're not concerned with the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 You got to explain the pivot.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: OK. Number one, if you know anything about me, Shannon, for 20 years, I've been supporting federal legislation banning late term abortion.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But you said there states should make these decisions.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: But -- the states -- here's what Dobbs says: elected officials can make the decision, state or federal. I'm not inconsistent.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, I had a bill on the floor of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  right across the Capitol that banned abortion at 20 weeks because the baby can feel pain. I had 51 Republicans voting with me and two Democrats.\par \par \uc2 To suggest that I'm new to the game opposing late term abortion is ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: No one's suggesting that.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Listen, I was the author of the Unborn Victims of Violence Act that made it a crime to hurt a baby if you attack the mother. You'd be charged with two crimes if you attack a mother and you hurt the baby. It passed 72 votes in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 I never suggested there's no place for the unborn in Washington, D.C. If you tell the pro-life movement that we're out of business in the nation's Capitol, that we can't set some minimum national standard to prevent Chinese abortion policy in Maryland or California, there'll be revolt by the pro-life community.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: The people are with me.\par \par \uc2 What am I saying? I will not sit on the side lines and watch this nation become China when it comes to aborting babies up to the moment of birth. I reject that. I will continue to introduce legislation at the national level, setting a minimum standard --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: -- at 15 weeks, no abortion except for life, rape -- save the life of the mother, rape, incest.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. Your record on that is clear.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's the timing that has everybody scratching their heads because "Axios" said you didn't --\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: I'm scratching my head.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: -- you didn't ask Senate Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: I've been doing this --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. But here's what he said -- here's what he says about this being a national federal issue. Here's what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): I think most of the members of my conference prefer that this be dealt with at the state level.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's been the argument for overturning Roe. So when did you tell him?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: It's not the argument for overturning Roe.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, that was part of the argument. The Republicans have consistently said, in an amicus brief to the Supreme Court, get rid of Roe, send this back to the states.\par \par \uc2 When did you tell him and what was his reaction?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: I have been -- the pro-life community met with him.\par \par \uc2 Senator McConnell is a good man. He's a pro-life candidate. He voted for my bill in 2020 to ban abortion on demand at 20 weeks because the child can feel pain.\par \par \uc2 We're not talking about transportation policy or -- to all the states rights people, there's a lot of things been done in this country under the name of states rights that was wrong.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: This is a child in being, developing, becoming a human being. At 20 weeks, you encourage the mother and father to sing to the unborn baby because they can associate your voice with who you are.\par \par \uc2 At 15 weeks, the baby can feel pain. You provide anesthesia to save the baby's life when you operate.\par \par \uc2 To expect the pro-life community to sit on the sidelines and watch abortion policy in Maryland and California abort a baby up to 38 weeks is not going to happen. Dobbs does not require that. Dobbs says state or federal officials can do it.\par \par \uc2 Here's what I would tell the Republican Party: be not afraid. Stand up for the baby all over the country. We're in line with France, for God sakes. We're three months more liberal than the French.\par \par \uc2 I am confident the American people would accept a national ban on abortion at 15 weeks with exceptions for the life of the mother, rape and incest and reject the Chinese abortion policy being pushed by the Democratic Party. And if we can't do this for the unborn, what good are we to the unborn?\par \par \uc2 And I'll tell you this as a pro-life politician: if you stand with the unborn, the pro-life voters are not going to stand with you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, let's talk about voters because you're doing this in the middle of the midterm that is tightening.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And you guys want to control the Senate, and there are those who say this could cost you the Senate.\par \par \uc2 And our polling out this week that shows that 75 percent of those who say abortion is their top issue that's going to motivate them and go to the polls, they're going to vote for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You've got Republicans now answering for this out in the campaign trial. You've got Don Bolduc up in New Hampshire who said this when he was asked about your proposal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DON BOLDUC (R), NEW HAMPSHIRE U.S. SENATE CANDIDATE: I will support no legislation, zero legislation at the federal level. We don't belong in the abortion arena. That is the states.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You got a lot other topics that poll well for you. Abortion doesn't for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: OK. Let's look at this way. This is a wrong track election.\par \par \uc2 I hope the general wins. I hope he wins. I hope Joe O'Dea who's pro-choice in Colorado wins, because he'll change the course of the country on inflation, on border policy, on crime. But abortion is on people's minds.\par \par \uc2 And here's what I would tell the general. The pro-life movement in this country does not believe the Constitution requires us to sit on the side lines and watch a baby dismembered in the 8th and 9th month of pregnancy. We don't believe that. The Dobbs decision said it is up to elected officials, state or federal.\par \par \uc2 So the position of the pro-life movement in this country that I am representing today is that there is a place, a voice for the unborn in the nation's Capitol and every Republican, except one or two, in 2020, voted for a 20-week ban.\par \par \uc2 Here's what I tell the general: when you're on the stump, look to your opponent and say, what restrictions would you put, if any, on aborting the child? And I promise you this, General, America is with you when -- when you say at 15 weeks, almost four months, this baby can suck its thumb, all organs are developed. It shouldn't be aborted unless there's a very rare circumstances.\par \par \uc2 The people are with you. The people are with us. The Constitution is with us. The 14th Amendment for it to mean anything means equal protection under the law.\par \par \uc2 I am not going to allow a baby in Maryland and California to be dismembered at 38 weeks, a horrible death without trying to help. And I'm going to keep fighting in that Capitol over there for the voice of the unborn. And this is a reasonable position. It puts us in the civilized world versus China or (INAUDIBLE) -- and Democrats are adopting China and North Korean abortion policies.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, it cannot pass. It cannot pass in the current Congress. So, is it just about making them take that vote?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: So -- you know, the Unborn Victims of Violence Act took me ten years. I introduced it. It took ten years to get a federal law to say, if you attack a pregnant woman and you hurt her child, you'll be charged with two crimes. It took me ten years. Late term abortion took 12 years. It will not pass tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 But here's the question: Should it pass? If you come to Washington only introducing laws that can pass tomorrow, you're not much good for the country.\par \par \uc2 Should we be a nation that allows abortion on demand like China, up to the moment of birth? Hell no. We should be a nation that sets a national minimum standard of 15 weeks, like the civilized world, like the French, the British and the Germans. We shouldn't be an outlier.\par \par \uc2 Until my last breath, I will be fighting for this cause in the nation's Capitol. There is nothing preventing abortion policy in Washington, D.C. based on constitutional principles or Dobbs.\par \par \uc2 Here's the question for the pro-life movement: Are you going to accept the Republican Party who tells you Washington is out of business? I don't think you will. I don't think you should.\par \par \uc2 I hope the general wins. I hope all our candidates win. We're on the wrong track on crime, on immigration, on taxes, on inflation.\par \par \uc2 But the Democrats are trying to put us on the Chinese track when it comes to abortion. They're trying to create national law that would allow a child to be aborted up until the moment of birth. That's the wrong track. Our alternative is the right track.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well -- they didn't have the votes to get it. You don't have to votes to get this. So, we'll watch as this continues to play out. It certainly will be --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: I will fight -- we will one day get these votes, as we did with the Unborn Victims of Violence Act, as we did banning late term abortions. We're the party against late term abortion. The Democratic Party is for abortion on demand, up to the moment of birth. The people are with us.\par \par \uc2 To the pro-life leaders out there, be not afraid. There's nothing wrong with being pro-life, folks.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We got to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 Senator Graham, your position is clear. Thank you for coming to talk to us about it. Good to see you on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 Up next, inflation numbers higher than expected this week as Americans try to cut their costs. We'll bring in White House Economic Council adviser Jared Bernstein on exactly how the president plans to help. Can he do it? Next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: The president admits there is more work to do to rein in the rising prices that have hit Americans so hard in the last year. But economists say they remain concerned, as signs in the U.S. and abroad point to continued struggles on inflation.\par \par \uc2 Joining us here in Washington is White House economic adviser Jared Bernstein.\par \par \uc2 Great to have you back on "FOX News Sunday".\par \par \uc2 JARED BERNSTEIN, WHITE HOUSE ECONOMIC ADVISER: Great to be back with you, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. So, new numbers this week, as we discussed back in July, they're still at 40-year high.\par \par \uc2 Market Watch puts it this way. They say: Do not be fooled by falling gas prices. Inflation has spread far and wide through the U.S. economy. They say groceries, rent, home furnishing, medical care, new cars, auto insurance, education, all of these things surged in August.\par \par \uc2 So, what now?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Well, first of all, this is why easing inflationary pressures is the president's number one top domestic priority.\par \par \uc2 And on gas prices, in fact, they didn't surge, at least they didn't surge up. In fact, they've been falling. They've been falling for 96 days in a row.\par \par \uc2 So, we're not talking about a blip. We're talking about a durable trend there, down $1.36 per gallon.\par \par \uc2 For the average driver, that's about $70 of savings per month. And if you aggregate that across all of the drivers in the country, it's coming close to half billion per day. So, that's a real bit of breathing room.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Inflation Reduction Act is very much in the mix now, and that's going to help reduce costs of prescription drugs, of health care, of energy. Very important to get that implemented.\par \par \uc2 But, yes, prices remain uncomfortably high. That's why it's the top priority. That's why we're doing everything we can, whether it's release of oil from the Strategic Reserves that the president oversaw to help on gas, whether it's our work in the ports, whether it's ocean shipping costs, a bill the president signed to help bring those costs down 60 percent.\par \par \uc2 We've got a lot more work to do, you said it correctly. But we are -- we are very much devoted to that cause.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Let's talk about the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, because "\uc2 Forbes\uc2  " says this. It was a big part of bringing down the cost of the gasoline. It says: Of course, if for some reason we had a true supply emergency and found ourselves needing that oil, it would be looked upon as a terrible decision -- that we're at multi-decade low on what's available there.\par \par \uc2 Energy Department spokeswoman says it's not likely we're going to replenish it likely until likely after fiscal year 2023.\par \par \uc2 Are we putting ourselves in a precarious situation?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: I don't think so. I mean, I think this is actually one of the real success stories against that part of inflation, gas prices. So, when they peaked at over $5 a gallon, the president oversaw a release of historical number of barrels, 180 million from the strategic reserves, got our allies to release another 60 million. So, 240 million barrels.\par \par \uc2 That definitely -- that move definitely has its fingerprints on this $1.30 -- what did I say?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thirty cents, yeah.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Actually $1.34, $1.34 decline since the peak in June, providing some much needed breathing room to families.\par \par \uc2 But look, we have a lot more work to do. And here I think the Inflation Reduction Act is so important. Not just what I said to you a minute about reducing costs in healthcare and prescription drugs and energy costs, but really thinking about the other side.\par \par \uc2 In this case, the Republicans have said that their top economic priority is repealing the Inflation Reduction Act. That is precisely the same thing as saying our top economic priority is raising the cost of health care, raising the cost of insulin to seniors, raising the cost of prescription drugs, raising the cost of energy. And that pushes it exactly the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. The CBO has said, though, that it's negligible, the impact of potentially the Inflation Reduction Act, and that is what is being done with student loan forgiveness essentially wipes out any benefits.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yeah, no, I think that the point there is to get under the hood and look what's in the Inflation Reduction Act in this regard. I think there's two parts of it.\par \par \uc2 The first is that when you cap out of pocket expenditures for people, seniors, with serious expensive illnesses like cancers or Alzheimer's, diabetes, you're talking about saving someone who's now spending $8,000, $10,000 a year, $6,000 or $8,000 a year because it's capped at $2,000.\par \par \uc2 When you're talking about finally doing something that both parties have fought for for decades and have failed to get across the legislative goal line, which is allowing Medicare to negotiate for lower drug costs, that is a true victory for American seniors in particular, the cost of insulin for seniors.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: And the president wants to extend that to kids. Republicans have blocked him.\par \par \uc2 So, our plan is to implement these very much in the spirit of helping households deal with these price pressures.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah, and those are important because those are critical cases. All of us would be cheering for people to have a better situation there. But let's talk about our polling numbers.\par \par \uc2 Has inflation caused you financial hardship over the last six months? Seventy-eight percent of Americans say yes. That is up 3 percent from the last time that you were here.\par \par \uc2 People feel like things are getting worse for them. They blame the president, 65 percent disapprove of how he's doing on inflation.\par \par \uc2 And even Cardi B is weighing in. She is a big supporter of Democrats and progressive policies. She's frustrated.\par \par \uc2 Here's what she had to say this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARDI B, MUSICIAN: I was looking at some areas. The way that the prices soar up. Like, how are people surviving? I want to know.\par \par \uc2 I -- you know, like, my family and my friends, they're so grateful to have me. But it's just like, yo, what happens to people that don't have a me?\par \par \uc2 All these promises are being made, but living is unbearable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: She doesn't think promises are being kept. So, what do you say to people who don't have a Cardi B in their lives?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Well, I think that when it comes to promises made, promises kept, this president and this White House really have a very strong track record.\par \par \uc2 And when it comes to the polling that you mentioned, I think you have to there also get under the hood -- I was reading the very article on foxnews.com this morning that reported on that poll. It had the following sentence: There are some small but clear improvements in Biden's ratings.\par \par \uc2 Now, small but clear -- and I think that very much stems from some of the things we've been talking about already, Shannon. The fact that over the past two months, inflation has been effectively flat. Now, I'm talking about July and August.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's still a 40-year high.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Exactly. So, just in those two months, we've had flat prices. But over the year, inflation continues to be uncomfortably high. And that's why we are trying to implement all the plans that we've talked about.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, when you think about investing in the future, empowering workers, building it here in America, providing American households with some breathing room, building more resilient supply chains, rewarding work not wealth through our tax code, those are not parts of an imaginary blueprint. Those are parts of a blueprint that is actively in play.\par \par \uc2 This is legislation that has somehow, by this president, this Congress, been moved. I'm talking, of course, about Inflation Reduction Act, bipartisan infrastructure law, the CHIPS Act, standing up a domestic semiconductor industry, standing up a domestic electric vehicle industry, reducing cost of prescription drugs, of health care, of energy -- all of those would be on the chopping block if Democrats are not able to continue to implement those plans.\par \par \uc2 And speaking to Cardi B and anyone else experiencing these pressures, which we know are real --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: -- these plans are in play and they are working.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Okay. So, let's talk about the future. We know consumers are frustrated. They are feeling the pain of this. So, let's look forward because it looks like employers are worried as well.\par \par \uc2 These are some of the headlines. I'm looking at employer freezes, hiring, all these things.\par \par \uc2 "The Business Insider" on Friday talks about the \uc2 FedEx\uc2  boss warning of a worldwide recession closing stores, freezing hiring, trimming hours, parking planes.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Financial Times\uc2  ", \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  plans to cut jobs, because they say deal- making is drying up. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  shrinks workforce by 100,000, hiring freezes, layoffs.\par \par \uc2 "Above the Law" says hiring freezes and stealth layoffs hit top 50 big law firms.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, what are they seeing that the White House doesn't, or are you guys just not willing to acknowledge it?\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: First of all, we're very willing to acknowledge any -- we're very much a fact-based economics team when it comes to all of this.\par \par \uc2 But I have to give you facts on the other side of that coin, OK?\par \par \uc2 Since this president got here, almost 10 million jobs created, an historical record. And unemployment rate close to a 50-year low, 3.7 percent. There are 18 states out there with unemployment at 3 percent or below.\par \par \uc2 These are historical numbers. It's about the tightest labor market on record.\par \par \uc2 Now, at the same time, we've been talking about inflationary pressure. We know that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is in a hiking cycle to try to dampen those inflationary pressures, as they should be. We know that's cooling off an overheated housing market.\par \par \uc2 So, these are all dynamics that are in play. But in the background is one of the strongest labor markets Americans have ever experienced. And that is such a critical tail wind given all the other pressures you've just taken us through.\par \par \uc2 And both are true. You have to keep both things in your head at the same time. There are challenges in this economy and you have an administration with a president overseeing economic team that is undertaking these challenges with a rigor that I, as a guy who's, you know, got aging gray hair over here, have never seen such an aggressive pushback on these kinds of, again, challenges.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: With wages being outpaced by inflation, prices, the cost of borrowing money up, Fed rate (ph) again this week -- folks are cheering for you at the White House to get this right.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: Thank you. Thanks for hearing (ph) me, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 BERNSTEIN: My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 All right. Coming up next, fed up Republican governors are sending migrants to Democratic-led cities and facing questions over whether that's actually permitted by law. And a judge rejects the Justice Department bid to restart its review of classified documents seized from Mar-a-Lago. Our legal panel weighs in next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Democrats have called it a cruel stunt. Republican Governors Greg Abbott and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  sending undocumented migrants from border states to cities run by Democrats. Republicans say Democrats will now see what southern communities are grappling with as border encounters reach record numbers. Democrats say might not even be legal. They want the Justice Department to step in.\par \par \uc2 So, joining us now two legal experts who can help us break it down. Former assistant U.S. attorney for the Southern District of New York, Andrew McCarthy, and George Washington University Law Professor Jonathan Turley. Both Fox News contributors.\par \par \uc2 Welcome, gentlemen. Good to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW MCCARTHY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I want to start with California Governor Newsom, who is calling on the DOJ potentially to step in here. He says that people were giving false representations about where they were being going, transported, that kind of thing, what would be waiting for them. He says, I urge U.S. DOJ to investigate whether the alleged fraudulent inducement would support charges of kidnapping under relevant state laws which could serve as a predicate offense for change - charges under RICO.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, you've written about this.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Yes, it's absurd. They suggest that this can be kidnapping. There might have been confusion. There might have been misrepresentation for some. But these are consensual trips and they have records of the consent. You have \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  saying that this is literally human trafficking, which is legally nonsense. I mean human trafficking is designed to prevent sexual and labor exploitation. These are people who have been released into the country by the Biden administration and have accepted trips to cities like Chicago, New York, in this case Martha's Vineyard. They have a right to go on those trips and the states have a right to facilitate them.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, here is what President Biden has had to say about what's happening at the federal and state level.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We have a process in place to manage migrants at the border. We're working to make sure it's safe and ordinarily and humane.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, Andy, I remember the Supreme Court, just a few years ago, having this fight with the state of Arizona where it wanted to take on immigration policy and it's very much the fed's place to do it. And the president says they've got it under control. So, what of these actions by these southern Republican governors?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Well, they absolutely do not have it under control. And what the governors are doing is exactly the same thing that the Democratic administration has been doing, they've actually been moving people throughout the country as well.\par \par \uc2 We have a statute that says that when people come into the United States illegally, they shall be detained until their proceedings are concluded. We're ignoring that statute. The Biden administration is cutting enforcement on administration. They're cutting the budget on detention. To single this out, given the botch that they've made of immigration policy, is just staggering.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, I want to turn to what's going on in Trump world because there's plenty of legal activity there.\par \par \uc2 Andy, let me start with you on this because a slew of subpoenas going out suggesting there are still plenty of active investigations going on. What say you?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: It's clear that they're ramping up investigative activity. They're looking at what they call the fake electors scheme. They're looking at tampering with the actual election equipment. There's a number of grand jury subpoenas that have gone to people who were in President Trump's main circle. But I think the immediate problem, at least from president - former President Trump's perspective is the investigation of Mar-a-Lago and the seizures there. At this point I think that's the one that's ramping up with a bullet.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and, Judge Aileen Cannon, who has denied the DOJ's request to be able to use some of these documents that they want to, absent going through first the vetting of the special master that's been appointed. She says, appointment of a special master to conduct a review of the seized materials is fully consonant with the foregoing principles and with the need to ensure at least the appearance of fairness and integrity under unprecedented circumstances.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, she's taken a lot of heat. People will say this is a Trump appointed judge. They say that she is going to get overturned on the appellate level. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, it's interesting that the Department of Justice filed a motion pending appeal. They didn't ask to stay the entire appointment of the special master, but instead asked only for access to about 100 classified documents. That's a smart move. In fact, I think it may be the first smart move I've seen from the Justice Department, you know, in hunting and litigation. The word is, you know, aim small, miss small. This is your best bet on those 100 documents.\par \par \uc2 But the special master may be already making progress on that. He's expected to prioritize those hundred documents. There's a very good chance that they will be released or given access to the Department of Justice. It will be interesting to see if the administration goes further to try to litigate these other thousands of documents that are going to be reviewed by this special master. I mean the court noted that the Department of Justice itself admitted that it may have taken attorney/client material, admitted that its taint (ph) teams had made some mistakes. And so the special master's going to drill down on that, but probably after he looks at those classified documents.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So we stand by now to see what happens with the appeal. It could end up at the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  , which is the next thing I want to talk about.\par \par \uc2 Justice Kagan is out there discussing those who have been criticizing the court for being too political and what happens when courts take certain actions. Here's what she said this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELENA KAGAN\uc2  , SUPREME COURT JUSTICE: When people see them as extensions of the political process, when people see them as trying just to impose personal preferences on a society, irrespective of the law, that's when there's a problem.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Andy, what do you make of all these now very public conversations about the integrity of the courts, pushing back against decisions they've made and whether you agree or disagree going to the heart of what the justices are doing?\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Well, I think to Justice Kagan's point, if you're going to worry about the public perception of the court, you're doing politics instead of law. And the reason that we insulate the judiciary from politics is so they can try to decide what the case is based on the law, whether that's a popular result or an unpopular result. So I think the minute that you start to take the position that the court needs to worry about the public perception of its decisions, unless what the court -- unless what the public thinks is that the court is making the decisions because of politics rather than because of what the law says, then I think you get away from what the business of judging is. They just can't worry about public perception and what the media is going to say. They have to try to figure out what the right answer is and give it. That's why we insulate them from politics.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: So, "The Wall Street Journal" editorial board says this about Justice Kagan. Says, no doubt she's frustrated that she isn't in the majority in many of the most significant cases, but now she knows how \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2  and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  felt for decades. They never attacked the legitimacy of the courts.\par \par \uc2 Professor.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, I think that's a good point. I just spoke to the 10th Circuit Judicial Conference. And at the conference also speaking was Chief Justice Roberts and Gorsuch. And Chief Justice Roberts really took offense at these attacks of the integrity of the court.\par \par \uc2 We're having a crisis of faith. But I think that's being fueled by a crisis of leadership. These attacks on the integrity of not just the court but on your own colleagues that are implied in these remarks.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All right, Andrew McCarthy, Jonathan Turley, we appreciate your time. Thank you so much. We'll see you again soon.\par \par \uc2 TURKEY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Up next, we're going to bring in our Sunday group on our latest Fox polls about the issues voters say will motivate them the most in November.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (September 11, 2022): The border is secure. We have a secure border in that that is the priority for any nation including ours and our administration.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's open not closed. The border's open. Everybody we (INAUDIBLE) the border is open. It's open because we entered. We come in. Free. No problem.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: And in -- but it's open for you to come illegally, right?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Illegally, yes, illegally. That's true (ph).\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  saying the administration has the border under control. And you saw there a contrasting view from a migrant who arrived outside the vice president's home in D.C. this week.\par \par \uc2 Time now for our Sunday group.\par \par \uc2 "USA Today" White House correspondent Francesca Chamber, former chief of staff to \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  , Josh Holmes, Fox News political analyst Juan Williams, and Fox News correspondent Gillian Turner.\par \par \uc2 Great to see you all.\par \par \uc2 JOSH HOLMES, FORMER CHIEF OF STAFF TO \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2  AND "RUTHLESS" PODCAST CO-HOST: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It feels a little - like we said, a little bit like "Family Feud" in here. I don't know what's going to happen.\par \par \uc2 FRANCESCA CHAMBERS, WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT, "\uc2 USA TODAY\uc2  ": The girls are going to win.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Francesca, I'll start with you.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, there's a very different view about what's going on. We have, and we'll dive into this, the southern border state governors, many of them red states, sending people to here in D.C., Martha's Vineyard, New York and elsewhere. Looks like a lot of political gamesmanship.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: The Biden administration's response has been that it's a political stunt and that it's inhumane. And the White House is seeking to use this as an opportunity to talk about what Democrats would like to do to reform the legal immigration system, including creating a pathway to citizenship for groups of immigrants, including farm workers and essential workers and whatnot.\par \par \uc2 But the Biden administration is facing tough questioning about what they're doing to help cities where the migrants are arriving and also where they're being bussed, like Washington, D.C., and also El Paso. And the Biden administration is also facing some of that criticism from D.C.'s mayor, who has said that when it comes to the Republican governors, that she's upset with them for - for - for bringing the migrants here, but also that the federal response has been lacking. She wants to see more help from FEMA.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It's been interesting to see Democrats and Republicans alike say, especially those in places like Texas, we need help. And when people show up at their doorstep, then, we really need help from the feds to get something done. But not a lot of Americans think they're doing well, or the president is on this. His job performance on immigration, he has got 32 percent approval but, Gillian, 60 percent disapproval on this issue. It's not one they want to talk about.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Part of the reason I think, Shannon, we see those numbers is because the border is not just a story about numbers and data crunching, though Border Patrol sources have already told our border teams that they are on track to surpass 2 million unvetted illegal immigrant crossings this fiscal year with weeks left to go.\par \par \uc2 It's also - the story provides a very visual narrative, right? We saw recently \uc2 Karine Jean Pierre\uc2  say to Peter Doocy that people don't just walk across the southern border. And this year - excuse me, this week, right, on cue, we got a very visual reminder from the Justice Department about exactly how people are getting here. There was this human smuggling sting. We saw photo after photo they released of thousands of migrants being rounded up, stuffed in suit cases, stuffed inside emptied out water tankers and then piled up on these flatbed trucks before getting driven across the southern border. So there is a constant visual reminder here about what exactly is happening at the border.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JUAN WILLIAMS, FOX NEWS POLITICAL ANALYST: And I think part of that is the human story.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think there's - I think it's important, you know, just to get beyond the politics for a second. The politics, it seems to me, is, you use these people, you make them into political props. And you say, well, wait a second, the Republican governors are upset. But wait, these people just walked thousands of miles. They are fleeing violence, political oppression and poverty. And each of us, as Americans, understands, you know what, we have family history. We understand where we came from. And to see them used as props is offensive.\par \par \uc2 And you stop and think about, well, wait a second, the Republican governors have a point. They're on the border states in some cases and their communities are being burdened by the presence of this surge of people. The people are coming oftentimes -- the people who were shipped, for example, they came legally. They came seeking some kind of legal step to --\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Seeking asylum.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Asylum that would consider their standing. But the reality is, the federal government gives added aid to those states. Doesn't give it to Martha's Vineyard. If the federal government had said, OK, well, fine, you want to start playing games and stunts like this, we'll pull our funding. Boy, I think you're going to see a different tune.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Well, OK. So, two of these governors who are at the -- in the middle of this, Governor DeSantis says about the letter to the DOJ that \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  sent, Governor Newsom, he said, I think your hair gel is interfering with your brain function. Governor Newsom tweets back, clearly your struggling, distracted and busy playing politics with people's lives. He says, all you want is attention. Let's have a debate. I'll bring my hair gel. You bring your hair spray. Name the time before election day. By the way, I am volunteering right now to moderate said debate.\par \par \uc2 But, Josh, there are real people in the middle of this.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Oh, no question. I mean, I think, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is desperately trying to get everybody's attention to say, hey, I can run for president here in 2024. I think that - that's what that's about.\par \par \uc2 Let me just address the humanitarian aspect that I think that Juan addressed here. I don't remember a lot of concern out of progressives or this administration when the Biden administration, during the campaign, essentially sent an invitation to people that they would be welcomed at the border and provided with American assistance. What we know is that that's caused a two year humanitarian crisis at the border. It has caused real suffering, human trafficking, opioids. All of those problems, that is the problem. Whether we send them to Martha's Vineyard once they're here or not, that's not the humanitarian crisis that's happening here. It's that tens of thousands of people that are living in squalor are being trafficked by coyotes to the border themselves, some in just horrible, reprehensible things done to women and children. That's the real problem here. Whether or not DeSantis decides to send them to Martha's Vineyard, which, by the way, I thought was a great idea because progressives everywhere in this administration they don't have problem - they're very generous at spending everybody else's resources, right? But when it comes to their own, boy, their favorite vacation destination, now we got a problem, right? So I think that's basically the story here.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: But I think that's a stunt. And let me just respond to Josh here. I think that you look back at specifically things like separating children from their moms, and that was under a Republican administration, that's not humane.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That's happened under Democrats too.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And that's not good, OK.\par \par \uc2 But the reality is that America and the Congress specifically needs to solve this problem.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, and - and -\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: And for 20 years we've known the solution.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: But what -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, we -\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Bump up security.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Do more in terms of the -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We've got to -\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The system, the judicial system, to deal with these people.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We've got to live it here. Yes. But with Republicans in control of the House, the White House and the Senate and Democrats with that same makeup have not gotten it done. So, there's plenty of blame to go around.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes, but neither has - but both sides prefers to play politic.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: They do. OK.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: And one party says there is no border security problem. So.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. Panel, we're going to take a little break here.\par \par \uc2 Up next, though, a debate between Dr. Oz and \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  could be one of the most watched 2022 midterm events, but is it actually going to happen? We're going to read the tea leaves, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-PA), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE NOMINEE: Do you think Dr. Oz thinks about working people?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: No.\par \par \uc2 FETTERMAN: How many of you have two gigantic ten (ph) mansions?\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE NOMINEE: He had a non-profit and he also has some properties he owns, but he's got tax liens. He ignores them. He said they feel through the cracks.\par \par \uc2 But it gets worse than this. You know, the hypocrisy runs deep.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Pennsylvania Senate candidates \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Dr. Mehmet Oz taking another round of pot shot at each other this week ahead of a potential debate next month.\par \par \uc2 We are back now with the panel.\par \par \uc2 Who thinks the debate's happening?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Oh, yes, that's a -\par \par \uc2 BREAM: All - all - maybe.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: I don't know.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, no.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: The Fetterman debate?\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: I hope so.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Josh, what do you - what do you think?\par \par \uc2 Well, listen, "The Philadelphia Inquirer: is on that's saying out there, communication is part of being a senator, perfectly reasonable to ask how well he's able to listen, speak, focus and understand.\par \par \uc2 So, do you think, Josh, it happens?\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Yes, well, I really hope so. And I know that Dr. Oz really hopes so. There's been a ton of trepidation by Fetterman and Fetterman's camp, for obvious reasons, right? He's been very, very, shall we say, lack of communicative on the campaign trail. Everything is fairly well scripted. It's sort of in (INAUDIBLE). It seems like he's having difficulty navigating the issues. And so it's hard to imagine in four weeks how that changes, which could be a - it seems like a big liability for him.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: And it's well after early voting starts, Juan.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I don't think there's any doubt there's going to be a debate. Fetterman has agreed to have a debate in late October.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: I think that - and, a little bit, this is silly season. People have strokes. And, guess what, people recover from strokes. And it takes them time to recover from strokes. I think a celebrity doctor should know this, right?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: But most of them aren't running for Senate.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: No, but you don't - it's -- he's not running to be president. He's not running for an executive function like a governor. He's running to be part of a legislative body. The GOP problem is that Oz is under performing and Fetterman - Fetterman could say, look, even if I have halting speech, I'll do better than a guy who lives in New Jersey. Just - just go at it.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: That - that seems to be working for him so far.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, let's talk more broadly about the midterms because our new Fox News polling shows a shift, congressional vote preference. Back in July, Republicans held the advantage here but now, Gillian, it has shifted to the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: I will say that the president himself is getting a boost this week. It's a surprising boost, first off, given the fact that the fundamental sort of voter building blocks in this election cycle are going to be the economy and then the political reality that most Americans blame the president for not relieving historic inflation. He's getting a boost this week from these I would describe them as sort of external factors. Things that he doesn't really have -- play a large hand in. The fact that former President Trump's back in the headline warring with the DOJ has a lot of people ginned up. The \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  ruling overturning Roe v. Wade. The president had very little hand in -- perhaps none in either of these issues. He is getting a definite boost from them this week.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK, so he's not getting a boost in our polling when we ask about his approve/disapprove. But also this, has the Biden administration been competent and effective in managing government? Now, 38 percent say yes, 52 percent, Francesca, say no. And that is a flip from a year ago when he was leading on that issue.\par \par \uc2 CHAMBERS: Well, over the past two months he has seen a roughly 5 percent bounce in the polling, but he still is under water. That's under - that is under 50 percent in - in -- in all of the polling. But Democrats are feeling better about the midterm elections at this point. And the White House has been putting President Biden out on - at official events to talk about things like infrastructure. And we're going to see a shift, though, soon. Next week he'll be going to Florida to talk about the midterm elections. It is supposed to be a speech about \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  's agenda. Of course, \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  on the ballot in that state also. And Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  . So, it's really an opportunity for President Biden to test his messaging for 2024 also in DeSantis and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  's home state.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 OK, so one of the big issues that we talked about with Senator Graham is this 15 week federal ban he's proposing on abortion. This is an issue that has not polled well for Republicans and they are - Juan is shaking his head. But this is like a gift to the Democrats. You should be excited about this. "Politico" magazine, Rick Lowry, writes in defense of it, he says, if the bill ever gets to a floor vote, it could force Senate Democrats to declare themselves against a late term abortion ban that will strike much of the public as reasonable.\par \par \uc2 Juan, you heard the senator say today that - and, listen, we know that's probably part of the plan, make them vote on this.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Right. But I think the way that it -- the senator defends it is to say, oh, it's a reasonable amount. It's 15 weeks. I think the idea is that that's the start, a national ban. And, clearly, the people who are opposed to abortion would like to it be a complete ban in this country.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: In some states it is.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: What we've seen - I'm sorry?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: In some states it is.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Right. If we - what we've seen in West Virginia, Indiana, even excluding rape, incest and the like.\par \par \uc2 So what we've got to see is that the abortion issue has now become a leading issue for American women in this election. We've seen a tremendous surge in registration among women since the Dobbs decision was announced. You've seen the vote in Kansas, a red state, saying, you know what, we don't want abortion just taken off the books.\par \par \uc2 I think that you're seeing people run away from it, scrub their website, scrub their previous statements in which they were taking on abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Others - others are embracing it. And "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  " is - is criticizing those who are now bringing their wives out onto the campaign trail, the Republicans, because of this issue. They say cue the emergence of gauzy campaign ads starring the wives of Republican Senate hopefuls getting personal about their hubbies, several recently spotted -- spotlighted by Politico. Gag. That's the word they use.\par \par \uc2 Josh, quick final word from you.\par \par \uc2 HOLMES: Yes, I mean, I think that characterization is pretty disgusting. It's sort of relevant whose spouse, man or woman, is on the - on the campaign trail, right? But as to this issue itself, look, Senator Graham's position, not unreasonable at all. And Democrats' position way out of the main stream. The problem is, it's a little bit like your friend coming over and asking how your grandma is three weeks after the funeral. It is not the right time for this, right?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: OK. We're going to leave it there. Panel, thank you very much. We will see you again next Sunday.\par \par \uc2 That is it for today. I'm Shannon Bream. Thank you for joining me. Have a great week. We will see you next FOX NEWS SUNDAY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXN000020220918ei9i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden's Border Crisis Comes To Liberal Enclaves\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Jeanine Pirro, Joe Concha, Jason Chaffetz \par \uc2 7,775 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: And welcome to HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 And breaking tonight, momentum for Republicans in key races all across the country. We're going to break down the latest polls. That's coming up straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 Also, Pennsylvania Senate candidate Dr. Oz will be here with a special message for the radical socialist trust fund brat in a hoodie, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , guy that is not fit for office, never had a job in his life.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we begin once again in beautiful Martha's Vineyard where after a full hours of intense emergency virtue signaling, well, the island's uber wealthy, uber woke residents they booted all of their newly arrived guests out of town. They're gone. As of 8:00 a.m. this morning, the 50 illegal immigrants who flew in from Florida or rounded up, loaded onto buses and shipped to a U.S. military base. How loving, thoughtful and accommodating of the left.\par \par \uc2 Now, according to local Democrats, there just wasn't enough room on the 55,000-acre island for the 50 illegal immigrants. Not our problem, they were saying. You have the liberal, racist, xenophobic, let's see, millionaires, billionaires who live on the island, they just say they don't have enough resources to accommodate them. I'm not buying that.\par \par \uc2 And over at MSDNC, you have Chuck Todd saying that the situation was inhumane because of the lack of infrastructure. What about Texas's 1.1 million illegals this year alone? Never mind the nearly million last year.\par \par \uc2 Now, it's not clear if Chuck was worried about the illegal immigrants or the long-suffering residents, the wealthy people in Martha's Vineyard who were forced to endure a whole whopping hours of uninvited guests.\par \par \uc2 Now, either way, it is clear tonight that America's virtuous liberals are no longer able to ignore the two-year crisis. By the way, the crisis they caused at our southern border and they know just who blame. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: It's a stunt using human beings to score political points.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: What's that the word inhumane was a good one to use in this particular case, so people don't know where they're going, the people that are there don't know you're coming.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: Some politicians would rather not only have an issue but exacerbate it to the extent of literally human trafficking as you said.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, NBC HOST: And it's all due to treating people as political pawns, leaving some migrants who might have believed they were going elsewhere as reported by NPR, on a literal island that doesn't have any infrastructure designed to help them at all. The word is inhumane.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Instead of working with us on solutions, Republicans are playing politics with human beings using them as props. What they're doing is simply wrong. It's un-American. It's reckless and we have a process in place to manage migrants of the border.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah, put them in Joe's cages put in the middle of a pandemic. Really? Okay, let's talk about trafficking because Joe has been doing it the entire time in the dark of night, putting illegal immigrants on planes and landing at 2:00, 3:00 and 4:00 a.m.\par \par \uc2 Is he a human trafficker? Is he aiding and abetting law breaking? By the way, it also gets worse.\par \par \uc2 Out in California, you got the -- well, not so bright Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , he wants \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  arrested for kidnapping. Now, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is so stupid and so strung out on his own insane wokeness that he's somehow unable to see the real crime committed right in front of him.\par \par \uc2 Now, President Biden swore an oath to uphold the laws of this land and our constitution, and yet, so far, Joe has refused to enforce the laws of the land and that would mean securing our southern border. This is a dereliction of duty. Three plus million illegal immigrants have simply walked across the border, unsecure, in the last two years, less than two years.\par \par \uc2 Now, the Biden administration has transported up to 700,000 of them on late night plane rides or in other words, he's been involved in human trafficking and aiding and abetting law breaking. He's also sent others on bus trips all across the country and he gives preferential treatment. No COVID tests for illegal immigrants, no vaccine mandates, many without even a background check to see if they have radical associations. You got a preferential treatment and they get a free \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  phone.\par \par \uc2 Now, meanwhile a record 66 known terrorists have been caught just trying to cross this year. We have no idea how many terrorists may have already snuck into this country undetected. That is an unmitigated security disaster.\par \par \uc2 Now, Biden did detain thousands of children and he put them in the cages that we showed you that he didn't want us to show you for a good while right in the right at the height of the pandemic. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  doesn't care because \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  is too stupid to realize what's even going on.\par \par \uc2 Now, today, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  had these nice words about little old Gavin and his threat to go to the DOJ. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R), FLORIDA: The governor of California sent a letter to the Department of Justice saying you need to prosecute Texas and Florida governors. And all I can say is I think his hair gel is interfering with his brain function.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. That's the line of the week for sure. It sums up pretty perfectly what's going on here. We're going to have a lot more on California's half-paid governor straight ahead tonight.\par \par \uc2 But first, let's get back to the oh so compassionate residents, the millionaires, the billionaires including the Obamas, they have 30 acres in Martha's Vineyard. Why not take of those acres and you could build facilities for the homeless people.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, by the way, those people at Martha's Vineyards could not be bothered to accommodate even new guests that were thrown out 8:00 a.m. this morning.\par \par \uc2 And apparently, the good folks of Martha's Vineyard are not familiar with a little town in Texas named Eagle Pass. Well, like Martha's Vineyard, Eagle Pass is the home to less than 30,000 permanent residents. But unlike Martha's Vineyard, Eagles Pass is forced to deal with hundreds of illegal immigrants every single day of the year. At any point in time, there are thousands of illegal immigrants in and around Eagles Pass.\par \par \uc2 Now, nearly every town and every city close to the border has experienced the exact same humanitarian crisis for nearly two long years. That includes El Paso where as we speak, there is a humanitarian crisis hundreds of illegal immigrant men women and children they're now sleeping in the street, they have no shelter, no bathrooms, no shower,