s not just the awards show. You also have special events and parties you can sign up for with your favorite FOX News personalities.\par \par \uc2 This is the event you do not want to miss. I also don't want to miss you tomorrow morning on "FOX and Friends." Be there, and now it's time for Tucker, our time is done.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER CARLSON\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening and welcome to TUCKER CARLSON TONIGHT. Happy Friday.\par \par \uc2 If there is one thing we have learned about the people who make the rules, it's that they have no intention of following them, any of them ever, ever.\par \par \uc2 They command you to wear a mask as you jog alone in the park while they head to dinner barefaced at the French Laundry. That happened.\par \par \uc2 They harangue you of the carbon footprint of your lawnmower, as they fly to Aspen on their private jets. You see that every day?\par \par \uc2 How many Members of Congress who have voted to expand the police powers of the \uc2 IRS\uc2  don't pay their own taxes? More than a few.\par \par \uc2 How many politicians who fervently support gun control have armed bodyguards that you pay for? Well, let's see every single one.\par \par \uc2 We could go on and on and on. Whatever they're demanding that you do this week, you can be dead certain they are not doing it themselves. Everyone notices this. It enrages people, but it is often misinterpreted.\par \par \uc2 People say this is hypocrisy, but it's not, its privilege.\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  walks into her hair appointment without a mask at the height of the COVID lockdowns, it is not because she forgot to bring a mask. No. She knows exactly what she is doing. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is affirming her position in the social order.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is in charge, you are not. \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  can do whatever she cares to do, you can't. That's the message she is sending.\par \par \uc2 Another word for this is a caste system, which is increasingly what we have in the United States. Caste systems are common in poor countries, the countries from which most of our immigrants come, but it is the opposite of the traditional American system, which was imperfect egalitarianism.\par \par \uc2 In the old America, every citizen was considered equal under the law, as well as in the eyes of God. Everybody had to follow the same rules because nobody underneath it all was more important than anyone else. We were all citizens.\par \par \uc2 Citizens is a term you rarely hear anymore. Citizenship implies inherent rights, rights the politicians cannot under any circumstances take from you because they did not bestow them in the first place, you were born with them.\par \par \uc2 But the people running our country no longer acknowledge this, they do not believe in inherent rights, nor do they believe in equality.\par \par \uc2 You may remember how hysterical they became a couple of years ago when a few old-fashioned souls dared to say that all lives matter, which they certainly do. The people in charge hated that. They don't think that all lives matter. In their view, some animals are more equal than others.\par \par \uc2 It's hard to think of a news story in recent memory that illustrates this phenomenon as clearly as what is happening on Martha's Vineyard. Two nights ago, Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida generously sent a planeload of 50 illegal aliens from Venezuela to the vineyard, which is a small, famously affluent island off the coast of Massachusetts.\par \par \uc2 Martha's Vineyard has everything you could ever want in the world except racial diversity. The island is wider than a Swedish Bluegrass Festival, and DeSantis was trying to help.\par \par \uc2 Under normal circumstances, the residents of Martha's Vineyard would have no choice, but to smile and say, "Thank you, Governor DeSantis." When government officials send you the blessed gift of diverse immigrants or refugees, you must accept. That's the rule.\par \par \uc2 It doesn't matter how disruptive these new people are, it's irrelevant how much they cost to support or how dramatically your quality of life may decline after they arrive. It doesn't matter if the crime rate spikes, if your daughter is afraid to go outside, if the schools become unusable, the hospitals fall apart, it doesn't matter. You never complain. You take it like a man.\par \par \uc2 You don't even mention it is happening, even in private text messages to your friends. If you do, you're a dangerous racist. You could wind up on a government list.\par \par \uc2 That's how America works. Everybody knows it because it's been going on for a very long time. That's the rule.\par \par \uc2 But it turns out the people of Martha's Vineyard aren't just anyone, they are the people who make the rules. And in this case, they had no intention of following them, not even for a day. The Venezuelans who arrived there two nights ago, bless their hearts, didn't know any of this. They had not perspective. They never heard of Martha's Vineyard. There were just thrilled to be there. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MSNBC REPORTER: I can tell you, they are not angry at \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . They're actually thanking him for having brought them to Martha's Vineyard.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, yes, they're not angry at \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . They're thanking him for bringing them to Martha's Vineyard. Why wouldn't they? They got a free flight to the Vineyard. And at first, it seemed like they had every reason to be grateful because the people of the town appeared to welcome them as they walked in from the airport three-and-a-half miles. They saw signs like this one: "On Martha's Vineyard." The sign reads, "We stand with immigrants, with refugees. All are welcome here. Hate has no business here."\par \par \uc2 In other words, no one who worked for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can go to Martha's Vineyard. But they didn't know that there was a caveat to that welcome. "Hate is not welcome here." Okay.\par \par \uc2 Then on Thursday, an elected official on the island gave the migrants even more reason for hope. And we're quoting: "This is a community rallying to support immigrant children and families," said State Representative Dylan Fernandes, "It's the best of America."\par \par \uc2 And then to prove his point that Martha's Vineyard will do whatever it can for immigrants, Fernandes uploaded this picture onto social media because of course, everything is a social media opportunity.\par \par \uc2 The picture so several Venezuelans gathered on a porch. One of them is wearing a shirt celebrating \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , "Notorious RBG," it says. Clearly, he got that from a local. But then you look closely and you ask yourself, are these people really welcome in Martha's Vineyard? What are they eating?\par \par \uc2 There is no kale or brie for the Venezuelans. No, instead, they got Corn Pops, Froot Loops, Rice Krispies, with a side of paper plates and napkins.\par \par \uc2 You've got to wonder what the Venezuelans thought of that. This seems like a nice place. The food is garbage. It's less healthy than that crap we eat in Caracas, way worse. Why are they feeding us this stuff?\par \par \uc2 Corn Pops, really? Froot Loops? What message are you sending?\par \par \uc2 And of course, the message is really clear. The serfs eat garbage. I think what do they eat? What does your average Venezuelan eat? I don't know, Froot Loops. Venezuelans love Froot Loops.\par \par \uc2 So, the Venezuelans may have gotten a hint that they weren't really welcomed there. But it was hard to know. The community seems to be rallying behind them. But then, if there was any doubt in the morning, it was gone by afternoon because the message changed very quickly. And the new message was, "Get the hell out of here, Brown people."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LISA BELCASTRO, SHELTER COORDINATOR FOR WINTER HOMES ON MARTHA'S VINEYARD: The difficult challenges are, we have to, at some point in time, and they have to move to somewhere else. Right? We cannot -- we don't have the services to take care of 50 immigrants and we certainly don't have housing. We're in a housing crisis as we are on this island.\par \par \uc2 And we don't -- we can't house everyone here that lives here and works here. We don't have housing for 50 more people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: "We don't have housing for 50 more people," says the lady on an island that literally has more than a hundred thousand empty beds at that exact moment, because it's a seasonal place. And more than a hundred thousand seasonal residents are gone.\par \par \uc2 So effectively, Martha's Vineyard was the largest dormitory in the United States. But no, there is a housing crisis. "At some point, they're going to have to leave," meaning in like eight minutes, max.\par \par \uc2 So there was a guy who was the leader of a nonprofit group who stepped up to help the refugees and he had a different take. He said the problem wasn't the housing at all, it was the migrants, and we're quoting now: "It's like me taking my trash out and just driving to different areas where I live and throwing my trash there." This man told NBC News. Oh, they're trash. They're human garbage.\par \par \uc2 They're not even real. They have no souls. Do they have souls, the Venezuelans? Do refugees have souls? I don't know. There's like trash. Somebody dumped them on the front lawn. Let's give them Froot Loops and get them out of here.\par \par \uc2 Yes, call the truck. Have them hauled away.\par \par \uc2 NBC News actually tweeted out that quote, calling our beloved sacred immigrant community, these non-documented immigrants "trash" and then once they've realized that they revealed how they really feel, they deleted it a few moments later.\par \par \uc2 Now, not everyone on the left, we should tell you was so intent on sending these migrants away. Novelist, Jane Chittick, for example, offered this alternative: "I would love to see the Obamas open up their huge property and erect tents and look after all these people while they're being processed," she said.\par \par \uc2 Well, actually, you know, we're always in search of the one decent liberal left in America. Maybe Jane Chittick is the one because that's a pretty fair point, and \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  has a long documented history of having people on his front lawn.\par \par \uc2 In fact, he set up a tent city to house hundreds of people for -- let's see, oh, himself, his own birthday party. So, could he do that again? Of course, and he should because to do anything less than that \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  himself told us would essentially punish the people of Martha's Vineyard.\par \par \uc2 Mistreating immigrants would rob them of what makes America a worthy country. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: America is a nation of immigrants. That's our strength. And the notion that somehow we would stop now in what has been a tradition of attracting talent and strivers and dreamers from all around the world, that would rob us of the thing that is most special about America.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Right? So, if people don't camp out on your lawn, people have no right to be here in the first place. You're only hurting yourself.\par \par \uc2 But Obama, even on Martha's Vineyard, where approximately a hundred percent of people voted for him and love him, even in Martha's Vineyard, he could not make the sale. He could not get people who actually live there to agree with that.\par \par \uc2 They did do one thing though, they set a big \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2 , which they called an urgent plea, and they told us this was a way to help the migrants and we're quoting from \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2 : "Martha's Vineyard is a commu