hatever it takes to get you in the mood and prepare for some selections from "Dinners with Ruth" by Nina Totenberg.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEX MCCASKILL, PRODUCER (voice over): There is no way to transition to this next sentence, dear reader, but every time I took my seat in the Supreme Court chamber before a big argument, I would look around and think, there is not one man in this entire chamber I would go on a date with, much less kiss.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So Nina Totenberg was supposed to be working, but actually she was having naughty thoughts and she decided that no man in the Supreme Court could possibly satisfy her desires and that's when, one \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  enters the picture.\par \par \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  noticed a certain article of clothing, Nina Totenberg was wearing. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: The one item of clothing I remember her complimenting me on was a scarf that I mistakenly spent a huge amounts of money on.\par \par \uc2 I bought it in a rush, wore it, and then a bill for $600.00 arrived and I was shocked. I thought, "Nina, are you crazy? You don't buy a scarf for $600.00," but I couldn't take it back and it's beautiful and Ruth loved it every time I wore it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So Totenberg is almost 80 years old and she's still talking about clothes. Tell us how feminism is real. But real or not, \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  loved that scarf. So naturally, before long, the two went on an intimate shopping trip together. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: The woman who adored opera also understood that life is in its own way performance, and she was prepared to command the stage. That said, shopping with Ruth was a humbling experience.\par \par \uc2 She was petite and beautiful and could wear almost anything and everything she put on fit. Her sizes were two, and occasionally four. Mine hovered around 10 or 12, the first sizes to sell out.\par \par \uc2 I also could never have worn what she did. On me, most of her clothes would have looked ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So there you have Nina Totenberg fantasizing about wearing \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 's clothes. This is going -- whoa. Nina Totenberg's heart was pounding like a gavel during heated appellate arguments.\par \par \uc2 But it wasn't just \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 's tight fitting clothes that seemed just right, it was also her hair. Nina would give anything for that hair.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: Once at a dinner party, I sat next to a man who had been in her Law School class. "She was not just beautiful, but exotic looking," he said, adding that on the first day of the Torts class, when the Professor addressed her as "Mrs. Ginsburg," there was an audible groan from her male classmates.\par \par \uc2 That response may have been one of the reasons Ruth, wore her hair pulled back in a scrunchie for most of her life. She had glorious, luxuriant hair, but she pulled it back to keep it out of her face, and so that people would take her seriously. Only occasionally when she dressed up, would she wear it down."\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: "When she dressed up." Do you follow all of that? There were audible groans. There was luxuriant hair. They were exotic looking, beautiful people.\par \par \uc2 Nina Totenberg doesn't need to go to Court to tell you about all of this appeal. Of course, this is moving towards something, a grand conclusion, a climax, if you will. Here is Nina Totenberg once more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCASKILL: And then of course, there is lust. Young people may think it wanes later in life. They are wrong.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Lust doesn't wane with age. That's the message of "Dinners with Ruth." And for the first time in a long time, we believe Nina Totenberg on that.\par \par \uc2 Well, a House hearing went off the rails when a Republican member of Congress exposed a so-called climate lawyer, the whole exchange made Sandy Cortes of Westchester very angry. That's next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: The Biden administration plans to outlaw fossil fuels, they say it often, but they never explain how society is going to run without them. Hey geniuses, how's that going to work? No idea.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, at a House hearing, Congressman \uc2 Clay Higgins\uc2  of Louisiana had the chance to question a so-called climate lawyer on the topic. In case you weren't watching \uc2 C-SPAN\uc2 . Here is how it went.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. CLAY HIGGINS (R-LA): Everything you have, your clothes, your glasses, the car you've gotten on, your phone. The table you're sitting at, the chair, the carpet under your feet -- everything you've got is petrochemical products. What would you do with that? Tell the world.\par \par \uc2 RAYA SALTER, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, ENERGY JUSTICE LAW AND POLICY CENTER: If I had that power in the world, what -- actually, I don't need that power, because what I would do is ask you, sir, from Louisiana --\par \par \uc2 HIGGINS: I am giving you the power. You present it, it is impossible.\par \par \uc2 SALTER: To search -- you, sir, from Louisiana to search your heart.\par \par \uc2 HIGGINS: You've got no answer, do you, young lady about what to do with petrochemical products. So, I'll move on. What are you going to do with ocean-going vessels? What do you do with the maritime industry?\par \par \uc2 SALTER: Well, we could again, I would ask you to search your heart for what is happening on your coast of Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 HIGGINS: You have no answers for this stuff.\par \par \uc2 SALTER: Of course we do.\par \par \uc2 HIGGINS: You know what you've got, young lady. You got a lot of noise, but you've got no answers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So the woman is just like a flat-out moron. She has no idea. But Sandy Cortez rode to the rescue, whining as always, and she accused Congressman Higgins of being abusive to women. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): In the four years that I've sat on this Committee. I have never seen Members of Congress, Republican or Democrat, disrespect a witness in the way that I have seen them disrespect you today.\par \par \uc2 Frankly, men who treat women like that in public, I fear how they treat them in private.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, you're a wife beater if you want a simple answer to a simple question. But the Congressman is totally unintimidated, which is why we are grateful he's joining us now.\par \par \uc2 Clay Higgins\uc2  represents the State of Louisiana. Congressman, thanks so much for coming on. I mean, did you -- I'm just wondering what's going through your mind? You asked the basic question. How is society going to function without petrochemicals? Did you expect her to give you some answer?\par \par \uc2 HIGGINS: Well, I knew that her positions were insane. I mean, Miss Salters is a radical climate attorney, likely a friend of AOC's. This was in the midst of a week where we had to endure Committee hearing after Committee hearing where the Democrats were smashing the American energy industry and promoting the absurdities of the furthest reaches of the Green New Deal.\par \par \uc2 And their star witness after two days of hearings on the second panel, on the second day was this lady, Miss Salter. So, I had possession of her written testimony. I'd read it, I was prepared and I knew how crazy it read.\par \par \uc2 But then when she delivered it in her opening statement, she was quite vitriolic. She was practically spitting at us. And her anger was just unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 So I knew when I had the chance to, I was going to -- I was going to shut her down with their own craziness.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Boy, you did such a good job. Thank you for doing that Congressman Higgins of Louisiana. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Oh, we have so much more to say, but the clock has ran out. We will see you Monday.\par \par \uc2 Have the best weekend with the ones you love. Try to unplug if you can. We are going to.\par \par \uc2 See you then.\par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020220917ei9g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Democrats And Republicans Spar Over Relocating Migrants; Nighttime Footage Shows Severity Of Migrant Surge; GOP Scrambles On Abortion Messaging; Special Master Appointed To Review Documents From Mar-a-Lago; Demand For Oil In U.S. And Abroad; Gas Prices Could Rise Again In Fail; Pennsylvania Senate Candidates \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  And Dr. Mehmet Oz Will Debate One Time Two Weeks Before Election Day; Crime Spikes In Affluent Area Of Washington D.C.; Ukraine Uncovers Evidence Of Atrocities By Russian Forces In Recently Retaken City; Texas And Florida Republican Governors Bus Migrants To Blue States; Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  Criticized For Federal Abortion Ban Bill\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Alexandria Hoff, Jacqui Heinrich, Shannon Bream, Bill  Melugin, Rich Edson, John Roberts, Lucas Tomlinson, Jeff Paul \par \uc2 8,077 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Luckily he was treated for his injuries and is OK but congratulations to gesture (PH) on a long career.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: I love it and I love prosecutors who prosecute animal cruelty crimes.\par \par \uc2 All right, that's it for us. We'll see you back here on Monday. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Good evening and welcome to Washington. I'm John Roberts in for Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 America's crime crisis hits home in the nation's capital why you may be paying sky high gasoline prices again in the near future and the fight over mail in ballots weeks ahead of the midterms.\par \par \uc2 But first, breaking tonight, the growing controversy of Republican governors and now sub Democrats busing illegal immigrants to Democrat run northern locations.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, migrants were dropped off outside the vice president's home at the Naval Observatory here in Washington and at Martha's Vineyard.\par \par \uc2 Democrats call the moves cruel and inhuman political stunts. The administration has said to be exploring legal options.\par \par \uc2 Republicans say they're giving Democrats a taste of their own medicine.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich starts us off tonight from 1600 Pennsylvania. Hi, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you, John.\par \par \uc2 For a second day, the White House hammered Republican governors for moving migrants away from overwhelmed border towns, just as there are reports that some within the administration are saying it's time to do just that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): Reporters pushed out the Oval Office door, the president taking no questions after the press secretary compared the Republican governors of Florida and Texas to human traffickers.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: These vulnerable migrants were reportedly misled about where they were headed, told they would be headed to Boston. These are the kinds of tactics we see from smugglers in places like Mexico and Guatemala.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Biden's Justice Department weighing legal options to stop the governors from moving migrants to sanctuary cities and states.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GREG ABBOTT (R-TX): I'm the former Attorney General in Texas as well as a former Texas Supreme Court justice. I know the law very well. Biden himself was doing that very same thing. The only difference is he was moving people in the darkness of night.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Team Biden not confirming cabinet officials huddled today for solutions.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): It's only when you have 50 illegal aliens end up in a very wealthy rich sanctuary enclave that he decides to scramble on this.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Also no response to reports Biden turned down a DHS plan to move migrants to the Canadian border to relieve crowding in the south or that the agency is getting frustrated the White House won't take a page from the GOP governor's books.\par \par \uc2 But the second gentleman sounded off on migrants dropped off in front of the V.P.'s residence.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, I do have a reaction. I think it was shameful.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: And officials had plenty to say about who is to blame.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: Trying to fix a system that was decimated by the last administration.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Even though border crossings hit record highs under Biden, not Trump.\par \par \uc2 So, I just want to confirm, the way that this administration sees it ending Remain in Mexico or Title 42 had nothing to do with the surge that we're seeing.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: So, let me just step back for a second and lay out what we have done under this administration. More individuals encountered at the border will be removed or expelled this year than any previous year.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Last night, President Biden called the governor's moves reckless and un-American but both governors DeSantis and Abbott say they've got no plans to stop.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the 50 migrants who are flown to Martha's Vineyard are at Joint Base Cape Cod and the National Guard there has been activated to assist, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, Jacqui Heinrich for starting us off tonight at the White House. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We have new nighttime drone images showing us the amazing amount of traffic along the southern border even in the dark of night. Some groups contain dozens of illegal immigrants coming into the United States unopposed.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Bill Melugin reports tonight from Eagle Pass, Texas. Hi, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Hi, John, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 That's right, look, in just a couple of hours early this morning here in Eagle Pass, we saw well over 500 illegal crossings. This small little border town continues to bear the brunt of this border crisis every single day.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): For the very first time, a brand new Fox News drone equipped with thermal imaging captures images of mass illegal crossings in the middle of the night in Eagle Pass, Texas this morning.\par \par \uc2 Migrants could be seen crossing the river and walking onto private property were over 100 gathered and waited for Border Patrol processing. DHS says more large groups are encountered in Eagle Pass than anywhere else along the border.\par \par \uc2 Border Patrol's Del Rio sector which includes Eagle Pass has now seen over 450,000 illegal crossings so far this year. That is double the same time last year.\par \par \uc2 In Uvalde, Fox News was with Texas DPS troopers as they pulled over a human smuggler from Michigan. Hidden inside his trunk, two illegal immigrants from Honduras, all of them arrested.\par \par \uc2 And in Kinney County, Texas DPS troopers pulled over this van and were shocked when they found 16 illegal immigrants being smuggled in the back.\par \par \uc2 Texas Attorney General \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2  says President Biden's refusal to change course is telling.\par \par \uc2 KEN PAXTON\uc2 , ATTORNEY GENERAL OF TEXAS: The numbers under the Biden administration have tripled. And they've done it on purpose. They're not trying to protect the border.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: In Nogales, Arizona, CBP agents seized 276,000 fentanyl pills hidden in a smuggler spare tire as the car came through the port of entry there.\par \par \uc2 And Border Patrol agents in the Tucson, Arizona sector found five pounds of fentanyl strapped to the body of a 15-year-old boy at a Border Patrol checkpoint.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And John, the state of Texas has now deployed thousands of National Guard soldiers down to the border throughout this border crisis and we are standing in the spot where the body of one of those soldiers was pulled out of the river earlier this year after he drowned while trying to save the lives of what ended up being drug smugglers. The State of Texas says the true crisis is right here. Not on East Coast sanctuary cities. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, Bill Melugin for us in Eagle Pass. Bill, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Abortion may be a key issue in the midterms, one leading Republican senator raising eyebrows with the timing of his proposal for new federal abortion restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alexandria Hoff has got the story tonight from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The MAGA Republicans won't control the Congress. It won't matter where you live. Women won't have the right to choose anywhere. They want a national ban.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDRIA HOFF, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): President Biden's claim that the GOP wants a national abortion ban has left some Republicans on the defensive.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  introduced a bill that was not an outright ban, but it would ban abortion after 15 weeks with exceptions for rape, incest, and to save the mother's life.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): I am proudly pro-life and I have no apology for being pro-life and there's no bad time to defend the unborn.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: Graham's plan is in line with most European nations but doing it on a national level strays from Republicans who say it's a question that states should handle, as they argued after the Supreme Court struck down Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): I think most of the members of my conference prefer that this be dealt with at the state level.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: In politics, timing is everything. The bill was announced two months ahead of the midterm elections providing messaging fodder for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 REP. ERIC SWALWELL (D-CA): You can call it whatever you want Senator Graham, but it's a government mandated pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: Telling them that they have to carry a child to term that is forced birth, regardless of the circumstances.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: But Republicans say Democrats haven't been as vocal in saying if there are any limits to their pro-choice position.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 , a co-sponsor of Graham's Bill noted, Republicans have laid out their limit and Democrats have not.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): If you don't support any restrictions, then your position is that you support abortion on demand for any reason, at any time up to the moment of birth.\par \par \uc2 HOFF: The latest Fox News poll shows that 66 percent of voters favor at least some restrictions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOFF (on camera): President Biden has not directly voiced support for abortions performed at any time for any reason, the White House saying only that the President does trust women to make that choice, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Alexandria Hoff in the Briefing Room. Thanks very much, Alexandria.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Fox News Chief Legal Correspondent and anchor of "FOX NEWS SUNDAY" Shannon Bream. Great to see you again.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): You too, John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: So, we'll go into abortion in a minute because that ties in well with your fine program on Sunday. But first of all, let's talk about the appointment of the special master Raymond Dearie who's going to be looking through all of these documents that were seized at Mar-a-Lago, it's going to take him a while.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: It will and what has been a request is that he would prioritize about 100 documents that the DOJ says are classified. They want to be able to use those.\par \par \uc2 For now, Judge Cannon out of Florida says you can't use the products of any of these documents that you got from Mar-a-Lago until the special master goes through.\par \par \uc2 Now, the special master has asked everybody to come to a hearing next Tuesday in Brooklyn, and he says we'll talk about how this is going to roll out.\par \par \uc2 So, some action there. But of course, the DOJ we've been hearing is going to appeal the fact that there is even a special master. We are waiting to see if that comes in tonight. We'll let folks know as soon as that is updated.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Dearie has got about 11,000 documents to go through which is going to take him a while. So, that's going to significantly delay the investigation.\par \par \uc2 But my understanding is even though Cannon has said he can't use the classified documents right now. She has put them at the head of the queue for Dearie's review.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: So, do you expect the DOJ is going to appeal her decision?\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You know what, there are people of two minds about that, that we expected they would, because they signaled that very strongly upfront, but you're right, she has prioritized those 100 documents to say, all right, special master, let's get to those first, let's maybe clear up that issue. But we've had a lot of signals today that the DOJ was actually going to file an appeal. So, we're waiting to see if that comes to fruition.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Now, this isn't a presidential election year, in which there can typically be an October surprise, we've seen that many times. But there is a possible October surprise here in the midterms in that the January 6 committee is holding its final hearing on the 28th of September and may do an early release of findings and recommendations prior to November the eighth.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, it's possible. And I know that they are talking to a number of people. I know of at least one that's scheduled to talk to them after the September 28th hearing.\par \par \uc2 So, that makes you question whether there is additional material they're going to be collecting after that hearing.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the DOJ is still very active in what they're doing with respect to January 6th, there have been almost 900 arrests with them connected to people from that day.\par \par \uc2 But they're looking at a number of things, people who were involved with these conversations about alternate electors, people who were involved in the rally, people who were involved in fundraising, potentially for legal efforts for the Trump campaign.\par \par \uc2 So, they've sent out a slew of subpoenas over the last few weeks or so.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And then, there's the lingering question of whether or not they would seek to indict former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes. And he said that it would not overwhelm this country. He's been admonished a little bit for suggesting some hints of violence, absolutely not suggesting that at all. I'm just saying I don't think it will go down well with a good section of the American people.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right, to "FOX NEWS SUNDAY". And back to the abortion issue, because you have a marquee guests coming up.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: We do have an exclusive with Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  to ask him questions about why when this issue has been so tricky for the GOP, and they've argued including him that it should be a state issue that he's now proposing this federal ban.\par \par \uc2 A lot of folks think pollsters it's not good for the GOP. Others say, though, listen, it gives them a place to land to state their position.\par \par \uc2 And as Senator Rubio said, they've asked the Democrats to take a vote on just how far into a pregnancy do you think they're comfortable with an abortion.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Looks like you're trying to get both Democrats and Republicans on record.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: I would say yes, on record, not for votes, not for yes votes, but definitely on record.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Just where you stand on the whole issue.\par \par \uc2 Jared Bernstein, also from Orleans.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Yes, we're going to talk about the economy. Been a tough week for the White House, but he's a good sport to come and we'll talk about it.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: All right. Good to see you. We'll see you on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: You too.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Thanks, Shannon.\par \par \uc2 Well, stocks were down again today falling to two month lows on global recession fears. The Dow lost 139. The S&P 500 fell 28. The NASDAQ was down 104.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Dow finished off more than four percentage points. The S&P 500 lost 4-3/4, NASDAQ fell 5-1/2.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Bret Baier on why we may not have seen the last of record high gasoline prices. Say what you say.\par \par \uc2 And later, we will take you to one of the nicest areas of Washington D.C. and tell you why it might not -- it might no longer be safe to go there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): Energy Secretary \uc2 Jennifer Granholm\uc2  met Thursday with the governors of six northeastern states to talk about their concerns over soaring fuel costs heading into winter.\par \par \uc2 The governors wrote to Granholm back in July. Reuters citing an Energy Department's source saying participants in the meeting talked about ways to ensure adequate supplies of electricity and heating fuels in the wake of Russia's invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we begin a new series looking into the future of how we use energy in the United States. We will compare the costs and benefits of renewable options with the security and reliability of traditional methods.\par \par \uc2 As we head into the fall, there is speculation that gas prices could rise once again, similar levels that we saw over the summer.\par \par \uc2 Bret Baier looks into some of the factors that may contribute to more pain at the pump.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): Thanks, John.\par \par \uc2 The United States, Saudi Arabia, and Russia, are the world's top three crude oil producers. Despite producing almost as much oil as we need in the U.S., we have still imported from those two other countries.\par \par \uc2 Now, one nation has gone off the market in the U.S. and in other parts of the world, creating a global domino effect for energy prices. The Biden administration has also been pressuring to decrease domestic drilling and to try to find clean energy alternatives.\par \par \uc2 In a new series, SPECIAL REPORT spotlight "ENERGY MATTERS". Why going green domestically is causing the U.S. to shop for energy abroad, at least now.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We're banning all imports of Russian oil and gas and energy.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): When President Biden announced sanctions on Russia back in March, it was merely a drop in the bucket. Just three percent of U.S. oil imports came from Russia in 2021.\par \par \uc2 In Europe, energy sanctions against the Kremlin were a risky move.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): The E.U. is obviously feeling a real crunch, energy wise. Is there pressure internally not to do the sanctions?\par \par \uc2 KEVIN BOOK, MANAGING DIRECTOR FOR RESEARCH, CLEARVIEW ENERGY PARTNERS: Well, there is some pressure internally. The sanctions are in place right now. So, the question would be whether or not they're willing to modify them.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Several countries rely on Russia to supply most of their energy, but that did not stop the \uc2 European Union\uc2  from implementing sanctions.\par \par \uc2 URSULA VON DER LEYEN, PRESIDENT, \uc2 EUROPEAN COMMISSION\uc2 : Russia is actively manipulating the gas market.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: European sanctions include import bans for petroleum products and crude oil delivered by sea. The E.U. along with the United Kingdom have also banned shipping companies from providing Russian vessels with insurance.\par \par \uc2 That move prevents Russia from shipping through European waters and hinders its ability to deliver oil to other regions.\par \par \uc2 BOOK: This Russia insurance ban, it goes into effect on December 5th. But if you go backwards from when refiners who are buying Russian oil and need those cargo, they have to make decisions about buying them, six weeks probably beforehand.\par \par \uc2 So, that means that you could have a bid, pressure to the upside on prices for crude oil, and therefore, gasoline in October.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Now, Europe is preparing to look elsewhere for energy.\par \par \uc2 VON DER LEYEN: The best thing is to fortify yourself and go towards other suppliers and have solidarity.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But the United States may not be one of those suppliers. In August, Energy Secretary \uc2 Jennifer Granholm\uc2  sent a letter to U.S. oil producers, asking them to build inventories and limit exports.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GRANHOLM\uc2 , UNITED STATES ENERGY SECRETARY: We're watching what happens globally. But we are doing everything possible to try to stabilize supply and demand, to keep those prices coming down.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: To keep prices down, President Biden, in March, set a plan to release crude oil from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve. But the 1 million barrels per day over six months has sent reserves tumbling to their lowest since 1984.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Will should we be concerned?\par \par \uc2 PHIL FLYNN, FOX BUSINESS NETWORK CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): I think we should be. I think the Biden administration prematurely released oil from the reserve. But at some point, that oil is going to have to be replaced. And if it isn't replaced, it's going to lead the United States consumer vulnerable to price spikes.\par \par \uc2 There is going to be nobody left in the bullpen to come out and save the day.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): The White House has attempted to suspend new leases for onshore oil and natural gas, saying such actions contribute to climate change.\par \par \uc2 Instead, the president has sought additional energy from abroad.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I'm doing all I can to increase the supply for the United States of America. The Saudi share that urgency.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President Biden's plead to the Saudis was met with calls to increase production domestically.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): I think we should be producing our own energy here. He'll go get it from Saudi Arabia or Venezuela. I guess that's not global warming, but U.S. energy is.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): U.S. producers extract oil and gas in a far, far more environmentally friendly manner than many of their competitors overseas.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Relying on oil from Saudi Arabia could also prove unpredictable. As the world's largest oil exporter, sets prices based on its need for additional production.\par \par \uc2 Following President Biden's trip to Saudi Arabia, U.S. gas prices did lower from the record highs we saw over the summer.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We said not -- what I was doing would make any difference. And guess what, is down $1.30 since the start of the summer. That continues to go down.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But experts believe we haven't seen the end of high gas prices.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Do you see, and for the people you talk to an increase coming in October?\par \par \uc2 FLYNN: We do. And in fact, I think we're seeing one of the most significant risks of a winter price spike that we've probably seen in many, many years.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): And consumer costs could increase again, as soon as next month.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): Are they singing that tune a little too early, considering what may happen in October?\par \par \uc2 BOOK: Any president who tries to take credit for the oil price is probably going to be blamed for the oil price when it changes to the wrong side, which is to say to the upside.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Also in coming weeks, the midterm elections. Many Americans are saying inflation is a top issue for them as the Biden administration tries to balance energy costs with efforts to curb climate change. That will be a big issue at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 John, we'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: It certainly will be. Bret, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, mail-in ballots for the November midterms are about to go out. Why some people say that is way too early?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Arkansas Republican gubernatorial candidate and former White House press secretary \uc2 Sarah Huckabee Sanders\uc2  says she has undergone successful surgery to treat thyroid cancer.\par \par \uc2 Sandra says her doctor found the cancer during a checkup earlier this month. She says the surgery removed her thyroid and surrounding lymph nodes and says she's doing well.\par \par \uc2 We are now 53 days from the November midterm elections. North Carolina has its ballots ready for those choosing to vote by mail. State Republicans there say officials have failed to follow the law. Pennsylvanians will begin receiving their ballots Monday. But the only scheduled debate between Senate candidates is still weeks away.\par \par \uc2 Here is senior national correspondent Rich Edson on that.\par \par \uc2 RICH EDSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Voters in Pennsylvania will start receiving absentee ballots, Monday. The one confirmed debate between Senate candidates, Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Mehmet Oz is five weeks after that.\par \par \uc2 LT. GOV. \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 , (D) PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: All of my doctors on the team believes that I'm absolutely fit to serve.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Fetterman had a stroke in May. For the October 25th debate he will used closed captioning to ensure he understands the questions. Fetterman's campaign shared his cognitive test results with "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " showing he is functioning in the normal range for his age, though "The Times" reports Fetterman's campaign did not make his doctors available for interviews.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ, (R) PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: He has been dodging all questions, not just debate questions but those from the press and from voters which is, frankly, insulting in the democracy.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Oz says if Fetterman can campaign with thousands of supporters he should be healthy enough to debate now.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR\uc2 , "AXIOS": It does seem like he is trying to run out the clock as late as possible when some of these early votes have already come in that people have already cast their ballot.\par \par \uc2 EDSON: Fetterman's campaign says that the debate is two weeks before Election Day, and that in 2020, "83 percent of ballots in Pennsylvania were cast in the final two weeks leading up to the election. Not that Dr. Oz would know that in 20 he voted in New Jersey."\par \par \uc2 Another midterm fight in North Carolina, the Republican Party there is suing the state board of elections for delaying the deadline for receiving absentee ballots until six days after the election.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 EDSON (on camera): This year the absentee ballot deadline falls on Veterans Day when there is no mail delivery. Republicans in North Carolina are also suing over rules restricting how often election observers can move across different polls places. John?\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS HOST: Rich Edson here in Washington for us tonight. Rich, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Now to America's crime crisis. We focus on an upscale neighborhood in the Washington, D.C., area that has recently been under assault by a string of armed robberies and violence. Lucas Tomlinson shows us tonight from Georgetown.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 LUCAS TOMLINSON, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Across the nation's capital, violent crime is on the rise. Armed carjacking is up 74 percent, robbery is up 13 percent, and murder is up this year compared to this time last year, which ended at 20-year highs.\par \par \uc2 PARIS MIZRAHI, D.C. BUSINESS OWNER: They broke my last window, and by the time alarm company called me and I can get here, there was about 150 kids in my basement.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Some businesses in Georgetown, one of Washington's premier neighborhoods, are closing their doors during the day and opening by appointment only. One store was robbed of $100,000 worth of merchandise.\par \par \uc2 MIZRAHI: They are looking for trouble.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: It's not limited to Georgetown. About four miles away on Eighth Street alone, robbery has doubled in the past year where \uc2 Washington Commanders\uc2  rookie running back Brian Robinson was recently shot twice in the leg. No arrests have been made.\par \par \uc2 And you think crime is getting worse?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, especially in this area. I just moved here, and somebody is getting shot, robbed, every day.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: Longtime resident Michael Mills blames a breakdown in the family structure.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MILLS, D.C. RESIDENT: We have a lot of single parent mothers out here who don't have no father figure.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: This young mother wants to leave Eighth Street. She asked us not to show her face.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I feel like looking for a little bit a safer environment.\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON: The vibe is supposed to be hip on Eighth Street with high end apartments and restaurants. A barber who also didn't want to show his faces says none of that matters.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I have been in this area since the 80s. Crime has always been here. It's less, but it's not going to change.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TOMLINSON (on camera): Police officers tell FOX they are not to blame for the rise in crime in the nation's capital. They blame the courts for turning criminals loose after they are arrested a day later. John?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Lucas Tomlinson on M Street in Georgetown for us tonight. Lucas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next a live report from Ukraine as investigators there uncover more atrocities of war.\par \par \uc2 First, here's what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 5 in New York as the longest running show in Broadway history announces that the curtain is coming down for the final time. "The Phantom of the Opera" will play its final performance at the Majestic Theater in February. The musical will celebrate its 35th year on Broadway in January.\par \par \uc2 To FOX 29 in Philadelphia as investigators search for a suspect who was placing explosives inside mailboxes. It is unclear if any have detonated. The \uc2 U.S. Postal Service\uc2  says putting explosives in a mail depository could carry a 20-year prison sentence.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Chicago from FOX 32, one of the big stories there tonight, Riot Fest returns this weekend to Douglas Park. The three- day music festival features punk rock and alternative bands. This year's lineup includes My Chemical Romance, The Misfits, and Nine Inch Nails.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Lines of mourners waiting to file past the casket of Queen Elizabeth II are stretching for miles tonight. Wait times are up to 24 hours long. Officials temporarily closed those lines earlier in the day. Thousands of people paying their respects to the queen, including our own Martha MacCallum, who stood in line for eight hours. The queen's funeral is scheduled for Monday.\par \par \uc2 Iran's supreme leader is reportedly canceling all meeting and public appearances after falling seriously ill. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " report the \uc2 Ayatollah Ali Khamenei\uc2  had surgery last week after suffering extreme stomach pains and a high fever. He is said to be on bed rest and under observation.\par \par \uc2 India's prime minister is telling Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  that now is not the time for war. \uc2 Narendra Modi\uc2  spoke with Putin as a summit of Asian countries in Uzbekistan. Putin says Moscow is doing everything it can to end the conflict in Ukraine. He says Ukrainian officials have rejected negotiations.\par \par \uc2 Putin says that his troops will press on with their attacks despite a Ukrainian counteroffensive. Meanwhile, the grim process of recovering bodies from a mass grave is underway tonight in Ukraine. Officials say hundreds of people are buried in a pit in the newly liberated city of Izyum. This comes as the United States commits another $600 million to the effort to repel Russian invaders.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Jeff Paul is in Kyiv tonight. We warn you, some of the images you are you are about to see may be disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Left in a forest, buried in shallow graves, discarded without dignity. Prosecutor in a newly liberated Ukrainian city of Izyum saying this is the scene of the latest mass grave.\par \par \uc2 OLEH SYNEHUBOV, KHARKIV REGION GOVERNOR (through translator): All of us can only testify that the world has to acknowledge that this is a genocide of the Ukrainian people.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Investigators so far have discovered more than 400 bodies. Most are civilians, all carry their own story of Russia's brutal occupation.\par \par \uc2 DMYTRO LUBINETS, UKRAINIAN PARLIAMENTARY MEMBER (through translator): Next to me, mind you, is a whole family, a young one with the grandparents.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Much like the scene in Bucha, there are signs of torture. Some are reportedly found with their arms tied behind their back, others with rope around their neck.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: In many instances these will amount to war crimes, which I think is the appropriate frame through which to look at this. That work is going on. There needs to be accountability.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: For those who survive the occupation, recovery feels impossible.\par \par \uc2 Back in March, this apartment complex here in Borodyanka (ph) was hit by Russian forces, not only destroying the building but killing an untold number of people who called this complex home. And you can see, they haven't even started to rebuild it or their lives. So you think about what some of the people in those newly liberated towns and villages are going through, just the start of a very painful process.\par \par \uc2 A devastating pattern of Russia's war.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): Today the world must see what the Russian army left behind itself, another mass burial of murdered people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Investigators say since some have been buried since March, determining the cause of death for each case is not going to be a one-week job. They even use metal detectors while they're out at the burial site in order to find possible hidden explosives. John?\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: So much tragedy for the Ukrainian people. Jeff Paul tonight in Kyiv. Jeff, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on immigration, the midterms, plus Winners and Losers. It's Friday.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight, at least 10 people are dead after heavy rains and flooding in central Italy. Residents were forced to clear mud from the streets after torrential rain swept through several towns. Nearly 16 inches of rain fell within two to three hours.\par \par \uc2 Kyrgyzstan is accusing neighboring Tajikistan of killing at least 24 people and wounding 87. It comes just hours after the security chiefs of the two countries agreed on a ceasefire. \uc2 Kyrgyzstan's health ministry\uc2  reported clashes along the border killed two people and wounded 55 others. Tajik border officials allege that Kyrgyz forces were shelling border villages.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Munich. One of the big stories there tonight, Oktoberfest returns after two years of pandemic cancellations. Oh, they are celebrating. Inflation has even impacted beer prices, though, with one litter costing 15 percent more than it did back in 2019. The festival is still expected to give Munich's tourism industry a much need boost, though.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight, and we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: For mayors like me that are dealing with a crisis not of our making, and we're not a border town. We don't have the ability. We're not Texas.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Vice President Harris said that the border is closed. Is the border closed? Do you believe that the border is closed, or is it open?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's open. Not closed. The border is open.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The border is open. Do you believe that all the migrants believe that the border is open?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes, everybody believes that the border is open. It's open because we enter. We come in. Free. No problem.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How did you learn that the border was open to come illegally? How did you learn to come?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Because we see it on the news that everybody coming illegally. So we also do the same.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: The battle over busing illegal migrants across the country continues. Let's bring in our panel now to talk more about this, Kimberley Strassel, a member of the editorial board at "The Wall Street Journal." Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman and host of THE FIVE, and Olivia Beavers, Congressional reporter at "Politico."\par \par \uc2 Harold, let's go to you first, because what you heard that illegal migrants say who was bused to the doorstep of our Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  at the Naval Observatory would seem to stand in sharp contrast with her declaration on "Meet the Press" over the weekend that the border is secure, the border is closed.\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: First off, thanks for having me and happy Friday.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Of course.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Both of those statements can be true, John, but only one rings true, and it's clear that many across Latin and Central America and perhaps around the globe believe that our border is not secure and that it's open.\par \par \uc2 We played politics with the border for a long time, both parties. But we are at a critical moment now where I think a different kind of leadership, a serious leadership is needed. I would encourage and appeal to my party and to the Republican Party to do some things that are uncomfortable. We can continue to bat around this issue, bat this issue around. We deal with it every political season.\par \par \uc2 But the seriousness that voters want are, I think, a few things. One, we need a wall. Two, we need to invest in this region of the world, in this hemisphere. We have not had a serious immigration bill in 40 years, since \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was president. We need more immigration judges there. We need more DEA, ICE workers there on the ground, and we need to be supportive of them as we try to interdict the drugs and to ensure that people are not allowed to come into our country unless indeed they are entitled to, by entitled I mean if it's legal for them to do it. But what is clear, the border is not secure. And anyone that says that I don't believe will be taken seriously or credibly as we seek a longterm solution to this problem.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Kimberley, Harold's pronouncements there stand in sharp contrast to what this White House is saying. They are saying that the border is closed. We don't need a wall. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLEY STRASSEL, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": Well, first of all, I entirely agree with her that we do need longer and lasting solutions and that both sides are going to come together. But to your point, John, what we know from all immigration attempts in the past is that this has not happened unless have you serious leadership coming from the White House because it's an uncomfortable policy topic, and neither side really wants to embrace it unless they are pushed.\par \par \uc2 I would also, by the way, I just tip my hat. Some people are calling the decision by Governor Abbott and DeSantis to send some of these migrants to other places as a stunt. But, if so, it's been pretty brilliant politically, because look what we are talking about here now. Basically, they have co-opted the other side into helping them popularize and put out there the real burdens that come from this unrestrained flow of migrants.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I want to bring in Olivia here, too, because \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  was pretty strident in her words today, describing what both DeSantis and Abbott had been doing. Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: They use them as political pawns, treated them like chattel in a cruel, premeditated political stunt. These are the kinds of tactics we see from smugglers in places like Mexico and Guatemala. And, for what? A photo op? Because these governors care about creating political theater.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I don't think there is any doubt here, Olivia, that everybody is playing politics on this. But what constantly gets lost in the back and forth is the fact that more than 2 million people have come across the border this fiscal year so far, and it's not over yet. And that's a new record. And the White House doesn't seem to be addressing it in any kind of way that's relieving the situation.\par \par \uc2 OLIVIA BEAVERS, "POLITICO": You're right, John. And with the vice president basically saying the border is secure, whether or not they are actually trying to make that argument, most voters don't believe that. And public opinion shows that about maybe 58 percent disagree with how President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has handled the issue of immigration, while only 33 percent approve.\par \par \uc2 And Republicans are receiving polling, I have seen some privately that's not published. I have seen some that's public. They know immigration is an issue that they continue hitting Democrats as they approach the midterms. And this, as Kimberley just pointed out, the governors got the attention that they wanted. And now we are covering it, we are talking about it. Democrats are talking about it. And they are getting praise from Republicans on Capitol Hill, saying now you know what it's like to be one of these border communities that are having people influx, coming into our areas. And so, it's going to be something that I think we're going to be seeing more and more maneuvers, political maneuvers like this since this one has worked out for them.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Something else the Democrats are talking about anew is abortion after \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  on Tuesday introduced a federal 15-week abortion ban. It also caught the attention of \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the vice president, earlier today. First of all, let's play \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  reacting to the timing of it, because he came out with that the same time the president was knocked back on his heels because of inflation. And then we'll listen to the Vice President. Listen here.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC): I don't think there is bad day to stand up for the unborn. What I'm trying to say, that America needs to have a policy that makes us a civilized nation.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: These so-called leaders, extremist so-called leaders are passing laws to criminalize healthcare providers and punish women around our nation. They are calling for a nationwide abortion ban that would include Illinois.\par \par \uc2 (BOOS)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Let's go around quickly on this, because I want to save time for winners and losers. Harold, it would seem that Senator Graham has given the Democrats a new cudgel with which to beat the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 FORD: Well, I think the issue of freedom and choice and abortion is something on the minds of a lot of voters. Women, men, people think of this as kind of an issue that deals with suburban women. But I'm a father of an eight and seven-year-old. I don't think about abortion. I think about their freedom and the choices we want. And so I think it's something that reverberates with voters across the country. If Republicans don't get this back to their terrain, it's likely to help Democrats. And Democrats have got to get a better message on crime and the border to be competitive over the next seven weeks.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Kimberley, one of the big points that Senator Graham is making here is what are you for? And he's throwing that out to both Democrats and Republicans.\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: Yes, I appreciate that. I think it was well meant. But one of the problems here is it allows Democrats to misstate what he is actually doing. It's not a nationwide ban. And it trips all over Republicans' long-term argument that this should be left to the states.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Right. Some thoughts from you, Olivia, quickly?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: Sure, I was on Capitol Hill, and just like we are starting to see trickle out more publicly, Republicans have mixed opinions about \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , especially since this is a hot button issue, putting this forward right before the midterms. I had actually one Republican member going I want to strangle him for this because it put them on their back feet. So there is a lot of different emotions when it comes to how Graham has handled it.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Winners and Losers, Harold, you go first.\par \par \uc2 FORD: My winner is Sheryl Lee Ralph for reminding people always believe in your dreams, never give up. You never know. She won an Emmy over the weekend, or last weekend.\par \par \uc2 And my loser is Putin. It seems he is losing in Ukraine against maybe the wishes that even some in our own country, and he and the Eurasian, I should say the Asian coalition he has with Xi seems to be weakening as a result of that last meeting they had.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: The big question, what will Putin do in response? Kimberley winner and loser?\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: My winner is the hundreds of thousands of Britons who are standing for hours and hours in line to pay tribute to the queen and honor her service to their nation. My loser is all of those Senate candidates on both sides who have yet to commit to any debate even as ballots are now already going out for early voting. The voters deserve more than that.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And Olivia, to you?\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: My winners are the Oregon State Beavers. I think they have a great mascot and they are two and zero.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BEAVERS: Hey. And then I think that the losers are the people who decided to look at their 401(k)s and recent investment and tried to dip into those.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: I learned a long time ago, don't look. Leak it to Beavers and the Oregon Beavers. Panel, thank you so much. Good to talk to you. Have a great weekend. We'll see you soon.\par \par \uc2 It's Friday, which means it is time for "Notable Quotables."\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN, (D-WV): This bill will produce more energy. We'll drill more oil. We'll produce more oil. We'll produce more natural gas.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Since we came to office, we've created nearly 10 million jobs, a record for any presidency up to this point.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Sweet dreams and flying machines in pieces on the ground.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That's what the Fed has to do is get the economy to calm down. If they do it right, they will slow it down but not tip it over.\par \par \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 , (D-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: Your extraordinary leadership has made this glorious day possible. That's an applause line.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Russia has turned out to be Belgium with nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The border is secure.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's open, not closed. The border is open. We came illegally. Not legal.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: My name is John Fetterwoman.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERS)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Abortion should be not available on demand. There should be very limited compensations.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY) SENATE MAJORITY LEADER: They're not going to be able to run and duck and bob and weave and tie themselves in pretzel knots.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: How does it compare to the corvette?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: It's a beautiful car, but I like the corvette.\par \par \uc2 KING CHARLES III, UNITED KINGDOM: She set an example of selfless duty which, with God's help, and your counsels, I am resolved faithfully to follow.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And Monday on SPECIAL REPORT, the funeral of Queen Elizabeth II.\par \par \uc2 Thanks so much for watching SPECIAL REPORT. I'm John Roberts in Washington. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" is coming up next with guest host Pete Hegseth, and it starts right now. Hi, Pete.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220917ei9g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Migrant Buses Arrive Near Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Residence; \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  Defends \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Comments On Border Security; College Students Face Hidden Danger From Fentanyl; Republican Governors Send Migrants North; Biden Immigration Policy Faces Criticism; Justice Department Vows To Appeal Judge Cannon Ruling; Both Sides Await Judge Cannon Special Master Pick; Ukrainian Counteroffensive Takes Large Amounts Of Territory From Invading Russia Forces; Biden Administration Plans To Send Additional $600 Million In Military Aid To Ukraine; Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin Campaigns With Nevada Republican Gubernatorial Candidate On Education Issues; Polls Indicate Inflation And Abortion Likely Significant Issues For Voters\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Bill Melugin, Gillian Turner, Byron  York, Jeff Paul, Mark Meredith \par \uc2 8,022 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: We never had extra time. We have time today. Do you have anything to promote?\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I'll promote my show, 7:00, we have \uc2 Sarah Palin\uc2  on these migrant flights landing in Martha's Vineyard.\par \par \uc2 GERALDO RIVERA, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT-AT-LARGE: Tomorrow I will speak about the --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Geraldo, let that marinate. Let it marinate.\par \par \uc2 RIVERA: Sorry.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You already had about a minute.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And now we're going to be late. That's it for us, SPECIAL REPORT is next. Hi, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: We almost made it. I'm torn between backstreet dogs and reunited sloths.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh, the sloths is great.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right. Thanks, Dana. Good evening. Welcome to Washington, I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, a dramatic escalation in the battle between Republican governors and the Biden administration over border security and what to do with the hundreds of thousands of illegal immigrants and amnesty seekers who are coming into the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Today, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  received a direct challenge to her contention that the border is secure with a plane full of migrants landing on the Democrat frequented island of Martha's Vineyard, and busloads rolling in front of the vice president's residence here in Washington D.C., all sent by Republican governors who have pledged to make a point about the crisis they see at the border.\par \par \uc2 The White House called it inhumane to use migrants as political pawns. This comes as one of the byproducts of the border crisis, the opioid outbreak, the boom of fentanyl throughout the country hits home for returning college and university students.\par \par \uc2 We have Fox team coverage tonight. Gillian Turner here in Washington with the increasing danger from the fentanyl laced drugs. Jacqui Heinrich at the White House with the shift on the border messaging from the Biden team and reaction to the Republican governor's moves.\par \par \uc2 But we began with Correspondent Bill Melugin along the southern border as he has been. Tonight he's in Eagle Pass, Texas. Good evening, Bill.\par \par \uc2 BILL MELUGIN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Bret, good evening, to you.\par \par \uc2 Just a few days ago, V.P. \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  repeatedly insisted that the border is secure. And now, Texas Governor Greg Abbott says he's decided he has to bring the border to her. And early this morning, that's exactly what happened.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (voice over): Two migrant buses sent by the state of Texas arrived near the vice president's residence at the Naval Observatory this morning. More than 100 migrants mostly single adult men were released there with Texas Governor Greg Abbott tweeting in part "V.P. Harris claims our border is secure and denies the crisis. We're sending migrants to her backyard."\par \par \uc2 The migrants were predominantly Venezuelans with one of them telling Fox's Griff Jenkins, the border is not closed.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It's open, not close. The border is open. It's open because we enter. We come in, free. No problem. Yes, we came illegally, not legal.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: On Wednesday, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  sent two planes with migrants to Martha's Vineyard, prompting outrage from Democrats. Governor DeSantis responded today.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): We are not a sanctuary state. And it's better to be able to go to a sanctuary jurisdiction. And yes, we will help facilitate that transport for you to be able to go to greener pastures.\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN: In Uvalde, Texas, Fox News was with Texas DPS as they pursued this human smuggler, the smuggler and several illegal immigrants bailed out of the car and ran off into the brush. Texas troopers went in and arrested all of them.\par \par \uc2 And in El Paso, Texas, Fox News video shows several hundred migrants gathering under a bridge after crossing illegally as the city deals with a surge of illegal crossings. CBP facilities there are over capacity, as are local NGOs and Border Patrol has started releasing migrants on city streets. Local say the situation is out of control.\par \par \uc2 IRENE ARMENDARIZ-JACKSON (R) CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE OF TEXAS: You know, if the Democrats really cared about the Hispanic population in the United States, they would actually control the border and bring border security, it should be a priority.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MELUGIN (on camera): And Bret, late this afternoon, California Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  sent a letter to Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  asking the DOJ to investigate both Texas and Florida for their transportation of migrants all around the country.\par \par \uc2 Governor Newsom went as far in that letter as suggesting they possibly look into kidnapping charges. DOJ tells Fox News they are aware of that letter, and they have no comment right now. We'll send it back to you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Bill Melugin live along the border. Bill, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Now to the slight change in the language we're hearing out of the Biden administration about the border. White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich has that part of the story live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 The White House says if these Republican governors really cared about border security, they would be pushing Republican lawmakers to fund the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 . And they say the fact that Fox News got a heads up about the migrant arrivals but not DHS shows that this is all a political stunt in their words.\par \par \uc2 But team Biden is taking no blame for record border crossings under this president's watch. They are not telling migrants to stop crossing illegally and they echo the V.P.'s claims that the border is secure.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We agree with her.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The White House is backing up the vice president's claim that the border is secure. But when pressed, a shift.\par \par \uc2 Do you think Americans believe that the border is secured?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: What I can tell you is the record and the process that we have been using to make sure that we do our best to secure the border.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Isn't there some risk, though, that voters might feel like they're being lied to?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: So, again, it is a broken -- a broken system, the immigration system, and it was -- it was decimated by the last administration.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The White House says the Justice Department is in charge of efforts to stop Republican governors from busing migrants from overwhelmed border towns to sanctuary cities. And the vice president's home also saying it's on Congress to fix the immigration system. This is how the White House sees its role.\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: How do we stop this? We can call this out, they're using desperate people.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The president spent his afternoon with civil rights leaders.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: White supremacy, all forms of hate, fueled by violence have no place in America.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Republicans say their attention to issues voters really care about will show in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-FL): I think it's inflation. I think it's the border crisis. I think it's crime. Those are probably the three biggest thing I hear about from voters all across the country.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: A Fox poll shows Biden is not faring much better than his predecessor, 42 percent of voters saying they find Biden favorable to Trump's 41 percent. And Biden only does one point better than Trump on unfavorability too.\par \par \uc2 Biden's biggest problem still inflation but he says his administrations work to avert a national railroad strike means grocery prices won't get worse.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): So, the railroad strike could still happen if union members reject the deal that their leaders negotiated but Labor Secretary \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2  says he's confident that the vote will pass.\par \par \uc2 But the fact that the White House threw together this last minute rose garden event to make the announcement with the president shows that they're pretty eager to take a victory lap when they can, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich live on the North Lawn. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Attorneys General from several states are asking President Biden to declare fentanyl a weapon of mass destruction. That request comes as college and university students return to school amid an ongoing and increasing opioid crisis.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Gillian Turner shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MADELEINE DEAN (D-PA): It has been a weapon of mass destruction, of mass casualty, of mass death.\par \par \uc2 GILLIAN TURNER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): American college students are back on campuses across the country. This academic year they face a threat much greater than bad grades. Lawmakers are warning them about recreational drugs typically consumed by students laced with fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 REP. DAVID TRONE (D-MD): So, whether it's a WFT or not, it's just semantics. It's that level of an emergency.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): The bottom line is it is basically a weapon that kills\par \par \uc2 TURNER: At Ohio State, two students have already died from overdoses, prompting police there to warn of Adderall pills spiked with fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 This fall first your undergrads there are required to complete drug, tobacco and alcohol misuse education.\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, a Bloomsburg new student died from overdosing and three others were found unconscious. Police there say they suspected fentanyl laced drugs.\par \par \uc2 STACEY BRUNOZZI, NURSE PRACTITIONER, BLOOMSBURG UNIVERSITY HEALTH CENTER: What comes from outside areas. And that very much they realize that rural communities in America are being targeted for this type of drugs. There's a lot of fear.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: Bloomsburg is warning students about rainbow fentanyl. Brightly colored pills designed to look like candy.\par \par \uc2 And some schools are offering free Narcan, a medicine used to treat overdoses to students, no questions asked.\par \par \uc2 There's this dire warning from the Bloomsburg's P.D., they say taking off label drugs in today's environment is like "playing Russian roulette with a revolver with five cylinders loaded".\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TURNER (on camera): Out on the West Coast, UCLA students there have now formed a rapid response group to try and help save the lives of students overdosing on fentanyl laced drugs in real time. This model could well be replicated at campuses all across the nation, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gillian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KRISTEN WELKER, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Does the White House stand by those comments that the border is secure?\par \par \uc2 JEAN-PIERRE: What we stand by is that we're doing everything that we can to make sure that we follow the process that's been put forth. That's why we have historic funding to do just that to make sure that you know -- to make sure that -- to make sure that the folks that we encounter at the border be removed or expelled.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: White House Press Secretary today with a lot of questions about b order.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, three states in particular are dealing with this crisis in this way, Texas, Arizona and Florida.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the numbers, border states sending migrants north by the numbers and there you see the numbers to Washington, New York, Chicago and Martha's Vineyard.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the numbers for the Del Rio sector in July, the encounters were 49,563. Del Rio, Texas has a population of 34,000. Martha's Vineyard got 50 migrants today, year round population about 17,000 goes down in the winter.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Byron York Chief Political Correspondent, The Washington Examiner. Marie Harf, former State Department spokeswoman under President \uc2 Obama\uc2  and syndicated radio host, \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Byron, what about this? There is an element here that these are humans that are traveling the country. This is a political move, but it's also something that does get attention.\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Yes, a lot of Republicans had been worried. They were frustrated that the crisis on the border was not getting enough attention. And this gets a lot of attention.\par \par \uc2 As for the fact that these are human beings and should be treated humanely and should be taken care of if they are in the United States. You have to remember, the Biden administration has been moving illegal border crossers into other cities around the country.\par \par \uc2 We've received reports of airplanes landing in the middle of the night at Westchester County Airport. In New York, we've seen reports of dozens of flights arriving in Jacksonville, Florida.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, NBC reported that the Biden administration actually had a plan to relocate illegal crossers to many cities inside the United States.\par \par \uc2 So, I think what the border state governors have done is to just push this problem into the national spotlight where it cannot be ignored.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, every number you just showed of border crossers you've gone to, Chicago or to New York or Washington. All of those are less than cross the border in a single day.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, Marie, what about that point? You know, there's a lot of uproar about the 50 migrants were flown into Martha's Vineyard and the islands ability to handle them.\par \par \uc2 But Del Rio, Texas, dealing with tens of thousands, and other cities dealing with millions really over the years along the border.\par \par \uc2 MARIE HARF, FORMER STATE DEPARTMENT SPOKESWOMAN: The immigration system is broken. This is a crisis. I think there's a big difference between the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  moving people to different cities, and these Republican governors often not telling these migrants where they're going, lying to them about where they may be going, and not alerting any of the local authorities, whether it's DHS, whether it's some of the charities that can actually help them.\par \par \uc2 And also flying them to places like Martha's Vineyard, where they do not have an infrastructure like we do in Washington or other cities to actually assist these migrants. So, this is a political stunt. This is being used for sort of media attention purposes, in part because the issues they had been using for the past few weeks and months, namely inflation, and some other issues are starting to get a little better for the president. And I think Republican governors know that this is something they're based in voters care about. And they believe this as a political issue they can win on so they are using these migrants as political pawns. And it is cruel.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You I'm not sure inflation is going the president's way as of yet, but I will agree abortion is a -- is a topic that Democrats have jumped on. What about this and how you see it?\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST, SALEM RADIO NETWORK: I see it that governors Abbott and DeSantis did in one day, more for both highlighting the issues of political dynamic, but also putting a human face on these migrants.\par \par \uc2 So, I've been thinking about it all day long, because I do not believe in cruelty as a weapon. But I don't think it is cruel. I've been following the individual stories of the migrants, and they are speaking they -- were offered the voluntary assistance to move to D.C. and to Martha's Vineyard, they did it all by consent. They are no doubt better off where they are tonight than where they were a month ago.\par \par \uc2 But in one stroke, Governors \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  have put a human face not only on the migrants, but on the communities that received them without any infrastructure, Marie.\par \par \uc2 I live in Southern California, for most of the last three decades, there isn't an infrastructure to receive 2 million people, that is a myth. The border has to be defended.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, just respond to that. Some of them are here illegally. Many of them are here illegally. Others are seeking asylum, which is a legal process along the border, but they did so of their own volition, they could go home. So that element of this has to be a part of it.\par \par \uc2 HARF: Well, some of them will argue they can't go home, which is why they've tried to go through this legal process of getting asylum or coming to this country.\par \par \uc2 But the fact is, we have responsibilities for legal or illegal immigrants that show up to our borders, and we have a question about what to do with them.\par \par \uc2 And DHS is overwhelmed. CBP is overwhelmed. ICE is overwhelmed. These organizations are overwhelmed, they need the increased funding they're getting. There is no perfect solution. I'm not pretending that there is one.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I know, but to blame the Trump administration was pretty rich for the White House press secretary to go down that road and say it was the Trump administration that's caused this problem?\par \par \uc2 HARF: So, I think every administration has failed to solve this problem and the members of Congress sitting blocks from us today, Bret are the real ones who have to solve it.\par \par \uc2 The Trump administration did some things that made it worse, certainly, but every, every answer can't be that Trump did it worse.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here is a Fox poll voter concern for illegal immigration. Concerned, 62 percent. Not concerned, 37. Not surprised President Biden's approval on immigration, approved, 32; 60 percent disapprove.\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR (on camera): This problem has gotten so much worse in the past nearly two years, because ---\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Not -- let me interrupt you. Not only on the broad problem, but also on the kids issue has exponentially worse than it was when all of those lawmakers went to the southern border and said, this is inhumane and horrific.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 YORK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It's worse now.\par \par \uc2 YOORK: And it has gotten worse because in the Democratic primaries, and then with the victory of President Biden, the message has been sent that if you cross illegally into the United States, you will be allowed to stay and that is true for enough people to have motivated more and more people to cross.\par \par \uc2 Finally, this has exposed the hypocrisy of some leaders in American cities. The mayor of Washington, D.C. Muriel Bowser declared a humanitarian emergency, as to call at the National Guard to deal with an influx that is far smaller than the many border areas experience every single day.\par \par \uc2 The same for the mayor of New York City, the mayor of Chicago, \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2  kind of flipped out when a very small number came. So, this has really shown that they were simply not prepared to deal with something like this.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Is there backlash because of the way this is done, Hugh, in a political sense?\par \par \uc2 If you're going to be a sanctuary city, you're a sanctuary. But the way this is done by these Republican governors, is there a political backlash at all?\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: They are on a cliff? The backlash is very, very possible if there is any improvident abuse of the migrants involved. So, they're dancing on a cliff. They did. They've right up to it. They have got the attention they wanted, they've driven home their point, now they have to not do it too much.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel. Thank you. We'll see you tomorrow on the show to talk politics.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Worries about inflation fueled another big sell off on Wall Street. The Dow lost 173 to close at its lowest level in two months. The S&P 500 fell 45. The NASDAQ dropped 167.\par \par \uc2 Up next, former President Trump warns of a negative backlash if he's indicted over the Mar-a-Lago documents, and provides a new defense will bring you that.\par \par \uc2 And later, Ukrainian troops are pushing Russians back. Tonight, a look at the devastation that's left behind and analysis of what could come next there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Former President Trump, says his supporters won't stand for it if he's criminally charged over handling of classified materials found at his Florida residents.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with our panelists, radio host \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 , the former president challenged that the documents he had were not classified. This as we await another key ruling in the legal battle over the Mar-a-Lago raid.\par \par \uc2 Our latest polls, Fox polls indicate the public may not be as supportive as the former president believes. Just 26 percent say taking the materials was appropriate. 65 percent disagree. More than half believe the \uc2 FBI\uc2  raid was the right thing to do.\par \par \uc2 Among Trump voters, only 46 percent support the president's actions. 38 percent do not. But almost four out of five think the \uc2 FBI\uc2  raid was wrong?\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Correspondent David Spunt has latest tonight from the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doubling down in a new interview, claiming the documents seized from Mar-a-Lago were declassified, and he, "can't imagine being indicted."\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES (via telephone): I think if it happened, I think you'd have problems in this country the likes of which perhaps we've never seen before. I don't think the people of the United States would stand for it.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Hours later, swift reaction from Democrats on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): Inviting the mob to return to the streets is exactly what happened here in January 6th, 2021. His careless and inflammatory rhetoric has its consequences.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Trump's new comments come as the clock continues to tick for Judge Aileen Cannon. Federal prosecutors are giving her until midnight to reverse part of her decision last week, telling them to temporarily stay away from seized classified Mar-a-Lago documents.\par \par \uc2 Last week, while ordering a neutral special master to review more than 11,000 documents taken from Trump's Florida home, she told DOJ to wait until the special master is finished.\par \par \uc2 DOJ officials argued that could take months. If \uc2 Canon\uc2  does not reverse her previous position and issue a partial stay, DOJ promises to go to the 11th Circuit Court of Appeals in Atlanta and ask a randomly selected three judge panel to issue a stay, which if granted, would allow them to continue poring through the documents.\par \par \uc2 Six of the 11 judges on the 11th Circuit were appointed by Trump.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): Bret, now, we wait to see who Judge Cannon will pick his special master. With all of this fighting going on, the Department of Justice and Trump attorneys have actually agreed on a candidate for special master.\par \par \uc2 He is a federal judge named Raymond Dearie out of the state of New York. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David, related to the former president, we've learned his former chief of staff is now complying with the DOJ subpoena?\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: Bret, that's correct. We have learned through a source close to \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  that he has given over a lot of material, the same material that he gave to the January 6th committee over on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 We're told a lot of that material has also been withheld because Meadows believes some of it is covered under executive privilege. That source close to Meadows says the DOJ is not requesting any testimony from the former chief of staff at this time. Bret.\par \par \uc2 David Spunt, live from the Justice Department. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, a live report from Ukraine as Russian soldiers retreat, their brutality is revealed.\par \par \uc2 Plus analysis from retired U.S. Lieutenant General Keith Kellogg.\par \par \uc2 First, "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: (voice-over): Pakistan clears a key highway to help aid workers get supplies to survivors of recent flooding there.\par \par \uc2 Before the opening, the military had been forced to deliver goods by helicopter or boat. Almost 1,500 people have died so far. More than a half million have been displaced in Pakistan.\par \par \uc2 1000s continue to line up to see the queen lying in state. This comes as Buckingham Palace shares more details of the queen's funeral to be held Monday. 2,000 guests are expected to attend, including President Biden and the first lady.\par \par \uc2 Following the funeral, the United Kingdom will observe two minutes of silence.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Geneva, Switzerland. One of the big stories there tonight. Roger Federer, announces he is retiring from professional tennis. The 41-year-old closes out a career with 20 Grand Slam titles and five seasons ranked number one in the world.\par \par \uc2 He says recent knee surgeries have contributed to his decision, which he calls bittersweet.\par \par \uc2 To some of the other stories "BEYOND OUR BORDERS" tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A newly declassified U.S. intelligence report says U.S. officials predicted two years ago the Islamic State group would likely regain much of its former strength and global influence. Experts say the odds of that were particularly high if American and other western forces reduced their role encountering the extremist movement. Analysts say many of the judgments in the 2020 report appear to have come to pass particularly as the group is resurgent now in Afghanistan following President Biden's withdrawal of American forces last year.\par \par \uc2 However, the director of the \uc2 National Counterterrorism Center\uc2  says experts believe now that the terrorist threat to the U.S. homeland is less acute than it has been at any time since the 9/11 attacks.\par \par \uc2 President Biden plans to meet at the White House tomorrow with relatives of \uc2 WNBA\uc2  star Brittney Griner and Michigan corporate security executive Paul Whelan. The two Americans remain jailed in Russia. The separate meetings are said to be the first person, in person encounter between the president and the families. The administration said in July it had made a substantial proposal to get them both home. Griner was sentenced to nine years in prison after pleading guilty to drug-related charges. Whelan is serving a 16-year sentence on espionage charges.\par \par \uc2 Russian leader \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and Chinese President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  met today in the former Soviet republic of Uzbekistan and they continue their growing alliance aimed at countering U.S. influence.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we are seeing some of the results of Russian occupation in Ukraine. President Zelenskyy says a mass burial site was uncovered in a city recaptured from the Russians. Correspondent Jeff Paul reports from Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: They operate machinery that likely at one point aim to kill them. But beyond the Russian tanks now in the hands of Ukrainian soldiers in the newly liberated Kharkiv region, it's the devastation left behind by fleeing Russian soldiers that's leaving the biggest mark. Homes destroyed, water pipes ruptured, and an electrical grid on life support. But it's what's buried beneath the rubble that officials fear could surpass the horrors of Bucha. Some investigators are bracing to discover the bodies of up to 1,000 innocent civilians as Russian forces continue to strike from afar.\par \par \uc2 RAISA SOROKOTYAGA, IZIUM RESIDENT (through translator): The house is broken. The roof is full of holes. Water is running into the house. The windows are broken. There are no entrance doors. What kind of life is this?\par \par \uc2 PAUL: A day after touring the damage and getting involved in a car crash during his travel back to Kyiv, Ukrainian President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  didn't miss a beat as he welcomed the \uc2 European Union\uc2 's top official. As the White House prepares to announce a new military aid package, President Zelenskyy emphasized the need for more weapons.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): We all understand that air and missile defense systems are important. however, I am not sure how to solve this very quickly.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: Also trying to shore up support, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , who met with his Chinese counterpart, President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2 , in Uzbekistan.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR PUTIN\uc2 , RUSSIAN PRESIDENT (through translator): We highly value the balanced position of our Chinese friends when it comes to the Ukraine crisis.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAUL (on camera): The Chinese government later put out a statement not mentioning Ukraine but promising to support Russia's core interests. Meanwhile, we are learning more about that new military aid package. The Biden administration plans to send an additional $600 million to Ukraine, bringing the total amount of aid coming into this country since the start of the war to more than $13 billion. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jeff Paul live in Kyiv. Jeff, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Joining us tonight, retired \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  Lieutenant General Keith Kellogg. He was national security adviser for then Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . General, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. KEITH KELLOGG (RET), FORMER PENCE NATIONAL SECURITY ADVISER: Thanks for having me, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I want to put up this map of Ukraine regained territory. And the counteroffensive has been really impressive over the past couple of weeks. What do you see about where this is going next?\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Look, when you look at what they've done, it is pretty impressive when they took the area around Kharkiv and Izium. And that was a little bit, and give great credit to the Ukrainians, but what's happened is the Russians thinned their lined. And they thinned their lines because, to me, the culminating fight is going to be to the south. That's going to be around Kherson, the Mykolaiv-Kherson area, because the Ukrainians are going for that and the Russians know that, because if the Ukrainian can take Kherson and they move towards Crimea and they cut that line with Crimea, that is a strategic defeat for the Russians, because just last month, President Zelenskyy said the war began in 2014, the war began in Crimea, it will end in Crimea. He is very clearly going after that. He is very clearly trying to cut the Russians off.\par \par \uc2 And if he does that, Putin has got a major problem. He will have sacrificed the majority of his units. They pulled in about 20 battalion tactical groups in there, that's about 20,000 troops to fend that off. But they don't have the tactical ability to fight back. Their tactics are basically not responsive and not very capable in fighting this fight against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: For the people who were concerned in America about all of the money we're spending, $600 million announced more tomorrow, is it having an effect? And do you think it's going to get to the end finish line that we expect?\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Yes, I'm going to criticize the Biden government in this. They took their time getting it to them. They did it in piecemeal. Those systems they're using, like HIMARS, the multiple long range rocket systems, those are critical to the fight. We developed that program years ago and called it assault breaker. It was designed to fight the Warsaw Pact and the Soviet Union and to defeat those elements. We have piecemealed them out to them. We should have given them all that stuff up front.\par \par \uc2 There's an MRS brigade seated in Europe. Give them everything they need to fight. Don't piecemeal it out, because you don't husband artillery, you don't give it to them a little bit of a time. If they do that and they give them a lot more artillery support and they give them some air support, that will give the Ukrainians the ability to fight.\par \par \uc2 I don't think the United States has made a decision that they want the Ukrainians to win or not. We should be very clear, our goal is to help the Ukrainians defeat the Russian army in Ukraine and have them withdraw. Then the political side can take over after that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They definitely haven't said that, but as far as the Russian military, but it seems like a paper tiger. It really does. A number of stories, including "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," "Rapid loss of territory in Ukraine reveals spent Russian military. Troops and logistics issues plus equipment losses could limit \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to defending territory he already holds." It really seems like there was nothing there to this army when it began.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Well, what happened is General Gerasimov, who is their senior four star, he came up with the Gerasimov doctrine a few years ago, and they did away with the Soviet style doctrine where you had mass formations like the battle of Kursk, 6,000 tanks moving across the plains. They came with things on BTGs, battalion tactical groups, soldiers. They don't fight combined arms well. We in the America army do. They have disparate units, they're small units, they're not well led. They don't have the senior leadership we have in the American army. Everything you look at about the Russian army is flawed, and the Ukrainians have taken advantage of that.\par \par \uc2 This is truly a paper tiger. I hate to say this, I'm probably insulting some people when I say it. Russian has turned out to be Belgium with nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: OK, that will get a quote. General, thank you.\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A fireball streaked across the skies of the United Kingdom last night. There were more than 800 reports to the U.K. meteor network. Images of the object came from northern England, Ireland, Scotland. The network says it may have been a piece of space junk falling back to earth. We continue to investigate.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we go live to Nevada for a report on the governor's race there, and one governor who has made education and parental rights winning issues for Republicans is trying to do it again out there.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, officials in Mississippi say the water in the capital of Jackson is now safe to drink for most people. This comes after nearly seven weeks of being forced to boil their water before drinking it or using it to brush their teeth. A state health department official says households with pregnant women or young children should still take precautions because of led leaves previously found in some homes on the city water system. The mayor says Jackson cannot afford the major repairs needed on that system without federal help.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are postponing a vote to protect same sex and interracial marriages until after the November midterm elections. The move comes just days after Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  vowed to put the Senate on the record over the issue in coming weeks. Democrats have struggled to get the 10 Republican votes they would need to break a Republican filibuster. GOP lawmakers have raised increasing concern about whether the measure would protect the rights of religious institutions, business owners, or others who oppose same sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, the Republican who won a big victory in the Virginia governor's race by focusing on education issues is now trying to export that success. Governor Glenn Youngkin is in Nevada to try to help GOP gubernatorial nominee Joe Lombardo. Correspondent Mark Meredith reports tonight from Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE LOMBARDO, (R) NEVADA GUBERNATORIAL NOMINEE: I want to be considered the educational governor, all right?\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Republican gubernatorial candidate Joe Lombardo is promising Nevada voters if elected he'll address the state's teacher shortage and poor test scores, essentially embracing the same playbook Virginia Governor Glenn Youngkin used one year ago.\par \par \uc2 LOMBARDO: From the very beginning his campaign has been based on education. And that's what mine has been based on.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Government data shows Nevada ranked significantly below other states in eighth grade math and reading scores. And Youngkin, who campaigned with Lombardo across the state today, tells FOX that can change if Nevada empowers parents to choose where their children attend school.\par \par \uc2 GOV. GLENN YOUNGKIN, (R) VIRGINIA: This is why the progressive left is pushing so hard, because they know that the Republican agenda on education is the winning agenda.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Polls show Lombardo essentially tied with his opponent, Democratic governor \uc2 Steve Sisolak\uc2 . Today Sisolak slammed Youngkin's visit while also pivoting voters away from education to abortion. Sisolak's campaign telling FOX, quote, "Lombardo and Youngkin share a number of similarities, namely attempting to hide their antichoice beliefs from voters." But top Democrats insist they are not ceding the education issue. Judith Whitmer is the chairwoman of the Nevada Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 JUDITH WHITMER, NEVADA STATE DEMOCRATIC PARTY CHAIR: I think that we see more parent-teacher cooperation and collaboration as far as the school system goes. In fact, we encourage it. We want parents to participate.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: The results of the race may also predict Youngkin's future political power, especially in this swing state.\par \par \uc2 Are you laying the groundwork for a 2024 run?\par \par \uc2 YOUNGKIN: I'm going to work for Republican gubernatorial candidates.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): Some Democrats in Virginia continue to criticize Youngkin for doing these trips out of state. But there are no signs that he is planning to slow down any time soon. And a source close to the governor told me today they expect him to be in even more battleground states between now and November's midterms. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I'm not sure we got a 2024 answer there, Mark.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: I tried, Bret. There is always tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: There's always tomorrow. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on where things stand 54 days ahead of the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 40 in Sacramento as fire crews report major progress against a week-old blaze in northern California. It's the state's largest fire this year. Evacuation orders are still in place for about 11,000 residents out there.\par \par \uc2 This is a live look at Miami from WSVN, gorgeous night there. One of the big stories there tonight, a \uc2 Chick-fil-A\uc2  employee helps a woman being carjacked. A man wielding a stick approached her and demanded her keys. The employee heard the woman calling for help, raced out to physically confront the attacker. Other witnesses then helped hold down the suspect until law enforcement officials arrived on the scene. A salute to you all.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: MAGA Republicans are destroying American democracy.\par \par \uc2 The spread fear and lies, lies told for profit and power.\par \par \uc2 We are far more united than we're divided, but we have to focus on it.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TOM COTTON, (R-AR): Just a couple weeks ago in his remarks in recent weeks of declaring 74 million people who voted Republican last election semi-fascist are beneath the dignity of any president, but especially one who promised to try deliver unity in his inaugural address.\par \par \uc2 KARL ROVE, FORMER GEORGE W. BUSH DEPUTY CHIEF OF STAFF: Can the Republicans in the remaining weeks of the campaign make this a referendum on \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , or will there be other things that intrude?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, the countdown is on to the midterms. "New York Post" writes, "Biden drops hate to get out the midterm vote. Every sane person already opposes mass murder and vicious attacks on innocent people, so there will be nothing to learn from the White House summit on hate. But denouncing hatred is one of the best ways to spur hatred, especially if you identify the haters to include practically half of the U.S. population."\par \par \uc2 If you look at our latest FOX News poll, extremely or very concerned about, 89 percent inflation, higher prices. There you see the future of U.S. democracy. Abortion has gained, but it's at 70 percent in this poll.\par \par \uc2 We are back with our panel, Byron York, \uc2 Hugh Hewitt\uc2 , and Marie Harf. OK Byron, it does seem like there's this battle between these two big issues of the economy, inflation, and abortion, and threats of democracy.\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, "THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER": Yes. And if you look at the new FOX poll, like a lot of pollsters, they celebrate inflation and gas prices from jobs and the economy. But if you add those two categories together, because they are quite related, you get 34 percent versus 16 percent who mentioned abortion. That doesn't mean that abortion is not important. It is important, and it probably will make a difference in some House races. Republicans have to remember that this is what they wanted. For decades, they had been saying the issue of abortion should be decided by the political branches inside the states with legislators, and that's what's happening. And if they lose some of these contests, well, that's the way it works.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: When you look at these polls around the country, especially the Senate races, Democrats seem well-positioned right now, Marie. But is there any trepidation in the Democratic Party about these early polls and how they close come Election Day?\par \par \uc2 MARIE HARF, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Certainly, because the historical precedent, as we all know, is midterm years are bad for incumbents. So we know that is there. But this is a new political world we're in, and after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and also after Dobbs, right? Abortion has skyrocketed in terms of how much voters care about it. Women are registering to vote much more than men are. And not just in House races but in Senate races. Republicans did want this, but now they kind of don't want to own it. And when you have \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  proposing a national abortion ban and very few Republican candidates willing to embrace that because they know in many of these states, whether it's Nevada, whether it's Pennsylvania, that a majority of voters don't want that, they don't want what the Republicans are selling. I think that is going to help Democrats. Democrats are cautious, but they are more optimistic today than they were a month ago or six months ago.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hugh, whenever it's called a national abortion ban, the specifics are 15 weeks and restrictions after that. And 80 plus percent, maybe more, European countries are at 14 weeks. We would have no restrictions, and that question turned around becomes a different question for Democrats on the trail.\par \par \uc2 HUGH HEWITT, SYNDICATED RADIO HOST: If people learn to talk about it correctly, which is what limits will you accept? Are you having no limits, for example, on eight month abortions and nine month abortions? Immediately that number drops off. And so it is the skill of the candidate that comes in. Combativeness is a superpower in politics until it destroys you. You can't be combative about abortion. You have to be empathetic about abortion because it's a terribly difficult human situation.\par \par \uc2 But I have to remind Marie and all the Democrats who are watching, everyone has got to eat. And the worst thing to happen to Democrats was the 13.4 percent year over year inflation tick up from --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Food.\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: -- last week in food. Everybody is going to eat, and they're going to start voting in September, and they're going to eat from now until the election, and every time they go to the supermarket they think about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We have a piece tomorrow about gas prices and where that may go in coming weeks, and every indication is it's ticking up.\par \par \uc2 YORK: The new inflation numbers were particularly bad for Americans who have to eat to live.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That would be all.\par \par \uc2 YORK: I'm one of them, and I think everyone else is. As far as abortion is concerned, it is, though, going to make a difference.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Is there a race that you are watching particularly, Marie, just like focusing in on, and if this turns, this --\par \par \uc2 HARF: I think that Fetterman-Dr. Oz race is an important one because you have a very polarizing Republican gubernatorial candidate in Mastriano, Fetterman now saying he'll debate, but there's a lot at play in this race. His health is in question. Dr. Oz running a very bad campaign until recently, maybe gotten a little bit better. Pennsylvania is one of those states I'm watching because it is so close, these are two very different, interesting candidates. And you have this governor's race that's messing with all of it. So that's one I'm watching.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, but on that race, Hugh, can you imagine another candidate and this debate back and forth and getting the concessions it appears that he is going to get for a debate October 25th, just days before the election?\par \par \uc2 HEWITT: No, because I think Dr. Oz cannot be seen to say no. He has got to say yes. He's got to be compassionate and empathetic. He's done exactly what he needed to do. Anytime, anywhere, under any circumstances Oz will debate Fetterman. Fetterman is losing every day.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And the race seems to still circle around those issues, but in policy differences they couldn't be more apart.\par \par \uc2 YORK: They could not be more apart. And frankly, Republicans are amazed, I think, that this lead with Fetterman has been so durable because they look at Fetterman and say he may simply not be up to the job, or at least for the next period of time. They can't believe that Fetterman's support has been as strong as it has been.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I think we will talk over about the election over the next 53 days.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, guys, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a special day. Take a look at this, military mother returning stateside two weeks early, giving her kids a big surprise at school, serving in Kuwait and Iraq for over a year. \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  reservist takes off the hat, and there are her kids. I love these. First disguised as a mascot of the school. A big reveal in front of her daughter, glad to see her home and safe, that's great.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, we debut our newest series SPECIAL REPORT Spotlight, Energy Matters.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and still unafraid. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts right now. Hey, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220916ei9f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 White House Celebrates Dem Inflation Reduction Act; Biden Pushes Green Energy Agenda Amid Economic Crises; Some \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  Trains Canceled Amid Possible Strike; White House Scrambles To Avoid Rail Strike; Zelenskyy: We Will Come To Our Other Territories; Ukraine Blitz Sends Putin's Forces Into Retreat; China's Xi To Meet Putin For Second Time This Year; Stage Set For Crucial Senate Race In New Hampshire; Democrat Senator Takes Aim At GOP Challenger Over Abortion; Fetterman Agrees To Debate Oz On October 25th; Mourners Line Up For Miles Along London Streets; UK Pub-Goers Raise A Glass To Remember The Queen\b0\par\par\uc2 Martha MacCallum, Jacqui Heinrich, Karl Rove, Joe Concha, Bret  Baier, Hillary Vaughn, Gerry Baker \par \uc2 8,441 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News Channel: The Story with Martha MacCallum\par \uc2 HUNDD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TYRUS MURDOCH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, electricity only just came out. So, that's pretty cool.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Oh, man, no comment.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: I was wondering who he was referring to when he said an older gentleman. I just don't know.\par \par \uc2 MURDOCH: No, we're not losing you. We need you. We need you here.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thanks for joining us, everybody.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: Thanks, Tyrus.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I'm Sandra Smith.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS: And I'm \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 . THE STORY with Martha starts right now live in London, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MARTHA MACCALLUM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Thank you very much, you guys. Good afternoon, everybody. I'm Martha MacCallum live from London this evening. $900 million to build electric vehicle charging stations across the U.S., President Biden touting use of that big chunk of change just moments ago, but the Environment and Climate Change rank low on the long list of voter concerns right now.\par \par \uc2 At the top, the high cost of living and inflation, which as we know, continue to soar. Karl Rove, and Joe Concha, Sandra Smith, not going to let her off the hook, she's going to stick around for us today as well, and Bret Baier also joining us this hour, as the President insists that the economy is right on track and celebrates it with James Taylor at the White House.\par \par \uc2 First, we go to White House Correspondent, Jacqui Heinrich. Hi, Jackie.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Hey, Martha. Well, even though that new Gallup poll shows that voters are far more concerned with the economy than they are with climate. The President remains fixated on climate telling reporters ahead of this trip that he's not concerned about yesterday's hotter than expected inflation reading or the stock market sell off before announcing that $900 million for electric vehicle chargers on highways across 35 states.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: A great American road trip is going to be fully electrified, whether you're driving coast to coast along I-10 or on I-75 here in Michigan. Charging stations will be up and easy to find as gas stations are now. We're also going to invest $7 billion to make American car companies and have the batteries and other critical materials they need.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The White House wants half of all new cars sold in the U.S. to be electric by 2030, saying that monies from the Chips Act can produce necessary semiconductors here at home, but they can't say when those cars would actually hit the market for Americans to buy them. And then there's the issue of charging them. Energy Secretary \uc2 Jennifer Granholm\uc2  said that the U.S. should look to California as the model for clean energy.\par \par \uc2 Well, they just asked the residents not to charge their E.V.s amid a brutal heatwave. And moreover, buying an electric car is too expensive for most Americans dealing with inflation right now. One of the cars, the president test drove today, costs more than $63,000.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN BARRASSO\uc2  (R-WY): The religion today and the Democrat Party is a religion to climate, they worship, the Sun God, the Wind God, and the God of Wishful Thinking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Yesterday, the White House took some heat for celebrating the Inflation Reduction Act just as that new inflation reading showed a worrying increase in a key inflation metric, and then there's the inconvenient fact that experts agree that 