 MACCALLUM: Could be, we'll be watching and as I said, Bret Baier is coming up. We're going to dig into the midterm races as we are inside eight weeks right now. Karl Rove, great to have you with us. Thanks for singing for us tonight. Glad to hear your favorite James Taylor tune. And Joe Concha, thank you very much. Great to see you both.\par \par \uc2 CONCHA: Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROVE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: See you soon. So, as voters continue to grapple with higher prices, many are not bringing home more money. Sandra Smith has that side of the income equation, which is a troubling story in America right now. And Bret Baier is back with a big update on a battleground race with the potential to decide the control of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  narrowing in on these races that everybody needs to get a close look at. Bret's coming up.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We have a big story breaking right now \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  canceling long distance trains starting tomorrow, in case of this possible road strike -- railroad strike that has been really percolating over the last couple of days. It says that it's trying to avoid passenger disruptions.\par \par \uc2 The talks do not involve \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  or its workers is a dispute with freight railroads. One union already rejecting a tentative deal. Under federal law, the workers are not allowed to strike until Friday. That is a big story that could be very disruptive to the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 And a quick look at this as prices on everyday expenses sore from a year ago. The amount of money that the average U.S. household is taking in remains pretty much the same, unfortunately, on the income side, according to the new Census Bureau data. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And you can be saying, hey, I got this raise. Your raise means nothing, if you had to spend it all on milk, eggs, and bacon.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Your paycheck is bigger. It's just not big enough.\par \par \uc2 JOE SCARBOROUGH, CO-HOST, MSNBC: If you're holding down a job, and you get a raise, and you're still falling behind every month. Well, you're not sitting there singing, Happy Days Are Here Again.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, you're sure aren't. That's correct.\par \par \uc2 Sandra Smith, co-host of America reports joins us now. Sandra, thanks so much for staying around to talk to us this evening.\par \par \uc2 So, two really big stories here. Talk to us a little bit about this potential railroad strike. And what's the impact of these freight railroads if they -- if they do indeed go on strike.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA SMITH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR: And the story is very much intertwined. Right, Martha? Where we just had the head of the Manufacturers' Association on with us last hour, who said this is only going to serve to potentially exacerbate this problem with inflation in this country.\par \par \uc2 8.3 percent inflation, Martha. You look at incomes that have done basically this, as you just reported for the past couple of years, while inflation is doing this. Everything that American workers are earning right now, any additional earnings, any additional income, it is getting eaten up by this inflation picture that does not seem to be improving.\par \par \uc2 And you've got this ongoing meeting happening at the White House right now, where they're trying to strike a deal. You've got \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2  , the labor secretary, saying, that they're -- both sides are acting in good faith. Both parties are negotiating in good faith. They have committed to staying at the table.\par \par \uc2 Martha, these meetings started at 9:00 Eastern Time this morning. They have been in there all day. So far, also, as you just reported a short time ago, 4,900 members of the \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers\uc2  , they voted to reject the agreement as these negotiations continue.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this could potentially bring what is already a very challenging economic situation into this country -- this could bring this economy to a standstill. We are dependent on these workers to transport goods in order to keep these prices down or at least keep them where they are. This could only serve to continue to raise prices even higher from here.\par \par \uc2 It's a tough situation, Martha. And you know, the workers, they don't feel like they're being properly represented. Even \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  acknowledging there is no sick leave for these workers, and that's a problem.\par \par \uc2 A lot of the railroad engineers are saying, this isn't about the money. And it seems the White House is making this more about the money. It's not about the money, this is about work, life, conditions. Work conditions, when they're on the job. Being able to take time off when they want to.\par \par \uc2 Something is got to get done here by that Friday deadline, or we could see this inflation problem get worse in this country for sure.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Yes, it's so interesting, you know, you wonder about what the pressures are that are really at work here. We know that so many places have a labor shortage, they don't have enough employees right now. And that may be putting some pressure. We've seen it with pilots, that that's putting pressure on them as well.\par \par \uc2 But President Biden, Sandra, has always been very supportive of unions throughout his entire career. So, is he supporting this strike?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: We'll see what ultimately happens. All we know is that they're both -- in the White House right now, both sides are trying to hash out an agreement. They know the pressure is on and this White House is facing what could be huge political implications if they don't strike a deal, and further exacerbate the inflation situation in this country.\par \par \uc2 Martha, something that stuck out to me in that report where we were talking about U.S. incomes failing to grow for a second straight year in this country, looking at the poverty rates.\par \par \uc2 Guess where we saw the poverty rate rise the most? With the 65 years old and older age group in this country, and the economist in the Wall Street Journal noting that's very likely happening, because guess what, they're living on fixed incomes, many of them.\par \par \uc2 If you're living on a fixed income in this country, and you're dealing with 8.3 percent spike in inflation, year over year, that's hardly manageable. So, it's a really tough political situation for this White House, they've got to hash out a deal somehow, because people are living through real American economic pain right now. And that's why it was tough to see that celebration at the White House yesterday for a lot of folks.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, they are hopeful that they're going to bring down drug prices, but the -- you know, it's going to take a while. And that's a big issue, obviously, for that part of the -- for the population as well. That's what the White House is might be banking on in part for that demographic, but we'll see if it works.\par \par \uc2 Sandra, thank you. Great to have you with us. Good to see you tonight.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Amazing coverage from there. Martha. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Oh, thank you, Sandra. Thanks. Great to see you. We'll see you soon.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, coming up, President Xi ventures out of China for the first time since the pandemic. Now, set to meet face to face with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  in the coming days, as momentum in the war on Ukraine shifts, not Russia's way toward Ukraine where they've just raised a Ukraine flag over an area that was occupied by the Russians.\par \par \uc2 John Kirby joins me live from the White House right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: President Zelenskyy, watching his country's flag rise above the newly retaken town of Izyum today, part of more than 3,000 square miles Ukraine claims to have recaptured from Russia. Just enormous developments that are going on in this battle.\par \par \uc2 The White House calling what has happened a shift in momentum. But cautioning that war, of course, remains unpredictable. With that, we bring in John Kirby, National Security Council coordinator for Strategic Communications.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: John, great to have you with us. Thank you very much for being here today.\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, COORDINATOR FOR STRATEGIC COMMUNICATIONS, NSC: You bet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: You know, I'd like to start if I may, by playing a sound bite from President Zelenskyy in Izyum. And here is what he had to say. Let's watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE: We know that truth is on our side, though. So, it means that we will come. I don't know when, and nobody knows when, but we have plans. So, we'll come because this is our land, and it's our people. That's why we will come.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: If you couldn't hear that, John, and I know you don't necessarily have a monitor there. But he's talking about Crimea.\par \par \uc2 And he's saying we have plans and we will come. So, is the United States supportive of their effort to retake Crimea from Russia?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Crimea is Ukraine. And we've been very clear about that. We do not recognize the so-called annexation by Russia of Crimea.\par \par \uc2 And Crimea is part of Ukraine, and Ukrainian armed forces are fighting to defend their country, their territory, and Crimea is that -- is that territory.\par \par \uc2 So, the other thing I'd say, Martha is we are not sort of telling the Ukrainians what they can go after, or what they can't. We're not -- we're not dictating the terms of their military operations then. This is their war. This is their fight. We're doing everything we can to sustain them and support them in that fight.\par \par \uc2 But President Zelenskyy, as the commander in chief, he gets to determine what the military targets are, and how he's going to defend and support his own territorial integrity.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: But John, just going back in time, when the Russians invaded Crimea and took it over, we did not send any military assistance. In fact, there was a lot of criticism of the Obama administration that they were just sending meals ready to eat, and that we weren't sending them what they really needed.\par \par \uc2 So, is it the case that our stance has changed on that, that now we would we do want to militarily support the retaking of Crimea from Russia?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: No, there's no -- there's not a shift here. And I think it's a little unfair, the criticism of the Obama administration. They sent a lot more than just meals ready to eat. There was -- there was weapons supplied to the Ukrainians, as well as significant training efforts by the United States, the U.K., our Canadian allies, as well, and others.\par \par \uc2 But look, we're involved right now in this reinvasion, if you will, since February, by Mr. Putin. And we have been nothing but clear that we're going to continue to support Ukraine's military for as long as it takes. The President has been steadfast about that.\par \par \uc2 And we're also clear that this is their fight. This is their war, and they get to determine where their operations are going to be in, and what they're going to go after.\par \par \uc2 If they need information and context. We certainly provide that, Martha. But these are their decisions to make.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Well, President Zelenskyy has talked about their energy resources and using them to supply Europe. Crimea, obviously, is a strategic area for Russia. And I'm curious about the potential for great escalation if Ukraine tries to take back Crimea, and they have made, you know, obvious head win -- obvious advantage in the northeast part of the country.\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Right, right.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: This is, yesterday, I spoke with the former deputy Supreme Allied Commander of \uc2 NATO\uc2  , Sir General Richard Sherriff. And here's what he said about the possibility of this escalating.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD SHERRIFF, FORMER DEPUTY SUPREME ALLIED COMMANDER OF \uc2 NATO\uc2  : If Russia decides to go seriously, serious escalation, and that potentially it brings in the threat of nuclear weaponry. The \uc2 NATO\uc2  mindset needs to change. \uc2 NATO\uc2  really needs to be prepared for worst case, which is war with Russia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Do you agree that the \uc2 NATO\uc2  mindset needs to change considerably?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: We have been from the very beginning, Martha, in fact, you and I have talked about this many times. One of the concerns and the things we've been looking at from the very beginning is not letting this escalate with Russia, not making this about the United States versus Russia or the West versus Russia, or \uc2 NATO\uc2  versus Russia, and certainly not allowing this to escalate beyond the brutality that it's already been visited -- now visiting on the Ukrainian people. Because that's not -- escalation is not in -- certainly not in our interest. It's not in the interest of our allies and partners in Europe, and it absolutely wouldn't be in the interest of the Ukrainian people who have already suffered so much.\par \par \uc2 So, we're providing them material support, we're certainly provide them information and intelligence as needed but we are not doing their targeting for them. And we are not taking upon ourselves to determine what their operations are going to look like or what they're not. I mean, this is President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  's call. Nobody benefits, though, nobody, from this war escalating beyond, again, the brutality that it's already -- that have already occurred.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: All right. I want to shift gears for this last question that I have for you. And it has to do with President Xi meeting with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: We have seen that their energy relationship has been really beneficial to Russia. They're selling -- Russia selling more oil to China than ever before. We've also seen some beneficial credit card activity that China has helped Russia to get around, the sanctions so that Russia is not suffering, that much pain in this. They've also been buying land in the United States near some military bases. Is there any change coming on the policy for Chinese investment with regard to any of this, John,\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I don't have anything to speak to today, Martha, in terms of changes in our policies with respect to an investment. You're right that China and other nations continue to buy Russian oil. That's why we want to put a price cap on that oil so that Putin can't benefit from those revenues.\par \par \uc2 We haven't seen any indications that China is -- that -- well, they're not supporting the sanctions. We haven't seen any indication that they're violating the sanctions or giving material support to Russia with respect to Ukraine. Mr. Xi, sorry, and Mr. Putin can talk about their meeting and characterize it how they want. We obviously don't want to see business as usual with Russia by anybody.\par \par \uc2 And China has a choice to make. They have a choice to make about whether they're going to join the rest of the international community here and rightly condemning this action, and taking action to hold Russia accountable or not. And so far, we haven't seen them actually do anything materially to hold Russia accountable.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK but last question. Given this kind of growing relationship, which they've talked about a lot over the past several years, is it a national security threat to not restrict Chinese investment in the United States and Chinese business dealings that affect us?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: I think the President has been very clear, even in his discussions with President Xi, that we're mindful of unfair trade and economic practices by China. I'm not going to speak to this particular issue today. I don't have anything to announce or speak to with respect to Chinese investment here at home, but it is something that we're watching closely and monitoring.\par \par \uc2 And we, you know, we refer to China as the pacing challenge, because not just economically but from a security and military perspective. They continue to provide or present challenges and threats to us in the Indo Pacific Region and around the world that we're going to stay focused on.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So the President is not concerned about the Chinese buying farmland, for example, in the middle of the heartland of the United States?\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: No. Look, of course, it's a matter of concern. I just don't have like a policy decision to speak to today, Martha. But obviously, it's a concern and we've been nothing but honest about and consistent about our concerns with Chinese trade and economic practices.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: OK, thank you so much. Good to talk to you. A lot of big topics going on all around the world, so, John, thank you very much. John Kirby joining us tonight from the White House and we thank him for doing that.\par \par \uc2 So coming up, I will speak to Bret Baier on what's being called MAGA's New Hampshire sweep and a big update out of Pennsylvania where Democratic Senate candidate \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  has now responded to growing calls to debate Republican challenger, Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So there is now a declared winner in a contest that could help decide control of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  , Retired General 