g that question around, saying to Democrats, are you for abortion in 15, 17, 20 weeks, it becomes a much stickier issue when you get to the campaign.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: Absolutely, I think that's absolutely right. Let's talk a little bit, before I let you go, about this breaking news, because we're just getting an update on \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2  , the Labor Secretary, and these discussions that are going on with the rail unions now going into Hour 7th on this. Where is this headed, Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Well, the Biden administration is really trying hard to avoid this strike. It is a national rail strike potentially, the first in 30 years. We're showing a picture of \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  there. \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  is not directly in the negotiation but they would be affected because of the railways being affected. And, you know, there's a possible implications down the road for all kinds of things, not only moving people but also moving a product across the country.\par \par \uc2 So, the administration is really trying double time to get this to work. But you're right, seven hours of negotiation, no breakthrough yet.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: President's always been very supportive of the unions all throughout his career. And this is a tough situation. We'll see what comes of it, especially given the supply chain problems that really fired up inflation early on in this process. Bret, great to see you. Thank you so much. We'll see you soon.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, bye, Martha.\par \par \uc2 MACCALLUM: So, mourners are now paying their final respects. We see the lines still moving behind us overlooking Westminster Hall this evening. And many of them, thousands of them have had an opportunity to go in and pay their final respects to Queen Elizabeth II as she lay in state at Westminster Hall.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: She has that magic about her, much more than normal people. She's an icon of icons. So I must at least 