alm during the whole thing.\par \par \uc2 She is President of Live Action. She joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Lila Rose, thank you for coming on and for showing how you really do win an argument not by drowning the other person out with rage or shouting him down, but by calmly saying what you really think. I think, you win people when you do that.\par \par \uc2 ROSE: Well, thanks, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I'm fighting and we're fighting for something really beautiful, it is human lives. And so, it's -- and what is there to be, you know, worked up over? It's the evil of abortion.\par \par \uc2 But at the end of the day, the truth sets people free. So, when we are equipped with the truth to share that and if I go on a show like "Dr. Phil" and sharing facts that are, you know, about scientific development of human life in the womb, facts about the evil of abortion. The facts speak for themselves. So it's a powerful message that I'm privileged to get to share.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: You never dismiss, I noticed the suffering of the young woman, or any woman who is pregnant and doesn't want to be. There is obviously real suffering there. She feels helpless and terrified.\par \par \uc2 But you also point out that abortion, killing this child hurts the woman, too and maybe that's why there is so much rage around this issue.\par \par \uc2 ROSE: I think, you're spot on, we're actually about to launch Can't Stay Silent, which is a white paper, but also a series of many videos, testimonies of women who've had abortions and have the courage afterwards to speak out about how devastating abortion was for them.\par \par \uc2 The metadata proves that there are mental health risks to women who have abortions afterwards, in the year after having an abortion, 150 percent more likely to commit suicide.\par \par \uc2 So, that message is part of the pro-life message to say, listen, don't do it. Choose life. There are people that want to help you and life is worth fighting for.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: And if you cared about women, as they say they do at Planned Parenthood, you would care about that, but they don't care about women.\par \par \uc2 ROSE: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: They care about more abortions.\par \par \uc2 ROSE: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Of course. Lila Rose, I appreciate your coming on and your calmness. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ROSE: Thanks, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So Gina Carano was a pretty big actress in Hollywood. She worked for \uc2 Disney\uc2 , but then she made the mistake of saying what she thought about COVID and the 2020 election. She wasn't very political, actually, but they tried to crush her anyway. She is unbowed. Joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Gina Carano was a pretty famous mixed martial artist and she became an actress and became even more successful. She appeared in films like "Deadpool" and "Fast and Furious." For a while she was on the \uc2 Disney\uc2  show, "The Mandalorian," but then her career hit a brick wall. Why? Well, she was fired because she had heterodox opinions on facemasks and the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 She wasn't particularly political or certainly not a partisan, but they crushed her for it. But she's unbowed. We sat down with her for a brand new episode of "\uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  Today." Pretty amazing interview.\par \par \uc2 Here is Gina Carano explaining how she responded when \uc2 Disney\uc2  told her she had to give her pronouns.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GINA CARANO, ACTRESS: When I wouldn't hashtag trans rights and I wouldn't put my pronouns in like, they kept on pressuring and finally, I just said, "I'm not -- I'm not doing this," you know, like, I'm not like, what are the pronouns? I didn't even know what people were talking about. I was just not clued in to the pronouns thing, which apparently been -- that's been happening for like, seven to eight years now, right? It's been happening before.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: But no one has ever explained its purpose.\par \par \uc2 CARANO: Nobody. No. I didn't know.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: You're just supposed to know.\par \par \uc2 CARANO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: And if you don't know you're a bigot.\par \par \uc2 CARANO: Yes. Well, then I put beep-bop-boop in my --\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Beep-bop-boop.\par \par \uc2 CARANO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, that, if I'm translating that just means middle finger to you, right?\par \par \uc2 CARANO: Well, that just meant, I can put anything I want in my bio. I can do whatever I want. That's all that was.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Because you thought it was a free country.\par \par \uc2 CARANO: Yes. Because I thought it was okay. Yes. I was speaking out about lockdowns, mass vaccinations, and pronouns way before anybody was even, like saying a word about it. And so I was a little bit ahead.\par \par \uc2 I'm sure if I would have done that now, it wouldn't have been a big deal. But because I was talking about this when nobody else was talking about it, especially nobody else in my industry, it was like \uc2 Lucasfilm\uc2  called me and they were like, okay, we're going to need you to get -- go on a Zoom call with 45 of our, you know LGBTQ community and we have to get you on the phone with these people we need to watch -- we need to have you watch all these trans -- these movies about the trans lives, documentaries.\par \par \uc2 And I was like, okay, I'll watch your documentaries and I'll talk to your people, but I'm not going to get on a Zoom call was 45 different people who can have their phones out videotaping me and I feel like that's extremely abusive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: We've just become fascinated in the last two years by bravery and where it emerges in people you would never expect it to emerge in. That's a brave woman and a really interesting conversation.\par \par \uc2 It's on "\uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  Today" tomorrow 7:00 AM on FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 So, the unelected Governor of New York, speaking of democracy, \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  is finally standing for election in November. \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  is running against her. He is catching up to Hochul in the polls and it is obvious why, because he's talking about things that people actually care about, not global warming, no, or equity, crime, which is completely out of control.\par \par \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  is a Member of Congress. He is now a candidate for the Governor of New York. He joins us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thanks so much for coming on. So a lot of Republicans across the country have decided to talk about things other than crime. Why are you talking about it?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R-NY): It's the number one issue. It's the reason why New York leads the entire nation and population losses, people feel like their safety is under attack, their wallets, their freedom, quality of their kid's education, and they believe that if they go down to places like Texas or Florida, and some of these other places that their money will go further, they will feel safer, they will live a freer, that's why New York leads the entire nation in population loss.\par \par \uc2 Now, we're seeing a debate in in Illinois and these other places about whether or not they should have cashless bail and get more DAs into power who refuse to enforce the law.\par \par \uc2 Well, in New York, we actually have this. We have the pro-criminal laws already passed. We're seeing the consequences of it. We have the DAs like Alvin Bragg, refusing to do his job. We even have some lax Judges refusing to do theirs, while law enforcement is getting entirely thrown under the bus.\par \par \uc2 So, even if you're a Democrat, even if you're an Independent, we're finding that people who have for their entire lives have voted Democrat, and they've realized that in order to save their city, if they live in New York City, if they want to save their state, that you need to have some balance and commonsense. You need to get tough on crime in order to be back in charge of your streets.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, exactly. But \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  cares deeply about safety. I remember, she is the one who want us to wear shower -- masks alone in the shower for safety reasons. Why isn't she bringing safety to New York streets?\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: You know, well, she has just been pandering to the left, whether it's tax and spend policies or these pro-criminal policies. We have cashless bail. She signed a bill called Less is More to release people early from prison who should remain behind bars.\par \par \uc2 They implemented something on April 1st called the Halt Act to limit the use of solitary confinement corrections facilities. But this isn't solitary confinement, like what you see in the movies, and what's happened over the course of these last few months is that attacks on corrections officers, on prison staff, and other inmates have gone up. They've skyrocketed.\par \par \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 's big solution is, well, why don't -- instead of calling them inmates, we could call them incarcerated individuals. Problem solved. We have people using drugs. Well, maybe we need more legal injection sites. You want to give out permits for more cannabis licenses. They're giving front of line access if you have a prior drug conviction.\par \par \uc2 You just have a lot of New Yorkers scratching their heads feeling like this is going very much in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Well, it's the end of civilization and anyone who is pushing violent crime and narcotics should not be running anything, and so we are firmly rooting for you, Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  of New York running for Governor of New York. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ZELDIN: Thank you, Tucker.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: So, we hear daily that the insurrection that took place on January 6th was worse than Pearl Harbor. Our question: Is anything more dangerous than an insurrection? There might be one thing, and we'll show you what it is, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Insurrection, insurrection, insurrection. Nothing is more dangerous than an insurrection. Oh, but that's not true. What is more dangerous than an insurrection? \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ditching his Secret Service detail and driving his Corvette back to the White House, they almost let that happen today for real. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: You've got go to all the way, everybody. This thing flies.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN, FIRST LADY OF THE UNITED STATES: Just push the button. Watch out.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I am trading my -- the head of my Secret Service detail. I am driving to him.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: He says he's driving home.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Yes. Given what he has done to the country, I think everyone can agree even Mrs. Biden, not a good idea for him to drive a motor vehicle.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us tonight. We'll be back tomorrow night, 8:00 PM. The show that is the sworn enemy of lying, pomposity, smugness, and groupthink. Have the best night. Here is \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST, "HANNITY": By the way, that is the Corvette Z06 that there is a waiting list a mile long, and I am like at the bottom. That is a great car, and no, he should not be driving that car.\par \par \uc2 If you can get one. I will pay a premium, Tucker. Good luck. All right.\par \par \uc2 CARLSON: Thank you, Sean.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document TCARL00020220915ei9e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Concern Over Inflation As President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  Touts Electric Vehicles; John Fetterman-Mehmet Oz Debate Set For October 25th; Racial Gap May Affect Georgia Election Outcome; Stunning Disclosure About Role Of Key \uc2 FBI\uc2  Informant In Trump Russia Probe; Stocks Plummet After Worrying Inflation Data; Consumer Prices Rise Unexpectedly; Social Media Executives Testify Before Congress On Security Of Their Platforms; Biden Administration Announcing Efforts To Crack Down On Expanding Criminal Enterprise Of Human Smuggling; Hundreds Of Thousands In United Kingdom Watch Procession Of Casket Of Queen Elizabeth II Followed By Royal Family; Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  Introduces Legislation For Federal Abortion Ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Mike Emanuel, Charles Watson,  Jonathan Turley, Chad Pergram, Gillian Turner, Greg Palkot, Katie Pavlich,  Harold Ford Jr., Brit Hume \par \uc2 7,606 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: Mom Ricki (PH), she was in a huge rush. She usually makes peanut butter and jelly but she didn't have peanut butter. So, she just put butter and jelly on the sandwich for little -- it's Ave and his mom Ricky. So, that's what happened. It was a butter and jelly sandwich. Yes, I agree, terrible sandwich.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That is the greatest fact check ever.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL LEGAL ANALYST: Yes, she should have done cream cheese.\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST: That's it for us, SPECIAL REPORT is up next with Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): Butter and jelly. No good. All right. Thanks, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Welcome to Fox News World Headquarters in New York just down from "THE FIVE". I'm Bret Baier.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, we have just released Fox polling indicating just how seriously Americans are taking the inflation crisis. 59 percent say they are extremely concerned about higher prices that suppress -- or surpasses worries about the future of U.S. democracy, abortion policy, and higher crime rates.\par \par \uc2 President Biden's apparent disconnect on inflation and the economy was in focus today in Detroit. The president appearing at the annual Auto Show to promote expensive electronic vehicles -- electric vehicles as Americans struggled to make ends meet on basics such as food and electricity with the inflation concerns.\par \par \uc2 It does follow yesterday's celebration by the president on the South Lawn of the White House touting what he called progress against inflation, despite the hard evidence to the contrary from his own federal government.\par \par \uc2 White House Correspondent Jacqui Heinrich starts us off tonight live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you, Bret. Well, today the average cost for an electric vehicle is nearly double the average American's yearly salary and yet, the president is focused on the health of the planet as a new Fox poll shows most Americans are far more concerned with what they can afford to eat for dinner.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I'm telling my Secret Service detail, I'm driving home.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice over): The president says he felt optimistic behind the wheel of a $63,000 electric vehicle.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We're choosing to build a better America and America is confronting the climate crisis.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Americans may not be as energized, more than a third saying in a new Fox poll, either inflation and high prices or the economy and jobs are their biggest reasons to vote. Climate not even making the list but the president dismissed the worrisome inflation report and the stock market sell off that followed on his way out of town.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Are you worried about the inflation number though, sir?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: No, I'm not. The stock market doesn't necessarily reflect the state of the economy as you well know.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Republicans are paying attention.\par \par \uc2 RONNA MCDANIEL\uc2 , CHAIRWOMAN, \uc2 RNC\uc2 : This is the number one issue we're seeing in our polling, the economy and inflation. Its death by a thousand cuts for a family who is getting hit at every single level. It's everything and Democrat policies cause this.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The White House implementing tax credits hoping half of all new cars sold in the U.S. will be electric by 2030. But industry leaders say good luck finding one that meets strict domestic content requirements. The White House can't say when semiconductor chip spending could bring them to market.\par \par \uc2 And then there's the issue of charging them, Biden announced 900 million to build Evie charging stations on highways across 35 states after Energy Secretary \uc2 Jennifer Granholm\uc2  called California the nation's model. Their residents were just told not to charge their electric cars amid a brutal heatwave.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN BARRASSO\uc2  (R-WY): Your religion today in the Democrat Party is a religion to climate. They worship the sun god, the wind god and the god of wishful thinking.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: But Biden's not shifting his messaging, telling Americans they can save some money by spending it on green goods and then Uncle Sam will give you a tax rebate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): And that will not help with groceries still up nearly 14 percent and the White House is acutely aware that it could get worse if a railroad labor dispute isn't resolved before Friday, and a nationwide strike happens for the first time in 30 years. And the White House is holding daily meetings with FEMA, the Department of Defense and other agencies planning to move food energy and health products by plane, truck and waterways if necessary, while the Labor Department brokers talks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Negotiations nine hours so far. Jacqui Heinrich live at the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 In tonight's Midterm Watch, Democrats continue to lead Republicans in congressional vote preference are just released. Fox poll indicates Democrats have a 44 to 41 percent edge among all registered voters.\par \par \uc2 However, it's tied among voters who say they are certain to participate in the November elections 45 percent each.\par \par \uc2 This evening, the increasing scrutiny from a formerly fawning media of Pennsylvania Democratic Senate nominee Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . He's facing Dr. Mehmet Oz in the November election for Senate in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Now, Fetterman is getting new questions about his health and his personal finances. Here's Chief Washington Correspondent Mike Emanuel.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MIKE EMANUEL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CHIEF CONGRESSIONAL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Amid growing calls for Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  to prove he is fit to serve in the Senate, Fetterman now says he'll debate Republican opponent Dr. Mehmet Oz on October 25th, two weeks before Election Day.\par \par \uc2 But the two campaigns are bickering over logistics. Dr. Oz tweeted his campaign is accommodating a closed captioning request for Fetterman. And he argues that is going to slow everything down.\par \par \uc2 So, Oz wants it to be a 90 minute debate. Fetterman counters that Oz is trying to move the goalposts and he tweeted Dr. Oz, see you on October 25. Dr. Oz has pressed for five debates.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R), SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF PENNSYLVANIA: Listen, either you're healthy enough to debate because your team keeps saying you're healthy or you're lying about your health. Either way, we need to know the truth.\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL: Fetterman suffered a stroke in May and did not campaign for three months. Oz has made it a flashpoint in the race.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D) SENATORIAL NOMINEE OF PENNSYLVANIA: I truly hope for each and every one of you. You didn't have a doctor in your life making fun of it, making light of it or telling you that you're not fit to be serve. No? But unfortunately, I do.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EMANUEL (on camera): A group that leans to the right called Foundation for Accountability and Civic Trust is asking the Senate Ethics Committee to investigate whether Fetterman failed to disclose assets in his personal financial disclosure.\par \par \uc2 County records reveal Fetterman owns eight properties in Braddock, Pennsylvania valued at about $95,000. Fetterman has criticized Oz for not being transparent about the number of homes he owns, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Republican Senate Candidate Chuck Morse has conceded his primary race to Don Bolduc in New Hampshire. Bolduc is a retired Army Brigadier General who some establishment figures in the GOP feared was too far to the right to win over independents and moderates in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 Bolduc now faces incumbent Democrat \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  who has been labeled one of the most vulnerable senators this November.\par \par \uc2 Now, let's head to Georgia where Democrats are struggling to attract the same robust black vote that helped flip that state blue in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Charles Watson is in Atlanta with that story tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES WATSON, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): Georgia Gubernatorial Candidate \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  is fighting to hold on to the black electorate that helped to nearly elevate her to the state's highest office in 2018. This time around has been shaky for Abrams.\par \par \uc2 According to a Fox News poll, her support among black voters fell as low as 78 percent in July, she's made some gains since polling at 91 percent among black voters in the new Quinnipiac poll, but it's still three points short of her election day numbers four years ago, according to Fox News exit polling.\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (R), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE OF GEORGIA: I need you all to say that you trust me. If black men vote for me, I win this election.\par \par \uc2 WATSON: Her biggest challenge may be black men who supported 12 points from her first gubernatorial bid according to a Fox News poll. Abrams is hoping to fix that downshift with outreach specific to that demographic, and she's enlisted the help of popular entertainers like Charlamagne Tha God and Rapper 21 Savage.\par \par \uc2 In a statement the Abrams' campaign said in part, our intentional outreach to black voters specifically black men should not be viewed as a challenge but instead as an opportunity for us to earn the votes of a community.\par \par \uc2 Incumbent Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , on the other hand seems to be reaping the benefits. Compared to 2018, he's nearly doubled his support from black men. The Kemp campaign put us in touch with one of his supporters. A conservative talk radio host, who says Kemp's pandemic response is one reason the governor is gaining traction.\par \par \uc2 SHELLEY WYNTER, ATLANTA TALK RADIO HOST: When the governor opened up the state, that benefitted men because we want to take care of families, we want to go to work. We don't want the government to tell us to stay at home and wait for a check to come once a month.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATSON (on camera): Statistically speaking, the Georgia gubernatorial race is tight as is the Senate race between incumbent \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and Republican challenger \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . They too have agreed to at least one debate, they'll meet next month in Savannah, Georgia, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Charles Watson in Atlanta. Charles, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks were up today after the worst day in two years on Tuesday, the Dow gaining 30, the S&P 500 finished ahead 13, the NASDAQ rose 86.\par \par \uc2 We told you last night on the show about new activity from Special Counsel \uc2 John Durham\uc2  in his investigation into the origins of the Trump Russia probe.\par \par \uc2 Durham's office is now going after a suspect accused of helping concoct a false narrative designed to cripple the 2016 campaign of then candidate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Joining us now \uc2 George Washington University\uc2  law professor Jonathan Turley. Jonathan, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 This filing from Durham, it did provide some info that we learned about this case that they're getting ready to go forward with. In simple terms, what are we learning?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN TURLEY, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CONTRIBUTOR: Well, we've learned a great deal from Durham much more than we actually received in some of these aspects from the Mueller report. He's already disclosed a great deal of how this Russian collusion case really was established the funding of the Clinton campaign of the dossier and the Alpha bank allegations, the use of their lawyers to fund these efforts.\par \par \uc2 But this focus is not on those lawyers and not on Steele, but who helped Steele put together the dossier, the critical character that played that role.\par \par \uc2 It turns out that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  not only paid Steele for a period, but then they paid this individual to collect this information, or to share his knowledge about possible Russian collusion. So, he was on the Russian payroll.\par \par \uc2 Now, the problem is that the American intelligence believed that the Steele dossier was a likely source of Russian disinformation. And so the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was effectively paying someone who's been accused of bringing in information that might have been disinformation from the Russian intelligence services.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is paying Igor Danchenko. And in the meantime, the Clinton campaign is paying to get this information into the bloodstream, and it's getting into the bloodstream.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: It is, they were very successful. What's interesting is the Clinton campaign denied that they funded the Steele dossier. That denial was even made into Congress when investigators asked campaign officials in the presence of their General Counsel Marc Elias.\par \par \uc2 And reporters later accused Elias and others of hiding that fact. But what we have now is a much more complete picture of how they did get this scandal into the bloodstream, how they worked the media, how they worked friends in the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . And now we find out that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  not only funded Steele himself or actually paid Steele himself rather than funded, but also paid his main source.\par \par \uc2 Now, the key here is that the Steele dossier has never been established as true. And American intelligence quickly through flags up that they thought this could be Russian disinformation coming through the dossier, which was funded by the Clinton campaign.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right, and you start to see how this all comes together here in making this case against then candidate Trump and then soon to be President Trump.\par \par \uc2 Last thing quickly, where do we think this goes? For the people who are frustrated by the time it took Durham to move things forward? And the people who are always frustrated that investigations never seem to end with anything? What does this end up being? A big report that spells everything out?\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Well, there is a hope that there could be a report that will help clarify this because every filing by Durham, we've learned critical facts that we didn't have before.\par \par \uc2 You can also see why Durham was so motivated in this investigation. There were stories about his unhappiness with the inspector general reports that did not include some of this information.\par \par \uc2 We don't know if he's preparing other cases. He has this trial next month, and to try to secure these convictions. What we don't know is whether he has anyone else that he is considering indicting or whether he plans to release a report.\par \par \uc2 I think that there is a great need for a single report for him to bring transparency to what has happened because there was clearly a great deal left out of the Mueller report.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jonathan Turley, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 TURLEY: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, our Common Ground segment on the economy, and later, mourners file past the casket of Queen Elizabeth II as it rests in Westminster Hall. We'll take you live to London.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): It's time now for our weekly segment, "COMMON GROUND", where we put people together who may be on opposite sides of the political spectrum or different sides of an issue, and see where they agree and find common ground.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, \uc2 Jason Furman\uc2  the chair of the Council of Economic Advisers for President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . And \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2 , the chair of the Council of Economic Advisers for President Trump.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): Gentlemen, thanks for being with us. On this "COMMON GROUND", you guys have pictures up there in the White House hanging next to each other for different administrations.\par \par \uc2 But on this week. Jason, you and Kevin see inflation pretty much the same way. Is that right?\par \par \uc2 JASON FURMAN\uc2 , FORMER CHAIR, WHITE HOUSE COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: Consumers have gotten some relief in the last two months because gasoline prices keep falling. That's good news. That's going to help people. But if you look at the underlying inflation rate, the type of inflation that's likely to last and persist, that's been stubbornly really high, and that's what it's going to take a big effort to bring back down.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kevin, both of you have been kind of sounding the alarm about inflation for some time.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN HASSETT\uc2 , FORMER CHAIRMAN, WHITE HOUSE COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: Yes, that's right. I think, Jason, and I started at about the same time. And usually, the kind of model that Jason uses to think about things is a little bit different than the kind of my model I use.\par \par \uc2 And sometimes we have big disagreements about tax cuts and stuff. But on inflation, both of our models have basically been saying the same thing for about a year and a half.\par \par \uc2 And the fact is that as Jason says, sadly, inflation is very high. And it's likely to abate a little bit because of declining energy prices. But I'm not sure that will stick through the winter.\par \par \uc2 And the Feds going to lift rates a whole bunch more than just three quarters point next week, if they're going to get ahead of the curve out inflation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jason, what does that look like, you know, as the Fed lifts rates, and how does that affect our overall economy as you see it?\par \par \uc2 FURMAN: Look, one of the biggest levers the Fed has, is raising mortgage rates. When they raise their interest rates, and when they're expected to raise their interest rates, mortgage rates go up. That's why mortgage rates have gone up so much this year, that's cooling the housing sector. That is, unfortunately, good news for the economy as a whole because it helps bring down inflation. But it can be tough for households too.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know as we look at issues that motivate voters heading into the midterms.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kevin, our new Fox News poll has inflation high prices at the highest at 19 percent. Abortion has ticked up to 16 percent. But inflation is still on people's minds. And really that's the case pretty much every election.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Yes, I think especially this one, you know, Mark Sandy's team did a calculation this week with the Consumer Price Index came out that the typical American family has to spend $460 more a month, this year compared to 12 months ago, just to get the same amount of consumption they had last year.\par \par \uc2 And so, if you haven't had a $5,000 raise, then you're behind. And that's why people are so upset about it, because it's really affecting their lives in a palpable way.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jason, the common ground is that the problem, sometimes the differences is how you fix it. Do you think there are big differences on both of your sides about how to fix inflation?\par \par \uc2 FURMAN: Yes, and look, there is we're an economy where some things are problem inflation. Other things are great, jobs is the number three on the list. You just showed 3.5 million jobs added just so far this year. That's twice the pace you'd have in a normal year. And that was done in just eight months. So, there are good things in the economy. And there are bad things in the economy.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the solution to the bad thing in the economy. It's largely the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's responsibility, they were assigned that responsibility. They have the tools to deal with it. But I do think Congress and the president could help by you know, cutting the deficit, rather than adding to the deficit.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. And, you know, politics obviously factors into all of this. To the president, the administration seems slow at the beginning. Take a listen to this kind of evolution and where they think they are now as far as dealing with it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think you'll see it change sooner than quicker than more rapidly than it will take than most people think. Every other aspect of the economy is racing ahead.\par \par \uc2 The price of some things go up -- went up last month, the price of others went down by the same amount. The result, zero inflation.\par \par \uc2 A single most important legislation passed in the Congress to combat inflation. And one of the most significant laws in our nation's history, in my view.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Obviously, the common ground is that it's a problem. But it says look at that Inflation Reduction Act, Kevin, and say that that is going to be a catalyst for reducing inflation is where a lot of, you know, at least lawmakers split up on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Yes, you know, I would say that Jason highlighted the deficit reduction is -- yes, would help right now on inflation. And I think that, you know, probably, if you look at the Inflation Reduction Act, that people will, you know, historians will look back and say it's not really named accurately.\par \par \uc2 I think if Jason and I were to sit down and develop a fiscal policy to try to help the Fed right now, it would look a heck of a lot different than that.\par \par \uc2 But you know, the political realities are that it's really hard to get stuff through the Senate. And a lot of times there are a lot of compromises made that makes something look irrational at the end.\par \par \uc2 And so, I kind of understand what the Democrats were doing. I don't like the bill very much, but it doesn't reduce -- it's not going to reduce inflation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jason, a lot of talk about --\par \par \uc2 FURMAN: (INAUDIBLE)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Go ahead. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 FURMAN: Guys, it's reducing the deficit. You may not like the way it's reducing the deficit. We may have different priorities. But on net, the \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2  says it's reducing the deficits. So, I think it provides a little bit of help. And, you know, every little bit --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But Jason, the only problem is, is that any help that it gave was erased by the student loan forgiveness.\par \par \uc2 FURMAN: Yes. So, I'm not a fan of the student loan forgiveness.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FURMAN: I think that was poorly targeted and poorly designed. So, no debate from me on that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And maybe that's also a common ground, Kevin?\par \par \uc2 HASSETT: Yes, it did. And I think that, you know, my colleague out here, John Cochrane, at the Hoover Institute, has a book coming out about how inflation takes off, when people decide that, you know, fiscal authorities aren't serious about, you know, basically balancing the budget and paying back the debt.\par \par \uc2 And I think that, you know, looking at the Biden administration policies this year, you could say that, that would be a reasonable interpretation that they haven't prioritized debt reduction.\par \par \uc2 Though previous administrations as well, and you could say that. But I think that what needs to happen if you want to help the Fed do this without a really hard landing is the Congress needs to have a deficit reduction package. Sort of, like Gramm-Rudman.\par \par \uc2 And if they don't do that, then the Fed is going to have a lot more work to do.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes. Last word, Jason. I mean, this soft landing, is it possible looking at what has to be done by the Fed to deal with the rising costs?\par \par \uc2 FURMAN: Look, it's certainly possible. Is it plausible? Is it the most likely outcome? Probably not. But I do think the Fed is in the right place now. I think they really understand the problem. I think they're acting vigorously.\par \par \uc2 And so, they're giving our economy the best chance it can have. And, you know, Congress and the president should do what they can to help the Fed, which is the frontline actor on this inflation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Jason Furman\uc2 , \uc2 Kevin Hassett\uc2 , thank you both for being here on "COMMON GROUND".\par \par \uc2 FURMAN: You bet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): Up next, our social media platform such as \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and TikTok, posing a threat to national security? Tech executives argue their case before Congress.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Justice Department is accusing three Iranian citizens of staging cyberattacks in this country. The assaults targeted power companies, local governments, small businesses, and nonprofit organizations, including a domestic violence shelter. Prosecutors say the hackers encrypted and stole data from victim networks and threatened to release it unless exorbitant ransom payments were made. The hackers are not believed to have been working on behalf of the Iranian government but instead for their own financial gain. The Justice Department says it believes the suspects are still in Iran.\par \par \uc2 Some of the top figures in social media are defending their platforms against allegations they threaten individual users and our collective national security in the U.S. The executives appearing before the Senate Homeland Security Committee today. Congressional correspondent Chad Pergram shows us tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: -- and nothing but the truth.\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Big heat on big tech.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 GARY PETERS\uc2 , (D-MI) SENATE HOMELAND SECURITY COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: There is a clear connection between online content and offline violence.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Senators want social media firms to explain how their algorithms work spreading potentially dangerous content. The firms even stumped former social media execs.\par \par \uc2 ALEX ROETTER, FORMER TWITTER ENGINEERING SENIOR VICE PRESIDENT: We actually don't know what's happening on these networks today.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ROB PORTMAN, (R-OH) SENATE HOMELAND SECURITY COMMITTEE: It's unfortunate that the American public must waited for whistleblower disclosures to find out about ways in which platforms are knowingly and unknowingly harming their users.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: \uc2 Twitter\uc2  whistleblower \uc2 Peiter "Mudge" Zatko\uc2  warned senators that the firm is vulnerable to foreign spies working there and tracking Americans.\par \par \uc2 PEITER ZATKO, FORMER TWITTER SECURITY CHIEF: I have not tweeted since I have left. I think people should look at the information they are getting off of it differently.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Zatko says \uc2 Twitter\uc2  values profit over security and follows everything.\par \par \uc2 ZATKO: What's the phone number? What's the latest I.P. address they've connected from?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: And it's not just twitter. There is particular concern about Chinese controlled TikTok and the data it can access.\par \par \uc2 GEOFFREY CAIN, LINCOLN NETWORK: TikTok, therefore, is a disaster waiting to happen for our security and the privacy of our citizens.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Security officials warn lawmakers to stay off TikTok because of the breadth of personal info the company siphons up.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITT ROMNEY, (R-UT) SENATE HOMELAND SECURITY COMMITTEE: I question whether we should allow an authoritarian regime to have a social media capability of the scale they have in our country.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  wants to