ver a year.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The border is secure. But we also have a broken immigration system.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: The White House insists the crisis is Former President Trump's fault.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE DEPUTY PRESS SECRETARY: We are taking unprecedented action. We had to fix something that was broken, especially by the last administration.\par \par \uc2 TURNER: A new \uc2 U.N\uc2 . report reveals human smuggling is serving abroad as well. Today nearly 50 million people worldwide are enslaved. That number has surged by 10 million over the last five years. Those 50 million are split between forced labor and forced marriage. Millions are children.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 TURNER (on camera): Right now we're awaiting the official August border crossing numbers. But CBP sources tell us the total number of encounters this fiscal year already surpassed the 2 million mark with a month left so spare. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Gillian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Singer, musician R. Kelly has been convicted of several child pornography charges. The R&B star found guilty on three counts but acquitted of conspiracy to obstruct justice. Today's ruling in Chicago comes after a New York federal judge sentenced the singer to 30 years in prison. That trial involved racketeering and sex trafficking charges.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we take you live to London as mourners pay their final respects to Queen Elizabeth II.\par \par \uc2 First, here is what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 17 in Des Moines, Iowa, where a judge orders a sex trafficking victim who killed her abuser to pay $150,000 in restitution. Pieper Lewis was 15 years old when she stabbed 37-year-old Zachary Brooks more than 30 times. Prosecutors argued Brooks was not an immediate threat because he was asleep during the killing.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Louisville, Kentucky, from WDRB. One of the big stories there tonight, Jim Beam is going green and ramping up production to meet growing demand. The bourbon producer outlining a $400 million expansion projects powered by renewable natural gas. Capacity will increase by 50 percent at one distillery, reduce greenhouse gas by the same percentage.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The number of worldwide coronavirus deaths is now at its lowest level since March of 2020. The \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  says a little more than 11,000 fatalities were reported in the past week worldwide. New cases fell to 3.1 million. The trends continue a weeklong decline in the virus in every part of the world.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, Denmark is no longer offering coronavirus boosters for those under 50 years old. The Danish health authority says younger people are generally not at higher risk for severe illness.\par \par \uc2 On Capitol Hill, Kentucky Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  renewing tough questioning of chief presidential and medical adviser Dr. \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2  over a lack of federal vaccination guidelines for those who have had previous infections on COVID-19. In a hearing at monkeypox today, Senator Paul played a video from 2004 where Fauci said the best protection is actually to get infected yourself.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAND PAUL, (R-KY): You seemed quite certain of yourself in 2004, but in 2022 there's a lot less certainty.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER: A vaccination following infection gives an added extra boost. And that film that you showed is really taken out of context.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: If you ignore whether they have been infected, you're ignoring a vaccine basically. People decry vaccine hesitancy. It's coming from the gobbledygook that you give us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The 2004 caller was asking Fauci on \uc2 C-SPAN\uc2 's "Washington Journal" why she had flulike symptoms following vaccination. Fauci responded, saying the caller could have an allergy, but if she had already had the flu, she would not need a vaccine.\par \par \uc2 People from all over Great Britain and really all over the world are paying their respects in person to Queen Elizabeth II. The casket holding her body is now resting at Westminster Hall where dignitaries and members of the public are filing past. Senior foreign affairs correspondent Greg Palkot has the latest tonight from London.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 GREG PALKOT, FOX NEWS SENIOR FOREIGN AFFAIRS CORRESPONDENT: Queen Elizabeth II leaving Buckingham Palace, her official residence, for the last time. The queen's basket adorned with a flag and a crown borne on a horse-drawn gun carriage, carried with full military honors as tens of thousands watched.\par \par \uc2 INGRID SEWARD, "MAJESTY" MAGAZINE: And I think when the hearse carrying the Queen's coffin came past, people were stunned into silence.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: Following on foot, her children, \uc2 King Charles III\uc2 , Princess Anne, Princes Andrew and Edward. Behind them, reunited again after much friction, Princes William and Harry, casting memories back to the funeral of the princes' late mother.\par \par \uc2 DUNCAN LARCOMBE, "THE SUN" FORMER ROYAL EDITOR: We haven't seen scenes like this in London really since Diana's death. But there is a very different feel now. It's a sense of loss that rather than tragedy.\par \par \uc2 PALKOT: The journey took 38 minutes as Big Ben gonged and canons blasted through the heart of official London to medieval Westminster Hall at the Houses of Parliament where the royals were joined by their spouses, including the wife of \uc2 King Charles\uc2 , Queen-Consort Camilla, William's Princess Kate, and Harry's Duchess of Sussex Meghan. \uc2 King Charles\uc2 , according to the White House, spoke by phone with President Biden today when he conveyed his wish to continue a close relationship with the king. Thousands of Britons and those around the world turned out to honor their beloved queen in person. The line stretched for miles. The waits lasted hours. For those we spoke with the late queen was worth it.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The queen is also my colonel in chief, so I'm here to pay my colonel in chief my respects.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Steadiness, honesty, grace, a confidence, and true to her job.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thank you, goodbye, hello -- all of the above.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PALKOT (on camera): All of the above. The Queen lies in state for four days until Monday morning, then the state funeral, hundreds of royals, heads of state, dignitaries are expected, including President Biden and the first lady. And then off to the final resting place for the queen, her beloved Windsor Castle, and a little bit of peace. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Greg Palkot live in London. Greg, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel with just reaction to our just released FOX News polls on the midterms plus the latest on the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN, (D-IL) SENATE JUDICIARY COMMITTEE CHAIRMAN: This is a clear referendum in this election on a woman's right to choose.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES, (D-NY): It's extreme if your top agenda is a nationwide ban on abortion.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICK SCOTT\uc2 , (R-FL): The Democrats, they don't want to be seen with Biden. He's toxic to Democrats. So, we stay on our message, talk about inflation, about the border crisis, about crime.\par \par \uc2 There will be a lot of noise out there about how Republicans get wind. But look at the polls. We keep getting better week after week after week.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Setting the table for the midterm elections, we have some new FOX News polls out tonight. The items of concern for the electorate, the people who were polled, voters are extremely concerned about inflation at 59 percent. The state of democracy at 50 percent. You see abortion ticking up there, but at 45 percent and crime at 43 percent.\par \par \uc2 The generic ballot, the candidates, the congressional vote preference as it stands tonight, you can see those other items there. Congressional vote preference is essentially tied with the people who are saying that they want to vote in this election. They'll be out at the ballot.\par \par \uc2 And let's bring in our panel, FOX News senior political analyst Brit Hume, Katie Pavlich, news editor at Townhall.com, and Harold Ford Jr., former Tennessee Congressman, co-host of THE FIVE. Brit, this seems like a battle between inflation, and Republicans obviously talking about that especially with the cpi numbers, and abortion, and Democrats talking about that and specifically about Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 's proposal. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: Yes, I think that's essentially correct, Bret. The Democrats would like this election to be about two things, really -- abortion, as you noted, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , to the extent they can make him the issue. Republicans would like it to be about inflation and the many failings of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  who stands poorly in the polls.\par \par \uc2 My sense about this is the Republicans issues are felt a little more strongly and Republican voters are a little bit more motivated. This was a poll, Bret, of registered voters. Later we'll no doubt have polls of likely voters that may give us an even clearer sense of where this stands.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here is Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  defending his proposal, and obviously the Senate majority leader reacting to it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R-SC): Abortion should be not available on demand. There should be very limited exceptions. And I think that's where the country is at. So I don't mind talking about pro-life issues.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY) SENATE MAJORITY LEADER: Months after women had their freedom of choice taken away by the MAGA Supreme Court, a nationwide abortion ban was actually their attempt to seem more mainstream.\par \par \uc2 People will know the Republican view -- abolish abortion everywhere.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Harold, it doesn't say that, that proposal. Obviously, Democrats are hopping on that and that talking point. But Senator Graham went on to say if you look at European nations around the world, most of them are at 14 weeks with restrictions on abortion. Some are even more restrictive than that. The U.S. is not. And will Democrats run into those questions later in this term?\par \par \uc2 HAROLD FORD JR., FORMER TENNESSEE REPRESENTATIVE: Maybe. First, thanks for having me on and thanks for doing common ground. I think we need to have more things like that to get people to understand you have got to govern. And this is one of them.\par \par \uc2 I think \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  is trying to foster some sort of commonsense agreement around reproductive rights and abortion. I think the one -- there are probably two problems he's going to run into, and they have to do with the bill because he allows for states to allow for more restrictive things if they want. But the court said that states could do that. So I don't know how the federal government could pass saying 15 weeks if already the court has said states have this authority.\par \par \uc2 But I get the political argument here. There's no doubt the Dobbs decision, as Brit said so aptly, has energized Democrats. Midterms are determined by really how voters view the party of the president at that time, and that Democrats are in the White House. And right now the base of the Democratic Party is energized. Ten weeks ago, it was only the base of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 So I think it comes down to independents. And Democrats still have a fight on their hands when it comes to the strength of the economy. I heard the president yesterday said despite the inflation numbers, the economy is strong. Gas prices are down and that is a big indicator. We will have to see over the next seven weeks. How that plays out.\par \par \uc2 But I've got to tell you that Democrats have found an issue to excite their base. And if it's base versus base, we've had one race already in this regard, a race here in New York, a special race where the Democrat ran on abortion. The Republican ran on how he thought that Biden was incompetent around the economy and inflation. And the Democrat won the race. It's a race that has flipped back and forth. It's upstate New York, not New York City. So at least we have some hard data around that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Katie?\par \par \uc2 KATIE PAVLICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Well, abortion is certainly a topic that Democrats have been talking about since the Dobbs decision. But Republicans, I think, are making a big mistake if they're going to just cede this issue on the campaign trail to Democrats when you hear them talk about how the Republican position is extreme. Well, if you look at the facts of \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 's, he is talking about a 15-week limit. That's what Governor Glenn Youngkin has also proposed in Virginia. And the polling shows that the vast majority of Americans actually do believe in a 15-week limit on abortion, including pro-life Americans.\par \par \uc2 You ask any candidate who is running for office on the Democratic side at this moment if they support any restrictions on abortion, including up to the moment of birth, and they will not give any kind of example of what they think is a reasonable limit to abortion. So, Republicans can hold them accountable for that position and ask them to explain what the majority of Americans would see as an extreme position, or they can try and simply only talk about inflation and the economy, which, of course, is driving the majority of the concerns for voters here. But Democrats are talking about it. So Republicans have to come up with an argument against it rather than just allowing Democrats to call them extreme on the issue.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, on one race where there has been a lot of back and forth even today about debates is the Pennsylvania Senate race, Mehmet Oz versus Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . Fetterman agreeing to a debate October 25th with stipulations for the recovery from his stroke. Oz agreeing to that. However, saying there's only going to be one and it's October 25th. People will already be voting for a few weeks by then.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Yes, this is a race of the doctor running against the patient. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  has had a stroke, as you noted. He is still not doing all that well, which is one reason why he doesn't want to debate. He has agreed to one. He will be pressed to agree to more. Whichever way it goes, there's a risk for Fetterman he will get in the debate and not do very well because he's not yet well. But the other side of that is the expectations for him in any debate will be very low and the media will see to that.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All right, panel, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 PAVLICH: Thanks, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a special day for a self-described standup guy. An Arizona veteran gets back on his feet with the help of a new robotic exoskeleton. Army combat veteran Richard Neider served in Operation Iraqi Freedom where he suffered a spinal cord injury. After nearly a decade in a wheelchair, Neider calls his new situation complete independence. He plans on riding shotgun on a cross-country motorcycle trip. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, the latest on the negotiations and the implications of a possible railroad strike. Also want to tell you that my book "To Rescue the Republic, Ulysses S. Grant, The Fragile Union, The Crisis of 1876" now out in paper book paperback -- I said that again -- wherever books are sold, also on FOX Nation.\par \par \uc2 That's it for us, fair, balanced, and unafraid. Here's Jesse.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220915ei9e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Vice President Kamala Harris Outright Lied About The Crisis At The Border By Claiming It Is Secure, When Recent Video Shows That Illegal Immigrants Wearing Camouflage Are Climbing Up The Wall In The Southern Border; White House Press Secretary \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  Also Downplays The Border Crisis By Pointing Fingers On The Previous Administration; Former Obama Advisor David Axelrod Says Another Election Denier Won Last Night In New Hampshire's Primary When Democrats Are Making Dirty Tricks, Meddling With The Election By Pouring Millions To Let A GOP Candidate Win; According To A New Poll, At Least 45 Percent Of San Francisco Residents Say They've Been A Victim Of A Crime; Insulting To Female Voters: NYT Trashes GOP Candidates Over Wives In Campaign Ads; $90,000 Parking Spot: SF Listing Offers One Car Space For Exorbitant Amount\b0\par\par\uc2 Dana Perino, Jesse Watters, Greg Gutfeld, Jessica Tarlov, Jeanine  Pirro \par \uc2 8,270 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: We'll see what happens tomorrow. Here's "THE FIVE."\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: Hello, everybody. I'm Jesse Watters along with Judge Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Dana Perino, and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5 o'clock in New York City, and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration can't stop lying to the American people about the border crisis just days after Kamala claimed the border is secure. We're getting more evidence that makes her look like a total fool.\par \par \uc2 Brand new video showing illegal immigrants in camouflage, scaling a partial wall on the border in Arizona. And there have already been more than two million border encounters so far this year, a record high. At least there's one Democrat willing to bus Kamala on her border lying.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS ANCHOR: Vice President Harris said this weekend the Southern border is, quote, "secure."\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): It's wrong. She's dead wrong on that. And I have said this, if we don't secure it, I voted every time for the wall, but we need the wall a lot more.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: The lying doesn't end there, listen to this outrageous claim from the White House.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We had to, to fix something that was broken, especially by the last administration, the Trump administration, which largely just tried to build a wall, an ineffective wall, along the border and couldn't even finish that in four years.\par \par \uc2 We're certainly doing a lot more to secure the border and could be doing even more if Republicans would stop their obstruction.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And by the way, where are all those liberal fact checkers on all of that? Fox News did some digging and it turns out nearly every major fact checker has completely ignored \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  since taking over for \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dana, are they going to have to walk back? We know they like to walk back. Are they going to have to walk back the border is secure line because that can't continue for that much longer.\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CO-HOST: Well, maybe they will after the midterm election, when they lose and you look at the exit polls and the border is an issue. But like, even just take just one issue of the border when they falsely accused the mounted border police of whipping migrants.\par \par \uc2 It wasn't true. They -- they made those guys sit on their rear ends for over 11 months, fake investigation. They finally released investigation and says, yes, actually that didn't happen. Did the White House walk that back? No. Did they apologize to them? Absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 So, that will, I don't think that's going to change. I think that what you're seeing from \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and the White House press secretary, and probably from Biden, if he ever does an interview, is that this is a strategic and craven and desperate move. Just to basically say, not us it's Trump. They want to run against Trump. They'll try to make that all about Trump.\par \par \uc2 But the one of the problems is there are no reporters on the border. Bill Melugin, amongst other Fox reporters. But Bill Melugin is certainly there and he is very passionate and has all the pictures, all the videos and everything. He sends all this out. I'll ask him on Newsroom. Anybody else down there? No.\par \par \uc2 The only time they go is if there's something, for example, of Governor Abbott sending migrants elsewhere, then they'll go to the border and cover it. So, it's no one -- this is crazy. Bill Melugin put out a tweet the other day that showed these guys in camouflage going over the wall. And all of these responses were saying that we, at Fox, set it up. Then it was fake.\par \par \uc2 So, now, you have border deniers and you do not actually have anybody talking about the things like the fentanyl crisis, for example. That would be one thing where, if I were Republicans, I would drive that home. Three hundred Americans die a day and they don't talk about it. They have the nerve to say that this is a -- this is Trump's fault. It's unbelievable to me.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: No way Fox could set that up. I have trouble ingesting tape. Greg, do you think these guys could have saved money on the camo because you don't need camouflage? You can just waltz right through.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CO-HOST: Yes, Dana is right. There's nobody watching and nobody caring. I think what's her name? What's Jean.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Jean-Pierre.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Jean-Pierre exists to make Kamala look smart. Kamala exists to make Mayorkas look smart. And I'm scared of saying that because I think he might label me a domestic terrorist for being insensitive to his feelings. Maybe he would feel differently if the people of the migrants were posting Biden memes, then he would be really, really -- why isn't he considered a domestic threat by not doing his job by his own definition? Right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, he's saying that like, if you, if you post something online a personal grievance, but what if you're just not doing your job and allowing terrorists to cross over? That seems to me like a threat. But I got to, again, I got to be careful.\par \par \uc2 But what you -- you hit the nail on the head. You get the sense that we do this, we've done these talk -- this story for years. You get the sense that no one is listening but our audience.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: And I think it's because the media only cares about the optics if it can be tethered to Republicans or to law enforcement, hence, going after the border patrol agent. They could pin that on law enforcement, even though, so they didn't have to say it was Biden's fault.\par \par \uc2 So, the only way to fix this is to elect a Republican, but not for the right reasons. Because you know, the Republicans, not because the Republicans will fix it, but because the media will then do their job when a Republican is in charge and there are migrant drownings and there are 300 fentanyl deaths a day. Then if they can pin that on a Republican, then it's a story. Because remember, remember what happened to the kids in cages. How miraculously that went away when Trump went away and how it didn't matter under Obama.\par \par \uc2 So, and lastly, I -- we have to stress this because we always get attacked for being anti-immigrant. We are very pro-immigrant. We're against illegal immigration, because both of those together are very American. One immigration self-selects for risk takers and America is nothing but risk takers and that's important. But what's not American is line cutting, right?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: So that's, and that's the fault of our bureaucrats, but we just don't like people cutting in line. We're pro-immigrant, we're against line cutting. That's America.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Judge, I have an idea. Tell me if I'm off the wall. Abbott hires --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CO-HOST: Now or later?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: -- Abbott hires a child actor puts him in a cage. And the next thing, you know, the media all flies down to Texas to cover.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes. That's absolutely going to -- absolutely going to happen. You're right. But, you know, I want to -- I want to start with something that Dana happened and that that's the issue of fentanyl, killing 300 people a day.\par \par \uc2 You know, Dana, you don't have to worry about that anymore. You know why?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Why?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Because President Biden decided to double the penalties for fentanyl possession. The only problem is the rest of the Democrats, if they do arrest them, they're letting them out on bail without bail immediately.\par \par \uc2 But look, what's going on in Washington right now is that these people will do and say anything to stay in power. It's all theater. It's all theater. They're decimating the border and I'll tell you how to get attention on this thing.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that nobody talks about is how much it's costing us in the middle of a recession maybe, or certainly it's 40-year high inflation to maintain the two million people that are coming through the border. And the estimates now is that, it's $20 billion a year, Mr. and Mrs. taxpayer that are coming out of your pockets for people as we say we'd love to have other than the fact that they're cutting in line.\par \par \uc2 But you have to fix something that's broken. And for \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  to say, you know, Trump built an ineffective wall and we're doing a lot more to secure the border. That is an out and out lie. We have never had the number of people that are coming through the border as they're coming through right now.\par \par \uc2 And the last thing I'm going to say is this, every time we get into a situation where we allow people to come in legally, we find out who they are. These people, we don't know who they are. They don't have to be mask. They don't have to be tested. And we don't know who among them is a criminal. They're not all, certainly, who's a pedophile, who's a thief, who's a this.\par \par \uc2 Anytime you have two million people there are criminals in there and nobody is protecting us. And the sanctuary cities are saying, you can all come, but I'm going to send you, \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2  is going to send them to a Republican area and redistrict, which is now the latest.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Are we wrong, Jessica, about any of this.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And please.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Definitely.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Continue.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Thank you. A couple of things. Greg, you said that I think only our viewers are watching this and I don't think that's true. If you look at what elected officials the Democratic Party are doing in states like Arizona and Texas, they're hearing it from people at home there.\par \par \uc2 People like \uc2 Ruben Gallego\uc2 , Mark Kelly did a whole spot of him building a section of the wall in Yuma. This is something that is penetrating. And as inflation and the economy becomes less of a priority issue, it's still the number one issue, but there are other issues that are creeping up important, an importance for the midterms.\par \par \uc2 That includes crime and safety, which we'll talk about later, national security, abortion, et cetera. And this is certainly part of that. But I think it's just a general misrepresentation of the issue of securing our border when this has been an issue under Republicans and Democrats.\par \par \uc2 We had the gang of eight, right? That was split there, Republicans and Democrats, they couldn't get anything done because the truth of the matter is, is that people in both states run by both parties like undocumented labor. And that will continue. Whether they're picking grapes in Oregon or oranges in Florida.\par \par \uc2 And until that stops, and it's not going to stop because these are hardworking people who do us a great service. They often take jobs that Americans don't want to take, and we're not going to solve this crisis that way.\par \par \uc2 I also want to say something about the language of all of this. I don't think using the term build the wall has the same scare factor as it used to. I think that Democrats have moved past that. If you hate Trump, you've hated him for five years now, and that that's going to continue.\par \par \uc2 So, I do think we need to move towards a better language of the way that we talk about these issues, because it's not animating people in the same way.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: OK. So, you do agree with us. Thank you, Jessica.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I'm glad that that's what you took away from that.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Coming up, liberals are complaining about those evil MAGA Republicans again as Democrats spend millions to help them get elected.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Democrats mean careful what you wish for. Former Obama advisor, David Axelrod, getting mocked over a clueless tweet that lament how some far-right candidates won after a key primary last night saying, quote, "another far right election denier wins. This time in New Hampshire. It feels like the Republicans are doing everything possible to frustrate their chances of regaining control of the Senate."\par \par \uc2 But maybe David should check with his fellow Democrats because they've been spending millions in campaign ads to boost those MAGA Republican candidates and bury the moderates. So much for that threat to democracy. And Elizabeth Warren, basically couldn't answer when called out on it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): I think this is just an extraordinarily dangerous. I am so deeply disturbed that there are still so many MAGA Republicans.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: So why are Democrats supporting -- they're pouring money into their campaign?\par \par \uc2 WARREN: I believe that these folks are very, very dangerous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: She said. All right, Greg, you know what, they're spending something like $53 million to Democrats on these MAGA candidates, pushing out the more moderate Republicans and then criticizing them and saying they're a threat to democracy, but like raising them on a pedestal. What's going on?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I'm beginning to think they really don't mean what they're saying.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Gee.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I don't know, but I mean, like trying to, I think trying to win an election is more important than avoiding the greatest existential threats since the Civil War tells me maybe they might've been exaggerating. And it also shows how little faith they have in their candidates, that they would shift their energies to the other candidates rather than try to like figure out who their best person is.\par \par \uc2 The other thing is, as I shuffle for my notes, I am curious. Why is this election meddling somehow less worse than the Russian spending? What was it? A hundred grand on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  ads? Was that what it was that ended up doing nothing? I mean, is there -- is there some principle here? Like, so it's OK if we meddle. But somebody else can't buy ads.\par \par \uc2 This is 10 times, a hundred times, a thousand times. This is an existential threat --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: -- to our election system. And it exposes their extreme MAGA grief. This is why we need the media, an actual objective media, because if they were so terrified of these monsters, right, why are they trying to help them win? A media would actually go crazy on this story.\par \par \uc2 Imagine if we, I'm going to say we, as a Republican, even though I'm a registered independent or I think I am, I don't know. I forgot. What if we decided that we would amplify the socialists in the Democratic Party?\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: You do. You -- there's --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I don't -- I don't pay, I don't spend any money.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes. Who?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I don't support any socialists, but you imagine if we did that, the media would go crazy if they found out like Fox was sending money to support socialist.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes. OK.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: But we don't do that because we're better people.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK. Jesse, isn't this about the Democrats having nothing to talk about, and instead, they're trying to make \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  the centerpiece and the person that they can focus everyone on so that they don't have to talk about what a mess our economy is in.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You explained it perfectly.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I don't have to add anything to that. And Greg is right. We are better people, but we lose.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We show up -- we show up with a knife to a gun fight --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 PERINO: High (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: -- but we lose with honor.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We should take notes and maybe start funneling money to the squad types and the Dem primaries we can knock them off easier in the general.\par \par \uc2 Greg also said something very smart yesterday and it's so rare, it stuck out. I remembered it. He said, it seems like the Democrats want it more because they'll do anything to stay in power.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm not even worried about this, what they're doing here with these primaries. This is small ball. What they did in the last election, they pushed the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  deal back after the election to hurt Trump. People probably died because of that. They brought the CIA into the last election to lie in that letter about the laptop, they blew up all the election laws, you know, got rid of voter I.D., pushed out the signature requirements.\par \par \uc2 Look at another election. I mean, they covered up Benghazi, a terror attack so they could win. They turned the \uc2 IRS\uc2  against the Tea Party. What was the last time I think \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said Republicans