ft "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " is calling out Fetterman's struggles and his lace of transparency on his health records, while raising very key important questions about why he doesn't want to debate any time soon. By the way, he hasn't take an single question from the press.\par \par \uc2 And "The Philly Inquirer" also published an opinion piece tonight questioning Fetterman's fitness for office and whether he's up to the job at all.\par \par \uc2 And get this, "The Daily Mail" has now discovered Fetterman did not disclose owning eight properties in Braddock, Pennsylvania, where he was a failed mayor. I think he missed 52 council meetings. That was his only job. He never had a real job in his life.\par \par \uc2 As I have been saying, Republicans need to unveil their version of a Contract with America. We expect Kevin McCarthy will do it. He's going to call it Commitments to America. We expect in about 10 days and put their stated positions in writing and sign it and expose all of these far-left extremism inside the Democratic Party with solutions that will solve the problems our country is facing.\par \par \uc2 Former Speaker of the House Newt Gingrich, he did it 28 years ago. I was the emcee. Back in 1994, Republicans won in a wave election.\par \par \uc2 Newt has now released his five-point plan to take back control of Congress. It includes focusing on rising crime, open borders, parental rights in school, and much, much more.\par \par \uc2 Here to explain FOX News contributor Newt Gingrich.\par \par \uc2 You know, I have appealed to Republicans for 28 years to follow your lead and do this. Kevin McCarthy is going to do this I think in about 10 days. You have your five point plan --\par \par \uc2 NEWT GINGRICH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yeah, look --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Yeah?\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: Well, but my five points which I'll get to are all within the framework of Kevin McCarthy and the House Republicans' Commitment to America. I mean, the contrast between a Biden Democrat who can't solve anything so they offer us hate and divisiveness and what the House Republicans are doing and what I hope frankly the Senate Republicans will pick up on and follow is to offer a series of very specific proposals, many more frankly than we had in the Contract.\par \par \uc2 And I tried to summarize that into five big areas because I think if we can stay on the big issues, we're going to have an amazing election this fall. The first is inflation and, of course, the numbers this week -- remember, it's not just general inflation.\par \par \uc2 Food prices are up 13 percent year over year. Every family that goes to grocery store knows it's more and more expensive, fewer and fewer choices, smaller packages. It's a nightmare. And that particularly hits the poor and it hits retirees.\par \par \uc2 Second is the whole question of crime. You mentioned Fetterman who likes to release murderers and has two convicted murderers on his payroll. Philadelphia has the highest murder rate in its history. Atlanta has a higher crime rate per capita than Chicago. And Warnock, the Democrat, is not going to be able to explain it. Just as Fetterman is going to be weighed down by his pro-criminal position,\par \par \uc2 Third is the border. You look at Mark Kelly who on the same day within 12 hours voted against a \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  amendment to hire 13,000 Border Patrol and then voted for the Biden position of 87,000 new IRS agents.\par \par \uc2 So you're sitting in Arizona with about 400,000 new illegal immigrants and your senator can't vote for more border patrol but can vote to have you audited? I think that Kelly's going to go down just based on that.\par \par \uc2 Fourth, for parents and generally for grandparents and relatives, the idea that parents ought to have the right to know what is being taught their kids, which the teachers union left-wing crazies are bitterly opposed to.\par \par \uc2 It's a 84 percent issue. Every Republican ought to be willing to campaign on it. It's going to make a very big difference.\par \par \uc2 And finally, I think we have to start talking again about totally overhauling the Pentagon and the intelligence community. I think there's a very real chance today that if we ended up in a big war with China, we'd lose it and lose it very fast, and the current Pentagon's too bureaucratic, too corrupt in it's lobbying relationships, and accepts too much incompetence and inefficiency. Now, this is going to be a major American crisis.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I want you to --\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: That's the framework I would set for the campaign.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I want you to address candidates because all the media wants them to talk about is January 6, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They want to talk about in demagogue and lie about that abortion is now illegal in America. Every woman needs to know that is a big lie.\par \par \uc2 Roe v. Wade was overturned. The states will decide if there are any restrictions at all, states like New York and California will have none and different states will have varying laws. But abortion is still legal in America.\par \par \uc2 The last thing they want to talk about is the usual playbook Republicans are racist and sexist and misogynistic and want dirty air and water et cetera.\par \par \uc2 So when the when candidates are badgered by the media mob, they need to learn the art of pivoting. How do you advise them to deal with the media, because that's all they're going to ask him about?\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: Well, what I've -- look, what I would say to every reporter is let me tell you about the price of food, let me tell you about the price of living, let me tell you about the local crime rate. I'll get it --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: What do you say to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and all those people that went into the Capitol on January 6? What's your answer?\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: My answer -- my answer to that is that's an important Washington issue, but right here at home, what matters is the crime rate that's threatening our citizens, the rising cost of food that is threatening every person in our district, the fact that we don't know what's happening in the schools and our parents deserve to know that --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Next question, what do you think about the drug issue the Supreme Court taking away a woman's right to have an abortion?\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: It's totally untrue. That is not what they did and I'm sorry that you buy that kind of falsehood. But let's go back to what really matters to every woman in America, which starts with what they're paying at the grocery store right now.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That is a great tutorial. You're good at what you do. I've learned from the best -- by the way, you would you had to be desperate in '94 to let me emcee that night. I have no idea --\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: No, no, we had several choices and the others weren't available. What can I tell you, baby?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Lawrence (ph) goes to bed by nine o'clock, that's why he was out.\par \par \uc2 GINGRICH: Actually no. Actually, my two daughters wanted you. So I had to do it for them.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: OK, I'm friends with the whole family.\par \par \uc2 Newt Gingrich, great advice for people. I hope Republicans are paying attention.\par \par \uc2 All right. Coming up, circle back \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  started her new job at MSDNC. Can you think of a better example of media collusion? We're going to remind you of some of the low lights in the White House from our previous job, straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. She's back. Former White House press secretary, circle back \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  started her new job at MSDNC this week, saying she is thrilled to join such a remarkable team, adding that she has a passion for, quote, calling out BS.\par \par \uc2 But if that's true then she certainly has an odd way of showing it because not once was she ever honest about the BS that came out of the Biden White House.\par \par \uc2 Here's just a small trip down memory lane. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: You may have noticed this week that your gas prices have gone up. I want to talk to you a little bit about why. A lot of it has to do with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It's broadly known and widely known, Peter, that there was a broad range of Russian disinformation back in 2020.\par \par \uc2 PETER DOOCY, FOX NEWS REPORTER: Why is the administration flying thousands of migrants from the border to Florida and New York in the middle of the night?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Well, I'm not sure that it's in the middle of the night. As individuals come across the border and they are both assessed for whether they have any symptoms, if they have symptoms, they are -- the intention is for them to be quarantined. That is our process. They are not intending to stay here for a lengthy period of time.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Presidents still believe that Build Back Better will not add a dime to the national debt?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Correct, it won't.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Why would -- why should Americans believe that?\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: Because it won't.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: People, it was crystal clear that things were not improving on supply chain, people couldn't get dishwashers and furniture and treadmills delivered on time, not to mention all sorts of other things. So why is --\par \par \uc2 PSAKI: The tragedy of the short -- the treadmill that's delayed.\par \par \uc2 And last thing I just want to do before we get to your questions, I often note I'm going to circle back. I hate to disappoint conservative \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , but I'm going to circle back on a number of things as we often do directly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Here with reaction, FOX News contributor Joe Concha, the best media reporter in the country, and the "FOX and Friends weekend" co-host, our friend Pete Hegseth. Good to see you both.\par \par \uc2 Pete, I think my two favorites are the tragedy of the treadmill and those 2:00 a.m. and 4:00 a.m. flights in obscure airports around the, country those are early morning flights, that's not the dark of night. I didn't know those things.\par \par \uc2 PETE HEGSETH, FOX & FRIENDS WEEKEND CO-HOST: No, no, that's -- it's -- she's always being straight up with everybody. I hope she's taking Peter Doocy with her -- actually hope she isn't, we don't want to lose him here at Fox. But unless Peter Doocy is going to be alongside with her on her new streaming show, it's unclear who would watch because the only reason we tuned in a lot of people is to see the exchange of a real question being asked of a White House press secretary.\par \par \uc2 Her -- it is worth be noting that her show will be on at MSNBC's streaming show, Sean. We had a former colleague who --\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: She's going to miss Peter.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: And it's been cancelled.\par \par \uc2 She's going to miss Peter and this as if you know people don't watch MSNBC, how often do they go to Peacock to watch an MSNBC streaming show from a flak? I don't know that she's being set up for success but at the same time, everyone inflated how good she was at what she did. Anyway without Peter Doocy, she would not have been interesting.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Joe, what's your take? Will she succeed or fail?\par \par \uc2 JOE CONCHA, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Pete is exactly right. Without the contrast of Peter Doocy or Jacqui Heinrich or someone to challenge her, then \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  comes across as very inauthentic and quite frankly not very interesting. And she's going to Peacock. This is like CNN plus, right? Who would pay to watch this when not that many people are watching MSNBC for free?\par \par \uc2 But I love this quote right out of band camp from \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 , Sean. What I hope to do is to bring that passion for explaining things, debunking things, calling out BS when you see it in my next job.\par \par \uc2 Will she -- will Jen be debunking herself, Sean? Because this is the same former press secretary who said Republicans want to defund the police, the same \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  once said that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  takes questions from the press four to five times per week, said that the withdrawal from Afghanistan couldn't be called anything but a success. I mean, come on.\par \par \uc2 So, good luck to \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2 . She's going to fit right in very well at MSNBC and they're on misfit toys just fine. We may be looking at the female version of the next Keith Olbermann at this point, Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: I think that's very well said.\par \par \uc2 By the way, Pete, congratulations again, "Battle for the American Mind", a massive New York Times number one bestseller. You've done great with it. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 HEGSETH: Thanks, Sean.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. We got to take a break. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 More "Hannity" right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Before we go tonight, we're sad to report that esteemed attorney, by the way, a friend of this program, \uc2 Ken Starr\uc2 , passed away earlier today at the early age of 76. Ken was a fearless defender of the rule of law, was an American patriot, a brilliant attorney. We will pray for his family and our condolences to all.\par \par \uc2 Tat's unfortunately all the time we have left this evening. Thank you as always for making the show possible. Please set your DVR, never miss an episode.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime let not your heart be troubled, are you wearing your pink tonight?\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SHAN000020220914ei9d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Sen. Joe Manchin (D-WV) Is Being Interviewed About His Side Deal On Permitting Reform That Faces Resistance; Biden Touts Inflation Reduction Act Despite Report; Biden Adviser Admits Numbers Didn't Meet Expectations; August Inflation Report Hotter Than Expected; Democrats And Republicans Vie For Voter Enthusiasm; \uc2 Ken Starr\uc2  Dead At 76; Primary Races To Decide Republican Party Candidates For Midterms Take Place In Delaware, New Hampshire, And Rhode Island; Republicans Campaign On Education Issues In New Mexico; \uc2 John Durham\uc2 's Office Investigating Suspect Igor Danchenko Accused Concocting False Narrative Against 2016 Presidential Candidate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ; Coffin On Queen Elizabeth II Making Its Way From Scotland To London; Biden White House Celebrates Inflation Reduction Act As New Economy Report Indicates Inflation Remains High In U.S.; Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  Proposes Legislation For National Abortion Ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Shannon Bream, Mark Meredith, David  Spunt, Jonathan Hunt \par \uc2 8,225 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (voice-over): Also, Janice Dean has a new podcast called, "The Janice Dean Podcast." It's real -- it's -- I think it's excellent. I think she's so good at radio, she got a great tone approach, everything. So, I did a little interview with her and that is up now at foxnewspodcast.com.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): I heard you talking about that today. Anyway, thanks for joining us today. Appreciate it. That's it for us. SPECIAL REPORT is up next.\par \par \uc2 Hey, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BRET BAIER, FOX NEWS CHANNEL ANCHOR (on camera): Judge, thanks for no hot tubs.\par \par \uc2 All right. Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier. "BREAKING TONIGHT", we are following two major stories.\par \par \uc2 First, sad news. The special counsel who pursued then-President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  during the Whitewater and \uc2 Monica Lewinsky\uc2  scandals has died.\par \par \uc2 Ken Starr\uc2 , dying from complications after surgery. We'll have more on that in a moment.\par \par \uc2 First, a stunning sell off on Wall Street following disappointing inflation news. The three major indices lost between four and five percentage points in just one day.\par \par \uc2 The Dow plummeting 1,276 today. The S&P 500 losing 178. The NASDAQ fell 633. All of this was sparked by new numbers indicating inflation is not going anywhere. But up anytime soon, it appears.\par \par \uc2 Food prices, other essentials skyrocketing in the latest CPI report. Consumer concern not far behind. And the president who is boasting about having inflation under control is facing criticism tonight.\par \par \uc2 We will talk with Democratic Senator Joe Manchin in just a moment. But first, White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich starts us off live from the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening to you, Bret. Well, either the White House expected far better numbers or the person who planned this party had an unfortunate scheduling oversight, gathering Democratic lawmakers on the sweltering South Lawn. On the same day, a new inflation reading came in hotter than expected.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): While the stock market plummeted, James Taylor played a song about death on the White House South Lawn.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JAMES TAYLOR, AMERICAN SINGER-SONGWRITER: You can sing this song when I'm gone.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You can sing this song when I'm gone.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: This the day marked by ironies. Democrats celebrating the Inflation Reduction Act that experts agree won't reduce inflation. On the very day, new inflation data showed a worrying increase.\par \par \uc2 Core inflation shot up to 6.3 percent in August, up from 5.9 percent in July. The president never once mentioning the numbers.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The future America is bright. And the promise of America is real. It is real.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The press secretary punted.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2 , WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: We have to remember, the Inflation Reduction Act is historic.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Biden's economic adviser, so far the only one at the White House acknowledging the circumstances.\par \par \uc2 GENE SPERLING, SENIOR WHITE HOUSE ADVISOR: I think we would have liked to have seen the core inflation numbers do better today.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Republicans seizing the moment.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2  (R-SD): This is an example again of just the complete split screen you see in this country between the White House, you know, celebrating and the American people dealing at the reality.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: President \uc2 Obama\uc2 's former economic adviser sees trouble ahead. Tweeting, "My interpretation, a lot of things that were supposed to bring down inflation have happened to the degree energy prices passed into core, we should be seeing the reverse now."\par \par \uc2 Biden seized on the reports only bright spot in a statement, writing, "Today's data shows more progress in bringing global inflation down in the U.S. economy. Overall, prices have been essentially flat in our country these last two months."\par \par \uc2 But a trip to the grocery store might have you scratching your head, with eggs still up nearly 40 percent, coffee up 17.6 percent, and milk up 17 percent. And the list goes on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): Now, one thing that does have the administration's attention, a looming national railroad strike. They're trying to find ways to use federal authority to keep critical supply chains open. If freight rail labor negotiations fail.\par \par \uc2 Now, I spoke to a couple of Cabinet members today. They would not go on the record, but suffice it to say they are basically sweating bullets, working very hard to try to avoid a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the president is now leaving the White House headed to Delaware for an unplanned trip to an undisclosed location. And he's only scheduled to be there about an hour. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich, live on the North Lawn. Jacqui, thank you.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Joe Manchin. Joe, thanks for sticking with what you said you do. This bill alone is going to lower the deficit by $300 billion over the next decade. And for this -- for this fiscal year, $1,500,000,000,000 reduction in a deficit. This bill finally delivers on a promise that I've made in American people.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  this afternoon on the South Lawn of the White House celebrating the inflation Reduction Act -- a big event there. Joining us now is West Virginia Democratic senator, Joe Manchin.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): Bret, great to be with you. It was interesting optics today you had the information from August inflation, 8.3 percent. Higher than expected.\par \par \uc2 If you take down energy inflation, food inflation, all of the numbers from the CPI today, not good, and the markets took a dive some 1,200 points. And yet, there was a celebration on the South Lawn. What do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, the Inflation Reduction Act is something my staff and I worked extremely hard on to make sure it was balanced. The most important thing we want to do is what are Republicans, my friends, and we've always talked about, produce more energy.\par \par \uc2 This bill will produce more energy. Will drill more oil, will produce more oil, will produce more natural gas.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But it's not reducing inflation. Well, if you put more product in the market, it makes sense that basically, the more product you have in the market, the more reduction it should be.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we're not putting a strain because supply and demand. So, the more product, we put natural gas for home heating, for your agility bills, natural or gasoline to pump, all this should reduce it.\par \par \uc2 On top of that we have the Medicare reduction, as far as putting that and being able to negotiate. That's in my state, 300,000 people on Medicare are going to see reduction, other drug prices, also capped $35 on their insulin.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, you don't have regrets right now for signing onto this bill?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: No. Bret, to be honest with you, I didn't sign on to it. Basically, me and my staff --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Created it.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: We worked on it. And we really worked hard. And we put a balance to it. I'm scared to death, and I was scared to death. And here we go around the world asking other people to produce energy that we wouldn't produce for our self.\par \par \uc2 And it's ridiculous when we have ourselves, our best ally is Canada. We have our friends around the world that we can work with. Not the ones we're depending on now. And China, and Russia, and what's happening in Europe. And if we don't have energy security in this nation, shame on us.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Right.\par \par \uc2 Well, part of this deal is a permanent deal that was not in the bill because it couldn't be. So, it was aside --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: But it was -- it was -- it was not aside. I mean, make sure you understand. It had to be pulled out of the bill. It was negotiated and everyone agreed in one bill.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Understood.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: And then, when you get --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Today, the Majority Leader said --\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: OK.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: That the permitting agreement is part of the broader agreement.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: And he's going to put it in the continuing resolution.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Correct.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: However, there are progressives who have already said no way, are we doing this. no way in the House and the Senate. So, are you surprised that progressives are baulking at that deal?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, I'm basically surprised that they haven't really looked at the deal. Because if they're concerned about renewable energy, which we all said we want to produce the energy through fossil, cleaner than anywhere in the world, displace the dirtier fossil around the world.\par \par \uc2 That's carbon reduction, if you will. We want to do that. But also, we want to invest into the clean technology for the energy of the future.\par \par \uc2 You cannot get the energy the future, you cannot get the transmission lines, unless you get permitting reform.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But you cannot get the votes, if you don't have the Democrats sign it on.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, you have -- yes, I'm -- hopefully my Republican friends.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, you call on Republicans to make this deal come across the finish line?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, the Republicans, basically -- can you imagine, you have 40 of them basically signed on to another permitting bill.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But when you signed on to the deal that you created, you weren't counting on Republicans to do that. You were counting the Democrats --\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I knew we couldn't do it with reconciliation, at that time, with Republicans, because we had a 15 percent minimum on taxes to the highest corporations, with a billion dollars or more book revenue for three years, we thought there should be at least a minimum 15 percent, everybody pays their fair share.\par \par \uc2 With that, that put my Republican friends in a bind, because they had worked on the 2017 reduction.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, going into this, when you did this big, you know, buying into this whole negotiation, you were thinking, well, I'm going to get this through with Republicans?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, here, let me just say, first of all, you said this big. The Democratic Party went from $6 trillion to $3-1/2 trillion, all over the board in the trillions. That was never going to happen.\par \par \uc2 When Build Back Better, when I basically could not go with Build Back Better, and I told them, there is not going to be this type of legislation, I can't sign on. I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 And that went on. I got beat up for over a year on this Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Oh, I know.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: And then it came April, and I saw what's happening with energy, the war in Ukraine, the things that's going on around the world, and us asking other people to do what we're not doing for ourselves. And that's when I said, we need to do an energy bill.\par \par \uc2 And to Schumer's -- to his credit, he says, you're chairman of Energy Committee, you have a staff, once you start writing us, and I said you probably won't like it, because it's going to be very balanced.\par \par \uc2 I've got to have fossil, we do it better. And I will invest in clean technology, we have a responsibility.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What if Schumer or Pelosi can't find the votes to pass the continuing resolution with this plan in it? What happens? Are you willing to shut down the government if it doesn't happen?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I won't be shutting it down, because I'm voting for it. And now, the people that voted against it now you asked me, my Republican friends, I really truly in my heart of hearts, believe that I have enough, great common sense.\par \par \uc2 Republicans that says listen, the IRA is behind us. That bill is over. It's already been signed. You can say everything you want to about it.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Only because of the permitting deal.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, no. Now, the permitting bill deal, they've been something -- I've been working for 10 years, I've been there.\par \par \uc2 This is something my Republican friends have said, Joe, we've got to have permitting reform.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, why not just leave it to Schumer and Pelosi to find the votes that they said they would find when you made the deal?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Well, first of all, we have to have 60 in the Senate. We only have 50 Democrats. Now, Bernie is not going to vote for it any way, shape, or form. So, if we have 45 or 49, Democrats, don't you think there will be at least 15 or more of Republicans who have always wanted, and knowing that we can't run this country, when it takes us 10 years to build something, and other countries around the world are going into (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But Republicans say they haven't seen anything. They haven't seen any legislation. \uc2 Shelley Moore Capito\uc2  says, "Since our calls for action and offers to see legislative tax from the permitting deal remain unheated, Republicans are now introducing this legislation today to deliver solutions to the roadblocks, delays, and postponements of key infrastructure projects around the country." Specifically, when it comes to permitting.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Right. And basically, it's in final -- they call it basically they're editing. Now, the bill has been written, OK? And they're editing going through it line by line, because you want to make sure when it goes into the C.R., it's in the proper form. That's what they're doing.\par \par \uc2 Now, that should be coming out, hopefully soon.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: For people at home, we're talking about the inside politics about how to get these deals done.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Yes. Right.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: They look at what they're seeing at the table. And inflation is not slowing. And the CPI report was bad to them.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Bret, I said a year and a half ago, and no one paid attention to me, I said inflation is real. Inflation will cripple you, you got to bring it to your knees.\par \par \uc2 And in the areas of the state, or areas of the country that have people who are struggling every day to make ends meet, you throw another six-eight percent on top of everything else. So, almost -- it's almost impossible for this (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But isn't it disingenuous to call this the Inflation Reduction Act?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: No, what you have at -- no.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: If we're looking at a CPI that actually goes up.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: It's the only thing that we have ever done that has a chance to really fight inflation.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Experts say that it's not going to fight inflation at the beginning, it's may over the long term. But there are independent, nonpartisan groups that look at it and say, it's not. And then, Senator, you add on to the student debt relief.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Horrible.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Which then erases any deficit reduction that this administration was touting,\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I had no control. And do -- I do not support that. I think it's wrong. Basically, I've told the people in West Virginia when they called and said, I need relief on my student debt. My staff would be able to give them a list of services they could do for our country or for the state that would offset. I think that's the best way to approach it.\par \par \uc2 That's not done. I don't approve of this the way it was done, I think it's harmful. But with that, you're going to sit back and not do anything, when we need energy produced. I'm going to sit back and say, you're the Energy Committee, you have a chance to write a bill that gives us energy security, it makes it really produce more gasoline, earn more oil, produce more gasoline produce more natural gas, more drilling.\par \par \uc2 Also, while we're investing into clean technology.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Are Democrats going to pass this?\par \par \uc2 Are Democrats going to pass this?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I hope Democrats and Republicans will come to their senses, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But you're going to need Republicans. Right?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: We do need.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I mean, in the House, too.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: In the house, too, we're going to need.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Are you calling Republicans in the House?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Absolutely, yes, we've been working with. I'm just saying, listen, put your politics aside. If you're mad at me or whatever, take me out of this equation. The bottom line is, you have a piece of legislation in front of you that's been passed.\par \par \uc2 Permitting is something we've always tried to do. All of us together. And I've been there with you. And why can't we look at that. We got a chance and it will never happen again. Not in the next decade, maybe not in my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 They were ever get a chance to pass permitting. Because usually Democrats have not supported permitting reform. This is one time, they benefit by it, and they see it but still yet, there are some -- there are some reluctant. No matter what -- any kind of situation to do anything that looks like it might help fossil. Well, I'm sorry.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Let me -- let me ask you two more politics questions.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Democrats in these primaries are spending $53 million plus in nine states to support MAGA candidates that they think will be more appealing to run against, even while saying that they are a threat to democracy. What do you think of that tactic?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I think that both parties attack that way. And they're both strategies on both sides. I think it's ridiculous. I said, let me fight my own battle. If you're going to invest, invest in me, and I'll fight my battle. Because you know what --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Vice President -- Vice President Harris said this weekend, the southern border is, "secure".\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: It's wrong. She is dead wrong on that. And I have said this, if we don't secure it, I voted every time for the wall. But we need the wall and a lot more. Technology, more agents. The 2013 immigration bill was still the best piece of legislation I think, that we've ever had before us. We couldn't get it passed through the Republican House at that time because of some politics involved there. And they -- the amnesty people were, you know, shot in the world embassy.\par \par \uc2 That piece of legislation would have corrected everything we have going wrong. But for anybody, the vice president, press -- anybody who say our borders are secured, that is not accurate. I've been there. It's wrong.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  introducing this national abortion ban for pregnancies after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: I've signed on to when it was 20 weeks. I've been pro-life at my good with exceptions in case of incest, rape, and life of the mother. I don't know where Lindsey is going with this. He came to me and he said Joe can you sign with the 20-weeks? I said, yes, I can sign on to the 20 week ban, as long as we have the exceptions built in. He said, fine. I haven't talked to him about this. And I'm sure you will, I haven't seen it yet.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Do you think politics is going to get better or worse?\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: You'd like to think it can get much worse. But it seems to get more toxic all the time. We better start saying what can we do for our country? That's all.\par \par \uc2 No matter what political hit you're going to take, if you have a chance to make something better, and you're afraid it's going to hurt your ratings, or your politics, and you put politics in front of our country, that's what's wrong.\par \par \uc2 And I don't hear that word said I think this is good for our country. It's always about what about the November election? What about the next cycle, Bret? You better be worried about what about the next cycle for the country, about America, how we bring it together.\par \par \uc2 And I tell people this, it's not the people in this country that are divided. It's the politics here that we're dividing. We're making you pick a side.\par \par \uc2 And then, basically, rewarding bad behavior by sending money to those people who are more outrageous. This is about our country, the United States of America, not the divided, and I don't care what side of the fence you're on. We've got to come together.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Senator Joe Manchin, we always appreciate you coming in.\par \par \uc2 MANCHIN: It's good to be with you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the death of former \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  Special Counsel \uc2 Ken Starr\uc2 , we look back at his storied career.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Breaking tonight, as we mentioned at the top of the program, the man who became the nemesis for then President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 , his wife, and by extension, millions of Democrats, has died. But \uc2 Ken Starr\uc2 's life and career was much more than just about that.\par \par \uc2 Here's Fox News Chief Legal Correspondent and anchor of "FOX NEWS SUNDAY", Shannon Bream.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEN STARR\uc2 , FORMER BILL CLINTON SPECIAL COUNSEL: The law is sacred. The facts have integrity, and we're going to go about our job.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON BREAM, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (voice over): \uc2 Kenneth Starr\uc2  wore many professional hats in a career that attracted equal amounts praise and criticism.\par \par \uc2 As a federal judge, government prosecutor, private lawyer, author and educator, Starr was unafraid to take on new challenges and defend his record against critics.\par \par \uc2 Best known as the independent counsel investigating President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and members of his administration, he took full advantage of his broad authority to probe an alleged cover up of the president's sexual relationship with White House intern \uc2 Monica Lewinsky\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 STARR: The facts will come out and the truth will come out.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: His findings led to the detailed Starr Reports, the 1998 impeachment and eventual Senate acquittal of a sitting president. Both men wound up sharing the Time Magazine Man of the Year title.\par \par \uc2 The Whitewater and impeachment saga divided the nation and much of the liberal anger was directed at the independent counsel that he was pursuing a political agenda with self-righteous zeal.\par \par \uc2 His defenders said Starr was a straight shooter doing his civic duty as he saw fit under impossible circumstances.\par \par \uc2 STARR: We are going by the book, we want the truth. We want all the truth, we want it completely.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: \uc2 Kenneth Winston Starr\uc2  was born in 1946 in Texas, raised in San Antonio, the son of a preacher. After law school, he rose quickly in his career, a clerked for Chief Justice Warren Burger of the Supreme Court, a Reagan Justice Department lawyer and then a federal appeals court judge.\par \par \uc2 STARR: There is only one Supreme Court under the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: In 1990, he was a finalist for a Supreme Court seat that went to David Souter. Sources at the time said some Bush administration officials feared Starr would be to independence and not reliably conservative. It was Souter ironically who carved out a liberal legacy on the bench, but Starr long maintained his role as a judge was to issue a rigid judicial philosophy.\par \par \uc2 STARR: I am now privileged to serve.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: Starr was also President Bush's Solicitor General, eventually arguing three dozen cases before the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 His reputation and resume eventually led Starr to the independent counsel role, investigating Arkansas land deals and eventually a series of controversies in the Clinton White House.\par \par \uc2 His Starr Report concluded the president's involvement in Whitewater and other matters, "May constitute grounds for impeachment, for perjury, obstruction of justice, witness tampering, and abuse of power."\par \par \uc2 In a 2018 memoir of that time, Star wrote and indulgent and prosperous nation readily forgave \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and instead blamed the prosecutor, that would be me.\par \par \uc2 After he resigned the independent counsel job, Starr continued his eclectic pursuits as a private lawyer, he separately represented accused child sex trafficker Jeffrey Epstein, the NRA and \uc2 ACLU\uc2  and worked Pro Bono on behalf of death row inmates.\par \par \uc2 STARR: Welcome to \uc2 Pepperdine University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BREAM: He headed \uc2 Pepperdine University\uc2 's law school then became president of \uc2 Baylor University\uc2  before resigning under pressure in 2016 amid a sexual assault scandal involving student athletes.\par \par \uc2 And Starr was on President Trump's legal team during his first impeachment trial in 2020.\par \par \uc2 STARR: Were crimes alleged in the articles and the common law of presidential impeachment? In Nixon, yes. In Clinton, yes. Here, no.\par \par \uc2 BREAM: \uc2 Ken Starr\uc2  was a renaissance man who forged a path of civic responsibility and judicial independence, whose remarkably varied lifespan the orbits of law and politics. Unapologetic and candid on the rule of law in American society.\par \par \uc2 In Washington, Shannon Bream, Fox News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Ken Starr\uc2  was 76 years old.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Welcome back to today's SPECIAL REPORT midterm watch. This is the final primary day of the midterm primary season. We are 55 days until the 2022 midterm elections, and we have three primaries today -- Delaware, New Hampshire, Rhode Island, some high stakes contests there.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to New Hampshire first. The Senate race there trying to unseat Democratic Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 , one of four Senate Democrats up for reelection this year in states that President Biden carried by fewer than 10 percentage points in 2020. Republicans, the frontrunners vying for this effort to unseat Hassan, retired Army General Don Bolduc, New Hampshire Senate President Chuck Morse, and former Londonderry town manager Kevin Smith. They believe these two are running neck and neck at the top.\par \par \uc2 In New Hampshire second's House district, Republicans are hoping to unseat Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Annie Kuster\uc2 . Leading the pack, you've got Keene Mayor George Hansel and businessman Robert Burns. Now a Democrat super PAC has bought ad time trying to boost Burns who is seen by Democrats as a less formidable candidate for Kuster in November, and that's actually one of the major stories this election cycle. Democrat spending on Republican primary candidates, MAGA candidates that they think they can beat, they have spent more than $53 million across nine states to do that while calling them a threat to democracy.\par \par \uc2 Meantime in New Hampshire's swing district, this is House district one, Republicans competing to take on vulnerable incumbent Democrat \uc2 Chris Pappas\uc2  in November. They include two former Trump staffers, Matt Mowers and Karoline Leavitt.\par \par \uc2 Inflation a big story across all of these races. A recent Gallup poll, 56 percent of Americans experiencing severe or moderate financial hardship due to the price increases.\par \par \uc2 Let's go to another state today, Rhode Island. Republicans are aiming to flip a seat to their control for the first time since 1991 in Rhode Island. They hope former Cranston mayor \uc2 Allan Fung\uc2 , Asian American, can do it.\par \par \uc2 Let's go out to New Mexico. Education there, particularly in the pandemic era, a major issue in the New Mexico governor's race. The Democrat incumbent faces a tough reelection battle against a former TV meteorologist. Right now, we rate this contest as a toss-up. Correspondent Mark Meredith is out in Albuquerque tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALICIA OTERO, PARENT: She's already in fourth grade and she still struggles to read simple words.\par \par \uc2 MARK MEREDITH, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Alicia Otero, an outspoken Republican, says her daughter Delilah (ph) and countless children all across New Mexico are still reeling from the effects of remote learning prompted by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 OTERO: They had remote learning and there was no instruction.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Newly released test scores paint a bleak picture of New Mexico's education standing, with only 35 percent of students in grades three through eight proficient in English and 25 percent in math. Former TV weatherman, now Republican gubernatorial candidate, Mark Ronchetti calls the rur89s embarrassing.\par \par \uc2 MARK RONCHETTI, (R) NEW MEXICO GUBERNATORIAL NOMINEE: We kept our kids out of school longer than any other state around us. And then when it came time to get them back in, we had no plan to catch them up.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: His opponent, the incumbent Democratic Governor \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2  insists education is improving, especially with teacher salaries on the rise. Her campaign surrogate, Democratic state senator Mimi Stewart, a retired teacher herself, says the state is addressing learning loss.\par \par \uc2 MIMI STEWART, (D) NEW MEXICO STATE SENATE: Yes, our scores are down. Every other state scores are down. They are going to go back up. It will take us a couple of years. You never do anything fast in education.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: Stewart says while education is important, she believes New Mexico voters will be focused on other matters come November.\par \par \uc2 STEWART: It's not the number one issue in this race. I think the number one issue is women's reproductive health.\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH: But Ronchetti and Republicans are betting big education will energize voters fed up with the state's track record.\par \par \uc2 RONCHETTI: I think everybody know in this state knows we are at a crisis point.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MEREDITH (on camera): Some new fundraising data out today shows the Democratic governor slightly outraising the Republican Ronchetti in this latest filing period. But both parties are still expected to spend a lot of time, money, and energy here in this race, and some notable Republicans are even expected to be in New Mexico between now and Election Day. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Mark Meredith live in Albuquerque. Mark, thanks.\par \par \uc2 The former chief of security at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  says there is an opportunity for foreign intelligence services to place an undercover spy at that company. Peter Zatko told the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  today there was at least one foreign agent, in his words, on the company's payroll. He also says his employers failed to adequately protect the data of their customers, possibly leading to a national security threat.\par \par \uc2 Breaking tonight, for the first time in a long time we are learning of new activity from the special counsel investigating the origins of the Trump- Russia probe. \uc2 John Durham\uc2 's office is now going after a suspect accused of helping concoct a false narrative designed to cripple the 2016 campaign of then candidate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Correspondent David Spunt is at the Justice Department tonight. Good evening, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Bret. That suspect is Igor Danchenko, and according to this filing made by the special counsel's office, and the judge approved to unseal some of this so the jury can hear during the trial next month, we are learning that Igor Danchenko was a paid informant of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  from March 2017 until October 2020. That's something we had not heard before. Igor Danchenko is a Russian national living in the United States. He's facing five charges of making a false statement to an \uc2 FBI\uc2  official. He was charged last November and served as a top source for former British spy \uc2 Christopher Steele\uc2 's dossier on then candidate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . That dossier turned out to be bogus.\par \par \uc2 John Durham\uc2  wants evidence shown to jurors outlining what he believes is a pattern of federal officials disparaging Trump during his candidacy and establishing bias. In his motion, Durham indicates he wants emails presented to a jury next month at trial. The firing reveals it was Danchenko who relayed information to \uc2 Christopher Steele\uc2  about Trump and the alleged, quote, lured sexual activity at the Ritz Carlton in Moscow. According to Durham, Danchenko lied about his source of those allegations, allegations that were never proven true.\par \par \uc2 Attorneys for Danchenko recently moved to dismiss the case, but ultimately Federal Judge Anthony Trenga said that's not going to happen. It was a Hail Mary. The trial kicks off October 11th, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow all of that. David, late this afternoon on another element, the magistrate judge in the Mar-a-Lago raid case released previously unseen information from that affidavit.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: He did. This is Judge Bruce Reinhart, the magistrate in south Florida dealing with the raid of Mar-a-Lago on August 8th. I want to show you two documents right here. This is the exact same page. You see the black lines here, or the black lines on this. Right here this is all new information. It deals with some of the grand jury information, specifically something new we are learning. We knew the feds, Bret, they wanted some surveillance video of the basement, the ground floor of Mar-a-Lago. The storage room is where some of those documents were located. We're told now for the first time that the feds wanted all video from January through June, and we're told the Trump team turned over that video on July 6th, just a few months ago. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We'll follow that as well. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Queen Elizabeth II makes a final journey to England. We'll have a live report from London as activities marking her life and legacy continue to a funeral all week.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Ukrainian troops are keeping up the pressure on retreating Russian forces. Ukraine's president says his troops have retaken more than 2,300 square miles of territory in just a matter of weeks. Officials are releasing footage showing their forces burning Russian flags and inspecting abandoned charred tanks.\par \par \uc2 The coffin containing the body of Queen Elizabeth II is in London tonight. It was flown to the British capital today from Scotland. Thousands of people filed past the casket over the past two days to pay their respects and then lined the streets during the late afternoon procession. Chief correspondent Jonathan Hunt is in London tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN HUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: On the final stages of queen Elizabeth's final journey, her coffin was carried from a \uc2 Royal Air Force\uc2  C- 17 at RAF Northolt, and then driven the 15 miles to Buckingham Palace. Thousands lining the streets largely in respectful silence, a few breakings out in applause as the hearse passed by.\par \par \uc2 The queen's only daughter, Princess Anne, has been ever present as the casket traveled from the royal residence of Balmoral in Scotland. The princess royal, as Anne is officially known, released a statement today, saying it has been an honor and privilege to accompany her mother, and she added, quote, "We may have been reminded how much of her presence and contribution to our national identity we took for granted." Princess Anne then joined other members of the royal family, including her brother \uc2 King Charles\uc2 , as the coffin arrived at Buckingham Palace.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the day, \uc2 King Charles\uc2  visited Northern Ireland, shaking the hand of the vice president of Sinn Fein, the nationalist party that believes Northern Ireland should not be part of the U.K. but part of a united Republic of Ireland. The handshake recalled a similar moment in 2012 when Queen Elizabeth shook the hand of \uc2 Martin McGuinness\uc2  then a senior Sinn Fein leader but previously a member of the Irish Republic Army, the same IRA that killed the Queen's cousin, Lord Louis Mountbatten in 1979.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): The Queen's palace will travel from Buckingham Palace to Westminster Hall tomorrow, transported by gun carriage, followed on foot by \uc2 King Charles\uc2 . And there is confirmation tonight that both the king's sons William and Harry will walk alongside their father. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jonathan Hunt live in London. Jonathan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the latest inflation report and the White House reaction, plus reaction to my interview with Democratic Senator Joe Manchin.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. At least 49 Armenian soldiers are killed in a large-scale attack from Azerbaijan. Armenia says artillery barrage and drone strikes after midnight. Azerbaijan claims its forces were responding to large scale provocations.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Ibiza, Spain. One of the big stories there tonight, hundreds of people participate in a Spanish bull chasing festival, but under orders that the animal should not be harmed with spears or darts, not gored. Animal rights protests have led to changes in the Toro de la Vega festival. However, the bull was still killed after the event and will be sold as prime beef.\par \par \uc2 Just some other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (SINGING)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I've seen fire and I've seen --\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The number one economic priority if they win back the House is to repeal the Inflation Reduction Act. This law is going to make a big difference for middle class and working class families. Jobs are up. People are back to work.\par \par \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 , (D-CA) HOUSE SPEAKER: Your extraordinary leadership has made this glorious day possible. That's an applause line.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R-KY) SENATE MINORITY LEADER: They could not look more out of touch if they tried.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: It was a little surreal today with the celebration on the South Lawn of the White House the same day that the consumer price index came out. The inflation numbers in August, 8.3 percent up year over year. Core inflation 6.3 percent according to the \uc2 U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . Food inflation, you can take a look down the list of all of the things you buy at the grocery store, going up, and each time, this time even more. And you can see eggs at 39.8 percent.\par \par \uc2 Meantime, you had the markets racketing to this because it was worse than expected, more than 1,200 points down on the Dow, 630 on the Nasdaq.\par \par \uc2 Let's bring in our panel, Byron York, chief political correspondent of "The Washington Examiner," Ben Domenech, editor at large of "The Spectator," host of "The Ben Domenech Podcast" on FOX News Radio, and \uc2 Mara Liasson\uc2 , national political correspondent of National Public Radio. Was it bad timing? Was it bad timing, Mara? It just seemed like it just hit a deaf tone.\par \par \uc2 MARA LIASSON\uc2 , NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, NATIONAL PUBLIC RADIO: Bad timing and bad planning, because the Inflation Reduction Act actually has much less to do with reducing inflation than all the other things that are in it the Democrats like, like climate change and reducing the cost of prescription drugs. But Inflation subject du jour. It's the thing that people care about. It's the number one economic indicator because it effects everyone when they go to the pump or the grocery store. So it was called the Inflation Reduction Act, and they held this ceremony to celebrate it on the day that inflation turns out to still be with us and it's pretty bad, even though we have been hearing for several weeks how prices at the pump are coming down. It doesn't seem to have affected the overall.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We're going to have a piece on energy on Friday. But the reduction of the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, Ben, is also a part of that, and that's really low now. But thoughts on the overall on the day?\par \par \uc2 BEN DOMENECH, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: The sad sort of bittersweet boomer rock adult contemporary of James Taylor seems to me to be a fitting piece of music to articulate the nature of this presidency. It's very -- it's very sad and depressing and something that could play behind you on an elevator music. "The Simpsons," by the way, literally did this. They had the exact same song with James Taylor in an episode where it was designed to encourage astronauts in space. Didn't really work.\par \par \uc2 The thing I think is happening here is unfortunately this White House does not know how to grapple with the inflation problem. It's the fundamental issue that they have to deal with at the moment, and they don't really know how to deal with it. They have approached it in many different ways. They've tried to do a reset. They've tried to adjust things. They've tried to have these types of events. Nothing is actually dealing with the fundamentals that concern so many Americans.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Some things coming out of the White House just have not been accurate when it comes to these numbers. And one thing today the president was saying, we spend but we pay, about Democrats, saying that the deficit is going to be reduced. Actually, the Biden administration has added nearly $5 trillion to the deficits according to the committee for a responsible federal budget. And then you add almost a half-a-trillion more on the student loan forgiveness.\par \par \uc2 BYRON YORK, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, "THE WASHINGTON EXAMINER": I think this was a really, actually an important day because for a month or more the story had been rising optimism among Democrats, that they were going to do better in the midterms than they thought. Maybe they weren't going to get slaughtered. Maybe they'd even hold the Senate or maybe even the House. And the reason they thought they could do it is because they thought inflation was going to be going down. They thought they could capitalize on the abortion decision from the Supreme Court. And they thought they could make the election more about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  than they could have before his latest legal problems.\par \par \uc2 And I think today was the day that inflation and the economy in general as an issue just came roaring back. And they could not ignore the fact that this is still and always has been the public's number one issue.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Which is another reason why Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  coming out today with a proposed piece of legislation for nationalizing an abortion ban after 15 weeks seemed also weirdly timed from a Republican point of view. Here is "Axios," "Graham's plan comes less than two months out from the midterm elections, with abortion expected to be an important issue for voters following the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade." I know a number of Republicans were saying, easy.\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Yes. There are a number of pro-life leaders who said please, let's not push for a national abortion ban right now. Let's let each state, the kind of thing we have been pushing for for 50 years, let's let each state work it out. Well, they didn't. This is going to be a big target for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So is that the battle of November, Ben, the battle between how much Dobbs and the overturning of Roe v. Wade versus how much you feel when you go to the grocery store paying more?\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: There is a narrative on the part of a lot of different reporters and Democrats that basically says these culture war issues are going to save us. They are going to prevent us from having the kind of wave that a lot of us anticipated.\par \par \uc2 But I think that when you actually look at the poll data, when you look at what is going on, the dynamics in key battleground states, that just isn't born out. It's not necessarily true. And I think they would like it to be true. They are going to keep saying that it's true. But I think we shouldn't be gaslit into believing that this is necessarily true. Instead, I think, fundamentals are likely to assert themselves once again, and those economic issues are going to drive voters to the polls.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: By the way, we just found out of that the president is going to Delaware tonight to vote in Delaware's primary. He was there over the weekend and could have voted over the weekend, and there's all kinds of social media saying this is $177,000 trip to go vote.\par \par \uc2 YORK: On those fundamentals, speaking of president, the historic factor is that a president's job approval rating is the single best predictor of how his party is going to do in the midterm elections. And here again, I was talking about Democratic optimism, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's approval rating, Real Clear Politics average did go from a little under 37 up to about 42, almost six points it went up. A lot of optimism among Democrats. But it's still 42. And presidents who have job approval ratings below 50 percent lose a lot of seats. It has never been otherwise.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So back to where we started and the Inflation Reduction Act celebration, is there some disconnect with the reality when people look at that and say, well, that's just not happening?\par \par \uc2 LIASSON: Well, see, the problem is once you call it the Inflation Reduction Act, you kind of have to show how it will reduce inflation, which it really doesn't for a very long time. You could have called it the climate change and prescription drug price act, and then they would have some real things to point to that are very popular.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Meantime, Joe Manchin needs Republicans to get this permitting deal through.\par \par \uc2 DOMENECH: Very much. Very much so. I think Manchin is in a very difficult position in so many different ways. And I think that he is going to be, if he decides to run again, very much a target of national Republicans for all the reasons.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: He's not up this time. All right, panel, thanks.\par \par \uc2 It's time for "Tuesday Tweets." Horace Granero asks "What has been your favorite handoff from the 5?" Not last night, but there have been a lot of great ones. I think this was one of the favorites.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jesse, I'm here, I just can't seem to get rid of this filter. I'm here though.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I'm not a bear. OK. Thanks, Jesse.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, it was funny at the time. It was during COVID. We ran out of things.\par \par \uc2 But up next, Ken Johnson wants to know "How many times after speaking with a guest do you think to yourself, oh darn, I wish I would have asked them this?" It happens quite a lot, actually, because you come to an end of an interview, and you say I had five more questions and I wanted to ask this. I wanted to ask some more specifics, but we run out of times sometimes. And we want to give you the most on a host of different topics. We'll continue to do that fair and balanced, and bring all sides to the table.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, brand new FOX polling on the midterm elections, including the generic ballots, top issues, and which party can handle the big challenges. Also, I want to tell you that my book, "To Rescue the Republic, President Grant, A Nation in Crisis," now out in paperback today you can get it.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for the SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. Here's Jesse. Hey, buddy.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220914ei9d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 President Biden Held A Celebration In The White House For Passing The Inflation Reduction Act All The While The Stock Market Tumbled And The Released Inflation Report Increased 0.1 Percent Even As Gas Prices Fell; Judge \uc2 Ken Starr\uc2 , The Independent Counsel Pursuing \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  In Whitewater Scandal And The \uc2 Monica Lewinsky\uc2  Scandal Is Dead At The Age Of 76; Supreme Court Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  Defends The Supreme Court After \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Lashes Out And Calls Them An Activist Court And Questioning Its Integrity; Refusing To Return: 1,300+ NYT Staffers Refuse To Go Back To Office; Playing Favorites: Media Ripped Texas For Power Outages, Went Easy On CA; Steve's Simply Happy Cookbook On Sale Now\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeanine Pirro, Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters, Dana Perino, Greg  Gutfeld, David Spunt, Steve Doocy \par \uc2 8,299 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: The Five\par \uc2 FOXFIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NEIL CAVUTO, FOX NEWS HOST: \uc2 Ken Starr\uc2  was the one behind that investigation that prompted that entire impeachment affair. Dead at age 76.\par \par \uc2 JEANINE PIRRO, FOX NEWS HOST: Hello, everyone. I'm Judge Jeanine Pirro along with Jessica Tarlov, Jesse Watters, Dana Perino, and Greg Gutfeld. It's 5:00 in New York City and this is THE FIVE.\par \par \uc2 President Biden throwing a tone-deaf party for the ages. On the same day the stock market had its worst day since June 2020, thanks to a worse than expected inflation report, Biden and Democrats thought it would be appropriate to hold a massive White House event to celebrate the passage of his so-called Inflation Reduction Act, which actually does nothing to solve the problem. Biden bringing 2,000 of his closest friends to the south lawn, including a soothing performance by James Taylor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: And how about James Taylor? A voice that heals our soul and unites the nation and a good friend.\par \par \uc2 Exactly four weeks ago today, I signed the Inflation Reduction Act into law. The single most important legislation passed in the Congress to combat inflation.\par \par \uc2 The soul of America is vibrant, the future of America is bright, and the promise of America is real.\par \par \uc2 This bill cut costs for families. Help reduce inflation at the kitchen table.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: But it didn't. Prices of foods spiked to their highest margins in more than four decades. And Biden's tone-deaf event wouldn't be complete without the inaccurate ramblings of good old Chuck and Nancy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Whoa! Look at this crowd! So, this is a great and celebratory day.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI (D-CA): Inflation Reduction Act so beautifully named for all that it does. Your extraordinary leadership has made this glorious day possible. I -- that's an applause line.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, Greg, inflation rose higher than expected in August, keeping prices painful for all Americans, and yet, it was higher, in fact, than predicted, and yet they are over there celebrating. What are they celebrating about?\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS HOST: I don't know. It's like underwear at a flea market, I'm not buying it. James Taylor again? Why are we torturing people? They did this to the French after a terrorist attack. I mean, come on! At least get somebody listenable. I could make some suggestions, may be the Melvin's, Slayer, Misfits, something good.\par \par \uc2 I'm pleading with Republicans. You got to get your act together. You're never going to get an opportunity like this. This is like the inmates are running the asylum. Nothing they say is true. It's just like, if you can't beat these guys, then you don't deserve to win. And it's sad. I go back to that rule. You bet on the person who wants it more.\par \par \uc2 And sometimes it feels like the Democrats just want the stuff more because they're just more ruthless. I mean, they're calling half of America terrorists. That means you came to play, right? They're making it so that if you decide to challenge them on anything, you're considered unpatriotic.\par \par \uc2 So, I would love -- and I would love to see the Republicans kind of get as nasty as they are. And if you look at what the Dems are doing, which is elevating, you know, the far-right candidates. Again, to remind you that politics, that's the Democrat's line of work. It's full-time. I mean, for Republicans, it used to be, you know, politics is something you do while running your business, but the Dems' politics is their business and that's why they're better at it.\par \par \uc2 It's just like I'm very sanguine. Is that the word? I learned that yesterday. I feel like, you know, that the Dems are now stepping it up and the Republicans are on the back foot.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, Jesse, meanwhile, it's the schizophrenic, what Greg is talking about, it's the schizophrenic approach where they're putting all this money into the right-wing candidates that are running against the Democrat candidate and yet they are trashing the right-wing candidate. What is the agenda here?\par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS HOST: Well, they are trying to win the Senate races with that, but permission to make an analogy, judge?\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: This would be like your wife leaving you at the altar and then just being like, you know what, let's go dancing and cut some cake, right? This would be like \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  on the night of the 2016 election firing off the fireworks because they were already paid for. Real people see a number drop like this and they cancel the party.\par \par \uc2 But politicians can't do that because the media covers everything they do. Because the media would then say, Biden cancels Inflation Reduction Act party after inflation soars. So, instead they have to pretend like everything is normal. And that's why this country is looking at them like they're crazy because you're in a recession, the Dow just dropped a thousand points, but it does not matter if you live in fantasy land, judge. You know, doesn't matter!\par \par \uc2 You can say that the Inflation Reduction Act reduces inflation by spending a trillion dollars. You can say we're not in a recession. You can say whatever you want. It gives me these vibes about Putin. Remember, did you here yesterday, they just lost a ton of territory in northeast Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: What was Vlad doing? He was at an unveiling of a Ferris wheel. It's like nothing matters if you just pretend like everything is fine. And until today, I thought the Republicans were going to lose the Senate, not anymore.\par \par \uc2 With this inflation number that hits and these Senate candidates that are falling apart and all the money the Republicans are injecting into these races, I'm a little more confident now, except \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  comes out today and talks about abortion, but everybody else is talking about inflation.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TARLOV, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: You stole my --\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Okay. Well, before we talk about that let's talk about the fact thing and that, you know, we got these Democrat Senate candidates, whether it's Fetterman who is refusing in Pennsylvania to debate Oz, or Mark Kelly who fumbled the other day, yesterday I believe, and he couldn't give any kind of a comment on whether or not \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is doing a good job and he says, I don't write report cards. We have that sound? Let's take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Is he done a good job, do you think?\par \par \uc2 MARK KELLY, SENATE CANDIDATE IN ARIZONA: Hey, you know, first of all, it's not my job to give him a report card. I would say, you know, mixed reviews.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: So, if he came out here and held an event, you'd attend that event (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Oh, I would certainly consider it.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Consider is not --\par \par \uc2 KELLY: I would probably look at it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yeah, he was fumbling right there. And that's his party. So, when we got this, so how come the Republicans aren't jumping all over this?\par \par \uc2 DANA PERINO, FOX NEWS HOST: Well, I think that they are. So, I'm going to give you some good news so you don't have to be sanguine anymore. So, last Tuesday when we came off of Labor Day, it did feel like the Democrats had the wind behind their back, right, and it was like wow, they're doing great. But by Friday, all these other polls have come out and like, oh, actually the Republicans are hanging in there pretty well.\par \par \uc2 They are -- they have less money than the Democrats and yet they are still able to hold really steady. People don't really focus until after Labor Day. And then the White House, you schedule events based on the calendar. You're looking at, oh, okay, well, the jobs numbers is coming out that day.\par \par \uc2 They didn't know what the inflation number was going to be, but why did they schedule the Inflation Reduction Act on the same day the inflation is coming up? That makes them look ridiculous. And everybody that's out there, great events, lots of fun, beautiful in September, a lot of those are staffers, okay? That's not Americans that are dealing with this or looking at their 401(K).\par \par \uc2 And here you have the White House, this juxtaposition is really strange. They're popping champagne for a bill that will take working people's money and give it to rich people so they can buy electric vehicles. And then there is the student loan debt. We know how that is going. And a lot of these races are tightening and people are forgetting. Remember what happened in New Jersey? There wasn't a single poll in all of New Jersey, and that Republican came this close to winning.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Right.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And that hasn't changed. Think about Youngkin. Think about education. Crime is a big issue. The border is a big issue. And this -- you have the Democrats saying maybe our polls are not as good as we think they are because their oversampling Democrats. So, I'm not saying that the Republicans are going to run away with it, but I do believe they are in better shape than we thought a week ago.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: I feel less sanguine.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, it's my turn.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: You know, Jessica, the truth is that the Dems seemed, as Dana said, they seemed to be in good shape. They had a good summer. They had a legislative success, but the truth is that, you know, the inflation is five times higher since Biden took office. Prices of food is not going down even though the price of gas is going down. So, how do they play this going into November?\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Well, I think the optics of today were absolutely abominable, and I feel very bad for Senator Joe Manchin who is going to be on with Bret after when we get on off air and he wants to talk about this act which he supported and they went to work really hard to get him on board with it, and then he's going to have to talk about, you know, the Dow ticker versus the president talking about this. So, I would have rescheduled especially since it was majority staffers. And you know, Thursday might have been a better day for this.\par \par \uc2 The races were always going to tighten. That's just what happens. People wake up, they start paying attention. And I know, Dana, you saw the piece in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " as well, Nate Cohn, one of the pollsters did an evaluation of the races based on the mistakes that were made in 2020. So, where Wisconsin was supposed to be a win by like eight points and it ended up being one point.\par \par \uc2 And when he re-did the tables, the Dems pick up one seat, it was the Fetterman seat in all of this, and that's still a pick up. I would be plenty happy if we ended up with one extra seat. I would be overjoyed if we ended up with a couple more, and I think it is feasible somewhere like Ohio, for instance.\par \par \uc2 But when you see some of these results that Mandela Barnes is up nine points for instance over Ron Johnson, I don't believe that. I think it's going to be a really close race. I will say, to Jesse's point about \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , just, I guess, waking up and saying, oh, I should go out by myself and say we're going to have a national abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 When I actually saw it previewed last night on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , I thought, oh, no, this is going to be terrible because he will be helping out women in these states with these highly restrictive bans. But no, it's even worse than that. The highly restrictive states get to keep their policies and he's just going after blue states or people with more lenient policies.\par \par \uc2 And there's been reporting after reporting about the spike in women that are registering, how angry they are, and that's Democrats, independent women, and Republican women. And \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 's face said it all when he got asked about it and he was -- what do you think of (inaudible).\par \par \uc2 PERINO: But that's always his face.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yeah, he looks so okay.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's his face.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Sure.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: But can I say \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  didn't go alone. He did have Marjorie Dannenfelser who is the head of the \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  who wanted --\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: But he doesn't have the votes behind him. I mean --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's fine, but he has introduced this very bill every year for 20 years.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: But it used to be 20 at weeks and now he's gone to 15. Also, I don't want to hear about --\par \par \uc2 PERINO: (Inaudible).\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I understand, but I don't want to hear from a man about abortion. I certainly don't want to hear it from a man who hasn't had kids about abortion. He should never have done this.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: So, did we -- men have no opinion on abortion. Okay, you have no opinion on -- can we pick something?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Vasectomies.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: I don't -- you do you.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: There you go.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: How about war? You have no opinion on war because we send boys off to war for generations.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Ladies go now and they do a great job.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: I think that they can have an opinion on abortion.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You know what I mean.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: And you know what meant, too.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: No, but I know what I mean.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: But I know more than you know.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Get the last word.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: He'll laugh anyway.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Alright. Fox news alert. Fox news alert. \uc2 Ken Starr\uc2 , former Fox News contributor and the special prosecutor whose investigation led to the impeachment of President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  has died at the age of 76. David Spunt has more. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPNDENT: The sad news, \uc2 Ken Starr\uc2 , 76-years-old, well known as the independent counsel probing the alleged cover-up of the affair of former President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and White House intern \uc2 Monica Lewinsky\uc2  back in 1998. He released the Starr Report, that's how many in America knew him, that eventually led to the '98 impeachment and eventual Senate acquittal of then sitting President \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Interestingly enough, both men, Clinton and Starr shared the "Time" magazine man of the year that year. His defenders they said Starr was a straight shooter doing his civic duty as he saw fit under very difficult circumstances.\par \par \uc2 The 76-year-old was born in 1946 in Texas. He was raised in San Antonio, the son of a preacher. Interestingly, I'm looking at his career here. He clerked for Chief Justice Warren Burger on the Supreme Court. He was a Reagan Justice Department lawyer and a federal appeals court judge. He had an impeccable resume.\par \par \uc2 In 1990, he was a finalist for a Supreme Court seat that actually went to David Souter. He was President Bush's solicitor general, eventually arguing three dozen cases before the Supreme Court. Again, most people know him for the Starr Report in 1998. It concluded the president's involvement in Whitewater and other matters "may constitute grounds for impeachment for perjury, obstruction of justice, witness tampering, and abuse of power."\par \par \uc2 And judge, we know that after leaving the public spotlight, he went to \uc2 Pepperdine University\uc2 's law school, then became president of \uc2 Baylor University\uc2  before leaving in 2016. He actually resigned after some pressure involving a scandal involving some student athletes.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Ken Starr\uc2 , 76-years-old, all the way from clerking for the former Chief Justice Warren Burger, to sitting on that appeals court, Judge Starr was well known in many circles. We're told that he died after some complications, and he will be remembered fondly by many across the country.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: David, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 SPUNT: You're welcome.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: And coming up next, the Supreme Court hitting back hard after Kamala attacks it on its legitimacy.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: The Supreme Court not taking Kamala's attacks lying down after the V.P. dissed the high court and said the decision to overturn Roe has made her worried about their integrity.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: I think this is an activist court. We had an established right for almost half a century, which is the right of women to make decisions about their own body, as an extension of what we have decided to be the privacy rights to which all people are entitled. And this court took that constitutional right away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  bashing Kamala's criticism and defending the Supreme Court's legitimacy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , CHIEF JUSTICE OF THE SUPREME COURT: The court has always decided controversial cases and that's the role of the Supreme Court. And that role doesn't change simply because people disagree with this opinion or that opinion. Simply because people disagree with an opinion is not a basis for questioning the legitimacy of the court.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Alright, so this is obviously I guess, it's referring to abortion, so I'm going to go to the guy first. No, what kind of politician, Jesse, would attack an institution like the Supreme Court? This is a threat to democracy. Is she aware that she is putting their lives in danger?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: You know, after someone tried to kill Kavanaugh, she better watch her words.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I'm not usually a word policeman, but they just had a death threat against Cannon, the judge in the spec