usly, it's --\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: I put the man in manchego.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Are you related to Peter Doocy?\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Who?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Peter Doocy. He's in his book a lot.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: He is in that book a lot.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Is some of this keto? Because I've seen cauliflower rice and shaved brussel sprouts, some keto.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: There's a story in the beginning of the book where we update what we've been doing since the last cookbook came out. And I revealed that after the last cookbook was released, I gained a little weight. I went on keto.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Keto.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: And I went on Dry January, and I wound up losing a crazy amount of weight.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Oh, Dry January is the worse.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Wow.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: All right, well, everybody, check out Steve and Kathy Doocy's new book, the Simply Happy Cookbook.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Congratulations. Great book.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And he was saying in the green room, as long as he sells more books than Kilmeade. That's all he cares. "ONE MORE THING" is up next.\par \par \uc2 DOOCY: Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: It's time now for "ONE MORE THING." Jesse?\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Well, Greg decided to litter my set with his birthday balloon so I had a little fun with it. Watch this, last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Let's do some text messages. We got Mark, Michael from Geneseo, New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: There. and then tonight we have a cannabis convention infiltration on "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME."\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK, Greg.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: All right, tonight, we got a great show. I got the wonderful, wonderful Jim Norton, Vivek Ramaswamy, Kat Timpf, and Tyrus.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: That's it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: That's it.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: OK. Today, I went to Fashion Week in New York City. And I was accompanied by Emma Watters, that's Jesse's wife in the middle. And there's Bill Hemmer at the Zang Toi Show. We've got a couple more pictures. If we can move it along because we don't have a lot of time. And there's a (INAUDIBLE) Emma Watters and me. And Cheryl Casone is there along with Anthony Vecchione and Rosanna Scotto. We were at Chara Boni show. And that's the end of that. OK.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Jessica.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Great. A retired nurse is being hailed as a hero after saving the life of a baby who stopped breathing aboard an airplane. Tamara Panzino was on a flight when she heard calls for a doctor over the loudspeaker. She rushed to the back of the plane where she found the blue in the face 3- month-old. Panzino was able to get the baby breathing again by performing a sternal rub which is an aggressive shake of the chest instead of CPR. She has been retired for more than 20 years. Much deserved applause on board. A real hero. Just awesome.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: That's so scary.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: Great, great end of story. Dana.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: OK, control room, can I play my thing with the dog or did they -- OK, so, check out this dog. He was little bit jealous because they brought home a cat. And I love this. He takes the -- look what he does to the cat, the kitten? He's like OK, see you later, buddy.\par \par \uc2 TARLOV: Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: See you later.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Oh, my God. He thinks it's a fish.\par \par \uc2 PERINO: And the cat is, you know, unfazed but that's -- also, Janice Dean has a new podcast called The Janice Dean Podcasts. It's -- I think it's excellent. I think she's so good at radio. She got a great tone, approach, everything. So, I did a little interview with her and that is up now at FoxNewsPodcast.com.\par \par \uc2 PIRRO: Well, I heard you talking about it today. Anyway, thanks for joining us today. I appreciate it. That's it for us. "SPECIAL REPORT" is up next. Hey, Bret!\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FOXFIV0020220914ei9d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Ukraine Retakes Territory As Russians Retreat; Pinched Wallets Amid Recession Fears; Bank Of America Partnership With Royal Family; King Charles III Honors Queen And Devotion To Service; British Public Pays Tribute To Queen Elizabeth II; Democrats Supporting New Hampshire Republican Senate Primary Candidate Who Said 2020 Election Was Stolen; Many Congressmembers Known For Bipartisan Dealmaking Leaving Office; President Trump's Lawyers Claim Criminal Investigation Into Materials Seized From Mar-a-Lago Has Spiraled Out Of Control; Democratic Candidate Competitive In States For Which Polls Overstated Previous Democratic Presidential Candidates Performance; Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  Criticizes \uc2 Supreme Court\uc2  for Overturning Roe Versus Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 Bret Baier, Jacqui Heinrich, Jeff Paul, Alex Hogan, Brit Hume,  Hillary Vaughn, Chad Pergram, David Spunt \par \uc2 8,261 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Special Report with Brett Baier\par \uc2 FNSPBB\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JESSE WATTERS, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): But I was to return for a touchdown, big game, put up a lot of points, defense, and look at that great, though.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, "JESSE WATTERS PRIME TIME: DEMOCRATS DECLARE WAR ON YOU". And I will ceremoniously pop Greg's balloon.\par \par \uc2 GREG GUTFELD, FOX NEWS CHANNEL HOST (on camera): If you can get it, that's going to be great.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: We'll get it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Yes, all right.\par \par \uc2 WATTERS: And you're going to wait until you see what we do to it.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Awesome.\par \par \uc2 Well, that's it for us, SPECIAL REPORT is up next. Hey, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Happy birthday, Greg. Big plans tonight?\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: Thank you. Yes, just me and you in the hot tub.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Thanks. Good evening. Welcome to Washington. I'm Bret Baier, remembering Queen Elizabeth II.\par \par \uc2 A solemn ceremony of Thanksgiving takes place in Scotland. In a filing, for President Trump's lawyers, say the Mar-a-Lago raid investigation is out of control and may not include classified documents, and it's getting nasty in New Hampshire's Republican Senate primary.\par \par \uc2 "BREAKING TONIGHT", President Biden says he's taken America from economic crisis to economic resurgence. The president talked about what he called unprecedented investments in his bipartisan infrastructure law to a group in Boston today.\par \par \uc2 But Americans are still focused on the economy, and specifically record inflation that more than half say is caused financial hardship for their household.\par \par \uc2 White House correspondent Jacqui Heinrich starts us off tonight. From the North Lawn. Good evening, Jacqui.\par \par \uc2 JACQUI HEINRICH, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (on camera): Good evening, Bret.\par \par \uc2 Officials here won't say if inflation has peaked yet. Tomorrow we'll see the numbers which could give us clues about how aggressive the Fed might be in their next set of rate hikes. For now, the president is out defending his spending that his critics say contributed to the problem.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We're going to be spending a lot of money.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (voice-over): President Biden hit the road to argue he's spending taxpayer dollars, where it's needed. Like $62 million at Boston's airport where the international terminal first opened in 1974.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Today, 50 years later, there's over 5.6 million passengers moving through. It means misconnections.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Lost baggage, long lines.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Republicans argue, costs are already too steep.\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): Every American should ask themselves, could they afford to give up one month's salary? Do you realize that's been taken from you because of how high inflation is?\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: A new Gallup poll shows, people are pinching pennies because of inflation. 24 percent say they're only buying essentials. 17 percent cancel a vacation, and 17 percent are driving less.\par \par \uc2 Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  trust the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  to get prices in check but admits aggressive rate hikes could tip the country into a recession.\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2  , UNITED STATES TREASURY SECRETARY: The Fed is going to need great skill and also some good luck.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Biden defending the spending.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: When you hear your Republican friends or anybody else, to tell you boy, they are spending a lot of money. Guess what we cut the deficit this year by over $1 trillion.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: The Committee for a Responsible Federal Budget, calls that a moot point, because Biden student debt cancellation wipes out more than double the deficit savings from the Inflation Reduction Act.\par \par \uc2 REP. DARRELL ISSA (R-CA): The red wave is coming. Let's not force the Fed to keep cranking up interest rates simply because we keep cranking up deficit spending.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Inflation may become a more complex challenge if a national railroad strike begins Friday. The Labor dispute threatening to shut down 30 percent of the country's freight and passenger services, potentially costing $2 billion a day in economic output.\par \par \uc2 Today, Biden personally intervened in the talks and the labor department says contingency planning is already having an impact, and a shutdown would be an unacceptable outcome for our economy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH (on camera): \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  just told Fox, they are preemptively cancelling three long distance routes starting tomorrow, because the passenger trains cross freight line tracks which could shut down without an agreement.\par \par \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  says if that strike happens, almost all services outside the Northeast Corridor could be impacted. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui, President Biden skip the ultra-MAGA rhetoric on this trip. Even calling for unity today.\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Yes, quite the departure from the Biden approach, as of recently, this morning, he had some kind words for the state's Republican governor. And then this afternoon, called for unity.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Gave me my word as a Biden. This cancer moonshot is one of the reasons why I ran for president. It's part of my unity agenda. Cancer does not discriminate red and blue. It doesn't care if you're a Republican or Democrat. Beating cancer is something we can do together.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HEINRICH: Biden was in Boston for the 60th anniversary of President Kennedy's moonshot speech. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jacqui Heinrich, live in the North Lawn. Jacqui, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's president is accusing Russian troops of deliberate and cynical attacks against civilian targets after being forced back by Ukrainian forces. The Ukrainian counter offensive -- counter offensive had another big day of gains today, pushing Russian personnel all the way back to the northeastern border in some places.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Jeff Paul is in Kyiv again tonight.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PAUL, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): An emotional embrace from the people who have suffered the most. Some brought to tears as they welcome Ukrainian soldiers to the Kharkiv region.\par \par \uc2 After more than 200 days of war, they are finally free of Russian occupation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translator): I still cannot believe that they have gone, and we are out of the spirit of death.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: One by one, blue and yellow flags are raised to the tops of buildings and more than 30 territories and counting. Abandoned Russian tanks now dot the sides of roads.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translator): The Russians were here in the morning. Then, at noon, they suddenly started shouting widely and began to run away. Charging off in tanks and armored vehicles.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: All proved the Ukrainian counter offensive is working.\par \par \uc2 However, as Russian troops pulled back, many in the region are left without running water and electricity as strikes continue.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine's President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  urged his people to be strong and patient as they enter a new chapter of the war.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY, PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE (through translator): I believe that this winter is a turning point, and it can lead to the rapidly occupation of Ukraine. We see how the Russian forces are fleeing in different directions.\par \par \uc2 PAUL: The withdrawal is now the source of rare criticism among vocal Russians. From military bloggers and staunch Russian patriots, to hosts on highly watched Russian state media.\par \par \uc2 Even Putin ally and Chechen leader \uc2 Ramzan Kadyrov\uc2  is publicly pointing out the \uc2 Russian Defense Ministry\uc2  's mistakes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 PAUL (on camera): All this happening as engineers at the Zaporizhzhia nuclear power plant have finally been able to safely shut down the last operating reactor. However, as shelling continues in the area, officials warn, they are not in the clear just yet. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Jeff Paul, live in Kyiv. Jeff, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Stocks here in the U.S. rows again today ahead of tomorrow's inflation report. The Dow gained 230. The S&P 500 was up 43, the NASDAQ jumped 154. Let's get some insight on the U.S. economy from \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  CEO, \uc2 Brian Moynihan\uc2  . Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2  , CHAIRMAN, \uc2 BANK OF AMERICA\uc2  : Good to see you, Bret. It's nice to be here.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, you have a very unique perspective on the economy. \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  is huge. You have more than 208,000 employees in the United States around the world.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (voice-over): You serve one out of every two American households. That's an amazing stat. 3.2 million U.S. small businesses and manage more than $3.8 trillion in client balances.\par \par \uc2 BAIER (on camera): So, as you all look at the U.S. economy today, the state of inflation, consumer spending, where do you see it?\par \par \uc2 MOYNIHAN: Well, first of all, as we look at the economy based on those statistics, and that data, we report every month and sort of where we see consumer spending. And so, everybody has acknowledged inflation stuff on people, and you add some segments on that already tonight.\par \par \uc2 But the reality is this, consumer was spending more. In the month of August 2022, they spent 10 percent more than they did in the month of August 2021. And so far, in September, they've likewise spent about 15 percent more. And that's good news one.\par \par \uc2 Good news two, is if you look at the account balances for our customers, even those in the below median income and median income, you know, one half times two times median income, those account balances are still holding that -- from the stimulus late --- yes, early in '21, all the way through now, they basically have held the balances are multiples of where they were pre-pandemic, and then the ability to borrow is there.\par \par \uc2 So, the risk of inflation and getting it under control, which the Fed has to do also has to work against one of the best things about America is we have a consumer base, it's employed and working and earning and spending.\par \par \uc2 And so, that's where the work has to be done to bring inflation back and control and get back in balance.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Just today, we learned that \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  plans to lay off hundreds of workers, maybe as soon as next week. What do you make of that? Will you have to do something like that to be (INAUDIBLE), you own \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  , obviously.\par \par \uc2 MOYNIHAN: Well, we're fine with our headcount. We just, you know -- just to actually maintain the same amount of employees at the beginning of -- at the end of second quarter, we had the beginning, we had to hire 7,000 people. That's how robust the employment market is in terms of, you know, turnover, and it's coming down, and our company in getting back to normal levels before 19.\par \par \uc2 So, I'm confident, if we need to manage headcount, when people leave us to go join other employers, we just won't fill all the jobs, but we're in good shape. And we employ 210,000 people. We've employed that amount for the last five or seven years. So, it's been relatively stable.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Here is the treasury secretary over the weekend, asked the same question about the economy.\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2  , UNITED STATES TREASURY SECRETARY: We're not in a recession. The labor market is exceptionally strong. The unemployment rate extremely low.\par \par \uc2 It is a risk when the Fed is tightening monetary policy to address inflation. So, it's certainly a risk that we're monitoring. And we are seeing some slowdown in growth, but that's natural.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, I guess the question is this. Do you think the White House is at odds with the Fed here and trying to stamp down inflation? The White House policies recently have really put a lot of spending out there.\par \par \uc2 MOYNIHAN: Well, the Fed has a dual mandate, inflation and price stability and through inflation and employment. And basically, they've got a tough job, as I explained with the consumers with unemployment, unemployment at, you know, 3.6 percent, people getting jobs, people getting raises and stuff that makes their job tough on inflation.\par \par \uc2 And so, that's what they're pointing out is they got to take that task very seriously. And they've got to move rates, and we have to move rates almost a four and a quarter percent by the time they get done raising rates, and then they'll bring them back down, as the economy adjusts to that.\par \par \uc2 But that is the number one job is to get inflation back under control, because that means people's earning power it pays is better for them in the future. And so, just think about, you know, oil prices were up 44 percent, and our spending three months ago. Then, went to 33 and 22.\par \par \uc2 So, as prices come down as provide some relief, but we worry a lot about rents. And so, that's what the Fed has to do is get the economy to calm down. If they do it right, it would be slow it down, but not tip it over.\par \par \uc2 And if they -- if they overshoot, it will be in negative territory. So, right now, you know, you've had a couple of quarters negative growth, we project this quarter be positive in our company, and the next two or three quarters after to be slightly negative.\par \par \uc2 But we're bouncing around zero now, as the economy tries to adjust in the United States.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: But you're not worried to the original question about the spending policies. You got the Inflation Reduction Act, a lot of people said it wasn't going to do that. But moving forward after that was the student loan cancelation that's just pumping a lot of money. Do you see any warning signs there?\par \par \uc2 MOYNIHAN: You know, I think -- you know, look, during the pandemic, from the prior administration, this administration, they had to do anything they could to put people back in a condition that will then head if the pandemic not happened, that was done.\par \par \uc2 And now, we've got to let the economy normalize. And so, look, long term, we've got to make sure that we have a strong U.S. economy, we have a good balance, you know, good budget that's in good shape. And you know, that's a critical of what we doing.\par \par \uc2 We got to make that transition back. And that's what we're in the process of doing. Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Yes, you had some drop in mortgage applications. We've seen that in recent weeks. Is that affecting you all? Or a sign of housing?\par \par \uc2 MOYNIHAN: Yes, it -- well, you remember that housing appreciation was up, yes, 19, 20 percent of market or something like that. That's not -- that's too fast. And so, what the -- what the rising rate structure will drop mortgage applications and both refinance and also slow down new home purchase that hopefully tips, the price appreciation more flat.\par \par \uc2 The reality is even the stock market from midnight teens up 40 percent from then. So, asset price inflation has been very strong, and the Fed again has to bring under control. And so, the intended outcome of a higher rate structure, to higher mortgage structure is to slow down the new activity.\par \par \uc2 As to the currently outstanding mortgages of which we have, you know, $600- 700 billion on our balance sheet and mortgage and mortgage-backed securities. They're all locked in a low rate.\par \par \uc2 So, it really is going to slow down new home purchases, which is the intended outcome of tightening financial conditions.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Couple of more things, \uc2 CBS\uc2  and \uc2 Forbes\uc2  had different versions of this story. And it says, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  offers zero down mortgage in minority communities. Various cities, five, U.S. cities: Charlotte, Dallas, Detroit, Los Angeles, Miami.\par \par \uc2 So, some see this is kind of a market top, a signal. They worried has hints of what led to the big housing implosion. Others see it as proof of recent discrimination. That's why you're doing it. Others see it is just not fair. So, explain it. Why are you doing it?\par \par \uc2 MOYNIHAN: Well, back in the Ford administration, and in the 1974, there is the Equal Credit Opportunity Act. And under that, were created the ability to make loans in neighborhoods that are, you know, not economically vibrant.\par \par \uc2 So, this is about granting a down payment, which we've been doing with lots of special programs for years. To a buyer who neither who they are, as long as they meet the income requirements to buy a house in a minority -- majority, majority minority neighborhood. And so, it's where they buy the house.\par \par \uc2 First time homebuyer, you go through counselling, we give you a grant to the down payment. So, there's equity in the home, and then you buy the house. So, it's about where you buy the house. And that's important. That's important that program. And that's why we're bringing out five markets.\par \par \uc2 You know, the idea, it has anything to do with zero down payment mortgage and stuff. It's -- this is a limited program, geared just like we did $5 billion and low moderate income financing last year to help revitalize these neighborhoods.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: So, what the concern about this is what we saw at the beginning of the housing bust, this is a different deal.\par \par \uc2 MOYNIHAN: There is no one that had to do more to get us in position this company after we bought countrywide the straightening that out. It -- believed me, that you know, our loan to value our mortgage portfolios in the 60 percent range. So, it's very well suited and high FICO scores, et cetera. So, delinquencies next to nothing.\par \par \uc2 So, the -- I wouldn't worry about that not responsible growth of \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  . We've driven a lot of great growth for the -- for the shareholders. But the reality is this program is a specialized program really about these neighborhoods and investing and revitalizing these neighborhoods.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Finally, with the passing of Queen Elizabeth II, now it's King Charles III and you had interaction with the now king in business.\par \par \uc2 MOYNIHAN: Yes. So, first, you know, the Queen was an unbelievable leader across a long, long time and to think of all the world leaders she dealt with, and all the people and what she's done to lead, and our condolences go out to the family and all the citizens of the U.K. and the Commonwealth and the rest of the world.\par \par \uc2 My reaction -- my interaction with King Charles has been around the sustainable markets initiative. And that's an issue that \uc2 King Charles\uc2  and I co-chair when he was \uc2 Prince Charles\uc2  for the last three or four years, which brings together private sector CEOs to drive the common ground and how we can help accelerate the transition to the new fuel economy.\par \par \uc2 So, oil companies, airline companies, cement makers, you know, hydrogen companies, everything in there. Basically, CEO is working together, and he has an incredible power to convene. And I hope he can continue to help us convene, because at the end of the day, he believes a private sector will drive this change, and it has to be fair to all involved.\par \par \uc2 And so, his long history of an environmental 50 plus years as a king, I'm hoping that he can continue to help us convene and drive the private sector position.\par \par \uc2 We met at the G7 in Cornwall, the G20 in Europe, COP26, he was able to get the private sector involve into positions to talk to the public sector and others about it has to be led by the private sector. It has to use the expertise of energy companies to help make it happen.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day that, you know, that's how it's going to get done right. And that's what we've been driving together.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 Brian Moynihan\uc2  , CEO of \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  , we appreciate your time tonight.\par \par \uc2 MOYNIHAN: Thank you, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, the solemn ceremony, we'll take you there to Scotland. A ceremony of thanksgiving for the life and legacy of Queen Elizabeth II, we'll have a live report from London.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I'm very happy to have been here. I'm happy to hear that she died in Scotland where she loved.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I just can't believe that she is not here because obviously the whole of my life I've known the queen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A service of thanksgiving was held today in Scotland following last week's death of Queen Elizabeth II. Members of the public will be paying their respects to the late monarch as memorials continue in the run up to next Monday's funeral.\par \par \uc2 Correspondent Alex Hogan is in London with the latest tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOGAN, FOX NEWS CHANNEL CORRESPONDENT (voice over): A day full of deep tradition to honor Britain's longest reigning monarch and welcome the new king. \uc2 King Charles III\uc2  visibly emotional at Westminster Hall early Monday for the first ceremony of the day in front of the \uc2 British Parliament\uc2  . The new sovereign acknowledging the incredible expectations ahead.\par \par \uc2 KING CHARLES PHILIP ARTHUR GEORGE III, KING OF THE UNITED KINGDOM: She sets an example of selfless duty, which with God's help, and your councils, I am resolved faithfully to follow.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: A similar message later in the day at the motion of condolence at Scottish Parliament.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in Edinburgh, the king led the grim processional alongside his siblings quietly marching behind the body of the queen through the heart of Scotland's capital.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It was eerie. It was eerily quiet.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Members of the Royal Regiment of Scotland carry the casket draped with the Royal Standard and a wreath of flowers moving her majesty from Hollywood House, the Queen's official residence there to St. Giles Cathedral, the somber setting giving people here one final goodbye.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You can't help but think of people that you've loved.\par \par \uc2 HOGAN: Psalms sung at the Queen's wedding to Prince Philip welcomed her coffin into the church. Mourners will have 24 hours to see her body before it's flown back to London tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOGAN (on camera): Queen Elizabeth II's body will lie in Westminster Hall for Wednesday until the morning of the funeral, which is set for one week from today. And here in London already people are lining up, camping out for that final farewell, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Alex Hogan live in London. Alex, thanks.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KING CHARLES\uc2  : My dear mother was the friend of man, the friend of truth. The friend of age and guide of youth. Few hearts like hers with virtue warmed, few heads with knowledge so informed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: \uc2 King Charles III\uc2  speaking to Parliament today, let's bring in Fox News Senior Political Analyst Brit Hume.\par \par \uc2 Brit, you have some reflections on Queen Elizabeth II from all your coverage here in Washington and abroad.\par \par \uc2 BRIT HUME, FOX NEWS SENIOR POLITICAL ANALYST: I do, Bret. I met her actually back in 1991 when I was covering the presidency of George H. W. Bush , and she was in Washington for a state visit. And they had a little reception for members of the media and I was introduced to her and we were all told ahead of time the things you should do and things you should not do and you're not supposed to reach out and touch her or anything like that, other than a handshake.\par \par \uc2 I found her to be quite pleasant and polite. I wouldn't call her friendly, but she was quite gracious, she was impressive in that way, very restrained in the way she behaved and there had been -- and there was an episode during that visit, a few could forget, which was there was a sermon on the South Lawn, a welcoming ceremony, complete with all the music and so forth that goes with that for a foreign visitor, particularly when so close to the United States.\par \par \uc2 And the head of platform setup for the president to speak and for her there to speak as well. And the White House staff had provided a box for her to stand on because she was much shorter than President Bush and the palace staff and apparently says no, no, her Royal Majesty does not stand on boxes, the consequence of which was that when she stood to speak, the microphone was at about face level with her or below it. And she had on this enormous purple and white hat, and you couldn't see your face.\par \par \uc2 So, you had this scene at the South Lawn at the White House of a talking hat. And it was -- how she got through it was fine but it was kind of amusing at the time.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: We have a video of that.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Oh, there you are. Yes, there it is. Well, yes, you can see better in that shot but the one that was live on the air, you couldn't see her face at all.\par \par \uc2 And he later -- he later took her to a \uc2 Baltimore Orioles\uc2  game, and she was quite curious about baseball, you could tell and I couldn't help but sympathize with her because she was probably as bewildered by what she was seeing there at that professional baseball game as I would be at a cricket match. There was I --\par \par \uc2 BAIER: You know, Brit, I want to ask you, there's a lot of people out here that throughout that I've heard, you know, OK, we get it. We're going to cover this nonstop and that the pomp and circumstance and all of this.\par \par \uc2 And from America's point of view, is it overdoing it. But just talking to you about when her coronation happened as queen, and you were a child going to see that.\par \par \uc2 HUME: I was a 10-year-old boy. And I was taken to the old MacArthur Theater in Washington, which is now a CVS drugstore. But it was a -- it was a -- it was a British film house in those days, a very nice theater. And I was taken to see a film that have been made at the coronation of Queen Elizabeth, but boy, did I ever not want to go.\par \par \uc2 Can you imagine of getting his juvenile delinquent as I was off the streets to go to see a film about the queen's coronation. And I absolutely was fascinated by it, impressed by it, more interested than I could ever have imagined.\par \par \uc2 And I thought of her as quite a remarkable figure at the time. And I've thought of it in that way ever since.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you know, that long reign of her, you know, British monarchs reign, but they don't rule. And they have a very limited role. And you know, some of born it better than others. And she seemed to bear it very well. She never seemed to, you know, put a foot wrong. She always maintained her restraint and her dignity and her grace.\par \par \uc2 And that is -- and I think that is reflected in the reaction you see in the faces of the British people. You see it these observances along the streets and in the churches and so on. And I suspect that there's a lot of sentiment in that -- in that very same vein in America as well, Bret.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Brit, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 HUME: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Up next, it is getting down and dirty in New Hampshire as Republicans trying to pick a nominee capable of defeating an incumbent Democratic senator. We'll take you there live.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I am the candidate that can be \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: They need an outsider. They need someone who's connected to the people of New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The Republican primary for the Senate seat in New Hampshire getting nasty and personal. The frontrunner is a retired Army general who some in the GOP consider too extreme to beat the Democratic incumbent in New Hampshire. Democrats are actually supporting him in tomorrow's primary election. FOX Business correspondent Hillary Vaughn reports from New Hampshire tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. DON BOLDUC (RET), \uc2 U.S. ARMY\uc2  : We've got a Republican problem and a Democrat problem. And I'm the only one that says it.\par \par \uc2 HILLARY VAUGHN, FOX BUSINESS CORRESPONDENT: Republicans and Democrats outside the granite state are getting in the middle of New Hampshire's Senate primary. Republicans are targeting friendly fire at their own frontrunner, retired Army General \uc2 Don Bolduc\uc2  who they see as too controversial to win over independents and putting at risk their shot at the Senate majority.\par \par \uc2 BOLDUC: All right, can I hug you?\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: And Democrats are meddling with the moderate in the race.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You have my vote, sir.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: By trying to take out Bolduc's opponent, New Hampshire Senate President Chuck Morse.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK MORSE, (R) NEW HAMPSHIRE SENATE CANDIDATE: \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  is spending $6 million against me to make sure that I don't run against her. I think it's clear that I can beat her.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The U.S. should team up with the \uc2 Taliban\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: But it's not just Democrats that think Morse is a threat to their Senate seat. A pop up super PAC with past ties to \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  spending $4 million on ads to boost Morse and bury Bolduc. And New Hampshire's governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  has endorsed Morse and called Bolduc a conspiracy theorist.\par \par \uc2 BOLDUC: I'm not extreme in the slightest. I'm being called names on both sides of the aisle now, right. I guess it just comes with the territory when you stand up for what you believe in.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN: But Bolduc has made head libraries saying the 2020 election was stolen and questioning the need for the \uc2 FBI\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 BOLDUC: I clarified those comments. They were taken out of context. And I would never be an advocate of getting rid of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN (on camera): We're at a gun range with candidate Karoline Leavitt who is running in the Republican House primary here in New Hampshire. It's a race that's pitting two former Trump staffers, Leavitt and Matt Mowers against each other in a bitter battle that's exposing rivalries in the Republican Party. They are fighting for a chance to face off against incumbent Democrat \uc2 Chris Pappas\uc2  in November. Pappas is calling his opponents' face-off a MAGA civil war, and says whoever the GOP nominee is will be pretty, quote, "banged up." Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Hillary, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Some big names on Capitol Hill are entering their final weeks in office. And there are concerns about what the leadership will look like after the November midterms and next year. Congressional correspondent Chad Pergram has our story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 CHAD PERGRAM, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Let's make a deal but with whom? Some long-time congressional dealmakers known for masterful horse trading won't be around next year.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN, (D-WV): It's going to be hard to replace the Rob Portmans of the world and the Roy Blunts of the world.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ROB PORTMAN, (R-OH): I'll be easily replaced.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: See you around.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Not necessarily. Deal-making takes time. The granularity of policymaking isn't riveting.\par \par \uc2 PORTMAN: Controversy sells, I guess. And I think it's a problem because most Americans look at Washington and see something that's broken.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: There's not much incentive to forge bipartisan solutions when lawmakers find it easier to own the other side on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 SEN. PATRICK LEAHY, (D-VT): When I first came here, I saw Republicans and Democrats working together. We're seeing less and less of that in both parties.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: This worries those who remain.\par \par \uc2 SEN. CHRIS COONS, (D-DE): Yes, I'm concerned that we may see more sharply partisan members of the Senate after these midterm elections, and it may be even harder to get things done.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Developing friendships across the aisle is paramount when it comes to things Congress must do, like funding the government or raising the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RICHARD SHELBY\uc2  , (R-AL): Relationships are important. I have worked with Senator Leahy. Senator Leahy's politics and mine are totally different. But we work together.\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: This Congress is known for lawmakers crafting bipartisan deals on infrastructure, guns, and veterans' health benefits.\par \par \uc2 SARAH BINDER, BROOKINGS INSTITUTION: The I think the worry is who replaces the folks who are leaving? Is there ever another Shelby? Is there another Leahy?\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM: Connecticut Democrat \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  and Republican \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  brokered a deal on firearms. Manchin, Coons, plus Senators \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2  , \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  , and \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  remain in the dealmaking mix.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 PERGRAM (on camera): And the future is murky about one of the most savvy dealmakers of all. It's unclear what happens to House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  at the end of this Congress. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Chad Pergram on the Hill. Chad, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Up next, former President Trump's lawyers paint a picture of a Justice Department investigation in disarray in a new filing. We'll have an update.\par \par \uc2 First, Beyond our Borders tonight. Pakistan is grappling with food shortages after months of deadly flooding there. Water had covered about a third of the country at one point, costing more than $10 billion in damages.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at Auckland, New Zealand. One of the big stories there tonight, New Zealand's prime minister announcing the lifting of most coronavirus restrictions after more than two years. People are no longer required to wear masks in most public areas there. All vaccine mandates will be lifted, including those for tourists wishing to visit New Zealand.\par \par \uc2 Just some of the other stories Beyond our Borders tonight. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: A \uc2 Blue Origin\uc2  rocket suffered its first launch failure today. The device from the company owned by billionaire \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  veered off course about a minute after liftoff. The capsule's launch abort system immediately evacuated, lifting the unmanned aircraft off the rocket. The capsule parachuted onto a remote desert floor. It was the ninth flight for that particular rocket.\par \par \uc2 Former President Trump's legal team has filed a motion objecting to the government's candidates for special master. His lawyers say the criminal investigation into materials seized from his Florida home has spiraled out of control. They want a judge to leave in place an order temporarily halting core aspects of the Justice Department probe. Correspondent David Spunt tells us where things stand tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 DAVID SPUNT, FOX NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  's attorneys out with a new filing, insisting some of the documents in question by the government may not be classified after all. On Monday, Trump's legal team filed a lengthy 21-page response to the government's argument that a third-party arbiter, called a special master, should have limited powers. Trump's team disagrees, calling the overall investigation a document storage dispute that has spiraled out of control.\par \par \uc2 The filing states "The government wrongfully seeks to criminalize the possession by the 45th president of his own presidential and personal records." Trump landed at Dulles Airport in northern Virginia on Sunday, prompting speculation on social media as to why he is in the area for only the second time second time since leaving office. Multiple sources close to the investigation deny the \uc2 FBI\uc2  planned to visit Trump's northern Virginia golf club or meet with Trump himself. On social media Trump announced he was working from the club on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Federal prosecutors want District Judge Aileen Cannon to reconsider her ruling from last week telling investigators to temporarily stop examining more than 100 classified government documents seized from Mar-a-Lago until a special master reviews them first. They wrote that Cannon's order could impede effort to identify the existence of any additional classified records that are not being properly stored. They argue Cannon's order poses a potential for ongoing risk to national security.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 SPUNT (on camera): Department of Justice officials, Bret, have until midnight to respond to the latest filing from Trump's legal team. Federal Judge Aileen Cannon is expected to make an announcement on the special master at some point, possibly later this week. The government is appealing this case to a higher court. Bret?\par \par \uc2 BAIER: David Spunt at the Justice Department. David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Breaking just minutes ago from "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2  ," the Justice Department has issued about 40 subpoenas in the past week seeking information about the actions of former President Trump and his associates related to the 2020 election and the January 6th attack. We will continue to follow that and follow up tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the panel on the march to the midterms. First, here is what some of our FOX affiliates around the country are covering tonight. FOX 9 in Minneapolis as 15,000 union nurses across Minnesota walk off the job after failing to reach an agreement with hospital executives. The group is calling for fair contracts to improve patient care and working conditions. It's believed to be the largest private sector nurse's strike in U.S. history.\par \par \uc2 WSVN in Miami where a fire destroys nearly a dozen vehicles outside Hard Rock Stadium during a \uc2 Miami Dolphins\uc2  game. Investigators believe a tailgate party likely sparked that blaze with a grill left there. No injuries were reported.\par \par \uc2 And this is a live look at New York City. One of the big stories there tonight from FOX 5, 19-year-old Carlos Alcaraz wins his first career slam at the U.S. Open. He is now the number one tennis player in the world, the youngest ever to sit atop the rankings. The Spaniard defeated Norwegian Casper Ruud in four sets to claim the title.\par \par \uc2 That's tonight's live look outside the beltway from SPECIAL REPORT. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 CHRIS SUNUNU\uc2  , (R) NEW HAMPSHIRE: At the end of the day, we should be concerned. As someone who has run for office a lot, you've always got to run like your three votes behind. Folks in November are going to make decisions based on those electricity rates, those gasoline rates, those inflation things.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT, (R-SC): I'm excited about the candidates that we have overall because I do believe it gives us the opportunity to win back the majority and to return sanity in Washington, which will reverberate around the country.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Imagine what we can do if we protect, and better yet, expand our majority in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: All kinds of thoughts about the midterms as we close in now. We will bounce around some Senate races, just look at the polls. They give us a snapshot in time. First of all, Ohio, a new "USA Today" poll has this very tight, with the Democrat Ryan slight lead but essentially tied. Pennsylvania Senate poll, the Real Clear Politics average recent polls showing it a little tighter than this. This is the average of polls for that race in Wisconsin. The Democrat up a little bit on the incumbent Ron Johnson.\par \par \uc2 But this is interesting in that "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  " came out with a piece saying, yes, the polling and warning signs are flashing again. Quote, "Democrat Senate candidates are outrunning expectations in the same places where the polls overestimated Mr. Biden in 2020 and Mrs. Clinton in 2016. It raises the possibility that the apparent Democratic strength in Wisconsin and elsewhere is a mirage, an artifact of persistent and unaddressed biases in survey research."\par \par \uc2 Let's start there with our panel, Josh Kraushaar, "\uc2 Axios\uc2 " senior political correspondent, Kimberley Strassel, a member of the editorial board at "The Wall Street Journal," and Richard Fowler, host of the nationally syndicated "Fowler Show," contributing writer for "Forbes" magazine.\par \par \uc2 Josh, let me start with this premise about these are states where you look back at the last two presidentials, and they were way off, and these are the races that everyone is saying, wait a second, Democrats are running out there.\par \par \uc2 JOSH KRAUSHAAR, "NATIONAL JOURNAL": Yes, there was a poll in the fall of 2016 by "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  " in Wisconsin showing \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  up by 17 points. He won the state by one point. So polling, especially at this early stage, we haven't even seen the campaigns fully get underway. A lot of Republican money is pouring into these big battlegrounds. I was just in Pennsylvania, and I know \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  in leading in some of these early polls, but he is not doing any debates. He has not agreed to debate. They're worries about his health after having a stroke. And the Oz campaign clearly has momentum. So the polls aren't capturing the late movement in a lot of these Senate races. There's also a lot of money going into attack ads against these Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Yes. And Georgia, you're got a piece, Richard, about the \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  race. "\uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  talks Georgia gubernatorial race expanding the electorate days before the midterms. Bellwether race in the deeply purple state. Abrams-Kemp rematch will test who can keep their coalition together while adding voters to Georgia's dynamically changing electorate." It seems like Kemp is holding his team together at least in the early polling there.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD FOWLER, TALK RADIO HOST: Yes, that's right. It looks like Kemp is holding his team together. And if you talk to the Abrams campaign, if you talk to \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  herself, what she says is that she is not looking at the polling. She is trying to rebuild her own electorate. Her electorate is the Biden coalition plus or minus a couple of other folks. She says she is talking to African American men, and her quote is this, if African American men vote for her, she wins. If she can expand the capacity of African American men, which she believes is the emerging electorate, she wins that race.\par \par \uc2 Herein lies the challenge for her. In the last election \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doubled his lead amongst African American men. So what is she going to do there? And so we're going to have to wait to see what happens in that race. But anybody who is predicting who wins these races, Bret, I have got to tell you, they are wrong, because I think the American voter is still making up their minds. And there is not just one issue. It's not just inflation. It's not just Dobbs. There is a lot of issues on the American voter's plate. And they are still making their mind up, and I think in Georgia they're definitely making their mind up.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kimberley, the vice president was on "Meet the Press" this weekend, and there were all kinds of things that I could play for you. But we'll just play one of them here and have you racket.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  , (D) VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think this is an activist court. It is not right that we take a constitutional right from the women of America and deprive them of the ability to make decisions about their own body.\par \par \uc2 We need to hold onto the Senate and get two more, and then we can put into law the protections of Roe v. Wade. Everything is on the line.\par \par \uc2 We have a secure border in that that is a priority for any nation, including ours and our administration.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BAIER: I'll let you pick.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLEY STRASSEL, "WALL STREET JOURNAL": Wow.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: Thanks, Bret. I think one of the notable things of what she said there, and she repeated it numbers of times, is they very much broadcast what their vision is if they actually do manage to keep the Senate, and in fact, expand the number of members that they have there. Their goal is going to be maybe to restructure the court. They are going to get rid of the filibuster. There is going to be dramatic changes.\par \par \uc2 And this is not something that has gotten a lot of play. Weirdly, it's also not something that a lot of Republican Senate candidates are talking about out on the ground. But the stakes here are a lot bigger than just the race- by-race agenda. The stakes here really do get to some of the fundamental institutions of the country. And Senate Democrats have made clear they will blow them up if they get the chance.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Josh, it's interesting, fundamental institutions. We heard the Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  respond to this criticism about the illegitimacy of the court. In this environment of threats to democracy and the president now saving ultra MAGA is a threat to democracy, the vice president is saying that the court is illegitimate, essentially, is quite something as well.\par \par \uc2 KRAUSHAAR: Yes, this is very heated rhetoric. I was actually just up in New Hampshire, and I asked a Democratic Congressman \uc2 Chris Pappas\uc2  about some of the heated rhetoric, the semi-fascist rhetoric that Biden used, and he said it was unacceptable. He said it was not healthy for democracy. So I think that may please the base of the Democratic Party. They are trying to rally the base ahead of the midterms. It alienates a lot of swing voters.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: What about that, Richard? I:s that the playbook for most of these Democratic races, or do you think it just varies around the country?\par \par \uc2 FOWLER: I definitely think it varies. In a House race on the micro, it definitely hurts that decision. But on the macro, if you talk to somebody like \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  , it matters, right. Listen to what he said yesterday. He says if women vote for me, I win. You can't hurt women because he is banking on Dobbs to win him suburban white women. So the contrast is very clear between how Democrats are running their Senate races versus how they are running their House races, which is why I say, when it comes down to Election Day, it's going to be a very interesting night.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: Kimberley, to the point, Lieutenant Governor Fetterman was at an women's event this weekend and called himself "Fetter-woman." It was a little cringeworthy.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 STRASSEL: No, Republicans are going to have to wrap their arms around this. I agree with Richard and Josh, there are a lot of issues beyond just abortion and inflation, but those are pretty big issues. And the GOP in coming out of Dobbs, they weren't necessarily certain of what their strategy where you're starting to see come candidates now try to go more on the offensive, talk about their opponents and their own extremism on the issue. But we'll see where it goes.\par \par \uc2 BAIER: The countdown is on. Panel, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Finally tonight, a SPECIAL REPORT salute. A Spanish goalkeeper Jeremias Ledesma sprang into action Saturday when a fan suffered a heart attack in the final minutes of a soccer match. Ledesma sprinted across the field, as you see here, hurling a defibrillator into the stands where the fan was successfully then treated by a medical team, later transferred to a local hospital, doing OK. A SPECIAL REPORT salute to that player, Ledesma, for his decisive actions.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on SPECIAL REPORT, the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  hears from \uc2 Twitter\uc2  's former chief security officer about his allegations that the company repeatedly failed to protect its users' data.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for inviting us into your home tonight. That's it for this SPECIAL REPORT, fair, balanced, and unafraid. "JESSE WATTERS PRIMETIME" starts right now. If you notice, I don't give you the same toss that THE FIVE gives me.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FNSPBB0020220913ei9c0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Obsessing Over Trump Now Main Focus On Democrats' Agenda\b0\par\par\uc2 Sean Hannity, Lara Trump, Kayleigh McEnany, Mike Huckabee, Leo  Terrell, Sara Carter \par \uc2 8,017 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 September 2022\par \uc2 Fox News: Hannity\par \uc2 SHAN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and Programming Copyright 2022 Fox News Network, LLC. ALL  RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC.  All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SEAN HANNITY\uc2 , FOX NEWS HOST: All right. Thank you, Tucker. I'll take the seven seconds. I appreciate it with gratitude and thanks.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to HANNITY.\par \par \uc2 And tonight, \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  is using 9/11 to trash Republicans. Humpty Dumpty has a big new job teaching your children. And Democrat \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2  who rails against your Second Amendment rights just lost track of her own guns.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we are only 57 days away from this tipping point midterm election. The housing market is on the verge of collapse, inflation is at a 40-plus year high, we have been in a recession for months and months and months. The southern border is in a state of chaos, violent crime is spiking all over the country. Fentanyl deaths, they too are at an all-time high.\par \par \uc2 So, now, with the midterms around the corner, Democrats want you to forget all about this. Instead, they want you to focus on only three main distractions. Here's their game plan for the next 57 days.\par \par \uc2 They are attempting to divide the country on the basis of race and gender and age, et cetera. It's their usual playbook. Their election year playbook, or election year lie, depending on how you want to characterize it. Republicans are racist, sexist, homophobic, xenophobic, Islamophobic, they want dirty air, water, you know, the routine.\par \par \uc2 Second, Democrats want to gin up hysteria around abortion and yet another lie, they will attempt to scare voters by claiming that abortion is illegal that simply is not true. After the Dobbs ruling, abortion is still very much legal in the vast majority of states, and I have it on good authority that Republicans in the Senate will bring up a national abortion law sometime this week that would have a mandate of time of 15 weeks which is what the Mississippi law called for.\par \par \uc2 And that brings us to the third distraction. As we speak, Democrats are trying to dehumanize and vilify, quote, those evil rascally MAGA Republicans and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  supporters, in order to rally their base. According to Biden, and other Democrats, half of America is now a threat to democracy simply because they now support or once supported \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So instead of trying to fix the economy or dare talk about it or the border or the fentanyl or opioid crisis, or the crime crisis, Democrats have been wasting almost all of their time and billions of your tax dollars with one investigation after investigation into all things \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and this has been going on now for years.\par \par \uc2 First, it was the Russia hoax and it was Comey's Crossfire Hurricane, Mueller's witch hunt, the emoluments clause investigation from the House Oversight Committee, a House Ways and Means investigation into Trump's taxes, another House investigation into Trump's hotel lease, another House investigation into foreign gift disclosures, a D.C. probe into Trump's inauguration fundraising, another similar investigation from the Southern District of New York and the Eastern District of New York and from New Jersey's attorney general..\par \par \uc2 One impeachment investigation into a regular phone call with Ukraine, a second impeachment investigation into January 6th, yet another House committee investigation into January 6th that is still ongoing, with more hearings to come.\par \par \uc2 And then there's the southern district of New York's investigation into pardons, a DOJ probe into Trump's political action committee. Another House investigation into the Trump administration's security clearance process, other investigations into Trump's property valuations and, of course, his social media, Truth Social, \uc2 the Trump Foundation\uc2 , as well as a variety of other tax investigations, and, of course, the National Archives investigation that led to the Mar-a-Lago raid.\par \par \uc2 You got all that? We'll ask you at the end of the show if you remember.\par \par \uc2 After all of this, Trump has not been charged with any crime whatsoever and they're trying hard, but there's been nothing. That's because the partisan Democrats in charge of all of these witch hunts, they're not investigating crimes. They're investigating a person in a blatant attempt to smear, slander, destroy him and destroy his supporters.\par \par \uc2 It's the old Soviet Union saying, show me the man, I'll find you a crime. Except they haven't actually found a crime yet, so I guess they'll make that up too. So do not take the bait. For the next 57 days, do not get distracted.\par \par \uc2 Democrats, they'll call you semi-fascist. They're the ones who stop your kids from going to school for two years. They're the ones who fired you if you refuse to vaccine. They're the ones that blocked you from going to church during COVID. They're the ones who hired new 87,000 IRS agents to audit you and every small business in the country.\par \par \uc2 They're the ones who caused inflation with record high spending. They're the ones who are blocking the production of oil gas and energy independence in this country. They're the ones who are emptying out our prisons. They're the ones that support defunding and dismantling police departments. They're the ones that want to end all cash bail and making all of our communities less safe.\par \par \uc2 Republicans on the other hand are now offering a new contract with America. They're calling it commitments to America. This will be out in the next two weeks.\par \par \uc2 What they stand for, what their vision for the future of this country is lower taxes, less bureaucracy, fewer regulations, energy independence, law and order, so we can be safe and secure, border security, schools that actually teach reading, writing, math, science, history and computers, and not CRT and social justice and gender identity.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Republicans are also vowing to conduct real oversight with investigations into the FBA -- the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , the CDC, the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , the DOJ, the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and the entire Biden administration. This will reverse itself.\par \par \uc2 Now, this election is about restoring sanity and common sense to government, a time when our president has lost touch with both of these realities. If he's even aware that today is Monday. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It's frustrating. It's inconvenient, and it's bad for the environment. And there's simply no reason for it. This is the United States of America for God's sake. This is who we are. This is not -- this is not what we should be doing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Here with reaction, Texas Senator Ted Cruz.\par \par \uc2 Senator, first question, do you agree with me and my premise here, they can't run on anything that they're successful at, anything that they've done to improve the country. So I say they've got three things -- January 6, hating \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , they got the usual playbook, Republicans are racist and sexist, xenophobic, and January 6th, and that's about it, and abortion.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Sean, you're exactly right. If you look at the issues that that are racking the country, that are hurting Americans across the country, you have number one, inflation. The Democrats have no plan for inflation other than to make it worse, to spend trillions more and drive inflation up even more. Gas prices, they have no plan to reduce gas prices other than to make it worse by hammering American oil and gas production.\par \par \uc2 Crime, they have no plan to reduce crime other than to make it worse by continuing to support radical leftist DAs and leftists who try to undermine defund or abolish the police and illegal immigration, they have no plan to stop. They don't want to stop. Their plan is more illegal immigration, more chaos at the border.\par \par \uc2 And so, their only political strategy which you can see their cronies in the corporate media leaning in on is distraction. They want to scream \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  all day long because there is nothing on Planet Earth they hate more than \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 . They want to unleash corrupt partisans at the Department of Justice and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to go after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to try to distract people.\par \par \uc2 And they want to send \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  up to give the most divisive and hateful speech we have seen a president give in our lifetimes, calling half of the country fascists. They're hoping to scare people into showing up and voting for an agenda that's failing. And what's amazing, they don't -- they don't plan to change. They don't promise change. They promise more of the same, more of the same disastrous policy agenda.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: All right. So in 2017, the media mob, they played a big part in the election. They peddled \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 's bought and paid for dirty Russian dossier. In 2020, they pushed the narrative that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 's laptop was nothing but Russian disinformation, it was anything but.\par \par \uc2 Now, the question is -- you're a debate champ at Harvard. \uc2 Alan Dershowitz\uc2  once said on this program, you were one of the smartest students he ever had the honor of teaching. So you have an ability to debate, and you're really good at it. Others do not have the same skills that you do.\par \par \uc2 When the media comes after any Republican candidate or conservative candidate, and they go after these three issues, can you please teach some of your colleagues because I've watched some of them and they need help, please teach them the art of pivoting away from what they want to talk about which is everyday \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , every day abortion and move on to the issues that are impacting this country?\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Sean, you are preaching to the choir. We need to be positive, affirmative with our message. We need to be happy warriors. The angrier they get, the more we should laugh and be joyful because their agenda doesn't work.\par \par \uc2 And you're right, the Democrats and the stooges in the corporate media, they just want to repeat the attacks. And it's a real mistake when you see Republicans that will have a long discussion about this particular attack or that that particular attack, rather than just going on affirmatively our solutions.\par \par \uc2 Look, what do people want? People want jobs with good wages. People want their families to be safe and secure. People want their rights protected. That is a common sense agenda that the overwhelming majority of Americans agree with.\par \par \uc2 And so, the corporate media doesn't want to talk about it at all. It's our responsibility to lean and explain, listen, was America better a couple of years ago when we had the lowest unemployment rate in 50 years or is it better now when people are hurting across this country? Was America better when gas was two dollars a gallon or four dollars a gallon under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? Was America better when we had the lowest rate of illegal immigration in 45 years, which is what it was the last year the Trump presidency, or is America better now with utter chaos and 4.2 million people coming across the border illegally?\par \par \uc2 It ain't complicated. Everybody knows. There isn't a Democrat out there that can defend this policy failure and so, they count on the media just to change the topic. We need to not fall for it. We need to instead urge the American people -- if you don't like the path we're on, we can change the path of November.\par \par \uc2 And, Sean, mark my words -- the media are trying to convince Americans suddenly the Democrats are surging in November. What utter garbage. Republicans are going to win the House and we're going to win the Senate.\par \par \uc2 And I can tell you, between now and election day, I am on the road nonstop campaigning for Senate candidates and House candidates because we're going to come in with big majorities to start the work of turning the country around.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Senator, I hope you're right and are you better off than you were since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's been president? On -- based on any measure you can make, I don't see we're better off in any way, shape, manner and form.\par \par \uc2 Now, Kevin McCarthy, I was there -- I was the emcee of the night that Newt Gingrich became speaker of the House. He had a Contract with America. That's 28 years ago. And I'm getting old senator. And anyway, but he's coming out with commitments for America.\par \par \uc2 I don't know why and I'm not this -- I'm not being critical of \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  here. As of now, he has not embraced the idea of the Senate doing it as well. I would like to see the Senate do it. If Mitch doesn't want to do it, then I assume the individual candidates can sign on to it.\par \par \uc2 There's something about a pledge of promise in writing that I think sends a powerful message that you mean it and you're going to get it done, and if you don't you're going to be held accountable.\par \par \uc2 CRUZ: Listen, I agree. And I will say, there's a division of thought in Washington. There's some people who've been in Washington a long time that think the way you win elections is just you don't stand for anything. You don't engage on issues.\par \par \uc2 I think that's exactly backwards. I think the way you win elections is you draw a line in the sand. I think the way you win elections is you say these are the stakes and here's what I will do when we win.\par \par \uc2 And when you're clear and when you follow through on your promises, that's what makes people show up to vote. And so, every candidate out there that is campaigning on specific, clear promises that they intend to follow through, I salute them.\par \par \uc2 But I'll tell you once we win, you and I both know that that the battle is not over on November 8th. That once we have a majority, we need to do something with the majority and so, that's why I'm fighting not just to win in November, but to elect strong conservatives that will then have the guts.\par \par \uc2 And let me tell you one of the things that we ought to commit to do. Every single Republican ought to say, we will not vote to fund these 87,000 new \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents. We're not going to fund Biden's army to harass Americans and we're going to take that money and put it on our southern border to secure our border. That ought to be a promise every Republican gets behind now and that we follow through on when we have the majorities next year.\par \par \uc2 Senator Ted Cruz of Texas, thank you.\par \par \uc2 All right. So, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  thinks that 74 million Americans are semi-fascists and threats to democracy, but perhaps no one has more disdain for we the American people than \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 . Yesterday on the 21st anniversary of 9/11, Hillary seemingly comparing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  supporters to terrorists. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEDO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER SECRETARY OF STATE: We have also I think been reminded about how important it is to try to deal with extremism of any kind, especially when it uses violence to try to achieve political and ideological goals. So I'm one who thinks that there are lessons still to be learned from what happened to us on that we should be very aware of uh during this time in our country and the world's history.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Here with reaction, FOX News contributor \uc2 Lara Trump\uc2 , "Outnumbered" co-host \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2 , and former Arkansas Governor \uc2 Mike Huckabee\uc2  is with us.\par \par \uc2 Lara, we'll start with you. Your reaction to that and is there anything more extreme than Hillary Clinton lecturing anybody on morality or the law or right and wrong? I think America rejected her policies outright.\par \par \uc2 LARA TRUMP, FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yeah. It's pretty rich coming from Hillary.\par \par \uc2 But, Sean, we know the truth. We know that the Democrats, whether it's Hillary Clinton or \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , love to throw out this label and slap it on anybody of extreme if you don't fall in lock step with what they think with their view. They do it all the time.\par \par \uc2 And we know that these are the people that make the rules. They can change definitions of words. It's amazing. Definitions like recession or woman. Man, they're just able to change those at the drop of a hat, no problem.\par \par \uc2 We know that they were happy to look at violent riots where people were killed where government buildings were burned to the ground small businesses destroyed across America. Those were mostly peaceful protests but if you happen to be in Washington, D.C. on January 6, maybe nowhere even near the Capitol having done nothing wrong, you deserve according to these people to have your lives destroyed.\par \par \uc2 And while we're at it, you could have a private server. You could Bleach Bit 33,000 emails and destroy government phones with hammers. That's fine. But if your last name is Trump, you deserve to have your home raided, and your wife have her personal belongings gone through by a dozen strangers. That's the truth.\par \par \uc2 And if we really want to get to the great divide here in America, we can go back and pinpoint where it all started and it started and this division in America started with that woman right there with \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and her lie about Russia collusion. So the next time she wants to get on a soap box and preach to Americans, maybe she should just step in front of a mirror instead.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: Well, is it extreme, \uc2 Kayleigh McEnany\uc2  that you give up the energy independence that you inherited and you're going to suck up to the Iranians, the Venezuelans, Saudi Arabia, and \uc2 OPEC\uc2  for the lifeblood of the world's economy, when we have all these natural resources here? To me, that's pretty extreme and it's costing all of us a fortune.\par \par \uc2 KAYLEIGH MCENANY\uc2 , "OUTNUMBERED" CO-HOST: Absolutely. Is it extreme to allow 2 million illegal immigrants to come through our borders during one fiscal year? To allow fentanyl to pour over our borders? To allow 685 illegal immigrants to die in the process as they try to cross the border? All of that's extreme.\par \par \uc2 I agree with Lara about the need for a mirror. We have a president who called MAGA Republicans, that's half the country, clear and present dangers, threats to democracy. They do not respect the Constitution, he said. I should say we, I'm a MAGA Republican -- and so on and so forth.\par \par \uc2 That was one week, and then the next week, Sean, I was stunned. I was sitting there I'm going through the news feed and I see that \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  draws this really opaque contrast between American citizens and the terrorists for whom I saw that that man we all have seen the picture of the falling man falling from the burning tower drawing a comparison between American citizens and the heinous monsters who committed one of the most evil acts in our country's history.\par \par \uc2 That is despicable and the very next new story I see is the fact that Khalid Sheikh Mohammed and four other Guantanamo detainees, terrorists, architects of 9/11 are not going to get the death penalty potentially. It's amazing.\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: You know, Governor, you just have a great sense of the political environment. I know a lot of people are predicting a wave election year. We've seen it in '94, 2010, 2016, I guess you could say as well. Others are predicting it.\par \par \uc2 I don't like to make that prediction. I like to act like we're five points behind, seven points behind and work that much harder. Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 MIKE HUCKABEE\uc2 , FOX NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: There's only two ways to run in an election, unopposed and scared. We're not unopposed, we better run scared, or we'll end up getting hurt.\par \par \uc2 The biggest thing we have to do is get the message out there. When \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  says that the MAGA Republicans are a threat, we need to just say now, let's make sure we all understand MAGA means Make America Great Again.\par \par \uc2 So, if that's something that is a threat to the country -- well, write me up for it because I'm all in for making America great.\par \par \uc2 Apparently, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not interested in making America great again and I would just say he's doing a heck of a job not making America great. He's making it awful.\par \par \uc2 And we need to quit acting like we're